 (CW
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Print to the comment window

  [47mCw[0m is a macro that expands to a function whose guard is [47mt[0m.  For a
  guarded variant of [47mcw[0m, see [fmx-cw].  For variants of [47mcw[0m that
  provide readable output (suffix [47m\"!\"[0m) and are never inhibited
  (suffix [47m\"+\"[0m), see [cw!], [cw+], and [cw!+].  For corresponding
  functions see [fmt-to-comment-window], [fmt-to-comment-window!],
  [fmt-to-comment-window+], and [fmt-to-comment-window!+].

  Example:

    (cw \"The goal is ~x0 and the alist is ~x1.~%\"
        (untranslate term t nil)
        unify-subst)

  Logically, this expression is equivalent to [47mnil[0m.  However, it has the
  effect of first printing to the so-called ``comment window'' the
  [47m[fmt][0m string as indicated.  Thus, [47mcw[0m is like [47mfmt[0m (see [fmt]) except
  in four important ways.  First, it is a macro whose calls expand to
  calls of a [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode function.  Second, it neither takes nor
  returns the ACL2 [47m[state][0m; logically [47mcw[0m simply returns [47mnil[0m, although
  it prints to a [3mcomment window[0m that just happens to share the
  terminal screen with the standard character output [47m[*standard-co*][0m.
  Third, its [47mfmt[0m args are positional references, so that for example

    (cw \"Answers: ~x0 and ~x1\" ans1 ans2)

  prints in the same manner as:

    (fmt \"Answers: ~x0 and ~x1\"
         (list (cons #\\0 ans1) (cons #\\1 ans2))
         *standard-co* state nil)

  And finally, output from [47mcw[0m is suppressed if the [47mCOMMENT[0m type of
  output is suppressed; see [set-inhibit-output-lst].

  Typically, calls of [47mcw[0m are embedded in [47m[prog2$][0m forms, e.g.,

    (prog2$ (cw ...)
            (mv a b c))

  which has the side-effect of printing to the comment window and
  logically returning [47m(mv a b c)[0m.

    General Form:
    (cw fmt-string arg1 arg2 ... argn)

  where n is between 0 and 9 (inclusive).  The macro uses
  [47m[fmt-to-comment-window][0m, passing it the column [47m0[0m and [evisc-tuple]
  [47mnil[0m, after assembling the appropriate alist binding the [47m[fmt][0m vars
  #\\0 through #\\9; see [fmt].  If you want

    (a) more than 10 vars,
    (b) vars other than the digit chars,
    (c) a different column, or
    (d) a different evisc-tuple,

  then call [47m[fmt-to-comment-window][0m instead.

  Finally, we discuss another way to create formatted output that also
  avoids the need to pass in the ACL2 [47m[state][0m.  The idea is to use
  wormholes; see [wormhole].  Below is a function you can write,
  along with some calls, providing an illustration of this approach.

    (defun my-fmt-to-comment-window (str alist)
      (wormhole 'my-fmt-to-comment-window
                '(lambda (whs) whs)
                (list str alist)
                '(pprogn
                  (fms (car (@ wormhole-input))
                       (cadr (@ wormhole-input))
                       *standard-co*
                       state
                       nil)
                  (value :q))
                :ld-verbose nil
                :ld-error-action :return ; harmless return on error
                :ld-prompt nil))

    ; A non-erroneous call:
    (my-fmt-to-comment-window \"Here is ~x0 for your inspection~%\"
                              (list (cons #\\0 'foo)))

    ; An error inside the fmt string (unbound fmt var); note that even
    ; with the error, the wormhole is exited.
    (my-fmt-to-comment-window \"Here is ~x1 for your inspection~%\"
                              (list (cons #\\0 'foo)))

    ; A guard violation in the binding; note that even with the error,
    ; the wormhole is exited.
    (my-fmt-to-comment-window \"Here is ~x0 for your inspection~%\"
                              (list (cons #\\0 (car 'foo))))


