 (DECLARE
  (PROGRAMMING ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Extra declarations that can occur in function definitions, [let]
  bindings, and so forth.

  Common Lisp provides a declaration mechanism that allows the
  programmer to explain additional information to the compiler.  For
  instance:

    * The programmer might declare that some variable always has some
      particular type.  The compiler might then, depending on its
      optimization/safety settings, either add run-time checks to
      ensure that this really is true, or optimize the compiled code
      by assuming the variable has the correct type.

    * The programmer might declare that some variable is [47mignore[0md.  The
      compiler might then, instead of warning the programmer that the
      variable is never used, explicitly check to make sure that it
      really is never used.

  ACL2 supports the above kinds of declarations, and also adds its own
  kinds of declarations for specifying things like the [guard]s and
  [measure]s of functions, as described in [xargs].

  There are also other kinds of Common Lisp declarations that ACL2 does
  not support, e.g., pertaining to inlining, safety settings,
  variable lifetime, and so forth.


Usage

  Examples:

    (declare (ignore x y z))
    (declare (ignorable x y z)
             (irrelevant w) ; for DEFUN only
             (type integer i j k)
             (type (satisfies integerp) m1 m2))
    (declare (xargs :guard (and (integerp i)
                                (<= 0 i))
                    :guard-hints ((\"Goal\" :use (:instance lemma3
                                                  (x (+ i j)))))))

  General Form:

    (declare d1 ... dn)

  where, in ACL2, each [47mdi[0m is of one of the following forms:

    [47m(ignore v1 ... vn)[0m
      where each [47mvi[0m is a variable introduced in the immediately superior
      lexical environment.  These variables must not occur free in
      the scope of the declaration.  This declaration can be useful
      for inhibiting compiler warnings; see also [set-ignore-ok].
    [47m(ignorable v1 ... vn)[0m
      where each [47mvi[0m is a variable introduced in the immediately superior
      lexical environment.  These variables need not occur free in
      the scope of the declaration.  This declaration can be useful
      for inhibiting compiler warnings; see also [set-ignore-ok].
    [47m(irrelevant v1 ... vn)[0m
      where each [47mvi[0m is a formal parameter declared at the top level of a
      surrounding [47m[defun][0m form, as shown below.  See
      [irrelevant-formals] for more information.
    [47m(type type-spec v1 ... vn)[0m
      where each [47mvi[0m is a variable introduced in the immediately superior
      lexical environment and [47mtype-spec[0m is a type specifier (as
      described in the documentation for [type-spec]).  This
      declaration can be useful for optimizing Common Lisp execution
      speed.  See also [the].
    [47m(xargs :key1 val1 ... :keyn valn)[0m
      where the legal values of the keys and values are described in the
      documentation for [xargs].  These declarations are only allowed
      at the top level of definitions ([47m[defun][0m and [47m[defmacro][0m, as
      shown below), and convey information such as the [guard] and
      [measure] for a function.
    [47m(optimize ...)[0m
      for example, [47m(optimize (safety 3))[0m.  This is allowed only at the top
      level of [47m[defun][0m forms and is probably only rarely of any
      interest.  See any Common Lisp documentation for more
      information.

  Declarations in ACL2 may occur only where [47mdcl[0m occurs in the following
  display (not including lambda objects, discussed later below, and
  not showing documentation strings here, which are essentially
  ignored by ACL2)):

    * [47m(DEFUN name args dcl ... dcl body)[0m

    * [47m(DEFMACRO name args dcl ... dcl body)[0m

    * [47m(LET ((v1 t1) ...) dcl ... dcl body)[0m

    * [47m(MV-LET (v1 ...) term dcl ... dcl body)[0m

    * [47m(FLET ((name args dcl ... dcl body) ...))[0m

    * [47m(MACROLET ((name args dcl ... dcl body) ...))[0m

  Each of the cases above permits certain declarations, as follows.

    * [47mDEFUN[0m: [47m(ignore ignorable irrelevant type optimize xargs)[0m

    * [47mDEFMACRO[0m: [47m(ignore ignorable type xargs)[0m

    * [47mLET[0m: [47m(ignore ignorable type)[0m

    * [47mMV-LET[0m: [47m(ignore ignorable type)[0m

    * [47mFLET[0m: [47m(ignore ignorable type)[0m

    * [47mMACROLET[0m: [47m(ignore ignorable type)[0m

  Of course, declarations are permitted in macro calls to the extent
  that they are permitted in the macroexpansions.  For example,
  [47mdeclare[0m forms generated by calls of [47m[let*][0m and [47m[case-match][0m may
  wind up in corresponding [47m[let][0m forms in the macroexpansions, where
  they would be subject to the restrictions on [47mdeclare[0m forms for [47mlet[0m
  shown just above.

  Also see [lambda] for discussion of lambda objects and their legal
  [47mdeclare[0m forms.

  [47mDeclare[0m is defined in Common Lisp.  See any Common Lisp documentation
  for more information.


Subtopics

  [Declare-stobjs]
      Declaring a formal parameter name to be a single-threaded object

  [Set-ignore-ok]
      Allow unused formals and locals without an [47mignore[0m or [47mignorable[0m
      declaration

  [Set-irrelevant-formals-ok]
      Allow irrelevant formals in definitions

  [Type-spec]
      Type specifiers can be used in Common Lisp type declarations and
      [the] forms, and may result in improved efficiency of
      execution.

  [Xargs]
      Extra arguments, for example to give [hints] to [47m[defun][0m")
 (DECLARE-STOBJS
  (STOBJ DECLARE)
  "Declaring a formal parameter name to be a single-threaded object

  When a [47m[defun][0m uses one of its formals as a single-threaded object
  ([stobj]), the [47mdefun[0m [3mmust[0m include a declaration that the formal is
  to be so used.  An exception is the formal ``[47m[state][0m,'' which if
  not declared as explained below, may still be used provided an
  appropriate global ``declaration'' is issued: see [set-state-ok].

  If the formal in question is [47mcounters[0m then an appropriate declaration
  is

    (declare (xargs :stobjs counters))

  or, more generally,

    (declare (xargs :stobjs (... counters ...)))

  where all the single-threaded formals are listed.

  For such a declaration to be legal it must be the case that all the
  names have previously been defined as single-threaded objects with
  [47m[defstobj][0m.

  When an argument is declared to be single-threaded the guard of the
  function is augmented by conjoining to it the condition that the
  argument satisfy the recognizer for the single-threaded object.
  Furthermore, the syntactic checks done to enforce the legal use of
  single-threaded objects are also sufficient to allow these guard
  conjuncts to be automatically proved.

  The obvious question arises: Why does ACL2 insist that you declare
  stobj names before using them in [47mdefun[0ms if you can only declare
  names that have already been defined with [47mdefstobj[0m?  What would go
  wrong if a formal were treated as a single-threaded object if and
  only if it had already been so defined?

  Suppose that one user, say Jones, creates a book in which [47mcounters[0m is
  defined as a single-threaded object.  Suppose another user, Smith,
  creates a book in which [47mcounters[0m is used as an ordinary formal
  parameter.  Finally, suppose a third user, Brown, wishes to use
  both books.  If Brown includes Jones' book first and then Smith's,
  then Smith's function treats [47mcounters[0m as single-threaded.  But if
  Brown includes Smith's book first, the argument is treated as
  ordinary.

  ACL2 insists on the declaration to ensure that the definition is
  processed the same way no matter what the context.")
 (DEFABBREV
  (MACROS EVENTS PROGRAMMING)
  "A convenient form of macro definition for simple expansions

    Examples:
    (defabbrev snoc (x y) (append y (list x)))
    (defabbrev sq (x) (declare (type (signed-byte 8) x)) (* x x))

    General Form:
    (defabbrev name (v1 ... vn) doc-string decl1 ... declk body)

  where [47mname[0m is a new function symbol, the [47mvi[0m are distinct variable
  symbols, and [47mbody[0m is a term.  The [47mdecli[0m, if supplied, should be
  legal [47mdeclare[0m forms; see [declare].  [47mDoc-string[0m, if non-[47mnil[0m, is an
  optional string that can provide documentation but is essentially
  ignored by ACL2.

  Roughly speaking, the [47mdefabbrev[0m event is akin to defining [47mf[0m so that
  [47m(f v1 ... vn) = body[0m.  But rather than do this by adding a new
  axiom, [47mdefabbrev[0m defines [47mf[0m to be a macro so that [47m(f a1 ... an)[0m
  expands to [47mbody[0m, with the ``formals,'' [47mvi[0m, replaced by the
  ``actuals,'' [47mai[0m.

  For example, if [47msnoc[0m is defined as shown in the first example above,
  then [47m(snoc (+ i j) temp)[0m is just an abbreviation for

    (append temp (list (+ i j))).

  In order to generate efficiently executable Lisp code, the macro that
  [47mdefabbrev[0m introduces uses a [47m[let][0m to bind the ``formals'' to the
  ``actuals.'' Consider the second example above.  Logically
  speaking, [47m(sq (ack i j))[0m is an abbreviation for [47m(* (ack i j) (ack i
  j))[0m.  But in fact the macro for [47msq[0m introduced by [47mdefabbrev[0m actually
  arranges for [47m(sq (ack i j))[0m to expand to:

    (let ((x (ack i j)))
      (* x x))

  which executes more efficiently than [47m(* (ack i j) (ack i j))[0m.

  In the theorem prover, the [47mlet[0m above expands to

    ((lambda (x) (* x x)) (ack i j))

  and thence to [47m(* (ack i j) (ack i j))[0m.

  It is important to note that the term in [47mbody[0m should not contain a
  call of [47mname[0m --- i.e., [47mdefabbrev[0m should not be used in place of
  [47mdefun[0m when the function is recursive.  ACL2 will not complain when
  the [47mdefabbrev[0m form is processed, but instead ACL2 will more than
  likely go into an infinite loop during macroexpansion of any form
  that has a call of [47mname[0m.

  It is also important to note that the parameters of any call of a
  macro defined by defabbrev will, as is the case for the parameters
  of a function call, be evaluated before the body is evaluated,
  since this is the evaluation order of [47m[let][0m.  This may lead to some
  errors or unexpected inefficiencies during evaluation if the body
  contains any conditionally evaluated forms like [47mcond[0m, [47mcase[0m, or [47mif[0m.
  Consider the following example.

    (defabbrev foo (x y)
      (if (test x) (bar y) nil))

  Notice a typical one-step expansion of a call of [47mfoo[0m (see [trans1]):

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (foo expr1 expr2)
     (LET ((X EXPR1) (Y EXPR2))
          (IF (TEST X) (BAR Y) NIL))
    ACL2 !>

  Now imagine that [47mexpr2[0m is a complicated expression whose evaluation
  is intended only when the predicate [47mtest[0m holds of [47mexpr1[0m.  The
  expansion above suggests that [47mexpr2[0m will always be evaluated by the
  call [47m(foo expr1 expr2)[0m, which may be inefficient (since perhaps we
  only need that value when [47mtest[0m is true of [47mexpr1[0m).  The evaluation
  of [47mexpr2[0m may even cause an error, for example in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode if
  the expression [47mexpr2[0m has been constructed in a manner that could
  cause a guard violation unless [47mtest[0m holds of [47mexpr1[0m.")
 (DEFABSSTOBJ (EVENTS STOBJ)
  "Define a new abstract single-threaded object

  We assume familiarity with single-threaded objects; see [stobj] and
  see [defstobj].  The event [47mdefabsstobj[0m defines a so-called
  ``abstract stobj'', a notion we introduce briefly now and then
  explain in more depth below.

  Recall that a [defstobj] event produces logical definitions for
  several functions: a recognizer, which characterizes the [stobj] in
  terms of lists; a creator, which produces an initial suitable list
  structure; and field accessors and updaters, defined in terms of
  [47m[nth][0m and [47m[update-nth][0m.  [47mDefabsstobj[0m provides a way to define
  alternate definitions for ``stobj primitives'' for a corresponding
  single-threaded object.  These stobj primitives include a
  recognizer, a creator, and other ``exported'' functions.  In
  essence, [47mdefabsstobj[0m establishes interface functions, or
  ``exports'', on a new stobj that is a copy of an existing stobj,
  its ``foundation'', which is either [3mconcrete[0m (introduced by
  [47m[defstobj][0m) or [3mabstract[0m (introduced by [47mdefabsstobj[0m).

  We begin below with an introduction to abstract [stobj]s.  We then
  explain the [47m[defabsstobj][0m event by way of an example.  We conclude
  by giving summary documentation for the [47mdefabsstobj[0m event.

  For another introduction to abstract stobjs, see the paper ``Abstract
  Stobjs and Their Application to ISA Modeling'' by Shilpi Goel,
  Warren A. Hunt, Jr., and Matt Kaufmann, in the proceedings of {ACL2
  Workshop 2013 | http://www.cs.uwyo.edu/~ruben/acl2-13}.

  [31;1mINTRODUCTION[0m

  We start with a brief review of [stobj]s and some potential problems
  with them, followed by an introduction to abstract stobjs and how
  they can avoid these problems.  Prior experience with stobjs will
  probably help the reader to absorb the ideas below.

  Recall that single-threaded objects, or [stobj]s, provide a way for
  ACL2 users to stay within the ACL2 logic --- where every data
  object is an atom or a [47m[cons][0m of data objects --- while obtaining
  the benefits of fast evaluation through destructive updates.
  Consider for example this very simple event.

    (defstobj st fld)

  This event introduces a recognizer, [47mstp[0m, and a creator, [47mcreate-st[0m,
  for a data structure consisting of a single field accessed and
  updated by functions [47mfld[0m and [47mupdate-fld[0m, respectively.  Each of
  these four primitive functions has both a logical definition, which
  is used when the prover reasons about the function, and an
  executable definition, which is used in raw Lisp.  In the logic,
  [47mstp[0m recognizes objects that have the requisite fields.  In raw
  Lisp, there is a ``live stobj'', which is typically an array object
  whose fields correspond to those specified by the [47m[defstobj][0m event.
  (If there is a single stobj field that is an array or hash-table
  field, then that field is the entire stobj in raw Lisp; but we
  ignore that case below.)

  Here are the logical definition and the executable definition,
  respectively, that are introduced for the field accessor, [47mfld[0m,
  introduced above.  Notice that since a stobj is represented in raw
  Lisp using an array, the raw Lisp accessor uses a raw Lisp array
  accessor, [47msvref[0m.  (You can see all the logical and executable
  definitions by evaluating the form [47m(trace$ defstobj-axiomatic-defs
  defstobj-raw-defs)[0m before evaluating the [47m[defstobj][0m form.)

    ; logical definition
    (defun fld (st)
      (declare (xargs :guard (stp st)
                      :verify-guards t))
      (nth 0 st))

    ; executable (raw Lisp) definition
    (defun fld (st)
      (svref st 0))

  Sophisticated programming with stobjs can provide efficient
  implementations of algorithms, but may require the preservation of
  a complex invariant.  One can, of course, define a function to
  implement such an invariant after introducing the stobj, as
  follows.

    ; Introduce a stobj.
    (defstobj st fld1 ... fldk)

    ; Define an invariant on that stobj.
    (defun good-stp (st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st))
      ...)

    ; Define some basic functions that update the stobj and preserve the
    ; invariant.
    (defun update-st (... st ...)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st
                      :guard (and (good-stp st) ...)))
      ...)
    ...

    ; Prove that the invariant is indeed preserved by those basic functions.
    (defthm good-stp-update-st
      (implies (and (good-stp st)
                    ...)
               (good-stp (update-st ... st ...))))
    ...

    ; Implement algorithms built on the basic functions.
    (defun foo (... st ...)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st
                      :guard (and (good-stp st) ...)))
      ... (update-st ... st ...) ...)

    ; Prove invariance theorems about these algorithms.
    (defthm good-stp-foo
      (implies (and (good-stp st)
                    ...)
               (good-stp (foo ... st ...))))
    ...

    ; Prove other properties of these algorithms.
    (defthm foo-is-correct
      (implies (and (good-stp st)
                    ...)
               (some-property (foo ... st ...))))
    ...

  But there are at least two potential difficulties in using stobjs as
  described above.

   1. When [47mfoo[0m is executed on concrete data in the ACL2 loop, the guard
      check may be expensive because [47m(good-stp st)[0m is expensive.

   2. Reasoning about [47mfoo[0m (using rules like [47mfoo-is-correct[0m above) involves
      proving hypotheses of invariance theorems, which may be
      complicated for the user to manage or slow for the theorem
      prover.

  The [47mdefabsstobj[0m event offers an opportunity to address these issues.
  It introduces a new stobj, which we call an ``abstract stobj'',
  which is associated with a corresponding ``foundational stobj''
  introduced by an earlier [47m[defstobj][0m or [47mdefabsstobj[0m event.  The
  [47mdefabsstobj[0m event specifies a logical ([47m:LOGIC[0m) and an executable
  ([47m:EXEC[0m) definition for each primitive operation, or ``stobj
  primitive'', involving that stobj.  As is the case for [47m[defstobj][0m,
  the logical definition is what ACL2 reasons about, and is
  appropriate to apply to an ACL2 object satisfying the logical
  definition of the recognizer function for the stobj.  The
  executable definition is applied in raw Lisp to a live stobj (as
  discussed above).

  Remark.  It is common to use ``a'' and ``c'' in a suffix to suggest
  ``abstract'' and ``concrete'', respectively.  The foundational
  stobj was, at one time, called the ``corresponding concrete
  stobj''.  That old terminology may still be appropriate in the
  common case that the foundational stobj is a concrete stobj (rather
  than another abstract stobj).  So below, a name like [47mst$c0[0m suggests
  a foundational (``concrete'') stobj for an abstract stobj named [47mst[0m,
  whose abstract stobj recognizer is [47mst$ap[0m, and so on.  End of
  Remark.

  We can picture a sequence of updates to an abstract stobj and its
  foundational stobj.  Initially in this picture, [47mst$a0[0m and [47mst$c0[0m are
  an abstract stobj and its foundation (respectively).  Then an
  update, [47mu1[0m, is applied with [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m functions [47mu$a1[0m and
  [47mu$c1[0m, respectively.  The resulting abstract and foundational stobj,
  [47mst$a1[0m and [47mst$c1[0m, correspond as before.  Then a second update, [47mu2[0m,
  is applied with [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m functions [47mu$a2[0m and [47mu$c2[0m,
  respectively --- again preserving the correspondence.  And so on.

    Abstract               u$a1       u$a2       u$a3
    (:logic)         st$a0  --> st$a1  --> st$a2  -->   ...

                       ^          ^          ^               ^
    Correspondence     |          |          |          ...  |
                       v          v          v               v

                           u$c1       u$c2       u$c3
    Foundation       st$c0  --> st$c1  --> st$c2  -->   ...
    (:exec)

  We conclude this introduction with some remarks about implementation.
  Consider an abstract stobj [47mst[0m with corresponding foundation [47mst$c[0m.
  The live stobjs for [47mst[0m and [47mst$c[0m have the same structure, but are
  distinct arrays.  Indeed, the raw Lisp creator function for [47mst$c[0m is
  called to create a new initial live stobj for [47mst[0m.  As we will see
  below, reads and writes in raw Lisp to the live stobj for [47mst[0m are
  ultimately performed using the primitive accessors and updaters
  defined for [47mst$c[0m.  One might think of the live stobjs for [47mst[0m and
  [47mst$c[0m as being congruent stobjs (see [defstobj]), except that the
  stobjs themselves are not truly congruent: in particular, the stobj
  primitives introduced for [47mst[0m may be applied to [47mst[0m, but field
  updaters of [47mst$c[0m may not.  As one might expect, the [47m:EXEC[0m function
  for an exported function is applied to the live stobj for [47mst[0m in raw
  Lisp.

  [31;1mEXAMPLE[0m

  We present examples, with detailed comments intended to explain
  abstract stobjs, in two community books:
  [47mbooks/demos/defabsstobj-example-1.lisp[0m and
  [47mbooks/demos/defabsstobj-example-2.lisp[0m.  In this section we outline
  the first of these.  We suggest that after you finish this
  [documentation] topic, you read through those two books.  There are
  other books [47mbooks/dmeos/defabsstobj-example-*.lisp[0m that may be
  helpful to read; in particular,
  [47mbooks/demos/defabsstobj-example-5.lisp[0m illustrates building an
  abstract stobj on top of another abstract stobj (as its so-called
  ``foundation'', as described below).

  Here is the first of two closely related [47mdefabsstobj[0m [events] from
  the book [47mdefabsstobj-example-1.lisp[0m, but in expanded form.  We will
  show the abbreviated form later, which omits most of the data in
  the form that is immediately below.  Thus most of the information
  shown here is default information.  We believe that the comments
  below explain most or all of what you need to know in order to
  start using [47mdefabsstobj[0m, and that you will learn the remainder when
  you see error messages.  For example, we do not say in the comments
  below that every [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m function must be
  [guard]-verified, but that is indeed a requirement.

    (defabsstobj st ; The new abstract stobj is named st.

    ; The foundational stobj for st is st$c:

      :foundation st$c

    ; The recognizer for the new abstract stobj is stp, which is defined to be
    ; st$ap in the logic, and is executed on the live stobj in raw Lisp using
    ; st$cp.

      :recognizer (stp :logic st$ap :exec st$cp)

    ; The initial stobj is defined as create-st (a function of no arguments),
    ; which is defined logically as create-st$a, though create-st$c is invoked to
    ; create the initial live stobj for st.  The :correspondence and :preserved
    ; keywords refer to proof obligations, discussed below.

      :creator (create-st :logic create-st$a :exec create-st$c
                          :correspondence create-st{correspondence}
                          :preserved create-st{preserved})

    ; Proof obligations are generated that involve a correspondence between the
    ; new abstract stobj and corresponding foundational stobj.  The function
    ; st$corr, which need not be executable (see :DOC defun-nx), takes two
    ; arguments, a foundational stobj and an abstract stobj.  This function symbol
    ; is used in the statements of the proof obligations.

      :corr-fn st$corr

    ; In this example we have four exports.  In each case a new function is
    ; introduced that has the same signature as its :EXEC function, except that
    ; st$c is replaced by st.  The :LOGIC and :EXEC functions are as specified,
    ; and the other keywords refer to proof obligations that we discuss below.

      :exports ((lookup :logic lookup$a
                        :exec mem$ci
                        :correspondence lookup{correspondence}
                        :guard-thm lookup{guard-thm})
                (update :logic update$a
                        :exec update-mem$ci
                        :correspondence update{correspondence}
                        :preserved update{preserved}
                        :guard-thm update{guard-thm})
                (misc :logic misc$a
                      :exec misc$c
                      :correspondence misc{correspondence})
                (update-misc :logic update-misc$a
                             :exec update-misc$c
                             :correspondence update-misc{correspondence}
                             :preserved update-misc{preserved})))

  Note that all stobj primitives (recognizer, creator, and exported
  functions) are defined in the ACL2 loop in terms of their [47m:LOGIC[0m
  functions and in raw Lisp in terms of their [47m:EXEC[0m functions.  In
  the ACL2 loop, a [47m[defun][0m form defines a function, while in raw
  Lisp, a [47m[defmacro][0m form defines a macro (for efficiency).  We first
  illustrate how that works for the recognizer.  (You can see all the
  logical and executable definitions by evaluating the form [47m(trace$
  defabsstobj-axiomatic-defs defabsstobj-raw-defs)[0m before evaluating
  the [47m[defstobj][0m form.)

    ; In the ACL2 loop:
    (defun stp (st)
      (declare (xargs :guard 't))
      (st$ap st))

    ; In raw Lisp:
    (defmacro stp (&rest args) (cons 'st$cp args))

  The definitions are made similarly for exported functions.  [Guard]s
  are derived from their [47m:LOGIC[0m functions as follows.  Consider the
  exported function [47mupdate[0m in our example.  Its [47m:LOGIC[0m function,
  [47mupdate$a[0m, has formals [47m(k val st$a)[0m and the following guard.

    (and (and (integerp k) (<= 0 k) (<= k 49))
         (and (integerp val) (<= 0 val))
         (st$ap st$a)
         (mem$c-entryp val))

  The formals of [47mupdate[0m are obtained by starting with the formals of
  its [47m:EXEC[0m function, [47mupdate-mem$ci[0m --- which are [47m(i v st$c)[0m --- and
  replacing the foundational stobj name [47mst$c[0m by the new stobj name
  [47mst[0m.  The formals of [47mupdate[0m are thus [47m(i v st)[0m.  The guard for [47mupdate[0m
  is obtained in two steps.  The first step is to substitute the
  formals of [47mupdate[0m for the formals of [47mupdate$a[0m in the guard for
  [47mupdate$a[0m, to obtain the following.

    (and (and (integerp i) (<= 0 i) (<= i 49))
         (and (integerp v) (<= 0 v))
         (st$ap st)
         (mem$c-entryp v))

  The second step is to replace, for each new stobj primitive [47mp[0m, the
  [47m:LOGIC[0m function for [47mp[0m by [47mp[0m itself.  The only [47m:LOGIC[0m function
  occurring in the formula just above is [47mst$ap[0m, which is the [47m:LOGIC[0m
  function for [47mstp[0m.  The guard for [47mupdate[0m is thus as follows.

    (and (and (integerp i) (<= 0 i) (<= i 49))
         (and (integerp v) (<= 0 v))
         (stp st)
         (mem$c-entryp v))

  Note that the [47m:EXEC[0m version of an abstract [stobj] export must not
  include the abstract stobj name among its formals.

  We turn now to the proof obligations, as promised above.  There are
  three types: [47m:CORRESPONDENCE[0m, [47m:PRESERVED[0m, and [47m:GUARD-THM[0m.  All
  required lemmas may be printed simply by defining the necessary
  [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m functions and then submitting the [47mdefabsstobj[0m
  event.  (To advanced users: also see [defabsstobj-missing-events]
  for a utility that returns the required formulas in translated
  form.)  Although the [47mdefabsstobj[0m event will fail if the required
  lemmas have not been proved, first it will print the [47m[defthm][0m forms
  that must be admitted in order to complete submission of the
  [47mdefabsstobj[0m event.  (Note that although those theorems are stated
  exactly in the form expected by the system, you are welcome to
  supply whatever [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m you prefer, even though the system
  creates [47m:rule-classes nil[0m by default.)

  The detailed theory explaining the need for these lemmas may be found
  in ACL2 source file [47mother-events.lisp[0m, in a comment entitled
  ``Essay on the Correctness of Abstract Stobjs''.  Here, we give an
  informal sense of the importance of these lemmas as we present
  examples of them.  Fundamental is the notion of evaluation in the
  logic versus evaluation using live stobjs, where one imagines
  tracking the current value of each abstract stobj during each of
  these two evaluations.

  We start with the [47m:CORRESPONDENCE[0m lemmas.  These guarantee that
  evaluation in the logic agrees with evaluation using live stobjs,
  in the sense that the only difference is between a logical stobj
  and a live stobj, where the two correspond in the sense of the
  function specified by [47m:CORR-FN[0m.  We start with the [47m:CREATOR[0m
  function where the statement is quite simple, stating that the
  [47m:CORR-FN[0m holds initially.

    (defthm create-st{correspondence}
      (st$corr (create-st$c) (create-st$a)))

  For the exported functions, there are essentially two cases.  If an
  exported function returns other than the new abstract stobj, then
  the theorem asserts the equality of the results of applying the
  [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m functions for the exported function.  Hypotheses
  include the [47m:CORR-FN[0m correspondence followed by the [guard] for the
  [47m:LOGIC[0m function, which is stated in terms of the formal parameters
  of the [47m:EXEC[0m function except using the abstract stobj (here, [47mst[0m) in
  place of the foundational stobj (here, [47mst$c[0m).  The conclusion uses
  the [47m:EXEC[0m formals, modified in the call of the [47m:LOGIC[0m function
  (here, [47mlookup$a[0m) to use the abstract stobj, as in the hypotheses.

    (defthm lookup{correspondence}
      (implies (and (st$corr st$c st)
                    (integerp i) (<= 0 i) (<= i 49)
                    (st$ap st))
               (equal (mem$ci i st$c)
                      (lookup$a i st)))
      :rule-classes nil)

  By contrast, if the exported function returns the new abstract stobj,
  then the conclusion uses the correspondence function instead of
  [47mEQUAL[0m, as in the following.

    (defthm update{correspondence}
      (implies (and (st$corr st$c st)
                    (integerp i) (<= 0 i) (<= i 49)
                    (integerp v) (<= 0 v)
                    (st$ap st)
                    (mem$c-entryp v))
               (st$corr (update-mem$ci i v st$c)
                        (update$a i v st)))
      :rule-classes nil)

  For exported functions that return multiple values, such conclusions
  are conjoined together over the returned values.

  The [47m:PRESERVED[0m lemmas guarantee that updates to the abstract stobj
  preserve its recognizer.  The fact that every exported function has
  this property provides justification for an optimization performed
  by ACL2 during generation of proof obligations for [guard]
  verification, by assuming that the recognizer always holds.  The
  [47m:PRESERVED[0m lemma for the [47m:CREATOR[0m shows that the recognizer holds
  initially.

    (defthm create-st{preserved}
      (st$ap (create-st$a)))

  Here is a typical such lemma, for the exported function [47mupdate[0m.  Note
  that there is no such lemma for [47mlookup[0m, since [47mlookup[0m does not
  return [47mst[0m.

    (defthm update{preserved}
      (implies (and (integerp i) (<= 0 i) (<= i 49)
                    (integerp v) (<= 0 v)
                    (st$ap st)
                    (mem$c-entryp v))
               (st$ap (update$a i v st))))

  Finally, we consider the [47m:GUARD-THM[0m lemmas.  These serve to guarantee
  that the [guard] holds for each call of an [47m:EXEC[0m function.  During
  guard verification, logical definitions are used; in particular,
  since each exported function is defined in the logic as the
  corresponding call of its [47m:LOGIC[0m function, guard verification shows
  that each call of the [47m:LOGIC[0m function for an exported function
  satisfies that function's guard.  But why is this true for raw Lisp
  evaluation using live stobjs, where the [47m:EXEC[0m function is called
  for an exported function?  The [47m:GUARD-THM[0m lemmas provide the
  answer, as they state that if the [47m:LOGIC[0m function's guard holds,
  then the [47m:EXEC[0m function's guard holds.  Here is an example.  Note
  that the hypotheses come from the correspondence of the
  foundational and abstract function as guaranteed by the [47m:CORR[0m
  function, together with the guard of the [47m:LOGIC[0m function; and the
  conclusion comes from the guard of the [47m:EXEC[0m function.

    (defthm lookup{guard-thm}
      (implies (and (st$corr st$c c)
                    (integerp i)
                    (<= 0 i)
                    (<= i 49)
                    (st$ap st))
               (and (integerp i)
                    (<= 0 i)
                    (< i (mem$c-length st$c))))
      :rule-classes nil)

  We conclude this EXAMPLE section by showing a short form for the
  [47mdefabsstobj[0m form displayed above.

    (defabsstobj st
      :exports ((lookup :exec mem$ci)
                (update :exec update-mem$ci)
                misc update-misc))

  [31;1mSUMMARY DOCUMENTATION[0m

  The General Form is as shown below, where the order of keywords is
  unimportant.  Duplicate keywords are discouraged; while permitted,
  only the first (leftmost) occurrence of a given keyword is used.
  Only the [47m:exports[0m keyword is required.

    (defabsstobj st
      :foundation foundation
      :recognizer recognizer
      :creator creator
      :corr-fn corr-fn
      :corr-fn-exists corr-fn-exists
      :congruent-to congruent-to
      :non-executable non-executable
      :protect-default protect-default
      :attachable att
      :exports (e1 ... ek))

  The keyword argument [47m:EXPORTS[0m must be supplied, and missing or [47mnil[0m
  keyword arguments have defaults as indicated below.  All arguments
  must satisfy the conditions below.

  Before we describe the arguments, we define a notion of a ``function
  spec'' and its ``completion''.  A function spec is either a symbol
  or else a list of the form

    (fn [47m:kwd1[0m val1 ... [47m:kwdn[0m valn),

  that is, a symbol followed by a [47m[keyword-value-listp][0m.  Each [47mvali[0m
  must be a symbol.  We view the case of a symbol, [47ms[0m, as the function
  spec [47m(s)[0m, with no keywords.  There must be no duplicate keywords.
  In each case that we expect a function spec, the context provides a
  set of valid keywords for that function spec; it is an error to
  provide any other keyword in the function spec.  Each function spec
  is interpreted as its ``completion'', obtained by extending the
  function spec with a default value for each valid keyword as
  indicated below.  With that interpretation, the ``exported
  function'' of a function spec is its [47mcar[0m, and that function symbol
  and each keyword value must be a guard-verified function symbol;
  and moreover, the [47m:EXEC[0m function must not include the new abstract
  stobj name, [47mst[0m, among its formals.

  We are ready to describe the arguments of [47mdefabsstobj[0m.

      [47mSt[0m is a symbol, which names the new abstract stobj.

      [47mFoundation[0m is the name of an existing stobj, which may have been
      introduced either with [47m[defstobj][0m or with [47mdefabsstobj[0m.

      [47mRecognizer[0m is a function spec (for the recognizer function).  The
      valid keywords are [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m.  The default for
      [47mrecognizer[0m is obtained by adding the suffix [47m\"P\"[0m to [47mname[0m.  The
      default value for [47m:LOGIC[0m is formed by adding the suffix [47m\"$AP\"[0m
      to [47mrecognizer[0m; for [47m:EXEC[0m, by adding the suffix [47m\"$CP\"[0m.  The
      [47m:EXEC[0m function must be the recognizer for the foundational
      stobj (which can be specified using the [47m:FOUNDATION[0m keyword).

      [47mCreator[0m is a function spec (for the creator function).  The valid
      keywords are [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m.  The default for [47mcreator[0m is
      obtained by adding the prefix [47m\"CREATE-\"[0m to [47mname[0m.  The default
      value for [47m:LOGIC[0m is formed by adding the suffix [47m\"$A\"[0m to
      [47mcreator[0m; for [47m:EXEC[0m, by adding the suffix [47m\"$C\"[0m.  The [47m:EXEC[0m
      function must be the creator for the foundational stobj (which
      can be specified using the [47m:FOUNDATION[0m keyword).

      [47mCorr-fn[0m is a known function symbol that takes two arguments (for the
      correspondence theorems).  The default for [47mcorr-fn[0m is obtained
      by adding the suffix [47m\"$CORR\"[0m to [47mname[0m.

      [47mCorr-fn-exists[0m is a Boolean with default [47mnil[0m, which will generally
      serve well.  See [stobj-attachment-restrictions] for a
      discussion of this argument.

      [47mCongruent-to[0m should either be [47mnil[0m (the default) or the name of an
      abstract stobj previously introduced (by [47m[defabsstobj][0m).  In
