  some stobj primitive, [47mf[0m (as opposed to using your own function,
  say, [47mfoo-length[0m or [47mfoo$a[0m).  Then every call of [47mlength[0m will be
  replaced by [47mf[0m when creating the guard of a stobj primitive from the
  guard of its [47m:LOGIC[0m function.  This might not be what you intended
  if you were using [47mlength[0m in that guard simply to compute the length
  of an ordinary list.

  Additional restrictions include the following.

    * All exported function names must be new (unless redefinition is on;
      see [ld-redefinition-action]), and there must be no duplicates
      among them.

    * The foundational stobj name must be a formal parameter of the [47m:EXEC[0m
      function of every function spec, except for the [47m:CREATOR[0m
      function spec.

    * The [47m:LOGIC[0m and [47m:EXEC[0m function for a function spec must agree on both
      the number of inputs and the number of outputs.

    * The foundational stobj must not be a [declare]d stobj of the [47m:LOGIC[0m
      function of any function spec.  (This restriction could perhaps
      be removed, but it is convenient for the implementation of the
      events generated by a call of [47mdefabsstobj[0m.)

    * The [47m:PROTECT[0m keyword is something that you should ignore unless you
      get an error message about it, pertaining to modifying the
      foundational stobj non-atomically.  In that case, you can
      eliminate the error by providing [47m:PROTECT t[0m in the function
      spec, or by providing [47mdefabsstobj[0m keyword argument
      [47m:PROTECT-DEFAULT t[0m at the top level, in order to restore the
      required atomicity.  The above explanation is probably all you
      need to know about [47m:PROTECT[0m, but just below is a more complete
      explanation for those who desire it.  Further information is
      also available if you need it; see [set-absstobj-debug], and
      see the example uses of these keywords in community book
      [47mbooks/demos/defabsstobj-example-2.lisp[0m.

  For those who are interested, here is a more detailed discussion of
  [47m:PROTECT[0m and [47m:PROTECT-DEFAULT[0m, as promised above.  It applies to
  any function spec for an export (hence not to the [47m:CREATOR[0m function
  spec).  If the [47m:EXEC[0m function is a stobj primitive, then clearly
  the following property holds: any execution of a call of that
  function can only update the foundational stobj at most once ---
  i.e., modification of the foundational stobj is atomic.  ACL2 can
  deduce this property not only for stobj primitives but for many
  other functions as well.  However, if ACL2 cannot deduce this
  property, then it will cause an error saying that the [47m:EXEC[0m
  function ``appears capable of modifying the foundational stobj,
  [47m<stobj_name>[0m, non-atomically.'' That message also explains how to
  eliminate this error: provide [47m:PROTECT t[0m for the function spec.
  Alternatively, all function specs without an explicit [47m:PROTECT[0m
  keyword can be implicitly supplied [47m:PROTECT t[0m by supplying the
  value [47mt[0m for the [47m:PROTECT-DEFAULT[0m keyword parameter of the
  [47mdefabsstobj[0m event.  However, beware that when [47m:PROTECT[0m is [47mt[0m, the
  generated raw Lisp code runs slightly less efficiently --- though
  perhaps with negligible efficiency loss if the [47m:EXEC[0m function is
  not trivial.  Community books
  [47mbooks/demos/defabsstobj-example-3.lisp[0m and
  [47mbooks/demos/defabsstobj-example-4.lisp[0m provide related information.
  Also see [set-absstobj-debug] for a potentially dangerous way to
  eliminate that inefficiency using argument [47m:ignore[0m.

  We conclude with some remarks.

  Unlike [47m[defstobj][0m, there is no [47m:renaming[0m argument.  Instead, the
  scheme described above provides a flexible way to assign names.
  Also unlike [47m[defstobj][0m, there is no [47m:inline[0m or [47m:non-memoizable[0m
  argument; [47m:inline[0m is essentially t, in the sense that stobj
  primitives are macros in raw Lisp; and the [47m:non-memoizable[0m argument
  is derived implicitly, to agree with non-memoizability of the
  foundational stobj.

  Those who use [hons-enabled] features, including function memoization
  (see [memoize]), may be aware that the memo table for a function is
  flushed whenever it is the case that one of its stobj inputs is
  updated.  In fact, such flushing happens even when a stobj that is
  congruent to one of its stobj inputs is updated.  For that purpose,
  an abstract stobj is considered to be congruent to its foundational
  stobj.


