 (DEFBADGE
  (APPLY$ ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Issue a badge for a function so [47m[apply$][0m can evaluate with it

  It is best to be somewhat familiar with the documentation of [47m[apply$][0m
  before reading this topic.

  Before using [47mdefbadge[0m or a utility like [47m[defun$][0m that relies on it:

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)


Badges versus Warrants

  It is easy to confuse [badge]s, which are issued by [47mdefbadge[0m, with
  [warrant]s, which are issued by [47m[defwarrant][0m.  Badges and warrants
  are necessary to ACL2's support of [47m[apply$][0m because ACL2 is
  actually first-order, functions cannot be passed around as objects,
  ordinary symbols play the role of ``function objects,'' and somehow
  the logic must allow the association of a symbol with the function
  it names.  Furthermore, to insure the consistency of the logic
  [47mapply$[0m is not allowed to handle certain functions such as the
  [47mrussell[0m function illustrated in [47m[introduction-to-apply$][0m.  But to
  determine whether a newly defined function is allowed to be known
  to [47mapply$[0m, the ACL2 system must be able to determine, in some
  sense, all the functions reachable from it.  And finally, to be
  able to prove theorems about the application of such function
  names, the link between the symbols and the functions and the
  analyzed properties of the functions must be available to the
  prover in the form of axioms.  Particularly vexing is the so-called
  ``[47mlocal[0m problem'' which raises the possibility of proving a theorem
  about the application of a name in the context of a local
  definition of the corresponding function and then exporting that
  theorem to a context where the name is defined differently (see
  [31;1mLesson 12[0m of [introduction-to-apply$]).

  Roughly speaking, badges are about syntax and warrants are about
  semantics.  The badge for a symbol is a data structure in the ACL2
  system that records information like whether the symbol names a
  known ACL2 function, how many arguments that function takes, how
  many results it returns, which arguments are treated like function
  objects to be [47m[apply$][0m'd, which are treated like expressions to be
  [47m[ev$][0m'd, and which are treated like ordinary ACL2 objects to be
  [47mcar[0m'd, [47mcdr[0m'd, consed, etc.  The warrant for a function is in fact
  another function (actually a 0-ary predicate) defined in the logic.
  That predicate asserts some facts about the symbol.  In particular,
  it specifies the badge of the symbol and it constrains the behavior
  of [47mapply$[0m on the symbol.

  [47mDefbadge[0m analyzes the definition of a function and constructs the
  badge if possible.  [47mDefbadge[0m does not affect the logic --- no
  definitions or axioms are added.  [47mDefbadge[0m can analyze both
  [47m:program[0m mode functions and [47m:logic[0m mode functions.  [47mDefwarrant[0m, on
  the other hand, can only analyze [47m:logic[0m mode functions because it
  must inspect the measure used to justify the termination of the
  function and, if it is successful, it adds a [47m:logic[0m mode definition
  of the warrant to the logic.  This definition links the symbol to
  the function via [47mbadge[0m and [47mapply$[0m and if the warrant is a
  hypothesis of a conjecture then the prover ``knows'' about the
  linkage.

  Both [47mdefbadge[0m and [47mdefwarrant[0m affect the top-level read-eval-print
  loop because that loop treats [47m:program[0m and [47m:logic[0m functions
  differently so that it can (a) allow [47m:program[0m mode terms to be
  evaluated to carry out commands such as [47m[defun][0m and [47m[defthm][0m, query
  the world or build and test prototypes, and (b) allow [47m:logic[0m mode
  terms to be evaluated while guaranteeing a certain correspondence
  with what can be proved.  See [guarantees-of-the-top-level-loop].


Requirements of [47mDefbadge[0m

    General Form:
    (defbadge fn)

  where [47mfn[0m is a defined function name in either [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  mode.  This command analyzes the body of [47mfn[0m to determine whether it
  satisfies certain stringent syntactic conditions discussed below.
  If the conditions are not met, [47mdefbadge[0m signals an error.
  Otherwise, it records a [badge] for [47mfn[0m.  Badges record the input
  and output arities of [47mfn[0m and specify which arguments are
  ``functions'' that may be applied with [47mapply$[0m, which are
  ``expressions'' that may be evaluated with [47m[ev$][0m, and which are
  neither.  The conditions checked are sufficient to allow [47m[apply$][0m
  to run the function safely at the top level of the ACL2
  read-eval-print loop.  However, in order to prove anything about a
  call of [47mapply$[0m on [47mfn[0m --- or even to evaluate such a call if [47mfn[0m is
  in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode, as discussed above --- [47mfn[0m will need a [warrant]
  as issued by [47m[defwarrant][0m.  [47mDefbadge[0m does not issue warrants, just
  badges.  [47mDefwarrant[0m can issue both badges and warrants.

  The first condition on [47mfn[0m is that it must be a defined function
  symbol.  Since [47mfn[0m must be defined it may not be a constrained
  function such as one introduced by [47m[defchoose][0m or [47m[encapsulate][0m.
  In addition, [47mfn[0m may not be one of a very few ``blacklisted''
  symbols (see the value of [47m*blacklisted-apply$-fns*[0m) like [47m[sys-call][0m
  (which requires a trust tag) or an [untouchable].  (For technical
  reasons, untouchables are disallowed even if they are on
  [47mtemp-touchable-fns[0m; see [47m[remove-untouchable][0m.)

  The other conditions depend on whether [47m[apply$][0m is reachable from [47mfn[0m.
  That is, can a call of [47mfn[0m lead to a call of [47mapply$[0m?  If [47mapply$[0m is
  not reachable from [47mfn[0m, then there are no more conditions on [47mfn[0m.  A
  badge for [47mfn[0m is computed and stored.  We are more precise about
  ``reachability'' later.

  If [47mapply$[0m is reachable from [47mfn[0m, then there are additional conditions
  that must be checked.  First, [47mfn[0m must not have been introduced with
  [47m[mutual-recursion][0m.  The current badging machinery is unable to
  enforce the syntactic restrictions for mutually-recursive cliques.
  Another restriction is that every function mentioned in the body of
  [47mfn[0m, except [47mfn[0m itself, must already have a badge.  Finally, [47mfn[0m must
  respect certain conventions regarding its use of [47m[apply$][0m and other
  [scion]s.  The basic idea of this last restriction is to make sure
  that [47mapply$[0m is always called on a ``known'' function symbol or
  [47m[lambda][0m object.  This restriction is enforced by checking the
  following conditions:

  [3m(a)[0m It must be possible for each formal of [47mfn[0m to be assigned one of
  three [ilk]s, [47m:FN[0m, [47m:EXPR[0m, or [47mNIL[0m, as described below.  The basic
  idea is that a formal can be assigned ilk [47m:FN[0m (or ilk [47m:EXPR[0m) iff it
  is sometimes passed into a [47m:FN[0m (or [47m:EXPR[0m) slot in the body of [47mfn[0m
  and is never passed into any other kind of slot.  A formal can be
  be assigned ilk [47mNIL[0m iff it is never passed into a slot of ilk [47m:FN[0m
  or [47m:EXPR[0m, i.e., if it is used exclusively as an ``ordinary''
  object.  We are more precise below.

  [3m(b)[0m Every [47m:FN[0m and [47m:EXPR[0m slot of every function called in the body of
  [47mfn[0m is occupied either by a formal of [47mfn[0m of the same ilk or, in the
  case of calls of functions other than [47mfn[0m, a quoted [tame] function
  symbol or quoted tame (preferably well-formed) [47mLAMBDA[0m object.  (See
  [47m[well-formed-lambda-objectp][0m.)

  This completes the list of restrictions imposed by [47mdefbadge[0m.


Discussion and Examples

  Note that if [47mapply$[0m is not reachable from [47mfn[0m, the restrictions
  imposed on [47mfn[0m are comparatively generous.  Such a [47mfn[0m could be
  badged and warranted despite being defined mutually recursively or
  in terms of unbadged or even unbadgeable functions.  Basically, if
  [47mfn[0m doesn't depend on [47mapply$[0m there is no danger that some argument
  of [47mfn[0m will be treated like a function object or an expression.

  After a successful [47mdefbadge[0m event for [47mfn[0m, the function [47m[badge][0m will
  return the computed badge (when executed in the top-level loop) and
  [47m[apply$][0m will be able to accept the [47mfn[0m as a functional argument.
  Here is an annotated script.  First, carry out these two events,
  defining [47mfoldr[0m as a [47m:program[0m mode function.

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

    (defun foldr (lst fn init)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (if (endp lst)
          init
          (apply$ fn
                  (list (car lst)
                        (foldr (cdr lst) fn init)))))

  Note the [47mapply$[0m call in the definition.  We see that [47mfoldr[0m treats its
  middle argument, [47mfn[0m, as a function of arity 2.  We can run [47mfoldr[0m,
  even without assigning a badge to [47mfoldr[0m, as long as we supply a
  badged function symbol of arity 2 as the middle argument.  Since
  the ACL2 primitive [47mcons[0m has a badge and has arity 2, we can use it:

    ACL2 !>(foldr '(a b c) 'cons '(d e f))
    (A B C D E F)

  Since [47mfoldr[0m has arity 3, we can try to apply it to a list of three
  things.

    (apply$ 'foldr (list '(a b c) 'cons '(d e f)))

    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The value of APPLY$-USERFN is not specified
    on FOLDR because FOLDR has not been badged.

  However, we can use [47mdefbadge[0m to compute and store the badge for
  [47mfoldr[0m.  The badge says [47mfoldr[0m has input arity 3, output arity 1, and
  treats its middle argument as a function.  We can recover the badge
  by calling the function [47m[badge][0m.  We can successfully apply [47mfoldr[0m.
  We can even use it in a [47mlambda[0m expression that we pass as the
  middle argument to [47mfoldr[0m.

    ACL2 !>(defbadge foldr)

    FOLDR now has the badge (APPLY$-BADGE 3 1 NIL :FN NIL) but has no warrant.
    T

    ACL2 !>(badge 'foldr)
    (APPLY$-BADGE 3 1 NIL :FN NIL)

    ACL2 !>(apply$ 'foldr (list '(a b c) 'cons '(d e f)))
    (A B C D E F)

    ACL2 !>(foldr '((a b c) (d e) (f g h) (i j k))
                  (lambda$ (x y)
                    (foldr x 'cons y))
                  nil)
    (A B C D E F G H I J K)


The ``Reachability'' Test

  We now clarify the test that we colloquially described above as
  whether [47mapply$[0m is reachable from [47mfn[0m.  The actual test is whether
  [47mapply$-userfn[0m is ancestral in [47mfn[0m.  That is, does [47mfn[0m call
  [47mapply$-userfn[0m, or a function that calls [47mapply$-userfn[0m, or a
  function that calls a function that calls [47mapply$-userfn[0m, etc.

  Since the only system functions that call [47mapply$-userfn[0m are [47mapply$[0m,
  [47mev$[0m, and [warrant]s, and since it is very unusual for a
  user-defined function to call directly [47mapply$-userfn[0m, [47mev$[0m, or
  warrants, we think of this test colloquially as whether [47mapply$[0m is
  ancestral in [47mfn[0m.

  The test and the onerous conditions imposed when [47mapply$[0m is reachable
  is crucial to the soundness of the ACL2 proof theory.  We discuss
  this further in the background material for [47m[apply$][0m, including
  {``Limited Second-Order Functionality in a First-Order Setting'' |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/kaufmann/papers/apply/index.html} by
  Matt Kaufmann and J Strother Moore and offer a fully fleshed out
  metalevel proof that [47mapply$[0m and all [47m:logic[0m-mode scions can be
  modeled in the comment titled [47mEssay on Admitting a Model for Apply$
  and the Functions that Use It[0m in the ACL2 source file
  [47mapply-raw.lisp[0m.

  But badges are more concerned with syntax (and, for [47m:program[0m mode
  functions, evaluation) than the proof theory.  Even if we convert
  [47mfoldr[0m to [47m:logic[0m mode we cannot prove anything interesting about
  what happens when it is applied with [47mapply$[0m.  We can't even prove
  that [47mfoldr[0m has a badge or what that badge is!

    ACL2 !>(verify-termination foldr)
    [Successful.  Output deleted.]
     FOLDR

    ACL2 !>(thm
             (equal (apply$ 'foldr (list x 'cons z))
                    (append x z)))
    [Unsuccessful.  Output deleted.]
    ******** FAILED ********

    ACL2 !>(thm
             (equal (badge 'foldr) '(APPLY$-BADGE 3 1 NIL :FN NIL)))
    [Unsuccessful.  Output deleted.]
    ******** FAILED ********

  In order to prove anything nontrivial about [47mfoldr[0m's badge or behavior
  under [47mapply$[0m we need the [47m[warrant][0m for [47mfoldr[0m.  Warrants are issued
  by [47m[defwarrant][0m.  If we execute (defwarrant foldr) and then amend
  the failed [47mthm[0m commands above by adding [47m(warrant foldr)[0m as a
  hypothesis, both amended formulas are provable.


How Ilks Are Assigned

  If a formal variable (or its slot among the actuals) has an ilk of
  [47m:FN[0m then the variable is ``used as a function'' in the sense that
  it might eventually reach the first argument of a call of [47mapply$[0m
  and is never passed into an ``ordinary'' slot like those for [47mcons[0m.
  Similarly, an ilk of [47m:EXPR[0m means the variable is ``used as an
  expression'' and may eventually reach the first argument of [47mev$[0m.
  An ilk of [47mNIL[0m means the variable is never used as a function or an
  expression.  The correctness of this algorithm is crucial to the
  safe evaluation of [47mapply$[0m on user-defined function symbols.  It
  also is crucial to the termination argument justifying the
  consistency of the proof theory created if and when [47mfn[0m is
  warranted.

  The key to the inductive correctness of the algorithm implicitly
  described below is the fact that when the ACL2 logic is being
  booted up the only function symbol with a slot of ilk [47m:FN[0m is [47mapply$[0m
  and the only function with a slot of ilk [47m:EXPR[0m is [47mev$[0m.  In both
  functions it is the first argument slot that is so distinguished.
  All other ACL2 primitives that use [47mapply$[0m or [47mev$[0m, e.g., [47mcollect$[0m,
  are defined, admissible, badged, and warranted under the same
  conditions user-defined functions are.

  Let [3mv [0m be the [3mi [0mth formal parameter of a defined function [3mfn[0m.  Then
  the ilk of [3mv [0m is [47m:FN[0m iff the value of [3mv [0m eventually makes its way
  into the first argument of [47mapply$[0m, either in the definition of [47mfn[0m
  or in some function ancestral to (i.e., eventually called by) [47mfn[0m.
  Another way to say this is that there is an occurrence of [3mv [0m in a
  slot of ilk [47m:FN[0m.  Furthermore, [3mv [0m is never used any other way:
  every place [3mv [0m occurs in the body of [3mfn [0m is in a slot of ilk [47m:FN[0m.
  And finally, in every recursive call of [3mfn [0m, [3mv [0m is passed
  identically in the [3mi [0mth argument position of the call.  We say such
  a [3mv [0m is ``used (exclusively) as a function.''

  The [3mi [0mth formal variable [3mv [0m has ilk [47m:EXPR[0m under analogous conditions
  except that instead of eventually getting into the first argument
  of [47mapply$[0m it eventually gets into the first argument of [47mev$[0m.  We
  say such a [3mv [0m is ``used (exclusively) as an expression.'' Note:
  [47m[ev$][0m is the natural notion of expression evaluation in this
  context: look up the values of variables in the alist argument to
  [47mev$[0m, return quoted constants, and otherwise [47mapply$[0m function symbols
  and [47mLAMBDA[0m objects to the recursively obtained list of values
  returned by evaluating the actuals.  However, [47mev$[0m first checks that
  the expression is [47m[tamep][0m.

  The [3mi [0mth formal variable [3mv [0m has ilk [47mNIL[0m if it never occurs in a [47m:FN[0m
  slot and never occurs in an [47m:EXPR[0m slot.  We say such a [3mv [0m is ``used
  (exclusively) as an ordinary object.''")
