  (EVENTS)
  "Define a Skolem (witnessing) function

    Examples:
    (defchoose choose-x-for-p1-and-p2 (x) (y z)
      (and (p1 x y z)
           (p2 x y z)))

    ; Axiom added by the event above:
    (IMPLIES (AND (P1 X Y Z) (P2 X Y Z))
             (LET ((X (CHOOSE-X-FOR-P1-AND-P2 Y Z)))
                  (AND (P1 X Y Z) (P2 X Y Z))))

    (defchoose choose-x-for-p1-and-p2 x (y z) ; equivalent to the above
      (and (p1 x y z)
           (p2 x y z)))

    ; The following is as above, but strengthens the axiom added to pick a sort
    ; of canonical witness, as described below.
    (defchoose choose-x-for-p1-and-p2 x (y z)
      (and (p1 x y z)
           (p2 x y z))
      :strengthen t)

    (defchoose choose-x-and-y-for-p1-and-p2 (x y) (z)
      (and (p1 x y z)
           (p2 x y z)))

    General Form:
    (defchoose fn
               (bound-var1 ... bound-varn)
               (free-var1 ... free-vark)
               body
               :strengthen b),

  where [47mfn[0m is the symbol you wish to define and is a new symbolic name
  (see [name]), [47m(bound-var1 ... bound-varn)[0m is a list of distinct
  `bound' variables (see below), [47m(free-var1 ... free-vark)[0m is the
  list of formal parameters of [47mfn[0m and is disjoint from the bound
  variables, and [47mbody[0m is a term.  The use of [47mlambda-list[0m keywords
  (such as [47m&optional[0m) is not allowed.  The [47m:strengthen[0m keyword
  argument is optional; if supplied, it must be [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m.

  The system treats [47mfn[0m very much as though it were declared in the
  [signature] of an [47m[encapsulate][0m event, with a single axiom exported
  as described below.  If you supply a [47m:use[0m hint (see [hints]), [47m:use
  fn[0m, it will refer to that axiom.  No rule (of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or
  otherwise; see [rule-classes]) is created for [47mfn[0m.

  [47mDefchoose[0m is only executed in [defun-mode] [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m; see
  [defun-mode].  Also see [defun-sk].

  In the most common case, where there is only one bound variable, it
  is permissible to omit the enclosing parentheses on that variable.
  The effect is the same whether or not those parentheses are
  omitted.  We describe this case first, where there is only one
  bound variable, and then address the other case.  Both cases are
  discussed assuming [47m:strengthen[0m is [47mnil[0m, which is the default.  We
  deal with the case [47m:strengthen t[0m at the end.

  The effect of the form

    (defchoose fn bound-var (free-var1 ... free-vark)
      body)

  is to introduce a new function symbol, [47mfn[0m, with formal parameters
  [47m(free-var1 ... free-vark)[0m.  Now consider the following axiom, which
  states that [47mfn[0m picks a value of [47mbound-var[0m so that the body will be
  true, if such a value exists:

    (1)   (implies body
                   (let ((bound-var (fn free-var1 ... free-vark)))
                     body))

  This axiom is ``clearly conservative'' under the conditions expressed
  above: the function [47mfn[0m simply picks out a ``witnessing'' value of
  [47mbound-var[0m if there is one.  For a rigorous statement and proof of
  this conservativity claim, see [conservativity-of-defchoose].

  Next consider the case that there is more than one bound variable,
  i.e., there is more than one bound-var in the following.

    (defchoose fn
               (bound-var1 ... bound-varn)
               (free-var1 ... free-vark)
               body)

  Then [47mfn[0m returns a multiple value with [47mn[0m components, and formula (1)
  above is expressed using [47m[mv-let][0m as follows:

    (implies body
             (mv-let (bound-var1 ... bound-varn)
                     (fn free-var1 ... free-vark)
                     body))

  We now discuss the case that [47m:strengthen t[0m is supplied.  For
  simplicity we return to our simplest case, with [47mdefchoose[0m applied
  to function [47mfn[0m, a single free variable [47my[0m, and a single bound
  variable [47mbound-var[0m.  The idea is that if we pick the ``smallest''
  witnessing [47mbound-var[0m for two different free variables [47my[0m and [47my1[0m,
  then either those two witnesses are the same, or else one is less
  than the other, in which case the smaller one is a witness for its
  free variable but not for the other.  (See comments in source
  function [47mdefchoose-constraint-extra[0m for more details.)  Below,
  [47mbody1[0m is the result of replacing [47my[0m by [47my1[0m in [47mbody[0m.

    (2)   (or (equal (fn y) (fn y1))
              (let ((bound-var (fn y)))
                (and body
                     (not body1)))
              (let ((bound-var (fn y1)))
                (and body1
                     (not body))))

  An important application of this additional axiom is to be able to
  define a ``fixing'' function that picks a canonical representative
  of each equivalence class, for a given equivalence relation.  The
  following events illustrate this point.

    (encapsulate
     ((equiv (x y) t))
     (local (defun equiv (x y) (equal x y)))
     (defequiv equiv))

    (defchoose efix (x) (y)
      (equiv x y)
      :strengthen t)

    (defthm equiv-implies-equal-efix-1
      (implies (equiv y y1)
               (equal (efix y) (efix y1)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use efix))
      :rule-classes (:congruence))

    (defthm efix-fixes
      (equiv (efix x) x)
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use ((:instance efix (y x))))))

  If there is more than one bound variable, then (2) is modified in
  complete analogy to (1) to use [47m[mv-let][0m in place of [47m[let][0m.

  Comment for logicians: As we point out in the documentation for
  [47m[defun-sk][0m, [47mdefchoose[0m is ``appropriate,'' by which we mean that it
  is conservative, even in the presence of [47mepsilon-0[0m induction.  For
  a proof, See [conservativity-of-defchoose].


