  (EVENTS)
  "Prove [congruence] rule

  [47mDefcong[0m is used to prove that one [equivalence] relation preserves
  another in a given argument position of a given function.

    Example:
    (defcong set-equal iff (memb x y) 2)

    is an abbreviation for

    (defthm set-equal-implies-iff-memb-2
      (implies (set-equal y y-equiv)
               (iff (memb x y) (memb x y-equiv)))
      :rule-classes (:congruence))

  See [congruence] and also see [equivalence].

    General Form:
    (defcong equiv1 equiv2 term k
      :package package
      :event-name event-name
      :rule-classes rule-classes
      :instructions instructions
      :hints hints
      :otf-flg otf-flg)

  where [47mequiv1[0m and [47mequiv2[0m are known [equivalence] relations; [47mterm[0m is a
  call of a function [47mfn[0m, other than [47mif[0m, on the correct number of
  distinct variable arguments, [47m(fn x1 ... xn)[0m; [47mk[0m is a positive
  integer less than or equal to the arity of [47mfn[0m; [47mpackage[0m, if
  supplied, is one of [47m:current[0m, [47m:equiv1[0m, [47m:equiv2[0m, [47m:function[0m or
  [47m:legacy[0m; [47mevent-name[0m, if supplied, is a symbol; and other arguments
  are as specified in the documentation for [47m[defthm][0m.  The [47mdefcong[0m
  macro expands into a call of [47m[defthm][0m.  The name of the [47m[defthm][0m
  event is [47mequiv1-implies-equiv2-fn-k[0m, unless an [47m:event-name[0m keyword
  argument is supplied, in which case [47mevent-name[0m is used.  The
  package of symbols generated, such as variables and [47m[defthm][0m names,
  is determined by the [47mpackage[0m argument: if it is not supplied or its
  value is [47m:current[0m, then the [47m[current-package][0m is used; if its value
  is [47m:equiv1[0m or [47m:legacy[0m, then the package of [47m:equiv1[0m is used; if its
  value is [47m:equiv2[0m, then the package of [47m:equiv2[0m is used; and if its
  value is [47m:function[0m, then the package of [47mfn[0m is used.  The term of
  the theorem is

    (implies (equiv1 xk yk)
             (equiv2 (fn x1... xk ...xn)
                     (fn x1... yk ...xn))).

  The rule-class [47m:[0m[47m[congruence][0m is added to the [47m[rule-classes][0m
  specified, if it is not already there.  All other arguments to the
  generated [47m[defthm][0m form are as specified by the keyword arguments
  above.

  NOTE: In normal usage, [47mdefcong[0m can only create classic [congruence]
  rules, not [patterned-congruence] rules.  However, if [47mfn[0m is a
  macro, it's possible for a patterned congruence rule to be
  generated, depending on what [47mterm[0m expands to.

  For example, the following produces a shallow patterned congruence
  rule:

    (defun f (x y) (and x y))
    (defmacro g (x) `(f ,x t))
    (defcong iff equal (g x) 1)"
