  [Prohibition-of-loop$-and-lambda$]
      Certain events do not allow [47m[loop$][0ms or [47m[lambda$][0ms

  [Sharp-dot-reader]
      Read-time evaluation of constants")
 (DEFEQUIV
  (EVENTS)
  "Prove that a function is an [equivalence] relation

    Example:
    (defequiv set-equal)

    is an abbreviation for
    (defthm set-equal-is-an-equivalence
      (and (booleanp (set-equal x y))
           (set-equal x x)
           (implies (set-equal x y) (set-equal y x))
           (implies (and (set-equal x y)
                         (set-equal y z))
                    (set-equal x z)))
      :rule-classes (:equivalence))

  See [equivalence].

    General Form:
    (defequiv equiv
      :package package
      :event-name event-name
      :rule-classes rule-classes
      :instructions instructions
      :hints hints
      :otf-flg otf-flg)

  where [47mequiv[0m is a function symbol of arity 2; [47mpackage[0m, if supplied, is
  one of [47m:current[0m, [47m:equiv[0m or [47m:legacy[0m; [47mevent-name[0m, if supplied, is a
  symbol; and all other arguments are as specified in the
  documentation for [47m[defthm][0m.  The [47mdefequiv[0m macro expands into a call
  of [47m[defthm][0m.  The name of the [47m[defthm][0m is [47mequiv-is-an-equivalence[0m,
  unless an [47m:event-name[0m keyword argument is supplied, in which case
  [47mevent-name[0m is used.  The package of symbols generated, such as
  variables and [47m[defthm][0m names, is determined by the [47mpackage[0m
  argument: if it is not supplied or its value is [47m:current[0m, then the
  [47m[current-package][0m is used; if its value is [47m:equiv[0m or [47m:legacy[0m (which
  are treated identically), then the package of [47m:equiv[0m is used.  All
  other arguments to the generated [47m[defthm][0m form are as specified by
  the other keyword arguments above.  The rule-class [47m:[0m[47m[equivalence][0m
  is added to the [rule-classes] specified, if it is not already
  there.  The term generated for the [47m[defthm][0m event states that [47mequiv[0m
  is Boolean, reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.")
 (DEFEVALUATOR
  (EVENTS)
  "Introduce an evaluator function

    Example:
    (defevaluator evl evl-list
      ((length x) (member-equal x y)))

  See [meta].

    General Form:
    (defevaluator ev ev-list
       ((g1 x1 ... xn_1)
        ...
        (gk x1 ... xn_k))
       :namedp flg)       ; [optional keyword argument]

  where [47mev[0m and [47mev-list[0m are new function symbols and [47mg1[0m, ..., [47mgk[0m are old
  function symbols with the indicated number of formals, i.e., each
  [47mgi[0m has [47mn_i[0m formals.  If the [47m:namedp[0m keyword argument is provided,
  its value should be Boolean.  If not provided, the default value
  for [47mflg[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  This function provides a convenient way to constrain [47mev[0m and [47mev-list[0m
  to be mutually-recursive evaluator functions for the symbols [47mg1[0m,
  ..., [47mgk[0m.  Roughly speaking, an evaluator function for a fixed,
  finite set of function symbols is a restriction of the universal
  evaluator to terms composed of variables, constants, lambda
  expressions, and applications of the given functions.  However,
  evaluator functions are constrained rather than defined, so that
  the proof that a given metafunction is correct vis-a-vis a
  particular evaluator function can be lifted (by functional
  instantiation) to a proof that it is correct for any larger
  evaluator function.  See [meta] for a discussion of metafunctions.

  If the [47m:namedp[0m [47mflg[0m is [47mnil[0m (the default) constraints have names of the
  form [3mev[0m[47m-CONSTRAINT-[0m[3mi[0m, e.g., [47mEV-CONSTRAINT-0[0m, [47mEV-CONSTRAINT-1[0m, etc.
  If [47mflg[0m is non-[47mnil[0m, the constraints are named more mnemonically,
  e.g., [47mEV-OF-VARIABLE[0m, [47mEV-OF-REVAPPEND-CALL[0m, etc.  We illustrate the
  [47m:namedp t[0m names below.

  [47mDefevaluator[0m executes an [47m[encapsulate][0m after generating the
  appropriate [47m[defun][0m and [47m[defthm][0m events.  Perhaps the easiest way
  to understand what [47mdefevaluator[0m does is to execute the keyword
  command

    :trans1 (defevaluator evl evl-list ((length x) (member-equal x y)))

  and inspect the output.  This trick is also useful in the rare case
  that the event fails because a hint is needed.  In that case, the
  output of [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m can be edited by adding hints, then submitted
  directly.

  Formally, [47mev[0m is said to be an ``evaluator function for [47mg1[0m, ..., [47mgk[0m,
  with mutually-recursive counterpart [47mev-list[0m'' iff [47mev[0m and [47mev-list[0m
  are constrained functions satisfying just the [constraint]s
  discussed below.

  [47mEv[0m and [47mev-list[0m must satisfy [constraint]s (0)-(7) and (k) below.
  When [47m:namedp nil[0m is supplied, the [3mi[0m in the generated constraint
  names are the parenthesized numbers below.  When [47m:namedp t[0m is
  supplied, the mnemonic names are those shown in brackets below.

    (0) How to ev an arbitrary function application:
        [EV-OF-FNCALL-ARGS]
        (implies (and (consp x)
                      (syntaxp (not (equal a ''nil)))
                      (not (equal (car x) 'quote)))
                 (equal (ev x a)
                        (ev (cons (car x)
                                  (kwote-lst (ev-list (cdr x) a)))
                            nil)))

    (1) How to ev a variable symbol:
        [EV-OF-VARIABLE]
        (implies (symbolp x)
                 (equal (ev x a) (and x (cdr (assoc-equal x a)))))

    (2) How to ev a constant:
        [EV-OF-QUOTE]
        (implies (and (consp x)
                      (equal (car x) 'quote))
                 (equal (ev x a) (cadr x)))

    (3) How to ev a lambda application:
        [EV-OF-LAMBDA]
        (implies (and (consp x)
                      (consp (car x)))
                 (equal (ev x a)
                        (ev (caddar x)
                            (pairlis$ (cadar x)
                                      (ev-list (cdr x) a)))))

    (4) How to ev an empty argument list:
        [EV-LIST-OF-ATOM]
        (implies (not (consp x-lst))
                 (equal (ev-list x-lst a)
                        nil))

    (5) How to ev a non-empty argument list:
        [EV-LIST-OF-CONS]
        (implies (consp x-lst)
                 (equal (ev-list x-lst a)
                        (cons (ev (car x-lst) a)
                              (ev-list (cdr x-lst) a))))

    (6) How to ev a non-symbol atom:
        [EV-OF-NONSYMBOL-ATOM]
        (implies (and (not (consp x))
                      (not (symbolp x)))
                 (equal (ev x a)
                        nil))

    (7) How to ev a cons whose car is a non-symbol atom:
        [EV-OF-BAD-FNCALL]
        (implies (and (consp x)
                      (not (consp (car x)))
                      (not (symbolp (car x))))
                 (equal (ev x a)
                        nil))

    (k) For each i from 1 to k, how to ev an application of gi,
        where gi is a function symbol of n arguments:
        [EV-OF-gi-CALL]
        (implies (and (consp x)
                      (equal (car x) 'gi))
                 (equal (ev x a)
                        (gi (ev x1 a)
                            ...
                            (ev xn a)))),
        where xi is the (cad...dr x) expression equivalent to (nth i x).

  [47mDefevaluator[0m defines suitable witnesses for [47mev[0m and [47mev-list[0m, proves
  the theorems about them, and constrains [47mev[0m and [47mev-list[0m
  appropriately.  We expect [47mdefevaluator[0m to work without assistance
  from you, though the proofs do take some time and generate a lot of
  output.  The proofs are done in the context of a fixed theory,
  namely the value of the constant [47m*defevaluator-form-base-theory*[0m.

  (Aside: (3) above may seem surprising, since the bindings of [47ma[0m are
  not included in the environment that is used to evaluate the lambda
  body, [47m(caddar x)[0m.  However, ACL2 lambda expressions are all [3mclosed[0m:
  in [47m(lambda (v1 ... vn) body)[0m, the only free variables in [47mbody[0m are
  among the [47mvi[0m.  See [term].)

  Acknowledgment: We thank Sol Swords and Jared Davis for their
  community book [47mtools/defevaluator-fast.lisp[0m, which provided the
  model on which the current [47mdefevaluator[0m is based.  The original
  [47mdefevaluator[0m was very inefficient, e.g., taking thousands of times
  longer than the current one on an evaluator interpreting 800
  function symbols.  We refactored their [47mdefevaluator-fast[0m (with
  permission) and made it the new implementation as of ACL2
  Version_7.2.  Both implementations produce the same constraints
  modulo the naming option provided by [47m:namedp[0m.")
 (DEFEXEC
  (EVENTS MBE)
  "Attach a terminating executable function to a definition

  Suppose you define a function [47m(fn x)[0m with a [guard] of [47m(good-input-p
  x)[0m, and you know that when the guard holds, the measure decreases
  on each recursive call.  Unfortunately, the definitional principle
  (see [defun]) ignores the guard.  For example, if the definition
  has the form

    (defun fn (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (good-input-p x)))
      (if (not-done-yet x)
          (... (fn (destr x)) ...)
        ...))

  then in order to admit this definition, ACL2 must prove the
  appropriate formula asserting that [47m(destr x)[0m is ``smaller than'' [47mx[0m
  under the assumption [47m(not-done-yet x)[0m but without the assumption
  [47m(good-input-p x)[0m, even if [47m(not-done-yet x)[0m is true.  In essence, it
  may be necessary to submit instead the following definition.

    (defun fn (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (good-input-p x)))
      (if (good-input-p x)
          (if (not-done-yet x)
              (... (fn (destr x)) ...)
            ...)
        nil)

  But it is unfortunate that when calls of [47mfn[0m are evaluated, for
  example when [47mfn[0m is applied to an explicit constant during a proof,
  then a call of [47mgood-input-p[0m must now be evaluated on each recursive
  call.

  Fortunately, [47mdefexec[0m provides a way to keep the execution efficient.
  For the example above we could use the following form.

    (defexec fn (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (good-input-p x)))
      (mbe :logic (if (good-input-p x)
                      (if (not-done-yet x)
                          (... (fn (destr x)) ...)
                        ...)
                    nil)
           :exec  (if (not-done-yet x)
                      (... (fn (destr x)) ...)
                    ...)))

  Here ``[47m[mbe][0m'' stands for ``must be equal'' and, roughly speaking,
  its call above is logically equal to the [47m:logic[0m form but is
  evaluated using the [47m:exec[0m form when the guard holds.  See [mbe].
  The effect is thus to define [47mfn[0m as shown in the [47m[defun][0m form above,
  but to cause execution of [47mfn[0m using the [47m:exec[0m body.  The use of
  [47mdefexec[0m instead of [47m[defun][0m in the example above causes a
  termination proof to be performed, in order to guarantee that
  evaluation always theoretically terminates, even when using the
  [47m:exec[0m form for evaluation.

    Example:

    ; Some of the keyword arguments in the declarations below are irrelevant or
    ; unnecessary, but they serve to illustrate their use.

    (defexec f (x)
      (declare (xargs :measure (+ 15 (acl2-count x))
                      :ruler-extenders :basic
                      :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable nth)))
                      :guard-hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable last)))
                      :guard (and (integerp x) (<= 0 x) (< x 25)))
               (exec-xargs
                      :test (and (integerp x) (<= 0 x))
                      :default-value 'undef ; defaults to nil
                      :measure (nfix x)
                      :ruler-extenders :basic
                      :well-founded-relation o<))
      (mbe :logic (if (zp x)
                      1
                    (* x (f (- x 1))))
           :exec  (if (= x 0)
                      1
                    (* x (f (- x 1))))))

  The above example macroexpands to the following.

    (ENCAPSULATE ()
     (LOCAL
      (ENCAPSULATE ()
       (SET-IGNORE-OK T)
       (SET-IRRELEVANT-FORMALS-OK T)
       (LOCAL (DEFUN F (X)
                (DECLARE
                 (XARGS :VERIFY-GUARDS NIL
                        :HINTS ((\"Goal\" :IN-THEORY (DISABLE NTH)))
                        :MEASURE (NFIX X)
                        :RULER-EXTENDERS :BASIC
                        :WELL-FOUNDED-RELATION O<))
                (IF (AND (INTEGERP X) (<= 0 X))
                    (IF (= X 0) 1 (* X (F (- X 1))))
                    'UNDEF)))
       (LOCAL (DEFTHM F-GUARD-IMPLIES-TEST
                (IMPLIES (AND (INTEGERP X) (<= 0 X) (< X 25))
                         (AND (INTEGERP X) (<= 0 X)))
                :RULE-CLASSES NIL))))
     (DEFUN F (X)
       (DECLARE (XARGS :MEASURE (+ 15 (ACL2-COUNT X))
                       :RULER-EXTENDERS :BASIC
                       :HINTS ((\"Goal\" :IN-THEORY (DISABLE NTH)))
                       :GUARD-HINTS ((\"Goal\" :IN-THEORY (DISABLE LAST)))
                       :GUARD (AND (INTEGERP X) (<= 0 X) (< X 25))))
       (MBE :LOGIC
            (IF (ZP X) 1 (* X (F (- X 1))))
            :EXEC
            (IF (= X 0) 1 (* X (F (- X 1)))))))

  Notice that in the example above, the [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m in the [47m[local][0m
  definition of [47mF[0m are inherited from the [47m:hints[0m in the [47m[xargs][0m of the
  [47mdefexec[0m form.  We discuss such inheritance below.

  CAVEAT: Termination is not considered for calls of [47m[mbe][0m under the
  top-level call.  Moreover, the [47m:exec[0m part of an [47m[mbe][0m call under
  the [47m:logic[0m part of any superior [47mmbe[0m call is completely ignored.

    General Form:
    (defexec fn (var1 ... varn)
      dcl ... dcl ; optionally, also one documentation string, as for defun
      (mbe :LOGIC logic-body
           :EXEC  exec-body))

  where the syntax is identical to the syntax of [47m[defun][0m where the body
  is a call of [47mmbe[0m, with the exceptions described below.  Thus, [47mfn[0m is
  the symbol you wish to define and is a new symbolic name and [47m(var1
  ... varn)[0m is its list of formal parameters (see [name]).  The first
  exception is that at least one [47mdcl[0m (i.e., [47m[declare][0m form) must
  specify a [47m:guard[0m, [47mguard[0m.  The second exception is that one of the
  [47mdcl[0ms is allowed to contain an element of the form [47m(exec-xargs ...)[0m.
  The [47mexec-xargs[0m form, if present, must specify a non-empty
  [47m[keyword-value-listp][0m each of whose keys is one of [47m:test[0m,
  [47m:default-value[0m, or one of the standard [47m[xargs][0m keys of [47m:measure[0m,
  [47m:ruler-extenders[0m, [47m:well-founded-relation[0m, [47m:hints[0m [47m:stobjs[0m, or [47m:dfs[0m.
  Any of these standard [47mxargs[0m keys that is present in an [47mxargs[0m of
  some [47mdcl[0m but is not specified in the (possibly nonexistent)
  [47mexec-xargs[0m form is considered to be specified in the [47mexec-xargs[0m
  form, as illustrated in the example above for [47m:hints[0m.  (So for
  example, if you want [47m:hints[0m in the final, non-local definition but
  not in the local definition, then specify the [47m:hints[0m in the [47mxargs[0m
  but specify [47m:hints nil[0m in the [47mexec-xargs[0m.)  If [47m:test[0m is specified
  and not [47mnil[0m, let [47mtest[0m be its value; otherwise let [47mtest[0m default to
  [47mguard[0m.  If [47m:default-value[0m is specified, let [47mdefault-value[0m be its
  value; else [47mdefault-value[0m is [47mnil[0m.  [47mDefault-value[0m should have the
  same [signature] as [47mexec-body[0m; otherwise the [47mdefexec[0m form will fail
  to be admitted.

  The above General Form's macroexpansion is of the form [47m(PROGN encap
  final-def)[0m, where [47mencap[0m and [47mfinal-def[0m are as follows.  [47mFinal-def[0m is
  simply the result of removing the [47mexec-xargs[0m declaration (if any)
  from its [47m[declare][0m form, and is the result of evaluating the given
  [47mdefexec[0m form, since [47mencap[0m is of the following form.

    ; encap
    (ENCAPSULATE ()
      (set-ignore-ok t)             ; harmless for proving termination
      (set-irrelevant-formals-ok t) ; harmless for proving termination
      (local local-def)
      (local local-thm))

  The purpose of [47mencap[0m is to ensure that the executable version of [47mname[0m
  terminates on all arguments.  Thus, [47mlocal-def[0m and [47mlocal-thm[0m are as
  follows, where the [47mxargs[0m of the [47m[declare][0m form are the result of
  adding [47m:VERIFY-GUARDS NIL[0m to the result of removing the [47m:test[0m and
  (optional) [47m:default-value[0m from the [47mexec-xargs[0m.

    ; local-def
    (DEFUN fn formals
      (DECLARE (XARGS :VERIFY-GUARDS NIL ...))
      (IF test
          exec-body
        default-value))

    ; local-thm
    (DEFTHM fn-EXEC-GUARD-HOLDS
      (IMPLIES guard test)
      :RULE-CLASSES NIL)

  We claim that if the above [47mlocal-def[0m and [47mlocal-thm[0m are admitted, then
  all evaluations of calls of [47mfn[0m terminate.  The concern is that the
  use of [47m[mbe][0m in [47mfinal-def[0m allows for the use of [47mexec-body[0m for a
  call of [47mfn[0m, as well as for subsequent recursive calls, when [47mguard[0m
  holds and assuming that the guards have been verified for
  [47mfinal-def[0m.  However, by [47mlocal-thm[0m we can conclude in this case that
  [47mtest[0m holds, in which case the call of [47mfn[0m may be viewed as a call of
  the version of [47mfn[0m defined in [47mlocal-def[0m.  Moreover, since guards
  have been verified for [47mfinal-def[0m, then guards hold for subsequent
  evaluation of [47mexec-body[0m, and in particular for recursive calls of
  [47mfn[0m, which can thus continue to be viewed as calls using [47mlocal=def[0m.")
 (DEFINDUCTOR
  (LOOP$-RECURSION)
  "Create an induction scheme for a [47mloop$[0m-recursive function

  [47mDefinductor[0m is a utility provided as part of the community book
  [47mprojects/apply/top[0m, which should be included in any session dealing
  with [47m[apply$][0m, [47m[loop$][0m, or [47m[loop$-recursion][0m.  [47m(Definductor fn)[0m
  attempts to create an induction scheme appropriate for the
  previously defined [47mloop$[0m-recursive function [47mfn[0m and prove an
  [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m rule so that certain calls of [47mfn[0m suggest that
  induction.

  [31;1mWarning:[0m [47mDefinductor[0m currently handles a very small class of
  [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions and may produce unhelpful error messages
  when given a function name outside of that class!  We hope to
  improve it and this documentation as we all get more experience
  with [47mloop$[0ms and [47mloop$[0m-recursion.

    Examples:
    (definductor copy-nat-tree)

    (definductor copy-nat-tree
                 :measure (my-measure x)
                 :hints ((\"Goal\" :use ...)))

    General Form:
    (definductor name &key measure well-founded-relation ruler-extenders hints)

  where [47mname[0m is the name of a previously admitted [47mloop$[0m-recursive
  function satisfying the restrictions listed below.  When successful
  it defines a function named [47mname-INDUCTOR[0m that suggests an
  induction scheme that is supposedly appropriate for [47mname[0m, admits it
  with a silent proof of its measure theorems, and then proves an
  [47m:[0m[47minduction[0m rule to associate that scheme with calls of [47mname[0m.  When
  omitted, the optional keyword arguments [47mmeasure[0m,
  [47mwell-founded-relation[0m, and [47mruler-extenders[0m default to the measure,
  well-founded relation, and ruler-extender settings used in the
  admittance of [47mname[0m.  The keyword argument [47mhints[0m defaults to [47mnil[0m.
  Note that an appropriate choice of [47mruler-extenders[0m can improve some
  induction schemes.  See [induction-coarse-v-fine-grained].


Restrictions

  The given function, [47mname[0m, must satisfy the following restrictions.
  [31;1mNote:[0m Because we anticipate this utility being further developed in
  the near future this list may not correspond to the latest
  implementation!

    * [47mName[0m must be a symbol naming a previously admitted [47mloop$[0m-recursive
      function.

    * Every recursive [47mloop$[0m in the body of [47mname[0m -- that is, every [47mloop$[0m
      that calls [47mname[0m recursively in the [47mwhen[0m, [47muntil[0m, or [47mbody[0m clauses
      of the [47mloop$[0m -- must have as its target(s) distinct measured
      variables or [47mcdr[0m-nests around such variables.

    * Every recursive [47mloop$[0m must use [47mIN[0m-iteration, not [47mON[0m- or
