 (DEFINE-PC-HELP
  (PROOF-BUILDER)
  "Define a macro command whose purpose is to print something

    Example:
    (define-pc-help pp ()
      (if (goals t)
          (io? proof-builder nil state
               (state-stack)
               (fms0 \"~|~y0~|\"
                     (list (cons #0
                                 (fetch-term (conc t)
                                             (current-addr t))))))
        (print-all-goals-proved-message state)))

    General Form:
    (define-pc-help name args &rest body)

  This defines a macro command named [47mname[0m, as explained further below.
  The [47mbody[0m should (after removing optional declarations) be a form
  that returns [47mstate[0m as its single value.  Typically, it will just
  print something.

  What [47m(define-pc-help name args &rest body)[0m really does is to create a
  call of [47mdefine-pc-macro[0m that defines [47mname[0m to take arguments [47margs[0m,
  to have the declarations indicated by all but the last form in
  [47mbody[0m, and to have a body that (via [47mpprogn[0m) first executes the form
  in the last element of body and then returns a call to the command
  [47mskip[0m (which will return [47m(mv nil t state)[0m).")
 (DEFINE-PC-MACRO
  (PROOF-BUILDER)
  "Define a proof-builder macro command

  A call of [47mdefine-pc-macro[0m defines a sort of macro, which is a tactic
  that generates [proof-builder] instructions.  This topic contains
  basic information about how to use this utility.  For somewhat
  sophisticated, but commented, examples, see the [community-book]
  [47mbooks/kestrel/utilities/proof-builder-macros.lisp[0m and associated
  tests in the same directory, [47mproof-builder-macros-tests.lisp[0m.

  We begin with the following example.

    (define-pc-macro ib (&optional term)
      (value
       (if term
           `(then (induct ,term) bash)
         `(then induct bash))))

  The example above captures a common paradigm: one attempts to prove
  the current goal by inducting and then simplifying the resulting
  goals.  (See [proof-builder-commands] for documentation of the
  command [47mthen[0m, which is itself a pc-macro command, and commands
  [47minduct[0m and [47mbash[0m.)  Rather than issuing [47m(then induct bash)[0m, or worse
  yet issuing [47minduct[0m and then issuing [47mbash[0m for each resulting goal,
  the above definition of [47mib[0m would let you issue [47mib[0m and get the same
  effect.

    General Form:
    (define-pc-macro cmd args dcl ... dcl body)

  where [47mcmd[0m is the name of the pc-macro that you want to define, [47margs[0m
  is its list of formal parameters.  [47mArgs[0m may include lambda-list
  keywords [47m&optional[0m and [47m&rest[0m; see [macro-args], but note that here,
  [47margs[0m may not include [47m&key[0m or [47m&whole[0m.

  The value of [47mbody[0m should be an [error-triple], of the form [47m(mv erp
  xxx state)[0m for some [47merp[0m and [47mxxx[0m.  If [47merp[0m is [47mnil[0m, then [47mxxx[0m is handed
  off to the interactive proof-builder's instruction interpreter.
  Otherwise, evaluation typically halts.")
 (DEFINE-PC-META
  (PROOF-BUILDER)
  "Define a proof-builder meta command

  Built-in meta commands of the interactive [proof-builder] include
  [47mundo[0m and [47mrestore[0m, and others ([47mlisp[0m, [47mexit[0m, and [47msequence[0m); see
  [proof-builder-commands].  The advanced proof-builder user can
  define these as well.  See ACL2 source file [47mproof-builder-b.lisp[0m
  for examples, and contact the ACL2 implementors if those examples
