 (DEFINE-TRUSTED-CLAUSE-PROCESSOR
  (EVENTS)
  "Define a trusted (unverified) goal-level simplifier

  This [documentation] assumes familiarity with [47m:clause-processor[0m
  rules; see [clause-processor].  Briefly put, a [3mclause-processor[0m is
  a user-defined function that takes as input the ACL2 representation
  of a goal --- a [clause] --- and returns a list of goals (i.e., a
  list of clauses).  A [47m:clause-processor[0m rule is a way to inform ACL2
  that a clause-processor has been proved correct and now may be
  specified in [47m:clause-processor[0m [hints].

  Here we describe a utility, [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m, that
  provides another way to inform ACL2 that a function is to be
  considered a clause-processor that can be specified in a
  [47m:clause-processor[0m hint.  You can find examples of correct and
  incorrect use of this utility in community book
  [47mbooks/clause-processors/basic-examples[0m.

  Consider the simple example already presented for [47m:clause-processor[0m
  rules (again, see [clause-processor]), for a simple
  clause-processor named [47mnote-fact-clause-processor[0m.  Instead of
  introducing an evaluator and proving a correctness theorem with
  [47m:rule-classes :clause-processor[0m, we can simply inform ACL2 that we
  trust the function [47mnote-fact-clause-processor[0m to serve as a
  clause-processor.

    (define-trusted-clause-processor
      note-fact-clause-processor
      nil
      :ttag my-ttag)

  A non-nil [47m:ttag[0m argument generates a [47m[defttag][0m event in order to
  acknowledge the dependence of the ACL2 session on the (unproved)
  correctness of this clause-processor.  That argument can be omitted
  if there is currently an active trust tag.  Note that the extra
  [47m[defttag][0m event will be [local] to the
  [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m event; that is, its effect will
  disappear after the [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m event
  completes.  This point becomes clear if one understands that a call
  of [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m expands to a call of
  [47m[encapsulate][0m, and a [47m[defttag][0m event is essentially [local] within
  any [47m[encapsulate][0m event, as is any event that sets the
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m.  See [defttag].  Because we are trusting
  this clause-processor, rather than having proved it correct, we
  refer to it as a ``trusted'' clause-processor to contrast with a
  proved-correct, or ``verified'', clause-processor.

  Now that the event displayed above has established
  [47mnote-fact-clause-processor[0m as a (trusted) clause-processor, we can
  use it in a [47m:clause-processor[0m hint, for example as follows.  Notice
  that the output is identical to that for the corresponding example
  presented for the verified case (see [clause-processor]), except
  that the word ``verified'' has been replaced by the word
  ``trusted''.

    ACL2 !>(thm (equal (car (cons x y))
                       x)
                :hints
                ((\"Goal\"
                  :clause-processor
                  (note-fact-clause-processor clause '(equal a a)))))

    [Note:  A hint was supplied for the goal above.  Thanks!]

    We now apply the trusted :CLAUSE-PROCESSOR function NOTE-FACT-CLAUSE-
    PROCESSOR to produce two new subgoals.

    Subgoal 2
    (IMPLIES (EQUAL A A)
             (EQUAL (CAR (CONS X Y)) X)).

    But we reduce the conjecture to T, by the :executable-counterpart of
    IF and the simple :rewrite rule CAR-CONS.

    Subgoal 1
    (EQUAL A A).

    But we reduce the conjecture to T, by primitive type reasoning.

    Q.E.D.

    Summary
    Form:  ( THM ...)
    Rules: ((:EXECUTABLE-COUNTERPART IF)
            (:EXECUTABLE-COUNTERPART NOT)
            (:FAKE-RUNE-FOR-TYPE-SET NIL)
            (:REWRITE CAR-CONS))
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    Proof succeeded.
    ACL2 !>

  Indeed, if one runs this example first and subsequently verifies the
  clause-processor, one will see the word ``trusted'' change to
  ``verified''.

  The general form is as follows.

    (define-trusted-clause-processor
      cl-proc           ;;; clause-processor function
      supporters        ;;; see below
      &key
      label             ;;; optional; cl-proc$label by default
      ttag              ;;; discussed above
      partial-theory    ;;; optional encapsulate event
      )

  We discussed the [47m:ttag[0m argument above, and we will discuss the
  [47msupporters[0m and [47m:partial-theory[0m arguments later below.  Let us turn
  our attention to the [47mlabel[0m argument and its ramifications for
  undoing and redundancy.

  As mentioned above, a successful [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m
  event results in an [47m[encapsulate][0m event.  If the [47m:label[0m argument is
  supplied with a non-[47mnil[0m value [47mL[0m, or if [47m:label[0m is omitted and [47mL[0m is
  the result of adding the suffix [47m\"$LABEL\"[0m to [47mcl-proc[0m, then the event
  [47m(deflabel L)[0m will be included under the resulting [47mencapsulate[0m form.
  Thus, you will be able to undo this [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m
  with [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m[47m L[0m.  Also, because of the criteria for redundant
  encapsulate events (see [redundant-encapsulate]), the entire form
  is considered redundant (skipped) if it is identical to one already
  executed in the current ACL2 [world], with one exception: if
  [47m:partial-theory[0m is [47mnil[0m or omitted, and also [47m:label nil[0m is supplied
  explicitly, then the event will not be redundant.  If the event is
  not redundant, then [47mcl-proc[0m must not already be designated as a
  trusted clause-processor.

  Note that [47mcl-proc[0m may be defined either in [47m:program[0m-mode or
  [47m:logic[0m-mode.

  The [47msupporters[0m argument should be a true list of function symbols in
  the current ACL2 world.  It is important that this list include
  user-defined functions whose definitions support the correctness of
  the clause-processor function.  Otherwise, [47m[local][0m definitions of
  those missing supporters can render the use of this
  clause-processor unsound, as discussed in the paper referenced at
  the end of the [clause-processor] documentation topic.  Below we
  discuss an additional reason that [47msupporters[0m is critical for
  soundness, in the case of dependent clause-processors.

  (Remark.  There could have been two notions of supporters: one for
  functions whose definitions support the correctness of the
  clause-processor function, and, in the case of dependent
  clause-processors, one for supporters of the ``promised
  encapsulate'' discussed below.  But for simplicity, a single
  [47msupporters[0m argument serves both purposes.)

  [31;1mDependent clause-processors and promised encapsulates[0m: The
  [47m:partial-theory[0m argument

  Suppose you want to introduce a clause-processor to reason about a
  complex hardware simulator that is implemented outside ACL2.
  Sawada and Reeber had just such a problem, as reported in their
  FMCAD 2006 paper.  Indeed, they used [47m[sys-call][0m to implement a
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m-mode function in ACL2 that can invoke that simulator.
  (This has been changed to [47m[sys-call*][0m since [47msys-call[0m cannot invoke
  the OS during proofs; see [sys-call].)  In principle one could code
  the simulator directly in ACL2; but it would be a tremendous amount
  of work that has no practical purpose, given the interface to the
  external simulator.  So: In what sense can we have a
  clause-processor that proves properties about a simulator when that
  simulator is not fully axiomatized in ACL2?  Our answer, in a
  nutshell, is this: The above [47m:partial-theory[0m argument provides a
  way to write merely some of the [constraint]s on the external tool
  (or even no constraints at all), with the understanding that such
  constraints are present implicitly in a stronger ``promised''
  [47mencapsulate[0m, for example by exporting the full definition.

  If a trusted clause-processor is introduced with a non-[47mnil[0m
  [47m:partial-theory[0m argument, we call it a ``dependent''
  clause-processor, because its correctness is dependent on the
  constraints implicitly introduced by the [47m:partial-theory[0m
  [47mencapsulate[0m form.  The implicit constraints should logically imply
  the constraints actually introduced by the explicit [47mencapsulate[0m,
  but they should also be sufficient to justify every possible
  invocation of the clause-processor in a [47m:clause-processor[0m hint.
  The user of a [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m form is making a
  guarantee --- or, is relying on a guarantee provided by the writer
  of that form --- that in principle, there exists a so-called
  ``promised encapsulate'': an [47mencapsulate[0m form with the same
  [signature] as the [47m:partial-theory[0m [47mencapsulate[0m form associated with
  the trusted clause-processor, but whose constraints introduced are
  the aforementioned implicit constraints.

  There are several additional requirements on a [47m:partial-theory[0m
  argument.  First, it must be an [47m[encapsulate][0m event with non-empty
  [signature].  Moreover, the functions introduced by that event must
  be exactly those specified in the signature, and no more.  And
  further still, the [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m form cannot be
  executed inside any [47m[encapsulate][0m form with non-empty [signature];
  we can think of this situation as attempting to associate more than
  one [47mencapsulate[0m with the functions introduced in the inner
  [47mencapsulate[0m.

  Moreover, soundness depends on inclusion of enough function symbols
  in the [47msupporters[0m argument, as follows.  Let [47mS[0m be the set of
  specified [47msupporters[0m augmented by the set of function symbols
  either introduced by, or in a property exported by, the
  [47m:partial-theory[0m argument, which we call the ``promised
  encapsulate''.  Then every function symbol constrained by the
  promised encapsulate is in [47mS[0m.

  The [47m:partial-theory[0m event will (in essence) be executed as part of
  the evaluation of the [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m form.  Again,
  a critical obligation rests on the user who provides a
  [47m:partial-theory[0m: there must exist (in principle at least) a
  corresponding promised encapsulate form with the same [signature]
  that could logically be admitted, whenever the above
  [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m form is evaluated successfully,
  that justifies the designation of [47mcl-proc[0m as a clause-processor.
  See also the paper mentioned above for more about promised
  encapsulates.  A key consequence is that the [constraint]s are
  unknown for the functions introduced in (the signature of) a
  [47m:partial-theory[0m [47m[encapsulate][0m form.  Thus, functional instantiation
  (see [functional-instantiation-example]) is disabled for function
  in the signature of a [47m:partial-theory[0m form.

  [31;1mA remark on the underlying implementation[0m

  You can see all of the current trusted clause-processors by issuing
  the command [47m(table trusted-cl-proc-table)[0m.  The resulting alist
  associates each trusted clause-processor with its supporters.

  Note that [47mdefine-trusted-clause-processor[0m is actually a macro that
  generates (among other things) a [47mtable[0m event for extending
  [47mtrusted-cl-proc-table[0m.  You are invited to use [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m to see
  expansions of calls of this macro.  In particular, you can see that
  the [47m:partial-theory[0m argument results in an [47mencapsulate[0m event that
  includes a call of the form [47m(set-unknown-constraints-supporters f1
  ... fk)[0m, which in effect makes that call of [47mencapsulate[0m into a call
  of [47mpartial-encapsulate[0m with supporters [47m(f1 ... fk)[0m.  See
  [partial-encapsulate].

  [31;1mA technique for using raw Lisp to define a trusted clause-processor[0m

  The following code is intended to give an idea for how one might
  define the ``guts'' of a trusted clause-processor in raw Lisp.  The
  idea is to stub out functions, such as [47macl2-my-prove below[0m, that
  you want to define in raw Lisp; and then, load a raw Lisp file to
  overwrite any such function with the real code.  But then we make
  any such overwritten function untouchable.  (This last step is
  important because otherwise, one can prove [47mnil[0m using a
  [47m:functional-instance[0m [47m:use[0m hint, by exploiting the fact that this
  function has executable code for which there is no corresponding
  definitional axiom.)  Note: The point here is only to illustrate
  the use of raw Lisp, so we do not bother to define or explain
  functions [47mhint-to-termlist[0m or [47mdisjoin-clause-segments-to-clause[0m,
  which this example assumes are defined elsewhere; their meanings
  are not important for this example.

    (defstub acl2-my-prove (term hint) t)

    (program)

    (defttag :my-cl-proc)

    (progn

    ; We wrap everything here in a single progn, so that the entire form is
    ; atomic.  That's important because we want the use of push-untouchable to
    ; prevent anything besides my-clause-processor from calling acl2-my-prove.

      (progn!

       (set-raw-mode-on state)

       (load \"my-hint-raw.lsp\") ; defines my-prove in raw Lisp

       (defun acl2-my-prove (term hint)
         (my-prove term hint)))

      (defun my-clause-processor (cl hint)
        (declare (xargs :guard (pseudo-term-listp cl)
                        :mode :program))
        (if (acl2-my-prove (disjoin cl) hint)
            (disjoin-clause-segments-to-clause
             (pairlis$ (hint-to-termlist hint) nil)
             cl)
          (prog2$ (cw \"~|~%NOTE: Unable to prove goal with ~
                      my-clause-processor and indicated hint.~|\")
                  (list cl))))

      (push-untouchable acl2-my-prove t)
      )")
 (DEFINED-CONSTANT (POINTERS)
                   "See [system-utilities].")
 (DEFINITION
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make a rule that acts like a function definition

  See [rule-classes] for a general discussion of rule classes and how
  they are used to build rules from formulas.  An example
  [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m formula from which a [47m:definition[0m rule might be built
  is:

    Examples:
    (defthm open-len-twice
      (implies (true-listp x)
               (equal (len x)
                      (if (null x)
                          0
                        (if (null (cdr x))
                            1
                          (+ 2 (len (cddr x)))))))
      :rule-classes :definition)

    ; Same as above, with :controller-alist made explicit:
    (defthm open-len-twice
      (implies (true-listp x)
               (equal (len x)
                      (if (null x)
                          0
                        (if (null (cdr x))
                            1
                          (+ 2 (len (cddr x)))))))
      :rule-classes ((:definition :controller-alist ((len t)))))

    General Form:
    (implies hyp (equiv (fn a1 ... an) body))

  where [47mequiv[0m is an equivalence relation and [47mfn[0m is a function symbol
  other than [47m[if][0m, [47m[hide][0m, [47m[force][0m or [47m[case-split][0m.  Such rules allow
  ``alternative'' definitions of [47mfn[0m to be proved as theorems but used
  as definitions.  These rules are not true ``definitions'' in the
  sense that they (a) cannot introduce new function symbols and (b)
  do not have to be terminating recursion schemes.  They are just
  conditional rewrite rules that are controlled the same way we
  control recursive definitions.  We call these ``definition rules''
  or ``generalized definitions''.

  Consider the general form above.  Generalized definitions are stored
  much as [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules for the function ``defined,'' [47mfn[0m above,
  but the procedure for applying them is a little different.  During
  rewriting, instances of [47m(fn a1 ... an)[0m are replaced by
  corresponding instances of [47mbody[0m provided the [47mhyp[0ms can be
  established, as for a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule; but when applying a
  [47m:definition[0m rule, the result of rewriting [47mbody[0m must also satisfy
  the criteria for function expansion.  There are two primary
  criteria, either of which permits expansion.  The first is that the
  ``recursive'' calls of [47mfn[0m in the rewritten body have arguments that
  already occur in the goal conjecture.  The second is that the
  ``controlling'' arguments to [47mfn[0m are simpler in the rewritten body.

  The notions of ``recursive call'' and ``controllers'' are complicated
  by the provisions for mutually recursive definitions.  Consider a
  ``clique'' of mutually recursive definitions.  Then a ``recursive
  call'' is a call to any function defined in the clique and an
  argument is a ``controller'' if it is involved in the measure that
  decreases in all recursive calls.  These notions are precisely
  defined by the definitional principle and do not necessarily make
  sense in the context of generalized definitional equations as
  implemented here.

  But because the heuristics governing the use of generalized
  definitions require these notions, it is generally up to the user
  to specify which calls in body are to be considered recursive and
  what the controlling arguments are.  This information is specified
  in the [47m:clique[0m and [47m:controller-alist[0m fields of the [47m:definition[0m rule
  class.

  The [47m:clique[0m field is the list of function symbols to be considered
  recursive calls of [47mfn[0m.  In the case of a non-recursive definition,
  the [47m:clique[0m field is empty; in a singly recursive definition, it
  should consist of the singleton list containing [47mfn[0m; otherwise it
  should be a list of all of the functions in the mutually recursive
  clique with this definition of [47mfn[0m.

  If the [47m:clique[0m field is not provided it defaults to [47mnil[0m if [47mfn[0m does
  not occur as a function symbol in [47mbody[0m and it defaults to the
  singleton list containing [47mfn[0m otherwise.  Thus, [47m:clique[0m must be
  supplied by the user only when the generalized definition rule is
  to be treated as one of several in a mutually recursive clique.

  The [47m:controller-alist[0m is an alist that maps each function symbol in
  the [47m:clique[0m to a mask specifying which arguments are considered
  controllers.  The mask for a given member of the clique, [47mfn[0m, must
  be a list of [47mt[0m's and [47mnil[0m's of length equal to the arity of [47mfn[0m.  A [47mt[0m
  should be in each argument position that is considered a
  ``controller'' of the recursion.  For a function admitted under the
  principle of definition, an argument controls the recursion if it
  is one of the arguments measured in the termination argument for
  the function.  But in generalized definition rules, the user is
  free to designate any subset of the arguments as controllers.
  Failure to choose wisely may result in the ``infinite expansion''
  of definitional rules but cannot render ACL2 unsound since the rule
  being misused is a theorem.

  If the [47m:controller-alist[0m is omitted it can sometimes be defaulted
  automatically by the system.  If the [47m:clique[0m is [47mnil[0m, the
  [47m:controller-alist[0m defaults to [47mnil[0m.  If the [47m:clique[0m is a singleton
  containing [47mfn[0m, the [47m:controller-alist[0m defaults to the controller
  alist computed by [47m(defun fn args body)[0m.  (The user can obtain some
  control over this analysis by setting the default ruler-extenders;
  see [rulers].)  If the [47m:clique[0m contains more than one function, the
  user must supply the [47m:controller-alist[0m specifying the controllers
  for each function in the clique.  This is necessary since the
  system cannot determine and thus cannot analyze the other
  definitional equations to be included in the clique.

  For example, suppose [47mfn1[0m and [47mfn2[0m have been defined one way and it is
  desired to make ``alternative'' mutually recursive definitions
  available to the rewriter.  Then one would prove two theorems and
  store each as a [47m:definition[0m rule.  These two theorems would exhibit
  equations ``defining'' [47mfn1[0m and [47mfn2[0m in terms of each other.  No
  provision is here made for exhibiting these two equations as a
  system of equations.  One is proved and then the other.  It just so
  happens that the user intends them to be treated as mutually
  recursive definitions.  To achieve this end, both [47m:definition[0m rules
  should specify the [47m:clique[0m [47m(fn1 fn2)[0m and should specify a suitable
  [47m:controller-alist[0m.  If, for example, the new definition of [47mfn1[0m is
  controlled by its first argument and the new definition of [47mfn2[0m is
  controlled by its second and third (and they each take three
  arguments) then a suitable [47m:controller-alist[0m would be [47m((fn1 t nil
  nil) (fn2 nil t t))[0m.  The order of the pairs in the alist is
  unimportant, but there must be a pair for each function in the
  clique.

  Inappropriate heuristic advice via [47m:clique[0m and [47m:controller-alist[0m can
  cause ``infinite expansion'' of generalized definitions, but cannot
  render ACL2 unsound.

  Note that the actual definition of [47mfn1[0m has the runic name
  [47m(:definition fn1)[0m.  The runic name of the alternative definition is
  [47m(:definition lemma)[0m, where [47mlemma[0m is the name given to the event
  that created the generalized [47m:definition[0m rule.  This allows
  theories to switch between various ``definitions'' of the
  functions.

  By default, a [47m:definition[0m rule establishes the so-called ``body'' of
  a function.  The body is used by [47m:expand[0m [hints], and it is also
  used heuristically by the theorem prover's preprocessing (the
  initial simplification using ``simple'' rules that is controlled by
  the [47mpreprocess[0m symbol in [47m:do-not[0m [hints]), induction analysis, and
  the determination for when to warn about non-recursive functions in
  rules.  The body is also used by some heuristics involving whether
  a function is recursively defined, and by the [47mexpand[0m, [47mx[0m, and [47mx-dumb[0m
  commands of the interactive [proof-builder].

  See [rule-classes] for a discussion of the optional field
  [47m:install-body[0m of [47m:definition[0m rules, which controls whether a
  [47m:definition[0m rule is used as described in the paragraph above.  Note
  that even if [47m:install-body nil[0m is supplied, the rewriter will still
  rewrite with the [47m:definition[0m rule; in that case, ACL2 just won't
  install a new body for the top function symbol of the left-hand
  side of the rule, which for example affects the application of
  [47m:expand[0m hints as described in the preceding paragraph.  Also see
  [set-body] and see [show-bodies] for how to change the body of a
  function symbol.

  Note only that if you prove a definition rule for function [47mfoo[0m, say,
  [47mfoo-new-def[0m, you will need to refer to that definition as
  [47mfoo-new-def[0m or as [47m(:DEFINITION foo-new-def)[0m.  That is because a
  [47m:definition[0m rule does not change the meaning of the symbol [47mfoo[0m for
  [47m:use[0m [hints], nor does it change the meaning of the symbol [47mfoo[0m in
  theory expressions; see [theories], in particular the discussion
  there of runic designators.  Similarly [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m [47mfoo[0m and [47m:[0m[47m[pf][0m [47mfoo[0m will
  still show the original definition of [47mfoo[0m.

  The definitional principle, [47m[defun][0m, actually adds [47m:definition[0m rules.
  Thus the handling of generalized definitions is exactly the same as
  for ``real'' definitions because no distinction is made in the
  implementation.  Suppose [47m(fn x y)[0m is [47m[defun][0m'd to be [47mbody[0m.  Note
  that [47m[defun][0m (or [47m[defuns][0m or [47m[mutual-recursion][0m) can compute the
  clique for [47mfn[0m from the syntactic presentation and it can compute
  the controllers from the termination analysis.  Provided the
  definition is admissible, [47m[defun][0m adds the [47m:definition[0m rule [47m(equal
  (fn x y) body)[0m.


