 (DEFMACRO-UNTOUCHABLE
  (MACROS EVENTS PROGRAMMING DEFMACRO)
  "Define an ``untouchable'' macro

  Strictly speaking, macros cannot be untouchable the way functions are
  untouchable; see [push-untouchable].  However, one can define a
  macro that is, in effect, untouchable, by using
  [47mdefmacro-untouchable[0m to introduce a trivial untouchable function
  into the definition.  Consider for example the following
  definition.

    (defmacro-untouchable mac (x)
      (list 'consp x))

  Let's look at the single-step macroexpansion of a call of the
  newly-defined macro, [47mmac[0m.

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (mac (f a))
     (PROG2$ (UNTOUCHABLE-MARKER 'MAC)
             (CONSP (F A)))
    ACL2 !>

  We see that this expansion is just as if we had used [47m[defmacro][0m
  instead of [47mdefmacro-untouchable[0m, except that a [47m[prog2$][0m wrapper
  lays down a call of [47muntouchable-marker[0m, which is a built-in
  untouchable function.  In effect, that call makes the macro, [47mmac[0m,
  untouchable.

  Of course, you are welcome to write your own variant of
  [47mdefmacro-untouchable[0m, introducing your own untouchable function.
  The result would presumably be roughly equivalent to using
  [47mdefmacro-untouchable[0m; but using [47mdefmacro-untouchable[0m has two
  advantages.  One advantage is that calls of a macro introduced with
  [47mdefmacro-untouchable[0m should have no Lisp execution overhead caused
  by the use of [47m[prog2$][0m or [47muntouchable-marker[0m, because of special
  handling provided by ACL2 for such calls of [47mprog2$[0m as well as
  inlining of [47muntouchable-marker[0m.  The other advantage is that an
  attempt to use the resulting macro without an active trust tag will
  generally give a more helpful error message, mentioning the prior
  use of [47mdefmacro-untouchable[0m as the source of the error.")
