 (DEFTTAG
  (INTERFACING-TOOLS EVENTS)
  "Introduce a trust tag (ttag)

  [31;1mIntroduction[0m.  This event is intended for advanced users who, in
  essence, want to build extensions of ACL2.  The typical intended
  use is to create [books] that extend the functionality of ACL2 in
  ways not allowed without a so-called ``active trust tag''.  A trust
  tag thus represents a contract: The writer of such a book is
  guaranteeing that the book extends ACL2 in a ``correct'' way as
  defined by the writer of the book.  The writer of the book will
  often have a small section of the book in the scope of an active
  trust tag that can be inspected by potential users of that book:

    <initial part of book, which does not use trust tags>
    (defttag :some-ttag) ; install :some-ttag as an active trust tag
    <various code that requires an active trust tag>
    (defttag nil)        ; remove active trust tag
    <final part of book, which does not use trust tags>

  Why might trust tags be needed?  The evaluation of certain functions
  can introduce bugs and even unsoundness, but can be useful in
  restricted ways that avoid such issues.  For example, [47m[sys-call][0m
  can be used in an unsafe way, for example to overwrite files, or
  worse; see [sys-call] for a frightening example from Bob Boyer.
  The following example shows that the function [47m[sys-call][0m is
  restricted by default, but can be called after installing an active
  trust tag.

    ACL2 !>(sys-call \"pwd\" nil)

    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The SYS-CALL function cannot be called unless
    a trust tag is in effect.  See :DOC defttag.

    ACL2 !>(defttag t) ; Install :T as an active trust tag.

    TTAG NOTE: Adding ttag :T from the top level loop.
     T
    ACL2 !>(sys-call \"pwd\" nil) ; print the current directory and return NIL
    /u/kaufmann
    NIL
    ACL2 !>(defttag nil) ; Remove the active trust tag (using value NIL).
     NIL
    ACL2 !>(sys-call \"pwd\" nil) ; Now we get the error again:

    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The SYS-CALL function cannot be called unless
    a trust tag is in effect.  See :DOC defttag.

    ACL2 !>

  Of course, using [47m[sys-call][0m with the Linux command [47mpwd[0m is not likely
  to cause any soundness problems!  So suppose we want to create a
  function that prints the working directory.  We might put the
  following [events] into a book that is to be certified.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defttag :pwd-ttag)
    (defun print-working-dir ()
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (sys-call \"pwd\" nil))
    (defttag nil) ; optional (books end with this implicitly)

  We can certify this book with a specification that [47m:pwd-ttag[0m is a
  legal trust tag:

    (certify-book \"pwd\" 0 t :ttags (:pwd-ttag))

  One can now use this book by executing [47m[include-book][0m with keyword
  parameter [47m:ttags (:pwd-ttag)[0m and then calling function
  [47mprint-working-dir[0m:

    (include-book \"pwd\" :ttags (:pwd-ttag))
    (print-working-dir) ; working directory is printed to terminal

  [31;1mDetailed documentation.[0m

    General Form:
    (defttag tag-name)

  where [47mtag-name[0m is a symbol.

  Note however that if [47mtag-name[0m is not [47mnil[0m, then it is converted to a
  ``corresponding [keyword]'': a symbol in the [47m\"KEYWORD\"[0m package with
  the same [47m[symbol-name][0m as [47mtag-name[0m.  Thus, for example, [47m(defttag
  foo)[0m is equivalent to [47m(defttag :foo)[0m.  Moreover, a non-[47mnil[0m symbol
  with a [47m[symbol-name][0m of [47m\"NIL\"[0m is illegal for trust tags; thus, for
  example, [47m(defttag :nil)[0m is illegal.

  This event introduces or removes a so-called active trust tag (or
  ``ttag'', pronounced ``tee tag'').  An active ttag is a [keyword]
  symbol that is associated with potentially unsafe evaluation.  For
  example, calls of [47m[sys-call][0m are illegal unless there is an active
  trust tag.  An active trust tag can be installed using a [47mdefttag[0m
  event.  If one introduces an active ttag and then writes
  definitions that contain calls of [47m[sys-call][0m, presumably in a
  defensibly ``safe'' way, then responsibility for those calls is
  attributed to that ttag.  This attribution (or blame!) is at the
  level of [books]; a book's [certificate] contains a list of ttags
  that are active in that book, or in a book that is included
  (possibly [local]ly), or in a book included in a book that is
  included (either inclusion being potentially [local]), and so on.
  We explain all this in more detail below.

  [47m(Defttag :tag-name)[0m is essentially equivalent to

    (table acl2-defaults-table :ttag :tag-name)

  and hence is [47m[local][0m to any [books] and [47m[encapsulate][0m [events] in
  which it occurs; see [ACL2-defaults-table].  We say more about the
  scope of [47mdefttag[0m forms below.

  Note: This is an event!  It does not print the usual event [summary]
  but nevertheless executes the above [47m[table][0m event and hence changes
  the ACL2 logical [world], and is so recorded.  Although no event
  summary is printed, it is important to note that the ``TTAG NOTE'',
  discussed below, is always printed for a non-nil [47m:tag-name[0m (unless
  deferred; see [set-deferred-ttag-notes]).

  [31;1mActive ttags.[0m Suppose [47mtag-name[0m names a non-[47mnil[0m symbol.  Then [47m(defttag
  :tag-name)[0m sets [47m:tag-name[0m to be the (unique) ``active ttag.'' There
  must be an active ttag in order for there to be any mention of
  certain functions, including [47m[sys-call][0m; evaluate the form
  [47m(strip-cars *ttag-fns*)[0m to see the full list of such symbols.  The
  macro [47m[progn!][0m similarly requires an active ttag.  On the other
  hand, [47m(defttag nil)[0m removes the active ttag, if any; there is then
  no active ttag.  The scope of a [47mdefttag[0m form in a book being
  certified or included is limited to subsequent forms in the same
  book before the next [47mdefttag[0m (if any) in that book.  Similarly, if
  a [47mdefttag[0m form is evaluated in the top-level loop, then its effect
  is limited to subsequent forms in the top-level loop before the
  next [47mdefttag[0m in the top-level loop (if any).  Moreover,
  [47m[certify-book][0m is illegal when a ttag is active; of course, in such
  a circumstance one can execute [47m(defttag nil)[0m in order to allow book
  certification.

  [31;1mTtag notes and the ``certifier.''[0m When a [47mdefttag[0m is executed with an
  argument other than [47mnil[0m, output is printed, starting on a fresh
  line with: [47mTTAG NOTE[0m.  For example:

    ACL2 !>(defttag :foo)

    TTAG NOTE: Adding ttag :FOO from the top level loop.
     :FOO
    ACL2 !>

  If the [47mdefttag[0m occurs in an included book, the message looks like
  this.

    TTAG NOTE (for included book): Adding ttag :FOO from file /u/smith/acl2/my-book.lisp.

  The ``[47mTTAG NOTE[0m'' message is always printed on a single line.  The
  intention is that one can search the standard output for all such
  notes in order to find all [3mdefttag[0m events.  In a sense, [3mdefttag[0m
  events can allow you to define your own system on top of ACL2 (for
  example, see [progn!]).  So in order for someone else (who we might
  call the ``certifier'') to be confident that your collection of
  [books] is meaningful, that certifier should certify all the
  user-supplied books from scratch and check either that no [47m:ttags[0m
  were supplied to [47m[certify-book][0m, or else look for every [47mTTAG NOTE[0m
  in the standard output in order to locate all [47mdefttag[0m [events] with
  non-[47mnil[0m tag name.  In this way, the certifier can in principle
  decide whether to be satisfied that those [47mdefttag[0m events did not
  allow inappropriate forms in the user-supplied books.

  In order to eliminate much of the output from [47mTTAG NOTE[0ms, see
  [set-deferred-ttag-notes].  Note however that the resulting
  security is somewhat less; therefore, a [47mTTAG NOTE[0m is printed when
  invoking [47mset-deferred-ttag-notes[0m to defer printing of ttag notes.

  [31;1mAllowed ttags when certifying and including books.[0m A [47mdefttag[0m form may
  not be evaluated unless its argument is a so-called ``allowed''
  ttag.  All ttags are allowed in the interactive top-level loop.
  However, during [47m[certify-book][0m and [47m[include-book][0m, the set of
  allowed ttags may be restricted according to the [47m:ttags[0m keyword
  argument.  If this argument is [47mnil[0m then no ttag is allowed, so a
  [47mdefttag[0m call will fail during book certification or inclusion in
  this case.  This restriction applies even to [47mdefttag[0m forms already
  evaluated in the so-called certification [world] at the time
  [47m[certify-book][0m is called.  But note that [47m(defttag nil)[0m is always
  legal.

  A [47m:ttags[0m argument of [47m[certify-book][0m and [47m[include-book][0m can have value
  [47m:all[0m, indicating that every ttag is allowed, i.e., no restriction
  is being placed on the arguments, just as in the interactive
  top-level loop.  In the case of [47minclude-book[0m, an omitted [47m:ttags[0m
  argument or an argument of [47m:default[0m is treated as [47m:all[0m, except that
  warnings will occur when the book's [certificate] includes ttags;
  but for [47mcertify-book[0m, an omitted [47mttags[0m argument is treated as [47mnil[0m.
  Otherwise, if the [47m:ttags[0m argument is supplied but not [47m:all[0m, then
  its value is a true list of ttag specifications, each having one of
  the following forms, where [47msym[0m is a non-[47mnil[0m symbol which is treated
  as the corresponding [keyword].

      (1) [47m:sym[0m

      (2) [47m(:sym)[0m

      (3) [47m(:sym x1 x2 ... xk)[0m, where k > 0 and each [47mxi[0m is a string, except
      that one [47mxi[0m may be [47mnil[0m.

  In Case (1), [47m(defttag :sym)[0m is allowed to occur in at most one book
  or else in the top-level loop (i.e., the certification world for a
  book under certification or a book being included).  Case (2)
  allows [47m(defttag :sym)[0m to occur in an unlimited number of books.
  For case (3) the [47mxi[0m specify where [47m(defttag :sym)[0m may occur, as
  follows.  The case that [47mxi[0m is [47mnil[0m refers to the top-level loop,
  while all other [47mxi[0m are filenames, where the [47m\".lisp\"[0m extension is
  optional and relative pathnames are considered to be relative to
  the connected book directory (see [cbd]).  Note that the
  restrictions on [47m(defttag :sym)[0m apply equally to any equivalent for
  based on the notion of ``corresponding keyword'' discussed above,
  e.g., [47m(defttag acl2::sym)[0m.

  An error message, as shown below, illustrates how ACL2 enforces the
  notion of allowed ttags.  Suppose that you call [47m[certify-book][0m with
  argument [47m:ttags (:foo)[0m, where you have already executed [47m(defttag
  :foo)[0m in the certification world (i.e., before calling
  [47m[certify-book][0m).  Then ACL2 immediately associates the ttag [47m:foo[0m
  with [47mnil[0m, where again, [47mnil[0m refers to the top-level loop.  If ACL2
  then encounters [47m(defttag foo)[0m inside that book, you will get the
  following error (using the book's absolute pathname, as shown):

    ACL2 Error in ( TABLE ACL2-DEFAULTS-TABLE ...):  The ttag :FOO associated
    with file /u/smith/work/my-book.lisp is not among the set of ttags permitted
    in the current context, specified as follows:
      ((:FOO NIL)).
    See :DOC defttag.

  In general the structure displayed by the error message, which is
  [47m((:FOO NIL))[0m in this case, represents the currently allowed ttags
  with elements as discussed in (1) through (3) above.  In this case,
  that list's unique element is [47m(:FOO NIL)[0m, meaning that ttag [47m:FOO[0m is
  only allowed at the top level (as represented by [47mNIL[0m).

  [31;1mAssociating ttags with books and with the top-level loop.[0m When a book
  is certified, each form [47m(defttag tag)[0m that is encountered for
  non-[47mnil[0m [47mtag[0m in that book or an included book is recorded in the
  generated [certificate], which associates the keyword corresponding
  to [47mtag[0m with the [full-book-name] of the book containing that
  [47mdefttag[0m.  If such a [47mdefttag[0m form is encountered outside a book,
  hence in the [portcullis] of the book being certified or one of its
  included books, then that keyword is associated with [47mnil[0m in the
  generated [certificate].  Note that the notion of ``included book''
  here applies to the recursive notion of a book either included
  directly in the book being certified or else included in such a
  book, where we account even for [local]ly included books.

  For examples of ways to take advantage of ttags, see [hacker],
  [include-raw], [quicklisp], and more generally [interfacing-tools].
  See also [ttags-seen], [progn!], [remove-untouchable],
  [set-raw-mode], and [sys-call].


