 (DEFUN-INLINE
  (DEFUN EVENTS)
  "Define a potentially inlined function symbol and associated macro

  You may be able to improve performance by replacing an event [47m(defun f
  ...)[0m with a corresponding event [47m(defun-inline f ...)[0m, in order to
  encourage the host Lisp compiler to inline calls of [47mf[0m.

    Example Form:
    (defun-inline lng (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp x)))
      (if (endp x) 0 (1+ (lng (cdr x)))))

    General Form:
    (defun-inline fn (var1 ... varn)
      dcl ... dcl ; optionally, also one documentation string; as for defun
      body)

  satisfying the same requirements as in the General Form for [47m[defun][0m.
  The effect is to define a macro [47mfn[0m and a function [47mfn$inline[0m (i.e.,
  a symbol in the same package as [47mfn[0m but whose [47m[symbol-name][0m has the
  suffix [47m\"$INLINE\"[0m), such that each call of [47mfn[0m expands to a call of
  the function symbol [47mfn$inline[0m on the same arguments.  Moreover,
  [47m[table][0m [events] are generated that allow the use of [47mfn[0m in [theory]
  expressions to represent [47mfn$inline[0m and that cause any untranslated
  (user-level) call of [47mfn$inline[0m to be printed as the corresponding
  call of [47mfn[0m.  The documentation string is an optional string that
  can provide documentation but is essentially ignored by ACL2.

  A form [47m(defun-inline f ...)[0m actually defines a function named
  [47mf$inline[0m and a corresponding macro named [47mf[0m whose calls expand to
  calls of [47mf$inline[0m, while providing the illusion that there is just
  the ``function'' [47mf[0m.  For example, the Example Form above
  macroexpands in one step to the following form.

    (progn (defmacro lng (non-stobj-var0)
             (list 'lng$inline non-stobj-var0))
           (add-macro-fn lng lng$inline)
           (defun lng$inline (x)
             (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp x)))
             (if (endp x) 0 (1+ (lng (cdr x))))))

  Note that the above call of [47m[add-macro-fn][0m generates the
  aforementioned two table events (see [add-macro-fn]), which provide
  the illusion that we are just defining a function [47mlng[0m, as you can
  see in the following log: [47mlng[0m appears rather than [47mlng$inline[0m.

    ACL2 !>(set-gag-mode nil)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(thm (equal (lng (append x y))
                       (+ (lng x) (lng y)))
                :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable lng))))

    [.. output omitted ..]

    Subgoal *1/2
    (IMPLIES (AND (NOT (ENDP X))
                  (EQUAL (LNG (APPEND (CDR X) Y))
                         (+ (LNG (CDR X)) (LNG Y))))
             (EQUAL (LNG (APPEND X Y))
                    (+ (LNG X) (LNG Y)))).

    [.. output omitted ..]

  Under the hood, ACL2 arranges that every function symbol with suffix
  [47m\"$INLINE\"[0m is presented to the compiler as one whose calls we would
  prefer to inline.  Technically: the Common Lisp form [47m(declaim
  (inline f$inline))[0m is generated for a function symbol [47mf$inline[0m
  before that symbol's definition is submitted.  However, the Common
  Lisp spec explicitly avoids requiring that the compiler respect
  this [47mdeclaim[0m form.  Fortunately, Common Lisp implementations often
  do respect it.

  Also see [defund-inline], see [defun-notinline], and see
  [defund-notinline].

  [3mRemarks[0m.

  (1) None of these macros (including [47mdefun-inline[0m) is supported for
  use inside a [47m[mutual-recursion][0m.

  (2) Every function symbol defined in ACL2 whose [47m[symbol-name][0m has the
  suffix [47m\"$INLINE\"[0m is proclaimed to be inline; similarly for
  [47m\"$NOTINLINE\"[0m and notinline.  These suffix restrictions are
  explained in a comment in the ACL2 source definition of macro
  [47mdefun-inline[0m.  Note that only the functions themselves are thus
  proclaimed, not their executable counterparts (also known as *1*
  functions; see [evaluation]).

  (3) No special treatment for inlining (or notinlining) is given for
  function symbols locally defined by [47m[flet][0m, with two exceptions:
  when explicitly declared [47minline[0m or [47mnotinline[0m by the [47mflet[0m form, and
  for symbols discussed in (1) and (2) above that, at some point in
  the current ACL2 session, were defined as function symbols in ACL2
  (even if not currently defined because of undoing or being
  [local]).

  (4) The function symbol actually being defined by [47m(defun-inline foo
  ...)[0m is [47mfoo$inline[0m.  As mentioned above, one can be oblivious to
  this fact when writing [theory] expressions or perusing prover
  output.  However, for other purposes (for example, [47m[verify-guards][0m
  and [47m[defabsstobj][0m [47m:exports[0m) you will need to supply the name of the
  function symbol rather than the name of the macro; e.g., for the
  above form [47m(defun-inline foo ...)[0m, you may subsequently issue the
  event [47m(verify-guards foo$inline)[0m but not [47m(verify-guards foo)[0m.

  (5) Obscure Remark.  Suppose that you certify a book with compilation
  (the default) in one host Lisp, saving the expansion file.  Suppose
  that you then compile in another host Lisp by using [47m[include-book][0m
  with argument [47m:load-compiled-file :comp[0m.  Then in subsequent
  sessions, including that book with the second host Lisp will not
  result in any inline or notinline behavior for functions defined in
  the book."
)
 (DEFUN-MODE
  (DEFUN)
  "Determines whether a function definition is a logical act

  Two ``[defun-mode]s'' are supported, [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.
  Roughly speaking, [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode allows you to prototype a
  function for execution without any proof burdens, while [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  mode allows you to add a new definitional axiom to the logic.  The
  system comes up in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.  Execution of functions whose
  [defun-mode] is [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m may render ACL2 unsound!  See
  [defun-mode-caveat].

  Note that calls of [47m[local][0m and of many [events] are skipped in
  [47m:program[0m mode; see [program].

  When you define a function in the ACL2 logic, that function can be
  run on concrete data.  But it is also possible to reason
  deductively about the function because each definition extends the
  underlying logic with a definitional axiom.  To ensure that the
  logic is sound after the addition of this axiom, certain
  restrictions have to be met.  One restriction is that every
  function called by the newly defined one must be in [47m:logic[0m mode.
  (However, see [defun-mode-lambdas] for some clarification
  concerning [47m[apply$][0m.)  But the most challenging restriction is that
  the system must prove that the recursion in the new definition
  terminates.

  Because ACL2 is a [programming] language, you often may wish simply
  to program in ACL2.  For example, you may wish to define your
  system and test it, without any logical burden.  Or, you may wish
  to define ``utility'' functions --- functions that are executed to
  help manage the task of building your system but functions whose
  logical properties are of no immediate concern.  Such functions
  might be used to generate test data or help interpret the results
  of tests.  They might create files or explore the ACL2 database.
  The termination arguments for such functions are an unnecessary
  burden provided no axioms about the functions are ever used in
  deductions.

  Thus, ACL2 introduces the idea of the ``[defun-mode]'' of a function.
  The [47m:mode[0m keyword of [47m[defun][0m's [47m[declare][0m [47mxarg[0m allows you to specify
  the [defun-mode] of a given definition.  If no [47m:mode[0m keyword is
  supplied, the default [defun-mode] is used; see
  [default-defun-mode].

  There are two [defun-mode]s, each of which is written as a keyword:

  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m --- logically undefined but executable outside deductive
  contexts.

  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m --- axiomatically defined as per the ACL2 definitional
  principle.

  It is possible to change the [defun-mode] of a function from
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m to [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.  We discuss this below.

  We think of functions having [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode as ``dangerous''
  functions, while functions having [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode are ``safe.'' The
  only requirement enforced on [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions is the
  syntactic one: each definition must be well-formed ACL2.  Naively
  speaking, if a [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode function fails to terminate then no
  harm is done because no axiom is added (so inconsistency is
  avoided) and some invocations of the function may simply never
  return.  This simplistic justification of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode execution
  is faulty because it ignores the damage that might be caused by
  ``mis-guarded'' functions.  See [defun-mode-caveat].

  We therefore implicitly describe an imagined implementation of
  [defun-mode]s that is safe and, we think, effective.  But please
  see [defun-mode-caveat].

  The default [defun-mode] is [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.  This means that when you
  [47m[defun][0m a function the system will try to prove termination.  If
  you wish to introduce a function of a different [defun-mode] use
  the [47m:mode[0m [47m[xargs][0m keyword.  Below we show [47mfact[0m introduced as a
  function in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.

    (defun fact (n)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (if (or (not (integerp n)) (= n 0))
          1
        (* n (fact (1- n)))))

  No axiom is added to the logic as a result of this definition.  By
  introducing [47mfact[0m in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode we avoid the burden of a
  termination proof, while still having the option of executing the
  function.  For example, you can type

    ACL2 !>(fact 3)

  and get the answer [47m6[0m.  If you type [47m(fact -1)[0m you will get a hard lisp
  error due to ``infinite recursion.''

  However, the ACL2 theorem prover knows no axioms about [47mfact[0m.  In
  particular, if the term [47m(fact 3)[0m arises in a proof, the theorem
  prover is unable to deduce that it is [47m6[0m.  From the perspective of
  the theorem prover it is as though [47mfact[0m were an undefined function
  symbol of arity [47m1[0m.  Thus, modulo certain important issues (see
  [defun-mode-caveat]), the introduction of this function in
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode does not imperil the soundness of the system ---
  despite the fact that the termination argument for [47mfact[0m was omitted
  --- because nothing of interest can be proved about [47mfact[0m.  Indeed,
  we do not allow [47mfact[0m to be used in logical contexts such as
  conjectures submitted for proof.

  It is possible to convert a function from [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode to [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  mode at the cost of proving that it is admissible.  This can be
  done by invoking

    (verify-termination fact)

  which is equivalent to submitting the [47m[defun][0m of [47mfact[0m, again, but in
  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.

    (defun fact (n)
      (declare (xargs :mode :logic))
      (if (or (not (integerp n)) (= n 0))
          1
        (* n (fact (1- n)))))

  This particular event will fail because the termination argument
  requires that [47mn[0m be nonnegative.  A repaired [47m[defun][0m, for example
  with [47m[=][0m replaced by [47m[<=][0m, will succeed, and an axiom about [47mfact[0m
  will henceforth be available.

  Technically, [47m[verify-termination][0m submits a redefinition of the
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode function.  This is permitted, even when
  [47m[ld-redefinition-action][0m is [47mnil[0m, because the new definition is
  identical to the old (except for its [47m:mode[0m and, possibly, other
  non-logical properties).

  See [guard] for a discussion of how to restrict the execution of
  functions.  [Guard]s may be ``verified'' for functions in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  mode; see [verify-guards].


Subtopics

  [Defun-mode-lambdas]
      :program mode functions in [47m[lambda][0m objects

  [Logic]
      To set the default [defun-mode] to [47m:logic[0m

  [Program]
      To set the default [defun-mode] to [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m")
 (DEFUN-MODE-CAVEAT
  (COMMON-LISP)
  "Potential soundness issue for functions with [defun-mode] [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m

  See [raw-lisp-error] for a general discussion of raw Lisp errors.
  Although the discussion below focuses on raw Lisp errors that arise
  from calls of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions, the soundness concerns
  expressed below apply to all raw Lisp errors.

  Technically speaking, in the current implementation, the execution of
  functions having [defun-mode] [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m may damage the ACL2 system
  in a way that renders it unsound.  In practice, we have never seen
  this happen; so, the explanations below can be viewed as extremely
  paranoid.  Nevertheless, here we document this concern, even if it
  should be taken with more than a grain of salt.

  See [defun-mode] for a discussion of [defun-mode]s.  That discussion
  describes an imagined implementation that is slightly different
  from this one.  This note explains that the current implementation
  is open to unsoundness.

  For discussion of a different soundness issue that is also related to
  function execution, see [generalized-booleans].

  The execution of a function having [defun-mode] [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m may
  violate Common Lisp [guard]s on the subroutines used.  (This may be
  true even for calls of a function on arguments that satisfy its
  [guard], because ACL2 has not verified that its [guard] is
  sufficient to protect its subroutines.)  When a [guard] is violated
  at runtime all bets are off.  That is, no guarantees are made
  either about the answer being ``right'' or about the continued
  rationality of the ACL2 system itself.

  For example, suppose you make the following [47m[defun][0m:

    (defun crash (i)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program :guard (integerp i)))
      (car i))

  Note that the declared guard does not in fact adequately protect the
  subroutines in the body of [47mcrash[0m; indeed, satisfying the guard to
  [47mcrash[0m will guarantee that the [47m[car][0m expression is in violation of
  its guard.  Because this function is admitted in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m-mode,
  no checks are made concerning the suitability of the guard.
  Furthermore, in the current ACL2 implementation, [47mcrash[0m is executed
  directly in Common Lisp.  Thus if you call [47mcrash[0m on an argument
  satisfying its guard you will cause an erroneous computation to
  take place.

    ACL2 !>(crash 7)

    Error: Caught fatal error [memory may be damaged]
    ...

  There is no telling how much damage is done by this errant
  computation.  In some lisps your ACL2 job may actually crash back
  to the operating system.  In other lisps you may be able to recover
  from the ``hard error'' and resume ACL2 in a damaged but apparently
  functional image.

  THUS, HAVING A FUNCTION WITH [defun-mode] [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m IN YOUR SYSTEM
  ABSOLVES US, THE ACL2 IMPLEMENTORS, FROM RESPONSIBILITY FOR THE
  SOUNDNESS OF OUR SYSTEM.

  Furthermore

  ACL2 DOES NOT YET PROVIDE ANY MEANS OF REGAINING ASSURANCES OF
  SOUNDNESS AFTER THE INTRODUCTION OF A FUNCTION IN [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m MODE,
  EVEN IF IT IS ULTIMATELY CONVERTED TO [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m MODE (since its
  execution could have damaged the system in a way that makes it
  possible to verify its termination and [guard]s unsoundly).

  Finally,

  THE VAST MAJORITY OF ACL2 SYSTEM CODE IS IN [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m MODE AND SO
  ALL BETS ARE OFF FROM BEFORE YOU START!

  This hopeless state of current affairs will change, we think.  We
  think we have defined our functions ``correctly'' in the sense that
  they can be converted, without ``essential'' modification, to
  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.  We think it very unlikely that a mis-guarded
  function in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode (whether ours or yours) will cause
  unsoundness without some sort of hard lisp error accompanying it.
  We think that ultimately we can make it possible to execute your
  functions (interpretively) without risk to the system, even when
  some have [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.  In that imagined implementation, code
  using functions having [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode would run more slowly, but
  safely.  These functions could be introduced into the logic ex post
  facto, whereupon the code's execution would speed up because Common
  Lisp would be allowed to execute it directly.  We therefore ask
  that you simply pretend that this is that imagined implementation,
  introduce functions in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode, use them as convenient and
  perhaps ultimately introduce some of them in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode and
  prove their properties.  If you use the system this way we can
  develop (or dismiss) this style of formal system development.  BUT
  BE ON THE LOOKOUT FOR SCREWUPS DUE TO DAMAGE CAUSED BY THE
  EXECUTION OF YOUR FUNCTIONS HAVING [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m MODE!")
 (DEFUN-MODE-LAMBDAS
  (DEFUN-MODE)
  ":program mode functions in [47m[lambda][0m objects

  A rough rule-of-thumb is that all functions mentioned in a [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  mode [47m[defun][0m must themselves be in [47m:logic[0m mode.  If [47mprgm[0m is a
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode function then [47m(defun fn (x) (prgm x))[0m is illegal.
  But there are situations (always involving [47m[apply$][0m) in which
  [47m:program[0m mode function symbols are allowed to be mentioned in
  [47m:logic[0m mode [47m[defun][0ms.  We explain here.

  Here are two example terms that mention the badged (see [47m[defbadge][0m)
  [47m:program[0m mode symbol [47mprgm[0m.

    (apply$ (lambda$ (e) (prgm e)) (list x))

    (loop$ for e in lst collect (prgm e))

  Are such terms permitted in the [47mdefun[0m of a [47m:logic[0m mode function?  The
  answer is complicated because it depends on whether the [47mdefun[0m is
  [47m[loop$][0m recursive and whether the [47mdefun[0m will also try to verify the
  [47m[guard][0ms of [47mfn[0m.

  First, a [47m:logic[0m mode function may [47mapply$[0m a quoted [47m:program[0m mode
  symbol, whether guards are being verified or not.  For example,
  [47m(apply$ 'prgm args)[0m is legal.  Here [47mprgm[0m is just a quoted symbol.
  Since [47mprgm[0m is a [47m:program[0m mode symbol, there are no axioms about it,
  so during proofs such a call of [47mapply$[0m is not evaluated or
  expanded.  If this form is evaluated at the top-level -- where
  [47m:program[0m mode functions are evaluable -- [47mapply$[0m checks the guards
  of [47mprgm[0m just as it would before any [47m:program[0m mode function is
  called.

  The situation is more complicated for use of [47m:program[0m mode functions
  in forms like [47m[lambda$][0m, well-formed quoted [47m[lambda][0m objects, and
  [47m[loop$][0m.  The reason is that in guard-verified code, these forms
  are generally executed via compiled code and that requires that the
  [47mloop$[0m and [47mlambda[0m objects be Common Lisp compliant, which in turn
  implies they must be analyzable by the prover.  Since formally
  [47mloop$[0ms are understood as calls of [scion]s on [47mlambda$[0m expressions
  it suffices for us to discuss the [47mlambda$[0m case alone.  Well-formed
  quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects are treated the same way.

  So again, suppose you're defining a [47m:logic[0m mode function [47mfn[0m.  Suppose
  [47mfn[0m is not [47mloop$[0m recursive and guards are not being verified during
  the [47mdefun[0m.  Then badged [47m:program[0m mode symbols may be used [47mlambda$[0m
  forms in the definition of [47mfn[0m.

  If [47mfn[0m is [47mloop$[0m recursive, i.e., [47m:[0m[47m[loop$-recursion][0m [47mt[0m is declared,
  then all symbols used as functions in [47mlambda$[0m forms must be in
  [47m:logic[0m mode.  The reason is that the measure conjectures generated
  must be in [47m:logic[0m mode to be provable.

  If [47mfn[0m is to be guard-verified, then all symbols used as functions in
  [47mlambda$[0m forms must be in [47m:logic[0m mode.  The reason is that the guard
  conjectures must be in [47m:logic[0m mode to be provable.

  When a [47m:program[0m function is permitted in a [47m:logic[0m mode definition the
  system will print a warning to alert you to the possibility that
  proofs will fail.  For example, the following events are legal

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

    (defun prgm (x)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (list 'hi x))

    (defbadge prgm)

    (defun fn1 (x)
      (declare (xargs :mode :logic))
      (apply$ 'prgm (list x)))

  but the last event will print:

    ACL2 Warning [Problematic-quoted-fns] in ( DEFUN FN1 ...): The definition of
    FN1 is in :LOGIC mode but mentions the :PROGRAM mode function HELLO in one or
    more :FN or :EXPR slots.  Conjectures about FN1 may not be provable until
    this program is converted to :LOGIC mode and warranted!  See :DOC
    verify-termination and defwarrant.

  Uses of [47mprgm[0m in quoted well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m objects, [47mlambda$[0m forms, and
  [47mloop$[0m forms will cause similar warning messages to be printed.")
 (DEFUN-NOTINLINE
  (DEFUN EVENTS)
  "Define a not-to-be-inlined function symbol and associated macro

  See [defun-inline] for an analogous utility that supports inlining.
  The present utility is probably far less useful; it tells the
  compiler [3mnot[0m to inline calls of the function being defined.  Also
  see [defund-notinline] for a variant of this event that disables
  the newly-defined function symbol.

  Under the hood, [47m(defun-inline f ...)[0m and [47m(defun-notinline f ...)[0m
  cause evaluation of Common Lisp forms [47m(declaim (inline f$inline))[0m
  and [47m(declaim (notinline f$notinline))[0m, respectively.  According to
  the Common Lisp spec, the compiler need not respect the first of
  these (for [47minline[0m), but it must respect the second of these (for
  [47mnotinline[0m).  Fortunately, Common Lisp implementations often do
  respect the first of these as well.")
 (DEFUN-NX
  (DEFUN EVENTS)
  "Define a non-executable function symbol

    Example:

    (defun-nx foo (x state)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (mv-let (a b c)
              (cons x state)
              (list a b c b a)))
    ; Note ``ill-formed'' call of foo just below.
    (defun bar (state y)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (foo state y))

  The macro [47mdefun-nx[0m introduces definitions using the [47m[defun][0m macro,
  always in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode, such that the calls of the resulting
  function cannot be evaluated.  Such a definition is admitted
  without enforcing syntactic restrictions for executability, in
  particular for single-threadedness (see [stobj]) and
  multiple-values passing (see [mv] and see [mv-let]).  After such a
  definition is admitted, the usual syntactic rules for [47m[state][0m and
  user-defined [stobj]s are relaxed for calls of the function it
  defines.  Also see [non-exec] for a way to designate subterms of
  function bodies, or subterms of code to be executed at the top
  level, as non-executable.

  The syntax of [47mdefun-nx[0m is identical to that of [47m[defun][0m.  A form

    (defun-nx name (x1 ... xk) ... body)

  generates the following definition.

    (defun name (x1 ... xk)
      (declare (xargs :non-executable t :mode :logic))
      ...
      (prog2$ (throw-nonexec-error 'name (list x1 ... xk))
              body))

  But [47mdefun-nx[0m does two other things.  Before executing the [47mdefun[0m form
  displayed above, ACL2 arranges that [47mstate[0m is allowed as a formal
  parameter, by first introducing [47m(set-state-ok t)[0m in a way that is
  [local] to the generated event.  After executing the [47mdefun[0m, the
  [executable-counterpart] [rune] for [47mname[0m is [disable]d.  You can
  evaluate [47m:trans1 (defun-nx ...)[0m for your [47mdefun-nx[0m form to see its
  single-step macroexpansion.

  Note that because of the insertion of the above call of
  [47mthrow-nonexec-error[0m, no formal is ignored when using [47mdefun-nx[0m.

  During proofs, the error is silent; it is ``caught'' by the proof
  mechanism and generally results in the introduction of a call of
  [47m[hide][0m during a proof.  If an error message is produced by
  evaluating a call of the function on a list of arguments that
  includes [47mstate[0m or user-defined [47m[stobj][0ms, these arguments will be
  shown as symbols such as [47m|<state>|[0m in the error message.  In the
  case of a user-defined stobj bound by [47m[with-local-stobj][0m or
  [47m[stobj-let][0m, the symbol printed will include the suffix [47m{instance}[0m,
  for example, [47m|<st>{instance}|[0m.

  It is harmless to include [47m:non-executable t[0m in your own [47m[xargs][0m
  [47m[declare][0m form; [47mdefun-nx[0m will still lay down its own such
  declaration, but ACL2 can tolerate the duplication.

  Note that [47m[defund-nx][0m is also available.  It is essentially identical
  to [47mdefun-nx[0m except that as with [47m[defund][0m, [47mdefund-nx[0m leaves the
  definition [rune] disabled for the new function symbol.

  If you use guards (see [guard]), please be aware that even though
  syntactic restrictions are relaxed for [47mdefun-nx[0m, guard verification
  proceeds exactly as for [47m[defun][0m.  If you want ACL2 to skip a form
  for purposes of generating guard proof obligations, use the macro
  [47m[non-exec][0m, which generates a call of [47mthrow-nonexec-error[0m that
  differs somewhat from the one displayed above.  See [non-exec].

  See [defun] for documentation of [47mdefun[0m.")
 (DEFUN-SK
  (EVENTS)
  "Define a function whose body has an outermost quantifier

    Examples:
    (defun-sk exists-x-p0-and-q0 (y z)
      (exists x
              (and (p0 x y z)
                   (q0 x y z))))

    (defun-sk exists-x-p0-and-q0 (y z) ; equivalent to the above
      (exists (x)
              (and (p0 x y z)
                   (q0 x y z))))

    (defun-sk forall-x-y-p0-and-q0 (z)
      (forall (x y)
              (and (p0 x y z)
                   (q0 x y z)))
      :strengthen t)

    General Form:
    (defun-sk fn (var1 ... varn)
      dcl_1 dcl_2 ... dcl_k
      body
      &key
      rewrite quant-ok skolem-name thm-name strengthen constrain
      verbose)

  where [47mfn[0m is the symbol you wish to define and is a new symbolic
  [name], [47m(var1 ... varn)[0m is its list of formal parameters, the
  optional [47mdcl_i[0m forms are [47m[declare][0m forms, and [47mbody[0m is its body,
  which must be quantified as described below.  The other arguments
  are explained below.

  For a simple example, see [defun-sk-example].  For a more elaborate
  example, see [Tutorial4-Defun-Sk-Example].  See
  [quantifier-tutorial] for a careful beginner's introduction that
  takes you through typical kinds of quantifier-based reasoning in
  ACL2.  Also see [quantifiers] for an example illustrating how the
  use of recursion, rather than explicit quantification with
  [47mdefun-sk[0m, may be preferable.

  Below we describe the [47mdefun-sk[0m event precisely.  First, let us
  consider the examples above.  The first example, again, is:

    (defun-sk exists-x-p0-and-q0 (y z)
      (exists x
              (and (p0 x y z)
                   (q0 x y z))))

  It is intended to represent the predicate with formal parameters [47my[0m
  and [47mz[0m that holds when for some [47mx[0m, [47m(and (p0 x y z) (q0 x y z))[0m
  holds.  In fact [47mdefun-sk[0m is a macro, and the call above adds the
  following two [events], as shown just below.  The first event
  guarantees that if this new predicate holds of [47my[0m and [47mz[0m, then the
  term shown, [47m(exists-x-p0-and-q0-witness y z)[0m, is an example of the
  [47mx[0m that is therefore supposed to exist.  (Intuitively, we are
  axiomatizing [47mexists-x-p0-and-q0-witness[0m to pick a witness if there
  is one.  We comment below on the use of [47m[defun-nx][0m; for now,
  consider [47mdefun-nx[0m to be [47m[defun][0m.)  Conversely, the second event
  below guarantees that if there is any [47mx[0m for which the term in
  question holds, then the new predicate does indeed hold of [47my[0m and [47mz[0m.

    (defun-nx exists-x-p0-and-q0 (y z)
      (let ((x (exists-x-p0-and-q0-witness y z)))
        (and (p0 x y z) (q0 x y z))))
    (defthm exists-x-p0-and-q0-suff
      (implies (and (p0 x y z) (q0 x y z))
               (exists-x-p0-and-q0 y z)))

  Now let us look at the third example from the introduction above:

    (defun-sk forall-x-y-p0-and-q0 (z)
      (forall (x y)
              (and (p0 x y z)
                   (q0 x y z))))

  The intention is to introduce a new predicate [47m(forall-x-y-p0-and-q0
  z)[0m which states that the indicated conjunction holds of all [47mx[0m and
  all [47my[0m together with the given [47mz[0m.  This time, the axioms introduced
  are as shown below.  The first event guarantees that if the
  application of function [47mforall-x-y-p0-and-q0-witness[0m to [47mz[0m picks out
  values [47mx[0m and [47my[0m for which the given term [47m(and (p0 x y z) (q0 x y z))[0m
  holds, then the new predicate [47mforall-x-y-p0-and-q0[0m holds of [47mz[0m.
  Conversely, the (contrapositive of) the second axiom guarantees
  that if the new predicate holds of [47mz[0m, then the given term holds for
  all choices of [47mx[0m and [47my[0m (and that same [47mz[0m).

    (defun-nx forall-x-y-p0-and-q0 (z)
      (mv-let (x y)
              (forall-x-y-p0-and-q0-witness z)
              (and (p0 x y z) (q0 x y z))))
    (defthm forall-x-y-p0-and-q0-necc
      (implies (not (and (p0 x y z) (q0 x y z)))
               (not (forall-x-y-p0-and-q0 z))))

  The examples above suggest the critical property of [47mdefun-sk[0m: it
  indeed does introduce the quantified notions that it claims to
  introduce.

  Notice that the [47m[defthm][0m event just above, [47mforall-x-y-p0-and-q0-necc[0m,
  may not be of optimal form as a rewrite rule.  Users sometimes find
  that when the quantifier is [47mforall[0m, it is useful to state this rule
  in a form where the new quantified predicate is a hypothesis
  instead.  In this case that form would be as follows:

    (defthm forall-x-y-p0-and-q0-necc
      (implies (forall-x-y-p0-and-q0 z)
               (and (p0 x y z) (q0 x y z))))

  ACL2 will turn this into one [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule for each conjunct, [47m(p0 x
  y z)[0m and [47m(q0 x y z)[0m, with hypothesis [47m(forall-x-y-p0-and-q0 z)[0m in
  each case.  In order to get this effect, use [47m:rewrite :direct[0m, in
  this case as follows.

    (defun-sk forall-x-y-p0-and-q0 (z)
      (forall (x y)
              (and (p0 x y z)
                   (q0 x y z)))
      :rewrite :direct)

  We now turn to a detailed description of [47mdefun-sk[0m, starting with a
  discussion of its arguments as shown in the \"General Form\" above.

  The third argument, [47mbody[0m, must be of the form

    (Q bound-vars term)

  where: [47mQ[0m is the symbol [47m[forall][0m or [47m[exists][0m, in the \"ACL2\" package;
  [47mbound-vars[0m is a variable or true list of variables disjoint from
  [47m(var1 ... varn)[0m and not including [47m[state][0m; and [47mterm[0m is a term.  The
  case that [47mbound-vars[0m is a single variable [47mv[0m is treated exactly the
  same as the case that [47mbound-vars[0m is [47m(v)[0m.

  The result of this event is to introduce a ``Skolem function,'' whose
  name is the keyword argument [47mskolem-name[0m if that is supplied, and
  otherwise is the result of modifying [47mfn[0m by suffixing \"-WITNESS\" to
  its name.  The following definition (or a corresponding rule; see
  the discussion of [47m:constrain[0m below) and one of the following two
  theorems (as indicated) are introduced for [47mskolem-name[0m and [47mfn[0m in
  the case that [47mbound-vars[0m (see above) is a single variable [47mv[0m.  The
  name of the [47m[defthm][0m event may be supplied as the value of the
  keyword argument [47m:thm-name[0m; if it is not supplied, then it is the
  result of modifying [47mfn[0m by suffixing \"-SUFF\" to its name in the case
  that the quantifier is [47m[exists][0m, and \"-NECC\" in the case that the
  quantifier is [47m[forall][0m.

    (defun-nx fn (var1 ... varn)
      (let ((v (skolem-name var1 ... varn)))
        term))

    (defthm fn-suff ;in case the quantifier is EXISTS
      (implies term
               (fn var1 ... varn)))

    (defthm fn-necc ;in case the quantifier is FORALL
      (implies (not term)
               (not (fn var1 ... varn))))

  In the [47mforall[0m case, however, the keyword pair [47m:rewrite :direct[0m may be
  supplied after the body of the [47mdefun-sk[0m form, in which case the
  contrapositive of the above form is used instead:

    (defthm fn-necc ;in case the quantifier is FORALL
      (implies (fn var1 ... varn)
               term))

  This is often a better choice for the \"-NECC\" rule, provided ACL2 can
  parse [47mterm[0m as a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule.  A second possible value of the
  [47m:rewrite[0m argument of [47mdefun-sk[0m is [47m:default[0m, which gives the same
  behavior as when [47m:rewrite[0m is omitted.  Otherwise, the value of
  [47m:rewrite[0m should be the term to use as the body of the [47mfn-necc[0m
  theorem shown above; ACL2 will attempt to do the requisite proof in
  this case.  If that term is weaker than the default, the properties
  introduced by [47mdefun-sk[0m may of course be weaker than they would be
  otherwise.  Finally, note that the [47m:rewrite[0m keyword argument for
  [47mdefun-sk[0m only makes sense if the quantifier is [47mforall[0m; it is thus
  illegal if the quantifier is [47mexists[0m.  Enough said about [47m:rewrite[0m!

  In the case that [47mbound-vars[0m is a list of at least two variables, say
  [47m(bv1 ... bvk)[0m, the definition above (with no keywords) is the
  following instead, but the theorem remains unchanged.

    (defun-nx fn (var1 ... varn)
      (mv-let (bv1 ... bvk)
              (skolem-name var1 ... varn)
              term))

  In order to emphasize that the last element of the list, [47mbody[0m, is a
  term, [47mdefun-sk[0m checks that the symbols [47m[forall][0m and [47m[exists][0m do not
  appear anywhere in it.  However, on rare occasions one might
  deliberately choose to violate this convention, presumably because
  [47m[forall][0m or [47m[exists][0m is being used as a variable or because a macro
  call will be eliminating ``calls of'' [47m[forall][0m and [47m[exists][0m.  In
  these cases, the keyword argument [47mquant-ok[0m may be supplied a
  non-[47mnil[0m value.  Then [47mdefun-sk[0m will permit [47m[forall][0m and [47m[exists][0m in
  the body, but it will still cause an error if there is a real
  attempt to use these symbols as quantifiers.

  The use of [47m[defun-nx][0m above, rather than [47m[defun][0m, disables certain
  checks that are required for evaluation, for example in the passing
  of multiple values.  However, there is a price: calls of these
  defined functions cannot be evaluated; see [defun-nx].  Normally
  that is not a problem, since these notions involve quantifiers.
  But if you prefer that [47m[defun][0m be used instead of [47mdefun-nx[0m, you can
  arrange that using [47m[declare][0m forms, given as the [47mdcl_i[0m as shown
  above.  These will become the [47mdeclare[0m forms in the generated
  [47m[defun][0m.  If the [47m[xargs][0m [declaration] form [47m:non-executable nil[0m is
  supplied, then [47m[defun][0m will be used in place of [47m[defun-nx][0m.

  [Guard] verification is performed for [47mdefun-sk[0m events under the same
  conditions as for [47mdefun[0m events.  (An exception, ignored here but
  discussed in a later paragraph below, occurs when keyword argument
  [47m:constrain t[0m is supplied.)  Thus, by default, guard verification
  will be attempted exactly when at least one of [47mtype[0m, [47m:guard[0m, or
  [47m:verify-guards t[0m is specified in a declaration (that is, in some
  [47mdcl_i[0m.  This default behavior can be modified just as it is for
  [47mdefun[0m; see [47m[set-verify-guards-eagerness][0m.  Technical note: such
  guard verification is implemented through a generated call of
  [47m[verify-guards][0m after the [47mencapsulate[0m that surrounds the
  definitions introduced; use [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m to see the expansion.

  [47mDefun-sk[0m is a macro implemented using [47m[defchoose][0m.  Hence, it should
  only be executed in [defun-mode] [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m; see [defun-mode] and see
  [defchoose].  You can use the command [47m:[0m[47m[pcb!][0m to see the event
  generated by a call of the [47mdefun-sk[0m macro.  If you want to watch
  the subsidiary events (including the [47mdefchoose[0m event) as they are
  being executed, use keyword option [47m:verbose t[0m.

  An advanced feature is argument [47m:strengthen t[0m, which generates the
  extra constraint that is generated for the corresponding [47mdefchoose[0m
  event; see [defchoose].  The name of that generated theorem will be
  obtained by adding the suffix [47m\"-STRENGTHEN\"[0m to the function symbol
  being defined, in the same package.

  If you find that the rewrite rules introduced with a particular use
  of [47mdefun-sk[0m are not ideal, even when using the [47m:rewrite[0m keyword
  discussed above (in the [47mforall[0m case), then at least two reasonable
  courses of action are available for you.  Perhaps the best option
  is to prove the [47m[rewrite][0m rules you want.  If you see a pattern for
  creating rewrite rules from your [47mdefun-sk[0m events, you might want to
  write a macro that executes a [47mdefun-sk[0m followed by one or more
  [47m[defthm][0m events.  Another option is to write your own variant of
  the [47mdefun-sk[0m macro, say, [47mmy-defun-sk[0m, for example by modifying a
  copy of the definition of [47mdefun-sk[0m from the ACL2 sources.

  There is one more keyword argument not explained above: [47m:constrain[0m.
  The default is [47mnil[0m; otherwise this argument must be a symbol, which
  we call [47mname-def[0m, except that in the case of [47mt[0m, [47mname-def[0m is
  obtained by adding the suffix [47m\"-DEFINITION\"[0m to [47mfn[0m.  For a non-[47mnil[0m
  [47m:constrain[0m argument, this [47mname-def[0m is the name of a rule of class
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m that equates [47m(fn var1 ... varn)[0m with the body of the
  definition of [47mfn[0m.  Furthermore, in this case of a non-[47mnil[0m
  [47m:constrain[0m value the definition of [47mfn[0m is local to the surrounding
  [47mencapsulate[0m, which contains a signature for [47mfn[0m.  As usual, the
  simplest way to see the effects of [47m:constrain[0m may be to apply
  [47m:trans1[0m to your [47mdefun-sk[0m form.  Note that constraining the function
  can make it possible to attach to it (see [defattach]) and to
  introduce it as a [guard]-verified function.  Also note that when
  [47m:constrain t[0m is specified: the guard of [47mfn[0m will automatically be [47mt[0m,
  no guard verification will be performed, and [47mfn[0m will nevertheless
  be a guard-verified (and constrained) function.

  If you want to represent nested quantifiers, you can use more than
  one [47mdefun-sk[0m event.  For example, in order to represent

    (forall x (exists y (p x y z)))

  you can use [47mdefun-sk[0m twice, for example as follows.

    (defun-sk exists-y-p (x z)
      (exists y (p x y z)))

    (defun-sk forall-x-exists-y-p (z)
      (forall x (exists-y-p x z)))

  Some distracting and unimportant warnings are inhibited during
  [47mdefun-sk[0m.

  Note for ACL2(r) users (see [real]): In ACL2(r), the keyword
  [47m:CLASSICALP[0m is also supported.  Its legal values are [47mt[0m (the
  default) and [47mnil[0m, and it determines whether or not (respectively)
  ACL2(r) will consider [47mfn[0m to be a classical function.  It must be
  the case that the value is [47mt[0m (perhaps implicitly, by default) if
  and only if [47mbody[0m is classical.

  Note that this way of implementing quantifiers is not a new idea.
  Hilbert was certainly aware of it in the first half of the 20th
  century!  Also see [conservativity-of-defchoose] for a technical
  argument that justifies the logical conservativity of the
  [47m[defchoose][0m event in the sense of the paper by Kaufmann and Moore
  entitled ``Structured Theory Development for a Mechanized Logic''
  (Journal of Automated Reasoning 26, no. 2 (2001), pp. 161--203).


