 (DOUBLE-REWRITE
  (REWRITE)
  "Cause a term to be rewritten twice

  Logically, [47mdouble-rewrite[0m is the [47m[identity][0m function: [47m(double-rewrite
  x)[0m is equal to [47mx[0m.  However, the ACL2 rewriter treats calls of
  [47mdouble-rewrite[0m in the following special manner.  When it encounters
  a term [47m(double-rewrite u)[0m, it first rewrites [47mu[0m in the current
  context, and then the rewriter rewrites the result.

  Such double-rewriting is rarely necessary, but it can be useful when
  rewriting under non-trivial equivalence relations (see
  [equivalence]).  The following example will illustrate the issue.

    ; Define an equivalence relation.
    (defun my-equiv (x y)
      (equal x y))
    (defequiv my-equiv)

    ; Define a unary function whose argument is preserved by my-equiv.
    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (ignore x))
      t)
    (defcong my-equiv equal (foo x) 1)

    ; Define some other unary functions.
    (defun g (x) x)
    (defun h1 (x) x)
    (defun h2 (x) x)

    ; Prove some lemmas and then disable the functions above.
    (defthm lemma-1
      (my-equiv (h1 x) (h2 x)))
    (defthm lemma-2
      (foo (h2 x)))
    (defthm lemma-3
      (implies (foo x)
               (equal (g x) x)))
    (in-theory (union-theories (theory 'minimal-theory)
                               '(lemma-1 lemma-2 lemma-3
                                 my-equiv-implies-equal-foo-1)))

    ; Attempt to prove a simple theorem that follows ``obviously'' from the
    ; events above.
    (thm (equal (g (h1 a)) (h1 a)))

  We might expect the proof of this final [47mthm[0m to succeed by the
  following reasoning.  It is immediate from [47mlemma-3[0m provided we can
  establish [47m(foo (h1 a))[0m.  By the [47mdefcong[0m event above, we know that
  [47m(foo (h1 a))[0m equals [47m(foo (h2 a))[0m provided [47m(my-equiv (h1 a) (h2 a))[0m;
  but this is immediate from [47mlemma-1[0m.  And finally, [47m(foo (h2 a))[0m is
  true by [47mlemma-2[0m.

  Unfortunately, the proof fails.  But fortunately, ACL2 gives the
  following useful warning when [47mlemma-3[0m is submitted:

    ACL2 Warning [Double-rewrite] in ( DEFTHM LEMMA-3 ...):  In the :REWRITE
    rule generated from LEMMA-3, equivalence relation MY-EQUIV is maintained
    at one problematic occurrence of variable X in hypothesis (FOO X),
    but not at any binding occurrence of X.  Consider replacing that occurrence
    of X in this hypothesis with (DOUBLE-REWRITE X).  See :doc double-
    rewrite for more information on this issue.

  We can follow the warning's advice by changing [47mlemma-3[0m to the
  following.

    (defthm lemma-3
      (implies (foo (double-rewrite x))
               (equal (g x) x)))

  With this change, the proof succeeds for the final [47mthm[0m above.

  In practice, it should suffice for users to follow the advice given
  in the ``[47mDouble-rewrite[0m'' warnings, by adding calls of
  [47mdouble-rewrite[0m around certain variable occurrences.  But this can
  cause inefficiency in large proof efforts.  For that reason, and
  for completeness, it seems prudent to explain more carefully what
  is going on; and that is what we do for the remainder of this
  [documentation] topic.  Optionally, also see the paper ``Double
  Rewriting for Equivalential Reasoning in ACL2'' by Matt Kaufmann
  and J Strother Moore, in the proceedings of the 2006 ACL2 Workshop
  (paper is published in the {ACM Digital Library |
  http://portal.acm.org/toc.cfm?id=1217975}); you might also find it
  for free {here |
  http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.90.7190&rep=rep1&type=pdf}.

  [31;1mSuggesting congruence rules.[0m

  Sometimes the best way to respond to a ``[47mDouble-rewrite[0m'' warning may
  be to prove a congruence rule.  Consider for example this rule.

    (defthm insert-sort-is-id
      (perm (insert-sort x) x))

  Assuming that [47mperm[0m has been identified as an [equivalence] relation
  (see [defequiv]), we will get the following warning.

    ACL2 Warning [Double-rewrite] in ( DEFTHM INSERT-SORT-IS-ID ...):
    In a :REWRITE rule generated from INSERT-SORT-IS-ID, equivalence relation
    PERM is maintained at one problematic occurrence of variable X in the
    right-hand side, but not at any binding occurrence of X.  Consider
    replacing that occurrence of X in the right-hand side with
    (DOUBLE-REWRITE X).  See :doc double-rewrite for more information on
    this issue.

  The problem is that the second occurrence of [47mx[0m (the right-hand side
  of the rule [47minsert-sort-is-id[0m) is in a context where [47mperm[0m is to be
  maintained, yet in this example, the argument [47mx[0m of [47minsert-sort[0m on
  the left-hand side of that rule is in a context where [47mperm[0m will not
  be maintained.  This can lead one to consider the possibility that
  [47mperm[0m could be maintained in that left-hand side occurrence of [47mx[0m,
  and if so, to prove the following congruence rule.

    (defcong perm perm (insert-sort x) 1)

  This will eliminate the above warning for [47minsert-sort-is-id[0m.  More
  important, this [47m[defcong][0m event would probably be useful, since it
  would allow rewrite rules with equivalence relation [47mperm[0m to operate
  on the first argument of any call of [47minsert-sort[0m whose context
  calls for maintaining [47mperm[0m.

  [31;1mDetails on double-rewrite.[0m

  The reader who wants these details may first wish to see
  [equivalence] for relevant review.

  The ACL2 rewriter takes a number of contextual arguments, including
  the generated equivalence relation being maintained (see
  [congruence]) and an association list that maps variables to terms.
  We call the latter alist the [47munify-subst[0m because it is produced by
  unifying (actually matching) a pattern against a current term; let
  us explain this point by returning to the example above.  Consider
  what happens when the rewriter is given the top-level goal of the
  [47mthm[0m above.

    (equal (g (h1 a)) (h1 a))

  This rewrite is performed with the empty alist ([47munify-subst[0m), and is
  begun by rewriting the first argument (in that same empty
  [47munify-subst[0m):

    (g (h1 a))

  Note that the only equivalence relation being maintained at this
  point is [47mequal[0m.  Now, the rewriter notices that the left-hand side
  of [47mlemma-3[0m, which is [47m(g x)[0m, matches [47m(g (h1 a))[0m.  The rewriter thus
  creates a [47munify-subst[0m binding [47mx[0m to [47m(h1 a)[0m: [47m((x . (h1 a)))[0m.  It now
  attempts to rewrite the hypothesis of [47mlemma-3[0m to [47mt[0m under this
  [47munify-subst[0m.

  Consider what happens now if the hypothesis of [47mlemma-3[0m is [47m(foo x)[0m.
  To rewrite this hypothesis under a [47munify-subst[0m of [47m((x . (h1 a)))[0m,
  it will first rewrite [47mx[0m under this [47munify-subst[0m.  The key
  observation here is that this rewrite takes place simply by
  returning the value of [47mx[0m in the [47munify-subst[0m, namely [47m(h1 a)[0m.  No
  further rewriting is done!  The efficiency of the ACL2 rewriter
  depends on such caching of previous rewriting results.

  But suppose that, instead, the hypothesis of [47mlemma-3[0m is [47m(foo
  (double-rewrite x))[0m.  As before, the rewriter dives to the first
  argument of this call of [47mfoo[0m.  But this time the rewriter sees the
  call [47m(double-rewrite x)[0m, which it handles as follows.  First, [47mx[0m is
  rewritten as before, yielding [47m(h1 a)[0m.  But now, because of the call
  of [47mdouble-rewrite[0m, the rewriter takes [47m(h1 a)[0m and rewrites it under
  the empty [47munify-subst[0m.  What's more, because of the [47mdefcong[0m event
  above, this rewrite takes place in a context where it suffices to
  maintain the equivalence relation [47mmy-equiv[0m.  This allows for the
  application of [47mlemma-1[0m, hence [47m(h1 a)[0m is rewritten (under
  [47munify-subst[0m = [47mnil[0m) to [47m(h2 a)[0m.  Popping back up, the rewriter will
  now rewrite the call of [47mfoo[0m to [47mt[0m using [47mlemma-2[0m.

  The example above explains how the rewriter treats calls of
  [47mdouble-rewrite[0m, but it may leave the unfortunate impression that
  the user needs to consider each [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rule
  carefully, just in case a call of [47mdouble-rewrite[0m may be
  appropriate.  Fortunately, ACL2 provides a ``[Double-rewrite]''
  warning to inform the user of just this sort of situation.  If you
  don't see this warning when you submit a ([47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m)
  rule, then the issue described here shouldn't come up for that
  rule.  Such warnings may appear for hypotheses or right-hand side
  of a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule, and for hypotheses or full conclusion (as
  opposed to just the trigger term) of a [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rule.

  If you do see a ``[Double-rewrite]'' warning, then should you add the
  indicated call(s) of [47mdouble-rewrite[0m?  At the time of writing this
  [documentation], the answer is not clear.  Early experiments with
  double rewriting suggested that it may be too expensive to call
  [47mdouble-rewrite[0m in every instance where a warning indicates that
  there could be an advantage to doing so.  And at the time of this
  writing, the ACL2 regression suite has about 1900 such warnings
  (but note that books were developed before [47mdouble-rewrite[0m or the
  ``[Double-rewrite]'' warning were implemented), which suggests that
  one can often do fine just ignoring such warnings.  However, it
  seems advisable to go ahead and add the calls of [47mdouble-rewrite[0m
  indicated by the warnings unless you run across efficiency problems
  caused by doing so.  Of course, if you decide to ignore all such
  warnings you can execute the event:
  [47m([0m[47m[set-inhibit-warnings][0m[47m \"Double-rewrite\")[0m.

  Finally, we note that it is generally not necessary to call
  [47mdouble-rewrite[0m in order to get its effect in the following case,
  where the discussion above might have led one to consider a call of
  [47mdouble-rewrite[0m: a hypothesis is a variable, or more generally, we
  are considering a variable occurrence that is a branch of the
  top-level [47mIF[0m structure of a hypothesis.  The automatic handling of
  this case, by a form of double rewriting, was instituted in ACL2
  Version_2.9 and remains in place with the introduction of
  [47mdouble-rewrite[0m.  Here is a simple illustrative example.  Notice
  that [47mfoo-holds[0m applies to prove the final [47m[thm][0m below, even without
  a call of [47mdouble-rewrite[0m in the hypothesis of [47mfoo-holds[0m, and that
  there is no ``[Double-rewrite]'' warning when submitting [47mfoo-holds[0m.

    (encapsulate
     (((foo *) => *)
      ((bar *) => *))

     (local (defun foo (x) (declare (ignore x)) t))
     (local (defun bar (x) (declare (ignore x)) t))

     (defthm foo-holds
       (implies x
                (equal (foo x) t)))
     (defthm bar-holds-propositionally
       (iff (bar x) t)))

    (thm (foo (bar y)))")
