 (EMBEDDED-EVENT-FORM
  (EVENTS)
  "Forms that may be embedded in other [events]

    Examples:
    (defun hd (x) (if (consp x) (car x) 0))
    (local (defthm lemma23 ...))
    (progn (defun fn1 ...)
           (local (defun fn2 ...))
           ...)

    General Form:
    An embedded event form is a term, x, such that:

    * [47mx[0m is a call of an event function other than [47m[defpkg][0m (see [events]
      for a listing of the event functions);

    * [47mx[0m is a call of [47m[encapsulate][0m, [47m[progn][0m, [47m[progn!][0m, or [47m[include-book][0m;

    * [47mx[0m is of the form [47m(LOCAL x1)[0m where [47mx1[0m is an embedded event form;

    * [47mx[0m is of the form [47m(MAKE-EVENT &)[0m, where [47m&[0m is any term whose expansion
      is an embedded event (see [make-event]);

    * [47mx[0m is of the form [47m(SKIP-PROOFS x1)[0m where [47mx1[0m is an embedded event form;

    * [47mx[0m is of the form [47m(WITH-CBD str x1)[0m, where [47mstr[0m is a string and [47mx1[0m is
      an embedded event form;

    * [47mx[0m is of the form [47m(WITH-GUARD-CHECKING-EVENT c x1)[0m or
      [47m(WITH-GUARD-CHECKING-EVENT (QUOTE c) form)[0m, where [47mc[0m is a member
      of the list [47m(t nil :nowarn :all :none)[0m and [47mx1[0m is an embedded
      event form;

    * [47mx[0m is of the form [47m(WITH-OUTPUT ... x1)[0m, [47m(WITH-PROVER-STEP-LIMIT ... x1
      ...)[0m, or [47m(WITH-PROVER-TIME-LIMIT ... x1)[0m, where [47mx1[0m is an
      embedded event form;

    * [47mx[0m macroexpands to one of the forms above; or

    * [intended only for the implementation] [47mx[0m is [47m(RECORD-EXPANSION x1 x2)[0m,
      where [47mx1[0m and [47mx2[0m are embedded event forms.

  However, we add the following restrictions for [47m[local][0m contexts.

    * An embedded event form may not set the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m when in
      the context of [47m[local][0m.  Thus for example, the form

          (local (table acl2-defaults-table :defun-mode :program))

      is not an embedded event form, nor is the form [47m(local (program))[0m,
      since the latter sets the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m implicitly.  An
      example at the end of the discussion below illustrates why
      there is this restriction.

    * A call of [47m[defaxiom][0m is illegal in the context of [47m[local][0m.  Without
      this restriction, one could locally assert a strong axiom like
      [47m(equal t nil)[0m and then non-locally prove that formula, leaving
      you in an ACL2 logical [world] in which it appears that the
      formula is actually provable without such an axiom.

    * A call of [47m[add-include-book-dir!][0m or [47m[delete-include-book-dir!][0m is
      illegal in the context of [47m[local][0m.  For an explanation, see
      [add-include-book-dir!].

  Only embedded event forms are allowed in a book after its initial
  [47m[in-package][0m form.  See [books].  However, you may find that
  [47m[make-event][0m allows you to get the effect you want for a form that
  is not an embedded event form.  For example, you can put the
  following into a book, which assigns the value 17 to [47m[state][0m global
  variable [47mx[0m:

    (make-event (er-progn (assign x 17)
                          (value '(value-triple nil)))
                :check-expansion t)

  For another use of [47mmake-event[0m to create embedded event forms, see
  [make-event-example-3].

  When an embedded event is executed while [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is
  [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m, those parts of it inside [47m[local][0m forms are
  ignored.  Thus,

    (progn (defun f1 () 1)
           (local (defun f2 () 2))
           (defun f3 () 3))

  will define [47mf1[0m, [47mf2[0m, and [47mf3[0m when [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is [47mnil[0m or [47mt[0m, but
  will define only [47mf1[0m and [47mf3[0m when [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is
  [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m.

  [3mDiscussion:[0m

  [47m[Encapsulate][0m, [47m[progn][0m, and [47m[include-book][0m place restrictions on the
  kinds of forms that may be processed.  These restrictions ensure
  that the non-local [events] are indeed admissible provided that the
  sequence of [47m[local][0m and non-local [events] is admissible when
  proofs are done, i.e., when [47mld-skip-proofs[0m is [47mnil[0m.  But [47m[progn!][0m
  places no such restrictions, hence is potentially dangerous and
  should be avoided unless you understand the ramifications; so it is
  illegal unless there is an active trust tag (see [defttag]).

  [47m[Local][0m permits the hiding of an event or group of [events] in the
  sense that [47m[local][0m [events] are processed when we are trying to
  establish the admissibility of a sequence of [events] embedded in
  [47m[encapsulate][0m forms or in [books], but are ignored when we are
  constructing the [world] produced by assuming that sequence.  Thus,
  for example, a particularly ugly and inefficient [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule
  might be made [47m[local][0m to an [encapsulate] that ``exports'' a
  desirable theorem whose proof requires the ugly lemma.

  To see why we can't allow just anything as an embedded event,
  consider allowing the form

    (if (ld-skip-proofsp state)
        (defun foo () 2)
        (defun foo () 1))

  followed by

    (defthm foo-is-1 (equal (foo) 1)).

  When we process the [events] with [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is [47mnil[0m, the
  second [47m[defun][0m is executed and the [47m[defthm][0m succeeds.  But when we
  process the [events] with [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m, the
  second [47m[defun][0m is executed, so that [47mfoo[0m no longer has the same
  definition it did when we proved [47mfoo-is-1[0m.  Thus, an invalid
  formula is assumed when we process the [47m[defthm][0m while skipping
  proofs.  Thus, the first form above is not a legal embedded event
  form.

  If you encounter a situation where these restrictions seem to prevent
  you from doing what you want to do, then you may find [47mmake-event[0m to
  be helpful.  See [make-event].

  [47m[Defpkg][0m is not allowed because it affects how things are read after
  it is executed.  But all the forms embedded in an event are read
  before any are executed.  That is,

    (encapsulate nil
                 (defpkg \"MY-PKG\" nil)
                 (defun foo () 'my-pkg::bar))

  makes no sense since [47mmy-pkg::bar[0m must have been read before the
  [47m[defpkg][0m for [47m\"MY-PKG\"[0m was executed.

  Finally, let us elaborate on the restriction mentioned earlier
  related to the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m.  Consider the following form.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (local (program))
     (defun foo (x)
       (if (equal 0 x)
           0
         (1+ (foo (- x))))))

  See [local-incompatibility] for a discussion of how [47m[encapsulate][0m
  processes event forms.  Briefly, on the first pass through the
  [events] the definition of [47mfoo[0m will be accepted in [47m[defun][0m mode
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m, and hence accepted.  But on the second pass the form
  [47m(local (program))[0m is skipped because it is marked as [47m[local][0m, and
  hence [47mfoo[0m is accepted in [47m[defun][0m mode [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.  Yet, no proof has
  been performed in order to admit [47mfoo[0m, and in fact, it is not hard
  to prove a contradiction from this definition!")
 (ENABLE
  (THEORIES THEORY-FUNCTIONS)
  "Adds names to current theory

    Example:
    (enable fact (:e fact) associativity-of-app)

    General Form:
    (enable name1 name2 ... namek)

  where each [47mnamei[0m is a runic designator; see [theories].  The result
  is the theory that contains all the names in the current theory
  plus those listed.  Note that this is merely a function that
  returns a theory.  The result is generally a very long list of
  [rune]s and you will probably regret printing it.

  For related utilities, see [disable] and see [e/d].

  The standard way to ``enable'' a fixed set of names, is as follows;
  see [hints] and see [in-theory].

    :in-theory (enable name1 name2 ... namek)  ; in a hint
    (in-theory (enable name1 name2 ... namek)) ; as an event
    (local ; often desirable, to avoid exporting from the current context
     (in-theory (enable name1 name2 ... namek)))

  Note that all the names are implicitly quoted.  If you wish to enable
  a computed list of names, [47mlst[0m, use the theory expression
  [47m(union-theories (current-theory :here) lst)[0m.

  To see the runes currently disabled that are among those created when
  a function symbol [47mFN[0m is introduced, evaluate [47m(disabledp 'FN)[0m.")
 (ENABLE-FORCING
  (FORCE)
  "To allow forced case splits

    General Form:
    ACL2 !>:enable-forcing    ; allowed forced case splits

  See [force] and see [case-split] for a discussion of forced case
  splits, which are turned back on by this command.  See
  [disable-forcing] for an example showing how to turn off forced
  case splits.

  [47mEnable-forcing[0m is actually a macro that [enable]s the
  [executable-counterpart] of the function symbol [47mforce[0m; see [force].
  When you want to use [hints] to turn on forced case splits, use a
  form such as one of the following (these are equivalent).

    :in-theory (enable (:executable-counterpart force))
    :in-theory (enable (force))")
