 (ENTER-BOOT-STRAP-MODE
  (HISTORY)
  "The first millisecond of the Big Bang

  ACL2 functions, e.g., [47m[if][0m, that show [47menter-boot-strap-mode[0m as their
  defining [command] are in fact [primitive]s.  It is impossible for
  the system to display defining axioms about these symbols.

  [47mEnter-boot-strap-mode[0m is a Common Lisp function but not an ACL2
  function.  It magically creates from [47mnil[0m an ACL2 property list
  [world] that lets us start the boot-strapping process.  That is,
  once [47menter-boot-strap-mode[0m has created its [world], it is possible
  to process the [47m[defconst][0ms, [47m[defun][0ms, and [47m[defaxiom][0ms, necessary to
  bring up the rest of the system.  Before that [world] is created,
  the attempt by ACL2 even to translate a [47m[defun][0m form, say, would
  produce an error because [47m[defun][0m is undefined.

  Several ACL2 functions show [47menter-boot-strap-mode[0m as their defining
  [command].  Among them are [47m[if][0m, [47m[cons][0m, [47m[car][0m, and [47m[cdr][0m.  These
  functions are characterized by axioms rather than definitional
  equations --- axioms that in most cases are built into our code and
  hence do not have any explicit representation among the rules and
  formulas in the system.")
