 (EQUALITY-VARIANTS
  (PROGRAMMING)
  "Versions of a function using different equality tests

  The ACL2 environment includes not only a logic but also a programming
  language, which is based on Common Lisp.  Execution efficiency may
  be increased by using fast equality tests: [47m[eq][0m for symbols and
  [47m[eql][0m for numbers, symbols, and characters (see [eqlablep]).
  Several list-processing functions built into ACL2 thus have three
  variants, depending on whether the equality function used is [47m[eq][0m,
  [47m[eql][0m, or [47m[equal][0m; a list is provided below.  ACL2 has taken
  measures to ensure that one can reason about a single logical
  function even when one uses these different variants.

  Consider for example the case of list membership.  Common Lisp
  provides a utility for this purposes, [47m[member][0m, which can take a
  [47m:TEST[0m keyword argument, default [47m[eql][0m.  So for example, one might
  write

    (member a x :TEST 'eq)

  if either [47ma[0m is a symbol or [47mx[0m is a list of symbols, so that the
  fastest equality test ([47m[eq][0m) may be used when comparing [47ma[0m to
  successive elements of the list, [47mx[0m.  One might elsewhere write
  [47m(member b (foo y))[0m, which is equivalent to [47m(member b (foo y) :TEST
  'eql)[0m, for example if [47mb[0m is a number.  If one wants to reason about
  both [47m(member a x :TEST 'eq)[0m and [47m(member b y)[0m, it might be helpful
  for both calls of [47mmember[0m to be the same logically, even though
  Common Lisp will execute them differently (using [47m[eq][0m or [47m[eql][0m,
  respectively).  ACL2 arranges that in fact, both references to
  [47m[member][0m generate calls of [47m[member-equal][0m in the theorem prover.

  In fact, since [47m[member][0m can take the optional [47m:TEST[0m keyword argument,
  then in ACL2 it must be defined as a macro, not a function (see
  [defun]).  ACL2 arranges that a call of [47mmember[0m generates a
  corresponding call of the function [47m[member-equal][0m, regardless of
  the value of [47mTEST[0m, in a manner that produces [47m[member-equal][0m in
  prover output.  More generally, you can expect ACL2 to treat your
  use of [47m[member][0m as though you had written [47m[member-equal][0m, for
  example in the way it stores [47m[rewrite][0m rules and other kinds of
  rules as well (see [rule-classes]).  We say little here about how
  this is all arranged by ACL2, other than to mention that [47m[mbe][0m is
  utilized (so, you might see mention in proof logs) of the function
  [47m[return-last][0m that implements [47m[mbe][0m.  Such details, which involve a
  notion of ``macro alias'' and probably can be ignored by most
  users, may be found elsewhere; see [equality-variants-details].

  As a convenience to the user, the macro [47mmember-eq[0m is provided that
  expands to a corresponding call of [47mmember[0m with [47m:TEST 'eq[0m, as
  follows.

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (member-eq (foo x) (bar y))
     (MEMBER (FOO X) (BAR Y) :TEST 'EQ)
    ACL2 !>

  For efficiency we recommend using the [47m-equal[0m equality variant, for
  example [47m[member-equal][0m or [47m([0m[47m[member][0m[47m ... :TEST 'equal)[0m, in certain
  contexts: [47m[defmacro][0m, [47m[defpkg][0m, [47m[defconst][0m, and [47m[value-triple][0m
  forms.  However, the implementation of equality variants has been
  designed so that when defining a function, one may choose freely in
  a definition an equality variant of primitive [47mF[0m, to get efficient
  execution but where subsequent reasoning is about [47mF-equal[0m.  For
  details about the above recommendation and for a discussion of the
  implementation, see [equality-variants-details].

  The following alphabetical list includes all primitives that have
  equality variants.  Each macro [47mF[0m listed below takes an optional
  [47m:TEST[0m keyword argument of [47m'eq[0m, [47m'eql[0m, or [47m'equal[0m, where [47m'eql[0m is the
  default.  For each such [47mF[0m, a function [47mF-equal[0m is defined such that
  for logical purposes (in particular theorem proving), each call of
  [47mF[0m expands to a corresponding call of [47mF-equal[0m.  For convenience, a
  macro [47mF-eq[0m is also defined, so that a call of [47mF-eq[0m expands to a
  corresponding call of [47mF[0m with [47m:TEST 'eq[0m.

    [47m[add-to-set][0m
    [47m[assoc][0m
    [47m[intersection$][0m ; (see Note below)
    [47m[intersectp][0m
    [47m[member][0m
    [47m[no-duplicatesp][0m
    [47mposition-ac[0m
    [47m[position][0m
    [47m[put-assoc][0m
    [47m[rassoc][0m
    [47m[remove][0m
    [47m[remove-assoc][0m
    [47m[remove-duplicates][0m
    [47m[remove1][0m
    [47m[remove1-assoc][0m
    [47m[set-difference$][0m ; (see Note below)
    [47m[subsetp][0m
    [47m[union$][0m ; (see Note below)

  Note: Three of the macros above have names ending with the character,
  `[47m$[0m': [47m[intersection$][0m, [47m[set-difference$][0m, and [47m[union$][0m.  In each
  case there is a corresponding Common Lisp primitive without the
  trailing `[47m$[0m': [47mintersection[0m, [47mset-difference[0m, and [47munion[0m.  However,
  Common Lisp does not specify the order of elements in the list
  returned by those primitives, so ACL2 has its own.  Nevertheless,
  the only use of the trailing `[47m$[0m' is to distinguish the primitives;
  associated functions and macros, for example [47munion-eq[0m and
  [47mintersection-equal[0m, do not include the `[47m$[0m' character in their
  names.

  We conclude with a brief discussion of [guards].  The expansion of
  any of the above macros depends on the keyword argument, which
  generates a function call with a guard suitable for the equality
  test being used.  Consider for example the call [47m(member x lst :test
  'eq)[0m, or equivalently, [47m(member-eq x lst)[0m.  Expanding these macros
  leads to a call of [47m[mbe][0m; you can see how that goes by using
  [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m.  Ultimately, the guard being checked is that of the
  function [47mmember-eq-exec[0m, which is as follows.

    (if (symbolp x)
        (true-listp lst)
      (symbol-listp lst))

  Care has been taken to ensure that this guard is checked during
  evaluation and also that it generates suitable proof obligations
  for guard verification (see [verify-guards]).  A guard violation
  might look something like this:

    ACL2 !>(member-eq 3 '(4 5))


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The guard for the function call
    (MEMBER-EQ-EXEC$GUARD-CHECK X LST), which is
    (IF (SYMBOLP X) (TRUE-LISTP LST) (SYMBOL-LISTP LST)), is violated by
    the arguments in the call (MEMBER-EQ-EXEC$GUARD-CHECK 3 '(4 5)).
    See :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this
    check with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.

    ACL2 !>

  Above, [47mmember-eq-exec$guard-check[0m is a function generated as part of
  ACL2's expansion of [47mmember[0m with [47m:test 'eq[0m, and this function symbol
  can be quite reasonably ignored.  The important thing is that it
  refers to the guard for [47mmember-eq-exec[0m, which as the name may
  suggest is intended to guard the execution of a call of
  [47m[member-eq][0m, or a call of [47m[member][0m with [47m:test 'eq[0m.  The important
  part of the message above is the guard actually being violated: [47m(IF
  (SYMBOLP X) (TRUE-LISTP LST) (SYMBOL-LISTP LST))[0m.


