 (EVALUATION
  (PROGRAMMING)
  "Evaluating ACL2 expressions

  This topic uses examples to expose how ACL2 uses Common Lisp to
  evaluate expressions.  Links are provided to topics that elaborate
  on aspects of evaluation in ACL2.

  At a high level, we can say that ACL2 evaluation takes place using
  the underlying host Common Lisp.  But consider the form [47m(car 3)[0m.
  In Common Lisp this is an error; typically you will be thrown into
  the Lisp debugger.  In the ACL2 read-eval-print loop, however, you
  may see the following.

    ACL2 !>(car 3)


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The guard for the function call (CAR X),
    which is (OR (CONSP X) (EQUAL X NIL)), is violated by the arguments
    in the call (CAR 3).
    See :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this
    check with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.

    ACL2 !>

  The message suggests that the function [47mcar[0m has a [guard], or
  precondition, that is violated by the proposed argument, [47m3[0m.  So
  clearly something more is going on here than just evaluation of the
  form [47m(car 3)[0m in Common Lisp.

  In fact, every ACL2 function symbol is associated with the following
  two Common Lisp definitions, as explained below:

    * the ``executable-counterpart'' definition, sometimes called the
      ``*1*'' function (pronounced ``star one star''); and

    * the ``submitted'' definition (sometimes called the ``raw Lisp''
      function).

  We may also speak of the ``executable-counterpart'' and ``submitted''
  functions, which are the two functions defined by the
  ``executable-counterpart'' and ``submitted'' definitions,
  respectively.

  Let us explore these two functions in the case of [47mcar[0m.  When [47m(car 3)[0m
  is evaluated in the ACL2 loop, the executable-counterpart of [47mcar[0m is
  applied to the argument [47m3[0m.  That executable-counterpart (which was
  hand-coded) first checks the guard of [47mcar[0m, which fails for [47m3[0m,
  leading to the error displayed above.  If instead the form to be
  evaluated by ACL2 is [47m(car '(4 5 6))[0m, the executable-counterpart of
  [47mcar[0m will first check that the guard of [47mcar[0m holds for the list
  argument [47m(4 5 6)[0m; then seeing that this is the case, it will apply
  the Common Lisp function [47mcar[0m --- which we consider to be the
  ``submitted'' function for [47mcar[0m --- to that list argument, returning
  [47m4[0m.

  The example above does not really explain why the submitted function
  is called ``submitted'', so let us consider the more common case of
  a defined function, as opposed to a [primitive] like [47mcar[0m.  Suppose
  we define the following functions [47mf[0m and [47mg[0m, each of which has an
  implicit [guard] (precondition) of [47mt[0m, i.e., the precondition always
  holds.

    (defun f (x)
      (car x))
    (defun g (x)
      (f x))

  ACL2 defines two functions named [47m\"F\"[0m: the executable-counterpart of
  [47mf[0m, sometimes called ``[47m*1*f[0m'', and the submitted function for [47mf[0m.  It
  also defines two such functions for [47mg[0m.  The executable-counterpart
  is generated by ACL2 to include not only code to check guards, but
  also code to invoke the executable-counterparts of its callees.
  (Such invocation can be avoided by a process known as verifying
  guards, as we explain further below.)  We can see what is going on
  by tracing calls of [47mf[0m (see [trace] for more about tracing); some
  analysis follows below this display.

    ACL2 !>(trace$ f g)
     ((F) (G))
    ACL2 !>(g '(4 5 6))
    1> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G (4 5 6))
      2> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F (4 5 6))
      <2 (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F 4)
    <1 (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G 4)
    4
    ACL2 !>

  It is now perhaps evident why sometimes we call the
  executable-counterpart functions the ``*1* functions'': the
  executable-counterpart is actually defined in a special ``*1*''
  package that corresponds to the package of the submitted function.
  The trace above shows that the executable-counterpart of [47mg[0m calls
  the executable-counterpart of [47mf[0m, rather than calling any submitted
  function.

  Such a trace can show us an error caused by applying [47mcar[0m to [47m3[0m.

    ACL2 !>(g 3)
    1> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G 3)
      2> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F 3)


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The guard for the function call (CAR X),
    which is (OR (CONSP X) (EQUAL X NIL)), is violated by the arguments
    in the call (CAR 3).
    See :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this
    check with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.

    ACL2 !>

  It is generally more efficient to invoke a submitted functions ---
  which has as its Common Lisp definition exactly the [47m[defun][0m form
  submitted to ACL2 --- than it is to invoke an
  executable-counterpart, which is generated by ACL2 to do auxiliary
  calculations including guard-checking.  So we next modify the
  example above to show that by performing a process called ``guard
  verification'', we can avoid *1* function calls, so that submitted
  functions are invoked instead.  This time we supply reasonable
  guards for [47mf[0m and [47mg[0m, in a fresh ACL2 session.

    (defun f (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (or (consp x) (null x))))
      (car x))
    (defun g (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (consp x)))
      (f x))

  This time, we see that the executable-counterpart of [47mg[0m directly calls
  the submitted function for [47mg[0m, because [47mg[0m was guard-verified when [47mg[0m
  was admitted and the value [47m'(4 5 6)[0m for the formal [47mx[0m of [47mg[0m satisfies
  the guard [47m(consp x)[0m of [47mg[0m.

    ACL2 !>(trace$ f g)
     ((F) (G))
    ACL2 !>(g '(4 5 6))
    1> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G (4 5 6))
      2> (G (4 5 6))
        3> (F (4 5 6))
        <3 (F 4)
      <2 (G 4)
    <1 (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G 4)
    4
    ACL2 !>

  We can also see that because [47m3[0m fails to satisfy the guard of [47mg[0m, a
  guard violation is signaled by the executable-counterpart of [47mg[0m,
  rather than (as before) by the executable-counterpart of [47mcar[0m.

    ACL2 !>(g 3)
    1> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G 3)


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The guard for the function call (G X), which
    is (CONSP X), is violated by the arguments in the call (G 3).
    See :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this
    check with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.

    ACL2 !>

  See if you can predict the corresponding traces if instead, [47mf[0m has a
  suitable guard and is guard-verified, but [47mg[0m is as in the first
  example (with an implicit guard of [47mt[0m, and not guard-verified):

    (defun f (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (or (consp x) (null x))))
      (car x))
    (defun g (x)
      (f x))

  The corresponding trace is as follows; discussion follows.

    ACL2 !>(g '(4 5 6))
    1> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G (4 5 6))
      2> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F (4 5 6))
        3> (F (4 5 6))
        <3 (F 4)
      <2 (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F 4)
    <1 (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G 4)
    4
    ACL2 !>(g 3)
    1> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G 3)
      2> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F 3)


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The guard for the function call (F X), which
    is (OR (CONSP X) (NULL X)), is violated by the arguments in the call
    (F 3).
    See :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this
    check with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.

    ACL2 !>

  The traces above show that first, the executable-counterpart of [47mg[0m
  calls the executable-counterpart of [47mf[0m.  This is because [47mg[0m is not
  guard-verified, so its executable-counterpart can only invoke other
  executable-counterparts.  However, since [47mf[0m is guard-verified, its
  executable-counterpart invokes its submitted function when the
  guard is satisfied, for example to generate the call [47m(F (4 5 6))[0m.
  If the guard for [47mf[0m fails to hold, however, the
  executable-counterpart for [47mf[0m causes a guard violation error, as
  shown above for the executable-counterpart call [47m(ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F
  3)[0m.

  See also [guard] and its subtopics, especially,
  [guards-and-evaluation], for a discussion of guards and their
  connection to evaluation.  Advanced system hackers who want to see
  the executable-counterpart definition for [47mf[0m may invoke [47m(trace!
  (oneify-cltl-code :native t))[0m before defining [47mf[0m in ACL2.


