 (EVISC-TABLE
  (EVENTS)
  "Support for abbreviated output

  The [47mevisc-table[0m is an ACL2 table (see [table]) whose purpose is to
  modify the print representations of specified non-[47mnil[0m objects.
  When a key (some object) is associated with a string value, then
  that string is printed instead of that key (as an abbreviation).
  For example, the following log shows how to abbreviate the key [47m(a b
  c)[0m with the token [47m<my-abc-list>[0m.

    ACL2 !>(table evisc-table '(a b c) \"<my-abc-list>\")

    Summary
    Form:  ( TABLE EVISC-TABLE ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     EVISC-TABLE
    ACL2 !>'(a b c)
    <my-abc-list>
    ACL2 !>'(4 5 a b c)
    (4 5 . <my-abc-list>)
    ACL2 !>

  Every value in this [table] must be either a string or [47mnil[0m, where [47mnil[0m
  eliminates any association of the key with an abbreviation.
  Continuing with the log above:

    ACL2 !>(table evisc-table '(a b c) nil)

    Summary
    Form:  ( TABLE EVISC-TABLE ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     EVISC-TABLE
    ACL2 !>'(a b c)
    (A B C)
    ACL2 !>'(4 5 a b c)
    (4 5 A B C)
    ACL2 !>

  It can be particularly helpful to use this table to abbreviate a
  constant introduced by [47m[defconst][0m by prefixing the constant name
  with [47m\"#.\"[0m, as we now describe.  Consider first the following
  example.

    (defconst *abc* '(1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))
    (table evisc-table *abc*
      (concatenate 'string \"#.\" (symbol-name '*abc*)))

  Then the constant [47m*abc*[0m is printed as follows --- very helpful if its
  associated structure is significantly larger than the 8-element
  list of numbers shown above!

    ACL2 !>*abc*
    #.*ABC*
    ACL2 !>

  What's more, the ACL2 reader will replace [47m#.*C*[0m, where [47m*C*[0m is defined
  by [47m[defconst][0m, by its value, regardless of [47mevisc-table[0m; see
  [sharp-dot-reader].  Continuing with the example above, we have:

    ACL2 !>(cdr (quote #.*ABC*))
    (2 3 4 5 6 7 8)
    ACL2 !>

  Of course, more care needs to be taken if packages are involved (see
  [defpkg]), as we now illustrate.

    ACL2 !>(defpkg \"FOO\" nil)

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFPKG \"FOO\" ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     \"FOO\"
    ACL2 !>(defconst foo::*a* '(1 2 3))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFCONST FOO::*A* ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     FOO::*A*
    ACL2 !>(table evisc-table foo::*a* \"#.foo::*a*\")

    Summary
    Form:  ( TABLE EVISC-TABLE ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     EVISC-TABLE
    ACL2 !>foo::*a*
    #.foo::*a*
    ACL2 !>'#.foo::*a*
    #.foo::*a*
    ACL2 !>(cdr '#.foo::*a*)
    (2 3)
    ACL2 !>

  We conclude by an example showing some extra care that may be
  important to consider taking.  We start with:

    (defconst |*BaR*| '(3 4))

  Then the following works just fine; but try it without the extra code
  for the [47mmay-need-slashes[0m case and you'll see that the sharp-dot
  printing is missing.  First:

    (table evisc-table
           |*BaR*|
           (let ((x (symbol-name '|*BaR*|)))
             (if (may-need-slashes x)
                 (concatenate 'string \"#.|\" x \"|\")
               (concatenate 'string \"#.\" x))))

  Then:

    ACL2 !>|*BaR*|
    #.|*BaR*|
    ACL2 !>

  The examples above illustrate how the [47mevisc-table[0m is used when
  printing evaluation results.  More generally, that [table] is used
  when [47m[fmt][0m (or a related function such as [47m[fms][0m) prints an object
  using [47m~x[0m [47m~X[0m, [47m~y[0m, [47m~Y[0m, [47m~f[0m, or [47m~F[0m.")
 (EVISC-TUPLE
  (IO)
  "Control suppression of details when printing

  ACL2 output is generally printed in full.  However, ACL2 can be
  directed to abbreviate, or ``eviscerate'', objects before printing
  them.  To ``eviscerate'' an object we replace certain substructures
  within it by strings that are printed in their stead.  Such
  replacement is made relative to a so-called ``evisc-tuple'', which
  has four components: [47m(evisc-tuple print-level print-length alist
  hiding-cars)[0m is the same as the value of [47m(list alist print-level
  print-length hiding-cars)[0m, and the components are used as follows
  (with priority order as discussed below).  The [47malist[0m component is
  used to replace any substructure occurring as a key by the
  corresponding string.  The [47mprint-level[0m and [47mprint-length[0m are
  analogous to Common Lisp variables [47m*print-level*[0m and
  [47m*print-length*[0m, respectively, and cause replacement of
  substructures deeper than [47mprint-level[0m by `[47m#[0m' and those longer than
  [47mprint-length[0m by `[47m...[0m'.  Finally, any [47m[consp][0m [47mx[0m that starts with one
  of the symbols in [47mhiding-cars[0m is printed as [47m<hidden>[0m.

  The following example illustrates the use of an evisc-tuple that
  limits the print-level to 3 --- only three descents into list
  structures are permitted before replacing a subexpression by `[47m#[0m'
  --- and limits the print-length to 4 --- only the first four
  elements of any list structure will be printed before replacing its
  tail by `[47m...[0m'.

    ACL2 !>(fms \"~x0~%\"
                (list (cons #\\0 '((a b ((c d)) e f g) u v w x y)))
                *standard-co*
                state
                (evisc-tuple 3 4 nil nil))

    ((A B (#) E ...) U V W ...)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>

  Notice that it is impossible to read the printed value back into
  ACL2, since there is no way for the ACL2 reader to interpret `[47m#[0m' or
  `[47m...[0m'.  To solve this problem, see [set-iprint].

  In the above example we pass an evisc-tuple explicitly to a printing
  function, in this case, [47m[fms][0m (see [fmt]).  But ACL2 also does its
  own printing, for example during a proof attempt.  There are global
  evisc-tuples that control ACL2's printing; see [set-evisc-tuple]
  and see [without-evisc].


Subtopics

  [Brr-evisc-tuple]
      Determines partial suppression of output from [brr-commands]")
 (EVISCERATE-HIDE-TERMS
  (IO)
  "To print [47m(hide ...)[0m as [47m<hidden>[0m

    Example:
    (assign eviscerate-hide-terms t)
    (assign eviscerate-hide-terms nil)

  [47mEviscerate-hide-terms[0m is a [47m[state][0m global variable whose value is
  either [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m.  The variable affects how terms are displayed by
  default (but not if you have set the term-evisc-tuple to other than
  its default; see [set-evisc-tuple]).  If [47mt[0m, terms of the form [47m(hide
  ...)[0m are printed as [47m<hidden>[0m.  Otherwise, they are printed
  normally.")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-BINARY-TREES
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction on binary trees

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Binary Trees[0m: To prove [47m(p x)[0m, for all [47mx[0m, by
  classical induction on binary tree structures, prove each of the
  following:

    [3mBase Case[0m:
    (implies (atom x) (p x))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (atom x))
                  (p (car x))
                  (p (cdr x)))
             (p x))

  An argument analogous to that given in
  [example-induction-scheme-on-lists] should convince you that [47m(p x)[0m
  holds for every object.

  A function that suggests this induction is:

    (defun flatten (x)
      (if (atom x)
          (list x)
          (app (flatten (car x))
               (flatten (cdr x))))).")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-DOWN-BY-2
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction downwards 2 steps at a time

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Natural Numbers Preserving Parity[0m: Here is
  another way to decompose natural numbers.  To prove [47m(p n)[0m, for all
  [47mn[0m, prove each of the following:

    [3mBase Case 1[0m:
    (implies (zp n) (p n))

    [3mBase Case 2[0m:
    (implies (equal n 1) (p n))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (zp n))
                  (not (equal n 1))
                  (p (- n 2)))
             (p n))

  Base Case 1 establishes that [47mp[0m holds for [47m0[0m (and all objects other
  than positive naturals).

  Base Case 2 establishes that [47mp[0m holds for [47m1[0m.

  The Induction Step establishes that if [47mn[0m is a natural number greater
  than [47m1[0m, and if [47mp[0m holds for [47mn[0m-2, then [47mp[0m holds for [47mn[0m.

  Note that we have thus proved that [47m(p n)[0m holds, for all [47mn[0m.  For
  example, [47m(p -7)[0m, [47m(p 'abc)[0m, and [47m(p 0)[0m are all established by Base
  Case 1.  [47m(p 1)[0m is established by Base Case 2.  [47m(p 2)[0m is established
  from [47m(p 0)[0m and the Induction Step.  Think about it!  [47m(p 3)[0m is
  established form [47m(p 1)[0m and the Induction Step, etc.

  A function that suggests this induction is:

    (defun parity (n)
      (if (zp n)
          'even
          (if (equal n 1)
              'odd
              (parity (- n 2))))).")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-NAT-RECURSION
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction on natural numbers

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Natural Numbers[0m: Induction is familiar in the
  arithmetic setting.  To prove [47m(p n)[0m, for all [47mn[0m, by classical
  induction on the construction of the natural numbers, prove each of
  the following:

    [3mBase Case[0m:
    (implies (zp n) (p n))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (zp n))
                  (p (- n 1)))
             (p n))

  The Base Case establishes that [47mp[0m holds for [47m0[0m.  In fact, because of
  the definition of [47m[zp][0m {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]), it
  establishes that [47m(p n)[0m holds when [47mn[0m is [47m0[0m and it holds when [47mn[0m is not
  a natural number.

  The Induction Step establishes that if [47mn[0m is a natural number other
  than [47m0[0m, and if [47mp[0m holds for [47mn[0m-1, then [47mp[0m holds for [47mn[0m.  The hypothesis
  [47m(p (- n 1))[0m above is called the [3minduction hypothesis[0m.

  A function that suggests this induction is

    (defun nat-recursion (n)
      (if (zp n)
          n
          (nat-recursion (- n 1))))

  Similarly, the term [47m(fact n)[0m suggests this induction if [47mfact[0m is
  defined:

    (defun fact (k)
     (if (zp k)
         1
         (* k (fact (- k 1))))).

  even though the formal parameter of this definition of [47mfact[0m is [47mk[0m, not
  [47mn[0m.")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-ON-LISTS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction on lists

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Lists[0m: To prove [47m(p x)[0m, for all [47mx[0m, by classical
  induction on the linear list structure, prove each of the
  following:

    [3mBase Case[0m:
    (implies (endp x) (p x))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp x))
                  (p (cdr x)))
             (p x))

  An argument analogous to that given for natural numbers,
  [example-induction-scheme-nat-recursion], establishes [47m(p x)[0m for
  every [47mx[0m.  For example, [47m(p -7)[0m, [47m(p 'abc)[0m, and [47m(p nil)[0m are all
  established by the Base Case.  [47m(p '(Friday))[0m follows from [47m(p nil)[0m,
  given the Induction Step.  That sentence bears thinking about!
  Think about it!  Similarly, [47m(p '(Yellow))[0m holds for the same
  reason.  [47m(p '(Thursday Friday))[0m follows from [47m(p '(Friday))[0m and the
  Induction Step, etc.

  A function that suggests this induction is

    (defun app (x y)
      (if (endp x)
          y
          (cons (car x)
                (app (cdr x) y)))).")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-ON-SEVERAL-VARIABLES
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction on several variables

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  [31;1mInduction on Several Variables[0m To [47m(p n x)[0m for all [47mn[0m and all [47mx[0m, prove
  each of the following:

    [3mBase Case 1[0m:
    (implies (endp x)
             (p n x))

    [3mBase Case 2[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp x))
                  (zp n))
             (p n x))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp x))
                  (not (zp n))
                  (p (- n 1) (cdr x)))
             (p n x))

  A function that suggests this induction is

    (defun nth (n x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (if (zp n)
              (car x)
              (nth (- n 1) (cdr x))))).")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-UPWARDS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction upwards

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  [31;1mInduction Upwards[0m: To [47m(p i max)[0m for all [47mi[0m and all [47mmax[0m, prove each of
  the following:

    [3mBase Case[0m:
    (implies (not (and (natp i)
                       (natp max)
                       (< i max)))
             (p i max))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and  (natp i)
                   (natp max)
                   (< i max)
                   (p (+ i 1) max))
             (p i max))

  Note that the induction hypothesis is about an [47mi[0m that is [3mbigger[0m than
  the [47mi[0m in in the conclusion.  In induction, as in recursion, the
  sense of one thing being ``smaller'' than another is determined by
  an arbitrary measure of all the variables, not the magnitude or
  extent of some particular variable.

  A function that suggests this induction is shown below.  ACL2 has to
  be told the measure, namely the difference between [47mmax[0m and [47mi[0m
  (coerced to a natural number to ensure that the measure is an
  ordinal).

    (defun count-up (i max)
      (declare (xargs :measure (nfix (- max i))))
      (if (and (natp i)
               (natp max)
               (< i max))
          (cons i (count-up (+ 1 i) max))
          nil)).")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-WITH-ACCUMULATORS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction scheme with accumulators

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  To prove [47m(p x a)[0m for all [47mx[0m and all [47ma[0m, prove each of the following:

    [3mBase Case[0m:
    (implies (endp x)
             (p x a))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp x))
                  (p (cdr x) (cons (car x) a)))
             (p x a))

  Note that in the induction hypothesis we assume [47mp[0m for a smaller [47mx[0m but
  a larger [47ma[0m.  In fact, we could include as many induction hypotheses
  as we want and use any terms we want in the [47ma[0m position as long as
  the [47mx[0m position is occupied by a smaller term.

  A function that suggests this particular induction is shown below.

    (defun rev1 (x a)
      (if (endp x)
          a
          (rev1 (cdr x) (cons (car x) a)))).

  A function that suggests a similar induction in which three induction
  hypotheses are provided, one in which the [47ma[0m position is occupied by
  [47m(cons (car x) a)[0m, another in which the [47ma[0m position is occupied by
  some arbitrary term [47mb[0m, and a third in which the [47ma[0m position is
  occupied by [47ma[0m, is suggested by the term [47m(rev1-modified x a b)[0m where

    (defun rev1-modified (x a b)
      (if (endp x)
          (list x a b)
          (list (rev1-modified (cdr x) (cons (car x) a) b)
                (rev1-modified (cdr x) b b)
                (rev1-modified (cdr x) a b))))

  Remember that the value of this term or function is irrelevant to the
  induction suggested.  Because ACL2's definitional principle insists
  that all the formal parameters play a role in the computation (at
  least syntactically), it is common practice when defining functions
  for their induction schemes to return the [47mlist[0m of all the formals
  (to ensure all variables are involved) and to combine recursive
  calls on a given branch with [47mlist[0m (to avoid introducing additional
  case analysis as would happen if [47mand[0m or [47mor[0m or other propositional
  functions are used).

  If you tried to prove [47m(p x a)[0m and suggested the induct hint
  [47m(rev1-modified x a (fact k))[0m, as by

    (thm (p x a)
         :hints ((\"Goal\" :induct (rev1-modified x a (fact k)))))

  the inductive argument would be:

    [3mBase Case[0m:
    (implies (endp x)
             (p x a))

    [3mInductive Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp x))
                  (p (cdr x) (cons (car x) a))
                  (p (cdr x) (fact k))
                  (p (cdr x) a))
             (p x a))")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTION-SCHEME-WITH-MULTIPLE-INDUCTION-STEPS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Induction scheme with more than one induction step

  See [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted-inductive-proof] for an
  explanation of what we mean by the induction [3msuggested[0m by a
  recursive function or a term.

  [31;1mSeveral Induction Steps[0m: To [47m(p x i a)[0m for all [47mx[0m, [47mi[0m, and [47ma[0m, prove each
  of the following:

    [3mBase Case 1[0m:
    (implies (zp i)
             (p x i a))

    [3mInduction Step 1[0m:
    (implies (and (not (zp i))
                  (equal (parity i) 'even)
                  (p (* x x)
                     (floor i 2)
                     a))
             (p x i a))

    [3mInduction Step 2[0m:
    (implies (and (not (zp i))
                  (not (equal (parity i) 'even))
                  (p x
                     (- i 1)
                     (* x a)))
             (p x i a))

  A function that suggests this induction is the binary exponentiation
  function for natural numbers.

    (defun bexpt (x i a)
      (cond ((zp i) a)
            ((equal (parity i) 'even)
             (bexpt (* x x)
                    (floor i 2)
                    a))
            (t (bexpt x
                      (- i 1)
                      (* x a)
                      )))).

  In order to admit this function it is necessary to know that [47m(floor i
  2)[0m is smaller than [47mi[0m in the case above.  This can be proved if the
  community book [47m\"arithmetic-5/top\"[0m has been included from the ACL2
  system directory, i.e.,

    (include-book \"arithmetic-5/top\" :dir :system)

  should be executed before defining [47mbexpt[0m.")
 (EXAMPLE-INDUCTIONS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Some examples of induction schemes in ACL2

  Here are some pages illustrating various induction schemes suggested
  by recursive functions.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Natural Numbers[0m: see
  [example-induction-scheme-nat-recursion].

  [31;1mInduction Preserving Even/Odd Parity[0m or
  [31;1mInduction Downwards by 2[0m or
  [31;1mInduction with Multiple Base Cases[0m: see
  [example-induction-scheme-down-by-2] for an induction in which the
  induction hypothesis decreases the induction variable by an amount
  other than 1.  This illustrates that the induction hypothesis can
  be about whatever term or terms are needed to explain how the
  formula recurs.  The example also illustrates an induction with
  more than one Base Case.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Lists[0m: see [example-induction-scheme-on-lists]
  for an induction over linear lists, in which we inductively assume
  the conjecture for [47m(cdr x)[0m and prove it for [47mx[0m.  It doesn't matter
  whether the list is [47mnil[0m-terminated or not; the Base Case addresses
  all the possibilities.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Binary (Cons) Trees[0m: see
  [example-induction-scheme-binary-trees] for an induction over the
  simplest form of binary tree.  Here the Induction Step provides two
  hypotheses, one about the left subtree and one about the right
  subtree.

  [31;1mInduction on Several Variables[0m: see
  [example-induction-scheme-on-several-variables] for an induction in
  which several variables participate in the case analysis and
  induction hypotheses.

  [31;1mInduction Upwards[0m: see [example-induction-scheme-upwards] for an
  induction scheme in which the induction hypothesis is about
  something ``bigger than'' the induction conclusion.  This
  illustrates that the sense in which the hypothesis is about
  something ``smaller'' than the conclusion is determined by a
  measure of all the variables, not the magnitude or extent of some
  single variable.

  [31;1mInduction with Auxiliary Variables[0m or
  [31;1mInduction with Accumulators[0m: see
  [example-induction-scheme-with-accumulators] for an induction
  scheme in which one variable ``gets smaller'' but another is
  completely arbitrary.  Such schemes are common when dealing with
  tail-recursive functions that accumulate partial results in
  auxiliary variables.  This example also shows how to provide
  several arbitrary terms in a non-inductive variable of a scheme.

  [31;1mInduction with Multiple Induction Steps[0m: see
  [example-induction-scheme-with-multiple-induction-steps] for an
  induction in which we make different inductive hypotheses depending
  on which case we're in.  This example also illustrates the handling
  of auxiliary variables or accumulators.")
 (EXECUTABLE-COUNTERPART
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "A rule for computing the value of a function

  This topic is about a type of [rune] that controls evaluation using
  the executable-counterpart of a function.  For discussion of
  executable-counterparts of functions, see [evaluation].

    Example:
    (:executable-counterpart length)

  which may be abbreviated in [theory] expressions in either of the
  following two ways (see [rune]).

    (:e length)
    (length)

  Every [47m[defun][0m introduces at least two rules used by the theorem
  prover.  Suppose [47mfn[0m is the name of a [47m[defun][0m'd function.  Then
  [47m(:definition fn)[0m is the rune (see [rune]) naming the rule that
  allows the simplifier to replace calls of [47mfn[0m by its instantiated
  body.  [47m(:executable-counterpart fn)[0m is the [rune] for the rule for
  how to evaluate the function on known constants.

  When typing [theories] it is convenient to know that [47m(fn)[0m is a runic
  designator that denotes [47m(:executable-counterpart fn)[0m.  See
  [theories].

  If [47m(:executable-counterpart fn)[0m is [enable]d, then when applications
  of [47mfn[0m to known constants are seen by the simplifier they are
  computed out by executing the Common Lisp code for [47mfn[0m (with the
  appropriate handling of [guard]s).  Suppose [47mfact[0m is defined as the
  factorial function.  If the executable-counterpart [rune] of [47mfact[0m,
  [47m(:executable-counterpart fact)[0m, is [enable]d when the simplifier
  encounters [47m(fact 12)[0m, then that term will be ``immediately''
  expanded to [47m479001600[0m.  Note that even if subroutines of [47mfn[0m have
  disabled executable-counterparts, [47mfn[0m will call their Lisp code
  nonetheless: once an executable-counterpart function is applied, no
  subsidiary enable checks are made.

  Such one-step expansions are sometimes counterproductive because they
  prevent the anticipated application of certain lemmas about the
  subroutines of the expanded function.  Such computed expansions can
  be prevented by disabling the executable-counterpart [rune] of the
  relevant function.  For example, if [47m(:executable-counterpart fact)[0m
  is [disable]d, [47m(fact 12)[0m will not be expanded by computation.  In
  this situation, [47m(fact 12)[0m may be rewritten to [47m(* 12 (fact 11))[0m,
  using the rule named [47m(:definition fact)[0m, provided the system's
  heuristics permit the introduction of the term [47m(fact 11)[0m.  Note
  that lemmas about multiplication may then be applicable (while such
  lemmas would be inapplicable to [47m479001600[0m).  In many proofs it is
  desirable to [disable] the executable-counterpart [rune]s of
  certain functions to prevent their expansion by computation.  See
  [executable-counterpart-theory].

  Finally: What do we do about functions that are ``constrained''
  rather than defined, such as the following?  (See [encapsulate].)

    (encapsulate (((foo *) => *))
                 (local (defun foo (x) x)))

  Does [47mfoo[0m have an executable-counterpart?  Yes: since the vast
  majority of functions have sensible executable-counterparts, it was
  decided that [31;1mall[0m functions, even such ``constrained'' ones, have
  executable-counterparts.  We essentially ``trap'' when such calls
  are inappropriate.  Thus, consider for example:

    (defun bar (x)
      (if (rationalp x)
          (+ x 1)
        (foo x)))

  If the term [47m(bar '3)[0m is encountered by the ACL2 rewriter during a
  proof, and if the [47m:executable-counterpart[0m of [47mbar[0m is [enable]d, then
  it will be invoked to reduce this term to [47m'4[0m.  However, if the term
  [47m(bar 'a)[0m is encountered during a proof, then since [47m'a[0m is not a
  [47m[rationalp][0m and since the [47m:executable-counterpart[0m of [47mfoo[0m is only a
  ``trap,'' then this call of the [47m:executable-counterpart[0m of [47mbar[0m will
  result in a ``trap.'' In that case, the rewriter will return the
  term [47m(hide (bar 'a))[0m so that it never has to go through this
  process again.  See [hide].")
 (EXECUTABLE-COUNTERPART-THEORY
  (THEORIES THEORY-FUNCTIONS)
  "[Executable-counterpart] rules as of logical name

    Examples:
    (executable-counterpart-theory :here)
    (executable-counterpart-theory 'lemma3)

  See [logical-name].

    General Form:
    (executable-counterpart-theory logical-name)

  Returns the theory containing all the [47m:[0m[47m[executable-counterpart][0m
  [rune]s, whether [enable]d or not, that existed immediately after
  [47m[logical-name][0m was introduced.  See the documentation for
  [theories], [logical-name], [executable-counterpart] and
  [47m[function-theory][0m.

  You may experience a fencepost problem in deciding which logical name
  to use.  [47m[Deflabel][0m can always be used to mark unambiguously for
  future reference a particular point in the development of your
  theory.  The order of [events] in the vicinity of an [47m[encapsulate][0m
  is confusing.  See [encapsulate].

  This ``function'' is actually a macro that expands to a term
  mentioning the single free variable [47m[world][0m.  When theory
  expressions are evaluated by [47m[in-theory][0m or the [47m:[0m[47m[in-theory][0m hint,
  [47m[world][0m is bound to the current ACL2 [world].")
