 (FAST-ALIST-CLEAN
  (FAST-ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "[47m(fast-alist-clean alist)[0m can be used to eliminate \"shadowed pairs\"
  from a fast alist.

  This [documentation] topic assumes familiarity with fast alists; see
  [fast-alists].  See [47m[fast-alist-clean!][0m, [47m[fast-alist-fork][0m, and
  [47m[fast-alist-fork!][0m for related utilities.

  Logically, [47m(fast-alist-clean alist)[0m is defined as follows:

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<fast-alist-clean>

    (defun fast-alist-clean (alist)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (fast-alist-fork alist
                       (if (consp alist)
                           (cdr (last alist))
                         alist)))

  The result is thus a corresponding fast alist, with the order
  reversed and with atoms and shadowed pairs removed, as per the
  definition above; see [fast-alist-fork] for details.  Moreover, if
  [47malist[0m is not a fast alist, then [47m(fast-alist-clean alist)[0m is
  executed in raw Lisp by calling [47mfast-alist-fork[0m as indicated above.

  However, if [47malist[0m is a fast alist, then a special definition under
  the hood provides a different handling of associated hash tables.
  After running [47m(fast-alist-clean alist)[0m to obtain a result,
  [47mcleaned-alist[0m, the hash table that had been associated with [47malist[0m
  will now be associated with [47mcleaned-alist[0m instead.  This saves the
  expense of creating a new hash table, but there is still the
