 (FAST-ALISTS
  (ALISTS PROGRAMMING HONS-AND-MEMOIZATION)
  "Alists with hidden hash tables for faster execution

  The implementation of fast alists is, in many ways, similar to that
  of ACL2 arrays.  Logically, [47m[hons-acons][0m is just like [47macons[0m, and
  [47m[hons-get][0m is similar to [47m[assoc-equal][0m.  But under the hood, hash
  tables are associated with these alists, and, when a certain
  [31;1mdiscipline[0m is followed, these functions execute with hash table
  speeds.

  What is this discipline?  Each [47mhons-acons[0m operation \"steals\" the hash
  table associated with the alist that is being extended.  Because of
  this, one must be very conscious of which object is the most recent
  extension of an alist and use that extension exclusively.

  The only penalty for a failure to keep track of the most recent
  extension is a loss of execution speed, not of correctness, and
  perhaps the annoyance of some [47m[slow-alist-warning][0ms.

  Maintaining discipline may require careful passing of a fast alist up
  and down through function calls, as with any single threaded object
  in an applicative setting, but there is [31;1mno syntactic enforcement[0m
  that insists you only use the most recent extension of an alist as
  there is for single threaded objects ([47m[stobj][0ms).  Also, even with
  perfectly proper code, discipline can sometimes be lost due to user
  interrupts and aborts.

  Performance Notes

  See [hons-acons] for how the final [47m[cdr][0m of a fast alist can be used
  as a size hint or as a name for reports printed by calling
  [47m[fast-alist-summary][0m.

  The keys of fast alists are always [normed].  Why?  In Common Lisp,
  equal-based hashing is relatively slow, so to allow the use of
  eql-based hash tables, [47m[hons-acons][0m and [47m[hons-get][0m always
  [47m[hons-copy][0m the keys involved.

  Since alist keys are frequently atoms, this norming activity may
  often be so fast that you do not need to think about it.  But if
  you are going to use large structures as the keys for your fast
  alist, this overhead can be significant, and you may want to ensure
  that your keys are normed ahead of time.

  There is [31;1mno automatic mechanism[0m for reclaiming the hash tables that
  are associated with alists.  Because of this, to avoid memory
  leaks, you should call [47m[fast-alist-free][0m to remove the hash table
  associated with alists that will no longer be used.


