 (FILE-LENGTH$
  (IO)
  "The size of a file in bytes

  This analogue of the Common Lisp function, [47mfile-length[0m, uses that
  function under the hood to compute efficiently the number of bytes
  [47mB[0m in the specified file if that number can be determined, returning
  [47m(mv B state)[0m; otherwise it returns [47m(mv nil state)[0m.  The [47m[guard][0m of
  [47mfile-length$[0m requires that the first argument is a string; the
  second argument is the ACL2 [47m[state][0m.  The logical definition does
  not actually look at the file and hence is not useful for reasoning
  about the number of bytes in the file.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<file-length$>

    (defun file-length$ (file state)
      (declare (xargs :guard (stringp file)
                      :stobjs state))
      (declare (ignore file))
      (mv-let (erp val state)
              (read-acl2-oracle state)
        (mv (and (null erp) (natp val) val)
            state)))")
 (FILE-READING-EXAMPLE
  (TUTORIAL5-MISCELLANEOUS-EXAMPLES)
  "Example of reading files in ACL2

  This example illustrates the use of ACL2's [io] primitives to read
  the forms in a file.  See [io].

  This example provides a solution to the following problem.  Let's say
  that you have a file that contains s-expressions.  Suppose that you
  want to build a list by starting with [47mnil[0m, and updating it
  ``appropriately'' upon encountering each successive s-expression in
  the file.  That is, suppose that you have written a function
  [47mupdate-list[0m such that [47m(update-list obj current-list)[0m returns the
  list obtained by ``updating'' [47mcurrent-list[0m with the next object,
  [47mobj[0m, encountered in the file.  The top-level function for
  processing such a file, returning the final list, could be defined
  as follows.  Notice that because it opens a channel to the given
  file, this function modifies [state] and hence must return [state].
  Thus it actually returns two values: the final list and the new
  [state].

    (defun process-file (filename state)
      (mv-let
       (channel state)
       (open-input-channel filename :object state)
       (mv-let (result state)
               (process-file1 nil channel state) ;see below
               (let ((state (close-input-channel channel state)))
                 (mv result state)))))

  The function [47mprocess-file1[0m referred to above takes the currently
  constructed list (initially, [47mnil[0m), together with a channel to the
  file being read and the [state], and returns the final updated
  list.  Notice that this function is tail recursive.  This is
  important because many Lisp compilers will remove tail recursion,
  thus avoiding the potential for stack overflows when the file
  contains a large number of forms.

    (defun process-file1 (current-list channel state)
      (mv-let (eofp obj state)
              (read-object channel state)
              (cond
               (eofp (mv current-list state))
               (t (process-file1 (update-list obj current-list)
                                 channel state)))))

  As an exercise, you might want to add [guard]s to the functions above
  and verify the guards (see [verify-guards]).  See [args] or make a
  call of the form [47m(guard 'your-function nil (w state))[0m to see the
  guard of an existing function.")
 (FILE-WRITE-DATE$
  (IO)
  "The write-date of a file as a natural number

  This analogue of the Common Lisp function, [47mfile-write-date[0m, uses that
  function under the hood to compute the write date [47mD[0m as a natural
  number if the write date can be determined, returning [47m(mv D state)[0m;
  otherwise it returns [47m(mv nil state)[0m.  That number is, quoting the
  Common Lisp HyperSpec, the number of seconds ``measured as an
  offset from the beginning of the year 1900 (ignoring leap
  seconds)''.  The [47m[guard][0m of [47mfile-write-date$[0m requires that the
  first argument is a string; the second argument is the ACL2
  [47m[state][0m.  The logical definition does not actually look at the file
  and hence is not useful for reasoning about the write date.

  This utility provides a reasonable way to determine whether a file
  exists, like Common Lisp's [47mprobe-file[0m, according to whether [47mD[0m is
  non-[47mnil[0m in when [47m(mv D state)[0m is returned.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<file-write-date$>

    (defun file-write-date$ (file state)
      (declare (xargs :guard (stringp file)
                      :stobjs state)
               (ignorable file))
      (mv-let (erp val state)
              (read-acl2-oracle state)
        (mv (and (null erp) (posp val) val)
            state)))")
 (FINALIZE-EVENT-USER
  (OUTPUT-CONTROLS)
  "User-supplied code to complete [events], e.g., with extra [summary]
  output

  This utility is intended for system hackers, not standard ACL2 users.

  ACL2 prints summaries at the conclusions of processing [events]
  (unless summaries are inhibited; see [set-inhibit-output-lst] and
  also see [set-inhibited-summary-types]).  You may arrange for
  processing to take place just after the [summary], by defining a
  function with argument list [47m(ctx body state)[0m that returns one
  value, namely [47mstate[0m.  We describe [47mctx[0m and [47mbody[0m at the end below,
  but you may simply prefer to ignore these arguments.)  Your
  function should normally be a [guard]-verified [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode
  function with no guard other than that provided by the input
  requirement on [47m[state][0m, that is, [47m(state-p state)[0m; but later below
  we discuss how to avoid this requirement.  You then attach (see
  [defattach]) your function to the function [47mfinalize-event-user[0m.
  The following example illustrates how this all works.

    (defun finalize-event-user-test (ctx body state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state)
               (ignore ctx body))
      (cond ((and (boundp-global 'abbrev-evisc-tuple state)
                  (open-output-channel-p *standard-co*
                                         :character
                                         state))
             (pprogn
              (if (eq (f-get-global 'abbrev-evisc-tuple state) :DEFAULT)
                  (princ$ \"Abbrev-evisc-tuple has its default value.\"
                          *standard-co*
                          state)
                (princ$ \"Abbrev-evisc-tuple has been modified.\"
                        *standard-co*
                        state))
              (newline *standard-co* state)))
            (t state)))

    (defattach-system finalize-event-user finalize-event-user-test)

  After admission of the two events above, an event [summary] will
  conclude with extra printout, for example:

    Note: Abbrev-evisc-tuple has its default value.

  If the attachment function (above, [47mfinalize-event-user-test[0m) does not
  meet all the requirements stated above, then you can use the
  [47m:skip-checks[0m argument of [47m[defattach][0m to get around the requirement,
  as illustrated by the following example.

    (defun finalize-event-user-test2 (ctx body state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state
                      :mode :program)
               (ignore ctx body))
      (observation
       'my-test
       \"~|Value of term-evisc-tuple: ~x0~|\"
       (f-get-global 'term-evisc-tuple state)))

    (defttag t) ; needed for :skip-checks t

    (defattach-system (finalize-event-user finalize-event-user-test2)
      :skip-checks t)

  So for example:

    ACL2 !>(set-term-evisc-tuple (evisc-tuple 2 7 nil nil) state)
     (:TERM)
    ACL2 !>(defconst *foo6* '(a b c))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFCONST *FOO6* ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    ACL2 Observation in MY-TEST:
    Value of term-evisc-tuple: (NIL 2 7 NIL)
     *FOO6*
    ACL2 !>

  Note that (as of this writing) the macro [47m[observation][0m expands to a
  call of a [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m-mode function.  Thus, the trick shown above
  involving [47m:skip-checks[0m allows the use of [47m:program[0m-mode functions;
  for example, you can print with [47m[fmt][0m.

  See community book [47mbooks/misc/defattach-bang.lisp[0m for a variant of
  [47m[defattach][0m that uses [47m[ec-call][0m to avoid issues of [guard]
  verification.

  Also see [initialize-event-user], which discusses the handling of
  [state] globals by that utility as well as by [47mfinalize-event-user[0m.

  Finally, as promised above, we briefly describe the arguments [47mctx[0m and
  [47mbody[0m.  These are the arguments passed to the call of macro
  [47mwith-ctx-summarized[0m under which [47mfinalize-event-user[0m (or
  [47minitialize-event-user[0m) was called.  Thus, they are unevaluated
  expressions.  For example, system function [47mdefthm-fn1[0m has a body of
  the following form.

    (with-ctx-summarized
     (cons 'defthm name)
     (let ((wrld (w state))
           (ens (ens state))
           .....

  Thus, when [47minitialize-event-user[0m and [47mfinalize-event-user[0m are called
  on behalf of [47m[defthm][0m, [47mctx[0m is the s-expression

    (cons 'defthm name)

  while [47mbody[0m is the following s-expression (with most code elided).

    (let ((wrld (w state))
          (ens (ens state))
          .....

  You might find it helpful to use [47m[trace$][0m to get a sense of [47mctx[0m and
  [47mbody[0m, for example, [47m(trace$ finalize-event-user)[0m.")
 (FIND-RULES-OF-RUNE
  (RUNE)
  "Find the rules named rune

    General Form:
    (find-rules-of-rune rune wrld)

  This function finds all the rules in [47mwrld[0m with [47m:[0m[47m[rune][0m rune.  It
  returns a list of rules in their internal form (generally as
  described by the corresponding [47mdefrec[0m).  Deciphering these rules is
  difficult since one cannot always look at a rule object and decide
  what kind of record it is without exploiting many system invariants
  (e.g., that [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m runes only name rewrite-rules).

  At the moment this function returns [47mnil[0m if the rune in question is a
  [47m:[0m[47m[refinement][0m rune, because there is no object representing
  [47m:[0m[47m[refinement][0m rules.  ([47m:[0m[47m[refinement][0m rules cause changes in the
  [47m'coarsenings[0m properties.)  In addition, if the rune is an
  [47m:[0m[47m[equivalence][0m rune, then congruence rules with that rune will be
  returned --- because [47m:[0m[47m[equivalence][0m lemmas generate some congruence
  rules --- but the fact that a certain function is now known to be
  an equivalence relation is not represented by any rule object and
  so no such rule is returned.  (The fact that the function is an
  equivalence relation is encoded entirely in its presence as a
  [47m'coarsening[0m of [47m[equal][0m.)")
