  (BASICS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Local binding of function symbols

  See [macrolet] for an analogous utility for defining macros locally.

    Example Form:
    ; The following evaluates to (mv 7 10):
    (flet ((f (x)
              (+ x 3))
           (g (x)
              (declare (type integer x))
              (* x 2)))
      (mv (f 4) (g 5)))

    General Forms:
    (flet (def1 ... defk) body)
    (flet (def1 ... defk) declare-form1 .. declare-formk body)

  where [47mbody[0m is a term, and each [47mdefi[0m is a definition as in [47m[defun][0m but
  with the leading [47mdefun[0m symbol omitted.  See [defun], but see
  [declare] for the declarations permitted directly under the [47mdefi[0m.
  On the other hand, regarding the [47mdeclare-formi[0m (if any are
  supplied): each must be of the form [47m(declare decl1 ... decln)[0m,
  where each [47mdecli[0m is of the form [47m(inline g1 ... gm)[0m or [47m(notinline g1
  ... gm)[0m, and each [47mgi[0m is defined by some [47mdefi[0m.

  The innermost [47mflet[0m or [47m[macrolet][0m binding of a symbol, [47mf[0m, above a call
  of [47mf[0m, is the one that provides the definition of [47mf[0m for that call.
  Note that neither [47mflet[0m nor [47mmacrolet[0m provide recursion: that is, the
  definition of [47mf[0m in an [47mflet[0m or [47mmacrolet[0m binding of [47mf[0m is ignored in
  the body of that binding.  Consider the following example.

    ; Give a global definition of f:
    (defun f (x) (+ x 3))
    ; Evaluate an expression using a local binding of f:
    (flet ((f (x) (cons x (f (1+ x)))))
      (f 4))

  In the above term [47m(cons x (f (1+ x)))[0m, [47mf[0m refers to the global
  definition of [47mf[0m above the [47mflet[0m expression.  However, [47m(f 4)[0m refers
  to the [47mflet[0m-binding of [47mf[0m, [47m(f (x) (cons x (f x)))[0m.  The result of
  the [47mflet[0m expression is thus obtained by evaluating [47m(f 4)[0m where [47m(f
  4)[0m is [47m(cons 4 (f (1+ 4)))[0m, where the latter call of [47mf[0m refers to the
  global definition; thus we have [47m(cons 4 (f 5))[0m, which evaluates to
  [47m(4 . 8)[0m.

  Although [47mflet[0m behaves in ACL2 essentially as it does in Common Lisp,
  ACL2 imposes the following restrictions and qualifications.

    * Every [47m[declare][0m form for a local definition ([47mdef1[0m through [47mdefk[0m,
      above) must be an [47mignore[0m, [47mignorable[0m, or [47mtype[0m expression.  Such
      [47mtype[0m declarations affect evaluation and [guard]-checking in a
      way that is completely analogous to such declarations that
      occur between the formal parameters and the body in a [47m[defun][0m
      form.

    * Each [47mdefi[0m must bind a different function symbol.

    * Each [47mdefi[0m must bind a symbol that is a legal name for an ACL2
      function symbol.  In particular, the symbol may not be in the
      keyword package or the main Lisp package.  Moreover, the symbol
      may not be a built-in ACL2 function or macro.

    * Every variable occurring in the body of a [47mdefi[0m must be a formal
      parameter of that [47mdefi[0m.  (This restriction is not enforced in
      Common Lisp.)

    * If the [47mflet[0m-binding [47mdefi[0m is in the body of a function [47mf[0m, then the
      [stobj] inputs for [47mdefi[0m are implicitly those of its inputs that
      are declared [stobj] inputs of [47mf[0m.

    * When an expression [47m(flet (... defi ...) ...)[0m occurs in the body of a
      [47mDO[0m [47m[loop$][0m expression, nevertheless constructs such as [47mPROGN[0m
      and [47mSETQ[0m that ACL2 permits in [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m bodies are not permitted
      in [47mdefi[0m (unless they occur within the scope of a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m
      expression in that body).  (This restriction is only for ACL2;
      for example, it may be reasonable to call [47mRETURN[0m in such
      situations but ACL2 does not allow that.)

  [47mFlet[0m bindings are evaluated in parallel.  Consider the following
  example.

    (defun f (x) x)
    (flet ((f (x) (cons x x))
           (g (x) (f x)))
      (g 3))

  The binding of [47mg[0m refers to the global value of [47mf[0m, not the
  [47mflet[0m-binding of [47mf[0m.  Thus, the [47mflet[0m expression evaluates to 3.
  Compare the [47mflet[0m expression above to the following one, which
  instead evaluates to [47m(3 . 3)[0m.

    (defun f (x) x)
    (flet ((f (x) (cons x x)))
      (flet ((g (x) (f x)))
        (g 3)))

  Under the hood, ACL2 translates [47mflet[0m bindings to [47m[lambda][0m expressions
  (see [term]), throwing away the [47minline[0m and [47mnotinline[0m declarations
  (if any).  The following example illustrates this point.

    ACL2 !>:trans (flet ((f (x) (cons x x))
                         (g (x y) (+ x y)))
                    (declare (inline f))
                    (f (g 3 4)))

    ((LAMBDA (X) (CONS X X))
     ((LAMBDA (X Y) (BINARY-+ X Y)) '3 '4))

    => *

    ACL2 !>

  [47mFlet[0m is part of Common Lisp.  See any Common Lisp documentation for
  more information.  We conclude by pointing out an important aspect
  of [47mflet[0m shared by ACL2 and Common Lisp: The binding is lexical, not
  dynamic.  That is, the [47mflet[0m binding of a function symbol only
  applies to calls of that function symbol in the body of the [47mflet[0m,
  not other calls made in the course of evaluation.  Consider the
  following example.  Suppose we define:

    (defun f (x) x)
    (defun g (x) (f x))
    (defun h (x)
      (flet ((f (x) (cons x x)))
        (g x)))

