 (FMT
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Formatted printing

  ACL2 provides the functions [47mfmt[0m, [47m[fmt1][0m, and [47m[fms][0m as substitutes for
  Common Lisp's [47mformat[0m function.  Also see [fmt!], see [fmt1!], and
  see [fms!] for versions of these functions that write forms to
  files in a manner that allows them to be read, by avoiding using
  backslash ([47m\\[0m) to break long lines.  There are also analogues of
  these functions that return a string without taking [47m[state][0m as an
  argument; see [printing-to-strings].  A convenient macro, [47mfmx[0m, is
  described below; also see [cw] and see [fmx-cw].

  All three print a given string under an alist pairing character
  objects with values, interpreting certain ``tilde-directives'' in
  the string.  [47mChannel[0m must be a character output channel (e.g.,
  [47m[*standard-co*][0m).

    General Forms:                                            result
    (fms string alist channel state evisc-tuple)         ; state
    (fmt string alist channel state evisc-tuple)         ; (mv col state)
    (fmt1 string alist column channel state evisc-tuple) ; (mv col state)

  [47m[Fms][0m and [47mfmt[0m print an initial newline to put [47mchannel[0m in column [47m0[0m;
  [47m[Fmt1][0m requires the current column as input.  Columns are numbered
  from [47m0[0m.  The current column is the column into which the next
  character will be printed.  (Thus, the current column number is
  also the number of [characters] printed since the last newline.)
  The [47mcol[0m returned by [47mfmt[0m and [47m[fmt1][0m is the current column at the
  conclusion of the formatting.  [47mEvisc-tuple[0m must be either [47mnil[0m
  (meaning no abbreviations are used when objects are printed) or an
  ``evisceration tuple''; see [evisc-tuple].

  We list the tilde-directives below.  The notation is explained after
  the chart.

    ~xx  pretty print vx (maybe after printing a newline)
    ~yx  pretty print vx starting in current column; end with newline
    ~Xxy like ~xx but use vy as the evisceration tuple
    ~Yxy like ~yx but use vy as the evisceration tuple
    ~@x  if vx is a string, \"str\",  recursively format \"str\"
         if vx is (\"str\" . a), recursively format \"str\" under a+
    ~#x~[...~/...~/ ... ~/...~] cases on vx
         ^    ^     ...   ^  if 0<=vx<=k, choose vxth alternative
         0    1     ...   k  if vx is a list of length 1, case 0; else 1
    ~*x  iterator: vx must be of the form
         (\"str0\" \"str1\" \"str2\" \"str3\" lst . a);
         if lst is initially empty, format \"str0\" under a+; otherwise,
         bind #\\* successively to the elements of lst and then
         recursively format \"stri\" under a+, where i=1 if there is one
         element left to process, i=2 if there are two left, and i=3
         otherwise.
    ~&x  print elements of vx with ~x, separated by commas and a
         final ``and''
    ~vx  print elements of vx with ~x, separated by commas and a
         final ``or''
    ~nx  if vx is a small positive integer, print it as a word, e.g.,
         seven;
         if vx is a singleton containing a small positive integer, print
           the corresponding ordinal as a word, e.g., seventh
    ~Nx  like ~nx but the word is capitalized, e.g., Seven or Seventh
    ~tx  tab out to column vx; newline first if at or past column vx
    ~cx  vx is (n . w), print integer n right justified in field of
         width w
    ~fx  print object vx flat over as many lines as necessary
    ~Fx  same as ~f, except that subsequent lines are indented to
         start one character to the right of the first character printed
    ~sx  if vx is a symbol, print vx, breaking on hyphens (unless the
         symbol would normally be printed with surrounding vertical bar
         characters (|), in which case print as with ~fx); if vx is a
         string, print the characters in it, breaking on hyphens; else
         vx is a number, to be printed using the current print-base and
         print-radix
    ~Sx  same as ~s, except that margins are ignored (hence no breaking on
         hyphens and no breaking when the column exceeds the value of
         the hard right-margin)
    ~    tilde space: print a space
    ~_x  print vx spaces
    ~
         tilde newline: skip following whitespace
    ~%   output a newline
    ~|   output a newline unless already on left margin
    ~~   print a tilde
    ~-   if close to right margin, output a hyphen and newline; else
         skip this char

  If [47mx[0m is a character, then [47mvx[0m is the value of [47m#\\x[0m under the current
  alist.  Consider for example the discussion above for [47m~y[0m, ``[47m~yx
  pretty print vx[0m'', applied to the following expression: [47m(fmt \"HELLO
  ~y7\" (list (cons #\\7 'world)) *standard-co* state nil)[0m.  Then in
  this example: [47m#\\x[0m is 7; and [47mvx[0m is the value of character [47m#\\7[0m under
  the given alist, which is the symbol, [47mWORLD[0m.  Thus, ACL2 will print
  [47mHELLO WORLD[0m.  When we say ``format [47mstr[0m under [47ma+[0m'' we mean: process
  the given string under an alist obtained by appending [47ma[0m to the
  current alist.  The following example illustrates how this works.

    ACL2 !>(fms \"~@0\"
                (list (cons #\\0 (cons \"~x0 ~@1\" (list (cons #\\0  'abc))))
                      (cons #\\1 \"-- and now: ~x0 again~%\"))
                *standard-co* state nil)

    ABC -- and now: ABC again
    <state>
    ACL2 !>

  Note: [47m~p[0m, [47m~q[0m, [47m~P[0m, and [47m~Q[0m are also currently supported, but are
  deprecated and generally avoided in this manual.  These are
  respectively the same as [47m~x[0m, [47m~y[0m, [47m~X[0m, and [47m~Y[0m, except that their
  arguments may be expected to be terms, preferably untranslated
  (user-level) terms, since at one time there was the possibility
  that they could be printed using infix notation in certain
  environments.  Infix printing is no longer supported, however.

  ACL2's formatting functions print to the indicated channel, keeping
  track of which column they are in.  [47m[Fmt1][0m can be used if the
  caller knows which column the channel is in (i.e., how many
  [characters] have been printed since the last newline).  Otherwise,
  [47mfmt[0m or [47m[fms][0m must be used, both of which output a newline so as to
  establish the column position at [47m0[0m.  Unlike Common Lisp's [47mformat[0m
  routine, [47mfmt[0m and its relatives break the output into lines so that,
  by default, an attempt is made to avoid printing past column [47m77[0m
  (the value of constant [47m*fmt-hard-right-margin-default*[0m), except
  when using [47m~S[0m.  See [set-fmt-hard-right-margin] for a discussion of
  how linebreaks are inserted and how to change the relevant default
  settings.  A right margin of 40 is used for pretty printing with
  [47m~y[0m, [47m~Y[0m, [47m~q[0m, and [47m~Q[0m and can be changed to a positive integer [47mN[0m with
  [47m(set-ppr-flat-right-margin N state)[0m.

  The formatting functions scan the string from left to right, printing
  each successive character unless it is a tilde [47m(~)[0m.  Upon
  encountering tildes the formatters take action determined by the
  character or [characters] immediately following the tilde.  The
  typical tilde-directive is a group of three successive [characters]
  from the string being printed.  For example, [47m~x0[0m is a 3 character
  [47mtilde-directive[0m.  The first character in a tilde-directive is
  always the tilde character itself.  The next character is called
  the ``command'' character.  The character after that is usually
  taken as the name of a ``format variable'' that is bound in the
  alist under which the string is being printed.  Format variables
  are, by necessity, [characters].  The objects actually printed by a
  tilde-directive are the objects obtained by looking up the
  command's format variables in the alist.  Typical format variable
  names are [47m0[0m, [47m1[0m, [47m2[0m, ..., [47m9[0m, [47ma[0m, [47mb[0m, [47mc[0m, etc., and if a tilde-directive
  uses the format variable [47m0[0m, as in [47m~x0[0m, then the character [47m#\\0[0m must
  be bound in the alist.  Some tilde commands take no arguments and
  others take more than one, so some directives are of length two and
  others are longer.

  It should be noted that this use of [characters] in the string to
  denote arguments is another break from Common Lisp's [47mformat[0m
  routine.  In Common Lisp, the directives refer implicitly to the
  ``next item to be printed.'' But in ACL2 the directives name each
  item explicitly with our format variables.

  The following text contains examples that can be evaluated.  To make
  this process easier, we use a macro, [47mfmx[0m.  It takes up to eleven
  arguments, the first of which is a format string, [47mstr[0m, and the
  others of which are taken as the values of format variables; for
  similar utilities that do not take or return [state], see [cw] and
  [fmx-cw].  The variables used are [47m#\\0[0m through [47m#\\9[0m.  The macro
  constructs an appropriate alist, [47ma[0m, and then evaluates [47m(fmt` str a
  0 *standard-co* state nil)[0m.

  Thus,

    (fmx \"~%Here is v0, ~x0, and here is v1, ~x1.\"
         (cons 'value 0)
         (cons 'value 1))

  is just an abbreviation for

    (fmt1 \"~%Here is v0, ~x0, and here is v1, ~x1.\"
          (list (cons #\\0 (cons 'value 0))
                (cons #\\1 (cons 'value 1)))
          0
          *standard-co*
          state
          nil)

  which returns [47m(mv 53 state)[0m after printing, on a separate line,

    Here is v0, (VALUE . 0), and here is v1, (VALUE . 1).

  We now devote special attention to three of the tilde-directives
  whose use is non-obvious.

  [3mThe Case Statement[0m

  [47m~#x[0m is essentially a ``case statement'' in the language of [47mfmt[0m.  The
  proper form of the statement is

    ~#x~[case-0~/case-1~/ ... ~/case-k~],

  where each of the [47mcase-i[0m is a format string.  In the most common use,
  the variable [47mx[0m has an integer value, [47mvx[0m, between [47m0[0m and [47mk[0m,
  inclusive.  The effect of formatting the directive is to format
  [47mcase-vx[0m.

  For example

    (fmx \"Go ~#0~[North~/East~/South~/West~].~%\" 1)

  will print ``Go East.'' followed by a newline and will return

  [47m(mv 0 state)[0m, while if you change the [47m1[0m above to [47m3[0m (the maximum legal
  value), it will print ``Go West.''

  In order to make it easier to print such phrases as ``there are seven
  cases'' requiring agreement between subject and verb based on the
  number of elements of a list, the case statement allows its
  variable to take a list as its value and selects [47mcase-0[0m if the list
  has length [47m1[0m and [47mcase-1[0m otherwise.

    (let ((cases '(a b c)))
      (fmx \"There ~#0~[is ~n1 case~/are ~n1 cases~].\"
           cases
           (length cases)))

  will print ``There are three cases.'' but if you change the

  [47m'(a b c)[0m above simply to [47m'(a)[0m it will print ``There is one case.''
  and if you change it to [47mnil[0m it will print ``There are zero cases.''

  [3mIndirection[0m

  Roughly speaking, [47m~@[0m will act as though the value of its argument is
  a format string and splice it into the current string at the
  current position.  It is often used when the phrase to be printed
  must be computed.  For example,

    (let ((ev 'DEFUN))
     (fmx \"~x0 is an event~@1.\"
          'foo
          (if (member-eq ev '(defun defstub encapsulate))
              \" that may introduce a function symbol\"
              \"\")))

  will print ``[47mfoo[0m is an event that may introduce a function symbol,''
  but if the value of [47mev[0m is changed from [47m'[0m[47m[defun][0m to [47m'[0m[47m[defthm][0m, it
  prints ``[47mfoo[0m is an event.'' The [47m~@[0m directive ``splices'' in the
  computed phrase (which might be empty).  Of course, this particular
  example could be done with the case statement

    ~#1~[~/ that may introduce a function symbol~]

  where the value of [47m#\\1[0m is appropriately computed to be [47m0[0m or [47m1[0m.

  If the argument to [47m~@[0m is a pair, it is taken to be a format string
  [47m[cons][0med onto an alist, i.e., [47m(\"str\" . a)[0m, and the alist, [47ma[0m, is
  used to extend the current one before [47m\"str\"[0m is recursively
  processed.  This feature of [47mfmt[0m can be used to pass around
  ``phrases'' that contain computed contextual information in [47ma[0m.  The
  most typical use is as ``error messages.'' For example, suppose you
  are writing a function which does not have access to [47m[state][0m and so
  cannot print an error message.  It may nevertheless be necessary
  for it to signal an error to its caller, say by returning two
  results, the first of which is interpreted as an error message if
  non-[47mnil[0m.  Our convention is to use a [47m~@[0m pair to represent such
  messages.  For example, the error value might be produced by the
  code:

    (cons
      \"Error:  The instruction ~x0 is illegal when the stack is ~x1.~%\"
      (list (cons #\\0 (current-instruction st))
            (cons #\\1 (i-stack st))))

  If the [47mcurrent-instruction[0m and [47mi-stack[0m (whatever they are) are [47m'(popi
  3)[0m and [47m'(a b)[0m when the [47m[cons][0m above is evaluated, then it produces

    '(\"Error:  The instruction ~x0 is illegal when the stack is ~x1.~%\"
      (#\\0 POPI 3)
      (#\\1 A B))

  and if this pair is made the value of the [47mfmt[0m variable [47m0[0m, then [47m~@0[0m
  will print

    Error:  The instruction (POPI 3) is illegal when the stack is (A B).

  For example, evaluate

    (let
     ((pair
      '(\"~%Error:  The instruction ~x0 is illegal when the stack is ~x1.~%\"
        (#\\0 POPI 3)
        (#\\1 A B))))
     (fmx \"~@0\" pair)).

  Thus, even though the function that produced the ``error'' could not
  print it, it could specify exactly what error message and data are
  to be printed.

  This example raises another issue.  Sometimes it is desirable to
  break lines in your format strings so as to make your source code
  more attractive.  That is the purpose of the [47mtilde-newline[0m
  directive.  The following code produces exactly the same output as
  described above.

    (let ((pair '(\"Error:  The instruction ~x0 ~
                  is illegal when the stack is ~
                  ~x1.~%\"
                  (#\\0 POPI 3)
                  (#\\1 A B))))
     (fmx \"~%~@0\" pair)).

  Finally, observe that when [47m~@0[0m extends the current alist, [47malist[0m, with
  the one, [47ma[0m, in its argument, the bindings from [47ma[0m are added to the
  front of [47malist[0m, overriding the current values of any shared
  variables.  This ensures that the variable values seen by the
  recursively processed string, [47m\"str\"[0m, are those from [47ma[0m, but if [47m\"str\"[0m
  uses variables not bound in [47ma[0m, their values are as specified in the
  original alist.  Intuitively, variables bound in [47ma[0m are local to the
  processing of [47m(\"str\" . a)[0m but [47m\"str\"[0m may use ``global variables.''
  The example above illustrates this because when the [47m~@0[0m is
  processed, [47m#\\0[0m is bound to the error message pair.  But when the
  [47m~x0[0m in the error string is processed, [47m#\\0[0m is bound to the illegal
  instruction.

  [3mIteration[0m

  The [47m~*[0m directive is used to process each element of a list.  For
  example,

    (let ((lst '(a b c d e f g h))) ; a true-list whose elements we exhibit
     (fmx \"~*0\"
          `(\"Whoa!\"          ; what to print if there's nothing to print
            \"~x*!\"           ; how to print the last element
            \"~x* and \"       ; how to print the 2nd to last element
            \"~x*, \"          ; how to print all other elements
            ,lst)))          ; the list of elements to print

  will print ``[47mA, B, C, D, E, F, G and H![0m''.  Try this example with
  other true list values of [47mlst[0m, such as [47m'(a b)[0m, [47m'(a)[0m, and [47mnil[0m.  The
  tilde-directives [47m~&0[0m and [47m~v0[0m, which take a true list argument and
  display its elements separated by commas and a final ``and'' or
  ``or,'' are implemented in terms of the more general [47m~*[0m.

  The [47m~*[0m directive allows the 5-tuple to specify in its final [47m[cdr][0m an
  alist with which to extend the current one before processing the
  individual elements.

  We often use [47m~*[0m to print a series of phrases, separated by suitable
  punctuation, whitespace and noise words.  In such use, the [47m~*[0m
  handles the separation of the phrases and each phrase is generally
  printed by [47m~@[0m.

  Here is a complex example.  In the [47m[let*][0m, below, we bind phrases to
  a list of [47m~@[0m pairs and then we create a [47m~*[0m 5-tuple to print out the
  conjunction of the phrases with a parenthetical ``finally!'' if the
  series is longer than 3.

    (let* ((phrases
            (list (list \"simplifying with the replacement rules ~&0\"
                        (cons #\\0 '(rewrite-rule1
                                    rewrite-rule2
                                    rewrite-rule3)))
                  (list \"destructor elimination using ~x0\"
                        (cons #\\0 'elim-rule))
                  (list \"generalizing the terms ~&0\"
                        (cons #\\0 '((rev x) (app u v))))
                  (list \"inducting on ~x0\"
                        (cons #\\0 'I))))
           (5-tuple
            (list
             \"magic\"                            ; no phrases
             \"~@*\"                              ; last phrase
             \"~@*, and~#f~[~/ (finally!)~] \"    ; second to last phrase
             \"~@*, \"                            ; other phrases
             phrases                            ; the phrases themselves
             (cons #\\f
                   (if (>(length phrases) 3) 1 0))))) ;print ``finally''?
      (fmx \"We did it by ~*0.\" 5-tuple))

  This [47m[let*][0m prints

    We did it by simplifying with the replacement rules REWRITE-RULE1,
    REWRITE-RULE2 and REWRITE-RULE3, destructor elimination using ELIM-
    RULE, generalizing the terms (REV X) and (APP U V), and (finally!)
    inducting on I.

  You might wish to try evaluating the [47m[let*][0m after removing elements
  of phrases.

  Most of the output produced by ACL2 is produced via [47mfmt[0m statements.
  Thus, inspection of the source code will yield many examples.  A
  complicated example is the code that explains the simplifier's
  work.  See [47m:[0m[47m[pc][0m [47msimplify-clause-msg1[0m.  An ad hoc example is
  provided by the function [47mfmt-doc-example[0m, which takes two
  arguments: an arbitrary true list and [47m[state][0m.  To see how
  [47mfmt-doc-example[0m works, [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m [47mfmt-doc-example[0m.

