  (LOOP$)
  "Iteration with [47m[loop$][0m over an interval of integers or a list

  This topic assumes that you have read the introduction to [47mloop$[0m
  expressions in ACL2; see [loop$].  Here we give more complete
  documentation on [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m expressions, beginning with informal
  discussion and then continuing with detailed syntax (General Form)
  and semantics.  For a discussion of proofs about [47mloop$[0ms, see
  [stating-and-proving-lemmas-about-loop$s].

  Examples of [47m[loop$][0m expressions, including [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms, may be found
  in [community-book] [47mprojects/apply/loop-tests.lisp[0m.

  The only allowed iteration clauses are [47mIN[0m, where the variable ranges
  over the elements of the given true list; [47mON[0m, where the variable
  ranges over the tails of the given true-list; and [47mFROM/TO/BY[0m, where
  the variable ranges over the integers between two bounds, stepping
  by a positive integer increment (or by 1 if no [47mBY[0m clause is
  provided).

  You may have as many iteration clauses as you wish, connected with
  [47mAS[0m.  Each must introduce a unique iteration variable and that
  variable may be optionally followed by an [47mOF-TYPE[0m [type-spec]
  specification.  [47mOF-TYPE[0m is a Common Lisp feature that allows the
  compiler to optimize operations on the variable in question.  Here
  is an example.

    (loop$ for v of-type (and integer (not (satisfies zerop)))
                 from 1 to 100
           sum (/ 1 v))

  Here is that same example with a more concise type specification.

    (loop$ for v of-type (integer 1 *)
                 from 1 to 100
           sum (/ 1 v))

  After all of the iteration clauses, you may have a termination test,
  signaled by [47mUNTIL[0m, and/or a conditional test, signaled by [47mWHEN[0m.  If
  both are provided, the [47mUNTIL[0m test must come first.  Iteration stops
  when the [47mUNTIL[0m test is satisfied.  The conditional test determines
  whether the loop body is executed for the current value of the
  iteration variables.

  Between the [47mUNTIL[0m symbol and the expression to be tested, and between
  the [47mWHEN[0m symbol and its expression, you may include a [47m:GUARD[0m
  clause.  This is useful if [guard] verification requires an
  invariant relating multiple iteration variables.  An example of a
  guarded [47mUNTIL[0m clause is

    (loop$ for u in lst1 as v in lst2
           until :guard (invariantp u v) (test u v)
           collect (body u v))

  ACL2 supports only five operators in [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms: [47mSUM[0m, [47mCOLLECT[0m,
  [47mALWAYS[0m, [47mTHEREIS[0m and [47mAPPEND[0m.  We anticipate adding other Common Lisp
  operators eventually.

  The special symbols noted above, sometimes called ``[47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m
  keywords'' or just ``[47mloop$[0m keywords'' may be in any package.  These
  are [47mFOR[0m, [47mIN[0m, [47mON[0m, [47mFROM[0m, [47mTO[0m, [47mBY[0m, [47mOF-TYPE[0m, [47mWHEN[0m, [47mUNTIL[0m, [47mSUM[0m, [47mCOLLECT[0m,
  [47mALWAYS[0m, [47mTHEREIS[0m, and [47mAPPEND[0m.

  Between the operator, e.g., [47mSUM[0m or [47mCOLLECT[0m, and the [47mloop$[0m body you
  may include a [47m:GUARD[0m clause as in

    (loop$ for u in lst1 as v in lst2
           collect :guard (invariantp u v) (body u v))

  This is sometimes necessary in the verification of the [guard]s for
  the [47mloop$[0m body because Common Lisp's [47mOF-TYPE[0m clauses do not permit
  you to relate one variable to another.

  See [lp-section-6] of the [47mLoop$[0m Primer for some exercises in writing
  [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0ms (with answers in a Community Book).  But remember to
  come back here when you get to the end of that section.


General Form

  The syntax of Common Lisp [47mloop[0m expressions is extremely complicated.
  Rather than try to write the abstract syntax of ACL2's [47mloop$[0m
  expressions in the same formal style, we take a different approach,
  which is workable because [47mloop$[0m allows fewer options.

  First we introduce the syntax of a ``target clause,'' a
  ``type-spec,'' and the ``operators.'' Then we describe the most
  elaborate form of a [47mloop$[0m expression in terms of these elements and
  ordinary ACL2 terms.  Every legal [47mloop$[0m expression can be produced
  by omitting certain optional elements from the most elaborate [47mloop$[0m
  form.  So we conclude the syntactic description of [47mloop$[0m by listing
  the elements that can be omitted.

  A [3mtarget clause[0m has one of four forms

    * [47mIN[0m [3mlist-expr[0m

    * [47mON[0m [3mexpr[0m

    * [47mFROM[0m [3mlo-expr[0m [47mTO[0m [3mhi-expr[0m

    * [47mFROM[0m [3mlo-expr[0m [47mTO[0m [3mhi-expr[0m [47mBY[0m [3mstep-expr[0m

  where [3mlist-expr[0m is a term (which is expected to evaluate to a true
  list), [3mexpr[0m is a term, [3mlo-expr[0m and [3mhi-expr[0m are terms (which are
  expected to evaluate to integers), and [3mstep-expr[0m is a term (which
  is expected to evaluate to a positive integer).

  The legal [3mtype-specs[0m are listed in [47m[type-spec][0m.

  The legal [3moperators[0m are [47mSUM[0m, [47mCOLLECT[0m, [47mALWAYS[0m, [47mTHEREIS[0m, and [47mAPPEND[0m.

  The most elaborate [47mloop$[0m expression is of the form

  [47m(LOOP$ FOR [0m[3mv1[0m[47m OF-TYPE [0m[3mspec1 target1[0m
  [47mAS [0m   [3mv2[0m[47m OF-TYPE [0m[3mspec2 target2[0m
  ...
  [47mAS [0m   [3mvn[0m[47m OF-TYPE [0m[3mspecn targetn[0m
  [47mUNTIL :GUARD [0m[3mguard1 until-expr[0m
  [47mWHEN [0m   [47m:GUARD [0m[3mguard2 when-expr[0m
  ; Note the [47mALWAYS[0m/[47mTHEREIS[0m Exceptions below!
  [3mop[0m[47m :GUARD [0m[3mguard3 body-expr[0m[47m)[0m

  where each [3mvi[0m   is a legal variable symbol and they are all distinct,
  each [3mtype-speci[0m   is a [47m[type-spec][0m, each [3mtargeti[0m   is a target
  clause, each [3mguardi[0m, [3muntil-expr[0m, and [3mwhen-expr[0m   is a term, [3mop[0m   is
  an operator, and [3mbody-expr[0m   is a term.  Furthermore, [3muntil-expr[0m,
  [3mwhen-expr[0m, and [3mbody-expr[0m   must be [tame]!

  [3mThe [47mALWAYS[0m[3m/[47mTHEREIS[0m[3m Exception:[0m Common Lisp prohibits loops with both a
  [47mWHEN[0m clause and either an [47mALWAYS[0m or a [47mTHEREIS[0m operator.  For
  example, if you are tempted to use [47mWHEN[0m [3mp[0m with [47mALWAYS[0m [3mq[0m you can
  instead write [47mALWAYS[0m [47m(implies [0m[3mp q[0m[47m)[0m or, if you want to evaluate [47mq[0m
  only when [47mp[0m is true, you can write [47mALWAYS[0m [47m(if [0m[3mp q[0m [47mt)[0m.

  The following elements may be omitted.

    * any line beginning with [47mAS[0m, [47mUNTIL[0m or [47mWHEN[0m,

    * any [47mOF-TYPE[0m [3mspeci[0m, and

    * any [47m:GUARD[0m [3mguardi[0m.

  As noted above, the [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m keywords (as used above) may be in any
  package.  These are [47mFOR[0m, [47mIN[0m, [47mON[0m, [47mFROM[0m, [47mTO[0m, [47mBY[0m, [47mOF-TYPE[0m, [47mWHEN[0m,
  [47mUNTIL[0m, [47mSUM[0m, [47mCOLLECT[0m, [47mALWAYS[0m, [47mTHEREIS[0m, and [47mAPPEND[0m.

  We give names to certain classes of the syntactic entities above.
  The [3mv1[0m, ..., [3mvn[0m are called the [3miteration variables[0m.  The [3mspec1[0m,
  ..., [3mspecn[0m are called [3mtype specs[0m, each corresponds to a certain
  iteration variable, and each gives rise to a [3mtype term[0m about its
  variable in the sense that ``[47mX OF-TYPE (SATISFIES NATP)[0m'' gives
  rise to the type term [47m(NATP X)[0m and ``[47mI OF-TYPE INTEGER[0m'' gives rise
  to the type term [47m(INTEGERP I)[0m.  The terms involved in the target
  expressions, e.g., the [3mlist-expr[0m and [3mexpr[0m in ``[47mIN[0m [3mlist-expr[0m'' and
  ``[47mON[0m [3mexpr[0m'' and the [3mlo-expr[0m, [3mhi-expr[0m and optional [3mstep-expr[0m in the
  ``[47mFROM[0m [3mlo-expr[0m [47mTO[0m [3mhi-expr[0m [47mBY[0m [3mstep-expr[0m'' targets are called [3mtarget
  terms[0m.  Finally, the [3muntil-expr[0m, [3mwhen-expr[0m, and [3mbody-expr[0m are
  called [3miterative forms[0m.

  We distinguish the target terms from the iterative forms because they
  are handled very differently at evaluation time.  When a [47mloop$[0m is
  evaluated, the target terms are evaluated just once.  But the
  iterative forms are evaluated multiple times as the iteration
  variables range over the values of the targets.

  A [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m expression with just one iteration variable and in which
  the iterative forms mention no free variable other than the
  iteration variable is called a [3msimple [47mloop$[0m[3m[0m (or, sometimes, a
  [3msimple loop[0m).  An example of a simple loop is

    (loop$ for x in lst when (evenp x) collect (+ 1 (sq x)))

  A [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m expression is called a [3mfancy [47mloop$[0m[3m[0m if it is not simple.
  Both of the following [47mloop$[0ms are fancy.

    (loop$ for x in xlst as y on ylst collect (expr x y))

    (loop$ for x in xlst collect (expr x z))

  The first is fancy because it has two iteration variables.  The
  second is fancy because the body freely uses the variable [47mz[0m which
  is not the iteration variable.  The free variables cannot be
  [stobj]s or [df]s, since the translation of the [47mloop$[0m will put them
  into a list; see discussion of the semantics, below.  In such cases
  you may wish to use [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m expressions (see [do-loop$]) instead.


Semantics

  [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m expressions are translated into calls of [scion]s, with the
  [47mUNTIL[0m and [47mWHEN[0m clauses translated into preprocessors of the
  targets.  But which scions are used depend on whether the loop is
  simple or fancy.  Recall that a fancy loop is one that has either
  or both of the following characteristics: (a) there is one or more
  [47mas[0m clauses, and/or (b) one of the iterative forms (the [47mUNTIL[0m, [47mWHEN[0m
  or loop body expression) refers to variables other than an
  iteration variable.  If the [47mloop$[0m expression is simple, the simple
  scions are used; otherwise the fancy scions are used.

    loop$               simple          fancy
    keyword             scion           scion
    ______________________________________________
    SUM                 sum$            sum$+
    COLLECT             collect$        collect$+
    ALWAYS              always$         always$+
    THEREIS             thereis$        thereis$+
    APPEND              append$         append$+
    UNTIL               until$          until$+
    WHEN                when$           when$+

  We deal with simple [47mloop$[0ms first.

  Semantics of Simple Loop$s

  For example, the simple [47mloop$[0m

    (loop$ for x in lst collect (+ 1 (sq x)))

  translates to (a term equivalent to)

    (collect$ (lambda$ (x)
                       (declare (ignorable x))
                       (+ 1 (sq x)))
              lst).

  [31;1mNote:[0m The actual translation is tagged with various markers that play
  a role in evaluation but which are logically irrelevant and which
  are removed during proof.  In this discussion we will not display
  the marked-up translations but logically equivalent terms instead.
  You can see the actual translations for yourself with [47m[trans][0m.

  In the translation the target term, [47mlst[0m, appears as an ordinary
  subterm of the translation.  But the iterative form, [47m(+ 1 (sq x))[0m,
  becomes the body of a [47m[lambda$][0m expression, which means its
  translation becomes a component of a quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m object.  When
  the [47mcollect$[0m is evaluated, the target term is evaluated once but
  the iterative form is evaluated once for each element of the value
  of the target.

  [47mUNTIL[0m and [47mWHEN[0m clauses are handled by preprocessing the target.
  E.g.,

    (loop$ for x in lst
           until (> x 100)
           when (evenp x)
           collect (+ 1 (sq x)))

  becomes

    (collect$ (lambda$ (x)
                       (declare (ignorable x))
                       (+ 1 (sq x)))
              (when$ (lambda$ (x)
                              (declare (ignorable x))
                              (evenp x))
                     (until$ (lambda$ (x)
                                      (declare (ignorable x))
                                      (> x 100))
                             lst)))

  So from a logical perspective, the presence of an [47mUNTIL[0m and/or [47mWHEN[0m
  clause in a [47mcollect[0m iteration over [47mlst[0m ``copies'' the target value.
  The [47muntil$[0m copies [47mlst[0m until encountering the first element on which
  its functional argument is true.  The [47mwhen$[0m then copies that
  (shortened?) target, keeping only the elements that satisfy its
  functional argument.  Finally, the [47mcollect$[0m then applies its
  functional argument and collects all the values.

  [47mON[0m and [47mFROM/TO/BY[0m targets are handled by listing all the elements in
  the given target.  For example,

    (loop$ for x on lst collect (expr x))

  which maps [47mx[0m over successive non-empty tails of [47mlst[0m and collects the
  value of [47mexpr[0m has the logical meaning

    (collect$ (lambda$ (x) (expr x))
              (tails lst))

  where, for example, [47m(tails '(1 2 3))[0m is [47m((1 2 3) (2 3) (3))[0m.

  Spiritually similarly,

    (loop$ for i from 1 to max by step collect (expr x))

  logically becomes

    (collect$ (lambda$ (x) (expr x))
              (from-to-by 1 max step))

  where, for example, [47m(from-to-by 1 10 2)[0m is [47m(1 3 5 7 9)[0m.

  Similar translations are done for the other operators, e.g., [47mSUM[0m and
  [47mALWAYS[0m.  The advantage of this translation style is that it allows
  compositional reasoning.  We discuss this further below.

  The following example illustrates basic [guard] proof obligations, in
  particular showing that [47mWHEN[0m clauses do not help with verifying
  guards for the [47mloop$[0m bodies.  (Similarly, [47mUNTIL[0m clauses do not help
  either.)  The basic problem is that ACL2 requires that [47m[lambda][0m
  objects be guard verifiable in isolation, not confined to the
  context in which a particular [47mlambda[0m object appears.  Consider the
  following.

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)
    (defun$ sq (n)
      (declare (xargs :guard (natp n)))
      (* n n))
    (defun foo (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (nat-listp lst)))
      (loop$ for x of-type (satisfies nat-listp) on lst
             when (consp x)
             sum (sq (car x))))

  Guard verification fails for [47mfoo[0m.  The summary says that a goal of
  [47mNIL[0m was generated.  Using [47m:[0m[47m[pso][0m we can see that the [47mNIL[0m goal came
  from:

    Subgoal 1.2
    (IMPLIES (NAT-LISTP LOOP$-IVAR)
             (INTEGERP (CAR LOOP$-IVAR))).

  Let's see what is going on by looking at the following simplified
  translation of the [47mloop$[0m expression.

    (sum$ '(lambda (loop$-ivar)
             (declare (type (satisfies nat-listp) loop$-ivar))
             (sq (car loop$-ivar)))
          (when$ '(lambda ...) (tails lst)))

  Notice that the [47mlambda[0m object supplied to [47msum$[0m cannot be guard
  verified in isolation: [47mnil[0m satisfies the [47m:guard[0m and [47m(car nil)[0m
  violates the guard of [47msq[0m.  The following modification, which adds a
  [47m:guard[0m directive after the [47mSUM[0m op keyword, solves the problem.

    (defun foo (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (nat-listp lst)))
      (loop$ for x of-type (satisfies nat-listp) on lst
             when (consp x)
             sum :guard (consp x)  ; note new :guard
             (sq (car x))))

  This new [47m:guard[0m may feel redundant, coming as it does after the [47mwhen
  (consp x)[0m clause.  But it is necessary given the compositional
  semantics.

  A simplified translation of the [47mdefun[0m above shows that the
  application [47m(sq (car x))[0m is protected by a suitable guard in the
  [47mlambda[0m object.

    (sum$ '(lambda (loop$-ivar)
             (declare (type (satisfies nat-listp) loop$-ivar)
                      (xargs :guard (consp loop$-ivar)))
             (sq (car loop$-ivar)))
          (when$ '(lambda ...) (tails lst)))

  Naively we might have expected that the guard proof obligation for
  the [47mloop$[0m body [47m(sq (car x))[0m could assume the [47mWHEN[0m clause, but that
  expectation would be wrong because of the compositional semantics
  we use and the fact that [47m[lambda][0m objects must be guard-verifiable
  on their own.  The reason for the latter requirement is that our
  implementation caches guard-verified [47mlambda[0m objects for evaluation
  in raw Lisp, which may take place in other contexts different from
  that in which the [47mlambda[0m first appeared.  See [print-cl-cache].

  Semantics of Fancy Loop$s

  An example of a fancy [47mloop$[0m is

    (loop$ for x in xlst as y in ylst collect (expr x y z))

  This loop exhibits both characteristics (a) and (b): it has an [47mAS[0m
  clause and the variable [47mz[0m appears in the loop body.  Either
  characteristic is sufficient to classify the loop as fancy.  So
  fancy scions are used.  Here is its semantic counterpart, i.e., its
  simplified translation.

    (collect$+
     (lambda$ (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
              (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp loop$-gvars)
                                          (equal (len loop$-gvars) 1)
                                          (true-listp loop$-ivars)
                                          (equal (len loop$-ivars) 2))))
              (let ((z (car loop$-gvars))
                    (x (car loop$-ivars))
                    (y (car (cdr loop$-ivars))))
                (declare (ignorable x y))
                (expr x y z)))
     (list z)
     (loop$-as (list xlst ylst)))

  Before we show the definition of [47mcollect$+[0m, note that the arguments
  above to [47mcollect$+[0m are (i) a [47mlambda$[0m expression that handles the
  evaluation of the iterative form, in this case [47m(expr x y z)[0m, where
  [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are iteration variables and [47mz[0m is a ``global'' variable not
  among the iteration variables; (ii) the list of values of the
  ``global'' variables, in this case the list containing [47mz[0m; and (iii)
  a target list constructed by [47mloop$-as[0m from the various targets
  provided in the [47mloop$[0m, in this case [47mxlst[0m and [47mylst[0m, supplying values
  for iteration variables [47mx[0m and [47my[0m respectively.  For example,
  [47m(loop$-as (list '(a b c d e) '(1 2 3)))[0m is [47m((a 1) (b 2) (c 3))[0m.
  These tuples contain successive corresponding values of [47mx[0m and [47my[0m.

  The definition of [47mcollect$+[0m is essentially

    (defun collect$+ (fn loop$-gvars lst)
      (if (endp lst)
          nil
          (cons (apply$ fn (list loop$-gvars (car lst)))
                (collect$+ fn loop$-gvars (cdr lst)))))

  We have omitted the guard and an [47m[mbe][0m form that make it run more
  efficiently.  All the fancy [47mloop$[0m scions are defined analogously.

  Inspection of the [47mlambda$[0m expression above reveals that it takes a
  list of global variable values and a list of iteration variable
  values, unpacks them with a [47mlet[0m that binds the global variables,
  here just [47mz[0m, to their values and binds the iteration variables,
  here [47mx[0m and [47my[0m, to the corresponding pair of values from the target,
  and then evaluates the [47mloop$[0m body, [47m(expr x y z)[0m.

  The names of the formals for the [47mlambda$[0m expressions generated by
  fancy [47mloop$[0m expressions are always [47mloop$-gvars[0m and [47mloop$-ivars[0m, for
  ``[47mloop$[0m global variables'' and ``[47mloop$[0m iteration variables.''

  [47mUNTIL[0m and [47mWHEN[0m clauses in a fancy [47mloop$[0m are handled exactly as they
  are in simple [47mloop$[0ms, except that the fancy scions are used since
  the target list is a list of tuples of iteration variable values
  and the [47mUNTIL[0m and [47mWHEN[0m forms may refer to global variables.

  See [lp-section-10] of the [47mLoop$[0m Primer for a step-by-step discussion
  of the evaluation of the formal semantics of an example of a fancy
  [47mLoop$[0m.  But remember to come back here when you get to the end of
  that section.

  Special Guard Conjectures for [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms

  Since every [47mloop$[0m expands to a call of a [47mloop$[0m scion on a lambda
  object and a target, one would expect that [guard] verification
  would generate the guard conjectures for that scion and target.
  Indeed, it does.  In particular, the lambda object must have the
  correct number of formals (which is guaranteed by translation) and
  the target must satisfy [47m[true-listp][0m.

  But in addition to the expected guard conjectures, we generate some
  special ones for the terms produced by translating [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m
  expressions.  We discuss the reasons in the next section, but here
  we just state what the special conjectures are.  We limit ourselves
  to a simple [47mloop$[0m.  Fancy [47mloop$[0ms generalize in the obvious way.
  The two classes of ``special guard conjectures'' for [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m
  expressions are as follows.

    * First, every element (or tail, in the case of [47mON[0m [47mloop$[0ms)) satisfies
      the type-spec, if any, and the [47mloop$[0m body's [47m:GUARD[0m, if any.

    * Second, the [47mloop$[0m body produces a value acceptable to the [47mloop$[0m
      operator, e.g., the body of a [47mSUM[0m [47mloop$[0m produces a number and
      the body of an [47mAPPEND[0m [47mloop$[0m produces a true list.

  See [lp-section-8] of the [47mLoop$[0m Primer for some exercises in
  converting recursive functions to guard verified functions using
  [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms rather than recursion (with answers in a Community
  Book).  But remember to come back here when you get to the end of
  that section.

  Discussion of Why LOOP$s Have Special Guards

  All of the simple [47mloop$[0m scions have the same guard, namely

    (AND (APPLY$-GUARD FN '(NIL))
         (TRUE-LISTP LST)),

  and all the fancy [47mloop$[0m scions have the same guard, namely

    (AND (APPLY$-GUARD FN '(NIL NIL))
         (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
         (TRUE-LIST-LISTP LST)).

  In addition to the normal guard conjectures that would be generated
  by calls of these scions, ACL2 generates some special guard
  conjectures because the normal guard conjectures are insufficient
  to guarantee the error-free execution of the corresponding Common
  Lisp [47mloop[0m expressions.

  For example, the simplified translation of the body of

    (defun foo (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (foo-guardp lst)))
      (loop$ for x of-type (satisfies spec) on lst sum (expr x)))

  is as follows.  (One might expect the second argument of the [47msum$[0m
  call to be [47m(tails lst)[0m, and of course that is what it equals,
  logically; the change is to support guards, as discussed in the
  Technical Note below.)

    (sum$ (lambda$ (loop$-ivar)
                   (declare (type (satisfies spec) loop$-ivar))
                   (expr loop$-ivar))
          (tails (prog2$ (let ((loop$-last-cdr (last-cdr lst)))
                           (declare (type (satisfies spec) loop$-last-cdr))
                           loop$-last-cdr)
                         lst)))

  Prior to the provision for special guards, the normal guard
  conjectures generated for [47mfoo[0m would be

    (and (implies (foo-guardp lst)                                 ; [1]
                  (apply$-guard
                   (lambda$ (loop$-ivar)
                     (expr loop$-ivar))
                   '(nil)))
         (implies (foo-guardp lst)                                 ; [2]
                  (true-listp (tails lst)))
         (implies (spec loop$-ivar) (expr-guardp loop$-ivar)))     ; [3]
         (implies (foo-guardp lst)                                 ; [4]
                  (let ((loop$-last-cdr (last-cdr lst)))
                       (spec loop$-last-cdr)))

  Conjectures [1] and [2] stem from the guard for [47msum$[0m and establish
  that the guard for [47mfoo[0m implies that [47msum$[0m is passed a function
  object of one argument and a true-list.  Conjecture [3] establishes
  that the guard on the [47mlambda$[0m implies the guard of its body.
  Conjecture [4] says that the final tail of [47mlst[0m (which is [47mnil[0m if [47mlst[0m
  is a true list; see [last-cdr]) satisfies [47mspec[0m.  At first [4] may
  be surprising, but inspection of Common Lisp reveals that even
  though [47m(expr x)[0m is never called on the final tail of [47mlst[0m,
  implementations running with high safety settings check that the
  final tail satisfies [47mspec[0m.  If you see a runtime guard violation
  involving variable [47mloop$-last-cdr[0m, you can reasonably assume that
  you are seeing case [4] above.  A guard proof failure involving a
  call of [47m[last-cdr][0m may also be from [4].

  (Technical Note.  The production of [4] is accomplished as follows
  for an expression [47m(loop for var of-type type-spec on lst ...)[0m: for
  the translation to a scion call, the target term, [47mlst[0m, is replaced
  by the following expression (simplifying slightly).

    (prog2$ (let ((loop$-last-cdr (last-cdr lst)))
              (declare (type type-spec loop$-last-cdr))
              loop$-last-cdr)
            lst)

  The [47m[declare][0m form causes formula [4] above to be generated as a
  guard proof obligation.  End of Technical Note.)

  The guard proof obligation shown as [4] above, for an expression
  [47m(loop$ for ... on ...)[0m, has a variant for an expression [47m(loop$ for
  n from i to j by k ...)[0m, where ``[47mby k[0m'' may be implicit if [47mk[0m is [47m1[0m.
  Instead of the requirement in [4] above that the bound variable
  [47mloop$-last-cdr[0m satisfy the [47mtype-spec[0m, variables [47mloop$-lo[0m, [47mloop$-hi[0m,
  and [47mloop$-by[0m, which are bound respectively to [47mi[0m, [47mj[0m, and [47mk[0m, are
  required to satisfy the [47mtype-spec[0m.  A fourth requirement is that
  the last value tested must also satisfy the [47mtype-spec[0m.  That last
  value tested is [47m(+ loop$-lo loop$-by (* loop$-by (floor (- loop$-hi
  loop$-lo) loop$-by)))[0m.  If you see a runtime guard violation
  involving variable [47mloop$-lo[0m, [47mloop$-hi[0m, [47mloop$-by[0m, or [47mloop$-final[0m,
  you can reasonably assume that you are seeing this variant of [4].

  But consider the raw Lisp [47mloop[0m generated by the [47mloop$[0m in the raw Lisp
  definition of [47mfoo[0m,

    (loop for x of-type (satisfies spec) on lst sum (expr x)).

  For this [47mloop[0m to execute without error we need to know that [5] every
  non-empty tail of [47mlst[0m satisfies [47mspec[0m, and [6] every non-empty tail
  [47mx[0m of [47mlst[0m is such that [47m(expr x)[0m returns a number.

  So when ACL2's guard verification process encounters a [47msum$[0m like that
  in the logical [47mdefun[0m of [47mfoo[0m, it generates two additional guard
  conjectures as described above.  These are as mentioned above: [5]
  every element (or tail) satisfies the type-spec, and [6] the value
  of the [47mloop$[0m body is acceptable to the [47mloop$[0m operator.

    (implies (and (warrant ...) ; see below                   ; [5]
                  (foo-guardp lst)
                  (member-equal newv (tails lst)))
             (spec newv))

    (implies (and (warrant ...) ; see below                   ; [6]
                  (foo-guardp lst)
                  (member-equal newv (tails lst)))
             (acl2-numberp
              (apply$ (lambda$ (loop$-ivar) (expr loop$-ivar))
                      (list newv))))

  Notice the addition of hypotheses above of the form [47m(warrant ...)[0m.
  ACL2 adds such [3m[warrant] hypotheses[0m for function symbols that might
  be [47m[apply$][0m'd during evaluation of a scion call (in this case,
  [47msum$[0m).

  In general, you may notice that ACL2 generates such ``special'' guard
  conjectures for all calls of [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m scions, whether or not they
  stemmed from uses of [47mloop$[0m.  [47mFROM/TO/BY[0m targets require that the
  bounds and step all satisfy the [47mOF-TYPE[0m specification, and the
  [47mAPPEND[0m operator requires that the loop body generate a [47m[true-listp][0m
  (instead of an [47m[ACL2-numberp][0m as required by the [47mSUM[0m operator).

  The Compromise Between Reasoning and Efficiency

  The translation of [47mloop$[0m expressions into formal terms reflects a
  compromise between facilitating compositional reasoning and
  efficient execution.

  One sign of that compromise is our use of scions to handle [47mUNTIL[0m and
  [47mWHEN[0m clauses.  As noted above, by translating

    (loop$ for x in lst until ... when ... collect ...)

  into

    (collect$ ... (when$ ... (until$ ... lst)))

  we're forcing the evaluation of the formal semantics to copy the
  target twice before collecting.  But it gives us the ability to
  reason compositionally about [47mcollect$[0m, [47mwhen$[0m, and [47muntil$[0m.  We could
  have defined a version of [47mcollect$[0m that took three [47mlambda$[0m
  expressions, one to terminate the collection, one to filter for the
  elements we're interested in, and one to transform those elements
  into the values we wish to collect.  This would avoid copying upon
  evaluation but make it more difficult to reason.

  Another example of compositionality is to consider a simple [47mloop$[0m
  over the [47mIN[0m target [47m(append a b)[0m.  There are 8 different ways you
  can do a simple [47mCOLLECT[0m over an [47m(append a b)[0m target,

    (loop$ for x in (append a b) collect (expr x))
    (loop$ for x on (append a b) collect (expr x))
    (loop$ for x in (append a b) until (stop x) collect (expr x))
    (loop$ for x on (append a b) until (stop x) collect (expr x))
    (loop$ for x in (append a b) when (test x) collect (expr x))
    (loop$ for x on (append a b) when (test x) collect (expr x))
    (loop$ for x in (append a b) until (stop x) when (test x)
           collect (expr x))
    (loop$ for x on (append a b) until (stop x) when (test x)
           collect (expr x))

  Similarly, there are 8 ways to [47mSUM[0m over an [47m(append a b)[0m target, 8
  ways to [47mAPPEND[0m over an [47m(append a b)[0m, and 4 ways each to [47mALWAYS[0m or
  [47mTHEREIS[0m over an [47m(append a b)[0m target.  Thus, there are 32 different
  simple [47mloop$[0ms over [47m(append a b)[0m.  And you can arrange to distribute
  the simple [47mloop$[0m over the [47m(append a b)[0m with just seven rewrite
  rules.

    (equal (collect$ fn (append a b))
           (append (collect$ fn a)
                   (collect$ fn b)))

    (equal (sum$ fn (append a b))
           (+ (sum$ fn a)
              (sum$ fn b)))

    (equal (always$ fn (append a b))
           (and (always$ fn a)
                (always$ fn b)))

    (equal (thereis$ fn (append a b))
           (or (thereis$ fn a)
               (thereis$ fn b)))

    (equal (append$ fn (append a b))
           (append (append$ fn a)
                   (append$ fn b)))

    (equal (until$ fn (append a b))
           (if (exists$ fn a)
               (until$ fn a)
               (append a (until$ fn b))))

    (equal (when$ fn (append a b))
           (append (when$ fn a)
                   (when$ fn b)))

  Thus, you can reason about [47mWHEN[0m and [47mUNTIL[0m clauses without having to
  consider how they are used in the superior [47mloop$[0m expression.

  To deal with fancy [47mloop$[0m you need seven more rewrite rules, one for
  each fancy [47mloop$[0m scion.  But since every simple [47mloop$[0m can be
  expressed by an appropriate use of fancy scions, we could have
  translated every [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m to fancy scions.  We chose to break
  compositionality here because we think simple [47mloop$[0ms are most
  common and wanted to keep their semantics simple.  I.e., we
  compromised.

  By the way, if you want the prover to convert every simple scion to
  its fancy counterpart you could prove rewrite rules like that
  below.

    (defthm convert-collect$-to-collect$+
      (implies (ok-fnp fn)
               (equal (collect$ fn lst)
                      (collect$+ `(lambda (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
                                    (,fn (car loop$-ivars)))
                                 nil
                                 (loop$-as (list lst)))))
      :hints ((\"[1]Goal\"
               :expand ((tamep (cons fn '(x)))
                        (tamep (cons fn '((car loop$-ivars))))))))")
