          "See [system-utilities].")
 (FORMULA
  (HISTORY WORLD ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "The formula of a name or [rune]

  The ACL2 system function, [47mformula[0m, returns the [term] associated with
  a given [rune] or symbolic name, returning [47mnil[0m if there is no such
  term.  Note that a non-[47mnil[0m result will be an ACL2 ``translated''
  term (see [term]).  Most ACL2 users probably will have no reason to
  know about this function.  But here we document this function for
  those who write system-level tools, since they might find this
  interface to the ACL2 logical [world] to be useful.

  When ACL2 is given a [47m:use[0m or [47m:by[0m hint, it looks for the [term] stored
  in the ACL2 logical [world] that is associated with the name given
  in the hint, which is a symbol or a [rune].  (See
  [lemma-instance].)  The utility used to find that term is [47mformula[0m,
  which ACL2 invokes as follows.

    (formula x t wrld)   ; for :use hints
    (formula x nil wrld) ; for :by hints

  The second argument can affect whether or not to use a normalized
  version of the term (in particular, a definition body) associated
  with [47mx[0m; see [normalize].  The value is [47mt[0m for [47m:use[0m [hints] because
  normalizing a term simplifies it, which is often desirable.  But
  for a [47m:by[0m hint, the non-normalized version of the term is used in
  order to increase the chance that the necessary subsumption test
  will succeed.  Even if the second argument is [47mt[0m, normalization
  might not take place.  In the unlikely case that you need to know
  more precisely the effect of supplying [47mt[0m, see the source code for
  [47mformula[0m.

  Here are some examples.  Note that [47m(w state)[0m returns the current ACL2
  logical [world].  First let us submit a few [events].

    (defun f1 (x) (cons 3 x))
    (defun f2 (x y) (implies x y))
    (defthm one-rule
      (and (equal (* 2 y) (+ y y))
           (equal (* 3 y) (+ y y y))))
    (defthm two-rules
      t
      :rule-classes ((:rewrite :corollary (equal (* 2 y) (+ y y)))
                     (:rewrite :corollary (equal (* 3 y) (+ y y y)))))

  Then:

    ACL2 !>(formula 'f1 nil (w state))
    (EQUAL (F1 X) (CONS '3 X))
    ACL2 !>(formula 'f1 t (w state))
    (EQUAL (F1 X) (CONS '3 X))
    ACL2 !>(formula 'f2 nil (w state))
    (EQUAL (F2 X Y) (IMPLIES X Y))
    ACL2 !>(formula 'f2 t (w state))
    (EQUAL (F2 X Y)
           (IF X (IF Y 'T 'NIL) 'T))
    ACL2 !>(formula 'one-rule nil (w state))
    (IF (EQUAL (BINARY-* '2 Y) (BINARY-+ Y Y))
        (EQUAL (BINARY-* '3 Y)
               (BINARY-+ Y (BINARY-+ Y Y)))
        'NIL)
    ACL2 !>(equal (formula 'one-rule nil (w state)) (formula 'one-rule t (w state)))
    T
    ACL2 !>(formula 'two-rules nil (w state))
    'T
    ACL2 !>(formula 'two-rules t (w state))
    'T
    ACL2 !>(formula '(:rewrite two-rules . 1) nil (w state))
    (EQUAL (BINARY-* '2 Y) (BINARY-+ Y Y))
    ACL2 !>(formula '(:rewrite two-rules . 2) nil (w state))
    (EQUAL (BINARY-* '3 Y)
           (BINARY-+ Y (BINARY-+ Y Y)))
    ACL2 !>(formula 'no-such-rule nil (w state))
    NIL
    ACL2 !>")
 (FORWARD-CHAINING
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make a rule to forward chain when a certain trigger arises

  See [rule-classes] for a general discussion of rule classes,
  including how they are used to build rules from formulas and a
  discussion of the various keywords in a rule class description.

    Examples:

    (defthm p-and-r-forward           ; When (p a) appears in a formula to be
     (implies (and (p x) (r x))       ; simplified, try to establish (p a) and
              (q (f x)))              ; (r a) and, if successful, add (q (f a))
     :rule-classes :forward-chaining) ; to the known assumptions.

    (defthm p-and-r-forward           ; as above with most defaults filled in
      (implies (and (p x) (r x))
               (q (f x)))
      :rule-classes ((:forward-chaining :trigger-terms ((p x))
                                        :corollary (implies (and (p x) (r x))
                                                            (q (f x)))
                                        :match-free :all)))

  To specify the triggering terms provide a non-empty list of terms as
  the value of the [47m:trigger-terms[0m field of the rule class object.

    General Form:
    Any theorem, provided an acceptable triggering term exists.

  The structure of this documentation is as follows.  First we give a
  brief overview of forward chaining and contrast it to backchaining
  (rewriting).  Then we lay out the syntactic restrictions on
  [47m:forward-chaining[0m rules.  Then we give more details about the
  process and point to a tool to assist you in debugging your
  [47m:forward-chaining[0m rules.

  [3mOverview and When to Use Forward Chaining[0m

  Forward chaining is performed as part of the simplification process:
  before the goal is rewritten a [3mcontext[0m is established.  The context
  tells the theorem prover what may be assumed during rewriting, in
  particular, to establish hypotheses of rewrite rules.  Forward
  chaining is used to extend the context before rewriting begins.
  For example, the [47m:forward-chaining[0m rule [47m(implies (p x) (p1 x))[0m
  would add [47m(p1 A)[0m to the context, where [47mA[0m is some term, if [47m(p A)[0m is
  already in the context.

  Forward chaining and backchaining are duals.  If a rewrite rule
  requires that [47m(p1 A)[0m be established and [47m(p A)[0m is known, it could be
  done either by making [47m(implies (p x) (p1 x))[0m a [47m:forward-chaining[0m
  rule or a [47m:rewrite[0m rule.  Which should you choose?

  As a rule of thumb, if a conclusion like [47m(p1 A)[0m is expected to be
  widely needed, it is better to derive it via forward chaining
  because then it is available ``for free'' during the rewriting
  after paying the one-time cost of forward chaining.  Alternatively,
  if [47m(p1 A)[0m is a rather special hypothesis of key importance to only
  a few rewrite rules, it is best to derive it only when needed
  through backchaining.  Thus forward chaining is pro-active and
  backward chaining (rewriting) is reactive.

  The following example illustrates that forward chaining may be
  indispensable in a scenario with [free-variables], whose handling
  is quite weak with rewrite rules.

    (defstub p0 (x) t)
    (defstub p1 (x) t)
    (defstub p2 (y) t)
    (defaxiom p0-implies-p1
      (implies (p0 x)
               (p1 x))
      :rule-classes :forward-chaining)
    (defaxiom p1-implies-p2
      (implies (p1 x)
               (p2 y)))
    ; ACL2 proves the following, but only because p0-implies-p1 is a
    ; :forward-chaining rule.  If p0-implies-p1 is instead a :rewrite
    ; rule, then the proof fails for the following event.
    (thm (implies (p0 x)
                  (p2 y)))

  [3mSyntactic Restrictions[0m

  Forward chaining rules are generated from the corollary term (see
  [rule-classes]) as follows.  First, every [47m[let][0m expression is
  expanded away (hence, so is every [47m[let*][0m and [47m[lambda][0m expression),
  and trivial ``guard holders'' are removed; see [guard-holders].  If
  the resulting term has the form [47m(implies hyp concl)[0m, then [47mconcl[0m is
  treated as a conjunction, with one forward chaining rule with
  hypothesis [47mhyp[0m created for each conjunct.  In the other case, where
  the corollary term is not an [47m[implies][0m, we process it as we process
  the conclusion in the first case.

  Note that unlike rewrite rules, a nested implication is not folded
  into a single implication.  Consider for example the following
  term.

    (implies (p1 x)
             (implies (p2 x)
                      (p3 x)))

  Although this term is parsed for a rewrite rule as [47m(implies (and (p1
  x) (p2 x)) (p3 x))[0m, that is not the case when this term is parsed
  for a forward-chaining rule, in which case [47m(p1 x)[0m is treated as the
  hypothesis and [47m(implies (p2 x) (p3 x))[0m is treated as the
  conclusion.

  The [47m:trigger-terms[0m field of a [47m:forward-chaining[0m rule class object
  should be a non-empty list of terms, if provided, and should have
  certain properties described below.  If the [47m:trigger-terms[0m field is
  not provided, it defaults to the singleton list containing the
  ``atom'' of the first hypothesis of the formula.  (The atom of [47m(not
  x)[0m is [47mx[0m; the atom of any other term is the term itself.)  If there
  are no hypotheses and no [47m:trigger-terms[0m were provided, an error is
  caused.

  A triggering term is acceptable if it is not a variable, a quoted
  constant, a lambda application, a [47m[let][0m- (or [47m[let*][0m-) expression,
  or a [47m[not][0m-expression, and every variable symbol in the conclusion
  of the theorem either occurs in the hypotheses or occurs in the
  trigger.

  [3mMore Details about Forward Chaining[0m

  [47m:Forward-chaining[0m rules are used by the simplifier [3mbefore[0m it begins
  to rewrite the literals of the goal.  (Forward chaining is thus
  carried out from scratch for each goal.)  If any term in the goal
  is an instance of a trigger of some forward chaining rule, we try
  to establish the hypotheses of that forward chaining theorem (from
  the negation of the goal).  To relieve a hypothesis we only use
  type reasoning (see [TYPE-REASONING]), evaluation of ground terms,
  and presence among our known assumptions.  We do not use rewriting.
  So-called free variables in hypotheses are treated specially; see
  [free-variables].  If all hypotheses are relieved, and certain
  heuristics approve of the newly derived conclusion, we add the
  instantiated conclusion to our known assumptions.  Since this might
  introduce new terms into the assumptions, forward chaining is
  repeated.  Heuristic approval of each new addition is necessary to
  avoid infinite looping as would happen with the rule [47m(implies (p x)
  (p (f x)))[0m, which might otherwise forward chain from [47m(p A)[0m to [47m(p (f
  A))[0m to [47m(p (f (f A)))[0m, etc.

  [3mCaution[0m.  Forward chaining does not actually add terms to the goals
  displayed during proof attempts.  Instead, it extends an associated
  [3mcontext[0m, called ``assumptions'' in the preceding paragraph, that
  ACL2 builds from the goal currently being proved.  (For insiders:
  forward chaining extends the [47m[type-alist][0m.)  The context starts out
  with ``obvious'' consequences of the negation of the goal.  For
  example, if the goal is

    (implies (and (p A) (q (f A)))
             (c A))

  then the context notes that [47m(p A)[0m and [47m(q (f A))[0m are non-[47mnil[0m and [47m(c A)[0m
  is [47mnil[0m.  Forward chaining is then used to expand the context.  For
  example, if a forward chaining rule has [47m(f x)[0m as a trigger term and
  has body [47m(implies (p x) (r (f x)))[0m, then the context is extended by
  binding [47m(r (f A))[0m to non-[47mnil[0m, provided the heuristics approve of
  this extension.  Note however that since [47m(r (f A))[0m is put into the
  context, not the goal, you will not see it in the goal formula.
  Furthermore, the assumption added to the context is just the
  instantiation of the conclusion of the rule, with no simplification
  or rewriting applied.  Thus, for example, if it contains an enabled
  non-recursive function symbol it is unlikely ever to match a
  (rewritten) term arising during subsequent simplification of the
  goal.

  However, forward-chaining does support the linear arithmetic
  reasoning package.  For example, suppose that forward-chaining puts
  [47m(< (f x) (g x))[0m into the context.  Then this inequality also goes
  into the linear arithmetic database, together with suitable
  instances of linear lemmas whose trigger term is a call of [47mg[0m.  See
  [linear].

  Debugging [47m:forward-chaining[0m rules can be difficult since their
  effects are not directly visible on the goal being simplified.
  Tools are available to help you discover what forward chaining has
  occurred see [forward-chaining-reports].


Subtopics

  [Case-split]
      Like force but immediately splits the top-level goal on the
      hypothesis

  [Forward-chaining-reports]
      To see reports about the forward chaining process")
 (FORWARD-CHAINING-REPORTS
  (FORWARD-CHAINING DEBUGGING)
  "To see reports about the forward chaining process

  Debugging forward-chaining rules can be hard because their effects
  are not directly visible on the goal.  In this documentation we
  tell you how to get reports on the forward chaining activity
  occurring in your proof attempts.  This documentation is written in
  several parts.  The first part is an introduction for the
  first-time user of forward chaining reports.  The next two parts
  describe how to read reports.  The last part describes how to
  monitor forward chaining activity only for selected runes, etc.  We
  recommend the new user of these reports read everything!

  [3mA Quick Introduction to Forward Chaining Reports[0m

  Caution: The reporting mechanism maintains some state, and if you
  have already used forward chaining reporting in a session, the
  directions below may not work as advertised!  To return to the
  default forward chaining reporting state, execute this form at the
  top level:

    (reset-fc-reporting)

  You can get a report about all forward chaining activity in
  subsequent proofs by doing:

    (set-fc-criteria t)

  Options will be discussed later that allow you to monitor the
  activity caused by particular [47m:forward-chaining[0m rules or terms.

  Then do a proof that is expected to cause some forward chaining.  In
  the proof output you will see lines like this:

    (Forward Chaining on behalf of PREPROCESS-CLAUSE:  (FC-Report 1))

  This is the only difference you should see in the proof output.

  After the proof attempt has terminated, you can execute:

    (fc-report k)

  for any [47mk[0m printed during the immediately preceding proof attempt.
  That will print a much longer report describing the activity that
  occurred during the [47mk[0mth use of forward chaining in that proof
  attempt.

  If you want to see these reports in real time (embedded in the proof
  output), do this before invoking the prover:

    (set-fc-report-on-the-fly t)

  Collecting the data used to generate these reports slows down the
  prover.  If you no longer wish to see such reports, do

    (set-fc-criteria nil)

  [3mHow To Read FC Reports[0m

  The report printed by [47m(fc-report k)[0m is of the form:

    Forward Chaining Report [3mk[0m:
    Caller: [3mtoken[0m
    Clause: ([3mlit1[0m [3mlit2[0m ... [3mlitn[0m)
    Number of Rounds: [3mm[0m
    Contradictionp: [3mbool[0m
    Activations: ([3mact1[0m [3mact2[0m ...)

  This report means that the [3mk[0mth use of forward chaining in the most
  recent proof attempt was done on behalf of [3mtoken[0m (see below).  The
  initial context (set of assumptions) consisted of the negations of
  the literals listed in the [clause] shown and the initial candidate
  trigger terms are all those appearing in that clause.  This
  invocation of forward chaining proceeded to do [3mm[0m rounds of
  successive extensions of the initial context and ultimately either
  reached a contradiction ([3mbool[0m = [47mT[0m) or returned an extended context
  ([3mbool[0m = [47mNIL[0m).  Note that reaching a contradiction from the
  negations of all the literals in a clause is ``good'' because it
  means the clause is true.  The report concludes with the final
  status of all the forward chaining rules fired during the process.
  We explain how to read one of these activation reports in the next
  section.

  Forward chaining is done on behalf of many proof techniques in the
  system.  Each is associated with a [3mtoken[0m.  The main proof technique
  that uses forward chaining is [47mSIMPLIFY-CLAUSE[0m.  This is the call of
  forward chaining that sets up the context used by the rewriter to
  relieve hypotheses during backchaining.  Another common caller of
  forward chaining is [47mPREPROCESS-CLAUSE[0m, the first process in the
  ACL2 waterfall (see [hints-and-the-waterfall]).  Forward chaining
  often proves ``near propositional'' goals (those depending just on
  boolean implications between basic predicates).  Other tokens you
  may see include [47mINDUCT[0m, which uses forward chaining to set up a
  context for applying [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m rules, and the definitional
  principle (and related utilities such as [47m[verify-termination][0m and
  [47m[verify-guards][0m) which uses forward chaining during the
  construction of both measure conjectures and guard conjectures.
  When used this way, the [3mtoken[0m is [47mdefun-or-guard-verification[0m.

  [3mHow to Read Activation Reports[0m

  The forward chaining report concludes with a list of activation
  reports.

    Activations: ([3mact1[0m [3mact2[0m ...)

  Each [3macti[0m is of the form:

    ([3mrune[0m
     (:TRIGGER [3minst-trig[0m)
     ((:UNIFY-SUBST [3msubst[0m)
      (:DISPOSITION [3moutcome-part1[0m [3moutcome-part2[0m [3minst-term[0m))
     ...)

  where the [47m...[0m indicates that the rest of the report consists of more
  of those tuples listing a [47m:UNIFY-SUBST[0m and [47m:DISPOSITION[0m.  We call
  each tuple a [3mdisposition[0m of the activation and each disposition
  describes a substitution [3msubst[0m identifying the final instantiation
  of the rule and how the activation fared.  Suppose there are [3mn[0m
  dispositions.  (If the rule in question contains no free variables,
  [3mn[0m will be 1.)

  This activation report means that during the forward chaining process
  in question, the [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m [rune] [3mrune[0m was fired due to
  the presence in the evolving context of the trigger term [3minst-trig[0m.
  (Note that [3minst-trig[0m is an instantiation of the trigger term of the
  named rule.  That is, the variable symbols you see in [3minst-trig[0m are
  those of the clause printed in the forward chaining report.)  The
  activation of [3mrune[0m by [3minst-trig[0m proceeded to split [3mn[0m ways as
  different choices were made for the [free-variables] occurring
  among the hypotheses.  Each of those [3mn[0m choices gave rise to a
  different substitution [3msubst[0m, and each succeeded or failed as
  described by the corresponding [47m:DISPOSITION[0m.

  The [47m:DISPOSITION[0m of an activation is described in three parts,
  [3moutcome-part1[0m, [3moutcome-part2[0m, and [3minst-term[0m.  [3mOutcome-part1[0m is
  either [47mSUCCESS[0m or [47mBLOCKED[0m, meaning that the instance given by [3msubst[0m
  either succeeded in the sense that all of its instantiated
  hypotheses were found in the context, or failed because some
  instantiated hypothesis was not found.

  If [47moutcome-part1[0m is [47mSUCCESS[0m then [3minst-term[0m is the instantiated
  conclusion produced by the rule.  [3mOutcome-part2[0m is [47mAPPROVED[0m if the
  instantiated conclusion was acceptable to our heuristics designed
  to prevent looping and not already known in the evolving context.
  [3mOutcome-part2[0m is [47mREJECTED[0m if the instantiated conclusion was not
  approved by our heuristics.  [3mOutcome-part2[0m is [47mREDUNDANT[0m if the
  instantiated conclusion was approved by the heuristics but already
  known true in the current evolving context.  If [47mAPPROVED[0m, the truth
  of the instantiated conclusion is added to the evolving context.
  Otherwise, it is not.

  If [47moutcome-part1[0m is [47mBLOCKED[0m then [47moutcome-part2[0m is one of three
  possible things: [47mFALSE[0m, in which case [3minst-term[0m is an instantiated
  hypothesis of the rule that is assumed false in the current
  context, [47mUNRELIEVED-HYP[0m, in which case [3minst-term[0m is an instantiated
  hypothesis whose truth value is not determined by the context, or
  [47mUNRELIEVED-HYP-FREE[0m, in which case [3minst-term[0m is an oddly
  instantiated hypothesis whose truth value is not determined by the
  context and which also contains free variables.  In the last case,
  the ``odd'' instantiation was by the substitution [3msubst[0m but
  extended so that free variables in the hypothesis are renamed to
  start with the prefix [47mUNBOUND-FREE-[0m to draw your attention to them.

  Note: All of the terms printed in the report are instantiated with
  the relevant unifying substitution (possibly extended to bind free
  variables).

  [3mSpecifying the Tracking Criteria[0m

  During a proof attempt, the forward chaining module stores
  information about the activations satisfying certain criteria.  The
  [3mcriteria[0m is a list of triples.  Each triple consists of a forward
  chaining rune, an instantiated trigger term, and an instantiated
  conclusion to watch for.  However, any or all of the components of
  such a triple may be [47mt[0m and that is given special significance.

  An activation [47msatisfies[0m a criteria if it satisfies at least one of
  the triples.  An activation satisfies a triple if it satisfies all
  three of the components.  Every activation satisfies the component
  [47mt[0m.  An activation satisfies a rune if the activation describes a
  firing of the named rule.  An activation satisfies an instantiated
  trigger term if the activation was created by that trigger being
  present in the context.  An activation satisfies an instantiated
  conclusion if the activation [3mcould[0m produce the instantiated
  conclusion (with the right choice of any free variables).

  Thus, the criteria is interpreted as a disjunction of conjunctions,
  making it possible to track a specific set of runes, triggers, and
  conclusions.

  For example, here is a triple that might appear in the criteria:

    ((:FORWARD-CHAINING ALISTP-FORWARD-TO-TRUE-LISTP)
     t
     t).

  This triple would cause every activation of the given rule to be
  tracked.  However, the triple

    ((:FORWARD-CHAINING ALISTP-FORWARD-TO-TRUE-LISTP)
     (ALISTP (MAKE-BINDINGS VARS (TOP-N (OP1 INST) (STACK S))))
     t)

  would only track activations of that rule fired by the specific term
  shown as the second element of the triple.  Furthermore

    (t
     (ALISTP (MAKE-BINDINGS VARS (TOP-N (OP1 INST) (STACK S))))
     t)

  would track any forward chaining rule triggered by that term, and

    (t
     t
     (TRUE-LISTP (MAKE-BINDINGS VARS (TOP-N (OP1 INST) (STACK S)))))

  would track any rule fired by any trigger that might lead to the
  specific term given as the third component above.

  Note: The condition on how an activation satisfies an instantiated
  conclusion is a little subtle.  Consider the activation of the
  forward chaining rule

    (implies (and (symbol-listp x)
                  (equal (len x) (len y)))
             (true-listp (make-bindings x y)))

  triggered by [47m(SYMBOL-LISTP VARS)[0m arising in the current context.
  This activation [3mcould[0m produce the specific conclusion shown in the
  last triple above, if it just happened that [47m(TOP-N (OP1 INST)
  (STACK S))[0m were chosen as the binding of the free variable [47my[0m.
  Thus, the activation of this rule triggered by [47m(SYMBOL-LISTP VARS)[0m
  satisfies the last triple above.

  Observe that the triple

    (t t t)

  is satisfied by every activation of any rule by any trigger term
  producing any conclusion.

  The function [47mset-fc-criteria[0m sets the criteria describing which
  activations are to be tracked.  For example, if you execute:

    (set-fc-criteria ((:FORWARD-CHAINING LEMMA1)
                      t t)
                     ((:FORWARD-CHAINING LEMMA2)
                      (ALISTP (BASIC-MAPPER A B))
                      t)
                     (t t (TRUE-LISTP (DLT D)))),

  the system would track all activations of the forward-chaining rule
  [47mLEMMA1[0m, plus those activations of forward-chaining rule [47mLEMMA2[0m
  triggered by the term given in the second triple, plus any
  activation of any rule that might derive [47m(TRUE-LISTP (DLT D))[0m.

  Because criteria generally mention variable symbols used in a
  specific conjecture, it is probably best to reconsider your
  criteria every time you want to track forward chaining.

  If the criteria is [47mnil[0m, then nothing is tracked.  Setting the
  criteria to [47mnil[0m is the way you turn off tracking and reporting of
  forward chaining activity.  You may do this either by
  [47m(set-fc-criteria)[0m or by [47m(set-fc-criteria nil)[0m.  (Technically the
  second form is an odd use of [47mset-fc-criteria[0m, which expects any
  supplied arguments to be triples; if the ``triple'' [47mnil[0m is the only
  one supplied, we take it to mean that the entire criteria should be
  [47mnil[0m.)

  To track every forward chaining activation you may set the criteria
  with either [47m(set-fc-criteria (t t t))[0m or use the abbreviation
  [47m(set-fc-criteria t)[0m.

  If, when you read a forward chaining report, you see no mention of an
  activation you have in mind, e.g., of a certain rune or deriving a
  certain conclusion, and you have set the criteria correctly, then
  the activation never happened.  (This is akin to using [47m:[0m[47m[brr][0m and
  [47m:[0m[47m[monitor][0m to monitor the application of a rewrite rule and then
  seeing no interactive break.)

  For some relevant functions to help you manage criteria and when the
  full reports are printed see [47m[fc-report][0m, [47m[show-fc-criteria][0m,
  [47m[set-fc-criteria][0m, [47m[reset-fc-reporting][0m, and
  [47m[set-fc-report-on-the-fly][0m.


