    ;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
    ;;; Example 3
    ;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

  The next pair of examples illustrates so-called ``binding
  hypotheses'' (see [free-variables]) and explores some of their
  subtleties.  The first shows binding hypotheses in action on a
  simple example.  The second shows how binding hypotheses interact
  with equivalence relations and explains the role of
  [47m[double-rewrite][0m.

  Our first example sets up a theory with two user-supplied rewrite
  rules, one of which has a binding hypothesis.  Below we explain how
  that binding hypothesis contributes to the proof.

    ; Define some unary functions.
    (defun f (x) (declare (ignore x)) t)
    (defun g (x) x)
    (defun h (x) x)
    (defun k (x) x)

    ; Prove some simple lemmas.  Note the binding hypothesis in g-rewrite.
    (defthm f-k-h
      (f (k (h x))))
    (defthm g-rewrite
           (implies (and (equal y (k x)) ; binding hypothesis
                         (f y))
                    (equal (g x) y)))

    ; Restrict to a theory that includes the above lemmas but avoids the above
    ; definitions.
    (in-theory (union-theories (theory 'minimal-theory)
                               '(f-k-h g-rewrite)))

    ; Prove a theorem.
    (thm (equal (g (h a)) (k (h a))))

  Let us look at how ACL2 uses the above binding hypothesis in the
  proof of the preceding [47mthm[0m form.  The rewriter considers the term
  [47m(g (h a))[0m and finds a match with the left-hand side of the rule
  [47mg-rewrite[0m, binding [47mx[0m to [47m(h a)[0m.  The first hypothesis binds [47my[0m to the
  result of rewriting [47m(k x)[0m in the current context, where the
  variable [47mx[0m is bound to the term [47m(h a)[0m; thus [47my[0m is bound to [47m(k (h
  a))[0m.  The second hypothesis, [47m(f y)[0m, is then rewritten under this
  binding, and the result is [47mt[0m by application of the rewrite rule
  [47mf-k-h[0m.  The rule [47mg-rewrite[0m is then applied under the
  already-mentioned binding of [47mx[0m to [47m(h a)[0m.  This rule application
  triggers a recursive rewrite of the right-hand side of [47mg-rewrite[0m,
  which is [47my[0m, in a context where [47my[0m is bound (as discussed above) to
  [47m(k (h a))[0m.  The result of this rewrite is that same term, [47m(k (h
  a))[0m.  The original call of [47mequal[0m then trivially rewrites to [47mt[0m.

  We move on now to our second example, which is similar but involves a
  user-defined equivalence relation.  You may find it helpful to
  review [47m:equivalence[0m rules; see [equivalence].

  Recall that when a hypothesis is a call of an equivalence relation
  other than [47mequal[0m, the second argument must be a call of
  [47m[double-rewrite][0m in order for the hypothesis to be treated as a
  binding hypothesis.  That is indeed the case below; an explanation
  follows.

    ; Define an equivalence relation.
    (defun my-equiv (x y) (equal x y))
    (defequiv my-equiv) ; introduces rule MY-EQUIV-IS-AN-EQUIVALENCE

    ; Define some unary functions
    (defun f (x) (declare (ignore x)) t)
    (defun g (x) x)
    (defun h1 (x) x)
    (defun h2 (x) x)

    ; Prove some simple lemmas.  Note the binding hypothesis in lemma-3.
    (defthm lemma-1
      (my-equiv (h1 x) (h2 x)))
    (defthm lemma-2
      (f (h2 x)))
    (defthm lemma-3
           (implies (and (my-equiv y (double-rewrite x)) ; binding hypothesis
                         (f y))
                    (equal (g x) y)))

    ; Restrict to a theory that includes the above lemmas but avoids the above
    ; definitions.
    (in-theory (union-theories (theory 'minimal-theory)
                               '(lemma-1 lemma-2 lemma-3
                                         my-equiv-is-an-equivalence)))

    ; Prove a theorem.
    (thm (equal (g (h1 a)) (h2 a)))

  The proof succeeds much as in the first example, but the following
  observation is key: when ACL2 binds [47my[0m upon considering the first
  hypothesis of [47mlemma-3[0m, it rewrites the term [47m(double-rewrite x)[0m in a
  context where it need only preserve the equivalence relation
  [47mmy-equiv[0m.  At this point, [47mx[0m is bound by applying [47mlemma-3[0m to the
  term [47m(g (h1 a))[0m; so, [47mx[0m is bound to [47m(h1 a)[0m.  The rule [47mlemma-1[0m then
  applies to rewrite this occurrence of [47mx[0m to [47m(h2 a)[0m, but only because
  it suffices to preserve [47mmy-equiv[0m.  Thus [47my[0m is ultimately bound to
  [47m(h2 a)[0m, and the proof succeeds as one would expect.

  If we tweak the above example slightly by disabling the user's
  [equivalence] [rune], then the proof of the [47m[thm][0m form fails
  because the above rewrite of [47m(double-rewrite x)[0m is done in a
  context where it no longer suffices to preserve [47mmy-equiv[0m as we dive
  into the second argument of [47mmy-equiv[0m in the first hypothesis of
  [47mlemma-3[0m; so, [47mlemma-1[0m does not apply this time.

    (in-theory (union-theories (theory 'minimal-theory)
                               '(lemma-1 lemma-2 lemma-3)))

    ; Proof fails in this case!
    (thm (equal (g (h1 a)) (h2 a)))")
 (FREE-VARIABLES-TYPE-PRESCRIPTION
  (FREE-VARIABLES)
  "Matching for free variable in [type-prescription] rules

  We assume familiarity with the issue of dealing with free variables
  in hypotheses; see [free-variables].

  By default, starting with Version 4.3, ACL2 attempts all possible
  matches for free variables.  Consider the following example.

    (defstub f1 (x) t)
    (defstub f2 (x y) t)
    (defstub f3 (y) t)

    (defaxiom f1-prop
      (implies (and (f2 x y) ; <-- y is free in this hypothesis
                    (f3 y))
               (f1 x))       ; <-- (f1 x) is the type-term (type is `non-nil')
      :rule-classes :type-prescription)

    ; Succeeds:
    (thm (implies (and (f2 a b)
                       (f3 b))
                  (f1 a)))

    ; The following fails unless we try more than one match for free variables in
    ; hypotheses.
    (thm (implies (and (f2 a b)
                       (f2 a c)
                       (f2 a d)
                       (f3 b))
                  (f1 a)))

  There may be times when you want to match only the first free
  variable.  In that case, you can write a function of two arguments,
  the [3mtype-prescription[0m [rune] being applied and the current ACL2
  world, that prohibits multiple matching for those times.  Your
  function is then `attached' to the built-in constrained function,
  [47moncep-ts[0m.  The following examples are intended to explain how this
  works.

  First, let us disallow all multiple matching of free variables (i.e.,
  implement the behavior often referred to as ``[47m:match-free :once[0m'';
  see [free-variables]).

    (defun oncep-tp-always (rune wrld)
      (declare (ignore rune wrld)
               (xargs :mode :logic :guard t))
      t)

    (defattach-system oncep-tp oncep-tp-always)

  The second [47mthm[0m form above will now fail, because only one
  free-variable match is permitted for the first hypothesis of rule
  [47mf1-prop[0m above.

  Now suppose that instead, we want to disallow multiple matches for
  free variables in hypotheses of type-prescription rules [3mexcept[0m for
  the rule [47mf1-prop[0m above.  With the following events, the second [47mthm[0m
  form above once again succeeds.

    (defun oncep-tp-always-except-f1-prop (rune wrld)
      (declare (ignore wrld)
               (xargs :mode :logic :guard (and (consp rune)
                                               (consp (cdr rune))
                                               (symbolp (cadr rune)))))
      (not (eq (base-symbol rune) 'f1-prop)))

    (defattach-system oncep-tp oncep-tp-always-except-f1-prop)

  In general, your [47m[defattach][0m event will attach a function symbol to
  [47moncep-tp[0m.  The [guard] of that function symbol must be implied by
  the guard of [47moncep-tp[0m:

    ACL2 !>:args oncep-tp

    Function         ONCEP-TP
    Formals:         (RUNE WRLD)
    Signature:       (ONCEP-TP * *)
                     => *
    Guard:           (AND (PLIST-WORLDP WRLD)
                          (CONSP RUNE)
                          (CONSP (CDR RUNE))
                          (SYMBOLP (CADR RUNE)))
    Guards Verified: T
    Defun-Mode:      :logic
    Type:            built-in (or unrestricted)

     ONCEP-TP
    ACL2 !>")
 (FREE_VARIABLES_IN_TOP-LEVEL_INPUT
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "Free Variables in Top-Level Input

    ACL2 !>[31;1m(equal (app (app a b) c)[0m
                  [31;1m(app a (app b c))))[0m

    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  Global variables, such as C, B, and A, are
    not allowed. See :DOC ASSIGN and :DOC @.

  ACL2 does not allow ``global variables'' in top-level input.  There
  is no ``top-level binding environment'' to give meaning to these
  variables.

  Thus, expressions involving no variables can generally be evaluated,

    ACL2 !>[31;1m(equal (app (app '(1 2) '(3 4)) '(5 6))[0m
                  [31;1m(app '(1 2) (app '(3 4) '(5 6))))[0m
    (1 2 3 4 5 6)

  but expressions containing variables cannot.

  There is an exception to this rule.  References to ``single-threaded
  objects'' may appear in top-level forms.  See [stobj] {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]).  A single-threaded object is an ACL2
  object, usually containing many fields, whose use is syntactically
  restricted so that it may be given as input only to certain
  functions and must be returned as output by certain functions.
  These restrictions allow single- threaded objects to be efficiently
  manipulated.  For example, only a single copy of the object
  actually exists, even though from a logical perspective one might
  expect the object to be ``copied on write.''

  The most commonly used single-threaded object in ACL2 is the ACL2
  system state, whose current value is always held in the variable
  [47m[state][0m {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]).

  ACL2 provides a way for you to use [47mstate[0m to save values of
  computations at the top-level and refer to them later.  See
  [assign] {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) and [@] {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]).")
 (FREQUENTLY-ASKED-QUESTIONS-BY-NEWCOMERS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Some questions newcomers frequently ask

  This FAQ is for people who've read through all the other sections of
  the tutorial introduction to the theorem prover (see
  [introduction-to-the-theorem-prover] and all the links from it that
  are not marked with the little warning sign (``{ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign])'').  Do not expect to understand our answers
  if you haven't taken the time to read through the tutorial.  In the
  answers below you will see more links into the hypertext reference
  manual.  While such links were marked ``{ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign])'' in the tutorial, they are not marked that
  way here.  When you enter the reference manual be prepared to
  explore and assemble a mini-course on the topic of interest, not a
  quick fix.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I find something in the [31;1mACL2 documentation[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  Try the
  ``Jump to'' or ``Search'' boxes at the {ACL2+Books Manual |
  https://acl2.org/doc/index.html}.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How does the theorem prover work?  [31;1mA[0m.  We really don't think you
  need to know much about the inner workings of the prover to become
  an effective user.  That doesn't mean the system is
  self-explanatory!  It means that stuff you need to learn is not how
  the theorem prover works but how to interact with it!  That is what
  [introduction-to-the-theorem-prover] is about.  However, if you
  want the most basic overview of the prover, see
  [architecture-of-the-prover].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mdefine a new function[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [defun].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mdefine a new predicate[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [defun].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mdefine a new relation[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [defun].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I define a [31;1mfunction or predicate that takes a varying
  number of arguments[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. You can't.  However, see [defmacro] to
  learn how to define a macro that takes a varying number of
  arguments and expands into an arbitrary term that you compute.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I define a [31;1mmacro that is sensitive to the state[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  You
  can't.  However, advanced users should consider [47m[make-event][0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I define [31;1mmutually recursive[0m functions?  [31;1mA[0m.  See
  [mutual-recursion].  However, you should realize that when two
  functions, say [47mf[0m and [47mg[0m, are mutually recursive, properties of [47mf[0m
  generally have to be stated simultaneously with properties of [47mg[0m,
  since inductive proofs about one function require inductive
  hypotheses about the other.  Furthermore, ACL2 does not know how to
  do inductions for mutually recursive functions and must be told.
  See [mutual-recursion-proof-example].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I declare the [31;1mtype signature of a function[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  You can't.
  ACL2 is a syntactically untyped language and all functions are
  defined on all objects in the ACL2 universe.  We recommend that the
  new user get used to this and only then explore the use of ACL2 to
  express and enforce a type regime.  In ACL2, the [3mguard[0m of a
  function is akin to the type signature of a function in typed
  languages.  However, ACL2 guards may be arbitrary terms, not just
  type conditions, they only concern the inputs to the function (not
  its result), and do not affect the axiom defining the function ---
  all functions are defined on every combination of objects.  You
  may, of course, prove theorems that establish that every function
  called in a definition or theorem is supplied with input satisfying
  its guards (which necessarily involves describe the outputs too).
  These formulas are called [3mguard conjectures[0m and the process of
  proving them is called [3mguard verification[0m.  Since guards are
  arbitrary ACL2 formulas, the ``type regimes'' one tends to enforce
  in ACL2 can be much for flexible and expressive than those in most
  programming languages.  However, that expressibility also means
  guard verification can be challenging (indeed undecidable).  On the
  other hand, if one limits oneself to simple type-like guards,
  lemmas can be proved that make most guard verification fully
  automatic and one can configure ACL2 to do guard verification
  automatically at [47mdefun[0m-time.  One may also delay guard verification
  until ``the right'' lemmas have been proved.  By doing guard
  verification one can make functions execute faster by allowing the
  code to avoid runtime checks.  This is especially valuable to
  industrial users who have large models used both in verification
  and as simulation engines for designed artifacts.  In addition,
  guard verification can give you the assurance that you are using
  your functions within their intended domains and hence is a form of
  functional specification and verification.  However, all these
  advantages aside, it is remarkably easy to drift away from the
  simplest type regimes and write a guard that raises deep
  mathematical problems.  New users should not try to confront these
  problems until they are comfortable with the theorem prover and
  with lemma development.  Therefore, we [31;1mstrongly[0m recommend that you
  forget about types and guards and get used to reasoning about total
  functions.  When you do decide to learn about them, be prepared for
  a complex story involving specification, execution efficiency, and
  proof management.  See [guard].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I tell [47mdefun[0m [31;1mwhat measure to use[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [xargs],
  specifically [47m:measure[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  I specified a measure that always returns a natural number but
  ACL2 is acting like it's not a natural number.  [31;1mA[0m.  There are two
  likely problems.  The most likely one is that your measure isn't
  really always a natural!  Suppose the formals of your [47mdefun[0m are [47mx[0m
  and [47my[0m and your measure is [47m(m x y)[0m.  Suppose the recursive calls of
  your function are protected by tests that ensure that [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are
  naturals.  Then you might assume [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are naturals in the
  measure.  But ACL2 has to prove [47m(o-p (m x y))[0m, where [47m[o-p][0m is the
  predicate that recognizes ordinals (and naturals are ordinals).
  Note that the theorem doesn't have any hypotheses!  You might
  intuitively think that your measure has to be an ordinal just under
  the conditions that lead to recursive calls.  That's not what ACL2
  enforces.  It has to be an ordinal, always.  So you must change
  your specified measure.  For example, consider wrapping [47m[nfix][0m
  around it or around its uses of [47mx[0m and [47my[0m to coerce those quantities
  to naturals.  The second most likely explanation is that your
  measure returns a natural, always, but ACL2 doesn't know that and
  it takes induction to prove.  This might happen if [47mm[0m involves some
  recursive functions.  In this case, prove [47m(natp (m x y))[0m before
  your [47mdefun[0m.  Perhaps you should consider making the [47mnatp[0m lemma a
  [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m lemma to make ACL2's typing algorithm aware of
  it.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I tell [47mdefun[0m [31;1mwhat well-founded relation to use[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See
  [xargs], specifically [47m:well-founded-relation[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I show that a [31;1mrelation is well-founded[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. Prove a theorem
  establishing that there is an order preserving embedding into the
  ordinals and store it with [47m:rule-classes[0m [47m:well-founded-relation[0m
  [well-founded-relation-rule].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  What is an [31;1mordinal[0m?  What does it mean to be [31;1mwell-founded[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.
  Ordinals are an extension of the natural numbers used to ensure
  that a process can't go on forever.  Like naturals, they can be
  added, multiplied, and exponentiated.  There is a sense of one
  ordinal being less than another.  Unlike the naturals, each of
  which is finite, the ordinals include infinite objects.  Now
  imagine ``standing'' on an ordinal and ``stepping'' to a smaller
  one.  Like the naturals, this ``walk down the ordinals'' can't go
  on forever, even if you start on an infinite ordinal.  That is
  because the ordinals are [3mwell-founded[0m.  See [47m[o-p][0m for more
  information about ordinals in ACL2 and about well-foundedness.  See
  [ordinals] for a deeper discussion and a discussion of books that
  can help configure ACL2 to reason about ordinals.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can provide [31;1mhints for the termination proofs[0m in [47mdefun[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See
  [xargs], specifically [47m:hints[0m (for the termination proofs) and
  [47m:guard-hints[0m (for the guard verification proofs).

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I define a [31;1mconstant[0m (something like a [31;1mglobal variable[0m)?  [31;1mA[0m.
  See [defconst].  But remember that as an applicative programming
  language, ACL2 does not have global variables!  You can define a
  symbol to have a fixed value and use the symbol sort of like a
  global variable in function definitions: you may refer to the value
  of the symbol in your functions without passing the variable in as
  formal parameter.  But you may not ever change the value of the
  symbol!

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I save the value of a top-level computation for future use?
  [31;1mA[0m. See [assign] and see [@].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I introduce [31;1mnew syntactic form[0m or [31;1mabbreviation[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See
  [defmacro].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How can create and modify an array?  [31;1mA[0m.  ACL2 is a functional
  language, so it is impossible to destructively modify an existing
  object; technically, all ``updates'' to objects must be implemented
  by ``copy-on-write'' semantics.  That said, ACL2 provides support
  for [arrays], provided you use them in a restricted way.  They give
  you constant-time access and change under the use restrictions.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I read from or write to a file?  How do I do IO?  [31;1mA[0m.  To
  manipulate files, your function must have [47m[state][0m as an argument,
  so you should read about the restrictions that imposes.  For
  input/output facilities, see [io].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I define a [31;1mstructure that can be destructively modified[0m?
  [31;1mA[0m. ACL2 is an [3mapplicative programming language[0m.  You can't modify
  objects arbitrarily!  You basically have to ``copy on write,''
  which means you construct new objects from old ones, making the
  changes you want in the new one.  If the [47mcar[0m of some object is [47m1[0m at
  one moment and [47m2[0m later, then the basic logical axiom [47m(car x)[0m = [47m(car
  x)[0m is violated!  However, if the only reference to the old object,
  e.g., [3mx[0m, was to pass it to the code that copied and ``changed'' it,
  then ACL2 can re-use the old object to produce the new one and the
  axioms would not object.  Such syntactic restrictions can make [3mx[0m a
  modifiable structure but they will impose a heavy burden on you as
  a programmer: if pass such an [3mx[0m to a function and the function
  modifies it, then you must pass [3mx[0m only to that function and you
  must return the modified value and use it henceforth.  Such objects
  are said to be [3msingle threaded[0m.  See [defstobj].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I write a universal quantifier?  An existential
  quantifier?  How can I say ``for all'' or ``there exists''?  [31;1mA[0m You
  can't literally write quantifiers.  But ACL2 has the power of full
  first order logic with quantification.  See [quantifiers].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I introduce an undefined or uninterpreted function symbol?
  Can I constrain it to have certain properties?  [31;1mA[0m.  See
  [encapsulate].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How can I hide a lemma?  I want to prove a lemma temporarily to
  use in another proof but I don't want the lemma around thereafter.
  [31;1mA[0m.  One way to get a similar effect is to prove the lemma and then
  disable it with an [47m(in-theory (disable ...))[0m event; see
  [in-theory].  Another way is to put the lemma and the theorem that
  needs it into an [47m[encapsulate][0m and wrap a [47m[local][0m around the lemma.

  [31;1mQ[0m. What is an [31;1mevent[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. An [3mevent[0m is a command that adds information
  to the ACL2 database (the ``logical world''), like [47mdefun[0m or [47mdefthm[0m.
  See [events].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I say that I [31;1mdo not want a rewrite rule[0m generated from a
  theorem?  [31;1mA[0m. The command

    (defthm [3mname[0m [3mformula[0m
            :rule-classes nil)

  will attempt to prove [3mformula[0m and, if successful, give [3mformula[0m the
  name [3mname[0m, which you may use in hints as a theorem, but it will
  build no rules from the formula.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I say that I want a formula to be [31;1mstored as a rewrite rule[0m?
  [31;1mA[0m. The command

    (defthm [3mname[0m [3mformula[0m)

  will attempt to prove [3mformula[0m and, if successful, it will give
  [3mformula[0m the name [3mname[0m and generate a rewrite rule from it, with the
  runic name [47m(:rewrite [0m[3mname[0m)].  It could happen that [3mformula[0m
  generates more than one rewrite rule, e.g., this happens if the
  conclusion is an [47mAND[0m.  In this case, each conjunctive branch
  through the conclusion generates a rule named [47m(:rewrite [0m[3mname[0m[47m . i)[0m,
  for [3mi[0m=1,2,...  For more details, see [rewrite].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I say that I want a formula to be [31;1mstored as a rewrite rule[0m
  [31;1mand some other kinds of rules[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. The command

    (defthm [3mname[0m [3mformula[0m
            :rule-classes ([3m:class1[0m ... [3mclassk[0m))

  will attempt to prove [3mformula[0m and, if successful, it will give
  [3mformula[0m the name [3mname[0m and generate a rule of each [3m:classi[0m
  specified.  Each [3m:classi[0m should either be a keyword, like [47m:REWRITE[0m
  or [47m:GENERALIZE[0m, naming a rule class (see [rule-classes]), or else
  should be a list that starts with a rule class and then lists the
  relevant modifiers.  Be sure to include [47m:REWRITE[0m among the rule
  classes if you want the formula to generate a rewrite rule.  It
  doesn't do that automatically if you explicitly specify
  [47m:rule-classes[0m!

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mrearrange[0m the shape of a formula before generating a rule
  from it?  [31;1mA[0m. See [rule-classes] and read about the [47m:corollary[0m
  modifier.

  [31;1mQ[0m. What is a [31;1mtype-prescription[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. ACL2 has an algorithm for
  determining the type of object returned by a term, where a type is
  one of the Common Lisp primitive datatypes such as natural,
  integer, Boolean, cons, true-listp, etc.  Rules provided by you can
  influence this algorithm.  See [type-prescription].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do [31;1mrewrite rules work[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. Re-read the tutorial sections:
  [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-1] and
  [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-2].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1msee what's in the database[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. You can't look at the
  entire database with user-friendly tools.  You can print the
  command that introduced a particular name, print the entire
  sequence of user commands, print the commands in the region between
  two commands, print all the rewrite rules that apply to a given
  term or function symbol, and many other options.  See [history].
  If you have loaded a book from another user, you might wish to read
  the source file.  For example, the source file for [47m(include-book
  \"arithmetic-5/top\" :dir :system)[0m is the file named
  [47marithmetic-5/top.lisp[0m on the [47macl2-sources/books/[0m directory where
  ever your ACL2 sources are installed.  Often, books are
  well-documented by comments by their authors.  Some books have
  [47mReadme[0m or [47mREADME[0m files on the same directory.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mundo[0m a command?  [31;1mA[0m. See [history], especially see [u]
  (``undo'') and see [ubt] (``undo back through''). [31;1mQ[0m. What [31;1mrewrite
  rules match[0m a given term?  [31;1mA[0m. See [pl].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  What were those [31;1mquestions to ask when looking at key checkpoints[0m?
  [31;1mA[0m.  See [introduction-to-key-checkpoints].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I figure out [31;1mwhy a rewrite rule won't fire[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. If you
  activate rewrite rule monitoring (see [brr]) and then install a
  monitor (see [monitor]) on the rule in question, ACL2 will enter an
  interactive break whenever the pattern of the rule is matched
  against a target in a proof.  So after installing the monitor,
  re-try the proof and then interact with the rewriter via break
  commands (see [brr-commands]).  Like all trace and break packages,
  you have to know what you're doing to use the break rewrite
  facility, so read the material in the reference manual.  If no
  interactive break happens after you've installed the monitor on
  your rule and re-tried the proof, it means no suitable target ever
  arose.  Don't forget to turn off monitoring when you're done as it
  slows down the system.

  [31;1mQ[0m. [31;1mWhy is a proof taking so long?[0m [31;1mA[0m. Unexpectedly poor performance on
  simple problems is usually a sign of cyclic rewriting or
  combinatoric explosion.  See [dmr] and see
  [accumulated-persistence].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I tell ACL2 [31;1mwhat induction to do[0m for a particular formula?
  [31;1mA[0m. When issuing the [47mdefthm[0m command for the formula, supply an
  [47m:induct[0m hint:

    (defthm [3mname[0m
            [3mformula[0m
            :hints ((\"Goal\" :induct (f x1 ... xn))))

  where [47mf[0m is a function that recurs the way you want the induction to
  unfold and [47mx1 ... xn[0m are the relevant variables in [3mformula[0m.  You
  usually have to define [47mf[0m appropriately for each formula that needs
  an induct hint, though sometimes you can re-use an earlier [47mf[0m or one
  of the functions in the formula itself.  It doesn't matter what
  value [47m(f x1 ... xn)[0m returns.  All that matters is how it recurs.
  The termination analysis for [47mf[0m justifies the induction done.  See
  [hints], especially the section on [47m:induct[0m hints; also see
  [induction].

  [31;1mQ[0m. ACL2 doesn't know [31;1msimple arithmetic[0m that can simplify the term [47m(+
  1 -1 x)[0m.  [31;1mA[0m.  You should load an arithmetic book whenever you're
  dealing with an arithmetic problem.  The simplest arithmetic book
  is typically loaded with the event [47m(include-book
  \"arithmetic/top-with-meta\" :dir :system)[0m.  If you're using [47mfloor[0m
  and [47mmod[0m or non-linear arithmetic like [47m(* x y)[0m you should use
  [47m(include-book \"arithmetic-5/top\" :dir :system)[0m.  See also the
  discussion of arithmetic books under the ``Lemma Libraries and
  Utilities'' link of the ACL2 home page, and see [community-books].

  [31;1mQ[0m. ACL2 is not using an [31;1marithmetic lemma[0m that I proved.  [31;1mA[0m. Lemmas
  concluding with arithmetic inequalities, like

    (implies (member e x)
             (< (len (delete e x)) (len x)))

  are not good rewrite rules because they rarely match targets because
  of intervening arithmetic operators.  For example, the above
  conclusion doesn't match [47m(< (LEN (DELETE E X)) (+ 1 (LEN X)))[0m.  You
  should store such lemmas as [47m:linear[0m rules by using the command:

    (defthm len-delete
      (implies (member e x)
               (< (len (delete e x)) (len x)))
      :rule-classes :linear)

  See [linear].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  What is a [31;1mlinear rule[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  See [linear].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I make ACL2 [31;1mtreat a relation as an equality[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. We assume
  you mean to treat the relation as an equivalence, i.e., replace one
  term by another when they are equivalent under your relation.  See
  [equivalence], see [congruence], and see [refinement].

  [31;1mQ[0m. One of my rewrite rules has a [31;1mhypothesis that doesn't rewrite[0m to
  true.  What do I do?  [31;1mA[0m. Prove a rewrite rule that establishes that
  hypothesis under the relevant assumptions from the context of the
  intended target.  Alternatively, undo the rule and restate it with
  a [47m[force][0m around the problematic hypothesis, making ACL2 assume the
  hypothesis when the rule is applied and raising the truth of the
  hypothesis as an explicit subgoal of the proof.  See also
  [47m[case-split][0m.  Of course, you should always state the strongest
  rewrite rules you can think of, eliminating hypotheses or shifting
  them to the right-hand side inside of [47mIF[0ms; see
  [strong-rewrite-rules].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I make ACL2 ``guess'' the [31;1mright instantiation of a free
  variable[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. You can provide a [47m:restrict[0m hint that names the
  problematic lemma and provides an instantiation of the free
  variable.  See [hints], specifically [47m:restrict[0m.  You could
  alternatively give a hint that [47m:uses[0m the rule and provides the
  appropriate instantiation in full.  See [hints], specifically [47m:use[0m.
  Recall that ACL2 guesses free variable instantiations by matching
  the problematic hypothesis to the assumptions in the context of the
  target.  If the appropriate assumption is present but ACL2 is
  finding another one, try undoing the problematic rule and proving
  it again, specifying the [47m:match-free :all[0m modifier of the [47m:rewrite[0m
  or [47m:linear[0m rule class.  See [rule-classes].  Alternatively, undo
  and prove the problematic rule again and use a [47m[bind-free][0m form to
  compute the appropriate instantiation.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I make ACL2 do a [31;1mcase split[0m to prove a certain subgoal?
  [31;1mA[0m. See [hints], specifically [47m:cases[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I [31;1mprevent ACL2 from using a rewrite rule[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  See
  [hints], specifically [47m:in-theory (disable ...)[0m.  If the use of the
  rule is problematic in only one subgoal and the lemma is needed in
  other subgoals, disable the lemma only in the problematic subgoal
  by specifying the subgoal name (e.g., [47m\"Subgoal 1/3.2.1\"[0m) as the
  goal specifier in the hint.  If the rule isn't needed anywhere in
  the proof, you could use the specifier [47m\"Goal\"[0m.  If you don't think
  the rule will ever be needed for a while, you could globally
  disable it with the event [47m(in-theory (disable ...))[0m (see
  [in-theory]) executed before the first problematic proof.  If the
  rule has never been used or must always be disabled, undo it and
  prove it again with [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How can I prevent ACL2 from running a definition on constants?  I
  tried disabling the function but that didn't work.  [31;1mA[0m.  If you have
  a function named [47mf[0m then disabling [47mf[0m will disable the definitional
  axiom about [47mf[0m.  But ACL2 has another kind of rule about [47mf[0m, telling
  it how to evaluate [47mf[0m.  The [rune] of this rule is
  [47m(:executable-counterpart f)[0m.  Try disabling that, as in the
  [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m [47m(([0m...  [47m:in-theory (disable (:executable-counterpart f))
  ...c[))[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I make ACL2 [31;1muse a rule[0m in a proof?  [31;1mA[0m. See [hints],
  specifically [47m:use[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How can I make ACL2 expand a function call in a proof?  [31;1mA[0m.  You
  can give an [47m:[0mSee [expand] hint.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  ACL2 sometimes aborts the proof attempt before showing me all of
  the subgoals.  How can I make it just keep going instead of
  aborting early?  [31;1mA[0m. See [otf-flg], which stands for Onward Thru the
  Fog FLaG.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I [31;1mcompute when I want a rule to fire[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  See [syntaxp].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How can I add [31;1mpragmatic advice to a lemma after it has been
  proved[0m?  For example, how can add a forced hypothesis, a backchain
  limit, or a [47msyntaxp[0m test?  [31;1mA[0m.  You can't.  You can undo the lemma,
  restate it appropriately, and prove it again.  This produces the
  cleanest database.  Alternatively, you can prove the restated lemma
  under a new name --- a task that should be easy since the old
  version is in the database and will presumably make the proof
  trivial --- and then disable the old name.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I [31;1mstop ACL2 from rewriting a term[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. If you need
  rewriting done but want to prevent some particular terms from being
  rewritten, see [hints], specifically [47m:hands-off[0m.  Alternatively,
  consider embedding the problematic term in a [47m[hide][0m.  Users
  sometime develop special theories (see [theory]) containing just
  the rules they want and then use hints to switch to those theories
  on the appropriate subgoals.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  Can I [31;1mcompute which subgoals a hint refers to[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  Yes, see
  [computed-hints].  This topic is for advanced users but knowing
  that it is possible might come in handy someday.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  I want the rewriter to [31;1malways use one theory and then switch to
  another[0m so that it doesn't use my most complicated before until
  doing the simple things.  [31;1mA[0m.  This is possible but is something for
  the advanced user.  It can be done using a form of
  [computed-hints].  See [using-computed-hints-7].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  Is there a way to attach [31;1mthe same hint to every defthm[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  See
  [default-hints].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How can I just tell ACL2 the proof steps?  [31;1mA[0m.  See [verify] and
  see [proof-builder].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I write [31;1mmy own simplifier[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  See [meta].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I add an axiom or just assume some lemma without proof?
  [31;1mA[0m. This is very dangerous but is a good strategy for exploring
  whether or not a certain set of lemmas (and their rules) is
  sufficient to prove your goal.  See [defaxiom] and see
  [skip-proofs].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can redefine a user-defined function?  [31;1mA[0m.  This is tricky.
  What if you've already proved theorems about the old definition and
  then wish to change it?  There are several options.  See
  [ld-redefinition-action] (and note specifically the discussion of
  updater function for it, [47mset-ld-redefinition-action[0m); also see
  [redef], see [redef!], see [redef+], and see [redef-].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mchange a function from[0m [47m:program[0m [31;1mmode to[0m [47m:logic[0m [31;1mmode[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.
  See [verify-termination].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mchange the guards[0m on a function?  [31;1mA[0m. You can't.  Undo it
  and redefine it.

  [31;1mQ[0m. What is [31;1mprogram mode[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [program].

  [31;1mQ[0m. What does the ACL2 [31;1mprompt[0m mean?  [31;1mA[0m.  See
  [introduction-to-a-few-system-considerations] or, specifically, see
  [prompt].

  [31;1mQ[0m. What is [31;1mlogic mode[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [logic].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mget into or out of[0m [47m:program[0m [31;1mmode?[0m [47m:Logic[0m [31;1mmode?[0m [31;1mA[0m. See
  [program] and see [logic].  You can enter these modes temporarily
  for a particular [47m[defun][0m by using [47m(declare (xargs :mode :program))[0m
  or [47m(declare (xargs :mode :logic))[0m after the list of formal
  parameters to the definition.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I quit from ACL2?  [31;1mA[0m.  This varies depending on the
  interface you're using.  See
  [introduction-to-a-few-system-considerations].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mload a file[0m of definitions and theorems created by
  someone else?  [31;1mA[0m. See [include-book].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mcreate my own book[0m of definitions and theorems?  [31;1mA[0m. See
  [books].

  [31;1mQ[0m. Where are the books referenced by [31;1m:dir :system[0m on my machine?  [31;1mA[0m.
  If your ACL2 is installed on the directory [3mdir[0m[47m/acl2-sources[0m and you
  follow the standard [installation] instructions, then the books are
  typically the files under the directory [3mdir[0m[47m/acl2-sources/books/[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I find out [31;1mwhat books are available[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. Go to the ACL2
  home page, [47mhttp://www.cs.utexas.edu/u/moore/acl2/[0m and look at the
  link labeled ``Lemma Libraries and Utilities.''

  [31;1mQ[0m. How do I [31;1mproduce my own book[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [books].

  [31;1mQ[0m. What is a [31;1mdecision procedure[0m?  What [31;1mdecision procedures does ACL2
  have[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. A [3mdecision procedure[0m is an algorithm that given enough
  time and space can determine, for all the formulas in a certain
  syntactic class of formulas, whether the formula is a theorem or
  not.  The most well-known decision procedure is for propositional
  calculus: by testing a formula under all the combinations Boolean
  assignments to the variables, one can decide if a propositional
  formula is a theorem.  The syntactic class consists of all formulas
  you can write using just variables, constants, and compositions of
  the functions [47mand[0m, [47mor[0m, [47mnot[0m, [47mimplies[0m, [47miff[0m, and [47mif[0m.  There are, of
  course, an exponential number of different assignments so the
  algorithm can be slow.  ACL2 contains a propositional decision
  procedure based on symbolic normalization that can be faster than
  trying all the combinations of truth values --- but is not
  guaranteed to be faster.  ACL2 also contains an optional [bdd]
  procedure.  ACL2 also contains a decision procedure for rational
  arithmetic involving variables, rational constants, addition,
  multiplication by rational constants, equality, and the various
  versions of arithmetic inequality ([47m<[0m, [47m<=[0m, [47m>=[0m, and [47m>[0m).  It can be
  extended with [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m lemmas.  ACL2 is complete for equality over
  uninterpreted (e.g., undefined and unconstrained) function symbols
  using an algorithm based on transitive closure of equivalence
  classes under functional substitutivity.  Finally, you can make
  ACL2 use other decision procedures, even ones implemented outside
  of ACL2; see [clause-processor].

  [31;1mQ[0m. ACL2 has the [31;1mchange of variable[0m trick ([31;1mdestructor elimination[0m)
  that it does to get rid of [47m(CAR X)[0m and [47m(CDR X)[0m by replacing [47mx[0m by
  [47m(CONS A B)[0m.  Is there a way to make ACL2 do that for other terms?
  [31;1mA[0m. Yes.  See [elim].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I [31;1mprevent destructor elimination[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [hints],
  specifically [47m:do-not '(eliminate-destructors)[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I [31;1mprevent cross-fertilization[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [hints],
  specifically [47m:do-not '(fertilize)[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I [31;1mprevent generalization[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [hints], specifically
  [47m:do-not '(generalize)[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m. How can I make ACL2 [31;1mimpose a restriction on a new variable[0m
  introduced by destructor elimination or generalization?  [31;1mA[0m.  See
  [generalize].

  [31;1mQ[0m. What [31;1mrule classes[0m are there?  [31;1mA[0m. See [rule-classes].

  [31;1mQ[0m. What is a [31;1mtheory[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [theories].

  [31;1mQ[0m. How are [31;1mhints inherited by the children of a subgoal[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See
  [hints-and-the-waterfall].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I use ACL2 under [31;1mEmacs[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [emacs].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I use ACL2 under [31;1mEclipse[0m?  [31;1mA[0m. See [ACL2-Sedan].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  How do I interrupt the prover?  [31;1mA[0m.  The keyboard sequence for
  interrupting a running process depends your operating system, host
  Common Lisp, and user interface (e.g., Emacs, Eclipse, etc.).  But
  perhaps a few examples will help you discover what you need to
  know.  If your host Common Lisp is GCL or Allegro and you are
  typing directly to the Common Lisp process, then you can interrupt
  a computation by typing ``ctrl-c'' (hold down the Control key and
  hit the ``c'' key once).  But other Lisps may require some other
  control sequence.  If you are typing to an Emacs process which is
  running the GCL or Allegro Common Lisp process in a shell buffer,
  you should type ctrl-c ctrl-c --- that is, you have to type the
  previously mentioned sequence twice in succession.  If you are
  running the ACL2 Sedan, you can use the [3mInterrupt Session[0m button on
  the tool bar.  The environment you enter when you interrupt depends
  on various factors and basically you should endeavor to get back to
  the top level ACL2 command loop, perhaps by typing some kind of
  Lisp dependent ``abort'' command like [47m:q[0m or [47m:a[0m.  You can usually
  determine what environment you're in by paying attention to the
  prompt.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  What is the [31;1mACL2 loop[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  That is the name given to the
  interactive environment ACL2 provides, a ``read-eval-print loop''
  in which the user submits successive commands by typing ACL2
  expressions and keyword commands.  See
  [introduction-to-a-few-system-considerations].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  What is [31;1mraw lisp[0m?  [31;1mA[0m.  That is our name for the host Common Lisp
  in which ACL2 is implemented.  See
  [introduction-to-a-few-system-considerations].  There is an ACL2
  mode named [3mraw mode[0m which is different from ``raw lisp.'' See
  [set-raw-mode].

  [31;1mQ[0m.  Can I get a tree-like view of a proof?  [31;1mA[0m.  See [proof-tree] for
  an Emacs utility that displays proof trees and allows you to
  navigate through a proof from the proof tree.  The ACL2 Sedan also
  supports proof trees and you should see the ACL2s documentation on
  that topic.

  [31;1mQ[0m.  I used the earlier Boyer-Moore theorem prover, Nqthm.  How is
  ACL2 different?  [31;1mA[0m. See [nqthm-to-ACL2].

  If you are reading this as part of the tutorial introduction to the
  theorem prover, use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [introduction-to-the-theorem-prover].")
