 (GUARD-FORMULA-UTILITIES
  (GUARD)
  "Utilities that show guard proof obligations

  See [verify-guards] and see [guard] for discussions of guards.  Here
  we discuss several utilities that provide the proof obligations for
  guards, and briefly compare what is offered by each.  Of course,
  see the documentation of each utility for details.

  The first pair of utilities captures formulas produced during guard
  verification.

      [47m[Verify-guards-formula][0m: Use this to see what the corresponding
      [47m[verify-guards][0m event prints, but without following through
      with a proof attempt.  This utility is only for output, without
      returning an interesting value.

      [47m[Guard-obligation][0m: This function is a programmatic version of the
      macro, [47m[verify-guards-formula][0m.  It provides the guard
      obligation as a set of [clause]s, along with other information.

  The second pair of utilities captures the formula provided when a
  [47m:guard-theorem[0m is supplied for a [47m:use[0m hint.

      [47m[Gthm][0m: This macro returns a user-level (``untranslated'')
      representation of the [term] that is generated for a
      [47m:guard-theorem[0m [lemma-instance].  Options control whether or
      not simplification and [guard-debug] are used.

      [47m[Guard-theorem][0m: This utility is like [gthm], except that it is a
      function rather than a macro and it returns a translated term
      (a [47m[termp][0m).

  We conclude by contrasting these two pairs of utilities.

    * The first pair can take as input as either a function symbol or a
      term; for the second pair a function symbol (not a term) is
      required.

    * The level of simplification differs between the two pairs.  See
      [guard-simplification] for a detailed explanation; here are
      highlights.  The first pair simplifies (by default) with
      respect to the [current-theory] before producing the guard
      proof obligation formula, as is typically done when verifying
      guards; but the second pair only performs the
      theory-independent simplification done for a [47m:guard-theorem[0m
      specified in a [47m:use[0m hint, or if a suitable option is supplied,
      no simplification at all.

    * The two pairs differ in their output [signature]s: in particular, the
      utilities in the first pair return multiple values while those
      in the second pair return a single value.


Subtopics

  [Gthm]
      The [guard] theorem for a given function symbol

  [Guard-obligation]
      The guard proof obligation

  [Guard-simplification]
      Levels of simplification for [guard] proof obligations

  [Guard-theorem]
      Use a previously-proved [guard] theorem

  [Guard-theorem-example]
      How to use a previously-proved [guard] theorem

  [Verify-guard-implication]
      [Guard] implication for [47m[memoize][0m keyword [47m:invoke[0m

  [Verify-guards-formula]
      View the guard proof obligation, without proving it")
