        [47mValue[0m is a true list of terms, each of which is of one of the forms
        [47m(let ((v1 t1)...) b)[0m or [47m(fn t1 ... tn)[0m, where [47mfn[0m is a defined
        function symbol with formals [47mv1, ..., vn,[0m and [47mbody[0m [47mb[0m.  Such a
        term is said to be ``expandable:'' it can be replaced by the
        result of substituting the [47mti[0m's for the [47mvi[0m's in [47mb[0m.  The terms
        listed in the [47m:expand[0m hint are expanded when they are
        encountered by the simplifier while working on the specified
        goal or any of its subgoals.  (There is no separate
        ``expand'' process.)  We permit [47mvalue[0m to be a single such
        term instead of a singleton list.  [31;1mRemarks[0m: (0) Note that in
        the event that a [47m:definition[0m rule has been admitted for [47mfn[0m,
        then by default, the body [47mb[0m is determined by the (most
        recently admitted such) rule rather than the original
        definition of [47mfn[0m; see [definition].  (1) Allowed are
        ``terms'' of the form [47m(:free (var1 var2 ...  varn) pattern)[0m
        where the indicated variables are distinct and [47mpattern[0m is a
        term.  Such ``terms'' indicate that we consider the indicated
        variables to be instantiatable, in the following sense:
        whenever the simplifier encounters a term that can be
        obtained from [47mpattern[0m by instantiating the variables [47m(var1
        var2 ...  varn)[0m, then it expands that term.  (2) Also allowed
        are ``terms'' of the form [47m(:with name term)[0m, where [47mname[0m is a
        function symbol, a macro name that denotes a function symbol
        (see [macro-aliases-table]), or a [rune].  The corresponding
        [definition] rule or (less often) [47m[rewrite][0m rule is then used
        in place of the current body for the function symbol of [47mterm[0m;
        see [show-bodies] and see [set-body].  If the rule is of the
        form [47m(implies hyp (equiv lhs rhs))[0m, then after matching [47mlhs[0m
        to the current term in a context that is maintaining
        equivalence relation [47mequiv[0m, ACL2 will replace the current
        term with [47m(if hyp rhs (hide term))[0m, or just [47mrhs[0m if the rule
        is just [47m(equal lhs rhs)[0m.  (3) A combination of both [47m:free[0m and
        [47m:with[0m, as described above, is legal.  (4) The term [47m:LAMBDAS[0m
        is treated specially.  It denotes the list of all lambda
        applications (i.e., [47m[let][0m expressions) encountered during the
        proof.  Conceptually, this use of [47m:LAMBDAS[0m tells ACL2 to
        treat lambda applications as a notation for substitutions,
        rather than as function calls whose opening is subject to the
        ACL2 rewriter's heuristics (specifically, not allowing lambda
        applications to open when they introduce ``too many'' if
        terms).

    [47m:hands-off[0m

        [47mValue[0m is a true list of function symbols or lambda expressions,
        indicating that under the specified goal applications of
        these functions are not to be rewritten.  Note however that
        subterms will still be rewritten; see [hide] if that is not
        what is intended.  (The community book
        [47mbooks/clause-processors/autohide.lisp[0m from Jared Davis may
        also be helpful in that case.) [47mValue[0m may also be a single
        function symbol or lambda expression instead of a list.

    [47m:[0m[47m[in-theory][0m

        [47mValue[0m is a ``theory expression,'' i.e., a term having at most the
        single free variable [47m[world][0m which when evaluated (with
        [47m[world][0m bound to the current ACL2 logical world (see
        [world])) will produce a theory to use as the current theory
        for the goal specified.  See [theories].

        Note that an [47m:[0m[47m[in-theory][0m hint will always be evaluated relative to
        the current ACL2 logical [world], not relative to the theory
        of a previous goal.  Consider the following example.

          (defthm prop
            (p (f (g x)))
            :hints ((\"Goal\"      :in-theory (disable f))
                    (\"Subgoal 3\" :in-theory (enable  g))))

        Consider in particular the theory in effect at [47mSubgoal 3[0m.  This call
        of the [47m[enable][0m macro enables [47mg[0m relative to the
        [47m[current-theory][0m of the current logical [world], [3mnot[0m relative
        to the theory produced by the hint at [47mGoal[0m.  Thus, the
        [47m[disable][0m of [47mf[0m on behalf of the hint at [47mGoal[0m will be lost at
        [47mSubgoal 3[0m, and [47mf[0m will be enabled at [47mSubgoal 3[0m if was enabled
        globally when [47mprop[0m was submitted.

