  "[47m(hons x y)[0m returns a [normed] object equal to [47m(cons x y)[0m.

  In the logic, [47mhons[0m is just [47m[cons][0m; we leave it enabled and would
  think it odd to ever prove a theorem about it.

  Under the hood, [47mhons[0m does whatever is necessary to ensure that its
  result is [normed].

  What might this involve?

  Since the car and cdr of any normed cons must be normed, we need to
  [47m[hons-copy][0m x and y.  This requires little work if x and y are
  already normed, but can be expensive if x or y contain large,
  un-normed cons structures.

  After that, we need to check whether any normed cons equal to [47m(x . y)[0m
  already exists.  If so, we return it; otherwise, we need to
  construct a new cons for [47m(x . y)[0m and install it as the normed
  version of [47m(x . y)[0m.

  Generally speaking, these extra operations make [47mhons[0m much slower than
  [47mcons[0m, even when given normed arguments.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<hons>

    (defun hons (x y)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (cons x y))


