 (HONS-CLEAR
  (HONS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "[47m(hons-clear gc)[0m is a drastic garbage collection mechanism that clears
  out the underlying Hons Space.

  Logically, [47mhons-clear[0m just returns nil; we leave it enabled and would
  think it odd to ever prove a theorem about it.

  Under the hood, [47mhons-clear[0m brutally (1) clears all the tables that
  govern which conses are [normed], then (2) optionally garbage
  collects, per the [47mgc[0m argument, then finally (3) re-norms the keys
  of [47m[fast-alists][0m and persistently normed conses; see
  [hons-copy-persistent].

  Notes.

    * The hash tables making up the Hons Space retain their sizes after
      being cleared.  If you want to shrink them, see [47m[hons-resize][0m.

    * CCL and GCL users might prefer [47m[hons-wash][0m, which is relatively
      efficient and allows for the garbage collection of normed
      conses without impacting their normed status.

    * It is not recommended to interrupt this function.  Doing so may cause
      persistently normed conses and fast alist keys to become
      un-normed, which might lead to less efficient re-norming and/or
      violations of the fast-alist discipline.

    * (For ACL2(p) users only; see [parallelism]) If parallel execution is
      enabled (see [set-parallel-execution]), as it is by default in
      ACL2(p), then [47mhons-clear[0m may be a no-op (other than to print a
      warning), in order to avoid thread-unsafe behavior.  (However,
      In CCL you are unlikely to see this restriction unless you are
      running more than one thread.)  To get around this restriction,
      you can instead use [47m[hons-clear!][0m, which however requires a
      [trust-tag].

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<hons-clear>

    (defun hons-clear (gc)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (declare (ignore gc))
      nil)")
