 (IF*
  (BDD)
  "For conditional rewriting with BDDs

  The function [47mIF*[0m is defined to be [47m[if][0m, but it is used in a special
  way by ACL2's [bdd] package.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<if*>

    (defun if* (x y z) (if x y z))

  As explained elsewhere (see [bdd-algorithm]), ACL2's [bdd] algorithm
  gives special treatment to [term]s of the form [47m(IF* TEST TBR FBR)[0m.
  In such cases, the algorithm simplifies [47mTEST[0m first, and the result
  of that simplification must be a constant (normally [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m, but
  any non-[47mnil[0m explicit value is treated like [47mt[0m here).  Otherwise, the
  algorithm aborts.

  Thus, [47mIF*[0m may be used to implement a sort of conditional rewriting
  for ACL2's [bdd] package, even though this package only nominally
  supports unconditional rewriting.  The following contrived example
  should make this point clear.

  Suppose that we want to prove that [47m(nthcdr (length x) (append x y))[0m
  is equal to [47my[0m, but that we would be happy to prove this only for
  lists having length 4.  We can state such a theorem as follows.

    (let ((x (list x0 x1 x2 x3)))
      (equal (nthcdr (length x) (append x y))
             y))

  If we want to prove this formula with a [47m:[0m[47m[bdd][0m hint, then we need to
  have appropriate rewrite rules around.  First, note that [47mLENGTH[0m is
  defined as follows (try [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m [47m[length][0m):

    (length x)
     =
    (if (stringp x)
        (len (coerce x 'list))
        (len x))

  Since [bdd]-based rewriting is merely very simple unconditional
  rewriting (see [bdd-algorithm]), we expect to have to prove a rule
  reducing [47m[stringp][0m of a [47m[cons][0m:

    (defthm stringp-cons
      (equal (stringp (cons x y))
             nil))

  Now we need a rule to compute the [47mLEN[0m of [47mX[0m, because the definition of
  [47mLEN[0m is recursive and hence not used by the [bdd] package.

    (defthm len-cons
      (equal (len (cons a x))
             (1+ (len x))))

  We imagine this rule simplifying [47m(LEN (LIST X0 X1 X2 X3))[0m in terms of
  [47m(LEN (LIST X1 X2 X3))[0m, and so on, and then finally [47m(LEN nil)[0m should
  be computed by execution (see [bdd-algorithm]).

  We also need to imagine simplifying [47m(APPEND X Y)[0m, where still [47mX[0m is
  bound to [47m(LIST X0 X1 X2 X3)[0m.  The following two rules suffice for
  this purpose (but are needed, since [47m[append][0m, actually
  [47m[binary-append][0m, is recursive).

    (defthm append-cons
      (equal (append (cons a x) y)
             (cons a (append x y))))

    (defthm append-nil
      (equal (append nil x)
             x))

  Finally, we imagine needing to simplify calls of [47m[nthcdr][0m, where the
  first argument is a number (initially, the length of [47m(LIST X0 X1 X2
  X3)[0m, which is 4).  The second lemma below is the traditional way to
  accomplish that goal (when not using BDDs), by proving a
  conditional rewrite rule.  (The first lemma is only proved in order
  to assist in the proof of the second lemma.)

    (defthm fold-constants-in-+
      (implies (and (syntaxp (quotep x))
                    (syntaxp (quotep y)))
               (equal (+ x y z)
                      (+ (+ x y) z))))

    (defthm nthcdr-add1-conditional
      (implies (not (zp (1+ n)))
               (equal (nthcdr (1+ n) x)
                      (nthcdr n (cdr x)))))

  The problem with this rule is that its hypothesis makes it a
  conditional [rewrite] rule, and conditional rewrite rules are not
  used by the [bdd] package.  (See [bdd-algorithm] for a discussion
  of ``BDD rules.'') (Note that the hypothesis cannot simply be
  removed; the resulting formula would be false for [47mn = -1[0m and [47mx =
  '(a)[0m, for example.)  We can solve this problem by using [47mIF*[0m, as
  follows; comments follow.

    (defthm nthcdr-add1
      (equal (nthcdr (+ 1 n) x)
             (if* (zp (1+ n))
                  x
                  (nthcdr n (cdr x)))))

  How is [47mnthcdr-add1[0m applied by the [bdd] package?  Suppose that the
  [bdd] computation encounters a [term] of the form [47m(NTHCDR (+ 1 N)
  X)[0m.  Then the [bdd] package will apply the [rewrite] rule
  [47mnthcdr-add1[0m.  The first thing it will do when attempting to
  simplify the right hand side of that rule is to attempt to simplify
  the term [47m(ZP (1+ N))[0m.  If [47mN[0m is an explicit number (which is the
  case in the scenario we envision), this test will reduce (assuming
  the executable-counterparts of [47m[zp][0m and [47m[binary-+][0m are [enable]d)
  to [47mt[0m or to [47mnil[0m.  In fact, the lemmas above (not including the lemma
  [47mnthcdr-add1-conditional[0m) suffice to prove our goal:

    (thm (let ((x (list x0 x1 x2 x3)))
           (equal (nthcdr (length x) (append x y))
                  y))
         :hints ((\"Goal\" :bdd (:vars nil))))

  If we execute the following form that disables the definition and
  executable-counterpart of the function [47m[zp][0m

    (in-theory (disable zp (zp)))

  before attempting the proof of the theorem above, we can see more
  clearly the point of using [47mIF*[0m.  In this case, the prover makes the
  following report.

    ACL2 Error in ( THM ...):  Unable to resolve test of IF* for term

    (IF* (ZP (+ 1 N)) X (NTHCDR N (CDR X)))

    under the bindings

    ((X (CONS X0 (CONS X1 (CONS X2 #)))) (N '3))

    -- use SHOW-BDD to see a backtrace.

  If we follow the advice above, we can see rather clearly what
  happened.  See [show-bdd].

    ACL2 !>(show-bdd)

    BDD computation on Goal yielded 21 nodes.
    ------------------------------

    BDD computation was aborted on Goal, and hence there is no
    falsifying assignment that can be constructed.  Here is a backtrace
    of calls, starting with the top-level call and ending with the one
    that led to the abort.  See :DOC show-bdd.

    (LET ((X (LIST X0 X1 X2 X3)))
         (EQUAL (NTHCDR (LENGTH X) (APPEND X Y)) Y))
      alist: ((Y Y) (X3 X3) (X2 X2) (X1 X1) (X0 X0))

    (NTHCDR (LENGTH X) (APPEND X Y))
      alist: ((X (LIST X0 X1 X2 X3)) (Y Y))

    (IF* (ZP (+ 1 N)) X (NTHCDR N (CDR X)))
      alist: ((X (LIST* X0 X1 X2 X3 Y)) (N 3))
    ACL2 !>

  Each of these term-alist pairs led to the next, and the test of the
  last one, namely [47m(ZP (+ 1 N))[0m where [47mN[0m is bound to [47m3[0m, was not
  simplified to [47mt[0m or to [47mnil[0m.

  What would have happened if we had used [47m[if][0m in place of [47mIF*[0m in the
  rule [47mnthcdr-add1[0m?  In that case, if [47mZP[0m and its
  [executable-counterpart] were disabled then we would be put into an
  infinite loop!  For, each time a term of the form [47m(NTHCDR k V)[0m is
  encountered by the BDD package (where k is an explicit number), it
  will be rewritten in terms of [47m(NTHCDR k-1 (CDR V))[0m.  We would
  prefer that if for some reason the term [47m(ZP (+ 1 N))[0m cannot be
  decided to be [47mt[0m or to be [47mnil[0m, then the BDD computation should
  simply abort.

  Even if there were no infinite loop, this kind of use of [47mIF*[0m is
  useful in order to provide feedback of the form shown above
  whenever the test of an [47mIF[0m term fails to simplify to [47mt[0m or to [47mnil[0m.")
