  (APPLY$)
  "Indicator of how an argument is used

  The [3milk[0m of the ith argument of a [47m[badge][0md function symbol [47mfn[0m is one
  of three tokens with the following meanings.

    * [47m:FN[0m - the ith argument is used exclusively as a function object by
      [47mfn[0m; informally this means that the argument is passed only into
      slots of ilk [47m:FN[0m in the definition of [47mfn[0m and that on some
      (syntactic) execution paths reaches the first argument of a
      call of [47m[apply$][0m.

    * [47m:EXPR[0m - the ith argument is used exclusively as a expression object
      by [47mfn[0m; informally this means that the argument is passed only
      into slots of ilk [47m:EXPR[0m in the definition of [47mfn[0m and that on
      some (syntactic) execution paths reaches the first argument of
      a call of [47m[ev$][0m.

    * [47mNIL[0m - the ith argument is never used as a function or expression
      object in the definition of [47mfn[0m.

  See [47m[badge][0m for more details.

  The [3milks[0m of all the arguments of [47mfn[0m are stored in the [47m[badge][0m of [47mfn[0m.
  If each ilk of [47mfn[0m is [47mNIL[0m the [47m[badge][0m stores a [47mT[0m as the ``list'' of
  ilks.

  The [47m[badge][0m of [47mfn[0m is computed by a successful call of [47m[defwarrant][0m on
  [47mfn[0m.

  Ilks are used by the various notions of [tame]ness controlling
  whether [47m[apply$][0m and [47m[ev$][0m can properly interpret a quoted function
  or expression object.")
