 (IN-TAU-INTERVALP
  (TAU-SYSTEM ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Boolean membership in a tau interval

    General Form:
    (in-tau-intervalp e x)

  Here, [47mx[0m should be an interval (see [47m[tau-intervalp][0m).  This function
  returns [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m indicating whether [47me[0m, which is generally but not
  necessarily a number, is an element of interval [47mx[0m.  By that is
  meant that [47me[0m satisfies the domain predicate of the interval and
  lies between the two bounds.

  Suppose [47mx[0m is an interval with the components [3mdom[0m, [3mlo-rel[0m, [3mlo[0m, [3mhi-rel[0m
  and [3mhi[0m.  Suppose [47m(<?  [0m[3mrel u v[0m[47m)[0m means [47m(< [0m[3mu v[0m[47m)[0m when [3mrel[0m is true and
  [47m(<= [0m[3mu v[0m[47m)[0m otherwise, with appropriate treatment of infinities.

  Then for [47me[0m to be in interval [47mx[0m, it must be the case that [47me[0m satisfies
  the domain predicate [3mdom[0m (where where [3mdom[0m=[47mnil[0m means there is no
  restriction on the domain) and [47m(<? [0m[3mlo-rel lo[0m[47m e)[0m and [47m(<? [0m[3mhi-rel[0m[47m e
  [0m[3mhi[0m[47m)[0m.  [Note: ``Appropriate treatment of infinities'' is slightly
  awkward if both infinities are represented by the same object, [47mnil[0m.
  However, this is handled by coercing [47me[0m to a number [3mafter[0m checking
  that it is in the domain.  By this trick, ``[47m<?[0m'' is presented with
  at most one ``infinity'' and it is always negative when in the
  first argument and positive when in the second.]

  Note that every element in an [47mINTEGERP[0m interval is contained in the
  analogous [47mRATIONALP[0m interval (i.e., the interval obtained by just
  replacing the domain [47mINTEGERP[0m by [47mRATIONALP[0m).  That is because every
  integer is a rational.  Similarly, every rational is an ACL2
  number.

  Note that an interval in which the relations are weak and the bounds
  are equal rationals is the ``unit'' or ``identity'' interval
  containing exactly that rational.

  Note that an interval in which the relations are strong and the
  bounds are equal rationals is empty: it contains no objects.

  Note that the interval [47m(make-tau-interval nil nil nil nil nil)[0m is the
  ``universal interval:'' it contains all ACL2 objects.  It contains
  all numbers because they satisfy the non-existent domain
  restriction and lie between minus infinity and plus infinity.  It
  contains all non-numbers because the interval contains [47m0[0m and ACL2's
  inequalities coerce non-numbers to [47m0[0m.  The universal interval is
  useful if you are defining a bounder (see [bounders]) for a
  function and do not wish to address a certain case: return the
  universal interval.

  Recall that [47m[make-tau-interval][0m constructs intervals.  Using
  [47mmake-tau-interval[0m we give several self-explanatory examples of
  [47min-tau-intervalp[0m:

    (in-tau-intervalp 3 (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP nil 0 nil 10))           = t
    (in-tau-intervalp 3 (make-tau-interval 'RATIONALP nil 0 nil 10))          = t
    (in-tau-intervalp 3 (make-tau-interval NIL nil 0 nil 10))                 = t

    (in-tau-intervalp -3 (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP nil 0 nil 10))          = nil
    (in-tau-intervalp 30 (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP nil 0 nil 10))          = nil

    (in-tau-intervalp 3/5 (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP nil 0 nil 10))         = nil
    (in-tau-intervalp 3/5 (make-tau-interval 'RATIONALP nil 0 nil 10))        = t

    (in-tau-intervalp #c(3 5) (make-tau-interval 'RATIONALP nil 0 nil 10))    = nil
    (in-tau-intervalp #c(3 5) (make-tau-interval 'ACL2-NUMBERP nil 0 nil 10)) = t

    (in-tau-intervalp 'ABC (make-tau-interval NIL nil 0 nil 10))              = t")
