  [47mInclude-book[0m is similar in spirit to [47m[encapsulate][0m in that it is a
  single event that ``contains'' other [events], in this case the
  [events] listed in the file named.  [47mInclude-book[0m processes the
  non-[47m[local][0m event forms in the file, assuming that each is
  admissible.  [47m[Local][0m [events] in the file are ignored.  You may use
  [47minclude-book[0m to load several [books], creating the logical [world]
  that contains the definitions and theorems of all of them.

  If any non-[47m[local][0m event of the book attempts to define a [name] that
  has already been defined --- and the book's definition is not
  syntactically identical to the existing definition --- the attempt
  to include the book fails, an error message is printed, and no
  change to the logical [world] occurs.  See [redundant-events] for
  the details.

  When a book is included, the default [defun-mode] (see
  [default-defun-mode]) for the first event is always [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.  That
  is, the default [defun-mode] ``outside'' the book --- in the
  environment in which [47minclude-book[0m was called --- is irrelevant to
  the book.  [Events] that change the [defun-mode] are permitted
  within a book (provided they are not in [47m[local][0m forms).  However,
  such changes within a book are not exported, i.e., at the
  conclusion of an [47minclude-book[0m, the ``outside'' default [defun-mode]
  is always the same as it was before the [47minclude-book[0m.

  Unlike every other event in ACL2, [47minclude-book[0m puts a burden on you.
  Used improperly, [47minclude-book[0m can be unsound in the sense that it
  can create an inconsistent extension of a consistent logical
  [world].  A certification mechanism is available to help you carry
  this burden --- but it must be understood up front that even
  certification is no guarantee against inconsistency here.  The
  fundamental problem is one of file system security.  See
  [certificate] for a discussion of the security issues.

  At the beginning of execution of an [47minclude-book[0m form, even before
  executing [portcullis] [command]s, the value of
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m is restored to the value it had at startup.
  After execution of an [47minclude-book[0m form, the value of
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m is restored to what it was immediately before
  that [47minclude-book[0m form was executed.  See [ACL2-defaults-table].

  An advanced option is [47m:uncertified-okp :ignore-certs[0m.  This tells
  ACL2 to ignore all [certificate] files while including the book and
  its sub-books, thus treating all these books as uncertified.  This
  option may be useful when writing a [47m.acl2x[0m file for the first
  ``run'' of a ``two-runs approach'' to certification; see
  [set-write-ACL2x].  Note however that ordinary certification (the
  second ``run'') will then fail unless care is taken to avoid
  [47m:uncertified-okp :ignore-certs[0m during that second certification
  run.  Finally (and speaking in general, not particularly about a
  [47m[certify-book][0m context), we emphasize that the option
  [47m:uncertified-okp :ignore-certs[0m applies when including all
  sub-books, not only for the current book.  In particular, a
  subsidiary [47minclude-book[0m call specifying [47m:uncertified-okp nil[0m will
  fail, because the superior [47m:ignore-certs[0m value causes the
  subsidiary book to be treated as uncertified, which conflicts with
  the requirement of [47m:uncertified-okp nil[0m.

  [31;1mBooks Directory.[0m We refer to the ``books directory'' of an executable
  image as the full pathname string of the directory associated with
  [47minclude-book[0m keyword option [47m:dir :system[0m for that image.  By
  default, it is the [47mbooks/[0m subdirectory of the directory where the
  sources reside and the executable image is thus built).  If those
  books reside elsewhere, the environment variable [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m
  can be set to the directory under which they reside.  In most
  cases, your ACL2 executable is a small script in which you can set
  this environment variable just above the line on which the actual
  ACL2 image is invoked, for example:

    export ACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS
    ACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS=/home/acl2/4-0/acl2-sources/books

  If you follow suggestions in the [installation] instructions, these
  books will be the ACL2 community books; see [community-books].  For
  another way to set the system books directory, which also permits
  similar handling for other directories, see [project-dir-alist].

  This concludes the guided tour through [books].  See
  [set-compile-fns] for a subtle point about the interaction between
  [47minclude-book[0m and on-the-fly [compilation].  See [certify-book] for
  a discussion of how to certify a book.


