 (INDUCTION
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make a rule that suggests a certain induction

    Example:
    (defthm recursion-by-sub2-induction-rule
      t
      :rule-classes ((:induction :pattern (* 1/2 i)
                                 :condition (and (integerp i) (>= i 0))
                                 :scheme (recursion-by-sub2 i))))

  In ACL2, as in Nqthm, the functions in a conjecture ``suggest'' the
  inductions considered by the system.  Because every recursively
  defined function must be admitted with a justification in terms of
  a measure that decreases in a well-founded way on a given set of
  ``controlling'' arguments, every recursive definition suggests a
  dual induction scheme that ``unwinds'' the function from a given
  application.  The case analysis in the induction scheme suggested
  by a function call is determined by the case analysis used to prove
  termination.  That case analysis chooses a subset of the tests
  governing recursive calls and then generates base cases for the
  combinations of tests that do not lead to recursive calls and
  induction steps for the combinations that do.  See [rulers] and
  [induction-coarse-v-fine-grained].

  For example, since [47m[append][0m (actually [47m[binary-append][0m, but we'll
  ignore the distinction here) decomposes its first argument by
  successive [47m[cdr][0ms as long as it is a cons, the induction scheme
  suggested by [47m(append x y)[0m has a base case supposing [47mx[0m to be an atom
  (i.e., not a cons) and then has an induction step in which the
  induction hypothesis is obtained by replacing [47mx[0m by [47m(cdr x)[0m.  This
  substitution decreases the same measure used to justify the
  definition of [47m[append][0m.  Observe that an induction scheme is
  suggested by a recursive function application only if the
  controlling actuals are distinct variables, a condition that is
  sufficient to ensure that the ``substitution'' used to create the
  induction hypothesis is indeed a substitution and that it drives
  down a certain measure.  In particular, [47m(append (foo x) y)[0m does not
  suggest an induction unwinding [47m[append][0m because the induction
  scheme suggested by [47m(append x y)[0m requires that we substitute [47m(cdr
  x)[0m for [47mx[0m and we cannot do that if [47mx[0m is not a variable symbol.

  Once ACL2 has collected together all the suggested induction schemes
  it massages them in various ways, combining some to simultaneously
  unwind certain cliques of functions and vetoing others because they
  ``flaw'' others.  We do not further discuss the induction
  heuristics here; the interested reader should see
  [induction-heuristics].

  However, unlike Nqthm, ACL2 provides a means by which the user can
  elaborate the rules under which function applications suggest
  induction schemes.  Such rules are called [47m:induction[0m rules.  The
  definitional principle automatically creates an [47m:induction[0m rule,
  named [47m(:induction fn)[0m, for each admitted recursive function, [47mfn[0m.
  It is this rule that links applications of [47mfn[0m to the induction
  scheme it suggests.  Disabling [47m(:induction fn)[0m will prevent [47mfn[0m from
  suggesting the induction scheme derived from its recursive
  definition (with an exception for induction schemes by way of
  user-defined induction rules, as discussed at the end below).  It
  is possible for the user to create additional [47m:induction[0m rules by
  using the [47m:induction[0m rule class in [47m[defthm][0m.

  Technically we are ``overloading'' [47m[defthm][0m by using it in the
  creation of [47m:induction[0m rules because no theorem need be proved to
  set up the heuristic link represented by an [47m:induction[0m rule.
  However, since [47m[defthm][0m is generally used to create rules and
  rule-class objects are generally used to specify the exact form of
  each rule, we maintain that convention and introduce the notion of
  an [47m:induction[0m rule.  An [47m:induction[0m rule can be created from any
  lemma whatsoever.

    General Form of an :induction Lemma or Corollary:
    T

    General Form of an :induction rule-class:
    (:induction :pattern pat-term
                :condition cond-term
                :scheme scheme-term)

  where [47mpat-term[0m, [47mcond-term[0m, and [47mscheme-term[0m are all terms, [47mpat-term[0m is
  the application of a function symbol, [47mfn[0m, [47mscheme-term[0m is the
  application of a function symbol, [47mrec-fn[0m, that suggests an
  induction, and, finally, every free variable of [47mcond-term[0m and
  [47mscheme-term[0m is a free variable of [47mpat-term[0m.  We actually check that
  [47mrec-fn[0m is either recursively defined --- so that it suggests the
  induction that is intrinsic to its recursion --- or else that
  another [47m:induction[0m rule has been proved linking a call of [47mrec-fn[0m as
  the [47m:pattern[0m to some scheme.

  The induction rule created is used as follows.  When an instance of
  the [47m:pattern[0m term occurs in a conjecture to be proved by induction
  and the corresponding instance of the [47m:condition[0m term is known to
  be non-[47mnil[0m (by [type-reasoning] alone), the corresponding instance
  of the [47m:scheme[0m term is created and the rule ``suggests'' the
  induction, if any, suggested by that term.  (Analysis of that term
  may further involve induction rules, though the applied rule is
  removed from consideration during that further analysis, in order
  to avoid looping.)  If [47mrec-fn[0m has a recursive definition, then the
  definition's dual induction scheme is suggested (i.e., unwinding
  the function).

  (Remark.  Unlike [47m:induct[0m [hints], the [47m:scheme[0m of an [47m:induction[0m rule
  only introduces induction schemes based on the top-level function
  symbol of the indicated term, not of any of its subterms.  End of
  Remark.)

  Consider, for example, the example given above,

    (:induction :pattern (* 1/2 i)
                :condition (and (integerp i) (>= i 0))
                :scheme (recursion-by-sub2 i)).

  In this example, we imagine that [47mrecursion-by-sub2[0m is the function:

    (defun recursion-by-sub2 (i)
      (if (and (integerp i)
               (< 1 i))
          (recursion-by-sub2 (- i 2))
          t))

  Observe that this function recursively decomposes its natural number
  argument by subtracting [47m2[0m from it repeatedly and stops when the
  argument is [47m1[0m or less.  The value of the function is irrelevant; it
  is its induction scheme that concerns us.  The induction scheme
  suggested by [47m(recursion-by-sub2 i)[0m is

    (and (implies (not (and (integerp i) (< 1 i)))   ; base case
                  (:p i))
         (implies (and (and (integerp i) (< 1 i))    ; induction step
                       (:p (- i 2)))
                  (:p i)))

  We can think of the base case as covering two situations.  The first
  is when [47mi[0m is not an integer.  The second is when the integer [47mi[0m is
  less than or equal to [47m1[0m.  In the base case we must prove [47m(:p i)[0m
  without further help.  The induction step deals with those natural
  numbers [47mi[0m greater than [47m1[0m, and inductively assumes the conjecture
  for [47mi-2[0m while proving it for [47mi[0m.  Let us call this scheme
  ``induction on [47mi[0m by twos.''

  Suppose the above [47m:induction[0m rule has been added.  Then an occurrence
  of, say, [47m(* 1/2 k)[0m in a conjecture to be proved by induction would
  suggest, via this rule, an induction on [47mk[0m by twos, provided [47mk[0m was
  known to be a nonnegative integer.  This is because the induction
  rule's [47m:pattern[0m is matched in the conjecture, its [47m:condition[0m is
  satisfied, and the [47m:scheme[0m suggested by the rule is that derived
  from [47m(recursion-by-sub2 k)[0m, which is induction on [47mk[0m by twos.
  Similarly, the term [47m(* 1/2 (length l))[0m would suggest no induction
  via this rule, even though the rule ``fires'' because it creates
  the [47m:scheme[0m [47m(recursion-by-sub2 (length l))[0m which suggests no
  inductions unwinding [47mrecursion-by-sub2[0m (since the controlling
  argument of [47mrecursion-by-sub2[0m in this [47m:scheme[0m is not a variable
  symbol).

  Continuing this example one step further illustrates the utility of
  [47m:induction[0m rules.  We could define the function [47mrecursion-by-cddr[0m
  that suggests the induction scheme decomposing its [47m[consp][0m argument
  two [47m[cdr][0ms at a time.  We could then add the [47m:induction[0m rule
  linking [47m(* 1/2 (length x))[0m to [47m(recursion-by-cddr x)[0m and arrange for
  [47m(* 1/2 (length l))[0m to suggest induction on [47ml[0m by [47m[cddr][0m.

  Observe that [47m:induction[0m rules require no proofs to be done.  Such a
  rule is merely a heuristic link between the [47m:pattern[0m term, which
  may occur in conjectures to be proved by induction, and the [47m:scheme[0m
  term, from which an induction scheme may be derived.  Hence, when
  an [47m:induction[0m rule-class is specified in a [47m[defthm][0m event, the
  theorem proved is irrelevant.  The easiest theorem to prove is, of
  course, [47mt[0m.  Thus, we suggest that when an [47m:induction[0m rule is to be
  created, the following form be used:

    (defthm name T
      :rule-classes ((:induction :pattern pat-term
                                 :condition cond-term
                                 :scheme scheme-term)))

  The name of the rule created is [47m(:induction name)[0m.  When that [rune]
  is [disable]d the heuristic link between [47mpat-term[0m and [47mscheme-term[0m
  is broken.

  Note that if [47mfn[0m is defined recursively and [47m(:induction fn)[0m is
  [disable]d, then normally the induction scheme for [47mfn[0m will not be
  available during a proof.  However, the induction scheme for [47mfn[0m is
  available, even if it is disabled, when it is indicated by
  application of a user-defined [47m:induction[0m rule.  So for the
  [47m:induction[0m rule above, [47mrecursion-by-sub2-induction-rule[0m: even if
  [47m(:induction recursion-by-sub2)[0m is disabled, nevertheless the
  induction scheme for [47mrecursion-by-sub2[0m will be available to apply
  to the instantiated [47m:scheme-term[0m.


