 (INTERN
  (SYMBOLP PACKAGES ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Create a new symbol in a given package

  [47m(intern symbol-name symbol-package-name)[0m returns a symbol with the
  given [47m[symbol-name][0m and the given [47m[symbol-package-name][0m.  We
  restrict Common Lisp's [47mintern[0m so that the second argument is either
  the symbol *main-lisp-package-name*, the value of that constant, or
  is one of \"ACL2\", \"ACL2-INPUT-CHANNEL\", \"ACL2-OUTPUT-CHANNEL\", or
  \"KEYWORD\".  To avoid that restriction, see [intern$].

  In ACL2 [47mintern[0m is actually implemented as a macro that expands to a
  call of a similar function whose second argument is a symbol.
  Invoke [47m:pe intern[0m to see the definition, or see
  [intern-in-package-of-symbol].

  To see why [47mintern[0m is so restricted consider [47m(intern \"X\" \"P\")[0m.  In
  particular, is it a symbol and if so, what is its
  [47m[symbol-package-name][0m?  One is tempted to say ``yes, it is a symbol
  in the package [47m\"P\"[0m.'' But if package [47m\"P\"[0m has not yet been defined,
  that would be premature because the imports to the package are
  unknown.  For example, if [47m\"P\"[0m were introduced with

    (defpkg \"P\" '(COMMON-LISP::X))

  then in Common Lisp [47m(symbol-package-name (intern \"X\" \"P\"))[0m returns
  [47m\"COMMON-LISP\"[0m.

  The obvious restriction on [47mintern[0m is that its second argument be the
  name of a package known to ACL2.  We cannot express such a
  restriction (except, for example, by limiting it to those packages
  known at some fixed time, as we do).  Instead, we provide
  [47m[intern-in-package-of-symbol][0m which requires a ``witness symbol''
  for the package instead of the package.  The witness symbol is any
  symbol (expressible in ACL2) and uniquely specifies a package
  necessarily known to ACL2.")
 (INTERN$
  (SYMBOLP PACKAGES ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Create a new symbol in a given package

  [47mIntern$[0m is a macro that behaves the same as the macro [47m[intern][0m,
  except for weakening the restriction to a fixed set of package
  names so that any package name other than [47m\"\"[0m is legal.  See
  [intern].  Note that if you evaluate a call [47m(intern$ x y)[0m for which
  there is no package with name [47my[0m that is known to ACL2, you will get
  an error.

  [47m(Intern$ x y)[0m expands to:

    (intern-in-package-of-symbol x (pkg-witness y))

  See [intern-in-package-of-symbol] and see [pkg-witness].")
 (INTERN-IN-PACKAGE-OF-SYMBOL
  (SYMBOLP PACKAGES ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Create a symbol with a given name

  Completion Axiom ([47mcompletion-of-intern-in-package-of-symbol[0m):

    (equal (intern-in-package-of-symbol x y)
           (if (and (stringp x)
                    (symbolp y))
               (intern-in-package-of-symbol x y)
             nil))

  [Guard] for [47m(intern-in-package-of-symbol x y)[0m:

    (and (stringp x) (symbolp y))

  Intuitively, [47m(intern-in-package-of-symbol x y)[0m creates a symbol with
  [47m[symbol-name][0m [47mx[0m [intern]ed in the package containing [47my[0m.  More
  precisely, suppose [47mx[0m is a string, [47my[0m is a symbol with
  [47m[symbol-package-name][0m [47mpkg[0m and that the [47m[defpkg][0m event creating [47mpkg[0m
  had the list of symbols [47mimports[0m as the value of its second
  argument.  Then [47m(intern-in-package-of-symbol x y)[0m returns a symbol,
  [47mans[0m, the [47m[symbol-name][0m of [47mans[0m is [47mx[0m, and the [47m[symbol-package-name][0m
  of [47mans[0m is [47mpkg[0m, unless [47mx[0m is the [47m[symbol-name][0m of some member of
  [47mimports[0m with [47m[symbol-package-name][0m [47mipkg[0m, in which case the
  [47m[symbol-package-name][0m of [47mans[0m is [47mipkg[0m.  Because [47m[defpkg][0m requires
  that there be no duplications among the [47m[symbol-name][0ms of the
  imports, [47mintern-in-package-of-symbol[0m is uniquely defined.

  For example, suppose [47m\"MY-PKG\"[0m was created by

    (defpkg \"MY-PKG\" '(ACL2::ABC COMMON-LISP::CAR)).

  Let [47mw[0m be [47m'my-pkg::witness[0m.  Observe that

    (symbolp w) is t                     ; w is a symbol
    (symbol-name w) is \"WITNESS\"         ; w's name is \"WITNESS\"
    (symbol-package-name w) is \"MY-PKG\"  ; w is in the package \"MY-PKG\"

  The construction of [47mw[0m illustrates one way to obtain a symbol in a
  given package: write it down as a constant using the double-colon
  notation.

  But another way to obtain a symbol in a given package is to create it
  with [47mintern-in-package-of-symbol[0m.

    (intern-in-package-of-symbol \"XYZ\" w) is MY-PKG::XYZ

    (intern-in-package-of-symbol \"ABC\" w) is ACL2::ABC

    (intern-in-package-of-symbol \"CAR\" w) is COMMON-LISP::CAR (i.e., ACL2::CAR)

    (intern-in-package-of-symbol \"car\" w) is MY-PKG::|car|")
