 (INTERSECTION$
  (LISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Elements common to the given lists

    General Forms:
    (intersection$ l1 l2 ... lk)
    (intersection$ l1 l2 ... lk :test 'eql) ; same as above
    (intersection$ l1 l2 ... lk :test 'eq)    ; same, but eq is equality test
    (intersection$ l1 l2 ... lk :test 'equal) ; same, but equal is equality test

  [47m(Intersection$ x y)[0m equals a list that contains the [47mmember[0ms of [47mx[0m that
  are also [47mmember[0ms of [47my[0m.  More precisely, the resulting list is the
  result of deleting from [47mx[0m those members that are not members of [47my[0m.
  The optional keyword, [47m:TEST[0m, has no effect logically, but provides
  the test (default [47m[eql][0m) used for comparing members of the two
  lists.

  [47mIntersection$[0m need not take exactly two arguments, though it must
  take at least one argument: [47m(intersection$ x)[0m is [47mx[0m, [47m(intersection$
  x y z ... :test test)[0m is [47m(intersection$ x (intersection$ y z ...
  :test test) :test test)[0m, and if [47m:TEST[0m is not supplied, then
  [47m(intersection$ x y z ...)[0m is [47m(intersection$ x (intersection$ y z
  ...))[0m.  For the discussion below we restrict ourselves, then, to
  the cases [47m(intersection$ x y)[0m and [47m(intersection$ x y :test test)[0m.

  The [guard] for a call of [47mintersection$[0m (in the two cases just above)
  depends on the test.  In all cases, both arguments must satisfy
  [47m[true-listp][0m.  If the test is [47m[eql][0m, then one of the arguments must
  satisfy [47m[eqlable-listp][0m.  If the test is [47m[eq][0m, then one of the
  arguments must satisfy [47m[symbol-listp][0m.

  See [equality-variants] for a discussion of the relation between
  [47mintersection$[0m and its variants:

      [47m(intersection-eq x lst)[0m is equivalent to [47m(intersection$ x lst :test
      'eq)[0m;

      [47m(intersection-equal x lst)[0m is equivalent to [47m(intersection$ x lst
      :test 'equal)[0m.

  In particular, reasoning about any of these primitives reduces to
  reasoning about the function [47mintersection-equal[0m.

  Note that [47mintersection-eq[0m can take any positive number of arguments,
  in analogy to [47mintersection$[0m; indeed, [47m(intersection-eq ...)[0m expands
  to [47m(intersection$ ... :test 'eq)[0m.  However, [47mintersection-equal[0m is a
  function, not a macro, and takes exactly two arguments.

  [47mIntersection$[0m is similar to the Common Lisp primitive [47mintersection[0m.
  However, Common Lisp does not specify the order of elements in the
  result of a call of [47mintersection[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<intersection-equal>

    (defun intersection-equal (l1 l2)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp l1) (true-listp l2))))
      (cond ((endp l1) nil)
            ((member-equal (car l1) l2)
             (cons (car l1)
                   (intersection-equal (cdr l1) l2)))
            (t (intersection-equal (cdr l1) l2))))")
