  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Challenge problem 1 for the new user of ACL2

  Start in a fresh ACL2, either by restarting your ACL2 image from
  scratch or executing

    :ubt! 1

  which will undo everything since the first user event.

  Then define this function:

    (defun rev (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (append (rev (cdr x)) (list (car x)))))

  Then use The Method to prove:

    (defthm triple-rev
      (equal (rev (rev (rev x))) (rev x)))

  When you've solved this problem, compare your answer to ours; see
  [introductory-challenge-problem-1-answer].

  Then, use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to
  [introductory-challenges].")
 (INTRODUCTORY-CHALLENGE-PROBLEM-1-ANSWER
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Answer to challenge problem 1 for the new user of ACL2

  This answer is in the form of an ACL2 script sufficient to lead ACL2
  to a proof.

    (defun rev (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (append (rev (cdr x)) (list (car x)))))

    ; Trying [47mtriple-rev[0m at this point produces a key checkpoint containing
    ; [47m(REV (APPEND (REV (CDR X)) (LIST (CAR X))))[0m, which suggests:

    (defthm rev-append
      (equal (rev (append a b))
             (append (rev b) (rev a))))

    ; And now [47mtriple-rev[0m succeeds.

    (defthm triple-rev
      (equal (rev (rev (rev x))) (rev x)))

    ; An alternative, and more elegant, solution is to prove the [47mrev-rev[0m
    ; instead of [47mrev-append[0m:

    ; (defthm rev-rev
    ;   (implies (true-listp x)
    ;            (equal (rev (rev x)) x)))

    ; [47mRev-rev[0m is also discoverable by The Method because it is
    ; suggested by the statement of [47mtriple-rev[0m itself: [47mrev-rev[0m
    ; simplifies a simpler composition of the functions in [47mtriple-rev[0m.

    ; Both solutions produce lemmas likely to be of use in future proofs
    ; about [47mrev[0m.

  Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [introductory-challenge-problem-1].")
