 (INVARIANT-RISK
  (PROGRAMMING ADVANCED-FEATURES GUARD DEBUGGING)
  "Potential slowdown for [program]-mode updates to [stobj]s or [arrays]

  You may see a warning like this:

    ACL2 Warning [Invariant-risk] in MY-FUNCTION:  Invariant-risk has been
    detected for a call of function MY-FUNCTION (as possibly leading to
    an ill-guarded call of UPDATE-FLD); see :DOC invariant-risk.

  Such warnings indicate potential slowdown due to aggressive
  protection by ACL2 against either:

    * writing a value of the wrong type to a [stobj] field; or

    * performing an out-of-bounds write to an ACL2 [array].

  Whenever a [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m-mode function call can perhaps lead to such a
  write, [guard]-checking is performed by ACL2, even though the
  normal expectation is to execute without such checks in Common
  Lisp; see [evaluation].  Consider the following example.

    (defstobj st (fld :type integer :initially 0))

    (defun f (n st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st :guard (integerp n)))
      (update-fld n st))

    ; This :program-mode wrapper for f fails to require (integerp n):
    (defun g (n st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st :mode :program))
      (f n st))

    ; Produces an invariant-risk warning:
    (g 3 st)

    ; Produces an invariant-risk warning and a guard violation:
    (g 'a st)

  Each of the two calls of [47mg[0m produces an [47m\"Invariant-risk\"[0m warning, and
  indeed [guard]s are checked for the ensuing calls of [47mf[0m, causing a
  guard violation for the second call of [47mg[0m.

  We may say that such [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m-mode functions have invariant-risk.
  Because of how the ``aggressive protection'' discussed above is
  implemented, recursive calls of invariant-risk functions are not
  traced; see [trace$].

  There are two general methods for avoiding such warnings: at runtime
  with [47m[set-check-invariant-risk][0m, and at definition time with
  [47m[set-register-invariant-risk][0m.  We describe each briefly below.
  For more information follow the links just above to their
  respective documentation topics.  For yet more detail about
  invariant-risk see [invariant-risk-details].  For tools that may
  help find sources of invariant-risk, see [community-book]
  [47mbooks/std/system/invariant-risk.lisp[0m.


Controlling runtime checking for invariant-risk

  You can avoid seeing warnings like the one displayed above (without
  affecting the check that is actually performed) by evaluating
  either one of the following forms.

    (set-check-invariant-risk t)
    (set-inhibit-warnings \"invariant-risk\")

  You can also replace such warnings by errors:

    (set-check-invariant-risk :error)

  Evaluate [47m(get-check-invariant-risk state)[0m to see the current setting
  for invariant-risk checking.  For details, including a dangerous
  way to remove invariant-risk checking completely at runtime, see
  [set-check-invariant-risk].


Eliminating invariant-risk checking done by specific functions

  On occasion you may define functions that you know avoid
  invariant-risk danger, even though ACL2 designates those functions
  as having invariant-risk.  Rather than removing invariant-risk
  checking for [3mall[0m functions at runtime with
  [47m[set-check-invariant-risk][0m, it is probably much safer to remove it
  only for the functions in a given book or [47m[encapsulate][0m event.  See
  [set-register-invariant-risk] for how to do that.


Subtopics

  [Invariant-risk-details]
      More details about [invariant-risk]

  [Set-check-invariant-risk]
      Affect certain [program]-mode updates to [stobj]s or [arrays]

  [Set-register-invariant-risk]
      Avoid [invariant-risk] checking for specified functions")
 (INVARIANT-RISK-DETAILS
  (INVARIANT-RISK)
  "More details about [invariant-risk]

  This topic, which may be unnecessary for most ACL2 users, expands on
  the documentation for [invariant-risk].  See that topic and see
  [evaluation] for relevant background.

  We refer below to the following example, which is slightly expanded
  from the one in the documentation for [invariant-risk].

    (defstobj st (fld :type integer :initially 0))

    (defun f1 (n st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st :guard (integerp n)))
      (update-fld n st))

    (defun f2 (n st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st :guard (integerp n)))
      (f1 n st))

    ; This :program-mode wrapper for f fails to require (integerp n):
    (defun g (n st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st :mode :program))
      (f2 n st))

    ; Trace f1 and f2 and their *1* functions.  (This is optional.)
    (trace$ f1 f2 g)

    ; Produces an invariant-risk warning; only *1* functions are called.
    (g 3 st)

    ; Produces an invariant-risk warning and a guard violation:
    (g 'a st)

    ; Defeat invariant-risk checking (risky; uses a trust tag):
    (set-check-invariant-risk nil)

    ; No warning because *1* functions are not called.
    (g 3 st)

    ; No warning because *1* functions are not called.
    (g 'a st)

    ; Non-integer field value!
    (assert-event (equal (fld st) 'a))

  To understand invariant-risk, consider the case that a [stobj]
  updater (such as [47mupdate-fld[0m above) is called on ill-guarded
  arguments.  In that case the resulting ACL2 [state] could be
  ill-formed, as in the example above: after turning off
  invariant-risk checking, the stobj [47mst[0m is defined to have an integer
  field yet winds up with a symbol in that field.  ACL2 arranges
  invariant-risk checking by installing special code in the
  executable-counterpart of a [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m-mode function to prevent it
  from calling its corresponding submitted function.  It does this
  when the [47m:program[0m-mode function has a non-nil [47m'invariant-risk[0m
  property; in the example above, note:

    ACL2 !>(getpropc 'g 'invariant-risk)
    UPDATE-FLD
    ACL2 !>

  When the function symbol [47mg[0m was defined, it inherited this property
  from [47mf2[0m, which in turn inherited it from [47mf1[0m, which in turn
  inherited it from the stobj updater, [47mupdate-fld[0m.  However, the
  aforementioned special code is only installed for [47m:program[0m mode
  functions, hence not for [47mf1[0m or [47mf2[0m.  Consider for example the
  following trace, from the call of [47m(g 3)[0m made above before turning
  off invariant-risk checking.

    1> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G 3 |<st>|)

    ACL2 Warning [Invariant-risk] in G:  Invariant-risk has been detected
    for a call of function G (as possibly leading to an ill-guarded call
    of UPDATE-FLD); see :DOC invariant-risk.

      2> (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F2 3 |<st>|)
        3> (F2 3 |<st>|)
          4> (F1 3 |<st>|)
          <4 (F1 |<st>|)
        <3 (F2 |<st>|)
      <2 (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::F2 |<st>|)
    <1 (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G |<st>|)
    <st>
    ACL2 !>

  This trace illustrates that even though [47mf2[0m has the [47m'invariant-risk[0m
  property, its executable-counterpart has permission to call its
  submitted function because [47mf2[0m is a guard-verified [47m:logic[0m-mode
  function.

  As an optimization, ACL2 avoids setting the [47m'invariant-risk[0m property
  on [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions whose [guard] is [47mt[0m or, more generally, a
  conjunction of [stobj] recognizer calls.  For example, the call of
  [47m(bar 3 st)[0m below does not cause an invariant-risk warning.

    (progn (defstobj st (reg :type (array (unsigned-byte 31) (8))
                             :initially 0))
           (defun foo (i st)
             (declare (xargs :guard t :stobjs st))
             (if (eql i 0) (update-regi i 3 st) st))
           (defun bar (i st)
             (declare (xargs :stobjs st :mode :program))
             (foo i st)))
    (bar 3 st)

  This optimization can hold even if the function is originally
  submitted without guard verification (typically, using [xargs]
  declaration [47m:verify-guards nil[0m), but only if its guards are
  verified before defining the [47m:program[0m-mode wrapper (e.g., [47mbar[0m
  above).  Thus we see an invariant-risk warning in the call of [47mbar[0m
  below.

    (progn (defstobj st (reg :type (array (unsigned-byte 31) (8))
                             :initially 0))
           (defun foo (i st)
             (declare (xargs :guard t :stobjs st :VERIFY-GUARDS NIL))
             (if (eql i 0) (update-regi i 3 st) st))
           (defun bar (i st)
             (declare (xargs :stobjs st :mode :program))
             (foo i st))
           (verify-guards foo) ; avoid invariant-risk by moving before bar
           )
    (bar 3 st) ; invariant-risk

  Here is code that will return a list of all [47m:program[0m-mode functions
  subject to invariant-risk checking that are user-defined (not built
  into ACL2).

    :q ; go into raw Lisp
    (let (ans (wrld (w *the-live-state*)))
      (dolist (p (list-all-packages))
        (do-symbols (sym p)
          (when (and (getpropc sym 'invariant-risk)
                     (programp sym wrld)
                     (not (getpropc sym 'predefined)))
            (push sym ans))))
      ans)

  To see exactly the executable-counterpart code generated for a
  [47m:program[0m-mode function with invariant-risk, evaluate [47m(trace!
  (oneify-cltl-code :native t))[0m before submitting its definition.")
 (INVISIBLE-FNS-TABLE
  (LOOP-STOPPER)
  "Functions that are invisible to the [loop-stopper] algorithm

    Examples:
    ACL2 !>(invisible-fns-table (w state))
    ((binary-+ unary--)
     (binary-* unary-/)
     (unary-- unary--)
     (unary-/ unary-/))

  Among other things, the setting above has the effect of making
  [47m[unary--][0m ``invisible'' for the purposes of applying permutative
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules to [47m[binary-+][0m trees.  Also see [add-invisible-fns]
  and see [remove-invisible-fns], which manage macro aliases (see
  [macro-aliases-table]), as well as see [set-invisible-fns-table].

  See [table] for a general discussion of tables.

  The ``invisible functions [table]'' is an alist with elements of the
  following form, where [47mfn[0m is a function symbol and the [47mufni[0m are
  unary function symbols in the current ACL2 [world], and [47mk[0m is at
  least 1.

    (fn ufn1 ufn2 ... ufnk)

  This [table] thus associates with certain function symbols, e.g., [47mfn[0m
  above, a set of unary functions, e.g., the [47mufni[0m above.  The [47mufni[0m
  associated with [47mfn[0m in the invisible functions table are said to be
  ``invisible with respect to [47mfn[0m.'' If [47mfn[0m is not the [47m[car][0m of any
  pair in the [47malist[0m, then no function is invisible for it.  Thus for
  example, setting the invisible functions alist to [47mnil[0m completely
  eliminates the consideration of invisibility.

  The notion of invisibility is involved in the use of the
  [47m:[0m[47m[loop-stopper][0m field of [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules to prevent the indefinite
  application of permutative rewrite rules.  Roughly speaking, if
  rewrite rules are being used to permute [47marg[0m and (ufni arg) inside
  of a nest of [47mfn[0m calls, and [47mufni[0m is invisible with respect to [47mfn[0m,
  then [47marg[0m and [47m(ufni arg)[0m are considered to have the same ``weight''
  and will be permuted so as to end up as adjacent tips in the [47mfn[0m
  nest.  See [loop-stopper].")
 (IO
  (INTERFACING-TOOLS STATE PROGRAMMING)
  "Input/output facilities in ACL2

    Example:
    (mv-let
      (channel state)
      (open-input-channel \"foo.lisp\" :object state)
      (mv-let (eofp obj state)
              (read-object channel state)
              (.
                .
                 (let ((state (close-input-channel channel state)))
                       (mv final-ans state))..)))

  Also see [std/io] and [file-reading-example].

  For advanced ways to control printing, see [print-control].

  For a discussion of formatted printing, see [fmt].

  To control ACL2 abbreviation (``evisceration'') of objects before
  printing them, see [set-evisc-tuple], see [without-evisc], and see
  [set-iprint].

  To redirect output to a file, see [output-to-file].

  ACL2 supports input and output facilities equivalent to a subset of
  those found in Common Lisp.  ACL2 does not support random access to
  files (with one exception: see [read-file-into-string]) or
  bidirectional streams.  In Common Lisp, input and output are to or
  from objects of type [47mstream[0m.  In ACL2, input and output are to or
  from objects called ``channels,'' which are actually symbols.
  Although a channel is a symbol, one may think of it intuitively as
  corresponding to a Common Lisp stream.  Channels are in one of two
  ACL2 packages, [47m\"ACL2-INPUT-CHANNEL\"[0m and [47m\"ACL2-OUTPUT-CHANNEL\"[0m.
  When one ``opens'' a file one gets back a channel whose
  [47m[symbol-name][0m is the file name passed to ``open,'' postfixed with
  [47m-n[0m, where [47mn[0m is a counter that is incremented every time an open or
  close occurs.

  There are three channels which are open from the beginning and which
  cannot be closed:

    acl2-input-channel::standard-character-input-0
    acl2-input-channel::standard-object-input-0
    acl2-input-channel::standard-character-output-0

  All three of these are really Common Lisp's [47m*standard-input*[0m or
  [47m*standard-output*[0m, appropriately.

  For convenience, three global variables are bound to these rather
  tedious channel names:

    *standard-ci*
    *standard-oi*
    *standard-co*

  Common Lisp permits one to open a stream for several different kinds
  of [47mio[0m, e.g. character or byte.  ACL2 permits an additional type
  called ``object''.  In ACL2 an ``io-type'' is a keyword, either
  [47m:character[0m, [47m:byte[0m, or [47m:object[0m.  When one opens a file, one
  specifies a type, which determines the kind of io operations that
  can be done on the channel returned.  The types [47m:character[0m and
  [47m:byte[0m are familiar.  Type [47m:object[0m is an abstraction not found in
  Common Lisp.  An [47m:object[0m file is a file of Lisp objects.  One uses
  [47mread-object[0m or [47mread-object-with-case[0m (see below) to read from
  [47m:object[0m files and [47m[print-object$][0m, its more flexible variant
  [47m[print-object$+][0m, or [47mprint-object$-preserving-case[0m (see below;
  also, [47mprint-object$-fn[0m) to print to [47m:object[0m files.  (The reading
  and printing are really done with the Common Lisp [47mread[0m and printing
  functions.  For those familiar with [47mread[0m, we note that the
  [47mrecursive-p[0m argument is [47mnil[0m.)  The function [47mread-object-suppress[0m is
  logically the same as [47mread-object[0m except that [47mread-object-suppress[0m
  throws away the second returned value, i.e. the value that would
  normally be read, simply returning [47m(mv eof state)[0m; under the hood,
  [47mread-object-suppress[0m avoids errors, for example those caused by
  encountering symbols in packages unknown to ACL2.

  The functions [47mread-object-with-case[0m is defined logically simply to be
  [47mread-object[0m, while the function [47mprint-object$-preserving-case[0m and
  macro [47m[print-object$+][0m are defined logically simply to be
  [47mprint-object$[0m.  However, these variants generally do I/O
  differently (except that when the host Lisp is GCL,
  [47mprint-object$-preserving-case[0m behaves the same as [47mprint-object$[0m).
  For [47mread-object-with-case[0m the value that is read is affected by an
  extra argument, namely, the second argument: the [3mmode[0m.  The mode is
  one of the keywords [47m:upcase[0m, [47m:downcase[0m, [47m:preserve[0m, or [47m:invert[0m,
  where [47m:upcase[0m gives the same behavior as [47mread-object[0m, and the other
  three modes are handled according to the specification for the
  Common Lisp function, [47mreadtable-case[0m (see for example {the Common
  Lisp HyperSpec's documentation for ``Examples of Effect of
  Readtable Case on the Lisp Reader'' |
  http://www.lispworks.com/documentation/HyperSpec/Body/23_aba.htm}).
  The function [47mprint-object$-preserving-case[0m is somewhat analogous:
  it is defined logically to be [47mprint-object$[0m and it has the same
  behavior, except that symbols are printed without adding escapes
  merely because of lowercase letters; for example, the symbol in the
  current package with name [47m\"abc\"[0m will be printed as [47mabc[0m, not as
  [47m|abc|[0m.  But note that [47mprint-object$-preserving-case[0m may still
  insert vertical bars, depending on the host Lisp, because different
  Lisp implementations choose to escape symbols differently.
  Consider the symbol typically printed as [47m|1u|[0m.  Using
  [47mprint-object$-preserving-case[0m, this prints simply as [47m1u[0m in CCL and
  Allegro CL, but it is printed as [47m|1u|[0m in SBCL, LispWorks, and CMUCL
  --- at least in the implementations that we tested!

  [47mFile-name[0m arguments are strings (except for the [47m:STRING[0m case
  discussed below).  ACL2 does not support the Common Lisp type
  [47m[pathname][0m; rather, the underlying host Lisp will interpret the
  given string as a pathname.  If the string represents a relative
  pathname, it will be elaborated to a full pathname using the
  connected book directory; see [cbd].  You can do that elaboration
  yourself with a directory [47mdir[0m using [47m(extend-pathname dir file-name
  state)[0m; see [extend-pathname].

  For the [47mfile-name[0m argument of the output-related functions listed
  below, ACL2 supports a special value, [47m:STRING[0m.  For this value, the
  channel connects (by way of a Common Lisp output string stream) to
  a string rather than to a file: as characters are written to the
  channel they can be retrieved by using [47mget-output-stream-string$[0m.

  Here are the names, formals and output descriptions of the ACL2 io
  functions.

    Input Functions:
      (open-input-channel (file-name io-type state) (mv channel state))
      (open-input-channel-p (channel io-type state) boolean)
      (close-input-channel (channel state) state)
      (read-char$ (channel state) (mv char/nil state)) ; nil for EOF
      (peek-char$ (channel state) char/nil)
      (read-byte$ (channel state) (mv byte/nil state)) ; nil for EOF
      (read-object (channel state) (mv eof-read-flg obj-read state))
      (read-object-with-case (channel mode state) (mv eof-read-flg obj-read state))
      (read-object-suppress (channel state) (mv eof-read-flg state))
      (read-file-into-string (file-name &key..) string/nil) ; macro (reads state)

    Output Functions:
      (open-output-channel  (file-name io-type state) (mv channel state))
      (open-output-channel! (file-name io-type state) (mv channel state))
      (open-output-channel-p (channel io-type state) boolean)
      (close-output-channel (channel state) state)
      (princ$ (obj channel state) state)
      (write-byte$ (byte channel state) state)
      (print-object$ (obj channel state) state)
      (print-object$+ (obj channel &key ...) state)
      (print-object$-preserving-case (obj channel state) state)
      (print-object$-fn (obj serialize-character channel state) state)
      (fms  (string alist channel state evisc-tuple) state)
      (fms! (string alist channel state evisc-tuple) state)
      (fmt  (string alist channel state evisc-tuple) (mv col state))
      (fmt! (string alist channel state evisc-tuple) (mv col state))
      (fmt1 (string alist col channel state evisc-tuple) (mv col state))
      (fmt1! (string alist col channel state evisc-tuple) (mv col state))
      (cw (string arg0 arg1 ... argn) nil)
      (get-output-stream-string$ (channel state
                                  &optional (close-p 't)
                                            (ctx ''get-output-stream-string$))
                                 (mv erp string state))

  Note that [47mopen-output-channel[0m and [47mopen-output-channel![0m will attempt
  to create directories as needed (but this is not the case for
  [47mopen-input-channel[0m).  For example, the following can succeed in
  writing to the indicated file by creating subdirectory [47m\"dir4\"[0m if
  that directory does not already exist.

    (mv-let
      (channel state)
      (open-output-channel \"dir4/foo4\" :object state)
      (pprogn (fms \"Here: ~x0\"
                   (list (cons #\\0 (make-list 10)))
                   channel state nil)
              (close-output-channel channel state)))

  The ``formatting'' functions are particularly useful; see [fmt] and
  see [cw].  In particular, [47m[cw][0m prints to a ``comment window'' and
  does not involve the ACL2 [47m[state][0m, so many may find it easier to
  use than [47m[fmt][0m and its variants.  The functions [47m[fms!][0m, [47m[fmt!][0m, and
  [47m[fmt1!][0m are the same as their respective functions without the
  ``[47m![0m,'' except that the ``[47m![0m'' functions are guaranteed to print
  forms that can be read back in (at a slight readability cost).

  When one enters ACL2 with [47m(lp)[0m, input and output are taken from
  [47m[*standard-oi*][0m to [47m[*standard-co*][0m.  Because these are synonyms for
  [47m*standard-input*[0m and [47m*standard-output*[0m, one can drive ACL2 io off
  of arbitrary Common Lisp streams, bound to [47m*standard-input*[0m and
  [47m*standard-output*[0m before entry to ACL2.

  The macro [47mget-output-stream-string$[0m returns the string accumulated
  into the given channel.  By default, a call of this macro closes
  the supplied output channel.  However, a third argument is optional
  (default [47mt[0m), and if it evaluates to [47mnil[0m then the channel remains
  open.  The fourth argument is an optional context, which generally
  evaluates to a symbol, for error reporting.  The following example
  illustrates.

    ACL2 !>
    (mv-let
       (channel state)
       (open-output-channel :string :object state)
       (pprogn (print-object$-fn 17 nil channel state)
               (print-object$-fn '(a b (c d)) nil channel state)
               (er-let*
                 ((str1 (get-output-stream-string$
                         channel state
                         nil))) ; keep the channel open
                 (pprogn (print-object$-fn 23 nil channel state)
                         (print-object$-fn '((e f)) nil channel state)
                         (er-let* ; close the channel
                           ((str2 (get-output-stream-string$ channel state)))
                           (value (cons str1 str2)))))))
     (\"
    17
    (A B (C D))\" . \"
    23
    ((E F))\")
    ACL2 !>

  Also see [printing-to-strings] for a discussion of formatted printing
  functions such as [47mfmt-to-string[0m that do not take a channel or
  [47m[state][0m argument and return a string.

  By default, symbols are printed in upper case when vertical bars are
  not required, as specified by Common Lisp.  See [set-print-case]
  for how to get ACL2 to print symbols in lower case.

  By default, numbers are printed in radix 10 (base 10).  See
  [set-print-base-radix] for how to get ACL2 to print numbers in
  radix 2, 8, or 16.

  To see the [guard] of an IO function, or indeed any function, see
  [args] or call the function [47mguard[0m; but some built-in functions
  (including some IO functions) will print the result using the
  variable [47mSTATE-STATE[0m.  While that is logically correct, if you want
  to execute the guard then you should replace that variable by [47mSTATE[0m
  and also replace each built-in function symbol of the form [47mxxx-p1[0m
  by corresponding function symbol [47mxxx-p[0m.  Consider the following
  example.

    ACL2 !>:args princ$

    Function         PRINC$
    Formals:         (X CHANNEL STATE-STATE)
    Signature:       (PRINC$ * * STATE)
                     => STATE
    Guard:           (AND (OR (ACL2-NUMBERP X)
                              (CHARACTERP X)
                              (STRINGP X)
                              (SYMBOLP X))
                          (STATE-P1 STATE-STATE)
                          (SYMBOLP CHANNEL)
                          (OPEN-OUTPUT-CHANNEL-P1 CHANNEL
                                                  :CHARACTER STATE-STATE))
    Guards Verified: T
    Defun-Mode:      :logic
    Type:            (CONSP (PRINC$ X CHANNEL STATE-STATE))
    Documentation available via :DOC
     PRINC$
    ACL2 !>(untranslate (guard 'princ$ nil (w state)) t (w state))
    (AND (OR (ACL2-NUMBERP X)
             (CHARACTERP X)
             (STRINGP X)
             (SYMBOLP X))
         (STATE-P1 STATE-STATE)
         (SYMBOLP CHANNEL)
         (OPEN-OUTPUT-CHANNEL-P1 CHANNEL
                                 :CHARACTER STATE-STATE))
    ACL2 !>

  If you want to execute the guard for [47m[princ$][0m, then according to the
  suggestion above, you should consider the guard for [47m(princ$ x
  channel state)[0m to be as follows.

    (AND (OR (ACL2-NUMBERP X)
             (CHARACTERP X)
             (STRINGP X)
             (SYMBOLP X))
         (STATE-P STATE)
         (SYMBOLP CHANNEL)
         (OPEN-OUTPUT-CHANNEL-P CHANNEL :CHARACTER STATE))

  For example, we can check the guard for a given value and channel as
  follows.

    ACL2 !>(let ((x 3) (channel *standard-co*))
             (AND (OR (ACL2-NUMBERP X)
                      (CHARACTERP X)
                      (STRINGP X)
                      (SYMBOLP X))
                  (STATE-P STATE)
                  (SYMBOLP CHANNEL)
                  (OPEN-OUTPUT-CHANNEL-P CHANNEL :CHARACTER STATE)))
    T
    ACL2 !>

  Comment for advanced users: Function [47m[open-output-channel!][0m is
  identical as a function to [47mopen-output-channel[0m, except that the
  former may be called even during [47m[make-event][0m expansion and
  [47m[clause-processor][0m [hints], but requires that there is an active
  trust tag (see [defttag]).

  Finally, we note that the [std/io] library contains useful file io
  functions whose definitions illustrate some of the features
  described above, as does the definition of [47m[write-list][0m in
  [community-book] [47mbooks/misc/file-io.lisp[0m.


