 (KEYWORD-COMMANDS
  (LD)
  "How keyword commands like [47m:u[0m and [47m:pbt[0m are processed

    Examples:
    user type-in                 form evaluated
    :pc 5                        (ACL2::PC '5)
    :pcs app rev                 (ACL2::PCS 'app 'rev)
    :length (1 2 3)              (ACL2::LENGTH '(1 2 3))
    :quit                        (ACL2::QUIT) ; Note: avoid optional argument

  When a keyword, [47m:key[0m, is read as a command, ACL2 determines whether
  the symbol with the same name in the [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package, [47macl2::key[0m, is
  a function or simple macro of n arguments.  If so, ACL2 reads [47mn[0m
  more objects, [47mobj1[0m, ..., [47mobjn[0m, and then acts as though it had read
  the following form (for a given [47mkey[0m):

    (ACL2::key 'obj1 ... 'objn)

  Thus, by using the keyword command hack you avoid typing the
  parentheses, the [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package name, and the quotation marks.

  See [ld-keyword-aliases] for how to customize this behavior.

  Note the generality of this hack.  Any function or macro in the
  [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package can be so invoked, not just ``commands.'' Indeed,
  there is no such thing as a distinguished class of commands.  Users
  may take advantage of the keyword command hack by defining
  functions and macros in the [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package.

  The one caveat is that when the keyword hack is used to invoke a
  macro, only the required arguments for that macro are read before
  calling that macro: none of the [47m&optional[0m, [47m&rest[0m, [47m&body[0m, or [47m&key[0m
  arguments are read for that call.  The macro is thus called with
  only its required arguments.  The following log illustrates this
  caveat.

    ACL2 !>:set-iprint t

    ACL2 Query (:SET-IPRINT):  Action  (T, NIL, RESET, RESET-ENABLE, SAME,
    Q or ?):
    ACL2 Observation in SET-IPRINT:  Iprinting has been enabled.
    ACL2 !>

  What happened?  First, the command [47m:set-iprint[0m was read.  Since the
  macro [47m[set-iprint][0m has no required arguments, the ACL2 evaluator
  was then called on the form [47m(set-iprint)[0m, that is, calling the
  macro on no arguments.  [47mSet-iprint[0m is defined to query the ACL2
  user when its first argument is omitted.  The log shows that query,
  which is set up to read the next form from the input stream.  That
  form was available immediately: the form [47mt[0m that had been supplied
  by the user.  So the query returned immediately and the [47mset-iprint[0m
  call was completed.")
