 (KWOTE
  (TERM ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Quote an arbitrary object

  For any object [47mx[0m, [47m(kwote x)[0m returns the two-element list whose
  elements are the symbol [47mquote[0m and the given [47mx[0m, respectively.  The
  guard of [47m(kwote x)[0m is [47mt[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<kwote>

    (defun kwote (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (mbe :logic (list 'quote x)
           :exec (cond ((eq x nil) *nil*)
                       ((eq x t) *t*)
                       ((eql x 0) *0*)
                       ((eql x 1) *1*)
                       ((eql x -1) *-1*)
                       (t (list 'quote x)))))")
 (KWOTE-LST
  (TERM ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Quote an arbitrary true list of objects

  The function [47mkwote-lst[0m applies the function [47mkwote[0m to each element of
  a given list.  The guard of [47m(kwote-lst lst)[0m is [47m(true-listp lst)[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<kwote-lst>

    (defun kwote-lst (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp lst)))
      (cond ((endp lst) nil)
            (t (cons (kwote (car lst))
                     (kwote-lst (cdr lst))))))")
 (L<
  (TERM APPLY$)
  "Ordering on naturals or lists of naturals

  The function [47ml<[0m is a straightforward ordering relation that compares
  two objects, each of which is a natural number or a list of natural
  numbers.  It may be convenient to apply [47mlex-fix[0m to two objects
  before comparing them with [47ml<[0m.  Below are the relevant definitions.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<l<>

    (defun l< (x y)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (lexp x) (lexp y))))
      (or (< (len x) (len y))
          (and (= (len x) (len y))
               (if (atom x) (< x y) (d< x y)))))

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<lexp>

    (defun lexp (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (or (natp x)
          (and (consp x) (nat-listp x))))

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<d<>

    (defun d< (x y)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (nat-listp x) (nat-listp y))))
      (and (consp x)
           (consp y)
           (or (< (car x) (car y))
               (and (= (car x) (car y))
                    (d< (cdr x) (cdr y))))))

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<lex-fix>

    (defun lex-fix (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (cond ((atom x) (nfix x))
            (t (nfix-list x))))

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<nfix-list>

    (defun nfix-list (list)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (if (consp list)
          (cons (nfix (car list))
                (nfix-list (cdr list)))
        nil))")
 (LAMBDA (TERM APPLY$)
  "Lambda expressions, [47mLAMBDA[0m objects, and [47mlambda$[0m expressions

  The word ``lambda'' occurs in several different contexts in ACL2.
  When we are being precise our meanings are as outlined below.

    * lambda expression -- This phrase is used to describe the syntactic
      entity beginning with the symbol [47mlambda[0m that is allowed to
      occupy the ``function'' position in an ACL2 [term].  Lambda
      expressions are most often created when [47mlet[0m expressions are
      translated into their formal counterparts.  We provide an
      example below.

    * [47mLAMBDA[0m object -- An ACL2 list constant interpreted as a ``function''
      by [47m[apply$][0m.  [47mLAMBDA[0m objects may be written in terms by quoting
      them.  However, we urge the user to introduce [47mLAMBDA[0m objects
      into terms by using the built-in macro [47m[lambda$][0m.  We provide
      examples below.

    * [47mlambda$[0m expressions -- These are untranslated terms beginning with
      the macro symbol [47mlambda$[0m.  They expand during translation to
      quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects.  We provide examples below.

  These three phrases are very similar but mean very different things.
  You should read carefully when you see us talk about lambda things!
  Unfortunately, we're not always as precise as we might be so you
  might have to disambiguate our usage by context.  If you see places
  in the documentation where you think we've messed up, please bring
  them to our attention!


About Lambda Expressions

  Consider the function [47modd-evenp[0m, defined with

    (defun odd-evenp (x)
      (if (zp x)
          -1
          (let ((ans (odd-evenp (- x 1))))
            (* (+ ans 1) (- ans 1)))))

  Distracting Aside: Can you see why we gave this function this name?
  Hint: We might have named it ``weird [47mevenp[0m.''

  The translated body of [47modd-evenp[0m is

    (if (zp x)
        '-1
        ((lambda (ans)
                 (binary-* (binary-+ ans '1)
                           (binary-+ '-1 ans)))
         (odd-evenp (binary-+ '-1 x))))

  The syntactic entity in the function position of the term in the
  false branch, namely

    (lambda (ans)
      (binary-* (binary-+ ans '1)
                (binary-+ '-1 ans)))

  is a lambda expression.

  Lambda expressions are integral to the formal representation of
  terms.  They are the formal mechanism by which local variables are
  introduced and thus allow repeated references to intermediate
  results without causing recomputation.  In ACL2 they obey the rules
  of Common Lisp.  In particular, while defining a recursive function
  it is allowed to call the function recursively within the lambda
  expression, e.g., to temporarily save the value of a recursive call
  for repeated use.  (This is not illustrated by [47modd-evenp[0m where the
  recursive call is outside of the lambda expression.)  For more
  details on the formal representation of ACL2 terms, see [term].


About [47mLAMBDA[0m Objects

  Prior to Version 8.0 when [47mapply$[0m was introduced, ``lambda
  expression'' was the only phrase the ACL2 developers used
  mentioning the word ``lambda.'' Some Community Books introduced
  various terms or objects mentioning the word but that is beyond the
  scope of this documentation.  Because ``lambda'' occurred in no
  other context before [47mapply$[0m we are not confident that every
  reference to what we are now calling ``lambda expressions'' were
  called by that precise phrase in old documentation.  If you see a
  place where we refer to these entities by another phrase, please
  let us know!

  When [47m[apply$][0m was introduced, [47mLAMBDA[0m objects became a formally
  supported concept in the ACL2 implementation and we started using
  ``[47mLAMBDA[0m objects'' to refer to them.  A [47mLAMBDA[0m object is generally
  a list, either of the form [47m(LAMBDA vars body)[0m or [47m(LAMBDA vars dcl
  body)[0m.  There are additional restrictions on [47mvars[0m, [47mdcl[0m, and [47mbody[0m
  that we discuss later.  But [47mapply$[0m treats any [47mconsp[0m object and
  tries to extract those components by rudimentary pattern matching.
  An example of a [47mLAMBDA[0m object is the list of length three [47m(LAMBDA
  (x) (BINARY-+ '1 X))[0m.

  Generally speaking when [47mLAMBDA[0m objects occur in translated terms they
  are [47mquote[0md, as in

    (collect$ '(LAMBDA (X) (BINARY-+ '1 X)) lst)

  To highlight the fact that these objects are constants, we try to
  write them in UPPERCASE and [47mtypewriter[0m font in this documentation.
  For the same reason, we generally write ``[47mLAMBDA[0m object'' rather
  than ``lambda object.''

  But of course there's no difference between the symbol [47mLAMBDA[0m and the
  symbol [47mlambda[0m.  Furthermore, [47mLAMBDA[0m objects need not be quoted.
  From a logical perspective they could just be consed up because
  they are just ordinary ACL2 list constants.  Their ``lambda''
  status comes from being treated as functions by [47mapply$[0m.  So one
  could write

    (collect$ (list 'lambda '(x) '(binary-+ '1 x)) lst)

  and we would say that the value of the term in the first argument of
  [47mcollect$[0m is a [47mLAMBDA[0m object.

  Beware, however, that consing up [47mLAMBDA[0m objects defeats the [ilk]
  analysis in [47m[defwarrant][0m and the [tame]ness analysis of [47mapply$[0m and
  hence prevents functions containing such terms from being [47mapply$[0md.

  According to the definitional axiom defining [47m[apply$][0m, any object
  satisfying [47mconsp[0m is treated as a [47mLAMBDA[0m object.  [47mApply$[0m uses
  ``accessor'' functions to extract the ``formals'' and ``body'' of
  the object and proceeds to [47m[ev$][0m the body in an alist binding the
  formals.  But [47mev$[0m insists that the expression object being
  evaluated be [tame] or else assigns it a default value.  This
  insistence on tameness is due to fundamental logical reasons;
  otherwise, [47mapply$[0m would allow us to prove [47mNIL[0m.  So axiomatically
  [47mapply$[0m operates as ``naively expected'' only on [47mtamep-lambdap[0m
  objects.  One consequence of this, which we expect will be a minor
  inconvenience, is that unlike ACL2's lambda expressions [47mapply$[0m's
  [47mLAMBDA[0m objects, when used in definitions of new functions, may not
  include recursive calls of the function being defined because they
  fail the tameness test.

  But wait!  There's more.  Execution efficiency of [47mapply$[0m imposes some
  non-logical restrictions.  These restrictions come from ACL2's
  execution story with respect to Common Lisp, and from the Common
  Lisp compiler.  To execute [47mLAMBDA[0m objects most efficiently they
  must be well-formed, which is a concept even stronger than
  tameness.  Among other requirements, well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m objects
  obey the ACL2 and Common Lisp rules on variable names (not every
  symbol is a legal variable), on the use of free variables, on the
  body being a fully translated formal term, that the declarations,
  if any, be meaningful to the Common Lisp compiler, etc.  You can
  read about well-formedness in [47m[well-formed-lambda-objectp][0m if you
  want, but we don't encourage beginners to go there!

  Note: Even well-formedness is not enough to guarantee execution of
  compiled code.  The [47mLAMBDA[0m object must also be guard verified (see
  [47m[verify-guards][0m for a discussion) and its guard must be satisfied
  by the arguments to which it is applied.

  Note: A peculiar aspect of [47mLAMBDA[0m objects is that they can be written
  as legal ACL2 constants [3mbefore[0m they are well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m objects,
  e.g., by referring to undefined functions, [47m:program[0m mode functions,
  unbadged functions, etc.  They are, after all, just arbitrary
  quoted objects and any value in ACL2 can be quoted.  An ill-formed
  [47mLAMBDA[0m object can [3mbecome[0m well-formed if the world is appropriately
  extended, e.g., the appropriate [47mdefun[0ms or [47mdefwarrant[0ms are made.
  Perhaps worse, they can be well-formed and then [3mbecome[0m ill-formed
  by an undo.  So at runtime [47mapply$[0m has to check that the function
  symbol or [47mLAMBDA[0m object is appropriate.  There is a sophisticated
  cache behind the execution machinery for [47mLAMBDA[0m objects in the
  evaluation theory.  See [47m[print-cl-cache][0m.


About Lambda$ Expressions

  Rather than force users to type well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m objects as quoted
  constants, ACL2 provides a macro allowing you to enter [47mLAMBDA[0m
  objects by typing something that looks like a lambda expression but
  which is properly translated and generates well-formed results (or
  causes a translation error).

  That macro -- which is not really a defined macro but is built into
  ACL2's translation mechanism -- is called [47m[lambda$][0m and uses of it
  in terms are called ``[47mlambda$[0m expressions.'' [47mLambda$[0m expressions
  may only be used in argument slots of [ilk] [47m:FN[0m.

  An example of a [47mlambda$[0m expression is the first argument of [47mcollect$[0m
  in

    (collect$ (lambda$ (x) (+ 1 x)) lst)

  That [47mlambda$[0m expression essentially translates to the quoted
  well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m object

    '(LAMBDA (X) (BINARY-+ '1 X))

  Note that the body is fully translated, unlike its appearance in the
  [47mlambda$[0m expression.  We say ``essentially'' because [47mlambda$[0m always
  add a [47m(DECLARE (IGNORABLE v1 ... vn))[0m that includes every formal.
  In addition, except when translating in theorems, [47mlambda$[0m tags the
  translated body with a [47m[return-last][0m expression to indicate it came
  from a [47mlambda$[0m.  Despite these differences, the meaning of the
  translated [47mlambda$[0m is the simple quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m object shown.

  [47mLambda$[0m expressions never appear in a fully translated term.  All the
  [47mlambda$[0m objects will have been translated into quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m
  objects.

  The body of a [47mlambda$[0m expression must return a single value that is
  neither a [stobj] nor a [df].  The following example illustrates
  this point.

    (defun$ f1 (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (mv x x))

    ; ERROR!  The body of the lambda$ returns two values.
    (defun f2 (y)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (apply$ (lambda$ (x) (f1 x))
              (list y)))

    ; Succeeds.
    (defun f2 (y) (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (apply$ '(lambda (x) (f1 x))
              (list y)))

  Finally, to see how a [47mlambda$[0m expression translates, see [47m[translam][0m.")
 (LAMBDA$ (APPLY$)
  "Lambda object constructor for use with [47mapply$[0m

  [47mLambda$[0m is a built-in ACL2 ``macro'' that allows you to enter
  well-formed fully-translated quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects in argument
  positions of ilk [47m:FN[0m.  We urge you to use [47mlambda$[0m instead of trying
  to type quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects meant for use by [47mapply$[0m.  We explain
  and document [47mlambda$[0m below.

  Intuitively, a quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m object is a quoted constant like

    '(LAMBDA (X) (BINARY-+ '1 X))

  e.g., a quoted constant beginning with the symbol [47mLAMBDA[0m and listing
  some formal variables, possibly some declarations, and a fully
  translated body satisfying various rules.  [47m[Apply$][0m can handle
  quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects provided they have the right basic shape and
  all the listed formals are symbols and the bodies are [tame].  But
  it is difficult to type fully translated bodies and, for runtime
  efficiency, it is important that the quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects satisfy
  additional (logically unnecessary) well-formedness restrictions
  allowing faster guard checking and compilation.

  One should strive to always enter ``well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m objects.'' The
  details of well-formedness may be found in
  [47m[well-formed-lambda-objectp][0m but our hope is that mastering those
  details is unnecessary because ACL2 provides a built-in ``macro,''
  [47mlambda$[0m, for constructing quoted well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m objects.  We
  urge you to use [47mlambda$[0m instead of typing quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects!
  That is, write [47m(lambda$ (x) (+ 1 x))[0m instead of [47m'(LAMBDA (X)
  (BINARY-+ '1 X))[0m.

  [47mLambda$[0m [3mterms may only appear in argument slots of ilk[0m [47m:FN[0m!

    Examples:
    (lambda$ (x y) (append x (list y)))

    (lambda$ (n lst str)
             (declare (type integer n)
                      (type string str)
                      (xargs :guard (and (posp n)
                                         (true-listp lst)
                                         (< (- n 1) (length lst)))))
             (nth (- n 1) lst))

    General Form:
    (lambda$ vars dcl* body)

  where the [47mlambda$[0m expression occurs in an argument position of ilk
  [47m:FN[0m, [47mvars[0m is a list of distinct variable names, [47mdcl*[0m is zero or
  more [47mDECLARE[0m forms as described below, and [47mbody[0m is a term returning
  the appropriate number of values, which is currently always 1
  except when used in the translation of an expression of the form
  [47m(loop$ ... DO ...)[0m.  [47mBody[0m must satisfy the same restrictions one
  would expect in a non-recursive [47m[defun][0m event with the same
  formals, declarations and body.  In particular, [47mbody[0m should contain
  no free variables other than those listed in [47mvars[0m.  [47mLambda$[0m always
  adds a declaration that every formal is ignorable and, hence, we
  prohibit you from adding [47mignore[0m or [47mignorable[0m declarations in the
  [47mlambda$[0m expression itself.  [47mLambda$[0m expands to a well-formed quoted
  [47mLAMBDA[0m object or else causes a translate-time error.

  The allowed [47mDECLARE[0m forms in [47mlambda$[0m are [47mtype[0m and [47mxargs[0m.
  Furthermore, the only [47m[xargs][0m keywords allowed are [47m:guard[0m and
  [47m:split-types[0m.  The other [47mXARGS[0m keywords, such as [47m:measure[0m, [47m:hints[0m
  or [47m:guard-hints[0m, play no role.


About [47mLambda$[0ms and Prover Output

  The translated form of a [47mlambda$[0m expression is a quoted [47m[lambda][0m
  object.  For example, [47m(collect$ (lambda$ (x) (+ 1 x)) lst)[0m
  translates to

    (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (X)
                     (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X))
                     (RETURN-LAST 'PROGN
                                  '(LAMBDA$ (X) (+ 1 X))
                                  (BINARY-+ '1 X)))
              LST)

  The [47mlambda$[0m has been replaced by a quoted [47mlambda[0m.

  The prover tries to print each quoted [47mlambda[0m object (that occurs an
  argument position of [ilk] :[47mFN[0m) as a [47mlambda$[0m expression that is
  (provably) functionally equal (see [47m[fn-equal][0m) to the original
  [47mlambda[0m object assuming the necessary warrants.  If the quoted
  [47mlambda[0m object was produced by the expansion of a [47mlambda$[0m expression
  and has not been simplified by subsequent rewriting, it will print
  as the original [47mlambda$[0m expression.  This can be unfortunate if the
  original [47mlambda$[0m expression was itself produced by a macro and
  contains logically irrelevant (but operationally important) tags.
  This phenomenon occurs most often when [47m[do-loop$][0ms are involved.

  Furthermore, since you are allowed to type in quoted [47mlambda[0m objects
  directly, you may --- or may not --- see them printed by the prover
  as [47mlambda$[0m expressions, depending on whether a suitable [47mlambda$[0m is
  found.  If a quoted [47mlambda[0m object contains a reference to a
  function symbol for which no [47m[warrant][0m has been issued there is
  probably no provably equivalent [47mlambda$[0m.

  The main lessons here are

    * you should use [47mlambda$[0m rather than quoted [47mlambda[0m objects in your
      prover input,

    * you should make sure to warrant every user-defined function in your
      [47mlambda$[0m expressions, and

    * if you see a quoted [47mlambda[0m object rather than a [47mlambda$[0m expression in
      your prover output, that quoted [47mlambda[0m object probably involves
      unwarranted symbols which will make it impossible to prove
      anything interesting about it.

  If you do not want the prover output to give special treatment to
  quoted [47mlambda[0m objects in :[47mFN[0m slots, do

    (defattach-system (untranslate-lambda-object-p
                      constant-nil-function-arity-0))

  With this attachment, the prover will print all quoted [47mlambda[0m objects
  as it would any other quoted constant.  You will see what is
  actually there.  One drawback is that the resulting formulas cannot
  always be read back in and translated because of a prohibition on
  ``counterfeiting'' expansions of [47mlambda$[0m.  See
  [gratuitous-lambda-object-restrictions].


About Guard Verification of Lambda Objects

  Quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects, whether produced by hand (don't!) or by
  [47mlambda$[0m may have guards.  If the [47mLAMBDA[0m object is well-formed its
  guard plays the same role the guard of a defined function symbol
  plays when the object is [47mapply$[0md.  If the guard can be verified to
  imply the guards of the body (which we call [3mguard verification[0m),
  and if the guard holds of the actuals to which the object is
  applied (which we call [3mguard checking[0m), a compiled version of the
  object is run.  Otherwise, depending on how [47m[set-guard-checking][0m
  has been configured, either an error is signaled or the object is
  interpreted under the axioms defining [47mapply$[0m and [47mev$[0m.  [47mApply$[0m
  caches its investigations into guard verification (but not guard
  checking) and compilation.  We discuss the cache in
  [47m[print-cl-cache][0m.

  When a guarded quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m object is used in a [47m:FN[0m slot of a
  function definition, its guards are verified as part of the guard
  verification step of [47mdefun[0m or [47mverify-guards[0m.  If that guard
  verification fails, checkpoints will be printed and you can use
  [47m:guard-hints[0m or [47m:hints[0m in the [47mdefun[0m or [47mverify-guards[0m events to
  supply the necessary guidance.  When successful, the guard verified
  [47mLAMBDA[0m objects in the [47mdefun[0m are recorded in the cache.

  But unlike defined function symbols, whose guards may be verified at
  [47mdefun[0m-time or at [47mverify-guards[0m-time, quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m expressions may
  be introduced without an associated event.  For example, the user
  may simply type

    ACL2 !>(apply$ (lambda$ (x)
                            (declare (type (satisfies natp) x))
                            (* x x))
                   '(5))

  giving [47mapply$[0m a [47mLAMBDA[0m object never before seen.

  So [47mapply$[0m must be ready to verify the guards of a quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m
  object before attempting to apply it.  This is in contrast to what
  happens when [47mapply$[0m is given a quoted function symbol.  ([47mApply$[0m can
  just look up whether a function symbol has been guard verified.)

  To try to verify the guards of a quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m expression, [47mapply$[0m
  limits itself to tau reasoning (see
  [introduction-to-the-tau-system]).  The idea is not to spend too
  much time making the decision as to whether compiled code can be
  used or not.  In addition, we don't want top-level evaluation, as
  shown in the user type-in above, to provoke full-blown theorem
  proving.

  Interpreting small quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects can be done relatively
  quickly.  After all, when the interpreter reaches a guard verified
  function symbol inside the [47mLAMBDA[0m body it runs compiled code.  It's
  only the body itself that is interpreted.

  But the tau system is pretty weak and so will be unable to verify
  some non-trivial guard conjectures, which will mean the [47mLAMBDA[0m
  object is interpreted.  If the [47mLAMBDA[0m object is very large or is
  being being used often, e.g., to map over a large object and check
  some property, you might really want to invest the time to verify
  its guards.  This can be done with [47m[verify-guards][0m, which as of
  Version 8.1 takes [47mLAMBDA[0m objects and [47mlambda$[0m terms and updates the
  cache.  E.g.,

    (verify-guards
      (lambda$ (x)
               (declare (type (satisfies natp) x))
               (* x x)))

  While this functionality is available to you, deciding that you need
  to use it is problematic.  [47mApply$[0m prints no warning that it has
  failed to verify the guards of a [47mLAMBDA[0m object and is running
  interpreted code.  However, the utility [47m[print-cl-cache][0m provides
  basic information about the cache and its documentation may help
  you discover which [47mLAMBDA[0m objects in use are unverified.")
