 (LAST
  (LISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "The last [47m[cons][0m (not element) of a list

  [47m(Last l)[0m is the last [47m[cons][0m of a list, [47ml[0m.  Here are examples.

    ACL2 !>(last '(a b . c))
    (B . C)
    ACL2 !>(last '(a b c))
    (C)

  [47m(Last l)[0m has a [guard] of [47m(listp l)[0m; thus, [47ml[0m need not be a
  [47m[true-listp][0m.

  [47mLast[0m is a Common Lisp function.  See any Common Lisp documentation
  for more information.  Unlike Common Lisp, we do not allow an
  optional second argument for [47mlast[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<last>

    (defun last (l)
      (declare (xargs :guard (listp l)))
      (if (atom (cdr l)) l (last (cdr l))))")
 (LAST-CDR
  (LISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "The last [47m[cdr][0m of a list

  [47m(Last-cdr x)[0m is [47mx[0m if [47mx[0m is an [47m[atom][0m, and otherwise is the last [47m[cdr][0m
  of a list.  Here are examples.

    ACL2 !>(last-cdr '(a b . c))
    C
    ACL2 !>(last-cdr '(a b c))
    NIL

  [47m(Last-cdr x)[0m has a [guard] of [47mt[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<last-cdr>

    (defun last-cdr (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (if (atom x) x (cdr (last x))))")
 (LAST-PROVER-STEPS
  (SET-PROVER-STEP-LIMIT WITH-PROVER-STEP-LIMIT
                         PROGRAMMING-WITH-STATE ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "The number of prover steps most recently taken

  For discussions of prover step limits, See [set-prover-step-limit]
  and see [with-prover-step-limit].

  The [summary] printed for an [event] typically includes a line
  showing the prover steps counted, for example as follows.

    Prover steps counted:  7240

  The value of the form [47m(last-prover-steps state)[0m indicates the number
  of prover steps taken, in the sense described below, for the most
  recent context in which an event [summary] would normally be
  printed.  Note that the value of [47m(last-prover-steps state)[0m is
  updated for all [events], and more generally, for all forms that
  are set up to print a [summary], such as calls of [47m[thm][0m or
  [47m[certify-book][0m --- regardless of whether or not summary output is
  inhibited (see [set-inhibit-output-lst] and see
  [set-inhibited-summary-types]).  In particular, the value is
  updated (typically to [47mnil[0m) for [47m[table][0m [events], even when no
  summary is printed; for example, the value is updated to [47mnil[0m for
  [47mtable[0m events such as [47m([0m[47m[logic][0m[47m)[0m, [47m([0m[47m[program][0m[47m)[0m, and even calls of
  [47m[set-prover-step-limit][0m.

  The value of [47m(last-prover-steps state)[0m is determined as follows,
  based on the most recent summary context (as described above):

      [47mnil[0m, if no prover steps were taken, e.g., with [47m(thm (equal x x))[0m;
      else,

      the (positive) number of steps taken, if the number of steps did not
      exceed the starting limit; else,

      the negative of the starting limit.

  Note that the ``most recently completed event'' in this sense
  includes compound events.  Consider the following example.

    (progn (thm (equal (append (append x y) z) (append x y z)))
           (thm (equal (car (cons x y)) x)))

  The summaries show (in some ACL2 versions, at least) 435 steps for
  the first call of [47mthm[0m and 7 steps for the second call of [47mthm[0m, with
  442 steps thus shown in the summary for the entire [47mprogn[0m call.
  Subsequent evaluation of [47m(last-prover-steps state)[0m returns 442.  On
  the other hand, suppose that [47m(set-prover-step-limit 440)[0m is
  evaluated immediately before evaluating the [47mprogn[0m call above.  Then
  the summaries show the following for the two [47mthm[0m calls and the
  [47mprogn[0m call, in order as follows.

    Prover steps counted:  435
    Prover steps counted:  More than 5
    Prover steps counted:  More than 440

  Since the most recently completed event is the [47mprogn[0m call, then
  [47m(last-prover-steps state)[0m returns -440.

  We conclude with two remarks for advanced users who wish to invoke
  [47mlast-prover-steps[0m in the development of utilities that track prover
  steps.

  Remark 1.  Suppose that you want to write a utility that takes some
  action based on the number of prover steps performed by the first
  event that is generated within a sequence of events, for example
  the number of prover steps taken to admit [47mf1[0m in the following
  example.

    (progn (defun f1 ...)
           (defun f2 ...))

  A solution is to record the steps taken by the first [47m[defun][0m before
  executing subsequent events, as follows (see [make-event]).

    (progn (defun f1 ...)
           (make-event (pprogn (f-put-global 'my-step-count
                                             (last-prover-steps state)
                                             state)
                               (value '(value-triple nil))))
           (defun f2 ...))

  Remark 2.  It is possible to write utilities that are treated as
  [events] for purposes of the discussion above; that is, their calls
  can be followed by evaluating [47m(last-prover-steps state)[0m as
  described above.  For an example of how to do this using the ACL2
  system macro [47mwith-ctx-summarized[0m, see the implementation of
  [47m[prove$][0m in [community-book] [47mbooks/tools/prove-dollar.lisp[0m.")
 (LD
  (MISCELLANEOUS)
  "The ACL2 read-eval-print loop, file loader, and [command] processor

    Examples:
    (LD \"foo.lisp\")              ; read and evaluate each form in file
                                 ; \"foo.lisp\", in order
    (LD \"foo.lisp\" :ld-pre-eval-print t)
                                 ; as above, but print each form to standard
                                 ; character output just before it is evaluated

    General Form:
    (LD standard-oi                  ; open obj in channel, stringp file name
                                     ; to open and close, or list of forms
                                     ; Optional keyword arguments:
        :dir                ...      ; directory spec if standard-oi is a string
        :standard-co        ...      ; open char out or file to open and close
        :proofs-co          ...      ; open char out or file to open and close
        :current-package    ...      ; known package name
        :useless-runes      ...      ; :write/:read/:read?/n/-n/nil
                                     ;   (-100 < n < 0 or 0 < n <= 100)
                                     ;   (see [useless-runes])
        :ld-skip-proofsp    ...      ; nil, 'include-book, or t
                                     ;   (see [ld-skip-proofsp])
        :ld-redefinition-action ...  ; nil or '(:a . :b)
        :ld-prompt          ...      ; nil, t, or some prompt printer fn
        :ld-missing-input-ok ...     ; nil, t, :warn, or warning message
        :ld-pre-eval-filter ...      ; :all, :query, or some new name
        :ld-pre-eval-print  ...      ; nil, t, or :never
        :ld-post-eval-print ...      ; nil, t, or :command-conventions
        :ld-evisc-tuple     ...      ; nil or '(alist nil nil level length)
        :ld-error-triples   ...      ; nil or t
        :ld-error-action    ...      ; :return!, :return, :continue, :error,
                                     ;   or (:exit N)
        :ld-query-control-alist ...  ; alist supplying default responses
        :ld-verbose         ...)     ; nil or t
        :ld-always-skip-top-level-locals      ; nil or t
        :ld-user-stobjs-modified-warning ...) ; nil, t, or :same

  [47mLd[0m is the top-level ACL2 read-eval-print loop.  (When you call [47m[lp][0m,
  a little initialization is done in raw Common Lisp and then [47mld[0m is
  called.)  [47mLd[0m is also a general-purpose ACL2 file loader and a
  [command] interpreter.  [47mLd[0m is actually a macro that expands to a
  function call involving [47m[state][0m.  [47mLd[0m returns an ``error triple''
  [47m(mv erp val state)[0m as explained below.  (For much more on error
  triples, see [programming-with-state].)

  See [rebuild] for a variant of [47mld[0m that skips proofs.  See
  [output-to-file] for examples showing how to redirect output to a
  file.

  The arguments to [47mld[0m, except for [47m:dir[0m, all happen to be global
  variables in [47m[state][0m (see [state] and see
  [programming-with-state]).  For example, [47m'[0m[47m[current-package][0m and
  [47m'[0m[47m[ld-verbose][0m are global variables, which may be accessed via [47m(@
  current-package)[0m and [47m(@ ld-verbose)[0m.  When [47mld[0m is called, it
  ``binds'' these variables.  By ``binds'' we actually mean the
  variables are globally set but restored to their old values on
  exit.  Because [47mld[0m provides the illusion of [state] global variables
  being bound, they are generally called ``[47mld[0m specials'' (after the
  Lisp convention of calling a variable ``special'' if it is
  referenced freely after having been bound).  (We say ``generally''
  because technically, [47mcurrent-package[0m and [47museless-runes[0m are not
  considered to be [47mld[0m specials.)

  Note that all arguments but the first are passed via keyword.  Any
  variable not explicitly given a value in a call retains its
  pre-call value, with the exception of [47m:[0m[47m[ld-error-action][0m, which
  generally defaults to [47m:return![0m if not explicitly specified.

  Just as an example to drive the point home: If [47m[current-package][0m is
  [47m\"ACL2\"[0m and you typed

    (ld *standard-oi* :current-package \"MY-PKG\")

  you would find yourself in (an inner) read-eval-print loop in which
  the [current-package] was [47m\"MY-PKG\"[0m.  You could operate there as
  long as you wished, changing the current package at will.  But when
  you typed [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m you would return to the outer read-eval-print loop
  where the current package would still be [47m\"ACL2\"[0m.

  Roughly speaking, [47mld[0m repeatedly reads a form from [47m[standard-oi][0m,
  evaluates it, and prints its result to [47m[standard-co][0m.  It does this
  until the form is [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m or evaluates to an error triple whose value
  component is [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m, or until the input channel or list is emptied.
  However, [47mld[0m has many bells and whistles controlled by the [47mld[0m
  specials.  Each such special is documented individually.  For
  example, see the documentation for [47m[standard-oi][0m,
  [47m[current-package][0m, [useless-runes], [47m[ld-pre-eval-print][0m, etc.

  A more precise description of [47mld[0m is as follows.  In the description
  below we use the [47mld[0m specials as variables, e.g., we say ``a form is
  read from [47m[standard-oi][0m.'' By this usage we refer to the current
  value of the named [state] global variable, e.g., we mean ``a form
  is read from the current value of [47m'[0m[47m[standard-oi][0m.'' This
  technicality has an important implication: If while interacting
  with [47mld[0m you change the value of one of the [47mld[0m specials, e.g.,
  [47m'[0m[47m[standard-oi][0m, you will change the behavior of [47mld[0m, e.g.,
  subsequent input will be taken from the new value.

  Three [47mld[0m specials are treated as channels: [47m[standard-oi][0m is treated
  as an object input channel and is the source of forms evaluated by
  [47mld[0m; [47m[standard-co][0m and [47m[proofs-co][0m are treated as character output
  channels and various flavors of output are printed to them.
  However, the supplied values of these specials need not actually be
  channels; several special cases are recognized.

  If the supplied value of one of these is in fact an open channel of
  the appropriate type, that channel is used and is not closed by [47mld[0m.
  If the supplied value of one of these specials is a string, the
  string is treated as a file name in (essentially) Unix syntax (see
  [pathname]) and a channel of the appropriate type is opened to/from
  that file.  Any channel opened by [47mld[0m during the binding of the [47mld[0m
  specials is automatically closed by [47mld[0m upon termination.  If
  [47m[standard-co][0m and [47m[proofs-co][0m are strings that represent the same
  file, only one channel to that file is opened and is used for both.

  As a special convenience, when [47m[standard-oi][0m is a string and the [47m:dir[0m
  argument is provided and not [47mnil[0m, we look up [47m:dir[0m just as is done
  for [47m[include-book][0m; also see [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m,
  [47m[add-include-book-dir!][0m, and [47m[project-dir-alist][0m.  Thus a suitable
  directory is prepended to create the filename.  Note that
  [47mstandard-oi[0m must be a string that is a relative pathname, not an
  absolute pathname.  For example, one can write [47m(ld
  \"arithmetic/top-with-meta.lisp\" :dir :system)[0m to [47mld[0m that particular
  [community-books] library.  (Of course, for certified [books] you
  should almost always use [47m[include-book][0m instead of [47mld[0m.)  If [47m:dir[0m is
  not specified, then a relative pathname is resolved using the
  connected book directory; see [cbd].  If you want to load a list of
  forms, then consider prepending a call of [47m[set-cbd][0m to that list
  rather than using [47m:dir[0m, which is not supported when [47mstandard-oi[0m is
  a list.

  Several other alternatives are allowed for [47m[standard-oi][0m.  If
  [47m[standard-oi][0m is a true list then it is taken as the list of forms
  to be processed.  If [47m[standard-oi][0m is a list ending in an open
  channel, then [47mld[0m processes the forms in the list and then reads and
  processes the forms from the channel.  Analogously, if
  [47m[standard-oi][0m is a list ending a string, an object input channel
  from the named file is opened and [47mld[0m processes the forms in the
  list followed by the forms in the file.  That channel is closed
  upon termination of [47mld[0m.

  In the cases that a string is to be converted to an object input
  channel --- that is, when [47m[standard-oi][0m is a string or is a list
  ending in a string --- an error occurs by default if the conversion
  fails, presumably because the file named by the string does not
  exist.  However, if keyword argument [47m:ld-missing-input-ok[0m is [47mt[0m,
  then [47mld[0m immediately returns without error in this case, but without
  reading or executing any forms, as though [47mstandard-oi[0m is [47mnil[0m and
  keyword arguments [47m:ld-verbose[0m and [47m:ld-prompt[0m both have value [47mnil[0m.
  The other legal values for [47m:ld-missing-input-ok[0m are [47mnil[0m, which
  gives the default behavior, and [47m:warn[0m, which behaves the same as [47mt[0m
  except that a warning is printed, which contains the same
  information as would be printed for the default error described
  above.

  The remaining [47mld[0m specials are handled more simply and generally have
  to be bound to one of a finite number of tokens described in the
  [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m entries for each [47mld[0m special.  Should any [47mld[0m special be
  supplied an inappropriate value, an error message is printed.

  Next, if [47m[ld-verbose][0m is [47mt[0m, [47mld[0m prints the message ``ACL2 loading
  name'' where [47mname[0m names the file or channel from which forms are
  being read.  At the conclusion of [47mld[0m, it will print ``Finished
  loading name'' if [47m[ld-verbose][0m is [47mt[0m.

  One may generally omit [47mld-user-stobjs-modified-warning[0m except when
  calling [47mld[0m in the body of a definition.  In that case this keyword
  argument is required, and the value [47mt[0m is recommended.  With that
  value, a warning will generally be printed if a user-defined
  [stobj] is modified by the call of [47mld[0m; value [47mnil[0m avoids such
  warnings, and value [47m:same[0m causes the value to be inherited from the
  existing value (generally from a superior call of [47mld[0m).  A
  discussion of these warnings is highly recommended reading before
  you give a value other than [47mt[0m; see [user-stobjs-modified-warnings].

  Finally, [47mld[0m repeatedly executes the ACL2 read-eval-print step, which
  may be described as follows.  A [prompt] is printed to
  [47m[standard-co][0m if [47m[ld-prompt][0m is non-[47mnil[0m.  The format of the
  [prompt] is determined by [47m[ld-prompt][0m.  If it is [47mt[0m, the default
  ACL2 [prompt] is used.  If it is any other non-[47mnil[0m value then it is
  treated as an ACL2 function that will print the desired [prompt].
  See [ld-prompt].  In the exceptional case where [47mld[0m's input is
  coming from the terminal [47m(*standard-oi*)[0m but its output is going to
  a different sink (i.e., [47m[standard-co][0m is not [47m[*standard-co*][0m), we
  also print the [prompt] to the terminal.

  [47mLd[0m then reads a form from [47m[standard-oi][0m.  If the object read is a
  keyword, [47mld[0m constructs a ``keyword command form'' by possibly
  reading several more objects.  See [keyword-commands].  This
  construction process is sensitive to the value of
  [47m[ld-keyword-aliases][0m.  See [ld-keyword-aliases].  Otherwise, the
  object read is treated as the command form.

  (Technical Aside.  Some special handling takes place when [47mld[0m is
  called in the scope of [47m[local][0m, as in [47m(local (ld <C>))[0m for a
  command, [47m<C>[0m.  In that case, after [47m<C>[0m is evaluated, then if the
  result is an [error-triple] and [47m<C>[0m is not already of the form
  [47m(local <C0>)[0m, then when the command is stored in the ACL2 [world]
  it is stored as [47m(local <C>)[0m instead of [47m<C>[0m.  See [ld-history] for a
  similar treatment of local commands.  End of Technical Aside.)

  [47mLd[0m next decides whether to evaluate or skip this form, depending on
  [47m[ld-pre-eval-filter][0m.  Initially, the filter must be either [47m:all[0m,
  [47m:query[0m, or a new name.  If it is [47m:all[0m, it means all forms are
  evaluated.  If it is [47m:query[0m, it means each form that is read is
  displayed and the user is queried.  Otherwise, the filter is a name
  and each form that is read is evaluated as long as the name remains
  new, but if the name is ever introduced then no more forms are read
  and [47mld[0m terminates.  See [ld-pre-eval-filter].

  If the form is to be evaluated, then [47mld[0m first prints the form to
  [47m[standard-co][0m, if [47m[ld-pre-eval-print][0m is [47mt[0m.  With this feature, [47mld[0m
  can process an input file or form list and construct a script of
  the session that appears as though each form was typed in.  See
  [ld-pre-eval-print].

  [47mLd[0m then evaluates the form, with [47m[state][0m bound to the current
  [state].  The result is some list of (multiple) values.  If a
  [state] is among the values, then [47mld[0m uses that [state] as the
  subsequent current [state].

  Depending on [47m[ld-error-triples][0m, [47mld[0m may interpret the result as an
  ``error.'' See [ld-error-triples].  We first discuss [47mld[0m's behavior
  if no error signal is detected (either because none was sent or
  because [47mld[0m is ignoring them because [47m[ld-error-triples][0m is [47mnil[0m).

  In the case of a non-erroneous result, [47mld[0m does two things: First, if
  the logical [world] in the now current [state] is different than
  the [world] before execution of the form, [47mld[0m adds to the [world] a
  ``[command] landmark'' containing the form evaluated.  See
  [command-descriptor].  Second, [47mld[0m prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m, but only if [47m[ld-post-eval-print][0m is not [47mnil[0m.  The
  result is printed as a list of (multiple) values unless
  [47m[ld-post-eval-print][0m is [47m:command-conventions[0m, [47m[ld-error-triples][0m is
  [47mt[0m, and the result is an [error-triple], i.e., of the form [47m(mv * *
  state)[0m.  In that case, only the non-erroneous ``value'' component
  of the result is printed.  See [ld-post-eval-print].

  Whenever [47mld[0m prints anything (whether the input form, a query, or some
  results) it ``eviscerates'' it if [47mld-evisc-tuple[0m is non-[47mnil[0m.
  Essentially, evisceration is a generalization of Common Lisp's use
  of [47m*print-level*[0m and [47m*print-length*[0m to hide large substructures.
  See [evisc-tuple] and also see [set-iprint].

  We now return to the case of a form whose evaluation signals an
  error.  In this case, [47mld[0m first restores the ACL2 logical [world] to
  what it was just before the erroneous form was evaluated.  Thus, a
  form that partially changes the [world] (i.e., begins to store
  properties) and then signals an error, has no effect on the
  [world].  You may see this happen on [command]s that execute
  several [events] (e.g., an [47m[encapsulate][0m or a [47m[progn][0m of several
  [47m[defuns][0m): even though the output makes it appear that the initial
  [events] were executed, if an error is signaled by a later event
  the entire block of [events] is discarded.

  After rolling back, [47mld[0m takes an action determined by
  [47m[ld-error-action][0m.  If the action is [47m:continue[0m, [47mld[0m merely iterates
  the read-eval-print step.  Note that nothing suggestive of the
  value of the ``erroneous'' form is printed.  If the action is
  [47m:return[0m, [47mld[0m terminates normally; similarly if the action is
  [47m:return![0m, but a special value is returned that can cause superior
  [47mld[0m commands to terminate; see [ld-error-action] for details.  If
  the action is [47m:error[0m, [47mld[0m terminates signaling an error to its
  caller.  If its caller is in fact another instance of [47mld[0m and that
  instance is watching out for error signals, the entire [world]
  created by the inner [47mld[0m will be discarded by the outer [47mld[0m if the
  inner [47mld[0m terminates with an error.  Note that if the action is
  [47m(:exit N)[0m, then there is no rolling back, because ACL2 quits
  immediately with exit status [47mN[0m.

  [47mLd[0m returns an error triple, [47m(mv erp val state)[0m.  [47mErp[0m is [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m
  indicating whether an error is being signaled.  If no error is
  signaled, [47mval[0m is the ``reason'' [47mld[0m terminated and is one of [47m:exit[0m
  (meaning [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m was read), [47m:eof[0m (meaning the input source was
  exhausted), [47m:error[0m (meaning an error occurred but has been
  suppressed), [47m:filter[0m (meaning the [47m[ld-pre-eval-filter][0m terminated
  [47mld[0m), [47m:missing-input[0m (meaning that the specified input file is
  missing, in the case that keyword [47m:ld-missing-input-ok[0m has a
  non-[47mnil[0m value so that an error is avoided), or a cons pair whose
  first component is the symbol [47m:STOP-LD[0m, which typically indicates
  that an error occurred while the value of variable
  [47m'[0m[47m[ld-error-action][0m was [47m:RETURN![0m.  See [ld-error-action] for details
  of this last case.


