 (LD-ALWAYS-SKIP-TOP-LEVEL-LOCALS
  (LD)
  "Determines whether [47m[ld][0m skips [47m[local][0m top-level forms

  [47mLd-always-skip-top-level-locals[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The
  accessor is [47m(ld-always-skip-top-level-locals state)[0m and the updater
  is [47m(set-ld-always-skip-top-level-locals val state)[0m.  The value of
  [47mld-always-skip-top-level-locals[0m must be either [47mnil[0m or [47mt[0m.  The
  initial value of [47mld-always-skip-top-level-locals[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, is controlled by
  various flags that control its behavior, and
  [47mld-always-skip-top-level-locals[0m is one of them.  When the value is
  [47mt[0m, [47m[local][0m [events] are skipped when they are at the top level in
  the following sense: they are not evaluated in the scope of either
  a call of [47m[certify-book][0m, [47m[include-book][0m, or [47m[encapsulate][0m, or else
  during [47m[make-event][0m expansion.")
 (LD-ERROR-ACTION
  (LD)
  "Determines [47m[ld][0m's response to an error

  [47mLd-error-action[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(ld-error-action state)[0m and the updater is [47m(set-ld-error-action val
  state)[0m.  [47mLd-error-action[0m must be [47m:continue[0m, [47m:return[0m, [47m:return![0m,
  [47m:error[0m, or [47m(:exit N)[0m for some natural number [47mN[0m.  The initial value
  of [47mld-error-action[0m is [47m:continue[0m, which means that the top-level
  ACL2 [command] loop (see [47m[lp][0m) will not exit when an error is
  caused by [47muser-typein[0m.  But the default value for [47mld-error-action[0m
  on calls of [47m[ld][0m is [47m:return![0m, with one exception: in the case that
  the value of [47m[ld][0m special [47mld-error-action[0m is [47m(:exit N)[0m, the default
  remains that value.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-error-action[0m is one of them.  Suppose that [47m[ld-error-triples][0m is
  [47mt[0m and a form evaluates to an [error-triple] [47m(mv erp val state)[0m.  If
  the ``error component'', [47merp[0m, is non-[47mnil[0m, then an error is said to
  have occurred.  If an error occurs, [47m[ld][0m's action depends on
  [47mld-error-action[0m.  If it is [47m:continue[0m, [47m[ld][0m just continues
  processing the forms in [47m[standard-oi][0m.  If it is [47m:return[0m or
  [47m:return![0m, [47m[ld][0m stops and returns as though it had emptied the
  channel.  If it is [47m:error[0m, [47m[ld][0m stops and returns, signaling an
  error to its caller by returning an error triple with non-[47mnil[0m error
  component, and reverting the logical [world] to its value just
  before that call of [47m[ld][0m.  If it is [47m(:exit N)[0m, then ACL2 quits with
  exit status [47mN[0m.  Later in this topic we discuss another case in
  which an error is said to have occurred: when the value component
  of an error triple is of the form [47m(:STOP-LD . x)[0m.

  To see this effect of [47m:ERROR[0m for [47mld-error-action[0m, consider the
  following example.

    (ld '((defun f (x) x) (defun bad (x)) (defun g (x) x)))

  When the [47m[defun][0m of [47mbad[0m fails (because its body is missing),
  evaluation of the [47mld[0m call stops; thus, the [47m[defun][0m of [47mg[0m is not
  evaluated.  The definition of [47mf[0m will be removed from the logical
  [world] before the call of [47mld[0m returns.

  However, by default each user call of [47mld[0m is made with a
  [47mld-error-action[0m of [47m:RETURN![0m (not [47m:ERROR[0m).  In the common case that
  all nested calls of [47mld[0m inside the ACL2 loop are made this way, an
  error will not roll back the logical [world].  However, it will
  still halt evaluation of forms for the current call of [47mld[0m and any
  parent calls of [47mld[0m (other than the call made on behalf of [47mlp[0m that
  entered the ACL2 loop in the first place), as though there were no
  more forms to evaluate.

  We have already discussed the behavior of [47mld[0m when an error occurs.
  But there is another case when [47mld[0m can stop processing more forms:
  when [47mld-error-action[0m is not [47m:CONTINUE[0m, [47m[ld-error-triples][0m is [47mt[0m, and
  evaluation of a form returns an error triple [47m(mv nil val state)[0m,
  where [47mnil[0m is the error component and whose ``value component'', [47mval[0m
  is a [47m[cons][0m pair whose [47m[car][0m is the symbol [47m:STOP-LD[0m.  Let [47mval[0m be
  the pair [47m(:STOP-LD . x)[0m.  If [47mld-error-action[0m is of the form [47m(:EXIT
  N)[0m, then ACL2 quits with exit status [47mN[0m.  Otherwise (i.e., when
  [47mld-error-action[0m is [47m:RETURN[0m, [47m:RETURN![0m, or [47m:ERROR[0m), the call of [47mld[0m
  returns the error triple [47m(mv nil (:STOP-LD n . x) state)[0m, where [47mn[0m
  is the value of [47m[state][0m global variable [47m'ld-level[0m at the time of
  termination.  The following example illustrates how this works.

    (ld '((defun f1 (x) x)
          (ld '((defun f2 (x) x)
                (mv nil '(:STOP-LD my-error more-info) state)
                (defun g2 (x) x)))
          (defun g1 (x) x)))

  The outer call of [47mld[0m returns the value

    (:STOP-LD 2 3 MY-ERROR MORE-INFO)

  and leaves us in a world the includes definitions for [47mf1[0m and [47mf2[0m, but
  no definition for [47mg1[0m or [47mg2[0m since neither of their two [47mdefun[0m forms
  was evaluated.  The value of [47m[state][0m global [47m'ld-level[0m is
  incremented from 1 to 2 when the outer [47mld[0m is entered and then again
  to 3 when the inner [47mld[0m is entered.  When the inner [47mld[0m encounters
  the error triple [47m(mv nil (:STOP-LD my-error more-info) state)[0m, it
  sees [47m:STOP-LD[0m in the [47mcar[0m of the value component and pushes the
  current value of [47m'ld-level[0m, 3, onto the [47m[cdr][0m of that value, to
  return the value triple [47m(mv nil (:STOP-LD my-error 3 more-info)
  state)[0m.  The outer of [47mld[0m then sees this value and returns [47m(mv nil
  (:STOP-LD my-error 2 3 more-info) state)[0m, since its current value
  of [47m'ld-level[0m is 2 after the inner [47mld[0m exits.

  That concludes our discussion of how these special [47m:STOP-LD[0m values
  are handled; but how are they created?  While they can be created
  directly by evaluation results as suggested in the example above,
  that is not the standard way.  Rather, [47mld[0m returns an error triple
  [47m(mv nil (:STOP-LD n) state)[0m, where [47mn[0m is the value of variable
  [47mld-level[0m at the time of termination, when the following conditions
  hold: an error occurs, [47mld-error-action[0m is [47mRETURN![0m (which is the
  default), and [47m[ld-error-triples][0m is [47mt[0m (the default).  The following
  example, which is a bit similar to the preceding one, illustrates
  both creation and handling of the special [47m:STOP-LD[0m values.

    (ld '((defun f1 (x) x)
          (ld '((defun f2 (x) x)
                (ld '((defun f3 (x) x)
                      (defun bad (x)) ; ERROR -- missing the body
                      (defun g3 (x) x)))
                (defun g2 (x) x)))
          (defun g1 (x) x)))

  The result is that [47mf1[0m, [47mf2[0m, and [47mf3[0m are defined, but none of [47mg1[0m, [47mg2[0m, or
  [47mg3[0m is defined.  Let's see why.  The innermost call of [47m[ld][0m has a
  default [47m:ld-error-action[0m of [47m:RETURN![0m (as do the other calls).  So
  when the definition of [47mbad[0m fails, then the innermost [47mld[0m returns [47m(mv
  nil (:STOP-LD 4) state)[0m.  The middle [47mld[0m sees this value, and since
  its [47m:ld-error-action[0m is not [47m:CONTINUE[0m (because it has the default
  value of [47m:RETURN![0m), it returns before considering the definition of
  [47mg2[0m, with value [47m(mv nil (:STOP-LD 3 4) state)[0m.  The topmost call of
  [47mld[0m similarly sees the [47m:STOP-LD[0m; stops evaluation of forms, without
  defining [47mg1[0m; and returns [47m(mv nil (:STOP-LD 2 3 4) state)[0m.")
 (LD-ERROR-TRIPLES
  (LD)
  "Determines whether a form caused an error during [47m[ld][0m

  [47mLd-error-triples[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(ld-error-triples state)[0m and the updater is [47m(set-ld-error-triples
  val state)[0m.  [47mLd-error-triples[0m must be either [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m.  The initial
  value of [47mld-error-triples[0m is [47mt[0m.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-error-triples[0m is one of them.  If this variable has the value [47mt[0m
  then when a form evaluates to an [error-triple] [47m(mv erp val state)[0m
  such that [47merp[0m is non-[47mnil[0m, then an error is deemed to have occurred.
  When an error occurs in evaluating a form, [47m[ld][0m rolls back the ACL2
  [world] to the configuration it had at the conclusion of the last
  error-free form.  Then [47m[ld][0m takes the action determined by
  [47m[ld-error-action][0m.")
 (LD-EVISC-TUPLE
  (LD)
  "Determines whether [47m[ld][0m suppresses details when printing

  [47mLd-evisc-tuple[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(ld-evisc-tuple state)[0m and an updater is [47m(set-ld-evisc-tuple val
  state)[0m, although the use of [47m[set-evisc-tuple][0m is preferred for
  updating.  [47mLd-evisc-tuple[0m must be either [47mnil[0m, which is its initial
  value, or a legal evisc-tuple: see [set-evisc-tuple].

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-evisc-tuple[0m is one of them.  [47m[Ld][0m may print the forms it is
  evaluating and/or the results of evaluation.  Depending on the
  value of [47mld-evisc-tuple[0m [47m[ld][0m may ``eviscerate'' objects before
  printing them.  See [set-evisc-tuple] for a discussion of
  evisceration and of how other evisc-tuples affect the printing of
  error messages and warnings, as well as other output not from [47mld[0m.")
 (LD-HISTORY
  (LD HISTORY)
  "Saving and querying command history

  See [ld] for background on the ACL2 read-eval-print loop.  The
  present topic pertains to a history kept for commands issued to
  that loop, which we call an ``ld-history'' (pronounced ``ell dee
  history'').  Here are some things to keep in mind when reading this
  topic.

    * Each entry in the history includes the input command, the value
      returned, and other information, as explained further below.

    * This is about commands, not events: that is, we are concerned with
      forms that are submitted for evaluation to the top-level loop.
      See [command].

    * An entry is saved for every command submitted by the user, even if it
      doesn't change the ACL2 [world] --- e.g., [47m(+ 3 4)[0m --- and even
      if it undoes commands --- e.g., [47m:[0m[47mubt[0m.

    * Keyword commands are turned into s-expressions before saving an
      entry; see [keyword-commands].  For example, the input [47m:ubt :x[0m
      is stored in an entry as the input [47m(ubt ':x)[0m.

    * When an entry is saved for a command [47mC[0m that is evaluated in the
      context of a call of [47m[local][0m, where [47mC[0m evaluates to an
      [error-triple], then [47mC[0m is stored in the entry as [47m(local C)[0m
      unless [47mC[0m is already a call of [47mlocal[0m.  (Technical Aside: This
      behavior supports local [portcullis] commands.)

    * The ld-history saves entries not only for commands issued in the
      original top-level loop, but also for commands issued in
      (recursive) calls of [47m[ld][0m --- but not during [47m[make-event][0m
      expansion.

    * Entries are generally saved even when there are errors.  However,
      entries are not saved for commands that exit with raw Lisp
      errors.

  Also see [community-books] file [47mbooks/demos/ld-history-input.lsp[0m for
  examples.  The output from calling [47m[ld][0m on that file (by calling
  the [47m[run-script][0m tool in [47mbooks/demos/ld-history-book.acl2[0m) is in
  [community-books] file [47mbooks/demos/ld-history-log.txt[0m.

  The ld-history is a stack, represented as a list with the most recent
  commands at the front.  But by default ACL2 is in [3msingle-entry
  mode[0m, where the list is kept at length 1: an entry is saved in the
  ld-history only for the most recent command, and the previous entry
  is discarded.  We now describe relevant utilities, including one
  that can switch to [3mmultiple-entry mode[0m, where all entries are kept
  until a utility is called explicitly to discard old entries.  Note
  that the mode is determined by the length of the ld-history:
  single-entry mode when length 1, multiple-entry mode when length 2
  or more.

    * [47m(ld-history state)[0m
      This utility returns a list of structures, denoted ``ld-history
      entries'', with the most recent one first.  By default, this
      list has length 1, but that can be changed; see
      [47madjust-ld-history[0m below.  Each entry in the list is recognized
      by the following predicate.  [31;1mNOTE[0m: This query is evaluated
      before the ld-history stored in the ACL2 state is updated with
      a new entry, based on the current command; so the previous
      command will be at the top of the returned stack, not the
      current command.  (In particular, submitting the form
      [47m(ld-history state)[0m at the ACL2 prompt does not put that form at
      the front of the returned list.)

    * [47m(weak-ld-history-entry-p x)[0m
      This function returns [47mt[0m if [47mx[0m has the shape of an entry in the
      ld-history list, else [47mnil[0m.

    * Here are accessors for an ld-history entry, which we think of as
      returning its fields.  The formal parameter [47mentry[0m below is an
      entry in (i.e., member of) [47m(ld-history state)[0m; an example call
      is thus [47m(ld-history-entry-input (car (ld-history state)))[0m.

        * [47m(ld-history-entry-input entry)[0m
          The user input

        * [47m(ld-history-entry-error-flg entry)[0m
          Non-[47mnil[0m when there was an error translating the user input

        * [47m(ld-history-entry-stobjs-out/value entry)[0m
          When [47m(ld-history-entry-error-flg entry)[0m is [47mnil[0m, this is a cons whose
          [47mcar[0m is the [stobjs-out] --- a list whose length is the
          number of values returned, with [47mnil[0m in each position except
          when occupied by a symbol indicating a returned [stobj] for
          that position --- and whose [47mcdr[0m is the returned value in
          the single-value case, but is the list of returned values
          in the [multiple-value] case.

        * [47m(ld-history-entry-stobjs-out entry)[0m
          When [47m(ld-history-entry-error-flg entry)[0m is [47mnil[0m, this is the
          stobjs-out as described above; otherwise this is [47mnil[0m.

        * [47m(ld-history-entry-value entry)[0m
          When [47m(ld-history-entry-error-flg entry)[0m is [47mnil[0m, this is the value or
          values as described above; otherwise this is [47mnil[0m.

        * [47m(ld-history-entry-user-data entry)[0m
          This is [47mnil[0m by default.  However, code can be provided to compute
          this field, as discussed below.

    * [47m(adjust-ld-history x state)[0m
      [47mX[0m is [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m, or an integer.  The result is an [error-triple] [47m(mv nil
      value state)[0m, where [47mvalue[0m and the effect are as follows, and
      where if there is no change (i.e., the effect is a no-op) then
      [47mvalue[0m is [47m(:no-change :length N)[0m where [47mN[0m is the current length
      of the ld-history.

        * [47mT[0m:
          Change to multiple-entry mode, where an entry is saved for every
          command, not just the most recent command.  There is no
          change if already in multiple-entry mode; otherwise the
          returned [47mvalue[0m is [47m(:saving-ld-history t)[0m.

        * [47mNIL[0m:
          Change to single-entry mode, where an entry is saved only for the
          most recent command.  There is no change if already in
          single-entry mode; otherwise the returned [47mvalue[0m is
          [47m(:saving-ld-history nil)[0m, and all old entries will be
          discarded (to produce a single-element ld-history).

        * Positive integer [47mk[0m:
          Replace the current ld-history by its first [47mk[0m entries, except there
          is no change if in single-entry mode or if [47mk[0m is not less
          than the length of the current ld-history.  Note that by
          ``current ld-history'' we refer to the ld-history in effect
          at the time [47madjust-ld-history[0m is invoked, which does not
          include the current command being evaluated.  (Thus, even
          if [47mk[0m is 1, multiple-entry mode will be preserved: the
          ld-history will have 2 entries immediately after the
          current command completes.)  The returned [47mvalue[0m is
          [47m(:ld-history-truncated :old-length LEN :new-length k)[0m,
          where [47mLEN[0m is the length of the current ld-history before
          the change.

        * Negative integer [47m-k[0m:
          This is intended to specify removal of the oldest [47mk[0m entries from the
          current ld-history.  Thus, it is treated identically to
          argument [47mk2[0m where [47mk2[0m is the sum of [47m-k[0m and the length of the
          current ld-history, but only if that sum is positive; else
          there is no change.  For example, suppose that [47m-k[0m is -3.
          If the current ld-history, [47mh[0m, has length 2 or 3, then there
          is no change; but if [47mh[0m has length 10, then it is to be
          replaced by [47m(take 7 h)[0m and the length will actually thus be
          8 after the current command completes.

  Note that a call of [47madjust-ld-history[0m is not an [event] that can be
  placed directly in [books] or [47m[encapsulate][0m forms.

  Remark.  If [47m(adjust-ld-history n state)[0m is evaluated while in
  multiple-entry mode, where n is a positive integer less than the
  current length of the ld-history, then the new ld-history after
  returning to the prompt will have length [47mn+1[0m.  That's essentially
  because it will have length [47mn[0m immediately after that call of
  [47madjust-ld-history[0m is evaluated, and then a new entry for the
  current command (which could be that call itself, if that's what
  was submitted at the prompt) will be pushed onto the ld-entry just
  before returning to the prompt.  We say ``essentially'' because
  there is a Special Case: when [47mn[0m is 1 then a 2-element list of
  entries [47m(e1 e2)[0m is created where [47me2[0m has fields that are all [47mnil[0m;
  then when the new entry [47me[0m is pushed onto the ld-history, [47me2[0m is
  dropped so that the new ld-history is [47m(e e1)[0m.  This special-case
  trick is also used in multiple-entry mode when [47mn[0m is [47m-k[0m where [47mk[0m is
  one less than the length of the current ld-history, since that is
  treated the same as [47m(adjust-ld-history 1 state)[0m; and this trick is
  also used when [47m(adjust-ld-history t state)[0m switches from
  single-entry mode to multiple-entry mode.  End of Remark.

  Finally we discuss the user-data field of a ld-history entry, which
  (as noted above) has default [47mnil[0m.  It is accessed using
  [47m(ld-history-entry-user-data entry)[0m.  It is set automatically when
  the ld-history is extended with a new entry: the function call
  [47m(set-ld-history-entry-user-data input error-flg stobjs-out/value
  state)[0m, is executed where the actuals are the other fields of the
  entry as indicated, e.g., the first actual is the input field of
  the new entry (as returned by the function,
  [47mld-history-entry-input[0m).  Although [47mset-ld-history-entry-user-data[0m
  returns [47mnil[0m by default, this can be changed by providing your own
  function with a [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m of [47mt[0m and the same formal parameters (which
  however may be renamed, other than [47mstate[0m).  To make that change,
  define a function, which here we call [47mmy-user-data[0m, and then attach
  it to [47mset-ld-history-entry-user-data[0m, as follows.

    (defun my-set-user-data (input error-flg stobjs-out/value state)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      ...)
    (defattach-system set-ld-history-entry-user-data my-set-user-data)

  The following example illustrates how to store the length of the ACL2
  [world] in the user-data.  Note that the [world] present in the
  [state] at the time the user-data is set, computed as [47m(w state)[0m, is
  almost the final world produced by the command --- it is missing
  just one triple, a so-called command marker.

    (defun my-world-length (input error-flg stobjs-out/value state)
      (declare (xargs :guard t :stobjs state)
               (ignore input error-flg stobjs-out/value))
      (len (w state)))

    (defattach-system set-ld-history-entry-user-data my-world-length)

  A subsequent inspection of the stored user-data shows the length of
  the current world, for example as follows.

    ACL2 !>(ld-history-entry-user-data (car (ld-history state)))
    125914
    ACL2 !>

  Notice that we used [47m[len][0m, not [47m[length][0m, since the [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m specified
  for our function needs to be [47mt[0m.  Alternative definitions, which
  however are less efficient, are as follows.

    (defun my-world-length (input error-flg stobjs-out/value state)
      (declare (xargs :guard t :stobjs state)
               (ignore input error-flg stobjs-out/value))
      (and (true-listp (w state))
           (length (w state))))

    (defun my-world-length (input error-flg stobjs-out/value state)
      (declare (xargs :guard t :stobjs state)
               (ignore input error-flg stobjs-out/value))
      (ec-call (length (w state))))

  Here is how to restore the original behavior, i.e., the default where
  the user-data is set to [47mnil[0m.

    (defattach-system set-ld-history-entry-user-data
                      set-ld-history-entry-user-data-default)")
