 (LD-KEYWORD-ALIASES
  (LD)
  "Abbreviation of some keyword commands

    Examples:
    (set-ld-keyword-aliases '((:q 0 q-fn)
                              (:e 0 exit-acl2-macro))
                            state)
    (ld-keyword-aliases state) ; current value of the ld-keyword-aliases table

  [47mLd-keyword-aliases[0m is the name of a ACL2 table (see [table]) and also
  the name of a function of [47mstate[0m that returns the value of this
  table.  That value must be an alist, each element of which is of
  the form [47m(:keyword n fn)[0m, where [47m:keyword[0m is a keyword, [47mn[0m is a
  nonnegative integer, and [47mfn[0m is a function symbol of arity [47mn[0m, a
  macro symbol, or a [47mlambda[0m expression of arity [47mn[0m.  When [47mkeyword[0m is
  typed as an [47m[ld][0m command, [47mn[0m more forms are read, [47mx1, ..., xn[0m, and
  the form [47m(fn 'x1 ... 'xn)[0m is then evaluated.  The initial value of
  the [47mld-keyword-aliases[0m [table] is [47mnil[0m.

  ACL2 provides functions to modify the [47mld-keyword-aliases[0m table, as
  follows.

      [47m(Set-ld-keyword-aliases val state)[0m: sets the table to [47mval[0m, which must
      be a legal alist as described above.  This is an event that may
      go into a book (see [events]), but its effect will be [local]
      to that book.

      [47mSet-ld-keyword-aliases![0m is the same as [47mset-ld-keyword-aliases[0m, except
      that its effect is not [local].  Indeed, the form
      [47m(set-ld-keyword-aliases val state)[0m is equivalent to the form
      [47m(local (set-ld-keyword-aliases! val state)[0m.

      [47m(Add-ld-keyword-alias key val state)[0m: modifies the table by binding
      the keyword [47mkey[0m to [47mval[0m, which must be a legal value as
      described above.  This is an event that may go into a book (see
      [events]), but its effect will be [local] to that book.

      [47mAdd-ld-keyword-alias![0m is the same as [47madd-ld-keyword-alias[0m, except
      that its effect is not [local].  Indeed, the form
      [47m(add-ld-keyword-alias key val state)[0m is equivalent to the form
      [47m(local (add-ld-keyword-alias! key val state)[0m.

  Consider the first example above:

    (set-ld-keyword-aliases '((:q 0 q-fn)
                              (:e 0 exit-acl2-macro))
                            state)

  With this event, [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m is redefined to have the effect of executing
  [47m(q-fn)[0m, so for example if you have defined [47mq-fn[0m with

    (defmacro q-fn ()
      '(er soft 'q \"You un-bound :q and now we have a soft error.\"))

  then [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m will cause an error, and if you have defined

    (defmacro exit-acl2-macro () '(exit-ld state))

  then [47m:e[0m will cause the effect (it so happens) that [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m normally has.
  If you prefer [47m:e[0m to [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m for exiting the ACL2 loop, you might even
  want to put such definitions of [47mq-fn[0m and [47mexit-acl2-macro[0m together
  with the [47mset-ld-keyword-aliases[0m form above into your
  [47m\"acl2-customization.lsp\"[0m file; see [ACL2-customization].

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-keyword-aliases[0m is one of them.  [47mLd-keyword-aliases[0m affects how
  keyword commands are parsed.  Generally speaking, [47m[ld][0m's command
  interpreter reads ``[47m:fn x1 ... xn[0m'' as ``[47m(fn 'x1 ... 'xn)[0m'' when
  [47m:fn[0m is a keyword and [47mfn[0m is the name of an [47mn[0m-ary function; see
  [keyword-commands].  But this parse is overridden, as described
  above, for the keywords bound in the [47mld-keyword-aliases[0m [table].")
 (LD-MISSING-INPUT-OK
  (LD)
  "Determine whether [47m[ld][0m causes an error for a missing file

  [47mLd-missing-input-ok[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(ld-missing-input-ok state)[0m and the updater is
  [47m(set-ld-missing-input-ok val state)[0m.  The value of
  [47mld-missing-input-ok[0m must be either [47mnil[0m, [47mt[0m, or [47m:warn[0m.  The initial
  value of [47mld-missing-input-ok[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, is controlled by
  various flags that control its behavior, and [47mld-missing-input-ok[0m is
  one of them.  In brief, the first argument of [47mld[0m can indicate a
  file from which to read input.  If the file does not exist, it is
  an error by default, but [47mld[0m becomes essentially a no-op if [47mt[0m or
  [47m:warn[0m is supplied for [47m:ld-missing-input-ok[0m, where [47m:warn[0m prints a
  warning.  Also see [ld].")
 (LD-POST-EVAL-PRINT
  (LD)
  "Determines whether and how [47m[ld][0m prints the result of evaluation

  [47mLd-post-eval-print[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(ld-post-eval-print state)[0m and the updater is
  [47m(set-ld-post-eval-print val state)[0m.  [47mLd-post-eval-print[0m must be
  either [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m, or [47m:command-conventions[0m.  The initial value of
  [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is [47m:command-conventions[0m.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is one of them.  If this global variable is [47mt[0m,
  [47m[ld][0m prints the result.  In the case of a form that produces a
  multiple value, [47m[ld][0m prints the list containing all the values
  (which, logically speaking, is what the form returned).  If
  [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is [47mnil[0m, [47m[ld][0m does not print the values.  This is
  most useful when [47m[ld][0m is used to load a previously processed file.

  Finally, if [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is [47m:command-conventions[0m and also
  [47mld-error-triples[0m is [47mt[0m, then [47m[ld][0m prints the result but treats
  ``error triples'' specially.  An ``error triple'' (see
  [error-triple]) is a result, [47m(mv erp val state)[0m, that consists of
  two ordinary values and [47m[state][0m.  Many ACL2 functions use such
  triples to signal errors.  The convention is that if [47merp[0m (the first
  value) is [47mnil[0m, then the function is returning [47mval[0m (the second
  value) as its conventional single result and possibly
  side-effecting [state] (as with some output).  If [47merp[0m is not [47mnil[0m,
  then an error has been caused, [47mval[0m is irrelevant and the error
  message has been printed in the returned [state].  Example ACL2
  functions that follow this convention include [47m[defun][0m and
  [47m[in-package][0m.  If such ``error producing'' functions are evaluated
  while [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is set to [47mt[0m, then you would see them
  producing lists of length 3.  This is disconcerting to users
  accustomed to Common Lisp (where these functions produce single
  results but sometimes cause errors or side-effect [state]).  For
  more information about error triples, see [programming-with-state].

  When [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is [47m:command-conventions[0m and a form produces
  an error triple [47m(mv erp val state)[0m as its value, [47m[ld][0m prints
  nothing if [47merp[0m is non-[47mnil[0m and otherwise [47m[ld][0m prints just [47mval[0m.
  Because it is a misrepresentation to suggest that just one result
  was returned, [47m[ld][0m prints the value of the global variable
  [47m'triple-print-prefix[0m before printing [47mval[0m.  [47m'triple-print-prefix[0m is
  initially [47m\" \"[0m, which means that when non-erroneous error triples
  are being abbreviated to [47mval[0m, [47mval[0m appears one space off the left
  margin instead of on the margin.

  In addition, when [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is [47m:command-conventions[0m and the
  value component of an error triple is the keyword [47m:invisible[0m then
  [47m[ld][0m prints nothing.  This is the way certain commands (e.g.,
  [47m:[0m[47m[pc][0m) appear to return no value.

  By printing nothing when an error has been signaled, [47m[ld][0m makes it
  appear that the error (whose message has already appeared in
  [state]) has ``thrown'' the computation back to load without
  returning a value.  By printing just [47mval[0m otherwise, we suppress the
  fact that [state] has possibly been changed.")
 (LD-PRE-EVAL-FILTER
  (LD)
  "Determines which forms [47m[ld][0m evaluates

  [47mLd-pre-eval-filter[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(ld-pre-eval-filter state)[0m and the updater is
  [47m(set-ld-pre-eval-filter val state)[0m.  [47mLd-pre-eval-filter[0m must be
  either [47m:all[0m, [47m:query[0m, or a new name that could be defined (e.g., by
  [47m[defun][0m or [47m[defconst][0m).  (There is actually a value that may on
  rare occasions be set by the ACL2 system, [47m:illegal-state[0m; we ignore
  that value here, but the curious reader is welcome to see
  [illegal-state].)  The initial value of [47mld-pre-eval-filter[0m is [47m:all[0m.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-pre-eval-filter[0m is one of them.  If the filter is [47m:all[0m, then
  every form read is evaluated.  If the filter is [47m:query[0m, then after
  a form is read it is printed to [47m[standard-co][0m and the user is asked
  if the form is to be evaluated or skipped.  If the filter is a new
  name, then all forms are evaluated until that name is introduced,
  at which time [47m[ld][0m terminates normally.

  The [47m:all[0m filter is, of course, the normal one.  [47m:Query[0m is useful if
  you want to replay selected [command]s in some file.  The new name
  filter is used if you wish to replay all the [command]s in a file
  up through the introduction of the given one.")
 (LD-PRE-EVAL-PRINT
  (LD)
  "Determines whether [47m[ld][0m prints the forms to be [47meval[0m'd

  [47mLd-pre-eval-print[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(ld-pre-eval-print state)[0m and the updater is [47m(set-ld-pre-eval-print
  val state)[0m.  [47mLd-pre-eval-print[0m must be either [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m, or [47m:never[0m.
  The initial value of [47mld-pre-eval-print[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-pre-eval-print[0m is one of them.  If this global variable is [47mt[0m,
  then before evaluating the form just read from [47m[standard-oi][0m, [47m[ld][0m
  prints the form to [47m[standard-co][0m.  If the variable is [47mnil[0m, no such
  printing occurs.  The [47mt[0m option is useful if you are reading from a
  file of [command]s and wish to assemble a complete script of the
  session in [47m[standard-co][0m.

  The value [47m:never[0m of [47mld-pre-eval-print[0m is rarely used.  During the
  evaluation of [47m[encapsulate][0m and of [47m[certify-book][0m forms, subsidiary
  events are normally printed, even if [47mld-pre-eval-print[0m is [47mnil[0m.
  Thus for example, when the user submits an [47m[encapsulate][0m form, all
  subsidiary events are generally printed even in the default
  situation where [47mld-pre-eval-print[0m is [47mnil[0m.  But occasionally one may
  want to suppress such printing.  In that case, [47mld-pre-eval-print[0m
  should be set to [47m:never[0m.  As described elsewhere (see
  [set-inhibit-output-lst]), another way to suppress such printing is
  to execute [47m(set-inhibit-output-lst lst)[0m where [47mlst[0m evaluates to a
  list including [47m'prove[0m and [47m'event[0m.")
 (LD-PROMPT
  (LD)
  "Determines the [prompt] printed by [47m[ld][0m

  [47mLd-prompt[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is [47m(ld-prompt
  state)[0m and the updater is [47m(set-ld-prompt val state)[0m.  [47mLd-prompt[0m
  must be either [47mnil[0m, [47mt[0m, or a function symbol that, when given an
  open output character channel and [state], prints the desired
  [prompt] to the channel and returns two values: the number of
  [characters] printed and the [state].  The initial value of
  [47mld-prompt[0m is [47mt[0m.

  The general-purpose ACL2 read-eval-print loop, [47m[ld][0m, reads forms from
  [47m[standard-oi][0m, evaluates them and prints the result to
  [47m[standard-co][0m.  Note that the ACL2 top-level loop (see [47m[lp][0m)
  results from an invocation of [47m[ld][0m.

  However, there are various flags that control [47m[ld][0m's behavior and
  [47mld-prompt[0m is one of them.  [47mLd-prompt[0m determines whether [47m[ld][0m prints
  a [prompt] before reading the next form from [47m[standard-oi][0m.  If
  [47mld-prompt[0m is [47mnil[0m, [47m[ld][0m prints no [prompt].  If [47mld-prompt[0m is [47mt[0m, the
  default [prompt] printer is used, which displays information that
  includes the current package, default [defun-mode], [guard]
  checking status (on or off), and [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m; see
  [default-print-prompt].

  If [47mld-prompt[0m is neither [47mnil[0m nor [47mt[0m, then it should be a function name,
  [47mfn[0m, such that [47m(fn channel state)[0m will print the desired [prompt] to
  [47mchannel[0m in [47m[state][0m and return [47m(mv col state)[0m, where [47mcol[0m is the
  number of [characters] output (on the last line output).  You may
  define your own [prompt] printing function, [47mfn[0m, and install it with
  [47m(set-ld-prompt 'fn state)[0m.  However, a trust tag must be active
  (see [defttag]) when you set [47mld-prompt[0m to other than [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m (with
  two exceptions: the functions [47mbrr-prompt[0m and [47mwormhole-prompt[0m, which
  print the prompt in the [break-rewrite] loop and the general
  [wormhole] loop, respectively).

  If you supply an inappropriate [prompt] function, i.e., one that
  causes an error or does not return the correct number and type of
  results, the following odd [prompt] will be printed instead:

    Bad Prompt
    See :DOC ld-prompt>

  which will lead you to this message.  You should either call [47m[ld][0m
  appropriately next time or assign an appropriate value to
  [47mld-prompt[0m.")
 (LD-QUERY-CONTROL-ALIST
  (LD)
  "How to default answers to queries

  [47mLd-query-control-alist[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor
  is [47m(ld-query-control-alist state)[0m and the updater is
  [47m(set-ld-query-control-alist val state)[0m.  Roughly speaking,
  [47mld-query-control-alist[0m is either [47mnil[0m (meaning all queries should be
  interactive), [47mt[0m (meaning all should default to the first accepted
  response), or an alist that pairs query ids to keyword responses.
  The alist may end in either [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m, indicating the default value
  for all ids not listed explicitly.  Formally, the
  [47mld-query-control-alist[0m must satisfy [47mld-query-control-alistp[0m.  The
  initial [47mld-query-control-alist[0m is [47mnil[0m, which means all queries are
  handled interactively.

  When an ACL2 query is raised, a unique identifying symbol is printed
  in parentheses after the word ``Query''.  This symbol, called the
  ``query id,'' can be used in conjunction with
  [47mld-query-control-alist[0m to prevent the query from being handled
  interactively.  By ``handled interactively'' we mean that the query
  is printed to [47m[*standard-co*][0m and a response is read from
  [47m[*standard-oi*][0m.  The alist can be used to obtain a ``default
  value'' for each query id.  If this value is [47mnil[0m, then the query is
  handled interactively.  In all other cases, the system handles the
  query without interaction (although text may be printed to
  [47m[standard-co][0m to make it appear that an interaction has occurred;
  see below).  If the default value is [47mt[0m, the system acts as though
  the user responded to the query by typing the first response listed
  among the acceptable responses.  If the default value is neither
  [47mnil[0m nor [47mt[0m, then it must be a keyword and one of the acceptable
  responses.  In that case, the system acts as though the user
  responded with the given keyword.

  Next, we discuss how the [47mld-query-control-alist[0m assigns a default
  value to each query id.  It assigns each id the first value paired
  with the id in the alist, or, if no such pair appears in the alist,
  it assigns the final [47m[cdr][0m of the alist as the value.  Thus, [47mnil[0m
  assigns all ids [47mnil[0m.  [47mT[0m assigns all ids [47mt[0m.  [47m'((:filter . nil)
  (:sysdef . :n) . t)[0m assigns [47mnil[0m to the [47m:filter[0m query, [47m:n[0m to the
  [47m:sysdef[0m query, and [47mt[0m to all others.

  It remains only to discuss how the system prints text when the
  default value is not [47mnil[0m, i.e., when the query is handled without
  interaction.  In fact, it is allowed to pair a query id with a
  singleton list containing a keyword, rather than a keyword, and
  this indicates that no printing is to be done.  Thus for the
  example above, [47m:sysdef[0m queries would be handled noninteractively,
  with printing done to simulate the interaction.  But if we change
  the example so that [47m:sysdef[0m is paired with [47m(:n)[0m, i.e., if
  [47mld-query-control-alist[0m is [47m'((:filter . nil) (:sysdef :n) . t)[0m, then
  no such printing would take place for [47msysdef[0m queries.  Instead, the
  default value of [47m:n[0m would be assigned ``quietly''.")
 (LD-REDEFINITION-ACTION
  (LD)
  "To allow redefinition without [undoing]

  The present topic discusses redefining functions, macros, and
  constants, using a mechanism that may be convenient but also may be
  unsafe or unsound.  See [undo] for what is often a better way to
  carry out redefinition.

  [47mLd-redefinition-action[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor
  is [47m(ld-redefinition-action state)[0m and the updater is
  [47m(set-ld-redefinition-action val state)[0m.

  [31;1mWARNING![0m If [47mld-redefinition-action[0m is non-[47mnil[0m then ACL2 is liable to
  be made unsafe or unsound, or behave in unexpected ways.  For
  example, redefining a macro or inlined function, [47mf[0m, that is called
  in the body of another function, [47mg[0m, may not cause the new version
  of [47mf[0m to be called by [47mg[0m.  In addition, for some Lisps, in particular
  GCL Version 2.7.0 or later, the return type [47mTP[0m inferred for the
  original definition of [47mf[0m might cause mishandling of the return
  value from [47mf[0m as [47mg[0m still expects that value's type to be [47mTP[0m.
  Redefinition should be viewed as a way to facilitate unsafe, but
  potentially useful, hacking.

  The keyword command [47m:[0m[47m[redef][0m will set [47mld-redefinition-action[0m to a
  convenient setting allowing unsound redefinition.  See below.

  When [47mld-redefinition-action[0m is [47mnil[0m, redefinition is prohibited.  In
  that case, an error message is printed upon any attempt to
  introduce a name that is already in use.  There is one exception to
  this rule.  It is permitted to redefine a function symbol in
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode to be a function symbol in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode provided
  the formals and body remain the same.  This is the standard way a
  function ``comes into'' logical existence.

  Throughout the rest of this discussion we exclude from our meaning of
  ``redefinition'' the case in which a function in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode is
  identically redefined in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.  At one time, ACL2 freely
  permitted the [signature]-preserving redefinition of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m
  mode functions but it no longer does.  See [redefining-programs].

  When [47mld-redefinition-action[0m is non-[47mnil[0m, you are allowed to redefine a
  name that is already in use.  [31;1mThe system may be rendered unsound[0m by
  such an act.  It is important to understand how dangerous
  redefinition is.  Suppose [47mfn[0m is a function symbol that is called
  from within some other function, say [47mg[0m.  Suppose [47mfn[0m is redefined so
  that its arity changes.  Then the definition of [47mg[0m is rendered
  syntactically ill-formed by the redefinition.  This can be
  devastating since the entire ACL2 system assumes that terms in its
  database are well-formed.  For example, if ACL2 executes [47mg[0m by
  running the corresponding function in raw Common Lisp the
  redefinition of [47mfn[0m may cause raw lisp to break in irreparable ways.
  As Lisp programmers we live with this all the time by following the
  simple rule: after changing the syntax of a function don't run any
  function that calls it via its old syntax.  This rule also works in
  the context of the evaluation of ACL2 functions, but it is harder
  to follow in the context of ACL2 deductions, since it is hard to
  know whether the database contains a path leading the theorem
  prover from facts about one function to facts about another.
  Finally, of course, even if the database is still syntactically
  well-formed there is no assurance that all the rules stored in it
  are valid.  For example, theorems proved about [47mg[0m survive the
  redefinition of [47mfn[0m but may have crucially depended on the
  properties of the old [47mfn[0m.  In summary, we repeat the warning: [31;1mall
  bets are off if you set[0m [47mld-redefinition-action[0m to [31;1mnon[0m-[47mnil[0m.

  ACL2 provides some enforcement of the concern above, by disabling
  [47m[certify-book][0m if any [world]-changing [events] exist in the
  certification [world] that were executed with a non-[47mnil[0m value of
  [47m'ld-redefinition-action[0m.  (This value is checked at the end of each
  top-level command, but the value does not change during evaluation
  of embedded event forms; see [embedded-event-form].)

  If at any point in a session you wish to see the list of all names
  that have been redefined, see [redefined-names].

  That said, we'll give you enough rope to hang yourself.  When
  [47mld-redefinition-action[0m is non-[47mnil[0m, it must be a pair, [47m(a .  b)[0m.
  The value of [47ma[0m determines how the system interacts with you when a
  redefinition is submitted.  The value of [47mb[0m allows you to specify
  how the property list of the redefined name is to be ``renewed''
  before the redefinition.

  There are several dimensions to the space of possibilities controlled
  by part a: Do you want to be queried each time you redefine a name,
  so you can confirm your intention?  (We sometimes make typing
  mistakes or simply forget we have used that name already.)  Do you
  want to see a warning stating that the name has been redefined?  Do
  you want ACL2 system functions given special protection from
  possible redefinition?  Below are the choices for part a:

      [47m:query[0m --- every attempt to redefine a name will produce a query.
      The query will allow you to abort the redefinition or proceed.
      It will will also allow you to specify the part [47mb[0m for this
      redefinition.  [47m:Query[0m is the recommended setting for users who
      wish to dabble in redefinition.

      [47m:warn[0m --- any user-defined function may be redefined but a
      post-redefinition warning is printed.  The attempt to redefine
      a system name produces a query.  If you are prototyping and
      testing a big system in ACL2 this is probably the desired
      setting for part [47ma[0m.

      [47m:doit[0m --- any user-defined function may be redefined silently
      (without query or warning) but when an attempt is made to
      redefine a system function, a query is made.  This setting is
      recommended when you start making massive changes to your
      prototyped system (and tire of even the warning messages issued
      by [47m:warn[0m).

  In support of our own ACL2 systems [programming] there are two other
  settings.  We suggest ordinary users not use them.

      [47m:warn![0m --- every attempt to redefine a name produces a warning but no
      query.  Since ACL2 system functions can be redefined this way,
      this setting should be used by the only-slightly-less-than
      supremely confident ACL2 system hacker.

      [47m:doit![0m --- this setting allows any name to be redefined silently
      (without query or warnings).  ACL2 system functions are fair
      game.  This setting is reserved for the supremely confident
      ACL2 system hacker.  (Actually, this setting is used when we
      are loading massively modified versions of the ACL2 source
      files.)

  Part [47mb[0m of [47mld-redefinition-action[0m tells the system how to ``renew''
  the property list of the name being redefined.  There are two
  choices:

      [47m:erase[0m --- erase all properties stored under the name, or

      [47m:overwrite[0m --- preserve existing properties and let the redefining
      overwrite them.

  It should be stressed that neither of these [47mb[0m settings is guaranteed
  to result in an entirely satisfactory state of affairs after the
  redefinition.  Roughly speaking, [47m:erase[0m returns the property list
  of the name to the state it was in when the name was first
  introduced.  Lemmas, type information, etc., stored under that name
  are lost.  Is that what you wanted?  Sometimes it is, as when the
  old definition is ``completely wrong.'' But other times the old
  definition was ``almost right'' in the sense that some of the work
  done with it is still (intended to be) valid.  In that case,
  [47m:overwrite[0m might be the correct [47mb[0m setting.  For example if [47mfn[0m was a
  function and is being re-[47m[defun][0m'd with the same [signature], then
  the properties stored by the new [47m[defun][0m should overwrite those
  stored by the old [47m[defun][0m but the properties stored by [47m[defthm][0ms
  will be preserved.

  In addition, neither setting will cause ACL2 to erase properties
  stored under other symbols!  Thus, if [47mFOO[0m names a rewrite rule
  which rewrites a term beginning with the function symbol [47mBAR[0m and
  you then redefine [47mFOO[0m to rewrite a term beginning with the function
  symbol [47mBAZ[0m, then the old version of [47mFOO[0m is still available (because
  the rule itself was added to the rewrite rules for [47mBAR[0m, whose
  property list was not cleared by redefining [47mFOO[0m).

  The [47mb[0m setting is only used as the default action when no query is
  made.  If you choose a setting for part a that produces a query
  then you will have the opportunity, for each redefinition, to
  specify whether the property list is to be erased or overwritten.

  The keyword command [47m:[0m[47m[redef][0m sets [47mld-redefinition-action[0m to the pair
  [47m(:query . :overwrite)[0m.  Since the resulting query will give you the
  chance to specify [47m:erase[0m instead of [47m:overwrite[0m, this setting is
  quite convenient.  But when you are engaged in heavy-duty
  prototyping, you may wish to use a setting such as [47m:warn[0m or even
  [47m:doit[0m.  For that you will have to invoke a form such as:

    (set-ld-redefinition-action '(:doit . :overwrite) state) .

  [47m[Encapsulate][0m causes somewhat odd interaction with the user if
  redefinition occurs within the encapsulation because the
  [encapsulate]d event list is processed several times.  For example,
  if the redefinition action causes a query and a non-local
  definition is actually a redefinition, then the query will be posed
  twice, once during each pass.  C'est la vie.

  Finally, it should be stressed again that redefinition is dangerous
  because not all of the rules about a name are stored on the
  property list of the name.  Thus, redefinition can render
  ill-formed terms stored elsewhere in the database or can preserve
  now-invalid rules.  See [redundant-events], in particular the
  section ``Note About Unfortunate Redundancies,'' for more
  discussion of potential pitfalls of redefinition.")
 (LD-SKIP-PROOFSP
  (LD)
  "How carefully ACL2 processes your [command]s

    Examples:
    ACL2 !>(set-ld-skip-proofsp t state)
     T
    ACL2 !s>(set-ld-skip-proofsp nil state)
     NIL
    ACL2 !>(set-ld-skip-proofsp 'include-book state)
     INCLUDE-BOOK
    ACL2 !s>

  A global variable in the ACL2 [47m[state][0m, called [47m'ld-skip-proofsp[0m,
  determines the thoroughness with which ACL2 processes your
  [command]s.  This variable may take on one of three values: [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m
  or [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m.  When [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is non-[47mnil[0m, the system
  skips all proofs, which of course can render the system unsound.
  The form [47m(set-ld-skip-proofsp flg state)[0m is the general-purpose way
  of setting [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m.  This global variable is an ``[47m[ld][0m
  special,'' which is to say, you may call [47m[ld][0m in such a way as to
  ``bind'' this variable for the dynamic extent of the [47m[ld][0m.

  When [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is non-[47mnil[0m, the default [prompt] displays the
  character [47ms[0m.  Thus, the [prompt]

    ACL2 !s>

  means that the default [defun-mode] is [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m (otherwise the
  character [47mp[0m, for [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m, would also be printed; see
  [default-print-prompt]) but ``proofs are being skipped.''

  Observe that there are two legal non-[47mnil[0m values, [47mt[0m and
  [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m.  When [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47mt[0m, ACL2 skips all proof
  obligations but otherwise performs all other required analysis of
  input [events].  When [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m, ACL2
  skips not only proof obligations but all analysis except that
  required to compute the effect of successfully executed [events].
  To explain the distinction, let us consider one particular event,
  say a [47m[defun][0m.  Very roughly speaking, a [47m[defun][0m event normally
  involves a check of the syntactic well-formedness of the submitted
  definition, the generation and proof of the termination conditions,
  and the computation and storage of various rules such as a
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rule and some [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules.  By
  ``normally'' above we mean when [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47mnil[0m.  How does a
  [47m[defun][0m behave when [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is non-[47mnil[0m?

  If [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47mt[0m, then [47m[defun][0m performs the syntactic
  well-formedness checks and computes and stores the various rules,
  but it does not actually carry out the termination proofs.  If
  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m, [47m[defun][0m does not do the
  syntactic well-formedness check nor does it carry out the
  termination proof.  Instead, it merely computes and stores the
  rules under the assumption that the checks and proofs would all
  succeed.  Observe that a setting of [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m is ``stronger''
  than a setting of [47mt[0m in the sense that [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m causes
  [47m[defun][0m to assume even more about the admissibility of the event
  than [47mt[0m does.

  As one might infer from the choice of name, the [47m[include-book][0m event
  sets [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m to [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m when processing the
  [events] in a book being loaded.  Thus, [47m[include-book][0m does the
  minimal work necessary to carry out the effects of every event in
  the book.  The syntactic checks and proof obligations were,
  presumably, successfully carried out when the book was certified.

  A non-[47mnil[0m value for [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m also affects the system's output
  messages.  Event summaries (the paragraphs that begin ``[Summary]''
  and display the event forms, rules used, etc.) are not printed when
  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is non-[47mnil[0m.  Warnings and observations are printed
  when [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47mt[0m but are not printed when it is
  [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m.

  Intuitively, [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m [47mt[0m means skip just the proofs and
  otherwise do all the work normally required for an event; while
  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m is ``stronger'' and means do as
  little as possible to process [events].  In accordance with this
  intuition, [47m[local][0m [events] are processed when [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47mt[0m
  but are skipped when [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m.

  The ACL2 system itself uses only two settings, [47mnil[0m and
  [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m, the latter being used only when executing the
  [events] inside of a book being included.  The [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m
  setting of [47mt[0m is provided as a convenience to the user.  For
  example, suppose one has a file of [events].  By loading it with
  [47m[ld][0m with [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m set to [47mt[0m, the [events] can all be checked
  for syntactic correctness and assumed without proof.  This is a
  convenient way to recover a state lost by a system crash or to
  experiment with a modification of an [events] file.

  The foregoing discussion is actually based on a lie.  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m
  is allowed two other values, [47m'initialize-acl2[0m and
  [47m'include-book-with-locals[0m.  The first causes behavior similar to [47mt[0m
  but skips [47m[local][0m [events] and avoids some error checks that would
  otherwise prevent ACL2 from properly booting.  The second is
  identical to [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m but also executes [47m[local][0m [events].
  These additional values are not intended for use by the user, but
  no barriers to their use have been erected.

  User-defined tools may temporarily modify the global setting of
  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m; for example, see [remove-hyps].

  We close by reminding the user that ACL2 is potentially unsound if
  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is set by the user.  Indeed, direct setting of
  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is illegal within a book (except during
  [47m[make-event][0m expansion).  We provide access to it simply to allow
  experimentation and rapid reconstruction of lost or modified
  logical [world]s.")
