 (LET (BASICS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Binding of lexically scoped (local) variables


Introduction

  Example

    (let ((x (* x x))
          (y (* 2 x)))
     (list x y))

  If the form above is executed in an environment in which [47mx[0m has the
  value [47m-2[0m, then the result is [47m'(4 -4)[0m.

  [47mLet[0m expressions bind variables so that their ``local'' values, the
  values they have when the ``body'' of the [47mlet[0m is evaluated, are
  possibly different than their ``global'' values, the values they
  have in the context in which the [47mlet[0m expression appears.  In the
  [47mlet[0m expression above, the local variables bound by the [47mlet[0m are [47mx[0m
  and [47my[0m.  They are locally bound to the values delivered by the two
  forms [47m(* x x)[0m and [47m(* 2 x)[0m, respectively, that appear in the
  ``bindings'' of the [47mlet[0m.  The body of the [47mlet[0m is [47m(list x y)[0m.

  Suppose that the [47mlet[0m expression above occurs in a context in which [47mx[0m
  has the value [47m-2[0m.  (The global value of [47my[0m is irrelevant to this
  example.)  For example, one might imagine that the [47mlet[0m form above
  occurs as the body of some function, [47mfn[0m, with the formal parameter
  [47mx[0m and we are evaluating [47m(fn -2)[0m.

  To evaluate the [47mlet[0m above in a context in which [47mx[0m is [47m-2[0m, we first
  evaluate the two forms specifying the local values of the
  variables.  Thus, [47m(* x x)[0m is evaluated and produces [47m4[0m (because [47mx[0m is
  [47m-2[0m) and [47m(* 2 x)[0m is evaluated and produces [47m-4[0m (because [47mx[0m is [47m-2[0m).
  Then [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are bound to these values and the body of the [47mlet[0m is
  evaluated.  Thus, when the body, [47m(list x y)[0m is evaluated, [47mx[0m is [47m4[0m
  and [47my[0m is [47m-4[0m.  Thus, the body produces [47m'(4 -4)[0m.

  Note that the binding of [47my[0m, which is written after the binding of [47mx[0m
  and which mentions [47mx[0m, nevertheless uses the global value of [47mx[0m, not
  the new local value.  That is, the local variables of the [47mlet[0m are
  bound ``in parallel'' rather than ``sequentially.'' In contrast, if
  the example [47mlet*[0m form:

    (let* ((x (* x x))
           (y (* 2 x)))
     (list x y))

  is evaluated when the global value of [47mx[0m is [47m-2[0m, then the result is [47m'(4
  8)[0m, because the local value of [47my[0m is computed after [47mx[0m has been bound
  to [47m4[0m.  [47m[Let*][0m binds its local variables ``sequentially.''

  General [47mlet[0m forms

    (let ((var1 term1) ... (varn termn)) body)
    and
    (let ((var1 term1) ... (varn termn))
     (declare ...) ... (declare ...)
     body)

  where the [47mvari[0m are distinct variables, the [47mtermi[0m are terms involving
  only variables bound in the environment containing the [47mlet[0m, and
  [47mbody[0m is a term involving only the [47mvari[0m plus the variables bound in
  the environment containing the [47mlet[0m.  Each [47mvari[0m must be used in [47mbody[0m
  or else [declare]d ignored.  In ACL2 the only [47m[declare][0m forms
