 (LIVE-STOBJ-IN-PROOF
  (RAW-LISP-ERROR)
  "Error messages about ``live'' [stobj]s during proofs

  It is possible to see an error like the following.  (This error is
  probably very rare, and perhaps can only occur during a proof.)

    ***********************************************
    ************ ABORTING from raw Lisp ***********
    ********** (see :DOC raw-lisp-error) **********
    Error:  A live stobj (for stobj ST) was unexpectedly encountered
            when evaluating a call of the function, ST-INIT.
            See :DOC live-stobj-in-proof.
    ***********************************************

  The solution is generally to [disable] the [executable-counterpart]
  of the offending function.  It may well be that the only way to get
  an unexpected ``live'' [stobj] is by the use of [47m[swap-stobjs][0m, as
  suggested by the example shown below (essentially provided by Sol
  Swords) --- which results in a different error message, shown
  below, than the one above.

  First introduce a pair of congruent [stobj]s.

    (defstobj st (fld))
    (defstobj st1 (fld1) :congruent-to st)

  Now define a function that ``initializes'' the stobj [47mst[0m by creating a
  new stobj [47mst1[0m and swapping the two (see [swap-stobjs]).

    (defun st-init (st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st)))
      (with-local-stobj st1
        (mv-let (st1 st)
          (swap-stobjs st1 st)
          st)))

  Here is a proof attempt that results in a raw Lisp error due to a
  live stobj being introduced by [47m[swap-stobjs][0m.

    ACL2 !>(thm (not (equal (st-init '(1)) '(nil))))

    ***********************************************
    Note:  SWAP-STOBJS has been called on stobjs named ST1 and ST,
    where the value of ST1 is a live stobj but the value of ST is not.
    This is an error, as such calls are unsupported; see :DOC swap-stobjs.
    Advanced users may find it helpful to evaluate the form
    (set-debugger-enable :bt)
    to see a backtrace of calls leading to this error;
    see :DOC set-debugger-enable.
      Will attempt to exit the proof in progress;
      otherwise, the next interrupt will abort the proof.
      For an immediate abort see :DOC abort-soft.
    ***********************************************

    The message above might explain the error.  If not, and
    if you didn't cause an explicit interrupt (Control-C),
    then it may help to see :DOC raw-lisp-error.

    To enable breaks into the debugger (also see :DOC acl2-customization):
    (SET-DEBUGGER-ENABLE T)

    Summary
    Form:  ( THM ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    *** Note: No checkpoints to print. ***

    ACL2 Error [Failure] in ( THM ...):  See :DOC failure.

    ******** FAILED ********
    ACL2 !>

  In fact, that formula is not a theorem!  Through Version 8.6, ACL2
  mistakenly proved this alleged theorem by evaluating the indicated
  call of [47mst-init[0m to obtain an actual Lisp array, because of how ACL2
  handles [stobj]s in Lisp.  But now ACL2 produces the error
  displayed just above.

  The error is avoided if we [disable] the [executable-counterpart] of
  the offending function, [47mst-init[0m.  Indeed, the following theorem,
  which contradicts the false claim above and disables the offending
  executable-counterpart, shows that the logical value of [47m(st-init
  '(1))[0m is indeed [47m'(nil)[0m.

    (thm (equal (st-init '(1)) '(nil))
         :hints((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable (:e st-init)))))")
 (LOCAL
  (EVENTS)
  "Hiding an event in an encapsulation or book

    Examples:
    (local (defthm hack1
             (implies (and (acl2-numberp x)
                           (acl2-numberp y)
                           (equal (* x y) 1))
                      (equal y (/ x)))))

    (local (defun double-naturals-induction (a b)
             (cond ((and (integerp a) (integerp b) (< 0 a) (< 0 b))
                    (double-naturals-induction (1- a) (1- b)))
                   (t (list a b)))))

    General Form:
    (local ev)

  where [47mev[0m is an event form.  If the current default [defun-mode] (see
  [default-defun-mode]) is [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m and [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is [47mnil[0m or
  [47mt[0m, then [47m(local ev)[0m is equivalent to [47mev[0m.  But if the current default
  [defun-mode] is [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m or if [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is
  [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m, then [47m(local ev)[0m is a [47mno-op[0m.  Thus, if such forms
  are in the event list of an [47m[encapsulate][0m event or in a book, they
  are processed when the encapsulation or book is checked for
  admissibility in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode but are skipped when extending the
  host [world].  Such [events] are thus considered ``local'' to the
  verification of the encapsulation or book.  The non-local [events]
  are the ones ``exported'' by the encapsulation or book.  See
  [encapsulate] for a thorough discussion.  Also see
  [local-incompatibility] for a discussion of a commonly encountered
  problem with such event hiding: you can't make an event local if
  its presence is required to make sense of a non-local one.

  Note that [events] that change the default [defun-mode], and in fact
  any [events] that set the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m, are disallowed
  inside the scope of [47mlocal[0m.  See [embedded-event-form].")
 (LOCAL-INCOMPATIBILITY
  (MISCELLANEOUS)
  "When non-local [events] won't replay in isolation

  Sometimes a ``[47m[local][0m incompatibility'' is reported while attempting
  to embed some [events], as in an [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[include-book][0m.
  This is generally due to the use of a locally defined name in a
  non-local event or the failure to make a witnessing definition
  [47m[local][0m.  (But see the Remark at the end of this topic about a
  related issue with guard-checking.)

  [47m[Local][0m incompatibilities may be detected while trying to execute the
  strictly non-[local] [events] of an [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[certify-book][0m
  call.  For example, [47mencapsulate[0m operates by first executing all the
  [events] (local and non-local) with [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m [47mnil[0m, to
  confirm that they are all admissible.  Then it attempts merely to
  assume the non-local ones to create the desired theory, by
  executing the [events] with [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m set to [47m'include-book[0m.
  [47mCertify-book[0m does something similar, so that when [47m[include-book][0m
  assumes the non-local events in the book, we are confident that the
  previously successful certification has performed the necessary
  admissibility check.

  How can a sequence of [events] admitted with [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m [47mnil[0m
  fail when [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is [47m'include-book[0m?  The key observation
  is that in the latter case only the non-local [events] are
  processed.  The [47m[local][0m ones are skipped and so the non-local ones
  must not depend upon them.

  Two typical mistakes are suggested by the detection of a [47m[local][0m
  incompatibility: (1) a locally defined function or macro was used
  in a non-[47m[local][0m event (and, in the case of [47m[encapsulate][0m, was not
  included among the [signature]s) and (2) the witnessing definition
  of a function that was included among the [signature]s of an
  [47m[encapsulate][0m was not made [47m[local][0m.

  An example of mistake (1) would be to include among your
  [encapsulate]d [events] both a local definition of [47mf[0m and a
  non-local theorem about [47mf[0m, for example as follows.

    (encapsulate ()
      (local (defun f (x) x))
      (defun g (x) x)

      (defthm f-is-g (equal (f x) (g x)))
      )

  In this case, either the [47m[defthm][0m should be made [47m[local][0m or, if we
  want to export the [47mdefthm[0m, then the [47m[defun][0m of [47mf[0m should be made
  non-local.  Similar analysis holds if the events in the [47mencapsulate[0m
  call above are instead in a book.

  An example of mistake (2) would be to include [47m((fn *) => *)[0m among
  your [signature]s and then to write [47m(defun fn (x) ...)[0m in your
  [events], instead of [47m(local (defun fn ...))[0m, as shown in the
  example below.  In essence, this is an error because [47mfn[0m is exported
  twice: once by the signature and once by the [47mdefun[0m.

    (encapsulate
      ( ((fn *) => *) )

      (defun fn (x) x)
      )

  One subtle aspect of [47m[encapsulate][0m is that if you constrain any
  member of a mutually recursive clique you must define the entire
  clique locally and then you must constrain those members of it you
  want axiomatized non-locally.

  Errors due to [47m[local][0m incompatibility should never occur in the
  inclusion of a fully certified book.  Certification ensures against
  it.  Therefore, if [47m[include-book][0m reports an incompatibility, we
  claim that earlier in the processing of the [47m[include-book][0m a
  warning was printed advising you that some book was uncertified.
  If this is not the case --- if [47m[include-book][0m reports an
  incompatibility and there has been no prior warning about lack of
  certification --- please report it to the ACL2 implementors.

  When a [47m[local][0m incompatibility is detected during the second pass of
  an [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[certify-book][0m, the logical [world] is restored
  to what it was immediately before that call was evaluated.

  Note that for [47mcertify-book[0m, local incompatibility could be caused by
  an event that is local either in the certification world (see
  [portcullis]) or in the book itself.  ACL2 checks during
  [47mcertify-book[0m for local incompatibility by rolling back the world
  --- back through the first portcullis command that generates a
  local event, or if there is none, then back through the first local
  event in the book (with the general exception of those inside an
  [47mencapsulate[0m, but see final Remark below) --- and then including the
  book.  But all that is skipped in the absence of either kind of
  local event.

  See [fast-cert] for a way to skip the local incompatibility check,
  although that may compromise soundness.

  Here is a subtle example of [47m[local][0m incompatibility.  The problem is
  that in order for [47mfoo-type-prescription[0m to be admitted using the
  specified [47m:typed-term[0m [47m(foo x)[0m, the conclusion [47m(my-natp (foo x))[0m
  depends on [47mmy-natp[0m being a [compound-recognizer].  This is fine on
  the first pass of the [47m[encapsulate][0m, during which lemma [47mmy-natp-cr[0m
  is admitted.  But [47mmy-natp-cr[0m is skipped on the second pass because
  it is marked [47m[local][0m, and this causes [47mfoo-type-prescription[0m to fail
  on the second pass.

    (defun my-natp (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (and (integerp x)
           (<= 0 x)))

    (defun foo (x)
      (nfix x))

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (local (defthm my-natp-cr
              (equal (my-natp x)
                     (and (integerp x)
                          (<= 0 x)))
              :rule-classes :compound-recognizer))
     (defthm foo-type-prescription
       (my-natp (foo x))
       :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable foo)))
       :rule-classes ((:type-prescription :typed-term (foo x)))))

  [31;1mRemark[0m.  We conclude by remarking that for ``local incompatibility''
  in the case of [47mcertify-book[0m, the notion of ``local'' is a bit more
  comprehensive than the name suggests, in that it can involve
  [guard]-checking in the following situation.  For example, in a
  fresh ACL2 session evaluate the [command] [47m(set-guard-checking
  :none)[0m followed by [47m(table foo 0 (car 3))[0m.  Then any attempt to
  certify a book should fail, since subsequent inclusion of that book
  would fail with a [guard] violation; that's because the
  [47mset-guard-checking[0m call was not saved in the [certificate] file,
  hence was not evaluated when evaluating [47m(car 3)[0m while attempting to
  include the book.

  The example above may be helpful while reading the following general
  explanation of how local incompatibility incorporates the setting
  of guard-checking.  Suppose you are evaluating [command]s directly
  in the top-level read-eval-print loop.  One of those commands might
  be a call of [47m[set-guard-checking][0m that weakens guard-checking from
  its default behavior, i.e., [47m(set-guard-checking VAL)[0m where [47mVAL[0m is
  other than [47mt[0m, [47m:nowarn[0m, or [47m:all[0m.  This command does not change the
  logical [world], so it will not be part of the book's [portcullis]
  commands.  In that sense, it is like a [local] portcullis command.
  ACL2 notices this case and insists that any portcullis event
  evaluated with guard-checking that is weaker than the default must
  be rolled back during the check for local incompatibilities.")
