  [47m[logic-term-list-listp][0m, [47mlogic-fns-list-listp[0m does not check that [47mx[0m
  is a list of lists of terms; rather, [47mlogic-fns-list-listp[0m should
  only be applied to such lists.  See [47m[defun-mode-lambdas][0m for some
  clarifications of how [47m:program[0m mode functions are allowed in
  certain [47m:logic[0m mode terms.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<logic-fns-list-listp>

    (defun logic-fns-list-listp (x wrld)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (plist-worldp wrld)
                                  (pseudo-term-list-listp x))))
      (cond ((endp x) t)
            (t (and (logic-fns-listp (car x) wrld)
                    (logic-fns-list-listp (cdr x) wrld)))))")
 (LOGIC-FNS-LISTP
  (TERMP ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for when a given list of [term]s calls only [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m-mode
  function symbols

  For a list [47mx[0m of [term]s in [world] [47mwrld[0m, [47m(logic-fns-listp x wrld)[0m is
  true if and only if every function symbol called in [47mx[0m is in
  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode in [47mwrld[0m.  Unlike [47m[logic-term-listp][0m, [47mlogic-fns-listp[0m
  does not check that [47mx[0m is a list of terms; rather, [47mlogic-fns-listp[0m
  should only be applied to such lists.  See [47m[defun-mode-lambdas][0m for
  some clarifications of how [47m:program[0m mode functions are allowed in
  certain [47m:logic[0m mode terms.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<logic-fns-listp>

    (defun logic-fns-listp (lst wrld)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (plist-worldp wrld)
                                  (pseudo-term-listp lst))))
      (cond ((endp lst) t)
            (t (and (logic-fnsp (car lst) wrld)
                    (logic-fns-listp (cdr lst) wrld)))))")
 (LOGIC-FNSP
  (TERMP ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for when a given [term] calls only [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m-mode function
  symbols

  For a [term] [47mx[0m in [world] [47mwrld[0m, [47m(logic-fnsp x wrld)[0m is true if and
  only if every function symbol called in [47mx[0m is in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode in
  [47mwrld[0m.  Unlike [47m[logic-termp][0m, [47mlogic-fnsp[0m does not check that [47mx[0m is a
  term; rather, [47mlogic-fnsp[0m should only be applied to terms.  See
  [47m[defun-mode-lambdas][0m for some clarifications of how [47m:program[0m mode
  functions are allowed in certain [47m:logic[0m mode terms.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<logic-fnsp>

    (defun logic-fnsp (term wrld)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (plist-worldp wrld)
                                  (pseudo-termp term))))
      (cond ((variablep term) t)
            ((fquotep term) t)
            ((flambdap (ffn-symb term))
             (and (logic-fnsp (lambda-body (ffn-symb term))
                              wrld)
                  (logic-fns-listp (fargs term) wrld)))
            ((programp (ffn-symb term) wrld) nil)
            (t (logic-fns-listp (fargs term) wrld))))")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Background knowledge in ACL2 logic for theorem prover tutorial

  You might think that in order to use the theorem prover you have to
  know the mathematical logic supported by ACL2.  But you need to
  know a lot less about it than you might think.

  Technically, a theorem is a formula that can be derived from axioms
  by using rules of inference.  Thus, to do a proof you have to know
  (a) the syntax of formulas, (b) the axioms, and (c) the rules of
  inference.  Traditionally, these things are spelled out in
  excruciating detail in treatments of mathematical logic --- and for
  good reason.

  The whole point of proving theorems is that it is a way to determine
  that a formula is ``always true'' (under some model of the axioms).
  By ``always true'' we actually mean what logicians mean when they
  say the formula is [3mvalid[0m: true in the model, for all possible
  values of the variables.  Here by ``model of the axioms'' we mean
  an understanding of the meaning of the various function symbols so
  that the axioms are true for all values of the variables.  If the
  variables in your conjecture can take on an infinite number of
  values, proof is often the [31;1monly[0m way to determine that a conjecture
  is ``always true.'' So if proof is being used to determine that a
  questionable formula is always true the proof must be carried out
  flawlessly.  Thus, the (a) syntax, (b) axioms, and (c) rules of
  inference must be described precisely and followed to the letter.

  But formal mathematical logic was invented to explain how people
  reason.  To the extent that logic mimics human reasoning, proofs
  can be seen as just extremely carefully crafted arguments.  Given
  that ACL2 is responsible for following the rules ``to the letter,''
  your main job is ``explain'' the big leaps.

  To use the theorem prover you must understand (a) the syntax, because
  you must be able to write formulas flawlessly.  But you don't have
  to know (b) the axioms and (c) the rules of inference at nearly the
  same level of precision, as long as you understand the basic
  structure and language of proofs.

  Below is part of a proof of a certain theorem.  You ought to be able
  to understand the following.  Since what we describe is a proof of
  one case of the formula, we hope that you're [3mconvinced[0m that the
  formula holds for that case.

  Read this and follow the links to confirm that you understand what
  happens.  Be sure to then use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return
  to this page and continue.

  [31;1mAn Annotated Proof of[0m

    (implies (true-listp z)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  ``We will prove that reversing the reverse of a [47mtrue-listp[0m yields the
  original list.  The formula stating this is above.  We will prove
  it by induction (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INDUCTIVE-PROOF]) on the list
  structure of [47mz[0m.

  The base case (see [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-BASE-CASE]) of
  the induction is:

    (implies (endp z)
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z))).

  This formula is equivalent, by propositional calculus (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS]), to

    (implies (and (endp z)
                  (true-listp z))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  Rewriting (see [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-REWRITING]) with
  the definition of [47mendp[0m produces:

    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (true-listp z))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  Rewriting repeatedly (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-REWRITING-REPEATEDLY]) starting
  with the definition of [47mtrue-listp[0m produces:

    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (equal z nil))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  Then using the second [3mhypothesis[0m, just substituting equals for equals
  (see [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-EQUALS-FOR-EQUALS]), we get

    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (equal z nil))
             (equal (rev (rev nil)) nil))

  Since the [3mconclusion[0m involves no variables, we can evaluate (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-EVALUATION]) it, getting

    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (equal z nil))
             T)

  But this is an instance (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE]) of the tautology (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS])
  [47m(implies p T)[0m.  Thus, the base case is proved.''

  Now it is time for a little quiz.  There are just three questions.

  [31;1mQ1[0m: The case above was the Base Case of an inductive proof of

    (implies (true-listp z)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  in which we did induction on the structure of the linear list [47mz[0m.
  What is the Induction Step?  That is, what do you have to prove
  besides the Base Case to complete this inductive proof?

  Below are four commonly given answers; choose one.  Then look here
  (see [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-Q1-ANSWER]) to find out if
  you're right.

    [3mInduction Step -- Choice (i)[0m:
    (implies (not (endp z))
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

    [3mInduction Step -- Choice (ii)[0m:
    (implies (true-listp (cdr z))
             (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))

    [3mInduction Step -- Choice (iii)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr x))) (cdr x)))
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

    [3mInduction Step -- Choice (iv)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (implies (true-listp (cdr z))
                           (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z))))
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  [31;1mQ2[0m: To prove the Induction Step we must prove one or more of the
  goals below.

  Which combinations are sufficient to imply the Induction Step?
  Decide what is required and then look here (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-Q2-ANSWER]) to find out if
  you're right.  To help you, the Induction Step is of the form:

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and [3mc[0m
                  (implies [3mp'[0m [3mq'[0m))
             (implies [3mp[0m [3mq[0m))

  and beside each candidate subgoal we show its structure in those
  terms.

    [3mSubgoal (i)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))                        ; (implies (and [3mc[0m
                  (true-listp z))                       ;               [3mp[0m)
             (true-listp (cdr z)))                      ;          [3mp'[0m)

    [3mSubgoal (ii)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))                        ; (implies (and [3mc[0m
                  (true-listp z)                        ;               [3mp[0m
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))  ;               [3mq'[0m)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))                   ;          [3mq[0m)

    [3mSubgoal (iii)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))                        ; (implies (and [3mc[0m
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))  ;               [3mq'[0m)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))                   ;          [3mq[0m)

    [3mSubgoal (iv)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))                        ; (implies (and [3mc[0m
                  (true-listp (cdr z))                  ;               [3mp'[0m
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))  ;               [3mq'[0m)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))                   ;          [3mq[0m)

  [31;1mQ3[0m: Suppose you know the theorem

    [3mTheorem[0m:
    (implies (p (f x))
             (equal (g (h x))
                    x))

  and you wish to rewrite the target [47m(g (h a))[0m to [47ma[0m in

    [3mGoal Conjecture[0m:
    (implies (and (q (f a))
                  (r a))
             (s (g (h a))))

  What must you prove to relieve the hypothesis of [3mTheorem[0m?

  After you've thought about it, look here (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-Q3-ANSWER]) for our answer.

  [31;1mEnd of the Quiz[0m

  If this page made sense, you're ready to read the introduction to the
  theorem prover.

  If you are reading this as part of the tutorial introduction to the
  theorem prover, use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [introduction-to-the-theorem-prover].")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-BASE-CASE
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "A brief explanation of base cases

  According to the sentence, the conjecture being proved is ``reversing
  the reverse of a [47mtrue-listp[0m yields the original list.'' The formula
  corresponding to this conjecture is:

    (implies (true-listp z)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z)).

  We're also told that this is an inductive proof.  Evidently we're
  doing an induction on the structure of the list [47mz[0m.  Then the [3mBase
  Case[0m is the formula:

    (implies (endp z)
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z))).

  Now use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the example proof in
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted].")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-EQUALS-FOR-EQUALS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Substitution of equals for equals

  Anytime you have an equality hypothesis relating two terms, e.g.,

    (equal [3mlhs[0m [3mrhs[0m)

  it is legal to substitute one for the other anyplace else in the
  formula.  Doing so does not change the truth value of the formula.

  You can use a [3mnegated equality[0m this way [3mprovided[0m it appears in the
  conclusion.  For example, it is ok to transform

    (implies (true-listp x)
             (not (equal x 23)))

  to

    (implies (true-listp 23)
             (not (equal x 23)))

  by substitutions of equals for equals.  That is because, by
  propositional calculus (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS]), we
  could rearrange the formulas into their [3mcontrapositive[0m forms:

    (implies (equal x 23)
             (not (true-listp x)))

  and

    (implies (equal x 23)
             (not (true-listp 23)))

  and see the equality as a hypothesis and the substitution of [47m23[0m for [47mx[0m
  as sensible.

  Sometimes people speak loosely and say ``substitution of equals for
  equals'' when they really mean ``substitutions of equivalents for
  equivalents.'' Equality, as tested by [47mEQUAL[0m, is only one example of
  an equivalence relation.  The next most common is propositional
  equivalence, as tested by [47mIFF[0m.  You can use propositional
  equivalence hypotheses to substitute one side for the other
  [3mprovided[0m the target term occurs in a propositional place, as
  discussed at the bottom of propositional calculus (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS]).

  Now use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the example proof in
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted].")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-EVALUATION
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Evaluation during proofs

  Any time you are proving a formula and see a subterm in the formula
  that contains no variables, you can just evaluate the subterm.

  This is familiar from algebra: It is not uncommon to rearrange a
  polynomial to collect all the constants and then add them up:

    (3x + 2 + 7y + 2)
    =
    (3x + 7y + (2 + 2))
    =
    (3x + 7y + 4).

  That last step is just evaluation.

  It happens often in ACL2 proofs because theorems involve constants
  and defined functions and when those constants ``drift into the
  maw'' of a function, the function call can be eliminated and
  replaced by a new constant.  ACL2 does this automatically; you
  don't have to tell it.  In fact, there are a few occasions where
  you might prefer it [3mnot[0m evaluate and those are the ones you have to
  look out for!  They'll be obvious when they happen because you'll
  see a mysterious constant crop up in the proof.

  Evaluation is legal because it is just the repeated use of
  unconditional rewriting to replace definitions by their
  instantiated bodies until no function calls remain.

  Now use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the example proof in
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted].")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INDUCTIVE-PROOF
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "A brief explanation of induction

  We start by showing classical induction on the natural numbers in an
  ACL2 setting before turning to a more general treatment of
  induction.

  [31;1mClassical Induction on Natural Numbers[0m: Induction is familiar in the
  arithmetic setting.  Let [47m(p n)[0m denote some formula involving the
  variable [47mn[0m (and perhaps some other variables which we don't
  exhibit).  Then to prove [47m(p n)[0m, for all [47mn[0m, by classical induction
  on the construction of the natural numbers, prove each of the
  following:

    [3mBase Case[0m:
    (implies (zp n) (p n))

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (zp n))
                  (p (- n 1)))
             (p n))

  The Base Case establishes that [47mp[0m holds for [47m0[0m.  In fact, because of
  the definition of [47m[zp][0m {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]), it
  establishes that [47m(p n)[0m holds when [47mn[0m is [47m0[0m and it holds when [47mn[0m is not
  a natural number.

  The Induction Step establishes that if [47mn[0m is a natural number other
  than [47m0[0m, and if [47mp[0m holds for [47mn[0m-1, then [47mp[0m holds for [47mn[0m.  The hypothesis
  [47m(p (- n 1))[0m above is called the [3minduction hypothesis[0m.

  Note that if the Base Case and Induction Step are valid, then we know
  [47m(p n)[0m, for all [47mn[0m.  You can convince yourself of this by picking any
  object and asking ``how do I know [47mp[0m holds for this object?'' For
  example, [47m(p -7)[0m, [47m(p 'abc)[0m, and [47m(p 0)[0m are all established by the
  Base Case.  What about [47m(p 1)[0m?  That follows from [47m(p 0)[0m, given the
  Induction Step.  Why?  To prove [47m(p 1)[0m using the Induction Step, you
  have to establish [47m(not (zp 1))[0m, which is true, and [47m(p (- 1 1))[0m,
  which is [47m(p 0)[0m, which is true by the Base Case.  So [47m(p 1)[0m is true.
  Similar reasoning proves [47m(p 2)[0m from from [47m(p 1)[0m, etc.  Clearly, for
  every natural number other than [47m0[0m we could reason like this to show
  that [47mp[0m holds.  Since the Base Case handled all the objects that are
  not natural numbers, and handled [47m0[0m, we know [47m(p n)[0m, for all [47mn[0m.

  There is a duality between recursion and induction that ACL2
  exploits.  The fact that the Base and Induction steps above are
  sufficient to prove [47mp[0m for all objects is related to the fact that
  the following recursion defines a total, terminating function:

    (defun nat-recursion (n)
      (if (zp n)
          n
          (nat-recursion (- n 1))))

  When this function is admitted we have to prove that if [47m(zp n)[0m does
  not hold, then [47m(- n 1)[0m is smaller, in some sense, than [47mn[0m.  This
  sense of ``smaller'' is determined by some [3mmeasure[0m of the
  arguments.  That measure must return an ordinal (see [ordinals]
  {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign])), but the most common measures
  return natural numbers, which are among the ordinals.  Furthermore,
  that measure should ensure that the terms in the recursive calls
  are smaller than the formals, i.e., the measure of [47m(- n 1)[0m must be
  smaller than the measure of [47mn[0m, when the recursive branches are
  taken.  This sense of ``smaller'' must be [3mwell-founded[0m: it must be
  impossible to have an infinitely descending chain of smaller
  things.  This is true of the less-than relation on the ordinals
  (see [47m[o<][0m {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign])).  Well-foundedness
  means that eventually any recursion must ``bottom out'' because
  things can't keep getting smaller forever.

  The recursion in [47mnat-recursion[0m [31;1msuggests[0m the induction shown above:
  the Base Case is defined by the [47mif[0m branch that does not lead to a
  recursive call.  The Induction Step is defined by the other branch.
  The induction hypothesis is defined by what we recur on, i.e., [47m(- n
  1)[0m.  The theorems proved when [47mnat-recursion[0m is introduced [3mjustify[0m
  the classical induction scheme noted above.

  Every recursively defined ACL2 function suggests a legal induction
  and vice versa.

  Furthermore, every call of a recursively defined function on distinct
  variable symbols also suggests a legal induction: just take the
  induction suggested by the function's recursive definition after
  renaming the formal parameters to be the variables in the call.

  For example, it should be clear that [47m(nat-recursion a)[0m suggests a
  Base Case defined by [47m(zp a)[0m, and induction step defined by [47m(not (zp
  a))[0m and an induction hypothesis about [47m(- a 1)[0m.

  Note that the term [47m(fact n)[0m suggests the same classical induction on
  natural numbers shown above, where [47mfact[0m is defined as follows (even
  though we've used the formal parameter [47mk[0m below).

    (defun fact (k)
      (if (zp k)
          1
          (* k (fact (- k 1)))))

  The induction suggested by a term like [47m(fact n)[0m is insensitive to the
  name of the formal parameter used in the [47mdefun[0m.

  The induction suggested by a function or term is insensitive to the
  value returned by the function or term.

  It doesn't matter what the function returns in its ``base case''
  (e.g., [47m1[0m in [47mfact[0m) or what the function ``does'' to its recursive
  call (e.g., multiply by [47mk[0m in the [47mdefun[0m of [47mfact[0m).

  All that matters is (i) how the [47mif[0m structure breaks down the cases on
  [47mk[0m, (ii) which branches lead to recursion, and (iii) what arguments
  are passed to the recursive calls.  Those things determine (i) the
  case analysis of the induction scheme, (ii) which cases are Base
  Cases and which are Induction Steps, and (iii) what the induction
  hypotheses are.

  For a selection of common inductions schemes in ACL2 (e.g., on the
  structure of natural numbers, lists, and trees and on several
  variables at once, multiple base cases, multiple induction
  hypotheses, multiple induction steps, etc.)  check this link (see
  [EXAMPLE-INDUCTIONS]).

  Every legal ACL2 induction corresponds to an admissible recursive
  function and vice versa.  Similarly, every legal ACL2 induction
  corresponds to a call of a recursively defined function on distinct
  variables.

  ACL2 chooses which induction to do by looking at the terms that occur
  in the conjecture.  For many elementary theorems, ACL2 chooses the
  right induction by itself.

  You may occasionally need to tell it what induction to do.  You do
  that by showing it a term that suggests the induction you want.
  We'll explain how you communicate this to ACL2 later.  If you
  understand how recursive functions suggest inductions, then you
  know what you need to know to use ACL2.

  The main point of this discussion of induction is familiarize you
  with the basic terms: [3mBase Case[0m (of which there may be several),
  [3mInduction Step[0m (of which there may be several), [3mInduction
  Hypothesis[0m (of which there may be several in each Induction Step),
  [3mmeasure[0m and [3mwell-founded relation[0m [3mjustifying[0m an induction, and the
  induction [3msuggested[0m by a term or recursive function definition.
  Furthermore, every Induction Hypothesis is always an instance (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE]) of the conjecture
  being proved: each induction hypothesis is obtained from the
  conjecture being proved by applying a [3msubstitution[0m replacing
  variables by terms.

  If you are reviewing the material taken for granted about logic while
  working your way through the introduction to the theorem prover,
  please use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the example
  proof in [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted].")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "A brief explanation of substitution instances

  Let [3mp[0m and [3mq[0m be terms or formulas (there is no difference in ACL2).
  Then we say [3mp[0m is an [3minstance[0m or [3msubstitution instance[0m of [3mq[0m if and
  only if [3mp[0m can be obtained from [3mq[0m by uniformly replacing the
  variables of [3mq[0m by terms.  Sometimes we call [3mp[0m the [3mtarget[0m and [3mq[0m the
  [3mpattern[0m because by choosing appropriate replacements we can make
  the pattern [3mmatch[0m many different targets.

  For example, the following [3mtarget[0m is an instance of the given
  [3mpattern[0m:

    [3mtarget[0m:      (APP (APP (REV A) (REV B)) (REV C))
    [3mpattern[0m:     (APP (APP   x       y    ) (REV z))

  The replacement or [3msubstitution[0m used in this match of the pattern to
  the target is:

    [3mvariable in pattern[0m          [3mreplacement term[0m
    x                              (REV A)
    y                              (REV B)
    z                              C

  Such substitutions are usually written this way in ACL2:

    ((x  (REV A))
     (y  (REV B))
     (z  C)).

  Please use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the page that
  mentioned ``instance.''")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "A brief explanation of propositional calculus

  It is impossible in this short introduction to teach you
  propositional calculus if you don't already know it!

  A typical use of propositional calculus is to observe that

    (implies (endp z)
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  is equivalent to:

    (implies (and (endp z)
                  (true-listp z))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  If this is surprising and you know propositional calculus, then the
  problem might be our notation.  We're exploiting the tautology

    [3m(p ---> (q ---> r)) <---> ((p & q) ---> r)[0m

  where [3m--->[0m and [3m<--->[0m are meant to be the traditional arrows denoting
  logical implication and logical equivalence.

  If you don't know propositional calculus, we'll say just a few things
  to help ease your journey.

  A [3mpropositional formula[0m, in ACL2, is any formula written entirely in
  terms of variable symbols, [47mT[0m, [47mNIL[0m, and the propositional functions
  [47mAND[0m, [47mOR[0m, [47mNOT[0m, [47mIMPLIES[0m, and [47mIFF[0m.  The ``tautology'' above in
  traditional notation is this propositional formula in ACL2:

    (IFF (IMPLIES P (IMPLIES Q R))
         (IMPLIES (AND P Q) R)).

  If you have a formula like

    (implies [3mhyp[0m
             [3mconcl[0m)

  then we say that formula is an [3mimplication[0m, that [3mhyp[0m is the
  [3mhypothesis[0m, and that [3mconcl[0m is the conclusion.  If the hypothesis is
  an [47mand[0m expression, as in

    (implies (and [3mhyp1[0m
                  [3mhyp2[0m
                  ...)
             [3mconcl[0m)

  then we call [3mhyp1[0m is the [3mfirst hypothesis[0m, [3mhyp2[0m is the [3msecond
  hypothesis[0m, etc.

  If a term is of the form

    (and [3mterm1[0m [3mterm2[0m ...)

  we say it is a [3mconjunction[0m and that [3mterm1[0m is the [3mfirst conjunct[0m,
  [3mterm2[0m is the [3msecond conjunct[0m, etc.  We treat an [47mor[0m-term analogously
  but call it a [3mdisjunction[0m and its arguments are [3mdisjuncts[0m.

  A [3mtautology[0m is any propositional formula that can be proved by
  testing it under all combinations of Boolean assignments to its
  variables.  We give an example of such a [3mtruth-table proof[0m below,
  but hasten to add that ACL2 does not generally use truth tables to
  recognize tautologies.  It primarily uses [normalization] and BDDs
  to recognize tautologies, which can be seen as a mix of symbolic
  manipulation and case analysis.

  Many tautologies have names, but ACL2 doesn't refer to them by name
  because it derives them from first principles.  We list a few here
  because we sometimes use the names in our documentation; more
  importantly, you should look at these formulas and convince
  yourself that they're always true for all Boolean values of the
  variables:

    [3mDouble Negation[0m:
    (iff (not (not p)) p)

    [3mDeMorgan[0m:
    (iff (not (and p q))
         (or (not p) (not q)))

    [3mDistributivity[0m:
    (iff (and p (or q r))
         (or (and p q)
             (and p r)))

    [3mPromotion[0m:
    (iff (implies p (implies q r))
         (implies (and p q) r))

    [3mImplicative Disjunction[0m:
    (iff (implies p q)
         (or (not p) q))

    [3mContrapositive[0m:
    (iff (implies p q)
         (implies (not q) (not p)))

    [3mGeneralized Contrapositive[0m:
    (iff (implies (and p r) q)
         (implies (and p (not q)) (not r)))

  There are, of course, many others, even with these same names!  For
  example, there is a dual version of DeMorgan showing how [47mnot[0m
  distributes over [47mor[0m, a dual version of Distributivity for [47mor[0m over
  [47mand[0m, etc.

  Dealing with propositional calculus will not generally be a problem
  for you because it is decidable and ACL2 has procedures that decide
  propositional formulas.  However, propositional calculus can lead
  to exponential explosion and can thus explain why ACL2 has ``gone
  out to lunch.'' In addition, sometimes if you are curious as to [3mwhy[0m
  ACL2 is working on a certain subgoal the reason can be traced back
  to propositional calculus.

  The most common example of this is that to prove a formula of the
  form

    (implies (implies p1 q1)
             (implies p2 q2))

  propositional calculus will convert it to

    (and (implies (and p2 (not p1)) q2)
         (implies (and p2 q1) q2))

  Many users are surprised that the first conjunct above does not have
  [47mq1[0m as a hypothesis.  If you ever stare at an ACL2 goal and say to
  yourself ``A hypothesis is missing!'' the chances are that
  propositional calculus is ``to blame.'' In particular, if you are
  trying to prove that [47m(implies p1 q1)[0m implies something, you must
  deal with the case that [47m(implies p1 q1)[0m is true because [47mp1[0m is
  false.  Think about it.

  Now we illustrate the truth table method for deciding tautologies,
  even though that is not what ACL2 generally uses.  Consider the
  formula called Promotion above:

    (IFF (IMPLIES P (IMPLIES Q R))
         (IMPLIES (AND P Q) R))

  The formula above is a tautology.  It contains three variables, [47mP[0m, [47mQ[0m,
  and [47mR[0m, and so there are 8 combinations of Boolean assignments to
  them.  If we let

    [3mformula1[0m:  (IMPLIES P (IMPLIES Q R))
    [3mformula2[0m:  (IMPLIES (AND P Q) R)

  then we wish to test the formula [47m(IFF [0m[3mformula1 formula2[0m[47m)[0m:

    P   Q   R       [3mformula1[0m   [3mformula2[0m   (IFF [3mformula1[0m [3mformula2[0m)
    ---------
    T   T   T            T         T       T
    T   T   NIL          NIL       NIL     T
    T   NIL T            T         T       T
    T   NIL NIL          T         T       T
    NIL T   T            T         T       T
    NIL T   NIL          T         T       T
    NIL NIL T            T         T       T
    NIL NIL NIL          T         T       T

  So we see that the formula always returns [47mT[0m and is thus a tautology.

  Recall that in the original example at the top of this page we were
  trying to prove the formula

    (implies (endp z)
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  This formula is an instance (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE]) of

    (implies p (implies q r)).

  The substitution required by the match is

    [3msigma[0m:
    ((p    (endp z))
     (q    (true-listp z))
     (r    (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  Since we know the tautology:

    (iff (implies p (implies q r))
         (implies (and p q) r)).

  is always true no matter what Boolean values [47mp[0m, [47mq[0m, and [47mr[0m have, then
  we know this instance of it (obtained by applying the substitution
  [3msigma[0m above) is always true:

    (iff (implies (endp z)                            [3mformula1'[0m
                  (implies (true-listp z)
                           (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))
         (implies (and (endp z)                       [3mformula2'[0m
                       (true-listp z))
                  (equal (rev (rev z)) z))).

  Thus, if we're trying to prove [3mformula1'[0m it is permitted to try to
  prove [3mformula2'[0m instead, because they return the same truthvalue.

  This sketch of propositional reasoning in ACL2 is a little suspect
  because we didn't address the possibility that the substitution
  might replace the propositional variables by non-propositional
  terms.  But the tautology was verified only on Boolean values for
  those variables.  This actually works out because in ACL2 all
  propositional testing is done against [47mnil[0m and any non-[47mnil[0m value,
  including [47mt[0m, is as good as another.  However, the tautology allows
  us to replace one formula by the other only in contexts in which we
  just care about propositional truth, i.e., whether the formula is
  [47mnil[0m or not.  When we prove a formula in ACL2 we are really
  establishing that it never returns [47mnil[0m, i.e., no matter what the
  values of the variables, the value of the formula is non-[47mnil[0m.

  A very simple example of this is with Double Negation.

    (iff (not (not p)) p)

  is a tautology.  This means that if we were trying to prove

    (implies (not (not p)) ...)

  we could transform it to

    (implies p ...).

  But if we were trying to prove:

    (equal (not (not p)) p)

  we could not prove it by using Double Negation!  The formula above
  claims that [47m(not (not p))[0m and [47mp[0m have identical values.  They do
  not!  For example, [47m(not (not 3))[0m is [47mt[0m, not [47m3[0m.  However, [47m(not (not
  3))[0m and [47mt[0m are propositionally equivalent (i.e., satisfy [47miff[0m)
  because one is as good as the other in a test.  [3mThat[0m is what Double
  Negation says.

  As long as you only use propositional formulas in propositional
  places this aspect of ACL2 should not affect you.

  Now please use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the example
  proof in [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted].")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-Q1-ANSWER
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "The inductive step of the [47mrev-rev[0m proof --- Answer to Question 1

  The correct answer to Question 1 in
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted] is [3mChoice (iv)[0m.

  The Induction Step of the inductive proof of

    (implies (true-listp z)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  for an induction on the linear list [47mz[0m is:

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (implies (true-listp (cdr z))
                           (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z))))
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  The second hypothesis above is the [3minduction hypothesis[0m.  The
  conclusion above is the formula we are trying to prove.  Each
  induction hypothesis is [3malways[0m an instance (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE]) of the formula being
  proved, i.e., it is obtained from the formula being proved by
  uniformly replacing the variables in the formula with terms.
  Notice how the induction hypothesis above is the same as the
  induction conclusion, except that all the [47mz[0ms have been replaced by
  [47m(cdr z)[0m.

  If you thought the right answer was

    [3mInduction Step -- Choice (i)[0m:
    (implies (not (endp z))
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  then perhaps you didn't understand that we're doing an inductive
  proof.  Certainly if you prove the Base Case already discussed and
  you prove [3mChoice (i)[0m above, then you will have proved the goal
  conjecture, but you would have done it by simple case analysis:
  prove it when [47m(endp z)[0m and prove it when [47m(not (endp z))[0m.  While
  logically valid, you probably can't prove [3mChoice (i)[0m directly
  because you have no induction hypothesis to work with.

  If you thought the right answer was:

    [3mInduction Step -- Choice (ii)[0m:
    (implies (true-listp (cdr z))
             (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))

  then perhaps you misunderstand the difference between the [3mInduction
  Step[0m and the [3mInduction Hypothesis[0m.  The Induction [3mStep[0m is the
  ``other half'' of the main proof, balancing the Base Case.  The
  Induction [3mHypothesis[0m is just a hypothesis you get to use during the
  Induction Step.  The question Q1 asked what is the Induction Step.

  If you thought the right answer was:

    [3mInduction Step -- Choice (iii)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr x))) (cdr x))) ; [3m``induction hyp''[0m
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  then you are making the most common mistake newcomers make to
  induction.  You are giving yourself an ``induction hypothesis''
  that is not an instance of the conjecture you're proving.  This
  alleged induction hypothesis says that [47m(rev (rev (cdr x)))[0m is [47m(cdr
  x)[0m, whereas the correct induction hypothesis says those two terms
  are equal [3mif[0m [47m(true-listp (cdr x))[0m.  This alleged induction
  hypothesis is a stronger claim than we're trying to prove.  It
  turns out that by making this mistake you can ``prove'' conjectures
  that are not always true!  Remember: the induction hypothesis is
  always an instance of the conjecture you're proving, not just some
  piece of it.  Of course, ACL2 ``knows'' this and will never make
  this mistake.  But we remind you of it because there may be times
  when you intuit a different hypothesis and don't understand why
  ACL2 doesn't use it.

  If this doesn't make sense, perhaps you should read about induction
  (see [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INDUCTIVE-PROOF]) again.

  When you understand why [3mChoice (iv)[0m is the correct answer, use your
  browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted] and go to question Q2.")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-Q2-ANSWER
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "The inductive step of the [47mrev-rev[0m proof --- Answer to Question 2

  The correct answer to Question 2 in
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted] is [3mSubgoal (i)[0m plus any one of
  the other other three.  For your reference, the four choices were:

    [3mSubgoal (i)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (true-listp z))
             (true-listp (cdr z)))

    [3mSubgoal (ii)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (true-listp z)
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

    [3mSubgoal (iii)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

    [3mSubgoal (iv)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (true-listp (cdr z))
                  (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  In particular, it is wrong to think the Induction Step of the proof
  of

    (implies (true-listp z)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  can be established by proving just [3mSubgoal (ii)[0m, [3mSubgoal (iii)[0m,
  [3mSubgoal (iv)[0m, or combinations of those three.  You must also prove
  [3mSubgoal (i)[0m or something like it!

  The Inductive Step for the conjecture above is

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  ; [3mInduction Hypothesis[0m:
                  (implies (true-listp (cdr z))
                           (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z))))
             ; [3mInduction Conclusion[0m:
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  Note that the Inductive Hypothesis is an implication:

    (implies (true-listp (cdr z))
             (equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z)))

  This hypothesis can be true two different ways.  The ``normal'' way
  --- the way everybody remembers --- is that [47m(true-listp (cdr z))[0m is
  true and thus [47m(equal (rev (rev (cdr z))) (cdr z))[0m is true.  But the
  way many people forget is that [47m(true-listp (cdr z))[0m is false.  You
  must prove the Induction Step even in this ``forgettable'' case.

  In this case, the Induction Step simplifies to

    [3mInduction Step[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (not (true-listp (cdr z))))
             (implies (true-listp z)
                      (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))

  By Promotion (see the list of tautologies in our discussion of
  propositional calculus (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS])) this
  is

    [3mInduction Step'[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (not (true-listp (cdr z)))
                  (true-listp z))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  Using the Contrapositive and rearranging the order of the hypotheses
  (see propositional calculus (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS]) again),
  this is

    [3mInduction Step''[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (true-listp z)
                  (not (equal (rev (rev z)) z)))
             (true-listp (cdr z)))

  Notice that [3mSubgoal (i)[0m implies [3mInduction Step''[0m:

    [3mSubgoal (i)[0m:
    (implies (and (not (endp z))
                  (truelistp z))
             (truelistp (cdr z)))

  Every inductive proof of an implication raises a case like this.  If
  we denote the conjecture [47m(implies p q)[0m as [47mp ---> q[0m, then the
  Induction Step will look like this:

      ( c  &  (p'  --->  q'))
    --->
      (p ---> q)

  where [47mc[0m is the test that determines the inductive step, (e.g., [47m(not
  (endp z))[0m) and [47mp'[0m and [47mq'[0m are inductive instances of [47mp[0m and [47mq[0m.
  Promotion produces

      ( c  & p & (p'  --->  q'))
    --->
      q

  It is then very common to prove that [47mp[0m implies [47mp'[0m,

    [3m(i)[0m:
    (c & p) ---> p'

  and then prove that [47mq'[0m implies [47mq[0m,

    [3m(ii)[0m:
    (c & p & q') ---> q

  These correspond exactly to our choices [3mSubgoal (i)[0m and [3mSubgoal (ii)[0m.

  It is sometimes helpful to remember this diagram:

    (c  &  (p'  --->  q')
            ^         |
            |         |
            |         v
     -->   (p   --->  q )

  When you understand why [3mSubgoals (i)[0m and [3m(ii)[0m are sufficient, use
  your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted] and go to question Q3.")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-Q3-ANSWER
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
