 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-REWRITING
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "A brief explanation of rewriting from the logical perspective

  First we give two examples of rewriting.  Then we give a rather
  detailed description.  We recommend you read the description, even
  if you understand the two examples, just so that you learn our
  terminology.

  [31;1mExample 1[0m: Suppose your goal conjecture is:

    [3mGoal Conjecture[0m:
    (implies (and (endp z)
                  (true-listp z))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  Then you can use the following theorem (actually the definitional
  axiom introduced by the [47mdefun[0m of [47mendp[0m):

    [3mDefinitional Axiom[0m: endp
    (equal (endp x)
           (not (consp x))).

  to [3mrewrite[0m the [3mGoal Conjecture[0m to

    [3mRewritten Goal Conjecture[0m:
    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (true-listp z))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  Note that in this example, rewriting replaced the call of [47mendp[0m by its
  body after instantiating its body with the actuals from the call.
  This is sometimes just called [3mexpanding[0m the definition of [47mendp[0m.
  (The notions of [3mformal[0m, [3mbody[0m, [3mcall[0m, and [3mactuals[0m are discussed in
  [programming-knowledge-taken-for-granted].)

  Expanding a definition is an example of [3munconditional[0m rewriting.  All
  definitions in ACL2 are just bare equalities relating a call of the
  function on its formals to its body.  Any time you use an equality
  theorem, whether a definitional equality or something more general
  like

    (equal (append (append x y) z)
           (append x (append y z)))

  to replace an instance of one side by the corresponding instance of
  the other in a goal conjecture, we call that [3munconditional[0m
  rewriting with the equality.

  [31;1mExample 2[0m: Suppose your goal conjecture is:

    [3mGoal Conjecture[0m:
    (implies (and (subsetp a b)
                  (true-listp b)
                  (member e a))
             (< (len (rm e b)) (len b))).

  This conjecture may be read ``if [47ma[0m is a subset of the [47mtrue-listp[0m [47mb[0m
  and [47me[0m is a member of [47ma[0m, then the result of removing [47me[0m from [47mb[0m has a
  shorter length than [47mb[0m.''

  You can use the following theorem:

    [3mTheorem[0m:
    (implies (member u v)
             (equal (len (rm u v))
                    (- (len v) 1)))

  to [3mrewrite[0m the [3mGoal Conjecture[0m to

    [3mRewritten Goal Conjecture[0m:
    (implies (and (subsetp a b)
                  (true-listp b)
                  (member e a))
             (< (- (len b) 1) (len b))).

  To do this you must know that the following subgoal is provable:

    [3mSubgoal to Relieve Hyp 1[0m:
    (implies (and (subsetp a b)
                  (true-listp b)
                  (member e a))
             (member e b)).

  This is an example of [3mconditional[0m rewriting.  In order to use the
  [3mTheorem[0m we had to establish that its hypotheses are satisfied.
  That is called [3mrelieving the hypotheses[0m and was done by proving the
  [3mSubgoal to Relieve Hyp 1[0m.  Conditional rewriting is the most
  commonly used proof technique in ACL2.

  Unconditional rewriting is just a special case, where there are no
  hypotheses to relieve.

  Expanding a definition is just another special case, where there are
  no hypotheses to relieve and the pattern is easy to match because
  it is a call of a function on distinct variables.

  This page discusses [3mrewriting[0m from the logical perspective.  It is
  important that you are familiar with the notions of a [3mtarget[0m term
  being an instance (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE]) of a [3mpattern[0m term.
  We often say the pattern [3mmatches[0m the target.  These notions involve
  a corresponding [3msubstitution[0m of terms for variables.  All these
  notions are discussed in the link for ``instance (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE])'' above and we
  recommend you read it before continuing.  Then use your browser's
  [31;1mBack Button[0m to come back here.

  You should also be aware of the terms introduced in our discussion of
  propositional calculus (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-PROPOSITIONAL-CALCULUS]).

  [3mRewriting[0m is a fundamental rule of inference in our system.  The rule
  allows you to use a theorem, i.e., an axiom, lemma, or definition,
  to replace one term by another in the goal conjecture you're trying
  to prove.

  Suppose you have a theorem that is of the form (or can be put into
  the form):

    [3mTheorem[0m:
    (implies (and [3mhyp1[0m
                  ...
                  [3mhypk[0m)
             (equal [3mpattern[0m
                    [3mreplacement[0m))

  From the logical perspective we don't care how the theorem was
  actually written when it was proved.  It might have no hypotheses
  (in which case the [3mhypi[0m could just be [47mt[0m), or it could have been
  written in a different but equivalent propositional style, [47m(or (not[0m
  [3mhyp1[0m[47m) ...)[0m, or the equality could have been written the other way
  around, [47m(equal [0m[3mreplacement[0m [3mpattern[0m[47m)[0m.  Such syntactic details don't
  matter.  Just take a theorem and use propositional calculus to
  rearrange it equivalently into this form for the purposes of this
  one rewrite step.

  Suppose some target term, [3mtarget[0m that occurs in your goal conjecture
  is an instance of [3mpattern[0m.  Let the corresponding substitution be
  [3msigma[0m.  If [3msigma[0m does not contain a binding for every variable that
  occurs in [3mTheorem[0m, then extend [3msigma[0m to [3msigma'[0m by adding one
  binding for each such variable.  (This is necessary only if [3mpattern[0m
  does not contain every variable in [3mTheorem[0m.)

  Let [3mreplacement'[0m be the result of instantiating [3mreplacement[0m with
  [3msigma'[0m.  Let [3mhypi'[0m be the result of instantiating [3mhypi[0m with [3msigma'[0m.

  Then the [31;1mRewrite Rule of Inference[0m tells us it is permitted to
  replace that occurrence of [3mtarget[0m in the goal by [3mreplacement'[0m ---
  [31;1mif you can prove[0m each [3mhypi'[0m in this context.  We make this last
  condition clear in a moment.

  The justification for this is that [3mTheorem[0m is true for all values of
  the variables.  Hence, it is true for the values specified by
  [3msigma'[0m.  If the [3mhypi'[0m are true, then the target is really equal to
  [3mreplacement'[0m.  But it is always permitted to replace something by
  something it's equal to.

  Rewriting thus involves several steps:

  (1) Finding a [3mtarget[0m and a [3mtheorem[0m to use to rewrite it to some more
  desirable [3mreplacement[0m.

  (2) Instantiating [3mpattern[0m in the (rearranged) theorem to match
  [3mtarget[0m.

  (3) Extending [3msigma[0m to [3msigma'[0m to bind all the variables in [3mTheorem[0m.

  (4) Establishing that the [3msigma'[0m instances of each of the [3mhypi[0m hold.
  This is called [3mrelieving the hypotheses[0m of the theorem and is
  discussed in greater detail below.

  (5) Replacing the occurrence of [3mtarget[0m in the goal conjecture by the
  [3msigma'[0m instance of [3mreplacement[0m, provided all the hypotheses are
  relieved.

  Step (4) above, [3mrelieving the hypotheses[0m, requires first identifying
  the ``context'' of the target in the goal conjecture.  To do this,
  use propositional calculus to rearrange the goal conjecture so the
  occurrence of [3mtarget[0m is in the conclusion and let [3mcontext[0m be the
  hypothesis.

    [3mRearranged Conjecture[0m:
    (implies [3mcontext[0m
             (... [3mtarget[0m ...))

  To relieve the hypotheses you must then prove each of the following
  subgoals:

    [3mSubgoal to Relieve Hyp i[0m:
    (implies [3mcontext[0m [3mhypi'[0m).

  It is important to note that this description of rewriting with
  [3mTheorem[0m describes the process from a strictly logical perspective.
  The syntax of the theorem and the goal don't matter.  You're free
  to use propositional calculus to rearrange them to put them into
  the appropriate forms to fit the descriptions given.  Clearly, if
  you have a candidate Theorem in the ``wrong'' form and but it can
  be rearranged with propositional calculus into the ``right'' form,
  then that rearranged theorem is also a [3mTheorem[0m and can be used as
  described.  But in the actual implementation of ACL2, the syntactic
  form of a proved [3mTheorem[0m affects how it is used by rewriting.  If a
  proved theorem takes the form of an implication concluding with an
  equality, ACL2 treats the left-hand side of the equality as [3mpattern[0m
  and the right-hand side as [3mreplacement[0m, unless you tell it
  otherwise.  We'll discuss this later.

  Furthermore, being from the logical perspective this discussion of
  rewriting does not address (a) how you extend [3msigma[0m to [3msigma'[0m ---
  any extension will do provided it allows you to relieve the
  hypotheses.  The ACL2 theorem prover uses certain heuristics which
  we'll discuss later, including methods by which you can guide the
  system in the selection.

  Crucially, it does not discuss whether it is a [3mgood idea[0m to do a
  particular rewrite!  For example, the definitional equality:

    (equal (len x)
           (if (endp x)
               0
               (+ 1 (len (cdr x)))))

  may be used repeatedly, endlessly, to replace [47m(len a)[0m by an ever
  growing sequence of terms:

    (len a)
    =
    (if (endp a)
        0
        (+ 1 (len (cdr a))))
    =
    (if (endp a)
        0
        (+ 1 (if (endp (cdr a))
                 0
                 (+ 1 (len (cdr (cdr a)))))))
    = ...

  The ACL2 implementation of rewriting uses certain heuristics and you
  can guide the system in its choices.  We'll discuss this later.

  Now use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the example proof in
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted].")
 (LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-REWRITING-REPEATEDLY
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Further information on expanding definitions via rewriting

  We assume you've read about ``instances (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-INSTANCE])'' and picked up our
  basic terminology including the ideas of [3mmatching[0m a [3mpattern[0m term to
  a [3mtarget[0m term, obtaining a [3msubstitution[0m and how to [3minstantiate[0m a
  term with a substitution.  We use these notions below without
  further citation.

  In addition, we assume you've read about ``rewriting (see
  [LOGIC-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED-REWRITING])'' where we
  introduced the idea of treating a theorem (axiom, definition, or
  lemma) as a [3mconditional rewrite[0m rule and replaced one term by an
  equivalent one provided we can [3mrelieve the hypotheses[0m.

  Suppose you're trying to prove [3mformula1[0m and you transform it to
  [3mformula2[0m by rewriting.  What happened?

    [3mformula1[0m:
    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (true-listp z))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

    [3mformula2[0m:
    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (equal z nil))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z))

  Evidently we replaced [47m(true-listp z)[0m by [47m(equal z nil)[0m.  But how did
  that happen?  What really happened was the sequential application
  of several unconditional rewrites and the use of replacement of
  equals by equals.

  The definition of [47mtrue-listp[0m is:

    (defun true-listp (x)
      (if (consp x)
          (true-listp (cdr x))
          (equal x nil))).

  By rewriting once with the definition of [47mtrue-listp[0m, we transform
  [3mformula1[0m to:

    [3mformula1'[0m:
    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (if (consp z)
                      (true-listp (cdr z))
                      (equal z nil)))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z)).

  Note how the call of [47mtrue-listp[0m has been replaced by the entire body
  of [47mtrue-listp[0m.

  Next, note that the first hypothesis above is that [47m(consp z)[0m is
  false.  That is, [47m(not (consp z))[0m is the same as [47m(equal (consp z)
  nil)[0m.  Thus, replacement of equals by equals means we can transform
  [3mformula1'[0m to

    [3mformula1''[0m:
    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (if nil
                      (true-listp (cdr z))
                      (equal z nil)))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z)).

  (We will explore replacement of equals by equals later.)

  Furthermore, we know the basic axiom about [47mif[0m:

    [3mAxiom[0m if-nil:
    (if nil x y) = y.

  Rewriting with this particular axiom, using [47m(if nil x y)[0m as the
  pattern and [47my[0m as the replacement, will transform [3mformula1''[0m to

    [3mformula2[0m:
    (implies (and (not (consp z))
                  (equal z nil))
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z)).

  Often when we say we derived one formula from another by
  ``expansion'' and or by ``rewriting'' we take many rewrite steps,
  as here.  We typically use hypotheses of the formula without noting
  ``replacement of equals by equals'' as when we replaced [47m(consp z)[0m
  by [47mnil[0m, and we typically omit to mention the use of basic axioms
  like [47mif-nil[0m above.

  Now use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to the example proof in
  [logic-knowledge-taken-for-granted].")
 (LOGIC-TERM-LIST-LISTP
  (TERMP ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for lists of lists of [term]s that call only [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m-mode
  function symbols

  This predicate strengthens [47m(term-list-listp x wrld)[0m, as it also
  requires [47m(logic-fns-list-listp x wrld)[0m, i.e., that every function
  symbol called in [47mx[0m is in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode in the [world], [47mwrld[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<logic-term-list-listp>

    (defun logic-term-list-listp (x w)
      (declare (xargs :guard (plist-worldp-with-formals w)))
      (and (term-list-listp x w)
           (logic-fns-list-listp x w)))")
 (LOGIC-TERM-LISTP
