 (LOOP$
  (ACL2-BUILT-INS PROGRAMMING)
  "Iteration with an analogue of the Common Lisp [47mloop[0m macro

  [47mLoop$[0m is the ACL2 analogue of the Common Lisp's iteration primitive,
  [47mloop[0m.  This topic introduces the two classes of ACL2 [47mloop$[0m
  expressions, [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms and [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms; see [for-loop$] and
  [do-loop$] (respectively) for their full documentation.

  [47mLoop$[0m was introduced into ACL2 in Version 8.2 (May, 2019), over 20
  years after ACL2 was first released.  So there are many experienced
  ACL2 users who have never used [47mloop$[0m.  The [47mLoop$[0m Primer, see
  [loop$-primer], is textbook-style introduction, meant to be read
  linearly and may be a good starting place for you.  The primer
  follows a ``monkey-see monkey-do'' approach, showing lots of
  examples.  Sample proofs are worked out in detail and then the
  reader is challenged to apply those lessons to exercises.
  Solutions to the exercises are provided in books among the
  Community Books.  Depending on your preferred learning style and
  familiarity with [47mloop$[0m and ACL2 in general, you might want to work
  your way through the primer (see [loop$-primer]) instead of
  bouncing around the hypertext user's manual.

  The Introduction below is followed by a discussion of types and
  guards.  For a discussion of how to state effective lemmas about
  [47mloop$[0ms and how to prove inductive theorems about [47mloop$[0ms see
  [stating-and-proving-lemmas-about-loop$s].  Also, for a summary of
  how the rewriter handles [47mapply$[0m, [47mev$[0m, and [47mloop$[0m [scion]s, see
  [rewriting-calls-of-apply$-ev$-and-loop$-scions].

  But before we get started we emphasize a few key points.

    * As noted, The Loop$ Primer (see [loop$-primer]) may be a good place
      to start if you're using ACL2 [47mloop$[0ms for the first time.

    * The Table of Contents of the Loop$ Primer (see [lp-section-0]) lists
      some sensible entry points to the primer.  You'll see sections
      devoted to examples, sample proofs, exercises, etc.  Keep the
      primer in mind as an additional resource.  For example, you
      might visit [47mLoop$[0m Primer Section 6 ([lp-section-6]) for some
      exercises on writing [47mloop$[0ms and, when you get to the end,
      ignore the ``Now go to [lp-section-7],'' and use your browser's
      Back key to return to the general hypertext user's manual.

    * [31;1mMany examples[0m of [47m[loop$][0m expressions may be found in [community-book]
      [47mprojects/apply/loop-tests.lisp[0m.

    * The semantics of [47mloop$[0m involve [47mapply$[0m.  Therefore, before using
      [47mloop$[0m, it is [31;1mstrongly recommended[0m that you include the same
      book as is typically included when using [47m[apply$][0m, as follows.

          (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

    * ACL2 [47mloop$[0m statements are executed as Common Lisp [47mloop[0m statements
      after the relevant [guard] conjectures are proved.  Common Lisp
      [47mloop[0m statements generally execute much faster than equivalent
      (tail-recursive) functions.

    * [31;1mWarning:[0m [47mLoop$[0m implements only a modest part of the functionality of
      Common Lisp's [47mloop[0m.  Aside from the simple fact that [47mloop$[0m
      allows only a limited subset of the syntax of [47mloop[0m, the main
      restriction is that the subexpressions of the [47mloop$[0m expression
      that are evaluated repeatedly must be [tame]!  These
      expressions include not only the loop body but, if present, the
      [47mUNTIL[0m test, the [47mWHEN[0m test, and the [47mFINALLY[0m clause (all
      discussed below).  Thus, all the function symbols used in these
      expressions must be [badge]d (see [47m[defbadge][0m and [47m[defwarrant][0m).
      Further restrictions are enforced for [47m[defun][0m'd functions in
      which recursive calls appear in [47mloop$[0m bodies, but we say little
      about that in this documentation topic; see [loop$-recursion].
      We recommend that users unfamiliar with [47mloop$[0m acquaint
      themselves with the material below and in [for-loop$] before
      wading into [47mloop$-recursion[0m!


Introduction to [47mloop$[0m

  As noted above, there are two classes of [47mloop$[0m expressions.  These
  are identified as follows.

    * [47mFOR[0m loop$ expressions:

          (loop$ FOR ...)

    * [47mDO[0m loop$ expressions:

          (loop$ WITH ... DO ...)

  Below we introduce these two classes of [47mloop$[0m expressions.  For full
  documentation see the topic for each class: see [for-loop$] for [47mFOR[0m
  [47mloop$[0ms and [do-loop$] for [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms.  In particular various
  restrictions are discussed in those topics, but for now we mention
  just the following.  While [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m expressions are often more
  convenient to use than [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m expressions, their [47mUNTIL[0m, [47mWHEN[0m, and
  body expressions are not permitted to reference [47m[state][0m or
  [stobj]s, and they always return a single value.  [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m
  expressions do not have these restrictions, but they may not use
  the idioms of [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms, like ``[47mFOR x IN ...[0m'' or ``[47mUNTIL p[0m''.

  ACL2's [47mloop$[0m is considerably more restrictive than Common Lisp's
  [47mloop[0m, but when an ACL2 [47mloop$[0m expression is translated without error
  it has the same meaning as the corresponding Common Lisp [47mloop[0m.
  [47mLoop$[0m supports [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m expressions (discussed below) in certain
  places and these are ignored by Common Lisp.

  Next we present some [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m examples.  They illustrate the three
  supported forms of [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m iteration: the use of [47mIN[0m, to iterate
  over elements of a list; the use of [47mON[0m, to iterate over the
  non-empty tails of a list; and the use of [47mFROM .. TO[0m, to iterate
  over a range of integers (optionally with [47mBY[0m to specify the
  increment at each step).  These examples also illustrate the use of
  [47mWHEN[0m to restrict which iterations are considered and the use of
  [47mUNTIL[0m to terminate early.  Additional keywords illustrated are
  [47mOF-TYPE[0m to specify types and [47mAS[0m to specify additional iteration
  variables.  They also illustrate some of the operations permitted
  at each iteration, such as [47mSUM[0m and [47mCOLLECT[0m.

    ACL2 !>(loop$ for x in '(1 2 3) sum (* x x))
    14
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for x in '(1 2 3) collect (* x x))
    (1 4 9)
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for x on '(1 2 3) collect x)
    ((1 2 3) (2 3) (3))
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for x of-type integer from -10 to 10 by 2 collect x)
    (-10 -8 -6 -4 -2 0 2 4 6 8 10)
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for i from 1 to 10
                  as  x in '(a b c d e f g)
                  collect (cons i x))
    ((1 . A)
     (2 . B)
     (3 . C)
     (4 . D)
     (5 . E)
     (6 . F)
     (7 . G))
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for i from 1 to 10
                  as  x in '(a b c d e f g)
                  until (> i 6)
                  collect (cons i x))
    ((1 . A)
     (2 . B)
     (3 . C)
     (4 . D)
     (5 . E)
     (6 . F))
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for i from 1 to 10
                  as  x in '(a b c d e f g)
                  until (> i 6)
                  when (evenp i)
                  collect (cons i x))
    ((2 . B) (4 . D) (6 . F))

  Finally we present two [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m examples.  We explore them further in
  the documentation specific to [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m expressions; see [do-loop$].

  Our first [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m example shows iteration with the indicated initial
  values for local variables [47mx[0m and [47my[0m.  They are modified at each
  iteration through the loop until [47mx[0m is empty, at which point the
  value of [47my[0m is returned.  (See [do-loop$] for more thorough
  explanations.)

    ACL2 !>(loop$ with x = '(a b c)
                  with y = nil
                  do (cond ((consp x)
                            (progn (setq y (cons (car x) y))
                                   (setq x (cdr x))))
                           (t (return y))))
    (C B A)
    ACL2 !>

  Our second example of a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m expression illustrates more features
  than the first.  Also, it illustrates the use of a [47mloop$[0m expression
  inside a definition, which is allowed for both [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms and [47mDO[0m
  [47mloop$[0ms.  We see here that [stobj]s and [multiple-value] returns are
  allowed for [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms, where the [47m:VALUES[0m keyword specifies the
  shape of the return.  We also see here the use of the optional
  [47m:GUARD[0m keyword of a [47mloop$[0m expression (legal for both classes of
  [47mloop$[0ms, as discussed further below) and the optional [47m:MEASURE[0m
  keyword of a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m expression.

  But notice the [47m[defwarrant][0m events below!  They are required because
  the functions [47mfld[0m and [47mupdate-fld[0m are not system primitives.  They
  are introduced by the user's [47m[defstobj][0m event below.  This is in
  contrast to the [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m in our first example above where no
  warrants are required because all the functions in that example are
  primitive.

  The semantics of [47mloop$[0m involve [47mapply$[0m and thus [47mloop$[0ms inherit the
  restrictions imposed on [47mapply$[0m and its [scion]s.  Among those
  restrictions are that (a) unwarranted [47m:logic[0m mode functions cannot
  be [47mapply$[0m'd in the top-level read-eval-print loop and (b) proofs
  about the applications of unwarranted functions are impossible.
  Restriction (a) means you cannot even execute a [47m:logic[0m mode [47mloop$[0m
  containing unwarranted functions.  Restriction (b) means you can't
  verify the [guard]s of such [47mloop$[0m.  This last point obviates the
  main reason for using [47mloop$[0m: its fast execution as a Common Lisp
  loop once guards are verified.

  Now here is our second example.

    (defstobj st fld)
    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system) ; needed for defwarrant
    (defwarrant fld)
    (defwarrant update-fld)

    (defun test-loop$ (i0 max st)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (natp i0) (natp max))
                      :stobjs st))
      (loop$ with i of-type (satisfies natp) = i0
             with cnt of-type integer = 0
             do
             :measure (nfix (- max i))
             :guard (and (natp max)
                         (natp cnt)
                         (stp st))
             :values (nil st) ; shape of return; can be omitted when it's (nil)
             (if (>= i max)
                 (loop-finish)
               (progn (setq st (update-fld i st))
                      (mv-setq (cnt i)
                               (mv (+ 1 cnt) (+ 1 i)))))
             finally
             :guard (stp st)
             (return
              (mv (list 'from i0 'to max 'is cnt 'steps 'and 'fld '= (fld st))
                  st))))

    ACL2 !>(test-loop$ 3 8 st)
    ((FROM 3 TO 8 IS 5 STEPS AND FLD = 7)
     <st>)
    ACL2 !>

  Both classes of [47mloop$[0m expressions ([47mFOR[0m and [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms) rely heavily on
  the ACL2 built-in function, [47m[apply$][0m: in each iteration through the
  loop, a [lambda] object based on the body of the [47mloop$[0m is given as
  the function argument of [47mapply$[0m.  Similarly, the [47mUNTIL[0m and [47mWHEN[0m
  clauses of [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms and the [47m:measure[0m and [47mFINALLY[0m clauses of [47mDO[0m
  [47mloop$[0ms are translated into [47mlambda[0m objects.  The functions in all
  those [47mlambda[0m objects must be warranted to evaluate the [47mloop$[0m and/or
  prove its properties (including its guards) because logically the
  application of an unwarranted function is undefined.  (However,
  recall from [guarantees-of-the-top-level-loop] that [47m:program[0m mode
  functions do not need warrants, just badges, for execution in the
  evaluation theory.)

  The documentation for [47m[apply$][0m illustrates a simple [47mdefun[0m that is
  inadmissible because the measure theorem cannot be proved without a
  warrant and warrants cannot be assumed during the proofs of the
  measure conjectures.  The same issue arises when one of the
  functions critically involved in a measure conjecture is defined
  using a [47mloop$[0m whose body involves a user-defined function.  For a
  simple example of this issue and how to work around it, see
  [community-book] [47mbooks/demos/measure-and-warrant.lisp[0m.


Types and guards in [47mloop$[0m expressions

  In this section we document basic aspects of the [47mOF-TYPE[0m and [47m:GUARD[0m
  keywords in [47m[loop$][0m expressions.  See [for-loop$] and [do-loop$]
  for detailed documentation on guards for [47mFOR[0m and [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m
  expressions, respectively.

  [47mLoop$[0m expressions execute fastest when they are [guard] verified.
  But the [47mloop$[0m body raises interesting guard verification problems,
  as do the [47mUNTIL[0m and [47mWHEN[0m tests of a [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m, because they are
  executed for many different values of the iteration variables.  The
  [47mFINALLY[0m clause of a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m raises a similar concern, since the
  values of its variables may be modified repeatedly by execution of
  the [47mloop$[0m body.  It may be necessary to provide type information or
  even stronger invariants to verify guards for [47mloop$[0ms.  We now
  provide a few examples illustrating the handling of guards in
  [47mloop$[0m.

  The first example below is an acceptable [47mloop$[0m expression but cannot
  be guard verified, as would be necessary if it appeared in a
  [47m[defun][0m that was to be guard verified.  The problem is that [47m(+ 1 x)[0m
  requires [47mx[0m to be numeric and, in general, we don't know anything
  about the value of [47mx[0m here.  (Actually, because the target range is
  just a constant below, ACL2 could deduce information about each
  value [47mx[0m takes on, but it doesn't.)  The second example can be guard
  verified and has the advantage of being standard Common Lisp, so
  compilers might optimize the handing of [47m(+ 1 x)[0m.  The third example
  can also be guard verified but since the [47m:GUARD[0m directive used here
  is ignored by Common Lisp it does not inform the compiler, so this
  example might execute more slowly than the previous one.  The last
  example shows the syntax and use of the ACL2-specific addition to
  [47mloop$[0m: the [47m:GUARD[0m directive protecting, in this case, the [47mloop$[0m
  body.  [47m:GUARD[0m is useful when you wish to add more guard information
  than can be expressed with the Common Lisp [47mOF-TYPE[0m directive.  The
  [47mOF-TYPE[0m and [47m:GUARD[0m directives are conjoined to form the actual
  guard protecting the [47mloop$[0m body.

    ACL2 !>(loop$ for x in '(1 2 3) collect (+ 1 x))
    (2 3 4)
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for x of-type integer in '(1 2 3) collect (+ 1 x))
    (2 3 4)
    ACL2 !>(loop$ for x in '(1 2 3) collect :guard (integerp x) (+ 1 x))
    (2 3 4)
    ACL2 !>(let ((max 10))
            (loop$ for x of-type integer in '(1 2 3)
                   collect
                   :guard (and (integerp max) (< x max))
                   (- max x)))
    (9 8 7)

  The guard on the [47m(- max x)[0m above is [47m(and (integerp x) (integerp max)
  (< x max))[0m and the compiler is informed that [47mx[0m is an integer by the
  [47mOF-TYPE[0m.

  The examples just above are of [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms.  Here is a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m
  example that illustrates types and guards.

    ACL2 !>(loop$ with i of-type integer = 7
                  with ans = nil
                  do
                  :guard (true-listp ans)
                  (progn (setq ans (cons i ans))
                         (setq i (- i 2))
                         (if (< i 0) (loop-finish) t))
                  finally
                  :guard (true-listp ans)
                  (return (reverse ans)))
    (7 5 3 1)
    ACL2 !>

  Neither [47m:GUARD[0m is necessary in order for this execution to complete.
  However, if this [47mloop$[0m is put into a definition --- [47m(defun foo ()
  (loop$ ...))[0m --- then both [47m:GUARD[0m expressions are necessary in
  order for the definition to be [guard]-verified.

  As suggested above, when a [47mloop$[0m expression occurs in the body of a
  [guard]-verified function, it will be executed as a Common Lisp
  [47mloop[0m expression, which can be much more efficient than executing
  without such guard verification.  This efficiency can also be
  gained in top-level [47mloop$[0m expressions if the [tau-system] completes
  (silently) the necessary guard verification.  See [print-cl-cache].


