 (LOOP$-RECURSION
  (LOOP$)
  "Defining functions that recur from within [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m expressions


Examples

    (defun$ nat-treep (x)
      (declare (xargs :loop$-recursion t
                      :measure (acl2-count x)))
      (cond
       ((atom x) (natp x))
       (t (and (true-listp x)
               (eq (car x) 'NATS)
               (loop$ for e in (cdr x) always (nat-treep e))))))

    (defun$ copy-nat-tree (x)
      (declare (xargs :loop$-recursion t
                      :measure (acl2-count x)))
      (cond
       ((atom x)
        (if (natp x)
            (if (equal x 0)
                0
                (+ 1 (copy-nat-tree (- x 1))))
            x))
       (t (cons 'nats
                (loop$ for e in (cdr x) collect (copy-nat-tree e))))))

  Notice that [47mnat-treep[0m and [47mcopy-nat-tree[0m each contain a simple [47mFOR[0m
  [47m[loop$][0m (also see [for-loop$]) in which the function being defined
  is called recursively.  [47mCopy-nat-tree[0m is a little more complicated
  than [47mnat-treep[0m because [47mcopy-nat-tree[0m also contains a recursive call
  outside of any [47mloop$[0m.  Notice also that both events specify the
  [47m[xargs][0m [47m:loop$-recursion t[0m and explicitly provide a [47m:measure[0m.  The
  usual other [47mxargs[0m are optional but [47m:loop$-recursion t[0m is required
  if recursion is used inside a [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m and the measure must be
  made explicit.

  Recursion is not allowed inside a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m expression.  In fact, ACL2
  disallows the use of [47m:loop$-recursion[0m in the [47mxargs[0m of any
  definition whose body contains a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m.  So this topic is only
  about [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms.

  Some examples of [47mloop$[0m-recursive definitions may be found in the book
  [47mprojects/apply/loop-recursion-examples.lisp[0m.

  [31;1mWarning:[0m We advise you to use [47m[defun$][0m rather than [47m[defun][0m when
  introducing a [47m:logic[0m mode [47mloop$[0m-recursive function.  When a
  [47mloop$[0m-recursive function is introduced it is assigned a badge.
  This is required because we cannot translate the body of a
  [47mloop$[0m-recursive function without a badge.  However, no warrant is
  assigned by [47mdefun[0m.  But it is impossible to reason about
  applications (with [47m[apply$][0m) of unwarranted functions.  Indeed, it
  is even impossible to execute such applications in the top-level
  ACL2 read-eval-print loop.  See [guarantees-of-the-top-level-loop].
  So a [47m:logic[0m mode [47mloop$[0m-recursive function --- which is guaranteed
  to involve applications of itself with [47mapply$[0m --- can't be reasoned
  about or executed (except on data not exercising the recursive
  calls inside [47mloop$[0ms).  These inconveniences can be avoided by using
  [47mdefun$[0m when introducing [47m:logic[0m mode [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions
  because [47mdefun$[0m expands to a [47mdefun[0m followed by a [47m[defwarrant][0m.

  [31;1mWarning:[0m Even though the functions defined above are recursive, ACL2
  does not generate induction schemes for them!  If you want to do
  inductive proofs about [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions you must provide a
  suitable [47m:induction[0m hint.  In addition, to be provable by
  induction, theorems about [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions must be
  suitably general.  This topic is discussed further in
  [loop$-recursion-induction].


Restrictions

  If a function being defined exhibits recursion from within a [47m[loop$][0m
  body or within the [47mWHEN[0m or [47mUNTIL[0m clauses of a [47mloop$[0m in a [47m[defun][0m of
  [3mfn[0m, then the [47mdefun[0m must include an [47m[xargs][0m declaration with
  [47m:loop$-recursion t[0m.  In addition,

    * the [47mxargs[0m declaration must include an explicit [47m:measure[0m,

    * [3mfn[0m must not be part of a [47m[mutual-recursion][0m clique,

    * every formal of [3mfn[0m must be ``ordinary,'' e.g., not of [47m[ilk][0m [47m:FN[0m or
      [47m:EXPR[0m,

    * [3mfn[0m must return a single value,

    * [3mfn[0m's measure must be of type [47m[natp][0m or be a lexicographic combination
      of natural numbers as defined by the [47mllist[0m function in the
      Community Books at [47mbooks/ordinals/[0m,

    * [3mfn[0m must be [47m[tame][0m, which implies it may not take or return [47m[state][0m or
      [47m[stobj][0ms,

    * every quoted [47m[lambda][0m object in the body of [3mfn[0m must be well-formed
      (see [47m[well-formed-lambda-objectp][0m), which implies that every
      such [47mlambda[0m object is tame and every function symbol, including
      [3mfn[0m, used in each must have a [47m[badge][0m,

    * every quoted [47mlambda[0m object in the body of [3mfn[0m that calls [3mfn[0m
      recursively must occur as the first argument of a [47mloop$[0m scion
      and [3mnot[0m in some arbitrary scion,

    * there is at least one recursive call of [3mfn[0m inside a quoted [47mlambda[0m
      object which means that the [47mloop$-recursion t[0m declaration was
      actually necessary, and

    * the necessary measure conjectures (see below) must be proved.

  These restrictions make a little more sense if you reflect on what
  has to be done to check and admit a [47mloop$[0m-recursive function.
  First, we assume that most ACL2 functions are not [47mloop$[0m-recursive.
  So to keep [47mdefun[0m-processing as fast as possible, we require you to
  declare when you are using [47mloop$[0m recursion.  That declaration
  triggers additional checks.

  But before checks can begin, we have to translate the body of [3mfn[0m and
  since [47mloop$[0ms translate into calls of [47mloop$[0m scions on quoted [47mlambda[0ms
  and those must be tame, and since some of those [47mlambda[0ms involve
  calls of [3mfn[0m, we must assign a badge to [3mfn[0m even before we have
  translated its body.  We assign a badge that declares [3mfn[0m to take
  the appropriate number of ordinary inputs, to return one result,
  and be tame.  We check those requirements after [3mfn[0m has been
  admitted.

  Because the measure guessing heuristics of ACL2 do not look for calls
  inside of quoted [47mlambda[0ms, the measure must be explicitly declared
  even if it [47macl2-count[0m.

  To find every recursive call in a quoted [47mlambda[0m object those objects
  must all be well-formed.  If such an object occurs as the first
  argument of a [47mloop$[0m scion then we know it is only applied to
  elements of the [47mloop$[0m scion's target.  Given that, we can
  investigate whether the recursive calls in the [47mlambda[0m object are on
  smaller things.  But if a recursive call were to occur in a quoted
  [47mlambda[0m object that was passed to some other kind of scion, we have
  no way to know (without extensive analysis) to what it might be
  applied.

  Some of these restrictions could be lifted with more analysis and
  coding.  For example, we could change the specification for the
  [47m:loop$-recursion[0m [47mxargs[0m keyword so that instead of taking on a
  Boolean value it takes on a badge.  Then we could tentatively
  assign that badge to [3mfn[0m before processing and check it afterwards.
  Our current thinking is to ask users to live with these
  restrictions and let us see whether [47mloop$[0m-recursion is useful
  (almost certainly it will be), whether the community can develop
  proof techniques and tools to reason about them as effectively as
  we reason about conventional recursive functions (we're
  optimistic), and whether investing time in lifting some of these
  restrictions is worth it given all the other ways we could improve
  ACL2.


Measure Conjectures

  Measure conjectures must be generated for the recursive calls inside
  [47mloop$[0m bodies.  (We also generate conjectures for the recursive
  calls the other [47mloop$[0m-expression components, e.g., the [47mWHEN[0m clause,
  exactly analogously, but we speak of the [47mloop$[0m body only below.  We
  also focus on simple [47mloop$[0ms here but the conjectures described
  generalize to fancy [47mloop$[0ms.)  Given a recursive call inside the
  body of a [47mloop$[0m with iteration variable [3mv[0m, we first generate a new
  variable symbol, [3mv'[0m.  Certain terms, e.g., tests and arguments to
  recursive calls, will be extracted from within the body and used in
  the measure conjecture.  We rename [3mv[0m to [3mv'[0m in these terms to
  distinguish occurrences of [3mv[0m outside the [47mloop$[0m from those inside.
  The measure conjecture requires us to prove that the given measure
  decreases, as usual.  But the ruling tests are those ruling the
  [47mloop$[0m, conjoined with the hypothesis that [3mv'[0m is a member of the
  target, conjoined with the (renamed) tests from inside the body
  ruling the recursive call.

  Below is an example.  We show only the measure conjectures generated
  from inside the [47mloop$[0m because there are two recursive calls in the
  [47mloop$[0m body.

    (defun$ fn (v)
      (declare (xargs :loop$-recursion t
                      :measure (acl2-count x)))
      (cond
       ((natp v)
        ...)
       ((true-listp v)
        (loop$ for v in (target v) sum
               (if (and (integerp v) (< v 0))
                   (fn (- v))
                   (fn v))))
       (t ...)))

  Note that [47mv[0m used both as the formal of [47mfn[0m and as the iterative
  variable of the [47mloop$[0m.  In the measure conjectures below, the
  iterative variable has been renamed to [47mnv0[0m.  Two measure
  conjectures are generated from within the [47mloop$[0m:

    (implies (and (not (natp v))
                  (true-listp v)
                  (member-equal nv0 (target v))
                  (and (integerp nv0) (< nv0 0)))
             (o< (acl2-count (- nv0))
                 (acl2-count v)))

    (implies (and (not (natp v))
                  (true-listp v)
                  (member-equal nv0 (target v))
                  (not (and (integerp nv0) (< nv0 0))))
             (o< (acl2-count nv0) (acl2-count v)))

  Note that these conjectures require that when [47mfn[0m is called from
  within the [47mloop$[0m the argument is smaller than the initial value of
  the formal.  Notice also that there is no [3ma priori[0m restriction on
  the size of [47m(target v)[0m.  However, because of the way [47mfn[0m is used
  inside this particular example [47mloop$[0m, there are restrictions on the
  size of the elements of [47m(target v)[0m.  These conjectures, along with
  those generated from calls outside the [47mloop$[0m are sufficient to
  guarantee that [47mfn[0m always terminates.

  [31;1mNote:[0m Careful readers might note that the way we handle measure
  conjectures for [47mloop$[0ms differs from the way we handle guard
  conjectures for [47mloop$[0ms.  In [47m[loop$][0m we explained that [47mloop$[0ms are
  translated into calls of [47mloop$[0m [47m[scion][0ms on quoted [47m[lambda][0m objects
  and that the guard conjectures for the [47mlambda[0m are insensitive to
  the context in which the object appeared.  But the measure
  conjectures are sensitive to the context: tests from outside the
  [47mloop$[0m are present in the measure conjectures.  This is deliberate.
  Guard conjectures are context insensitive because the
  implementation caches compiled [47mlambda[0m objects without remembering
  the contexts in which they first occurred.  Thus, a [47mlambda[0m object
  generated by a [47mloop$[0m may be called on anything and we cannot
  guarantee that the input guard to the [47mlambda[0m object will be
  satisfied.  [3mBut we can guarantee that the computation carried out
  by the [47mlambda[0m[3m will terminate![0m The [47mlambda[0m object generated for [47mfn[0m
  above terminates no matter what it is called on -- even if called
  on elements not in [47m(target v)[0m -- because [47mfn[0m terminates.

  Finally, recall that [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions do not automatically
  suggest induction schemes and that special care must be taken when
  formulating inductively provable conjectures about them.  See
  [loop$-recursion-induction].


Subtopics

  [Definductor]
      Create an induction scheme for a [47mloop$[0m-recursive function")
 (LOOP$-RECURSION-INDUCTION
  (LOOP$)
  "Advice on inductive theorems about [47m[loop$][0m-recursive functions

  [31;1mWarning:[0m This documentation topic is fairly preliminary because we do
  not have a lot of experience yet with [47m[apply$][0m, [47m[loop$][0m, and
  [47m[loop$-recursion][0m.  We assume here that readers are familiar with
  the topics cited above.


Definductor

  The principles sketched here are illustrated concretely in the
  tutorial community book [47mprojects/apply/copy-nat-tree.lisp[0m.  As
  noted in that book, the inductions are hinted manually so the
  reader can see from first principles what is involved.  However, in
  the book [47mprojects/apply/top[0m we provide a utility that sometimes
  automates the creation of the inductive hint function and
  associates it with the given [47mloop$[0m-recursive function.  See
  [47m[definductor][0m.  The examples worked manually in [47mcopy-nat-tree.lisp[0m
  are recapitulated towards the end of
  [47mprojects/apply/definductor-tests.lisp[0m, without the manually
  provided hints.  In addition, the book
  [47mprojects/apply/loop-recursion-examples.lisp[0m gives some other
  examples of loop$-recursive functions and inductive theorems about
  them.


Some Basic Principles for Inductive Proofs about [47mLoop$[0m-Recursive
Functions

  If ACL2 did not have [47mloop$[0m, functions defined in terms of
  [47mloop$[0m-recursion would have to be defined with [47m[mutual-recursion][0m.
  Reconsider the example presented in [47m[loop$-recursion][0m.

    (defun$ nat-treep (x)
      (declare (xargs :loop$-recursion t
                      :measure (acl2-count x)))
      (cond
       ((atom x) (natp x))
       (t (and (true-listp x)
               (eq (car x) 'NATS)
               (loop$ for e in (cdr x) always (nat-treep e))))))

    (defun$ copy-nat-tree (x)
      (declare (xargs :loop$-recursion t
                      :measure (acl2-count x)))
      (cond
       ((atom x)
        (if (natp x)
            (if (equal x 0)
                0
                (+ 1 (copy-nat-tree (- x 1))))
            x))
       (t (cons 'nats
                (loop$ for e in (cdr x) collect (copy-nat-tree e))))))

  Without [47mloop$[0m the latter function would have to be defined this way.
  We name it [47mmr-copy-nat-tree[0m, where the ``[47mmr[0m'' reminds us this is
  part of a mutually recursive clique.

    (mutual-recursion
     (defun mr-copy-nat-tree (x)
       (cond
        ((atom x)
         (if (natp x)
             (if (equal x 0)
                 0
                 (+ 1 (mr-copy-nat-tree (- x 1))))
             x))
        (t (cons 'nats
                 (mr-copy-nat-tree-list (cdr x))))))

     (defun mr-copy-nat-tree-list (x)
       (cond
        ((endp x) nil)
        (t (cons (mr-copy-nat-tree (car x))
                 (mr-copy-nat-tree-list (cdr x)))))))

  Note that we define [47mmr-copy-nat-tree[0m by copying the definition of
  [47mcopy-nat-tree[0m (renaming recursive calls appropriately) but
  replacing the use of [47mloop$[0m in [47mcopy-nat-tree[0m with a call of a
  mutually-recursive function here called [47mmr-copy-nat-tree-list[0m.  We
  call [47mmr-copy-nat-tree-list[0m the ``list counterpart'' of
  [47mmr-copy-nat-tree[0m.  However, in general there may be more than just
  two functions in the clique and so we refer generically to
  [47mmr-copy-nat-tree-list[0m as a ``co-member'' of the clique.

  How would you prove theorems about [47mmr-copy-nat-tree[0m?  Four principles
  are known to most ACL2 users of [47mmutual-recursion[0m.  These principles
  apply to [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions as well as to mutually recursive
  ones.  We state them here in terms of both kinds of definitions.

    * While simple recursive functions suggest ``appropriate'' inductions,
      mutually recursive functions and [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions do
      not.  If you want to prove a theorem about such functions you
      have to arrange some kind of induction hint.

    * You can't generally prove an isolated theorem about a single member
      of a mutually recursive clique or a [47mloop$[0m-recursive function.
      Instead, you must state a conjecture about every member of the
      clique, conjoin all those theorems together, and prove them
      inductively all at once.  Applied to proving a theorem about
      [47mmr-copy-nat-tree[0m this advice means we must conjoin the theorem
      about that function with ``the same'' theorem about
      [47mmr-copy-nat-tree-list[0m, and more generally with theorems about
      every co-member of the clique.  Of course, what we mean by
      ``the same'' theorem about different members of the clique
      depends on what each member contributes to the overall
      computation.  Applied to the [47mloop$[0m-recursive function
      [47mcopy-nat-tree[0m this advice means we must simultaneously prove a
      theorem about every [47mloop$[0m in the function.

    * Inductively provable theorems must be sufficiently general.  When
      dealing with a mutually recursive clique, each conjunct must
      address itself to the ``general'' call of the co-member in
      question, e.g., to [47m(mr-copy-nat-tree-list x)[0m not to the
      specific call inside [47mmr-copy-nat-tree[0m, which is
      [47m(mr-copy-nat-tree-list (cdr x))[0m.  When dealing with a
      [47mloop$[0m-recursive function, each conjunct must address itself to
      the general target, not to specific target in the [47mloop$[0m
      recursive function.  In the case of the [47mloop$[0m-recursive
      function [47mcopy-nat-tree[0m we need a conjunct about [47m(loop$ for e in
      x collect (copy-nat-tree e))[0m, not about [47m(loop$ for e in (cdr x)
      collect (copy-nat-tree e))[0m.

    * Finally, the induction scheme used must provide an inductive
      hypothesis about every recursive call of every co-member of the
      clique or, in the case of [47mloop$[0m-recursion, about every
      recursive unwinding of each [47mloop$[0m.

  [31;1mRemember:[0m Because [47mloop$[0m-recursive functions call themselves
  recursively with [47mapply$[0m, any theorem about a [47mloop$[0m recursive
  function must almost certainly include a warrant hypothesis for
  that function!

  We illustrate these principles in the community book
  [47mprojects/apply/copy-nat-list.lisp[0m.")
 (LOOP-STOPPER
  (REWRITE)
  "Limit application of permutative rewrite rules

  See [rule-classes] for a discussion of the syntax of the
  [47m:loop-stopper[0m field of [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule-classes.  Here we describe
  how that field is used, and also how that field is created when the
  user does not explicitly supply it.

  For example, the built-in [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule [47mcommutativity-of-+[0m,

    (implies (and (acl2-numberp x)
                  (acl2-numberp y))
             (equal (+ x y) (+ y x))),

  creates a rewrite rule with a loop-stopper of [47m((x y binary-+))[0m.  This
  means, very roughly, that the term corresponding to [47my[0m must be
  ``smaller'' than the term corresponding to [47mx[0m in order for this rule
  to apply.  However, the presence of [47m[binary-+][0m in the list means
  that certain functions that are ``invisible'' with respect to
  [47m[binary-+][0m (by default, [47m[unary--][0m is the only such function) are
  more or less ignored when making this ``smaller'' test.  We are
  much more precise below.

  Our explanation of loop-stopping is in four parts.  First we discuss
  ACL2's notion of ``term order.'' Next, we bring in the notion of
  ``invisibility'', and use it together with term order to define
  orderings on terms that are used in the loop-stopping algorithm.
  Third, we describe that algorithm.  These topics all assume that we
  have in hand the [47m:loop-stopper[0m field of a given rewrite rule; the
  fourth and final topic describes how that field is calculated when
  it is not supplied by the user.

  ACL2 must sometimes decide which of two terms is syntactically
  simpler.  It uses a total ordering on terms, called the ``term
  order.'' Under this ordering constants such as [47m'(a b c)[0m are simpler
  than terms containing variables such as [47mx[0m and [47m(+ 1 x)[0m.  Terms
  containing variables are ordered according to how many occurrences
  of variables there are.  Thus [47mx[0m and [47m(+ 1 x)[0m are both simpler than
  [47m(cons x x)[0m and [47m(+ x y)[0m.  If variable counts do not decide the
  order, then the number of function applications are tried.  Thus
  [47m(cons x x)[0m is simpler than [47m(+ x (+ 1 y))[0m because the latter has one
  more function application.  Finally, if the number of function
  applications do not decide the order, a lexicographic ordering on
  Lisp objects is used.  See [term-order] for details.

  When the loop-stopping algorithm is controlling the use of
  permutative [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules it allows [47mterm1[0m to be moved leftward
  over [47mterm2[0m only if [47mterm1[0m is smaller, in a suitable sense.  Note:
  The sense used in loop-stopping is [31;1mnot[0m the above explained term
  order but a more complicated ordering described below.  The use of
  a total ordering stops rules like commutativity from looping
  indefinitely because it allows [47m(+ b a)[0m to be permuted to [47m(+ a b)[0m
  but not vice versa, assuming [47ma[0m is smaller than [47mb[0m in the ordering.
  Given a set of permutative rules that allows arbitrary permutations
  of the tips of a tree of function calls, this will normalize the
  tree so that the smallest argument is leftmost and the arguments
  ascend in the order toward the right.  Thus, for example, if the
  same argument appears twice in the tree, as [47mx[0m does in the
  [47m[binary-+][0m tree denoted by the term [47m(+ a x b x)[0m, then when the
  allowed permutations are done, all occurrences of the duplicated
  argument in the tree will be adjacent, e.g., the tree above will be
  normalized to [47m(+ a b x x)[0m.

  Suppose the loop-stopping algorithm used term order, as noted above,
  and consider the [47m[binary-+][0m tree denoted by [47m(+ x y (- x))[0m.  The
  arguments here are in ascending term order already.  Thus, no
  permutative rules are applied.  But because we are inside a
  [47m+-expression[0m it is very convenient if [47mx[0m and [47m(- x)[0m could be given
  virtually the same position in the ordering so that [47my[0m is not
  allowed to separate them.  This would allow such rules as [47m(+ i (-
  i) j) = j[0m to be applied.  In support of this, the ordering used in
  the control of permutative rules allows certain unary functions,
  e.g., the unary minus function above, to be ``invisible'' with
  respect to certain ``surrounding'' functions, e.g., [47m[+][0m function
  above.

  Briefly, a unary function symbol [47mfn1[0m is invisible with respect to a
  function symbol [47mfn2[0m if [47mfn2[0m belongs to the value of [47mfn1[0m in
  [47m[invisible-fns-table][0m; also see [set-invisible-fns-table], which
  explains its format and how it can be set by the user.  Roughly
  speaking, ``invisible'' function symbols are ignored for the
  purposes of the term-order test.

  Consider the example above, [47m(+ x y (- x))[0m.  The translated version of
  this term is [47m(binary-+ x (binary-+ y (unary-- x)))[0m.  The initial
  [47m[invisible-fns-table][0m makes [47m[unary--][0m invisible with respect to
  [47m[binary-+][0m.  The commutativity rule for [47m[binary-+][0m will attempt to
  swap [47my[0m and [47m(unary-- x)[0m and the loop-stopping algorithm is called to
  approve or disapprove.  If term order is used, the swap will be
  disapproved.  But term order is not used.  While the loop-stopping
  algorithm is permuting arguments inside a [47m[binary-+][0m expression,
  [47m[unary--][0m is invisible.  Thus, instead of comparing [47my[0m with [47m(unary--
  x)[0m, the loop-stopping algorithm compares [47my[0m with [47mx[0m, approving the
  swap because [47mx[0m comes before [47my[0m.

  Here is a more precise specification of the total order used for
  loop-stopping with respect to a list, [47mfns[0m, of functions that are to
  be considered invisible.  Let [47mx[0m and [47my[0m be distinct terms; we specify
  when ``[47mx[0m is smaller than [47my[0m with respect to [47mfns[0m.'' If [47mx[0m is the
  application of a unary function symbol that belongs to [47mfns[0m, replace
  [47mx[0m by its argument.  Repeat this process until the result is not the
  application of such a function; let us call the result [47mx-guts[0m.
  Similarly obtain [47my-guts[0m from [47my[0m.  Now if [47mx-guts[0m is the same term as
  [47my-guts[0m, then [47mx[0m is smaller than [47my[0m in this order iff [47mx[0m is smaller
  than [47my[0m in the standard term order.  On the other hand, if [47mx-guts[0m is
  different than [47my-guts[0m, then [47mx[0m is smaller than [47my[0m in this order iff
  [47mx-guts[0m is smaller than [47my-guts[0m in the standard term order.

  Now we may describe the loop-stopping algorithm.  Consider a rewrite
  rule with conclusion [47m(equiv lhs rhs)[0m that applies to a term [47mx[0m in a
  given context; see [rewrite].  Suppose that this rewrite rule has a
  loop-stopper field (technically, the [47m:heuristic-info[0m field) of [47m((x1
  y1 . fns-1) ... (xn yn . fns-n))[0m.  (Note that this field can be
  observed by using the command [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m with the name of the rule; see
  [pr].)  We describe when rewriting is permitted.  The simplest case
  is when the loop-stopper list is [47mnil[0m (i.e., [47mn[0m is [47m0[0m); in that case,
  rewriting is permitted.  Otherwise, for each [47mi[0m from 1 to [47mn[0m let [47mxi'[0m
  be the actual term corresponding to the variable [47mxi[0m when [47mlhs[0m is
  matched against the term to be rewritten, and similarly correspond
  [47myi'[0m with [47my[0m.  If [47mxi'[0m and [47myi'[0m are the same term for all [47mi[0m, then
  rewriting is not permitted.  Otherwise, let [47mk[0m be the least [47mi[0m such
  that [47mxi'[0m and [47myi'[0m are distinct.  Let [47mfns[0m be the list of all
  functions that are invisible with respect to every function in
  [47mfns-k[0m, if [47mfns-k[0m is non-empty; otherwise, let [47mfns[0m be [47mnil[0m.  Then
  rewriting is permitted if and only if [47myi'[0m is smaller than [47mxi'[0m with
  respect to [47mfns[0m, in the sense defined in the preceding paragraph.

  It remains only to describe how the loop-stopper field is calculated
  for a rewrite rule when this field is not supplied by the user.
  (On the other hand, to see how the user may specify the
  [47m:loop-stopper[0m, see [rule-classes].)  Suppose the conclusion of the
  rule is of the form [47m(equiv lhs rhs)[0m.  First of all, if [47mrhs[0m is not
  an instance of the left hand side by a substitution whose range is
  a list of distinct variables, then the loop-stopper field is [47mnil[0m.
  Otherwise, consider all pairs [47m(u . v)[0m from this substitution with
  the property that the first occurrence of [47mv[0m appears in front of the
  first occurrence of [47mu[0m in the print representation of [47mrhs[0m.  For each
  such [47mu[0m and [47mv[0m, form a list [47mfns[0m of all functions [47mfn[0m in [47mlhs[0m with the
  property that [47mu[0m or [47mv[0m (or both) appears as a top-level argument of a
  subterm of [47mlhs[0m with function symbol [47mfn[0m.  Then the loop-stopper for
  this rewrite rule is a list of all lists [47m(u v . fns)[0m.

