 (LP-BACKGROUND-REVIEW-1-ANSWERS
  (LP-SECTION-1)
  "Answers to background review of basic ACL2 knowledge

    (defun abc (x)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program
                      :guard (true-listp x)))
      (cond
       ((endp x) nil)
       (t (cons (cons '? (car x))
                (abc (cdr x))))))

------------------------------
  1: Informally, what happens if you type that expression at the
  top-level of the ACL2 read-eval-print loop?

  Answer: It defines [47mabc[0m as a Lisp program.  Note the [47mdeclare[0m that says
  the [47m:mode[0m is [47m:program[0m.  By the way, when Lisp and ACL2 read
  symbols, like [47mabc[0m, [47mx[0m, [47mdeclare[0m, etc., they automatically convert
  them to UPPERCASE.  So it doesn't matter if we write [47mabc[0m or [47mABC[0m, or
  [47mAbc[0m, etc.

------------------------------
  2: More formally, how does the utterance above affect the ACL2 logic?

  Answer: It doesn't.  Defining a [47m:program[0m introduces no axioms into
  the logic.

------------------------------
  3: A little bit of Lisp knowledge will tell you that all terms are
  either variables, constants (usually quoted), or calls of
  functions, where calls are of the form [47m(fn a1 ... an)[0m, where [47mfn[0m is
  a function symbol or [47mlambda[0m object that takes [47mn[0m arguments and the
  [47mai[0m are terms.  But the [47mcond[0m expression above is not of this form.
  [47mCond[0m is a macro that translates into a formal term.  What formal
  term does the [47mcond[0m expression translate to?  And if you didn't
  know, how would you find out?

  Answer: The expression

    (cond
     ((endp x) nil)
     (t (cons (cons '? (car x))
              (abc (cdr x)))))

  translates to

    (if (endp x)
        'nil
        (cons (cons '? (car x))
              (abc (cdr x))))

  To see the translation of an expression use [47m[trans][0m.

------------------------------
  4: Let [47mx[0m be the object that prints as [47m((A . 1) (B . 2) (C . 3))[0m.
  What is the value of [47m(car x)[0m or is that a nonsensical question?
  Several of the questions below are nonsensical!

  Answer: [47m(car x)[0m is [47m(A . 1)[0m if [47mx[0m is [47m((A . 1) (B . 2) (C . 3))[0m.

------------------------------
  5: Given the value of [47mx[0m in question 4, what is the value of
  [47m(true-listp x)[0m?

  Answer: [47mT[0m, because [47mx[0m is a nest of conses whose rightmost terminal is
  [47mNIL[0m.

------------------------------
  6: Given the value of [47mx[0m in question 4, what is the value of [47m(cons '?
  (car x))[0m?

  Answer: [47m(? A . 1)[0m

------------------------------
  7: Given the value of [47mx[0m in question 4, what is the value of [47m(cdr x)[0m?

  Answer: [47m((B . 2) (C . 3))[0m

------------------------------
  8: What is the value [47m(abc '((a . 1) (b . 2) (c . 3)))[0m?

  Answer: [47m((? A . 1) (? B . 2) (? C . 3))[0m

------------------------------
  9: What is the difference between [47m'((a . 1) (b . 2) (c . 3))[0m and [47m((A
  . 1) (B . 2) (C . 3))[0m?

  Answer: [47m'((a . 1) (b . 2) (c . 3))[0m is a constant term that evaluates
  to the list [47m((A . 1) (B . 2) (C . 3))[0m.  The quote mark in front an
  object forms a term that evaluates to that object.  And remember,
  case is generally irrelevant in symbols.

------------------------------
  10: What is the value of [47m(abc ((a . 1) (b . 2) (c . 3)))[0m?

  Answer: That's a nonsensical question.  [47m(abc ((a . 1) (b . 2) (c .
  3)))[0m is not a well-formed term.  For example, [47m(a . 1)[0m is not a
  function symbol or a [47mlambda[0m expression, or a macro.

------------------------------
  11: How would you explain what [47mabc[0m is, i.e., how it ``works'' or what
  it ``does?''

  Answer: The symbol [47mABC[0m is the name of a Lisp program that takes one
  argument and returns one result.  It can be run, but nothing can be
  proved about it because there are no axioms defining it.  When [47mabc[0m
  is called on [47mx[0m it copies the [47mcdr[0m-chain of [47mx[0m, adding the question
  mark symbol to each new element.

------------------------------
  12: What is the value of [47m(abc 3 4 5)[0m?

  Answer: Nonsensical. [47mABC[0m takes only one argument, not three.

------------------------------
  13: What is the value of [47m(abc '(3 . 4))[0m?

  Answer: Actually, it is [47m((? . 3))[0m, but ACL2 won't evaluate [47m(abc '(3 .
  4))[0m in the default configuration of the top-level read-eval-print
  loop because [47m(3 . 4)[0m is not a [47m[true-listp][0m.  The guard on [47mABC[0m
  stipulates that the argument to [47mABC[0m is ``supposed'' to be a
  [47mtrue-listp[0m.  So the read-eval-print loop causes a guard violation
  error.  We explain why the value of [47m(abc '(3 . 4))[0m is actually [47m((?
  . 3))[0m in the next answer.

------------------------------
  14: How do you get ACL2 to compute and print the value of [47m(abc '(3 .
  4))[0m?

  Answer: The top-level read-eval-print loop has a parameter that
  controls whether ACL2 checks the guards or not.  By default, that
  parameter is set to [47mT[0m, meaning it does check guards.  But by
  executing [47m(set-guard-checking nil)[0m the parameter is set to [47mNIL[0m and
  then guards are not checked.  With that setting, ACL2 evaluation
  just ignores the guard and runs the body of the program.

  So here is how [47m(abc '(3 . 4))[0m is evaluated.  First, it checks whether
  [47m[endp][0m holds on [47m(3 . 4)[0m.  The definition of [47mendp[0m is [47matom[0m, which
  means ``is not a [47m[cons][0m pair,'' and [47m(3 . 4)[0m is a [47mcons[0m pair.  So the
  answer to the [47mendp[0m test is [47mNIL[0m and the execution of [47m(abc '(3 . 4))[0m
  branches to the [47m(cons (cons '? (car x)) (abc (cdr x)))[0m, where [47mx[0m is
  [47m(3 . 4)[0m.

  But the [47mcar[0m and [47mcdr[0m of the pair [47m(3 . 4)[0m are [47m3[0m and [47m4[0m respectively.  So
  the value of [47m(abc '(3 . 4))[0m is [47m(cons (cons '? 3) (abc 4))[0m.

  But what is the value of [47m(abc 4)[0m?  [47m(Endp 4)[0m is [47mT[0m because [47m4[0m is an
  atom.  So the value of [47m(cons (cons '? 3) (abc 4))[0m is [47m(cons (cons '?
  3) NIL)[0m, which is printed as [47m((? . 3))[0m.

------------------------------
  15: How can you upgrade [47mabc[0m from a [47m:program[0m mode function to a [47m:logic[0m
  mode function?

  Answer: Invoke [47m[verify-termination][0m like this [47m(verify-termination
  abc)[0m.

------------------------------
  16: What is the axiom added to the ACL2 logic when [47mabc[0m is upgraded?

  Answer:

    <b>Definitional Axiom for ABC</b>
    (abc x)
    =
    (if (endp x)
        nil
        (cons (cons '? (car x))
              (abc (cdr x)))).

  Note that the guard is irrelevant in the logic.  [47m(Abc x)[0m is always
  equal to the right-hand side of the axiom above (the ``body'' of
  [47mabc[0m), regardless of what [47mx[0m is.

------------------------------
  17: Is [47m(true-listp (abc x))[0m a theorem in ACL2?

  Answer: Yes!  It may not seem so because the [47mdefun[0m of [47mabc[0m mentions
  the [47m:guard[0m of [47m(true-listp x)[0m and yet the conjecture here doesn't
  constrain [47mx[0m.  But guards of ACL2 functions are logically
  irrelevant.  The Definitional Axiom added for [47mabc[0m does not mention
  the guard.  So [47m(true-listp (abc x))[0m is a theorem no matter what [47mx[0m
  is!

  Proof of [47m(true-listp (abc x))[0m by induction on [47mx[0m:

    Base Case: (atom x)                  ; i.e., x is not a consp
    (true-listp (abc x))
    =                                    ; def abc
    (true-listp (if (endp x) nil ...))
    =                                    ; def endp and (atom x).
    (true-listp (if T nil ...))
    =                                    ; axiom about IF
    (true-listp nil)
    =                                    ; def true-listp
    T

    Induction Step: (consp x)
    Induction Hyp:  (true-listp (abc (cdr x)))

    (true-listp (abc x))
    =                                    ; def abc
    (true-listp (if (endp x) nil (cons (cons '? (car x)) (abc (cdr x)))))
    =                                    ; def endp and (consp x)
    (true-listp (if NIL nil (cons (cons '? (car x)) (abc (cdr x)))))
    =                                    ; axiom about IF
    (true-listp (cons (cons '? (car x)) (abc (cdr x))))
    =                                    ; def true-listp
    (true-listp (abc (cdr x)))
    =                                    ; Induction Hyp
    T

    Q.E.D.

  If you ask ACL2 to do the proof (see the next question) its proof
  doesn't mention induction.  That's because when [47mabc[0m was defined
  ACL2 does a little inductive reasoning to deduce the ``type'' of
  the output and deduced that it is always a [47mtrue-listp[0m.  That type
  inference essentially does the inductive proof above.

------------------------------
  18: What would you type to get ACL2 to try to prove it?

  Answer:

    (thm (true-listp (abc x)))

  or

    (defthm little-theorem-about-abc
      (true-listp (abc x)))

------------------------------
  19: How would you describe the behavior of the [47m:logic[0m function [47mdef[0m
  below?  By the way, we're naming certain functions in these reviews
  with consecutive letters of the alphabet, e.g., [47mabc[0m, [47mdef[0m, [47mghi[0m, [47mjkl[0m,
  [47mmno[0m, etc.  These names are not mnemonic or acronyms.  Don't read
  anything into the names!

    (defun def (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (append (def (cdr x)) (list (car x)))))

  Answer: It reverses [47mx[0m, i.e., it returns a list containing the
  elements of [47mx[0m in reverse order.  E.g., [47m(def '(a b c))[0m returns [47m(C B
  A)[0m.

------------------------------
  20: Define [47msq[0m to square a number.  E.g., [47m(sq 9)[0m returns [47m81[0m and [47m(sq
  -1/2)[0m returns [47m1/4[0m.

  Answer:

    (defun sq (x) (* x x))

  We could add a declaration, like [47m(declare (xargs :guard (rationalp
  x)))[0m or [47m(declare (xargs :guard (acl2-numberp x)))[0m.  But we've
  chosen to keep things simple here.

------------------------------
  21: How would you describe the behavior of [47msq*[0m below?

    (defun sq* (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (cons (sq (car x))
                (sq* (cdr x)))))

  Answer: It squares every element of [47mx[0m and returns the list of those
  results in the same order.  E.g., [47m(sq* '(1 2 3 -1/2 #c(0 1)))[0m is [47m(1
  4 9 1/4) -1[0m.

------------------------------
  22: What would you type to make ACL2 prove the following conjecture?

    (equal (sq* (def x)) (def (sq* x)))

  Answer: To get ACL2 to try to prove the theorem you could type

    (defthm sq*-def
      (equal (sq* (def x)) (def (sq* x))))

  But ACL2 would fail to find the proof unless you'd proved some lemmas
  about [47mdef[0m.  You might see a ``checkpoint'' like

    *** Key checkpoint under a top-level induction
        before generating a goal of NIL (see :DOC nil-goal): ***

    Subgoal *1/2''
    (IMPLIES (AND (CONSP X)
                  (EQUAL (DEF (SQ* (CDR X)))
                         (SQ* (DEF (CDR X)))))
             (EQUAL (APPEND (SQ* (DEF (CDR X)))
                            (LIST (* (CAR X) (CAR X))))
                    (SQ* (APPEND (DEF (CDR X)) (LIST (CAR X))))))

  What would that suggest you do?

  What we'd do is prove the following lemma first and then try [47msq*-def[0m.

    (defthm sq*-append
      (equal (sq* (append a b))
             (append (sq* a) (sq* b))))

  When that lemma is available as a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule during the proof of
  [47msq*-def[0m the checkpoint above would be further simplified, the
  induction hypothesis of the [47msq*-def[0m conjecture would be used, and
  the proof of [47msq*-def[0m would succeed.

  The experienced ACL2 user would anticipate this in a simple problem
  like this one.  But in a messier problem the experienced user would
  just be emotionally prepared for the proof attempt to fail and
  inspect the checkpoints to try to figure out how to complete the
  proof.  See [the-method].

------------------------------
  23: Prove [47m(nat-listp (ghi 0 max))[0m, where

    (defun ghi (i max)
      (declare (xargs :measure (nfix (- (+ 1 (nfix max)) (nfix i)))))
      (let ((i (nfix i))
            (max (nfix max)))
        (cond ((> i max) nil)
              (t (cons i (ghi (+ 1 i) max))))))

  Answer: You can't prove [47m(nat-listp (ghi 0 max))[0m by induction with
  that [47m0[0m in one of the controlling arguments.  You have to generalize
  first.

    (defthm nat-listp-ghi-lemma
       (nat-listp (ghi i max)))

    (defthm nat-listp-ghi
       (nat-listp (ghi 0 max)))

------------------------------
  That completes the answers to this review of basic ACL2 knowledge.
  If your answers don't largely agree with ours, you're probably not
  yet ready to read the material in the [loop$-primer].  We recommend
  that you take the ``Recursion and Induction'' course [31;1m(which should
  be somehow linked in here!)[0m.

  (Return to the Table of Contents (see [LP-SECTION-0]).)")
