 (LP-BACKGROUND-REVIEW-2-ANSWERS
  (LP-SECTION-1)
  "Answers to the review of [47m[apply$][0m and related concepts

  Below are our answers to the Review of [47m[apply$][0m and related concepts.
  If your answers basically agree with ours, we think you're ready to
  follow the discussion in [47m[loop$-primer][0m.  If not, we recommend that
  you read [introduction-to-apply$] and then [47m[apply$][0m before reading
  about [47mloop$[0m.

  But our answers are sometimes quite long and elaborate --- more like
  little lectures on the subject than just answers.  So don't expect
  to reproduce our answers.  It's up to you to decide if you know
  this stuff.  As you use [47mloop$[0m, ACL2 will print warnings and error
  messages about functions not having badges or warrants or
  expression not being tame.  We don't want your reaction to be
  ``Ack! What's that all about?'' We want it to be at least ``Oh yes,
  I remember now.'' So we recommend that you read these little
  mini-lectures in full.

------------------------------
  Consider these events, executed as the first three events in new ACL2
  sessions.

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

    (defun sq (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (natp x)))
      (* x x))

    (defun ghi (fn lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (apply$-guard fn '(nil))
                                  (true-listp lst))))
      (cond
        ((endp lst) nil)
        (t (cons (apply$ fn (list (car lst)))
                 (ghi fn (cdr lst))))))

  The three events are executed without error and [47msq[0m and [47mghi[0m are
  admitted in [47m:logic[0m mode.  By the way, we're naming certain
  functions in these reviews with consecutive letters of the
  alphabet, e.g., [47mabc[0m, [47mdef[0m, [47mghi[0m, [47mjkl[0m, [47mmno[0m, etc.  These names are not
  mnemonic or acronyms.  Don't read anything into the names!  Note
  that unlike the review of elementary ACL2 features (see
  [47m[lp-background-review-1][0m), here we define [47m(sq x)[0m with a guard of
  [47m(natp x)[0m.  Both [47msq[0m and [47mghi[0m are guard verified upon admission.

------------------------------
  1: What is the value of [47m(ghi 'sq '(1 2 3))[0m?  Hint: This is a trick
  question!

  Answer: Note that [47mghi[0m is a [scion] of [47mapply$[0m.  It calls [47mapply$[0m,
  passing in the first argument of [47mghi[0m as the ``function'' to [47mapply$[0m.
  The evaluation of the above term fails with an error message
  because [47msq[0m is a user-defined [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode function but has not
  been [warrant]ed (see [47m[defwarrant][0m).  [47m:Logic[0m mode functions must be
  warranted to be applied (with [47mapply$[0m) at the top-level of the ACL2
  read-eval-print loop.  Furthermore, if you ever try to prove a
  theorem whose proof requires expansion of the applications (with
  [47mapply$[0m) of such functions, their warrants must be provided as
  hypotheses.  We'll get to that later.

  By the way, [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions must be [47m[badge][0md (see
  [47m[defbadge][0m) to be applied at the top-level, but they need not be
  warranted (because [47m:program[0m mode functions cannot be warranted or
  used meaningfully in conjectures to be proved).  See
  [guarantees-of-the-top-level-loop] for a discussion of the badge
  and warrant restrictions enforced by top-level evaluation.

------------------------------
  2: How do you assign a badge and warrant to [47msq[0m?

  Answer: [47m(defwarrant sq)[0m.

  [47m[Defwarrant][0m assigns both a badge and a warrant to its argument,
  which must be a [47m:logic[0m mode function satisfying certain
  restrictions on how it uses its arguments and how the termination
  of the function is justified.  If the function cannot be badged or
  cannot be warranted, an error is printed.

  [47m[Defbadge][0m just assigns a badge and can work on [47m:program[0m or [47m:logic[0m
  mode functions.

------------------------------
  3: What is the name of the ``warrant function for [47msq[0m?'' What does
  [47m(warrant sq)[0m abbreviate?  What does [47m(warrant sq)[0m imply about [47mbadge[0m
  and [47mapply$[0m?

  Answer: The warrant function for [47msq[0m is named [47mapply$-warrant-sq[0m, which
  is a 0-ary predicate.  [47m(Warrant sq)[0m is just an abbreviation for a
  call of [47mapply$-warrant-sq[0m and if more than one function symbol
  appears after the symbol [47mwarrant[0m then it expands to the conjunction
  of each of their predicates.  We'll show an example later.

  [47mApply$-warrant-sq[0m is introduced as a constrained function using
  [47m[defun-sk][0m.  Here is the event in question, but the details here
  are not critical to the present discussion.

    (defun-sk apply$-warrant-sq ()
      (forall (args)
        (and
         (equal (badge-userfn 'sq) '(apply$-badge 1 1 . t))
         (equal (apply$-userfn 'sq args)
                (sq (car args)))))
      :constrain t)

  What is critical is the role that [47m(apply$-warrant-sq)[0m plays in the
  following theorem, which is proved as a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule by
  [47m(defwarrant sq)[0m.

    (defthm apply$-sq
      (implies (force (apply$-warrant-sq))
               (and (equal (badge 'sq)
                           '(apply$-badge 1 1 . t))
                    (equal (apply$ 'sq args)
                           (sq (car args))))))

  which allows the prover to reduce [47m(badge 'sq)[0m to [47m(apply$-badge 1 1 .
  t)[0m and to reduce any instance of [47m(apply$ 'sq args)[0m to [47m(sq (car
  args))[0m.

  The [47m[badge][0m of [47msq[0m tells the prover that [47msq[0m is a function of one
  argument that returns one result.  The ``[47m . t[0m'' means ``all the
  arguments are ordinary objects,'' i.e., never treated like
  ``functions.''

  The second part of the rule above will reduce the typical application
  of [47msq[0m, e.g., [47m(apply$ 'sq (list e))[0m, to [47m(sq e)[0m.

  Finally, note that name of the rule is [47mapply$-sq[0m, both the [47mbadge[0m and
  [47mapply$[0m reductions are conditional on the warrant hypothesis for [47msq[0m,
  and that the warrant hypothesis is [47m[force][0md.

------------------------------
  4: Can [47mghi[0m be warranted?  If so, what command do you type?

  Answer: Yes!  Just type [47m(defwarrant ghi)[0m.

------------------------------
  5: What is the name of the warrant function for [47mghi[0m?  Explain what
  the warrant for [47mghi[0m tells us.

  Answer: The warrant function for [47mghi[0m is named [47mapply$-warrant-ghi[0m.
  [47m(warrant ghi)[0m abbreviates [47m(apply$-warrant-ghi)[0m, and, by the way,
  [47m(warrant sq ghi ...)[0m expands to

    (and (apply$-warrant-sq)
         (apply$-warrant-ghi)
         ...).

  The rule [47mapply$-ghi[0m is proved by [47mdefwarrant[0m.

    (defthm apply$-ghi
      (and (implies (force (apply$-warrant-ghi))
    		(equal (badge 'ghi)
    		       '(apply$-badge 2 1 :fn nil)))
           (implies (and (force (apply$-warrant-ghi))
    		     (tamep-functionp (car args)))
    		(equal (apply$ 'ghi args)
    		       (ghi (car args) (car (cdr args)))))))

  Notice that [47mapply$-ghi[0m is really two [47m:rewrite[0m rules, both conditioned
  on the forced warrant for [47mghi[0m.

  The first rule tells us what the [47m[badge][0m of [47mghi[0m is.  In particular we
  see that the first argument of [47mghi[0m has ``[ilk]'' [47m:[0m[47mfn[0m, which means
  that argument is treated as a function and can eventually be
  applied with [47mapply$[0m.  Arguments of ilk [47m:fn[0m are never treated as
  ordinary objects.  The badge of [47mghi[0m also tells us the second
  argument has ilk [47mnil[0m, which means it is an ordinary object (and is
  never treated as a function).  The details are in [47m[defbadge][0m.

  Note that the second conjunct of the [47mapply$-ghi[0m rule above rewrites
  instances of [47m(apply$ 'ghi args)[0m and would rewrite [47m(apply$ 'ghi
  (list x y))[0m to [47m(ghi x y)[0m.  However, it can only be used if the
  first argument passed to [47mghi[0m, namely [47mx[0m above, is a [tame] function.
  Ilks, warrants, and tameness are concepts that are critical to the
  soundness of ACL2's handling of [47mapply$[0m.  For details see [47m[apply$][0m.

------------------------------
  6: The following definition is admissible.  Can it be warranted?

    (defun mno (x y z)
      (if (endp x)
          z
          (apply$ y
                  (list (car x)
                        (mno (cdr x) y z)))))

  Answer: Yes.  Its badge is determined to be [47m(apply$-badge 3 1 nil :fn
  nil)[0m.

  For example,

    (mno '(1 2 3) 'cons '(4 5 6))
    =
    (cons 1 (mno '(2 3) 'cons '(4 5 6)))
    =
    (cons 1 (cons 2 (mno '(3) 'cons '(4 5 6))))
    =
    (cons 1 (cons 2 (cons 3 (mno 'nil 'cons '(4 5 6)))))
    =
    (cons 1 (cons 2 (cons 3 '(4 5 6))))
    =
    '(1 2 3 4 5 6)

  In fact, [47mmno[0m is more often named ``[47mfoldr[0m'' in the functional
  programming literature.

------------------------------
  7: Is [47m(equal (mno x 'cons y) (append x y))[0m a theorem?

  Answer: Yes!  You might think we need to add the warrant hypothesis
  for [47mcons[0m since [47m(apply$ 'cons ...)[0m must be expanded to do the proof.
  However, most ACL2 primitives, like [47mcons[0m, are built into the
  definition of [47mapply$[0m and warrants are not necessary for them.  In
  fact, the primitives don't have warrant functions.  E.g., [47m(apply$
  'cons (list x y))[0m is [47m(cons x y)[0m, unconditionally.  The complete
  list of [47mapply$[0m primitives may be exhibited by evaluating

    (append '(BADGE TAMEP TAMEP-FUNCTIONP SUITABLY-TAMEP-LISTP
                    APPLY$ EV$)
            (strip-cars *badge-prim-falist*)).

  However, [3muser-defined function symbols[0m need warrants in order to be
  applied (with [47mapply$[0m).  But not all user-defined symbols can be
  warranted.

------------------------------
  8: The following [47mdefun[0m is admissible but cannot be warranted.  Why?

    (defun xyz (x) (apply$ x (list x)))

  Answer: It is impossible to classify the argument [47mx[0m.  Is it a
  function or is it an ordinary object?  It's used both ways.
  [47m(Defwarrant xyz)[0m fails.

  In order for [47mapply$[0m to be able to handle a function symbol certain
  restrictions must hold.  These restrictions guarantee that there is
  a model that makes all warrants true.  Here's a way to think about
  it.  Imagine that [47mapply$[0m is defined as a big case analysis that
  enumerates all the functions that [47mapply$[0m can handle and calls them
  appropriately.  That big definition of [47mapply$[0m has some measure that
  explains why it terminates --- a fairly messy measure given that
  [47mapply$[0m can all [47mghi[0m which calls [47mapply$[0m.  Now imagine you want to
  define a new function, e.g., [47mxyz[0m, and change the definition of that
  big [47mapply$[0m to include all the old functions plus [47mxyz[0m.  You'll need
  to weave the measure of [47mxyz[0m into the measure of the big [47mapply$[0m.
  [47mDefwarrant[0m enforces restrictions that tell us this is possible.  Of
  course, the restrictions are overly restrictive: to be perfect
  [47mdefwarrant[0m would have to solve the halting problem.

  While the failure of [47m(defwarrant xyz)[0m prevents us from being able to
  use [47mapply$[0m to apply [47mxyz[0m, it is possible to evaluate some calls of
  [47mxyz[0m and to prove theorems about them.

    (thm
      (equal (xyz '(lambda (x) (len x))) 3)

  In the proof of the theorem above, [47mxyz[0m applied the given [47mlambda[0m
  object to itself and computed that its length is 3.

------------------------------
  9: Recall [47mghi[0m as defined at the beginning of this review.  Assume [47msq[0m
  has been warranted.  What is the value of [47m(ghi 'sq '(1 2 3))[0m?

  Answer: [47m(1 4 9)[0m.

  Recall that an earlier question established that [47mghi[0m can be warranted
  and one might think warranting [47mghi[0m is necessary for [47m(ghi 'sq '(1 2
  3))[0m to be evaluated without error.  But that's untrue.  [47mGhi[0m is not
  being applied (with [47mapply$[0m) here, so whether it has been warranted
  or not is irrelevant.  However, [47msq[0m is being applied.

------------------------------
  10: Is [47m(nat-listp (ghi 'sq lst))[0m a theorem?  If not, what theorem
  does this suggest?

  Answer: No, it's not a theorem as written.  For example [47m(ghi 'sq
  '(-1/2 #c(0 1)))[0m has the value [47m(1/4 -1)[0m, although to get that value
  computed by the top-level read-eval-print loop you have to turn off
  guard checking (see [47m[set-guard-checking][0m).

  The following is a theorem.

    (implies (and (warrant sq)
                  (nat-listp lst))
             (nat-listp (ghi 'sq lst)))

  The [47mwarrant[0m hypothesis for [47msq[0m is necessary because to prove anything
  interesting about [47m(apply$ 'sq ...)[0m we need the warrant for [47msq[0m.  The
  [47mnat-listp[0m hypothesis is necessary because otherwise [47msq[0m may not
  return a natural number.

------------------------------
  11: What is the value of the term below?

    (ghi (lambda$ (e) (ghi 'sq e))
         '((1 2 3)
           (4 5 6)
           (7 8 9)))

  Answer: [47m((1 4 9) (16 25 36) (49 64 81))[0m.

  [47m[Lambda$][0m is an interesting ACL2 feature.  It translates the body
  term and constructs a [47mlambda[0m object suitable for [47mapply$[0m.

  The [47mghi[0m term above is also interesting.  It shows that the outer call
  of [47mghi[0m can successfully [47mapply$[0m the [47mlambda[0m object which itself calls
  [47mghi[0m on [47msq[0m.

------------------------------
  12: Suppose you want to define [47mjkl[0m to be something like what's shown
  below and you want the guards verified.

    (defun jkl (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard ...))
      (ghi (lambda$ (e) (ghi 'sq e)) lst)))

  Notice that the body of [47mjkl[0m is the doubly nested [47mghi[0m term just
  tested, except now instead of applying it to a constant list we're
  applying it to the argument of [47mjkl[0m.

  What is the appropriate [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m and do you have to make other
  modifications to the suggested [47mdefun[0m of [47mjkl[0m?

  Answer: First, we define a predicate that recognizes a list of
  [47mnat-listp[0ms, i.e., a list of lists, where each element of each of
  the inner lists is a natural number.

    (defun nat-list-listp (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp lst)))
      (if (endp lst)
          t
          (and (nat-listp (car lst))
               (nat-list-listp (cdr lst)))))

  We could warrant [47mnat-list-listp[0m with [47m(defwarrant nat-list-listp)[0m if
  we wanted to, but because it is only used in a guard below, a
  warrant is not necessary.  If we intend to apply it with [47mapply$[0m we
  would need a warrant and we can always warrant it when needed.

  We can then define [47mjkl[0m with the :[47mguard[0m shown below.  The [47mtrue-listp[0m
  conjunct of the guard below is necessary because [47mnat-list-listp[0m has
  [47mtrue-listp[0m as its guard.  But note that we also modified the
  [47m[lambda$][0m expression to express the guard on its local variable [47me[0m.

    (defun jkl (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp lst)
    			      (nat-list-listp lst))))
      (ghi (lambda$ (e)
                    (declare (xargs :guard (nat-listp e)))
                    (ghi 'sq e))
           lst))

  [47mJkl[0m is guard verified upon admission.  Consider what that means.  In
  particular, every time [47msq[0m is called it is called on something
  satisfying the guard of [47msq[0m.  The guard proof obligations generated
  for a nest of functions using [47mapply$[0m can be quite messy.

------------------------------
  13: Can [47mjkl[0m be warranted?

  Answer: Yes.  [47m(defwarrant jkl)[0m succeeds.  A key observation is that
  the [47mlambda$[0m expression in its definition is a [47mtame[0m function (see
  [47mtame[0m).  That is syntactically determined because the body of the
  [47mlambda$[0m is [47m(ghi 'sq e)[0m, [47mghi[0m requires a tame function as its first
  argument, and [47msq[0m is a tame function.

------------------------------
  14: Is this a theorem?  If not, what theorem does it suggest?

    (implies (nat-list-listp lst)
             (nat-list-listp (jkl lst)))

  Answer: It is not quite a theorem as written.  We need to add warrant
  hypotheses for the functions being applied with [47mapply$[0m.  Note that
  [47mjkl[0m is not being applied, so we don't need its warrant.  The outer
  [47mghi[0m in the definition of [47mjkl[0m is also not being applied.  But the
  [47m[lambda$][0m expression passed as an argument to the outer [47mghi[0m is
  being applied by the outer [47mghi[0m, and to evaluate the body of that
  [47mlambda[0m object we [47mapply$[0m [47mghi[0m, so we need the warrant for [47mghi[0m there.
  Furthermore, that inner [47mghi[0m will apply [47msq[0m.  So we need the warrant
  for that.

  Thus, the following is indeed a theorem.

    (implies (and (warrant ghi sq)
                  (nat-list-listp lst))
             (nat-list-listp (jkl lst)))

  For some heuristic advice on how to figure out the necessary
  warrants, see the section ``Determining the Necessary Warrants'' in
  see [47m[warrant][0m.

  However, if you omit the warrant hypotheses ACL2 will often
  ``remind'' you at the end of failed proof attempts by exhibiting
  checkpoints derived from forcing the warrants when [47mapply$[0m rules are
  applied.  If the warrants are omitted in this particular theorem
  checkpoints require proving both [47m(apply$-warrant-sq)[0m and
  [47m(apply$-warrant-ghi)[0m.

  Finally, one might worry that it is impossible for all the listed
  warrants to be true at once, so perhaps listing a bunch of warrants
  might make your theorems vacuously valid.  But [47mdefwarrant[0m ensures
  that there is a model of the ACL2 logic in which all warrants are
  valid.  Thus, adding warrant hypotheses in no way makes your
  theorems less meaningful.

------------------------------
