 (LP-SECTION-10
  (LOOP$-PRIMER)
  "The Evaluation of the Formal Semantics of a Fancy [47mLoop$[0m


LP10: The Evaluation of the Formal Semantics of a Fancy [47mLoop$[0m

  Below we show the evaluation of a fancy [47mloop$[0m that uses the function
  [47mpackn[0m to create a list of symbols.  We start with an example of
  [47mpackn[0m so you can infer its behavior.  But our real interest is the
  behavior of the [47mloop$[0m.

    ACL2 !>(packn (list 'R2 '- 'D 2))
    R2-D2
    ACL2 !>(let ((mark '*))
             (loop$ for x in '(a * b c d e * f g h i j * k)
                    as  y in '(u v * w * u v * x y z)
                    as  i from 0 to 100 by 3
                    when (and (evenp i)
                              (not (eq x mark))
                              (not (eq y mark)))
                    collect (packn (list x y '_ i))))
    (AU_0 GX_24 IZ_30)
    ACL2 !>

  We now explain carefully the evaluation of the [47mloop$[0m expression above
  in an environment in which [47mmark[0m is bound to the asterisk symbol, [47m*[0m.

  The first three lines of the [47mloop$[0m introduce the so-called [3miteration
  variables[0m, [47mx[0m, [47my[0m, and [47mi[0m, and their respective [3mranges[0m.

  The [47mwhen[0m clause specifies a predicate on the iteration variables,
  identifying the ``good'' cases, i.e., the values of the iteration
  variables that we care about.  However, note that the predicate
  above mentions the variable [47mmark[0m, which is not one of the iteration
  variables.  We call such variables [3mglobals[0m because their values are
  not set within the scope of the [47mloop[0m.

  The [47mcollect[0m clause specifies an expression to be evaluated on the
  ``good'' iteration variable values and collects the results of
  those evaluations.

  If you replace the symbol [47mloop$[0m above by [47mloop[0m you get a legal Common
  Lisp [47mloop[0m expression with the same value (if executed when [47mmark[0m is
  bound to [47m*[0m).

  For our purposes, the easiest way to think about this particular
  statement is to decompose it into three steps.

  Step 1: The lines introducing the iteration variables and their
  ranges cause us to make a table of corresponding values of [47mx[0m, [47my[0m,
  and [47mi[0m, as shown below in the form of a list of triples.  This list
  is as long as the shortest individual range.

    ; x y  i
    ((A U  0)
     (* V  3)
     (B *  6)
     (C W  9)
     (D * 12)
     (E U 15)
     (* V 18)
     (F * 21)
     (G X 24)
     (H Y 27)
     (I Z 30)).

  Step 2: the [47mwhen[0m clause maps over the above table and identifies the
  entries as ``good'' or ``bad'', where the good ones have an even
  value for [47mi[0m and values for [47mx[0m and [47my[0m that are not the asterisk mark,
  [47m*[0m.  We annotate the table accordingly below.

    ; x y  i
    ((A U  0)        ; GOOD!
     (* V  3)        ; bad: odd i (and asterisk)
     (B *  6)        ; bad: asterisk
     (C W  9)        ; bad: odd i
     (D * 12)        ; bad: asterisk
     (E U 15)        ; bad: odd i
     (* V 18)        ; bad: asterisk
     (F * 21)        ; bad: odd i (and asterisk)
     (G X 24)        ; GOOD!
     (H Y 27)        ; bad: odd i
     (I Z 30)).      ; GOOD!

  Effectively the [47mwhen[0m clause pares down the table to just the good
  entries.

    ; x y  i
    ((A U  0)        ; GOOD!
     (G X 24)        ; GOOD!
     (I Z 30)).      ; GOOD!

  Step 3: the [47mcollect[0m clause maps over pared-down table, evaluating and
  collecting the results of [47m(packn (list x y '_ i))[0m for each triple
  in the table.

    (AU_0
     GX_24
     IZ_30).

  The ACL2 semantics of the [47mloop$[0m statement reflects this understanding
  of the statement.  (The display below is the output of [47m:tca[0m on the
  [47mloop$[0m, but with the [47mDECLARE[0m forms deleted and the three comments
  added.)

      (collect$+                                        ; step 3
       (lambda$ (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
                (let ((x (car loop$-ivars))
                      (y (cadr loop$-ivars))
                      (i (caddr loop$-ivars)))
                  (packn (list x y '_ i))))
       nil
       (when$+                                          ; step 2
        (lambda$ (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
                 (let ((mark (car loop$-gvars))
                       (x (car loop$-ivars))
                       (y (cadr loop$-ivars))
                       (i (caddr loop$-ivars)))
                   (and (evenp i)
                        (not (eq x mark))
    		    (not (eq y mark)))))
        (list mark)
        (loop$-as (list '(a * b c d e * f g h i j * k)  ; step 1
                        '(u v * w * u v * x y z)
                        (from-to-by 0 100 3)))))

  In step 1, the [47mloop$-as[0m function takes a list of the individual
  ranges and builds the first version of the iteration variable
  table.  The function [47mfrom-to-by[0m just enumerates the integers from
  its first argument to its second argument in steps of size given by
  its third argument.

  In step 2, the [47mwhen$+[0m function takes three arguments.  Note that the
  last argument of the [47mwhen$+[0m function is the iteration variable
  table computed by step 1.  The first argument of the [47mwhen$+[0m
  function is a [47mlambda$[0m expression representing the predicate used to
  identify the ``good'' entries.  But that predicate may also require
  the value of any global variables used.  So the [47mlambda$[0m expression
  has two arguments, [47mloop$-gvars[0m, the list of values of the global
  variables, and [47mloop$-ivars[0m, a tuple of values corresponding to a
  line in the iteration variable table.  The middle argument of the
  [47mwhen$+[0m provides the values of the global variables and, as noted
  earlier, the last argument is the iteration variable table.  The
  [47mwhen$+[0m maps over the iteration variable table and collects the
  ``good'' lines, returning them as a list.

  In step 3, the [47mcollect$+[0m takes three arguments.  The first is a
  [47mlambda$[0m expression on the [47mloop$-gvars[0m and [47mloop$-ivars[0m as above.
  The second and third arguments are the global variable values and
  the ``good'' lines from the pared-down iteration variable table as
  computed by step 2.  Note that since the [47mcollect[0m clause does not
  mention any global variables the global variable tuple in the
  second argument of the [47mcollect$+[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  Of course, Common Lisp does not decompose the corresponding [47mloop[0m
  statement this way but generates much more efficient compiled code.

  The ACL2 semantics is rendered compositionally to make it easier to
  reason about [47mloop$[0m statements.  If the guards of the [47mloop$[0m
  statement --- which we haven't discussed yet --- are verified then
  the ACL2 loop$ statement is rendered into the corresponding Common
  Lisp [47mloop[0m statement, compiled, and efficiently executed.

  Now go to [lp-section-11] (or return to the Table of Contents (see
  [LP-SECTION-0])).")
 (LP-SECTION-11
  (LOOP$-PRIMER)
  "Proving Theorems about [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0ms


LP11: Proving Theorems about [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0ms

  Proving theorems about [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m in ACL2 is essentially no different
  than proving theorems about other constructs in ACL2.  Indeed,
  since [47mloop$[0ms turn into calls of [scion]s of [47m[apply$][0m, proving
  things about them is exactly the problem of proving things about
  scions.  However, [47mloop$[0ms raise some problems that are not fully
  technical.

    * Iteration is so common in programming --- and the meaning of [47mloop$[0m
      statements are intuitively obvious --- that you often don't
      think anything logically complicated is going on!  But when
      proofs are desired you must keep induction in mind and you must
      train yourself to see the need for generalization in order to
      allow the necessary inductions.

    * You're not syntactically aware of the presence of [47mapply$[0m and the
      concomitant need for [tame]ness and [warrant]s.

    * You don't see the translations of [47mloop$[0ms when you type them but they
      appear ``unexpectedly'' in the checkpoints of failing proofs
      and that can throw you for a loop (so to speak).

    * The translations of [47mloop$[0ms, especially of fancy [47mloop$[0m, are pretty
      complicated in and of themselves and can be difficult to
      understand, much less generalize and manipulate formally.

    * The ACL2 user rarely has to think about local variable bindings, but
      those issues arise quite often in dealing with loop$s (and
      nested [47mlambda[0m objects in general).

  With these points in mind we will now work through a proof about a
  fancy [47mloop$[0m.  In the next section we'll challenge you to do some
  proofs about other [47mloop$[0ms.  The proof description below is rather
  long because we cast it as a narrative of the proof discovery
  process and the actual chronology of events submitted, including
  failed events and our thoughts about the failures.

  Sample Question: Define the function [47mall-pairs[0m recursively so that
  [47m(all-pairs imax jmax)[0m creates a list of all pairs [47m(i . j)[0m where [47m1
  <= i <= imax[0m and [47m1 <= j <= jmax[0m.  Then define the [47mloop$[0m version of
  [47mall-pairs[0m and prove it is equivalent to [47mall-pairs[0m.  Both versions
  must be guard verified.

  Sample Solution (as a narrative of the discovery).

  As usual, we make sure we're operating in a session where

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

  We will construct our pairs with

    (defun make-pair (i j)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (cons i j))

  Here is our recursive definition.  It involves two helper functions.
  [47mAll-pairs-helper2[0m keeps [47mi[0m fixed and runs [47mj[0m from [47m1[0m to [47mjmax[0m to
  compute the list of pairs [47m((i . 1) (i . 2) ... (i . jmax))[0m.
  [47mAll-pairs-helper1[0m just calls the former helper for each [47mi[0m from [47m1[0m to
  [47mimax[0m and appends the lists together.  The top-level [47mall-pairs[0m calls
  [47mall-pairs-helper1[0m with [47mi[0m properly initialized to [47m1[0m.

    (defun all-pairs-helper2 (i j jmax)
      (declare (xargs :measure (nfix (- (+ (nfix jmax) 1) (nfix j)))
                      :guard (and (natp i) (natp j) (natp jmax))))
      (let ((j (nfix j))
            (jmax (nfix jmax)))
        (cond
         ((> j jmax) nil)
         (t (cons (make-pair i j)
                  (all-pairs-helper2 i (+ 1 j) jmax))))))

    (defun all-pairs-helper1 (i imax jmax)
      (declare (xargs :measure (nfix (- (+ (nfix imax) 1) (nfix i)))
                      :guard (and (natp i) (natp imax) (natp jmax))))
      (let ((i (nfix i))
            (imax (nfix imax)))
        (cond
         ((> i imax) nil)
         (t (append (all-pairs-helper2 i 1 jmax)
                    (all-pairs-helper1 (+ 1 i) imax jmax))))))

    (defun all-pairs (imax jmax)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (natp imax) (natp jmax))))
      (all-pairs-helper1 1 imax jmax))

  Here is a simple test.

    ACL2 !>(all-pairs 2 4)
    ((1 . 1)
     (1 . 2)
     (1 . 3)
     (1 . 4)
     (2 . 1)
     (2 . 2)
     (2 . 3)
     (2 . 4))

  And here is our first attempt to define the [47mloop$[0m version, which is
  considerably ``simpler'' (or, at least, shorter).  But this will
  fail!  Can you say why without reading the error message?

    (defun all-pairs-loop$ (imax jmax)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (natp imax) (natp jmax))))
      (loop$ for i from 1 to imax
             append
             (loop$ for j from 1 to jmax
                    collect (make-pair i j))))

    ACL2 Error [Translate] in ( DEFUN ALL-PAIRS-LOOP$ ...):  The body of
    a LAMBDA object, lambda$ term, or loop$ statement should be fully badged
    but MAKE-PAIR is used in
    ((LAMBDA (I J) (MAKE-PAIR I J)) (CAR LOOP$-GVARS) (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
    and has no badge. ...
    ... Note:  this error occurred in the context
    (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
             (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                         (EQUAL # 1)
                                         (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                        (EQUAL # 1))))
             (LET ((I (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                   (J (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
                  (DECLARE (IGNORABLE I J))
                  (MAKE-PAIR I J))).

  The error message above includes some extraneous possible explanation
  of the error which we've omitted.

  The error tells us we forgot to assign a badge to [47mmake-pair[0m.  We
  could call [47m(defbadge make-pair)[0m at this point.  But since we intend
  to prove things about [47mall-pairs-loop$[0m we'll actually need the
  warrant for the [47mmake-pair[0m, since [47mmake-pair[0m is user-defined and is
  used in the body of a [47mloop$[0m.  So we'll go ahead and create the
  warrant.

    (defwarrant make-pair)

  (Had we just badged [47mmake-pair[0m at this point and proceeded another
  error message would crop up in due course and tell us to warrant
  it.)

  Trying the [47mdefun[0m again causes another failure with the checkpoint
  shown below.

    (defun all-pairs-loop$ (imax jmax)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (natp imax) (natp jmax))))
      (loop$ for i from 1 to imax
             append
             (loop$ for j from 1 to jmax
                    collect (make-pair i j))))

    *** Key checkpoint at the top level: ***

    Subgoal 1.2'
    (IMPLIES (AND (CONSP LOOP$-IVARS)
                  (NOT (CDR LOOP$-IVARS))
                  (CONSP LOOP$-GVARS)
                  (NOT (CDR LOOP$-GVARS)))
             (INTEGERP (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)))

    ACL2 Error [Failure] in ( DEFUN ALL-PAIRS-LOOP$ ...):  The proof of
    the guard conjecture for ALL-PAIRS-LOOP$ has failed; see the discussion
    above about :VERIFY-GUARDS and :GUARD-DEBUG.  See :DOC failure.

  We need to prove, as part of the guard verification, that [47m(CAR
  LOOP$-GVARS)[0m is an integer.  At this point we might want to refresh
  our idea of what the formal semantics of that nested loop$ is!

  We do not recommend using [47m:trans[0m for that refreshment!  You can try
  it and you'll see 67 lines of output with a lot of extra stuff in
  it used to help evaluate such forms efficiently.  Instead we'll use
  [47m:tca[0m (``translate, clean, and annotate'') which produces about half
  as much output and shows the original form of each [47mloop$[0m together
  with formal semantics, complete with [47m:guard[0m declarations on the
  [47mLAMBDA[0m objects and correspondences between the variable names used
  in the original statement with the components of the formals
  [47mLOOP$-GVARS[0m and [47mLOOP$-IVARS[0m in the [47mlambda$[0m terms generated.

  (Note: We have manually inserted some comments to identify certain
  lines of the display below.)

    ACL2 !>:tca (loop$ for i from 1 to imax
                       append
                       (loop$ for j from 1 to jmax
                              collect (make-pair i j)))
    (PROG2$
      '(LOOP$ FOR I FROM 1 TO IMAX
              APPEND
              (LOOP$ FOR J FROM 1 TO JMAX
                     COLLECT (MAKE-PAIR I J)))
      (APPEND$+                                                      ; [0]
       (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)                            ; [1]
         (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)        ; [2]
                                     (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 1)
                                     (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                     (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 1))
                         :SPLIT-TYPES T))
         (LET ((JMAX (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))                              ; [3]
               (I (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
           (PROG2$
            '(LOOP$ FOR J FROM 1 TO JMAX COLLECT (MAKE-PAIR I J))
            (COLLECT$+
             (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
               (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                           (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 1)
                                           (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                           (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 1))
                               :SPLIT-TYPES T))
               (LET ((I (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                     (J (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
                 (MAKE-PAIR I J)))
             (LIST I)
             (LOOP$-AS (LIST (FROM-TO-BY 1 JMAX 1)))))))             ; [4]
       (LIST JMAX)                                                   ; [5]
       (LOOP$-AS (LIST (FROM-TO-BY 1 IMAX 1)))))                     ; [6]

  We're trying to verify the guards all the functions in that
  expression, and we have the [47m:guard[0m in the [47mdefun[0m of [47mall-pairs-loop$[0m
  to work with.  That [47m:guard[0m is [47m(and (natp imax) (natp jmax))[0m.

  So what functions above require an argument to be an integer?  Hint:
  There is only one such function but it occurs in two places.

  [47mFrom-to-by[0m requires all three of its arguments to be integers and
  that function appears on lines [4] and [6].  Line [4] requires [47mjmax[0m
  to be an integer and line [6] requires [47mimax[0m to be an integer.  But
  line [6] is the formal term representing the ``[47mfrom 1 to imax[0m''
  range of the [47mappend[0m [47mloop$[0m formalized with the fancy scion [47mAPPEND$+[0m
  at line [0].  That [47mimax[0m is literally the first formal variable of
  the defun of [47mall-pairs-loop$[0m whose [47m:guard[0m says [47mimax[0m and [47mjmax[0m are a
  naturals.  So [6] is not the problem.

  The [47mjmax[0m in line [4], on the other hand, is actually a [47mlet[0m-bound
  variable (see line [3]) within the [47mlambda[0m object (line [1]) that is
  the body of the [47mappend[0m loop$ [line 0].  [47mjmax[0m is bound inside that
  [47mlambda[0m on line [3] to [47m(car loop$-gvars)[0m and the [47m:guard[0m (line [2])
  of that [47mlambda[0m does not require [47m(car loop$-gvars)[0m to be an integer.
  We need to add that requirement to [2].

  So how do we change the [47m:guard[0m of a [47mlambda[0m generated by the
  translation of the [47mappend[0m [47mloop$[0m?  We add ``[47m:guard (natp jmax)[0m''
  after the [47mappend[0m operator.  (Note that [47m(integerp jmax)[0m would
  suffice here but we added the stronger condition ensured by the
