  "Examples of [47mDO[0m [47mLoop$[0ms


LP13: Examples of [47mDO[0m [47mLoop$[0ms

  Below is a log of an ACL2 session demonstrating the behavior of a few
  [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms.  As usual with [47mloop$[0ms, one should always work in a
  session in which the book shown below is included.  The examples
  below do not illustrate guards or proofs about [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms.

  We'll describe the syntax and semantics of [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms later.  But we
  expect you can intuit the syntax and semantics of these [47mloop$[0m
  statements from these examples.  The following may help if you're
  unfamiliar with the following Common Lisp primitives.

  In ACL2, [31;1mThese primitives may only be used within [47mDO[0m[31;1m [47mloop$[0m[31;1ms![0m

    * [47m(RETURN [0m[3mexpr[0m[47m)[0m terminates the [47mloop$[0m and returns the value of [3mexpr[0m as
      the value.

    * [47m(SETQ [0m[3mvar expr[0m[47m)[0m evaluates [3mexpr[0m and assigns the value to the variable
      [3mvar[0m.  Parallel assignment with [47mmv-setq[0m is also supported but
      not illustrated here.

    * [47m(PROGN [0m[3mstmt_1 ... stmt_n[0m[47m)[0m evaluates each [3mstmt_i[0m in turn and returns
      the value of the last one.

    * [47m(LOOP-FINISH)[0m terminates the iteration in the [47mloop$[0m and passes
      control to the [47mFINALLY[0m clause, if any.

  We start the session with the following commands, whose output we do
  not display here.  Note that we use [47m[defstobj][0m to introduce a
  single-threaded object, [47mst[0m, with one field, [47mfld1[0m.  We initialize
  [47m(fld1 st)[0m to 0. In addition, we warrant two of the functions
  introduced by the [47m[defstobj][0m.  We warrant those functions because
  they are user-defined [47m:logic[0m mode functions that we'll use in a [47mDO[0m
  [47mloop$[0m.  [47mDefstobj[0m does not automatically badge or warrant the
  functions it defines but they are warrantable.

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)
    (defstobj st fld1)
    (update-fld1 0 st)
    (defwarrant fld1)
    (defwarrant update-fld1)

  So having set up our session, we now experiment with [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms.

    ; Reverse the elements of the initial value of temp.

    ACL2 !>(loop$ with temp = '(a b c)
                  with  ans = nil
                  do
                  (cond ((endp temp) (return ans))
                        (t (progn (setq ans (cons (car temp) ans))
                                  (setq temp (cdr temp))))))
    (C B A)

    ; Reverse the elements of lst down to the first xxx, or return
    ; not-found if there is no xxx in lst.

    ACL2 !>(defun reverse-to-xxx (lst)
             (loop$ with temp = lst
                    with  ans = nil
                    do
                    (cond ((endp temp) (return 'not-found))
                          (t (progn (cond ((eq (car temp) 'xxx) (loop-finish))
                                          (t (setq ans (cons (car temp) ans))))
                                    (setq temp (cdr temp)))))
                    finally
                    (return ans)))

    Since REVERSE-TO-XXX is non-recursive, its admission is trivial.  We
    could deduce no constraints on the type of REVERSE-TO-XXX.

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN REVERSE-TO-XXX ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     REVERSE-TO-XXX

    ACL2 !>(reverse-to-xxx '(a b c xxx d e f))
    (C B A)

    ACL2 !>(reverse-to-xxx '(a b c d e f))
    NOT-FOUND

    ; In the next example we will reverse the elements of the initial value of
    ; temp, except for the xxx's which we just drop but count.  We use (fld1 st)
    ; to store the accumulated count.  This loop$ returns the reversed elements
    ; and the final value of st.  But first we'll show that (fld1 st) is
    ; initially 0.

    ACL2 !>(fld1 st)
    0

    ACL2 !>(loop$ with temp = '(a b c xxx d e xxx f g)
                  with ans = nil
                  do
                  :values (nil st)
                  (cond ((endp temp) (return (mv ans st)))
                        (t (progn
                             (cond ((eq (car temp) 'xxx)
                                    (setq st (update-fld1 (+ 1 (fld1 st)) st)))
                                   (t (setq ans (cons (car temp) ans))))
                             (setq temp (cdr temp))))))
    ((G F E D C B A) <st>)

    ACL2 !>(fld1 st)
    2

  We included the last example showing that stobjs can be used inside
  of [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms just to alert you to that feature.  However, in the
  rest of this primer we do not deal with stobjs in [47mloop$[0m because
  there is enough to cover without that!

  See the ACL2 documentation topic [DO-loop$] for a more thorough
  discussion of [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms.

  Now go to [lp-section-14] (or return to the Table of Contents (see
  [LP-SECTION-0])).")
