  (LOOP$-PRIMER)
  "Informal Syntax and Semantics of [47mDO[0m [47mLoop$[0ms


LP15: Informal Syntax and Semantics of [47mDO[0m [47mLoop$[0ms

  The ``most elaborate'' [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m looks like this.

    (LOOP$ WITH var1 OF-TYPE spec1 = init1 ; a WITH declaration
           WITH var2 OF-TYPE spec2 = init2
           ...
           DO
           :measure m
           :guard do-guard
           :values v
           do-body
           FINALLY
           :guard fin-guard
           fin-body)

  where much of that is optional: ``[47mOF-TYPE speci[0m'', ``[47m= initi[0m'' (when
  ``[47mOF-TYPE speci[0m'' is present), ``[47m:MEASURE m[0m'', the two ``[47m:GUARD[0m
  ...'' clauses, ``[47m:VALUES v[0m'', and ``[47mFINALLY fin-body[0m''.  If the
  [47m:MEASURE[0m is omitted, ACL2 tries to guess a likely measure using the
  same heuristic it does with recursive [47m[defun][0ms.  If [47m:VALUES[0m is
  omitted then [47mv[0m defaults to [47m(nil)[0m.

  All ACL2 function symbols in [47mm[0m, [47mdo-body[0m, and [47mfin-body[0m must be
  [badge]d so [47m[apply$][0m can handle them.  Furthermore, they must be
  [warrant]ed if proofs are to be done about them or if they are in
  [logic] mode and are called during evaluation.

  As you've already inferred from our examples, [47mdo-body[0m and [47mfin-body[0m
  are not normal ACL2 terms!  They allow restricted uses of [47mRETURN[0m,
  [47mPROGN[0m, [47mSETQ[0m, [47mMV-SETQ[0m, and [47mLOOP-FINISH[0m.

  As for semantics, every legal [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m translates into a term of the
  form

    (DO$ m-lambda
         alist
         do-body-lambda
         fin-body-lambda
         a5
         a6)

  where [47mm-lambda[0m, [47mdo-body-lambda[0m, and [47mfin-body-lambda[0m are quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m
  objects derived from the respective terms in the [47mloop$[0m statement.
  [47mAlist[0m is an association list that maps the variables of those terms
  to their initial values.  We discuss the other three arguments
  later.

  All three of these [47mLAMBDA[0m objects operate on [47malist[0m.  The [47mm-lambda[0m may
  return a natural, and otherwise must return a list of naturals
  which is treated as a lexicographic tuple whose first component is
  the most significant.  The other two [47mLAMBDA[0m objects return a triple
  of the form [47m(exit-token value new-alist)[0m.  The [47mexit-token[0m is
  [47m:RETURN[0m, [47m:LOOP-FINISH[0m, or [47mNIL[0m, and indicates what happens next: the
  [47mvalue[0m is to be returned as the value of the [47mloop$[0m, the
  [47mfin-body-lambda[0m is to be applied to the [47mnew-alist[0m, or the [47mloop$[0m is
  to iterate again on [47mnew-alist[0m.  But before the iteration, the
  [47mm-lambda[0m is applied to the [47mnew-alist[0m and must be of smaller measure
  according to [47m[l<][0m for iteration to continue.

  If the given measure fails to decrease, then, logically speaking, [47ma5[0m
  is used to compute a default answer.  (That argument is actually
  the output signature of the [47mloop$[0m specifying how many values are to
  be returned and whether each value is an ordinary object, a
  double-float, or a [stobj].)  However, in execution, an error is
  signaled if the measure fails to decrease.  Such runtime errors
  (including [47mOF-TYPE[0m and guard violations if guards are being
  checked) are reported using [47ma6[0m which is just a quoted constant
  about the original [47mloop$[0m statement.  (In fact, [47ma6[0m is logically
  irrelevant and the theorem prover replaces quoted non-[47mnil[0m final
  argument to [47mdo$[0m by [47mnil[0m in proofs as part of the cleaning-up
  process.)

  Consider this simple [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m and its cleaned-up semantics as shown
  by the [47m:[0m[47m[tcp][0m command.  (We have re-pretty-printed it to add
  comments and highlight some symmetries.)

      ACL2 !>:tcp (loop$ with i = n
                         with ans = 0
                         do
                         (if (zp i)
                             (return ans)
                             (progn (setq ans (+ 1 ans))
                                    (setq i (- i 1)))))
       (DO$
    ;    measure lambda:
         (LAMBDA$ (ALIST)
           (ACL2-COUNT (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST))))

    ;    initial alist:
         (LIST (CONS 'I N)
               (CONS 'ANS 0))

    ;    do-body lambda:
         (LAMBDA$ (ALIST)
           (IF (ZP (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
               (LIST :RETURN
                     (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))
                     (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                           (CONS 'ANS (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST)))))
               (LIST NIL
                     NIL
                     (LIST (CONS 'I (+ -1 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST))))
                           (CONS 'ANS (+ 1 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))))

    ;    fin-body lambda (irrelevant here)
         (LAMBDA$ (ALIST)
           (LIST NIL
                 NIL
                 (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                       (CONS 'ANS (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))

    ;    irrelevant args a5 and a6
         '(NIL) NIL)

  [47mASSOC-EQ-SAFE[0m is just [47mASSOC-EQ[0m with a slightly weaker guard.  Think
  of [47m(CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'var ALIST))[0m as the current value of the
  local variable [47mvar[0m.  The measure is that the [47mACL2-COUNT[0m of the
  current value of [47mI[0m decreases.  The output of the [47mdo-body[0m [47mlambda[0m is
  a triple.  The true branch of the [47mIF[0m indicate iteration is to stop
  and return the current value of [47mANS[0m.  The false branch indicates
  iteration is to continue with the new alist given as the third
  element of the triple.  The [47mfin-body[0m [47mlambda[0m is irrelevant because
  there is no [47m:LOOP-FINISH[0m exit in the do-body.

  Of course, the semantics of [47mdo$[0m is explicit in its definition.  So
  you might want to execute [47m:pe do$[0m in your ACL2 session and just
  read how [47mdo$[0m operates.

  We realize the above descriptions are pretty sketchy.  But in the
  coming discussions and proof problems we'll limit ourselves to [47mDO[0m
  [47mloop$[0ms without [47mof-type[0ms or [47mguards[0m, they'll all return single
  non-[47m[stobj][0m values so the [47m:values[0m option won't be needed, and you
  won't really need to use the [47mfinally[0m clause or any fancier bodies
  than we show in our examples.

  For more details about both the syntax and semantics of [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms see
  [do-loop$].  [31;1mBut beware!  That link takes you out of the [47mloop$[0m[31;1m
  primer![0m To get back here either use your browser's ``back'' button
  or remember to return to [47mlp-section-15[0m!

  Now go to [lp-section-16] (or return to the Table of Contents (see
  [LP-SECTION-0])).")
 (LP-SECTION-16
  (LOOP$-PRIMER)
  "Proving Theorems about [47mDO[0m [47mLoop$[0ms


LP16: Proving Theorems about [47mDO[0m [47mLoop$[0ms

  Let's prove a theorem about a [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m.  Specifically, let's prove

    (defthm main
      (implies (natp n)
               (equal (loop$ with i = n
                             with ans = 0
                             do
                             (if (zp i)
                                 (return ans)
                                 (progn (setq ans (+ 1 ans))
                                        (setq i (- i 1)))))
                      n)))

  A few words of warning are appropriate.

    * The Method (see [the-method]) is a good way to proceed: try to prove
      the theorem expecting it to fail and look at the checkpoints.

    * Unsurprisingly, the theorem above has to be [3mgeneralized[0m before it can
      be proved.  But it can feel strange to generalize a [47mloop$[0m.

    * If we prove a lemma that rewrites a [47mloop$[0m expression we have to
      remember that before rewrite rules are applied the interior
      terms are rewritten.  In particular, ACL2 can rewrite the
      bodies of the [47mlambda[0m objects.  The most common changes are that
      non-recursive functions are generally expanded (depending on
      the theory in use) and [47mIF[0ms are normalized.  That means we need
      to normalize the bodies of any [47mloop$[0m we use in a lemma if we
      expect that lemma to match a term being rewritten!  This
      observation is relevant here because [47m[zp][0m is non-recursively
      defined and so will expand when the body of the [47mloop$[0m above is
      rewritten.

    * ACL2 does not display [47mdo$[0m terms as [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms.  So get used to reading
      [47mdo$[0m terms and thinking of [47mloop$[0m statements!  (We may fix this
      someday but at the moment we find it advantageous to really see
      the terms the prover is dealing with!)

  [31;1mA Tedious Recipe for Proving Theorems about [47mDO[0m[31;1m [47mLoop$[0m[31;1ms[0m

  When you first start proving theorems about [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms it might be
  helpful to follow the tedious recipe below.  It will familiarize
  you with the semantics of [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms and teach you certain
  techniques that are easy applications of lessons you've already
  internalized as an experienced ACL2 user, albeit one unfamiliar
  with [47mloop$[0m.  But after a little experience you'll find it
  straightforward to skip some steps!

    * Use The Method to find the normal form of the [47mloop$[0m.

    * Define a recursive function that computes the value of the [47mloop$[0m
      using the same algorithm as the [47mloop$[0m. This function can serve
      two purposes.  First, it can be an intermediate stop on the way
      to proving that the [47mloop$[0m satisfies your specification.  We'll
      make this clear in the next point.  Second, it can suggest an
      induction scheme that is probably useful.  We'll return to this
      point at the end of our recipe for proving things about [47mDO[0m
      [47mloop$[0ms.

    * Prove that the [47mloop$[0m computes the same value as the function.  We
      frequently refer to this as ``lemma 1'' in the recipe.
      Generally you will have to generalize the [47mloop$[0m to prove it by
      the induction suggested by the function.  And you will have to
      write the normal form of the [47mloop$[0m body instead of the
      ``pretty'' form used in the main theorem so this lemma can be
      used to hit the rewritten [47mloop$[0m in the proof of the main
      theorem later.

    * Prove that the function satisfies the specification.  We frequently
      refer to this as ``lemma 2''.

    * Prove that the [47mloop$[0m satisfies the specification by chaining together
      the two lemmas.

  Of course, as with all ``recipes'', sometimes you have to adjust
  depending on the ingredients at hand.  Sometimes you can just write
  the body of the [47mloop$[0m in normal form to begin with.  Sometimes you
  do not have to define a special function because the [47mloop$[0m itself
  or a function already in the conjecture suggests an appropriate
  induction.  Sometimes you may find it easier to copy the [47mdo$[0m form
  revealed by The Method into your statement of lemma 1 and
  generalize that form, rather than try to express lemma 1 as a
  [47mloop$[0m.  You may also be content to skip the ``intermediate stop''
  of lemma 1 altogether and prove that the [47mloop$[0m satisfies a
  generalized specification, sometimes providing an [47m:induct[0m hint
  instead of inserting the special function into the lemma.
  Sometimes you do not have to generalize.  Sometimes instead of
  proving lemma 1 and lemma 2 you can just prove the main goal
  directly.  As an experienced ACL2 user you will recognize when you
  can skip steps in this recipe.  We spell the recipe out rigidly
  here just to give you one promising way to proceed.

  We're going to prove the theorem

    (defthm main
      (implies (natp n)
               (equal (loop$ with i = n
                             with ans = 0
                             do
                             (if (zp i)
                                 (return ans)
                                 (progn (setq ans (+ 1 ans))
                                        (setq i (- i 1)))))
                      n)))

  both ways, first by following the tedious recipe, and then the way a
  user familiar with [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m proofs might do it.

  So here goes!  Following the recipe to prove [47mmain[0m above, we first try
  The Method.  The prover tries an induction suggested by the [47mDO[0m
  [47mloop$[0m, namely induction on [47mN[0m by [47m-1[0m, but without instantiating [47mANS[0m
  because the initial value of [47mANS[0m is the constant [47m0[0m.  We know this
  proof will fail and it does.  The pre-induction checkpoint is shown
  below.

    *** Key checkpoint at the top level: ***

    Goal''
    (IMPLIES
     (AND (INTEGERP N) (<= 0 N))
     (EQUAL
      (DO$
         (LAMBDA$ (ALIST)
                  (ACL2-COUNT (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST))))
         (CONS (CONS 'I N) '((ANS . 0)))
         (LAMBDA$
              (ALIST)
              (IF (INTEGERP (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                  (IF (< 0 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                      (LIST NIL NIL
                            (LIST (CONS 'I
                                        (+ -1 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST))))
                                  (CONS 'ANS
                                        (+ 1 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))
                      (LIST :RETURN (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))
                            (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                                  (CONS 'ANS
                                        (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))
                  (LIST :RETURN (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))
                        (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                              (CONS 'ANS
                                    (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST)))))))
         (LAMBDA$ (ALIST)
                  (LIST NIL NIL
                        (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                              (CONS 'ANS
                                    (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))
         '(NIL) NIL)
      N))

  Look carefully at the [47mdo-body[0m [47mlambda[0m, the second [47mlambda[0m object in the
  [47mdo$[0m.  The [47m(ZP I)[0m in our original [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m has opened up,
  introducing [47m(INTEGERP i)[0m and [47m(< 0 i)[0m and [47mIF[0m normalization -- except
  instead of seeing the simple variable [47mI[0m we see its current value in
  the [47mALIST[0m being computed on each iteration.

  [31;1mThis is valuable information! It tells us what the rewritten body of
  the [47mloop$[0m[31;1m looks like in the theory in which our proof is being
  conducted.  If we prove a [47m:rewrite[0m[31;1m rule expecting it to fire during
  the proof of our [47mmain[0m[31;1m theorem, its body must match that shown
  above![0m

  Now we define the recursive function that is supposed to be
  operationally equivalent to the [47mloop$[0m.  This will suggest the
  induction.

    (defun copy-nat-ac (i ans)
      (if (zp i)
          ans
          (copy-nat-ac (- i 1)
                       (+ 1 ans))))

  Next comes the hard part.  We state the lemma that equates the [47mloop$[0m
  to the function, but we have to generalize it so it can be proved
  by induction and we have to use the normal form of the body.  We
  could state the lemma in terms of [47mDO$[0m of course, but with a little
  practice you can usually ``reverse engineer'' the desired lemma
  into a [47mloop$[0m.  So here is the so-called ``lemma 1''.

    (defthm lemma1
      (implies (and (natp n)
                    (natp ans0))
               (equal (loop$ with i = n
                             with ans = ans0
                             do
                             (if (integerp i)
                                 (if (< 0 i)
                                     (progn (setq ans (+ 1 ans))
                                            (setq i (- i 1)))
                                     (return ans))
                                 (return ans)))
                      (copy-nat-ac n ans0))))

  Note that we generalized the initial value of [47mans[0m in the [47mloop$[0m from [47m0[0m
  to [47mans0[0m and used [47mans0[0m as the accumulator in [47m(copy-nat-ac n ans0)[0m.

  By the way, we could have written [47mlemma1[0m in terms of the [47mDO$[0m term
  shown in the checkpoint, rather than as a [47mloop$[0m.  But we have to
  generalize that [47m0[0m whether we use a [47mloop$[0m or the [47mDO$[0m term.  The
  generalized [47mDO$[0m form of [47mlemma1[0m is

    (defthm lemma1
      (implies
       (and (natp n)
            (natp ans0))
       (equal
        (DO$
         (LAMBDA$ (ALIST)
                  (ACL2-COUNT (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST))))
         (CONS (CONS 'I N)
               (cons (cons 'ans ans0)
                     nil)) ; note generalization of 0!
         (LAMBDA$
          (ALIST)
          (IF (INTEGERP (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
              (IF (< 0 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                  (LIST NIL NIL
                        (LIST (CONS 'I
                                    (+ -1 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST))))
                              (CONS 'ANS
                                    (+ 1 (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))
                  (LIST :RETURN (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))
                        (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                              (CONS 'ANS
                                    (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))
              (LIST :RETURN (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))
                    (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                          (CONS 'ANS
                                (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST)))))))
         (LAMBDA$ (ALIST)
                  (LIST NIL NIL
                        (LIST (CONS 'I (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'I ALIST)))
                              (CONS 'ANS
                                    (CDR (ASSOC-EQ-SAFE 'ANS ALIST))))))
         '(NIL) NIL)
        (copy-nat-ac n ans0)))).

  The UPPERCASE part above was just copied from the checkpoint and then
  the pair in the initial alist binding [47mANS[0m, which was [47m'(ANS . 0)[0m,
  was replaced by [47m(cons (cons 'ans ans0) nil)[0m.  So while it looks
  messy, it's not hard to enter.  Furthermore, it saves us from
  having to figure out the normal form --- it's already in the
  checkpoint.  The [47mDO$[0m term in this version of [47mlemma1[0m is just the
  formal translation of the generalized [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m we wrote in the
  earlier version of [47mlemma1[0m.

  Next we prove ``lemma 2'' equating the recursive function with the
  (generalized) specification.  This theorem does not involve [47mloop$[0m
  and its proof should be utterly familiar to you.

    (defthm lemma2
      (implies (and (natp n)
                    (natp ans0))
               (equal (copy-nat-ac n ans0)
                      (+ n ans0))))

  Finally, we prove our main theorem.

    (defthm main
      (implies (natp n)
               (equal (loop$ with i = n
                             with ans = 0
                             do
                             (if (zp i)
                                 (return ans)
                                 (progn (setq ans (+ 1 ans))
                                        (setq i (- i 1)))))
                      n)))

  The proof rewrites the ``pretty'' [47mdo-body[0m [47mlambda[0m into the normal
  form, [47mlemma1[0m rewrites the new form of the [47mloop$[0m after instantiating
  [47mans0[0m to [47m0[0m, to [47m(copy-nat-ac n 0)[0m, then [47mlemma2[0m hits that to [47m(+ n 0)[0m,
  and arithmetic does the rest.

  As we noted, our recipe is overly rigid.  Here is another sequence of
  events that proves [47mmain[0m.  The experienced user realizes that the
  generalized [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m will in fact suggest the appropriate induction
  to ACL2.  So no special function is introduced.  Furthermore, the
  user --- who has proved several theorems about [47mloop$[0ms involving [47mIF[0m
  and [47mZP[0m will know how to write the normal form.  That user would
  just do this.

    (defthm lemma
      (implies
       (and (natp n) (natp ans0))
       (equal (loop$ with i = n
                     with ans = ans0
                     do
                     (if (integerp i)
                         (if (< 0 i)
                             (progn (setq ans (+ 1 ans))
                                    (setq i (- i 1)))
                             (return ans))
                         (return ans)))
              (+ n ans0))))

    (defthm main
      (implies (natp n)
               (equal (loop$ with i = n
                             with ans = 0
                             do
                             (if (zp i)
                                 (return ans)
                             (progn (setq ans (+ 1 ans))
                                    (setq i (- i 1)))))
                      n)))

  For more details about rewriting [47mlambda[0m objects you can leave the
  [47mloop$[0m primer and read [rewrite-lambda-object] and
  [rewriting-versus-cleaning-up-lambda-objects].  But remember to
  come [31;1mback[0m here to [47mlp-section-16[0m!

  Now go to [lp-section-17] (or return to the Table of Contents (see
  [LP-SECTION-0])).")
