  "Challenge Problems about [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0ms


LP6: Challenge Problems about [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0ms

  The questions below ask you to write and evaluate some [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms at
  the top-level of ACL2.

  Our answers to the problems in this section are in [community-books]
  file [47mdemos/loop-primer/lp6.lisp[0m.  Feel free to look at our answers
  after you have worked on a problem.  But remember: reading an
  answer is not as helpful as finding it yourself.  If you give up on
  a problem, look at our answer for just that problem, so maybe you
  take a little more insight into your work on subsequent problems.

  Don't forget to start your session by including the standard book for
  [47mapply$[0m.

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

  Don't bother with adding [47m:guard[0m expressions and don't define any
  functions of your own yet.  All of these questions can be answered
  by [47mloop$[0m expressions involving only ACL2 primitives and the
  constant function [47mbags[0m which we introduce below for convenience.

  Let a ``bag'' be a true list of symbols.  The function below returns
  a constant list of bags.  You may use [47m(bags)[0m in your solutions.
  Notice that some elements of [47m(bags)[0m contain the symbol [47mx[0m and other
  do not.

    (defun bags ()
      '((a b c)
        (d x e f)
        (g h i x)
        (j)
        (x k l)))

  Here is a sample question and how you might check that your [47mloop$[0m
  computes the expected value.

------------------------------
  Sample Question: Build an alist that pairs the first symbol in every
  bag of [47m(bags)[0m with the number of other symbols in the bag.  The
  expected value is [47m((A . 2) (D . 3) (G . 3) (J . 0) (X . 2))[0m.

  Sample Check of a Solution:

    (equal (loop$ for bag in (bags)
                  collect (cons (car bag) (len (cdr bag))))
           '((A . 2) (D . 3) (G . 3) (J . 0) (X . 2)))

  Of course, you could just type the [47mloop$[0m statement at the top-level
  of your ACL2 session and visually inspect the answer.  If you want
  to make a certified book out of your answers we recommend
  expressing the checks as [47mdefthm[0m events of [47m:rule-classes nil[0m, as in

    (defthm check-sample-question
      (equal (loop$ for bag in (bags)
                    collect (cons (car bag) (len (cdr bag))))
             '((A . 2) (D . 3) (G . 3) (J . 0) (X . 2)))
      :rule-classes nil)

  However, if your [47mloop$[0m involves a user-defined function and you
  express your answers as [47mdefthm[0ms then each theorem will need to have
  appropriate warrant hypotheses, as illustrated below.

    (defun my-len (x) (if (atom x) 0 (+ 1 (my-len (cdr x)))))
    (defwarrant my-len)
    (defthm check-my-sample-question
      (implies (warrant my-len)
               (equal (loop$ for bag in (bags)
                             collect (cons (car bag) (my-len (cdr bag))))
                      '((A . 2) (D . 3) (G . 3) (J . 0) (X . 2))))
      :rule-classes nil)

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-1:[0m Concatenate all the bags in [47m(bags)[0m.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m[47m(A B C D X E F G H I X J X K L)[0m

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-2:[0m Collect the bags that contain the symbol [47mx[0m.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m[47m((D X E F) (G H I X) (X K L))[0m

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-3:[0m If you used some version of [47mmember[0m in your solution to LP6-2,
  now do it without using any version of [47mmember[0m.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m[47m((D X E F) (G H I X) (X K L))[0m

  Don't use any version of [47mmember[0m in your answers to the remaining
  questions in this section.

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-4:[0m Remove all the [47mx[0ms from each bag in [47m(bags)[0m and concatenate the
  resulting bags.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m[47m(A B C D E F G H I J K L)[0m

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-5:[0m Collect the first symbol of each of the bags in [47m(bags)[0m that
  contains an [47mx[0m.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m[47m(D G X)[0m

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-6:[0m From each bag in [47m(bags)[0m, remove the elements preceding the
  first [47mx[0m and collect the resulting bags.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m[47m(NIL (X E F) (X) NIL (X K L))[0m

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-7:[0m Let's say the ``alternating sum of signed squares'' of a list
  of numbers is the sum of the squares of the elements, except the
  squares of elements in even positions (0-based) are added and the
  squares in odd positions are subtracted.  So the alternating sum of
  signed squares of [47m(1 2 3 4 5)[0m is [47m(+ 1 -4 9 -16 25)[0m = [47m15[0m.  Compute
  the alternating sum of signed squares of the lengths of the bags in
  [47m(bags)[0m

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m[47m(+ 9 -16 16 -1 9)[0m = [47m17[0m.

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-8:[0m Collect all the function symbols in the current ACL2 world
  with an arity greater than 9.

  Three Hints: First, the term [47m(function-theory :here)[0m returns a list
  of the runes of all the [47m:logic[0m mode functions currently in the ACL2
  world.  However, it expands to an expression involving the variable
  [47mworld[0m which can be obtained by [47m(w state)[0m.  And [47m[state][0m may not be
  used in [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m!  So you'll have to [47m[let][0m bind [47mworld[0m to [47m(w state)[0m
  and write your [47mloop$[0m inside the scope of that [47mlet[0m.

  Second, every [47m[rune][0m returned by [47mfunction-theory[0m is of the form
  [47m(:DEFINITION fn . x)[0m, where [47mfn[0m is the name of a function.

  Third, the arity of a symbol [47mfn[0m is obtained from the world by [47m(arity
  fn world)[0m, except sometimes (for reasons we won't go into!) it
  returns [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m As of ACL2 Version 8.5 (after including the
  [47m\"projects/apply/top\"[0m book) the answer was [47m(MEMOIZE-FORM SEARCH-FN
  SEARCH-FN-GUARD BUILD-STATE1)[0m, but that may change.

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP6-9:[0m Collect the name of every theorem about [47mEXPT[0m in the current
  world.

  Three More Hints: First, the ACL2 world is a list of triples of the
  form [47m(name property . val)[0m representing the current property lists.
  In ACL2 Version 8.5, the length of the world (after including the
  [47m\"projects/apply/top\"[0m book) is 137,846, so it's too big to just look
  at!

  Second, each event that named a theorem has a triple of the form
  [47m(name THEOREM . val)[0m, where [47mname[0m is the event name and [47mval[0m is the
  translated version of the theorem.  (Thus, you won't find macros
  like [47m+[0m or [47mappend[0m in [47mval[0m!  Instead it would contain the function
  symbols appearing in their expansions, e.g., [47mbinary-+[0m and
  [47mbinary-append[0m.  But since we're looking for [47mexpt[0m and it is not a
  macro, this doesn't matter.)

  Third, the ACL2 term [47m(all-fnnames term)[0m returns a list of all the
  function symbols used in the fully translated term [47mterm[0m.

  [31;1mExpected Value:[0m As of ACL2 Version 8.5 (after including the
  [47m\"projects/apply/top\"[0m book) the answer was
  [47m(APPLY$-PRIM-META-FN-EV-CONSTRAINT-462
  RATIONALP-EXPT-TYPE-PRESCRIPTION
  EXPT-TYPE-PRESCRIPTION-NON-ZERO-BASE)[0m

------------------------------
  These exercises highlight three important lessons about [47mloop$[0m.

  The first is that with [47mloop$[0m you can avoid defining a lot of
  recursive functions.  Imagine doing these same exercises without
  [47mloop$[0m.

  The second is that it is not always a good idea to ``inline'' [47mloop$[0ms
  instead of defining functions.  The clearest example of that arises
  above when we prohibited you from using [47mmember[0m!  Just because
  [47mmember[0m can be replaced by a [47mloop$[0m doesn't mean it should be!
  [47mMember[0m is an exceptionally useful function that enjoys a lot of
  elegant properties.  By introducing the name [47mmember[0m and proving its
  properties we can use it conveniently and appeal to those
  properties often.  Of course, [47mmember[0m is a Common Lisp primitive, so
  we're not free to define it, but if we were to define a function
  like that we could use a [47mloop$[0m in its definition.

  Another example of this second lesson is in question LP6-7.
  Depending on the project at hand, the notion of ``alternating sum
  of signed squares'' might be a useful one in its own right.  So
  perhaps if such a problem came up you might define
  [47m(alternating-sum-of-signed-squares lst)[0m, perhaps using [47mloop$[0m in the
  [47mdefun[0m, and then use

    (alternating-sum-of-signed-squares
      (loop$ for bag in (bags) collect (len bag)))

  to compute the quantity requested in LP6-7.

  The question raised by this second lesson isn't so much whether you
  use a [47mloop$[0m to express the concept but whether you use a [47mdefun[0m to
  give the concept a name.  A good rule of thumb is: [3mif the concept
  has a natural name, define it![0m

  Of course, there is the usual trade-off between execution efficiency
  and modularity.  There are various ways to deal with this trade-off
  in ACL2 but since they are not unique to [47mloop$[0m we won't discuss
  them.

  The third lesson is highlighted by LP6-8 and LP6-9.  [47mLoop$[0m can be
  very useful in extracting data about your current ACL2 session, if
  you are familiar with the ACL2 system-level utilities (see
  [system-utilities] for some of them).  Perhaps more relevant is the
  observation that if you are building a big model involving lots of
  data, you might find [47mloop$[0m handy in querying your own data.

  Now go to [lp-section-7] (or return to the Table of Contents (see
  [LP-SECTION-0])).")
