  (LOOP$-PRIMER)
  "Using [47mLoop$[0ms and Guards in [47mDefun[0ms


LP7: Using [47mLoop$[0ms and Guards in [47mDefun[0ms

  [47mLoop$[0m statements are most efficient when they are guard verified.
  [47mLoop$[0ms typed at the top-level are typically not guard verified and
  so they are not executed by running compiled Common Lisp [47mloop[0ms.
  Instead, their formal semantics is executed which is akin to saying
  they are interpreted.  But execution efficiency was one of the
  motivational factors in the introduction of [47mloop$[0m and that is best
  achieved by using [47mloop$[0ms in guard verified function definitions.

  Here is an example.  We've already discussed [47mmember[0m and the fact that
  calls of [47mmember[0m can be replaced by [47mloop$[0ms.  [47mMember[0m is a macro that
  expands logically to [47mmember-equal[0m, where

    (defun member-equal (x lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp lst)))
      (cond ((endp lst) nil)
            ((equal x (car lst)) lst)
            (t (member-equal x (cdr lst)))))

  Let's define an equivalent function, named [47mmember-equal-loop$[0m, using
  [47mloop$[0m.

    (defun member-equal-loop$ (x lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp lst)))
      (loop$ for tail of-type (satisfies true-listp) on lst
             thereis
             (if (equal x (car tail)) tail nil)))

    (defthm member-equal-loop$-is-member-equal
       (equal (member-equal-loop$ x lst)
              (member-equal x lst)))

  Since [47mmember-equal[0m has a guard of [47m(true-listp lst)[0m, we will give our
  [47mmember-equal-loop$[0m the same guard.  But in our [47mloop$[0m above we also
  wrote that the iteration variable [47mtail[0m satisfies [47mtrue-listp[0m.  We do
  that so that ACL2 can prove the guard on [47m(car tail)[0m in the [47mthereis[0m
  clause.  (We could change ACL2 to infer this type for [47mtail[0m in this
  special case, but more generally we would prefer to have an
  effective heuristic for transferring arbitrary properties of [47mlst[0m to
  relevant properties of [47mtail[0m.)

  The guard obligations for [47mmember-equal-loop$[0m are obscure because we
  haven't explained the formal semantics of [47mloop$[0m yet.  But we do not
  discuss the guard obligations of [47mloop$[0ms in the primer.  If you
  define a function containing a [47mloop$[0m and try to verify its guards
  the system will generate the necessary obligations and try to prove
  them.  Our solutions to all the exercises in this primer can be
  guard verified automatically.  For more details on [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m guards
  and the guard obligations they generate, see [for-loop$],
  specifically, the sections ``Special Guard Conjectures for [47mFOR[0m
  [47mLoop$[0ms'' and ``Discussion of Why [47mLoop$[0ms Have Special Guards.''

  After admitting [47mmember-equal-loop$[0m ACL2 can prove inductively that it
  is equal to [47mmember-equal[0m.  We'll come back to that later too.

  An alternative way to specify the type of [47mtail[0m would be to add a
  [47m:guard[0m rather than an [47mof-type[0m expression.

    (defun member-equal-loop$ (x lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp lst)))
      (loop$ for tail on lst
             thereis
             :guard (true-listp tail)
             (if (equal x (car tail)) tail nil)))

  The [47m:guard[0m feature of ACL2 is more flexible than [47mof-type[0m because
  guards allow you to use multiple variables to express a constraint,
  while [47mof-type[0m implicitly limits the assertion to the variable being
  introduced.  For example, with a guard you could say [47m(subsetp-equal
  tail lst)[0m while you cannot express such a constraint with [47mof-type[0m.
  However, [47mof-type[0m is understood by the Common Lisp compiler which
  might optimize the compiled code using that type information, while
  guards are not seen by the compiler.

  When you use a [47mloop$[0m in a [47mdefun[0m to be guard verified, be sure to
  constrain its iteration variables (and global variables)
  appropriately so you can verify the guards of the body.  (You are
  also allowed to specify different guards for any [47mwhen[0m and [47muntil[0m
  expressions.)  We generally split our constraints between [47mof-type[0m
  and [47m:guard[0m to inform the compiler of simple types, while more
  elaborate guard conditions are sometimes necessary to verify the
  guards of the body, etc.  ACL2 will take any [47mof-type[0m information
  and conjoin it to any [47m:guard[0m when doing guard verification.

  Now go to [lp-section-8] (or return to the Table of Contents (see
  [LP-SECTION-0])).")
 (LP-SECTION-8
  (LOOP$-PRIMER)
  "Challenge Problems about [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0m in [47mDefun[0ms


LP8: Challenge Problems about [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0m in [47mDefun[0ms

  Do the problems below, starting in a fresh ACL2 session that starts
  with the standard [47m(include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)[0m.
  Each problem starts with one or more recursive functions.  Admit
  those functions in your session.  Then define equivalent versions
  using [47mloop$[0ms instead of recursion.  Make sure each of your [47mdefun[0ms
  is admitted and guard verified.  Try to ensure that the [47mloop$[0m
  version is unconditionally equal to the recursive version of each
  function.  But you might find more elegant solutions if you're
  willing to condition your equivalences on the guards of the
  recursive versions.  After all, a main motivation for using [47mloop$[0m
  is the runtime efficiency of the compiled raw Lisp, and since no
  ACL2 function is executed in raw Lisp unless the guards are
  verified.

  Finally, you need not use ACL2 to prove that your [47mloop$[0m functions
  correctly implement their recursive counterparts.  But you should
  be aware that with a few exceptions the proofs of our solutions
  were completely automatic.  We'll focus on proving theorems about
  [47mloop$[0ms later in the primer.

  Our answers to the problems in this section are in [community-books]
  file [47mdemos/loop-primer/lp8.lisp[0m.

------------------------------
  Sample Question: [47m(Sum-vals alist)[0m sums the value components of an
  alist.  Note its guard.

    (defun symbol-to-integer-alistp (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (if (atom x)
          (equal x nil)
          (and (consp (car x))
               (symbolp (caar x))
               (integerp (cdar x))
               (symbol-to-integer-alistp (cdr x)))))

    (defun sum-vals (alist)
      (declare (xargs :guard (symbol-to-integer-alistp alist)))
      (cond ((endp alist) 0)
            (t (+ (cdar alist) (sum-vals (cdr alist))))))

  E.g., [47m(sum-vals '((a . 1) (b . 2) (c . 3))) = 6[0m.

  Define [47msum-vals-loop$[0m so that it is unconditionally equivalent to
  [47msum-vals[0m but uses [47mloop$[0m instead of recursion.

  Sample Solution:

    (defun sum-vals-loop$ (alist)
      (declare (xargs :guard (symbol-to-integer-alistp alist)))
      (loop$ for pair in alist
             sum
             :guard (and (consp pair)
                         (integerp (cdr pair)))
             (cdr pair)))

  The [47m(consp pair)[0m in the [47mloop$[0m [47m:guard[0m is necessary because the [47mloop$[0m
  body contains [47m(cdr pair)[0m and Common Lisp expects [47mcdr[0m to be applied
  to a [47mconsp[0m or [47mnil[0m.  The [47m(integerp (cdr pair))[0m is necessary because
  the value of the [47mloop$[0m body is being [47msum[0mmed and so must be (at
  least) a number to satisfy Common Lisp's expectation on [47m+[0m.

  Verification of Sample Solution:

    (defthm sum-vals-loop$-is-sum-vals
      (equal (sum-vals-loop$ alist)
             (sum-vals alist))
      :rule-classes nil)

  It is conceivable in some Common Lisps that the following would
  compile into more efficient code.

    (defun sum-vals-loop$ (alist)
      (declare (xargs :guard (symbol-to-integer-alistp alist)))
      (loop$ for pair of-type cons in alist
             sum
             :guard (integerp (cdr pair))
             (the integer (cdr pair))))

  The thinking is that the [47mof-type cons[0m and [47m(the integer (cdr pair))[0m
  could in principle allow the compiler to do type checking that
  could eliminate runtime tests to avoid errors.  That's because
  [47mof-type[0m and [47mthe[0m are Common Lisp primitives, as are the particular
  [47m[type-spec][0ms used in [47msum-vals-loop$[0m.  Whether such a well-declared
  version of [47msum-vals-loop$[0m would actually run faster on your Common
  Lisp depends on the compiler and the optimization proclamations.
  In CCL and SBCL with ACL2's default proclamations, this
  well-declared version runs no faster than the original sample
  solution does.  We raise this point simply to alert the reader to
  the difference between compiler directives and [47m:guard[0ms.

------------------------------
  [31;1mLP8-1[0m Define [47msum-vals-loop$[0m which is like our solution shown for
  [47msum-vals[0m above, except instead of using [47msymbol-to-integer-alist[0m as
  the [47m:guard[0m write the [47m:guard[0m as [47m(and (true-listp alist) (loop$
  ...))[0m.  Be sure your definition is admitted and guard verified.

  If you want a slightly more challenging problem, omit the [47m(true-listp
  alist)[0m from the [47m:guard[0m and use a [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m ``[47mON[0m'' [47malist[0m.  FYI:
  Common Lisp requires ``[47mIN[0m'' [47mloop$[0ms to be over a [47mtrue-listp[0m target,
  but there is no such requirement for ``[47mON[0m'' [47mloop$[0ms.

  You can always convert an ``[47mIN[0m'' [47mloop$[0m governed by a [47mtrue-listp[0m check
  to an ``[47mON[0m'' [47mloop$[0m without the check.  See our solutions.  But
  because of that, we'll use the more elegant ``[47mIN[0m'' solutions in the
  rest of these problems.

