 (LP-SECTION-9
  (LOOP$-PRIMER)
  "Semantics of [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0ms


LP9: Semantics of [47mFOR[0m [47mLoop$[0ms

  For a thorough discussion of the semantics of [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms see
  [for-loop$], specifically the section ``Semantics'' which includes
  two subsections, ``Semantics of Simple [47mLoop$[0ms'' and ``Semantics of
  Fancy [47mLoop$[0ms.'' ``Fancy'' [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms are [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms involving
  multiple iteration variables and/or use of ``global'' (i.e.,
  non-iteration) variables in the [47mloop$[0m body or the [47muntil[0m or [47mwhen[0m
  clauses. We discuss [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms later.  In this section of the primer
  we just present some examples to drive home a few important points
  about [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms, namely

    * The semantics of a [47mloop$[0m statement is obtained by translating the
      [47mloop$[0m statement, as with the command [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m.

    * However, translation inserts a lot of tags into the formal term it
      produced.  These tags allow us to execute [47mloop$[0m statements more
      efficiently in the top-level ACL2 read-eval-print [47mloop$[0m.  The
      tags on a translated [47mloop$[0m, [3mterm[0m, can be removed by the [47m:[0m[47m[tc][0m
      (``translate and clean'') command and its variants [47m:tca[0m and
      [47m:tcp[0m.  When we exhibit semantics we typically show these
      simplified, equivalent terms produced by one of these commands.

    * The semantics of [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms look quite different from the semantics
      of [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms.  Furthermore, the semantics of simple [47mloop$[0ms have
      a different form than those of fancy [47mloop$[0m.  So ``minor''
      changes in the syntax of a [47mloop$[0m statement --- the addition of
      an [47mAS[0m clause, the use of global variable in the body of the
      [47mloop$[0m, or the use of [47mDO[0m instead of, say, [47mcollect[0m --- can cause
      radical changes in the form of the semantics.

    * The semantics of [47mloop$[0ms always involve calls of [47m[scion][0ms on lambda
      objects derived from the [47mwhen[0m, [47muntil[0m, and [47mloop$[0m body clauses.
      The scions for simple [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0ms are [47msum$[0m, [47mcollect$[0m, [47malways$[0m,
      [47mthereis$[0m, [47mappend$[0m, [47muntil$[0m, and [47mwhen$[0m; the scions for fancy [47mFOR[0m
      [47mloop$[0ms are [47msum$+[0m, [47mcollect$+[0m, [47malways$+[0m, [47mthereis$+[0m, [47mappend$+[0m,
      [47muntil$+[0m, and [47mwhen$+[0m; and the scion for [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0ms is [47mdo$[0m.

    * The iteration variables become formals of the [47mlambda[0m objects and
      standard names are used in place of the user's names.

  Now we drive home these points with some examples.  The next section
  (see [LP-SECTION-10]) elaborates further.

  Here is the full, formal translation of a simple [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m.

    ACL2 !>:trans (loop$ for x of-type integer in lst
                         when (evenp x)
                         collect (* x x))

    (RETURN-LAST
     'PROGN
     '(LOOP$ FOR X OF-TYPE INTEGER IN LST
             WHEN (EVENP X)
             COLLECT (* X X))
     (COLLECT$
       '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                (DECLARE (TYPE INTEGER LOOP$-IVAR)
                         (XARGS :GUARD (INTEGERP LOOP$-IVAR)
                                :SPLIT-TYPES T)
                         (IGNORABLE LOOP$-IVAR))
                (RETURN-LAST 'PROGN
                             '(LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                                       (DECLARE (TYPE INTEGER LOOP$-IVAR))
                                       (LET ((X LOOP$-IVAR))
                                            (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X))
                                            (* X X)))
                             ((LAMBDA (X) (BINARY-* X X))
                              LOOP$-IVAR)))
       (WHEN$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                       (DECLARE (TYPE INTEGER LOOP$-IVAR)
                                (XARGS :GUARD (INTEGERP LOOP$-IVAR)
                                       :SPLIT-TYPES T)
                                (IGNORABLE LOOP$-IVAR))
                       (RETURN-LAST 'PROGN
                                    '(LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                                              (DECLARE (TYPE INTEGER LOOP$-IVAR))
                                              (LET ((X LOOP$-IVAR))
                                                   (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X))
                                                   (EVENP X)))
                                    ((LAMBDA (X) (EVENP X)) LOOP$-IVAR)))
              LST)))

  However, the logical meaning of this is easier to understand by
  looking at one of the [47mtc[0m variations.

    ACL2 !>:tc (loop$ for x of-type integer in lst
                          when (evenp x)
                          collect (* x x))
     (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                        (BINARY-* LOOP$-IVAR LOOP$-IVAR))
               (WHEN$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR) (EVENP LOOP$-IVAR))
                      LST))
    ACL2 !>:tcp (loop$ for x of-type integer in lst
                           when (evenp x)
                           collect (* x x))
     (COLLECT$ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                        (* LOOP$-IVAR LOOP$-IVAR))
               (WHEN$ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                               (EVENP LOOP$-IVAR))
                      LST))
    ACL2 !>:tca (loop$ for x of-type integer in lst
                           when (evenp x)
                           collect (* x x))
     (PROG2$
       '(LOOP$ FOR X OF-TYPE INTEGER IN LST
               WHEN (EVENP X)
               COLLECT (* X X))
       (COLLECT$ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                    (DECLARE (TYPE INTEGER LOOP$-IVAR)
                             (XARGS :GUARD (INTEGERP LOOP$-IVAR)
                                    :SPLIT-TYPES T))
                    (LET ((X LOOP$-IVAR))
                         (* X X)))
                 (WHEN$ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                           (DECLARE (TYPE INTEGER LOOP$-IVAR)
                                    (XARGS :GUARD (INTEGERP LOOP$-IVAR)
                                           :SPLIT-TYPES T))
                           (LET ((X LOOP$-IVAR))
                                (EVENP X)))
                        LST)))

  Note that [47m:tc[0m prints the term generated by translating the [47mloop$[0m and
  then removing all tags.  The guard and type declarations are
  logically irrelevant.  What you see is what the theorem prover will
  see (if all the necessary warrants are assumed) before it starts to
  rewrite the term.  Note that the [47mlambda[0m object is just a quoted
  list constant and the body has been translated so that the [47m*[0m macro
  was expanded in terms of [47mbinary-*[0m.

  [47m:Tcp[0m (``p'' for ``pretty'') introduces familiar system macros.

  [47m:Tca[0m (``a'' for ``annotations'') includes the original [47mloop$[0m
  statement and shows declarations (if any) and the correspondence
  between the user's iteration variable ([47mx[0m here) and the formal
  variable used in the [47mlambda[0m objects generated ([47mloop$-ivar[0m here).

  The translation of [47mloop$[0m bodies into [47mlambda[0m objects standardizes the
  variable name, either as [47mloop$-ivar[0m for simple [47mloop$[0ms which have
  just one iteration variable, or as [47mloop$-gvars[0m and [47mloop$-ivars[0m for
  fancy [47mloop$[0ms which may have one or more global variables and one or
  more iteration variables.  We'll deal with fancy [47mloop$[0ms immediately
  below, but values are passed for [47mloop$-gvars[0m and [47mloop$-ivars[0m as
  tuples listing all the global values and all the current iteration
  variable values.

  The advantage of this standardization is that choosing different
  names for the iteration variables does not affect the formal
  semantics.

  However, if we make a simple syntactic change, like introducing a
  global variable by changing the body of the loop$ from [47m(* x x)[0m to
  [47m(* x a)[0m, where [47ma[0m is not an iteration variable, we drastically
  change the semantics because we've converted the simple [47mloop$[0m into
  a fancy [47mloop$[0m.  The (clean and pretty) semantics of

    (loop$ for x in lst when (evenp x) collect (* x a))

  is

    (COLLECT$+ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                        (* (CAR LOOP$-IVARS) (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)))
               (LIST A)
               (WHEN$+ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                                (EVENP (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
                       NIL
                       (LOOP$-AS (LIST LST))))

  The annotated semantics of that [47mloop$[0m is

    (PROG2$
     '(LOOP$ FOR X IN LST
             WHEN (EVENP X)
             COLLECT (* X A))
     (COLLECT$+
       (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                            (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 1)
                                            (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                            (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 1))
                                :SPLIT-TYPES T))
                (LET ((A (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                      (X (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
                     (* X A)))
       (LIST A)
       (WHEN$+ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                        (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                                    (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 0)
                                                    (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                                    (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 1))
                                        :SPLIT-TYPES T))
                        (LET ((X (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
                             (EVENP X)))
               NIL
               (LOOP$-AS (LIST LST)))))

  Logically, we could have typed either of the above terms instead of
  the fancy [47mloop$[0m.  But operationally, the [47mloop$[0m executes faster
  (when guards are verified).

  Notice that the simple scions [47mCOLLECT$[0m and [47mWHEN$[0m have been replaced
  by their fancy counterparts, [47mCOLLECT$+[0m and [47mWHEN$+[0m.  The [47mlambda[0m
  objects now have two formals, one for holding a tuple containing
  all of the global variable values and one for a tuple holding all
  of the current iteration variable values.  The global variable
  value tuple, [47m(LIST A)[0m, is passed into the fancy scions if the
  corresponding [47mlambda[0m object uses globals (as for the [47mcollect$+[0m but
  not for the [47mwhen$+[0m [47mlambda[0m where [47mNIL[0m is passed instead).  The list
  over which the iteration variable ranges, [47mLST[0m, is now a list of
  tuples constructed by [47mLOOP$-AS[0m.  Finally, the bodies of the [47mlambda[0ms
  now access the relevant values with [47mCAR[0m and [47mCDR[0m nests around the
  global and iteration tuples. (No [47mCDR[0ms appear above because there is
  only one global and one iteration variable in this example.)

  The following similar but still fancier [47mloop$[0m illustrates the general
  situation.  This [47mloop$[0m has two iteration variables, [47mx[0m and [47my[0m taking
  on values from two ranges, [47mxlst[0m, and [47mylst[0m, and uses two global
  variables, [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m, in the body.  We've added comments to name the
  components of the two tuples.

    ACL2 !>:tcp (loop$ for x in xlst
                       as  y in ylst
                       when (evenp x)
                       collect (* x y a b))
     (COLLECT$+ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                         (* (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)               ; x
                            (CADR LOOP$-IVARS)              ; y
                            (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)               ; a
                            (CADR LOOP$-GVARS)))            ; b
                (LIST A B)
                (WHEN$+ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                                 (EVENP (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))) ; x
                        NIL
                        (LOOP$-AS (LIST XLST YLST))))

  Notice how the bodies of the [47mlambda[0m objects now use components of the
  [47mloop$-gvars[0m and [47mloop$-ivars)[0m for the current values of the global
  variables [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m and the iteration variables [47mx[0m and [47my[0m.  The values
  of [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are corresponding elements of [47mxlst[0m and [47mylst[0m as grouped
  together by [47m(loop$-as (list xlst ylst))[0m.

  For a still-more elaborate [47mFOR[0m [47mloop$[0m and a step-by-step description
  of how the value of the formal semantics is computed, go to
  [lp-section-10].

  Now go to [lp-section-10] (or return to the Table of Contents (see
  [LP-SECTION-0])).")
 (MACRO-ALIASES-TABLE
  (MACROS)
  "A [table] used to associate function names with macro names

    Example Form:
    (table macro-aliases-table 'append 'binary-append)

  This example associates the function symbol [47m[binary-append][0m with the
  macro name [47m[append][0m.  As a result, the name [47m[append][0m may be used as
  a runic designator (see [theories]) by the various theory
  functions.  Thus, for example, it will be legal to write

    (in-theory (disable append))

  as an abbreviation for

    (in-theory (disable binary-append)).

    General Form:

    (table macro-aliases-table 'macro-name 'function-name)

  or more generally

    (table macro-aliases-table macro-name-form function-name-form)

  where [47mmacro-name-form[0m and [47mfunction-name-form[0m evaluate to values [47mMAC[0m
  and [47mFN[0m, subject to the following requirements.

    * [47mMAC[0m is a symbol that is a macro name.

    * [47mFN[0m is a symbol.

    * [47mFN[0m is either a function symbol or else a symbol that does not have a
      definition (a [3mnew name[0m).  In particular, [47mFN[0m is not a macro
      name.

  After admitting the [table] event displayed above, we may say that
  [47mMAC[0m is a [3mmacro-alias for[0m [47mFN[0m.


Further Explanation

  See [table] for a general discussion of tables and the [47mtable[0m [event],
  which is used to manipulate tables.  The table,
  [47m[macro-aliases-table][0m, is an alist that can associate macro symbols
  with function symbols, so that macro names may be used as runic
  designators (see [theories] and discussion below).  For a
  convenient way to add entries to this [table], see
  [add-macro-alias], which allows the second argument to be, itself,
  a macro-alias.  To remove entries conveniently from the [table],
  see [remove-macro-alias].

  As hinted above, this [table] is used by theory functions; see
  [theories].  For example, in order that [47m(disable append)[0m be
  interpreted as [47m(disable binary-append)[0m, it suffices that [47m(table
  macro-aliases-table 'append 'binary-append)[0m, has been executed.  In
  fact, this [table] does indeed establish many of the macros
  provided by the ACL2 system as macro-aliases, such as establishing
  [47m[append][0m as a macro-alias for [47m[binary-append][0m.  This only takes
  place when the macro is ``essentially the same thing as'' a
  corresponding function; for example, [47m(append x y)[0m and
  [47m(binary-append x y)[0m represent the same term, for any expressions [47mx[0m
  and [47my[0m.

  Note that the value [47mFN[0m of [47mfunction-name-form[0m (above) is allowed to be
  a symbol to be defined later as a function symbol.  The following
  examples illustrate some approaches that work and some that do not.

    ;;;;;;;;;;
    ;;; Example 1: No errors, with function defined before setting the table.
    ;;;;;;;;;;

    (defun fn (x) x)
    (defmacro mac (x) (list 'fn x))
    (table macro-aliases-table 'mac 'fn) ; or (add-macro-alias mac fn)
    (in-theory (disable mac))

    ;;;;;;;;;;
    ;;; Example 2: No errors, with function defined after setting the table.
    ;;;;;;;;;;

    (defmacro mac (x) (list 'fn x))

    ; Legal, even though fn is not yet defined:
    (table macro-aliases-table 'mac 'fn) ; or (add-macro-alias mac fn)

    (defun fn (x) x)
    (in-theory (disable mac))

    ;;;;;;;;;;
    ;;; Example 3: ERROR.
    ;;;;;;;;;;

    (defun fn (x) x)
    (defmacro mac1 (x) (fn x))
    (defmacro mac2 (x) (list 'mac1 x))

    ; BAD, since the table event above anticipates fn to be defined as
    ; a function, not a macro (but, no error yet) -- but not (yet) an error:
    (table macro-aliases-table 'mac2 'mac1) ; or (add-macro-alias mac2 mac1)

    ; ERROR, since mac2 does not designate rules, because it aliases a macro:
    (in-theory (disable mac2))

    ; OK, but too late to help with that in-theory event; see below:
    (table macro-aliases-table 'mac1 'fn) ; or (add-macro-alias mac1 fn)

    ; ERROR, since mac2 is still associated with mac1, not with fn:
    (in-theory (disable mac2))

    ;;;;;;;;;;
    ;;; Example 4: Fixed version of Example 3.
    ;;;;;;;;;;

    (defun fn (x) x)
    (defmacro mac1 (x) (fn x))
    (defmacro mac2 (x) (list 'mac1 x))
    (add-macro-alias mac1 fn); or (table macro-aliases-table 'mac1 'fn)

    ; The following is equivalent to (add-macro-alias mac2 fn), since
    ; mac1 is a macro-alias for fn by virtue of the preceding event.
    (add-macro-alias mac2 mac1)

    ; Success:
    (in-theory (disable mac2))")
 (MACRO-ARGS
  (MACROS)
  "The formals list of a macro definition

    Examples:
    (x y z)
    (x y z &optional max (base '10 basep))
    (x y &rest rst)
    (x y &key max base)
    (&whole sexpr x y)

  The ``lambda-list'' of a macro definition may include simple formal
  parameter names as well as appropriate uses of the following
  lambda-list keywords from Common Lisp, respecting the order shown:

    * [47m&whole x[0m
      [47mX[0m does not represent an actual parameter; rather, it is bound to the
      entire macro call.

    * [47m&optional x1 x2 ...[0m
      The actual for any [47mxi[0m may be omitted, in which case the actual for
      every [47mxj[0m with [47mj > i[0m must also be omitted.

    * [47m&rest x[0m
      [47mX[0m does not represent an actual parameter; rather, it is bound to the
      list of all actuals provided from that position onward.

    * [47m&body x[0m
      This is identical to [47m&rest x[0m.

    * [47m&key x[0m
      The call may have a keyword argument for [47mx[0m by including [47m:x val[0m after
      all required actual parameters, in which case the formal [47mx[0m is
      bound to the actual [47mval[0m.

    * [47m&allow-other-keys[0m
      Keyword arguments not specified by [47m&key[0m are allowed but ignored.

  ACL2 does not support the Common Lisp lambda-list keywords [47m&aux[0m and
  [47m&environment[0m.  In addition, there are the following restrictions:

      (1) initialization forms in [47m&optional[0m and [47m&key[0m specifiers must be
      quoted values;

      (2) [47m&allow-other-keys[0m may only be used once, as the last specifier;
      and

      (3) destructuring is not allowed.

  The use of default values is allowed, so that an optional or keyword
  argument may be given in any of the following forms.

    * [47marg[0m or, equivalently, [47m(arg)[0m

    * [47m(arg 'init)[0m

    * [47m(arg 'init supplied-p)[0m

  For the second and third forms above, [47minit[0m is the value when the
  argument is not supplied; for the first form, that default value is
  [47mnil[0m.  Note that it must be quoted.  The [47msupplied-p[0m argument is [47mnil[0m
  when the argument is not supplied, else is [47mt[0m, as illustrated by the
  demo below.

  You are encouraged to experiment with the macro facility.  One way to
  do so is to define a macro that does nothing but return the
  quotation of its arguments, e.g.,

    (defmacro demo (x y &optional (opt '5) &key (key1 '6 key1p) key2)
      (list 'quote (list x y opt key1 key1p key2)))

  You may then execute the macro on some sample forms, e.g.,

      term                         value
    (demo 1 2)                (1 2 5 6 NIL NIL)
    (demo 1 2 3)              (1 2 3 6 NIL NIL)
    (demo 1 2 3 :key1 6)      (1 2 3 6 T NIL)
    (demo 1 2 3 :key1 7)      (1 2 3 7 T NIL)
    (demo 1 2 3 :key2 8)      (1 2 3 6 NIL 8)
    (demo 1 2 :key1 3)        error:  non-even key/value arglist
                              (because :key1 is used as opt)

  In particular, Common Lisp specifies (hence so does ACL2) that if you
  use both [47m&rest[0m and [47m&key[0m, then both will be bound using the same
  list of arguments.  The following example should serve to
  illustrate how this works.

    ACL2 !>(defmacro foo (&rest args &key k1 k2 k3)
             (list 'quote (list args k1 k2 k3)))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFMACRO FOO ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     FOO
    ACL2 !>(foo :k1 3 :k2 4 :k3 5)
    ((:K1 3 :K2 4 :K3 5) 3 4 5)
    ACL2 !>(foo :k1 3 :k2 4)
    ((:K1 3 :K2 4) 3 4 NIL)
    ACL2 !>(foo :k1 3 :bad-key 7)

    ACL2 Error in macro expansion:  Illegal key/value args (:BAD-KEY 7)
    in macro expansion of (FOO :K1 3 :BAD-KEY 7).  The argument list for
    FOO is
    (&REST ARGS &KEY K1 K2 K3).

    ACL2 !>

  If we don't want to get the error above, we can use
  [47m&allow-other-keys[0m, as follows.

    ACL2 !>(defmacro bar (&rest args &key k1 k2 k3
                                &allow-other-keys)
             (list 'quote (list args k1 k2 k3)))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFMACRO BAR ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     BAR
    ACL2 !>(bar :k1 3 :bad-key 7)
    ((:K1 3 :BAD-KEY 7) 3 NIL NIL)
    ACL2 !>

  Also see [trans].


Subtopics

  [Set-duplicate-keys-action]
      Control action for macro calls with duplicate keyword arguments")
 (MACRO-COMMAND
  (PROOF-BUILDER)
  "Compound command for the interactive proof-builder

  The interactive [proof-builder] supports the definition of compound
  commands, called ``macro commands'', which expand into zero or more
  other commands.  Some of these are ``atomic'' macro commands; these
  are viewed as a single command step when completed successfully.

  More [documentation] may be written on the interactive
  [proof-builder].  For now, we simply point out that there are lots
  of examples of the use of [47mdefine-pc-macro[0m and
  [47mdefine-pc-atomic-macro[0m in the ACL2 source file
  [47m\"proof-builder-b.lisp\"[0m.  The former is used to create macro
  commands, which can be submitted to the interactive loop (see
  [verify]) and will ``expand'' into zero or more commands.  The
  latter is similar, except that the undoing mechanism (see
  [ACL2-pc::undo]) understands atomic macro commands to represent
  single interactive commands.  Also see [ACL2-pc::comm] and see
  [ACL2-pc::commands] for a discussion of the display of interactive
  commands.

  Also see [toggle-pc-macro] for how to change a macro command to an
  atomic macro command, and vice versa.")
 (MACROLET
  (BASICS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Local binding of macro symbols

  See [flet] for an analogous utility for defining functions locally.

    Example Form:
    (defun f1 (x)
      (macrolet ((mac (a) (list 'quote a)))
        (cons x (mac x))))

  The Example Form above is equivalent to the following, in which the
  call of local macro [47mmac[0m has been expanded.

    (defun f1-alt (x)
      (cons x (quote x)))

  The General Forms are similar to those of [47m[flet][0m.

    General Forms:
    (macrolet (def1 ... defk) body)
    (macrolet (def1 ... defk) declare-form1 .. declare-formk body)

  where [47mbody[0m is a term, and each [47mdefi[0m is a definition as in [47m[defmacro][0m
  but with the leading [47mdefmacro[0m symbol omitted.  See [defmacro], but
  see [declare] for the declarations permitted directly under the
  [47mdefi[0m.  On the other hand, regarding the [47mdeclare-formi[0m (if any are
  supplied): each must be of the form [47m(declare decl1 ... decln)[0m,
  where each [47mdecli[0m is of the form [47m(inline g1 ... gm)[0m or [47m(notinline g1
  ... gm)[0m, and each [47mgi[0m is defined by some [47mdefi[0m.  Unlike the related
  utility [47m[flet][0m, those [47minline[0m and [47mnotinline[0m declarations are
  unlikely to have any effect.

  The innermost [47m[flet][0m or [47mmacrolet[0m binding of a symbol, [47mf[0m, above a call
  of [47mf[0m, is the one that provides the definition of [47mf[0m for that call.
  Note that neither [47mflet[0m nor [47mmacrolet[0m provide recursion: that is, the
  definition of [47mf[0m in an [47mflet[0m or [47mmacrolet[0m binding of [47mf[0m is ignored in
  the body of that binding.

  The following requirements are imposed by Common Lisp and enforced by
  ACL2.

    * Every variable occurring in the body of a [47mdefi[0m must be a formal
      parameter name of that [47mdefi[0m.

    * No function or macro symbol called in the body of a [47mdefi[0m may be
      defined by a superior [47mflet[0m or [47mmacrolet[0m binding.  (Not every
      Common Lisp implementation includes this restriction for
      superior [47mmacrolet[0m bindings, but at least one (GCL) does so we
      include it in ACL2.)

  Although [47mmacrolet[0m behaves in ACL2 essentially as it does in Common
  Lisp, ACL2 imposes the following restrictions and qualifications.

    * Every [47m[declare][0m form for a local definition ([47mdef1[0m through [47mdefk[0m,
      above) must be an [47mignore[0m, [47mignorable[0m, or [47mtype[0m expression.

    * Each [47mdefi[0m must bind a different symbol.

    * Each [47mdefi[0m must bind a symbol that is a legal name for an ACL2 macro.
      In particular, the symbol may not be in the keyword package or
      the main Lisp package.  Moreover, the symbol may not be a
      built-in ACL2 function or macro.

    * When an expression [47m(macrolet (... defi ...) ...)[0m occurs in the body
      of a [47mDO[0m [47m[loop$][0m expression, nevertheless constructs such as
      [47mPROGN[0m and [47mSETQ[0m that ACL2 permits in [47mDO[0m [47mloop$[0m bodies are not
      permitted in [47mdefi[0m (unless they occur within the scope of a [47mDO[0m
      [47mloop$[0m expression in that body).

  [47mMacrolet[0m bindings are evaluated in parallel.  Consider the following
  example.

    (defun f1 (x) (cons x 'x))
    (macrolet ((f1 (x) x)
               (f2 () (list 'quote
    ; The following reference is to the global f1,
    ; not to the identity macro just above.
                            (f1 3))))
      (f2))

  The [47mmacrolet[0m form above evaluates to [47m(3 . x)[0m, not to 3, as explained
  in the comment above.  Here is a somewhat analogous form that one
  might expect to evaluate to 3, but that is not the case; see below.

    (macrolet ((f1 (x) x))
      (macrolet ((f2 () (list 'quote (f1 3))))
        (f2)))

  The body of [47mf2[0m calls a symbol, [47mf1[0m, that is bound by a superior
  [47mmacrolet[0m binding.  As noted above, this is illegal (also for
  superior [47mflet[0m bindings).

  Under the hood, ACL2 expands away [47mmacrolet[0m bindings.  The following
  example illustrates this point.

    ACL2 !>:trans (macrolet ((mac (a) (list 'cons a a)))
                    (car (mac b)))

    (CAR (CONS B B))

    => *

    ACL2 !>

  [47mMacrolet[0m is part of Common Lisp.  See any Common Lisp documentation
  for more information.  We conclude by pointing out an important
  aspect of [47mmacrolet[0m shared by ACL2 and Common Lisp: The binding is
  lexical, not dynamic.  That is, the [47mmacrolet[0m binding of a symbol
  only applies to calls of that symbol in the body of the [47mmacrolet[0m,
  not other calls made in the course of evaluation.  See [flet] for
  discussion of this point.")
