 (MAKE-EVENT
  (EVENTS MACROS)
  "Evaluate (expand) a given form and then evaluate the result

  [47mMake-event[0m is a utility for generating [events].  It provides a
  capability not offered by Lisp macros (see [defmacro]), as it
  allows access to the ACL2 [state] and logical [world].  In essence,
  the expression [47m(make-event form)[0m replaces itself with the result of
  evaluating [47mform[0m --- let's call that result [47mev[0m --- as though one had
  submitted [47mev[0m instead of the [47mmake-event[0m call.  For example,
  [47m(make-event (quote (defun f (x) x)))[0m is equivalent to the event
  [47m(defun f (x) x)[0m.

  We assume basic familiarity with the ACL2 [3mstate[0m.  For relevant
  background, see [state] and perhaps see [programming-with-state].

  There are several simple examples below.  For examples that can give
  additional insight into the use of [47mmake-event[0m for tool development,
  see [make-event-example-1] and [make-event-example-2].  Also see
  the [47mmake-event/[0m subdirectory of the ACL2 [community-books] for more
  examples, for example, [47mbooks/make-event/search-generation.lisp[0m.

  We break this documentation into the following sections.

    * [31;1mIntroduction[0m

    * [31;1mDetailed Documentation[0m

    * [31;1mError Reporting[0m

    * [31;1mRestriction to Event Contexts[0m

    * [31;1mExamples Illustrating How to Access State[0m

    * [31;1mAdvanced Expansion Control[0m

  We begin with an introduction, which focuses on examples and
  introduces the key notion of ``expansion phase''.


Introduction

  [47mMake-event[0m is particularly useful for those who program using the
  ACL2 [47m[state][0m; see [programming-with-state].  That is because the
  evaluation of [47mform[0m may read and even modify the ACL2 [47m[state][0m.

  Suppose for example that we want to define a constant [47m*world-length*[0m,
  that is the length of the current ACL2 [world].  A [47mmake-event[0m form
  can accomplish this task, as follows.

    ACL2 !>(length (w state))
    98883
    ACL2 !>(make-event
            (list 'defconst '*world-length* (length (w state))))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFCONST *WORLD-LENGTH* ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    Summary
    Form:  ( MAKE-EVENT (LIST ...))
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.01 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.01)
     *WORLD-LENGTH*
    ACL2 !>*world-length*
    98883
    ACL2 !>(length (w state))
    98890
    ACL2 !>

  How did this work?  First, evaluation of the form [47m(list 'defconst
  '*world-length* (length (w state)))[0m returned the event form
  [47m(defconst *world-length* 98883)[0m.  Then that event form was
  automatically submitted to ACL2.  Of course, that changed the ACL2
  logical [world], which is why the final value of [47m(length (w state))[0m
  is greater than its initial value.

  The example above illustrates how the evaluation of a [47mmake-event[0m call
  takes place in two phases.  The first phase evaluates the argument
  of the call, in this case [47m(list 'defconst '*world-length* (length
  (w state)))[0m, to compute an event form, in this case [47m(defconst
  *world-length* 98883)[0m.  We call this evaluation the ``expansion''
  phase.  Then the resulting event form is evaluated, which in this
  case defines the constant [47m*world-length*[0m.

  Now suppose we would like to introduce such a [47m[defconst][0m form any
  time we like.  It is common practice to define macros to automate
  such tasks.  Now we might be tempted simply to make the following
  definition.

    ; WRONG!
    (defmacro define-world-length-constant (name state)
      (list 'defconst name (length (w state))))

  But ACL2 rejects such a definition, because the formal parameter
  [47m\"STATE\"[0m is bound to the syntactic object in the macro call, not to
  the actual ACL2 [state]; see [defmacro].  You can try to experiment
  with other such direct methods to define a macro that accesses the
  ACL2 state, but they won't work.

  Instead, however, you can use the approach illustrated by the
  [47mmake-event[0m example above to define the desired macro, as follows.

    (defmacro define-world-length-constant (name)
      `(make-event (list 'defconst ',name (length (w state)))))

  Here is a log that may help to explain this macro, assuming it has
  been defined as displayed just above.

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (define-world-length-constant *foo*)
     (MAKE-EVENT (LIST 'DEFCONST
                       '*FOO*
                       (LENGTH (W STATE))))
    ACL2 !>(LIST 'DEFCONST
                 '*FOO*
                 (LENGTH (W STATE)))
    (DEFCONST *FOO* 109707)
    ACL2 !>
    ACL2 !>(define-world-length-constant *foo*)

