  The expansion phase can be used for computation that has side
  effects, generally by modifying state.  Here is a modification of
  the above example that does not change the ACL2 world at all, but
  instead saves the length of the world into a state global variable
  (see [assign]).

    (make-event
     (er-progn (assign my-world-length (length (w state)))
               (value '(value-triple nil))))

  Notice that this time, the value returned by the expansion phase is
  not a single value; rather, it is an [error-triple] whose value
  component is an event form, namely, the event form [47m(value-triple
  nil)[0m.  Evaluation of that event form does not change the ACL2 world
  (see [value-triple]).  Thus, the sole purpose of the [47mmake-event[0m
  call above is to change the [state] by associating the length of
  the current logical world with the state global, [47mmy-world-length[0m.
  After evaluating this form, [47m(@ my-world-length)[0m provides the length
  of the ACL2 world, as illustrated by the following transcript.

    ACL2 !>:pbt 0
               0:x(EXIT-BOOT-STRAP-MODE)
    ACL2 !>(length (w state))
    109700
    ACL2 !>(make-event
                (er-progn (assign my-world-length (length (w state)))
                          (value '(value-triple nil))))

    Summary
    Form:  ( MAKE-EVENT (ER-PROGN ...))
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.01 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.01)
     NIL
    ACL2 !>(length (w state))
    109700
    ACL2 !>:pbt 0
               0:x(EXIT-BOOT-STRAP-MODE)
    ACL2 !>

  When [47mmake-event[0m is invoked by a book, it is expanded during book
  certification but not, by default, when the book is included.
  Consider again the example [47m(define-world-length-constant *foo*)[0m
  given above.  If that form is in a book, then the value of [47m*foo*[0m
  will be the length of the world at the time this form was invoked
  during book certification, regardless of world length at
  [47m[include-book][0m time.  That is because the expansion, [47m(DEFCONST
  *FOO* 109700)[0m, is recorded in the book's [certificate] and re-used
  during a subsequent [47m[include-book][0m.

  However, the keyword [47m:check-expansion[0m may be given the value [47mt[0m so
  that the expansion is done even during [47minclude-book[0m, which comes
  with a check that the result is the same as it was during book
  certification.  This keyword can be useful for side effects.  For
  example, if you insert the following form in a book, then the
  length of the world will be printed when the form is encountered,
  whether during [47m[certify-book][0m or during [47m[include-book][0m.

    (make-event
     (pprogn (fms \"Length of current world: ~x0~|\"
                  (list (cons #\\0 (length (w state))))
                  *standard-co* state nil)
             (value '(value-triple nil)))
     :check-expansion t)


Detailed Documentation

    Examples:

    ; Trivial example: evaluate (quote (defun foo (x) x)) to obtain
    ; (defun foo (x) x), which is then evaluated.
    (make-event (quote (defun foo (x) x)))

    ; Evaluate (generate-form state) to obtain (mv nil val state), and
    ; then evaluate val.  (Generate-form is not specified here, but
    ; imagine for example that it explores the state and then generates
    ; some desired definition or theorem.)
    (make-event (generate-form state))

    ; As above, but make sure that if this form is in a book, then when
    ; we include the book, the evaluation of (generate-form state)
    ; should return the same value as it did when the book was
    ; certified.
    (make-event (generate-form state)
                :CHECK-EXPANSION t)

    General Form:
    (make-event form
                :CHECK-EXPANSION chk
                :ON-BEHALF-OF obj
                :EXPANSION? form
                :SAVE-EVENT-DATA save)

  where [47mchk[0m is [47mnil[0m (the default), [47mt[0m, or the intended ``expansion
  result'' from the evaluation of [47mform[0m (as explained below); [47mobj[0m is
  an arbitrary ACL2 object, used only in reporting errors in
  expansion, i.e., in the evaluation of form; and [47msave[0m is arbitrary
  but is considered only as either [47mnil[0m or non-[47mnil[0m.  The [47m:EXPANSION?[0m
  keyword is discussed in the final section, on Advanced Expansion
  Control.  See [make-event-details] for discussion of the
  [47m:ON-BEHALF-OF[0m and :SAVE-EVENT-DATA keywords.

  We strongly recommend that you browse some [47m.lisp[0m files in the
  community books directory [47mbooks/make-event/[0m.  You may even find it
  helpful, in order to understand [47mmake-event[0m, to do so before
  continuing to read this documentation.  You may also find it useful
  to browse community book [47mbooks/std/testing/eval.lisp[0m, which
  contains definitions of macros [47mmust-succeed[0m and [47mmust-fail[0m that are
  useful for testing and are used in many books in the
  [47mbooks/make-event/[0m directory, especially [47meval-tests.lisp[0m.  Another
  example, [47mbooks/make-event/defrule.lisp[0m, shows how to use macros
  whose calls expand to [47mmake-event[0m forms, which in turn can generate
  [events].  For more examples, see file [47mbooks/make-event/Readme.lsp[0m.
  Other than the examples, the explanations here should suffice for
  most users.  If you want explanations of subtler details, see
  [make-event-details].

  Note that [47mmake-event[0m is generally legal only where an embedded event
  form is expected: essentially, at the top level of a book or the
  read-eval-print loop, possibly within surrounding calls of
  [47mmake-event[0m or of event constructors such as [47m[progn][0m and
  [47m[encapsulate][0m.  For details see the section ``Restriction to Event
  Contexts'', below.

  [47mMake-event[0m is related to Lisp macroexpansion in the sense that its
  argument is evaluated to obtain an expansion result, which is
  evaluated again.  Let us elaborate on each of these notions in
  turn: ``is evaluated,'' ``expansion result'', and ``evaluated
  again.'' The final section, on Advanced Expansion Control, will
  generalize these processes in a way that we ignore for now.

      ``is evaluated'' --- The argument can be any expression, which is
      evaluated as would be any expression submitted to ACL2's top
      level loop.  Thus, [47m[state][0m and user-defined [47m[stobj][0ms may appear
      in the form supplied to [47mmake-event[0m.  Henceforth, we will refer
      to this evaluation as ``expansion.'' Expansion is actually done
      in a way that restores ACL2's built-in [47m[state][0m global
      variables, including the logical [world], to their
      pre-expansion values (with a few exceptions --- see
      [make-event-details] --- and where we note that changes to
      user-defined [47m[state][0m global variables (see [assign]) are
      preserved).  So, for example, events might be evaluated during
      expansion, but they will disappear from the logical [world]
      after expansion returns its result.  Moreover, proofs are
      enabled by default during expansion (see [ld-skip-proofsp]) if
      keyword [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m is supplied a non-[47mnil[0m value or a
      certain attachment is made (see (4) below).

      ``expansion result'' --- The above expansion may result in an
      ordinary (non-[47m[state][0m, non-[47m[stobj][0m) value, which we call the
      ``expansion result.'' Or, expansion may result in a multiple
      value of the form [47m(mv erp val state)[0m, or, more generally, [47m(mv
      erp val state stobj-1 ... stobj-k)[0m where each [47mstobj-i[0m is a
      [stobj]; then the expansion result is [47mval[0m unless [47merp[0m is not
      [47mnil[0m, in which case there is no expansion result, and the
      original [47mmake-event[0m evaluates to a soft error.  In either case
      (single or multiple value), either [47mval[0m is an embedded event
      form (see [embedded-event-form]), or else the original
      [47mmake-event[0m evaluates to a soft error, printed as described
      under ``Error Reporting'' below.
      ([3mTechnical remark[0m: The expansion result described above may be
      modified for [47m[include-book][0m, [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m, and
      [47m[add-include-book-dir!][0m, replacing [book-name]s to indicate
      [full-book-name]s.  For further details see comments in source
      function [47mmake-include-books-absolute[0m.  End of technical
      remark.)

      ``evaluated again'' --- the expansion result is evaluated in place of
      the original [47mmake-event[0m.

  The expansion process can invoke subsidiary calls of [47mmake-event[0m, and
  the expansion result can (perhaps after macroexpansion) be a call
  of [47mmake-event[0m.  It can be useful to track all these [47mmake-event[0m
  calls.  The [state] global variable [47mmake-event-debug[0m may be set to
  a non-[47mnil[0m value, for example [47m(assign make-event-debug t)[0m, in order
  to see a trace of the expansion process, where a level is displayed
  (as in ``[47m3>[0m'') to indicate the depth of subsidiary expansions.

  Expansion of a [47mmake-event[0m call will yield an event that replaces the
  original [47mmake-event[0m call.  In particular, if you put a [47mmake-event[0m
  form in a book, then in essence it is replaced by its expansion
  result, created during the proof pass of the [47m[certify-book][0m
  process.  We now elaborate on this idea of keeping the original
  expansion.

  A [47mmake-event[0m call generates a ``[47mmake-event[0m replacement'' that may be
  stored by the system.  In the simplest case, this replacement is
  the expansion result.  When a book is certified, these replacements
  are stored in a book's certificate (technically, in the
  [47m:EXPANSION-ALIST[0m field).  Thus, although the book is not textually
  altered during certification, one may imagine a ``book expansion''
  corresponding to the original book, in which events are substituted
  by replacements that were generated during the proof phase of
  certification.  A subsequent [47m[include-book][0m will then include the
  book expansion corresponding to the indicated book.  When a book is
  compiled during [47m[certify-book][0m, it is actually the corresponding
  book expansion, stored as a temporary file, that is compiled
  instead.  That temporary file is deleted after compilation unless
  one first evaluates the form [47m(assign keep-tmp-files t)[0m.  Note
  however that all of the original forms must still be legal
  [events]; see [embedded-event-form].  So for example, if the first
  event in a book is [47m(local (defmacro my-id (x) x))[0m, and is followed
  by [47m(my-id (make-event ...))[0m, the final ``[47minclude-book[0m'' pass of
  [47m[certify-book][0m will fail because [47mmy-id[0m is not defined when the
  [47mmy-id[0m call is encountered.

  A [47mmake-event[0m replacement might not be the expansion when either of
  the keyword arguments [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m or [47m:EXPANSION?[0m is supplied.
  We deal with the latter in the final section, on Advanced Expansion
  Control.  If [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION t[0m is supplied and the expansion is
  [47mexp[0m, then the replacement is obtained from the original [47mmake-event[0m
  call, by substituting [47mexp[0m for [47mt[0m as the value of keyword
  [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m.  Such a [47mmake-event[0m call --- during the second
  pass of an [47m[encapsulate][0m, or during event processing on behalf of
  [47m[include-book][0m other than when including a book near the end of its
  certification process --- will do the expansion again and check
  that the expansion result is equal to the original expansion
  result, [47mexp[0m.  In the unusual case that you know the expected
  expansion result, [47mres[0m, you can specify [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION res[0m in the
  first place, so that the check is also done during the initial
  evaluation of the [47mmake-event[0m form.  IMPORTANT BUT OBSCURE DETAIL:
  That expansion check is only done when processing events, not
  during a preliminary load of a book's compiled file.  The following
  paragraph elaborates.

  (Here are details on the point made just above, for those who use the
  [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m argument to perform side-effects on the [state].
  When you include a book, ACL2 generally loads a compiled file
  before processing the events in the book; see [book-compiled-file].
  While it is true that a non-[47mnil[0m [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m argument causes
  [47m[include-book][0m to perform expansion of the [47mmake-event[0m form during
  event processing it does [3mnot[0m perform expansion when the compiled
  file (or expansion file; again, see [book-compiled-file]) is
  loaded.)

  ACL2 performs the following space-saving optimization: when the
  expansion result is a [47m[local][0m event, then the [47mmake-event[0m
  replacement is [47m(local (value-triple :ELIDED))[0m.

  The notion of ``expansion'' and ``replacement'' extend to the case
  that a call of [47mmake-event[0m is found in the course of macroexpansion.
  The following example illustrates this point.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (defmacro my-mac ()
       '(make-event '(defun foo (x) x)))
     (my-mac))
    :pe :here

  The above call of [47m[pe][0m shows that the form [47m(my-mac)[0m has a [47mmake-event[0m
  expansion (and replacement) of [47m(DEFUN FOO (X) X)[0m:

    (ENCAPSULATE NIL
                 (DEFMACRO MY-MAC
                           NIL
                           '(MAKE-EVENT '(DEFUN FOO (X) X)))
                 (DEFUN FOO (X) X))


Error Reporting

  Suppose that expansion produces a soft error as described above.
  That is, suppose that the argument of a [47mmake-event[0m call evaluates
  to a multiple value [47m(mv erp val state ...)[0m where [47merp[0m is not [47mnil[0m.
  If [47merp[0m is a string, then that string is printed in the error
  message.  If [47merp[0m is a [47m[cons][0m pair whose [47m[car][0m is a string, then the
  error prints [47m\"~@0\"[0m with [47m#\\0[0m bound to that [47mcons[0m pair; see [fmt].
  Any other non-[47mnil[0m value of [47merp[0m causes a generic error message to be
  printed.


Restriction to Event Contexts

  A [47mmake-event[0m call must occur either at the top level, or during
  [47mmake-event[0m expansion, or as an argument of an event constructor.
  We explain in more detail below.  This restriction is imposed to
  enable ACL2 to track expansions produced by [47mmake-event[0m.

  The following examples illustrate this restriction.

    ; Legal:
    (progn (with-output
            :on summary
            (make-event '(defun foo (x) x))))

    ; Illegal:
    (mv-let (erp val state)
            (make-event '(defun foo (x) x))
            (mv erp val state))

  More precisely: a [47mmake-event[0m call that is not itself evaluated during
  [47mmake-event[0m expansion is subject to the following requirement.
  After macroexpansion has taken place, such a [47mmake-event[0m call must
  be in an ``event context'', defined recursively as follows.  (All
  but the first two cases below correspond to similar cases for
  constructing events; see [embedded-event-form].)

    * A form submitted at the top level, or more generally, supplied to a
      call of [47m[ld][0m, is in an event context.

    * A form occurring at the top level of a book is in an event context.

    * If [47m([0m[47m[local][0m[47m x1)[0m is in an event context, then so is [47mx1[0m.

    * If [47m([0m[47m[skip-proofs][0m[47m x1)[0m is in an event context, then so is [47mx1[0m.

    * If [47m([0m[47m[make-event][0m[47m x ...)[0m is in an event context and its expansion [47mx1[0m
      is an embedded event form, then [47mx1[0m is in an event context.

    * If [47m([0m[47m[with-output][0m[47m ... x1)[0m, [47m([0m[47m[with-prover-step-limit][0m[47m ... x1 ...)[0m, or
      [47m([0m[47m[with-prover-time-limit][0m[47m ... x1)[0m is in an event context, then
      so is [47mx1[0m.

    * For any call of [47m[progn][0m or [47m[progn!][0m, each of its arguments is in an
      event context.

    * For any call of [47m[encapsulate][0m, each of its arguments except the first
      (the signature list) is in an event context.

    * If [47m(RECORD-EXPANSION x1 x2)[0m is in an event context, then [47mx1[0m and [47mx2[0m
      are in event contexts.  Note: [47mrecord-expansion[0m is intended for
      use only by the implementation.

  Low-level remark, for system implementors.  There is the one
  exception to the above restriction: a single [47m[state-global-let*][0m
  form immediately under a [47mprogn![0m call.  For example:

    (progn! (state-global-let* <bindings> (make-event ...)))

  However, the following form may be preferable (see [progn!]):

    (progn! :STATE-GLOBAL-BINDINGS <bindings> (make-event ...))

  Also see [remove-untouchable] for an interesting use of this
  exception.


Avoiding large [47mmake-event[0m forms in [certificate] files

  The [certificate] file for a book contains expansions of [47mmake-event[0m
  forms from the book.  (Those interested may find details about this
  in an Implementation Note about ``The book expansion'' in the
  documentation topic, [make-event-details].)  Those expansions can
  be very large if one is not careful.  Consider the difference
  between the following two events.

    (make-event
     `(defconst *foo* ,(length (w state))))

    (make-event
     `(defconst *foo* (length ',(w state))))

  The first generates an expansion such as [47m(defconst *foo* 122700)[0m
  (where the numeric value depends on the [world] in which the
  [47mmake-event[0m form is evaluated).  The second, however, generates an
  expansion of the form [47m(defconst *foo* (length '<wrld>))[0m, where
  [47m<wrld[0m is an ACL2 world --- a very large structure.  The [47m.cert[0m file
  for a book containing the second form will therefore contain many
  megabytes.  Moreover, with the second form the length of that world
  will need to be computed when the book is included (which may be
  fast, but could be slow for a different such computation).


Examples Illustrating How to Access State

  You can modify the ACL2 [state] by doing your state-changing
  computation during the expansion phase, before expansion returns
  the event that is submitted.  Let us look at some examples and then
  consider a restriction for many built-in state globals.

  First consider the following.  Notice that expansion modifies a state
  global, [47mmy-global[0m, during [47mmake-event[0m expansion (see [assign]); and
  then, expansion returns a [47m[defun][0m event to be evaluated.

    (make-event
      (er-progn (assign my-global (length (w state)))
                (value '(defun foo (x) (cons x x)))))

  Then we get:

    ACL2 !>(@ my-global)
    72271
    ACL2 !>:pe foo
     L        1:x(MAKE-EVENT (ER-PROGN # #))

    >L            (DEFUN FOO (X) (CONS X X))
    ACL2 !>

  Here's a slightly fancier example, where the computation affects the
  [47m[defun][0m.  In a new session, execute:

    (make-event
      (er-progn (assign my-global (length (w state)))
                (value `(defun foo (x) (cons x ,(@ my-global))))))

  Then:

    ACL2 !>(@ my-global)
    72271
    ACL2 !>:pe foo
     L        1:x(MAKE-EVENT (ER-PROGN # #))

    >L            (DEFUN FOO (X) (CONS X 72271))
    ACL2 !>

  Because [47mmake-event[0m creates [event] forms that can go into [books] and
  [47m[encapsulate][0m events, you can use [47mmake-event[0m forms such as those
  above to modify the ACL2 [state] using books and [47mencapsulate[0m
  events.  The most common way to do this is for the [47mmake-event[0m
  expansion to be a trivial event form such as [47m(value-triple nil)[0m,
  such as the following.

    (make-event (er-progn (assign my-list-of-10 (make-list 10))
                          (value '(value-triple nil))))

  The desired effect will take place during calls of [47m[certify-book][0m,
  but it will generally [31;1mnot[0m take place during [47m[include-book][0m on a
  certified book because the expansion is evaluated (it is stored in
  the book's [certificate] for this purpose), but the expansion is
  merely [47m(value-triple nil)[0m.  To ensure that the original [47mmake-event[0m
  call is evaluated even when including the book, use
  [47m:check-expansion t[0m, for example as follows.

    (make-event (er-progn (assign my-list-of-10 (make-list 10))
                          (value '(value-triple nil)))
                :check-expansion t)

  Note that ACL2 [table] [events] may avoid the need to use [state]
  globals.  For example, instead of the example above, consider this
  example in a new session.

    (make-event
      (let ((world-len (length (w state))))
        `(progn (table my-table :STORED-WORLD-LENGTH ,world-len)
                (defun foo (x) (cons x ,world-len)))))

  Then:

    ACL2 !>(table my-table)
     ((:STORED-WORLD-LENGTH . 72271))
    ACL2 !>:pe foo
              1:x(MAKE-EVENT (LET # #))

    >L            (DEFUN FOO (X) (CONS X 72271))
    ACL2 !>

  Many built-in [state] globals revert after expansion.  If your own
  state global (like [47mmy-global[0m above) can be set during expansion,
  then the new value will persist.  But that persistence will fail
  for many state globals, specifically, those that are stored in the
  list, [47m*protected-system-state-globals*[0m.  We advise users [31;1mnot[0m to
  assume that system state modifications, such as the state of
  guard-checking, will persist after executing a [47mmake-event[0m form;
  persistence depends on the relevant state globals not being in the
  list, [47m*protected-system-state-globals*[0m.

  That advice may suffice for most users.  But if you want to
  understand the point above more deeply, then consider the following
  example.

    (make-event
     (er-progn (set-guard-checking :none) ; sets state global 'guard-checking-on
               (assign my-global (car 3))
               (value (list 'value-triple (@ my-global)))))

  This [47mmake-event[0m form succeeds and afterwards, the value of [47m(@
  my-global)[0m is [47mnil[0m, which of course is the value expected after the
  call above of [47m[assign][0m.  However, the value of [47m(@
  guard-checking-on)[0m after executing this [47mmake-event[0m form is [47mt[0m, not
  [47m:none[0m.  That is because the symbol [47mguard-checking-on[0m belongs to the
  list of symbols stored in the constant,
  [47m*protected-system-state-globals*[0m, and thus the value of the state
  global [47mguard-checking-on[0m is reverted to its initial value (which
  was assume here is the default, [47mt[0m) after the [47mmake-event[0m form
  completes execution.

  [31;1mRemarks on [47m*Protected-system-state-globals*[0m[31;1m for advanced users.[0m

    * The constant [47m*protected-system-state-globals*[0m is defined to include
      all built-in state globals, [3mexcept[0m for those that the ACL2
      implementors have decided can safely retain values set during
      [47mmake-event[0m expansion.  If you find state globals that you would
      like to be added to this list of exceptions, please contact the
      ACL2 implementors.  Note for example that it would not be safe
      to allow [47mguard-checking-on[0m as an exception, since ACL2 relies
      on the persistence of this state global's value after each
      event (for explanation, see the Essay on Guard Checking in the
      ACL2 sources).

    * As noted in a comment in the example above, the macro
      [47m[set-guard-checking][0m sets the state global, [47m'guard-checking-on[0m.
      A natural question is how one might know this.  For most ACL2
      users (one might say, traditional ACL2 users), it should
      suffice simply not to expect system state modifications (like
      the state of guard-checking) to persist after executing a
      [47mmake-event[0m form.  For system implementors, one however needs to
      work with the ACL2 system by looking at the definition of the
      utility --- in this example, [47mset-guard-checking[0m --- or at the
      least, get a sense of its expansion by using macroexpansion
      (for example see [trans1]).


Advanced Expansion Control

  We conclude this [documentation] section by discussing additional
  control over [47mmake-event[0m expansion.  The discussion below is split
  into the following parts; further discussion follows.  The first
  three parts are illustrated in community book
  [47mbooks/make-event/make-event-keywords-or-exp.lisp[0m.

  (1) The value produced by expansion may have the form [47m(:DO-PROOFS
  exp)[0m, which specifies [47mexp[0m as the expansion result, to be evaluated
  without skipping proofs even when including a book.

  (2) The value produced by expansion may have the form [47m(:OR exp-1 ...
  exp-k)[0m, which specifies that the first form [47mexp-i[0m to evaluate
  without error is the expansion result.

  (3) The keyword argument [47m:EXPANSION?[0m can serve to eliminate the
  storing of [47mmake-event[0m replacements, as described above for the
  ``book expansion'' of a book.

  (4) In contexts where proofs are normally skipped (see
  [ld-skip-proofsp]), the expansion phase normally takes place with
  proofs skipped unless the [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m keyword is supplied a
  non-[47mnil[0m value.  However, proofs can be made to take place
  unconditionally during the expansion phase by using an attachment,
  as discussed below.

  We now elaborate on each of these.

  (1) [47m:DO-PROOFS[0m ``call'' produced by expansion.

  We have discussed the expansion result produced by the expansion
  phase of evaluating a [47mmake-event[0m call.  However, if the expansion
  phase produces an expression of the form [47m(:DO-PROOFS exp)[0m, then the
  expansion result is actually [47mexp[0m.  The [47m:DO-PROOFS[0m wrapper indicates
  that even if proofs are currently being skipped (see
  [ld-skip-proofsp]), then evaluation of [47mexp[0m should take place with
  proofs not skipped.  For example, proofs will be performed when
  evaluating the [47mmake-event[0m expansion, namely the indicated [47mdefthm[0m
  event, in the following example.

    (set-ld-skip-proofsp t state)
    (make-event '(:DO-PROOFS
                  (defthm app-assoc (equal
                                     (append (append x y) z)
                                     (append x y z)))))

  Note that such use of [47m:DO-PROOFS[0m causes proofs to be performed when
  evaluating the expansion while including an uncertified book.  But
  when including a certified book, then unless [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m is
  supplied a non-[47mnil[0m value, the [47mmake-event[0m replacement will just be
  the expansion, which does not include the [47m:DO-PROOFS[0m wrapper and
  hence will be evaluated with proofs skipped.

  (2) [47m:OR[0m ``call'' produced by expansion.

  There may be times where you want to try different expansions.  For
  example, the community book [47mbooks/make-event/proof-by-arith.lisp[0m
  attempts to admit a given event, which we'll denote [47mEV[0m, by trying
  events of the following form as [47mBOOK[0m varies over different
  community books.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (local (include-book BOOK :DIR :SYSTEM))
     EV)

  A naive implementation of this macro would evaluate all such
  [47m[encapsulate][0m events until one succeeds, and then return that
  successful event as the expansion.  Then that event would need to
  be evaluated again!  With some hacking one could avoid that
  re-evaluation by using [47m[skip-proofs][0m, but that won't work if you
  are trying to create a certified book without skipped proofs.
  Instead, the implementation creates an expansion of the form [47m(:OR
  ev-1 ev-2 ... ev-k)[0m, where the list [47m(ev-1 ev-2 ... ev-k)[0m enumerates
  the generated encapsulate events.  In general, for this
  ``disjunctive case'' of a result from expansion, each [47mev-i[0m is
  evaluated in sequence, and the first that succeeds without error is
  considered to be the expansion result --- and a repeat evaluation
  is avoided.  If evaluation of each [47mev-i[0m results in an error, then
  so does the [47mmake-event[0m call.

  This special use of [47m:OR[0m in a value produced by expansion does not
  permit nesting such as [47m(:OR ev-1 (:OR ev-2 ev-3))[0m.  That is, when
  an expansion result is [47m(:OR ev-1 ev-2 ... ev-k)[0m, none of the [47mev-i[0m
  may be of the form [47m(:OR ...)[0m.  Note that it is allowed for [47mev-i[0m to
  be a call of [47mmake-event[0m, even one involving this special use of
  [47m:OR[0m.  If [47mev-i[0m is [47m(:DO-PROOFS ev-i')[0m, then [47mev-i'[0m is considered to be
  the expansion in place of [47mev-i[0m.

  (3) The [47m:EXPANSION?[0m keyword argument.

  If keyword argument [47m:EXPANSION?[0m has a non[47mnil[0m value, then the
  [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m keyword must be omitted or have value [47mnil[0m or [47mt[0m,
  hence not a cons pair.

  The idea of the [47m:EXPANSION?[0m keyword is to give you a way to avoid
  storing expansion results in a book's [certificate].  Roughly
  speaking, when the expansion result matches the value of
  [47m:EXPANSION?[0m, then no expansion result is stored for the event by
  book certification; then when the book is later included, the value
  of [47m:EXPANSION?[0m is used as the expansion, thus bypassing the
  expansion phase.  One could say that the event is its own
  make-event replacement, but it is more accurate to say that there
  is no make-event replacement at all, since nothing is stored in the
  certificate for this event.  Below, we elaborate on make-event
  replacements when [47m:EXPANSION?[0m is used and also discuss other
  properties of this keyword.

  We modify the notion of ``expansion result'' for [47mmake-event[0m forms to
  comprehend the use of the [47m:EXPANSION?[0m keyword.  For that purpose,
  let's consider a call of [47mmake-event[0m to be ``reducible'' if it has
  an [47m:EXPANSION?[0m keyword with non-[47mnil[0m value, [47mexp[0m, and its
  [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m keyword is missing or has value [47mnil[0m, in which case
  the ``reduction'' of this [47mmake-event[0m call is defined to be [47mexp[0m.
  The expansion result as originally defined is modified by the
  following ``recursive reduction'' process: recur through the
  original expansion, passing through calls of [47m[local][0m,
  [47m[skip-proofs][0m, [47m[with-output][0m, [47m[with-prover-step-limit][0m, and
  [47m[with-prover-time-limit][0m, and replacing (recursively) any reducible
  call of [47mmake-event[0m by its reduction.  Furthermore, we refer to two
  forms as ``reduction equivalent'' if their recursive reductions are
  equal.  Note that the recursive reduction process does not pass
  through [47m[progn][0m or [47m[encapsulate][0m, but that process is applied to
  the computation of expansions for their subsidiary [47m[make-event][0m
  calls.

  To explain further the effect of [47m:EXPANSION? exp[0m, we split into the
  following two cases.

  [31;1mCase 1[0m: Evaluation is not taking place when including a book or
  evaluating the second pass of an [47m[encapsulate][0m event; more
  precisely, the value of [47m(ld-skip-proofsp state)[0m is not the symbol
  [47mINCLUDE-BOOK[0m.  There are two subcases.

    * Case 1a: The expansion result is not reduction-equivalent to [47mexp[0m.
      Then the [47mmake-event[0m call is processed as though the [47m:EXPANSION?[0m
      keyword had been omitted.

    * Case 2a: The expansion result is reduction-equivalent to [47mexp[0m.  Then
      there is no [47mmake-event[0m replacement for this call of [47mmake-event[0m;
      no replacement will be put into the [certificate] file for a
      book containing this [47mmake-event[0m call.  When that book is
      subsequently included, the original form will be evaluated in
      the manner described in the next case.

  [31;1mCase 2[0m: Evaluation is taking place when including a book or
  evaluating the second pass of an [47m[encapsulate][0m event; more
  precisely, the value of [47m(ld-skip-proofsp state)[0m is the symbol
  [47mINCLUDE-BOOK[0m.  Then the expansion is [47mexp[0m.  The expansion phase is
  skipped unless [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m is [47mt[0m.

  The [47m:EXPANSION?[0m keyword can be particularly useful in concert with
  the disjunctive (``[47m:OR[0m'') case (2) discussed above.  Suppose that
  expansion produces a value as discussed in (2) above, [47m(:OR exp-1
  ... exp-k)[0m.  If one of these expressions [47mexp-i[0m is more likely than
  the others to be the expansion, then you may wish to specify
  [47m:EXPANSION? exp-i[0m, as this will avoid storing a [47mmake-event[0m
  replacement in that common case.  This could be useful if the
  expressions are large, to avoid enlarging the [certificate] file
  for a book containing the [47mmake-event[0m call.

  It is legal to specify both [47m:EXPANSION? exp[0m and [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION t[0m.
  When either [47m(ld-skip-proofsp state)[0m is the symbol [47mINCLUDE-BOOK[0m, or
  evaluation is taking place in raw Lisp, then this combination is
  treated the same as if [47m:EXPANSION?[0m is omitted and the value of
  [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m is [47mexp[0m.  Otherwise, this combination is treated
  the same as [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION t[0m, modified to accommodate the effect
  of [47m:EXPANSION?[0m as discussed above: if the expansion is indeed the
  value of [47m:EXPANSION?[0m, then no [47mmake-event[0m replacement is generated.

  (4) Unconditionally enabling proofs during the expansion phase using
  an attachment.

  Proofs are normally skipped during the expansion phase unless the
  [47m:CHECK-EXPANSION[0m keyword is supplied a non-[47mnil[0m value.  To cause
  proofs to take place unconditionally during the expansion phase,
  evaluate one of the following two forms, as explained below.

    (defattach (always-do-proofs-during-make-event-expansion
                constant-t-function-arity-0)
      :system-ok t)

    (defattach (always-do-proofs-during-make-event-expansion
                constant-all-function-arity-0)
      :system-ok t)

  The first of these is probably preferred in most cases, since it does
  not have any effect while evaluating an [47m[include-book][0m form (more
  precisely, when [47m(ld-skip-proofsp state)[0m is [47m'include-book[0m).  The
  second removes that restriction.

  To restore the default behavior, evaluate the following.

    (defattach (always-do-proofs-during-make-event-expansion
                constant-nil-function-arity-0)
      :system-ok t)

  (If you would like an explanation of [47mdefattach[0m in general, see
  [defattach].)


