 (MAKE-EVENT-DETAILS
  (MAKE-EVENT)
  "Details on [47m[make-event][0m expansion

  The normal user of [47mmake-event[0m can probably ignore this section, but
  we include it for completeness.  We assume that the reader has read
  and understood the basic documentation for [47mmake-event[0m (see
  [make-event]), but we begin below with a summary of expansion.


Introduction

  Here is a summary of how we handle expansion involving [47mmake-event[0m
  forms.

  [47m(make-event form :check-expansion nil)[0m

  This shows the [47m:check-expansion[0m default of [47mnil[0m, and is typical user
  input.  We compute the expansion [47mexp[0m of [47mform[0m, which is the
  expansion of the original [47mmake-event[0m expression and is evaluated in
  place of that expression.

  [47m(make-event form :check-expansion t)[0m

  The user presumably wants it checked that the expansion doesn't
  change in the future, in particular during [47m[include-book][0m.  If the
  expansion of [47mform[0m is [47mexp[0m, then we will evaluate [47mexp[0m to obtain the
  value as before, but this time we record that the expansion of the
  original [47mmake-event[0m expression is [47m(make-event form :check-expansion
  exp)[0m rather than simply [47mexp[0m.

  [47m(make-event form :check-expansion exp) ; exp a cons[0m

  This is generated for the case that [47m:check-expansion[0m is [47mt[0m, as
  explained above.  Evaluation is handled as described in that above
  case, except here we check that the expansion result is the given
  [47mexp[0m.  (Actually, the user is also allowed to supply such a form.)
  The original [47mmake-event[0m expression does not undergo any expansion
  (intuitively, it expands to itself).

  Now let us take a brief look at how we expand [47m[progn][0m and
  [47m[encapsulate][0m forms.  More details are found further below (see
  ``Detailed semantics'').

  [47m(progn ... (make-event form ...) ...)[0m

  The expansion is obtained, roughly speaking, by replacing the
  [47mmake-event[0m form by its expansion, [47mexp[0m, except that if
  [47m:check-expansion exp[0m is supplied explicitly, then no such
  replacement takes place.

  Expansion for [47m(encapsulate ... (make-event form ...) ...)[0m is similar
  to the case for [47mprogn[0m, except that if the expansion of [47mform[0m is [47mexp[0m,
  then what is stored is [47m(record-expansion (make-event form ...)
  exp)[0m.  Also as for [47mprogn[0m, the exception is that when
  [47m:check-expansion exp[0m is supplied explicitly, no such replacement
  takes place.  Here, [47mrecord-expansion[0m is a macro that simply returns
  its second argument, but is used for checking redundancy of
  [47mencapsulate[0m forms (see [redundant-encapsulate]).

  Certain ``wrappers'' around a [47mmake-event[0m are restored as the last
  part of the expansion process, in particular for [47mmake-event[0m calls
  that are inside calls of [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[progn][0m, as well as in
  events processed during a book's certification, including
  [portcullis] commands from the certification [world].  For example,
  if you evaluate a form [47m(local (with-prover-step-limit 100
  (make-event ...)))[0m where the [47mmake-event[0m call has expansion [47m<exp>[0m,
  and then you certify a book, the book's [certificate] file will
  include among its portcullis commands the form [47m(local
  (with-prover-step-limit 100 <exp>))[0m.  Macroexpansion is performed
  to determine the wrappers.  The wrappers thus restored are as
  follows.

    local
    skip-proofs
    with-cbd
    with-guard-checking-event
    with-output
    with-prover-step-limit
    with-prover-time-limit

  The discussion below references this process as the final expansion
  being ``rebuilt from'' the form.


Detailed semantics

  In our explanation of the semantics of [47mmake-event[0m, we assume
  familiarity with the notion of ``embedded event form'' (see
  [embedded-event-form]).

  Let's say that the ``actual embedded event form'' corresponding to a
  given form is the underlying call of an ACL2 event: that is,
  [47m[local][0ms are dropped when [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m is [47m'include-book[0m, and
  macros are expanded away, thus leaving us with a [47m[progn][0m, a
  [47m[make-event][0m, or an event form (possibly [47m[encapsulate][0m), any of
  which might have surrounding [47m[local][0m, [47m[skip-proofs][0m, or
  [47m[with-output][0m calls.

  Thus, such an actual embedded event form can be viewed as having the
  form [47m(rebuild-expansion wrappers base-form)[0m where [47mbase-form[0m is a
  [47mprogn[0m, a [47mmake-event[0m, or an event form (possibly [47mencapsulate[0m), and
  [47mwrappers[0m are (as in ACL2 source function [47mdestructure-expansion[0m) the
  result of successively removing the event form from the result of
  macroexpansion, leaving a sequence of [47m(local)[0m, [47m(skip-proofs)[0m, and
  [47m(with-output ...)[0m forms.  In this case we say that the form
  ``destructures into'' the indicated [47mwrappers[0m and [47mbase-form[0m, and
  that it can be ``rebuilt from'' those [47mwrappers[0m and [47mbase-form[0m.

  Elsewhere we define the notion of the ``expansion result'' from an
  evaluation (see [make-event]), and we mention that when expansion
  concludes, the ACL2 logical [world] and most of the [47mstate[0m are
  restored to their pre-expansion values.  Specifically, after
  evaluation of the argument of [47mmake-event[0m (even if it is aborted),
  the ACL2 logical world is restored to its pre-evaluation value, as
  are all state global variables in the list
  [47m*protected-system-state-globals*[0m.  Thus, assignments to
  user-defined state globals (see [assign]) do persist after
  expansion, since they are not in that list.

  We recursively define the combination of evaluation and expansion of
  an embedded event form as shown below.  We also simultaneously
  define the notion of ``expansion takes place,'' which is assumed to
  propagate upward (in a sense that will be obvious), such that if no
  expansion takes place, then the expansion of the given form is
  considered to be itself.  It is useful to keep in mind a goal that
  we will consider later: Every [47mmake-event[0m subterm of an expansion
  result has a [47m:check-expansion[0m field that is a [47m[consp][0m, where for
  this purpose [47mmake-event[0m is viewed as a macro that returns its
  [47m:check-expansion[0m field.

      If the given form is not an embedded event form, then simply cause a
      soft error, [47m(mv erp val state)[0m where [47merp[0m is not [47mnil[0m.
      Otherwise:

      If the evaluation of the given form does not take place (presumably
      because [47m[local][0m events are being skipped), then no expansion
      takes place.  Otherwise:

      Let [47mx[0m be the actual embedded event form corresponding to the given
      form, which destructures into wrappers [47mW[0m and base-form [47mB[0m.  Then
      the original form is evaluated by evaluating [47mx[0m, and its
      expansion is as follows.

      If [47mB[0m is [47m(make-event form :check-expansion val)[0m, then expansion takes
      place if and only if [47mval[0m is not a [47mconsp[0m and no error occurs, as
      now described.  Let [47mR[0m be the expansion result from protected
      evaluation of [47mform[0m, if there is no error.  [47mR[0m must be an
      embedded event form, or it is an error.  Then evaluate/expand
      [47mR[0m, where if [47mval[0m is not [47mnil[0m then state global [47m'ld-skip-proofsp[0m
      is initialized to [47mnil[0m.  (This initialization is important so
      that subsequent expansions are checked in a corresponding
      environment, i.e., where proofs are turned on in both the
      original and subsequent environments.)  It is an error if this
      evaluation causes an error.  Otherwise, the evaluation yields a
      value, which is the result of evaluation of the original
      [47mmake-event[0m expression, as well as an expansion, [47mE_R[0m.  Let [47mE[0m be
      rebuilt from [47mW[0m and [47mE_R[0m.  The expansion of the original form is
      [47mE[0m if [47mval[0m is [47mnil[0m, and otherwise is the result of replacing the
      original form's [47m:check-expansion[0m field with [47mE[0m, with the added
      requirement that if [47mval[0m is not [47mt[0m (thus, a [47mconsp[0m) then [47mE[0m must
      equal [47mval[0m or else we cause an error.

      If [47mB[0m is [47m(progn form1 form2 ...)[0m (and similarly for [47m[progn!][0m), and at
      least one [47mformi[0m has an expansion, then the expansion of the
      original form is obtained by replacing each [47mformi[0m by its
      expansion and then rebuilding the entire [47mprogn[0m call from [47mB[0m.

      If [47mB[0m is [47m(encapsulate sigs form1 form2 ...)[0m, then after evaluating [47mB[0m,
      the expansion of the original form is the result of rebuilding
      from [47mB[0m, with wrappers [47mW[0m, after replacing each [47mformi[0m in [47mB[0m for
      which expansion takes place by [47m(record-expansion formi formi')[0m,
      where [47mformi'[0m is the expansion of [47mformi[0m.  Note that these
      expansions are determined as the [47mformi[0m are evaluated in
      sequence (where in the case of [47mencapsulate[0m, this determination
      occurs only during the first pass).  Except, if no expansion
      takes place for any [47mformi[0m, then the expansion of the original
      form is itself.

      Otherwise, the expansion of the original form is itself.

  (Optional Implementation Notes.  The latest expansion of a
  [47m[make-event][0m, [47m[progn][0m, [47m[progn!][0m, or [47m[encapsulate][0m is temporarily
  stored in state global [47m'last-make-event-expansion[0m, except that if
  no expansion has taken place for that form then
  [47m'last-make-event-expansion[0m has value [47mnil[0m.  Expansions ultimately
  show up in the world's [47m[command][0m tuples; for example, immediately
  after processing a command its expansion is
  [47m(access-command-tuple-last-make-event-expansion (cddr (car (w
  state))))[0m.  Top-level expansions do not include rebuilding of
  wrappers, although such wrappers are restored when constructing
  [portcullis] commands as discussed above.  End of Implementation
  Notes.)

  Similarly to the [47m[progn][0m and [47m[encapsulate][0m cases above, book
  certification causes a book to be replaced by its so-called ``book
  expansion,'' where each event [47mev[0m for which expansion took place
  during the proof pass of certification is replaced by its
  expansion, but with certain [47m[local][0m events elided.

  Optional Implementation Note.  The book expansion is actually
  implemented by way of the [47m:expansion-alist[0m field of its
  [certificate], which associates 0-based positions of top-level
  forms in the book (not including the initial [47m[in-package][0m form)
  with their expansions.  Thus, the book's source file is not
  overwritten; rather, the certificate's expansion-alist is applied
  when the book is included or compiled.  End of Implementation Note.

  It is straightforward by computational induction to see that for any
  expansion of an embedded event form, every [47mmake-event[0m sub-event has
  a [47m[consp][0m [47m:check-expansion[0m field.  Here, by ``sub-event'' we mean
  to expand macros; and we also mean to traverse [47mprogn[0m and
  [47mencapsulate[0m forms as well as [47m:check-expansion[0m fields of [47mmake-event[0m
  forms.  Thus, we will only see [47mmake-event[0m forms with [47mconsp[0m
  [47m:check-expansion[0m fields in the course of [47minclude-book[0m forms, the
  second pass of [47mencapsulate[0m forms, and raw Lisp.  This fact
  guarantees that an event form will always be treated as its
  original expansion.


Notes on ttags

  See [defttag] for documentation of the notion of ``trust tag''
  (``ttag'').  We note here that even if an event [47m(defttag tag-name)[0m
  for non-[47mnil[0m [47mtag-name[0m is admitted only during the expansion phase of
  a [47m[make-event][0m form, then such expansion will nevertheless still
  cause [47mtag-name[0m to be recorded in the logical [world] (assuming that
  the [47mmake-event[0m form is admitted).  So in order to certify such a
  book, a suitable [47m:ttags[0m argument must be supplied; see
  [certify-book].

  ACL2 does provide a way to avoid the need for [47m:ttags[0m arguments in
  such cases.  The idea is to certify a book twice, where the results
  of [47mmake-event[0m expansion are saved from the first call of
  [47m[certify-book][0m and provided to the second call.  See
  [set-write-ACL2x].

  Finally, we discuss a very unusual case where certification does not
  involve trust tags but a subsequent [47m[include-book][0m does involve
  trust tags: a [47mmake-event[0m call specifying [47m:check-expansion t[0m, whose
  expansion generates a [47m[defttag][0m event during [47m[include-book][0m but not
  [47m[certify-book][0m.  Consider the following book.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (make-event
     (er-progn
      (if (@ skip-notify-on-defttag) ; non-nil when including a certified book
          (pprogn
           (fms \"Value of (@ skip-notify-on-defttag): ~x0~|\"
                (list (cons #0 (@ skip-notify-on-defttag)))
                *standard-co* state nil)
           (encapsulate
            ()
            (defttag :foo)
            (value-triple \"Imagine something bad here!\")))
        (value nil))
      (value '(value-triple :some-value)))
     :check-expansion t)

  This book certifies successfully without the need for a [47m:ttags[0m
  argument for [47m[certify-book][0m.  Indeed, the above book's
  [certificate] does not specify [47m:foo[0m as a trust tag associated with
  the book, because no [47mdefttag[0m event was executed during book
  certification.  Unfortunately, if we try to include this book
  without specifying a value of [47m:ttags[0m that allows [47m:foo[0m, book
  inclusion will cause executing of the above [47m[defttag][0m.  If that
  inclusion happens in the context of certifying some superior book
  and the appropriate [47m:ttags[0m arguments have not been provided, that
  certification will fail.


Expansion errors and the [47m:ON-BEHALF-OF[0m keyword argument

  Consider the case that expansion returns an [error-triple] [47m(mv erp
  val state)[0m, where [47merp[0m is not [47mnil[0m.  Then [47mmake-event[0m may conclude
  with an error message, as follows.

    * If keyword [47m:ON-BEHALF-OF[0m has value [47m:quiet![0m then there is no such
      concluding error message.

    * Otherwise, if [47merp[0m is a string, then the concluding error message is
      produced by printing [47merp[0m with the call [47m(er soft ctx erp)[0m, where
      [47mctx[0m is the context (see [ctx]).

    * Otherwise, if [47merp[0m is a message (see [msg]), then the concluding error
      message is produced by the call [47m(er soft ctx \"~@0\" erp)[0m, where
      [47mctx[0m is the context (see [ctx]).

    * Otherwise, if keyword [47m:ON-BEHALF-OF[0m has value [47m:quiet[0m then there is no
      concluding error message.

    * Otherwise, if keyword [47m:ON-BEHALF-OF[0m is not supplied or has value [47mnil[0m
      --- so we are now in the typical case --- then the concluding
      error message is ``Error in MAKE-EVENT from expansion of:''
      followed by the form.

    * Otherwise, keyword [47m:ON-BEHALF-OF[0m has a value [47mx[0m other than [47m:quiet![0m,
      [47m:quiet[0m, or [47mnil[0m, in which case the concluding error message is
      as just above, except that after ``Error in MAKE-EVENT'' is
      stated ``on behalf of'' followed by [47mx[0m.

  Note that errors generated during expansion are not affected by the
  cases above; those only control the concluding error message, if
  any.


The [47m:save-event-data[0m keyword argument

  See [get-event-data] for relevant background on data stored for each
  event.  The association list stored in [47mlast-event-data[0m is normally
  replaced every time an event concludes (at the summary phase), and
  that holds for calls of [47mmake-event[0m.  But there is the following
  exception: when [47mmake-event[0m is called with a non-[47mnil[0m value for
  keyword [47m:save-event-data[0m, then that association list persists from
  the expansion phase.  This is how the [47m[thm][0m macro is able to
  populate the [47mlast-event-data[0m association list without having that
  list be smashed when the surrounding [47mmake-event[0m (used for
  implementing [47mthm[0m) concludes.")
 (MAKE-EVENT-EXAMPLE-1
  (MAKE-EVENT)
  "An example use of [47m[make-event][0m

  Here, we develop a reasonably self-contained example that illustrates
  how to use [47mmake-event[0m to develop tools, by solving a challenge
  posed by Alessandro Coglio.  For another such example, see
  [make-event-example-2].

  The challenge is to develop a programmatic method for solving the
  following sort of problem.

   1. Create a [47m[defun][0m form.

   2. Submit it to ACL2, obtaining a new ACL2 [state] whose [world]
      includes the function just submitted.

   3. Access various elements of this function (e.g., unnormalized body).

   4. Create and return a new [47mdefun[0m that's based on elements of the
      previous one.

   5. Submit this new defun via a [47m[make-event][0m, but in a state that does
      not include the previous [47mdefun[0m.

  We illustrate how to do this sort of thing by specifying the ``new
  [47mdefun[0m that's based on elements of the previous one'' to be as
  follows: add the formal, [47my[0m, and modify the body so that [47my[0m is consed
  onto the old body.  Of course, this is a trivial example that could
  be done without [47mmake-event[0m; but we solve it in a way that shows how
  to solve any such problem.  For simplicity, let's not worry about
  the case that [47my[0m is already a formal of the existing [47mdefun[0m.  Here
  are the main steps.

    * (a) Submit the [47mdefun[0m.

    * (b) Gather information from the resulting world.  In this case, we
      access the formals and body of the definition.

    * (c) Create the desired event.

  The following code does those three things, as explained in comments
  below, which include references to the three steps above.

    (er-progn

    ; Each of the two forms below returns an error triple (see :DOC
    ; error-triple), so we can evaluate both by using er-progn, which
    ; returns the last (second) error triple.

     (defun foo (x) (cons x x)) ; (a)
     (let ((formals (formals 'foo (w state))) ; (b)
           (body (body 'foo nil (w state))))
       (value `(defun foo ,(cons 'y formals) ; (c)
                 (cons y ,body)))))

  So far so good: we have computed an error triple [47m(mv nil val state)[0m
  whose value component, [47mval[0m, is the desired [47mdefun[0m form.  However,
  that leaves us in a world that includes the first [47mdefun[0m form.  For
  a solution to the original challenge (for our specific case), that
  must not be the case, and moreover the second [47mdefun[0m form should be
  included in the current world.  Fortunately, [47m[make-event][0m is
  perfectly suited to do both of these things.  Consider the
  following form, which simply wraps [47mmake-event[0m around the code
  displayed just above.

    (make-event (er-progn
                 (defun foo (x) (cons x x))
                 (let ((formals (formals 'foo (w state)))
                       (body (body 'foo nil (w state))))
                   (value `(defun foo ,(cons 'y formals)
                             (cons y ,body))))))

  The expansion phase (see [make-event]) computes the new [47mdefun[0m form
  --- the one with the extra formal and modified body --- and then
  that new [47mdefun[0m form is evaluated in the original world, which does
  not include the first [47mdefun[0m form.

  We complete the job by making a programmatic solution, with a macro
  that expands to such a [47mmake-event[0m form.  We make it nice by
  inhibiting all output except error output.

    (defmacro cons-y-onto-body (def new-name)
      `(make-event
        (with-output!
          :off :all
          :on error
          (er-progn
           ,def
           (let* ((name ',(cadr def))
                  (new-name ',new-name)
                  (formals (formals name (w state)))
                  (body (body name nil (w state))))
             (value (list 'defun new-name (cons 'y formals)
                          (list 'cons 'y body))))))
        :on-behalf-of :quiet!))

  This could be improved by doing some error checking, but we leave
  that as an exercise.

  Below is a log, with comments added, that shows uses of the macro
  above.

    ; First we call the macro successfully.  Notice that although we inhibited
    ; output during the expansion phase (using with-output!), below we see output
    ; from the resulting new defun event.

    ACL2 !>(cons-y-onto-body (defun f (x) x) new-f)

    Since NEW-F is non-recursive, its admission is trivial.  We observe
    that the type of NEW-F is described by the theorem (CONSP (NEW-F Y X)).
    We used primitive type reasoning.

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN NEW-F ...)
    Rules: ((:FAKE-RUNE-FOR-TYPE-SET NIL))
    Time:  0.01 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.01)

    Summary
    Form:  ( MAKE-EVENT (WITH-OUTPUT! :OFF ...) ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.03 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.03)
     NEW-F
    ACL2 !>:pe new-f ; Check that the new definition was indeed submitted.
     L         2:x(CONS-Y-ONTO-BODY (DEFUN F # ...) NEW-F)

    >L             (DEFUN NEW-F (Y X) (CONS Y X))
    ACL2 !>:pe f ; Check that the old definition was NOT submitted.


    ACL2 Error in :PE:  The object F is not a logical name.  See :DOC logical-
    name.

    ; The defun below is ill-formed, so we get an error when it is submitted,
    ; during the expansion phase.  Our use of with-output! allowed error messages,
    ; so we see the error message in this case.

    ACL2 !>(cons-y-onto-body (defun g (x) (+ y y)) new-g)


    ACL2 Error in ( DEFUN G ...):  The body of G contains a free occurrence
    of the variable symbol Y.


    Summary
    Form:  ( MAKE-EVENT (WITH-OUTPUT! :OFF ...) ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    ACL2 Error in ( MAKE-EVENT (WITH-OUTPUT! :OFF ...) ...):  See :DOC
    failure.

    ******** FAILED ********
    ACL2 !>")
 (MAKE-EVENT-EXAMPLE-2
  (MAKE-EVENT)
  "An example use of [47m[make-event][0m

  Here, we develop a reasonably self-contained example that illustrates
  how to use [47mmake-event[0m to develop tools.  For another such example,
  see [make-event-example-1].  We thank Yan Peng for putting forward
  this problem.

  We begin by discussing prerequisites for this presentation.  Next, we
  present the challenge problem, followed by code that solves the
  problem together with an example that illustrates its use.
  Finally, we start from the beginning and show how to develop the
  solution step-by-step.


Prerequisites

  We assume some general ACL2 and Common Lisp background, including
  familiarity with [macros] and backquote.  But we do some
  programming below that goes beyond that general background in our
  use of the ACL2 [state].  See [programming-with-state] for relevant
  discussion, including state global variables, [47m[f-get-global][0m,
  [47m[f-put-global][0m, [error-triple]s and their construction using [47mvalue[0m,
  [47m[value-triple][0m, and [47m[pprogn][0m.  We also assume familiarity with both
  [47m[progn][0m and the notion of legal event form; see
  [embedded-event-form].  If you are not familiar with these notions,
  then the discussion below might still make some sense, but you
  might need to read about these notions in order to complete your
  understanding.

  Next, we present the challenge problem that we will solve below.


Challenge problem

      Define a utility that evaluates a given sequence of [events], even
      continuing past errors, which stores the list of event forms
      that caused errors.

  Let us call this utility [47mprogn+[0m, since it can be viewed as an
  enhancement of [47m[progn][0m.  So a successful solution should allow us
  to evaluate the following form to define function symbols [47mf1[0m, [47mf2[0m,
  and [47mf3[0m, as well as the event [47mgood-thm[0m, and also store the events
  for [47mbad-thm[0m and [47mbad-fn[0m.

    (progn+ (defun f1 (x) x)
            (defthm good-thm (equal 1 1) :rule-classes nil)
            (value-triple (cw \"@@HI~%\"))
            (defthm bad-thm (equal x y) :rule-classes nil) ; error!
            (defun f2 (x) x)
            (defun bad-fn (x) y) ; error!
            (defun f3 (x) x)
            )

  We next present a definition of [47mprogn+[0m, together with output from the
  example displayed above.  After that we will start from the
  beginning and describe how one might develop [47mprogn+[0m from scratch.


The completed code and a test

    (defmacro save-progn+-error (x)
      `(make-event
        (pprogn (f-put-global 'progn+-errors
                              (cons ,x
                                    (f-get-global 'progn+-errors state))
                              state)
                (value '(value-triple nil)))))

    (defun progn+-fn (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp lst) :mode :program))
      (cond ((endp lst) nil)
            (t (cons `(make-event '(:or ,(car lst) (save-progn+-error ',(car lst))))
                     (progn+-fn (cdr lst))))))

    (defmacro progn+ (&rest lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp lst)
                                  lst)))
      `(progn (make-event (pprogn (f-put-global 'progn+-errors nil state)
                                  (value '(value-triple nil))))
               ,@(progn+-fn lst)))

    ; Example:
    (progn+ (defun f1 (x) x)
            (defthm good-thm (equal 1 1) :rule-classes nil)
            (value-triple (cw \"@@HI~%\"))
            (defthm bad-thm (equal x y) :rule-classes nil)
            (defun f2 (x) x)
            (defun bad-fn (x) y)
            (defun f3 (x) x)
            )

  After running the example (which is the final form above), we expect
  the resulting logical [world] to contain definitions of function
  symbols [47mf1[0m, [47mf2[0m, and [47mf3[0m, as well as the event [47mgood-thm[0m, but that no
  events would be stored for [47mbad-thm[0m or [47mbad-fn[0m.  Moreover, we expect
  that a state global variable holds the event forms for [47mbad-thm[0m and
  [47mbad-fn[0m.  Let us check that all of these expectations are met.

    ACL2 !>:pe f1
               4:x(PROGN+ (DEFUN F1 # ...)
                          (DEFTHM GOOD-THM # ...)
                          ...)

    >L             (DEFUN F1 (X) X)
    ACL2 !>:pe f2
               4:x(PROGN+ (DEFUN F1 # ...)
                          (DEFTHM GOOD-THM # ...)
                          ...)

    >L             (DEFUN F2 (X) X)
    ACL2 !>:pe f3
               4:x(PROGN+ (DEFUN F1 # ...)
                          (DEFTHM GOOD-THM # ...)
                          ...)

    >L             (DEFUN F3 (X) X)
    ACL2 !>(@ progn+-errors) ; same as (f-get-global 'progn+-errors state)
    ((DEFUN BAD-FN (X) Y)
     (DEFTHM BAD-THM (EQUAL X Y)
             :RULE-CLASSES NIL))
    ACL2 !>


Development of the solution

  We now start from the beginning.  Recall that [47m[progn][0m does almost
  what we ask [47mprogn+[0m to do, except that [47m[progn][0m stops at the first
  error.  So our approach will be to define a macro that is much like
  [47mprogn[0m, except that each event is modified as follows: if evaluation
  fails for an event, then that event is pushed onto the value of a
  specific state global (see [programming-with-state]),
  [47mprogn+-errors[0m.  Moreover, since [47m[progn][0m is applied only to legal
  event forms (see [embedded-event-form]), we need this modification
  to be a legal event form, too.  Fortunately, a call of [47m[make-event][0m
  is a legal event form.  So our plan is for [47m(progn+ ... <event> ...)[0m
  to expand to a call [47m(progn ... (make-event ...) ...)[0m so that the
  indicated [47mmake-event[0m call attempts to evaluate [47m<event>[0m, and if that
  attempt fails, then [47m<event>[0m it is pushed onto [47mprogn+-errors[0m.

  Thus, we begin with a simple piece of the task, by showing how to
  create an event that will push a specific form, [47m(defthm bad-thm
  (equal x y) :rule-classes nil)[0m, onto [47mprogn+-errors[0m.

    (make-event
     (pprogn (f-put-global 'progn+-errors
                           (cons '(defthm bad-thm (equal x y) :rule-classes nil)
                                 (f-get-global 'progn+-errors state))
                           state)
             (value '(value-triple nil))))

  It might seem more natural to write [47m(f-put-global ...)[0m, instead of
  the more complex [47m(pprogn (f-put-global ...) (value '(value-triple
  nil)))[0m.  However, the argument to [47mmake-event[0m must evaluate to
  either: (1) a legal event form; or (2) an [error-triple], [47m(mv nil x
  state)[0m, where [47mx[0m is a legal event form.  Since the [47mf-put-global[0m call
  modifies state, (1) is not an option; so we choose (2).  Since the
  role of [47mx[0m is only to provide an event form, we choose [47mx[0m to be the
  trivial event form [47m(value-triple nil)[0m.

  We follow good programming practice by wrapping the functionality
  displayed above into a concise utility, as follows.

    (defmacro save-progn+-error (x)
    ; Push x (which is evaluated) onto the value of state global 'progn+-errors.
      `(make-event
        (pprogn (f-put-global 'progn+-errors
                              (cons ,x
                                    (f-get-global 'progn+-errors state))
                              state)
                (value '(value-triple nil)))))

  Before we write [47mprogn+[0m, it helps to approximate its expansion in the
  specific ``Example'' displayed above.  We know in advance which two
  forms will fail, so we `cheat' by using [47mprogn[0m but with those two
  forms replaced by calls to [47msave-progn+-error[0m.

    (f-put-global 'progn+-errors nil state)
    (progn  (defun f1 (x) x)
            (defthm good-thm (equal 1 1) :rule-classes nil)
            (value-triple (cw \"@@HI~%\"))
            (save-progn+-error
             '(defthm bad-thm (equal x y) :rule-classes nil))
            (defun f2 (x) x)
            (save-progn+-error
             '(defun bad-fn (x) y))
            (defun f3 (x) x)
            )

  Our next task is to find a way to tell ACL2 that it should run a
  given event, and if that event fails it should save it using
  [47msave-progn+-error[0m.  Fortunately, the [47m:OR[0m keyword of [47m[make-event][0m is
  perfect for this task.  Consider the following two examples.

    (make-event '(:or (defun f2 (x) x)
                      (save-progn+-error '(defun f2 (x) x))))

    (make-event '(:or (defun bad-fn (x) y)
                      (save-progn+-error '(defun bad-fn (x) y))))

  In the first example, [47mmake-event[0m invokes the given call of [47m[defun][0m,
  which runs without error; and [47mmake-event[0m then exits without error.
  In the second example, [47mmake-event[0m invokes the given call of [47mdefun[0m,
  which causes an error; so then [47mmake-event[0m evaluates the second
  argument of the [47m:OR[0m, to invoke [47msave-progn+-error[0m on that same [47mdefun[0m
  call.  Since that call of [47msave-progn+-error[0m evaluates without error
  (because it expands to [47m(value-triple nil)[0m), the [47mmake-event[0m call
  exits without error.  See [make-event] for more information about
  the [47m:OR[0m keyword.

  We can now define [47mprogn+[0m to be the result of modifying each of its
  forms as in the two examples displayed just above.  First we write
  a function that transforms a list by applying that modification to
  each of its members.

    (defun progn+-fn (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (true-listp lst) :mode :program))
      (cond ((endp lst) nil)
            (t (cons `(make-event '(:or ,(car lst) (save-progn+-error ',(car lst))))
                     (progn+-fn (cdr lst))))))

  We complete our definition of [47mprogn+[0m, by generating a [47mprogn[0m call with
  the desired modification of each form in the list, using [47mprogn+-fn[0m
  --- but preceded by yet another [47mmake-event[0m call, this one for
  initializing our state global to [47mnil[0m.

    (defmacro progn+ (&rest lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp lst)
                                  lst)))
      `(progn (make-event (pprogn (f-put-global 'progn+-errors nil state)
                                  (value '(value-triple nil))))
               ,@(progn+-fn lst)))

  [31;1mExercise[0m: Modify this tool so that instead of merely updating a state
  global, it prints the failed events at the end of execution; and
  moreover, it prints them in their original order.  See
  [make-event-example-2-exercise] for a solution.


Subtopics

  [Make-event-example-2-exercise]
      Solution to an exercise from [47m[make-event-example-2][0m")
 (MAKE-EVENT-EXAMPLE-2-EXERCISE
  (MAKE-EVENT-EXAMPLE-2)
  "Solution to an exercise from [47m[make-event-example-2][0m

  See [make-event-example-2] for a worked example using [47m[make-event][0m,
  concluding with an exercise.  Here we present a solution to that
  exercise.  It assumes that we have evaluated the definitions of
  [47msave-progn+-error[0m, [47mprogn+-fn[0m, and [47mprogn+[0m from
  [make-event-example-2].

    (defmacro progn+-errors (&rest lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp lst)
                                  lst)))
      `(progn (progn+ ,@lst)
              (make-event
               (list 'value-triple
                     (list 'quote
                           (reverse (f-get-global 'progn+-errors state)))))))")
 (MAKE-EVENT-EXAMPLE-3
  (MAKE-EVENT)
  "Using [47m[make-event][0m to define [47m[thm][0m

  The definition of [47m[thm][0m provides a simple, yet informative, example
  use of [47mmake-event[0m.  Formerly (through ACL2 Version 8.1), this was
  the definition of [47mthm[0m, where [47mthm-fn[0m provides an interface to the
  prover.

    (defmacro thm (term &key hints otf-flg)
      (list 'thm-fn
            (list 'quote term)
            'state
            (list 'quote hints)
            (list 'quote otf-flg)))

  However, this version of [47mthm[0m did not permit calls of [47mthm[0m in [books]
  or [47m[encapsulate][0m forms.  To remedy that deficiency, the definition
  was changed to the following; below we explain components of this
  definition.  (It has since been updated further, but that is not
  relevant here so we don't comment here on further updates.)

    (defmacro thm (&whole event-form
                          term &key hints otf-flg)
      `(with-output :off summary :stack :push
