 (MBT
  (MBE ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Introduce a test into the logic that, however, evaluates to [47mt[0m

  The macro [47mmbt[0m (``must be true'') can be used in order to add code in
  order to admit function definitions in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode, without
  paying a cost in execution efficiency.  Examples below illustrate
  its intended use.

  Semantically, [47m(mbt x)[0m equals [47mx[0m.  However, in raw Lisp [47m(mbt x)[0m ignores
  [47mx[0m entirely, and macroexpands to [47mt[0m.  ACL2's [guard] verification
  mechanism ensures that the raw Lisp code is only evaluated when
  appropriate, since a guard proof obligation [47m(equal x t)[0m is
  generated.  See [verify-guards] and, for general discussion of
  guards, see [guard].

  Also see [mbe], which stands for ``must be equal.'' Although [47mmbt[0m is
  more natural in many cases, [47mmbe[0m has more general applicability.  In
  fact, [47m(mbt x)[0m is essentially defined to be [47m(mbe :logic x :exec t)[0m.
  Another related utility is [47m[mbt*][0m, which generates the same proof
  obligation as [47mmbt[0m but logically, is simply [47mt[0m.

  We can illustrate the use of [47mmbt[0m on the following generic example,
  where [47m<g>[0m, [47m<test>[0m, [47m<rec-x>[0m, and [47m<base>[0m are intended to be terms
  involving only the variable [47mx[0m.

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard <g>))
      (if <test>
          (foo <rec-x>)
        <base>))

  In order to admit this function, ACL2 needs to discharge the proof
  obligation that [47m<rec-x>[0m is smaller than [47mx[0m, namely:

    (implies <test>
             (o< (acl2-count [47m<rec-x>[0m)
                  (acl2-count x)))

  But suppose we need to know that [47m<g>[0m is true in order to prove this.
  Since [47m<g>[0m is only the [guard], it is not part of the logical
  definition of [47mfoo[0m.  A solution is to add the guard to the
  definition of [47mfoo[0m, as follows.

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard <g>))
      (if (mbt <g>)
          (if <test>
              (foo <rec-x>)
            <base>)
        nil))

  If we do this using [47m<g>[0m rather than [47m(mbt <g>)[0m, then evaluation of
  every recursive call of [47mfoo[0m will cause the evaluation of (the
  appropriate instance of) [47m<g>[0m.  But since [47m(mbt <g>)[0m expands to [47mt[0m in
  raw Lisp, then once we verify the guards of [47mfoo[0m, the evaluations of
  [47m<g>[0m will be avoided (except at the top level, when we check the
  guard before allowing evaluation to take place in Common Lisp).

  Other times, the guard isn't the issue, but rather, the problem is
  that a recursive call has an argument that itself is a recursive
  call.  For example, suppose that [47m<rec-x>[0m is of the form [47m(foo
  <expr>)[0m.  There is no way we can hope to discharge the termination
  proof obligation shown above.  A standard solution is to add some
  version of this test:

    (mbt (o< (acl2-count [47m<rec-x>[0m) (acl2-count x)))

  Here is a specific example based on one sent by Vernon Austel.

    (defun recurX2 (n)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (integerp n) (<= 0 n))
                      :verify-guards nil))
      (cond ((zp n) 0)
            (t (let ((call (recurX2 (1- n))))
                 (if (mbt (< (acl2-count call) n))
                     (recurX2 call)
                   1 ;; this branch is never actually taken
                   )))))

    (defthm recurX2-0
     (equal (recurX2 n) 0))

    (verify-guards recurX2)

  If you [47m([0m[47m[trace$][0m[47m acl2-count)[0m, you will see that evaluation of
  [47m(recurX2 2)[0m causes several calls of [47m[ACL2-count][0m before the
  [47m[verify-guards][0m.  But this evaluation does not call [47macl2-count[0m
  after the [47mverify-guards[0m, because the ACL2 evaluation mechanism uses
  raw Lisp to do the evaluation, and the form [47m(mbt (< (acl2-count
  call) n))[0m macroexpands to [47mt[0m in Common Lisp.

  You may occasionally get warnings when you compile functions defined
  using [47mmbt[0m.  (For commands that invoke the compiler, see
  [compilation].)  These can be inhibited by using an [47mignorable[0m
  [47m[declare][0m form.  Here is a simple but illustrative example.  Note
  that the declarations can optionally be separated into two
  [47m[declare][0m forms.

    (defun foo (x y)
      (declare (ignorable x)
               (xargs :guard (equal x t)))
      (and (mbt x) y))")
