  ACL2 [world] stating the equality of calls of [47mfn[0m and [47mg[0m on a
  duplicate-free argument list; for example, if the formals list of
  [47mfn[0m is [47m(x1 ... xn)[0m, then the theorem could be [47m(equal (fn x1 .... xn)
  (g x1 ... xn))[0m.  If ACL2 finds no such theorem, it will print a
  [47m[defthm][0m event that you may wish to submit.  Next we describe a
  potential second proof obligation, which will similarly be printed
  if it is not met.  Let [47mguard-fn[0m be the [guard] for [47mfn[0m, and let
  [47mguard-g[0m be the result of substituting the formals of [47mfn[0m for the
  formals of [47mg[0m in the guard for [47mg[0m.  If [47mguard-fn[0m tautologically
  implies [47mguard-g[0m (for example, the two are equal or [47mguard-g[0m is [47m'T[0m),
  then there is no further proof obligation.  Otherwise, there must
  be a theorem in the current ACL2 [world] of the form [47m(implies
  guard-fn guard-g)[0m.  See [verify-guard-implication] for a utility
  that makes it easy for you to prove such a theorem.  Finally,
  contrary to the usual defaults, the values of keyword [47m:recursive[0m,
  [47m:condition[0m and [47m:stats[0m default to [47mnil[0m.  Indeed, it is an error to
  specify a non-nil value for [47m:recursive[0m.  The alternate defaults of
  [47mnil[0m for [47m:condition[0m and [47m:stats[0m can avoid memoization overhead when
  one simply wishes to call [47mg[0m in place of [47mfn[0m; you may override those
  defaults if you actually want to save computed values and use
  [47m(memsum)[0m to see statistics.

  Keyword parameter [47m:recursive[0m is [47mt[0m by default, which means that
  recursive calls of [47mfn[0m will be memoized just as ``top-level'' calls
  of [47mfn[0m.  When [47m:recursive[0m is instead set to [47mnil[0m, memoization is only
  done at the top level.  Using [47m:recursive nil[0m is similar to writing
  a wrapper function that just calls [47mfn[0m, and memoizing the wrapper
  instead of [47mfn[0m.

  A non-[47mnil[0m value for [47m:commutative[0m can be supplied if [47mfn[0m is a binary
  function in [47m:logic[0m mode.  Suppose that the [47mmemoize[0m event is
  successful, and consider a subsequent call of [47mfn[0m for which some
  argument to [47mfn[0m is either a rational number or, in some host Lisps
  (currently either CCL, or GCL Version 2.6.12 or later) a [hons].
  (Indeed, for such GCL versions this is true for any cons, not just
  a hons.)  Then when the evaluation of [47mfn[0m on those arguments is
  memoized, the evaluation of [47mfn[0m on the swap of those arguments is,
  in essence, also memoized.  If [47m:commutative[0m is supplied and is not
  [47mnil[0m or [47mt[0m, then it should be the name of a previously-proved theorem
  whose formula states the commutativity of [47mfn[0m, i.e., is the formula
  [47m(equal (fn x y) (fn y x))[0m for a pair [47m{x,y}[0m of distinct variables.
  If [47m:commutative[0m is [47mt[0m --- but not merely an expression that
  evaluates to [47mt[0m --- then an attempt to prove such a lemma will be
  made on-the-fly.  The name of the lemma is the symbol in the same
  package as [47mfn[0m, obtained by adding the suffix [47m\"-COMMUTATIVE\"[0m to the
  [47m[symbol-name][0m of [47mfn[0m.  If the proof attempt fails, then you may want
  first to prove the lemma yourself with appropriate hints and
  perhaps supporting lemmas, and then supply the name of that lemma
  as the value of [47m:commutative[0m.  Note that because most output is
  inhibited by default, you might wish to supply keyword argument
  [47m:verbose t[0m if the event fails.

  If [47m:commutative[0m is supplied, and a non-commutative condition is
  provided by [47m:condition[0m or [47m:condition-fn[0m, then although the results
  will be correct, the extra memoization afforded by [47m:commutative[0m is
  unspecified.

  If [47m:memo-table-init-size[0m is supplied, then it should be a positive
  integer specifying the initial size of an associated hash table.

  Argument [47m:aokp[0m is relevant only when attachments are used; see
  [defattach] for background on attachments.  When [47m:aokp[0m is [47mnil[0m, the
  default, computed values are not stored when an attachment was
  used, or even when an attachment may have been used because a
  function was called that had been memoized using [47m:aokp t[0m.
  Otherwise, computed values are always stored, but saved values are
  not used except when attachments are allowed.  To summarize:

    aokp=nil (default): ``Pure'', i.e., values do not depend on attachments
    - Fetch: always legal
    - Store: only store resulting value when attachments were not used

    aokp=t: ``Impure'', i.e., values may depend on attachments
    - Fetch: only legal when attachments are allowed (e.g., not during proofs)
    - Store: always legal

  Note that for this handling of [47m:aokp[0m, the computation of a value
  returned by function [47m[apply$-userfn][0m or [47m[badge-userfn][0m is
  considered to have used an attachment.

  The default value for [47m:stats[0m is essentially [47mt[0m.  (Technically, this
  can be subverted by using raw Lisp, to change the default by
  changing the values of variables [47m*record-xxx*[0m introduced in ACL2
  source file [47mmemoize-raw.lisp[0m.)  When [47m:stats[0m has its default value
  (assuming the above raw Lisp changes are not made) or value [47mt[0m,
  calls of memoized functions will collect information that can be
  displayed by calling [47m[memsum][0m.  But if [47m:stats[0m is [47mnil[0m, this
  information will not be collected, possibly resulting in better
  performance for the memoized function.  As of this writing,
  built-in memoized functions use [47m:stats nil[0m to benefit performance.

  If [47m:ideal-okp[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then it is permitted to
  memoize an ``ideal-mode'' function: one in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode whose
  [guard]s have not been verified.  However, this might not have the
  effect you desire, because you will be memoizing the submitted
  version of the function, not its executable-counterpart that is
  executed in the ACL2 loop and on behalf of the prover (see
  [evaluation]).  Thus, if you memoize an ideal-mode function and
  then call it at the top-level of the ACL2 loop (or, for example, it
  is called to evaluate a ground term arising in a proof or in the
  evaluation of a [meta]function in a proof), the effects of
  memoization will not be felt because the raw Lisp code is not run
  unless guards are verified.

  There are circumstances in which the raw Lisp code of an ideal-mode
