 (MEMOIZE-PARTIAL
  (MEMOIZE)
  "[47m[Memoize][0m a partial version of a limited (`clocked') function

  See [memoize] for relevant background.  Here we describe a utility
  that supports effective memoization for functions that have been
  introduced by [47m[defun][0m, as follows, under certain restrictions.

    (defun fn-limit (x1 ... xn limit)
      (declare ...) ; optional, as usual for defun ; ;
      (if (zp limit)
          <base>
        (let ((limit (1- limit)))
          <body>)))

  Here [47mfn-limit[0m represents a function symbol and [47m(x1 ... xn limit)[0m
  denotes a non-empty list of formal parameters, where we call the
  final formal (here, shown as [47mlimit[0m) the ``limit variable''.  [47m<Base>[0m
  is any syntactically legal expression, but [47m<body>[0m is restricted as
  follows: the occurrences of the limit variable are exactly as the
  final parameter in each recursive call of [47mfn[0m.  More precisely, that
  restriction must hold after [47m<body>[0m is converted to a translated
  term (see [term]).  In other words, the translation of [47m<body>[0m must
  have the property that the limit variable only occurs in recursive
  calls, and in those calls it occurs exactly as the final argument
  and not in any other arguments.

  Such use of a ``limit'' argument to obtain termination is an old
  trick that pre-dates ACL2.  Such ``limited'' functions have
  traditionally often been called ``clocked'' functions.  In this
  documentation we refer to such a function as a ``total'' function,
  because the limit argument guarantees that its evaluation
  terminates.  We will be generating a corresponding ``partial''
  function that avoids the limit argument, and hence might not
  terminate --- but when it does, it computes the desired value, and
  uses memoization to do so, which can speed up computation.

  We organize the rest of this topic as follows: General Form, Basic
  Example, Example with Mutual Recursion, Detailed Documentation, and
  Optional Technical Remarks.


General Form:

    (memoize-partial fn+ ; see below
                     :kwd1 val1 ... :kwdn valn)

  where the supplied [47m:kwdi[0m and [47mvali[0m are valid keywords and values for
  [47m[memoize][0m.  In the simplest case, [47mfn+[0m is a symbol.  Later below,
  after providing an example, we give more complete documentation,
  including discussion of [47m[mutual-recursion][0m and the general case for
  [47mfn+[0m.  None of the arguments is evaluated.


Basic Example

  The following example from [community-book] file
  [47mbooks/demos/memoize-partial-input.lsp[0m illustrates the use of
  [47m[memoize-partial][0m.  Consider the {Collatz Conjecture |
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collatz_conjecture}, sometimes known
  as the 3n+1 problem.  For any positive integer [47mn[0m, compute a new
  integer as [47mn/2[0m if [47mn[0m is even, else as [47m3*n+1[0m.  The Collatz conjecture
  states that this process eventually reaches [47m1[0m.  Since this is an
  open problem, we can't realistically hope to prove termination, so
  we may code the above algorithm by using a limit argument, as
  follows.  This function returns, unless the limit is reached, the
  number of steps required for the computation to terminate (reach
  1).

    (defun collatz-limit (n limit)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (natp n)
                                  (natp limit))
                      :measure (acl2-count limit)))
      (if (zp limit)
          (prog2$ (er hard? 'collatz-limit
                      \"Limit exceeded!\")
                  0)
        (let ((limit (1- limit)))
          (if (int= n 1)
              0
            (1+ (collatz-limit (if (evenp n)
                                   (floor n 2)
                                 (1+ (* 3 n)))
                               limit))))))

  A moment's reflection suggests that if this algorithm terminates,
  then there are no cycles, so memoization from a single call will be
  useless.  However, memoization can be useful if we make distinct
  calls.  But memoization might not be very useful even then, because
  after we save the result of a call [47m(collatz-limit n0 limit0)[0m, a
  later call on [47mn0[0m might be with a different limit, say,
  [47m(collatz-limit n0 limit1)[0m.  So we prefer to memoize using the
  corresponding partial function, without a limit argument.  We can
  do that as follows.

    (memoize-partial collatz)

  Under the hood this generates, essentially, the following Common Lisp
  definition, which is to be memoized.

    (defun collatz (n)
      (if (int= n 1)
          0
        (1+ (collatz (if (evenp n)
                         (floor n 2)
                       (1+ (* 3 n)))))))

  Since the [47mlimit[0m argument has disappeared, memoization is now more
  effective.  But how can one define [47mcollatz[0m in the ACL2 logic?  The
  [47mmemoize-partial[0m call shown above actually generates the following
  events, which define [47mcollatz[0m to capture the notion of the
  ``eventual stable value, as [47mlimit[0m goes to infinity, of [47m(collatz n
  limit)[0m''.  It probably is not important to read these events unless
  one plans to reason about [47mcollatz[0m, and even then one might be able
  to complete proofs without reading these events.

    (DEFCHOOSE COLLATZ-LIMIT-CHANGE (LARGE)
      (N LIMIT)
      (AND (NATP LARGE)
           (<= LIMIT LARGE)
           (NOT (EQUAL (COLLATZ-LIMIT N LIMIT)
                       (COLLATZ-LIMIT N LARGE)))))
    (DEFCHOOSE COLLATZ-LIMIT-STABLE (LIMIT)
      (N)
      (AND (NATP LIMIT)
           (EQUAL (COLLATZ-LIMIT N LIMIT)
                  (COLLATZ-LIMIT N (COLLATZ-LIMIT-CHANGE N LIMIT)))))
    (DEFUN COLLATZ (N)
      (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (LET ((LIMIT 0))
                                  (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LIMIT))
                                  (AND (NATP N) (NATP LIMIT)))))
      (COLLATZ-LIMIT N (NFIX (COLLATZ-LIMIT-STABLE N))))

  The upshot is a logical definition of [47m(collatz n)[0m as the eventual
  value (if any), as [47mlimit[0m increases without bound, of [47m(collatz n
  limit)[0m --- together with an installed Common Lisp definition that
  is actually executed and, moreover, is memoized.

  We note that the call [47m(memoize-partial collatz)[0m also generates a
  [47m[table][0m event as well as the form [47m(MEMOIZE 'COLLATZ :TOTAL
  'COLLATZ-LIMIT)[0m.  However, these details are best ignored by the
  user; we do not expect those events to be generated by hand, and in
  particular it is probably inadvisable to use the [47m:TOTAL[0m keyword of
  [47m[memoize][0m other than indirectly using [47mmemoize-partial[0m.  Failure to
  heed this advice could make failures more difficult to debug.

  See [community-book] file [47mbooks/demos/memoize-partial-input.lsp[0m for
  an application of this use of [47mmemoize-partial[0m, to compute the total
  number of steps required (without accounting for memoization) to
  run the Collatz algorithm to completion on the first [47mn[0m positive
  integers.


Example with Mutual Recursion

  The following example, also from [community-book] file
  [47mbooks/demos/memoize-partial-input.lsp[0m, illustrates the use of
  [47mmemoize-partial[0m on functions that have been defined using
  [47m[mutual-recursion][0m.  (The suffix ``[47m-bdd[0m'' below is intended to
  denote ``bounded'', to suggest what the ``[47m-limit[0m'' suffix suggested
  in the first example.)

    (mutual-recursion
     (defun evenlp-bdd (x bound)
       (declare (xargs :guard (natp bound)))
       (if (zp bound)
           'ouch
         (let ((bound (1- bound)))
           (if (consp x) (oddlp-bdd (cdr x) bound) t))))
     (defun oddlp-bdd (x bound)
       (declare (xargs :guard (natp bound)))
       (if (zp bound)
           'ouch
         (let ((bound (1- bound)))
           (if (consp x) (evenlp-bdd (cdr x) bound) nil)))))

    (memoize-partial ((evenlp evenlp-bdd) (oddlp oddlp-bdd)))

  Notice that this time the argument of [47mmemoize-partial[0m is a list of
  tuples.  Each tuple is a list with two elements: the name of the
  partial function to be defined, followed by the corresponding total
  (limited) function that has already been defined.  Indeed, the
  preceding example abbreviates [47m(memoize-partial ((collatz
  collatz-limit)))[0m.  Such abbreviations are discussed in the Detailed
  Documentation section below.

  Recall that in the first example, the [47mmemoize-partial[0m call defined
  functions [47mcollatz-limit-change[0m and [47mcollatz-limit-stable[0m using
  [47m[defchoose][0m, and then function [47mcollatz[0m using [47m[defun][0m.  In the
  present example such a trio of functions is introduced for each of
  the functions defined in the corresponding [47mmutual-recursion[0m ---
  equivalently, for each of the tuples supplied to [47mmemoize-partial[0m.

    (DEFCHOOSE EVENLP-BDD-CHANGE (LARGE)
      (X BOUND)
      (AND (NATP LARGE)
           (<= BOUND LARGE)
           (NOT (EQUAL (EVENLP-BDD X BOUND)
                       (EVENLP-BDD X LARGE)))))
    (DEFCHOOSE EVENLP-BDD-STABLE (BOUND)
      (X)
      (AND (NATP BOUND)
           (EQUAL (EVENLP-BDD X BOUND)
                  (EVENLP-BDD X (EVENLP-BDD-CHANGE X BOUND)))))
    (DEFUN EVENLP (X)
      (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (LET ((BOUND 0))
                                  (DECLARE (IGNORABLE BOUND))
                                  (NATP BOUND))))
      (EVENLP-BDD X (NFIX (EVENLP-BDD-STABLE X))))
    (DEFCHOOSE ODDLP-BDD-CHANGE (LARGE)
      (X BOUND)
      (AND (NATP LARGE)
           (<= BOUND LARGE)
           (NOT (EQUAL (ODDLP-BDD X BOUND)
                       (ODDLP-BDD X LARGE)))))
    (DEFCHOOSE ODDLP-BDD-STABLE (BOUND)
      (X)
      (AND (NATP BOUND)
           (EQUAL (ODDLP-BDD X BOUND)
                  (ODDLP-BDD X (ODDLP-BDD-CHANGE X BOUND)))))
    (DEFUN ODDLP (X)
      (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (LET ((BOUND 0))
                                  (DECLARE (IGNORABLE BOUND))
                                  (NATP BOUND))))
      (ODDLP-BDD X (NFIX (ODDLP-BDD-STABLE X))))

  The corresponding Common Lisp functions, to be executed for
  [guard]-verified calls of [47mevenlp[0m and [47moddlp[0m, are defined as one
  might expect, in analogy to the Common Lisp definition in the first
  example.  Moreover, they are memoized.  Here are (again,
  essentially) the Common Lisp definitions of [47mevenlp[0m and [47moddlp[0m.

    (DEFUN EVENLP (X)
      (IF (CONSP X)
          (ODDLP (CDR X))
          T))
    (DEFUN ODDLP (X)
      (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X))
      (IF (CONSP X)
          (EVENLP (CDR X))
          NIL))


Detailed Documentation

  Recall the General Form

    (memoize-partial fn+ ; see below
                     :kwd1 val1 ... :kwdn valn)

  where the supplied [47m:kwdi[0m and [47mvali[0m represent keyword options to be
  used for a generated call of [47m[memoize][0m.  We discuss later below how
  [47mfn+[0m specifies either a recursively-defined function symbol or a
  sequence of function symbols defined by [47mmutual-recursion[0m --- all
  [guard]-verified.  We call the final formal parameter, which must
  be the same for each function symbol defined in the
  [47mmutual-recursion[0m case, the ``limit variable''.  The translated body
  (see [term] regarding the notion of ``translated'') of each of
  these function symbols must be of the following form, or of the
  corresponding form using [47m[cond][0m in place of [47m[if][0m, where here [47mlimit[0m
  denotes the limit variable.

    (if (zp limit)
        <base>
      (let ((limit (1- limit)))
        <body>))

  Moreover, each limit variable occurring in [47m<body>[0m must occur as the
  final argument of a call of the defined function --- in the mutual
  recursion case, of the functions defined by the [47mmutual-recursion[0m
  --- and conversely, each such call must have the limit variable as
  its final argument.

  Let us refer to each specified function as the ``total'' function.  A
  successful invocation admits a sequence of three definitions for
  each total function, which we call the ``changed'', ``stable'', and
  ``partial'' function.  Here are those three definitions, where
  ``[47m...[0m'' denotes the formals of the partial function (that is, the
  formals of the total function other than the final formal, which is
  the limit variable).  We write [47mLIMIT[0m below to denote the limit
  variable.  Note that [47mLARGE[0m is replaced by a fresh variable when
  necessary, to avoid duplicating an existing formal.

    (DEFCHOOSE <changed> (LARGE)
      (... LIMIT)
      (AND (NATP LARGE)
           (<= LIMIT LARGE)
           (NOT (EQUAL (<total> ... LIMIT)
                       (<total> ... LARGE)))))
    (DEFCHOOSE <stable> (LIMIT)
      (...)
      (AND (NATP LIMIT)
           (EQUAL (<total> ... LIMIT)
                  (<total> ... (<change> ... LIMIT)))))
    (DEFUN <partial> (...)
      (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (LET ((LIMIT 0))
                                  (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LIMIT))
                                  <guard_of_total>)))
      (<total> ... (NFIX (<stable> ...))))

  Under the hood, however, a Common Lisp definition is installed for
  [47m<partial>[0m that avoids the limit variable, essentially by
  eliminating it in [47m<body>[0m to produce a corresponding term, [47m<body'>[0m,
  with the following result.  (Low-level details about exactly what
  is generated are discussed in the Optional Technical Remarks below,
  but should probably be ignored by most users.)

    (defun <partial> (...)
      <body'>

  We turn now to the first argument of [47mmemoize-partial[0m, denoted [47mfn+[0m
  above, with discussion of its legal forms and its relation to the
  other (keyword) arguments of [47mmemoize-partial[0m.  The most flexible
  form for [47mfn+[0m is a list of tuples, where each tuple is of the
  following form and the keyword arguments are optional and in any
  order.

    (<partial> <total>
     :change <change>
     :stable <stable>
     :kwd1 val1 ... :kwdn valn)

  The symbols [47m<partial>[0m, [47m<total>[0m, [47m<change>[0m, and [47m<stable>[0m are as
  discussed above, and the arguments [47m:kwd1 val1 ... :kwdn valn[0m are as
  supplied to [47m[memoize][0m.  There should be a single tuple in the
  singly recursive case.  In the case that the partial functions were
  defined by a [47mmutual-recursion[0m defining functions [47mf1[0m, ..., [47mfn[0m, then
  the [47m<partial>[0m components of the tuples (their [47m[car][0ms) must also be
  [47mf1[0m, ..., [47mfn[0m.

  The keyword/value pairs of the tuple are optional.  The default for
  [47m<change>[0m is obtained by adding the suffix [47m\"-CHANGE\"[0m to [47m<total>[0m, to
  create a symbol in the same package as that of [47m<total>[0m; similarly
  for [47m<stable>[0m and the suffix [47m\"-STABLE\"[0m.  The [47m<total>[0m function is
  defined as described above, with an under-the-hood Common Lisp
  definition also as described above, and [memoize]d.  The [47mmemoize[0m
  call uses the keywords supplied in the tuple other than [47m:change[0m and
  [47m:stable[0m.  For any [47mmemoize[0m keywords not specified in the tuple, the
  keywords specified at the top level of the [47mmemoize-partial[0m call ---
  that is, the keyword arguments (if any) following the first
  argument (that is, following [47mfn+[0m) --- are also used in that
  generated [47mmemoize[0m call.

  There are some abbreviations allowed for [47mfn+[0m, as follows.

    * A symbol [47mfn[0m for [47mfn+[0m abbreviates the list [47m(fn)[0m, which is an
      abbreviation as noted below.

    * If [47mfn+[0m is a list [47m(t1 ... tk)[0m, then each [47mti[0m that is a symbol, say [47mfn[0m,
      abbreviates the tuple [47m(fn fn-limit)[0m, where [47mfn-limit[0m is obtained
      by adding the suffix [47m\"-LIMIT\"[0m to [47mfn[0m, to create a symbol in the
      same package as that of [47mfn[0m.

  Consider our first example, [47m(memoize-partial collatz)[0m.  In this case
  [47mfn+[0m is [47mcollatz[0m.  By the first rule above, this abbreviates
  [47m(collatz)[0m, which the second rule says is an abbreviation for
  [47m(collatz collatz-limit)[0m.  The default values are computed to treat
  this as the following tuple.

    (collatz collatz-limit
             :change collatz-limit-change
             :stable collatz-limit-stable)

  Note that the keyword value [47m:condition nil[0m for [47mmemoize[0m can be used to
  install executable definitions without actually saving values,
  essentially by merely profiling (except that unlike [47m[profile][0m the
  recursive calls are also affected by the [47mmemoize[0m call).  See the
  example involving ``worse-than'' functions in community book
  [47mbooks/demos/memoize-partial-input.lsp[0m.

  We conclude this section by discussing restrictions pertaining to
  [stobj]s.  Recall that memoization is illegal (other than
  profiling) for functions that return a stobj or have the ACL2
  [state] as an input.  Those same restrictions apply to
  [47mmemoize-partial[0m.  However, it is legal for the given (``total'')
  functions to have user-defined stobjs as inputs, as long as they
  are not returned.  In that case, the generated definition of the
  partial function will be made with a call of [47m[non-exec][0m wrapped
  around the body.


Optional Technical Remarks.

  Warning: These are technical remarks that are completely optional.

  Our general remark is to invite readers who want to dive below the
  user level, including implementation and foundational issues, to
  read the comment entitled ``Essay on Memoization with Partial
  Functions (Memoize-partial)'' in ACL2 source file
  [47mother-events.lisp[0m.

  Our more specific remark concerns generated Common Lisp definitions.
  Recall that in the Basic Example above, we say that the Common Lisp
  definition of [47mcollatz[0m is essentially as follows.

    (defun collatz (n)
      (if (int= n 1)
          0
        (1+ (collatz (if (evenp n)
                         (floor n 2)
                       (1+ (* 3 n)))))))

  We say ``essentially'' because the actual Common Lisp definition of
  [47mcollatz[0m is a bit different, as follows.

    (DEFUN COLLATZ (N)
      (DECLARE (IGNORABLE N))
      (FLET ((COLLATZ-LIMIT (N LIMIT)
                            (DECLARE (IGNORE LIMIT))
                            (COLLATZ N)))
            (DECLARE (INLINE COLLATZ-LIMIT))
            (LET ((LIMIT 0))
                 (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LIMIT))
                 (IF (INT= N 1)
                     0
                     (1+ (COLLATZ-LIMIT
                          (IF (EVENP N) (FLOOR N 2) (1+ (* 3 N)))
                          LIMIT))))))

  A little reflection will conclude that these two definitions are
  equivalent.  The latter, however, avoids a macroexpansion issue
  discussed in the aforementioned Essay.")
