 (META
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make a [47m:meta[0m rule (a hand-written simplifier)

  See [rule-classes] for a general discussion of rule classes,
  including how they are used to build rules from formulas and a
  discussion of the various keywords in a rule class description.

  Meta rules extend the ACL2 simplifier with hand-written code to
  transform certain terms to equivalent ones.  To add a meta rule,
  the [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m formula must establish that the hand-written
  ``metafunction'' preserves the meaning of the transformed term.

    Examples:
    (defthm fn-correct-1                ; Modify the rewriter to use fn to
      (equal (evl x a)                  ; transform terms that are calls of
             (evl (fn x) a))            ; nth or of foo.
      :rule-classes ((:meta :trigger-fns (nth foo))))

    (defthm fn-correct-2                ; As above, but this illustrates
      (implies (and (pseudo-termp x)    ; that without loss of generality we
                    (alistp a))         ; may restrict x to be shaped like a
               (equal (evl x a)         ; term and a to be an alist.
                      (evl (fn x) a)))
      :rule-classes ((:meta :trigger-fns (nth foo))))

    (defthm fn-correct-3                ; As above (with or without the
      (implies (and (pseudo-termp x)    ; hypotheses on x and a), with the
                    (alistp a)          ; additional restriction that the
                    (evl (hyp-fn x) a)) ; meaning of (hyp-fn x) is true in
               (equal (evl x a)         ; the current context.  That is, the
                      (evl (fn x) a)))  ; applicability of the transformation
      :rule-classes                     ; may be dependent upon some computed
      ((:meta :trigger-fns (nth foo)))) ; hypotheses.

  While our intention is that the set of ACL2 documentation topics is
  self-contained, readers might find it useful to see the following
  paper for an introduction to meta reasoning in ACL2.

      W. A. Hunt, Jr., R. B. Krug, M. Kaufmann, J S. Moore and E. W. Smith,
      ``Meta Reasoning in ACL2.'' TPHOLs 2005, ed. J. Hurd and T. F.
      Melham, LNCS 3603, Springer-Verlag, Berlin, 2005, pp. 163--178.

  A non-[47mnil[0m list of function symbols must be supplied as the value of
  the [47m:trigger-fns[0m field in a [47m:meta[0m rule class object (except that a
  macro alias can stand in for a function symbol; see
  [add-macro-alias]).

    General Forms:
    (implies (and (pseudo-termp x)        ; this hyp is optional
                  (alistp a)              ; this hyp is optional
                  (ev (hyp-fn x ...) a)   ; this hyp is optional
                  ; meta-extract hyps may also be included (see below)
                  )
             (equiv (ev x a)
                    (ev (fn x ...) a)))

  where [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation, [47mx[0m and [47ma[0m are distinct
  variable names, and [47mev[0m is an evaluator function (see below), and [47mfn[0m
  is a function symbol, as is [47mhyp-fn[0m when provided.  The arguments to
  [47mfn[0m and [47mhyp-fn[0m should be identical.  In the most common case, both
  take a single argument, [47mx[0m, which denotes the term to be simplified.
  If [47mfn[0m and/or [47mhyp-fn[0m are [guard]ed, their [guard]s should be
  trivially implied by [47m[pseudo-termp][0m.  We say the theorem above is a
  ``metatheorem'' or ``metalemma'' and [47mfn[0m is a ``metafunction'', and
  [47mhyp-fn[0m is a ``hypothesis metafunction''.

  If ``[47m...[0m'' is empty, i.e., the metafunctions take just one argument,
  we say they are ``vanilla flavored.'' If ``[47m...[0m'' is non-empty, we
  say the metafunctions are ``extended.'' Extended metafunctions can
  access [47m[state][0m and context sensitive information to compute their
  results, within certain limits.  We discuss vanilla metafunctions
  here and recommend a thorough understanding of them before
  proceeding (at which time see [extended-metafunctions]).

  If a metafunction application to a term, [47mu[0m, evaluates to a result of
  the form [47m(if TEST NEW-TERM u)[0m, then [47mTEST[0m is treated as an
  ``implicit hypothesis''.  For discussion of this relatively
  advanced feature, see [meta-implicit-hypothesis].

  Additional hypotheses are supported, called ``meta-extract
  hypotheses''.  These allow metafunctions to depend on the validity
  of certain terms extracted from the context or the logical [world].
  These hypotheses provide a relatively advanced form of metatheorem
  so we explain them elsewhere; see [meta-extract].

  One might think that metafunctions and (if supplied) hypothesis
  metafunctions must be executable: that is, not constrained (i.e.,
  introduced in the [signature] of [47m[encapsulate][0m [events]), and not
  [declare]d [47m:[0m[47m[non-executable][0m.  After all, there is no point in
  installing a simplifier that cannot be run!  However, such a
  restriction is not enforced, because one could introduce a
  metafunction using [47m[encapsulate][0m and then use [47m[defattach][0m to attach
  it to an executable function; see [defattach].

  We defer discussion of the case in which there is a hypothesis
  metafunction and for now address the case in which the other two
  hypotheses are present.

  In the discussion below, we refer to the argument, [47mx[0m, of [47mfn[0m and
  [47mhyp-fn[0m as a ``(translated) term,'' i.e., an object satisfying the
  ACL2 built-in predicate [47mtermp[0m.  When these metafunctions are
  executed by the simplifier, they will be applied to (the quotations
  of) terms.  But during the proof of the metatheorem itself, [47mx[0m may
  not be the quotation of a term.  If the [47m[pseudo-termp][0m hypothesis
  is omitted, [47mx[0m may be any object.  Even with the [47m[pseudo-termp][0m
  hypothesis, [47mx[0m may merely ``look like a term'' but use non-function
  symbols or function symbols of incorrect arity.  In any case, the
  metatheorem is stronger than necessary to allow us to apply the
  metafunctions to terms, as we do in the discussion below.  We
  return later to the question of proving the metatheorem.

  Suppose the general form of the metatheorem above is proved with the
  [47m[pseudo-termp][0m and [47m[alistp][0m hypotheses.  Then when the simplifier
  encounters a term, [47m(h t1 ... tn)[0m, that begins with a function
  symbol, [47mh[0m, listed in [47m:trigger-fns[0m, it applies the metafunction, [47mfn[0m,
  to the quotation of the term, i.e., it evaluates [47m(fn '(h t1 ...
  tn))[0m to obtain some result, which can be written as [47m'val[0m.  If [47m'val[0m
  is different from [47m'(h t1 ... tn)[0m and [47mval[0m is a well-formed term (see
  the next paragraph), then [47m(h t1 ... tn)[0m is replaced by [47mval[0m, which
  is then passed along for further rewriting.  Because the
  metatheorem establishes the correctness of [47mfn[0m for all terms (even
  non-terms!), there is no restriction on which function symbols are
  listed in the [47m:trigger-fns[0m.  Generally, of course, they should be
  the symbols that head up the terms simplified by the metafunction
  [47mfn[0m.

  Note that the result of applying a metafunction (or a hypothesis
  metafunction) must be a term, i.e., must satisfy the predicate
  [47m[termp][0m in the current logical [world].  If not, then an error
  occurs.  See [term-table] for how one obtains some assistance
  towards testing that [47mval[0m is indeed a term.  ACL2 actually enforces
  a stronger requirement, disallowing calls of certain ``forbidden''
  function symbols as described in [47m[set-skip-meta-termp-checks][0m.
  Both of these runtime checks can be avoided by proving that the
  metafunction (and the corresponding hypothesis metafunction, if
  any) always produces an acceptable term.  See
  [well-formedness-guarantee].  Alternatively, with an active trust
  tag (see [defttag]) you can tell ACL2 to skip these tests; see
  [47m[set-skip-meta-termp-checks][0m.

  The ``evaluator'' function, [47mev[0m, is a function that can evaluate a
  certain class of expressions, namely, all of those composed of
  variables, constants, and applications of a fixed, finite set of
  function symbols, [47mg1[0m, ..., [47mgk[0m.  Generally speaking, the set of
  function symbols handled by [47mev[0m is chosen to be exactly the function
  symbols recognized and manipulated by the metafunctions being
  introduced.  For example, if [47mfn[0m manipulates expressions in which
  [47m'[0m[47m[equal][0m and [47m'[0m[47m[binary-append][0m occur as function symbols, then [47mev[0m is
  generally specified to handle [47m[equal][0m and [47m[binary-append][0m.  The
  actual requirements on [47mev[0m become clear when the metatheorem is
  proved.  The standard way to introduce an evaluator is to use the
  ACL2 macro [47m[defevaluator][0m, though this is not strictly necessary.
  See [defevaluator] if you want details.

  [Aside for the logic-minded.] Why are we justified in using
  metafunctions this way?  Suppose [47m(fn 'term1)[0m is [47m'term2[0m.  What
  justifies replacing [47mterm1[0m by [47mterm2[0m?  The first step is to assert
  that [47mterm1[0m is [47m(ev 'term1 a)[0m, where [47ma[0m is an alist that maps [47m'var[0m to
  [47mvar[0m, for each variable [47mvar[0m in [47mterm1[0m.  This step is incorrect,
  because [47m'term1[0m may contain function symbols other than the ones,
  [47mg1[0m, ..., [47mgk[0m, that [47mev[0m knows how to handle.  But we can grow [47mev[0m to a
  ``larger'' evaluator, [47mev*[0m, an evaluator for all of the symbols that
  occur in [47mterm1[0m or [47mterm2[0m.  We can prove that [47mev*[0m satisfies the
  [constraint]s on [47mev[0m, provided no [47m[defaxiom][0m events are adding
  constraints to [47mev[0m (or callers of [47mev[0m, and recursively); ACL2 checks
  this additional property.  Hence, the metatheorem holds for [47mev*[0m in
  place of [47mev[0m, by functional instantiation.  We can then carry out
  the proof of the [equivalence] of [47mterm1[0m and [47mterm2[0m as follows: Fix [47ma[0m
  to be an alist that maps the quotations of the variables of [47mterm1[0m
  and [47mterm2[0m to themselves.  Then,

    term1 = (ev* 'term1 a)      ; (1) by construction of ev* and a
          = (ev* (fn 'term1) a) ; (2) by the metatheorem for ev*
          = (ev* 'term2 a)      ; (3) by evaluation of fn
          = term2               ; (4) by construction of ev* and a

  Note that in line (2) above, where we appeal to the (functional
  instantiation of the) metatheorem, we can relieve its (optional)
  [47m[pseudo-termp][0m and [47m[alistp][0m hypotheses by appealing to the facts
  that [47mterm1[0m is a term and [47ma[0m is an alist by construction.  [End of
  Aside for the logic-minded.]

  There are subtleties related to the notion of ``growing'' [47mev[0m to a
  ``larger'' evaluator, as mentioned in the paragraph just above.
  For corresponding restrictions on [47m:meta[0m rules, see
  [evaluator-restrictions].

  Finally, we turn to the second case, in which there is a hypothesis
  metafunction.  In that case, consider as before what happens when
  the simplifier encounters a term, [47m(h t1 ... tn)[0m, where [47mh[0m is listed
  in [47m:trigger-fns[0m.  This time, after it applies [47mfn[0m to [47m'(h t1 ... tn)[0m
  to obtain the quotation of some new term, [47m'val[0m, it then applies the
  hypothesis metafunction, [47mhyp-fn[0m.  That is, it evaluates [47m(hyp-fn '(h
  t1 ... tn))[0m to obtain some result, which can be written as
  [47m'hyp-val[0m.  If [47mhyp-val[0m is not in fact a term, the metafunction is
  not used.  Provided [47mhyp-val[0m is a term, the simplifier attempts to
  establish (by conventional backchaining) that this term is non-[47mnil[0m
  in the current context.  Note that this backchaining is done just
  as it is done for hypotheses of [rewrite] (and [linear]) rules,
  namely, by rewriting with special attention to calls of certain
  functions including [47m[force][0m, [47m[case-split][0m, [47m[syntaxp][0m, and
  [47m[bind-free][0m.  If this attempt to establish this term fails, then
  the meta rule is not applied.  Otherwise, [47m(h t1...tn)[0m is replaced
  by [47mval[0m as in the previous case (where there was no hypothesis
  metafunction).

  Why is it justified to make this extension to the case of hypothesis
  metafunctions?  First, note that the rule

    (implies (and (pseudo-termp x)
                  (alistp a)
                  (ev (hyp-fn x) a))
             (equal (ev x a)
                    (ev (fn x) a)))

  is logically equivalent to the rule

    (implies (and (pseudo-termp x)
                  (alistp a))
             (equal (ev x a)
                    (ev (new-fn x) a)))

  where [47m(new-fn x)[0m is defined to be [47m(list 'if (hyp-fn x) (fn x) x)[0m.
  (If we're careful, we realize that this argument depends on making
  an extension of [47mev[0m to an evaluator [47mev*[0m that handles [47m[if][0m and the
  functions manipulated by [47mhyp-fn[0m.)  If we write [47m'term[0m for the
  quotation of the present term, and if [47m(hyp-fn 'term)[0m and [47m(fn 'term)[0m
  are both terms, say [47mhyp1[0m and [47mterm1[0m, then by the previous argument
  we know it is sound to rewrite term to [47m(if hyp1 term1 term)[0m.  But
  since we have established in the current context that [47mhyp1[0m is
  non-[47mnil[0m, we may simplify [47m(if hyp1 term1 term)[0m to [47mterm1[0m, as desired.

  We now discuss the role of the [47m[pseudo-termp][0m hypothesis.
  [47m(Pseudo-termp x)[0m checks that [47mx[0m has the shape of a term.  Roughly
  speaking, it ensures that [47mx[0m is a symbol, a quoted constant, or a
  true list consisting of a [47mlambda[0m expression or symbol followed by
  some pseudo-terms.  Among the properties of terms not checked by
  [47m[pseudo-termp][0m are that variable symbols never begin with
  ampersand, [47mlambda[0m expressions are closed, and function symbols are
  applied to the correct number of arguments.  See [pseudo-termp].

  There are two possible roles for [47m[pseudo-termp][0m in the development of
  a metatheorem: it may be used as the [guard] of the metafunction
  and/or hypothesis metafunction and it may be used as a hypothesis
  of the metatheorem.  Generally speaking, the [47m[pseudo-termp][0m
  hypothesis is included in a metatheorem only if it makes it easier
  to prove.  The choice is yours.  (An extreme example of this is
  when the metatheorem is invalid without the hypothesis!)  We
  therefore address ourselves the question: should a metafunction
  have a [47m[pseudo-termp][0m [guard]?  A [47m[pseudo-termp][0m [guard] for a
  metafunction, in connection with other considerations described
  below, improves the efficiency with which the metafunction is used
  by the simplifier.

  To make a metafunction maximally efficient you should (a) provide it
  with a [47m[pseudo-termp][0m [guard] and exploit the [guard] when possible
  in coding the body of the function (see [guards-and-evaluation],
  especially the section on efficiency issues), (b) verify the
  [guard]s of the metafunction (see [verify-guards]), and (c) compile
  the metafunction (see [comp]).  When these three steps have been
  taken the simplifier can evaluate [47m(fn 'term1)[0m by running the
  compiled ``primary code'' (see [guards-and-evaluation]) for [47mfn[0m
  directly in Common Lisp.  (Note however that explicit compilation
  may be suppressed; see [compilation].)

  Before discussing efficiency issues further, let us review for a
  moment the general case in which we wish to evaluate [47m(fn 'obj)[0m for
  some [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m function.  We must first ask whether the [guard]s of
  [47mfn[0m have been verified.  If not, we must evaluate [47mfn[0m by executing
  its logic definition.  This effectively checks the [guard]s of
  every subroutine and so can be slow.  If, on the other hand, the
  [guard]s of [47mfn[0m have been verified, then we can run the primary code
  for [47mfn[0m, provided [47m'obj[0m satisfies the [guard] of [47mfn[0m.  So we must next
  evaluate the [guard] of [47mfn[0m on [47m'obj[0m.  If the [guard] is met, then we
  run the primary code for [47mfn[0m, otherwise we run the logic code.

  Now in the case of a metafunction for which the three steps above
  have been followed, we know the [guard] is (implied by)
  [47m[pseudo-termp][0m and that it has been verified.  Furthermore, we know
  without checking that the [guard] is met (because [47mterm1[0m is a term
  and hence [47m'term1[0m is a [47m[pseudo-termp][0m).  Hence, we can use the
  compiled primary code directly.

  We strongly recommend that you compile your metafunctions, as well as
  all their subroutines (unless explicit compilation is suppressed;
  see [compilation]).  Guard verification is also recommended.

  Finally, we present a very simple example of the use of [47m:meta[0m rules,
  based on one provided by Robert Krug.  This example illustrates a
  trick for avoiding undesired rewriting after applying a
  metafunction or any other form of rewriting.  To elaborate: in
  general, the term [47mt2[0m obtained by applying a metafunction to a term
  [47mt1[0m is then handed immediately to the rewriter, which descends
  recursively through the arguments of function calls to rewrite [47mt2[0m
  completely.  But if [47mt2[0m shares a lot of structure with [47mt1[0m, then it
  might not be worthwhile to rewrite [47mt2[0m immediately.  (A rewrite of
  [47mt2[0m will occur anyhow the next time a goal is generated.)  The trick
  involves avoiding this rewrite by wrapping [47mt2[0m inside a call of
  [47m[hide][0m, which in turn is inside a call of a user-defined
  ``unhiding'' function, [47munhide[0m.

    (defun unhide (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      x)

    (defthm unhide-hide
      (equal (unhide (hide x))
             x)
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :expand ((hide x)))))

    (in-theory (disable unhide))

    (defun my-plus (x y)
      (+ x y))

    (in-theory (disable my-plus))

    (defevaluator evl evl-list
      ((my-plus x y)
       (binary-+ x y)
       (unhide x)
       (hide x)))

    (defun meta-fn (term)
      (declare (xargs :guard (pseudo-termp term)))
      (if (and (consp term)
               (equal (length term) 3)
               (equal (car term) 'my-plus))
          `(UNHIDE (HIDE (BINARY-+ ,(cadr term) ,(caddr term))))
        term))

    (defthm my-meta-lemma
      (equal (evl term a)
             (evl (meta-fn term) a))
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable my-plus)))
      :rule-classes ((:meta :trigger-fns (my-plus))))

  Notice that in the following (silly) conjecture, ACL2 initially does
  only does the simplification directed by the metafunction; a second
  goal is generated before the commutativity of addition can be
  applied.  If the above calls of [47mUNHIDE[0m and [47mHIDE[0m had been stripped
  off, then [47mGoal'[0m would have been the term printed in [47mGoal''[0m below.

    ACL2 !>(thm
            (equal (my-plus b a)
                   ccc))

    This simplifies, using the :meta rule MY-META-LEMMA and the :rewrite
    rule UNHIDE-HIDE, to

    Goal'
    (EQUAL (+ B A) CCC).

    This simplifies, using the :rewrite rule COMMUTATIVITY-OF-+, to

    Goal''
    (EQUAL (+ A B) CCC).

  The discussion above probably suffices to make good use of this
  [47m(UNHIDE (HIDE ...))[0m trick.  However, we invite the reader who
  wishes to understand the trick in depth to evaluate the following
  form before submitting the [47m[thm][0m form above.

    (trace$ (rewrite :entry (list (take 2 arglist))
                     :exit (list (car values)))
            (rewrite-with-lemma :entry (list (take 2 arglist))
                                :exit (take 2 values)))

  The following annotated subset of the trace output (which may appear
  a bit different depending on the underlying Common Lisp
  implementation) explains how the trick works.

        2> (REWRITE ((MY-PLUS B A) NIL))>
          3> (REWRITE-WITH-LEMMA
                  ((MY-PLUS B A)
                   (REWRITE-RULE (:META MY-META-LEMMA)
                                 1822
                                 NIL EQUAL META-FN NIL META NIL NIL)))>

    We apply the meta rule, then recursively rewrite the result, which is the
    (UNHIDE (HIDE ...)) term shown just below.

            4> (REWRITE ((UNHIDE (HIDE (BINARY-+ B A)))
                         ((A . A) (B . B))))>
              5> (REWRITE ((HIDE (BINARY-+ B A))
                           ((A . A) (B . B))))>

    The HIDE protects its argument from being touched by the rewriter.

              <5 (REWRITE (HIDE (BINARY-+ B A)))>
              5> (REWRITE-WITH-LEMMA
                      ((UNHIDE (HIDE (BINARY-+ B A)))
                       (REWRITE-RULE (:REWRITE UNHIDE-HIDE)
                                     1806 NIL EQUAL (UNHIDE (HIDE X))
                                     X ABBREVIATION NIL NIL)))>

    Now we apply UNHIDE-HIDE, then recursively rewrite its right-hand
    side in an environment where X is bound to (BINARY-+ B A).

                6> (REWRITE (X ((X BINARY-+ B A))))>

    Notice that at this point X is cached, so REWRITE just returns
    (BINARY-+ B A).

                <6 (REWRITE (BINARY-+ B A))>
              <5 (REWRITE-WITH-LEMMA T (BINARY-+ B A))>
            <4 (REWRITE (BINARY-+ B A))>
          <3 (REWRITE-WITH-LEMMA T (BINARY-+ B A))>
        <2 (REWRITE (BINARY-+ B A))>


