  world is the one stored in the metafunction context, [47mmfc[0m, not in
  the input [47mstate[0m.

      CASE [47mobj[0m = (list :typeset TERM ...):

      The value is the value of [47m(typespec-check ts TERM)[0m, where [47mts[0m is the
      value of [47m(mfc-ts TERM mfc state :forcep nil :ttreep nil)[0m, and
      where [47m(typespec-check ts val)[0m is defined to be true when [47mval[0m
      has type-set [47mts[0m.  (Exception: If [47mval[0m satisfies [47mbad-atom[0m then
      [47mtypespec-check[0m is true when [47mts[0m is negative.)

      CASE [47mobj[0m = (list :rw+ TERM ALIST OBJ EQUIV ...):

      We assume below that [47mEQUIV[0m is a symbol that represents an equivalence
      relation, where [47mnil[0m represents [47m[equal][0m, [47mt[0m represents [47m[iff][0m, and
      otherwise [47mEQUIV[0m represents itself (an equivalence relation in
      the current logical [world]).  For any other [47mEQUIV[0m the value is
      [47m*t*[0m.  Now let [47mrhs[0m be the value of [47m(mfc-rw+ TERM ALIST OBJ EQUIV
      mfc state :forcep nil :ttreep nil)[0m.  Then the value is the term
      [47m(list 'equv (sublis-var ALIST TERM) rhs)[0m, where equv is the
      equivalence relation represented by [47mEQUIV[0m, and [47msublis-var[0m is
      defined to substitute a variable-binding alist into a term.

      CASE [47mobj[0m = (list :rw TERM OBJ EQUIV ...):

      The value is the same as above but for an [47mALIST[0m of [47mnil[0m, i.e., for the
      case that [47mobj[0m is [47m(list :rw+ TERM nil OBJ EQUIV ...)[0m.

      CASE [47mobj[0m = (list :ap TERM ...):

      The value is [47m(list 'not TERM)[0m if [47m(mfc-ap TERM mfc state :forcep nil)[0m
      is true, else is [47m*t*[0m.

      CASE [47mobj[0m = (list :relieve-hyp HYP ALIST RUNE TARGET BKPTR ...):

      The value is [47m(sublis-var alist hyp)[0m --- see above for a discussion of
      [47msublis-var[0m --- if the following is true.

        (mfc-relieve-hyp hyp alist rune target bkptr mfc state
                         :forcep nil :ttreep nil)

      Otherwise the value is [47m*t*[0m.

      CASE otherwise:

      If no case above applies, then the value is [47m*t*[0m.

  We conclude by considering the fourth of the four forms above (and
  implicitly, its special case represented by the third form above):

    (evl (meta-extract-global-fact+ obj st state) aa)

  The discussion above is for the function [47mmeta-extract-global-fact+[0m,
  but assumes that the logical [world]s of [47mst[0m and [47mstate[0m are equal;
  otherwise the value returned is [47m*t*[0m.  Of course, since a call of
  [47mmeta-extract-global-fact[0m expands to a corresponding call of
  [47mmeta-extract-global-fact+[0m in which the last two arguments are both
  [47mstate[0m, that condition holds automatically for that case.  But the
  [47mstate[0m mentioned in the meta-extract hypotheses of a [meta] rule or
  [clause-processor] rule is in essence an initial state.  In the
  case of a clause-processor rule, the clause-processor function may
  modify that initial state (say, by printing or modifying some state
  globals) without changing its world, and then pass that modified
  state to [47mfncall-term[0m.  While [47mfncall-term[0m may produce a different
  result for this modified state than for the initial state, both are
  valid: other than its world, the state is used only for heuristic
  purposes, such as determining whether guard-checking may cause an
  error.  A useful instance of the hypothesis displayed above will be
  one in which [47mst[0m is that modified state.")
