 (MIXED-MODE-FUNCTIONS
  (APPLY$)
  "[47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions can [47m[apply$][0m [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions

  Because [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions can be given badges it is possible
  to [47m[apply$][0m them from within [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions.
  Colloquially, we call such [47m:logic[0m mode functions ``mixed mode,''
  but that is a misnomer.  They are indisputably in [47m:logic[0m mode.

  First, note that the only way to introduce a [47m:program[0m mode function
  into [47m:logic[0m mode functions is to use the quoted [47m:program[0m mode
  function name in an argument slot of [ilk] [47m:FN[0m, use the [47m:program[0m
  mode function in a quoted [47m[lambda][0m object or [47m[lambda$][0m expression
  in a slot of [ilk] [47m:FN[0m or use it in a quoted expression in a slot
  of ilk [47m:EXPR[0m.  We do not allow [47m:program[0m mode functions to be called
  directly from [47m:logic[0m mode functions.  For example, if [47mprgm[0m is
  defined as a [47m:program[0m mode function of one argument, and has been
  assigned a badge by [47m[defbadge][0m

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :mode :logic))
      (apply$ 'prgm (list x)))

  is legal but

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :mode :logic))
      (prgm x))

  is not.

  Second, the presence of a [47m:program[0m mode function in a [47m:logic[0m mode
  function prohibits the [47m:logic[0m mode function from being guard
  verified.

  Mixed-mode functions raise interesting questions for top-level
  evaluation and evaluation and rewriting during proofs.

  When a [47m:program[0m mode function is [47mapply$[0med, it is always done in
  [47m[safe-mode][0m.  In general, evaluating a [47m:program[0m mode function at
  the top-level can cause hard Lisp errors.  For example,

    (defun prgm (x) (declare (xargs :mode :program)) (car x))

  Then [47m(prgm 23)[0m causes a hard Lisp error in both CCL and SBCL, but
  [47m(prgm 'abc)[0m returns numbers in both of those Common Lisps, but they
  return different numbers.  Furthermore, there is no guarantee
  across all Common Lisps that [47m(prgm 'abc)[0m will always return the
  same number throughout a given ACL2 session; the value could
  conceivably change as memory is allocated, compacted, garbage
  collected, etc., since according to the CLTL standard, one is not
  supposed to apply [47mCAR[0m to any symbol other than [47mNIL[0m but no error
  need be signaled.  It is likely that a CLTL implementation of [47mCAR[0m
  just accesses memory where the [47mCAR[0m component of a cons is supposed
  to be!

  We tolerate such behavior when [47m:program[0m mode functions are directly
  called at the top-level because there are no axioms about them and
  we regard the evaluation of such programs from within the ACL2 loop
  as just a convenience to the user.

  But [47mapply$[0m is a [47m:logic[0m mode function and we must guarantee that when
  any [47m:logic[0m mode function is evaluated functional substitutivity
  holds: identical calls must yield identical results.  That is,
  [47mapply$[0m must behave like a [3mfunction[0m and not give different answers
  to the same questions over time when errors are not signaled.  We
  also strive to achieve the goal that [47m:logic[0m mode functions never
  cause hard Lisp errors other than resource errors like stack
  overflow or memory exhaustion.  So when [47mapply$[0m is given a badged
  [47m:program[0m mode function, e.g., had we badged [47mprgm[0m and then evaluated
  [47m(apply$ 'prgm '(abc))[0m, it must at least return the same ACL2 object
  every time!  To achieve this end [47mapply$[0m runs [47m:program[0m mode
  functions in [47m[safe-mode][0m.  (Safe mode does shift into raw Lisp on
  calls of guard verified [47m:logic[0m mode functions which might be called
  from within the [47m:program[0m mode function.  But a mixed mode function
  cannot be guard verified because the [47m:program[0m mode functions used
  within it cannot be guard verified.)

  This means that a top-level call of a mixed mode function generally
  runs slower than a corresponding call of an otherwise identical
  [47m:program[0m mode function.  (And, on the positive side, it means that
  mixed mode functions actually behave like [3mfunctions[0m while [47m:program[0m
  mode ones may not!)  The only way to speed up a mixed-mode function
  is to convert the [47m:program[0m mode functions in it to [47m:logic[0m mode with
  [47m[verify-termination][0m and verify the guards.

  As for proofs, since there are no axioms about [47m:program[0m mode
  functions, if a mixed-mode function is expanded in a proof all
  [47m:program[0m mode functions in it are treated as though they are
  undefined.  In particular, the absence of a warrant on the [47m:program[0m
  mode function [47mprgm[0m means that [47m(apply$ 'prgm '(abc))[0m, is not
  evaluated by the prover despite the fact that it is a ground term.")
