 (MUST-BE-EQUAL
  (MBE ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Attach code for execution

  The form [47m(must-be-equal logic exec)[0m evaluates to [47mlogic[0m in the ACL2
  logic but evaluates to [47mexec[0m in raw Lisp.  The point is to be able
  to write one definition to reason about logically but another for
  evaluation.  Please see [mbe] and see [mbt] for appropriate macros
  to use, rather than calling [47mmust-be-equal[0m directly, since it is
  easy to commute the arguments of [47mmust-be-equal[0m by accident.

  In essence, the guard for [47m(must-be-equal x y)[0m is [47m(equal x y)[0m.
  However, note that [47mmust-be-equal[0m is a macro: [47m(must-be-equal logic
  exec)[0m expands to [47m(mbe1 exec logic)[0m, which expands to a call of
  [47m[return-last][0m.")
 (MUTUAL-RECURSION
  (EVENTS PROGRAMMING DEFUN)
  "Define some mutually recursive functions

  See [defun] for relevant background.

    Example:
    (mutual-recursion
     (defun evenlp (x)
       (if (consp x) (oddlp (cdr x)) t))
     (defun oddlp (x)
       (if (consp x) (evenlp (cdr x)) nil)))

    General Form:
    (mutual-recursion def1 ... defn)

  where each [47mdefi[0m is a call of [47m[defun][0m, [47m[defund][0m, [47m[defun-nx][0m, or
  [47mdefund-nx[0m.  Note that although one definition is acceptable, we
  focus on the case of at least two definitions, since normally one
  would not bother with [47mmutual-recursion[0m otherwise.

  When mutually recursive functions are introduced it is necessary to
  do the termination analysis on the entire clique of definitions.
  Each [47m[defun][0m form specifies its own measure, either with the
  [47m:measure[0m keyword [47mxarg[0m (see [xargs]) or by default to [47m[ACL2-count][0m.
  When a function in the clique calls a function in the clique, the
  measure of the callee's actuals must be smaller than the measure of
  the caller's formals --- just as in the case of a simply recursive
  function.  But with mutual recursion, the callee's actuals are
  measured as specified by the callee's [47m[defun][0m while the caller's
  formals are measured as specified by the caller's [47m[defun][0m.  These
  two measures may be different but must be comparable in the sense
  that [47m[o<][0m decreases through calls.

  If you want to specify [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m or [47m:guard-hints[0m (see [xargs]), you
  can put them in the [47m[xargs][0m declaration of any of the [47m[defun][0m
  forms, as the [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m from each form will be appended together, as
  will the [47m:[0m[47m[guard-hints][0m from each form.

  However, for the following [47m[xargs][0m declarations, listed
  alphabetically, it is illegal to specify more than one value,
  though it is legal to specify the same value more than once.

    :GUARD-DEBUG
    :GUARD-SIMPLIFY
    :LOOP$-RECURSION
    :MEASURE-DEBUG
    :MODE
    :NON-EXECUTABLE
    :NORMALIZE
    :OTF-FLG
    :SPLIT-TYPES
    :VERIFY-GUARDS
    :WELL-FOUNDED-RELATION

  Thus, for example, you may specify [47m:guard-debug t[0m in two different
  [47mdefun[0m forms in your [47mmutual-recursion[0m call, in which case the
  [guard-debug] feature will be active; but you must not specify
  [47m:guard-debug t[0m in one [47mdefun[0m but [47m:guard-debug nil[0m in another.  It
  suffices to specify such a value once, as that will apply
  throughout analysis of the [47mmutual-recursion[0m form.

  You may find it helpful to use a lexicographic order, the idea being
  to have a measure that returns a list of two arguments, where the
  first takes priority over the second.  Here is an example.

    (include-book \"ordinals/lexicographic-ordering\" :dir :system)

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (set-well-founded-relation l<) ; will be treated as LOCAL

     (mutual-recursion
      (defun foo (x)
        (declare (xargs :measure (list (acl2-count x) 1)))
        (bar x))
      (defun bar (y)
        (declare (xargs :measure (list (acl2-count y) 0)))
        (if (zp y) y (foo (1- y))))))

  The [47m[guard][0m analysis must also be done for all of the functions at
  the same time.  If any one of the [47m[defun][0ms specifies the
  [47m:[0m[47m[verify-guards][0m [47mxarg[0m to be [47mnil[0m, then [guard] verification is
  omitted for all of the functions.  Similarly, if any one of the
  [47m[defun][0ms specifies the [47m:non-executable[0m [47mxarg[0m to be [47mt[0m, or if any of
  the definitions uses [47m[defun-nx][0m or [47mdefund-nx[0m, then every one of the
  definitions will be treated as though it specifies a
  [47m:non-executable[0m [47mxarg[0m of [47mt[0m.

  Technical Note: Each [47mdefi[0m above must be a call of [47m[defun][0m, [47m[defund][0m,
  [47m[defun-nx][0m, or [47mdefund-nx[0m.  In particular, it is not permitted for a
  [47mdefi[0m to be an arbitrary form that macroexpands into a [47m[defun][0m form.
  This is because [47mmutual-recursion[0m is itself a macro, and since
  macroexpansion occurs from the outside in, at the time
  [47m(mutual-recursion def1 ... defk)[0m is expanded the [47mdefi[0m have not yet
  been macroexpanded.

  Suppose you have defined your own [47m[defun][0m-like macro and wish to use
  it in a [47mmutual-recursion[0m expression.  Well, you can't.  (!)  But
  you can define your own version of [47mmutual-recursion[0m that allows
  your [47m[defun][0m-like form.  Here is an example.  Suppose you define

    (defmacro my-defun (&rest args) (my-defun-fn args))

  where [47mmy-defun-fn[0m takes the arguments of the [47mmy-defun[0m form and
  produces from them a [47m[defun][0m form.  As noted above, you are not
  allowed to write [47m(mutual-recursion (my-defun ...) ...)[0m.  But you
  can define the macro [47mmy-mutual-recursion[0m so that

    (my-mutual-recursion (my-defun ...) ... (my-defun ...))

  expands into [47m(mutual-recursion (defun ...) ... (defun ...))[0m by
  applying [47mmy-defun-fn[0m to each of the arguments of
  [47mmy-mutual-recursion[0m.

    (defun my-mutual-recursion-fn (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard (alistp lst)))

    ; Each element of lst must be a consp (whose car, we assume, is always
    ; MY-DEFUN).  We apply my-defun-fn to the arguments of each element and
    ; collect the resulting list of DEFUNs.

      (cond ((atom lst) nil)
            (t (cons (my-defun-fn (cdr (car lst)))
                     (my-mutual-recursion-fn (cdr lst))))))

    (defmacro my-mutual-recursion (&rest lst)

    ; Each element of lst must be a consp (whose car, we assume, is always
    ; MY-DEFUN).  We obtain the DEFUN corresponding to each and list them
    ; all inside a MUTUAL-RECURSION form.

      (declare (xargs :guard (alistp lst)))
      (cons 'mutual-recursion (my-mutual-recursion-fn lst))).


