  "Check for duplicates in a list

    General Forms:
    (no-duplicatesp x)
    (no-duplicatesp x :test 'eql)   ; same as above (eql as equality test)
    (no-duplicatesp x :test 'eq)    ; same, but eq is equality test
    (no-duplicatesp x :test 'equal) ; same, but equal is equality test

  [47m(no-duplicatesp lst)[0m is true if and only if no member of [47mlst[0m occurs
  twice in [47mlst[0m.  The optional keyword, [47m:TEST[0m, has no effect
  logically, but provides the test (default [47m[eql][0m) used for comparing
  elements of [47mlst[0m.

  The [guard] for a call of [47mno-duplicatesp[0m depends on the test.  In all
  cases, the argument must satisfy [47m[true-listp][0m.  If the test is
  [47m[eql][0m, then the argument must satisfy [47m[eqlable-listp][0m.  If the test
  is [47m[eq][0m, then the argument must satisfy [47m[symbol-listp][0m.

  See [equality-variants] for a discussion of the relation between
  [47mno-duplicatesp[0m and its variants:

      [47m(no-duplicatesp-eq x lst)[0m is equivalent to [47m(no-duplicatesp x lst
      :test 'eq)[0m;

      [47m(no-duplicatesp-equal x lst)[0m is equivalent to [47m(no-duplicatesp x lst
      :test 'equal)[0m.

  In particular, reasoning about any of these primitives reduces to
  reasoning about the function [47mno-duplicatesp-equal[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<no-duplicatesp-equal>

    (defun no-duplicatesp-equal (l)
