 (NOTE-1-1
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "Acl2 Version 1.1 Notes

  The new features are extensively documented.  The relevant topics
  are:

  It is especially important to read all of the [documentation] for
  [books] before trying to use books.  However, the new [47m:more[0m keyword
  command is so handy for reading long [documentation] strings that
  we recommend you start with [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m more if reading at the terminal.
  Some documentation has been written for [guard]s which you might
  find interesting.")
 (NOTE-1-2
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "Acl2 Version 1.2 Notes

  Hacker mode has been eliminated and [programming] mode has been
  added.  [Programming] mode is unsound but does syntax checking and
  permits redefinitions of names.  See [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m [47mload-mode[0m and [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m
  [47mg-mode[0m.

  The arguments to [47m[ld][0m have changed.  [47m[Ld][0m is now much more
  sophisticated.  See [ld].

  For those occasions on which you wish to look at a large list
  structure that you are afraid to print, try [47m(walkabout x state)[0m,
  where [47mx[0m is an Acl2 expression that evaluates to the structure in
  question.  I am afraid there is no [documentation] yet, but it is
  similar in spirit to the Interlisp structure editor.  You are
  standing on an object and commands move you around in it.  E.g., 1
  moves you to its first element, 2 to its second, etc.; 0 moves you
  up to its parent; [47mnx[0m and [47mbk[0m move you to its next sibling and
  previous sibling; [47mpp[0m prettyprints it; [47m[q][0m exits returning [47mnil[0m; [47m[=][0m
  exits returning the thing you're standing on; [47m(= symb)[0m assigns the
  thing you're standing on to the [state] global variable [47msymb[0m.

  Several new [hints] have been implemented, including [47m:by[0m and [47m:do-not[0m.
  The old [47m:do-not-generalize[0m has been scrapped in favor of such new
  [hints] as [47m:do-not[0m [47m(generalize elim)[0m.  [47m:By[0m lets you say ``this goal
  is subsumed by'' a given lemma instance.  The [47m:by[0m hint also lets
  you say ``this goal can't be proved yet but skip it and see how the
  rest of the proof goes.'' See [hints].")
 (NOTE-1-3
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "Acl2 Version 1.3 Notes

  [Programming] mode has been eliminated.  Instead, all functions have
  a ``color'' which indicates what can be done with the function.
  For example, [47m:red[0m functions can be executed but have no axioms
  describing them.  Thus, [47m:red[0m functions can be introduced after
  passing a simple syntactic check and they can be redefined without
  undoing.  But nothing of consequence can be proved about them.  At
  the other extreme are [47m:gold[0m functions which can be executed and
  which also have passed both the termination and the [guard]
  verification proofs.  The color of a function can be specified with
  the new [47m[xargs][0m keyword, [47m:color[0m, which, if omitted defaults to the
  global setting of [47mld-color[0m.  [47mLd-color[0m replaces [47mload-mode[0m.  Setting
  [47mld-color[0m to [47m:red[0m causes behavior similar to the old [47m:g-mode[0m.
  Setting [47mld-color[0m to [47m:gold[0m causes behavior similar to the old
  [47m:v-mode[0m.  It is possible to prototype your system in [47m:red[0m and then
  convert [47m:red[0m functions to [47m:[0m[47mblue[0m individually by calling
  [47m[verify-termination][0m on them.  They can then be converted to [47m:gold[0m
  with [47m[verify-guards][0m.  This allows us to undertake to verify the
  termination and [guard]s of system functions.  See [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m color for
  an introduction to the use of colors.

  Type prescription rules have been added.  Recall that in Nqthm, some
  [47m[rewrite][0m rules were actually stored as ``[type-prescription]s.''
  Such rules allow the user to inform Nqthm's primitive type
  mechanism as to the kinds of shells returned by a function.
  Earlier versions of Acl2 did not have an analogous kind of rule
  because Acl2's type mechanism is complicated by [guard]s.  Version
  1.3 supports [47m[type-prescription][0m rules.  See [type-prescription].

  Three more new [rule-classes] implement congruence-based rewriting.
  It is possible to identify a binary relation as an equivalence
  relation (see [equivalence]), to show that one equivalence relation
  refines another (see [refinement]) and to show that a given
  equivalence relation is maintained when rewriting a given function
  call, e.g., [47m(fn ...xk...)[0m, by maintaining another equivalence
  relation while rewriting the [47mk[0mth argument (see [congruence]).  If [47mr[0m
  has been shown to be an [equivalence] relation and then [47m(implies
  hyps (r (foo x) (bar x)))[0m is proved as a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule, then
  instances of [47m(foo x)[0m will be replaced by corresponding instances of
  [47m(bar x)[0m provided the instance occurs in a slot where the
  maintenance of [47mr-equivalence[0m is known to be sufficient and [47mhyps[0m can
  be established as usual.

  In Version 1.2, [rule-classes] were simple keywords, e.g., [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m
  or [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m.  In Version 1.3, [rule-classes] have been elaborated to
  allow you to specify how the theorem ought to be used as a rule.
  That is, the new [rule-classes] allows you to separate the
  mathematical statement of the formula from its interpretation as a
  rule.  See [rule-classes].

  Rules used to be named by symbols, e.g., [47m[car][0m and [47mcar-cons[0m were the
  names of rules.  Unfortunately, this was ambiguous because there
  are three rules associated with function symbols: the symbolic
  definition, the executable-counterpart, and the
  [type-prescription]; many different rules might be associated with
  theorems, depending on the rule classes.  In Version 1.3 rules are
  named by ``[rune]s'' (which is just short hand for ``rule names'').
  Example [rune]s are [47m(:definition car)[0m, [47m(:executable-counterpart
  car)[0m, and [47m(:type-prescription car . 1)[0m.  Every rule added by an
  event has a different name and you can [enable] and [disable] them
  independently.  See [rune] and see [theories].

  The identity function [47m[force][0m, of one argument, has been added and
  given a special interpretation by the functions responsible for
  establishing hypotheses in backchaining: When the system fails to
  establish some hypothesis of the form [47m(force term)[0m, it simply
  assumes it is true and goes on, delaying until later the
  establishment of term.  In particular, pushes a new subgoal to
  prove term in the current context.  When that subgoal is attacked,
  all of the resources of the theorem prover, not just rewriting, are
  brought to bear.  Thus, for example, if you wish to prove the rule
  [47m(implies (good-statep s) (equal (exec s n) s'))[0m and it is your
  expectation that every time [47mexec[0m appears its first argument is a
  [47mgood-statep[0m then you might write the rule as [47m(implies (force
  (good-statep s)) (equal (exec s n) s'))[0m.  This rule is essentially
  an unconditional rewrite of [47m(exec s n)[0m to [47ms'[0m that spawns the new
  goal [47m(good-statep s)[0m.  See [force].  Because you can now specify
  independently how a theorem is used as a rule, you need not write
  the [47m[force][0m in the actual theorem proved.  See [rule-classes].

  Version 1.3 supports a facility similar to Nqthm's [47m[break-lemma][0m.
  See [break-rewrite].  You can install ``[monitor]s'' on [rune]s
  that will cause interactive breaks under certain conditions.

  Acl2 also provides ``[wormhole]s'' which allow you to write functions
  that cause interaction with the user but which do not require that
  you have access to [47m[state][0m.  See [wormhole].

  The rewriter now automatically backchains to stronger recognizers.
  There is no user hook to this feature but it may simplify some
  proofs with which older versions of Acl2 had trouble.  For example,
  if the rewriter is trying to prove [47m(rationalp (foo a b c))[0m it is
  now smart enough to try lemmas that match with [47m(integerp (foo a b
  c))[0m.")
 (NOTE-1-4
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "Acl2 Version 1.4 Notes

  Once again [47m[ld][0m only takes one required argument, as the [47mbind-flg[0m has
  been deleted.

  Three commands have been added in the spirit of [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m.  [47m:[0m[47m[Pe!][0m is
  similar to [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m but it prints all [events] with the given name,
  rather than just the most recent.  The command [47m:[0m[47m[pf][0m prints the
  corollary formula corresponding to a name or [rune].  The command
  [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m (print lemmas) prints rules whose top function symbol is the
  given name.  See [pe!], see [pf], and see [pl].

  Book naming conventions have been changed somewhat.  The
  once-required [47m.lisp[0m extension is now prohibited!  Directories are
  supported, including a notion of ``connected book directory''.  See
  [book-name].  Also, the second argument of [47m[certify-book][0m is now
  optional, defaulting to [47m0[0m.

  [Compilation] is now supported inside the Acl2 loop.  See [comp] and
  see [set-compile-fns].

  The default color is now part of the Acl2 [world]; see [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m
  [47mdefault-color[0m.  [47mLd-color[0m is no longer an [47m[ld][0m special.  Instead,
  colors are [events]; see the documentation for [47mred[0m, [47mpink[0m, [47mblue[0m, and
  [47mgold[0m.

  A [table] exists for controlling whether Acl2 prints comments when it
  [force]s hypotheses of rules; see [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m [47mforce-table[0m.  Also, it is
  now possible to turn off the forcing of assumptions by disabling
  the definition of [force]; see [force].

  The event [47mdefconstant[0m is no longer supported, but a very similar
  event, [47m[defconst][0m, has been provided in its place.  See [defconst].

  The event for defining [congruence] relations is now [47m[defcong][0m
  (formerly, [47mdefcon[0m).

  Patterns are now allowed in [47m:expand[0m [hints].  See the documentation
  for [47m:expand[0m inside the documentation for [hints].

  We have improved the way we report rules used by the simplifier.  All
  [rune]s of the same type are reported together in the running
  commentary associated with each goal, so that for example,
  executable-counterparts are listed separately from definitions, and
  rewrite rules are listed separately from [linear] rules.  The
  preprocessor now mentions ``simple'' rules; see [simple].

  The mechanism for printing warning messages for new rewrite rules,
  related to subsumption, now avoids worrying about nonrecursive
  function symbols when those symbols are [disable]d.  These messages
  have also been eliminated for the case where the old rule is a
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rule.

  Backquote has been modified so that it can usually provide
  predictable results when used on the left side of a rewrite rule.

  Time statistics are now printed even when an event fails.

  The Acl2 trace package has been modified so that it prints using the
  values of the Lisp globals [47m*print-level*[0m and [47m*print-length*[0m
  (respectively).

  [Table] has been modified so that the [47m:clear[0m option lets you replace
  the entire [table] with one that satisfies the [47mval[0m and key guards
  (if any); see [table].

  We have relaxed the translation rules for [47m:measure[0m [hints] to
  [47m[defun][0m, so that the same rules apply to these terms that apply to
  terms in [47m[defthm][0m [events].  In particular, in [47m:measure[0m [hints]
  [47m[mv][0m is treated just like [47m[list][0m, and [47m[state][0m receives no special
  handling.

  The [loop-stopper] test has been relaxed.  The old test required that
  every new argument be strictly less than the corresponding old
  argument in a certain [term-order].  The new test uses a
  lexicographic order on term lists instead.  For example, consider
  the following rewrite rule.

    (equal
     (variable-update var1
                      val1 (variable-update var2 val2 vs))
     (variable-update var2
                      val2 (variable-update var1 val1 vs)))

  This rule is permutative.  Now imagine that we want to apply this
  rule to the term

    (variable-update u y (variable-update u x vs)).

  Since the actual corresponding to both [47mvar1[0m and [47mvar2[0m is [47mu[0m, which is
  not strictly less than itself in the [term-order], this rule would
  fail to be applied in this situation when using the old test.
  However, since the pair [47m(u x)[0m is lexicographically less than the
  pair [47m(u y)[0m with respect to our [term-order], the rule is in fact
  applied using our new test.

  Messages about [events] now contain a space after certain left
  parentheses, in order to assist emacs users.  For example, the
  event

    (defthm abc (equal (+ (len x) 0) (len x)))

  leads to a [summary] containing the line

    Form:  ( DEFTHM ABC ...)

  and hence, if you search backwards for ``[47m(defthm abc[0m'', you won't
  stop at this message.

  More tautology checking is done during a proof; in fact, no goal
  printed to the screen, except for the results of applying [47m:use[0m and
  [47m:by[0m [hints] or the top-level goals from an induction proof, are
  known to Acl2 to be tautologies.

  The [47m[ld-query-control-alist][0m may now be used to suppress printing of
  queries; see [ld-query-control-alist].

  Warning messages are printed with short summary strings, for example
  the string ``[47mUse[0m'' in the following message.

    ACL2 Warning [Use] in ( THM ...):  It is unusual to :USE the formula
    of an enabled :REWRITE or :DEFINITION rule, so you may want to consider
    disabling (:REWRITE FOO) in the hint provided for Goal.  See :DOC using-
    enabled-rules.

  At the end of the event, just before the time is printed, all such
  summary strings are printed out.

  The keyword command [47m:u[0m has been introduced as an abbreviation for
  [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m [47m:[0m[47m[max][0m.  Printing of query messages is suppressed by [47m:u[0m.

  The keyword [47m:cheat[0m is no longer supported by any event form.

  Some irrelevant formals are detected; see [irrelevant-formals].

  A bug in the application of metafunctions was fixed: now if the
  output of a metafunction is equal to its input, the application of
  the metafunction is deemed unsuccessful and the next metafunction
  is tried.

  An example has been added to the documentation for [equivalence] to
  suggest how to make use of [equivalence] relations in rewriting.

  The following Common Lisp functions have been added to Acl2:
  [47m[alpha-char-p][0m, [47m[upper-case-p][0m, [47m[lower-case-p][0m, [47m[char-upcase][0m,
  [47m[char-downcase][0m, [47m[string-downcase][0m, [47m[string-upcase][0m, and
  [47mdigit-charp-p[0m.

  A documentation section called [47m[proof-builder][0m has been added for the
  interactive facility, whose documentation has been slightly
  improved.  See in particular the documentation for [proof-builder],
  [47m[verify][0m, and [macro-command].

  A number of [events] that had been inadvertently disallowed in
  [books] are now permitted in [books].  These are: [47m[defcong][0m,
  [47mdefcor[0m, [47m[defequiv][0m, [47m[defrefinement][0m, [47m[defstub][0m, and
  [47m[verify-termination][0m.")
 (NOTE-1-5
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "Acl2 Version 1.5 Notes

  Acl2 now allows ``complex rationals,'' which are complex numbers
  whose real parts are rationals and whose imaginary parts are
  non-zero rationals.  See [complex].

  A new way of handling [47m[force][0md hypotheses has been implemented.
  Rather than cause a case split at the time the [47m[force][0m occurs, we
  complete the main proof and then embark on one or more ``forcing
  rounds'' in which we try to prove the [force]d hypotheses.  See
  [forcing-round].  To allow us to compare the new handling of
  [47m[force][0m with the old, Version 1.5 implements both and uses a flag
  in [47m[state][0m to determine which method should be used.  Do [47m(assign
  old-style-forcing t)[0m if you want [47m[force][0m to be handled as it was in
  Version 1.4.  However, we expect to eliminate the old-style forcing
  eventually because we think the new style is more effective.  To
  see the difference between the two approaches to forcing, try
  proving the associativity of [append] under both settings of
  [47mold-style-forcing[0m.  To get the new behavior invoke:

    (thm (implies (and (true-listp a) (true-listp b))
                  (equal (append (append a b) c)
                         (append a (append b c)))))

  Then [47m(assign old-style-forcing t)[0m and invoke the [47mthm[0m [command] above
  again.

  A new [47m:cases[0m [hints] allows proof by cases.  See [hints].

  [47m[Include-book][0m and [47m[encapsulate][0m now restore the
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m when they complete.  See [include-book] and
  see [encapsulate].

  The [guard]s on many Acl2 primitives defined in [47maxioms.lisp[0m have been
  weakened to permit them to be used in accordance with lisp custom
  and tradition.

  It is possible to attach heuristic filters to [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules to
  limit their applicability.  See [syntaxp].

  A tutorial has been added (but as of Version_3.6.1 it has become
  obsolete).

  [Events] now print the Summary paragraph listing [rune]s used, time,
  etc., whether they succeed or fail.  The format of the ``[failure]
  banner'' has been changed but still has multiple asterisks in it.
  [47mThm[0m also prints a Summary, whether it succeeds or fails; but [47mthm[0m is
  not an event.

  A new event form [47m[skip-proofs][0m has been added; see [skip-proofs].

  A user-specific customization facility has been added in the form of
  a book that is automatically included, if it exists on the current
  directory.  See [ACL2-customization].

  A facility for conditional metalemmas has been implemented; see
  [meta].

  The acceptable values for [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m have changed.  In the old
  version (Version 1.4), a value of [47mt[0m meant that proofs and [47m[local][0m
  [events] are to be skipped.  In Version 1.5, a value of [47mt[0m means
  proofs (but not [47m[local][0m [events]) are to be skipped.  A value of
  [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m means proofs and [47m[local][0m [events] are to be
  skipped.  There are two other, more obscure, acceptable values.
  See [ld-skip-proofsp].

  In order to turn off the forcing of assumptions, one should now
  [disable] the [47m:[0m[47m[executable-counterpart][0m of [47m[force][0m (rather than the
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m of [47m[force][0m, as in the previous release); see [force].

  The macros [47m[enable-forcing][0m and [47m[disable-forcing][0m make it convenient
  to [enable] or [disable] forcing.  See [enable-forcing] and see
  [disable-forcing].

  The new commands [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[pr!][0m print the rules created by an event
  or command.  See [pr] and see [pr!].

  The new [history] [command]s [47m:[0m[47m[puff][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[puff*][0m will replace a
  compound [command] such as an [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[include-book][0m by
  the sequence of [events] in it.  That is, they ``[puff] up'' or
  ``lift'' the subevents of a [command] to the [command] level,
  eliminating the formerly superior [command] and lengthening the
  [history].  This is useful if you want to ``partially undo'' an
  [47m[encapsulate][0m or book or other compound [command] so you can
  experiment.  See [puff] and see [puff*].

  Theory expressions now are allowed to use the free variable [47m[world][0m
  and prohibited from using the free variable [47m[state][0m.  See
  [theories], although it is essentially the same as before except it
  mentions [47m[world][0m instead of [47m[state][0m.  See [world] for a discussion
  of the Acl2 logical [world].  Allowing [47m[in-theory][0m [events] to be
  state-sensitive violated an important invariant about how [books]
  behaved.

  [47m[Table][0m keys and values now are allowed to use the free variable
  [47m[world][0m and prohibited from using the free variable [47m[state][0m.  See
  the note above about theory expressions for some explanation.

  The macro for minus, [47m[-][0m, used to expand [47m(- x 3)[0m to [47m(+ x -3)[0m and now
  expands it to [47m(+ -3 x)[0m instead.  The old macro, if used in the
  left-hand sides of rewrite rules, produced inapplicable rules
  because the constant occurs in the second argument of the [47m[+][0m, but
  potential target terms generally had the constant in the first
  argument position because of the effect of [47mcommutativity-of-+[0m.

  A new class of rule, [47m:linear-alias[0m rules, allows one to implement the
  Nqthm package and similar hacks in which a [disable]d function is
  to be known equivalent to an arithmetic function.

  A new class of rule, [47m:built-in-clause[0m rules, allows one to extend the
  set of clauses proved silently by [47m[defun][0m during measure and
  [guard] processing.  See [built-in-clause].

  The new command [47m[pcb!][0m is like [47m[pcb][0m but sketches the [command] and
  then prints its subsidiary [events] in full.  See [pcb!].

  [47m:[0m[47m[Rewrite][0m class rules may now specify the [47m:[0m[47m[loop-stopper][0m field.
  See [rule-classes] and see [loop-stopper].

  The rules for how [loop-stopper]s control permutative rewrite rules
  have been changed.  One effect of this change is that now when the
  built-in commutativity rules for [47m[+][0m are used, the terms [47ma[0m and [47m(-
  a)[0m are permuted into adjacency.  For example, [47m(+ a b (- a))[0m is now
  normalized by the commutativity rules to [47m(+ a (- a) b)[0m; in Version
  1.4, [47mb[0m was considered syntactically smaller than [47m(- a)[0m and so [47m(+ a
  b (- a))[0m is considered to be in normal form.  Now it is possible to
  arrange for unary functions be be considered ``invisible'' when
  they are used in certain contexts.  By default, [47m[unary--][0m is
  considered invisible when its application appears in the argument
  list of [47m[binary-+][0m.  See [loop-stopper] and see :DOC
  set-invisible-fns-table.

  Extensive documentation has been provided on the topic of Acl2's
  ``term ordering.'' See [term-order].

  Calls of [47m[ld][0m now default [47m[ld-error-action][0m to [47m:return[0m rather than to
  the current setting.

  The [command] descriptor [47m:x[0m has been introduced and is synonymous
  with [47m:[0m[47m[max][0m, the most recently executed [command].  [History]
  [command]s such as [47m:[0m[47m[pbt][0m print a [47m:x[0m beside the most recent
  [command], simply to indicate that it [31;1mis[0m the most recent one.

  The [command] descriptor [47m:x-23[0m is synonymous with [47m(:x -23)[0m.  More
  generally, every symbol in the keyword package whose first
  character is [47m#\\x[0m and whose remaining [characters] parse as a
  negative integer is appropriately understood.  This allows [47m:[0m[47m[pbt][0m
  [47m:x-10[0m where [47m:[0m[47m[pbt][0m [47m(:max -10)[0m or [47m:[0m[47m[pbt][0m [47m(:here -10)[0m were previously
  used.  The old forms are still legal.

  The order of the arguments to [47m[defcong][0m has been changed.

  The simplifier now reports the use of unspecified built-in type
  information about the primitives with the phrase ``primitive type
  reasoning.'' This phrase may sometimes occur in situations where
  ``propositional calculus'' was formerly credited with the proof.

  The function [47m[pairlis][0m has been replaced in the code by a new
  function [47m[pairlis$][0m, because Common Lisp does not adequately
  specify its [47m[pairlis][0m function.

  Some new Common Lisp functions have been added, including [47m[logtest][0m,
  [47m[logcount][0m, [47m[integer-length][0m, [47m[make-list][0m, [47m[remove-duplicates][0m,
  [47m[string][0m, and [47m[concatenate][0m.  The source file
  [47m/slocal/src/acl2/axioms.lisp[0m is the ultimate reference regarding
  Common Lisp functions in Acl2.

  The functions [47m[defuns][0m and [47m[theory-invariant][0m have been documented.
  See [defuns] and see [theory-invariant].

  A few symbols have been added to the list [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.

  A new key has been implemented for the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m,
  [47m:irrelevant-formals-ok[0m.  See [set-irrelevant-formals-ok].

  The connected book directory, [47m[cbd][0m, must be nonempty and begin and
  end with a slash.  It is set (and displayed) automatically upon
  your first entry to [47m[lp][0m.  You may change the setting with
  [47m[set-cbd][0m.  See [cbd].

  [47m:[0m[47m[oops][0m will undo the last [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m.  See [oops].

  Documentation has been written about the ordinals.  See :DOC
  [47me0-ordinalp[0m and see :DOC [47me0-ord-<[0m.  [Note added later: Starting
  with Version_2.8, instead see [o-p] and see [o<].

  The color [events] --- (red), (pink), (blue), and (gold) --- may no
  longer be enclosed inside calls of [47m[local][0m, for soundness reasons.
  In fact, neither may any event that sets the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m.
  See [embedded-event-form].

  See [ld-keyword-aliases] for an example of how to change the exit
  keyword from [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m to something else.

  The attempt to install a [monitor] on [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules stored as
  simple abbreviations now causes an error because the application of
  abbreviations is not tracked.

  A new message is sometimes printed by the theorem prover, indicating
  that a given simplification is ``specious'' because the subgoals it
  produces include the input goal.  In Version 1.4 this was detected
  but not reported, causing behavior some users found bizarre.  See
  [specious-simplification].

  [47m:[0m[47m[Definition][0m rules are no longer always required to specify the
  [47m:clique[0m and [47m:controller-alist[0m fields; those fields can be defaulted
  to system-determined values in many common instances.  See
  [definition].

  A warning is printed if a macro form with keyword arguments is given
  duplicate keyword values.  Execute [47m(thm t :doc nil :doc \"ignored\")[0m
  and read the warning printed.

  A new restriction has been placed on [47m[encapsulate][0m.  Non-[47m[local][0m
  recursive definitions inside the [47m[encapsulate][0m may not use, in
  their tests and recursive calls, the constrained functions
  introduced by the [47m[encapsulate][0m.  See [subversive-recursions].
  (Note added in Version 2.3: Subversive recursions were first
  recognized by us here in Version 1.5, but our code for recognizing
  them was faulty and the bug was not fixed until Version 2.3.)

  The [events] [47m[defequiv][0m, [47m[defcong][0m, [47m[defrefinement][0m, and
  [47m[defevaluator][0m have been reimplemented so that they are just macros
  that expand into appropriate [47m[defthm][0m or [47m[encapsulate][0m [events];
  they are no longer primitive [events].  See the [documentation] of
  each affected event.

  The [47mdefcor[0m event, which was a shorthand for a [47m[defthm][0m that
  established a [corollary] of a named, previously proved event, has
  been eliminated because its implementation relied on a technique we
  have decided to ban from our code.  If you want the effect of a
  [47mdefcor[0m in Version 1.5 you must submit the corresponding [47m[defthm][0m
  with a [47m:by[0m hint naming the previously proved event.

  Error reporting has been improved for inappropriate [47m[in-theory][0m
  [hints] and [events], and for syntax errors in rule classes, and
  for non-existent filename arguments to [47m[ld][0m.

  Technical Note: We now maintain the Third Invariant on [47mtype-alists[0m,
  as described in the Essay on the Invariants on Type-alists, and
  Canonicality.  This change will affect some proofs, for example, by
  causing a to rewrite more quickly to [47mc[0m when [47m(equiv a b)[0m and [47m(equiv
  b c)[0m are both known and [47mc[0m is the canonical representative of the
  three.")
 (NOTE-1-6
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "Acl2 Version 1.6 Notes

  A new key has been implemented for the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m,
  [47m:ignore-ok[0m.  See [set-ignore-ok].

  It is now legal to have color [events], such as [47m(red)[0m, in the
  [portcullis] of a book.  More generally, it is legal to set the
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m in the [portcullis] of a book.  For example,
  if you execute [47m:red[0m and then certify a book, the event [47m(red)[0m will
  show up in the [portcullis] of that book, and hence the definitions
  in that book will all be red (except when overridden by appropriate
  declarations or [events]).  When that book is included, then as
  always, its [portcullis] must first be ``raised,'' and that will
  cause the default color to become red before the [events] in the
  book are executed.  As always, the value of [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m
  immediately after execution of an [47m[include-book][0m, [47m[certify-book][0m,
  or [47m[encapsulate][0m form will be the same as it was immediately before
  execution (and hence, so will the default color).  See [portcullis]
  and, for more about books, see [books].

  A theory [47m[ground-zero][0m has been defined to contain exactly those
  rules that are [enable]d when Acl2 starts up.  See [ground-zero].

  The function [47m[nth][0m is now [enable]d, correcting an oversight from
  Version 1.5.

  Customization files no longer need to meet the syntactic restrictions
  put on [books]; rather, they can contain arbitrary Acl2 forms.  See
  [ACL2-customization].

  Structured directory names and structured file names are supported;
  see especially the documentation for [pathname], [book-name], and
  [47m[cbd][0m.

  Acl2 now works with some Common Lisp implementations other than akcl,
  including Lucid, Allegro, and MCL.

  A facility has been added for displaying proof trees, especially
  using emacs; see [proof-tree].

  There is a considerable amount of new [documentation], in particular
  for the printing functions [47m[fmt][0m, [47m[fmt1][0m, and [47m[fms][0m, and for the
  notion of Acl2 term (see [term]).

  It is possible to introduce new well-founded relations, to specify
  which relation should be used by [47m[defun][0m, and to set a default
  relation.  See [well-founded-relation-rule].

  It is possible to make functions suggest new inductions.  See
  [induction].

  It is possible to change how Acl2 expresses [type-set] information;
  in particular, this affects what clauses are proved when [force]d
  assumptions are generated.  See [type-set-inverter].

  A new restriction has been added to [47m[defpkg][0m, having to do with
  undoing.  If you undo a [47m[defpkg][0m and define the same package name
  again, the imports list must be identical to the previous imports
  or else an explanatory error will occur.  See
  [package-reincarnation-import-restrictions].

  [47m[Theory-invariant][0m and [47m[set-irrelevant-formals-ok][0m are now embedded
  event forms.

  The command [47m:[0m[47m[good-bye][0m may now be used to quit entirely out of Lisp,
  thus losing your work forever.  This command works in akcl but may
  not work in every Common Lisp.

  A theory [47m[ground-zero][0m has been added that contains exactly the
  [enable]d rules in the [startup] theory.  See [ground-zero].

  [47mDefine-pc-macro[0m and [47mdefine-pc-atomic-macro[0m now automatically define
  [47m:red[0m functions.  (It used to be necessary, in general, to change
  color to [47m:red[0m before invoking these.)

  For a proof of the well-foundedness of [47me0-ord-<[0m on the [47me0-ordinalp[0ms,
  see [proof-of-well-foundedness].  [Note added later: Starting with
  Version_2.8, [47m[o<][0m and [47m[o-p][0m replace [47me0-ord-<[0m and [47me0-ordinalp[0m,
  respectively.]

  Free variables are now handled properly for hypotheses of
  [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules.

  When the system is loaded or saved, [47m[state][0m is now bound to
  [47m*the-live-state*[0m.

  [47m[Certify-book][0m has been modified so that when it compiles a file, it
  loads that object file.

  [47m[Defstub][0m has been modified so that it works when the color is hot
  ([47m:red[0m or [47m:pink[0m).

  Several basic, but not particularly commonly used, [events] have been
  added or changed.  The obscure axiom [47msymbol-name-intern[0m has been
  modified.  The definition of [47mfirstn[0m has been changed.  [47m[Butlast][0m is
  now defined.  The definition of [47m[integer-length][0m has been modified.
  The left-hand side of the rewrite rule [47mrational-implies2[0m has been
  changed from [47m(* (numerator x) (/ (denominator x)))[0m to [47m(* (/
  (denominator x)) (numerator x))[0m, in order to respect the fact that
  [47m[unary-/][0m is invisible with respect to [47m[binary-*][0m.  See
  [loop-stopper].

  The `preprocess' process in the waterfall (see [hints] for a
  discussion of the [47m:do-not[0m hint) has been changed so that it works
  to avoid case-splitting.  The `simplify' process refuses to force
  (see [force]) when there are [47m[if][0m terms, including [47m[and][0m and [47m[or][0m
  terms, in the goal being simplified.

  The function [47mapply[0m is no longer introduced automatically by
  translation of user input to internal form when functions are
  called on inappropriate explicit values, e.g., [47m(car 3)[0m.

  The choice of which variable to use as the measured variable in a
  recursive definition has been very slightly changed.")
 (NOTE-1-7
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 1.7 (released October 1994) Notes

  [47m[Include-book][0m now takes (optionally) an additional keyword argument,
  indicating whether a compiled file is to be loaded.  The default
  behavior is unchanged, except that a warning is printed when a
  compiled file is not loaded.  See [include-book].

  A markup language for [documentation] strings has been implemented,
  and many of the source files have been marked up using this
  language (thanks largely to the efforts of Laura Lawless).  See
  markup.  Moreover, there are translators that we have used to
  provide versions of the ACL2 [documentation] in info (for use in
  emacs), html (for Mosaic), and tex (for hardcopy) formats.

  A new event [47mdefdoc[0m has been implemented.  It is like [47m[deflabel][0m, but
  allows redefinition of [doc] strings and has other advantages.  See
  [47mdefdoc[0m.

  We used to ignore corollaries when collecting up the axioms
  introduced about constrained functions.  That bug has been fixed.
  We thank John Cowles for bringing this bug to our attention.

  The macro [47m[defstub][0m now allows a [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m keyword argument, so that
  [documentation] may be attached to the name being introduced.

  A new command [47m[nqthm-to-ACL2][0m has been added to help Nqthm users to
  make the transition to ACL2.  See [nqthm-to-ACL2], which also
  includes a complete listing of the relevant tables.

  Many function names, especially of the form ``foo[47m-lst[0m'', have been
  changed in order to support the following convention, for any
  ``foo'':

  [47m(foo-listp lst)[0m represents the notion [47m(for x in lst always foop x)[0m.

  A complete list of these changes may be found at the end of this
  note.  All of them except [47msymbolp-listp[0m and [47mlist-of-symbolp-listp[0m
  have the string ``[47m-lst[0m'' in their names.  Note also that
  [47mkeyword-listp[0m has been renamed [47m[keyword-value-listp][0m.

  Accumulated persistence has been implemented.  It is not connected to
  [47m:[0m[47m[brr][0m or rule monitoring.  See [accumulated-persistence].

  [47m:Trigger-terms[0m has been added for [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rule classes, so you can
  hang a [linear] rule under any addend you want.  See [linear],
  which has been improved and expanded.

  ACL2 now accepts [47m256[0m [characters] and includes the Common Lisp
  functions [47m[code-char][0m and [47m[char-code][0m.  However, ACL2 controls the
  lisp reader so that [47m#\\c[0m may only be used when [47mc[0m is a single
  standard character or one of [47mNewline[0m, [47mSpace[0m, [47mPage[0m, [47mRubout[0m, [47mTab[0m.  If
  you want to enter other [characters] use [47m[code-char][0m, e.g., [47m(coerce
  (list (code-char 7) (code-char 240) #a) 'string)[0m.  See
  [characters].  Note: our current handling of [characters] makes the
  set of theorems different under Macintosh Common Lisp (MCL) than
  under other Common Lisps.  We hope to rectify this situation before
  the final release of ACL2.

  A new [table], [47m[macro-aliases-table][0m, has been implemented, that
  associates macro names with function names.  So for example, since
  [47m[append][0m is associated with [47m[binary-append][0m, the form [47m(disable
  append)[0m it is interpreted as though it were [47m(disable
  binary-append)[0m.  See [macro-aliases-table], see [add-macro-alias]
  and see [remove-macro-alias].

  The implementation of conditional metalemmas has been modified so
  that the metafunction is applied before the hypothesis metafunction
  is applied.  See [meta].

  The Common Lisp functions [47m[acons][0m and [47m[endp][0m have been defined in the
  ACL2 logic.

  We have added the symbol [47m[declare][0m to the list [47m*acl2-exports*[0m, and
  hence to the package [47m\"ACL2-USER\"[0m.

  A new hint, [47m:restrict[0m, has been implemented.  See [hints].

  It used to be that if [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m were given a number that is greater than
  the largest current [command] number, it treated that number the
  same as [47m:[0m[47m[max][0m.  Now, an error is caused.

  The [table] [47m:force-table[0m has been eliminated.

  A command [47m:[0m[47m[disabledp][0m (and macro [47m[disabledp][0m) has been added; see
  [disabledp].

  [Compilation] via [47m:[0m[47m[set-compile-fns][0m is now suppressed during
  [47m[include-book][0m.  In fact, whenever the [state] global variable
  [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m has value [47m'[0m[47m[include-book][0m.

  Here are some less important changes, additions, and so on.

  Unlike previous releases, we have not proved all the theorems in
  [47maxioms.lisp[0m; instead we have simply assumed them.  We have deferred
  such proofs because we anticipate a fairly major change in Version
  1.8 in how we deal with [guard]s.

  We used to (accidentally) prohibit the ``redefinition'' of a [table]
  as a function.  That is no longer the case.

  The check for whether a [corollary] follows tautologically has been
  sped up, at the cost of making the check less ``smart'' in the
  following sense: no longer do we expand primitive functions such as
  [47m[implies][0m before checking this propositional implication.

  The [command] [47m[ubt!][0m has been modified so that it never causes or
  reports an error.  See [ubt!].

  ACL2 now works in Harlequin LispWorks.

  The user can now specify the [47m:trigger-terms[0m for [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules.  See
  [linear].

  The name of the system is now ``ACL2''; no longer is it ``Acl2''.

  The raw lisp counterpart of [47m[theory-invariant][0m is now defined to be a
  no-op as is consistent with the idea that it is just a call of
  [47m[table][0m.

  A bug was fixed that caused [proof-builder] [instructions] to be
  executed when [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m was [47mt[0m.

  The function [47m[rassoc][0m has been added, along with a corresponding
  function used in its [guard], [47mr-eqlable-alistp[0m.

  The [47m[in-theory][0m event and hint now print a warning not only when
  certain ``primitive'' [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rules are [disable]d, but also
  when certain ``primitive'' [47m:[0m[47m[executable-counterpart][0m rules are
  [disable]d.

  The modified version of [47mtrace[0m provided by ACL2, for use in raw Lisp,
  has been modified so that the lisp special variable [47m*trace-alist*[0m
  is consulted.  This alist associates, using [47m[eq][0m, values with their
  print representations.  For example, initially [47m*trace-alist*[0m is a
  one-element list containing the pair [47m(cons state
  '|*the-live-state*|)[0m.

  The system now prints an observation when a form is skipped because
  the default color is [47m:red[0m or [47m:pink[0m.  (Technically: [47mwhen-cool[0m has
  been modified.)

  Additional protection exists when you submit a form to raw Common
  Lisp that should only be submitted inside the ACL2 read-eval-print
  loop.

  Here is a complete list of the changes in function names described
  near the top of this note, roughly of the form

    foo-lst --> foo-listp

  meaning: the name ``[47mfoo-lst[0m'' has been changed to ``[47mfoo-listp[0m.''

    symbolp-listp    --> symbol-listp
    list-of-symbolp-listp  --> symbol-list-listp
                           {for consistency with change to symbol-listp}
    rational-lst     --> rational-listp
                         {which in fact was already defined as well}
    integer-lst      --> integer-listp
    character-lst    --> character-listp
    stringp-lst      --> string-listp
    32-bit-integer-lst   --> 32-bit-integer-listp
    typed-io-lst     --> typed-io-listp
    open-channel-lst --> open-channel-listp
    readable-files-lst   --> readable-files-listp
    written-file-lst --> written-file-listp
    read-file-lst    --> read-file-listp
    writeable-file-lst   --> writable-file-listp
                         {note change in spelling of ``writable''}
    writeable-file-lst1  --> writable-file-listp1
    pseudo-termp-lst     --> pseudo-term-listp
    hot-termp-lst --> hot-term-listp {by analogy with pseudo-term-listp}
    weak-termp-lst   --> weak-term-listp
    weak-termp-lst-lst   --> weak-termp-list-listp
    ts-builder-case-lstp -> ts-builder-case-listp
    quotep-lst       --> quote-listp
    termp-lst        --> term-listp
    instr-lst        --> instr-listp
    spliced-instr-lst    --> spliced-instr-listp
    rewrite-fncallp-lst  --> rewrite-fncallp-listp
    every-occurrence-equiv-hittablep1-lst -->
                every-occurrence-equiv-hittablep1-listp
    some-occurrence-equiv-hittablep1-lst  -->
                some-occurrence-equiv-hittablep1-listp
                {by analogy with the preceding, even though it's a
                 ``some'' instead of ``all'' predicate]
    almost-quotep1-lst   --> almost-quotep1-listp
    ffnnames-subsetp-lst --> ffnnames-subsetp-listp
    boolean-lstp     --> boolean-listp
    subst-expr1-lst-okp  --> subst-expr1-ok-listp")
 (NOTE-1-8
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 1.8 (May, 1995) Notes

  See [note-1-8-update] for yet more recent changes.

  [Guard]s have been eliminated from the ACL2 logic.  A summary is
  contained in this brief note.  Also see [defun-mode] and see
  [set-guard-checking].

  [Guard]s may be included in [defuns] as usual but are ignored from
  the perspective of admission to the logic: functions must terminate
  on all arguments.

  As in Nqthm, primitive functions, e.g., [47m[+][0m and [47m[car][0m, logically
  default unexpected arguments to convenient values.  Thus, [47m(+ 'abc
  3)[0m is [47m3[0m and [47m(car 'abc)[0m is [47mnil[0m.  See [programming], and see the
  [documentation] for the individual primitive functions.

  In contrast to earlier versions of ACL2, Version 1.8 logical
  functions are executed at Nqthm speeds even when [guard]s have not
  been verified.  In versions before 1.8, such functions were
  interpreted by ACL2.

  Colors have been eliminated.  Two ``[defun-mode]s'' are supported,
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.  Roughly speaking, [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m does what
  [47m:red[0m used to do, namely, allow you to prototype functions for
  execution without any proof burdens.  [47m:[0m[47m[Logic][0m mode does what [47m:blue[0m
  used to do, namely, allow you to add a new definitional axiom to
  the logic.  A global [default-defun-mode] is comparable to the old
  default color.  The system comes up in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.  To change
  the global [defun-mode], type [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m at the
  top-level.  To specify the [defun-mode] of a [47m[defun][0m locally use

     [47m(declare (xargs :mode mode))[0m.

  The [prompt] has changed.  The initial [prompt], indicating [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  mode, is

    ACL2 !>

  If you change to [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode the [prompt] becomes

    ACL2 p!>

  [Guard]s can be seen as having either of two roles: (a) they are a
  specification device allowing you to characterize the kinds of
  inputs a function ``should'' have, or (b) they are an efficiency
  device allowing logically defined functions to be executed directly
  in Common Lisp.  If a [guard] is specified, as with [47m[xargs][0m
  [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m, then it is ``verified'' at defun-time (unless you also
  specify [47m[xargs][0m [47m:verify-guards nil[0m).  [Guard] verification means
  what it always has: the input [guard] is shown to imply the
  [guard]s on all subroutines in the body.  If the [guard]s of a
  function are verified, then a call of the function on inputs
  satisfying the [guard] can be computed directly by Common Lisp.
  Thus, verifying the [guard]s on your functions will allow them to
  execute more efficiently.  But it does not affect their logical
  behavior and since you will automatically get Nqthm speeds on
  unverified logical definitions, most users will probably use
  [guard]s either as a specification device or only use them when
  execution efficiency is extremely important.

  Given the presence of [guard]s in the system, two issues are
  unavoidable.  Are [guard]s verified as part of the [47m[defun][0m process?
  And are [guard]s checked when terms are evaluated?  We answer both
  of those questions below.

  Roughly speaking, in its initial [state] the system will try to
  verify the [guard]s of a [47m[defun][0m if a [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m is supplied in the
  [47m[xargs][0m and will not try otherwise.  However, [guard] verification
  in [47m[defun][0m can be inhibited ``locally'' by supplying the [47m[xargs][0m
  [47m:[0m[47m[verify-guards][0m [47mnil[0m.  ``Global'' inhibition can be obtained via
  the [47m:[0m[47m[set-verify-guards-eagerness][0m.  If you do not use the [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m
  [47m[xargs][0m, you will not need to think about [guard] verification.

  We now turn to the evaluation of expressions.  Even if your functions
  contain no [guard]s, the primitive functions do and hence you have
  the choice: when you submit an expression for evaluation do you
  mean for [guard]s to be checked at runtime or not?  Put another
  way, do you mean for the expression to be evaluated in Common Lisp
  (if possible) or in the logic?  Note: If Common Lisp delivers an
  answer, it will be the same as in the logic, but it might be
  erroneous to execute the form in Common Lisp.  For example, should
  [47m(car 'abc)[0m cause a [guard] violation error or return [47mnil[0m?

  The top-level ACL2 loop has a variable which controls which sense of
  execution is provided.  To turn ``[guard] checking on,'' by which
  we mean that [guard]s are checked at runtime, execute the top-level
  form [47m:set-guard-checking t[0m.  To turn it off, do [47m:set-guard-checking
  nil[0m.  The status of this variable is reflected in the [prompt].

    ACL2 !>

  means [guard] checking is on and

    ACL2 >

  means [guard] checking is off.  The exclamation mark can be thought
  of as ``barring'' certain computations.  The absence of the mark
  suggests the absence of error messages or unbarred access to the
  logical axioms.  Thus, for example

    ACL2 !>(car 'abc)

  will signal an error, while

    ACL2 >(car 'abc)

  will return [47mnil[0m.

  Note that whether or not [guard]s are checked at runtime is
  independent of whether you are operating in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode or
  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode and whether theorems are being proved or not.
  (Although it must be added that functions defined in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m
  mode cannot help but check their [guard]s because no logical
  definition exists.)

  Version 1.8 permits the verification of the [guard]s of theorems,
  thus insuring that all instances of the theorem will evaluate
  without error in Common Lisp.  To verify the [guard]s of a theorem
  named [47mname[0m execute the event

    (verify-guards name).

  If a theorem's [guard]s have been verified, the theorem is guaranteed
  to evaluate without error to non-[47mnil[0m in Common Lisp (provided
  resource errors do not arise).

  Caveat about [47m[verify-guards][0m: [47m[implies][0m is a function symbol, so in
  the term [47m(implies p q)[0m, [47mp[0m cannot be assumed true when [47mq[0m is
  evaluated; they are both evaluated ``outside.'' Hence, you cannot
  generally verify the [guard]s on a theorem if [47m[implies][0m is used to
  state the hypotheses.  Use [47m[if][0m instead.  In a future version of
  ACL2, [47m[implies][0m will likely be a macro.

  See sum-list-example.lisp for a nice example of the use of Version
  1.8.  This is roughly the same as the documentation for
  [guard-example].

  We have removed the capability to do ``old-style-forcing'' as existed
  before Version 1.5.  See [note-1-5].

  NOTE: Some low level details have, of course, changed.  One such
  change is that there are no longer two distinct type prescriptions
  stored when a function is admitted with its [guard]s verified.  So
  for example, the type prescription [rune] for [47m[binary-append][0m is
  now

    (:type-prescription binary-append)

  while in Versions 1.7 and earlier, there were two such [rune]s:

    (:type-prescription binary-append . 1)
    (:type-prescription binary-append . 2)

  Nqthm-style forcing on [linear] arithmetic assumptions is no longer
  executed when forcing is [disable]d.

  Functional instantiation now benefits from a trick also used in
  Nqthm: once a [constraint] generated by a [47m:functional-instance[0m
  lemma instance (see [lemma-instance]) has been proved on behalf of
  a successful event, it will not have to be re-proved on behalf of a
  later event.

  [47m[1+][0m and [47m[1-][0m are now macros in the logic, not functions.  Hence, for
  example, it is ``safe'' to use them on left-hand sides of rewrite
  rules, without invoking the common warning about the presence of
  nonrecursive function symbols.

  A new [documentation] section [file-reading-example] illustrates how
  to process forms in a file.

  A new [proof-builder] command [47mforwardchain[0m has been added; see
  [ACL2-pc::forwardchain].

  It is now possible to use quantifiers.  See [defun-sk] and see
  [defchoose].

  There is a new event [47m[set-inhibit-warnings][0m, which allows the user to
  turn off warnings of various types.  see [set-inhibit-warnings].

  An unsoundness relating [47m[encapsulate][0m and [47m:functional-instance[0m
  [hints] has been remedied, with a few small effects visible at the
  user level.  The main observable effect is that [47m[defaxiom][0m and
  non-local [47m[include-book][0m [events] are no longer allowed in the
  scope of any [47m[encapsulate][0m event that has a non-empty [signature].

  When [47m[certify-book][0m is called, we now require that the default
  [defun-mode] (see [default-defun-mode]) be [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.  On a related
  note, the default [defun-mode] is irrelevant to [47m[include-book][0m; the
  mode is always set to [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m initially, though it may be changed
  within the book and reverts to its original value at the conclusion
  of the [47m[include-book][0m.  A bug in [47m[include-book][0m prevented it from
  acting this way even though the [documentation] said otherwise.

  The [documentation] has been substantially improved.  A new section
  ``Programming'' contains [documentation] of many useful functions
  provided by ACL2; see [programming].  Also, the [documentation] has
  been ``marked up'' extensively.  Thus in particular, users of
  Mosaic will find many links in the [documentation].

  The symbols [47m[force][0m, [47m[mv-nth][0m, and [47macl2-count[0m have been added to the
  list [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.

  We now permit most names from the main Lisp package to be used as
  names, except for names that define functions, macros, or
  constants.  See [name].

  We have changed the list of imports from the Common Lisp package to
  ACL2, i.e., the list [47m*common-lisp-symbols-from-main-lisp-package*[0m,
  to be exactly those external symbols of the Common Lisp package as
  specified by the draft Common Lisp standard.  In order to
  accommodate this change, we have renamed some ACL2 functions as
  shown below, but these and other ramifications of this change
  should be transparent to most ACL2 users.

    warning      --> warning$
    print-object --> print-object$

  Proof trees are no longer enabled by default.  To start them up,
  [47m:[0m[47m[start-proof-tree][0m.

  We have added the capability of building smaller images.  The easiest
  way to do this on a Unix (trademark of AT&T) system is: [47mmake small[0m.

  Here we will put some less important changes, additions, and so on.

  We have added definitions for the Common Lisp function [47m[position][0m
  (for the test [47m[eql][0m), as well as corresponding versions
  [47m[position-equal][0m and [47m[position-eq][0m that use tests [47m[equal][0m and [47m[eq][0m,
  respectively.  See [position], see [position-equal], and see
  [position-eq].

  The [47m[defthm][0m event [47mrational-listp-implies-rationalp-car[0m no longer
  exists.

  We fixed a bug in the hint mechanism that applied [47m:by[0m, [47m:cases[0m, and
  [47m:use[0m [hints] to the first induction goal when the prover reverted
  to proving the original goal by induction.

  We fixed a bug in the handling of [47m(set-irrelevant-formals-ok :warn)[0m.

  In support of removing the old-style forcing capability, we deleted
  the initialization of [state] global [47mold-style-forcing[0m and deleted
  the definitions of [47mrecover-assumptions[0m,
  [47mrecover-assumptions-from-goal[0m, [47mremove-assumptions1[0m,
  [47mremove-assumptions[0m, and [47msplit-on-assumptions[0m, and we renamed
  [47msplit-on-assumptions1[0m to [47msplit-on-assumptions[0m.

  The special value [47m'none[0m in the [proof-builder] commands [47mclaim[0m and [47m[=][0m
  has been replaced by [47m:none[0m.

  A bug in the handling of [hints] by subgoals has been fixed.  For
  example, formerly a [47m:do-not[0m hint could be ``erased'' by a [47m:use[0m hint
  on a subgoal.  Thanks go to Art Flatau for noticing the bug.

  The functions [47mweak-termp[0m and [47mweak-term-listp[0m have been deleted, and
  their calls have been replaced by corresponding calls of
  [47m[pseudo-termp][0m and [47mpseudo-term-listp[0m.  The notion of [47m[pseudo-termp][0m
  has been slightly strengthened by requiring that terms of the form
  [47m(quote ...)[0m have length 2.

  Performance has been improved in various ways.  At the prover level,
  backchaining through the recognizer alist has been eliminated in
  order to significantly speed up ACL2's rewriter.  Among the other
  prover changes (of which there are several, all technical): we no
  longer clausify the input term when a proof is interrupted in favor
  of inducting on the input term.  At the [io] level, we have
  improved performance somewhat by suitable declarations and
  proclamations.  These include technical modifications to the macros
  [47m[mv][0m and [47m[mv-let][0m, and introduction of a macro [47mthe-mv[0m analogous to
  the macro [47m[the][0m but for forms returning multiple values.

  The function [47mspaces[0m now takes an extra argument, the current column.

  A bug in the [proof-builder] [47mequiv[0m command was fixed.

  The function [47mintersectp[0m has been deleted, because it was essentially
  duplicated by the function [47m[intersectp-equal][0m.

  We now proclaim functions in AKCL and GCL before compiling [books].
  This should result in somewhat increased speed.

  The function [47mrepeat[0m has been eliminated; use [47m[make-list][0m instead.

  The [proof-builder] command [47mexpand[0m has been fixed so that it
  eliminates [47m[let][0m (lambda) expressions when one would expect it to.

  A new primitive function, [47m[mv-nth][0m, has been introduced.  [47m[Mv-nth][0m is
  equivalent to [47m[nth][0m and is used in place of [47m[nth][0m in the
  translation of [47m[mv-let][0m expressions.  This allows the user to
  control the simplification of [47m[mv-let][0m expressions without
  affecting how [47m[nth][0m is treated.  In that spirit, the rewriter has
  been modified so that certain [47m[mv-nth][0m expressions, namely those
  produced in the translation of [47m(mv-let (a b c)(mv x y z) p)[0m, are
  given special treatment.

  A minor bug in [47muntranslate[0m has been fixed, which for example will fix
  the printing of conjunctions.

  [47mTranslate[0m now takes a [47mlogicp[0m argument, which indicates whether it
  enforces the restriction that [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions do not
  occur in the result.

  The modified version of [47mtrace[0m provided by ACL2, for use in raw Lisp,
  has been modified so that the lisp special variable [47m*trace-alist*[0m
  has a slightly different functionality.  This alist associates,
  using [47m[eq][0m, symbols with the print representations of their values.
  For example, initially [47m*trace-alist*[0m is a one-element list
  containing the pair [47m(cons 'state '|*the-live-state*|)[0m.  Thus, one
  may cons the pair [47m(cons '*foo* \"It's a FOO!\")[0m on to [47m*trace-alist*[0m;
  then until [47m*foo*[0m is defined, this change will have no effect, but
  after for example

    (defconst *foo* 17)

  then [47mtrace[0m will print [47m17[0m as [47m\"It's a FOO!\"[0m.

  [47mTrace[0m also traces the corresponding logic function.

  [Proof-tree] display has been improved slightly in the case of
  successful proofs and certain event failures.

  The function [47mpositive-integer-log2[0m has been deleted.

  The macro [47m[skip-proofs][0m now prints a warning message when it is
  encountered in the context of an [47m[encapsulate][0m event or a book.
  See [skip-proofs].

  Some functions related to [47mthe-fn[0m and [47mwormhole1[0m now have [defun-mode]
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m, but this change is almost certain to be inconsequential
  to all users.")
 (NOTE-1-8-UPDATE
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 1.8 (Summer, 1995) Notes

  ACL2 can now use Ordered Binary Decision Diagram technology.  See
  [bdd].  There is also a [proof-builder] [47mbdd[0m command.

  ACL2 is now more respectful of the intention of the function [47m[hide][0m.
  In particular, it is more careful not to dive inside any call of
  [47m[hide][0m during equality substitution and case splitting.

  The [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]) [47m[ld-pre-eval-print][0m may now be used to
  turn off printing of input forms during processing of [47m[encapsulate][0m
  and [47m[certify-book][0m forms, by setting it to the value [47m:never[0m, i.e.,
  [47m(set-ld-pre-eval-print :never state)[0m.  See [ld-pre-eval-print].

  The TUTORIAL documentation section (now obsolete) has, with much help
  from Bill Young, been substantially improved to a bona fide
  introduction.

  The term pretty-printer has been modified to introduce [47m(<= X Y)[0m as an
  abbreviation for [47m(not (< Y X))[0m.

  Forward chaining and linear arithmetic now both benefit from the
  evaluation of ground subterms.

  A new macro [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m has been defined.  This should
  be used when setting the [state] global [47minhibit-output-lst[0m; see
  [set-inhibit-output-lst] and see [proof-tree].

  The test for redundancy in definitions includes the [guard] and type
  declarations.  See [redundant-events].

  See [generalized-booleans] for a discussion of a potential soundness
  problem for ACL2 related to the question: Which Common Lisp
  functions are known to return Boolean values?

  Here we will put some less important changes, additions, and so on.

  A bug has been fixed so that now, execution of [47m:comp t[0m (see [comp])
  correctly handles non-standard characters.

  A bug in [47m[digit-char-p][0m has been fixed, so that the ``default'' is
  [47mnil[0m rather than [47m0[0m.

  [47m[True-listp][0m now tests the final [47m[cdr][0m against [47mnil[0m using [47m[eq][0m instead
  of [47m[equal][0m, for improved efficiency.  The logical meaning is,
  however, unchanged.

  [47m[Put-assoc-equal][0m has been added to the logic (it used to have
  [47m:[0m[47m[defun-mode][0m [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m, and has been documented.")
 (NOTE-1-9
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 1.9 (Fall, 1996) Notes

  By default, when the system is started it is illegal to use the
  variable [47m[state][0m as a formal parameter of a function definition.
  The aim is to prevent novice users from stumbling into the
  Byzantine syntactic restrictions on that variable symbol.  Use

    :set-state-ok t

  or, equivalently,

    (set-state-ok t)

  to switch back to the old default mode.  See [set-state-ok]

  [47mSet-state-ok[0m is an event that affects the ACL2 defaults table (see
  [ACL2-defaults-table]).  Recall that when books are included, the
  defaults table is restored to its pre-inclusion state.  Thus, while
  a [47mset-state-ok[0m form will permit the book to define a [47mstate[0m-using
  function, it will not permit the user of the book to make such a
  definition.  We recommend putting [47m(set-state-ok t)[0m in any book that
  defines a [47mstate[0m using function.

  Books certified under Version 1.8 must be recertified under Version
  1.9.  See :DOC version.

  The simplifier has been made to look out for built-in clauses,
  whereas in past versions such clauses were only noticed by the
  ``preprocessor'' at the top of the waterfall.  THIS CHANGE MAY
  PREVENT OLD SCRIPTS FROM REPLAYING!  The undesirable side-effect is
  caused by the fact that [47m:HINTS[0m require you to refer to clauses by
  their exact name (see [goal-spec]) and because the new simplifier
  proves more clauses than before, the goals produced have different
  names.  Thus, if a script uses [47m:HINTS[0m that refer to clauses other
  than \"Goal\", e.g., \"Subgoal 1.3\" then the hint may be applied to a
  different subgoal than originally intended.

  The use of built-in-clauses has been made more efficient.  If a set
  of clauses arise often in a piece of work, it might be advantageous
  to build them in even if that results in a large set (hundreds?) of
  built-in clauses.  See [built-in-clause]

  Wormholes can now be used in :logic mode functions. See [wormhole]

  It is now possible to provide ``computed hints.'' For example, have
  you ever wished to say ``in all goals with a name like this, :use
  that'' or ``if this term is in the subgoal, then :use that''?
  Well, see [computed-hints] and the extraordinarily long example in
  see [using-computed-hints].

  [47mHide[0m terms may be rewritten with :rewrite rules about [47mhide[0m.  See
  [hide], where we also now explain why [47mhide[0m terms are sometimes
  introduced into your proof attempts.

  A bug that sometimes caused the ``non-lazy IF'' hard error message
  was fixed.

  A bug that sometimes caused a hard error in forward chaining was
  fixed.

  A bug in print-rules (:pr) was fixed.

  We report the use of :executable-counterparts in the evaluation of
  SYNTAXP forms.

  Some documentation errors were fixed.

  A bug in parent-tree tracking in add-literal-and-pt was fixed.

  A bug in ok$, go$ and eval$ was fixed.

  Clausify now optimizes (mv-nth 'k (list x0 ... xk ... xn)) to xk.")
 (NOTE-2-0
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.0 (July, 1997) Notes

  This is the first version of ACL2 released under the copyright of the
  University of Texas (UT).  Future releases of ACL2 will be made
  from UT rather than Computational Logic, Inc. (CLI).  Version 2.0
  is just Version 1.9 as released by CLI, with a few bugs fixed.

  A bug causing an infinite loop was fixed in functional instantiation.
  The bug manifested itself when two conditions occurred
  simultaneously: First, the functional substitution replaces a
  function symbol, e.g., [47mFOO[0m, with a [47mLAMBDA[0m expression containing a
  free variable (a variable not among in the [47mLAMBDA[0m formals).  And,
  second, in one of the constraints being instantiated there is a
  call of the function symbol [47mFOO[0m within the scope of another [47mLAMBDA[0m
  expression.  Unless you used such a functional substitution, this
  bug fix will not affect you.

  Less important notes:

  The implementation of [47mPRINC$[0m was changed so that it was no longer
  sensitive to the external setting of [47m*print-base*[0m and other Common
  Lisp special variables.

  Typographical errors were fixed in the documentation.")
 (NOTE-2-1
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.1 (December, 1997) Notes

  The identity function [47m[case-split][0m has been added.  It is similar to
  [47m[force][0m but causes an immediate split of the top-level goal on
  whether the indicated hypothesis is true.

  Less important notes:

  Minor bugs in the documentation were fixed.")
 (NOTE-2-2
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.2 (August, 1998) Notes

  Important changes:

  A bug was fixed in the compile command, [47m:comp[0m.  The compiled code
  produced by [47m:comp[0m in previous versions could be wildly incorrect
  because of a confusion between the printer and the reader regarding
  what was the current Lisp [47m*package*[0m.  This bug could manifest
  itself only if you used the [47m:comp[0m command to compile previously
  uncompiled functions while the current package was different from
  [47m\"ACL2\"[0m.  What happened in that situation depended upon what symbols
  were imported into your current package.  The most likely behavior
  is that the compiler would break or complain or the resulting
  compiled code would call functions that did not exist.

  There have been no other important changes to the code.

  However, this release contains some useful new books, notably those
  on the [47mbooks[0m subdirectories [47mcli-misc[0m and [47mihs[0m.  Both have [47mREADME[0m
  files.  The [47mihs[0m books provide support for integer hardware
  specifications.  These books were crucial to Bishop Brock's
  successful modeling of the Motorola CAP.  We thank Bishop for
  producing them and we thank all those who worked so hard to get
  these books released.  We highly recommend the [47mihs[0m books to those
  modeling ALUs and other arithmetic components of microprocessors or
  programming languages.

  In previous versions of ACL2, the arithmetic books, found on
  [47mbooks/arithmetic/[0m, included the addition of several unproved axioms
  stating properties of the rationals that we believed could be
  derived from our ``official'' axioms but which we had not
  mechanically proved.  The axioms were found in the book
  [47mrationals-with-axioms.lisp[0m, which was then used in the uppermost
  arithmetic books [47mtop.lisp[0m and [47mtop-with-meta.lisp[0m.  John Cowles has
  now provided us with ACL2 proofs of those ``axioms'' and so in this
  release you will find both [47mrationals-with-axioms.lisp[0m and
  [47mrationals-with-axioms-proved.lisp[0m.  The former is provided for
  compatibility's sake.  The latter is identical but contains [47mdefthm[0ms
  where the former contains [47mdefaxiom[0ms.  The top-most books have been
  rebuilt using ``[47m-axioms-proved[0m'' book.  Thanks John.

  Less important notes:

  Bishop Brock found a bug in [47mtranslated-acl2-unwind-protectp4[0m.  Jun
  Sawada reported a bug in linear arithmetic that caused us not to
  prove certain trivial theorems concluding with [47m(not (equal i j))[0m.
  We have fixed both.

  We now prohibit definitions that call certain event commands such as
  [47mDEFTHM[0m and [47mTABLE[0m because our Common Lisp implementations of them
  differ from their ACL2 meanings (so that compiled books can be
  loaded correctly and efficiently).

  Minor bugs in the documentation were fixed.")
 (NOTE-2-3
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.3 (October, 1998) Notes

  Important changes:

  Versions of ACL2 preceding this one contain a subtle soundness bug!
  We found a flaw in our detection of [subversive-recursions].  The
  bug allowed some subversive recursions to slip through undetected.

  We believe it would have been difficult to have exploited this flaw
  inadvertently.  In particular, the following five conditions are
  necessary.
  (1) Introduce a constrained function, say [47mf[0m, via an [47mencapsulate[0m.
  (2) In the same encapsulation, define a clique of mutually recursive
  functions.  This clique must be non-[47mlocal[0m and in [47m:logic[0m mode.
  (3) In that mutually recursive clique, use the constrained function
  [47mf[0m (perhaps indirectly) so that the termination argument for the
  clique depends on properties of the [3mwitness[0m for [47mf[0m.  Thus, [47mf[0m or some
  other function dependent upon [47mf[0m, must be used in an argument in a
  recursive call or in a term governing a recursive call.
  Furthermore, the use of [47mf[0m must be such that the termination proof
  cannot be done without exploiting properties of the witness for [47mf[0m.
  Other uses of the constrained functions in the clique are ok.
  (4) Fail to include the exploited properties of [47mf[0m among the
  constraints of the encapsulation.
  (5) Later, outside the encapsulation, explicitly use a functional
  instantiation in which [47mf[0m is replaced by a function not enjoying the
  crucial properties.
  See [subversive-recursions] for details.

  Less important notes:

  We have begun to write some introductory tutorial material for those
  who wish to learn to program in ACL2.  Most of this material is
  HTML-based.  See the Hyper-Card on the ACL2 home page.

  The documentation of [47m[verify-guards][0m was improved to explain why one
  might wish to verify the ``guards'' of a [47mdefthm[0m event.  The missing
  documentation was noticed by John Cowles.

  A bug was fixed in cross fertilization.  The bug caused the system to
  report that it had substituted one term for another when in fact no
  substitution occurred.  The bug was noticed by Bill McCune.")
 (NOTE-2-4
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.4 (August, 1999) Notes

  Important changes:

  We corrected a soundness bug in Version 2.3 related to the handling
  of [47m[immediate-force-modep][0m.  The bad behavior was noticed by Robert
  Krug.  Thanks!

  We corrected a bug that permitted [47m[verify-guards][0m to accept a
  function even though a subfunction had not yet had its guards
  verified.  Thanks to John Cowles for noticing this.

  User defined single-threaded objects are now supported.  See [stobj].

  Less important notes:

  We corrected a bug that prevented the intended expansion of some
  recursive function calls.

  We changed the handling of the primitive function [47m[illegal][0m, which is
  logically defined to be [47mnil[0m but which is programmed to signal an
  error, so that when it is evaluated as part of a proof, it does not
  signal an error.  The old handling of the function prevented some
  guard proofs involving [47m[the][0m or [47m[let][0ms with internal declarations.

  We corrected a bug that permitted some [47mLOCAL[0m [47mDEFAXIOM[0m events to slip
  into certified books.

  We corrected a bug that prevented the correct undoing of certain
  [47mDEFPKG[0m forms.

  Changes were made to support CMU Lisp.  Pete Manolios helped with
  these changes.

  Changes were made to make the make files more compatible with Allegro
  Common Lisp.  Jun Sawada, who has been a great help with keeping
  ACL2 up and running at UT on various platforms, was especially
  helpful.  Thanks Jun.")
 (NOTE-2-5
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.5 (June, 2000) Notes

  Important Changes:

  Concurrent with the release of ACL2 Version 2.5 is the publication of
  two books about ACL2.  See the ``Books and Papers about ACL2 and
  Its Applications'' on the ACL2 Home Page.

  The [47mbooks[0m subdirectory now contains many new certifiable books,
  including solutions to the exercises in the two published books and
  full scripts for the case studies.  See [47mbooks/README.html[0m.

  Improved Unix [47mMakefile[0m support for book certification has also been
  written.  See [47mbooks/README.html[0m.

  The list of symbols in [47m*acl2-exports*[0m has been considerably expanded.
  If you have packages built by importing [47m*acl2-exports*[0m you might
  want to look carefully at the new value of that constant.  The new
  value includes all [47m:logic[0m mode functions as of Version 2.5, as well
  as all documented macros and all built-in theorem names.

  [47m[Include-book][0m and [47m[certify-book][0m were modified to have some
  additional keyword arguments.  It is possible to certify a book
  containing [47m[defaxiom][0m and/or [47m[skip-proofs][0m events and get warning
  messages or errors signaled, according to the settings of these new
  flags.  In addition, it is possible to specify in [47minclude-book[0m
  whether the book must be certified (under penalty of error if not).
  The default values of these new arguments cause warnings to be
  printed rather than errors signaled.

  The above change involved altering the form of certificate files.
  When books certified under previous versions are included, more
  warnings will be generated because these books are considered
  possibly to contain [47mdefaxiom[0m and/or [47mskip-proofs[0m events.

  We anticipate further changes to this aspect of books and consider
  the current mechanisms (for controlling whether warnings or errors
  are signaled) just a prototype.  See also the discussion below of
  ``soundness related'' warnings.  Your suggestions are welcome.

  A discrepancy between ACL2 and Common Lisp was fixed, having to do
  with [47mdeclare ignore[0m.  In past versions of ACL2, a formal parameter
  of a [47mdefun[0m was considered ignored if it was not used in the body,
  the guard or the measure of the [47mdefun[0m.  That meant that a variable
  used only in the guard could not be declared ignored in ACL2; but
  some Common Lisp compilers would complain because the variable was
  not used in the body.  Now, ACL2 considers a variable ignored if it
  is not used in the body.

  ACL2 can now be built in releases 5.0 and later of Allegro Common
  Lisp.  (Other releases of Allegro Common Lisp and of other lisps
  continue to be supported as well.)  This includes Allegro Common
  Lisp running on Windows 98 platforms.  John Cowles helped us do
  some testing and answered questions for us.  Thanks John!

  We incorporated Ruben Gamboa's changes to allow the building of a
  variant, ACL2(r), of ACL2, in which the user can reason about the
  real numbers using non-standard analysis.  See [real].  Note that
  ACL2(r) and ACL2 have different underlying theories, and books
  certified in one system may not be included in the other.  For
  backward compatibility and to ensure a smooth transition, ACL2 is
  built by default, not ACL2(r).  This is a compile-time switch; see
  the makefile for instructions.  There should be no changes to ACL2
  resulting from the capability of building ACL2(r) from the same
  sources.  Also see [acknowledgments] for more on the history of
  ACL2(r).

  A large number of bugs (some affecting soundness) were fixed, and
  many small new features were added.  See below.

  Less Important Changes:

  Some warnings are now considered ``soundness related,'' namely, those
  that advise you that an uncertified book has been included or that
  a book containing [47mDEFAXIOM[0m or [47mSKIP-PROOFS[0m events.  (Technically,
  [47mDEFAXIOM[0ms do not imperil soundness in the proof- theoretic sense,
  though they may imperil the validity of theorems.  But you should
  know when a book has added an axiom to your logic!)  In previous
  versions of ACL2, all warnings were inhibited if the token [47mwarning[0m
  was included in the argument to [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m.  Now,
  soundness related warnings are printed even if [47mwarning[0ms have been
  inhibited.  To inhibit all warnings, supply the token [47mwarning![0m to
  [47mset-inhibit-output-lst[0m.

  Several bugs in [47m[defstobj][0m were fixed, relating to the possibility
  that some of the subfunctions introduced by the [47mdefstobj[0m were
  already defined.

  [47m:[0m[47m[Puff][0m no longer tries to expand [47m[defstobj][0m events.  Previously, the
  attempt would cause a hard error.

  A soundness bug was fixed.  The bug might have been exercised if you
  had an alternative definition (implies hyps (equiv (fn ...) body))
  in which equiv is an equivalence relation other than EQUAL.  In
  this case, calls of fn might have been expanded to body in places
  that were not equiv-hittable.

  An obscure soundness bug was fixed.  The bug was exercised only if
  you had a metafunction with a computed hypothesis (i.e., a ``meta
  hypothesis function''), the hypothesis contained a free variable,
  i.e., a variable not involved in the term being rewritten, and the
  free variable occurred in the output of the metafunction.  The
  possibility of this bug was brought to our attention by Robert
  Krug.

  We fixed a bug in the handling of [47mhide[0m related to the question of
  whether a variable symbol occurs in a term.  The old code did not
  find the variable and could cause the system to throw away a
  hypothesis about it on the grounds that it was never mentioned.
  Rob Sumners helped discover this problem.

  The handling of [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m rules was generalized, permitting arbitrary
  known equivalence relations instead of merely [47mequal[0m in the
  concluding equality.

  The printing of runes (rule names; see [rune]) used has been made
  \"deterministic,\" both in proof output and in proof attempt
  summaries, by sorting the runes before printing.

  The handling of free variables has been improved for hypotheses such
  as [47m(< 0 X)[0m, and more generally, any hypotheses involving a
  comparison with [47m0[0m (even for example [47m(< X 1)[0m where [47mX[0m is known to be
  an integer, which is handled as [47m(<= X 0)[0m).  Thanks to Robert Krug
  for bringing relevant examples to our attention.

  A new value, [47m:comp[0m, has been implemented for the [47m:load-compiled-file[0m
  keyword of [47m[include-book][0m.  If this value is supplied, then a
  compiled file will always be loaded, even if that requires creating
  the compiled file first.

  The event [47minclude-book[0m now generates a warning when a compiled file
  is expected but not found (see [include-book]).  Formerly, it only
  did so when executed at the top level; it failed to generate the
  warning when executed on behalf of a surrounding [47minclude-book[0m
  command.

  Certain redefinition warnings generated by Allegro Common Lisp have
  been eliminated.

  A new key has been implemented for the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m,
  [47m:bogus-mutual-recursion-ok[0m, set with
  [47m:[0m[47m[set-bogus-mutual-recursion-ok][0m.  Thanks to David Russinoff for
  pointing out the utility of such a key.

  A bug was fixed in [47m[defun-sk][0m that prevented its generated events
  from being accepted when guard verification is being performed.
  Thanks to Bill Young for bringing this problem to our attention.  A
  second bug was brought to our attention by Pete Manolios, which was
  causing certain [47m[defun-sk][0m events to be rejected.  That problem has
  been fixed, and an \"Infected\" warning has also been eliminated.

  The command [47m[good-bye][0m now works with Allegro Common Lisp.

  A low-level bug was fixed that could, for example, cause an error
  such as \"Error: Expected 5 args but received 4 args\" when
  interrupting a [47mlocal[0m event.

  A bug has been fixed in the [proof-builder] related to definition
  expansion.  Thanks to Pete Manolios for bringing this to our
  attention with a simple example.

  A bug has been fixed related to the [47m:[0m[bdd] hint in the presence of
  [equivalence] relations.  Thanks to Pete Manolios for bringing this
  to our attention with a simple example.

  The functions [47m[position][0m and [47m[position-equal][0m formerly required the
  second argument to be a true list.  In accordance with Common Lisp,
  we now also allow the second argument to be a string.  This could
  cause earlier proofs about these functions to fail unless
  [47m[true-listp][0m is known to hold where necessary.

  Robert Krug wrote a patch, which has been incorporated, to prevent
  certain infinite loops that can arise in linear arithmetic.
  Thanks, Robert!

  The macro [47m[let*][0m no longer requires the bound variables to be
  distinct.

  An obscure bug was fixed related to congruence rules.  The bug would
  sometimes cause ACL2 to behave as though no rules (other than
  equality) were available for some argument positions.  Thanks to
  Pete Manolios for bringing this bug to our attention.

  Documentation topics have been added for [47m[hard-error][0m and [47m[prog2$][0m,
  and the documentation for [47m[illegal][0m has been improved.  Thanks to
  Rob Sumners for a useful suggestion in the examples in
  documentation for [47mprog2$[0m and a fix in documentation for [47m[sublis][0m.

  The event form [47m[certify-book][0m was made more secure, in that it can
  now catch attempts to write a book to disk during its
  certification.  Thanks to Rob Sumners for pointing out the
  insecurity of the existing mechanism.

  A Y2K problem was fixed with our applicative handling of dates.

  Accessors and updaters for [47m[stobj][0ms have been made more efficient
  when the underlying lisp is Allegro Common Lisp, by the use of
  appropriate simple array declarations.

  A raw Lisp break had been possible when a certified book that had no
  guard verification was included in a session after
  [47m([0m[47m[set-verify-guards-eagerness][0m[47m 2)[0m.  This has been fixed.

  The keyword command [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m can now be used to compile only raw Lisp
  functions, excluding executable counterparts, by supplying the
  argument [47m:raw[0m.

  Rewrite rule [47mnth-of-character-listp[0m was removed from source file
  axioms.lisp since it is essentially subsumed by [47mcharacterp-nth[0m.

  Printing has been sped up.  In one example the improvement was over
  50% in both Allegro and GCL.

  We now allow printing in a \"downcase\" mode, where symbols are printed
  in lower case.  All printing functions except [47mprint-object$[0m now
  print characters in lower case for a symbol when the ACL2 state
  global variable [47mprint-case[0m has value [47m:downcase[0m and vertical bars
  are not necessary for printing that symbol.  See [io] for a
  discussion of the macros [47macl2-print-case[0m and [47mset-acl2-print-case[0m.
  The default printing remains unchanged, i.e., symbols are printed
  in upper case when vertical bars are not required.

  A low-level printing function ([47mprin1$[0m) was modified so that it is not
  sensitive to various Common Lisp globals related to printing.  So
  for example, the function [47m[fmt][0m is no longer sensitive to the value
  of Common Lisp global [47m*print-case*[0m.  (The preceding paragraph
  explains how to control the case for printing in ACL2.)

  The definition of [47m[array1p][0m was fixed so that the [47m:maximum-length[0m of
  an array must be strictly greater than the number specified in the
  [47m:dimensions[0m field; they may no longer be equal.  This was always
  the intention; the documentation (see [arrays]) has remained
  unchanged.  The corresponding change was also made to [47m[array2p][0m.
  Allegro Common Lisp formerly caused an error when [47m[compress1][0m was
  called on an array where the numbers above were equal; now, we get
  a guard violation instead, which is appropriate.

  In the context of theories, a name now represents not just the
  corresponding [47m:definition[0m [rune], as it has done in earlier
  versions of ACL2, but also the corresponding [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m rune.
  See [theories] for a discussion of runic designators.  Most users
  will rarely, if ever, notice this change.  One situation where this
  change will make a difference is after executing [47m(in-theory
  (current-theory 'foo))[0m followed by [47m(in-theory (enable bar))[0m, where
  function [47mbar[0m is introduced after event [47mfoo[0m, and [47mbar[0m is recursively
  defined.  The latter [47m[in-theory][0m form now enables the rune
  [47m(:induction bar)[0m, which implies that the prover can use the
  induction scheme stored at definition time for [47mbar[0m.  Formerly, the
  rune [47m(:induction bar)[0m was not enabled by [47m(in-theory (enable bar))[0m,
  and hence the induction scheme for [47mbar[0m was ignored even when
  explicit [47m:induct[0m hints were supplied.

  You may now supply [47m[xargs][0m keyword pair [47m:normalize nil[0m in order to
  prevent certain definitions from ``hanging'' when there are many
  [47mif[0m-subexpressions.  see [defun].

  We now translate type declarations of [47mreal[0m into guards, as we have
  already done for other types such as [47mrational[0m.  For example,
  [47m(declare (type real x))[0m generates the [guard] [47m(rationalp x)[0m.  See
  [type-spec].

  The theorem prover now behaves reasonably under the combination of
  specifying a value of [47mt[0m both for [47m:[0m[47m[otf-flg][0m and for a hint
  [47m:do-not-induct[0m.  Previously, it aborted the first time it would
  have otherwise pushed a goal for induction, but now, it will
  continue and wait until all induction subgoals have been pushed
  before it aborts.

  We changed slightly the definition of [47m[round][0m.  However, we believe
  that the new definition is equivalent to the old.

  The definition of Common Lisp function [47m[substitute][0m has been added.

  The following changes have been made in the use of file names within
  ACL2.  We thank Warren Hunt and John Cowles for running some tests
  of these changes on Macintosh and Windows 98 platforms
  (respectively).

      (1) Names of directories and files now use a syntax like that used
      for Unix (trademark of AT&T), where directories are separated
      using the ``[47m/[0m'' character even when the operating system is not
      Unix or Linux.  See [pathname].  ACL2 also continues to support
      its notion of [3mstructured pathnames[0m from Version 2.4 and before,
      but might not do so in future releases and hence no longer
      documents such syntax.

      (2) The command [47m:[0m[47m[set-cbd][0m may now take a relative pathname as an
      argument.

      (3) When the macro [47m[ld][0m is given a file name as a value for
      [47m[standard-oi][0m, then if that file name is a relative pathname it
      refers to the result of prepending the connected book directory
      (see [pathname], see [cbd], and see [set-cbd]) in order to
      obtain an absolute pathname.  Similarly for the [47mld[0m specials
      [47m[standard-co][0m and [47m[proofs-co][0m.

  It is no longer necessary to issue [47m:[0m[47m[set-state-ok][0m[47m t[0m if you include a
  [stobj] declaration for [47m[state][0m, for example:

    (declare (xargs :stobjs state))

  See [declare-stobjs].

  The [proof-builder] has been cleaned up a bit, including the
  documentation and the capability (once again) to define pc-macro
  commands (see [define-pc-macro]) and proof-builder meta commands
  (see [define-pc-meta]).

  Recall that events generate summaries that include a line beginning
  with ``[47mWarnings:[0m'', which is followed (on the same line) by zero or
  more brief strings that summarize the warnings generated by that
  event.  Formerly, this warnings summary for an [47m[encapsulate][0m or
  [47m[include-book][0m event did not include the summary strings for
  warnings generated by subsidiary events.  This has been fixed.

  Macro [47m[cw][0m has been documented and now expands to a call of a
  [47m;[0m[47m[logic][0m mode function.  See [cw] for a way to print to the screen
  without having to involve the ACL2 [47m[state][0m.  Thanks to Rob Sumners
  for suggesting that we document this useful utility.

  Functions [47mduplicates[0m, [47madd-to-set-equal[0m, [47mintersection-eq[0m, [47mevens[0m, and
  [47modds[0m are now [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions.")
 (NOTE-2-5{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.5(r) (June, 2000) Notes

  Important changes to non-standard version:

  Please see [note-2-5] for changes to Version 2.5 of ACL2.  We hope to
  write more documentation for ACL2(r) in the future.")
 (NOTE-2-6
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 (November, 2001) Notes

  Because of the large number of modifications, we have divided up the
  Version 2.6 notes into the following subtopics.

    * New functionality (see [note-2-6-new-functionality])

    * Changes in proof engine (see [note-2-6-proofs])

    * Changes in rules and definitions (see [note-2-6-rules])

    * Guard-related changes (see [note-2-6-guards])

    * proof-builder changes (see [note-2-6-proof-builder])

    * System-level changes (see [note-2-6-system])

    * Other (minor) changes (see [note-2-6-other])


Subtopics

  [Note-2-6-guards]
      ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Guard-related Changes

  [Note-2-6-new-functionality]
      ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on New Functionality

  [Note-2-6-other]
      ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Other (Minor) Changes

  [Note-2-6-proof-builder]
      ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on proof-builder Changes

  [Note-2-6-proofs]
      ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Changes in Proof Engine

  [Note-2-6-rules]
      ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Changes in Rules and Constants

  [Note-2-6-system]
      ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on System-level Changes")
 (NOTE-2-6-GUARDS
  (NOTE-2-6)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Guard-related Changes

  When you [47m[declare][0m that a function treats certain formals as
  [47m:[0m[47m[stobj][0m[47ms[0m, the [guard] of the function is automatically extended to
  include the corresponding stobj-recognizer calls.  For example, if
  a definition includes [47m(declare (xargs :stobjs (ST)))[0m then the guard
  of the function is changed by the addition of the conjunct [47m(ST-P
  ST)[0m.

  One impact of this is that if you use the built-in ACL2 [47m[state][0m as a
  formal parameter of a function, [47m(STATE-P STATE)[0m is added to the
  guard.  This may introduce a guard where there was none in previous
  versions of the system.  In older versions, therefore, no attempt
  would be made to [47m[verify-guards][0m, while in the new version, we
  would attempt guard verification.  You may wish to add [47m(declare
  (xargs :verify-guards nil))[0m to such definitions.

  A related change affects users who do not use stobjs or [47mstate[0m.  In
  previous versions of the system --- as now --- a [47mtype[0m declaration
  extended the guard you provided explicitly.  Thus, if you wrote
  [47m(declare (type integer n))[0m then [47m(INTEGERP n)[0m was added to your
  guard.  This is still the case and [47m:stobjs[0m recognizers are
  similarly added.  But in older versions of the system we ``added''
  the conjuncts without checking whether they were already present in
  the guard you provided.  This sometimes produced such guards as
  [47m(and (integerp n) (integerp n))[0m where the first was produced by
  your [47mtype[0m declaration and the second was your [47m:guard[0m.  We now
  eliminate redundant conjuncts; this may rearrange the order of the
  conjuncts.

  The guard conjectures for functions using [47mstobj[0ms have been simplified
  somewhat by taking advantage of the syntactic restrictions checked
  for single-threaded objects.

  The following functions have been modified so that character and
  string arguments are restricted to standard characters.  (See
  [standard-char-p] and see [standard-char-listp].)

      [47m[upper-case-p][0m [47m[lower-case-p][0m [47m[char-upcase][0m [47m[char-downcase][0m
      [47mstring-downcase1[0m [47m[string-downcase][0m [47mstring-upcase1[0m
      [47m[string-upcase][0m [47m[char-equal][0m [47mstring-equal1[0m [47m[string-equal][0m

  Also, function [47mstandard-string-alistp[0m replaces function
  [47mstring-alistp[0m, with concomitant changes in the guard to
  [47m[assoc-string-equal][0m, and in variable [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.  Also, lemma
  [47mstandard-string-alistp-forward-to-alistp[0m replaces lemma
  [47mstring-alistp-forward-to-alistp[0m.  There is a new lemma
  [47mstandard-char-p-nth[0m, which has also been added to [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.

  The guard had been inadvertently omitted from the definition of the
  function [47m[substitute][0m (and its subroutine [47msubstitute-ac[0m).  This
  omission has been corrected; also, the guard is slightly stronger
  than the documentation had claimed (and that has been corrected).")
 (NOTE-2-6-NEW-FUNCTIONALITY
  (NOTE-2-6)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on New Functionality

  A fundamental change is the provision of the ``nu-rewriter'' for
  simplifying expressions composed of [47mNTH[0m, [47mUPDATE-NTH[0m, and
  [47mUPDATE-NTH-ARRAY[0m applications and [47mLET[0m expressions and other calls
  of non-recursive functions or [47mLAMBDA[0m expressions involving those
  symbols.  The nu-rewriter applies the obvious rewrite rule for [47m(NTH
  i (UPDATE-NTH j v s))[0m and the analogous rule for [47mUPDATE-NTH-ARRAY[0m.
  See nu-rewriter.  The nu-rewriter can be enabled with
  [47mset-nu-rewriter-mode[0m.

  A new flag has been added to the [47mxargs[0m of [47m[defun][0m permitting the
  declaration that the function is [47mnon-executable[0m.  The usage is
  [47m(declare (xargs :non-executable t))[0m and the effect is that the
  function has no executable counterpart.  On the positive side: the
  function is permitted to use single-threaded object names and
  functions arbitrarily, as in theorems rather than as in executable
  definitions.  Such functions are not permitted to declare any names
  [47m:[0m[47m[stobj][0m[47ms[0m but accessors, etc., may be used, just as in theorems.

  A new flag has been added to permit the system to abbreviate output
  by introducing [47mLET*[0m notation identifying common subterms.  The
  formula being proved is not affected; this flag changes its
  displayed form only.  See [set-let*-abstractionp].

  A ``raw mode'' has been added, primarily for faster loading of
  applications.  see [set-raw-mode].

  Functions [47m[alphorder][0m and [47m[lexorder][0m have been put in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.
  [47mLexorder[0m is now a total order ordering of the ACL2 universe, and
  theorems are included to that effect.  Thanks to Pete Manolios for
  suggesting the idea and providing events to use, and to Rob Sumners
  for assistance with some modifications.  See also the new book
  [47mbooks/misc/total-order[0m for an irreflexive total order.

  The ACL2 user can now make system calls to the host operating system.
  See [sys-call] and see [sys-call-status].  Thanks to Rob Sumners
  for working out this idea with Pete Manolios and Robert Krug, who
  we also thank, and for working out the implementation with us.

  It is no longer required to use absolute [pathname]s in
  [47m[include-book][0m forms that have been executed before a
  [47m[certify-book][0m.  Any relative pathname strings in such contexts
  will be expanded into absolute pathnames before they are saved in
  the [47m[portcullis][0m of the [47m[certificate][0m of the book being certified.

  ACL2 can now be built on top of Allegro Common Lisp 6.0, and also on
  Windows platforms on top of Allegro Common Lisp and GCL.  Thanks to
  Pete Manolios and Vinay K. Siddhavanahalli for their help with
  Windows.

  Rob Sumners has designed and provided an initial implementation for
  two improvements to [47m[defstobj][0m (also see [stobj]).  First, array
  fields can now be resized.  Resize and length functions are
  provided for array fields, which can be used to resize stobj array
  fields dynamically.  The recognizers for array fields have been
  simplified to accommodate this change, so that they only check that
  each element of the array field has the specified type.  Second,
  performance has been improved for stobjs with a large number of
  fields, by changing their Common Lisp implementation to store the
  fields in a simple vector instead of a list.

  Now [stobj]s may be bound locally; see [with-local-stobj].  Thanks to
  Rob Sumners, who encouraged us to implement this capability, was an
  early user of it, and participated usefully in discussions on its
  design.

  New functions [47m[fms!][0m, [47m[fmt!][0m, and [47m[fmt1!][0m are the same as their
  respective functions without the ``[47m![0m,'' except that the ``[47m![0m''
  functions are guaranteed to print forms that can be read back in
  (at a slight readability cost).

  We added [47m[extended-metafunctions][0m, metafunctions which allow [47m[state][0m
  and context sensitive rewriting to some extent.  We thank Robert
  Krug for pushing for and on this idea.

  The documentation has been improved.  In particular, a new
  documentation topic provides a gentle introduction to ACL2 [47m[arrays][0m
  --- see [arrays-example] --- and additional documentation has been
  provided for getting started with proof trees in emacs --- see
  [proof-tree].

  New Makefile targets [47mfasl[0m and [47mo[0m have been added to the [47mbooks/[0m
  directory of the distribution.  For example, you might first
  certify books using an ACL2 built on top of GCL (which creates
  compiled files with suffix [47mo[0m).  Then, when standing in the [47mbooks/[0m
  directory, you might execute the command

      make fasl ACL2=my-allegro-acl2

  which will create compiled ([47m.fasl[0m) files for Allegro Common Lisp,
  assuming that [47mmy-allegro-acl2[0m starts up an ACL2 built on that
  Common Lisp.

  The macro [47m[let*][0m now allows variables to be declared ignored.  See
  [let*] and see [let].

  The user may now control backchaining.  This feature was designed and
  primarily implemented by Robert Krug (though the authors of ACL2
  are responsible for any errors); thanks, Robert!  See
  [backchain-limit].

  It is now possible to ``slow down'' the rate at which case splits are
  generated by the simplifier.  See [set-case-split-limitations].

  Accesses to [stobj]s using [47m[nth][0m or [47m[update-nth][0m are now displayed
  using symbolic constants instead of numeric indices.  For example,
  given the event

    (defstobj foo a b :renaming ((b c)))

  then the term [47m(nth 0 foo)[0m will be displayed (for example, during
  proofs) as [47m(nth *a* foo)[0m while [47m(nth 1 foo)[0m will be displayed as
  [47m(nth *c* foo)[0m.  The [47m[defstobj][0m event now correspondingly introduces
  a [47m[defconst][0m event for each field accessor function, introducing a
  constant whose name is obtained from the accessor's name by
  prefixing and suffixing a ``[47m*[0m,'' as in the example above: accessor
  [47ma[0m generates [47m(defconst *a* 0)[0m and accessor [47mc[0m generates [47m(defconst *c*
  1)[0m. See [nth-aliases-table] for how to extend this feature for
  alternate names of [stobj]s.

  Computed hints have been improved.  It is now possible to detect
  within a computed hint whether the goal clause is stable under
  simplification; it is also possible for a computed hint to change
  the list of available hints.  See [computed-hints].

  It is now possible to provide ``default hints'' that are appended to
  the hints explicitly provided.  See [set-default-hints].

  Using computed hints (see [computed-hints]) and default hints (see
  [set-default-hints]) it is possible to implement a book that
  supports ``priority phased simplification.'' Using this book you
  can assign priorities to your rules and cause the theorem prover to
  simplify each goal maximally under all the rules of one priority
  before enabling rules of the next priority.  See
  [47mbooks/misc/priorities.lisp[0m.

  The macro [47m[defabbrev][0m has been improved to allow [47m[declare][0m forms and
  documentation strings and to do more error-checking.  Thanks to Rob
  Sumners for designing this enhancement and providing the first
  implementation.  See [defabbrev].

  Further changes were made to support CMU Lisp.  Wolfhard Buss helped
  with these changes.

  A new table was added that is used when printing proof output, so
  that nests of right-associated calls of a binary function are
  replaced by corresponding macro calls, as has been the case for
  [47m[binary-+][0m and [47m[+][0m, [47m[binary-append][0m and [47m[append][0m, and so on.  See
  [add-binop].

  Operators [47m[logand][0m, [47m[logior][0m, [47m[logxor][0m, and [47m[logeqv][0m are now macros
  (formerly, they were functions) that call corresponding binary
  functions (e.g., [47mbinary-logand[0m) defined in source file
  [47m\"axioms.lisp\"[0m.  Thanks to Rob Sumners for this enhancement.  Proof
  output will however continue to show calls of [47m[logand][0m, [47m[logior][0m,
  [47m[logxor][0m, and [47m[logeqv][0m.

  Function [47m([0m[47m[allocate-fixnum-range][0m[47m fixnum-lo fixnum-hi)[0m sets aside
  more \"permanent\" fixnums in GCL.

  ACL2 now runs under [47mCLISP[0m.  Thanks to Wolfhard Buss and Sam Steingold
  for their assistance with the port.

  Michael ``Bogo'' Bogomolny has created a search engine, accessible
  from the ACL2 home page.  For that purpose he modified the HTML
  translator to create one file per topic (a good idea in any case).
  Thanks, Bogo!

  An emacs file of potential (but optional) use for ACL2 users may be
  found in [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m.  In particular, this file supports
  the use of proof trees (see [proof-tree]).

  Some [books] have been added or modified.  In particular, Robert Krug
  has contributed [47mbooks/arithmetic-2/[0m, which provides an alternative
  to the existing collection of books about arithmetic,
  [47mbooks/arithmetic/[0m.  For a discussion of the distributed books see
  the link to [47mREADME.html[0m in the installation instructions.")
 (NOTE-2-6-OTHER
  (NOTE-2-6)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Other (Minor) Changes

  Warning strings are now case-insensitive.  See
  [set-inhibit-warnings].

  ACL2 causes a warning when an [in-theory] hint or event causes a
  0-ary function's definition to be disabled but its
  [47m:[0m[47m[executable-counterpart][0m to be enabled.

  A minor modification has been made to [47m[defstobj][0m that can have a
  positive impact on performance in Allegro Common Lisp.  (For Lisp
  hackers: the stobj name was formerly declared special, and that was
  disabling Allegro's tail-merging routing for compilation of some
  recursive functions using stobjs.)  The downside is that stobj
  names can no longer be evaluated in raw Lisp.  However, raw Lisp is
  not the right place to be evaluating ACL2 forms anyhow; see
  [set-raw-mode].  We thank Rob Sumners for bringing this issue to
  our attention.

  Before Version 2.6, there has been the following problem with
  [47m[defstub][0m and [47m[encapsulate][0m in the case that the current package is
  not the ACL2 package.  If a [signature] was specified using the
  symbol [47m=>[0m, then that symbol had to have been imported into the
  current package from the ACL2 package when the current package was
  defined.  There are no longer any package restrictions on the use
  of [47m=>[0m.  Thanks to John Cowles for bringing this problem to our
  attention.

  Bugs in [47m[defun-sk][0m have been fixed.  [47mDefun-sk[0m forms introducing
  functions of no arguments were failing to be admitted, for example:
  [47m(defun-sk always-p1 () (forall (x) (p1 x)))[0m.  Thanks to John Cowles
  for bringing this problem to our attention.  Also, [47mdefun-sk[0m failed
  on an example in the documentation (see
  [tutorial4-defun-sk-example]), as pointed out by Matyas Sustik;
  this bug has been fixed as well.

  The trace mechanism has been fixed to handle [stobj]s, and to avoid
  the printing of so-called [3menabled structures[0m.

  The [47m[brr][0m command [47m:type-alist[0m now produces more readable output.

  An [47m[include-book][0m of an uncertified book no longer loads an
  associated compiled file.

  We added a few checks to make sure that the underlying lisp is
  suitable, for example checking that the reader is case-insensitive
  and reads in symbols with upper-case names where appropriate.

  We now warn when forcing (see [force]) or immediate force mode (see
  [immediate-force-modep]) change state between enabled and disabled.
  Also see [enable-immediate-force-modep] and see
  [disable-immediate-force-modep] for information about these new
  macros, which may be used to control immediate force mode.

  We have eliminated the use of a low-level raw Lisp constant,
  [47m*most-recent-multiplicity*[0m.  Our test suite saw a speed-up of
  approximately 2% as a result for an ACL2 image built on GCL (but no
  significant speed-up for an ACL2 image built on Allegro Common
  Lisp).  We thank Rob Sumners for suggesting this improvement.

  Fixnum declarations are now realized as [47m(signed-byte 29)[0m instead of
  [47m(signed-byte 27)[0m.  We check that the underlying Common Lisp
  recognizes objects of type [47m(signed-byte 29)[0m as fixnums, with the
  exception of CLISP, which is said to have an efficient bignum
  implementation.

  A new documentation topic [functional-instantiation-example]
  illustrates functional instantiation.

  A bug has been fixed in the monitoring of runes (see [monitor]).
  Thanks to Dave Greve for sending an example that clearly showed the
  problem.

  A warning is now issued when it is detected that a
  [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule may not be as strong as it appears
  because it is not sufficient to prove itself by type reasoning.

  An error is caused for rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m when the function
  symbol [47mIF[0m is among the [47m:trigger-fns[0m.  ([47mIF[0m was ignored anyhow; the
  point of this change is to avoid misleading the user.)

  A minor bug has been fixed in [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m, evident for example if this
  command was applied to [47mIF[0m.

  A minor hole in [47m:[0m[47m[set-bogus-mutual-recursion-ok][0m did not permit the
  acceptance of [47m[mutual-recursion][0m forms that include constant
  function definitions.  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Eric Smith
  for coming up with a simple example illustrating the problem.

  The temporary files \"TMP.lisp\" and \"TMP1.lisp\" written out by [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m
  are now written to the connected book directory (see [cbd]).

  Previously, the Allegro compiler was not eliminating tail recursion
  for executable counterparts of functions, because of the way one of
  its flags had been set.  As a result, calls of functions whose
  guards had not been verified could run out of stack space when this
  was not necessary.  This situation has been fixed.

  Executable counterparts could have slow array accesses.  This has
  been fixed (specifically, constants are no longer replaced with
  their values in the definitions of executable counterparts).

  Various improvements have been made to the documentation.  Thanks in
  particular to Eric Smith for pointing out a numbers of places where
  fixes were in order.

  File \"mcl-acl2-startup.lisp\" has been updated, thanks to feedback
  from Philippe Georgelin.

  Inefficiencies in GCL fixnum computations were remedied for macros [47m+f[0m
  and [47m*f[0m.  Thanks to Rob Sumners for pointing out this issue.")
 (NOTE-2-6-PROOF-BUILDER
  (NOTE-2-6)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on proof-builder Changes

  The proof-builder command [47m=[0m, when used with no arguments, now reports
  which hypothesis is being used.

  The output from [proof-builder] command [47mtype-alist[0m has been improved.

  A slight change has been made to the [proof-builder] for commands
  [47mpromote[0m, [47mcasesplit[0m, [47mequiv[0m, and [47m=[0m, so that terms of the form [47m(if x
  nil y)[0m are recognized as conjunctions, [47m(and (not x) y)[0m.  Thanks to
  Pete Manolios for suggesting that we consider such a change.

  There is a new [proof-builder] command [47mprint-all-concs[0m that prints
  all the conclusions of the unproved goals.

  A new [47m[proof-builder][0m command, [47mrunes[0m, has been added.  It reports the
  [rune]s that have participated in the interactive proof up to the
  current point.")
 (NOTE-2-6-PROOFS
  (NOTE-2-6)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Changes in Proof Engine

  Certain optimizations are performed when converting terms to clausal
  form.  For example, [47m(< 0 1)[0m is known to be [47mt[0m, [47m(HARD-ERROR ctx str
  alist)[0m is known to be [47mnil[0m, and [47m(INTEGERP n)[0m is known to imply
  [47m(RATIONALP n)[0m.

  In earlier versions of ACL2, the conversion of a term to clausal form
  expanded [47mLAMBDA[0m applications.  That may no longer occur.  Some
  proofs may slow down (or fail) because your [47mLAMBDA[0m-expressions are
  not expanded away when you ``expected'' them to be.

  Robert Krug found a soundness bug in our linear arithmetic package.
  The bug was caused by the derivation of an equation from two
  inequalities without taking adequate precautions to ensure that
  both sides of the inequalities were numeric.  Robert also kindly
  provided a fix which we adopted.  Thanks Robert!

  We fixed a bug that could prevent the application of a metatheorem.

  A bug has been fixed that had caused bogus forcing rounds (see
  [forcing-round]).  The bug could occur when the hypothesis of a
  rule was forced (see [force]) before the prover decided to start
  over and prove the original goal by induction.  Thanks to Rob
  Sumners for drawing our attention to this problem.

  Some low-level fixes have been made that prevent certain infinite
  loops, based on reports by users.  We thank Yunja Choi, Matt
  Wilding, and Pete Manolios for reporting such problems.

  An obscure potential soundness hole has been fixed by redoing the way
  evaluation takes place in the ACL2 loop and during theorem proving.
  We expect that users will see no difference based on this change.
  (Those interested in the details can see the long comment ``Essay
  on Evaluation in ACL2'' in source file interface-raw.lisp.)

  A small change was made in computation for a heuristic that controls
  backchaining.  This will speed up proofs dramatically in a very few
  cases but should have a very small impact in general.

  The simplifier has been modified to avoid eliminating hypotheses of
  goals that can be established by contextual (specifically,
  type-set) reasoning alone.  We believe that this change will
  generally strengthen ACL2's reasoning engine, although on rare
  occasions a lemma that formerly was provable may require user
  assistance.  Thanks to Robert Krug for suggesting this change and
  providing its implementation.

  Case splits are now limited, by default.  This may allow some proof
  attempts to provide output where previously the prover would appear
  to ``go out to lunch.'' For a more complete discussion, including
  instructions for how users can control case splitting, see
  [set-case-split-limitations].

  A bug has been fixed in the handling of [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules by
  the [bdd] package.  Thanks to Rob Sumners for discovering this bug
  and supplying a helpful example.

  ACL2 may now use the built-in induction scheme for a function symbol
  even if that function symbol is disabled.  Formerly, if a function
  symbol was disabled then its induction scheme was only considered
  if an explicit induction hint was supplied, other than [47m:induct t[0m.

  We eliminated the rule-class [47mlinear-alias[0m.  This rule class was
  seldom used and complicated the linear arithmetic decision
  procedure in ways that made it difficult to extend to handle some
  non-linear special cases.  The only use of the rule-class that we
  know of was in our own [47mNqthm[0m books, which were an attempt to
  provide an embedding of the Nqthm logic and theorem prover into
  ACL2.  But that facility was also practically never used, as far as
  we know.  So both [47mlinear-alias[0m rules and the [47mNqthm[0m books have been
  eliminated.

  In earlier versions of ACL2, when the [47mIF[0m-form of [47m(AND p q)[0m was
  assumed true --- as when rewriting the [47malpha[0m expression in [47m(IF (AND
  p q) alpha beta)[0m --- the assumption mechanism did not deduce that [47mp[0m
  and [47mq[0m are true, only that their conjunction, in its [47mIF[0m-form, is
  true.  This has long been known as a deficiency in both ACL2 and
  the earlier Nqthm but it was tedious to do better when one
  considered the full range of [47mIF[0m-forms one might encounter in the
  test of another [47mIF[0m.  Rather than code all the cases, we just waited
  until clausification got rid of them.  Robert Krug developed a
  pretty nice treatment of the general case and we added it in this
  version.  This also involved a surprising number of changes
  elsewhere in the system because the improved handling of
  assumptions caused the theorem prover often to ``erase'' hypotheses
  provided by [47m:use[0m hints because it could simplify them to [47mt[0m.  Thank
  you Robert!

  In response to a suggestion from Robert Krug, we added [47mmfc-ap[0m so that
  extended metafunctions can take advantage of linear arithmetic.
  See [extended-metafunctions].

  There is less delay in printing goals.  In previous versions, a goal
  was not printed until its subgoals were created (or the goal was
  proved).  Now, the goal is printed essentially as soon as it is
  created.

  A small technical change has been made in the function [47m[term-order][0m,
  to give priority on the function symbol count over the weighting of
  constants.  So for example, while previously the term [47m(f)[0m preceded
  the constant 2, that is no longer the case.  If this change is
  noticed at all, it will probably be noticed in how so-called
  [3mpermutative[0m rewrite rules are applied; see [loop-stopper].  Thanks
  to Robert Krug for suggesting this improvement and providing part
  of the implementation.")
 (NOTE-2-6-RULES
  (NOTE-2-6)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on Changes in Rules and Constants

  The following symbols have been added to the list constant
  [47m*common-lisp-specials-and-constants*[0m: [47mREPLACE[0m, [47mFILL[0m, [47mCHARACTER[0m, [47m=[0m,
  [47mBREAK[0m, and [47mPRIN1[0m.  This was done in support of ports to Allegro 6.0
  and Windows platforms (see [note-2-6-new-functionality]).

  The list of symbols in [47m*acl2-exports*[0m has been modified, for example
  to include [47mshow-accumulated-persistence[0m and the legal arguments to
  [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m.

  Functions [47m[zp][0m and [47m[zip][0m are now handled slightly differently.  They
  are are now disabled, but each comes with a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule that
  allows their expansion on non-variable terms, and also with a
  [47m:[0m[47m[compound-recognizer][0m rule that avoids the need for opening up
  these functions when applied to variables.  The resulting behavior
  should be very similar to the behavior of previous versions, except
  that case splits will be avoided when these functions are applied
  to variables.

  Function [47mstandard-string-alistp[0m replaces function [47mstring-alistp[0m.  For
  further discussion, see [note-2-6-guards].

  Rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m whose conclusion is a term of the form
  [47m(equal lhs rhs)[0m have always been stored in the expected way: [47mlhs[0m
  rewrites to [47mrhs[0m.  This way of storing [47m:rewrite[0m rules has been
  extended to allow [47m[=][0m, [47m[eq][0m, or [47m[eql][0m in place of [47m[equal][0m.

  Rewrite rule [47mnth-update-nth[0m, in source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m, has been
  strengthened.

  A new rewrite rule [47mequal-constant-+[0m has been added to the book
  [47marithmetic/equalities[0m.  This should generally be a beneficial
  change, but existing proofs involving the arithmetic books could
  conceivably be affected.

  Function [47m[symbol-package-name][0m and constant [47m*main-lisp-package-name*[0m
  have undergone small changes.  This change should rarely be noticed
  by users and is discussed elsewhere; see [note-2-6-system].

  We mention here that proofs involving [stobj]s may need to be
  modified because of changes in auxiliary functions generated by
  [47m[defstobj][0m.  (These changes were made in support of a new resizing
  capability, mentioned elsewhere in these release notes; see
  [note-2-6-new-functionality].

  In the distributed book directory [47mbooks/arithmetic/[0m, the book
  [47mrationals-with-axioms-proved.lisp[0m has been renamed [47mrationals.lisp[0m.

  (ACL2(r) only) Rewrite rules [47mrealp-+[0m, [47mrealp-*[0m, [47mrealp-unary--[0m, and
  [47mrealp-unary-/[0m have been added in analogy to existing rules
  [47mrationalp-+[0m, [47mrationalp-*[0m, [47mrationalp-unary--[0m, and [47mrationalp-unary-/[0m.
  Thanks to Jun Sawada for suggesting this change.

  The definition of [47m[aref1][0m has been modified slightly.  Previously, if
  [47m*my-a*[0m were an array then [47m(aref1 'some-name *my-a* :header)[0m would
  evaluate to the [47mcdr[0m of the [47m[header][0m of [47m*my-a*[0m rather than to its
  [47m[default][0m.  See [arrays].

  Changes have been made in the [47mihs[0m books, based on suggestions from
  Jun Sawada, that support its use with ACL2(r) (see [real]).  The
  primary change is to replace calls of [47m[rationalp][0m with calls of
  [47m[real/rationalp][0m, which should have no effect on users of standard
  ACL2.")
 (NOTE-2-6-SYSTEM
  (NOTE-2-6)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6 Notes on System-level Changes

  We modified the tracking of [47m[skip-proofs][0m events and the use of
  [47m[state][0m global [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m in order to avoid some soundness
  issues.  For example, [47m[skip-proofs][0m events buried in
  locally-included books are now tracked.  The ``Essay on
  Skip-proofs'' in source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m gives several examples of
  dicey behavior that is no longer supported.

  We fixed a problem with some of the makefiles, so that recursive
  invocations of `make' now use the version of `make' specified on
  the command line.

  Files were fixed to help non-Unix/Linux users with book
  certification.  Thanks to John Cowles for finding some problems and
  suggesting fixes to [47mbooks/certify-numbers.lisp[0m,
  [47mbooks/arithmetic/certify.lsp[0m, and [47mbooks/cowles/certify.lsp[0m.  We
  thank Scott Burson for noticing and fixing some other such
  problems.  Moreover, a bdd test was being ignored entirely in
  Version 2.5; this problem has been fixed as well.

  A minor change in system function save-acl2-in-allegro will allow
  this function to continue to work in Allegro CL versions starting
  (someday) with 10.0.  Thanks to Art Flatau for suggesting such a
  fix.

  The [47mbooks/case-studies/[0m directory has been removed.  These books are
  in support of the first (1998) ACL2 workshop, and are accessible
  via the {ACL2 home page |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/}.  Also, the
  [47mbooks/cli-misc[0m directory has been renamed [47mbooks/misc[0m, and the
  [47mbooks/nqthm[0m directory has been removed.

  The notion of ACL2 version has been slightly modified to catch
  unsoundness due to implementation dependencies.  See [version].
  Another change to eliminate such unsoundness is that built-in
  symbols now have a [47m[symbol-package-name][0m of [47m\"COMMON-LISP\"[0m;
  formerly, this string was [47m\"LISP\"[0m for ACL2 images built on GCL.  See
  [symbol-package-name].  At a low level, the (undocumented) constant
  [47m*main-lisp-package-name*[0m is now [47m\"COMMON-LISP\"[0m; before, it was
  [47m\"LISP\"[0m for GCL.")
 (NOTE-2-6{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.6(r) (November, 2001) Notes

  Important changes to non-standard version: None since Version 2.5.

  Please see [note-2-6] for changes to Version 2.6 of ACL2.  We hope to
  write more documentation for ACL2(r) in the future.")
 (NOTE-2-7
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 (November, 2002) Notes

  The Version_2.7 notes are divided into the subtopics below.  Here we
  give only a brief summary of a few of the changes that seem most
  likely to impact existing proofs.  Not included in this brief
  summary, but included in the subtopics, are descriptions of
  improvements (including bug fixes and new functionality) that
  should not get in the way of existing proof efforts.

  In particular, please see [note-2-7-new-functionality] for discussion
  of a number of new features that you may find useful.

  Acknowledgments and elaboration, as well as other changes, can be
  found in the subtopics listed below.

  Bug fixes (see [note-2-7-bug-fixes])

    * Three soundness bugs were fixed.  These bugs were probably rarely
      hit, so users may well not notice these changes.

    * [47m[Certify-book][0m now requires [47m:skip-proofs-ok t[0m (respectively,
      [47m:defaxioms-okp t[0m) if there are [47m[skip-proofs][0m (respectively,
      [47m[defaxiom][0m) events in the book or any included sub-books.

    * When [47m:by[0m hints refer to a definition, they now use the original body
      of that definition rather than the simplified (``normalized'')
      body.

    * When [47m[ld][0m is applied to a stringp file name, it now temporarily sets
      the connected book directory (see [cbd]) to the directory of
      that file while evaluating forms in that file.

  New functionality (see [note-2-7-new-functionality])

    * ACL2 now works harder to apply [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules with
      free variables in the hypotheses.  See
      [note-2-7-new-functionality], in particular its first two
      paragraphs, for details.  [Forward-chaining] also does more
      with free variables.

  Changes in proof engine (see [note-2-7-proofs])

    * Some prover heuristics have changed slightly.  Among other
      consequences, this can cause subgoal [hints] to change.  For
      example, suppose that the Version_2.6 proof of a particular
      theorem generated \"Subgoal 2\" and \"Subgoal 1\" while Version_2.7
      only generates the second of these.  Then a subgoal hint
      attached to \"Subgoal 1\" in Version_2.6 would have to be
      attached to \"Goal'\" in Version_2.7.  (See [goal-spec].)  The
      full topic has details (see [note-2-7-proofs]).

  Changes in rules and definitions (see [note-2-7-rules])

    * The package name of a generated variable has changed for [47m[defcong][0m.

  Guard-related changes (see [note-2-7-guards])

    * [47m[Guard][0m verification formerly succeeded in a few cases where it
      should have failed.

    * Guards generated from type declarations now use functions
      [47msigned-byte-p[0m and [47munsigned-byte-p[0m, now defined in source file
      [47maxioms.lisp[0m and formerly defined rather similarly under
      [47mbooks/ihs/[0m.

  proof-builder changes (see [note-2-7-proof-builder])

    * See the above doc topic.

  System-level changes (see [note-2-7-system])

    * See the above doc topic.

  Other changes (see [note-2-7-other])

    * A new [47m[table][0m, [47m[invisible-fns-table][0m, takes the place of the handling
      of invisible functions in the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m,

    * The [47m[theory-invariant][0m event has been modified so that the default
      action is an error rather than a warning.

    * Proof output that reports destructor elimination no longer uses the
      word ``generalizing''.

  Again, please proceed to the subtopics for more thorough release
  notes.


Subtopics

  [Note-2-7-bug-fixes]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Bug Fixes

  [Note-2-7-guards]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Guard-related Changes

  [Note-2-7-new-functionality]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on New Functionality

  [Note-2-7-other]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Miscellaneous Changes

  [Note-2-7-proof-builder]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on proof-builder Changes

  [Note-2-7-proofs]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Changes in Proof Engine

  [Note-2-7-rules]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Changes in Rules and Constants

  [Note-2-7-system]
      ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on System-level Changes")
 (NOTE-2-7-BUG-FIXES
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Bug Fixes

  Francisco J. Martin-Mateos emailed us a soundness bug (!) in our
  handling of functional instantiation (for example see
  [functional-instantiation-example]).  We are grateful for that
  email, which clearly illustrated the problem.  It is included just
  below the definition of [47mpush-clause[0m in ACL2 source file [47mprove.lisp[0m,
  where we have fixed the bug.  This bug was fixed in a re-release of
  Version 2.6 in February, 2002.

  Rob Sumners emailed us a soundness bug (!) in function
  [47mcommutative-p1[0m, which is used by the ACL2 [bdd] package.  We are
  grateful for his help; his email gave a proof of nil and also
  pointed to the problem function.  This bug was fixed in a
  re-release of Version 2.6 in February, 2002.

  We discovered and fixed a soundness bug illustrated by the book
  below, which was certifiable in Version 2.6 and ends in a proof of
  [47mnil[0m.  The event [47m(verify-guards foo)[0m should have been rejected,
  because [47mfoo[0m calls a function whose guards have not been verified,
  namely, [47mbar[0m.  However, ACL2 did not notice the call of function [47mbar[0m
  in the body of [47mfoo[0m because it was looking in the simplified
  (normalized) body of [47mfoo[0m rather than in the original body of [47mfoo[0m.
  During processing of the book below, the logical definition of [47mzp[0m
  is used before [47m(verify-guards foo)[0m, and [47m(zp -3)[0m reduces to [47mt[0m in the
  logic.  After [47m(verify-guards foo)[0m, ACL2 simplifies [47m(foo -3)[0m by
  going into raw Lisp, where [47m(zp -3)[0m is evaluated and reduces to [47mnil[0m.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defun bar (x)
      (zp x))
    (defthm zp-false-on-negatives
      (implies (< x 0)
               (bar x))
      :rule-classes :type-prescription)
    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (rationalp x)
                      :verify-guards nil))
      (if (< x 0)
          (if (bar x) 0 1) ; simplified body reduces this line to 0
        17))
    (defthm foo-of-minus-3-is-0
      (equal (foo -3) 0)
      :rule-classes nil)
    (verify-guards foo)
    (defthm foo-of-minus-3-is-1
      (equal (foo -3) 1)
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm bug
      nil
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use (foo-of-minus-3-is-0 foo-of-minus-3-is-1))))

  The above bug exploited the fact that [47m[zp][0m has a different definition
  in raw Lisp than in the logic for arguments that violate its
  guard).  The following example caused a hard error in raw Lisp,
  though not a soundness error.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defun bar (x)
      (cons (car x) (car x)))
    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t
                      :verify-guards nil))
      (if (bar x) x nil))
    (verify-guards foo)
    (defthm bug
      (equal (foo 3) t)
      :rule-classes nil)

  We have made a minor change to the notion of the [3mformula[0m of a
  function symbol, related to the change above, which however is
  unlikely to be noticeable.

  In order to make it harder to hit problems like the guard problem
  above, we have slightly modified the raw Lisp definition of [47m[zp][0m.

  A [47m[break-rewrite][0m command, [47m:ancestors[0m, was broken, but has been
  fixed.  Thanks to Eric Smith for bringing the problem to our
  attention, and to Robert Krug for supplying the final part of the
  fix.

  Some [proof-builder] commands caused errors when all goals have
  already been proved.  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Matt Wilding
  for reporting this bug.

  Fixed a bug in [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m.  When compiling uncompiled functions with
  very large definitions, ACL2 was inserted a backslash ([47m\\[0m) character
  into generated files.

  Fixed the [47m:type-alist[0m [47m:[0m[47m[brr][0m command (see [brr-commands]), whose
  output was difficult to read when typed after an [47m:eval[0m..

  Fixed some clumsy handling of errors when including an uncertified
  book, for example, with the error message when including an
  uncertified book with a bad [47m[deftheory][0m event.  Thanks to Eric
  Smith for pointing out this problem.

  Two modifications to [47m[certify-book][0m now cause it to reflect natural
  expectations with respect to soundness.  First, it now has default
  values of [47mnil[0m instead of [47mt[0m for keyword arguments [47m:skip-proofs-okp[0m
  and [47m:defaxioms-okp[0m.  Thanks to Robert Krug for suggesting this
  change and the ACL2 seminar at the University of Texas for
  discussing it.  Second, when [47m:skip-proofs-okp[0m (respectively,
  [47m:defaxioms-okp[0m) is [47mnil[0m, either explicitly or by default, then
  [47m[skip-proofs][0m commands (respectively, [47m[defaxiom][0m events) are
  disallowed inside any included books, regardless of the keyword
  parameters passed to [47m[include-book][0m.  This had not been the case
  for previous versions of ACL2, regardless of the values of
  [47m:skip-proofs-okp[0m or [47m:defaxioms-okp[0m passed to [47m[include-book][0m.

  Improved warnings and errors for [47m[certify-book][0m and [47m[include-book][0m to
  mention the [portcullis] as a possible source of [47m[skip-proofs][0m and
  [47m[defaxiom][0ms.

  ACL2 formerly caused an error when [hints] in a [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m were not
  well-formed.  This situation could arise as follows when certifying
  a book.  A lemma FOO is proved [47m[local][0mly to the book (or, is
  present in a sub-book that is included locally).  The [47m:corollary[0m of
  a subsequent theorem, BAR, disables that rule in a hint.  When BAR
  is proved, this is not a problem.  But [47m[certify-book][0m makes a
  second pass after processing the events in a book: it essentially
  does an [47m[include-book][0m.  During the [47minclude-book[0m pass, FOO is not
  known (because it was [47m[local][0m), and therefore ACL2 fails to process
  the [47m[disable][0m of FOO in an [47m[in-theory][0m hint.  The fix is that
  during [47m[include-book][0m, [hints] are ignored in corollaries just as
  they have been for the main theorem (or definition).

  It was possible for guard verification to succeed where it should
  have failed.  We have fixed the bug (which was in source function
  (ironically named!) [47mfcons-term-smart[0m).  Thanks to Robert Krug for
  sending us an example of bungled guard verification.  It turns out
  that this bug was also present in Version_2.6.

  The [proof-builder] command [47m=[0m has been improved.  Formerly, it could
  fail to apply when certain [47m[implies][0m terms were in the context.
  Thanks to Pete Manolios for bringing this problem to our attention.

  The command [47m[add-binop][0m failed to work.  This has been fixed.  Thanks
  to Rob Sumners for pointing out this problem.  Also see
  [note-2-7-other] for a discussion of how this and another [table]
  are no longer part of the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m.

  Book certification could cause a segmentation fault in cases where
  the certification world (see [certify-book]) has a very large
  number of events.  This has been fixed.

  We now allow empty [47m:use[0m [hints] and empty hints, as requested by Eric
  Smith.  Examples:

    (\"Goal\" :use ())
    (\"Goal\")

  A large [47m[mutual-recursion][0m nest could cause a stack overflow when
  executing either [47m:pr FN[0m, [47m:pr! FN[0m, or [47m:monitor (:definition FN) t[0m,
  where [47mFN[0m is in that large mutual recursion nest.  This has been
  fixed (implementation detail: function [47mactual-props[0m has been made
  tail-recursive).  NOTE: If you just want the definition of [47mFN[0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[pf][0m[47m FN[0m can be much faster than [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m[47m FN[0m if [47mFN[0m is in a large
  [47m[mutual-recursion][0m.

  Hard Lisp errors could occur when including uncertified books.  This
  has been fixed; ACL2 now does syntax-checking formerly omitted when
  including uncertified books.

  Previously, the evaluation of [47m[defstobj][0m and [47m[mutual-recursion][0m forms
  could cause ``undefined'' warnings when the form was compiled.
  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Eric Smith for bringing a
  [47mmutual-recursion[0m example to our attention.

  A bug has been fixed in the syntactic check for valid [47m:[0m[47m[loop-stopper][0m
  values.  Formerly, valid [47m:loop-stopper[0m values were erroneously
  restricted to lists of length at most 2 (a minor problem, since
  these lists typically have length 1), and the function symbol(s)
  need not have been defined in the current ACL2 [world].  Thanks to
  Eric Smith for sending an example to demonstrate the latter
  problem.

  Functions definitions that are [47m:non-executable[0m (see [xargs]) had
  never been recognized as redundant, but this has been fixed.
  Thanks to Vernon Austel for pointing out this problem.

  Compilation using [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m now compiles user-defined [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode
  functions.  Formerly only [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions could be compiled
  using [47m:comp[0m.

  Handling of [47m:by[0m hints has been improved in essentially three ways.
  The primary change is that now, when the current goal exactly
  matches the supplied lemma instance, the subsumption test will
  always succeeds (see [hints], in particular the discussion of [47m:by[0m).
  Second, certain proof failures involving [47m:by[0m [hints] were failing
  silently, with duplicate messages ``As indicated by the hint, this
  goal is subsumed by....'' This could happen when the original goal
  was among the goals generated by applying the hint.  This problem
  has been fixed by no longer considering this proof step to be
  specious (see [specious-simplification]).  Third and finally, when
  the [lemma-instance] refers to a definition, the original body of
  that definition is used rather than the simplified (``normalized'')
  body.

  In addition to the above, we now recognize more cases of specious
  simplification (see [specious-simplification]).  Thanks to Eric
  Smith for bringing this issue to our attention.

  Fixed building of ACL2 under CLISP so that (1) the appropriate ACL2
  startup message is printed out when ACL2 starts up, and (2) the
  lisp process supplied to make, e.g., LISP=/usr/bin/clisp, is the
  one written out to the saved ACL2 file.  Thanks to Dave Greve and
  Noah Friedman for suggesting (2).  Also, ACL2 now works with CLISP
  2.30.  We have accommodated a change in CLISP's handling of streams
  and its package-locking mechanism, as well as certain non-standard
  characters that formerly could cause CLISP 2.30 to break, even when
  those characters are in comments.

  Eliminated compiler warnings for CMU Lisp.

  Fixed an incorrect error supplied when book certification proceeded
  so quickly that the file write dates of the book ([47m.lisp[0m file) and
  the corresponding compiled file are equal.  Now that error only
  occurs if the compiled file has a strictly earlier write date,
  which probably should never happen.

  Fixed an infinite loop when executing [47mmake clean-books[0m (and hence
  `make' with targets that call [47mclean-books[0m, namely,
  [47mcertify-books-fresh[0m, [47mregression-fresh[0m, and
  [47mregression-nonstd-fresh[0m), which could occur when any subdirectories
  of [47mbooks/[0m are missing --- even [47mworkshops/[0m, which is intended to be
  optional.  Thanks to Pete Manolios for pointing out this bug.

  The [47m[include-book][0m command now works properly even when filenames, or
  their directories or parent directories (etc.) are links.  Thanks
  to Matt Wilding for pointing out this problem.

  The commands [47m:[0m[47m[puff][0m [47m:[0m[47m[puff*][0m have been fixed.  Formerly, there was a
  bug when [47m:puff[0m or [47m:puff*[0m caused the execution of an [47m[include-book][0m
  for an absolute [pathname], [47mP[0m, that was other than the current
  connected book directory (see [cbd]).  When including [47mP[0m, any
  subsidiary [47m[include-book][0m with a relative pathname would be
  erroneously considered relative to the current [47m[cbd][0m rather than
  relative to the directory of [47mP[0m.  Thanks to Pete Manolios and Matt
  Wilding for pointing out this problem.

  It had been possible in a ``large'' ACL2 image to call
  [47m[verify-termination][0m successfully on built-in function [47m[sys-call][0m,
  with undesirable results.  This hole has been plugged.  Thanks to
  Rob Sumners for pointing out this problem.  The new function [47m[gc$][0m
  must also stay in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.

  ACL2 no longer warns when certifying a book based on [47m[local][0m
  functions whose [guard]s have not yet been verified.  Thanks to
  Pete Manolios for pointing out this issue.

  An occasional ``slow array warning'' had been possible during proofs.
  The following sequence shows how to evoke that warning in previous
  versions.

    (in-theory (disable binary-append))
    (in-theory (enable binary-append))
    (in-theory (disable binary-append))
    (ubt 2)
    (thm (equal (car (cons x y)) x))

  (See [note-2-7-other] for a discussion of a change to [47m[compress1][0m in
  support of this fix; however, users should not need to read that
  discussion.)

  The raw Lisp code for [47m[defchoose][0m had a small bug, which was only
  evidenced in CLISP implementations as far as we know.  It has been
  fixed.

  When [47m[ld][0m is applied to a stringp file name, it now temporarily sets
  the connected book directory (see [cbd]) to the directory of that
  file while evaluating forms in that file.  To see the effect of
  this change, imagine a subdirectory [47m\"sub\"[0m of the current directory,
  and imagine executing [47m(ld \"sub/foo.lisp\")[0m, where file [47mfoo.lisp[0m
  contains the form [47m(include-book \"bar\")[0m.  Presumably the intention
  was to consider the file [47mbar.lisp[0m in the same directory, [47msub/[0m, as
  [47mfoo.lisp[0m.  [47mLd[0m now honors that intention, but in previous versions
  [47m\"bar.lisp\"[0m would have been a reference to a file in the current
  directory, not in [47msub/[0m.

  For users of [47mrun-acl2[0m [perhaps there are none!]: A fix has been
  provided by a Debian user via Camm Maguire so that acl2-mode will
  work in Xemacs, which apparently uses variable [47mlisp-mode-shared-map[0m
  rather than [47mshared-lisp-mode-map[0m.

  ACL2 has, for a long time (always?), had a mechanism for avoiding
  re-proving [constraint]s generated by [47m:functional-instance[0m
  [lemma-instance]s in [47m:use[0m and [47m:by[0m hints.  But this mechanism had
  not applied to defined (as opposed to constrained) functions.  This
  has been fixed.  Thanks to Francisco J. Martin-Mateos (ChesKo) for
  pointing out this problem by sending a clear example.")
 (NOTE-2-7-GUARDS
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Guard-related Changes

  It was possible for guard verification to succeed where it should
  have failed.  See the discussion under [note-2-7-bug-fixes].

  There have been changes in the guards generated from type
  declarations for the following cases.  Thanks to Dave Greve and
  Matt Wilding for suggesting such changes.

    (type (signed-byte n) val)
    (type (unsigned-byte n) val)
    (type (integer m n) val)

  The following examples illustrate the changes.

    (type (signed-byte 4) x)
    ==> [old] (AND (INTEGERP X) (<= -8 X) (<= X 7))
    ==> [new] (SIGNED-BYTE-P 4 X)

    (type (unsigned-byte 4) x)
    ==> [old] (AND (INTEGERP X) (<= 0 X) (<= X 15))
    ==> [new] (UNSIGNED-BYTE-P 4 X)")
 (NOTE-2-7-NEW-FUNCTIONALITY
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on New Functionality

  ACL2 now has a more powerful technique for relieving a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or
  [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rule's hypothesis that contains free variables.  A new
  [documentation] section has been written describing the handling
  free variables in rules; see [free-variables].  In brief, the
  primary change is that when a free-variable match for the current
  hypothesis fails to allow subsequent hypotheses to be relieved,
  then additional matches may be attempted until they have all been
  tried.  Also see [rule-classes] (discussion of [47m:match-free[0m).  Also
  see [set-match-free-error], see [set-match-free-default], and see
  [add-match-free-override] for interfaces provided to the user for
  controlling the way ACL2 deals with free variables in hypotheses.
  We thank Rob Sumners for several helpful discussions about the
  designs of those interfaces, as well as Eric Smith and Robert Krug
  for helpful related discussions.  Robert Krug also found a
  performance bug in a preliminary version, for which we are
  grateful.

  WARNING: Book certification attempts may take much longer now that,
  by default, ACL2 looks for more free variable matches (see
  paragraph just above).  You can get the old behavior by inserting
  the form

    (set-match-free-default :once)

  just after the initial [47m[in-package][0m form.  However, rules from
  included books that have free variables can still slow down
  certification.  This can be fixed by inserting

    (add-match-free-override :once t)

  before the first event in the file that generates a proof.

  [Forward-chaining] has been made more powerful in the presence of
  free variables (see [free-variables]), thanks to a contribution by
  Erik Reeber.  Both before and now, when an attempt is made to
  relieve (prove) a hypothesis of a [47m:forward-chaining[0m rule in the
  case that at least one variable in that hypothesis is not yet
  bound, ACL2 looks in the current context for an instance of that
  hypothesis.  If it finds one, then it binds the unbound variables
  and continues to the next hypothesis.  What is new is that ACL2 can
  now look for multiple instances of that hypothesis.  Consider the
  following example; an explanation is below.

    (encapsulate (((op * *) => *))
                 (local (defun op (x y) (< x y)))
                 (defthm transitivity-of-op
                   (implies (and (op x y) (op y z)) (op x z))
                   :rule-classes :forward-chaining))

    ; fails in Version_2.6; succeeds in in Version_2.7
    (thm (implies (and (op a b) (op b c) (op b e)) (op a c)))

  Before Version_2.7, the proof of the [47mthm[0m above fails.  When the
  [47m:forward-chaining[0m rule [47mtransitivity-of-op[0m binds [47mx[0m to [47ma[0m and [47my[0m to [47mb[0m,
  it then looks for an instance of [47m(op y z)[0m in the current context,
  with [47my[0m bound to [47mb[0m but [47mz[0m unbound.  It happens to find [47m(op b e)[0m
  before [47m(op b c)[0m, and it then adds [47m(op a e)[0m to the context.  But
  starting with Version_2.7, it continues to look for additional
  instances and finds [47m(op b c)[0m in the context as well, chaining
  forward to [47m(op a c)[0m and thus proving the theorem.

  A new macro, [47m[bind-free][0m, provides a simple way to get much or most
  of the power of [meta]functions.  Thanks to Eric Smith for coming
  up with the idea and to Robert Krug for providing an implementation
  (which we modified only very slightly) and documentation. See
  [bind-free] and see [bind-free-examples].

  With the addition of [47m[bind-free][0m (mentioned above), [47m[syntaxp][0m has
  become a macro, although that change should be transparent to the
  user.  More importantly, the argument of [47msyntaxp[0m may now refer to
  variables [47mmfc[0m and [47mstate[0m, giving [47msyntaxp[0m some of the power of
  extended metafunctions; see [syntaxp] and see
  [extended-metafunctions].  Thanks to Robert Krug for implementing
  that extension.  Also, the argument of [47m[syntaxp][0m may now include
  calls of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions.  See [syntaxp] and see
  [syntaxp-examples] (thanks to Robert Krug for updating the former
  and creating the latter documentation).

  The linear-arithmetic decision procedure (see [linear-arithmetic])
  has now been extended so that ACL2 can reason about non-linear
  arithmetic as well (see [non-linear-arithmetic] for how to turn on
  this feature).  We thank Robert Krug for the initial implementation
  of this, and Eric Smith for finding a couple of bugs in it.

  Some [47m[trace][0m utilities have been made available in the ACL2 loop.

    * Function [47m[trace$][0m (and also [47m[untrace$][0m) calls the corresponding
      underlying Lisp routine [47mtrace[0m (and [47muntrace[0m), which however
      continues (as it has for some time) to be enhanced for GCL and
      Allegro CL.

    * Macro [47m[open-trace-file][0m causes trace output to go to a specified
      file.  Macro [47m[close-trace-file][0m causes trace output to go to
      the screen (which is the default).

    * Macro [47mwith-error-trace[0m (or, [47mwet[0m for short) causes a backtrace to be
      written out for many failures, including guard violations.  See
      [trace], see [trace$], and see :DOC [47mwet[0m [** NOTE: eliminated
      after Version 3.3].

  A new [47m[theory][0m, [47m[minimal-theory][0m has been provided (see [theories]).
  It can be particularly useful for speeding up proofs involving [47m:use[0m
  [hints].

  New [47m[events][0m [47m[defund][0m and [47m[defthmd][0m behave exactly like [47m[defun][0m and
  [47m[defthm][0m, respectively, except that these new events disable the
  new name.

  The new macro [47m[with-output][0m can be used to suppress output that would
  normally result from evaluation of a form.

  The form [47m([0m[47m[pstack][0m[47m)[0m can give the user an idea of what the prover has
  been up to during a proof, or after a user-aborted proof.
  Moreover, by evaluating [47m(verbose-pstack t)[0m (see [verbose-pstack])
  one can get [trace]-like information about prover functions,
  including time summaries, printed to the screen during a proof.
  Thanks to Bill Legato and Robert Krug for initiating this work and
  to Robert for providing some initial implementation.

  The new command [47m:[0m[47m[comp-gcl][0m is identical in functionality, except
  that it always leaves [47m.c[0m and [47m.h[0m files when compiling in GCL.
  Thanks to Rob Sumners and Vernon Austel for suggesting such a
  capability.

  The macro [47m[e/d][0m provides a convenient way to [47m[enable][0m some rules and
  [47m[disable][0m others.  It was formerly in a book supplied with the
  distribution, [47mbooks/ihs/ihs-init.lisp[0m, written by Bishop Brock (who
  we thank for providing this useful macro).

  New distributed books include those in [47mbooks/ordinals/[0m,
  [47mbooks/rtl/rel3/[0m, and [47mbooks/misc/simplify-defuns.lisp[0m (which is
  documented in [47mbooks/misc/simplify-defuns.txt[0m).

  The [47m:expand[0m hint now accepts a special value, [47m:LAMBDAS[0m, that tells
  the ACL2 rewriter to expand all lambda applications ([47m[let][0m
  expressions).  See [hints].

  A new function [47m[zpf][0m has been added as a fast test against 0 for
  nonnegative fixnums.

  A new macro [47m[gc$][0m allows the user to call the garbage collector of
  the underlying Common Lisp.  Thanks to Rob Sumners for suggesting
  this feature.

  It is now possible to [47m[monitor][0m [simple] (abbreviation) rules.
  However, as a warning explains, they are still not considered
  monitored during preprocessing; see [monitor].  Thanks to Robert
  Krug for providing this improvement.

  The second argument of [47m[certify-book][0m, if supplied, formerly had to
  be either [47mt[0m or a non-negative integer.  Now it can be the symbol [47m?[0m,
  in the [47mACL2[0m package, indicating that the usual check should be
  suppressed on the number of commands that have been executed to
  create the world in which [47m[certify-book][0m was called.")
 (NOTE-2-7-OTHER
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Miscellaneous Changes

  Made several minor [documentation] improvements.  We are grateful to
  Eric Smith for suggesting (most of) these.

  Improved [47m(show-bdd)[0m (see [bdd]) to give more useful feedback when
  there are ``leaf'' terms not known to be Boolean.

  Sped up processing of large mutual-recursion nests.  In one large
  example the speedup was roughly two orders of magnitude.

  Modified event printing so that if both [47m'prove[0m and [47m'event[0m are
  inhibited, then events are no longer printed on behalf of
  [47m[certify-book][0m, [47m[encapsulate][0m, or [47m[defstobj][0m.  Thanks to Eric Smith
  for prompting consideration of such a change.

  The following technical change was made to support [47mwith-error-trace[0m
  and [47mwet[0m (see [note-2-7-new-functionality]), but may be of interest
  to those who do low-level programming using the ACL2 logical
  [47m[world][0m.  The [47m'unnormalized-body[0m property is now stored not only
  for functions defined in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode, but also for functions
  defined by the user in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.  ([47m:Program[0m mode Functions
  built into ACL2 still have their [47m'unnormalized-body[0m property
  omitted, in order to save space.)

  The handling of ``invisible'' functions for purposes of controlling
  rewriting (see [loop-stopper]) has been moved to a new table; see
  [invisible-fns-table].  Macros that access and modify this table
  are called ``[47m...-invisible-fns-table[0m'' in place of their former
  names, ``[47m...-invisible-fns-alist[0m.'' This feature was formerly
  implemented in the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m, which prevented a book
  from exporting lists of invisible functions intended to work with
  the [rewrite] rules developed in the book.  Thanks to Eric Smith
  and Rob Sumners for suggesting this change.  See
  [set-invisible-fns-table] (formerly [47mset-invisible-fns-alist[0m), and
  also see [add-invisible-fns] and see [remove-invisible-fns], which
  provides ways to incrementally add to and remove from this table,
  respectively.  The handling of printing binary function call nests
  using macros (See [add-binop]) has also been moved out of the
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m as suggested by Eric and Rob, but this
  feature didn't work anyhow (see [note-2-7-bug-fixes]).
  Incidentally, the symbols [47mbinop-table[0m, [47m[add-binop][0m, and
  [47m[remove-binop][0m have all been added to the list [47m*acl2-exports*[0m (see
  [ACL2-user]), [47m[add-invisible-fns][0m and [47m[remove-invisible-fns][0m have
  been added to that list, and [47mset-invisible-fns-alist[0m has been
  replaced in that list by [47m[set-invisible-fns-table][0m.  Function
  [47minvisible-fns-alistp[0m is no longer defined and has been removed from
  [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.

  We now enforce the stated restriction on the pairings in
  [47mmacro-aliases-table[0m (see [macro-aliases-table]), namely, that it
  associates names of macros with names of functions (with respect to
  the current ACL2 logical [world]).  We make a similar requirement
  on [47m[invisible-fns-table][0m.

  The [47m[theory-invariant][0m event has been modified so that the default
  action is an error rather than a warning.  Thanks to Eric Smith for
  suggesting this change.  Also, the value returned upon successful
  execution of a [47m[theory-invariant][0m event is now the key.

  Proof output that reports destructor elimination no longer uses the
  word ``generalizing''.  This small change may help in browsing
  proof output, since now ``generaliz'' takes you to true uses of
  generalization.  Thanks to Matyas Sustik for suggesting such a
  change.

  The command [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m now prints an abbreviated controller-alist for
  [47m;[0m[47m[definition][0m rules.  Formerly the output from [47m:pl[0m could be
  overwhelming when the supplied function was part of a large
  [47m[mutual-recursion][0m nest.

  The defaults for keyword parameters of [47m[certify-book][0m have changed.
  See [note-2-7-bug-fixes], in particular, the discussion there of
  two modifications to [47mcertify-book[0m.

  Technical changes have been made to [47m[compress1][0m and [47m[compress2][0m that
  should usually be invisible to users.  The next paragraph describes
  them in detail, only for completeness (i.e., that description can
  be ignored by most users).  But first, here is an example showing
  an effect on users.  The slow array warning was not there
  previously.  Notice that the warning only arises if the event form
  is changed.  The solution is to be sure that redundant [47m[defconst][0m
  forms are syntactically identical.

    ACL2 !>(defconst *a* (compress1 'demo
                                    '((:header :dimensions (5)
                                               :maximum-length 15
                                               :default uninitialized
                                               :name demo)
                                      (1 . one)
                                      (0 . zero))))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFCONST *A* ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     *A*
    ACL2 !>(aref1 'demo *a* 0)
    ZERO
    ACL2 !>(defconst *a* (compress1 'demo
                                    '((:header :dimensions (5)
                                               :maximum-length 15
                                               :default uninitialized
                                               :name demo)
                                      (1 . one)
                                      (0 . zero))))

    This event is redundant.  See :DOC redundant-events.

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFCONST *A* ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     :REDUNDANT
    ACL2 !>(aref1 'demo *a* 0)
    ZERO
    ACL2 !>(defconst *a* (compress1 'demo
                                    '((:header :dimensions (5)
                                               :maximum-length 15
                                               :default uninitialized
                                               :name demo)
                                      (0 . zero)
                                      (1 . one))))

    This event is redundant.  See :DOC redundant-events.

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFCONST *A* ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     :REDUNDANT
    ACL2 !>(aref1 'demo *a* 0)

    **********************************************************
    Slow Array Access!  A call of AREF1 on an array named
    DEMO is being executed slowly.  See :DOC slow-array-warning
    **********************************************************

    ZERO
    ACL2 !>

  As before, the von Neumann structure stored in the [47m'acl2-array[0m
  property of the array name contains the array list object in its
  [47m[car][0m.  However, previously it was the case that [47mcompress1[0m and
  [47mcompress2[0m did not update that [47mcar[0m when its new value would be equal
  to its old value.  This was done largely in support of some
  type-set tables defined using [47m[defconst][0m in [47mtype-set-b.lisp[0m.  The
  new versions of [47m[compress1][0m and [47m[compress2][0m are simpler in that no
  such exception is made in the case of equal lists, although instead
  the entire compression process is short-circuited when the input
  array list object is [47m[eq][0m to the [47mcar[0m of the [47m'acl2-array[0m property.
  This change was made because the equality test was causing a ``slow
  array access'' warning to be printed in rare cases during proofs,
  as described elsewhere (see [note-2-7-bug-fixes]).

  We no longer distribute documentation specific to Lucid Emacs.  The
  Info documentation in directory [47mdoc/EMACS/[0m works well both for Gnu
  Emacs and XEmacs.

  A little-advertised macro, [47mvalue[0m, has long been allowed for top-level
  forms in [books]; see [embedded-event-form].  This has been
  replaced by a new macro, [47mvalue-triple[0m.  The two have the same
  semantics at the top-level of books, where [47m[state][0m is ``live''.
  However, [47mvalue-triple[0m should be used at the top-level of a book,
  while [47mvalue[0m should be used in function definitions (as before).
  This change eliminates a warning put out by the Allegro Common Lisp
  compiler for top-level [47mvalue[0m forms in [books].")
 (NOTE-2-7-PROOF-BUILDER
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on proof-builder Changes

  Output from the [proof-builder] can now be inhibited by supplying the
  symbol [47mproof-builder[0m in the list given to [set-inhibit-output-lst].")
 (NOTE-2-7-PROOFS
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Changes in Proof Engine

  An improvement in the linear arithmetic heuristics has been provided
  by Robert Krug.  For information about this change, search for the
  comment in [47madd-linear-lemma[0m (file [47mrewrite.lisp[0m) that begins as
  follows.

    ; Previous to Version_2.7, we just went ahead and used the result of

  Thanks, Robert!  Also thanks to Eric Smith for providing a motivating
  example.

  The non-linear-arithmetic addition (see [non-linear-arithmetic]) led
  to several small changes in the linear-arithmetic decision
  procedure (see [linear-arithmetic]).  Two of these changes could
  affect existing proofs.

      First, when we are setting up the initial arithmetic database (which
      we call the ``pot-lst''), we have always scanned it to see if
      there were any pairs of inequalities from which we could derive
      a previously unknown equality.  In some cases we added this
      equality to the clause and in others we used it to rewrite the
      clause, substituting one side of the equality for the other
      throughout the clause.  Previously, the heuristics that we used
      to determine whether we performed the substitution differed
      from those used in several other places in the code.  This has
      now been regularized, and similar heuristics are now used
      throughout the code.

      The second change to the linear-arithmetic decision procedure is that
      we now explicitly add inequalities derived from type reasoning
      to the pot-lst.  Previously, we performed cancellations against
      these inequalities without adding them to the pot-lst.  This
      change results in there being more inequalities in the pot-lst
      than before, and so more chances for there to be a pair of
      inequalities from which an equality can be derived.  In effect,
      certain simple consequences of the current goal (see
      [type-set]) may now be added as hypotheses of the goal or used
      to perform equality substitutions.

  A slight improvement has been made to the way certain rewrite rules
  are stored.  It was already the case that a rewrite rule rule whose
  conclusion [47mC[0m is not a call of a known equivalence relation (or
  [47m[eq][0m, [47m[eql][0m, or [47m[=][0m) is stored as [47m(iff C t)[0m, except that if ACL2
  can determine (using its [47m[type-set][0m mechanism) that [47mC[0m is Boolean,
  then the rule is stored as [47m(equal C t)[0m.  The improvement is that if
  [47mC[0m and [47mC'[0m are Boolean, then a rule stated as [47m(iff C C')[0m is stored as
  [47m(equal C C')[0m.  Thanks to Pete Manolios for providing an example
  that led us to consider this improvement.

  The heuristic use of equalities (fertilization) has been modified.
  Previously, ACL2 would sometimes substitute using an equality but
  keep the equality, and then undo the substitution by using the
  equality again.  Now, when ACL2 keeps an equality after using it,
  it puts the equality inside a call of [47m[hide][0m.  Descendants of that
  goal that are unchanged by simplification will have this call of
  [47m[hide][0m removed so that the equality can once again contribute to
  the proof.  This change can cause some proofs to succeed that
  otherwise would fail.  In the unlikely event that a proof fails
  that formerly succeeded, the following hint on \"Goal\" may fix the
  problem (see [hints]):

    :expand ((:free (x) (hide x)))

  We have refined the heuristics employed when an [47m[if][0m form is assumed
  true or false.  Our previous attempt (see [note-2-6-proofs] for the
  original announcement) was not as general as we had believed.  We
  have also improved some low-level code responsible for rewriting [47mIF[0m
  expressions.  In earlier versions of ACL2, it was possible to have
  the truth or falsity of an [47mIF[0m expression explicitly recorded in the
  type-alist, and yet not use this information during rewriting.
  This problem has been corrected.  Thanks to Robert Krug for
  noticing this problem and implementing the fix.

  We have sped up the rewriter in some cases where there are large
  collections of mutually-recursive functions (see
  [mutual-recursion]).  (Implementation notes: technically, we have
  modified the way function [47mbeing-openedp[0m operates on the [47mfnstack[0m,
  and we have modified [47m*current-acl2-world-key-ordering*[0m as described
  in the essay above its definition.)

  [Forward-chaining] is now done in the preprocessing phase of proof
  attempts (see the discussion of [47m:DO-NOT[0m --- see [hints]).  This is
  part of a technical change, made in support of translation of type
  declarations to [guard]s (see [note-2-7-guards]).  Previously,
  whenever ACL2 checked for [built-in-clause]s, it then looked for a
  contradiction using [47m[type-set][0m reasoning if it did not find a
  suitable built-in clause.  The change is to perform
  forward-chaining in such cases (i.e., when a built-in clause is not
  found).

  A couple of changes have been made in the generation of goals for
  [forcing-round]s.  Thanks to Eric Smith for bringing issues to our
  attention that led to these changes.  For one, [guard]s are no
  longer relevant in such goal generation.  Formerly, the addition of
  a guard could make a proof fail that otherwise succeeded.
  Secondly, contextual information is now always kept when it
  involves a constrained constant, i.e., a zero-ary function
  introduced in the signature of an [47m[encapsulate][0m.")
 (NOTE-2-7-RULES
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on Changes in Rules and Constants

  The [47m[defcong][0m macro has been slightly changed.  The difference is
  that the variable generated with suffix [47m-EQUIV[0m will now be in the
  same package as the name of the variable from which it is
  generated, rather than always belonging to the ACL2 package.
  Thanks to Hanbing Liu for suggesting this change.  (Note that a
  couple of books have been modified to accommodate this change,
  e.g., [47mbooks/finite-set-theory/set-theory[0m.)

  In Version_2.6, a change was made for rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m whose
  conclusion is a term of the form [47m(EQV lhs rhs)[0m, where [47mEQV[0m is [47m[=][0m,
  [47m[eq][0m, or [47m[eql][0m: the rule was stored as though [47mEQV[0m were [47m[equal][0m.
  (See [note-2-6-rules].)  This change has been extended to rules of
  class [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m.")
 (NOTE-2-7-SYSTEM
  (NOTE-2-7)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7 Notes on System-level Changes

  ACL2 now runs (once again) under LispWorks, specifically, LispWorks
  4.2.0.  However, we needed a patch, which presumably will be
  unnecessary after 4.2.7.  From LispWorks support:

      Users with LispWorks4.2.7 should ask us at lisp-support@xanalys.com
      for the transform-if-node patch. It will be helpful if they
      quote (Lisp Support Call #11372) when doing so. Also, they must
      send a bug form generated from their LispWorks image:
      instructions at
      http://www.lispworks.com/support/bug-report.html.

  File [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m has been improved so that failed attempts
  to certify a book will cause the `make' to fail.  Previously, an
  existing [47m.cert[0m file was left in place, and that sufficed for the
  `make' to be considered a success.  Now, the old [47m.cert[0m file is
  first removed when recertification is found to be necessary.

  A change has been made to source file [47macl2.lisp[0m to accommodate GCL
  2.4.3.  (ACL2 Version 2.6 does not work with some versions of GCL
  2.4.3.)

  The error message has been improved when certain forms are typed to
  raw Lisp and the ACL2 loop has never been entered (with [47m([0m[47m[lp][0m[47m)[0m).

  The following symbols in the ACL2 package have been made untouchable,
  meaning that they are not available to the user: [47mev-fncall[0m, [47mev[0m,
  [47mev-lst[0m, [47mev-acl2-unwind-protect[0m, [47mev-fncall![0m, and
  [47muser-stobj-alist-safe[0m.  The reason is that these functions can not
  be called safely except under certain restrictions.  If you want to
  call the ACL2 evaluator, consider using the built-in system
  functions [47mtrans-eval[0m or simple-translate-and-eval.

  CLISP Version_2.30 implements a notion of ``locking'' the \"LISP\"
  package that is incompatible with building ACL2.  (CLISP
  Version_2.27 does not appear to have had this feature.)  We have
  gotten around this problem by unlocking the \"LISP\" package in ACL2
  images built on such CLISPs.

  Automatic proclaiming for GCL, which has (for a long time) been done
  for functions in compiled books, has been improved.  Formerly, the
  only time a non-trivial output type (i.e., other than [47mt[0m) was
  inferred was when macroexpansion produced an explicit call of
  [47m[the][0m.  Now, [47m[if][0m expressions can also generate non-[47mt[0m output types.
  Consider the following example.

    (defmacro the-fixnum (n)
      (list 'the '(signed-byte 29) n))
    (defmacro 1+f (x)
      (list 'the-fixnum
            (list '1+ (list 'the-fixnum x))))
    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (type (unsigned-byte 27) x))
      (if (zp x)
          0
        (1+f (foo (1-f x)))))

  Formerly, the [47mproclaim[0m forms for [47mfoo[0m, before and after this
  improvement, are as shown below.

    (PROCLAIM '(FTYPE (FUNCTION ((UNSIGNED-BYTE 27)) T) FOO))                ;old
    (PROCLAIM '(FTYPE (FUNCTION ((UNSIGNED-BYTE 27)) (SIGNED-BYTE 29)) FOO)) ;new

  Compiler info messages sent to error stream were eliminated for
  CMUCL.")
 (NOTE-2-7{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.7(r) (November, 2002) Notes

  In source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m, in order for proofs to succeed, ([47mmake
  proofs[0m), the definitions of [47m[ACL2-count][0m and [47mexplode-atom[0m have been
  modified slightly, and lemma [47mstandard-numberp-one[0m [modified after
  Version_3.4 to become [47mstandardp-one[0m] has been given [47m:rule-classes
  nil[0m.

  All [47m[skip-proofs][0m forms have been eliminated from the nonstd books,
  thanks to Ruben Gamboa.

  The directory [47mbooks/sqrt/[0m, which was intended for ACL2(r), has been
  moved to [47mbooks/nonstd/sqrt/[0m and added as appropriate to
  [47mbooks/nonstd/Makefile[0m.

  Please see [note-2-7] for changes to Version_2.7 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-2-8
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 (March, 2004) Notes

  BRIEF SUMMARY.

  The Version_2.8 notes are divided into the indicated subtopics.  Here
  we give only a brief summary of just a few of the major new
  features and changes that seem most likely to impact existing
  proofs.  Not included in this brief summary, but included in the
  subtopics, are descriptions of many improvements (including bug
  fixes and new functionality) that should not get in the way of
  existing proof efforts.  In the description below we also omit
  discussion of changes that will become clear by way of error
  messages if they affect you.

  In particular, please see [note-2-8-new-functionality] for discussion
  of a number of new features that you may find useful.

  Acknowledgments and elaboration, as well as other changes, can be
  found in the subtopics listed below.

  Some of the bug fixes (see [note-2-8-bug-fixes])

    * Some soundness bugs were fixed.

    * The handling of free variables in hypotheses (see [free-variables])
      of rewrite and linear rules had a bug that prevented some
      proofs from going through.  Now that this bug has been fixed,
      you may find some proofs running much more slowly than before.
      You can use [47m[accumulated-persistence][0m and
      [47m[add-match-free-override][0m to remedy this situation; see
      [note-2-8-bug-fixes] for details.

    * The [default-hints] in the current logical [world] are no longer
      ignored by [47m[verify-guards][0m.

    * Forms violating guard-checking such as [47m(defconst *silly* (car 3))[0m are
      now allowed in [books].

  Some of the new functionality (see [note-2-8-new-functionality])

    * WARNING: You may find that [47mcontrol-d[0m (in emacs, [47mcontrol-c control-d[0m)
      can throw you completely out of Lisp where it had not formerly
      done so.

    * ACL2 now starts up inside the ACL2 loop --- that is, [47m([0m[47m[lp][0m[47m)[0m is
      executed automatically --- when built on CLISP or Allegro CL.
      This was already the case for GCL and CMUCL, and it still is
      not true for LispWorks.

    * See [note-2-8-ordinals] for a discussion of a significant change in
      ordinal representation, and in particular, for how to preserve
      existing proofs that depend on the previous ordinal
      representation.

    * Macros [47m[mbe][0m (``must be equal''), [47m[mbt][0m (``must be true''), and
      [47m[defexec][0m have been introduced, which allow the user to attach
      alternate executable definitions to functions.

    * The user can now control multiple matching for free variables in
      hypotheses for [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m rules, as has already been
      supported for [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules.

    * It is no longer necessary to specify [47m(set-match-free-error nil)[0m in
      order to avoid errors when a rule with free variables in its
      hypotheses is missing the [47m:match-free[0m field.

    * The form [47m(break-on-error)[0m causes, at least for most Lisps, entry into
      the Lisp debugger whenever ACL2 causes an error.

    * A new [47m[table][0m has been provided so that advanced users can override
      the built-in [47muntranslate[0m functionality.  See
      [user-defined-functions-table].

    * The [47m[pstack][0m (`process [prover] stack'') mechanism, formerly denoted
      [47mcheckpoints[0m, has been improved.  One of these improvements is
      to show actual parameters with [47m(pstack t)[0m rather than formals.

    * The [47m[defstobj][0m event is now allowed to take an [47m:inline[0m argument,
      which can speed up execution.

    * Macro [47m[cw-gstack][0m no longer takes arguments for the [47mgstack[0m or
      [47m[state][0m.  To print terms in full rather than abbreviated:
      [47m(cw-gstack :evisc-tuple nil)[0m.

    * The [47m[include-book][0m event now has an additional (optional) keyword,
      [47m:dir[0m.  In particular, [47m(include-book \"foo/bar\" :dir :system)[0m
      will include the indicated book after prepending the path of
      the built-in [47mbooks/[0m directory.  You will probably not find [47m:dir
      :system[0m to be useful if you move the executable image or
      distributed books; see [include-book], in particular its
      ``soundness warning''.

    * The printing of results in raw mode (see [set-raw-mode]) may now be
      partially controlled by the user: see [47madd-raw-arity[0m.  [Note
      added 2021: this utility has been removed and is no longer
      necessary.]

    * For those using Unix/Linux `make': A [47mcert.acl2[0m file can contain forms
      to be evaluated before an appropriate [47m[certify-book][0m command is
      invoked automatically (not included in [47mcert.acl2[0m).

  Some of the changes in the proof engine (see [note-2-8-proofs])

    * ACL2 now prevents certain rewriting loops; see [rewrite-stack-limit].

    * Small changes have been made to heuristics for controlling rewriting
      during proofs by induction and in handling certain ``weak''
      [compound-recognizer] rules.

    * The handling of free variables in a hypothesis of a [rewrite] rule
      (see [free-variables]) has been improved in the case that the
      hypothesis is of the form [47m(equiv x y)[0m, where [47mequiv[0m is a known
      equivalence relation (see [equivalence]).

    * We have modified how the ACL2 simplifier handles the application of a
      defined function symbol to constant arguments, by avoiding the
      introduction of [hide] when evaluation fails if the term can be
      rewritten.

    * The generation of \"Goal\" for recursive (and mutually-recursive)
      definitions now uses the subsumption/replacement limitation
      (default 500).  See [case-split-limitations].

    * Default hints now apply to hints given in definitions, not just
      theorems.  See [default-hints].

    * Linear arithmetic now uses the conclusions of [47m[forward-chaining][0m
      rules, and [47m[type-set][0m now uses a small amount of linear
      reasoning when deciding inequalities.

  Some of the changes in rules, definitions, and constants (see
  [note-2-8-rules])

    * See the above doc topic.

  Guard-related changes are described in see [note-2-8-bug-fixes]

  Some of the proof-builder changes (see [note-2-8-proof-builder])

    * Added new [proof-builder] commands [47mwrap1[0m, [47mwrap[0m, and [47mwrap-induct[0m, to
      combine multiple conjuncts or goals.

    * The [47mtype-alist[0m command now takes optional arguments that control
      whether or not the governors and/or conclusion are used in
      computing the context.

  Some of the system-level changes (see [note-2-8-system])

    * ACL2 now runs on OpenMCL and on MCL 5.0.

  Some of the other changes (see [note-2-8-other])

    * Emacs file [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m has been updated (see [note-2-8-other]
      for details).

    * When [47m:pl[0m is given a term other than a symbol, it will print all
      rewrite rules that match that term.

    * A new function, [47m[pkg-witness][0m, returns a symbol in the given package.

    * The list constant [47m*acl2-exports*[0m has been extended.

    * A new release of the rtl library has been included: [47mbooks/rtl/rel4/[0m.
      See the [47mREADME[0m file in that directory.

  Again, please proceed to the subtopics for more thorough release
  notes.


