  [Note-2-8-system]
      ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on System-level Changes")
 (NOTE-2-8-BUG-FIXES
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on Bug Fixes

  We have fixed a soundness bug in the tautology checker's handling of
  expressions of the form [47m(not (not x))[0m.  This bug has gone back at
  least as far as Version_2.4.  All of the regression tests passed
  after the fix, without modification.  So we hope that this bug has
  rarely bitten anyone.  Thanks to Qiang Zhang for sending us a proof
  of [47mnil[0m that led us to this fix: [47m(thm (equal (and p q) (not (or (not
  p) (not q)))))[0m.  And thanks to Matyas Sustik for an observation
  that led to an improvement of our initial fix.

  The preceding version (2.7) introduced a soundness bug in handling of
  ACL2 [arrays], in which functions [47m[compress1][0m and [47m[compress2][0m were
  returning the input alist rather than compressing it appropriately.
  Here is a proof of [47mnil[0m that no longer succeeds, based on a bug
  report from Warren Hunt, whom we thank for bringing this problem to
  our attention.

    (defthm bad
      (not (let* ((ar2 (aset1 'my-array ar1 3 10))
                  (ar3 (compress1 'my-array ar2))
                  (ar4 (reverse (reverse ar2)))
                  (ar5 (compress1 'my-array ar4)))
             (and (equal ar2 ar4)
                  (not (equal ar3 ar5)))))
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm contradiction
      nil
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use
               ((:instance bad
                           (ar1 (compress1 'my-array
                                           '((3 . 5)
                                             (:HEADER :DIMENSIONS (5)
                                                      :MAXIMUM-LENGTH 6
                                                      :DEFAULT 0
                                                      :NAME MY-ARRAY)))))))))

  On a related note, a new function [47m[flush-compress][0m can be used for
  subtle control of under-the-hood raw Lisp support for fast array
  access, although we expect it to be very rare that users need this
  extra support.

  Previous versions have had two soundness bugs that can occur when
  using the [proof-builder]:

    * The first bug pertains to the [47mexpand[0m command, and hence [47mx[0m and [47mx-dumb[0m
      commands (which call [47mexpand[0m); see [proof-builder-commands].
      The bug can occur when applying the above commands when the
      current term is a call of a constrained function symbol for
      which there is a [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rule.  Now, the [47mexpand[0m command
      will succeed only when the function symbol of the current term
      is a defined function symbol, in which case the original
      definition is always used, in analogy to how the [47m:expand[0m hint
      works in the prover; see [hints].  Thanks to John Erickson for
      sending an example that led us to wonder if there might be a
      soundness problem.

    * The second bug pertains to the [47ms[0m command (and commands that call it,
      e.g., [47ms-prop[0m).  The proof-builder forms a context out of the
      top-level hypotheses and the [47mif[0m-terms governing the current
      term.  If there is a contradiction in the top-level hypotheses,
      the proof-builder can appropriately consider the goal to be
      proved, and it does so.  But formerly, the criterion was
      weaker: the contradiction could involve the combination of the
      top-level hypotheses and [47mif[0m-term governors.  Thanks to Rob
      Sumners for noticing this bug.

  A soundness bug could be provoked in some Lisps by applying [47m[defpkg][0m
  to the empty string.  This has been disallowed.

  We fixed a soundness bug related to packages caused by a failure to
  track axioms introduced [47m[local][0mly on behalf of [47m[defpkg][0m events.
  See [hidden-death-package].

  We fixed a soundness bug caused by a failure to check that a
  [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule can be processed when proofs are skipped
  or under a [47m[defequiv][0m event.  The former case can occur when
  processing an [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[include-book][0m event, where the rule
  could depend on a [47m[local][0m [47m:[0m[47m[compound-recognizer][0m rule preceding the
  proposed [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule under the same [47m[encapsulate][0m or
  [47m[include-book][0m event.  See [local-incompatibility] for such an
  example.

  We fixed a potential soundness bug relating to reclassifying a
  [47m:program[0m mode function to [47m:logic[0m mode (as done by
  [47m[verify-termination][0m or the submission of an appropriate
  ``redundant'' definition) without adequate checking that [47m[stobj][0m
  usage was identical.  Allegedly redundant definitions must now
  preserve the [47mstobjs[0m declaration as well as the formals, body, guard
  and type declarations.  We thank Vernon Austel for pointing out
  this problem.

  It was possible to get a raw Lisp error by introducing a [47m[local][0mly
  defined function with [guard] verification inhibited and then
  subsequently introducing the same definition non-locally without
  that inhibition.  The following example will clarify.

    (encapsulate nil
      (local
        (defun foo (x) (declare (xargs :guard t :verify-guards nil)) (car x)))
      (defun foo (x) (declare (xargs :guard t)) (car x)))
    ; The following causes a raw lisp error because ACL2 runs the Common Lisp
    ; definition of foo, because it thinks that foo's guard of t was verified.
    (thm (equal (foo 3) xxx))

  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this problem to our attention.  We
  are particularly grateful to Jared because his example exploited
  this bug by applying it to a function defined using [47m[mbe][0m
  (introduced in this same version, 2.8), in order to prove [47mnil[0m!

  The sort of error message shown below can legitimately occur when
  certifying a book in a certification world where there was an
  [47m[include-book][0m command with a relative pathname (see [pathname]).
  However, it was occurring more often than necessary.  This has been
  fixed.

      ACL2 Error in (CERTIFY-BOOK \"foo\" ...): The certification world has
      include-book commands for book \"bar\" that correspond to
      different full pathnames, namely \"/u/dir1/bar\" and
      \"/u/dir2/bar\".  ACL2 cannot currently certify a book in such a
      world.  To work around this problem, use an absolute pathname
      for at least one of these books (see :DOC pathname).

  Bugs were fixed in [47m[with-output][0m, in particular related to the use of
  values [47m:all[0m.  Also, documentation for [47mwith-output[0m has been
  improved.  Thanks to Vernon Austel for pointing out the bugs.

  Fixed a lisp error occurring when [47mbash[0m proof-builder command was
  given illegal syntax, e.g., [47m(bash ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable
  binary-append))))[0m instead of [47m(bash (\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable
  binary-append)))[0m.

  We added an appropriate guard to [47m[find-rules-of-rune][0m, which will
  avoid hard lisp errors when this function is called on non-[rune]
  arguments.  Thanks to Eric Smith for pointing out this issue.

  It was possible for a redundant [47m[include-book][0m form (see
  [redundant-events]) to leave a [command] in the ACL2 logical
  [world] and to cause (re-)loading of a compiled file.  These
  behaviors have been fixed.  In particular, if [47mbook1[0m has already
  been included in the current ACL2 [world] and [47m(include-book
  \"book1\")[0m occurs in [47mbook2[0m, then the compiled file for [47mbook1[0m will not
  be loaded again when [47mbook2[0m is included.  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  bringing our attention to these problems, and to Eric Smith for
  bringing up a special case earlier (where \"//\" occurred in the
  book-name).

  The summary printed at the end of a proof had not listed [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m
  rules used in a proof.  This has been corrected.

  The use of proof trees in emacs redefined `[47mcontrol-c control-c[0m' in
  such a way that in telnet mode, the telnet session was interrupted
  and perhaps could not be continued.  This has been fixed.

  Source function [47mload-theory-into-enabled-structure[0m contained a
  guard-violating call of [47m[compress1][0m.  Thanks to Vernon Austel for
  bringing this problem to our attention; even though this bug was
  benign (as he pointed out), we like keeping the source code free of
  guard violations.

  A number of proof-builder atomic macros caused a hard error when all
  goals have already been proved.  This has been fixed.  Thanks to
  John Erickson for sending an example of the issue.

  A bug has been fixed in [47m[add-match-free-override][0m.  Formerly, a
  [47m[table][0m [guard] violation occurred when calling
  [47m[add-match-free-override][0m more than once with first argument other
  than [47m:clear[0m.

  Definitions of functions involving large constants could cause stack
  overflows.  This has been fixed, at least in some of the most
  egregious cases (by making a source function [47mfn-count-evg[0m
  tail-recursive).  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this problem
  to our attention.

  Evaluation of computed hints could cause stack overflows.  This has
  been fixed.  Thanks to Eric Smith for bringing this problem to our
  attention.

  Evaluation of [47m:[0m[47m[monitor][0m on [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m [rune]s is now fast even if
  the specified function is part of a very large [47m[mutual-recursion][0m
  nest.  Thanks to Eric Smith for sending an example showing that
  this wasn't always the case.

  Fixed a bug in [47mbooks/bdd/cbf.lisp[0m that was causing certification of
  distributed bdd books to fail when the connected book directory
  (see [cbd]) differs from the current working directory.  Thanks to
  Scott Guthery for bringing this bug to our attention and supplying
  a helpful log.

  Duplicate rule names have been eliminated from warnings generated
  upon the use of enabled [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rules.  Thanks
  to Eric Smith for pointing out this problem.

  The trace utilities (see [trace]), as modified for GCL and Allegro
  Common Lisp, had failed to show more than the first return value
  for so-called ``[47m*1*[0m'' functions (essentially,
  [executable-counterpart] functions) when they were returning
  multiple values (via [mv]).  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Erik
  Reeber for pointing out this problem.  Also, it is now possible to
  refer to [47marglist[0m in [trace$] forms when ACL2 is built on GCL, not
  just when ACL2 is built on Allegro Common Lisp.

  Uses of [47m[hide][0m introduced during proofs by failed attempts to
  evaluate constrained functions (see [hide]) are now tracked, so
  that the [rune] [47m(:DEFINITION HIDE)[0m will show up in the summary.

  The following bug, introduced back in Version 2.7, has been fixed.
  The bug applied only to GCL and may well not have affected anyone.
  But the function proclamation computed by ACL2 for compilation
  usually had an output type of [47mnil[0m where it should have been [47mt[0m.

  The macro [47m[gc$][0m had a bug exhibited when it was supplied one or more
  arguments.  This has been fixed.

  The macro [47m[defabbrev][0m broke when supplied a string and no
  documentation, e.g., [47m(defabbrev foo () \"\")[0m.  Thanks to Rob Sumners
  for noticing this problem and providing a fix, which we have
  incorporated.

  For ACL2 executables built on Allegro Common Lisp, a Lisp error
  occurred when [47m[trace$][0m was called on other than a defined function
  symbol.  Now ACL2 prints a more useful error message.

  The proof-builder no longer accepts a [47m([0m[47m[verify][0m[47m)[0m command when some
  function symbol in the original goal no longer exists in the
  current ACL2 logical [world].  Thanks to John Erickson for bringing
  this issue to our attention.

  The function [47mld-redefinition-action[0m may now be called by the user.
  Thanks to Vernon Austel for suggesting that we remove this symbol
  from the list of so-called untouchables.

  The handling of free variables in hypotheses (see [free-variables])
  of rewrite and linear rules had a bug that prevented some proofs
  from going through.  Here is a simple example, essentially provided
  by Diana Moisuc, who we thank for bringing this issue to our
  attention.  The proof of the [47m[thm][0m below had failed, but now will
  succeed.  This particular bug prevented, for example, the [47m:all[0m
  behavior from occurring when the first hypothesis of the rule does
  not have free variables.  NOTE: Now that this bug has been fixed,
  you may find some proofs running much more slowly than before.  You
  can use [47m[accumulated-persistence][0m to locate rules that are slowing
  down your proofs because of excessive attention to free variables,
  and then execute [47m[add-match-free-override][0m for those rules (or,
  just change the rules themselves to specify [47m:once[0m in the
  [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m).

    (defstub foo1 (* ) => *)
    (skip-proofs
     (defthm aux-foo1
       (implies (and (integerp a)
                     (integerp i)
                     (equal (foo1 0)  (list 0 i)))
                (equal (foo1 a) (list 0 (+ a i))))
       :rule-classes ((:rewrite :match-free :all))))
    (thm
     (implies (and (integerp i)
                   (integerp a)
                   (equal (foo1 0) (list 0 i)))
              (equal (foo1 a) (list 0 (+ a i)))))

  Formerly, creation of large arrays could cause an error in the
  underlying Common Lisp implementation without helpful messages for
  the user.  Now, we check Common Lisp restrictions on arrays and
  print a helpful error message if they are violated, namely: each
  dimension must be less than the value of Common Lisp constant
  [47marray-dimension-limit[0m, and the product of the dimensions must be
  less than the value of Common Lisp constant [47marray-total-size-limit[0m.
  Thanks to Warren Hunt for bringing this issue to our attention.
  Note: this change also removes a former restriction of [47m[stobj][0m
  array fields to size smaller than 2^28-1, provided the underlying
  Lisp can support larger arrays.

  The [default-hints] in the current logical [world] were ignored by
  [47m[verify-guards][0m.  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Jared Davis for
  pointing out this bug and sending a helpful example.

  The [47m[brr][0m mechanism has been cleaned up in order to avoid hard errors
  and infinite loops that can arrive when typing interrupts
  ([47mcontrol-c[0m) or end-of-files ([47mcontrol-d[0m) inside the [47m[brr][0m loop.
  Thanks to Dave Greve, Olga Matlin, Eric Smith, and Serita Van
  Groningen for bringing this issue to our attention.  As a
  byproduct, if you type [47mcontrol-d[0m (or if inside emacs, [47mcontrol-c
  control-d[0m), you may now quit entirely out of ACL2 and lisp (see
  [good-bye]) in some cases where you formerly would not have, for
  example when sitting at the ACL2 prompt (which formerly, in Allegro
  Common Lisp for example, would merely take you into raw Lisp rather
  than quitting everything).

  We have eliminated structural flaws in the HTML documentation pages
  that could make them unreadable in some browsers.  Thanks to Bill
  Young for bringing this issue to our attention and to Joe Hendrix
  for diagnosing the problem.

  The [proof-builder] could run very slowly after many instructions in
  a given session.  This has been fixed; thanks to Art Flatau for
  bringing this problem to our attention.  (Implementation detail: We
  now keep tag-trees duplicate-free when we accumulate them into
  state.  This change could have minor speed advantages for some
  top-level proofs too, not just in the proof-builder.)

  The printing of accesses to stobjs using nth or update-nth has been
  done using symbolic constants since ACL2 Version_2.6.  However,
  there was a bug that prevented this feature from working for
  [47m[update-nth][0m except at a top-level call.  This has been fixed.
  Thanks to Julien Schmaltz for bringing this problem to our
  attention.  For example, consider these events:

    (defstobj st field0 field1)
    (thm (equal (nth 1 (update-nth 0 17 st)) (car (cons xxx yyy)))
         :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable nth update-nth))))

  Before the fix, the proof attempt of the above silly thm printed the
  following.

    (NTH 1 (UPDATE-NTH *FIELD0* 17 ST))

  After the fix, we instead see the following.

    (NTH *FIELD1* (UPDATE-NTH *FIELD0* 17 ST))

  It is now possible to certify and subsequently include [books] that
  require guard-checking to be off.  For example, the book can
  contain the form [47m(defconst *silly* (car 3))[0m even though [47m3[0m fails to
  satisfy the guard of [47m[car][0m.  Formerly, it was necessary to execute
  [47m:[0m[47m[set-guard-checking][0m[47m nil[0m before a [47m[certify-book][0m or [47m[include-book][0m
  in order for such a form to be handled without error.  Thanks to
  Hanbing Liu for bringing this problem to our attention.

  Fixed a [proof-builder] bug that could probably cause a strange
  error, ``Attempt to access the plist field''.  Thanks to Bill Young
  for bringing this problem to our attention.

  Fixed a [proof-builder] bug that was failing to record applications
  of rewrite rules using the proof-builder's [47m:rewrite[0m command,
  causing the proof summary to omit mention of that rule (for
  example, when using the proof-builder's [47m:exit[0m command to generate
  an [47m:instructions[0m hint).  Thanks to Bill Young for pointing out this
  bug.

  Modernized some of the proof-tree emacs and infix printing stuff,
  thanks to suggestions made by Camm Maguire.")
 (NOTE-2-8-GUARDS
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on Guard-related Changes

  All the guard-related changes may be found elsewhere; in particular,
  see [note-2-8-bug-fixes].")
 (NOTE-2-8-NEW-FUNCTIONALITY
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on New Functionality

  WARNING: You may find that [47mcontrol-d[0m (in emacs, [47mcontrol-c control-d[0m)
  can throw you completely out of Lisp where it had not formerly done
  so.

  (CLISP and Allegro CL only) ACL2 now starts up inside the ACL2 loop
  --- that is, [47m([0m[47m[lp][0m[47m)[0m is executed automatically --- when built on
  CLISP or Allegro CL.  This was already the case for GCL and CMUCL,
  and it still is not true for LispWorks.  Thanks to Joe Corneli for
  bringing the CLISP command-line option [47m\"-i\"[0m to our attention, which
  led to this CLISP change and inspired reconsideration of how to do
  this for Allegro CL.

  Pete Manolios and Daron Vroon have changed the representation of
  ordinals in ACL2, defined algorithms for ordinal arithmetic, and
  created a library of theorems to reason about ordinal arithmetic.
  We thank them for these nice contributions.  See
  [note-2-8-ordinals] for details, in particular, for how to preserve
  existing proofs that depend on the previous ordinal representation.

  Sometimes users create rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m that cause an
  infinite loop in the ACL2 rewriter.  This has lead to Lisp stack
  overflows and even segmentation faults.  Now, the depth of calls of
  functions in the ACL2 rewriter is limited, and under user control.
  See [rewrite-stack-limit].

  Macros [47m[mbe][0m (``must be equal'') and [47m[mbt][0m (``must be true'') have
  been introduced, which allow the user to attach fast executable
  definitions to (presumably slower) [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions.  Thanks
  to Vernon Austel for a key idea.  Also provided is a macro
  [47m[defexec][0m, which employs [47m[mbe][0m but enforces the requirement that
  the executable definition also terminates.  Thanks to Jose Luis
  Ruiz Reina for collaborating in the design and development of
  [47m[defexec][0m, and for useful comments from a number of others as well
  in the development of [47mmbe[0m including Joe Hendrix and Rob Sumners.

  Definitions have been added for functions [47m[rassoc-eq][0m and
  [47m[rassoc-equal][0m, which are like [47m[rassoc][0m but use different tests and
  have different guards.  (Compare [47m[assoc-eq][0m and [47m[assoc-equal][0m,
  which are in similar relation to [47m[assoc][0m.)

  The user can now control multiple matching for free variables in
  hypotheses for [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m rules, as has already been
  supported for [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules.  For
  [47m:forward-chaining[0m rules, ``free variables'' are those in the
  hypotheses not bound by a given trigger term.  As for [47m:rewrite[0m and
  [47m:linear[0m rules, free-variable matching may be limited to the first
  successful attempt by specifying [47m:match-free :once[0m with
  [47m:forward-chaining[0m in the [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m, and
  [47m[add-match-free-override][0m may be used to modify the behavior of an
  existing rule.  Thanks to Erik Reeber for most of the
  implementation of these new capabilities, as well as significant
  assistance with a corresponding new documentation topic (see
  [free-variables-examples-forward-chaining]).

  It is no longer necessary to specify [47m(set-match-free-error nil)[0m in
  order to avoid errors when a rule with free variables in its
  hypotheses is missing the [47m:match-free[0m field.  (This was already
  true during book certification, but now it is the case in
  interactive sessions as well.)

  The form [47m(break-on-error)[0m causes, at least for most Lisps, entry into
  the Lisp debugger whenever ACL2 causes an error.  See
  [break-on-error].  Thanks to John Erickson for providing
  encouragement to provide this feature.

  A new [47m[table][0m has been provided so that advanced users can override
  the built-in [47muntranslate[0m functionality.  See
  [user-defined-functions-table].

  The [47m[pstack][0m mechanism (formerly denoted [47mcheckpoints[0m) has been
  improved.  The ``process [prover] stack,'' or pstack, is
  automatically printed when proofs abort.  Evaluation of function
  calls on explicit arguments during proofs is now tracked.  Actual
  parameters are shown with [47m(pstack t)[0m rather than formals.  Thanks
  to Bill Legato for suggesting the first two of these improvements
  and, in general, encouraging changes that make ACL2 easier to use.

  The [47m[defstobj][0m event is now allowed to take an [47m:inline[0m argument,
  which can speed up execution.  Thanks to Rob Sumners for suggesting
  and implementing this new feature.

  Macro [47m[assert$][0m has been added in order to make it easy to write
  assertions in one's code.  Semantically, [47m(assert$ test form)[0m is the
  same as [47mform[0m, but it causes a hard error (using [47m[illegal][0m) if [47mtest[0m
  evaluates to [47mnil[0m.

  Macro [47m[cw-gstack][0m no longer takes arguments for the gstack or
  [47m[state][0m.  However, it now takes a keyword argument (which is
  optional), [47m:evisc-tuple[0m, that can be used to control how it prints
  terms.  In particular, [47mcw-gstack[0m abbreviates large terms by
  default, but [47m(cw-gstack :evisc-tuple nil)[0m causes terms to be
  printed in full.  Thanks to Robert Krug and Eric Smith for
  requesting this improvement.

  The advanced user now has more control over the evisceration of
  terms.  See [ld-evisc-tuple], in particular the new paragraph on
  ``The printing of error messages and warnings.''

  The [47m[include-book][0m event now has an additional (optional) keyword,
  [47m:dir[0m.  The value of [47m:dir[0m should be a keyword that is associated
  with an absolute directory pathname to be used in place of the
  current book directory (see [cbd]) for resolving the first argument
  of [47minclude-book[0m to an absolute pathname.  At start-up, the only
  such keyword is [47m:system[0m, so that for example [47m(include-book
  \"arithmetic/top\" :dir :system)[0m will include the book
  [47m\"arithmetic/top\"[0m under the [47m\"books/\"[0m directory of your ACL2
  installation.  But you can associate ``projects'' with keywords
  using [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m, e.g., [47m(add-include-book-dir
  :my-project \"/u/smith/project0/\")[0m.  See [add-include-book-dir] and
  also see [delete-include-book-dir] and see [include-book].  Note:
  You will probably not find [47m:dir :system[0m to be useful if the
  distributed books are not placed in the path of their original
  location, pointed to by [47m:dir :system[0m, which will often happen if
  the executable image is obtained from another site.  Also see
  [include-book], in particular its ``soundness warning''.

  The printing of results in raw mode (see [set-raw-mode]) may now be
  partially controlled by the user: see [47madd-raw-arity[0m.  [Note added
  2021: this utility has been removed and is no longer necessary.]
  Also, newlines are printed when necessary before the value is
  printed.

  For those using Unix/Linux `make': A [47mcert.acl2[0m file can contain forms
  to be evaluated before an appropriate [47m[certify-book][0m command is
  invoked automatically (not included in [47mcert.acl2[0m).

  Jared Davis has contributed a new set of books for ordered finite set
  theory to the standard distribution,
  [47mbooks/finite-set-theory/osets-0.81/[0m.  See the [47mREADME[0m file in that
  directory.  Thanks, Jared.

  Robert Krug has contributed two related changes (thanks, Robert!) in
  support of stronger arithmetic reasoning.  First, one can now
  enable and disable nonlinear arithmetic with a [47m:nonlinearp[0m hint,
  which will override the default provided by [47m[set-non-linearp][0m
  (initially, [47mnil[0m).  See [hints].  Second, [computed-hints] can now
  have access to the [47mHISTORY[0m, [47mPSPV[0m, and [47mCTX[0m variables of the
  waterfall, which (for example) allows the writing of a hint which
  will enable nonlinear arithmetic on precisely those goals that are
  [47mstable-under-simplificationp[0m.  See [computed-hints].

  Robert Krug has contributed a new set of arithmetic books to the
  standard distribution, [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/[0m.  See the [47mREADME[0m file in
  that directory.  Thanks, Robert.")
 (NOTE-2-8-ORDINALS
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on Changes to the Ordinals

  Please see [ordinals].")
 (NOTE-2-8-OTHER
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on Miscellaneous Changes

  Execution of [47m[table][0m events has been sped up in many cases by
  avoiding excessive consing.

  ACL2 now warns if [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m (or [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m) rules contain free
  variables on the right-hand side.  Thanks to Dave Greve for raising
  this issue.

  Emacs file [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m has been updated to better comprehend
  the notion of the ``ACL2 shell'', which is the buffer to which ACL2
  forms are written by commands defined in the above file.  Thus,
  command [47mcontrol-t e[0m has been modified always to write to the ACL2
  shell (which is [47m\"*shell*\"[0m by default), and the following new
  commands have been defined.

      o control-t c
      Set the ACL2 shell to the current buffer.  o control-t b
      Change to the ACL2 shell.

  The commands [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m may now be given a macro name that
  corresponds via the [47mmacro-aliases-table[0m to a function name, so that
  for example [47m:pl append[0m is treated the same as [47m:pl binary-append[0m.  A
  more interesting improvement, for [47m:pl[0m only, is that [47m:pl[0m may now
  take any term.  When [47m:pl[0m is given a term other than a symbol, it
  will print all rewrite rules that match that term.  Thanks to David
  Russinoff, Robert Krug, and Bill Legato for getting this going.

  A new function, [47m[pkg-witness][0m, returns a symbol in the given package.

  The installation instructions have been updated, for example to give
  more guidance on obtaining Lisp implementations and to mention the
  acl2-help mailing list.

  Jared Davis has suggested some symbols to be added to [47m*acl2-exports*[0m,
  and we have done so.  Thanks, Jared.

      o [47mMFC[0m (used in [47m[syntaxp][0m and [47m[extended-metafunctions][0m; thanks also to
      Robert Krug for this one) o [47mID[0m, [47mCLAUSE[0m, [47mWORLD[0m, and
      [47mSTABLE-UNDER-SIMPLIFICATIONP[0m (used in [47m[computed-hints][0m) o
      [47m[set-default-hints][0m

  The command [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m has been improved so that when the event is inside
  an included book, the path of included books (from the top-level
  book down to the one containing the event) is shown.  Thanks to
  Eric Smith (perhaps among others) for pointing out the utility of
  this improvement.

  A new release of the rtl library has been included: [47mbooks/rtl/rel4/[0m.
  See the [47mREADME[0m file in that directory.")
 (NOTE-2-8-PROOF-BUILDER
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on proof-builder Changes

  Added new [proof-builder] commands [47mwrap1[0m, [47mwrap[0m, and [47mwrap-induct[0m.
  [47mWrap[0m replaces multiple goals by their conjunction: [47m(wrap instr1
  instr2 ...)[0m employs [47mwrap1[0m so that the indicated instructions create
  only at most one new goal.  [47mWrap-induct[0m is a simple example of the
  use of [47mwrap[0m, so that induction creates only one goal (the
  conjunction of the base and induction steps).  [47mWrap1[0m can be used
  immediately after a prover call ([47mbash[0m, [47mprove[0m, [47mreduce[0m, [47mbdd[0m, or
  [47minduct[0m) to collapse the new goals into one.  See
  [proof-builder-commands].

  The [proof-builder] command [47m=[0m failed to work as expected when a
  governing [47mIF[0m-test of the current term is T.  This has been fixed
  (by fixing source function [47mconjuncts-of[0m).  Thanks to Yoann
  Padioleau for bringing this problem to our attention.

  The [47mtype-alist[0m command now takes optional arguments that control
  whether or not the governors and/or conclusion are used in
  computing the context that is printed (see
  [proof-builder-commands], specifically subtopic [47mtype-alist[0m).
  Thanks to Rob Sumners for suggesting this improvement.

  The macro [47m[toggle-pc-macro][0m has always taken an optional second
  argument of [47matomic-macro[0m or [47mmacro[0m.  However, this was not clearly
  documented, and those two symbols had to be in the [47mACL2[0m package.
  Both of these problems have been remedied.  Thanks to John Erickson
  for bringing the lack of documentation of the second argument to
  our attention.")
 (NOTE-2-8-PROOFS
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on Changes in Proof Engine

  ACL2 now prevents certain rewriting loops; see [rewrite-stack-limit].

  During the computation of [47m[constraint][0ms for functional instantiation,
  [47m(prog2$ term1 term2)[0m and [47m(the type term2)[0m are now treated as [47mterm2[0m.

  A change has been made in heuristics for controlling rewriting during
  proofs by induction.  Formerly, during induction proofs, ACL2
  suppressed rewriting of certain ``induction hypothesis'' terms, and
  forced expansion of certain ``induction conclusion'' terms, until
  rewriting had stabilized.  This meddling with the rewriter is still
  turned off when rewriting has stabilized, but it is now turned off
  earlier once an ancestor has been through the rewriter and the
  current goal is free of ``induction conclusion'' terms.  Thanks to
  Dave Greve and Matt Wilding for providing an example and associated
  analysis that led us to look for a heuristic modification.

  A change has been made in the heuristics for handling certain
  ``weak'' [compound-recognizer] rules when building contexts.  Those
  who want to dig deeply into this change are welcome to look at the
  code following the call of [47mmost-recent-enabled-recog-tuple[0m in the
  code for function [47massume-true-false[0m in the ACL2 sources.

  The handling of free variables in a hypothesis of a [rewrite] rule
  (see [free-variables]) has been improved in the case that the
  hypothesis is of the form [47m(equiv x y)[0m, where [47mequiv[0m is a known
  equivalence relation (see [equivalence]).  Previously, if the
  rewriter was attempting to rewrite the hypothesis [47m(equiv x y)[0m of a
  rewrite rule, in a context where [47mx'[0m is an instance of [47mx[0m, then the
  rewriter could fail to notice a term [47m(equiv x' y')[0m true in the
  current context where [47my'[0m is an instance of [47my[0m, in the case that [47mx'[0m
  precedes [47my'[0m in the [47m[term-order][0m.  This has been remedied.  This
  improvement applies regardless of whether [47mx[0m, [47my[0m, or (we believe)
  both are already fully instantiated in the present context.  Thanks
  to Joe Hendrix for bringing up an example and to Vernon Austel for
  providing another, simple example.

  A very minor change has been made to the rewriter in the case that an
  equality appears on the left-hand side of a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule.
  Formerly, when such an equality [47m(equal x y)[0m was commuted to [47m(equal
  y x)[0m in order for the rule to match the current term, then all
  equalities on the instantiated right-hand side of the rule were
  commuted, except for those occurring inside another equality.  The
  instantiated right-hand side is no longer modified.  It seems very
  unlikely that this change will cause proofs to fail, though we
  cannot completely rule out that possibility.

  We have modified how the ACL2 simplifier handles the application of a
  defined function symbol to constant arguments in certain cases,
  which we now describe.  As before, ACL2 attempts to simplify such a
  function application by evaluation, provided the
  [47m:[0m[47m[executable-counterpart][0m of the function is enabled.  And as
  before, if that evaluation fails due to a subroutine call of a
  constrained function (introduced by [47m[encapsulate][0m), ACL2 may wrap a
  call of [47mhide[0m around this function application.  (See [hide].)  But
  now, ACL2 attempts to apply definitions and rewrite rules in the
  case that this evaluation fails, and only if the resulting term is
  unchanged does ACL2 wrap [47m[hide][0m around this function application.
  Thanks to Matt Wilding for bringing up the idea of this
  modification.

  The generation of \"Goal\" for recursive (and mutually-recursive)
  definitions now uses the subsumption/replacement limitation
  (default 500).  See [case-split-limitations].

  Default hints now apply to hints given in definitions, not just
  theorems.  See [default-hints].

  Thanks to Robert Krug for implementing the following two improvements
  involving linear arithmetic reasoning: linear arithmetic now uses
  the conclusions of [47m[forward-chaining][0m rules, and [47m[type-set][0m now
  uses a small amount of linear reasoning when deciding inequalities.")
 (NOTE-2-8-RULES
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on Changes in Rules, Definitions, and
  Constants

  The [theory] [47m[minimal-theory][0m has been changed by adding the
  [definition] [rune] for [47m[mv-nth][0m to the theory.  A corresponding
  change has been made to the theory warning mechanism, which was
  failing to warn if the definition of [47mmv-nth[0m is disabled, even
  though calls of [47mmv-nth[0m can be expanded by special-purpose code in
  the rewriter.  Thanks to Serita Van Groningen for pointing out this
  problem with the theory warning mechanism.

  The [47m[defevaluator][0m event has been modified so that in the body of the
  evaluator function, to add a new case [47m(ATOM X)[0m (returning [47mnil[0m) has
  been inserted immediately after the case [47m(EQ (CAR X) 'QUOTE)[0m.  This
  is a no-op semantically but may speed up proofs.  Thanks to Warren
  Hunt for suggesting this change.

  A new form of [47m:[0m[47m[compound-recognizer][0m rule is now allowed:

    (if (fn x) concl1 concl2)

  This is equivalent to an existing form:

    (and (implies (fn x) concl1)
         (implies (not (fn x)) concl2))

  Thanks to Josh Purinton for bringing this to our attention.

  Rewrite rules [47mrealpart-+[0m and [47mimagpart-+[0m have been added in order to
  simplify the [47m[realpart][0m and [47m[imagpart][0m (respectively) of a sum.
  They follow from a theorem [47madd-def-complex[0m that equates a sum with
  the complex number formed by adding real and imaginary parts.  All
  three of these theorems may be found in source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m.
  Thanks to Eric Smith for raising a question leading to these
  additions, as well as to Joe Hendrix and Vernon Austel for helpful
  suggestions.")
 (NOTE-2-8-SYSTEM
  (NOTE-2-8)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8 Notes on System-level Changes

  ACL2 now runs on OpenMCL, ``an opensourced Common Lisp
  implementation, derived from Digitool's Macintosh Common Lisp
  product.'' Thanks to Greg Wright and Robert Krug for doing most of
  the work for this port.

  When [47m([0m[47m[lp][0m[47m)[0m is first executed, the underlying raw Lisp package will
  change to [47m\"ACL2\"[0m (if that is not already the current package in raw
  Lisp).  This is a minor change that will probably not be noticed,
  since up to now it has probably been the case that the ACL2
  executable starts up with [47m\"ACL2\"[0m as the underlying raw Lisp
  package.  But this change was made because we have been informed
  that ACL2 executables based on OpenMCL need not start up with
  [47m\"ACL2\"[0m as the underlying raw Lisp package.

  ACL2 now runs on MCL 5.0.  Thanks to Pascal Costanza for updates to
  the instructions in file [47mmcl-acl2-startup.lisp[0m and for an update to
  the ACL2 sources (parameter [47m*compiled-file-extension*[0m).")
 (NOTE-2-8{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.8(r) (March, 2003) Notes

  The [47mMakefile[0m has been modified by adding a new target, [47mclean-links[0m.
  This can be used in order to remove all soft links, which is useful
  if the directory is copied or moved to a new location or if there
  are file system changes that cause problems with link pathnames.

  Please also see [note-2-8] for changes to Version_2.8 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-2-9
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.9 (October, 2004) Notes


Table of Contents

    * BUG FIXES

    * NEW FUNCTIONALITY

    * CHANGES IN PROOF ENGINE

    * GUARD-RELATED CHANGES

    * PROOF-BUILDER CHANGES

    * SYSTEM-LEVEL CHANGES

    * BOOK CHANGES

    * MISCELLANEOUS CHANGES

  [31;1mBUG FIXES.[0m

  We fixed a soundness bug due to a conflict between user-supplied
  package names and internal package names (obtained by prepending a
  Lisp constant, [47m*1*-package-prefix*[0m) and user-supplied package
  names.  For example, the form [47m(defpkg \"ACL2_*1*_MYPKG\" ())[0m is no
  longer legal.  Thanks to Robert Krug for asking a question that led
  directly to the discovery of this bug.

  We fixed a soundness bug that allows [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions to call
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions.  The fix furthermore prevents functions
  with [guard]s verified from calling functions with guards not
  verified.  We had thought we already prevented all this, but there
  was a problem with the interaction of [47m[local][0m definitions and
  redundancy checking (see [redundant-events]).

  We fixed a soundness bug that could occur when built-in functions
  were called during macroexpansion (a hole in so-called
  ``safe-mode'').

  Fixed a minor bug in system functions [47mgenvar1[0m and [47mgenvar[0m, where [47m[eq][0m
  had been used in place of [47m[eql][0m.  This bug was discovered during
  the plugging of a hole in safe-mode, mentioned just above.

  We fixed handling of the [47m:inline[0m keyword for [47m[defstobj][0m, which
  previously could cause raw Lisp errors in OpenMCL and CMU Common
  Lisp.  Thanks to John Matthews for bringing this issue to our
  attention.

  Calls of [47m[include-book][0m could result in a state for which some
  function definitions were not compiled that should have been.  The
  result could be performance degradation or even stack overflows.
  This situation could arise in the following two ways.

      o Inclusion of a book with an absolute pathname that differs from the
      absolute pathname at certification time, presumably because of
      the use of soft links.  Thanks to Bob Boyer and Warren Hunt for
      bringing a stack overflow to our attention that led us to this
      fix.

      o Large [47m[mutual-recursion][0m nests (more than 20 functions) are
      executed in a superior book.

  We fixed some performance bugs that can increase the speed of
  [47m[include-book][0m calls by a factor of close to 2.  Thanks to Eric
  Smith for asking if we could speed up [47m[include-book][0m processing; we
  have done so in the past, but primarily focusing on large
  [47m[mutual-recursion][0m nests (which have nothing in particular to do
  with the current improvements).  Also, thanks to Rob Sumners for a
  very useful remark early in the process that kept us from drawing
  an incorrect conclusion at that point.

  We fixed [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m so that it can be run on a form that returns multiple
  values, which it could not do previously.  Thanks to Eric Smith for
  pointing out this problem.

  Fixed a bug in the Allegro ACL2 trace utility (see [trace$]) that was
  causing ``[47m10>[0m'' to be printed as ``[47m9>[0m'', ``[47m11>[0m'' to be printed as
  ``[47m10 >[0m'', ``[47m12>[0m'' to be printed as ``[47m11 >[0m'', and so on.

  Fixed a [proof-builder] bug that was preventing the use of the [47mDV[0m
  command (or a numerical command) on [47m[let][0m expressions.  Thanks to
  Bill Young for pointing out this problem.

  Fixed a bug in a comment on how to set [47mACL2_BOOKS_DIR[0m in the
  makefile.  Thanks to Dave Greve for pointing out this problem.

  Fixed a potential soundness bug in the linear arithmetic routines.
  Thanks to Jared Davis for noticing this problem and to Robert Krug
  for implementing the fix.  (Technical details: We had been assuming
  that polynomials were being normalized --- see the definition of
  good-polyp in linear-a.lisp --- but this assumption was false.)

  When the macro [47m[open-trace-file][0m is opened twice in succession, it
  now automatically closes the first trace output channel before
  opening another.

  It is now possible to use `make' to build ACL2 on Windows systems
  that support `make'.  Thanks to Pete Manolios and Mike Thomas for
  pointing out the problem, to Jared Davis and Mike for helping us to
  analyze the problem, and to Jared for testing the fix.

  Fixed a bug in the [guard] of [47m[with-output][0m that was causing a
  needless guard violation.

  [31;1mNEW FUNCTIONALITY.[0m

  The new events [47m[add-default-hints][0m and [47m[remove-default-hints][0m allow
  one to append to or subtract from the current list of default
  hints.  The event [47m[set-default-hints][0m continues to set the list of
  default hints, discarding the previous value of the
  [47m[default-hints][0m.  Note that [47m[set-default-hints][0m is still [47m[local][0m to
  the [47m[encapsulate][0m or book in which it occurs, and
  [47m[add-default-hints][0m has the same property, although neither is
  implemented any longer using the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m.  New events
  [47m[add-default-hints!][0m, [47m[remove-default-hints!][0m, and
  [47m[set-default-hints!][0m are the same as [47m[add-default-hints][0m,
  [47m[remove-default-hints][0m, and [47m[set-default-hints][0m, respectively,
  except that the former three events are not [47m[local][0m to their
  enclosing [47m[encapsulate][0m or book.  Thanks to Jared Davis for
  suggesting these enhancements.

  OpenMCL's tracing routines have been modified in a similar manner as
  those of Allegro CL.  Thanks to Robert Krug for providing this
  enhancement.

  Guard-checking can now be caused to happen on recursive calls.  See
  ``GUARD-RELATED CHANGES'' below for details.

  Advanced users can now inhibit compilation of so-called ``*1*
  functions'' with the [47m:comp[0m command; see [comp].  Thanks to Rob
  Sumners for suggesting this enhancement.

  Added new legal argument [47mhard?[0m for the [47m[er][0m macro, which is now
  documented.  See [er].  Thanks to Rob Sumners for a discussion
  leading to this change.  Also, the three legal first arguments to
  [47m[er][0m --- [47mhard[0m, [47mhard?[0m, and [47msoft[0m --- may now be in any package
  (thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this issue to our attention).

  We have removed the requirement that for a rule's hypothesis
  [47m(bind-free term var-list)[0m, at least one variable must occur free in
  [47mterm[0m.  For example, the expression [47m(bind-free (bind-divisor a b)
  (x))[0m was legal because [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m occur free in [47m(bind-divisor a b)[0m;
  but [47m(bind-free (foo (bar)) (x))[0m was not legal.  The latter is no
  longer disallowed. (Technical note: this allows [47m[bind-free][0m to be
  used to create explicit substitutions in metafunction hypotheses.)

  The following two enhancements have been implemented for rules of
  class [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m.  Thanks to Eric Smith for requesting more control of
  reasoning with [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules, which led to these enhancements, and
  to him and Robert Krug for helpful discussions.

      o It is now possible to control backchaining in rules of class
      [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m by providing a [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m argument, as was
      already allowed for rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m.
      See [rule-classes].  However, unlike those other two rule
      classes, the value for [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m is prohibited from
      being a non-empty list; it must be either [47mnil[0m or a non-negative
      integer.

      o (For advanced users.) It is now legal for hypothesis metafunctions
      to generate, in essence, calls of [47m[syntaxp][0m and [47m[bind-free][0m,
      handled essentially as they are handled in hypotheses of
      [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules.  We say ``essentially''
      primarily because both [47m[syntaxp][0m and [47m[bind-free][0m are actually
      macros, but hypothesis metafunctions must generate translated
      terms (see [term]).  The enterprising advanced user can call
      [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m to see examples of translated terms corresponding to
      calls of [47m[syntaxp][0m and [47m[bind-free][0m.

  A new command [47m:[0m[47m[trans!][0m has been added, which is like [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m except
  that [47m:[0m[47m[trans!][0m ignored issues of single-threadedness.  See
  [trans!].  Thanks to Eric Smith for suggesting this addition.

  The [47m:[0m[47m[pf][0m command now works when the argument is the name of a macro
  associated with a function by [macro-aliases-table].

  [31;1mCHANGES IN PROOF ENGINE.[0m

  The simplifier has been changed slightly in order to avoid using
  [forward-chaining] facts derived from a literal (essentially, a
  top-level hypothesis or conclusion) that has been rewritten.  As a
  practical matter, this may mean that the user should not expect
  forward-chaining to take place on a term that can be rewritten for
  any reason (generally function expansion or application of rewrite
  rules).  Formerly, the restriction was less severe:
  forward-chaining facts from a hypothesis could be used as long as
  the hypothesis was not rewritten to [47mt[0m.  Thanks to Art Flatau for
  providing an example that led us to make this change; see the
  comments in source function [47mrewrite-clause[0m for details.

  The rewriter has been modified to work slightly harder in relieving
  hypotheses.  Thanks to Eric Smith for providing an example that
  inspired the following, which illustrates the issue.  Suppose we
  introduce functions [47mfoo[0m and [47mbar[0m with the (non-[47m[local][0m) properties
  shown below.

    (encapsulate
     (((foo *) => *)
      ((bar *) => *))

     (local (defun foo (x) (declare (ignore x)) t))
     (local (defun bar (x) (declare (ignore x)) t))

     (defthm foo-holds
       (implies x
                (equal (foo x) t)))
     (defthm bar-holds-propositionally
       (iff (bar x) t)))

  Consider what happens when ACL2's rewriter is used to prove the
  following theorem.

    (thm (foo (bar y)))

  With ACL2's inside-out rewriting, [47m(bar y)[0m is first considered, but
  rewrite rule [47mbar-holds-propositionally[0m does not apply because the
  context requires preserving equality, not mere Boolean ([47miff[0m)
  equivalence.  Then the rewriter moves its attention outward and
  sees the term [47m(foo (bar y))[0m.  It attempts to apply the rule
  [47mfoo-holds[0m, in a context created by binding its variable [47mx[0m to the
  term [47m(bar y)[0m.  It then attempts to relieve the hypothesis [47mx[0m of rule
  [47mfoo-holds[0m in that context.  Before this change, ACL2 basically
  assumed that since rewriting was inside out, then [47m(bar y)[0m had
  already been rewritten as much as possible, so the rewrite of [47mx[0m in
  the aforementioned context (binding [47mx[0m to [47m(bar y)[0m) simply returned
  [47m(bar y)[0m, and the attempt to relieve the hypothesis of [47mfoo-holds[0m
  failed.  The change is essentially for ACL2's rewriter to make a
  second pass through the rewriter when the attempt fails to rewrite
  a variable to [47mt[0m, this time using the fact that we are in a Boolean
  context.  (We mention that source function [47mrewrite-solidify-plus[0m
  implements this idea, for those who want to dig deeply into this
  issue.)  In our example, that means that the rewriter considers
  [47m(bar y)[0m in a Boolean context, where it may apply the rule
  [47mbar-holds-propositionally[0m to relieve the hypothesis successfully.

  When [47m([0m[47m[set-non-linearp][0m[47m t)[0m has been executed, [non-linear-arithmetic]
  can now be applied in some cases for which it previously was not.
  Thanks to Robert Krug for supplying this modification and to Julien
  Schmaltz for providing a motivating example.

  We modified the rewriter to avoid certain infinite loops caused by an
  interaction of the opening of recursive functions with equality
  reasoning.  (This change is documented in detail in the source
  code, in particular functions [47mrewrite-fncall[0m and
  [47mfnstack-term-member[0m.)  Thanks to Fares Fraij for sending us an
  example that led us to make this change.

  The [47m:[0m[47m[executable-counterpart][0m of function [47m[hide][0m is now disabled when
  ACL2 starts up.  This removes an anomaly, for example that

    (thm (not (equal 1 (* (hide 0) a))))

  succeeded while

    (thm (equal (foo (equal 1 (* (hide 0) a))) (foo nil)))

  failed.  Now both fail.

  The theory [47m*s-prop-theory*[0m is no longer used by the [3mproof-builder[0m; it
  has been replaced by [47m(theory '[0m[47m[minimal-theory][0m.  We have left the
  constant [47m*s-prop-theory*[0m defined in the source code in support of
  existing books, however.  This change eliminates annoying theory
  warnings printed upon invocation of [proof-builder] commands
  [47ms-prop[0m, [47msl[0m, and [47msplit[0m.

  Terms are now kept in an internal form that avoids calls of primitive
  functions (built-ins without explicit definitions; see code for
  [47mcons-term[0m for details), in favor of the constants that result from
  evaluating those calls.  So for example, the internal form for
  [47m(cons 1 2)[0m is [47m(quote (1 . 2))[0m.  This change was made at around the
  same time as changes in support of [47m[bind-free][0m; see above.  One
  consequence is that the splitting of goals into cases (technically,
  source function [47mclausify[0m and even more technically, source function
  [47mcall-stack[0m) has been modified, which can cause subgoal numbers to
  change.

  [31;1mGUARD-RELATED CHANGES.[0m

  Guard-checking can now be caused to happen on recursive calls, where
  this was formerly not the case for [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions and,
  perhaps more important, for [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode functions whose [guard]s
  have not been verified.  Moreover, a warning is printed when ACL2
  does not rule out the exclusion of guard-checking on recursive
  calls.  See [set-guard-checking].  Thanks to David Rager for
  bringing this issue to our attention, and to Rob Sumners and the
  Univ. of Texas ACL2 seminar in general for their feedback.

  Guard violations are reported with less of the offending term hidden.
  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting that we look at this issue.

  [31;1mPROOF-BUILDER CHANGES.[0m

  We fixed the [proof-builder] so that diving works as you might expect
  for a macro call [47m(op a b c)[0m representing [47m(binary-op a (binary-op b
  c))[0m.  In the past, if the current term was of the form [47m(append t1
  t2 t3)[0m, then [47m(DV 3)[0m (and [47m3[0m) would dive to [47mt3[0m even though the
  corresponding function call is [47m(binary-append t1 (binary-append t2
  t3))[0m.  This is still the case, but now this behavior holds for any
  macro associated with a function in [47mbinop-table[0m (see [add-binop]).
  Moreover, users can now write customized diving functions; see
  [dive-into-macros-table], and also see
  [47mbooks/misc/rtl-untranslate.lisp[0m for example calls to
  [47m[add-dive-into-macro][0m.  Of course, the old behavior can still be
  obtained using the [proof-builder]'s [47mDIVE[0m command; see
  [proof-builder-commands].

  The [47mrunes[0m command in the [proof-builder] now shows only the [rune]s
  used by the most recent primitive or macro command (as shown by
  [47m:comm[0m), unless it is given a non-[47mnil[0m argument.  Also,
  [proof-builder] command [47mlemmas-used[0m has been added as, in essence,
  an alias for [47mrunes[0m.

  (The following two items are also mentioned above under ``BUG
  FIXES.'')

  Fixed a [proof-builder] bug that was preventing the use of the [47mDV[0m
  command (or a numerical command) on [47m[let][0m expressions.  Thanks to
  Bill Young for pointing out this problem.

  The theory [47m*s-prop-theory*[0m is no longer used by the [3mproof-builder[0m; it
  has been replaced by [47m(theory '[0m[47m[minimal-theory][0m.  We have left the
  constant [47m*s-prop-theory*[0m defined in the source code in support of
  existing books, however.  This change eliminates annoying theory
  warnings printed upon invocation of [proof-builder] commands
  [47ms-prop[0m, [47msl[0m, and [47msplit[0m.

  [31;1mSYSTEM-LEVEL CHANGES.[0m

  Fixed a problem with building ACL2 on CMUCL in some systems (source
  function [47msave-acl2-in-cmulisp[0m).  Thanks to Bill Pase for bringing
  this to our attention.

  The installation instructions have been extended to include
  instructions for building on GCL in Mac OS X.  Thanks to Jun Sawada
  and Camm Maguire.

  Initial pre-allocation of space has been updated for GCL to reflect
  more current GCL executables (we considered GCL 2.6.1-38).  This
  can help avoid running out of memory for large ACL2 sessions.

  The main [47mMakefile[0m has been replaced by [47mGNUmakefile[0m, in order to
  enforce the use of GNU `make'.  If you use another `make' program,
  you'll get an error message that may help you proceed.

  (GCL only) SGC is no longer turned on for GCL 2.6 sub-versions
  through 2.6.3 if [47msi::*optimize-maximum-pages*[0m is bound to [47mT[0m, due to
  an apparent issue with their interaction in those sub-versions.
  Also, we have eliminated preallocation for all versions after 2.6.1
  because GCL doesn't need it (comments are in source function
  [47msave-acl2-in-akcl[0m).  Thanks to Camm Maguire for excellent GCL help
  and guidance, and to Camm and Bob Boyer for useful discussions.

  We have removed support for so-called ``small'' images.  Thus,
  [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[pc][0m, [47m[verify-termination][0m, and other commands
  are fully supported in ACL2 saved images.  Because of this and
  other changes in the generation of the so-called ``*1*'' logical
  functions, related to guards (as described above in -GUARD-RELATED
  CHANGES'', and related to the discussion of safe-mode in ``BUG
  FIXES'' above), image sizes have increased substantially.

  We no longer [47mtime[0m or run ``[47mnice[0m'' the certification of individual
  books.  The file [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m had done these by default,
  and some individual distributed and workshop book directories had
  [47mMakefile[0ms that did so as well.  Thanks to Mike Thomas, who pointed
  out the lack of [47mnice[0m on some Windows systems (and we decided on
  this simple solution).  Overall targets in [47mbooks/Makefile[0m still
  [47mtime[0m their runs by default, and the particular [47mtime[0m program is now
  controlled by a [47mMakefile[0m variable.

  Failures during [47mmake certify-books[0m or [47mmake regression[0m now show up in
  the log as ``[47m**CERTIFICATION FAILED**[0m'', regardless of the
  operating system (as long as it supports `make').  Formerly, one
  searched for ``[47m**[0m'' but this did not appear in openMCL runs.

  We have eliminated ``Undefined function'' warnings that could occur
  in OpenMCL.

  [31;1mBOOK CHANGES.[0m

  Reconciled the definitions of [47mfirstn[0m in [47mbook/misc/csort.lisp[0m,
  [47mbooks/bdd/bdd-primitives.lisp[0m,
  [47mbooks/ordinals/ordinal-definitions.lisp[0m, and
  [47mbooks/data-structures/list-defuns.lisp[0m.  Thanks to Ray Richards for
  bringing this issue to our attention.

  Distributed book [47mbooks/misc/defpun[0m now can handle [stobj]s where it
  did not previously.  Thanks to John Matthews for bringing this
  issue to our attention.

  The \"make\" variable [47mCOMPILE_FLG[0m in file [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m
  formerly only had an effect if there was a [47mcert.acl2[0m file present.
  That oversight has been remedied.

  File [47m\"books/arithmetic/certify.lsp\"[0m was missing a [47m[certify-book][0m
  command for [47m\"natp-posp\"[0m.  Thanks to John Cowles for noticing this
  deficiency and supplying a fix.  (This file is of use to those who
  want to certify the [47m\"books/arithmetic/\"[0m books without using
  [47m\"make\"[0m.)

  A few small changes have been made to [47m\"books/rtl/rel4\"[0m.

  Small changes were made to books [47mmisc/symbol-btree[0m and
  [47mmisc/rtl-untranslate[0m.  In particular, the definition of
  [47msymbol-btreep[0m was strengthened.

  We made a minor fix to [47mbooks/ordinals/e0-ordinal.lisp[0m, adding
  [47m(verify-guards ob+)[0m and hence [47m(verify-guards ocmp)[0m as well.  This
  was necessitated by the fix prohibiting functions with guards
  verified from calling functions with guards not verified (see also
  the related discussion under ``BUG FIXES'' above).

  [31;1mMISCELLANEOUS CHANGES.[0m

  Further sped up processing of large [47m[mutual-recursion][0m nests
  (extending what was done for Version_2.7), perhaps by a factor of
  two in some cases.

  As promised in Version_2.5 (see [note-2-5]), structured pathnames are
  no longer supported.  So for example, the argument to
  [47m[include-book][0m must now be a string constant.

  Some documentation has been improved, for [stobj]s thanks to
  suggestions by John Matthews and much of the rest thanks to
  feedback from Eric Smith.

  The function [47m[current-package][0m is now available to users (it has been
  taken off the list of so-called ``untouchables'').  Thanks to Jared
  Davis for bringing this issue to our attention.

  The documentation for topic [using-computed-hints-7] has been
  improved.  Thanks to Doug Harper and Eric Smith for inspiring this
  improvement.

  We added several symbols to [47m*acl2-exports*[0m: [47m[cw][0m, [47m[er][0m, [47m[intern$][0m,
  [47m[set-case-split-limitations][0m, and [47m[set-difference-eq][0m.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for suggesting most of these.

  Now, a [47m[table][0m event that sets the value for a key, [47m(table tbl key
  val :put)[0m, is redundant (see [redundant-events]) when it does not
  change the value associated with an existing key of the table.  In
  particular, [47m[define-pc-macro][0m is now fully redundant when it does
  not change an existing [proof-builder] macro-command definition.
  Thanks to Bill Young for bringing the latter issue to our
  attention.

  The definitions of unused system functions [47mev-w[0m and [47mev-w-lst[0m have
  been deleted.

  ACL2 now prints a warning if a [47m[defpkg][0m event introduces a package
  name with lower-case letters, since there is opportunity for later
  confusion in that case.  Thanks to Frederic Peschanski for bringing
  this problem to our attention and Sandip Ray for encouragement.

  ACL2 now works in Version 19 of CMU Common Lisp.

  The function [47m[sys-call][0m has been modified so that for ACL2 built on
  Allegro Common Lisp in Unix or Linux, the existing environment is
  used.  Thanks to Erik Reeber for bringing this issue to our
  attention.

  The function [47m[disabledp][0m can now be given a macro name that has a
  corresponding function; see [macro-aliases-table].  Also,
  [47m[disabledp][0m now has a [guard] of [47mt[0m but causes a hard error on an
  inappropriate argument.")
 (NOTE-2-9-1
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.9.1 (December, 2004) Notes

  (GCL only) A bug in [47m[symbol-package-name][0m has been fixed that could
  be exploited to prove [47mnil[0m, and hence is a soundness bug.  Thanks to
  Dave Greve for sending us an example of a problem with [47m[defcong][0m
  (see below) that led us to this discovery.

  ACL2 now warns when [47m[defcong][0m specifies [47m[equal][0m as the first
  equivalence relation, e.g., [47m(defcong equal iff (member x y) 2)[0m.
  The warning says that the rule has no effect because [47m[equal][0m
  already refines all other equivalence relations.  Formerly, this
  caused an error unless [47m:event-name[0m was supplied (see [defcong]),
  and in fact the error was a nasty raw Lisp error on GCL platforms
  due to some mishandling of packages by ACL2 that has been fixed
  (see the paragraph about [47m[symbol-package-name][0m above).  Thanks to
  Dave Greve for sending a helpful example in his report of this
  problem.

  (GCL only) The build process was broken for GCL 2.6.0 (and perhaps
  some earlier versions), and has been fixed.  Thanks to Jose Luis
  Ruiz-Reina for bringing this problem to our attention.

  (GCL only) We have increased the hole size to at least 20% of
  max-pages, which may eliminate some garbage collection at the
  expense of larger virtual memory (not larger resident memory or
  larger image).  Thanks to Camm Maguire for helpful explanations on
  this topic.

  We have clarified the [guard] warning message that is printed during
  evaluation of recursively-defined functions whose [guard]s have not
  been verified, for example:

    ACL2 Warning [Guards] in TOP-LEVEL:  Guard-checking may be inhibited
    on some recursive calls of executable counterparts (i.e., in the ACL2
    logic), including perhaps EVENLP.  To check guards on all recursive
    calls:
      (set-guard-checking :all)
    To leave behavior unchanged except for inhibiting this message:
      (set-guard-checking :nowarn)

  And, ACL2 no longer prints that message when the [guard] was
  unspecified for the function or was specified as [47mT[0m.  Thanks to
  Serita Nelesen for bringing the latter issue to our attention.
  Finally, ACL2 now prints such a warning at most once during the
  evaluation of any top-level form; thanks to Bill Young for pointing
  out this issue.

  The function [47m[verbose-pstack][0m has been enhanced to allow specified
  prover functions [31;1mnot[0m to be traced.  See [verbose-pstack].

  Added [47m[lp][0m, [47mwet[0m, and [47m[set-non-linearp][0m to [47m*acl2-exports*[0m, and hence
  to the [47m\"[0m[47m[ACL2-user][0m[47m\"[0m package.

  The distributed book [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/bind-free/integerp.lisp[0m has
  been modified in order to prevent potential looping; specifically,
  the definition of function [47mreduce-integerp-+-fn-1[0m.  Thanks to
  Robert Krug for providing this change.

  A small improvement was made in the [47mwet[0m failure message when the
  error occurs during translation to internal form.  Thanks to Jared
  Davis for pointing out the obscurity of some [47mwet[0m error messages.

  We have improved ACL2's evaluation mechanism for the function
  [47mbad-atom<=[0m, which now is specified to return [47mnil[0m if neither
  argument is a so-called ``bad atom'' (as recognized by function
  [47mbad-atom[0m).  The following events had caused a hard error, for
  example.  (We're sorry that [47mbad-atom[0m and [47mbad-atom<=[0m are not
  documented, but we also consider it unlikely that anyone needs such
  documentation; otherwise, please contact the implementors.)

    (defun foo (x y) (declare (xargs :guard t)) (bad-atom<= x y))
    (defun bar (x y) (declare (xargs :guard t)) (foo x y))
    (thm (equal (bar 3 4) 7))

  We have also changed the guard on [47m[alphorder][0m to require both
  arguments to be atoms.

  For forms [47m(local x)[0m that are skipped during [47m[include-book][0m, or during
  the second pass of [47m[certify-book][0m or [47m[encapsulate][0m, ACL2 had
  nevertheless checked that [47mx[0m is a legal event form.  This is no
  longer the case.

  The [proof-builder] now does non-linear arithmetic when appropriate.
  It had formerly ignored [47m[set-non-linearp][0m executed in the ACL2
  command loop.

  Incremental releases are now supported.  See [version] and {obsolete
  after Version 4.3} set-tainted-okp.  Thanks to Hanbing Liu for
  discovering a flaw in our original design.

  The pattern-matching algorithm for [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules has been made
  slightly more restrictive, thanks to a suggestion and examples from
  Robert Krug.  For example, previously one could get an infinite
  loop as follows.

    (defstub foo (x) t)
    (defaxiom foo-axiom
      (equal (foo (+ 1 x))
             (foo x)))
    (thm (foo 0)) ; or replace 0 by any integer!

  That is because the term [47m(foo 0)[0m was considered to match against the
  pattern [47m(foo (+ 1 x))[0m, with [47mx[0m bound to [47m-1[0m.  While such matching is
  sound, it leads to an infinite loop since it allows [47mfoo-axiom[0m to
  rewrite [47m(foo 0)[0m to [47m(foo -1)[0m, and then [47m(foo -1)[0m to [47m(foo -2)[0m, and so
  on.  The fix is to insist that the new value, in this case [47m-1[0m, is
  no larger in size according to [47m[ACL2-count][0m than the old value, in
  this case [47m0[0m.  Since that test fails, the match is considered to
  fail and the loop no longer occurs.  An analogous fix has been made
  for multiplication, where now we only match when the new term is
  still a non-zero integer.  That change avoids a loop here.

    (defstub foo (x) t)
    (defaxiom foo-axiom
      (equal (foo (* 2 x))
             (foo x)))
    (thm (foo 0)) ; or try (thm (foo 4))

  Added macro [47mfind-lemmas[0m in [47mbooks/misc/find-lemmas.lisp[0m (see brief
  documentation there) for finding all lemmas that mention all
  function symbols in a given list.

  [47m:Restrict[0m [hints] now work for [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rules, though they
  continue to be ignored by the preprocessor and hence you may want
  to use [47m:do-not '(preprocess)[0m with any restrict hints.  Thanks to
  John Matthews for pointing out the lack of support for [47m:definition[0m
  rules in [47m:restrict[0m hints.

  Some books have been updated.  In particular, there is a new
  directory [47mbooks/workshops/2004/[0m in workshops distribution, for the
  2004 ACL2 workshop.  There is also a new version of Jared Davis's
  ordered sets library, formerly in
  [47mbooks/finite-set-theory/osets-0.81/[0m but now in
  [47mbooks/finite-set-theory/osets/[0m.

  Fixed a bug in the (under-the-hood) raw Lisp definition of
  [47m[defchoose][0m, which had been causing a warning in CMU Common Lisp.

  [Technical improvements related to the use of ``[47mmake dependencies[0m''
  for certifying distributed books:]
  File [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m now does a better job with ``[47mmake
  dependencies[0m,'' specifically with respect to handling [47m*.acl2[0m files
  and handling [47m[include-book][0m commands with [47m:dir :system[0m.  Regarding
  the latter, suppose for example that book [47mbasic.lisp[0m contains the
  line:

    (include-book \"arithmetic/top-with-meta\" :dir :system)

  Then [47mmake dependencies[0m would generate the following line:

    basic.cert: $(ACL2_SRC_BOOKS)/arithmetic/top-with-meta.cert

  Thus, if [47m:dir :system[0m is used with [47m[include-book][0m, the corresponding
  [47mMakefile[0m should define the variable [47mACL2_SRC_BOOKS[0m.  A standard
  [47mMakefile[0m header for a books directory could thus be as follows.

    # The following variable should represent the ACL2 source directory.  It is the
    # only variable in this Makefile that may need to be edited.
    ACL2_SRC = ../../../../../..

    ACL2_SRC_BOOKS = $(ACL2_SRC)/books
    include $(ACL2_SRC_BOOKS)/Makefile-generic
    ACL2 = $(ACL2_SRC)/saved_acl2h

  Finally, the ``[47m-s[0m'' flag may now be omitted when running ``[47mmake
  dependencies[0m.''")
 (NOTE-2-9-2
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.9.2 (April, 2005) Notes

  Also see [note-2-9-1] for other changes since the last
  non-incremental release (Version_2.9).

  There was a bug in non-linear arithmetic (see
  [non-linear-arithmetic]) that caused the following error:

    ACL2 !>(include-book \"rtl/rel4/lib/top\" :dir :system)
    ....
    ACL2 !>(set-non-linearp t)
     T
    ACL2 !>(thm
     (implies (and (bvecp a 77)
                   (bvecp b 50))
              (bvecp (fl (/ (* a b) (expt 2 23)))
                     104))
     :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable bvecp))))

    [Note:  A hint was supplied for the goal above.  Thanks!]

    By the simple :definition BVECP, the :executable-counterparts of EXPT
    and UNARY-/ and the simple :rewrite rule ASSOCIATIVITY-OF-* we reduce
    the conjecture to

    Goal'
    (IMPLIES (AND (INTEGERP A)
                  (<= 0 A)
                  (< A 151115727451828646838272)
                  (INTEGERP B)
                  (<= 0 B)
                  (< B 1125899906842624))
             (BVECP (FL (* A B 1/8388608)) 104)).

    HARD ACL2 ERROR in VARIFY:  This should not have happened.  The supposed
    variable, '1/8388608, is instead a constant.

    ACL2 !>

  Thanks to Robert Krug for providing a fix for the above error.

  Guard-checking was being inhibited (since v2-9) for calls of built-in
  primitives on explicit values, e.g., [47m(car 3)[0m.  This has been fixed.

  Guard-related warnings could be printed during proofs (this bug was
  introduced in Version_2.9.1).  These warnings have been eliminated.

  Compound-recognizer rules [47mnatp-compound-recognizer[0m and
  [47mposp-compound-recognizer[0m are now built into ACL2 for predicates
  [47m[natp][0m and [47m[posp][0m, and hence have been deleted from book
  [47mnatp-posp.lisp[0m (where they were called [47mnatp-cr[0m and [47mposp-cr[0m,
  respectively).

  The function [47mfile-clock-p[0m, which recognizes a component of the ACL2
  [47m[state][0m, is now defined using [47m[natp][0m instead of [47m[integerp][0m.  Thanks
  to Jared Davis for suggesting this change.  (Technical explanation
  about functions in ACL2 source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m: With a [47mfile-clock[0m
  of -1, the call of [47mmake-input-channel[0m in [47mopen-input-channel[0m will
  create a channel that can't be closed; see the guard of
  [47mclose-input-channel[0m.)

  (Allegro CL users only) Support is now provided for building an
  Allegro CL application, provided you have an Allegro CL dynamic
  runtime license.  (Our belief is that with such a license, many
  users can use the same application, rather than each user needing a
  separate license.)  See new GNUmakefile target [47mallegro-app[0m and file
  [47mbuild-allegro-exe.cl[0m for more information.

  The new home page now contains a link to a new page
  [47mother-releases.html[0m, which contains information about other ACL2
  releases.  (This is in one's local home page, but may not show up
  on the central ACL2 home page until the next non-incremental
  release.)  Thanks to Warren Hunt for suggesting this addition.

  We thank Erik Reeber for suggesting a solution to output redirection
  using [47m[sys-call][0m, which we have described at the end of its
  documentation.

  A new documentation topic fixes the flawed argument for
  conservativity of the [47m[defchoose][0m event that appears in Appendix B
  of Kaufmann and Moore's paper, ``Structured Theory Development for
  a Mechanized Logic'' (Journal of Automated Reasoning 26, no. 2
  (2001), pp. 161--203).  See [conservativity-of-defchoose].  Thanks
  to John Cowles and Ruben Gamboa for helpful feedback on drafts of
  this note.

  The solution to exercise 6.15 in [47mbooks/textbook/chap6/solutions.txt[0m
  has been fixed.  Thanks to Aaron Smith for pointing out the
  problem.

  A new documentation topic [defun-sk-example] gives a little more help
  in using [47m[defun-sk][0m effectively.  Thanks to Julien Schmaltz for
  presenting this example as a challenge.

  (GCL only) There is now a way to speed up GCL builds of ACL2, at the
  cost of perhaps a percent or so in performance of the resulting
  image.  Using `make' one supplies the following.

    LISP='gcl -eval \"(defparameter user::*fast-acl2-gcl-build* t)\"

  Various makefiles have been improved in several ways.

      (1) Parallel book certification, using GNU make's [47m-j[0m option, can be
      used.

      (2) Book certifications now stops at the first failure if
      [47mbooks/Makefile[0m or [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m is used, and returns
      non-zero exit status.  However, the various make targets in the
      ACL2 source directory ([47mregression[0m, [47mcertify-books[0m, etc.) still
      continue past failures unless you provide [47mACL2_IGNORE=' '[0m on
      the `make' command line.

      (3) The build process has been modified (file [47mGNUmakefile[0m) so that it
      stops upon a failed compile or a failed initialization.

      (4) The automatic dependency generation (from ``[47mmake dependencies[0m''
      has been improved so that commands of the form [47m(ld
      \"my-book.lisp\")[0m in [47m.acl2[0m files cause the appropriate
      dependencies to be generated.

  Thanks to comments from several users that led to the above Makefile
  improvements: Ray Richards, Doug Harper, and the Rockwell ACL2
  users for (1) and (2) (and inspiring (4)), and David Rager for (2)
  and (3).  In particular, Doug Harper sent a replacement for the
  [47m.date[0m mechanism, which was interfering with [47mmake -n[0m; so, these
  files are no longer written.

  A mechanism has been added for saving output.  In particular, you can
  now call [47m[ld][0m on a file with output turned off, for efficiency, and
  yet when a proof fails you can then display the proof attempt for
  the failed (last) event.  See set-saved-output.  Another new
  command --- see [set-print-clause-ids] --- causes subgoal numbers
  to be printed during proof attempts when output is inhibited.

  Documentation has been added for using ACL2's makefile support to
  automate the certification of collections of books.  See
  [books-certification-classic].

  Fixed a bug in [47m[sys-call-status][0m that was causing hard Lisp errors.

  Improved [47m[cw-gstack][0m to allow a [47m:frames[0m argument to specify a range
  of one or more frames to be printed.  see [cw-gstack].

  Fixed a bug in [proof-builder] command [47mforwardchain[0m.  Thanks to
  Ming-Hsiu Wang for bringing this bug to our attention.

  We have provided a mechanism for saving an executable image.  See
  [saving-and-restoring] and see [save-exec].  We have eliminated
  obsolete functions [47mnote-lib[0m and [47mmake-lib[0m.

  Modified the [47m[ground-zero][0m [theory] so that it contains all of the
  built-in rules (in ACL2 source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m).  It had formerly
  failed to include rules from some definitions and theorems near the
  end of [47maxioms.lisp[0m.

  A new event, [47m[set-enforce-redundancy][0m, allows the enforcement of
  [47m[defthm][0m, [47m[defun][0m, and most other events during book development.
  See [set-enforce-redundancy].

  A bug has been fixed that had allowed [47m[deftheory][0m [events] to cause a
  hard Lisp error when calling [47m[union-theories][0m on ill-formed
  theories after, for example:

    :set-guard-checking nil
    (in-theory (union-theories '((:rewrite no-such-rule))
                               (current-theory 'ground-zero)))

  The handling of [guard] checking has been modified somewhat in a way
  that should only very rarely affect users.  (An ``Essay on Guard
  Checking'' in the ACL2 source code explains this point to anyone
  interested in implementation details.)

  (GCL ONLY) Removed the -dir setting in the ACL2 wrapper script for
  GCL.  This should generally have no effect for most users, but it
  eliminates a potential source of error down the road.

  Several interesting new definitions and lemmas have been added to the
  rtl library developed at AMD, and incorporated into
  [47mbooks/rtl/rel4/lib/[0m.  Other book changes include a change to lemma
  [47mtruncate-rem-elim[0m in [47mbooks/ihs/quotient-remainder-lemmas.lisp[0m, as
  suggested by Jared Davis.

  The macro [47m[real/rationalp][0m may now be referred to in [47m[in-theory][0m
  [events] and [hints], thanks to a new [47m[add-macro-alias][0m event.
  Thanks to Jared Davis for this suggestion.

  ACL2 terms of the form [47m(if p 'nil 't)[0m are now printed as [47m(not p)[0m,
  where in some setting they had been printed as [47m(and (not p) t)[0m.
  Thanks to Robert Krug for this improvement.

  (GCL ONLY) Added profiling support, based heavily on code supplied by
  Camm Maguire.  See file [47msave-gprof.lsp[0m for instructions.  Thanks to
  Camm, and also to David Hardin for inspiring this addition.

  Added support for preprocessing before printing (untranslating) a
  term.  See [user-defined-functions-table], in particular the
  discussion of [47muntranslate-preprocess[0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for
  inspiring this addition, and for providing a book that takes
  advantage of it ([47mbooks/misc/untranslate-patterns.lisp[0m).

  The documentation has been improved for explaining how [rune]s are
  assigned; see [rune].  Thanks to Robert Krug for pointing out
  inaccuracies in the existing documentation.")
 (NOTE-2-9-3
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.9.3 (August, 2005) Notes

  Also see [note-2-9-1] and see [note-2-9-2] for other changes since
  the last non-incremental release (Version_2.9).

  We fixed a soundness bug that exploited the ability to define
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions that are improperly guarded, and then to
  use those functions in [47m[defconst][0m forms.  The fix is to evaluate
  [47m[defconst][0m forms using the same ``safe-mode'' that is used in
  macroexpansion (see [guards-and-evaluation]).  Here is a proof of
  [47mnil[0m that succeeded in Allegro Common Lisp (but not, for example,
  GCL).  See also a long comment in source function [47mdefconst-fn[0m for
  an example that does not require the use of [47m:set-guard-checking[0m.

    :set-guard-checking nil ; execute before certifying the book below

    (in-package \"ACL2\")

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (local (defun f1 ()
              (declare (xargs :mode :program))
              (char-upcase (code-char 224))))
     (local (defconst *b* (f1)))
     (defun f1 ()
       (char-upcase (code-char 224)))
     (defconst *b* (f1))
     (defthm bad
       (not (equal *b* (code-char 224)))
       :rule-classes nil))

    (defthm ouch
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use bad))
      :rule-classes nil)

  We fixed a soundness hole due to the fact that the \"LISP\" package
  does not exist in OpenMCL.  We now explicitly disallow this package
  name as an argument to [47m[defpkg][0m.  Thanks to Bob Boyer and Warren
  Hunt for bringing an issue to our attention that led to this fix.

  ACL2 now requires all package names to consist of standard characters
  (see [standard-char-p], none of which is lower case.  The reason is
  that we have seen at least one lisp implementation that does not
  handle lower case package names correctly.  Consider for example
  the following raw lisp log (some newlines omitted).

    >(make-package \"foo\")
    #<\"foo\" package>
    >(package-name (symbol-package 'FOO::A))
    \"foo\"
    >(package-name (symbol-package '|FOO|::A))
    \"foo\"
    >

  Distributed book [47mbooks/textbook/chap10/compiler[0m, as well as workshop
  books in directory [47mbooks/workshops/2004/cowles-gamboa/support/[0m,
  were modified to accommodate the above change.

  Added [47mnewline[0m, [47m[add-to-set-eql][0m, [47mthe-fixnum[0m, and [47mthe-fixnum![0m to
  [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing these to our
  attention.

  Added a line to [47macl2.lisp[0m to support CMUCL running on Mac OSX, thanks
  to a suggestion from Fabricio Chalub Barbosa do Rosario.

  The executable scripts for saved ACL2 images now include [47m$*[0m, so that
  command-line arguments will be passed along.

  (For GCL profiling only) Fixed a colon ([47m:[0m) that should have been a
  semicolon ([47m;[0m) in file [47msave-gprof.lsp[0m.  Thanks to David Hardin for
  pointing out this bug.

  The documentation for [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m rules has been expanded and improved,
  thanks to useful feedback from Hanbing Liu.

  Fixed a bug in the guard for function [47minclude-book-dir[0m.

  For those who want to experiment with an alternate implementation of
  [47m[mv][0m and [47m[mv-let][0m, there is now support for under-the-hood
  implementation of these in terms of raw Lisp functions [47mvalues[0m and
  [47mmultiple-value-bind[0m, respectively.  The regression suite has seen
  about a 10% speed-up in Allegro CL and about an 8% slowdown in GCL
  for builds with this change.  See the makefile ([47mGNUmakefile[0m) for
  examples of how to build ACL2 by including the feature,
  [47m:acl2-mv-as-values[0m.  Source file [47minit.lsp[0m has been renamed to
  [47minit.lisp[0m in support of this change (technical detail: otherwise
  GCL loads the init file too soon, before its [47m-eval[0m argument is
  evaluated).  Thanks to David Rager for inspiring this change, by
  pointing out the problematic use of globals by the existing [47m[mv][0m
  implementation from the standpoint of supporting parallel
  evaluation.  This capability is experimental: there is likely to be
  some remaining work to be done on it.

  A change related to the one just above is that we now limit the
  maximum number of arguments to any call of [47m[mv][0m to 32.  Thanks to
  Bob Boyer for raising a question that lead to this change.

  Eliminated some compiler warnings in OpenMCL.

  In the rtl library ([47mbooks/rtl/rel4/[0m), functions [47mbits[0m and [47msetbits[0m have
  had their [guard]s improved (as they had been too restrictive,
  especially for [47msetbits[0m).

  A new function [47m[time$][0m permits timing of forms, by using (under the
  hood) the host Common Lisp's [47mtime[0m utility.

  We fixed an infinite loop that could occur during destructor
  elimination (see [elim]).  Thanks to Sol Swords to bringing this to
  our attention and sending a nice example, and to Doug Harper for
  sending a second example that we also found useful.

  The method of speeding up GCL-based builds (see [note-2-9-2]) has
  changed slightly from Version_2.9.2.  Now, in the `make' command:

    LISP='gcl -eval \"(defparameter user::*fast-acl2-gcl-build* t)\"

  We improved the pretty-printer's handling of keywords.  For example,
  before this change one might see the following printed by ACL2.

    (MODIFY TH S :KEY1 VAL1 :KEY2
            (IF (IF X Y Z) AAAAAAAAAA BBBBBBB))

  Now, the above might print as follows.  Notice that we have avoided
  breaking after a keyword (see [keywordp]) that is preceded by other
  forms on the same line.

    (MODIFY TH S
            :KEY1 VAL1
            :KEY2 (IF (IF X Y Z) AAAAAAAAAA BBBBBBB))

  See [note-2-9-3-ppr-change] for a detailed discussion of this change.

  (GCL ONLY) Evaluation in a break is no longer inhibited by ACL2 when
  built on top of GCL, so GCL now matches other Common Lisps in this
  respect.

  For ACL2 built on most host Common Lisps, you will see the string
  [47m[RAW LISP][0m in the prompt, at least at a break, to emphasize that
  one is inside a break and hence should probably quit from the
  break.  See [breaks].

  Jared Davis suggested improvements to lemmas [47mlen-update-nth[0m (in
  source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m) and [47mappend-true-listp-type-prescription[0m
  (in [47mbooks/meta/term-defuns.lisp[0m), which have been incorporated.
  The former required a change in [47mbooks/workshops[0m book
  [47m2004/ruiz-et-al/support/q-dag-unification.cert[0m, which has been
  made.

  The [proof-builder] command [47mrewrite[0m allows further binding of free
  variables in hypotheses, with new optional argument
  [47minstantiate-free[0m.  proof-builder command [47mshow-rewrites[0m ([47msr[0m) gives
  corresponding additional information.  Documentation for these
  commands has been improved; see [proof-builder-commands].  Thanks
  to John Matthews and Bill Young for suggestions and feedback
  leading to these improvements.

  Fixed downcase printing so that the package name of a symbol is also
  downcased.  For example, after execution of [47m(defpkg \"FOO\" nil)[0m and
  [47m(set-acl2-print-case :downcase)[0m, [47m'foo::ab[0m will print back as the
  same, rather than as [47m'FOO::ab[0m.

  It is now possible to control the output so that numbers are printed
  in binary, octal, or hex, though the default is still radix 10.
  See [set-print-base].  Note that in support of this change,
  built-in functions [47m[explode-nonnegative-integer][0m and [47mexplode-atom[0m
  now take an extra [47mprint-base[0m argument.  Different support for radix
  conversion may be found in a book newly contributed by Jun Sawada,
  [47mbooks/misc/radix.lisp[0m.

  Built-in axiom [47mcar-cdr-elim[0m is now only an [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m rule.  It was
  formerly both an [47m:elim[0m rule and a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule.  A new rule,
  [47mcons-car-cdr[0m, takes the place of the old [47m:rewrite[0m rule, but is
  instead a hypothesis-free rule that can cause a case split (see
  source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m).  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting
  this change.

  Lemmas about [47m[alphorder][0m ([47malphorder-reflexive[0m, [47malphorder-transitive[0m,
  [47malphorder-anti-symmetric[0m, and [47malphorder-total[0m) are now available.
  (They had been [47m[local][0m in source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m.)  Thanks to
  Serita Nelesen for bringing this issue to our attention.

  ACL2 has, for some time, printed a space in the event summary after
  the open parenthesis for a [47m[defthm][0m event, in order to ease
  backward searching for the original form, for example [47m(defthm bar
  ...)[0m:

    Form:  ( DEFTHM BAR ...)

  The intention was that this extra space should be printed for every
  event form; but it was missing in some cases, for example, for
  [47m[verify-guards][0m.  This has been fixed.

  In analogy to [47m[include-book][0m, now [47m[ld][0m takes the (optional) keyword
  argument [47m:dir[0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for providing an
  implementation of this feature and to Eric Smith and Jeff Marshall
  for requesting this feature.

  We fixed a bug in [47m[include-book][0m that could cause an error when
  redefinition is on, for example:

    (set-ld-redefinition-action '(:warn! . :overwrite) state)
    (include-book \"/u/acl2/books/arithmetic/top\")

  The behavior of [47m[include-book][0m now matches the documentation:
  handling of compiled files for uncertified [books] will follow the
  same rules as for certified books.  In particular, if you create an
  object file in raw Lisp for some book, then including that book
  will load that object file.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing
  this issue to our attention.

  New documentation explains the interaction of redefinition and
  redundancy.  See [redundant-events] --- the ``Note About
  Unfortunate Redundancies'' is new.  Thanks to Grant Passmore for
  providing examples that led us to write this additional
  documentation.

  Solutions to exercises in { How To Prove Theorems Formally |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/publications/how-to-prove-thms}
  are now available in distributed book
  [47mbooks/misc/how-to-prove-thms.lisp[0m.  Also in that directory may be
  found a new book [47mhanoi.lisp[0m that contains a solution to the Towers
  of Hanoi problem.


Subtopics

  [Note-2-9-3-ppr-change]
      Change in pretty-printing for ACL2 Version_2.9.3")
 (NOTE-2-9-3-PPR-CHANGE
  (NOTE-2-9-3)
  "Change in pretty-printing for ACL2 Version_2.9.3

  We have improved pretty-printing in ACL2 Version_2.9.3 to handle
  keywords a little differently.  To see a discussion of the basics
  of this change, see [note-2-9-3].  In this note we describe it in
  considerable detail.

  Those who wish to understand the ACL2 pretty-printer's implementation
  can now find considerably more comments on it in the source code.
  In this note, we do not focus on the implementation.  Rather, we
  motivate the change and show how the improved prettyprinter
  performs.

  Why do we want better keyword handling?  Imagine a macro that builds
  a new state from an old state by changing the values in the
  affected fields, leaving everything else unchanged.  One could
  write

    (modify th s :key1 val1 :key2 val2 :key3 val3)

  where the three keys identify fields in the state.

  To make it easier to read new concrete states, we may have a function
  that prints them ``relative'' to a given base state, expressing the
  new state as a modification of the given base state.  So we may
  find ourselves prettyprinting modify forms like that above.

  The previous prettyprinter will sometimes print the form above as
  follows.

    (modify th s :key1
            val1
            :key2 val2 :key3 val3)

  This can be unpleasant to read, because of the way [47m:key1[0m and [47mval1[0m are
  separated.  Here is an example of the old prettyprinter and the new
  one, both printing an expression from the ACL2 source code in a
  width of 40:

    Old:
    (ADD-TO-TAG-TREE
     'ASSUMPTION
     (MAKE
      ASSUMPTION :TYPE-ALIST TYPE-ALIST
      :TERM TERM :REWRITTENP REWRITTENP
      :IMMEDIATEP IMMEDIATEP :ASSUMNOTES
      (LIST
       (MAKE
            ASSUMNOTE :CL-ID
            NIL :RUNE RUNE :TARGET TARGET)))
     TTREE)

    New:
    (ADD-TO-TAG-TREE
         'ASSUMPTION
         (MAKE ASSUMPTION
               :TYPE-ALIST TYPE-ALIST
               :TERM TERM
               :REWRITTENP REWRITTENP
               :IMMEDIATEP IMMEDIATEP
               :ASSUMNOTES
               (LIST (MAKE ASSUMNOTE
                           :CL-ID NIL
                           :RUNE RUNE
                           :TARGET TARGET)))
         TTREE)

  Basically the change we made forces the prettyprinter to print each
  [47m:key[0m on a new line unless they all fit on a single line.  So we
  would now get either

    (modify th s :key1 val1 :key2 :val2 :key3 val3)

  or

    (modify th s
            :key1 val1
            :key2 val2
            :key3 val3)

  Furthermore, we fixed it so that if [47mval1[0m (say) is a big s-expression
  we may still print it on the same line as its key.  The old
  prettyprinter enforced the rule that if you wanted to print [47m(foo a
  b)[0m and [47mb[0m gets broken up into several lines, then it has to start on
  a new line.  Thus, we'd never print

    (foo a (bbb
            (mum x)))

  but would print instead

    (foo a
         (bbb
          (mum x)))

  Now, if a is a keyword, we can print the first way.

  So here are some nice examples of prettyprinted keyword forms.  All
  of these are printed for a page of width 40.

    <--            40 chars               ->
    xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

    (MODIFY TH S :KEY1 V1 :KEY2 V2)

    (MODIFY TH S :KEY1 V1 :KEY2 V2 :KEY3 V3)

    (MODIFY TH S1                               ; Because of the extra char
            :KEY1 V1                            ; in S1 the flat size exceeds
            :KEY2 V2                            ; 40 and we break it.
            :KEY3 V3)

  The old ppr would have printed this as:

    (MODIFY
         TH S1 :KEY1 V1 :KEY2 V2 :KEY3 V3)

  Returning to new examples:

    <--            40 chars               ->
    xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

    (MODIFY TH S
            :KEY1 (IF (IF X Y Z) AAAA BBBB)
            :KEY2 VAL2
            :KEY3 VAL3)

  Now we extend [47mAAAA[0m and [47mBBBB[0m by one char each, so it would overflow
  the right margin if printed as above, and we get:

    <--            40 chars               ->
    xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

    (MODIFY
         TH S
         :KEY1 (IF (IF X Y Z) AAAAX BBBBX)
         :KEY2 VAL2
         :KEY3 VAL3)

  If we make these names even longer we force the value off the line
  containing [47m:key1[0m:

    (MODIFY
         TH S
         :KEY1
         (IF (IF X Y Z) AAAAXXXXX BBBBXXXXX)
         :KEY2 VAL2
         :KEY3 VAL3)

  Here are some examples from the ACL2 source code, printed in 40
  characters:

    (DEFTHM
     ALPHORDER-ANTI-SYMMETRIC
     (IMPLIES (AND (NOT (CONSP X))
                   (NOT (CONSP Y))
                   (ALPHORDER X Y)
                   (ALPHORDER Y X))
              (EQUAL X Y))
     :HINTS
     ((\"Goal\"
       :IN-THEORY
       (UNION-THEORIES
        '(STRING< SYMBOL<)
        (DISABLE
           CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY))
       :USE
       ((:INSTANCE SYMBOL-EQUALITY (S1 X)
                   (S2 Y))
        (:INSTANCE BAD-ATOM<=-ANTISYMMETRIC)
        (:INSTANCE
             CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY
             (C Y))
        (:INSTANCE
             CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY
             (C X)))))
     :RULE-CLASSES
     ((:FORWARD-CHAINING
       :COROLLARY
       (IMPLIES
          (AND (ALPHORDER X Y)
               (NOT (CONSP X))
               (NOT (CONSP Y)))
          (IFF (ALPHORDER Y X) (EQUAL X Y)))
       :HINTS
       ((\"Goal\"
         :IN-THEORY (DISABLE ALPHORDER))))))

  Here is that same one, printed in a width of 60.

    (DEFTHM
     ALPHORDER-ANTI-SYMMETRIC
     (IMPLIES (AND (NOT (CONSP X))
                   (NOT (CONSP Y))
                   (ALPHORDER X Y)
                   (ALPHORDER Y X))
              (EQUAL X Y))
     :HINTS
     ((\"Goal\"
         :IN-THEORY
         (UNION-THEORIES
              '(STRING< SYMBOL<)
              (DISABLE CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY))
         :USE ((:INSTANCE SYMBOL-EQUALITY (S1 X)
                          (S2 Y))
               (:INSTANCE BAD-ATOM<=-ANTISYMMETRIC)
               (:INSTANCE CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY (C Y))
               (:INSTANCE CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY
                          (C X)))))
     :RULE-CLASSES
     ((:FORWARD-CHAINING
          :COROLLARY (IMPLIES (AND (ALPHORDER X Y)
                                   (NOT (CONSP X))
                                   (NOT (CONSP Y)))
                              (IFF (ALPHORDER Y X) (EQUAL X Y)))
          :HINTS ((\"Goal\" :IN-THEORY (DISABLE ALPHORDER))))))

  Just for comparison, here is the above printed in 60 columns by the
  old prettyprinter.

    (DEFTHM
     ALPHORDER-ANTI-SYMMETRIC
     (IMPLIES (AND (NOT (CONSP X))
                   (NOT (CONSP Y))
                   (ALPHORDER X Y)
                   (ALPHORDER Y X))
              (EQUAL X Y))
     :HINTS
     ((\"Goal\" :IN-THEORY
              (UNION-THEORIES
                   '(STRING< SYMBOL<)
                   (DISABLE CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY))
              :USE
              ((:INSTANCE SYMBOL-EQUALITY (S1 X)
                          (S2 Y))
               (:INSTANCE BAD-ATOM<=-ANTISYMMETRIC)
               (:INSTANCE CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY (C Y))
               (:INSTANCE CODE-CHAR-CHAR-CODE-IS-IDENTITY
                          (C X)))))
     :RULE-CLASSES
     ((:FORWARD-CHAINING
           :COROLLARY
           (IMPLIES (AND (ALPHORDER X Y)
                         (NOT (CONSP X))
                         (NOT (CONSP Y)))
                    (IFF (ALPHORDER Y X) (EQUAL X Y)))
           :HINTS
           ((\"Goal\" :IN-THEORY (DISABLE ALPHORDER))))))

  Of course, given that you cannot tell for sure whether the keywords
  you're seeing are part of a keyword/value parameter list or part of
  some constant containing random keywords, the prettyprinter can't
  solve the problem perfectly.  We just tried to make it work nicely
  on well-formed keyword/value parameter lists.

  For example, here is a form printed by each prettyprinter.

    Old:
    (MEMBER
     X
     '(:MONDAY
          :MON :TUESDAY :TUES :WEDNESDAY
          :WED :THURSDAY :THURS :FRIDAY
          :FRI :SATURDAY :SAT :SUNDAY :SUN))

    New:
    (MEMBER X
            '(:MONDAY :MON
                      :TUESDAY :TUES
                      :WEDNESDAY :WED
                      :THURSDAY :THURS
                      :FRIDAY :FRI
                      :SATURDAY :SAT
                      :SUNDAY :SUN))

  The new way is not how one would print it by hand!  But then, neither
  is the old way.")
 (NOTE-2-9-4
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.9.4 (February, 2006) Notes

  Also see [note-2-9-1], see [note-2-9-2], and see [note-2-9-3] for
  other changes since the last non-incremental release (Version_2.9).

  A soundness bug has been fixed that was due to inadequate checking of
  [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules in the presence of [47m[local][0m [events].  Specifically, a
  [47mlocal[0m [47m[defevaluator][0m event is insufficient for supporting a [47m:meta[0m
  rule (an example is shown in source function [47mchk-acceptable-rules[0m).
  Thanks to Dave Greve and Jared Davis for bringing this bug to our
  attention, by sending a proof of [47mnil[0m that exploited this bug.  The
  fix is to check legality of [47m:meta[0m rules even when skipping proofs
  during an [47m[include-book][0m event or the second pass of an
  [47m[encapsulate][0m event.

  Fixed problem with parallel make for workshop books by adding a
  dependency line to [47mbooks/workshops/2003/Makefile[0m.

  Default hints (see [set-default-hints]) no longer prevent the use of
  [47m:INSTRUCTIONS[0m (see [proof-builder]).  Thanks to Jared Davis for
  pointing out this problem.

  New functions [47m[remove-eq][0m and [47m[remove-equal][0m have been defined, in
  analogy to [47m[remove][0m.  These two symbols have also been added to
  [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.  Thanks to David Rager for pointing out that
  [47mremove-equal[0m was missing.  Moreover, the definitions of [47mdelete1-eq[0m
  and [47mdelete1-equal[0m have been eliminated.  Function [47mremove1-eq[0m, now
  in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode in source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m, serves in place of
  [47mdelete1-eq[0m, with corresponding new function definitions for
  [47m[remove1][0m and [47m[remove1-equal][0m.

  The symbol [47m[assert$][0m has been added to [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for the suggestion.

  Added SBCL support.  Thanks to Juho Snellman for significant
  assistance with the port.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for suggesting the
  use of feature [47m:acl2-mv-as-values[0m with SBCL, which can allow
  thread-level parallelism in the underlying lisp; we have done so
  when feature [47m:sb-thread[0m is present.

  We have continued to incorporate suggestions for wording improvements
  in documentation and error messages.  Thanks to all who send these
  suggestions, especially to Eric Smith, who has suggested the vast
  majority of them.

  Made a small improvement to errors and warnings caused on behalf of
  [set-enforce-redundancy], to indicate when an event of the same
  name already exists.

  Fixed a bug in [47mbooks/misc/rtl-untranslate.lisp[0m that was causing a
  guard violation when adding a new entry for an existing key.

  Fixed a bug in translation to internal form that caused [47m[defun-sk][0m
  and [47m[defchoose][0m to have difficulties handling ignored variables in
  [47m[let][0m forms.  Thanks to Sandip Ray for bringing this issue to our
  attention with a helpful example.

  The form [47m(push :acl2-mv-as-values *features*)[0m has been added in
  source file [47macl2-init.lisp[0m for SBCL and OpenMCL only, in order to
  support parallel execution (looking to the future...).

  Default-hints (see [set-default-hints]) were being ignored inside the
  [proof-builder], but no longer.  Thanks to John Erickson for
  bringing this problem to our attention and providing a simple
  example of it.

  Modified the [47mTAGS[0m [47m\"make\"[0m target (specifically, function [47mmake-tags[0m) so
  that it is gracefully skipped if the [47metags[0m program is not found.
  Thanks to David Rager for pointing out this issue.

  Sandip Ray has re-worked the supporting materials for his ACL2
  Workshop 2003 talk (originally with John Matthews and Mark Tuttle),
  to run in a few minutes.  The result is in
  [47mworkshops/2003/ray-matthews-tuttle/support/[0m and is included in the
  full ACL2 regression suite.  Thanks, Sandip.

  Debian releases of ACL2 had created superfluous [47m.cert.final[0m files
  when certifying books.  This has been fixed; thanks to Jared Davis
  for noticing this problem.

  Jared Davis has pointed out that ``If you add a [47m:backchain-limit-lst
  0[0m to a rewrite rule whose hypotheses are all forced, then ACL2
  `really assumes them' without trying to relieve them right there
  through rewriting.'' Relevant documentation has been added for
  [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m; see [rule-classes].

  A new version of the rtl library has been included in
  [47mbooks/rtl/rel5/[0m.  Thanks to David Russinoff for contributing hand
  proofs for the new lemmas, and to Matt Kaufmann for carrying out
  their mechanization.

  Fixed a bug in [47m[save-exec][0m that was failing to set the initial [47mcbd[0m
  according to the current directory when starting up ACL2.  Thanks
  to Camm Maguire for bringing our attention to this problem.

  Variables introduced during [47mlet*[0m abstraction are now in the current
  package.  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting such a change.  See
  [set-let*-abstractionp].

  It is now allowed for two definitions to be considered the same from
  the standpoint of redundancy (see [redundant-events]) when one
  specifies a [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m of [47mt[0m and the other has no explicit [47m:guard[0m
  (hence, the guard is implicitly [47mt[0m).  Thanks to Jared Davis for
  bringing this issue to our attention.

  (For users of [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m) There have been a few enhancements
  to distributed file [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m (skip this paragraph if you
  don't use that file):

      o [47mControl-t q[0m continues to compare windows ignoring whitespace, but
      now, a prefix argument can be given to control case is also
      ignored (ignore case if positive, else use case).

      o [47mControl-t Control-l[0m has been defined to be similar to [47mControl-t l[0m,
      except that proofs are skipped and output is suppressed.

      o [47mControl-t u[0m has been defined to print, to the shell buffer, a
      [47m:[0m[47m[ubt!][0m form for the command containing the cursor.

      o Control-t Control-f buries the current buffer.

      o [47mMeta-x new-shell[0m now puts the new shell buffer in [47mshell-mode[0m
      (thanks to David Rager for noticing this issue).

  Linear arithmetic has been modified so that we do not generate the
  equality [47m(equal term1 term2)[0m from the pair of inequalities [47m(<=
  term1 term2)[0m and [47m(<= term2 term1)[0m in the case that we would have to
  [47m[force][0m both [47mterm1[0m and [47mterm2[0m to be [47m[ACL2-numberp][0ms.  Thanks to Dave
  Greve for providing a motivating example and to Robert Krug for
  providing a fix.

  The event [47m[delete-include-book-dir][0m had not been allowed inside
  [books] and [47m[encapsulate][0m forms.  This was an oversight, and has
  been fixed.

  Sandip Ray has contributed a new library of books to support proofs
  of partial and total correctness of sequential programs based on
  assertional reasoning, in [47mbooks/symbolic/[0m.  This work is based on
  the paper J. Matthews, J S. Moore, S. Ray, and D. Vroon, ``A
  Symbolic Simulation Approach to Assertional Program Verification,''
  currently in draft form.  In particular, the books include the
  macro [47mdefsimulate[0m, which automatically transforms inductive
  assertion proofs of correctness of sequential programs to the
  corresponding interpreter proofs.  See the [47mREADME[0m in that
  directory.

  We have changed the implementation of [47m:dir :system[0m for [47m[ld][0m and
  [47m[include-book][0m.  This change will not affect you if you build an
  ACL2 executable in the normal manner, leaving in place the [47mbooks/[0m
  subdirectory of the source directory; nor will it affect you if you
  download a GCL Debian binary distribution.  The change is that if
  environment variable [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m is set, then it specifies
  the distributed books directory, i.e., the directory determined by
  [47m:dir :system[0m.  You may find it convenient to set this variable in
  your ACL2 script file (typically, [47msaved_acl2[0m).  If it is set when
  you build ACL2, the generated script for running ACL2 will begin by
  setting [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m to that value.  Thanks to various people
  who have discussed this issue, in particular Jared Davis who sent
  an email suggesting consideration of the use of an environment
  variable, and to Eric Smith who helped construct this paragraph.
  (Note that this use of [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m replaces the use of
  [47mACL2_SRC_BOOKS[0m described previously; see [note-2-9-1].)

  ACL2 now automatically deletes files [47mTMP*.lisp[0m created during the
  build process and created by [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m.  If you want these to be
  saved, evaluate [47m(assign keep-tmp-files t)[0m in the ACL2 loop or in
  raw Lisp.  The [47mclean[0m target for the standard `make' process for
  certifying books (see [books-certification-classic]) will however
  delete all files [47mTMP*.*[0m.

  The [47mTMP[0m files discussed just above now generally include the current
  process ID in their names, e.g., [47mTMP@16388@1.lisp[0m instead of
  [47mTMP1.lisp[0m.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for suggesting this measure, which
  will reduce the possibility that two different processes will
  attempt to access the same temporary file.

  Now, [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m will print the information formerly printed by [47m:pe![0m,
  slightly enhanced to work for logical names that are strings, not
  just symbols.  Thanks to Warren Hunt for leading us to this change
  by suggesting that [47m:pe nth[0m print the definition of [47m[nth][0m.

  We eliminated spurious warnings that could occur in raw mode in
  OpenMCL or CMUCL when [stobj]s are present.  We thank Juho Snellman
  for pointing out the relevant bug and appropriate fix.

  [47mMfc-rw[0m now takes a third argument that can specify an arbitrary known
  equivalence relation; see [extended-metafunctions].  Thanks to Dave
  Greve for discussions suggesting this improvement.

  A small modification to a symbol-reading function allows
  documentation string processing on Windows systems that use CR/LF
  for line breaks.  Thanks to William Cook for bringing this issue to
  our attention.

  The documentation has been improved on how to control the printing of
  ACL2 terms.  See [user-defined-functions-table].  Thanks to Sandip
  Ray for asking a question that led to the example presented there.

  We fixed an inefficiency that could cause an [47m[ld][0m command to seem to
  hang at its conclusion.  Thanks to Sandip Ray for pointing out this
  problem.

  We checked that ACL2 runs under LispWorks 4.4.5, and have inhibited
  redefinition warnings.

  Two changes have been made on behalf of congruence-based reasoning.
  Thanks to Dave Greve for examples and discussions that have led to
  these changes, and to Eric Smith and Vernon Austel, who also sent
  relevant examples.

      o When a call of the new unary function [47m[double-rewrite][0m is
      encountered by the rewriter, its argument will be rewritten
      twice.  This solves certain problems encountered in
      congruence-based rewriting.  Warnings for [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and
      [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules will suggest when calls of [47m[double-rewrite][0m on
      variables in hypotheses are likely to be a good idea.  See
      [double-rewrite].

      o Hypotheses of the form [47m(equiv var (double-rewrite term))[0m, where
      [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation and [47mvar[0m is a free
      variable (see [free-variables]), will bind [47mvar[0m to the result of
      rewriting [47mterm[0m twice.  Previously, hypotheses of the form
      [47m(equal var term)[0m would bind a free variable [47mvar[0m, but the call
      had to be of [47mequal[0m rather than of an arbitrary known
      equivalence relation.

  The following improvements were made in support of ACL2 on top of
  OpenMCL.

      o New versions of OpenMCL that do not have [47m:mcl[0m in Lisp variable
      [47m*features*[0m will now work with ACL2.  Thanks to David Rager for
      bringing this issue to our attention.

      o Added support for OpenMCL 1.0 for 64-bit DarwinPPC/MacOS X, thanks
      to Robert Krug.

      o Fixed tracing in OpenMCL so that the level is reset to 1 even if
      there has been an abort.

      o Added support in OpenMCL for [47mWET[0m.

      o Incorporated suggestions from Gary Byers for printing the ``Welcome
      to OpenMCL'' prompt before initially entering the ACL2 loop
      and, and for setting useful environment variable
      [47mCCL_DEFAULT_DIRECTORY[0m in the ACL2 script.

  Fixed a long-standing bug in forward-chaining, where variable-free
  hypotheses were being evaluated even if the
  [executable-counterpart]s of their function symbols had been
  disabled.  Thanks to Eric Smith for bringing this bug to our
  attention by sending a simple example that exhibited the problem.

  Improved reporting by the [break-rewrite] utility upon failure to
  relieve hypotheses in the presence of free variables, so that
  information is shown about the attempting bindings.  See
  [free-variables-examples-rewrite].  Thanks to Eric Smith for
  requesting this improvement.  Also improved the [break-rewrite]
  loop so that terms, in particular from unifying substitutions, are
  printed without hiding subterms by default.  The user can control
  such hiding (``evisceration''); see :DOC [47mset-brr-term-evisc-tuple[0m.

  A new directory [47mbooks/defexec/[0m contains books that illustrate the use
  of [47m[mbe][0m and [47m[defexec][0m.  Thanks to the contributors of those books
  (see the [47mREADME[0m file in that directory).

  The directories [47mbooks/rtl/rel2[0m and [47mbooks/rtl/rel3[0m are no longer
  distributed.  They are still available by email request.
  (Subdirectory [47mrel1/[0m supports some of the optional [47mworkshop/[0m books,
  so it is still distributed.)

  Added book [47mbooks/misc/sticky-disable.lisp[0m to manage [theories] that
  might otherwise be modified adversely by [47m[include-book][0m.  Thanks to
  Ray Richards for a query that led to our development of this tool.

  The commands [47m(exit)[0m and [47m(quit)[0m may now be used to quit ACL2 and Lisp
  completely; in fact they macroexpand to calls of the same function
  as does [47m[good-bye][0m (which is now a macro).  Thanks to Jared Davis
  for suggesting the new aliases.  (OpenMCL-only comment:) These all
  work for OpenMCL even inside the ACL2 loop.

  The macro [47mwet[0m now hides structure by default on large expressions.
  However, a new optional argument controls this behavior, for
  example avoiding such hiding if that argument is [47mnil[0m.  Thanks to
  Hanbing Liu for pointing out that [47mwet[0m was not helpful for very
  large terms.

  We have fixed a bug in the forward-chaining mechanism that, very
  rarely, could cause a proof to be aborted needlessly with an
  obscure error message.  Thanks to Jared Davis for sending us an
  example that evoked this bug.

  Fixed a bug that was causing proof output on behalf of
  [47m:functional-instance[0m to be confusing, because it failed to mention
  that the number of constraints may be different from the number of
  subgoals generated.  Thanks to Robert Krug for pointing out this
  confusing output.  The fix also causes the reporting of rules used
  when silently simplifying the constraints to create the subgoals.

  Fixed a bug in handling of leading [47m./[0m in pathnames, as in:
  [47m(include-book \"./foo\")[0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this
  bug to our attention.

  Made a small fix for handling of free variables of
  [47m:[0m[forward-chaining] rules, which had erroneously acted as though a
  hypothesis [47m(equal var term)[0m can bind the variable [47mvar[0m.

  A small change has been made for [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules for
  hypotheses of the form [47m(equal var term)[0m, where [47mvar[0m is a free
  variable and no variable of [47mterm[0m is free (see [free-variables]).
  As with [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules, we now bind [47mvar[0m to [47mterm[0m
  even if [47m(equal u term)[0m happens to be known in the current context
  for some term [47mu[0m.  Also as with [47m:rewrite[0m and [47m:linear[0m rules, similar
  handling is given to hypotheses [47m(equiv var (double-rewrite term))[0m
  where [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation.

  We changed the handling of free variables in hypotheses of [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m
  rules being handled by the [proof-builder]'s [47mrewrite[0m ([47mr[0m) command,
  in complete analogy to the change described just above for
  [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules.

  The installation instructions have been updated for obtaining GCL on
  a Macintosh.  Thanks to Robert Krug for supplying this information
  and to Camm Maguire for simplifying the process by eliminating the
  [47mgettext[0m dependency.

  The macro [47m[comp][0m is now an event, so it may be placed in [books].

  Previously, a [47m[save-exec][0m call could fail because of file permission
  issues, yet ACL2 (and the underlying Lisp) would quit anyhow.  This
  has been fixed.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for bringing this
  problem to our attention.

  Jared Davis, with assistance from David Rager, has updated his
  ordered sets library, [47mbooks/finite-set-theory/osets/[0m.  See file
  [47mCHANGES.html[0m in that directory.

  A new function, [47m[reset-kill-ring][0m, has been provided for the rare
  user who encounters memory limitations.  See [reset-kill-ring].")
 (NOTE-2-9-5
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "Changes in Version 3.0 since Version 2.9.4

  Fixed a bug in [47m[cw-gstack][0m that was causing a hard error when
  attempting to report on a forced assumption.  Thanks to Jared Davis
  for pointing this out and sending an example that helped us to
  determine a fix.

  Added [47m[set-backchain-limit][0m to the set of legal [events] that can be
  placed in [47m[encapsulate][0m forms and [books].  Thanks to John Cowles
  for bringing this issue to our attention.

  Fixed a bug that broke [47mwet[0m.  Thanks to David Rager for bringing this
  bug to our attention.

  Guard verification now evaluates ground subexpressions (those with no
  free variables) when computing the guard conjecture for the body of
  a function.  Thanks to Jared Davis for useful conversations leading
  to this change.  See [verify-guards], in particular its ``Note on
  computation of guard conjectures and evaluation'' near the end of
  that topic, for more details.

  Added a warning when a [47m[theory-invariant][0m is redefined.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for suggesting a warning in this case and providing an
  informative example.  Also, [47m[theory-invariant][0ms are now maintained
  more completely, as they are checked at the end of every event
  except for events executed on behalf of an [47m[include-book][0m or the
  second pass of an [47m[encapsulate][0m.

  Fixed the handling of runic designators to match their specification
  (see [theories]), so that disabling the name of a [47m[defthm][0m event
  [47m[disable][0ms all rules generated for that event.

  (For those who do numerous builds using feature [47m:acl2-mv-as-values[0m,
  currently only OpenMCL and multi-threaded SBCL by default:) You can
  speed up builds by adding the following parameter to `make', under
  conditions described in [47mGNUmakefile[0m: [47mUSE_ACL2_PROCLAIMS=:REUSE[0m.

  Arranged that traced functions (see [trace$]) are automatically
  untraced when events are undone (for example see [ubt]), at least
  for most underlying Common Lisp implementations.

  The macro [47m[defun-sk][0m now creates non-executable functions, which
  allows [47m[stobj][0ms to be used where they had previously been
  prohibited.  More generally, the user now has control over
  [47m[declare][0m forms to be used by the underlying [47m[defun][0m'd function;
  see [defun-sk].  Thanks to Sandip Ray for pointing out the need for
  such a modification.

  [47m:[0m[47m[Definition][0m rules are now treated, at least by default, as truly
  first-class definitions.  In particular, [47m:expand[0m [hints] use the
  latest [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rule by default.  You may specify
  [47m:install-body nil[0m to get the previous behavior of [47m:definition[0m
  rules; See [definition], and you may choose a previously-installed
  [47m:definition[0m rule to provide the current body; see [set-body].  Also
  see [rule-classes] for details of the [47m:install-body[0m field, and see
  [hints] to see a new [47m:with[0m directive for controlling expansion.
  The [47m:with[0m directive for [47m:expand[0m hints can even direct the use of a
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule for expansion!  Thanks to various people, including
  Sandip Ray and Rob Sumners, for discussions on the issue of the
  applicability of [47m:definition[0m rules for [47m:expand[0m [hints].

  [Constraint]s for functional instantiation now use the original
  definition rather than a simplified (``normalized'') version of it.

  Fixed a bug that caused the prompt to stay the same when
  guard-checking is off (see [set-guard-checking]) and raw-mode is
  changed (see [set-raw-mode]).

  Lemma names in directory [47mbooks/ordinals[0m have been changed by
  replacing [47m/\\[0m with [47m&[0m and replacing [47m\\/[0m with [47mV[0m.  We made this change
  because backslash is an escape character and hence disappears
  unless it is itself escaped.

  Fixed [proof-tree] output so that failed non-proof events do not
  cause the proof-tree to be re-printed.  Thus for example, if you
  have already advanced the checkpoint marker, it will not be reset
  by subsequent failed non-proof events.  Thanks to Pete Manolios and
  Peter Dillinger for bringing this bug to our attention.

  Fixed a bug that was preventing the printing of [stobj] fields as
  constants instead of numbers in certain cases.  (Note that this bug
  only affected printing, not soundness.) Thanks to Eric Smith for
  bringing this problem to our attention and providing the following
  example (which now works fine).

    (defstobj st fld1 fld2)
    (in-theory (disable update-nth))
    (defund run (st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st))) ;adding this didn't seem to help..
      st)

    ;works great; *fld1* prints as  *fld1*
    (thm (equal (update-nth *fld1* 'abc st)
                (car (cons x y))))

    ;*fld1* gets printed as 0, presumably because the call to run intervenes.
    (thm (equal (update-nth *fld1* 'abc (run st))
                (car (cons x y))))

  The macro [47m[progn][0m now allows the use of macros defined within its
  bodies even when at the event level, as illustrated by the
  following example.

    (progn (defmacro my-defun (&rest args)
             `(defun ,@args))
           (my-defun g (x) x))

  Thanks to Anna Slobodova for bringing this issue to our attention.  A
  related change is that all arguments of [47m[progn][0m must now be
  embedded event forms (see [embedded-event-form]), so use [47m[er-progn][0m
  instead if this is not the case.

  The change to [47m[progn][0m mentioned above also fixes a bug in handling
  [local] events inside a [47m[progn][0m that is inside an [47m[encapsulate][0m or
  in a book.  For example, the following form formerly caused an
  error.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (defun foo (x) x)
     (progn (local (defun bar (x) x))
            (defun abc (x) x)))

  We fixed two bugs in [47m:[0m[47m[puff][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[puff*][0m.  The first, brought to our
  attention by Eric Smith (who we thank), caused a cryptic error
  message when puffing a command with no subsidiary stored events;
  try, for example, [47m(encapsulate () (value-triple 3))[0m.  The second
  was due to a failure to restore the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m.  Suppose
  for example that we have certified the book [47mfoo.lisp[0m, which
  contains [47m([0m[47m[program][0m[47m)[0m followed by some definitions and/or theorems.
  Now suppose we start ACL2 and execute the following.

    (include-book \"foo\")
    (defthm test-thm
      (equal x x)
      :rule-classes nil)

  If we now execute [47m:[0m[47m[puff][0m[47m 1[0m, ACL2 will roll back the world to before
  the [47m[include-book][0m; then ``puff'' the include-book, which will
  leave us in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode; and finally skip re-execution of the
  [47m[defthm][0m because such [events] are skipped in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.  The
  fix is to re-install the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m immediately after
  the [47m[include-book][0m to its pre-[47m[include-book][0m value.

  A new event, [47m[make-event][0m, provides something like macros that take
  state.  For example, one can use it to put tests into certified
  books, do proof search, and generate new function names.  Many
  examples appear in directory [47mbooks/make-event/[0m.  See [make-event].
  Thanks to Bob Boyer and Jared Davis for useful feedback and to
  Warren Hunt, David Rager, and Sandip Ray for helpful discussions
  leading to some of the examples in directory [47mbooks/make-event/[0m.

  In support of [47m[make-event][0m, which is described in the preceding
  paragraph, [47mcertify-book[0m has a new keyword argument,
  [47m:save-expansion[0m, that controls whether the result of expanding
  [47m[make-event][0m forms is written out to a file.  See [certify-book];
  and for a discussion of book expansion files, see [make-event].

  We fixed a soundness bug that did not correctly detect [47m[local][0m
  events.  For example, the following event was admitted.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (local
      (encapsulate
       ()
       (local (progn (program))) ; or, (local (with-output :off summary (program)))
       (set-irrelevant-formals-ok t)
       (defun foo (x)
         (declare (xargs :measure (acl2-count x)))
         (1+ (foo x)))))
     (defthm inconsistent
       nil
       :hints ((\"Goal\" :use foo))
       :rule-classes nil))

  A new value for [guard] checking, [47m:none[0m, is now allowed.  If you
  execute [47m:[0m[47m[set-guard-checking][0m[47m :none[0m, then no guard checking will
  take place (but raw Lisp code will not be executed in this case).
  As a result, you should never see a guard violation, even for calls
  of [47m:[0m[47mprogram[0m mode functions.  We thank Pete Manolios, who has long
  wanted this feature, and also Peter Dillinger, for asking for it.
  New documentation explains the interaction between the [defun-mode]
  and the value supplied to [47m:[0m[47m[set-guard-checking][0m.  See
  [guard-evaluation-table], see [guard-evaluation-examples-script],
  and see [guard-evaluation-examples-log].

  In the course of adding the [guard]-checking value [47m:none[0m described in
  the paragraph above, we eliminated an optimization that eliminated
  guard checking for some recursive calls of [47m:[0m[logic] mode
  mutually-recursive functions that have not had their guards
  verified.  But we doubt that this change will be noticed by any
  users!)

  The ACL2 hyper-card has been enhanced, thanks to David Rager, with a
  listing of ``Useful EMACS Commands'' to match comments in
  [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m.

  Users contributed books following the [47mReadme.lsp[0m methodology:
  [47mdata-structures/memories[0m and [47municode[0m (Jared Davis), [47mproofstyles[0m
  (Sandip Ray and J Moore).

  Made some improvements to [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m (a file discussed
  elsewhere; see [books-certification-classic]).  In particular,
  improved handling of [47m.acl2[0m files for [47mdependencies[0m target.

  (Only OpenMCL and, with feature [47m:acl2-mv-as-values[0m, GCL) Fixed a bug
  that was causing proclaiming to fail when definitions are submitted
  interactively.

  The default stack size has been increased for several lisps.

  (Very technical) A restriction has been weakened on the use of
  [47m[local][0m [stobj]s under a call of an ACL2 evaluator ([47mtrans-eval[0m or
  [47msimple-translate-and-eval[0m).  Now, the error can only take place for
  [47m[stobj][0m names that occur in the term being evaluated.  Thanks to
  Erik Reeber for bringing this issue to our attention.

  The notion of ``ancestor'' has been changed slightly.  This notion is
  used by extended metafunctions and [break-rewrite] (see
  [extended-metafunctions] and see [brr@]), and also with backchain
  limits (see [backchain-limit] and see [set-backchain-limit]).
  Basically, each time a hypothesis is encountered during application
  of a [rewrite] rule, that hypothesis is pushed (after instantiating
  and negating) onto the current list of ancestors before it is
  rewritten.  However, hypotheses of the form [47m(equal var term)[0m, where
  [47mvar[0m is free (see [free-variables]), had not been included in the
  ancestors (similarly for [47m(equiv var (double-rewrite term))[0m where
  [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation).  Now such ``binding
  hypotheses'' are included in a special way in ancestors data
  structures.  In particular, [47m(null (mfc-ancestors mfc))[0m will now be
  true if and only if the term being rewritten is part of the current
  goal as opposed to a hypothesis from a rule encountered during
  backchaining, even if that hypothesis is a binding hypothesis.
  Thanks to Dave Greve for bringing this issue to our attention.

  Termination and induction analysis now continue through both
  arguments of [47m[prog2$][0m, not just the second.  (Normally, the
  gathering up of [47m[if][0m tests stops at function calls; but it
  continued through the second argument of [47m[prog2$][0m, and now it will
  continue through both arguments.)  Thanks to Sol Swords for
  discussion leading to this change.

  The ACL2 distribution is now kept on the http server rather than the
  ftp server (but the home page has not been moved).  Thanks to
  Robert Krug for letting us know that some ACL2 users have found it
  inconvenient to fetch ACL2 using ftp.

  The file [47mbooks/README.html[0m has been renamed to [47mbooks/Readme.html[0m,
  since some browsers don't show the former in the directory listing.")
 (NOTE-2-9{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 2.9(r) (October, 2004) Notes

  No changes have been made for support of non-standard analysis, other
  than a minor modification or two in [47mbooks/nonstd/[0m books.

  Please also see [note-2-9] for changes to Version_2.9 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-3-0
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.0 (June, 2006) Notes

  Please see [note-2-9-5] for a description of changes since Version
  2.9.4.  These include the new [47m[make-event][0m feature, a soundness bug
  fix, an improvement for [47m:expand[0m [hints], evaluation in the logic by
  way of [47m:[0m[47m[set-guard-checking][0m[47m :none[0m, and many other improvements.

  More generally, there have been several incremental releases since
  Version 2.9: see [note-2-9-1], see [note-2-9-2], see [note-2-9-3],
  see [note-2-9-4], and see [note-2-9-5].

  A very few users have contributed books following the instructions on
  the web.  We expect that when more contributions come in, we will
  give more attention to the question of how to organize the
  distributed and workshop books.  For now, we have simply added the
  new contributions according to the old-style distribution
  methodology.")
 (NOTE-3-0-1
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.0.1 (August, 2006) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  documentation has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Fixed a soundness bug, introduced in the previous release, due to a
  failure to disallow [47m[table][0m [events] that set the
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m in a [47m[local][0m context.  Here is a proof of [47mnil[0m
  that exploits the bug.

    (encapsulate ()
     (local (program))
     (defun foo ()
       (declare (xargs :measure 17))
       (+ 1 (foo))))
    (thm
     nil
     :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable foo (foo)) :use foo)))

  Fixed a bug in the alternatives to [47m[good-bye][0m, which are the [47m[exit][0m
  and [47m[quit][0m commands.  Thanks to Jared Davis and Peter Dillinger for
  pointing this out right away.

  The definition of [47m[len][0m has been highly optimized in raw Lisp.
  Thanks to Bob Boyer and Warren Hunt for suggesting such an
  improvement and providing a lot of help in coming up with the
  current implementation.

  The clause subsumption algorithms have been improved, both to improve
  efficiency during warnings for [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules and to punt when
  the subsumption computation for induction appears to be blowing up.
  Thanks to Robert Krug for bringing this issue to our attention and
  supplying a useful example.

  A bug has been fixed that prevented [47m[time$][0m from working properly in
  OpenMCL and multi-threaded SBCL (actually, in any ACL2 image where
  feature [47m:acl2-mv-as-values[0m is present).  Thanks to Sol Swords for
  bringing this problem to our attention.

  A [type-spec] of the form [47m(satisfies pred)[0m carries the requirement
  that [47mpred[0m be a unary function symbol in the current ACL2 [world];
  otherwise, it is illegal.  Thanks to Dave Greve for pointing out
  that Common Lisp has this requirement.

  Installed a fix provided by Gary Byers (for ACL2 source function
  [47minstall-new-raw-prompt[0m), for OpenMCL, that fixes an issue exposed
  in some versions of OpenMCL when compiler optimization is off.

  Fixed a bug in contributed book [47mmisc/untranslate-patterns.lisp[0m that
  was causing calls of [47madd-untranslate-pattern[0m to be rejected in
  [books].  Thanks to Ray Richards for pointing out this bug and to
  Jared Davis for assisting in the fix.

  Fixed a bug in [47m[defstobj][0m when keywords [47m:initially[0m and [47m:resizable[0m are
  both supplied.  In this case, the definition of the resizing
  function mistakenly failed to quote the [47m:initially[0m value, even
  though this value is not to be evaluated.  One could even get an
  error in this case, as in the following example supplied by Erik
  Reeber, whom we thank for bringing this bug to our attention:

    (defstobj $test
      (test-x :type (array t (5)) :initially (0) :resizable t))

  A new feature, [47m[with-prover-time-limit][0m, allows the setting of time
  limits during proofs.  This is [31;1mnot[0m a general-purpose time-limit
  utility, nor is it guaranteed to implement a strict bound; it only
  attempts to limit time approximately during proofs.  Thanks to Pete
  Manolios and Daron Vroon, who made the most recent request for such
  a feature, and to Robert Krug for a helpful discussion.

  (GCL only) Fixed a bug in the procedure for building a profiling
  image.  Thanks to Sol Swords for bringing this bug to our attention
  and to Eric Smith for bringing a subsequent problem to our
  attention.

  Handling of [theories] can now use significantly less time and space.
  A regression suite run took about 25% longer before this change
  than it did after making this change (and also the ones in the next
  two paragraphs).  Thanks to Vernon Austel for bringing this issue
  to our attention and for supplying code, quite some time ago, that
  provided detailed, useful implementation suggestions.  Also thanks
  to the folks at Rockwell Collins, Inc. for pushing the limits of
  the existing implementation, thus encouraging this improvement.

  Fixed a performance bug in obtaining executable counterpart symbols.

  We now avoid certain computations made on behalf of warnings, when
  such warnings are disabled.

  We have relaxed the checks made when including an uncertified book,
  to match the checks made when including a certified book.  Thanks
  to Eric Smith for suggesting this change.

  Fixed a bug in [47m:[0m[47m[pso][0m (see set-saved-output) that caused an error
  when printing the time summary.

  Made fixes to avoid potential hard Lisp errors caused by the use of
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions.  The fix was to use a ``safe mode,''
  already in use to prevent such errors during macroexpansion; see
  [guards-and-evaluation].  However, such errors were possible during
  evaluation of macro [guard]s, for example as follows:

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (car x))
    (defmacro mac (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (foo 3)))
      x)
    (defun g (x)
      (mac x))

  A similar issue existed for calls of [47m[defpkg][0m, [47m[in-theory][0m, [47m[table][0m,
  [47m[make-event][0m, and [47mvalue-triple[0m, but has been fixed for all but
  [47min-theory[0m and [47mmake-event[0m, where technical issues have caused us to
  defer this change.

  Fixed a bug in [47mwet[0m that caused problems in OpenMCL, and perhaps other
  Lisp implementations, when the argument to [47mwet[0m calls, or depends
  on, certain built-ins including [47m[prog2$][0m, [47m[time$][0m, [47m[mbe][0m, and
  [47m[must-be-equal][0m.  Thanks to David Rager for bringing this problem
  to our attention.

  The file [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m has been improved so that when book
  certification fails with `make', the failure message contains the
  book filename.

  Documentation has been written to explain how to avoid an expensive
  immediate rewrite of the result of applying a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m
  rule.  See [meta].  Thanks to Robert Krug for supplying this trick,
  and to Eric Smith and Dave Greve for useful discussions.

  (OpenMCL only) OpenMCL-based ACL2 image names formerly had extension
  [47m\".dppccl\"[0m, which was correct only for some platforms (including
  32-bit Darwin PPC).  That has been fixed, thanks to a suggestion
  from Gary Byers.

  It is now legal to attach both a [47m:use[0m and a [47m:cases[0m hint at the same
  goal.  Thanks to Eric Smith for (most recently) requesting this
  feature.

  It is now permissible to include the same symbol more than once in
  the imports list of a [47m[defpkg][0m form (i.e., its second argument).
  Also, the evaluation of [47m[defpkg][0m forms with long import lists now
  uses a reasonably efficient sorting routine to check for two
  different symbols with the same name (see also
  [47mbooks/misc/sort-symbols.lisp[0m).  If you currently call a function
  like [47mremove-duplicates-eql[0m for your imports list, as had been
  suggested by a [47m[defpkg][0m error message, then you may experience some
  speed-up by removing that call.  Thanks to Eric Smith for helping
  to discover this issue through profiling.

  Made miscellaneous efficiency improvements not listed above (for
  example, following a suggestion of Eric Smith to avoid checking for
  so-called ``bad Lisp objects'' during [47m[include-book][0m, which saved
  almost 3% in time on one large example).

  Modified the notion of ``untouchable'' to separate the notion of
  untouchable functions and macros from the notion of untouchable
  state global variables.  See [push-untouchable].  Thanks to Bob
  Boyer for sending an example, [47m(put-global 'ld-evisc-tuple t state)[0m,
  that suggested to us the need for more restrictive handling of
  untouchables.  In particular, many [47mld[0m specials (see [ld]) are now
  untouchable.  You may be able to work around this restriction by
  calling [47m[ld][0m; see for example the change to
  [47mbooks/misc/expander.lisp[0m.  But please contact the ACL2 implementors
  if this sort of workaround does not appear to be sufficient for
  your purposes.

  Fixed a bug in function [47mset-standard-oi[0m (see [standard-oi]).

  Fixed a bug in the use of [47m[ld-evisc-tuple][0m.  The bad behavior was an
  improper use of the print-level and print-length components of the
  tuple (specifically, taking its [47m[caddr][0m and [47m[cadddr][0m instead of
  taking its [47m[cadr][0m and [47m[caddr][0m).  Thanks to Bob Boyer for bringing
  this bug to our attention.

  A new argument to the [47mcompile-flg[0m argument of [47m[certify-book][0m, [47m:all[0m,
  causes creation of a file to be compiled in place of the given
  book, where that file contains not only a copy of the book (with
  [47m[make-event][0m forms expanded) but also contains definitions of the
  so-called ``executable counterparts'' of the functions defined in
  the book.  Then, functions defined in the book will be run compiled
  when including the book, even for functions whose [guard]s have not
  been verified, or are in [47m:program[0m mode and running in so-called
  ``safe mode'' (for example, during expansion of macros).  The
  default behavior, value [47mt[0m of [47mcompile-flg[0m, is unchanged.  Moreover,
  a new [47m:comp![0m argument of [47m[include-book][0m now compiles the executable
  counterparts when creating the book's compiled file, and unlike
  [47m:comp[0m, always deletes the old compiled file first so that one
  always gets a fresh compile.

  Now, [47m[certify-book][0m gives a \"Guards\" warning only for [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode
  functions that are defined in the given book but have not had their
  guards verified.  Previously, it also warned about such functions
  that were defined in the certification world or in sub-books.

  A new command, [47m[redo-flat][0m, facilitates the debugging of failed
  [47m[encapsulate][0m and [47m[progn][0m forms by evaluating preliminary forms in
  order to leave one at the point of failure.  See [redo-flat].
  Thanks to Ray Richards and others for asking for such a utility,
  and to Sandip Ray for useful discussions.

  We have changed the automatic declaration of function types (still
  done in GCL and OpenMCL only, for now).  Our motivation was to
  avoid the assumption that Common Lisp functions return one value
  when ACL2 says that they do; thanks to Bob Boyer for bringing this
  issue to our attention with the example of defining [47m(foo x y)[0m to be
  [47m(floor x y)[0m.  ACL2 was saying that [47mfoo[0m returns a single value, but
  because [47mfloor[0m returns two values in raw Lisp, so does [47mfoo[0m.  Other
  changes to automatic declaration include comprehending [47m[defund][0m,
  not just [47m[defun][0m.

  A new function, [47m[mod-expt][0m, computes [47m(mod (expt base exp) m)[0m, and
  does so efficiently in some implementations (currently only in GCL
  2.7.0, which is not yet released).  Thanks to Warren Hunt for
  suggesting such an addition.

  New functions [47m[getenv$][0m and [47m[setenv$][0m have been made available for
  reading and writing environment variables.  Thanks to Jun Sawada
  for requesting these utilities.

  The query utility [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m has been improved in several ways.  As
  before, [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules are only printed if the argument is a symbol;
  but the information printed for them is now more appropriate.  The
  following are changes for the case that the argument is not a
  symbol, but rather, a term.  (1) Rules are displayed that have
  [equivalence] relations other than [47m[equal][0m.  (2) All matching
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rules are displayed, where previously [47m:definition[0m
  rules were only shown if they were ``simple'' rules (sometimes
  known as ``abbreviations''); see [simple].  (3) The ``Equiv'' field
  is printed for terms, not just symbols.  (4) The substitution is
  shown that, when applied to the left-hand side of the rule, will
  yield the specified term.  Thanks to Eric Smith for suggesting
  these changes.

  The [proof-builder] command [47m;show-rewrites[0m has been improved to match
  the changes described above for [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m.  In particular,
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rules that are not ``[simple]'' are now displayed by
  the [proof-builder]'s [47mshow-rewrites[0m (and [47msr[0m) command, and the
  [proof-builder]'s [47mrewrite[0m command has been correspondingly modified
  to accept these [47m:definition[0m rules.

  Fixed `make' targets [47mcopy-distribution[0m, [47mcopy-workshops[0m, and
  [47mcopy-nonstd[0m so that they should also work for non-developers.

  Fixed a bug that was causing [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m to display [47m[syntaxp][0m hypotheses
  oddly in some cases, in particular [47m(syntaxp (let ...))[0m.  (The
  problem was in the ``untranslate'' display of the internal form of
  [47msyntaxp[0m calls.)  Thanks to Robert Krug for bringing this problem to
  our attention.  We also removed the restriction on [47m[bind-free][0m that
  its argument could not be a variable, a constant, or (more
  interestingly) a [47m[lambda][0m application (i.e., a [47m[let][0m or [47m[mv-let][0m
  expression).")
 (NOTE-3-0-1{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.0.1(r) (August, 2006) Notes

  No significant changes have been made since Version 3.0 for support
  of non-standard analysis in particular.

  Please also see [note-3-0-1] for changes to Version 3.0.1 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-3-0-2
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.0.2 (December, 2006) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  documentation has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Fixed soundness bugs in the handling of primitive function
  [47m[pkg-witness][0m, and improved its documentation.  (The executable
  counterpart returned an incorrect default value, and the axiom
  [47msymbol-package-name-pkg-witness-name[0m needed [47mpkg-name[0m to be other
  than [47m\"\"[0m in order to avoid the default value of \"ACL2\".)  As
  fallout, a new built-in [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m rule,
  [47msymbol-package-name-of-symbol-is-not-empty-string[0m, now asserts that
  the [47m[symbol-package-name][0m of a symbol is never [47m\"\"[0m.  Thanks to Mike
  Gordon for bringing these soundness bugs to our attention by
  attempting to prove translations of ACL2 axioms in HOL4.

  Fixed a soundness bug in linear arithmetic, due to incomplete
  tracking of forced assumptions while deriving inequalities.  Thanks
  to Robert Krug for providing a fix and a proof of [47mnil[0m before the
  fix.

  Fixed a soundness bug in the redundancy criterion for [47m[defun][0m events,
  which has been modified; see [redundant-events].  This bug is
  illustrated below.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger and Jared Davis for
  contributions to an email thread that led us to discover this bug.
  The solution is that for a definition to be redundant with an
  earlier definition, ACL2 no longer ignores [47m:[0m[47m[measure][0m [47m[xargs][0m
  except when skipping proofs (e.g., during [47m[include-book][0m).
  However, a new ``measure'', [47m(:? v1 ... vk)[0m, is supported, for
  specifying a measured subset of the set of formals, i.e., a set of
  formals that serves as the set of parameters for some valid
  measure.

    (encapsulate
     ()

     (local (defun foo (x y)
              (declare (xargs :measure (acl2-count y)))
              (if (and (consp x) (consp y))
                  (foo (cons x x) (cdr y))
                y)))

    ; So the following is redundant -- but it guesses a measure
    ; of (acl2-count x), which isn't right!
     (defun foo (x y)
       (if (and (consp x) (consp y))
           (foo (cons x x) (cdr y))
         y)))

    ; end of encapsulate

    ; Now we prove a non-theorem by exploiting the bug above,
    ; erroneously replacing formal y by a constant in the induction
    ; scheme hinted below.  (This should not be allowed, as y should be
    ; labeled as a measured formal.)

    (defthm bad
      (atom x)
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :induct (foo x '(3)))))

    ; It's easy to get a contradiction by instantiating the
    ; non-theorem just above.
    (defthm contradiction
      nil
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use ((:instance bad (x '(7)))))))

  Fixed a bug in [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m and the [proof-builder]'s [47mshow-rewrites[0m ([47msr[0m)
  command that was causing a Lisp break.  For [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m, also improved
  the display of unifying substitutions, modified output to take
  binding hypotheses [47m(equal var term)[0m into account properly, and
  arranged for inclusion of [meta] rules that modify the given term.
  Thanks to Eric Smith for bringing these issues to our attention.

  Introduced new utilities for undoing [command]s, [47m:[0m[47m[ubu][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[ubu!][0m,
  which are analogous to [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[ubt!][0m (respectively) except
  that they only undo back up to, but not including, the indicated
  command.

  Fixed a performance bug, pointed out by Eric Smith, that was negating
  efforts made for the preceding release to avoid computation for
  disabled warnings.

  Added [47m[time$][0m and [47mvalue-triple[0m to [47m*acl2-exports*[0m.  Thanks to Bob
  Boyer and Erik Reeber (respectively) for bringing these issues to
  our attention.

  Improved the automatic proclaiming of function types for GCL and
  OpenMCL, specifically to use an output format consistent with the
  Common Lisp spec.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for bringing this issue to
  our attention.

  Added [47mbooks/misc/transfinite.lisp[0m, which deals with transfinite
  induction in ACL2.  Thanks to Eric Smith for contributing this
  book.

  Added [47mbooks/misc/process-book-readme.lisp[0m to the distribution.
  Thanks to Sandip Ray for pointing out its omission.

  Added contributions [47mbooks/concurrent-programs/bakery/[0m and
  [47mbooks/concurrent-programs/german-protocol/[0m.  These contributions
  can be used as tutorials, especially by new ACL2 users, for
  learning how to model concurrent protocols in ACL2 and the steps
  involved in reasoning about their correctness.  Thanks to Sandip
  Ray for these contributions.  See the [47mReadme.lsp[0m files in these
  directories.

  Theory invariants may now involve the variable [47mENS[0m instead of the
  variable [47mTHEORY[0m.  The practical effect of this change is that any
  expression of the form [47m(MEMBER-EQUAL rune THEORY)[0m occurring in a
  [47m[theory-invariant][0m expression should be replaced by [47m(ACTIVE-RUNEP
  rune)[0m.  See [theory-invariant].  Thanks to Eric Smith and Dave
  Greve for pointing out an inefficiency in the handling of theory
  invariants that led to this change, which can speed up their
  handling by orders of magnitude on large examples, and to Eric for
  testing this change and pointing out problems with an early
  implementation of it.

  Theory invariants (see [theory-invariant]) are no longer checked on
  theories defined by [47m[deftheory][0m [events].  After all, one can
  define a theory with [47mdeftheory[0m that is not intended to be used as
  the current theory, but rather is intended to be combined with
  other [theories] (see [theory-functions]).  Thanks to Eric Smith
  for bringing this issue to our attention.

  [47m[Theory-invariant][0m errors had been reported with very little detail
  when warnings were inhibited.  This problem has been fixed; thanks
  to Eric Smith for bringing it to our attention and providing an
  example.  We have also improved the handling of redundancy for
  [47m[theory-invariant][0m [events].

  The macro [47m[defun-sk][0m now has a new optional keyword, [47mrewrite[0m, that
  can be used to change the form of the [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule generated
  when the quantifier is [47m[forall][0m.  Thanks to Eric Smith and Sandip
  Ray for useful discussions on this topic.  We have also slightly
  modified the [hints] for the [47m[defthm][0m event underneath a [47mdefun-sk[0m
  in order to make the proof more reliably efficient.

  A new event, [47m[reset-prehistory][0m, allows setting of a barrier before
  which undoing is illegal.  An argument to this macro allows the
  barrier to be made permanent; otherwise, it can be removed with
  [47m:[0m[47m[ubt-prehistory][0m.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for useful
  conversations leading to the addition of [47m[reset-prehistory][0m.

  A new query, [47m([0m[47m[wormhole-p][0m[47m [0m[47m[state][0m[47m)[0m, allows users to determine
  whether or not they are in a [47m[wormhole][0m.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger
  for providing this utility.

  [47mValue-triple[0m no longer evaluates its form during [47m[include-book][0m, and
  in raw Lisp its calls trivially macroexpand to [47mnil[0m, without any
  consideration of its argument.  This change avoids errors and
  warnings when [stobj] names occur in the argument.

  We fixed what could be considered a soundness hole that could occur
  by exploiting redefinition in a particular way.  Thanks to Peter
  Dillinger for raising a question that led to discovery of this
  hole.

  A bug has been fixed in handling of illegal [theory] expressions.
  Thanks to Eric Smith, who reported this problem and provided the
  example [47m(in-theory '((:definition natp) (:rewrite doesntexist)))[0m to
  show how a hard error could occur.

  Improved error reporting by [47m[certify-book][0m when the certification
  [world] contains inadmissible forms.

  Modified [47m[defchoose][0m to add two new keyword arguments.  There is now
  a [47m:doc[0m keyword argument; previously, an optional documentation
  string (see [47mdoc-string[0m) was to be placed just before the body,
  without a keyword.  There is also a [47m:strengthen[0m argument that
  strengthens the axiom added, which allows for the definition of
  ``fixing'' functions for equivalence relations that choose
  canonical representatives of equivalence classes.  See [defchoose].
  Thanks for Dave Greve for useful discussions that led us to this
  [47m:strengthen[0m enhancement.

  Added [47mbooks/misc/bash.lisp[0m, which provides utilities for simplifying
  a goal into a list of subgoals (as documented at the top of that
  file).  Thanks to Dave Greve for requesting this utility and
  suggesting refinements to its functionality, which have been
  incorporated.

  (For Emacs users only) The command [47mmeta-x new-shell[0m provided by file
  [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m now puts you in shell-mode, which for example
  supports directory tracking.  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting
  this change.

  Fixed some mishandling of [stobj]s by [47m[make-event][0m expansion.

  Introduced a new event, [47m[defttag][0m, that introduces a ``trust tag''
  (``ttag'') allowing for extensions of ACL2 and for the use of
  generally unsafe ACL2 constructs.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger,
  Sandip Ray, and Erik Reeber for useful discussions on [47mdefttag[0m and
  the following related items.

      A new event, [47m[remove-untouchable][0m, can be used to give users access
      to system functions and data structures.  We also fixed a bug
      in [47m[push-untouchable][0m; and, it no longer is a no-op in
      [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for proposing
      [47m[remove-untouchable][0m and suggesting that it and
      [47m[push-untouchable][0m be functional in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.

      Raw-mode (see [set-raw-mode]) no longer disables [47m[certify-book][0m.
      However, [47m[set-raw-mode][0m is now disallowed unless there is an
      active ttag (see [defttag]).  If you want to execute
      [47m([0m[47m[set-raw-mode][0m[47m t)[0m and there is no active ttag, consider
      executing [47m([0m[47m[set-raw-mode-on!][0m[47m)[0m instead.

      Redefinition of system functions is disallowed unless there is an
      active ttag.  However, [47m[redef!][0m now introduces [47m(defttag
      :redef!)[0m in order to allow redefinition of system functions.

      A new event, [47m[progn!][0m, is a legal embedded event form that can go in
      [books] and both [47m[encapsulate][0m and [47m[progn][0m forms (see
      [embedded-event-form]), and is similar to [47m[progn][0m except that
      it allows arbitrary forms.  Thus, a [47m[progn!][0m form is
      potentially dangerous and can only be evaluated if there is an
      active ttag.

      See [ttags-seen] for information about how to find the ttags known in
      the current ACL2 [world], and for related caveats.

      A new book created with Peter Dillinger, [47mbooks/misc/hacker.lisp[0m
      (added after Version_3.3: now [47mbooks/hacking/hacker.lisp[0m), uses
      [47m[progn!][0m to define utilities [47mwith-raw-mode[0m and
      [47mwith-redef-allowed[0m, which respectively allow raw Lisp
      evaluation and redefinition to take place within a certifiable
      book (!).

  Macro [47m[with-output][0m is no longer allowed in function bodies because
  it does not have (and has never had) any effect in raw Lisp.  See
  [with-output] for a workaround.

  Fixed a bug in redundancy of [47m[defstobj][0m in raw Lisp, which caused an
  error when certifying a book with a redundant [47m[defstobj][0m event
  whose [47m[stobj][0m had already been modified.  Here is an example:

    (defstobj st fld)
    (update-fld 3 st)
    (certify-book \"foo\" 1) ; where foo.lisp contains (defstobj st fld)

  New books illustrating [47m[make-event][0m have been contributed in
  directory [47mbooks/make-event/[0m: [47mdotimes.lisp[0m (David Rager),
  [47mstobj-test.lisp[0m, and [47mlogical-tangent.lisp[0m (Peter Dillinger).

  Modified [47mprint-object$[0m (see [io]) so that it no longer prints an
  extra space at the end.

  Replaced the ``draconian restriction to avoid capture'' that had
  prevented some [47m:functional-instance[0m [hints] from being legal.  The
  corresponding check now only requires that no variable free in the
  functional substitution is captured by a [47m[let][0m or [47m[mv-let][0m (or
  [47m[lambda][0m) binding.  See [lemma-instance].

  Added new extended metafunction, [47mmfc-rw+[0m, which is equivalent to
  [47mmfc-rw[0m except that it takes an alist argument, which may be useful
  for efficiency.  See [extended-metafunctions].  Thanks to Robert
  Krug for suggesting this more efficient variant of [47mmfc-rw[0m.

  Added support for the [47mignorable[0m [47m[declare][0m form.

  We now cause an error on a call of [47mopen-input-channel[0m (see [io]) with
  an argument string whose first character is the [47m|[0m character.
  Thanks to Bob Boyer for providing an example (several months ago)
  showing the danger of such calls, namely that the following command
  would log you out and kill all of your processes when running on
  top of GCL in Linux:
  [47m(open-input-channel \"|kill -9 -1\" :object state)[0m

  Restricted the use of [47m[make-event][0m to contexts in which it can be
  tracked properly, under legal [events] (see [embedded-event-form]).
  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for bringing an example to our attention
  that led to this fix.

  Fixed a bug that was avoiding [guard]-checking for the functions
  [47m[compress1][0m and [47m[compress2][0m.  Thanks to David Rager for bringing
  this bug to our attention.

  Added an error message when a [47m[defun][0m or [47m[mutual-recursion][0m event
  fails, to clarify whether failure is for the [measure] conjecture
  or for the [guard] conjecture.  Thanks to David Rager for
  requesting clarification for such failures.

  Fixed a bug in reporting of [guard] violations (hard Lisp error) when
  certain macros (for example, [47m[cond][0m) are used in the [guard].
  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this problem to our attention
  and providing assistance with the solution, in particular by
  providing a helpful example.

  Grant Passmore has contributed a resolution/paramodulation prover
  written in ACL2, in directory [47mbooks/deduction/passmore/[0m.  Thanks,
  Grant.

  Improved the error message when illegal theories are encountered.

  Improved the suppression of output for [47minhibit-output[0m arguments of
  routines in the book [47mbooks/misc/expander.lisp[0m.  Thanks to Qiang
  Zhang for pointing out the possibility for improvement here.

  Added a new directory [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/extra/[0m that extends
  [47mbooks/arithmetic-3[0m with additional rules, contributed by Alex
  Spiridonov with guidance from Robert Krug.  WARNING: This directory
  is under development.  It may undergo large changes in future
  releases, so please consider it experimental and subject to change.
  Feedback is welcomed.

  As part of the work mentioned just above, Robert Krug and Alex
  Spiridonov contributed improvements to [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/[0m:

      o A new rule [47m|(* (/ x) (/ (expt x n)))|[0m in [47mbind-free/collect.lisp[0m,
      which is important for reducing [47mcollect-*[0m expressions though it
      slowed down one proof (see comment above this rule in
      [47mbind-free/collect.lisp[0m).

      o Slight improvements of rules [47mintegerp-mod[0m and [47mrationalp-mod[0m in
      [47mfloor-mod/floor-mod.lisp[0m.

      o To avoid conflict with [47mbooks/rtl/rel6/arithmetic/[0m, renamed rule
      [47mmod-minus[0m to [47mmod-neg[0m in [47mfloor-mod/floor-mod.lisp[0m, and renamed
      [47mintegerp-+-reduce-leading-constant[0m to
      [47mintegerp-+-reduce-leading-rational-constant[0m in
      [47mbind-free/integerp.lisp[0m.

  (GCL on Windows only) Made a low-level change to avoid multiplying
  stacks for GCL on Windows, since GCL 2.6.6 broke while doing this.

  Fixed bugs in linear arithmetic (rarely evidenced, it seems)
  involving using [47m<[0m to compare complex rational constants.  Thanks to
  Robert Krug for helping with the fixes.

  Added a new event, [47m[assert-event][0m, for checking that forms evaluate
  to non-[47mnil[0m values.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for suggesting and
  collaborating on this addition.")
 (NOTE-3-0{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.0(r) (June, 2006) Notes

  No significant changes have been made since Version 2.9 for support
  of non-standard analysis in particular.

  Please also see [note-3-0] for changes to Version 3.0 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-3-1
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.1 (December, 2006) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  documentation has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Please see [note-3-0-2] for a description of changes since Version
  3.0.1, and also see [note-3-0-1] for additional changes since
  Version 3.0.")
 (NOTE-3-1{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.1(r) (December, 2006) Notes

  No significant changes have been made since Version 3.0 for support
  of non-standard analysis in particular.

  Please also see [note-3-1] for changes to Version 3.1 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-3-2
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.2 (April, 2007) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Before this release, a raw Lisp error could put the ACL2 user into
  the debugger of the host Common Lisp.  Now, such cases will
  generally put the user back at the top-level loop after an
  informative message.  For details, see [set-debugger-enable]; also
  see [break$].

  Fixed a soundness bug that was allowing unknown packages to sneak
  into a book and wreak havoc.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for sending
  an interesting example that led us to an exploration resulting in
  finding this bug.  (A comment in the source code for [47mnote-3-2[0m shows
  such an example.)  That example led us to fix a couple of other
  bugs related to packages.  See [hidden-death-package] if you are
  generally interested in such issues, and for associated examples,
  see comments in [47mnote-3-2[0m in the ACL2 source code.

  Fixed subtle soundness bugs related to [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules by restricting
  evaluators (see [defevaluator]), as discussed in a new
  documentation topic: see [evaluator-restrictions].

  Fixed a soundness bug that was allowing redefinition from [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m to
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.  This prohibition had been in ACL2 for awhile but
  was accidentally removed in the preceding version.

  Fixed a soundness bug related to [47m[trace$][0m.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger
  for bringing it to our attention and for useful discussions, and
  providing a proof of [47mnil[0m, the essence of which is illustrated as
  follows:

    (value-triple (trace$ (bar :entry (defun foo () 17))))

  Thus, [47m[trace$][0m could be used to cause destructive raw Lisp behavior.
  Now, [47mtrace$[0m fails unless it is either given a list of symbols or
  else there is an active trust tag (see [defttag]); otherwise,
  consider using [47mtrace![0m instead.

  Closed a loophole that could be viewed as compromising soundness.  It
  was possible to write files during book certification by exploiting
  [47m[make-event][0m expansion, but that is no longer the case by default.
  A new function [47m[open-output-channel!][0m is identical as a function to
  [47mopen-output-channel[0m, except that the new function may be called
  even during [47m[make-event][0m expansion and [47m[clause-processor][0m [hints],
  but requires that there is an active trust tag (see [defttag]).
  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for producing a convincing example
  (forging a [certificate] during book certification; see
  [open-output-channel!]) and to him, Sandip Ray, and Jared Davis for
  useful discussions on the topic.

  Added book [47mbooks/defexec/reflexive/reflexive.lisp[0m to illustrate
  reflexive functions.

  ACL2 now generates scripts that invoke the saved image with [47mexec[0m.
  (Previously this was only done for GCL and CLISP.)  The benefit of
  this change can be to avoid the lingering of ACL2 processes after
  enclosing processes have exited.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for
  pointing out this issue.

  ACL2 has a better implementation of [47m([0m[47m[good-bye][0m[47m)[0m (hence of synonyms
  [47m([0m[47m[quit][0m[47m)[0m and [47m([0m[47m[exit][0m[47m)[0m).  As a result, you should now be able to
  exit ACL2 and Lisp from within the ACL2 read-eval-print loop with
  any of the above; formerly, this was not supported for some Lisp
  implementations, and was slow in OpenMCL.  Thanks to SBCL developer
  Harald Hanche-Olsen for useful advice.

  Fixed a bug in raw-mode (see [set-raw-mode]) that was causing hard
  errors when evaluating calls of [47m[er-progn][0m, or of macros expanding
  to such calls.

  Fixed a few Makefile dependencies, necessary only for parallel
  `make'.

  A new book, [47mmisc/defpun-exec-domain-example.lisp[0m, provides an example
  showing how partial functions which return a unique value for
  arguments in a specified domain can be efficiently executed with
  ACL2.  Execution is achieved using the [47m[mbe][0m construct.  Thanks to
  Sandip Ray for providing this example.

  Existing function [47m[mod-expt][0m computes [47m(mod (expt base exp) mod)[0m with
  great efficiency in GCL, but not in other Lisps.  Now, the book
  [47marithmetic-3/floor-mod/mod-expt-fast.lisp[0m defines a function
  [47mmod-expt-fast[0m that should provide significantly improved
  performance for such expressions in other Lisps as well, though
  still probably not as fast as when using [47mmod-expt[0m in GCL.  Thanks
  to Warren Hunt, with contributions from Robert Krug, for providing
  this book,

  Modified macro [break-on-error] to print an error message before
  entering a break, and to cause a hard error if the underlying Lisp
  cannot handle it (formerly, a raw Lisp break would occur).  Thanks
  to Bob Boyer for bringing these issues to our attention.

  The book [47mbooks/misc/defpun.lisp[0m, as well as other books related to
  the [47mdefpun[0m macro, has been modified to avoid namespace collisions
  by prefixing function symbol names with [47m\"DEFPUN-\"[0m; for example [47mbase[0m
  has been replaced by [47mdefpun-base[0m.  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  providing a first version of this update to [47mdefpun.lisp[0m.

  A theory, [47mbase[0m, in [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/bind-free/top.lisp[0m, has been
  renamed [47marithmetic-3-bind-free-base[0m, to avoid potential name
  conflicts.

  Fixed [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/bind-free/banner.lisp[0m to print (as before) a
  message about how to turn on non-linear arithmetic, by modifying
  the call of [47mvalue-triple[0m to use [47m:on-skip-proofs t[0m.  Thanks to
  Robert Krug for bringing this issue to our attention.

  Modified [47mbooks/Makefile-subdirs[0m and [47mbooks/Makefile-psubdirs[0m so that
  they can be used with [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m.  Thus, one can set
  things up so that `make' can be used to certify books both in the
  current directory and subdirectories, for example as follows.

    ACL2 = ../../saved_acl2

    arith-top: top all
    all: top

    DIRS = pass1 bind-free floor-mod
    include ../Makefile-subdirs
    include ../Makefile-generic

    top.cert: top.lisp
    top.cert: bind-free/top.cert
    top.cert: floor-mod/floor-mod.cert
    top.cert: floor-mod/mod-expt-fast.cert

  An experimental extension of ACL2 is under development by Bob Boyer
  and Warren Hunt to support function memoization, hash conses, and
  an applicative version of hash tables.  The default build of ACL2
  does not include this extension, other than simple logic
  definitions of functions in new source file [47mhons.lisp[0m.  Future
  versions of ACL2 may fully incorporate this experimental extension.

  The [47m[defevaluator][0m event macro has been modified primarily by adding
  a new constraint as follows, where [47mevl[0m is the evaluator.  The idea
  is that for the evaluation of a function call, one may replace each
  argument by the quotation of its evaluation and then also replace
  the alist environment with [47mnil[0m.

    (DEFTHMD UNHIDE-evl-CONSTRAINT-0
      (IMPLIES (AND (CONSP X)
                    (SYNTAXP (NOT (EQUAL A ''NIL)))
                    (NOT (EQUAL (CAR X) 'QUOTE)))
               (EQUAL (evl X A)
                      (evl (CONS (CAR X)
                                 (KWOTE-LST (UNHIDE-evl-LIST (CDR X) A)))
                           NIL))))

  In order to support this change, there is another change: an
  evaluator maps [47mnil[0m to [47mnil[0m (note [47m(AND X (CDR (ASSOC-EQ X A)))[0m in
  place of [47m(CDR (ASSOC-EQ X A))[0m below).

    (DEFTHM UNHIDE-evl-CONSTRAINT-1
      (IMPLIES (SYMBOLP X)
               (EQUAL (UNHIDE-evl X A)
                      (AND X (CDR (ASSOC-EQ X A))))))

  With the new [47m[defevaluator][0m, Dave Greve has been able to do a proof
  about beta reduction that seemed impossible before (see
  [47mbooks/misc/beta-reduce.lisp[0m).  Thanks to Dave for suggesting an
  initial version of this change.

  Explicit compilation is now avoided for OpenMCL, resulting in fewer
  files to manage (no more files resulting from compilation) and,
  according to some tests, slightly faster run times.  See
  [compilation].  Thanks to Bob Boyer and Warren Hunt for suggesting
  this possibility.

  Now, the [47mterm-evisc-tuple[0m (see [ld-evisc-tuple]) is overridden by
  state global [47muser-term-evisc-tuple[0m in all cases.  Formerly, this
  was only the case when [47mterm-evisc-tuple[0m was called with non-[47mnil[0m
  first argument.

  Symbols with the dot ([47m.[0m) character are generally no longer printed
  with vertical bars.  For example, before this change:

    ACL2 !>'ab.c
    |AB.C|
    ACL2 !>

  After this change:

    ACL2 !>'ab.c
    AB.C
    ACL2 !>

  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting this improvement.

  Fixed bugs in [47mguard[0m verification for theorems.  The following
  examples illustrate these bugs.  If either theorem's body is
  executed in raw Lisp there is likely to be a hard Lisp error, even
  though [47m[verify-guards][0m was supposed to ensure against that
  behavior.

    ; Example: Verify-guards failed to check that all functions in the theorem
    ; had already been guard-verified.
    (defun my-car (x) (car x))
    (defthm my-car-compute-example (equal (my-car 3) (my-car 3)))
    (verify-guards my-car-compute-example)

    ; Example: Verify guards of a theorem whose body uses state improperly.
    (defthm bad-state-handler
      (if (state-p state)
          (equal (car state) (car state))
        t)
      :rule-classes nil)
    (verify-guards bad-state-handler)

  See [gcl] for an example, developed with Warren Hunt and Serita
  Nelesen, that shows how to get fast fixnum (small integer)
  arithmetic operations in GCL.

  Fixnum declarations are now realized as [47m(signed-byte 30)[0m and
  [47m(unsigned-byte 29)[0m instead of what was generally [47m(signed-byte 29)[0m
  and [47m(unsigned-byte 28)[0m.  MCL users may thus find better performance
  if they switch to OpenMCL.  Note that some definitions have changed
  correspondingly; for example, [47m[zpf][0m now [47m[declare][0ms its argument to
  be of type [47m(unsigned-byte 29)[0m instead of [47m(unsigned-byte 28)[0m.  A few
  [books] may thus need to be adjusted; for example, changes were
  made to books in [47mbooks/data-structures/memories/[0m.

  ACL2's rewriter now avoids removing certain true hypotheses and false
  conclusions.  When a hypothesis rewrites to true or a conclusion
  rewrites to false, ACL2 formerly removed that hypothesis or
  conclusion.  Now, it only does such removal when the hypothesis or
  conclusion is either a call of [47m[equal][0m or an equivalence relation
  (see [equivalence]), or else is sufficiently trivial (roughly,
  either redundant with another hypothesis or conclusion or else
  trivially true without considering the rest of the goal).  A
  specific example may be found in source file [47msimplify.lisp[0m; search
  for ``; But we need to do even more work''.  Thanks to Robert Krug
  for providing the idea for this improvement and its initial
  implementation.  As is common with heuristic changes, you may find
  it necessary on occasion to rename some subgoals in your [hints].
  And in this case, you might also find it necessary on rare
  occasions to add [47m:do-not '(generalize)[0m [hints].

  A new function, [47mmfc-relieve-hyp[0m, allows (for example) for more
  powerful [47m[bind-free][0m hypotheses, by providing an interface to the
  rewriter's routine for relieving hypotheses.  See
  [extended-metafunctions].  Thanks to Robert Krug for providing the
  idea for this feature and its initial implementation.

  Two improvements have been made to non-linear arithmetic (see
  [non-linear-arithmetic]).  One allows for deducing strict
  inequality ([47m<[0m) for the result of certain polynomial
  multiplications, where previously only non-strict inequality ([47m<=[0m)
  was deduced.  A second allows the use of the product of two
  polynomials when at least one of them is known to be rational.  We
  had previously restricted the use of the product to the case where
  both were known to be rational.  Thanks to Robert Krug for these
  improvements.

  (OpenMCL and Allegro CL only) Fixed ACL2's redefinitions of raw Lisp
  [47mtrace[0m and [47muntrace[0m in OpenMCL and Allegro CL so that when given no
  arguments, they return the list of traced functions.  For [47mtrace[0m,
  this is an ANSI spec requirement.  Note that [47m[trace$][0m and
  [47m[untrace$][0m continue to return [47mnil[0m in the ACL2 loop.

  Fixed a bug that was allowing the symbol [47m&whole[0m to appear in other
  than the first argument position for a [47m[defmacro][0m event, in
  violation of the Common Lisp spec (and leading to potentially
  surprising behavior).  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for bringing this
  bug to our attention.

  It had been illegal to use [47m[make-event][0m under some calls of [47m[ld][0m.
  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this issue
  to our attention with a simple example, in essence:

    (ld '((defmacro my-defun (&rest args) `(make-event '(defun ,@args)))
          (my-defun f (x) x)))

  ACL2 no longer prohibits certain [47m[make-event][0m forms when including
  uncertified [books].  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for first bringing
  this issue to our attention.

  Hard errors arose when using [break-rewrite] stack display commands,
  in particular [47m:path[0m and [47m:frame[0m, from inside the [proof-builder].
  This has been fixed.

  Fixed a bug that could cause functions that call system built-ins
  [47mf-put-global[0m, [47mf-get-global[0m, or [47mf-boundp-global[0m to cause a raw Lisp
  error even when proving theorems.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger, for
  reporting such a failure for the form [47m(thm (w '(1 2 3)))[0m.

  Renamed the formal parameters of function [47mset-equal[0m in distributed
  book [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/bind-free/normalize.lisp[0m so that more
  distributed books can be included together in the same session.  In
  particular books [47mbooks/data-structures/set-theory[0m and
  [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/extra/top-ext[0m can now be included together.
  Thanks to Carl Eastlund for bringing this problem to our attention
  and to Robert Krug for suggesting the formals renaming as a fix.

  Metafunctions must now be executable.  See [meta].

  New utilities allow for user-defined simplifiers at the goal level,
  both verified and unverified (``trusted''), where the latter can
  even be defined by programs outside ACL2.  See [clause-processor],
  which points to a new directory [47mbooks/clause-processors/[0m that
  contains examples of these new utilities, including for example a
  system (``SULFA'') contributed by Erik Reeber that implements a
  decision procedure (thanks, Erik).  Also see
  [proof-builder-commands] for the new [proof-builder] command
  [47mclause-processor[0m (or for short, [47mcl-proc[0m).

  The rewriter has been tweaked to run faster in some cases involving
  very large terms.  Thanks to Eric Smith and Jared Davis for
  providing a helpful example that helped us locate the source of
  this inefficiency.

  Added [47mbooks/make-event/defspec.lisp[0m.  This book shows how one can
  mimic certain limited forms of higher-order statements in ACL2 by
  use of macros, [47m[make-event][0m, and [47m[table][0m events.  Thanks to Sandip
  Ray for his contribution.

  A new release of the RTL library, [47mbooks/rtl/rel7/[0m, replaces the
  previous version, [47mbooks/rtl/rel6/[0m.  Thanks to Hanbing Liu and David
  Russinoff for providing this new version.

  We thank David Russinoff for providing a proof of the law of
  quadratic reciprocity.  See [47mbooks/quadratic-reciprocity/Readme.lsp[0m.

  Eliminated a slow array warning (see [slow-array-warning]) that could
  occur when exiting a [wormhole] after executing an [47m[in-theory][0m
  event in that wormhole.  Thanks to Dave Greve for bringing this
  problem to our attention.

  A new accessor, [47m(mfc-rdepth mfc)[0m, provides a new field, the remaining
  rewrite stack depth, which has been added to metafunction context
  structures; see [extended-metafunctions].  Thanks to Eric Smith for
  suggesting this addition.

  The algorithms were modified for collecting up rule names and other
  information used in proofs, into so-called ``tag-trees''.
  Tag-trees are now free of duplicate objects, and this change can
  dramatically speed up some proofs that involve many different
  rules.  Thanks to Eric Smith for doing some profiling that brought
  this issue to our attention, and for reporting that this change
  reduced proof time on an example by about 47% (from 3681.46
  reported seconds down to 1954.69).

  All legal [47mxargs[0m keywords may now be used in [47m[verify-termination][0m
  [events].  In particular, this is the case for [47m:normalize[0m.

  (SBCL and CMUCL only) Fixed a problem with stobj array resizing
  functions that was causing a hard error in ACL2 images built on
  SBCL or CMUCL.

  A new [table], [47m[evisc-table][0m, allows you to introduce print
  abbreviations, for example for large constants.  Moreover, a new
  reader macro --- [47m#,[0m --- makes it convenient to reference constants
  even inside a quote.  See [evisc-table].  Thanks to Bob Boyer and
  Warren Hunt for useful discussions leading to this feature.

  The macros in [47mbooks/misc/expander.lisp[0m now have a new keyword
  argument, [47m:simplify-hyps-p[0m.  The default behavior is as before, but
  now case splitting from hypothesis simplification can be avoided.
  For details, evaluate [47m(include-book \"misc/expander\" :dir :system)[0m
  and then [47m:doc!  defthm?[0m and [47m:doc! symsym[0m.  Thanks to Daron Vroon
  for sending a question that prompted this additional functionality.

  ACL2 failed to apply [47m:[0m[47m[restrict][0m hints to rules of class
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m, except for the simplest sorts (see [simple]).  This
  has been fixed.  Thanks to Jared Davis for pointing out this bug by
  sending a small example.

  Added a new [47m:msg[0m argument to [47massert-event[0m; see [assert-event].  The
  implementation of [47mvalue-triple[0m has been modified to support this
  change.

  Fixed a bug in macro [47mio?[0m that now allows the [47mcommentp[0m argument to be
  [47mt[0m.  This provides a way other than [47mcw[0m to print without modifying
  state, for example as follows.  (Warning: Certain errors may leave
  you in a [wormhole], in which case use [47m:a![0m to abort.)

    ACL2 !>(prog2$ (io? event t state
                        ()
                        (fms \"Howdy~%\" nil *standard-co* state nil))
                   (+ 3 4))

    Howdy
    7
    ACL2 !>:set-inhibit-output-lst (proof-tree event)
     (PROOF-TREE EVENT)
    ACL2 !>(prog2$ (io? event t state
                        ()
                        (fms \"Howdy~%\" nil *standard-co* state nil))
                   (+ 3 4))
    7
    ACL2 !>

  ACL2 now disallows calls of [47m[progn!][0m inside function bodies, just as
  it already disallowed such calls of [47m[progn][0m, since in both cases
  the Common Lisp meaning differs from the ACL2 meaning.

  Redefinition of system functions now always requires an active trust
  tag (see [defttag]).  This restriction was intended before, but
  there was a hole that allowed a second redefinition without an
  active trust tag.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for pointing out this
  bug.

  [47m[Verify-termination][0m has been disabled for a few more built-in
  functions that are in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.  (If you are curious about
  which ones they are, evaluate [47m(f-get-global
  'built-in-program-mode-fns state)[0m.)  [Note added for Version_3.4:
  This state global has been changed to 'program-fns-with-raw-code.]
  Moreover, such functions now will execute only their raw Lisp code,
  so for example they cannot be called during macroexpansion.  Thanks
  to Peter Dillinger and Sandip Ray for useful discussions on details
  of the implementation of this restriction.

  New untouchable state global variables, [47mtemp-touchable-vars[0m and
  [47mtemp-touchable-fns[0m, can control the enforcement of untouchability.
  See [remove-untouchable].  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for suggesting
  these features.

  The ``TTAG NOTE'' string was being printed by [47m[encapsulate][0m events
  whenever an active trust tag was already in effect (see [defttag]),
  even if the encapsulate event contained no [47m[defttag][0m event.  This
  has been fixed.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for a query leading to
  this fix.

  Fixed a bug in [47m[progn!][0m that could leave the user in raw-mode (see
  [set-raw-mode]).  This could occur when certifying a book with a
  [47mcompile-flg[0m value of [47mt[0m (see [certify-book]), when that book
  contained a [47m[progn!][0m event setting raw-mode to [47mt[0m without setting
  raw-mode back to [47mnil[0m:

    (progn! (set-raw-mode t) ...)")
 (NOTE-3-2-1
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.2.1 (June, 2007) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  (OpenMCL and multi-threaded SBCL only) Fixed a soundness bug in the
  evaluation of [47m[mbe][0m forms in the presence of Lisp feature
  [47m:acl2-mv-as-values[0m.  Thanks to Sol Swords for reporting this
  problem and sending a simple proof of [47mnil[0m (which can be found in a
  comment in the ACL2 sources, in [47m(deflabel note-3-2-1 ...)[0m).

  Added a new utility, [47m[dmr][0m (Dynamically Monitor Rewrites), for
  watching the activity of the rewriter and some other proof
  processes.  See [dmr].  We thank Robert Krug for useful
  contributions.

  Fixed a bug in evaluation of calls of [47m[with-prover-time-limit][0m.

  Fixed the writing of executable scripts when building ACL2, so that
  the build-time value of environment variable [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m is
  no longer written there.  Thanks to Dave Greve for discussing this
  change.

  Fixed bugs in [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m (which are similarly present in the
  [proof-builder]'s [47msr[0m ([47mshow-rewrites[0m) command.  The first bug was
  evident from the following forms sent by Robert Krug, which caused
  an error.

    (include-book \"arithmetic-3/floor-mod/floor-mod\" :dir :system)
    :pl (mod (+ 1 x) n)

  The second bug was due to a failure to take note of which rules are
  disabled, and could be seen by executing the following (very
  slow!).

    (defstub modulus () t)
    (include-book \"arithmetic-3/floor-mod/floor-mod\" :dir :system)
    :pl (mod (+ x y) (modulus))

  Modified [47m[certify-book][0m so that by default, all
  executable-counterpart functions (sometimes called ``*1*
  functions'') are compiled.  This is the behavior that was already
  supported with a [47mcompile-flg[0m argument of [47m:all[0m; the change is that
  argument [47mt[0m now has this behavior as well (and [47m:all[0m is supported
  only for legacy purposes).  A new value for [47mcompile-flg[0m, [47m:raw[0m,
  gives the behavior formerly produced by value [47mt[0m, namely where
  executable-counterpart functions are not compiled.  The above
  changes are irrelevant if compilation is suppressed; see
  [compilation].  Finally, if environment variable [47mACL2_COMPILE_FLG[0m
  is set, then after converting to upper-case this environment
  variable's value of [47m\"T\"[0m, [47m\"NIL\"[0m, or [47m\":RAW\"[0m will determine the value
  of the optional [47mcompile-flg[0m argument to be [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m, or [47m:raw[0m,
  respectively, when this argument is not explicitly supplied.

  Modified [47m[include-book][0m so that [47m:comp[0m argument now acts like [47m:comp![0m,
  i.e., compiling a file that includes the file together with all
  executable counterpart (so-called ``*1*'') functions.  A new
  argument, [47m:comp-raw[0m, has the behavior that [47m:comp[0m had formerly,
  i.e., compiling the actual book only.

  The function [47m[nonnegative-integer-quotient][0m is now computed in raw
  Lisp by calling [47m[floor][0m on its arguments.  This change was
  suggested by Peter Dillinger, in order to avoid stack overflows
  such as reported by Daron Vroon.  A new book,
  [47mbooks/misc/misc2/misc.lisp[0m, contains a proof of equivalence of
  [47m[nonnegative-integer-quotient][0m and [47m[floor][0m, and serves as a
  repository for other miscellaneous proofs, including those
  justifying ACL2 modifications such as this one.

  Enhanced [47m[accumulated-persistence][0m to break down results by useful
  vs.  useless rule applications.  In particular, this provides
  information about which rules were ever applied successfully, as
  requested by Bill Young.

  Added coverage of [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules to the [47m[accumulated-persistence][0m
  statistics.

  Fixed a bug that was causing a [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m hint to fire on a
  subgoal of the goal to which it was attached, when the original
  application didn't change the clause.  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  pointing out this bug and providing a useful example.

  Fixed a bug in handling of computed [hints] related to the
  [47mstable-under-simplificationp[0m parameter (see [computed-hints]).
  There were actually two bugs.  A minor but confusing bug was that
  the same goal was printed twice upon application of such a hint.
  The major bug was that [47m:use[0m [hints] (as well as other ``top''
  hints: [47m:by[0m, [47m:cases[0m, and [47m:clause-processor[0m) were not being applied
  properly.  Thanks to Jared Davis for sending an example some time
  ago that showed the duplicate printing, and to Dave Greve for
  sending an example showing mis-application of [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m
  [hints].  Note that you may find that existing computed hints using
  the [47mstable-under-simplificationp[0m parameter no longer have the same
  behavior; see a comment about computed hints in [47mnote-3-2-1[0m, ACL2
  source file [47mld.lisp[0m, for an example of how you might want to fix
  such computed hints.

  David Russinoff has contributed an updated version of
  [47mbooks/quadratic-reciprocity/[0m including minor modifications of the
  treatment of prime numbers and a proof that there exist infinitely
  many primes.  Thanks to David for contributing this work, and to
  Jose Luis Ruiz-Reina for posing the challenge.

  Reduced the sizes of some [certificate] ([47m.cert[0m) files by relaxing the
  test that allows original [47m[defpkg][0m [events] to be placed there,
  rather than evaluating the import list term into an explicit list
  of symbols.

  Improved execution efficiency slightly for function [47mrcdp[0m in file
  [47mbooks/misc/records.lisp[0m, by using [47m[mbe][0m to introduce a
  tail-recursive body.

  The executable script generated by [47m[save-exec][0m (and by the normal
  build process) now includes a time stamp as a comment.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for suggesting this change in order to support his use
  of [47momake[0m.  In the process, we also arranged that the startup banner
  for an executable created by [47m[save-exec][0m shows all of the build
  (save) times, not just the one for the original image.

  Sped up most redundant [47m[defpkg][0m [events] by avoiding evaluation and
  sorting of the imports list in the case of identical event forms.
  And, for [47m[defpkg][0m events that are not redundant, sped up their
  processing in Allegro CL (and perhaps other Lisps, but apparently
  not GCL) by using our own [47mimport[0m function.

  Modified [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m so that it refuses to associate a
  keyword with a different directory string than one it is already
  bound to.  See [delete-include-book-dir] for how to remove the
  existing binding first.  Thanks to Robert Krug for pointing out
  that without this change, one can find it difficult to debug a
  failure due to rebinding a keyword with [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m.

  Added a new value for the [47m:do-not-induct[0m hint (see [hints]),
  [47m:otf-flg-override[0m, which causes ACL2 to ignore the [47m:[0m[47m[otf-flg][0m when
  considering whether to abort the proof because of a [47m:do-not-induct[0m
  hint.  Thanks to Daron Vroon for suggesting such an addition.

  Modified the printing of messages for entering and exiting raw mode
  (see [set-raw-mode]), so that in particular they are inhibited
  during [47m[include-book][0m or whenever [47mobservation[0ms are inhibited (see
  [set-inhibit-output-lst]).  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for
  suggesting such a change.

  (For system hackers only.)  The handling of [events] of the form
  [47m(progn! (state-global-let* ...))[0m had a bug that was causing
  bindings to be evaluated twice.  Moreover, the use of system
  function [47m[state-global-let*][0m is suspect in raw Lisp.  We have
  eliminated special treatment of [47mstate-global-let*[0m by [47mprogn![0m in
  favor of a new keyword argument, [47mstate-global-bindings[0m, that
  provides the intended functionality.  See [progn!].  Moreover,
  special handling that allowed [47m[make-event][0m forms under
  [47mstate-global-let*[0m has been removed; the desired effect can be
  obtained using (progn! :state-global-bindings ...).  Thanks to
  Peter Dillinger for pointing out the above bug and collaborating on
  these changes.

  Incorporated backward-compatible enhancements to
  [47mbooks/misc/expander.lisp[0m from Daron Vroon (documented near the top
  of that file).

  The specification of [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m had required that only a
  single ([47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, or [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m) rule be generated.  We
  have weakened this restriction to allow more than one rule provided
  that each rule has the same list of hypotheses.  For example, the
  rule class [47m(:rewrite :backchain-limit-lst 1)[0m is now legal for the
  corollary formula [47m(implies (f x) (and (g x) (h x)))[0m, where this was
  not formerly the case.  Thanks to Dave Greve for bringing this
  issue to our attention.")
 (NOTE-3-2-1{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.2.1(r) (June, 2007) Notes

  Please also see [note-3-2-1] for changes to Version 3.2.1 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-3-2{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.2(r) (April, 2007) Notes

  Changed the default distributed [books] directory for ACL2(r) from
  [47mbooks/[0m to [47mbooks/nonstd/[0m.  See [include-book], in particular the
  discussion of ``Distributed Books Directory''.

  Added directory [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/[0m and its subdirectories to
  [47mbooks/nonstd/[0m.  (But a chunk of theorems from
  [47marithmetic-3/extra/ext.lisp[0m are ``commented out'' using
  #-:non-standard-analysis because they depend on [47mbooks/rtl/rel7/[0m,
  which is not yet in [47mbooks/nonstd/[0m; feel free to volunteer to remedy
  this!)

  Incorporated changes from Ruben Gamboa to some (linear and
  non-linear) arithmetic routines in the theorem prover, to
  comprehend the reals rather than only the rationals.

  Please also see [note-3-2] for changes to Version 3.2 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-3-3
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.3 (November, 2007) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Below we roughly organize the changes since Version 3.2.1 into new
  features, bug fixes, prover algorithm enhancements, and
  miscellaneous.  Also see [note-3-2-1] for other changes since
  Version 3.2.

  [31;1mNEW FEATURES[0m

  A new ``gag-mode'' provides less verbose, more helpful feedback from
  the theorem prover, in support of The Method (see [the-method]).
  See [set-gag-mode].  We recommend the use of gag-mode, which may
  become the default in future ACL2 releases, and we welcome
  suggestions for improvement.  We thank Robert Krug and Sandip Ray
  for helpful feedback in the design of [gag-mode].  Note that when
  proofs fail, then even without gag-mode and even if proof output is
  inhibited, the summary will contain a useful listing of so-called
  ``key checkpoints'' (see [set-gag-mode]).

  Added support for a leading `[47m~[0m' in filenames.  Thanks to Bob Boyer
  for suggesting this enhancement.  Note that since `[47m~/[0m' depends on
  the user, it is disallowed in [books] to be certified (see
  [certify-book]), since otherwise an [47m[include-book][0m form in a book,
  [47mb[0m, could have a different meaning at certification time than at the
  time [47m[include-book][0m is later executed on book [47mb[0m.

  Made a change to allow [47m(time$ FORM)[0m and [47m(with-prover-time-limit TIME
  FORM)[0m when [47mFORM[0m includes [47m[make-event][0m calls that change the ACL2
  [world].  Thanks to Jared Davis for requesting such support for
  [47m[time$][0m.

  Computed [hints] (see [computed-hints]) may now produce a so-called
  ``error triple'', i.e., a result of the form [47m(mv erp val state)[0m,
  where a non-[47mnil[0m [47merp[0m causes an error, and otherwise [47mval[0m is the value
  of the hint.  It remains legal for a computed hint to return a
  single ordinary value; indeed, the symbol form of a computed hint
  must still be a function that returns an ordinary single value.

  New hints provide additional control of the theorem prover, as
  follows.  See [hints] for more details, and see new distributed
  book directory [47mbooks/hints/[0m for examples, in particular file
  [47mbasic-tests.lisp[0m in that directory for simple examples.

      o The hint [47m:OR (hints-1 ... hints-k)[0m causes an attempt to prove the
      specified goal using each [47mhints-i[0m in turn, until the first of
      these succeeds.  If none succeeds, then the prover proceeds
      after heuristically choosing the ``best'' result, taking into
      account the goals pushed in each case for proof by induction.

      o A custom hint is a keyword that the user associates with a
      corresponding hint-generating function by invoking
      [47m[add-custom-keyword-hint][0m.  Thus, a custom hint may be viewed
      as a convenient sort of computed hint.

      o A custom hint, [47m:MERGE[0m, is implemented in distributed book
      [47mbooks/hints/merge.lisp[0m.  It is useful for combining hints.

      o A sophisticated yet useful custom hint is the [47m:CONSIDER[0m hint
      implemented in distributed book [47mbooks/hints/consider-hint.lisp[0m.
      With this hint, you can for example give the equivalent of a
      [47m:USE[0m hint without the need to supply an instantiation.  Include
      that book in order to see documentation online with [47m:doc
      consideration[0m, and see the book
      [47mbooks/hints/consider-hint-tests.lisp[0m for examples.

  A new hint, [47m:[0m[47m[reorder][0m, allows the specification of which subgoals
  are to be considered first.  Thanks to Sandip Ray for putting
  forward this idea.

  Enhanced [47mset-saved-output[0m by supporting a second argument of [47m:same[0m,
  which avoids changing which output is inhibited.

  Added macros [47mthm?[0m and [47mnot-thm?[0m to distributed book
  [47mbooks/make-event/eval.lisp[0m, so that it's easy to test within a
  certified book that a proof attempt succeeds or that it fails.

  Added printing function [47m[cw!][0m, which is analogous to [47m[cw][0m just as
  [47m[fmt!][0m is to [47m[fmt][0m, i.e., printing so that the result can be read
  back in.  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting this enhancement
  (after doing his own implementation).

  The ACL2 customization file can now be specified using environment
  variable [47mACL2-CUSTOMIZATION[0m [note: starting with Version_4.0,
  [47mACL2_CUSTOMIZATION[0m].  See [ACL2-customization].  Thanks to Peter
  Dillinger for requesting this feature.

  Added new emacs capabilities for proof trees (all documented in
  emacs):

      o New function start-proof-tree-noninteractive, for example
      (start-proof-tree-noninteractive \"*shell*\")

      o C-z o Switch to another frame

      o C-z b Switch to prooftree buffer

      o C-z B Switch to prooftree buffer in \"prooftree-frame\" frame

  Added Common Lisp function, [47msearch[0m, as a macro in [47m[logic][0m mode, with
  limited support for keyword arguments.  Thanks to Warren Hunt for
  requesting this addition.

  Sandip Ray has contributed a book, [47mbooks/make-event/defrefine.lisp[0m,
  that provides a collection of macros to aid in reasoning about ACL2
  functions via refinement.

  Wrote and incorporated new utility for listing all the theorems in an
  included book.  See [47mbooks/misc/book-thms.lisp[0m.  Thanks to Jared
  Davis for requesting this functionality.

  The new distributed book [47mmisc/defp.lisp[0m generalizes the [47m[defpun][0m
  macro to allow more general forms of tail recursion.

  (Low-level printing improvement) A new function,
  [47mset-ppr-flat-right-margin[0m, allows the right margin for certain
  kinds of ``flat'' printing to exceed column 40.  Thanks to Jared
  Davis for pointing out that state global variables
  [47m'fmt-hard-right-margin[0m and [47m'fmt-soft-right-margin[0m are not alone
  sufficient to extend the right margin in all cases.

  The event [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m can now take a relative pathname as
  an argument.  Formerly, it required an absolute pathname.

  A new book, [47mbooks/misc/defopener.lisp[0m, provides a utility creating a
  theorem that equates a term with its simplification.

  ACL2 now provides limited support for the Common Lisp primitive [47mFLET[0m,
  which supports local function bindings.  See [flet].  Thanks to
  Warren Hunt for requesting this feature.

  Added a definition of [47m[boole$][0m, a close analogue of Common Lisp
  function [47mboole[0m.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for providing an initial
  implementation.

  [31;1mBUG FIXES[0m

  Fixed [47m[defstobj][0m to inhibit a potentially useless theory warning.

  Fixed a bug in the application of [47m[certify-book][0m to relative
  pathnames for files in other than the current directory.  Thanks to
  Amr Helmy for bringing this bug to our attention.

  Fixed a bug in [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m for displaying rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m.
  Thanks to Jared Davis for finding this bug and providing a fix.

  Formerly, [47m[set-default-backchain-limit][0m was not a legal event form
  for [47m[encapsulate][0m forms and [books].  This has been fixed.  Thanks
  to Robert Krug and Sandip Ray for bringing this bug to our
  attention.

  Fixed the handling of [hints] in [proof-builder] commands for the
  prover, such as [47mbash[0m --- see [proof-builder-commands] --- so that
  the user can override the default settings of hints, in particular
  of [47m:do-not[0m and [47m:do-not-induct[0m hints attached to [47m\"Goal\"[0m.  This fix
  also applies to the distributed book [47mmisc/bash.lisp[0m, where Robert
  Krug noticed that he got an error with [47m:hints ((\"Goal\" :do-not
  '(preprocess)))[0m; we thank Robert for pointing out this problem.

  Fixed a bug in handling of [stobj]s occurring in guards of functions
  whose [guard]s have been verified.  In such cases, a raw Lisp error
  was possible when proving theorems about non-''live'' [stobj]s.  We
  thank Daron Vroon for sending us an example that highlighted this
  bug.  The following (simpler) example causes such an error in
  previous versions of ACL2.

    (defstobj st fld)
    (defun foo (st)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs st :guard (fld st)))
      st)
    (thm (equal (foo '(3))
                '(3)))

  The [dmr] feature for dynamic monitoring of rewrites had a bug, where
  the file used for communicating with emacs was the same for all
  users, based on who built the ACL2 executable image.  This has been
  fixed.  Thanks to Robert Krug for bringing this bug to our
  attention.

  Fixed a bug in some warnings, in particular the warning for including
  an uncertified book, that was giving an incorrect warning summary
  string.

  Inclusion of uncertified books erroneously re-used [47m[make-event][0m
  expansions that were stored in stale [certificate]s.  This is no
  longer the case.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this bug to
  our attention.

  Fixed a bug that was disallowing calls of [47m[with-output][0m in [events]
  that were executing before calling [47m[certify-book][0m.

  Modified the functionality of [47mbinop-table[0m so other than binary
  function symbols are properly supported (hence with no action based
  on right-associated arguments).  See [add-binop].

  Fixed small [proof-builder] issues related to packages.  Emacs
  commands [47mctrl-t d[0m and [47mctrl-t ctrl-d[0m now work properly with colon
  (`[47m:[0m') and certain other punctuation characters.  The [47mp-top[0m command
  now prints ``[47m***[0m'' regardless of the current package.

  Fixed a bug that allowed [47m[certify-book][0m to succeed without specifying
  value [47mt[0m for keyword argument [47m:skip-proofs-okp[0m, even with
  [47m[include-book][0m [events] in the certification [world] depending on
  events executed under [47m[skip-proofs][0m.

  Improved [47m[show-accumulated-persistence][0m in the following two ways.
  Thanks to Robert Krug and Bill Young for requesting these
  improvements and for providing useful feedback.

      o It can provide more complete information when aborting a proof.

      o The [47m:frames[0m reported for a rule are categorized as ``useful'' and
      ``useless'' according to whether they support ``useful'' or
      ``useless'' [47m:tries[0m of that rule, respectively.  See
      [accumulated-persistence] for further explanation.

  Modified [47m[make-event][0m so that the reported time and warnings include
  those from the expansion phase.  In analogy with [47m[encapsulate][0m and
  [47m[progn][0m, the rules reported still do not include those from
  subsidiary events (including the expansion phase).  A related
  change to [47m[ld][0m avoids resetting summary information (time,
  warnings) with each top-level form evaluation; [events] already
  handle this information themselves.

  Fixed [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m so that all warnings are inhibited
  when [47mwarning![0m but not [47mwarning[0m is included in the list.  Formerly,
  only soundness-related warnings were inhibited in this case.
  Thanks to Eric Smith for bringing this bug to our attention.

  Distributed directory [47mdoc/HTML/[0m now again includes installation
  instructions (which was missing in Version_3.2.1), in
  [47mdoc/HTML/installation/installation.html[0m.

  Some fixes have been made for [proof-tree] support.

      o [Proof-tree] output is no longer inhibited automatically during
      [47m[certify-book][0m, though it continues to be inhibited by default
      (i.e., ACL2 continues to start up as though
      [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m has been called with argument
      [47m'(proof-tree)[0m).

      o Fixed a bug in Xemacs support for [proof-tree] help keys [47mC-z h[0m and
      [47mC-z ?[0m.

      o Fixed a bug in [proof-tree]s that was failing to deal with the case
      that a goal pushed for proof by induction is subsumed by such a
      goal to be proved later.  Now, the proof-tree display regards
      such subsumed goals as proved, as is reported in the theorem
      prover's output.

  Fixed a bug that was disallowing [47m[value-triple][0m forms inside
  [47m[encapsulate][0m forms in a certification [world] (see [portcullis]).

  If the [47m:load-compiled-file[0m argument of a call of [47m[include-book][0m is
  [47m:comp[0m, then an existing compiled file will be loaded, provided it
  is more recent than the corresponding book (i.e., [47m.lisp[0m file).  A
  bug was causing the compiled file to be deleted and then
  reconstructed in the case of [47m:comp[0m, where this behavior was
  intended only for [47m:comp![0m.

  Fixed a bug that was avoiding compilation of some executable
  counterparts (sometimes called ``*1* functions'') during
  [47m[certify-book][0m, and also during [47m[include-book][0m with
  [47m:load-compiled-file[0m value of [47m:comp[0m or [47m:comp![0m).  Thanks to Eric
  Smith for sending a small example to bring this bug to our
  attention.

  Incorporated a fix from Eric Smith for a typo (source function
  [47mancestors-check1[0m) that could cause hard Lisp errors.  Thanks, Eric!

  Fixed the following issues with packages and book [certificate]s.
  See [hidden-death-package] if you are generally interested in such
  issues, and for associated examples, see comments on ``Fixed the
  following issues with packages'' in [47mnote-3-3[0m in the ACL2 source
  code.

      o Reduced the size of [47m.cert[0m files by eliminating some unnecessary
      [47m[defpkg][0m events generated for the [portcullis].

      o Fixed a bug that has caused errors when reading symbols from a
      [portcullis] that are in undefined packages defined in locally
      included books.

      o Fixed a bug that could lead to failure of [47m[include-book][0m caused by
      a subtle interaction between [47m[set-ignore-ok][0m and [47m[defpkg][0m
      events generated for the [portcullis] of a [certificate].

  [31;1mPROVER ALGORITHM ENHANCEMENTS[0m

  Non-linear arithmetic (see [non-linear-arithmetic]) has been improved
  to be more efficient or more powerful in some cases.  Thanks to
  Robert Krug for contributing these improvements.

  Improved certain (so-called ``[47m[type-set][0m'') reasoning about whether
  or not expressions denote integers.  Thanks to Robert Krug for
  contributing code to implement this change, along with examples
  illustrating its power that are now distributed in the book
  [47mbooks/misc/integer-type-set-test.lisp[0m.

  Improved ACL2's heuristics for relieving hypotheses, primarily to use
  linear reasoning on conjuncts and disjuncts of the test of an [47m[if][0m
  expression.  For example, given a hypothesis of the form [47m(if (or
  term1 term2) ...)[0m, ACL2 will now use linear reasoning to attempt to
  prove both [47mterm1[0m and [47mterm2[0m, not merely for [47mterm2[0m.  Thanks to Robert
  Krug for supplying examples illustrating the desirability of such
  an improvement and for useful discussions about the fix.

  Made a slight heuristic change, so that when a hypothesis with [47m[let][0m
  or [47m[mv-let][0m subterms (i.e. [47m[lambda][0m subterms) rewrites to [47mt[0m, then
  that hypothesis is necessarily eliminated.  Thanks to Jared Davis
  for sending an example that led us to develop this change, and
  thanks to Robert Krug for a helpful discussion.

  [31;1mMISCELLANEOUS[0m

  Added documentation on how to use [47m[make-event][0m to avoid duplicating
  expensive computations, thanks to Jared Davis.  See
  [using-tables-efficiently].

  Modified the error message for calls of undefined functions to show
  the arguments.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for requesting this
  enhancement.

  Modified utilities [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[pr!][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m, and [47m:[0m[47m[show-bodies][0m to
  incorporate code contributed by Jared Davis.  That code defines
  low-level source functions [47minfo-for-xxx[0m that collect information
  about rules, which is thus available to advanced users.

  Dynamic monitoring of rewrites (see [dmr]) has been improved in the
  following ways, as suggested by Robert Krug.

      o Some stale entries from the rewrite stack are no longer printed, in
      particular above [47mADD-POLYNOMIAL-INEQUALITIES[0m.

      o An additional rewrite stack entry is made when entering non-linear
      arithmetic (see [non-linear-arithmetic]).

      o An [47mADD-POLYNOMIAL-INEQUALITIES[0m entry is printed with a counter, to
      show how often this process is called.

  Modified [47m[save-exec][0m so that the newly-saved image will have the same
  raw Lisp package as the existing saved image.  This is a very
  technical change that will likely not impact most users; for
  example, the package in the ACL2 read-eval-print loop (see [lp])
  had already persisted from the original to newly-saved image.
  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting this change.

  Changed [47m[make-event][0m expansion so that changes to [47m[gag-mode][0m,
  [47m[set-print-clause-ids][0m, [47mset-fmt-soft-right-margin[0m, and
  [47mset-fmt-hard-right-margin[0m will persist after being evaluated during
  [47mmake-event[0m expansion.  (Specifically,
  [47m*protected-system-state-globals*[0m has been modified; see
  [make-event-details].)  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this
  issue to our attention.

  Output from the [proof-builder] is now always enabled when invoking
  [47m[verify][0m, even if it is globally inhibited (see
  [set-inhibit-output-lst]).

  Improved the message printed when an [47m:induct[0m hint fails, to give more
  information in some cases.  Thanks to Rex Page for suggesting where
  an improvement could be made and providing useful feedback on an
  initial improvement.

  Added a warning for [congruence] rules (see [defcong]) that specify
  [47m[iff][0m as the second equivalence relation when [47m[equal][0m can be used
  instead.  Those who heed these warnings can eliminate certain
  subsequent [47m[double-rewrite][0m warnings for [rewrite] rules with
  conclusions of the form [47m(iff term1 term2)[0m, and hence implicitly for
  Boolean conclusions [47mterm1[0m that are interpreted as [47m(iff term1 t)[0m.
  Thanks to Sarah Weissman for sending us an example that highlighted
  the need for such a warning.

  Modified macro [47m:[0m[47m[redef!][0m (which is for system implementors) so that
  it eliminates untouchables.

  Several improvements have been made to the experimental
  hons/memoization version of ACL2.  See [hons-and-memoization].

  The distributed books directory, [47m(@ distributed-books-dir)[0m, is now
  printed in the start-up message.")
 (NOTE-3-3{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.3(r) (November, 2007) Notes

  Please also see [note-3-3] for changes to Version 3.3 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-3-4
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.4 (August, 2008) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Below we roughly organize the changes since Version 3.3 into changes
  to existing features, new features, bug fixes, new and updated
  books, and Emacs support.  Each change is described just once,
  though of course many changes could be placed in more than one
  category.

  [31;1mCHANGES TO EXISTING FEATURES[0m

  Fixed a long-standing potential infinite loop in the rewriter.
  Thanks to Sol Swords for sending a concise example illustrating the
  looping behavior.  (Those interested in details are welcome to look
  at the comment about loop behavior in source function
  [47mrewrite-equal[0m.)

  Incorporated a slight strengthening of non-linear arithmetic
  contributed by Robert Krug (thanks, Robert).  With non-linear
  arithmetic enabled, the problem was essentially that ACL2 made the
  following ``optimization'': given inequalities [47m(< a u)[0m and [47m(< b v)[0m,
  for positive rational constants [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m terms [47mu[0m and [47mv[0m of which at
  least one is known to be rational, infer [47m(< (* a b) (* u v))[0m.
  Without this optimization, however, ACL2 now infers the stronger
  inequality obtained by direct multiplication of the two given
  inequalities.  To see the effect of this change, submit the event
  [47m(set-non-linearp t)[0m followed by:

    (thm (implies (and (rationalp x) (< 3 x)
                       (rationalp y) (< 4 y))
                  (< 0 (+ 12 (* -4 x) (* -3 y) (* x y)))))

  The utility [47m[set-checkpoint-summary-limit][0m has been modified in
  several ways: it now takes a single argument (no longer takes
  [47m[state][0m as an argument); a natural number [47mn[0m abbreviates the pair [47m(n
  . n)[0m; the argument is no longer evaluated, but it still optionally
  may be quoted; and a new value, [47mt[0m, suppresses all printing of the
  checkpoint summary.  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting most of
  these improvements.

  There was formerly a restriction on [47m[mbe][0m that the [47m:exec[0m argument may
  not contain a call of [47m[mbe][0m.  This restriction has been removed,
  thanks to a request from Jared Davis and Sol Swords.  Thanks also
  to Sandip Ray, who pointed out that this restriction may have been
  in place in order that [47m[defexec][0m can guarantee termination using
  the [47m:exec[0m code; its [documentation] has therefore been updated to
  clarify this situation.

  Rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m are now stored by performing certain
  logical simplifications on the left side of the conclusion: [47m(prog2$
  X Y)[0m is replaced by [47mY[0m, [47m(mbe :logic X :exec Y)[0m is replaced by [47mX[0m
  (more precisely, the analogous change is made to the generated call
  of [47m[must-be-equal][0m); and [47m(the TYPE X)[0m is replaced by [47mX[0m (again, the
  change is actually made on the macroexpanded form).  Thanks to
  Jared Davis and Sol Swords for requesting this change.  An
  analogous change has also been made for rules of class
  [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m.

  The [47m[trace$][0m utility has been reimplemented to work independently of
  the underlying Lisp trace.  It thus works the same for every host
  Lisp, except as provided by an interface to the underlying host
  Lisp trace (the [47m:native[0m option).  Note that the host Lisp trace
  continues to be modified for GCL, Allegro CL, and CCL (OpenMCL);
  see [trace].  See [trace$] for updated detailed documentation on
  tracing options, many of which are new, for example an [47m:evisc-tuple[0m
  option that can be set to [47m:no-print[0m if you want the function traced
  without the usual entry and exit printing.  The previous [47m[trace$][0m
  had some issues, including the following, which have all been
  fixed.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for assistance in determining
  desired functionality of the new [47m[trace$][0m and for helping to test
  it.

      Recursive calls were not always shown in the trace for two reasons.
      (1) Compiler inlining could prevent recursive calls from being
      shown in the trace, in particular in CCL (OpenMCL).  Thanks to
      Jared Davis and Warren Hunt for pointing out this issue and
      requesting a fix, and to Bob Boyer and Gary Byers for relevant
      helpful discussions.  (2) ACL2's algorithm for producing
      executable counterparts prevented tracing of recursive calls
      even after [47m(set-guard-checking :none)[0m.  Thanks to Peter
      Dillinger for requesting a fix.

      It was possible to exploit a bug in the interaction of multiple
      values and trace to prove a contradiction.  An example is in a
      comment in [47m(deflabel note-3-4 ...)[0m in the ACL2 source code.

      Certain large structures could cause expensive computations for
      printing even when a [47m:cond[0m condition was specified and
      evaluated to [47mnil[0m.

      [47m[Trace!][0m now suppresses printing of the event summary, and returns
      the value that would be returned (if there is an active trust
      tag) by the corresponding call of [47m[trace$][0m.

      Some bugs have been fixed in the underlying native trace installed by
      ACL2 for GCL, Allegro CL, and CCL (OpenMCL), including the
      following.  In GCL it had been impossible to use the variable
      [47mARGLIST[0m in a [47m:cond[0m expression.  In Allegro CL and CCL, a
      [47m[trace$][0m bug mishandled tracing non-ACL2 functions when
      directives such as [47m:entry[0m and [47m:exit[0m were supplied.  GCL trace
      now hides the world even when tracing non-ACL2 functions.
      Tracing in CCL no longer causes a Lisp error when untracing a
      traced function defined outside the ACL2 loop; for example
      [47m(trace$ len1)[0m followed by [47m(untrace$ len1)[0m no longer causes an
      error.

  The macro [47mwet[0m has been changed, for the better we think.  see [wet].

  The generation of goals for [forcing-round]s has been changed to
  avoid dropping assumptions formerly deemed ``irrelevant''.  (A
  simple example may be found in a comment in source function
  [47munencumber-assumption[0m, source file [47mprove.lisp[0m.)  Thanks to Jared
  Davis for sending us an example that led us to make this change.

  Modified the implementation of [47m[make-event][0m so that in the
  [certificate] of a book, [47m[local][0m events arising from [47m[make-event][0m
  forms are elided.  For example, if [47m(make-event <form>)[0m expands to
  [47m(local <expanded-form>)[0m, then where the latter had been stored in
  the certificate, now instead [47m(local (value-triple :ELIDED))[0m will be
  stored.  Thanks to Eric Smith for requesting this improvement.  He
  has reported that a preliminary version of this improvement shrunk
  a couple of his [47m.cert[0m files from perhaps 40MB each to about 140K
  each.

  Now, a [47m[table][0m event that sets the entire table, [47m(table tbl nil alist
  :clear)[0m, is redundant (see [redundant-events]) when the supplied
  [47malist[0m is equal to the current value of the table.  Thanks to Peter
  Dillinger for requesting this change.

  The event constructor [47m[progn!][0m now returns the value that is returned
  by evaluation of its final form if no error occurs, except that it
  still returns [47mnil[0m if that final evaluation leaves ACL2 in raw-mode.

  [47m:[0m[47m[Pso][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[psog][0m have been improved so that they show the key
  checkpoint summary at the end of a failed proof.  (For a discussion
  of key checkpoints, see [set-gag-mode].)  As a result, a call of
  [47m[set-checkpoint-summary-limit][0m now affects subsequent evaluation of
  [47m:[0m[47m[pso][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[psog][0m.  In particular, you no longer need to
  reconstruct a proof (by calling [47m[thm][0m or [47m[defthm][0m) in order to see
  key checkpoints that were omitted due to the limit; just call
  [47m[set-checkpoint-summary-limit][0m and then run [47m:pso[0m or [47m:psog[0m.

  The [proof-builder] behaves a little differently under [gag-mode].
  Now, proof-builder commands that call the theorem prover to create
  new proof-builder goals, such as [47mbash[0m and [47minduct[0m (see
  [proof-builder-commands]), will show key checkpoints when in
  [gag-mode].  As before, proof-builder commands [47mpso[0m and [47mpso![0m (and
  now, also [47mpsog[0m) --- see [pso], see [psog], and see [pso!] --- can
  then show the unedited proof log.  However, unlike proof attempts
  done in the ACL2 loop, such proof attempts will not show a summary
  of key checkpoints at the end, because from a prover perspective,
  all such goals were considered to be temporarily ``proved'' by
  giving them ``byes'', to be dispatched by later proof-builder
  commands.

  A little-known feature had been that a [measure] of [47m0[0m was treated as
  though no measure was given.  This has been changed so that now, a
  [measure] of [47mnil[0m is treated as though no measure was given.

  Expanded [47m*acl2-exports*[0m to include every documented symbol whose name
  starts with [47m\"SET-\"[0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for remarking that
  [47m[set-debugger-enable][0m was omitted from [47m*acl2-exports*[0m, which led to
  this change.

  The [trace] mechanism has been improved so that the [47m:native[0m and
  [47m:multiplicity[0m options can be used together for Lisps that support
  the trace [47m:exit[0m keyword.  These Lisps include GCL and Allegro CL,
  whose native trace utilities have been modified for ACL2.  For SBCL
  and CCL (OpenMCL), which use the built-in Lisp mechanism for
  returning multiple values in ACL2 (see [mv]), the use of
  [47m:multiplicity[0m with [47m:native[0m remains unnecessary and will be ignored.
  In support of this change, the modification of native Allegro CL
  tracing for ACL2 was fixed to handle [47m:exit[0m forms correctly that
  involve [47m[mv][0m.

  [31;1mNEW FEATURES[0m

  The command [47m:[0m[47m[redef!][0m is just like [47m:[0m[47m[redef][0m, but prints a warning
  rather than doing a query.  The old version of [47m:redef![0m was for
  system hackers and has been renamed to [47m:[0m[47m[redef+][0m.

  Introduced a new utility for evaluating a function call using the
  so-called executable counterpart --- that is, executing the call in
  the logic rather than in raw Lisp.  See [ec-call].  Thanks to Sol
  Swords for requesting this utility and participating in its
  high-level design.

  See [print-gv] for a new utility that assists with debugging guard
  violations. Thanks to Jared Davis for requesting more tool
  assistance for debugging guard violations.

  Improved the guard violation error message to show the positions of
  the formals, following a suggestion of Peter Dillinger.

  Added new [47m[guard-debug][0m capability to assist in debugging failed
  attempts at [guard] verification.  See [guard-debug].  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for requesting a tool to assist in such debugging and
  to him, Robert Krug, and Sandip Ray for useful discussions.

  New utilities provide the formula to be proved by [47m[verify-guards][0m.
  See [verify-guards-formula] and see [guard-obligation], Thanks to
  Mark Reitblatt for making a request leading to these utilities.
  These utilities can be applied to a term, not just an event name;
  thanks to Peter Dillinger for correspondence that led to this
  extension.

  A new utility causes runes to be printed as lists in proof output
  from simplification, as is done already in proof summaries.  See
  [set-raw-proof-format].  Thanks to Jared Davis for requesting this
  utility.

  An experimental capability allows for parallel evaluation.  See
  [parallelism].  Thanks to David Rager for providing an initial
  implementation of this capability.

  Defined [47m[xor][0m in analogy to [47m[iff][0m.  Thanks to Bob Boyer, Warren Hunt,
  and Sol Swords for providing this definition.

  Improved distributed file [47mdoc/write-acl2-html.lisp[0m so that it can now
  be used to build HTML documentation files for [documentation]
  strings in user [books].  See the comment in the definition of
  macro [47macl2::write-html-file[0m at the end of that file.  Thanks to
  Dave Greve and John Powell for requesting this improvement.

  It is now possible to specify [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m for non-recursive function
  definitions (which can be useful when definitions are automatically
  generated).  See [set-bogus-defun-hints-ok].  Thanks to Sol Swords
  for requesting such a capability.

  Keyword argument [47m:dir[0m is now supported for [47m[rebuild][0m just as it has
  been for [47m[ld][0m.

  We relaxed the criteria for functional substitutions, so that a
  function symbol can be bound to a macro symbol that corresponds to
  a function symbol in the sense of [47m[macro-aliases-table][0m.  So for
  example, a functional substitution can now contain the doublet [47m(f
  +)[0m, where previously it would have been required instead to contain
  [47m(f binary-+)[0m.

  We now allow arbitrary packages in raw mode (see [set-raw-mode]) ---
  thanks to Jared Davis for requesting this enhancement --- and more
  than that, we allow arbitrary Common Lisp in raw mode.  Note
  however that for arbitrary packages, you need to be in raw mode
  when the input is read, not just when the input form is evaluated.

  Two new keywords are supported by the [47m[with-output][0m macro.  A
  [47m:gag-mode[0m keyword argument suppresses some prover output as is done
  by [47m[set-gag-mode][0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for asking for a
  convenient way to set [gag-mode] inside a book, in particular
  perhaps for a single theorem; this keyword provides that
  capability.  A [47m:stack[0m keyword allows sub-[events] of [47m[progn][0m or
  [47m[encapsulate][0m to ``pop'' the effect of a superior [47m[with-output][0m
  call.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for requesting such a feature.
  See [with-output].

  The command [47m[good-bye][0m and its aliases [47m[exit][0m and [47m[quit][0m now all take
  an optional status argument, which provides the Unix exit status
  for the underlying process.  Thanks to Florian Haftmann for sending
  a query to the ACL2 email list that led to this enhancement.

  Keyword commands now work for macros whose argument lists have lambda
  list keywords.  For a macro with a [47mlambda[0m list keyword in its
  argument list, the corresponding keyword command reads only the
  minimum number of required arguments.  See [keyword-commands].

  It is now legal to [47m[declare][0m variables [47mignorable[0m in [47m[let*][0m forms, as
  in [47m(let* ((x (+ a b)) ...) (declare (ignorable x)) ...)[0m.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for requesting this enhancement.

  Added a warning when more than one hint is supplied explicitly for
  the same goal.  It continues to be the case that only the first
  hint applicable to a given goal will be applied, as specified in
  the user-supplied list of [47m:hints[0m followed by the
  [47m[default-hints-table][0m.  Thanks to Mark Reitblatt for sending a
  question that led both to adding this clarification to the
  [documentation] and to adding this warning.

  You may now use [47m[set-non-linear][0m, [47m[set-let*-abstraction][0m,
  [47mset-tainted-ok[0m, and [47m[set-ld-skip-proofs][0m in place of their versions
  ending in ``[47mp[0m''.  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting
  consideration of such a change.  All ``[47mset-[0m'' utilities now have a
  version without the final ``[47mp[0m'' (and most do not have a version
  with the final ``[47mp[0m'').

  Added a \"Loop-Stopper\" warning when a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule is specified
  with a [47m:[0m[47m[loop-stopper][0m field that contains illegal entries that
  will be ignored.  Thanks to Jared Davis for recommending such a
  warning.

  Added a substantial documentation topic that provides a beginner's
  guide to the use of quantification with [47m[defun-sk][0m in ACL2.  Thanks
  to Sandip Ray for contributing this guide, to which we have made
  only very small modifications.  See [quantifier-tutorial].

  [47m[Defun-sk][0m now allows the keyword option [47m:strengthen t[0m, which will
  generate the extra constraint that is generated for the
  corresponding [47mdefchoose[0m event; see [defchoose].  Thanks to Dave
  Greve for suggesting this feature.

  [31;1mBUG FIXES[0m

  Fixed a soundness bug related to the use of [47m[mbe][0m inside
  [47m[encapsulate][0m events.  An example proof of [47mnil[0m (before the fix) is
  in a comment in [47m(deflabel note-3-4 ...)[0m in the ACL2 source code.
  We therefore no longer allow calls of [47m[mbe][0m inside [47m[encapsulate][0m
  events that have non-empty [signature]s.

  Fixed a bug related to the definition of a function supporting the
  macro [47m[value-triple][0m.  Although this bug was very unlikely to
  affect any user, it could be carefully exploited to make ACL2
  unsound:

    (defthm fact
      (equal (caadr (caddr (value-triple-fn '(foo 3) nil nil)))
             'value) ; but it's state-global-let* in the logic
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm contradiction
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use fact :in-theory (disable (value-triple-fn))))
      :rule-classes nil)

  Non-[47m[local][0m definitions of functions or macros are no longer
  considered redundant with built-ins when the built-ins have special
  raw Lisp code, because ACL2 was unsound without this restriction!
  A comment about redundant definitions in source function
  [47mchk-acceptable-defuns[0m shows how one could prove [47mnil[0m without this
  new restriction.  Note that system utility [47m:[0m[47m[redef+][0m removes this
  restriction.

  Although ACL2 already prohibited the use of certain built-in
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions for [47m[verify-termination][0m and during
  macroexpansion, we have computed a much more complete list of
  functions that need such restrictions, the value of constant
  [47m*primitive-program-fns-with-raw-code*[0m.  [This constant was renamed
  [47m*initial-program-fns-with-raw-code*[0m after Version 8.0.]

  Modified what is printed when a proof fails, to indicate more clearly
  which event failed.

  Fixed a problem with [47m[dmr][0m in CCL (OpenMCL) that was causing a raw
  Lisp break after an interrupt in some cases.  Thanks to Gary Byers
  for a suggestion leading to this fix.

  Fixed bugs in [proof-builder] code for dealing with free variables in
  hypotheses.

  Upon an abort, the printing of [47m[pstack][0m and [gag-mode] summary
  information for other than GCL was avoided when inside a call of
  [47m[ld][0m.  This has been fixed.

  (Windows only) Fixed bugs for ACL2 built on SBCL on Windows,
  including one that prevented [47m[include-book][0m parameters [47m:dir :system[0m
  from working, and one that prevented certain compilation.  Thanks
  to Peter Dillinger for bringing these to our attention and
  supplying a fix for the second.  Thanks also to Andrew Gacek for
  bringing [47m[include-book][0m issues to our attention.  Also, fixed
  writing of file [47msaved_acl2[0m at build time so that for Windows,
  Unix-style pathnames are used.

  Fixed a hard Lisp error that could occur with keywords as [47m[table][0m
  names, e.g., [47m(table :a :a nil :put)[0m.  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  bringing this problem to our attention and providing this example.

  Fixed handling of [47m:OR[0m [hints] so that proof attempts under an [47m:OR[0m
  hint do not abort (reverting to induction on the original input
  conjecture) prematurely.  Thanks to Robert Krug for pointing out
  this problem and pointing to a possible initial fix.

  (SBCL and CLISP only) It is now possible to read symbols in the
  [47m\"COMMON-LISP\"[0m package inside the ACL2 command loop (see [lp]).
  This could cause a raw Lisp error in previous versions of ACL2
  whose host Common Lisp was SBCL or CLISP.  Thanks to Peter
  Dillinger for bringing this issue to our attention.

  Fixed a bug that was preventing certain [hints], such as [47m:do-not[0m
  hints, from being used after the application of an [47m:or[0m hint.
  Thanks to Robert Krug for bringing this bug to our attention.

  (Hons version only) Fixed a bug in the interaction of [47m[memoize][0m
  ([47m[hons][0m version only) with event processing, specifically in
  interaction with failures inside a call of [47m[progn][0m or
  [47m[encapsulate][0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this bug to our
  attention and sending an example.  A simplified example may be
  found in a comment in source function [47mtable-cltl-cmd[0m, source file
  [47mhistory-management.lisp[0m; search for ``Version_3.3'' there.

  Fixed [47m[cw-gstack][0m so that its [47m:evisc-tuple[0m is applied to the top
  clause, instead of using [47m(4 5 nil nil)[0m in all cases.  If no
  [47m:evisc-tuple[0m is supplied then [47m(term-evisc-tuple t state)[0m is used
  for the top clause, as it is already used for the rest of the
  stack.

  Fixed a bug in the interaction of [proof-tree]s with [47m:induct[0m hint
  value [47m:otf-flg-override[0m.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for reporting
  this bug and sending an example that evokes it.

  Fixed bugs in [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m and [47m[find-rules-of-rune][0m for the case of rule
  class [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m.  Thanks to Robert Krug and Jared Davis for bringing
  these related bugs to our attention.

  Improved failure messages so that the key checkpoints are printed
  only once when there is a proof failure.  Formerly, a proof failure
  would cause the key checkpoints to be printed for every
  [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[certify-book][0m superior to the proof attempt.

  Fixed a bug in generation of [guard]s for calls of [47m[pkg-witness][0m.
  Thanks to Mark Reitblatt for sending an example showing this bug.
  The bug can be in play when you see the message: ``HARD ACL2 ERROR
  in MAKE-LAMBDA-APPLICATION: Unexpected unbound vars (\"\")''.  A
  distillation of Mark's example that causes this hard error is as
  follows.

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (let ((y x)) (pkg-witness y)))

  The [47m[cond][0m macro now accepts test/value pairs of the form [47m(T val)[0m in
  other than the last position, such as the first such pair in [47m(cond
  (t 1) (nil 2) (t 3))[0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for sending this
  example and pointing out that ACL2 was sometimes printing goals
  that have such a form, and hence cannot be submitted back to ACL2.
  A few macros corresponding to [47m[cond][0m in some books under [47mbooks/rtl[0m
  and [47mbooks/bdd[0m were similarly modified.  (A second change will
  probably not be noticeable, because it doesn't affect the final
  result: singleton [47m[cond][0m clauses now generate a call of [47m[or][0m in a
  single step of macroexpansion, not of [47m[if][0m.  For example, [47m(cond (a)
  (b x) (t y))[0m now expands to [47m(OR A (IF B X Y))[0m instead of [47m(IF A A
  (IF B X Y))[0m.  See the source code for [47mcond-macro[0m for a comment
  about this change.)

  Fixed a bug in the interaction of [proof-builder] command [47mDV[0m,
  including numeric (``diving'') commands, with the [47m[add-binop][0m
  event.  Specifically, if you executed [47m(add-binop mac fn)[0m with [47mfn[0m
  having arity other than 2, a [proof-builder] command such as 3 or
  [47m(dv 3)[0m at a call of [47mmac[0m could have the wrong effect.  We also fixed
  a bug in diving with [47mDV[0m into certain [47mAND[0m and [47mOR[0m calls.  Thanks to
  Mark Reitblatt for bringing these problems to our attention with
  helpful examples.

  Fixed a couple of bugs that were causing an error, ``HARD ACL2 ERROR
  in RENEW-NAME/OVERWRITE''.  Thanks to Sol Swords for bringing the
  first of these bugs to our attention.

  Fixed a bug that could cause [47m[certify-book][0m to fail in certain cases
  where there are [47m[local][0m [47m[make-event][0m forms.

  Fixed a bug in [47m[start-proof-tree][0m that could cause Lisp to hang or
  produce an error.  Thanks to Carl Eastlund for sending an example
  to bring this bug to our attention.

  Fixed a bug in the proof output, which was failing to report cases
  where the current goal simplifies to itself or to a set including
  itself (see [specious-simplification]).

  Fixed a bug in [47m[with-prover-time-limit][0m that was causing a raw Lisp
  error for a bad first argument.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for
  pointing out this bug.

  The following was claimed in [47m:doc[0m [47m[note-3-3][0m, but was not fixed until
  the present release:
  Distributed directory [47mdoc/HTML/[0m now again includes installation
  instructions, in [47mdoc/HTML/installation/installation.html[0m.

  In certain Common Lisp implementations --- CCL (OpenMCL) and
  LispWorks, at least --- an interrupt could leave you in a break
  such that quitting the break would not show the usual summary of
  key checkpoints.  This has been fixed.

  [31;1mNEW AND UPDATED BOOKS[0m

  Updated [47mbooks/clause-processors/SULFA/[0m with a new version from Erik
  Reeber; thanks, Erik.

  Added new books directory [47mtools/[0m from Sol Swords.  See
  [47mbooks/tools/Readme.lsp[0m for a summary of what these books provide.

  The distributed book [47mbooks/misc/file-io.lisp[0m includes a new utility,
  [47mwrite-list![0m, which is like [47mwrite-list[0m except that it calls
  [47m[open-output-channel!][0m instead of [47m[open-output-channel][0m.  Thanks to
  Sandip Ray for requesting this utility and assisting with its
  implementation.

  Added [47mrecord-update[0m macro supplied by Sandip Ray to distributed book
  [47mbooks/misc/records.lisp[0m.

  Sandip Ray has contributed books that prove soundness and
  completeness of different proof strategies used in sequential
  program verification.  Distributed directory [47mbooks/proofstyles/[0m has
  three new directories comprising that contribution: [47msoundness/[0m,
  [47mcompleteness/[0m, and [47mcounterexamples/[0m.  The existing
  [47mbooks/proofstyles/[0m directory has been moved to its subdirectory
  [47minvclock/[0m.

  Jared Davis has contributed a profiling utility for ACL2 built on CCL
  (OpenMCL).  See [47mbooks/misc/oprof.lisp[0m.  Thanks, Jared.

  ACL2 utilities [47m[getprop][0m and [47m[putprop][0m take advantage of
  under-the-hood Lisp (hashed) property lists.  The new book
  [47mbooks/misc/getprop.lisp[0m contains an example showing how this works.

  Added the following new book directories: [47mbooks/paco/[0m, which includes
  a small ACL2-like prover; and [47mbooks/models/jvm/m5[0m, which contains
  the definition of one of the more elaborate JVM models, M5, along
  with other files including JVM program correctness proofs.  See
  files [47mReadme.lsp[0m in these directories, and file [47mREADME[0m in the
  latter.

  Added [3mbooks[0m about sorting in [47mbooks/sorting[0m.  See [47mReadme.lsp[0m in that
  directory for documentation.

  Added book [47mbooks/misc/computed-hint-rewrite.lisp[0m to provide an
  interface to the rewriter for use in computed hints.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for requesting this feature.

  Jared Davis has provided a pseudorandom number generator, in
  [47mbooks/misc/random.lisp[0m.

  Robert Krug has contributed a new library, [47mbooks/arithmetic-4/[0m, for
  reasoning about arithmetic.  He characterizes it as being more
  powerful than its predecessor, [47mbooks/arithmetic-3/[0m, and without its
  predecessor's rewriting loops, but significantly slower than its
  predecessor on some theorems.

  Incorporated changes from Peter Dillinger to verify guards for
  functions in [47mbooks/ordinals/lexicographic-ordering.lisp[0m (and one in
  [47mordinal-definitions.lisp[0m in that directory).

  A new directory, [47mbooks/hacking/[0m, contains a library for those who
  wish to use trust tags to modify or extend core ACL2 behavior.
  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for contributing this library.  Obsolete
  version [47mbooks/misc/hacker.lisp[0m has been deleted.  Workshop
  contribution [47mbooks/workshops/2007/dillinger-et-al/code/[0m is still
  included with the workshops/ tar file, but should be considered
  deprecated.

  In [47mbooks/make-event/assert.lisp[0m, changed [47massert![0m and [47massert!-stobj[0m to
  return [47m(value-triple :success)[0m upon success instead of
  [47m(value-triple nil)[0m, following a suggestion from Jared Davis.

  [31;1mEMACS SUPPORT[0m

  Changed [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m so that the fill column default (for the
  right margin) is only set (still to 79) in lisp-mode.

  Modified Emacs support in file [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m so that names of
  events are highlighted just as [47m[defun][0m has been highlighted when it
  is called.  Search in the above file for [47mfont-lock-add-keywords[0m for
  instructions on how to eliminate this change.

  The name of the temporary file used by some Emacs utilities defined
  in file [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m has been changed to have extension [47m.lsp[0m
  instead of [47m.lisp[0m; thus it is now [47mtemp-emacs-file.lsp[0m.  Also, `make'
  commands to `clean' books will delete such files (specifically,
  [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m has been changed to delete
  [47mtemp-emacs-file.lsp[0m).")
 (NOTE-3-4{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.4(r) (August, 2008) Notes

  Please also see [note-3-4] for changes to Version 3.4 of ACL2.

  Fixed makefiles, [47mbooks/nonstd/Makefile[0m and [47mGNUmakefile[0m.  The old
  set-up seemed to work fine as long as all books certified, but it
  was really broken, for example only certifying some of the books in
  [47mbooks/nonstd/nsa/[0m, and then only when required by books in other
  directories.  Also fixed the ``[47mclean[0m'' target to clean links rather
  than to make links.")
 (NOTE-3-5
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.5 (May, 2009) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Below we roughly organize the changes since Version 3.4 into the
  following categories: changes to existing features, new features,
  heuristic improvements, bug fixes, new and updated books, Emacs
  support, and experimental [47m[hons][0m version.  Each change is described
  in just one category, though of course many changes could be placed
  in more than one category.

  [31;1mCHANGES TO EXISTING FEATURES[0m

  Many improvements have been made to ACL2's ``evisceration'' mechanism
  for hiding substructures of objects before they are printed, and to
  related documentation:

      o A new documentation topic explains evisc-tuples.  See
      [evisc-tuple].

      o A new interface, [47m[set-evisc-tuple][0m, has been provided for setting
      the four global evisc-tuples.  See [set-evisc-tuple].

      o A new mode, ``iprinting'', allows eviscerated output to be read
      back in.  See [set-iprint].

      o Function [47mdefault-evisc-tuple[0m has been deprecated and will probably
      be eliminated in future releases; use [47mabbrev-evisc-tuple[0m
      instead.  Also eliminated is the brr-term-evisc-tuple (also the
      user-brr-term-evisc-tuple).  The term-evisc-tuple controls
      printing formerly controlled by the brr-term-evisc-tuple or
      user-brr-term-evisc-tuple.

      o ACL2 output is done in a more consistent manner, respecting the
      intention of those four global evisc-tuples.  In particular,
      more proof output is sensitive to the term-evisc-tuple.  Again,
      see [set-evisc-tuple].

      o A special value, [47m:DEFAULT[0m, may be provided to [47m[set-evisc-tuple][0m in
      order to restore these [evisc-tuple]s to their original
      settings.

      o (Details for heavy users of the evisc-tuple mechanism) (1) There
      are no longer [state] globals named [47muser-term-evisc-tuple[0m or
      [47muser-default-evisc-tuple[0m.  (2) Because of the above-mentioned
      [47m:DEFAULT[0m, if you have referenced state globals directly, you
      should use accessors instead, for example [47m(abbrev-evisc-tuple
      state)[0m instead of [47m(@ abbrev-evisc-tuple)[0m.  (3) For uniformity,
      [47m[set-trace-evisc-tuple][0m now takes a second argument, [47mstate[0m.

  Improved [47m[break-on-error][0m in several ways.  First, it breaks earlier
  in a more appropriate place.  Thanks to Dave Greve for highlighting
  this problem with the existing implementation.  Also,
  [47m[break-on-error][0m now breaks on hard errors, not only soft errors
  (see [er], options [47mhard[0m and [47mhard?[0m).  Thanks to Warren Hunt and Anna
  Slobodova for sending an example that showed a flaw in an initial
  improvement.  Finally, new options cause printing of the call stack
  for some host Common Lisps.  See [break-on-error].  Thanks to Bob
  Boyer for requesting this feature.

  [47m[Trace!][0m may now be used in raw Lisp (though please note that all
  soundness claims are off any time you evaluate forms in raw Lisp!).
  Thanks to Bob Boyer for feedback that led to this enhancement.

  ACL2 now searches for file [47macl2-customization.lsp[0m in addition to (and
  just before) its existing search for [47macl2-customization.lisp[0m; See
  [ACL2-customization].  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting this
  change, which supports the methodology that files with a [47m.lisp[0m
  extension are certifiable books (thus avoiding the need to set the
  [47mBOOKS[0m variable in makefiles; see [books-certification-classic]).

  Improved the error message for illegal [47m[declare][0m forms of the form
  [47m(type (satisfies ...))[0m.  Thanks to Dave Greve for sending an
  example highlighting the issue.

  If trace output is going to a file (because [47m[open-trace-file][0m has
  been executed), then a note will be printed to that effect at the
  time that a call of [47m[trace$][0m or [47m[trace!][0m is applied to one or more
  [trace] specs.

  The notion of redundancy (see [redundant-events]) has been made more
  restrictive for [47m[mutual-recursion][0m events.  Now, if either the old
  or new event is a [47m[mutual-recursion][0m event, then redundancy
  requires that both are [47m[mutual-recursion][0m events that define the
  same set of function symbols.  Although we are not aware of any
  soundness bugs fixed by this modification, nevertheless we believe
  that it reduces the risk of soundness bugs in the future.

  The definition of [47mtrace*[0m has been moved to a book, [47mmisc/trace1.lisp[0m.
  A new version, used in ACL2s, is in book [47mmisc/trace-star.lisp[0m.
  [Trace] utilities [47m[trace$][0m and [47m[trace!][0m are still built into ACL2.
  [Note: File [47mmisc/trace1.lisp[0m was deleted after Version 4.2.]

  Certain [certificate] files will now be much smaller, by printing in
  a way that takes advantage of structure sharing.  Certifying the
  following example produces a [47m.cert[0m file of over 3M before this
  change, but less than 1K after the change.

    (defun nest (i)
      ;; Makes an exponentially-sized nest of conses i deep.
      (if (zp i)
          nil
        (let ((next (nest (1- i))))
          (cons next next))))

    (make-event
     `(defconst *big* ',(nest 20)))

  Thanks to Sol Swords for providing the above example and to him as
  well as to Bob Boyer, Jared Davis, and Warren Hunt for encouraging
  development of this improvement.  We have also applied this
  improvement to the printing of function definitions to files on
  behalf of [47m[certify-book][0m and [47m[comp][0m.

  Names of symbols are now printed with vertical bars according to the
  Common Lisp spec.  Formerly, if the first character of a symbol
  name could be the first character of the print representation of a
  number, then the symbol was printed using vertical bars ([47m|..|[0m)
  around its name.  Now, a much more restrictive test for ``potential
  numbers'' is used, which can result in fewer such vertical bars.
  Base 16 is now carefully considered as well; see [set-print-base].
  Thanks to Bob Boyer for requesting this improvement.  Note that
  macros [47mset-acl2-print-base[0m and [47mset-acl2-print-case[0m have been
  replaced by functions; see [set-print-base] and see
  [set-print-case].

  The ACL2 reader now supports `[47m#.[0m' syntax in place of the `[47m#,[0m syntax
  formerly supported.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for requesting this
  change.  See [sharp-dot-reader].  NOTE that because of this change,
  `[47m#.[0m' no longer causes an abort; instead please use [47m(a!)[0m or
  optionally, if in the ACL2 loop, [47m:a![0m; see [a!].

  Some small changes have been made related to [gag-mode]:

      o [Gag-mode] now suppresses some messages that were being printed
      upon encountering disjunctive splits from [47m:OR[0m [hints].  Thanks
      to Sol Swords for suggesting this improvement.

      o ACL2 had printed ``[47mQ.E.D.[0m'' with all output suppressed and
      [47m[gag-mode][0m enabled.  Now, ``[47mQ.E.D.[0m'' will be suppressed when
      [47mPROVE[0m and [47mSUMMARY[0m output are suppressed, even if [47mgag-mode[0m is
      enabled.

      o The use of [47m[set-gag-mode][0m had drastic effects on the inhibited
      output (see [set-inhibit-output-lst]), basically inhibiting
      nearly all output (even most warnings) when turning on gag-mode
      and enabling all output except [47mproof-tree[0m output when turning
      off gag-mode.  Now, [47m[set-gag-mode][0m only inhibits or enables
      proof ([47mPROVE[0m) output, according to whether gag-mode is being
      turned on or off (respectively).  The related utility
      [47mset-saved-output[0m has also been modified, basically to eliminate
      [47m:all[0m as a first argument and to allow [47mt[0m and [47m:all[0m as second
      arguments, for inhibiting prover output or virtually all
      output, respectively (see set-saved-output).

  A [47m[defstub][0m event [signature] specifying output of the form [47m(mv ...)[0m
  now introduces a [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule asserting that the new
  function returns a [47m[true-listp][0m result.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for
  sending the following example, which motivated this change.

    (defstub census (*) => (mv * *))
    (defn foo (x)
      (mv-let (a1 a2)
              (census x)
              (list a1 a2)))

  Improved the efficiency of [47m[string-append][0m so that in raw Lisp, it
  calls [47m[concatenate][0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting this
  change, including the use of [47m[mbe][0m.  A minor change was made to the
  definition of [47m[concatenate][0m to support this change, and the lemma
  [47mappend-to-nil[0m was added (see below).

  The checksum algorithm used for [certificate] files of [books] has
  been changed.  Thanks to Jared Davis for contributing the new code.
  This change will likely not be noticed unless one is using the
  experimental [47m[hons][0m version of ACL2, where it can greatly speed up
  book certification and inclusion because of function memoization
  (of source function [47mfchecksum-obj[0m).

  Fewer calls are made to the checksum algorithm on behalf of
  [47m[certify-book][0m and a few other operations.  Thanks to Jared Davis
  for providing data that helped lead us to these changes.

  Formatted printing directives [47m~p[0m, [47m~q[0m, [47m~P[0m, and [47m~Q[0m are deprecated,
  though still supported.  See [fmt].  Instead, please use [47m~x[0m, [47m~y[0m,
  [47m~X[0m, and [47m~Y[0m (respectively).  As a by-product, rule names in proof
  output are no longer hyphenated.

  A new keyword, [47m:multiplicity[0m, is available for tracing raw Lisp
  functions using the ACL2 [trace] utility.  See [trace$].

  Users may now control whether or not a slow array access results in a
  warning printed to the screen (which is the default, as before),
  and if so, whether or not the warning is followed by a break.  See
  [slow-array-warning].

  On linux-like systems (including Mac OS X and SunOS), [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m will
  now write its temporary files into the [47m\"/tmp\"[0m directory, which is
  the value of [47m[state][0m global [47m'tmp-dir[0m.  You can change that
  directory with [47m(assign tmp-dir \"<your_temp_directory_path>\")[0m.

  The messages printed for uncertified books have been enhanced.
  Thanks to Jared Davis for requesting such an improvement.

  A function definition that would be redundant if in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode is
  now considered redundant even if it (the new definition) is in
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode.  That is, if a definition is ``downgraded'' from
  [47m:logic[0m to [47m:program[0m mode, the latter ([47m:program[0m mode) definition is
  considered redundant.  Previously, such redundancy was disallowed,
  but we have relaxed that restriction because of a scenario brought
  to our attention by Jared Davis: include a book with the [47m:logic[0m
  mode definition, and then include a book with the [47m:program[0m mode
  definition followed by [47m[verify-termination][0m.  Thanks, Jared.

  The ACL2 reader no longer accepts characters other than those
  recognized by [47m[standard-char-p][0m except for [47m#\\Tab[0m, [47m#\\Page[0m, and
  [47m#\\Rubout[0m (though it still accepts strings containing such
  characters).  As a result, no [47m[make-event][0m expansion is allowed to
  contain any such unacceptable character or string.  Thanks to Sol
  Swords for sending an example that led us to make this restriction.
  A simple example is the following book:

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defconst *my-null* (code-char 0))
    (make-event `(defconst *new-null* ,*my-null*))

  For this book, a call of [47m[certify-book][0m formerly broke during the
  compilation phase, but if there was no compilation, then a call of
  [47m[include-book][0m broke.  Now, the error occurs upon evaluation of the
  [47m[make-event][0m form.

  ACL2 now collects up [guard]s from [47m[declare][0m forms more as a user
  might expect, without introducing an unexpected ordering of
  conjuncts.  We thank Jared Davis for sending us the following
  illustrative example, explained below.

    (defun f (x n)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (stringp x)
                                  (natp n)
                                  (= (length x) n)))
               (type string x)
               (ignore x n))
      t)

  Formerly, a guard was generated for this example by unioning the
  conjuncts from the [47m:guard[0m onto a list containing the term [47m(string
  x)[0m generated from the [47mtype[0m declaration, resulting in an effective
  guard of:

    (and (natp n)
         (= (length x) n)
         (stringp x))

  The guard of this guard failed to be verified because [47m(stringp x))[0m
  now comes after the call [47m(length x)[0m.  With the fix, contributions
  to the guards are collected up in the order in which they appear.
  So in the above example, the effective guard is the specified
  [47m:guard[0m; the contribution [47m(stringp x)[0m comes later, and is thus
  dropped since it is redundant.  NOTE by the way that if [47m:guard[0m and
  [47m:stobjs[0m are specified in the same [47m[xargs][0m form, then for purposes
  of collecting up the effective guard as described above, [47m:stobjs[0m
  will be treated as though it comes before the [47m:guard[0m.

  Modified [47m[close-output-channel][0m to try to do a better job flushing
  buffers.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for helpful correspondence.

  The notion of ``subversive recursion'' has been modified so that some
  functions are no longer marked as subversive.  See
  [subversive-recursions], in particular the discussion elaborating
  on the notion of ``involved in the termination argument'' at the
  end of that [documentation] topic.

  Formerly, [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules for new definitions inside
  [47m[encapsulate][0m forms were sometimes added as [constraint]s.  This is
  no longer the case.  See also discussion of the ``soundness bug in
  the forming of constraints'', which is related.

  [31;1mNEW FEATURES[0m

  It is now possible to affect ACL2's termination analysis (and
  resulting induction analysis).  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for
  requesting this feature.  The default behavior is essentially
  unchanged.  But for example, the following definition is accepted
  by ACL2 because of the use of the new [47m:ruler-extenders[0m features;
  See [ruler-extenders].

    (defun f (x)
      (declare (xargs :ruler-extenders :all))
      (cons 3
            (if (consp x)
                (f (cdr x))
              nil)))

  The following lemma was added in support of the improvement to
  [47m[string-append][0m described above:

    (defthm append-to-nil
      (implies (true-listp x)
               (equal (append x nil)
                      x)))

  A mechanism has been provided for users to contribute documentation.
  See [managing-ACL2-packages] for an example, which contains a link
  to an external web page on effective use of ACL2 packages, kindly
  provided by Jared Davis.  ACL2 [documentation] strings may now link
  to external web pages using the new symbol, [47m~url[0m; see markup.  Of
  course, those links appear in the web version of the documentation,
  but you made need to take a bit of action in order for these to
  appear as links in the Emacs Info version; see [documentation].

  Added [47m[intersectp][0m (similar to [47m[intersectp-eq][0m and
  [47m[intersectp-equal][0m).

  The user now has more control over how ACL2 prints forms; See
  [print-control].  Thanks to Bob Boyer for useful discussions
  leading to this enhancement.

  Some Common Lisp implementations only allow the syntax
  [47mpkg-name::expression[0m when [47mexpression[0m is a symbol.  The ACL2 reader
  has been modified to support a package prefix for arbitrary
  expressions; see [sharp-bang-reader].  Thanks to Hanbing Liu for a
  query that led to this feature and to Pascal J. Bourguignon for
  suggesting an implementation.

  Ill-formed [documentation] strings need not cause an error.  See
  [47mset-ignore-doc-string-error[0m.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for requesting
  this feature.

  Type declarations are now permitted in [47mlet*[0m forms; see [let*], see
  [declare], and see [type-spec].

  (For Lisp programmers) Macro [47mwith-live-state[0m has been provided for
  programmers who refer to free variable [47mSTATE[0m, for example with
  macros that generate uses of [47mSTATE[0m, and want to avoid compiler
  warnings when evaluating in raw Lisp.  For example, the following
  form can be submitted either inside or outside the ACL2 loop to get
  the desired effect (see [47mdoc-string[0m): [47m(with-live-state (f-put-global
  'doc-prefix \" \" state))[0m.  For another example use of this macro,
  see the definition of [47mtrace$[0m (ACL2 source file [47mother-events.lisp[0m).

  (System hackers only) Added [47m:[0m[47m[redef-][0m to undo the effect of
  [47m:[0m[47m[redef+][0m.  See [redef-].

  Function [47m[random$][0m is a built-in random number generator.  See
  [random$].  Thanks to Sol Swords for requesting this feature and
  providing an initial implementation.

  [31;1mHEURISTIC IMPROVEMENTS[0m

  Sped up [guard] generation for some functions with large if-then-else
  structures in their bodies.  Thanks to Sol Swords for sending an
  illustrative example.

  Sped up [guard] generation in some cases by evaluating ground
  (variable-free) subexpressions.  Thanks to Bob Boyer for sending a
  motivating example: [47m(defn foo (x) (case x ((1 2) 1) ((3 4) 3) ...
  ((999 1000) 999)))[0m.

  Modified slightly a heuristic association of ``size'' with constants,
  which can result in significant speed-ups in proofs involving
  constants that are very large [47mcons[0m trees.

  Added a restriction in the linear arithmetic procedure for deleting
  polynomial inequalities from the linear database.  Formerly, an
  inequality could be deleted if it was implied by another
  inequality.  Now, such deletion requires that certain heuristic
  ``parent tree'' information is at least as restrictive for the
  weaker inequality as for the stronger.  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  bringing a relevant example to our attention and working with us to
  figure out some surprising behavior, and to Robert Krug for making
  a key observation leading to the fix.

  (GCL especially) Improved compiled code slightly by communicating to
  raw Lisp the output type when a function body is of the form [47m(the
  character ...)[0m.  This tiny improvement will probably only be
  observed in GCL, if at all.

  Applied a correction suggested by Robert Krug to the variant of
  [47m[term-order][0m used in parts of ACL2's arithmetic reasoning.

  [31;1mBUG FIXES[0m

  Fixed bugs in the handling of [47m[flet][0m expressions, one of which had
  the capability of rendering ACL2 unsound.  Thanks to Sol Swords for
  pointing out two issues and sending examples.  One example
  illustrated how ACL2 was in essence throwing away outer [47m[flet][0m
  bindings when processing an inner [47mflet[0m.  We have exploited that
  example to prove a contradiction, as follows: this book was
  certifiable before this fix.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")

    (defun a (x)
      (list 'c x))

    ; Example from Sol Swords, which failed to be admitted (claiming that
    ; function A is undefined) without the above definition of A.
    (defun foo1 (x y)
       (flet ((a (x) (list 'a x)))
         (flet ((b (y) (list 'b y)))
           (b (a (list x y))))))

    (defthm not-true
      (equal (foo1 3 4)
             '(b (c (3 4))))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
               :in-theory
               (disable (:executable-counterpart foo1))))
      :rule-classes nil)

    (defthm contradiction
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use not-true))
      :rule-classes nil)

  Sol's second example, below, pointed to a second bug related to
  computing output signatures in the presence of nested [47mflet[0m
  expressions, which we have also fixed: this form failed before the
  fix.

    :trans (flet ((foo (a) (list (flet ((bar (b) b)) a)))) x)

  Fixed a subtle soundness bug in the forming of constraints from
  deduced type prescriptions.  As a result, when ACL2 prints a
  warning message labeling encapsulated functions as ``subversive'',
  ACL2 will no longer deduce [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules for those
  functions.  Examples that exploit the bug in ACL2 Version_3.4 may
  be found in comments in ACL2 source function
  [47mconvert-type-set-to-term[0m (file [47mother-processes.lisp[0m) and especially
  in function [47mputprop-type-prescription-lst[0m (file [47mdefuns.lisp[0m).  For
  more on the general issue of ``subversive recursions,'' see
  [subversive-recursions].)

  Fixed a soundness bug in the handling of inequalities by the
  [type-set] mechanism, which was using the inequality database
  inside the body of a [47m[lambda][0m.

  Fixed a long-standing soundness bug in [47m[compress1][0m and [47m[compress2][0m,
  whose raw Lisp code gave the logically incorrect result in the case
  of a single entry other than the [47m[header][0m, where that entry mapped
  an index to the [47m[default][0m value.  Also fixed soundness bugs in
  [47m[compress1][0m, in the case of [47m:order >[0m, where the raw Lisp code could
  drop the [47m[header][0m from the result or, when the input alist had
  entries in ascending order, fail to return an alist in descending
  order.  For example, the following book certified successfully.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defthm true-formula-1
      (equal (compress1 'a '((:HEADER :DIMENSIONS (4) :MAXIMUM-LENGTH 5
                                      :DEFAULT 1 :NAME A :ORDER <)
                             (1 . 1)))
             '((:HEADER :DIMENSIONS (4) :MAXIMUM-LENGTH 5
                        :DEFAULT 1 :NAME A :ORDER <)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable (compress1))))
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm ouch-1
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use true-formula-1))
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm true-formula-2
      (equal (compress1 'a '((:HEADER :DIMENSIONS (4) :MAXIMUM-LENGTH 5
                                      :DEFAULT NIL :NAME A :ORDER >)
                             (1 . 1)
                             (2 . 2)))
             '((:HEADER :DIMENSIONS (4) :MAXIMUM-LENGTH 5
                        :DEFAULT NIL :NAME A :ORDER >)
               (2 . 2)
               (1 . 1)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable (compress1))))
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm ouch-2
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use true-formula-2))
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm true-formula-3
      (equal (compress1 'a '((:HEADER :DIMENSIONS (3) :MAXIMUM-LENGTH 4
                                      :NAME A :ORDER >)
                             (1 . B)
                             (0 . A)))
             '((:HEADER :DIMENSIONS (3) :MAXIMUM-LENGTH 4
                        :NAME A :ORDER >)
               (1 . B)
               (0 . A)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable (compress1))))
      :rule-classes nil)
    (defthm ouch-3
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use true-formula-3))
      :rule-classes nil)

  Fixed a soundness bug involving measured subsets and
  [47m[verify-termination][0m, by changing [47m[verify-termination][0m so that it
  uses [47m[make-event][0m.  See [verify-termination], in particular the
  discussion about [47m[make-event][0m near the end of that [documentation]
  topic.  Peter Dillinger first raised the idea to us of making such
  a change; when we found this soundness bug, we were certainly happy
  to do so!

  Fixed a bug that could cause a hard Lisp error but not, apparently,
  unsoundness.  The bug was in the lack of attention to the order of
  guard and type declarations when checking for redundancy.  In the
  following example, the second definition was redundant during the
  first pass of the [47m[encapsulate][0m form.  The second definition,
  however, was stored on the second pass with a guard of [47m(and (consp
  (car x)) (consp x))[0m, which caused a hard Lisp error when evaluating
  [47m(foo 3)[0m due to a misguided attempt to evaluate [47m(car 3)[0m in raw Lisp.
  The fix is to restrict redundancy of definitions so that the guard
  and type declarations must be in the same order for the two
  definitions.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (local (defun foo (x)
              (declare (xargs :guard (consp x)))
              (declare (xargs :guard (consp (car x))))
              x))
     (defun foo (x)
       (declare (xargs :guard (consp (car x))))
       (declare (xargs :guard (consp x)))
       x))
    ; Now we get a hard Lisp error from evaluation of the guard of foo:
    (foo 3)

  Fixed a bug in the guard violation report for function
  [47m[intern-in-package-of-symbol][0m.  Thanks to Dave Greve for bringing
  this bug to our attention.

  Made a change to allow certain [hints], in particular certain
  [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m hints, that had previously caused errors during
  termination proofs by viewing the function being defined as not yet
  existing.  Thanks to Sol Swords for bringing this issue to our
  attention with a nice example.

  ACL2 now properly handles interrupts (via control-c) issued during
  printing of the checkpoint summary.  Previously it was possible on
  some platforms to make ACL2 hang when interrupting both during a
  proof and during the ensuing printing of the checkpoint summary.
  Thanks to Jared Davis and Sol Swords for bringing this problem to
  our attention.

  Fixed a bug that was preventing, inside some book [47m\"b\"[0m, the use of a
  [47m:dir[0m argument to [47m[include-book][0m that refers to a directory defined
  using [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m earlier in the book [47m\"b\"[0m.  (We found
  this ourselves, but we thank John Cowles for observing it
  independently and sending us a nice example.)

  (GCL and CCL only) Fixed a bug in certain under-the-hood type
  inferencing.  Thanks to Sol Swords for sending an example using
  [stobj]s defined with the [47m:inline[0m keyword, along with a helpful
  backtrace in CCL, and to Gary Byers for his debugging help.

  Fixed a bug in [47m[print-gv][0m, which was mishandling calls of functions
  with more than one argument.

  Fixed a bug in the handling of [47m[and][0m and [47m[or][0m terms by the
  [proof-builder] command [47mDV[0m, including numeric (``diving'')
  commands.  Thanks for Mark Reitblatt for bringing this problems to
  our attention with a helpful example.

  Fixed printing of goal names resulting from the application of [47m:OR[0m
  [hints] so that they aren't ugly when working in other than the
  [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package.  Thanks to Sol Swords for bringing this issue to
  our attention.

  Fixed [proof-tree] printing so that interrupts will not cause
  problems with hiding ordinary output because of incomplete
  proof-tree output.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for pointing out this
  issue.

  Fixed a hard error that could be caused by mishandling a [47m[force][0md
  hypothesis during [forward-chaining].  Thanks to Peter Dillinger
  for bringing this bug to our attention by sending a useful example.

  Fixed a bug that could cause simplifications to fail because of
  alleged ``specious simplification.'' This bug could appear when
  deriving an equality from the linear arithmetic database, and then
  attempting to add this equality to the current goal's hypotheses
  when it was already present.  Thanks to Eric Smith for sending a
  helpful example (in July 2005!) that helped us debug this issue.

  Fixed a bug in processing of [47m:[0m[47m[type-set-inverter][0m rules.

  Fixed a bug that was causing an error, at least for an underlying
  Lisp of CCL (OpenMCL), when [47m[ec-call][0m was applied to a term
  returning multiple values.  Thanks to Sol Swords for sending an
  example that brought this bug to our attention.

  Fixed handling of array orders to treat keyword value [47m:order :none[0m
  correctly from an array's [47m[header][0m.  Previously, there were two
  problems.  One problem was that [47m:order :none[0m was treated like the
  default for [47m:order[0m, [47m<[0m, while [47m:order nil[0m was treated in the manner
  specified by [47m:order :none[0m (see [arrays]).  Now, both [47m:order :none[0m
  and [47m:order nil[0m are treated as [47m:order nil[0m had been treated, i.e., so
  that there is no reordering of the alist by [47m[compress1][0m.  The other
  problem with this case of [47m:order[0m was that the [47m:maximum-length[0m field
  of the [47m[header][0m was not being respected: the length could grow
  without bound.  Now, as previously explained (but not previously
  implemented) --- see [arrays] --- a [47m[compress1][0m call made on behalf
  of [47maset1[0m causes a hard error if the header of the supplied array
  specifies an [47m:order[0m of [47m:none[0m or [47mnil[0m.

  An [47mignorable[0m [47m[declare][0m form had caused an error in some contexts when
  it should have been allowed.  In particular, this problem could
  arise when using an [47mignorable[0m declaration at the top level in a
  [47m[defabbrev][0m form.  It could also arise upon calling
  [47m[verify-termination][0m when the corresponding [47m[defun][0m form contained
  an [47mignorable[0m declaration at the top level.  These bugs have been
  fixed.

  Contrary to existing documentation (see [make-event-details]), the
  value of ``[47m[ld][0m special variable'' [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m was always set
  to [47mnil[0m during [47m[make-event][0m expansion, not merely when the
  [47mmake-event[0m form has a non-[47mnil[0m value for keyword parameter
  [47m:check-expansion[0m.  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Sol Swords for
  bringing this issue to our attention.

  We have disallowed the certification of a book when not at the
  top-level, either directly in the top-level loop or at the top
  level of [47m[ld][0m.  Before this restriction, the following would
  certify a book with a definition such as [47m(defun foo (x) (h x))[0m that
  calls function [47mh[0m before defining it, if the certification was by
  way of the form such as:

    (er-progn (defun h (x) x) (certify-book \"my-book\"))

  But a subsequent [47m[include-book][0m of [47m\"my-book\"[0m would then fail, because
  [47mh[0m is not defined at the top level.

  Printing with [47m[fmt][0m directive [47m~c[0m now works properly even when the
  print-base is other than 10.  Thanks to Sol Swords for reporting
  this bug and providing a fix for it.

  (SBCL, CMUCL, and CCL only) Fixed a bug in [47m[sys-call-status][0m in the
  case that the underlying Common Lisp is SBCL, CMUCL, or CCL
  (OpenMCL).  Thanks to Jun Sawada for bringing this bug to our
  attention and providing a fix.

  Fixed a bug that was preventing [47m[local][0m [47m[defstobj][0m events in
  [47m[encapsulate][0m events.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this bug
  to our attention.

  Fixed a bug evidenced by error message ``Unexpected form in
  certification world'', which could result from attempting to
  certify a book after evaluating an [47m[encapsulate][0m form with a local
  [47m[defmacro][0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis for pointing out this bug and
  sending the example:

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (local (defmacro foo (x) `(table foo 'bar ,x)))
     (local (foo 3)))

  Formerly, evaluating a [47m[trace$][0m form inside a [wormhole] such as the
  [break-rewrite] loop could leave the user in a bad state after
  returning to the top level, in which that function could not be
  untraced.  This has been fixed.  Note however that when you proceed
  from a break in the [break-rewrite] loop, the tracing state will be
  the same as it was when you entered that break: all effects of
  calling [47m[trace$][0m and [47m[untrace$][0m are erased when you proceed from
  the break.

  A [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m of [47m(and)[0m is no longer ignored.  Thanks to Sol Swords for
  bringing this bug to our attention.

  A bug has been fixed that could result in needlessly weak induction
  schemes in the case that a recursive call is made in the first
  argument of [47m[prog2$][0m.  This has been fixed by including [47m[prog2$][0m as
  a default ruler-extender in the new ruler-extenders feature (see
  above, and see [ruler-extenders]).  For details on this bug see
  Example 11 in distributed book
  [47mbooks/misc/misc2/ruler-extenders-tests.lisp[0m.

  (For CCL/OpenMCL on Windows) ACL2 should now build on CCL (OpenMCL)
  on Windows.  Thanks to David Rager for bringing this issue to our
  attention and helping with a fix that worked for CCL 1.2, and to
  the CCL team for improving handling of Windows-style filenames in
  CCL 1.3.

  [31;1mNEW AND UPDATED BOOKS[0m

  See {ReleaseVersionNumbers |
  https://code.google.com/p/acl2-books/wiki/ReleaseVersionNumbers}
  for a list of books in Version 3.5 of ACL2 but not Version 3.4.

  Run the shell command

     [47msvn log -r 94:HEAD[0m

  to see all changes to [47mbooks/[0m since the release of Version 3.4.

  Here are just a few highlights.

  Thanks largely to Jared Davis, many [47mMakefile[0ms have been improved to
  do automatic dependency analysis.  See
  [books-certification-classic] for how to get your own [47mMakefile[0ms to
  do this by adding a line: [47m-include Makefile-deps[0m.

  Libraries [47mbooks/arithmetic-4/[0m and [47mbooks/rtl/rel7/[0m have been
  eliminated from the default book certification ([47mmake regression[0m),
  in favor of new libraries [47mbooks/arithmetic-5/[0m and [47mbooks/rtl/rel8/[0m
  contributed by Robert Krug and Hanbing Liu, respectively.  They and
  Jun Sawada have arranged the compatibility of these libraries;
  i.e., it is possible to evaluate both of the following in the same
  session:

    (include-book \"arithmetic-5/top\" :dir :system)
    (include-book \"rtl/rel8/lib/top\" :dir :system)

  Library [47mbooks/rtl/rel1/[0m is no longer certified by default (though it
  is still distributed in support of ACL2(r); see [real]).

  [31;1mEMACS SUPPORT[0m

  Slightly modified [47mControl-t e[0m (defined in [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m) to
  comprehend the notion of an ``ACL2 scope'', and added [47mControl-t o[0m
  to insert a superior [47m[encapsulate][0m defining such a scope.  See the
  Emacs documentation for [47mControl-t e[0m (generally obtained after
  typing [47mControl-h k[0m).

  Modified distributed file [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m so that if you put the
  following two forms in your [47m~/.emacs[0m file above the form that loads
  [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m, then Emacs will not start up a shell.  Thanks
  to Terry Parks for leading us to this modification.

    (defvar acl2-skip-shell nil)
    (setq acl2-skip-shell t)

  [31;1mEXPERIMENTAL HONS VERSION[0m

  Bob Boyer and others have contributed numerous changes for the
  experimental ``[47mhons[0m'' version of ACL2 (see [hons-and-memoization]).

  The ACL2 [47m[state][0m can now be queried with [47mhons-enabledp[0m so that a
  result of [47mt[0m says that one is in the experimental [47mhons[0m version,
  while [47mnil[0m says the opposite.")
 (NOTE-3-5{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.5(r) (May, 2009) Notes

  Please also see [note-3-5] for changes in Version 3.5 of ACL2.

  This release incorporates improvements from Ruben Gamboa in support
  for non-standard analysis in ACL2(r), in the following ways:

  ACL2(r) now supports non-classical objects that are not also numeric,
  e.g., non-classical cons pairs.  Consequently, the built-in
  [47mstandard-numberp[0m has been replaced with [47m[standardp][0m.

  If [47mf[0m is a classical function, the value [47m(f x1 ... xn)[0m is guaranteed
  to be standard when the [47mxi[0m are standard.  ACL2(r) can now recognize
  this fact automatically, using [47mdefun-std[0m.  For example, the
  following can be used to assert that the square root of 2 is a
  standard value.

    (defthm-std sqrt-2-rational
      (standardp (acl2-sqrt 2)))

  More generally, the expression [47m(f x1 ... xn)[0m can contain free
  variables, but the result is guaranteed to be standard when the
  variables take on standard variables, as in the following:

    (defthm-std sqrt-x-rational
      (implies (standardp x)
               (standardp (acl2-sqrt x))))

  A potential soundness bug in [47m[encapsulate][0m was fixed.  Specifically,
  when a classical, constrained function is instantiated with a
  lambda expression containing free variables, it may produce
  non-standard values depending on the values of the free variables.
  This means that the functional instantiation cannot be used to
  justify a non-classical theorem.  For example, consider the
  following sequence:

    (encapsulate
      ((f (x) t))
      (local (defun f (x) x)))
    (defthm-std f-x-standard
      (implies (standardp x)
               (standardp (f x))))
    (defthm plus-x-standard
      (implies (standardp x)
               (standardp (+ x y)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
               :use ((:functional-instance f-x-standard
                                           (f (lambda (x) (+ x y))))))))
    (defthm plus-x-eps-not-standard
      (implies (standardp x)
               (not (standardp (+ x (/ (i-large-integer)))))))
    (defthm nil-iff-t
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
              :use ((:instance plus-x-standard
                               (y (/ (i-large-integer))))
                    (:instance plus-x-eps-not-standard)))))

  ACL2(r) also supports the introduction of non-classical functions
  with [47m[defchoose][0m.  These behave just as normal functions introduced
  with [47m[defchoose][0m, but they have a non-classical choice property.

  Finally, ACL2(r) now comes with a revamped library supporting
  non-standard analysis, still distributed separately as
  [47mbooks/nonstd/[0m.  The new library uses [47m[defchoose][0m to state more
  natural and useful versions of the IVT, MVT, etc.  It also supports
  the introduction of inverse functions, e.g., logarithms.  Finally,
  the library has much more extensive support for differentiation.")
 (NOTE-3-6
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.6 (August, 2009) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Below we roughly organize the changes since Version 3.5 into the
  following categories of changes: existing features, new features,
  heuristic improvements, bug fixes, distributed books, Emacs
  support, and experimental [hons-and-memoization] version.  Each
  change is described in just one category, though of course many
  changes could be placed in more than one category.

  Note that (starting with ACL2 Version 3.5) LispWorks is no longer
  supported as a platform for ACL2, as the LispWorks compiler could
  not handle the ACL2 sources; see comments in the ACL2 sources about
  ``function size'' being ``too large''.

  [31;1mCHANGES TO EXISTING FEATURES[0m

  In the [47m[xargs][0m [47m[declare][0m form, the function symbol (or symbols,
  plural, in the case of [47m[mutual-recursion][0m) being defined may now be
  used in the specified [47m:measure[0m and [47m:[0m[47m[guard][0m terms.  Note that, the
  definition(s) of the new symbol(s) are still not used in the
  termination proof.  Thanks to Daron Vroon for discussions leading
  to this addition for the measure and to Dave Greve for requesting
  such an enhancement for the guard.

  Processing of the [47m:guard-hints[0m in an [47m[xargs][0m [47m[declare][0m form is now
  delayed until the start of [guard] verification.  As a result, the
  function symbol(s) being defined may now be used as one might
  expect in such hints, for example in an [47m:in-theory[0m form.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for suggesting that we make such an improvement and
  providing an example.

  Made a low-level change to [47m[make-event][0m in support of the ACL2s
  utility ``[47mdynamic-make-event[0m''.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for
  explaining the issue leading to this change.

  Modified the failure message printed when a measure conjecture fails
  to be proved, to indicate whether or not the hint [47m:ruler-extenders
  :all[0m would create a different measure conjecture.  Thanks to Peter
  Dillinger for suggesting such a modification.

  A call of [47m[add-default-hints][0m had added hints to the end of the
  current list of default hints.  Now, it adds them to the beginning
  of that list, so that they are tried first.  However,
  [47m[add-default-hints][0m now takes a keyword argument, [47m:at-end[0m.  If that
  argument is supplied and evaluates to other than [47mnil[0m, then the
  previous behavior occurs.

  When [47m[save-exec][0m is used to save ACL2 images, the build dates are now
  printed on separate lines at startup and in the executable script.
  Thanks To Bob Boyer for requesting some newlines.

  [47m[Forward-chaining][0m rules are now generated so that every [47m[let][0m (also
  [47m[let*][0m and [47m[lambda][0m) expression is expanded away, as is every call
  of a so-called ``guard holder'' ([47m[must-be-equal][0m, [47m[prog2$][0m,
  [47m[ec-call][0m, [47m[the][0m).  These were formerly only expanded away in the
  conclusion.  Thanks to Konrad Slind for a helpful email leading to
  this change.

  [47m[Current-theory][0m now causes a hard error when applied to a name not
  found in the current ACL2 logical [world], rather than simply
  returning [47mnil[0m.

  When the underlying Common Lisp is GCL, ACL2 no longer uses the [47m#n=[0m
  reader macro when writing out certain files, including
  [certificate] files.  In all other Lisps, it now uses the [47m#n=[0m
  ``print-circle'' feature not only for [certificate] files and
  ``[47mexpansion.lsp[0m'' files written for example in support of
  [47m[make-event][0m, but also for files written in support of [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m.
  This is all managed with new [state] global variable
  [47mprint-circle-files[0m; see [print-control].  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  pointing out that GCL is limited by default to 1024 indices for
  [47m#n=[0m.

  [31;1mNEW FEATURES[0m

  A documentation topic explains in some detail how [hints] work with
  the ACL2 proof ``waterfall'': see [hints-and-the-waterfall].  This
  topic may be useful to those who write computed hints (see
  [computed-hints]) or other advanced hints.

  Added a new hint keyword, [47m:no-thanks[0m, that avoids printing the usual
  ``Thanks'' message for [hints].  Thanks to Peter Dillinger for
  requesting this feature.

  Added a new hint keyword, [47m:backtrack[0m, that checks the goals produced
  by processing a goal, and can cause the goal to be re-processed
  using a new hint.  See [hints].  Thanks to Pete Manolios for a
  conversation that led to the idea of this hint.

  Added a new class of hints, [override-hints], that is similar to
  [default-hints], except that override-hints are always applied,
  even if the user has supplied a hint explicitly for the goal.  See
  [override-hints].  Thanks to Pete Manolios and Harsh Raju Chamarthi
  for useful discussions on this topic, including its application to
  testing.

  When a goal ready to be pushed for proof by induction is given the
  new hint ``[47m:do-not-induct :otf[0m'', it is indeed pushed for proof by
  induction, rather than causing immediate failure as in the case of
  the hint ``[47m:do-not-induct t[0m''.  Instead, the proof fails when the
  goal is later picked to be proved by induction.  Thanks to Peter
  Dillinger for discussions leading to this feature.

  Related to computed hints only: Each history entry in the list stored
  in variable [47mHIST[0m (see [computed-hints]) now has a [47m:CLAUSE[0m field,
  which provides access to a goal's parent, parent's parent, and so
  on (within the same induction and forcing round only).

  It is now possible to inhibit warnings produced by [47m[in-theory][0m
  [events] and [hints] that occur when certain built-in definitions
  and executable-counterparts are disabled: just evaluate [47m(assign
  verbose-theory-warning nil)[0m.  Thanks to Jared Davis (and probably
  others in the past) for requesting such a mechanism.

  [31;1mHEURISTIC IMPROVEMENTS[0m

  A source function ([47mlinearize-lst[0m) was replaced by tail-recursive
  code, which can avoid a stack overflow.  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  sending a helpful example.

  The heuristics for limiting [forward-chaining] have been slightly
  relaxed, to allow derivations based on the occurrence of all
  arguments of the forward-chaining rule's conclusion in the goal
  (after stripping leading calls of [47mNOT[0m).  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  contributing this improvement and providing a motivating example.

  We simplified induction schemes by eliminating each hypothesis of the
  form [47m(not (equal term (quote const)))[0m for which some other
  hypothesis in the same case equates term with some (presumably
  other) quoted constant.  Thanks to Dave Greve for requesting an
  improvement of this sort.

  [31;1mBUG FIXES[0m

  Fixed a soundness bug related to redundancy of [47m[encapsulate][0m events
  (see [redundant-events]) and [ruler-extenders].  A proof of [47mnil[0m in
  ACL2 Version 3.5 appears in a comment in [47m(deflabel note-3-6 ...)[0m in
  the ACL2 source code.  The fix is to insist that in order for one
  [47m[encapsulate][0m event to be redundant with another, they must be
  evaluated with the same [default-ruler-extenders].  Analogous to
  this issue of [default-ruler-extenders] for [47m[encapsulate][0ms is an
  issue of the [default-verify-guards-eagerness], which has similarly
  been fixed.

  Fixed soundness bugs related to the handling of
  [subversive-recursions] for [constraint]s.  Proofs of [47mnil[0m in ACL2
  Version 3.5 appear in a comment in [47m(deflabel note-3-6 ...)[0m in the
  ACL2 source code.

  Fixed a bug that could cause the following error during calls of
  [47m[certify-book][0m in the presence of calls of [47m[skip-proofs][0m in the
  book:

    ACL2 Warning [Skip-proofs] in

    HARD ACL2 ERROR in FMT0: Illegal Fmt Syntax.  The tilde-@ directive at
    position 0 of the string below is illegal because its variable evaluated
    to 0, which is neither a string nor a list.

    \"~@0\"

  Thanks to Dave Greve for reporting this bug and making available a
  very helpful test case.

  The [47m:corollary[0m of a rule (see [rule-classes]) failed to use the
  [47m[default-hints][0m of the logical [world].  This has been fixed.

  (CCL only) We removed a call, for CCL 1.3 (and beyond) only, of
  foreign function [47msync[0m in the closing of output channels.  Thanks to
  Daron Vroon for reporting issues with such a call on a non-Intel
  platform.

  Fixed a bug in reporting failures when monitoring rewrite rules with
  free variables in the hypotheses, that could cause a hard Lisp
  error (from which ACL2 continues, however).  Thanks to Eric Smith
  for sending a very helpful example with his bug report.

  Fixed the handling of [47m:induct[0m [hints], which had thrown away hint
  information from parent goals.  For example, the [47m[thm][0m form below
  failed to prove even though the second hint is in some sense
  superfluous; induction occurs automatically at [47m\"Goal'\"[0m even without
  the hint on that.  The failure was due to discarding the hint
  information on [47m\"Goal\"[0m.

    (in-theory (disable append))
    (thm (equal (cdr (cons a (append (append x y) z))) (append x y z))
         :hints
         ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable append))
          (\"Goal'\" :induct t) ; failed unless this line is commented out
          ))

  Fixed a bug in the [47m[args][0m command that was failing to show the
  formals of primitives (built-in functions like [47mconsp[0m that do not
  come with explicit definitions).  Thanks to John Cowles for
  pointing out this bug.  (At a lower level, the bug was that
  primitives failed to have [47m'stobjs-in[0m or [47m'stobjs-out[0m properties.)

  Fixed bugs in the utility supporting moving directories of certified
  books, sometimes used in Debian builds (as described in source
  function [47mmake-certificate-file[0m).  Thanks to Alan Dunn for pointing
  out such a bug, in paths associated with [47m[defpkg][0m events in
  [portcullis] commands in [certificate]s (which are used for error
  reporting).  There were also bugs, now fixed, that prevented
  renaming some book paths.  Please note that this mechanism is not
  guaranteed to be sound; in particular, it can probably misbehave
  when macros are used to generate portcullis events.  However, it
  seems likely that such issues will be very rare.

  Eliminated warnings that could arise when tracing a function with
  [47m[trace$][0m.  Now, when [47m[trace$][0m is applied to a function without
  option [47m:native[0m, that function's declarations and documentation are
  discarded.

  Fixed a bug that could cause a failure when building an executable
  image using SBCL as the underlying Common Lisp.  Thanks to Jun
  Sawada for reporting this failure.  We made a similar fix for
  CMUCL.

  Fixed a bug in [47m[save-exec][0m in the case that an absolute pathname is
  supplied.  Thanks to Jared Davis for bringing this bug to our
  attention.

  Fixed a bug in the use of [47m[trace$][0m with the [47m:native[0m and [47m:multiplicity[0m
  options that caused hard errors for some underlying Lisps.

  Fixed a bug in the interaction of [47m[trace$][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m, which caused
  error as [47m[comp][0m tried to re-trace functions that it temporarily
  untraced.

  [31;1mNEW AND UPDATED BOOKS AND RELATED INFRASTRUCTURE[0m

  See {ReleaseVersionNumbers |
  https://code.google.com/p/acl2-books/wiki/ReleaseVersionNumbers}
  for a list of books in Version 3.6 of ACL2 but not Version 3.5.
  For [3mchanges[0m to existing books rather than [3madditions[0m, see the {log
  entries | http://code.google.com/p/acl2-books/source/list} starting
  with revision r286 up through revision r329.

  It is no longer required to specify a value for environment (or
  `make') variable [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m when running `make' in the
  distributed book directory, even when that directory is not under
  the directory containing the ACL2 executable.  Thanks to Peter
  Dillinger for providing this improvement, by modifying
  [47mbooks/Makefile-generic[0m and, as needed, distributed book Makefiles.

  Thanks to Peter Dillinger, some books in support of the ACL2 Sedan
  (ACL2s) are more easily available for ACL2 users who do not use
  ACL2s.  See [ACL2-sedan].

  [31;1mEMACS SUPPORT[0m

  If the following form is evaluated before the load of
  [47memacs/emacs-acl2.el[0m, then variables are now set to reflect the
  directory containing that file.

    (if (boundp '*acl2-sources-dir*)
        (makunbound '*acl2-sources-dir*))

  Fixed info-dir-entry line in generated info file (by patching
  [47mdoc/write-acl2-texinfo.lisp[0m).  Thanks to Alan Dunn for providing
  this patch.

  [31;1mEXPERIMENTAL HONS VERSION[0m

  Bob Boyer and others have contributed numerous changes for the
  experimental ``[47mhons[0m'' version of ACL2 (see [hons-and-memoization]).
  A number of these have been crafted to work specifically with CCL
  (Clozure Common Lisp, formerly OpenMCL), which is now required as
  the underlying Lisp for the ``[47mhons[0m'' version of ACL2.

  A heuristic (source function [47mtoo-many-ifs[0m has been made more scalable
  (for the non-HONS version as well, in fact), but with no functional
  change.  Thanks to Jared Davis for noticing performance issues and
  suggesting fixes.

  Other changes including the following, quoting Bob Boyer:

      The CCL CASE macro now does better than a dumb linear search in some
      CASEes.

      SH and CSH are functions to talk to the underlying Gnu-Linux from
      CCL.  Good for repeated calling when you simply cannot afford
      the copying cost of a FORK because you are using, say, a dozen
      gigabytes.

      Added CCL compiler-macros for IF and OR, to support some 'coverage'
      analysis, cf. IF-REPORT, extending the profiling.

      Introduced the type 'mfixnum' so that things like counting honses and
      counting calls to memoized or profiled functions can run fast
      in CCL 64 bits and yet still run at all under 32 bits.

      Moved all HONSes to CCL's newish static space, which permits the
      address of a cons to be used as a hash key, as in most Lisps.
      (CCL moves most conses and most everything when it does a
      compacting-gc.)

      Quite a few changes in the memoize-fn reporting.

      Added a timer facility, cf. call-with-timeout.  Good for running
      under throttle some gross thoughts like 'Is it true that you
      can't fit 12 pigeons into 11 holes' on some propositional
      calculus systems/functions.

      Added rwx-size, pid-owner-cmdlines, rss, and proc-stat to help see
      what is really going on virtually in Gnu-Linux.

      Fixed at least one bug in compact-print-file and helped make its
      integration into ACL2's read-object$ a little more sound.
      Still worried some about *print-readably* vs. readtable-case.
      Does anyone else stay awake late at night worrying about
      readtable-case?

      Revised how the *watch-dog-process* interrupts the main process for
      the thousandth time, cf. watch.  Haven't changed it in weeks,
      which means that (a) it is getting tolerable or (b) I've run
      out of gas.")
 (NOTE-3-6-1
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.6.1 (September, 2009) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  The essential changes to ACL2 since Version 3.6 are the two bug fixes
  described below.  There was also some functionality-neutral code
  refactoring, as requested by Daron Vroon in support of the ACL2
  Sedan (see [ACL2-sedan]).  Also see {ReleaseVersionNumbers |
  https://code.google.com/p/acl2-books/wiki/ReleaseVersionNumbers}
  for a list of books in Version 3.6.1 of ACL2 but not Version 3.6.
  For [3mchanges[0m to existing books rather than [3madditions[0m, see the {log
  entries | http://code.google.com/p/acl2-books/source/list} starting
  with revision r329 up through revision 350.

  Fixed a soundness bug in the handling of [ruler-extenders],
  specifically in the handling of [47m[let][0m-expressions.  Thanks to Pete
  Manolios, who sent us a proof of [47mnil[0m, essentially as follows.  In
  the termination proof for [47mfoo[0m below, the binding of [47mx[0m to [47m(cons t x)[0m
  was not substituted into the recursive call of [47mfoo[0m for purposes of
  the termination proof.

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :ruler-extenders :all))
      (let ((x (cons t x)))
        (if (endp (cdr x))
            x
          (cons t (foo (cdr x))))))

    (defthm foo-bad
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
               :use ((:instance foo (x '(3))))
               :in-theory (disable foo (foo))))
      :rule-classes nil)

  Fixed a typo in code supporting [ruler-extenders] (specifically,
  swapped arguments in a recursive call of ACL2 source function
  [47mget-ruler-extenders2[0m, which could cause problems for functions
  defined using [47m[mutual-recursion][0m).  Thanks to Daron Vroon for
  bringing this bug to our attention, pointing out the swapped
  arguments.")
 (NOTE-3-6{R}
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 3.6(r) (August, 2009) Notes

  Please also see [note-3-6] for changes in Version 3.6 of ACL2.")
 (NOTE-4-0
  (RELEASE-NOTES)
  "ACL2 Version 4.0 (July, 2010) Notes

  NOTE!  New users can ignore these release notes, because the
  [documentation] has been updated to reflect all changes that are
  recorded here.

  Below we roughly organize the changes since Version 3.6.1 into the
  following categories of changes: existing features, new features,
  heuristic improvements, bug fixes, distributed books, Emacs
  support, and experimental versions.  Each change is described in
  just one category, though of course many changes could be placed in
  more than one category.  Also see [note-3-6-1] for other changes
  since Version 3.6.

  [31;1mCHANGES TO EXISTING FEATURES[0m

  There have been extensive changes to the handling of compiled files
  for books, which may generally be invisible to most users.  The
  basic idea is that when compiled files are loaded on behalf of
  [47m[include-book][0m, they are now loaded before events in the book are
  processed, not afterwards.  This can speed up calls of
  [47minclude-book[0m, especially if the underlying Lisp compiles all
  definitions on the fly (as is the case for CCL and SBCL).  One
  functional change is that for keyword argument [47m:load-compiled-file[0m
  of [47m[include-book][0m, the values [47m:comp-raw[0m, [47m:try[0m, and [47m:comp![0m are no
  longer legal.  (Note that the handling of [47m:comp-raw[0m was actually
  broken, so it seems that this value wasn't actually used by anyone;
  also, the handling of [47m:comp![0m formerly could cause an error in some
  Lisp platforms, including SBCL.)  Another change is that if
  [47m[include-book][0m is called with [47m:load-compiled-file t[0m, then each
  sub-book must have a compiled file or a so-called ``expansion
  file''; see [book-compiled-file].  In the unlikely event that this
  presents a problem, the makefile provides a way to build with
  compilation disabled; see [compilation].  Users of raw mode (see
  [set-raw-mode]) will be happy to know that [47m[include-book][0m now works
  if there is an up-to-date compiled file for the book, since
  [47m[portcullis][0m commands are now incorporated into that compiled file.
  The mechanism for saving expansion files has changed, and the
  [47m:save-expansion[0m argument of [47m[certify-book][0m has been eliminated; see
  [certify-book].  More discussion of ACL2's new handling of book
  compilation is described in a new documentation topic; see
  [book-compiled-file].

  It was possible to get a hard Lisp error when certifying a book with
  a redundant [47m[defconst][0m form whose term is not identical to the
  existing [47m[defconst][0m form for the same name.  The following example
  illustrates the problem, which has been fixed (as part of the
  change in handling of compiled files for books, described above).
  Imagine that after the initial [47m(in-package \"ACL2\")[0m form, file
  [47mfoo.lisp[0m has just the form [47m(defconst *a* (append nil nil))[0m.  Then
  before the fix, we could have:

    ACL2 !>(defconst *a* nil)
    [[output omitted]]
    ACL2 !>(certify-book \"foo\" 1)
    [[initial output omitted]
    * Step 5:  Compile the functions defined in \"/v/joe/foo.lisp\".
    Compiling /v/joe/foo.lisp.
    End of Pass 1.
    End of Pass 2.
    OPTIMIZE levels: Safety=0 (No runtime error checking), Space=0, Speed=3
    Finished compiling /vjoe/foo.lisp.
    Loading /v/joe/foo.lisp

    Error: Illegal attempt to redeclare the constant *A*.

  The [47m[wormhole][0m facility has been changed to repair a bug that allowed
  guard violations to go undetected.  The major change has to do with
  the treatment of what used to be called the ``pseudo-flag''
  argument which has been replaced by a quoted lambda expression.
  See [note-4-0-wormhole-changes] for help in converting calls of
  [47m[wormhole][0m.  Also see see [wormhole] and see [wormhole-eval].

  The function [47massign-wormhole-output[0m has been eliminated but its
  functionality can be provided by [47m[wormhole-eval][0m.

  The ACL2 tutorial has been greatly expanded, for example to include a
  self-contained discussion of rewriting.  See [ACL2-tutorial].

  Formerly, the [47m[mbe][0m macro and [47m[must-be-equal][0m function were
  disallowed in any definition within an encapsulate having a
  non-empty signature.  Now, these are allowed provided the
  definition has been declared to be non-executable (see [defun-nx]).
  As a result, [47m[defevaluator][0m [events] may now include
  [47m[must-be-equal][0m among the function symbols known by the evaluator;
  this had not previously been allowed.  Thanks to Sol Swords for
  discussions leading to this relaxation for [47m[defevaluator][0m.

  [47m[Princ$][0m now prints strings more efficiently.  Thanks to Jared Davis
  for suggesting the improvements to [47mprinc$[0m.

  The use of [47m[xargs][0m declaration [47m:non-executable t[0m no longer requires
  the absence of [47m[state][0m or declared [47m[stobj][0ms among the formal
  parameters of a function definition.  As before, the use of
  [47m:non-executable t[0m turns off single-threadedness checking for the
  body, and also as before, attempts to execute the function will
  fail.  Thanks to Sol Swords for requesting this relaxation (for
  automatic generation of so-called ``flag functions'' for
  definitions using [47m[mutual-recursion][0m).

  The [documentation] has been improved for explaining to advanced
  users the details of the ACL2 hint mechanism; see
  [hints-and-the-waterfall], and see the example about
  [47mnonlinearp-default-hint[0m in distributed book
  [47mbooks/hints/basic-tests.lisp[0m.  Thanks to Robert Krug for useful
  discussions, in particular suggesting the above example as one to
  be explained with the documentation.

  The [47mtime$[0m macro has been enhanced to allow user control of the timing
  message that is printed, and of when it is printed.  See [time$].
  Thanks to Jared Davis for providing the essential design, helpful
  documentation (largely incorporated), and an initial implementation
  for raw Lisp.

  The [47m:ttags[0m argument to [47m[include-book][0m had been required when
  including a certified book that uses trust tags.  This is no longer
  the case: essentially, [47m:ttags[0m defaults to [47m:all[0m except that warnings
  will be printed.  Thanks to Jared Davis for requesting such a
  relaxation, and to him and Sandip Ray for useful discussions.

  The definition of [47m[mv-let][0m has been modified so that the single-step
  macroexpansion (see [trans1]) of its calls can be evaluated.
  Thanks to Pete Manolios for bringing this evaluation issue to our
  attention and ensuing discussions.

  All calls of so-called ``guard-holders'' --- [47m[prog2$][0m,
  [47m[must-be-equal][0m (from calls of see [mbe]), [47m[ec-call][0m, and [47m[mv-list][0m
  --- are now removed before storing hypotheses of rules of class
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m.  Thanks to Sol Swords for requesting this
  enhancement and sending the following example in the case of
  [rewrite] rules.

    (defthm foo
      (prog2$ (cw \"asdf\")
              (and (equal (car (cons x y)) x)
                   (equal (cdr (cons x y)) y))))

  The handling of [47m[fmt][0m directive [47m~s[0m has been modified so that if the
  argument is a symbol that would normally be printed using vertical
  bars ([47m|[0m), then that symbol is printed as with [47m~f[0m.  Thanks to Jared
  Davis for providing the following example showing that treatment of
  [47m~s[0m was a bit unexpected: [47m(cw \"~s0.~%\" '|fo\\|o|)[0m.

  Error messages have been improved for ill-formed terms (in ACL2's
  so-called ``translation'' routines).  Thanks to Jared Davis for
  requesting such an enhancement.

  Modified [47m[defun-sk][0m so that it executes in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.
  Previously, evaluation of a [47m[defun-sk][0m event in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode
  caused a somewhat inscrutable error, but now, [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode is
  treated the same as [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode for purposes of [47m[defun-sk][0m.

  The ``system hacker'' commands [47m([0m[47m[redef+][0m[47m)[0m and [47m([0m[47m[redef-][0m[47m)[0m are now
  embedded event forms (see [embedded-event-form]), hence may be used
  in [books] as well as in [47m[progn][0m and [47m[encapsulate][0m [events].  Also,
  these two commands are now no-ops in raw Lisp.

  The function symbol [47mworldp[0m (in the [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package) has been renamed
  to [47mplist-worldp[0m.

  The function [47mgc$-fn[0m (resulting from macroexpansion of [47m[gc$][0m) is now
  in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.  Thanks to Jared Davis for requesting this
  change.

  The user now has control over whether compilation is used, for
  example whether or not [47m[certify-book][0m compiles by default, using
  function [47mset-compiler-enabled[0m.  See [compilation].

  Modified the conversion of relative to absolute pathnames in
  [portcullis] [command]s for book certification.  Now, more
  pathnames remain as relative pathnames.

  The [47m\"Ttags\"[0m warning that can be printed by [47m[include-book][0m is now
  given even if [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m has specified `[47mwarning[0m'.  To
  suppress it, specify [47mwarning![0m instead, for example,
  [47m(set-inhibit-output-lst '(acl2::warning! acl2::proof-tree))[0m.

  On occasion, ACL2 prints the message ``Flushing current installed
  world'' as it cleans up when certain actions (installing a [world])
  are interrupted.  This operation has been sped up considerably.  If
  your session includes many [events], you can probably speed up any
  such operation further by invoking [47m[reset-prehistory][0m.  Thanks to
  Jared Davis for sending a query that led us to make this
  improvement.

  Calls of the form [47m(ec-call (must-be-equal logic exec))[0m are no longer
  allowed, since we do not have confidence that they would be handled
  correctly.

  The underlying function for [47m[good-bye][0m (and hence for [47m[exit][0m and
  [47m[quit][0m) is now in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.  Thanks to Jared Davis for
  requesting this enhancement.

  We now require that every function symbol in the [signature] of an
  [47m[encapsulate][0m event have a [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode definition at the end of
  the first pass, not merely a [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode definition (which
  formerly was sufficient).  You can still define such a function in
  [47m:program[0m mode, provided it is followed by a [47m:logic[0m mode definition
  (where of course both definitions are [local], since we are
  discussing functions introduced in the [signature]).  Thanks to
  Carl Eastlund for bringing this issue to our attention.  (Note: An
  analogous modification has been made for [47m:[0m[47m[bdd][0m [hints] as well.)

  The following functions now have raw Lisp implementations that may
  run faster than their ACL2 definitions: [47m[assoc-eq][0m, [47m[assoc-equal][0m,
  [47m[member-eq][0m, [47m[member-equal][0m, [47msubsetp-eq[0m, [47m[subsetp-equal][0m,
  [47m[remove-eq][0m, [47m[remove-equal][0m, [47m[position-eq][0m, and [47m[position-equal][0m.
  Thanks to Jared Davis for suggesting that we consider such an
  improvement.

  We now avoid infinite loops caused when tracing functions that
  implement [47m[trace$][0m.  Thanks to Rob Sumners and Eric Smith for
  useful discussions.

  The implementation of [47m[trace!][0m has been modified slightly, to
  accommodate the fix for ``some holes in the handling of trust
  tags'' described later, below.

  This item applies unless the host Lisp is GCL.  An interrupt
  (control-c) will now cause a proof to exit normally in most cases,
  by simulating a timeout, as though [47m[with-prover-time-limit][0m had
  been called with a time-limit of 0.  If the first interrupt doesn't
  terminate the proof, a second one should do so (because a
  different, more ``severe'' mechanism is used after the first
  attempt).  As a result, [47m[redo-flat][0m should work as one might expect
  even if a proof is interrupted.  Thanks to Dave Greve for
  requesting this enhancement to [47m[redo-flat][0m.  Technical note: for
  reasons related to this change, time-limits are no longer checked
  in evaluator functions ([47mev-fncall[0m, [47mev[0m, [47mev-lst[0m, [47mev-fncall-w[0m, [47mev-w[0m,
  and [47mev-w-lst[0m).

  It is now legal for [proof-builder] [macro-command]s to appear in
  [47m:[0m[47m[instructions][0m that are used in place of [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m.  Thanks to
  Sandip Ray for (most recently) requesting this feature.

  The value of [47m:command-conventions[0m for [47m[ld][0m special variable
  [47mld-post-eval-print[0m is now treated as though it were [47mt[0m if the value
  [47m[ld][0m special variable [47mld-error-triples[0m is [47mnil[0m.  The following
  example illustrates this change.

    ACL2 !>(ld-post-eval-print state) ; default
    :COMMAND-CONVENTIONS
    ACL2 !>(ld-error-triples state) ; default
    T
    ACL2 !>(set-ld-error-triples nil state)
    *** Then, before the change:
    ACL2 !>(mv t 3 state)
     3
    *** Instead, after the change:
    ACL2 !>(mv t 3 state)
    (T 3 <state>)

  The default behavior of [47m[ld][0m has been changed.  Formerly when an
  error occurred that halted a subsidiary call of [47mld[0m, then the parent
  [47mld[0m would continue.  That is no longer the case.  Consider the
  following example.

    (ld '((ld '((defun f (x) x)
                (defun bad (x)) ; ERROR -- missing the body
                ))
          (defun g (x) x)))

  Formerly, [47mg[0m would be defined in the resulting logical [world].  Now,
  the error halts not only the inner [47mld[0m but also the outer [47mld[0m.  See
  [ld], and for details of the new default value for
  [47m:ld-error-action[0m, [47m:RETURN![0m, see [ld-error-action].  Also see the
  paragraph below about a new utility, [47m:[0m[47m[p!][0m.  Thanks to Robert Krug
  and Sandip Ray for helpful discussions.

  Environment variable [47mACL2-CUSTOMIZATION[0m has been replaced by
  [47mACL2_CUSTOMIZATION[0m --- that is, the hyphen has been replaced by an
  underscore --- so that it can be set conveniently in the [47mbash[0m
  shell.  See [ACL2-customization].

  The ``[47mWarnings:[0m'' summary is now omitted when there are no warnings,
  where formerly ``[47mWarnings: None[0m'' was printed.  Thanks to Jared
  Davis for suggesting this change.

  We have modified the generation of [47mconstraint[0ms for [47m[encapsulate][0m
  [events] in two primary ways, neither of them likely to affect many
  users.  One change is that the virtual movement of definitions and
  theorems to in front of an [47m[encapsulate][0m event, or of definitions
  to behind that event, is no longer inhibited in the case of nested
  encapsulates with non-empty [signature]s.  The following example
  illustrates the other change, as discussed below.

    (encapsulate
     ((f (x) t))
     (local (defun f (x) x))
     (defun g (x) (cons x (f x)))
     (defun h (x) (g x))
     (defthm h-is-f (equal (car (h x)) x)))

  Previously, the [constraint] on [47mf[0m and [47mh[0m was essentially the
  conjunction of the definition of [47mh[0m and the theorem [47mh-is-f[0m.  Now,
  the definition of [47mg[0m is conjoined as well; moreover, [47mg[0m receives the
  same [constraint] as do [47mf[0m and [47mh[0m, where previously [47mg[0m was only
  constrained by its definition.  While we are not aware of a
  soundness bug caused by the previous approach, the new approach
  follows more precisely the intended notion of [constraint].

  The use of [47m[trace$][0m (or [47m[trace!][0m) option [47m:multiplicity[0m had been
  required when option [47m:native[0m was supplied.  This is no longer the
  case.  Also, a bug has been fixed that had prevented [47m:multiplicity[0m
  from working properly in GCL and Allegro CL.

  Several errors have been eliminated that formerly occurred when the
  constraints for a function symbol were unknown because it was
  constrained using a dependent clause-processor (see
  [define-trusted-clause-processor].  Now, it is assumed that the
  [47msupporters[0m argument in a [47m[define-trusted-clause-processor][0m event is
  such that every ancestor of any function symbol constrained by the
  ``promised encapsulate'' of that event among, or ancestral in,
  those [47msupporters[0m.  Thanks to Sol Swords, Sandip Ray, and Jared
  Davis for helpful discussions.

  The notion of [constraint] for functions introduced by [47m[defun][0m has
  been modified slightly.  No longer do we remove from the body of
  the definition calls of so-called ``guard-holders'': [47m[prog2$][0m,
  [47m[must-be-equal][0m, [ec-call], and [mv-list], and uses of [47mthe-error[0m
  generated by [47m[the][0m.  Also, we now expand calls of [47mthe-error[0m with
  the same aggressive heuristics applied to a number of other
  functions (technically, adding it to the list
  [47m*expandable-boot-strap-non-rec-fns*[0m).

  [31;1mNEW FEATURES[0m

  A new event, [47m[defattach][0m, allows evaluation of calls of constrained
  ([47m[encapsulate][0md) functions.  In particular, users can now, in
  principle, soundly modify ACL2 source code; please feel free to
  contact the ACL2 implementors if you are interested in doing so.
  See [defattach].

  Eric Smith has noticed that if you exit the [break-rewrite] loop
  using [47m:[0m[47m[a!][0m during an [47m[ld][0m of a file, then all changes to the
  logical [world] are discarded that were made during that call of
  [47m[ld][0m.  A new utility, [47m:[0m[47m[p!][0m, pops just one level instead, and
  avoids discarding that work.  (This change is related to an item
  above, ``The default behavior of [47m[ld][0m has been changed.'') Thanks
  to Eric for pointing out this issue.

  New function [47m[mv-list][0m is the identity function logically, but
  converts multiple values to lists.  The first argument is the
  number of values, so an example form is as follows, where [47mfoo[0m
  returns three values: [47m(mv-list 3 (foo x y))[0m.  Thanks to Sol Swords
  for requesting this feature and for reporting a soundness bug in
  one of our preliminary implementations.

  A new [47m[state][0m global variable, [47mhost-lisp[0m, has as its value a keyword
  whose value depends on the underlying Common Lisp implementation.
  Use [47m(@ host-lisp)[0m to see its value.

  It is now possible to write [documentation] for HTML without error
  when there are links to nonexistent documentation topics.  See the
  comments in macro [47macl2::write-html-file[0m at the end of file
  [47mdoc/write-acl2-html.lisp[0m.  When there are such errors, they should
  be easier to understand than previously.  Thanks to Alan Dunn for
  providing the initial modifications.

  It is now possible to inhibit specified parts of the Summary printed
  at the conclusion of an event.  See [set-inhibited-summary-types].
  Also see [with-output], in particular the discussion of the new
  [47m:summary[0m keyword.  Thanks to Sol Swords for requesting more control
  over the Summary.

  A new [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m keyword, [47m:case-split-limitations[0m, can override the
  default case-split-limitations settings (see
  [set-case-split-limitations]) in the simplifier.  Thanks to Ian
  Johnson for requesting this addition and providing an initial
  implementation.

  It is now possible to defer and avoid some ttag-related output; see
  [set-deferred-ttag-notes].  Thanks to Jared Davis for requesting
  less verbosity from ttag-related output.

  A new [command], [47m:[0m[47m[pl2][0m, allows you to restrict the rewrite rules
  printed that apply to a given term.  See [pl2].  Thanks to Robert
  Krug for requesting such a capability.

  ACL2 now provides a utility for canonicalizing filenames, so that
  soft links are resolved; see [canonical-pathname].  Moreover, ACL2
  uses this utility in its own sources, which can eliminate some
  issues.  In particular, [47m[include-book][0m with argument [47m:ttags :all[0m no
  longer breaks when given a book-name differing from the book-name
  that was used at certification time; thanks to Sol Swords for
  reporting that problem.  Also, certain errors have been eliminated
  involving the combination of packages in the certification world
  and trust tags; thanks to Jared Davis for sending an example of
  that problem.

  You can now suppress or enable guard-checking for an individual form;
  see [with-guard-checking].  Thanks to Sol Swords for requesting
  this feature.

  The [47m[walkabout][0m utility has been documented (thanks to Rob Sumners
  for suggesting this documentation).  This utility can make it easy
  to explore a large [47mcons[0m tree.  New interactive commands [47m(pp n)[0m and
  [47m(pp print-level print-length)[0m have been added to restrict how much
  of the current object is displayed.  See [walkabout].

  Rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m may now be provided a
  [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m keyword.  The default behavior is unchanged,
  but now [47m[type-set][0m is sensitive not only to the new
  [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m of a [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule (if supplied)
  but to the [47m[default-backchain-limit][0m of the current logical
  [world].  Setting of backchain-limits can now specify either the
  new (type-set) limit or the old limit (for rewriting); see
  [set-default-backchain-limit] and see [set-backchain-limit].
  Moreover, the functions [47m[default-backchain-limit][0m and
  [47m[backchain-limit][0m now take a second argument of [47m:ts[0m or [47m:rewrite[0m to
  specify which backchain-limit is desired.

  [31;1mHEURISTIC IMPROVEMENTS[0m

  The so-called ``too-many-ifs'' heuristic has been modified.  Such a
  heuristic has been employed in ACL2 (and previous Boyer-Moore
  provers) for many years, in order to limit the introduction of
  calls of [47m[if][0m by non-recursive functions.  Most users need not be
  concerned with this change, but two proofs in the regression suite
  (out of thousands) needed trivial adjustment, so user proofs could
  need tweaking.  In one application, this modification sped up
  proofs by 15%; but the change in runtime for the regression suite
  is negligible, so such speedups may vary.  Thanks to Sol Swords for
  providing a test from ACL2 runs at Centaur Technology, which was
  useful in re-tuning this heuristic.

  Guard proof obligations could have size quadratic in the number of
  clauses in a [47m[case][0m statement.  This inefficiency has been removed
  with a change that eliminates a hypothesis of the form [47m(not (eql
  term constant))[0m when there is already a stronger hypothesis,
  equating the same term with a different constant.  Thanks to Sol
  Swords for bringing this problem to our attention and suggesting an
  alternate approach to solving it, which we may consider in the
  future if related efficiency problems persist.

  We adjusted the heuristics for determining induction schemes in the
  presence of [ruler-extenders], when handling calls of a function
  symbol that is a ruler-extender, in either of two cases: either the
  function takes only one argument; or the function is [47m[prog2$][0m or
  [47m[ec-call][0m, and the first argument contains no recursive call.
  These cases are treated more directly as though the ruler-extender
  call is replaced by the unique (in the case of [47m[prog2$][0m and
  [47m[ec-call][0m, the second) argument.

  A new [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule, [47mtrue-listp-append[0m, has been added:

    (implies (true-listp b)
             (true-listp (append a b)))

  If you are interested in the motivation for adding this rule, see
  comments in [47mtrue-listp-append[0m in ACL2 source file [47maxioms.lisp[0m.

  The use of [47m:forward-chaining[0m lemmas has been improved slightly.  In
  previous versions, a conclusion derived by forward chaining was
  discarded if it was derivable by type-set reasoning, since it was
  ``already provable.'' But this heuristic prevented the conclusion
  from triggering further forward chaining.  This has been fixed.
  Thanks to Dave Greve for pointing out this problem.

  The fundamental utility that turns an [47mIF[0m expression into a set of
  clauses has been optimized to better handle tests of the form
  [47m(equal x 'constant)[0m and their negations.  This eliminates an
  exponential explosion in large case analyses.  But it comes at the
  inconvenience of sometimes reordering the clauses produced.  The
  latter aspect of this change may require you to change some Subgoal
  numbers in proof hints.  We apologize for the inconvenience.

  Certification can now run faster (specifically, the compilation
  phase) for books with very large structures generated by
  [47m[make-event][0m, when there is significant sharing of substructure,
  because of a custom optimization of the Lisp reader.  Thanks to Sol
  Swords for bringing this efficiency issue to our attention.

  Jared Davis reported inefficiency in certain [47m[make-event][0m evaluation
  due to a potentially expensive ``bad lisp object'' check on the
  expansion produced by the [47mmake-event[0m.  This check has been
  eliminated except in the case that the expansion introduces
  packages (for example, by including a book during the expansion
  phase that introduces packages).  Thanks to Jared for providing a
  helpful example.

  The application of rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m had the potential to
  loop (as commented in ACL2 source function [47mapply-induction-rule[0m).
  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Daron Vroon and Pete Manolios for
  sending nice examples causing the loop.

  Heuristics have been tweaked so that false goals may be simplified to
  [47mnil[0m that had formerly been left unchanged by simplification,
  perhaps resulting in useless and distracting proofs by induction.
  Thanks to Pete Manolios for pointing out this issue by sending the
  following example: [47m(thm (<= (+ 1 (acl2-count x)) 0))[0m.  (Technical
  explanation: When every literal in a clause simplifies to [47mnil[0m, even
  though we might not normally delete one or more such literals, we
  will replace the entire clause by the false clause.)

  Improved the efficiency of the built-in function, [47m[take][0m.  Thanks to
  Bob Boyer for suggesting this improvement.

  ACL2 can now use evaluation to relieve hypotheses when applying
  [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules.  Thanks to Peter Dillinger and Dave
  Greve for requesting this enhancement, and to Robert Krug for a
  relevant discussion long ago.

  Evaluation has been sped up during theorems for calls of [47m[mv-let][0m, by
  avoiding repeated evaluation of the expression to which its
  variables are bound.  Thanks to Sol Swords for requesting this
  improvement and sending an illustrative example.

  Modified a heuristic to avoid the opening up non-recursive function
  calls on calls of [47m[hide][0m involving [47m[if][0m-expressions.  For example,
  the [47m[thm][0m form below is now admitted

    (defun bar (x)
      (cons x x))
    (thm (equal (bar (hide (if a b c)))
         (cons (hide (if a b c)) (hide (if a b c)))))

  [31;1mBUG FIXES[0m

  Fixed a soundness bug in destructor elimination, which was preventing
  some cases from being generated.  Thanks to Eric Smith for
  reporting this bug and sending a helpful example.  (Technical
  detail: the fixes were in ACL2 source functions
  [47mapply-instantiated-elim-rule[0m and [47meliminate-destructors-clause1[0m, and
  comments in the former contain Eric's example.)

  Fixed a bug that supported a proof of [47mnil[0m by exploiting the fact that
  [portcullis] [command]s were not included in check-sum computations
  in a book's [certificate].  For such a proof of [47mnil[0m, see the
  relevant comment in the ACL2 source file [47mld.lisp[0m under [47m(deflabel
  note-4-0 ...)[0m.

  Changed the implementation of [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m.  The previous
  implementation could allow relative pathnames to be stored in the
  [portcullis] [command]s of [certificate]s of [books], which perhaps
  could lead to unsoundness (though we did not try to exploit this to
  prove [47mnil[0m).  Thanks to Jared Davis for reporting a bug in our first
  new implementation.  An additional change to both
  [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m and [47m[delete-include-book-dir][0m is that these
  now work in raw-mode (see [set-raw-mode]).  (Thanks to Dave Greve
  for suggesting a reduction in the warnings we produced related to
  raw-mode.)  Note that it is no longer permitted to make a direct
  call of the form [47m(table acl2-defaults-table :include-book-dir-alist
  ...)[0m; use [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m instead.

  Fixed a soundness bug related to [47m[xargs][0m keyword [47m:non-executable[0m.
  New macros, [47mdefun-nx[0m and [47mdefund-nx[0m, have been provided for
  declaring functions to be non-executable; see [defun-nx].  While we
  expect this bug to occur only rarely if at all in practice, the
  following example shows how it could be evoked.

    ;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
    ;;; Book sub.lisp
    ;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defun f ()
      (declare (xargs :guard t
                      :non-executable t))
      (mv-let (a b c)
              (mv 3 4)
              (declare (ignore a b))
              c))
    (defun g ()
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (prog2$ (mv-let (x y z)
                      (mv 2 3 4)
                      (declare (ignore x y z))
                      nil)
              (f)))
    (defthm g-nil
      (equal (g) nil)
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable (f))))
      :rule-classes nil)
    ;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
    ;;; Book top.lisp
    ;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (include-book \"sub\")
    (defthm contradiction
      nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use g-nil))
      :rule-classes nil)

  The modification described above pertaining to [47m[defun-nx][0m also
  prevents execution of non-executable functions that have been
  [trace]d.  The following example illustrates the problem; now, the
  following [47m[defun][0m of [47mg[0m is illegal, and the problem disappears if
  [47m[defun-nx][0m is used instead.

    (defun g (x) ; Use defun-nx to avoid an error after Version_3.6.1.
      (declare (xargs :guard t :non-executable t))
      x)
    (g 3) ; causes error, as expected
    (trace$ g)
    (g 3) ; returned 3 before the bug fix; after fix, causes error as expected

  A hard error was possible when attempting to include an uncertified
  book containing [events] of the form [47m(make-event '(local ...))[0m.
  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Sol Swords for bringing this issue
  to our attention.

  Fixed a bug in the heuristic improvement described for Version_3.6
  (see [note-3-6]) as ``We simplified induction schemes....'' The bug
  had prevented, in unusual cases such as the following (notice the
  impossible case), a proof by induction.

    (defun foo (a x)
      (and (consp x)
           (case a
             (0 (foo (car x) (cdr x)))
             (1 (foo (cdr x) (car x)))
             (0 (foo a (cons x x))))))
    (in-theory (disable (:type-prescription foo)))
    (thm (atom (foo a x)))

  Macro [47m[cw-gstack][0m did not work with an [47m:evisc-tuple[0m argument.  This
  has been fixed by changing [47mcw-gstack[0m so that it now evaluates its
  arguments.  Thanks to Sol Swords for bringing this bug to our
  attention.

  Fixed a bug in [47m:[0m[47m[pso][0m during the printing of failure messages for
  termination proofs.

  Fixed a bug in the handling of [47m#.[0m (see [sharp-dot-reader]).  Thanks
  to Bob Boyer for bringing this bug to our attention.

  Replaced a hard Lisp error with a clean error, in certain cases that
  a [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m value is erroneously supplied as a non-[47mnil[0m atom.
  Example: [47m(thm (equal x x) :hints 3)[0m.

  Fixed a bug in the interaction of function tracing with conversion of
  a function from [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m to [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.  The following example
  illustrates what had been wrong.

    (defun f (x)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (car x))
    (f 3) ; raw Lisp hard error
    (trace$ f)
    (f 3) ; raw Lisp hard error (still)
    (defun f (x) (car x)) ; upgrade f to :logic mode
    (f 3) ; clean guard violation; f is no longer traced
    (trace$) ; uh oh - f is shown as traced
    (untrace$ f)
    (f 3) ; OUCH: hard Lisp error because old :program mode definition of
          ; the executable counterpart (sometimes called *1*f) was restored!

  Made a fix so that when building ACL2 with `make' option
  [47mACL2_SAFETY=3[0m, there will no longer be any safety-0 compiled code
  generated.  Thanks to Gary Byers for bringing this bug to our
  attention.

  Fixed a bug in the handling of [override-hints] that generate custom
  keyword hints (see [custom-keyword-hints]) involving the variable
  [47mstable-under-simplificationp[0m.  Thanks to Ian Johnson for bringing
  this bug to our attention with explanation that included a helpful
  example, included as comment in the ACL2 source code for function
  [47mapply-override-hint[0m.

  The [47msaved_acl2[0m script in CLISP could contain unexpected characters
  where simple newlines were expected.  Dave Greve found this in a
  Cygwin environment on Windows.  Thanks to Dave for reporting this
  bug and experimenting with a fix, and thanks to the CLISP folks for
  providing helpful information.

  Fixed a bug that could make [47m:[0m[47m[oops][0m cause an error.  Also, the [47m[oops][0m
  command can no longer take you back before a [47m[reset-prehistory][0m
  event.

  (GCL only) Fixed a bug that could occur when calling [47mtrace[0m in raw
  Lisp in GCL.

  Proof summaries have been improved, so that they account for [rune]s
  used in rewriting that takes place when generating goals to be
  proved in a forcing round.  Thanks to Jared Davis for sending us an
  example illustrating this issue.

  Fixed a bug that (at least in CCL) could put extra backslashes (`[47m\\[0m')
  in a pathname that ACL2 writes out to the executable script created
  by a build.  Thanks to Gary Byers for explaining that the CCL
  behavior is legal (for our previous use of Common Lisp function
  [47mmerge-pathnames[0m).

  We closed some holes in the handling of trust tags (also known as
  ``ttags''; see [defttag]) by [47m[include-book][0m.  The following example
  illustrates this rather subtle situation.  Consider the following
  book.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (make-event
     (er-progn
      (encapsulate
       ()
       (defttag :foo)
       (value-triple \"Imagine something bad here!\"))
      (value '(value-triple :some-value)))
     :check-expansion t)

  Formerly, the following commands succeeded.

    (certify-book \"test3\" 0 t :ttags :all)
    :u
    (include-book \"test3\" :ttags nil)

  But because of [47m[make-event][0m keyword argument [47m:check-expansion t[0m, we
  know that the event [47m(defttag :foo)[0m is evaluated by the above
  [47m[include-book][0m form, and hence the [47m:ttags[0m argument of [47minclude-book[0m,
  above, should have specified [47m:foo[0m.  The problem was that [47m[defttag][0m
  forms evaluated during [47m[make-event][0m expansion did not contribute to
  the trust tag information stored in the book's [certificate].
  Note: Because of this change, one should avoid using [47m[make-event][0m
  with [47m:check-expansion t[0m when the expansion would introduce a
  [47m[defttag][0m event during [47m[include-book][0m but not [47m[certify-book][0m time.
  For an example illustrating this issue, see [make-event-details],
  specifically the new version of the section labeled ``A note on
  ttags'' at the end of that [documentation] topic.

  Closed a small loophole that had the potential, in rare
  circumstances, to violate atomicity of under-the-hood updates for
  ACL2 [arrays].

  The following example was formerly allowed, but resulted in a
  guard-verified function (here, [47mg[0m) whose guard proof obligation is
  not a theorem outside the [47m[encapsulate][0m event.  We now disallow
  [guard] verification for functions introduced non-[local]ly inside
  an [47m[encapsulate][0m event unless we determine that the proof
  obligations hold outside the [47m[encapsulate][0m event as well.

    (encapsulate
     ((f (x) t))
     (local (defun f (x) (declare (xargs :guard t)) (consp x)))
     ;; ERROR!
     (defun g (x)
       (declare (xargs :guard (f x)))
       (car x)))

  The use of [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m on [stobj] functions had potentially caused a hard
  Lisp error; for example, this could occur when [47m(defstobj foo fld)[0m
  was followed by [47m:comp foop[0m.  This has been fixed.

  Fixed a bug that could cause a raw Lisp error when the first argument
  of [47m[with-local-stobj][0m is not a symbol.

  It had been possible to use the reserved keyword
  :computed-hints-replacement as the name of a custom keyword hint
  (see [custom-keyword-hints]).  This has been fixed.  Thanks to Dave
  Greve, who pointed out a confusing hint error message (which has
  also been fixed) that led us to this issue.

  Fixed a bug that could cause a hard Lisp error, instead of a graceful
  ACL2 error, if keyword [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m in a rule class is
  given a cons that is not a true list, such as [47m(1 . 1)[0m.

  Eliminated an error that could occur when redefining a function as a
  macro and then compiling, as in the example below.

    (defun foo (x) x)
    :redef!
    (defmacro foo (x) x)
    :comp t

  Thanks to Eric Smith for sending the above example in his bug report.

  Fixed a bug that could result in an assertion when a
  [clause-processor] causes an error.

  [31;1mNEW AND UPDATED BOOKS AND RELATED INFRASTRUCTURE[0m

  See the {log entries |
  http://code.google.com/p/acl2-books/source/list} for a record of
  books changed or added since the preceding release, with log
  entries.

  We note in particular the new [47msystem/[0m directory, which begins to
  specify ACL2 system code in anticipation of opening the
  architecture of ACL2 (see [defattach] for a relevant tool).  Some
  system functions were changed slightly (but with the expectation of
  not generally affecting ACL2 behavior) in support of the
  development of this directory.  Those interested in contributing to
  further such efforts are invited to contact the ACL2 implementors.

  New utilities have been provided for certifying most of the
  distributed books with more `make'-level parallelism.  For example,
  we have obtained close to a 12x reduction in time by using `[47mmake -j
  24 regression-fast[0m' on a 24-processor machine.  For more
  information see [47mbooks/make-targets[0m, or to include the
  [47mbooks/workshops[0m in the regression run, see
  [47mbooks/regression-targets[0m.  Thanks to Sol Swords for providing these
  nice utilities.

  The top-level makefile, [47mGNUmakefile[0m, has been fixed so that the build
  processes (which are inherently sequential) will ignore the [47m-j[0m
  option of `make'.  Note that regressions can still, however, be
  done in parallel, as the [47m-j[0m option will be passed automatically to
  the appropriate `make' command.

  [31;1mEMACS SUPPORT[0m

  [31;1mEXPERIMENTAL VERSIONS[0m

  The HONS version, supported primarily by Bob Boyer and Warren Hunt
  (see [hons-and-memoization]), has undergone numerous improvements.
  For example, keyword argument [47m:FORGET[0m is now supported when calling
  [47m[memoize][0m from within the ACL2 loop, and system function [47mworse-than[0m
  is [memoize]d with the [47m:condition[0m that both terms are function
  applications (clearing the memo-table after each prover
  invocation).  Thanks to Jared Davis and Sol Swords for
  investigating the memoization of [47mworse-than[0m, and with suitable
  [47mcondition[0m.  Thanks also to Jared Davis for contributing structural
  modifications to the implementation of [47m[hons][0m.

  David Rager contributed modifications to the parallel version (see
  [parallelism]), which include taking advantage of atomic increments
  available at least since Version 1.0.21 of SBCL and Version 1.3 of
  CCL.


