 (O-P
  (ORDINALS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "A recognizer for the ordinals up to epsilon-0

  Using the nonnegative integers and lists we can represent the
  ordinals up to [47mepsilon-0[0m. The ordinal representation used in ACL2
  has changed as of Version_2.8 from that of Nqthm-1992, courtesy of
  Pete Manolios and Daron Vroon; additional discussion may be found
  in ``Ordinal Arithmetic in ACL2'', {proceedings of ACL2 Workshop
  2003 | http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/workshop-2003/}.
  Previously, ACL2's notion of ordinal was very similar to the
  development given in ``New Version of the Consistency Proof for
  Elementary Number Theory'' in The Collected Papers of Gerhard
  Gentzen, ed. M.E. Szabo, North-Holland Publishing Company,
  Amsterdam, 1969, pp 132-213.

  The following essay is intended to provide intuition about ordinals.
  The truth, of course, lies simply in the ACL2 definitions of [47mo-p[0m
  and [47m[o<][0m.

  Very intuitively, think of each non-zero natural number as by being
  denoted by a series of the appropriate number of strokes, i.e.,

    0             0
    1             |
    2             ||
    3             |||
    4             ||||
    ...           ...

  Then ``[47momega[0m,'' here written as [47mw[0m, is the ordinal that might be
  written as

    w             |||||...,

  i.e., an infinite number of strokes.  Addition here is just
  concatenation.  Observe that adding one to the front of [47mw[0m in the
  picture above produces [47mw[0m again, which gives rise to a standard
  definition of [47mw[0m: [47mw[0m is the least ordinal such that adding another
  stroke at the beginning does not change the ordinal.

  We denote by [47mw+w[0m or [47mw*2[0m the ``[47mdoubly infinite[0m'' sequence that we
  might write as follows.

    w*2           |||||... |||||...

  One way to think of [47mw*2[0m is that it is obtained by replacing each
  stroke in [47m2[0m [47m(||)[0m by [47mw[0m.  Thus, one can imagine [47mw*3[0m, [47mw*4[0m, etc., which
  leads ultimately to the idea of ``[47mw*w[0m,'' the ordinal obtained by
  replacing each stroke in [47mw[0m by [47mw[0m.  This is also written as ``[47momega[0m
  squared'' or [47mw^2[0m, or:

     2
    w             |||||... |||||... |||||... |||||... |||||... ...

  We can analogously construct [47mw^3[0m by replacing each stroke in [47mw[0m by [47mw^2[0m
  (which, it turns out, is the same as replacing each stroke in [47mw^2[0m
  by [47mw[0m).  That is, we can construct [47mw^3[0m as [47mw[0m copies of [47mw^2[0m,

     3              2       2       2       2
    w              w  ...  w  ...  w  ...  w ... ...

  Then we can construct [47mw^4[0m as [47mw[0m copies of [47mw^3[0m, [47mw^5[0m as [47mw[0m copies of [47mw^4[0m,
  etc., ultimately suggesting [47mw^w[0m.  We can then stack [47momega[0ms, i.e.,
  [47m(w^w)^w[0m etc.  Consider the ``limit'' of all of those stacks, which
  we might display as follows.

           .
          .
         .
        w
       w
      w
     w
    w

  That is epsilon-0.

  Below we begin listing some ordinals up to [47mepsilon-0[0m; the reader can
  fill in the gaps at his or her leisure.  We show in the left column
  the conventional notation, using [47mw[0m as ``[47momega[0m,'' and in the right
  column the ACL2 object representing the corresponding ordinal.

    ordinal            ACL2 representation

    0                  0
    1                  1
    2                  2
    3                  3
    ...                ...
    w                 '((1 . 1) . 0)
    w+1               '((1 . 1) . 1)
    w+2               '((1 . 1) . 2)
    ...                ...
    w*2               '((1 . 2) . 0)
    (w*2)+1           '((1 . 2) . 1)
    ...                ...
    w*3               '((1 . 3) . 0)
    (w*3)+1           '((1 . 3) . 1)
    ...                ...

     2
    w                 '((2 . 1) . 0)
    ...                ...

     2
    w +w*4+3          '((2 . 1) (1 . 4) . 3)
    ...                ...

     3
    w                 '((3 . 1) . 0)
    ...                ...

     w
    w                 '((((1 . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0)
    ...                ...

     w  99
    w +w  +w4+3       '((((1 . 1) . 0) . 1) (99 . 1) (1 . 4) . 3)
    ...                ...

      2
     w
    w                 '((((2 . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0)

    ...                ...

      w
     w
    w                 '((((((1 . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0)
    ...               ...

  Observe that the sequence of [47mo-p[0ms starts with the natural numbers
  (which are recognized by [47m[natp][0m). This is convenient because it
  means that if a term, such as a measure expression for justifying a
  recursive function (see [o<]) must produce an [47mo-p[0m, it suffices for
  it to produce a natural number.

  The ordinals listed above are listed in ascending order.  This is the
  ordering tested by [47m[o<][0m.

  The ``[47mepsilon-0[0m ordinals'' of ACL2 are recognized by the recursively
  defined function [47mo-p[0m.  The base case of the recursion tells us that
  natural numbers are [47mepsilon-0[0m ordinals.  Otherwise, an [47mepsilon-0[0m
  ordinal is a list of [47m[cons][0m pairs whose final [47m[cdr][0m is a natural
  number, [47m((a1 . x1) (a2 . x2) ... (an . xn) . p)[0m.  This corresponds
  to the ordinal [47m(w^a1)x1 + (w^a2)x2 + ... + (w^an)xn + p[0m.  Each [47mai[0m
  is an ordinal in the ACL2 representation that is not equal to 0.
  The sequence of the [47mai[0m's is strictly decreasing (as defined by
  [47m[o<][0m). Each [47mxi[0m is a positive integer (as recognized by [47m[posp][0m).

  Note that infinite ordinals should generally be created using the
  ordinal constructor, [47m[make-ord][0m, rather than [47m[cons][0m. The functions
  [47m[o-first-expt][0m, [47m[o-first-coeff][0m, and [47m[o-rst][0m are ordinals
  destructors.  Finally, the function [47m[o-finp][0m and the macro [47m[o-infp][0m
  tell whether an ordinal is finite or infinite, respectively.

  The function [47m[o<][0m compares two [47mepsilon-0[0m ordinals, [47mx[0m and [47my[0m.  If both
  are integers, [47m(o< x y)[0m is just [47mx<y[0m.  If one is an integer and the
  other is a [47m[cons][0m, the integer is the smaller.  Otherwise, [47m[o<][0m
  recursively compares the [47m[o-first-expt][0ms of the ordinals to
  determine which is smaller.  If they are the same, the
  [47m[o-first-coeff][0ms of the ordinals are compared.  If they are equal,
  the [47m[o-rst][0ms of the ordinals are recursively compared.

  Fundamental to ACL2 is the fact that [47m[o<][0m is well-founded on
  [47mepsilon-0[0m ordinals.  That is, there is no ``infinitely descending
  chain'' of such ordinals.  See [proof-of-well-foundedness].

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<o-p>

    (defun o-p (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (if (o-finp x)
          (natp x)
        (and (consp (car x))
             (o-p (o-first-expt x))
             (not (eql 0 (o-first-expt x)))
             (posp (o-first-coeff x))
             (o-p (o-rst x))
             (o< (o-first-expt (o-rst x))
                 (o-first-expt x)))))")
