  (ORDINALS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Returns the rest of an infinite ordinal

  An ACL2 infinite ordinal is a list whose elements are
  exponent-coefficient pairs (see [o-p] and see [o-infp]).  The first
  exponent and first coefficient of an ordinal can be obtained by
  using [47m[o-first-expt][0m and [47m[o-first-coeff][0m respectively.  To obtain
  the rest of the ordinal (for recursive analysis), use the [47mo-rst[0m
  function. It returns the rest of the ordinal after the first
  exponent and coefficient are removed.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<o-rst>

    (defun o-rst (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (consp x)))
      (cdr x))")
 (O<
  (ORDINALS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "The well-founded less-than relation on ordinals up to [47mepsilon-0[0m

  If [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are both [47mo-p[0ms (see [o-p]) then [47m(o< x y)[0m is true iff [47mx[0m is
  strictly less than [47my[0m.  [47mo<[0m is well-founded on the [47m[o-p][0ms.  When [47mx[0m
  and [47my[0m are both nonnegative integers, [47mo<[0m is just the familiar ``less
  than'' relation ([47m[<][0m).

  [47mo<[0m plays a key role in the formal underpinnings of the ACL2 logic.
  In order for a recursive definition to be admissible it must be
  proved to ``terminate.'' By terminate we mean that the arguments to
  the function ``get smaller'' as the function recurs and this sense
  of size comparison must be such that there is no ``infinitely
  descending'' sequence of ever smaller arguments.  That is, the
  relation used to compare successive arguments must be well-founded
  on the domain being measured.

  The most basic way ACL2 provides to prove termination requires the
  user to supply (perhaps implicitly) a mapping of the argument
  tuples into the ordinals with some ``measure'' expression in such a
  way that the measures of the successive argument tuples produced by
  recursion decrease according to the relation [47mo<[0m.  The validity of
  this method rests on the well-foundedness of [47mo<[0m on the [47m[o-p][0ms.

  Without loss of generality, suppose the definition in question
  introduces the function [47mf[0m, with one formal parameter [47mx[0m (which might
  be a list of objects).  Then we require that there exist a measure
  expression, [47m(m x)[0m, that always produces an [47m[o-p][0m.  Furthermore,
  consider any recursive call, [47m(f (d x))[0m, in the body of the
  definition.  Let [47mhyps[0m be the conjunction of terms, each of which is
  either the test of an [47m[if][0m in the body or else the negation of such
  a test, describing the path through the body to the recursive call
  in question.  Then it must be a theorem that

    (IMPLIES hyps (O< (m (d x)) (m x))).

  When we say [47mo<[0m is ``well-founded'' on the [47m[o-p][0ms we mean that there
  is no infinite sequence of [47m[o-p][0ms such that each is smaller than
  its predecessor in the sequence.  Thus, the theorems that must be
  proved about [47mf[0m when it is introduced establish that it cannot recur
  forever because each time a recursive call is taken [47m(m x)[0m gets
  smaller.  From this, and the syntactic restrictions on definitions,
  it can be shown (as on page 44 in ``A Computational Logic'', Boyer
  and Moore, Academic Press, 1979) that there exists a function
  satisfying the definition; intuitively, the value assigned to any
  given [47mx[0m by the alleged function is that computed by a sufficiently
  large machine.  Hence, the logic is consistent if the axiom
  defining [47mf[0m is added.

  See [o-p] for a discussion of the ordinals and how to compare two
  ordinals.

  The definitional principle permits the use of relations other than [47mo<[0m
  but they must first be proved to be well-founded on some domain.
  See [well-founded-relation-rule].  Roughly put, alternative
  relations are shown well-founded by providing an order-preserving
  mapping from their domain into the ordinals.  See [defun] for
  details on how to specify which well-founded relation is to be
  used.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<o<>

    (defun o< (x y)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (o<g x) (o<g y))))
      (cond ((o-finp x) (or (o-infp y) (< x y)))
            ((o-finp y) nil)
            ((not (equal (o-first-expt x)
                         (o-first-expt y)))
             (o< (o-first-expt x) (o-first-expt y)))
            ((not (= (o-first-coeff x) (o-first-coeff y)))
             (< (o-first-coeff x) (o-first-coeff y)))
            (t (o< (o-rst x) (o-rst y)))))")
