 (OK-IF
  (BREAK-REWRITE)
  "Conditional exit from [47mbreak-rewrite[0m

  Recall that one way to exit from a [break-rewrite] interactive break
  is to type the command [47m:ok[0m.  See [brr-commands].  The [47m:ok[0m command
  exits silently, without printing the result of attempting to apply
  the rule that caused the break.  The [47m:ok-if[0m command takes a term as
  an argument and is like [47m:ok[0m but exits only if the term evaluates to
  non-[47mnil[0m.

    Example Form:
    :ok-if (null (brr@ :wonp))

    General Form:
    :ok-if expr

  where [47mexpr[0m is a term involving no free variables other than [47mstate[0m and
  returning one non-[47mstate[0m result which is treated as Boolean.  This
  form is intended to be executed from within [47mbreak-rewrite[0m (see
  [break-rewrite]).

  Consider first the simple situation that the [47m(ok-if term)[0m is a
  command read by [47mbreak-rewrite[0m.  Then, if the term is non-[47mnil[0m,
  [47mbreak-rewrite[0m exits and otherwise it does not.

  More generally, [47mok-if[0m returns an ACL2 error triple [47m(mv erp val
  state)[0m.  (See [ld] or see [programming-with-state] for more on
  error triples.)  If any form being evaluated as a command by
  [47mbreak-rewrite[0m returns the triple returned by [47m(ok-if term)[0m then the
  effect of that form is to exit [break-rewrite] if term is non-[47mnil[0m.
  Thus, one might define a function or macro that returns the value
  of [47mok-if[0m expressions on all outputs and thus create a convenient
  new way to exit [47mbreak-rewrite[0m.

  The exit test, [47mterm[0m, generally uses [47mbrr@[0m to access context sensitive
  information about the attempted rule application.  See [brr@].
  [47mOk-if[0m is useful inside of command sequences produced by break
  conditions.  See [monitor].  [47m:ok-if[0m is most useful after an [47m:eval[0m
  command has caused [47mbreak-rewrite[0m to try to apply the rule because
  in the resulting break environment [47mexpr[0m can access such things as
  whether the rule succeeded, if so, what term it produced, and if
  not, why.  There is no need to use [47m:ok-if[0m before [47m:eval[0ming the rule
  since the same effects could be achieved with the break condition
  on the rule itself.  Perhaps we should replace this concept with
  [47m:eval-and-break-if[0m?  Time will tell.")
