 (OOPS
  (HISTORY)
  "Undo a [47m:u[0m or [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m

  The keyword [command] [47m:oops[0m will undo the most recent [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m (or [47m:u[0m,
  which we here consider just another [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m).  A second [47m:oops[0m will
  undo the next most recent [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m, a third will undo the [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m
  before that one, and a fourth [47m:oops[0m will return the logical [world]
  to its configuration before the first [47m:oops[0m.

  Consider the logical world (see [world]) that represents the current
  extension of the logic and ACL2's rules for dealing with it.  The
  [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m and [47m:u[0m [command]s ``roll back'' to some previous [world]
  (see [ubt]).  Sometimes these [command]s are used to inadvertently
  undo useful work and user's wish they could ``undo the last undo.''
  That is the function provided by [47m:oops[0m.

  [47m:Oops[0m is best described in terms of an implementation.  Imagine a
  ring of four [world]s and a marker ([47m*[0m) indicating the current ACL2
  [world]:

          *
        w0
      /    \\
    w3      w1
      \\    /
        w2

  This is called the ``kill ring'' and it is maintained as follows.
  When you execute an event the current [world] is extended and the
  kill ring is not otherwise affected.  When you execute [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m or
  [47m:u[0m, the current [world] marker is moved one step counterclockwise
  and that [world] in the ring is replaced by the result, say [47mw0'[0m, of
  the [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m or [47m:u[0m.

         w0
       /    \\
    *w0'     w1
       \\    /
         w2

  If you were to execute [events] at this point, [47mw0'[0m would be extended
  and no other changes would occur in the kill ring.

  When you execute [47m:oops[0m, the marker is moved one step clockwise.  Thus
  the kill ring becomes

          *
        w0
      /    \\
    w0'     w1
      \\    /
        w2

  and the current ACL2 [world] is [47mw0[0m once again.  That is, [47m:oops[0m
  ``undoes'' the [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m that produced [47mw0'[0m from [47mw0[0m.  Similarly, a
  second [47m:oops[0m will move the marker to [47mw1[0m, undoing the undo that
  produced [47mw0[0m from [47mw1[0m.  A third [47m:oops[0m makes [47mw2[0m the current [world].
  Note however that a fourth [47m:oops[0m restores us to the configuration
  previously displayed above in which [47mw0'[0m has the marker.

  In general, the kill ring contains the current [world] and the three
  most recent [world]s in which a [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m or [47m:u[0m were done.

  While [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m may appear to discard the information in the [events]
  undone, we can see that the [world] in which the [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m occurred is
  still available.  No information has been lost about that [world].
  But [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m does discard information!  [47m:[0m[47m[Ubt][0m discards the
  information necessary to recover from the third most recent [47m[ubt][0m!
  An [47m:oops[0m, on the other hand, discards no information, it just
  selects the next available [world] on the kill ring and doing
  enough [47m:oops[0mes will return you to your starting point.

  We can put this another way.  You can freely type [47m:oops[0m and inspect
  the [world] that you thus obtain with [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[pc][0m, and other
  [history] [command]s.  You can repeat this as often as you wish
  without risking the permanent loss of any information.  But you
  must be more careful typing [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m or [47m:u[0m.  While [47m:oops[0m makes [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m
  seem ``safe'' because the most recent [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m can always be undone,
  information is lost when you execute [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m.

  We note that [47m:ubt[0m and [47m:u[0m may remove compiled definitions (but note
  that in some Lisps, including CCL (OpenMCL) and SBCL, functions are
  always compiled).  When the original world is restored using [47m:oops[0m,
  restored functions will not generally be compiled (except for Lisps
  as above), though the user can remedy this situation; see [comp].

  Finally, we note that our implementation of [47moops[0m can use a
  significant amount of memory, because of the saving of old logical
  [world]s.  Most users are unlikely to experience a memory problem,
  but if you do, then you may want to disable [47moops[0m by evaluating
  [47m(reset-kill-ring 0 state)[0m; see [reset-kill-ring].")
