  [O>=]
      The greater-than-or-equal relation for the ordinals

  [Proof-of-well-foundedness]
      A proof that [47m[o<][0m is well-founded on [47m[o-p][0ms")
 (OTF-FLG
  (DEFTHM THM XARGS)
  "Allow more than one initial subgoal to be pushed for induction

  This keyword argument for certain [events] controls whether the
  theorem prover will abandon its initial work upon encountering the
  second subgoal to push for proof by induction: [47m:otf-flg[0m is [47mnil[0m for
  that behavior, but [47mt[0m if it should instead continue ``Onward Through
  the Fog'' and complete [waterfall] processing before starting any
  proof by induction.

  The default value for [47m:otf-flg[0m is [47mnil[0m except during processing of
  [47m[defun][0m events, where the default is [47mt[0m for both termination and
  [guard] proofs (see [defun]).  Note that the default for [47m:otf-flg[0m
  is thus [47mt[0m for processing [47m[verify-termination][0m events, since they
  abbreviate [47mdefun[0m events.  However, the default for [47m:otf-flg[0m is [47mnil[0m
  for processing [47m[verify-guards][0m events.


Further Explanation

  Suppose you submit a conjecture to the theorem prover and the system
  splits it up into many subgoals.  Any subgoal not proved by other
  methods is eventually set aside for an attempted induction proof.
  But upon setting aside the second such subgoal, the system chickens
  out and decides that rather than prove n>1 subgoals inductively, it
  will abandon its initial work and attempt induction on the
  originally submitted conjecture.  The [47m:otf-flg[0m (Onward Thru the
  Fog) allows you to override this chickening out. When [47m:otf-flg[0m is
  [47mt[0m, the system will push all the initial subgoals and proceed to try
  to prove each, independently, by induction.

  Even when you don't expect induction to be used or to succeed,
  setting the [47m:otf-flg[0m is a good way to force the system to generate
  and display all the initial subgoals.

  For [47m[defthm][0m and [47m[thm][0m, [47m:otf-flg[0m is a keyword argument that is a peer
  to [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m.  It may be supplied as in the
  following examples; also see [defthm].

    (thm (my-predicate x y) :rule-classes nil :otf-flg t)

    (defthm append-assoc
      (equal (append (append x y) z)
             (append x (append y z)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :induct t))
      :otf-flg t)

  The [47m:otf-flg[0m may be supplied to [47m[defun][0m via the [47m[xargs][0m declare
  option.  When you supply an [47m:otf-flg[0m hint to [47mdefun[0m, the flag is
  effective for the termination proofs and the guard proofs, if any.")
