 (PACKAGES-FOR-GENERATED-SYMBOLS
  (PACKAGES)
  "Convention for packages of generated symbols

  ACL2 utilities, whether defined in the ACL2 sources or in books, will
  ideally provide the following default behavior when feasible:
  generated symbols should be in the [47m[current-package][0m.  This is the
  ACL2 version of the general principle enforced by almost all
  programming languages: names introduced are, by default, in the
  current namespace.  We do not claim that ACL2 currently adheres to
  this principle in all cases, but our hope that this situation is
  improved over time.  Indeed, the utilities [47m[defequiv][0m,
  [47m[defrefinement][0m and [47m[defcong][0m have been improved to conform to this
  principle; see [note-8-3].  We hope that future utilities, not only
  in ACL2 but also in the [community-books], will respect this
  principle when feasible.  Conforming to this principle requires
  using [47m[make-event][0m in a top level form to determine the
  [47m[current-package][0m from [47m[state][0m and then passing this package to
  functions that generate symbols.  The section [47mSymbol generation
  utilities[0m of ACL2 source file [47mdefthm.lisp[0m contains utilities that
  are useful for generating symbols.  The code was adapted from
  similar code in [47m[ACL2s][0m; see the community book
  [47macl2s/utilities.lisp[0m which includes even more such utilities.  To
  see an example of a utility that generates symbols in the current
  package, see [47mbooks/acl2s/defunc.lisp[0m, which contains the
  [47m[ACL2s::defunc][0m utility.  Other examples include [47m[defequiv][0m,
  [47m[defrefinement][0m and [47m[defcong][0m in [47mdefthm.lisp[0m.")
