 (PAIRLIS
  (LISTS ALISTS)
  "See [pairlis$]

  The Common Lisp language allows its [47mpairlis[0m function to construct an
  alist without specifying a single order!  So we have to define our
  own version: See [pairlis$].")
 (PAIRLIS$
  (LISTS ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Zipper together two lists

  The Common Lisp language allows its [47m[pairlis][0m function to construct
  an alist in any order!  So we have to define our own version,
  [47mpairlis$[0m.  It returns the list of pairs obtained by [47m[cons][0ming
  together successive respective members of the given lists until the
  first list runs out.  (Hence in particular, if the second argument
  is [47mnil[0m then each element of the first argument is paired with [47mnil[0m.)

  The [guard] for [47mpairlis$[0m requires that its arguments are true lists.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<pairlis$>

    (defun pairlis$ (x y)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp x) (true-listp y))))
      (mbe :logic (cond ((endp x) nil)
                        (t (cons (cons (car x) (car y))
                                 (pairlis$ (cdr x) (cdr y)))))
           :exec (pairlis$-tailrec x y nil)))")
 (PAIRLIS-X1
  (LISTS ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Cons a given element to each member of a list

  The call [47m(pairlis-x1 x1 lst)[0m conses [47mx1[0m onto each element of the
  true-list, [47mlst[0m.")
 (PAIRLIS-X2
  (LISTS ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Cons each element of a list with a given element

  The call [47m(pairlis-x2 lst x2)[0m creates an alist consing each element of
  [47mlst[0m, a true-list, with [47mx2[0m.")
 (PAND
  (PARALLEL-PROGRAMMING ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Parallel, Boolean version of [47m[and][0m

  This [documentation] topic relates to the experimental extension of
  ACL2 supporting parallel execution and proof; see [parallelism],
  and see [parallel-programming], which has a disclaimer.

    Example Forms:
    (pand (subsetp-equal x y)
          (subsetp-equal y x))

    (pand (declare
           (granularity
            (and (> (length x) 500)
                 (> (length y) 500))))
           (subsetp-equal x y)
           (subsetp-equal y x))

    General Form:
    (pand (declare (granularity expr)) ; optional granularity declaration
          arg1 ... argN)

  where [47mN >= 0[0m and each [47margi[0m and [47mexpr[0m are arbitrary terms.

  [47mPand[0m evaluates its arguments in parallel.  It returns a Boolean
  result: [47mnil[0m if any argument evaluates to [47mnil[0m, else [47mt[0m.  Note that
  [47mpand[0m always returns a Boolean result, even though [47mand[0m can return a
  non-[47mnil[0m value other than [47mt[0m, namely the value of its last argument.
  (A moment's reflection will make it clear that in order for [47m[por][0m
  to parallelize efficiently, it needs to return a Boolean value; so
  [47mpand[0m returns a Boolean value for consistency with [47m[por][0m.)

  Another difference between [47mpand[0m and [47m[and][0m is that for a call of [47mpand[0m,
  even if an argument evaluates to [47mnil[0m, a subsequent argument may be
  evaluated.  Consider the following example (where [47mcw[0m prints a
  string; see [cw]).

    (defun bar ()
      (pand (equal (make-list 100000) nil) ; evaluates to nil
            (cw \"hello world~%\")))

  When [47m(bar)[0m is evaluated, the above arguments of [47mpand[0m can execute in
  parallel, causing ``[47mhello world[0m'' to be printed to the terminal.
  If we had used [47mand[0m rather than [47mpand[0m, then since [47m(equal (make-list
  100000) nil)[0m evaluates to [47mnil[0m, the above call of [47m[cw][0m would be
  avoided and no such printing would take place.  Even with [47mpand[0m,
  such printing [3mmight[0m not take place, depending on resources, timing
  of thread creation, and whether or not parallel execution is
  enabled (see [set-parallel-execution]).

  Note that unlike the case for [47m[and][0m, the definition of [47mpand[0m does not
  provide [47m(consp x)[0m as a [guard] to [47m(car x)[0m in the call of [47mpand[0m
  below:

    (defun h (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (pand (consp x) (equal (car x) 'foo)))

  As a result, [guard] verification will fail for the above definition.
  If [47mpand[0m were replaced by [47mand[0m, then [guard] verification would
  succeed.

  See [parallelism-tutorial] for another example.  Also see
  [parallelism-at-the-top-level] for restrictions on evaluating
  parallelism primitives from within the ACL2 top-level loop.
  Finally see [early-termination] to read how [47mpand[0m can offer more
  efficiency than [47m[and][0m by avoiding evaluation of some of its
  arguments.")
