 (PKG-WITNESS
  (PACKAGES ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Return a specific symbol in the indicated package

  For any string [47mpkg[0m that names a package currently known to ACL2,
  [47m(pkg-witness pkg)[0m is a symbol in that package whose [47m[symbol-name][0m
  is the value of constant [47m*pkg-witness-name*[0m.  Logically, this is
  the case even if the package is not currently known to ACL2.
  However, if [47mpkg-witness[0m is called on a string that is not the name
  of a package known to ACL2, a hard Lisp error will result.

  [47m(Pkg-witness pkg)[0m has a guard of [47m(and (stringp pkg) (not (equal pkg
  \"\")))[0m.  If [47mpkg[0m is not a string, then [47m(pkg-witness pkg)[0m is equal to
  [47m(pkg-witness \"ACL2\")[0m")
 (PL
  (HISTORY)
  "Print the rules for the given name or term

    Examples:
    :pl foo        ; prints rules that rewrite some call of foo
    :pl quote      ; prints rules that rewrite quoted constants
    :pl (+ x y)    ; prints rules that rewrite (+ x y)
    :pl '(4 1 2 3) ; prints rules that rewrite '(4 1 2 3)

  Also see [pl2], which restricts output to rules that you specify for
  a given term.

  [47mPl[0m takes one argument, which should be a symbol or a term.

  First suppose that the argument is a symbol.  Then it should be the
  symbol [47mquote[0m, a function symbol, or else a macro alias for a
  function symbol (see [macro-aliases-table]), which is treated as
  the corresponding function symbol.  When the argument to [47mpl[0m is
  [47mquote[0m, [47mpl[0m displays the rules that rewrite quoted constants, i.e.,
  the [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m rules.  Otherwise, [47m:pl[0m displays
  rules that apply to terms whose top function symbol is the one
  specified, specifically, rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m, and [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m.  For each class, rules are displayed in
  order from the most recently submitted rule to the oldest, with two
  exceptions: [47m:rewrite[0m, [47m:definition[0m, and [47m:meta[0m rules are considered
  as one ``class'' for this purpose; and only the current (most
  recent) [47m:elim[0m rule is displayed.

  Otherwise the argument should be a term.  Note that the term may have
  user-level syntax (that is, it may be an untranslated term; see
  [term]), for example one that is obtained from the theorem prover's
  output; in particular, macro calls are permitted.  When supplied a
  term, [47m:pl[0m displays rules that are (possibly) applicable to the
  given term, in order (as above, most recent rule first) for each of
  these four cases: first [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m and
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m rules, then [47m:meta[0m rules, then [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules, and
  finally [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rules.  Each rule is displayed with
  additional information, such as the hypotheses that remain after
  applying some simple techniques to discharge them that are likely
  to apply in any context.  (Those techniques include
  [type-reasoning], [forward-chaining], and some attempts to deal
  with [free-variables] including handling of binding hypotheses,
  [47m[syntaxp][0m and [47m[bind-free][0m.)

  It is important to remember that rules displayed as ``applicable'' by
  [47mpl[0m may in fact not be used because of logical requirements, like
  failure to relieve the hypotheses in the context in which the
  target occurs, or because of heuristics such as those controlled by
  the [47m:[0m[47m[loop-stopper][0m.

  A reminder of this can sometimes be seen in the output of [47mpl[0m.  For
  example, if the list of un-discharged hypotheses contains [47mnil[0m then
  the hypotheses of this instance the rule are known, by trivial
  means, to be unsatisfiable.

  Similarly, [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m rules of form [47m[2][0m are not
  actually applied unless a certain computation produces a quoted
  constant.  For example,

    ACL2 !>(include-book \"demos/rewrite-quoted-constant-examples\" :dir :system)

  includes a form [47m[2][0m rule, named [47mform-2-rule[0m, which will rewrite any
  quoted constant occurring a position admitting [47mset-equalp[0m as a
  congruence.  The rule will apply the function [47mdrop-dups-and-sort[0m to
  the constant and replace the constant by the result --- if the
  result is a quoted constant.  That function coerces the constant to
  a [47m[true-listp][0m, drops duplicate elements, and sorts the list.
  Thus, [47m'(3 1 1 2 . 77)[0m in a [47mset-equalp[0m occurrence would be rewritten
  to [47m'(1 2 3)[0m.  However, that replacement is only made if
  [47m(drop-dups-and-sort '(3 1 1 2 . 77))[0m is rewritten to a quoted
  constant.

    ACL2 !>:pl '(3 1 1 2 . 77)

  will include an entry for [47mform-2-rule[0m, but the entry reads:

    New term: (DROP-DUPS-AND-SORT '(3 1 1 2 . 77))
    Hypotheses: <none>
    Equiv: SET-EQUALP
    Substitution: ((LST '(3 1 1 2 . 77)))
    WARNING:  The new term above is only used if it rewrites to a quoted
    constant!

  How might the replacement not be made?  One way is if
  [47mdrop-dups-and-sort[0m and its [47m[executable-counterpart][0m are both
  disabled and there are no [47m:rewrite[0m rules about that function.

  Note that for [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules, only those are displayed that meet two
  conditions: the application of the metafunction returns a term
  different from the input term, and if there is a hypothesis
  metafunction then it also returns a term.  (A subtlety: In the case
  of extended metafunctions (see [extended-metafunctions]), a trivial
  metafunction context is used for the application of the
  metafunction.)

  Note that some rule classes are not handled by [47m:pl[0m.  In particular,
  if you want to see all [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m rules, issue the command
  [47m:print-clause-processor-rules[0m, and for trusted clause-processors,
  [47m(table trusted-cl-proc-table)[0m; see [clause-processor] and see
  [define-trusted-clause-processor].")
 (PL2
  (HISTORY)
  "Print rule(s) for the given form

    Examples:
    :pl2 (+ x y) nil ; prints rules that apply to (+ x y)
    :pl2 (+ x y) foo ; prints rules named foo that apply to (+ x y)
    :pl2 (+ x y) (:rewrite foo) ; if the rule with rune (:rewrite foo) applies
                                ;   to (+ x y), then print it
    :pl2 (+ x y) (:type-prescription foo)
                                ; as above, but for the indicated
                                ;   type-prescription rule

  [47mPl2[0m takes two arguments.  The first is a term.  The second is either
  [47mnil[0m or a ``rule-id'' that is either a symbol or a [rune].  The
  result is to print rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m, [47m:meta[0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, and [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m that apply to the given term.
  Indeed, [47m:pl2[0m prints exactly what is printed by applying [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m to
  the first argument --- see [pl] --- except that if the second
  argument is not [47mnil[0m then it is used to filter the rules printed, as
  follows.

      If the rule-id is a symbol, then only rules whose name is that symbol
      will be printed.

      If the rule-id is a [rune], then at most one rule will be printed:
      the rule named by that rune (if the rule would be printed by
      [47m:[0m[47m[pl][0m).")
 (PLET
  (PARALLEL-PROGRAMMING ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Parallel version of [47m[let][0m

  This [documentation] topic relates to the experimental extension of
  ACL2 supporting parallel execution and proof; see [parallelism],
  and see [parallel-programming], which has a disclaimer.

    Example Forms:
    (plet ((a (fibonacci (- x 1)))
           (b (fibonacci (- x 2))))
          (+ a b))

    (plet (declare (granularity (> x 35)))
          ((a (fibonacci (- x 1)))
           (b (fibonacci (- x 2))))
          (+ a b))

    General Form:
    (plet (declare (granularity expr)) ; optional granularity declaration
          ((var1 val1)
           ...
           (varN valN))
          (declare ...) ... (declare ...) ; optional declarations
          body)

  The syntax of [47mplet[0m is identical to the syntax of [47m[let][0m, except that
  [47mplet[0m permits an optional granularity declaration in the first
  argument position; see [granularity].  In the logic a call of [47mplet[0m
  macroexpands to the corresponding call of [47m[let][0m, where the
  granularity declaration (if any) is dropped.

  [47mPlet[0m cause the evaluation of each [47mvali[0m above to be done in parallel
  before processing the body.  If the above [47m[granularity][0m declaration
  is present, then its expression ([47mexpr[0m above) is first evaluated,
  and if the result is [47mnil[0m, then such parallelism is avoided.  Even
  if parallelism is not thus avoided, parallelism may be limited by
  available resources.

  See [parallelism-at-the-top-level] for restrictions on evaluating
  parallelism primitives from within the ACL2 top-level loop.")
 (PLIST-WORLDP (POINTERS)
               "See [system-utilities].")
 (PLUSP
  (NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Test whether a number is positive

  [47m(Plusp x)[0m is true if and only if [47mx > 0[0m.

  The [guard] of [47mplusp[0m requires its argument to be a rational ([real],
  in ACL2(r)) number.

  [47mPlusp[0m is a Common Lisp function.  See any Common Lisp documentation
  for more information.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<plusp>

    (defun plusp (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (real/rationalp x)))
      (> x 0))")
