  fields from the rule.  See [pr] for further details.")
 (PRACTICE-FORMULATING-STRONG-RULES
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "A few simple checkpoints suggesting strong rules

  Consider these definitions:

    (defun rev (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (append (rev (cdr x)) (list (car x)))))

    (defun nats-below (j)
      (if (zp j)
          '(0)
          (cons j (nats-below (- j 1)))))

  We assume you are familiar with such ACL2 built-ins as [47mappend[0m,
  [47mmember[0m, [47msubsetp[0m and [47mtrue-listp[0m.  When we use throw-away names like
  [47mFOO[0m, [47mBAR[0m, and [47mMUM[0m below we mean to suggest some arbitrary function
  you shouldn't think about!  We're just trying to train your eye to
  ignore irrelevant things.

  Below are some terms that should suggest rewrite rules to you.
  Imagine that each of these terms occurs in some Key Checkpoint.
  What rules come to mind?  Try to think of the strongest rules you
  can.

  Term 1:
  [47m(TRUE-LISTP (APPEND (FOO A) (BAR B)))[0m

  Answers: See [practice-formulating-strong-rules-1]

  Term 2:
  [47m(TRUE-LISTP (REV (FOO A)))[0m

  Answers: See [practice-formulating-strong-rules-2]

  Term 3:
  [47m(MEMBER (FOO A) (APPEND (BAR B) (MUM C)))[0m

  Answers: See [practice-formulating-strong-rules-3]

  Term 4:
  [47m(SUBSETP (APPEND (FOO A) (BAR B)) (MUM C))[0m

  Answers: See [practice-formulating-strong-rules-4]

  Term 5:
  [47m(SUBSETP (FOO A) (APPEND (BAR B) (MUM C)))[0m

  Answers: See [practice-formulating-strong-rules-5]

  Term 6:
  [47m(MEMBER (FOO A) (NATS-BELOW (BAR B)))[0m

  Answers: See [practice-formulating-strong-rules-6]

  We recommend doing all of these little exercises.  When you're
  finished, use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to
  [strong-rewrite-rules].")
 (PRACTICE-FORMULATING-STRONG-RULES-1
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Rules suggested by [47m(TRUE-LISTP (APPEND (FOO A) (BAR B)))[0m

  What rules come to mind when looking at the following subterm of a
  Key Checkpoint?  Think of [3mstrong[0m rules (see
  [strong-rewrite-rules]).

    (TRUE-LISTP (APPEND (FOO A) (BAR B)))

  Obviously, you must think about the conditions under which [47m(APPEND x
  y)[0m returns a true-list.  Recall that [47mAPPEND[0m concatenates [47mx[0m and [47my[0m,
  with [47my[0m being the terminal sublist.  Its definition is equivalent to

    (defun append (x y)
      (if (endp x)
          y
          (cons (car x)
                (append (cdr x) y))))

  Technical Note: [47mAppend[0m is really a macro that permits you to write
  calls of [47mappend[0m with more than two arguments.

  In a sense, [47mappend[0m ``expects'' its arguments to be lists ending in
  [47mnil[0m, so-called [47mtrue-listp[0ms.  (Such expectations are formalized in
  ACL2 by the notion of the ``[guard]'' {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) of the function, but we strongly recommend
  not investigating guards until you're good at using the system.)

  New users frequently start every new theorem by listing all their
  expectations on the arguments of functions in the problem.  For
  example, if the new user wants to make some statement about when
  [47m(append x y)[0m is a [47mtrue-listp[0m, it is not uncommon for him or her
  first to write:

    (implies (and (true-listp x)
                  (true-listp y))
             ...)

  to get ``comfortable.'' Then, thinking about when [47m(append x y)[0m is a
  [47mtrue-listp[0m is easy: it always returns a [47mtrue-listp[0m.  It's always a
  [47mtrue-listp[0m.'' This thinking produces the theorem:

    (defthm true-listp-append-really-weak
      (implies (and (true-listp x)
                    (true-listp y))
               (true-listp (append x y))))

  You'll note we gave it a name suggesting it is ``really weak.''

  One sense in which it is weak is that it has an unnecessary
  hypothesis.  If [47my[0m is a [47mtrue-listp[0m, then [47m(append x y)[0m is too,
  whether [47mx[0m is a [47mtrue-listp[0m or not.  In ACL2, all functions are
  total.  Logically speaking, it doesn't matter whether [47mendp[0m expects
  its argument to be a [47mtrue-listp[0m or not, it behaves.  [47m(Append x y)[0m
  either returns [47my[0m or a [47mcons[0m whose second argument is generated by
  [47mappend[0m.  Thus, if [47my[0m is a [47mtrue-listp[0m, the answer is too.  So here is
  an improved version of the rule:

    (defthm true-listp-append-weak
      (implies (true-listp y)
               (true-listp (append x y))))

  We still think of it as ``weak'' because it has a hypothesis that
  limits its applicability.

  The strong version of the rule is

    (defthm true-listp-append-strong
      (equal (true-listp (append x y))
             (true-listp y))).

  That is, [47mappend[0m returns a [47mtrue-listp[0m [3mprecisely[0m when its second
  argument is a [47mtrue-listp[0m.  We recommend that the strong version be
  made a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) rule.

  The weak version of the rule allows us to reduce [47m(TRUE-LISTP (APPEND
  x y))[0m to true if we can show that [47m(TRUE-LISTP y)[0m is true.  But
  suppose [47m(TRUE-LISTP y)[0m is actually false.  Then [47m(TRUE-LISTP (APPEND
  x y))[0m would not simplify under the weak version of the rule.  But
  under the strong version it would simplify to [47mNIL[0m.

  Technical Note: The weak version of the rule is a useful
  [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) rule.  The
  type mechanism cannot currently exploit the strong version of the
  rule.

  The strategy of ``getting comfortable'' by adding a bunch of
  hypotheses before you know you need them is not conducive to
  creating strong rules.  We tend to state the main relationship that
  we intuit about some function and then add the hypotheses we need
  to make it true.  In this case, there were no necessary hypotheses.
  But if there are, we first identify them and then we ask ``what can
  I say about the function if these hypotheses aren't true?'' and try
  to strengthen the statement still further.

  Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [practice-formulating-strong-rules].")
 (PRACTICE-FORMULATING-STRONG-RULES-2
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Rules suggested by [47m(TRUE-LISTP (REV (FOO A)))[0m

  What rules come to mind when looking at the following subterm of a
  Key Checkpoint?  Think of [3mstrong[0m rules (see
  [strong-rewrite-rules]).

    (TRUE-LISTP (REV (FOO A)))

  The definition of [47mrev[0m in this problem is

    (defun rev (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (append (rev (cdr x)) (list (car x)))))

  Since the definition terminates with an [47mendp[0m test and otherwise [47mcdr[0ms
  the argument, the author of [47mrev[0m was clearly expecting [47mx[0m to be a
  [47mtrue-listp[0m.  (Indeed, the ``[guard]'' {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) for [47mrev[0m must include [47m(true-listp x)[0m since
  that is [47mendp[0m's guard.)  So you're naturally justified in limiting
  your thoughts about [47m(rev x)[0m to [47mx[0m that are true-lists.  This gives
  rise to the theorem:

    (defthm true-listp-rev-weak
      (implies (true-listp x)
               (true-listp (rev x))))

  This is the kind of thinking illustrated in the earlier [47mappend[0m
  example (see [practice-formulating-strong-rules-1]), and, to
  paraphrase Z in [3mMen in Black[0m, it exemplifies ``everything we've
  come to expect from years of training with typed languages.''

  But logically speaking, the definition of [47mrev[0m does not require [47mx[0m to
  be a [47mtrue-listp[0m.  It can be any object at all: ACL2 functions are
  total.  [47mRev[0m either returns [47mnil[0m or the result of appending a
  singleton list onto the right end of its recursive result.  That
  call of [47mappend[0m always returns a [47mtrue-listp[0m since the singleton list
  is a true list.  (See [practice-formulating-strong-rules-1].)

  So this is a theorem and a very useful [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) rule:

    (defthm true-listp-rev-strong
      (true-listp (rev x))).

  Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [practice-formulating-strong-rules].")
 (PRACTICE-FORMULATING-STRONG-RULES-3
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Rules suggested by [47m(MEMBER (FOO A) (APPEND (BAR B) (MUM C)))[0m

  What rules come to mind when looking at the following subterm of a
  Key Checkpoint?  Think of [3mstrong[0m rules (see
  [strong-rewrite-rules]).

    (MEMBER (FOO A) (APPEND (BAR B) (MUM C)))

  Since [47m(append x y)[0m contains all the members of [47mx[0m and all the members
  of [47my[0m, [47me[0m is a member of [47m(append x y)[0m precisely when [47me[0m is a member of
  [47mx[0m or of [47my[0m.  So a strong statement of this is:

    (defthm member-append-strong-false
      (equal (member e (append x y))
             (or (member e x)
                 (member e y))))

  However, this is not a theorem because [47mmember[0m is not Boolean.
  [47m(Member e x)[0m, for example, returns the first tail of [47mx[0m that starts
  with [47me[0m, or else [47mnil[0m.  To see an example of this formula that
  evaluates to [47mnil[0m, let

    e = 3
    x = '(1 2 3)
    y = '(4 5 6).

  Then the left-hand side, [47m(member e (append x y))[0m evaluates to [47m(3 4 5
  6)[0m while the right-hand side evaluates to [47m(3)[0m.

  However, the two sides are propositionally equivalent (both either
  [47mnil[0m or non-[47mnil[0m together).  So this is a useful [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m {ICON}
  (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) rule:

    (defthm member-append-strong
      (iff (member e (append x y))
           (or (member e x)
               (member e y)))).

  It tells the system that whenever it encounters an instance of
  [47m(MEMBER e (APPEND x y))[0m in a propositional occurrence (where only
  its truth value is relevant), it should be replaced by this
  disjunction of [47m(MEMBER e x)[0m and [47m(MEMBER e y)[0m.

  The following two formulas are true but provide much weaker rules and
  we would not add them:

    (implies (member e x) (member e (append x y)))

    (implies (member e y) (member e (append x y)))

  because they each cause the system to backchain upon seeing [47m(MEMBER e
  (APPEND x y))[0m expressions and will not apply unless one of the two
  side-conditions can be established.

  There is a rewrite rule that is even stronger than
  [47mmember-append-strong[0m.  It is suggested by the counterexample,
  above, for the [47mEQUAL[0m version of the rule.

    (defthm member-append-really-strong
      (equal (member e (append x y))
             (if (member e x)
                 (append (member e x) y)
                 (member e y))))

  While [47mmember-append-strong[0m only rewrites [47mmember-append[0m expressions
  occurring propositionally, the [47m-really-strong[0m version rewrites
  [3mevery[0m occurrence.

  However, this rule will be more useful than [47mmember-append-strong[0m only
  if you have occurrences of [47mmember[0m in non-propositional places.  For
  example, suppose you encountered a term like:

    (CONS (MEMBER e (APPEND x y)) z).

  Then the [47m-strong[0m rule does not apply but the [47m-really-strong[0m rule
  does.

  Furthermore, the [47m-really-strong[0m rule, by itself, is not quite as good
  as the [47m-strong[0m rule in propositional settings!  For example, if you
  have proved the [47m-really-strong[0m rule, you'll notice that the system
  still has to use induction to prove

    (IMPLIES (MEMBER E A)
             (MEMBER E (APPEND B A))).

  The [47m-really-strong[0m rule would rewrite it to

    (IMPLIES (MEMBER E A)
             (IF (MEMBER E A)
                 (APPEND (MEMBER E A) B)
                 (MEMBER E B)))

  which would further simplify to

    (IMPLIES (MEMBER E A)
             (APPEND (MEMBER E A) B))

  What lemma does this suggest?  The answer is the rather odd:

    (implies x (append x y))

  which rewrites propositional occurrences of [47m(APPEND x y)[0m to [47mT[0m if [47mx[0m is
  non-[47mnil[0m.  This is an inductive fact about [47mappend[0m.

  A problem with the [47m-really-strong[0m rule is that it transforms even
  propositional occurrences of [47mmember[0m into mixed propositional and
  non-propositional occurrences.

    (defthm member-append-really-strong
      (equal (member e (append x y))      ; <-- even if this is a propositional occurrence
             (if (member e x)
                 (append (member e x) y)  ; <-- the member in here is not!
                 (member e y))))

  So if you are using the [47m-really-strong[0m lemma in a situation in which
  all your [47mmember[0m expressions are used propositionally, you'll
  suddenly find yourself confronted with non-propositional uses of
  [47mmember[0m.

  Our advice is not to use the [47m-really-strong[0m version unless your
  application is inherently using [47mmember[0m in a non-propositional way.

  Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [practice-formulating-strong-rules].")
