  "Rules suggested by [47m(SUBSETP (APPEND (FOO A) (BAR B)) (MUM C))[0m

  What rules come to mind when looking at the following subterm of a
  Key Checkpoint?  Think of [3mstrong[0m rules (see
  [strong-rewrite-rules]).

    (SUBSETP (APPEND (FOO A) (BAR B)) (MUM C))

  When is [47m(append x y)[0m a subset of [47mz[0m?  When everything in [47mx[0m is in [47mz[0m and
  everything in [47my[0m is in [47mz[0m.  We would make it a rewrite rule:

    (defthm subsetp-append-1-strong
      (equal (subsetp (append x y) z)
             (and (subsetp x z)
                  (subsetp y z))))

  We put the ``[47m-1-[0m'' in the name because there is a comparable theorem
  for when the [47mappend[0m is in the second argument of the [47msubsetp[0m; see
  [practice-formulating-strong-rules-5].

  This strong rule is better than the conditional rule;

    (defthm subsetp-append-1-weak
      (implies (and (subsetp x z)
                    (subsetp y z))
               (subsetp (append x y) z)))

  for all the usual reasons.

  Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [practice-formulating-strong-rules].")
 (PRACTICE-FORMULATING-STRONG-RULES-5
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Rules suggested by [47m(SUBSETP (FOO A) (APPEND (BAR B) (MUM C)))[0m

  What rules come to mind when looking at the following subterm of a
  Key Checkpoint?  Think of [3mstrong[0m rules (see
  [strong-rewrite-rules]).

    (SUBSETP (FOO A) (APPEND (BAR B) (MUM C)))

  When is [47mx[0m a subset of [47m(append y z)[0m?  Clearly it is if [47mx[0m is a subset
  of [47my[0m or [47mx[0m is a subset of [47mz[0m.  We could write that:

    (defthm subsetp-append-2-weak
      (implies (or (subsetp x y)
                   (subsetp x z))
               (subsetp x (append y z))))

  The rule generated from this is: ``if you ever encounter (an instance
  of) [47m(SUBSETP x (APPEND y z))[0m, backchain to the [47mor[0m above and try to
  establish it.  If you can establish it, replace the target by [47mT[0m.''

  This does not fully characterize the situation though.  For example,
  [47m'(1 2 3 4)[0m is a subset of [47m(append '(1 3) '(2 4))[0m without being a
  subset of either argument of the [47mappend[0m.

  However, no obvious equivalence comes to mind --- indeed, to express
  any of the ideas floating around here requires defining and
  introducing more functions, which is not recommended unless those
  functions are already in the problem.

  For example, if you defined the concept of ``[47mset-minus[0m'' so that
  [47m(set-minus x y)[0m consists of those elements of [47mx[0m not in [47my[0m, then you
  could prove:

    (defthm subset-append-2-strong-but-messy
      (equal (subsetp x (append y z))
             (and (subsetp (set-minus x z) y)
                  (subsetp (set-minus x y) z))))

  But this rewrite rule would ``trade'' [47mappend[0m away and introduce
  [47mset-minus[0m.  That might be a good strategy if [47mset-minus[0m were already
  in the problem.  But if it were not, it might not be.  We wouldn't
  recommend this rule unless it were helpful in normalizing the
  expressions in the problem.

  We recommend sticking with the weak version of the rule,

    (defthm subsetp-append-2-weak
      (implies (or (subsetp x y)
                   (subsetp x z))
               (subsetp x (append y z)))).

  This illustrates the fact that sometimes there is no strong version
  of a rule!

  Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [practice-formulating-strong-rules].")
 (PRACTICE-FORMULATING-STRONG-RULES-6
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Rules suggested by [47m(MEMBER (FOO A) (NATS-BELOW (BAR B)))[0m

  What rules come to mind when looking at the following subterm of a
  Key Checkpoint?  Think of [3mstrong[0m rules (see
  [strong-rewrite-rules]).

    (MEMBER (FOO A) (NATS-BELOW (BAR B)))

  The definition of [47mNATS-BELOW[0m is

    (defun nats-below (j)
      (if (zp j)
          '(0)
          (cons j (nats-below (- j 1)))))

  Thus, [47m(nats-below 7)[0m is [47m(7 6 5 4 3 2 1 0)[0m.  So when is [47mk[0m a member of
  [47m(nats-below j)[0m?

  The weakest version is

    (defthm member-nats-below-weak
      (implies (and (natp k)
                    (natp j)
                    (<= k j))
               (member k (nats-below j))))

  But clearly we could improve this to:

    (defthm member-nats-below-weak-better
      (implies (and (natp k)
                    (natp j))
               (iff (member k (nats-below j))
                    (<= k j))))

  or even

    (defthm member-nats-below-weak-better
      (implies (natp j)
               (iff (member k (nats-below j))
                    (and (natp k)
                         (<= k j)))))

  Clearly though, we'd like to get rid of the [47m(natp j)[0m hypothesis and
  the neatest plausible version is:

    (defthm member-nats-below-weak-neatest
      (iff (member k (nats-below j))
           (and (natp j)
                (natp k)
                (<= k j))))

  But it is not a theorem!  For example, if [47mj[0m is [47m-1[0m and [47mk[0m is 0, then
  the left-hand side above returns [47mt[0m, because [47m(nats-below j)[0m is [47m(0)[0m,
  but the right-hand side is [47mnil[0m.

  But this suggests a strategy for dealing with necessary hypotheses,
  like [47m(natp j)[0m.  We can move them into an [47mIF[0m on the right-hand side!
  Something like this might be a useful rewrite rule:

    (iff (member k (nats-below j))
         (if (natp j)
             (and (natp k)
                  (<= k j))
             ...)).

  We know, from [47mmember-nats-below-weak-better[0m, that if [47m(natp j)[0m is
  true, the [47mmember[0m is equivalent to [47m(and (natp k) (<= k j))[0m.  So now
  consider what we know if [47m(natp j)[0m is false.  If we can think of
  some term it's equivalent to and that term is simpler than the
  [47mmember[0m expression, we have a strong rule.

  But by inspection of the definition of [47mnats-below[0m, we see that when
  [47m(natp j)[0m is false, [47m(nats-below j)[0m is the list [47m(0)[0m because [47m(zp j)[0m is
  t.  That is, [47mnats-below[0m treats all non-natural arguments like they
  were [47m0[0m.  Thus, when [47m(natp j)[0m is false, [47m(member k (nats-below j))[0m is
  [47m(member k '(0))[0m, which is [47m(equal k 0)[0m.

  So the strong version is

    (defthm member-nats-below-strong
       (iff (member k (nats-below j))
            (if (natp j)
                (and (natp k)
                     (<= k j))
                (equal k 0))))

  This is a great [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) rule.
  It gets rid of the [47mmember[0m and [47mnats-below[0m and introduces arithmetic.

  This example illustrates the idea of putting an [47mif[0m on the
  right-hand-side of the equivalence.  Many users tend to limit
  themselves to propositional forms inside [47miff[0m or to simple
  expressions inside of [47mequal[0m.  But it is quite natural to use [47mif[0m to
  express what the answer is: if [47mj[0m is a natural, then [47mk[0m is in
  [47m(nats-below j)[0m precisely if [47mk[0m is a natural less than or equal to [47mj[0m;
  if [47mj[0m is not a natural, then [47mk[0m is in [47m(nats-below j)[0m precisely if [47mk[0m
  is [47m0[0m.

  The one weakness of the rule above is that it only rewrites [47m(member x
  (nats-below y))[0m when an instance of that target term occurs in a
  propositional setting, e.g., when the governing equivalence
  relation is [47miff[0m.  But, thanks to Andrei Koltsov, an even stronger
  version is available as an unconditional [47mequal[0mity rewrite:

    (defthm member-nats-below-stronger
      (equal (member x (nats-below y))
             (if (natp x)
                 (if (natp y)
                     (if (<= x y)
                         (nats-below x)
                         nil)
                     (if (= x 0) '(0) nil))
                 nil)))

  The key observation is that when [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are both naturals and [47m(<= x
  y)[0m, the result that [47mmember[0m computes can be expressed with
  [47mnats-below[0m.  You may wonder whether this stronger rule allows the
  immediate proof of the earlier [47mmember-nats-below-strong[0m and the
  answer is yes, because from the definition of [47mnats-below[0m ACL2 has
  observed that [47m(nats-below x)[0m is a [47mcons[0m whenever [47mx[0m is a natural.

  Use [47mif[0m to lay out the cases you must consider, if you can think of a
  simpler, equivalent expression for every possible case.

  Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m now to return to
  [practice-formulating-strong-rules].")
 (PRE-BUILT-BINARY-DISTRIBUTIONS
  (ABOUT-ACL2)
  "Pre-built binary distributions of ACL2

  Visit the ``Recent changes to this page'' link on the {ACL2 home page
  | http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/} to see if there are
  other shortcuts available.

  [31;1mWARNING[0m: Some of these packages might be for old versions of ACL2.  We
  recommend that you use the latest version of ACL2 (Version 8.6).


Linux/Mac/Windows Binaries in ACL2s

  The ACL2 Sedan (see [ACL2-sedan]), also known as ACL2s, is an
  Eclipse-based IDE for ACL2 that is distributed with pre-certified
  books and pre-built binaries, though it is not always based on the
  latest ACL2 version.  The ACL2 Sedan is distributed via a Homebrew
  package for Linux and macOS users, and as a Windows Subsystem for
  Linux distro for Windows users.

  See [ACL2s::ACL2s-installation] for instructions for installing the
  ACL2 Sedan. Note that these instructions are typically intended for
  students at Northeastern, but hopefully are useful for anyone
  interested in installing ACL2s.


Windows

  In the past, a Windows Installer for ACL2 has included a Unix
  environment, pre-certified standard and workshop books, and a copy
  of Gnu Emacs.  This capability has largely been superseded; see
  [windows-installation].


macOS

  ACL2 versions are sometimes been made available {under MacPorts |
  https://ports.macports.org/port/acl2/}.

  Some folks maintain {a Homebrew package |
  https://formulae.brew.sh/formula/acl2} that tracks the latest
  stable version of ACL2.


Debian GNU Linux

  A Debian Gnu Linux package is available, which is likely to work on
  other Linux systems as well.  Thanks to Camm Maguire for
  maintaining this package, and for pointing out that as Debian
  packages are simply [47mar[0m and [47mtar[0m archives, they can be unpacked on
  any linux system, and who has said: ``If someone is running Debian,
  all they want to do is 'apt-get install acl2', doing likewise for
  any optional add-on package they wish as well, e.g. emacs, infix,
  etc.'' Alternatively, Debian GNU Linux users may wish to {download
  the ACL2 Debian package for Linux |
  http://packages.qa.debian.org/a/acl2.html}.  An alternate location
  you might want to check is {[47mbackports.debian.org[0m |
  http://backports.debian.org}.


Docker images

  Some folks maintain Docker images for ACL2. Below is a nonexhaustive
  list of such images; feel free to add to this list. DH refers to
  Docker Hub and GHCR refers to the GitHub Container Registry.

    * DH: {atwalter/acl2 | https://hub.docker.com/r/atwalter/acl2/}, GHCR:
      {mister-walter/acl2 |
      https://github.com/mister-walter/acl2-docker/pkgs/container/acl2}

    * DH: {atwalter/acl2s | https://hub.docker.com/r/atwalter/acl2s/},
      GHCR: {mister-walter/acl2s |
      https://github.com/mister-walter/acl2s-docker/pkgs/container/acl2s}")
