 (PRIMITIVE
  (PROGRAMMING ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Primitive functions built into ACL2 without definitions

  The ACL2 [ground-zero] [theory] includes axioms about some built-in
  functions that are not given explicit definitions, such as [47m[<][0m,
  [47m[car][0m, and [47m[symbolp][0m.  We sometimes call such functions [3mprimitives[0m.
  The built-in constant [47m*primitive-formals-and-guards*[0m is an alist
  that associates each primitive with its list of formals and its
  [guard]; that is, each element of that alist is of the form [47m(name
  formals guard)[0m.  The value of this constant is displayed below.

  [31;1mDefinition: [0m<*primitive-formals-and-guards*>

    (defconst *primitive-formals-and-guards*
      '((acl2-numberp (x) 't)
        (bad-atom<= (x y)
                    (if (bad-atom x) (bad-atom y) 'nil))
        (binary-* (x y)
                  (if (acl2-numberp x)
                      (acl2-numberp y)
                    'nil))
        (binary-+ (x y)
                  (if (acl2-numberp x)
                      (acl2-numberp y)
                    'nil))
        (unary-- (x) (acl2-numberp x))
        (unary-/ (x)
                 (if (acl2-numberp x)
                     (not (equal x '0))
                   'nil))
        (< (x y)
           (if (rationalp x) (rationalp y) 'nil))
        (car (x)
             (if (consp x) 't (equal x 'nil)))
        (cdr (x)
             (if (consp x) 't (equal x 'nil)))
        (char-code (x) (characterp x))
        (characterp (x) 't)
        (code-char (x)
                   (if (integerp x)
                       (if (< x '0) 'nil (< x '256))
                     'nil))
        (complex (x y)
                 (if (rationalp x) (rationalp y) 'nil))
        (complex-rationalp (x) 't)
        (coerce (x y)
                (if (equal y 'list)
                    (stringp x)
                  (if (equal y 'string)
                      (character-listp x)
                    'nil)))
        (cons (x y) 't)
        (consp (x) 't)
        (denominator (x) (rationalp x))
        (equal (x y) 't)
        (if (x y z) 't)
        (imagpart (x) (acl2-numberp x))
        (integerp (x) 't)
        (intern-in-package-of-symbol
             (str sym)
             (if (stringp str) (symbolp sym) 'nil))
        (numerator (x) (rationalp x))
        (pkg-imports (pkg) (stringp pkg))
        (pkg-witness (pkg)
                     (if (stringp pkg)
                         (not (equal pkg '\"\"))
                       'nil))
        (rationalp (x) 't)
        (realpart (x) (acl2-numberp x))
        (stringp (x) 't)
        (symbol-name (x) (symbolp x))
        (symbol-package-name (x) (symbolp x))
        (symbolp (x) 't)))")
 (PRINC$
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Print an atom

  Use [47mprinc$[0m to do basic printing of atoms (i.e., other than [47mcons[0m
  pairs).  In particular, [47mprinc$[0m prints a string without the
  surrounding double-quotes and without escaping double-quote
  characters within the string.  Note that [47mprinc$[0m is sensitive to the
  print-base, print-radix, and print-case; see [set-print-base-radix]
  and see [set-print-case].  [47mPrinc$[0m returns [47m[state][0m.

    Examples:
    ACL2 !>(princ$ \"Howdy ho\" (standard-co state) state)
    Howdy ho<state>
    ACL2 !>(pprogn (princ$ \"Howdy ho\" (standard-co state) state)
                   (newline (standard-co state) state))
    Howdy ho
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(princ$ \"ab\\\"cd\" *standard-co* state)
    ab\"cd<state>
    ACL2 !>
    ACL2 !>(princ$ 17 *standard-co* state)
    17<state>
    ACL2 !>(set-print-base 16 state) ; might be nicer to use set-print-base-radix
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(princ$ 17 *standard-co* state)
    11<state>
    ACL2 !>(set-print-radix t state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(princ$ 17 *standard-co* state)
    #x11<state>
    ACL2 !>(princ$ 'xyz *standard-co* state)
    XYZ<state>
    ACL2 !>(set-print-case :downcase state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(princ$ 'xyz *standard-co* state)
    xyz<state>
    ACL2 !>

  The [guard] for [47m(princ$ x channel state)[0m is essentially as follows;
  see [io] for an explanation of guards of certain built-in functions
  that take [state], such as [47mprinc$[0m.

    (and (or (acl2-numberp x)
             (characterp x)
             (stringp x)
             (symbolp x))
         (state-p1 state-state)
         (symbolp channel)
         (open-output-channel-p1 channel :character state-state))

  See [fmt] for more sophisticated printing routines, and see [io] for
  general information about input and output.


Subtopics

  [Newline]
      Print a newline to a given output channel")
 (PRINT-BASE-P
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for print bases that are understood by functions such as
  [explode-nonnegative-integer] and [explode-atom].

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<print-base-p>

    (defun print-base-p (print-base)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (and (member print-base '(2 8 10 16))
           t))")
 (PRINT-CL-CACHE
  (APPLY$)
  "Information about the cache supporting [47mapply$[0m

    General Form:
    (print-cl-cache)

  Logically this function always returns [47mNIL[0m but it prints to the
  comment window information about the cache that supports the
  application of quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects by [47m[apply$][0m.  The name stands
  for Print Compiled Lambda Cache.

  In general there is a non-empty cache line for each [47mLAMBDA[0m object
  used in a [47mdefun[0m or seen by [47mverify-guards[0m or by [47mapply$[0m in the
  evaluation theory.  But the cache has a maximal size.  If a new
  [47mLAMBDA[0m object is seen when the cache is full, the least recently
  used line is re-used for the new [47mLAMBDA[0m object.  By default the
  maximal cache size is 1000.  This can be changed -- with the
  side-effect of clearing the cache -- by exiting the ACL2 loop with
  [47m:q[0m and doing [47m(setq *cl-cache* k)[0m, where [47mk[0m is the new maximal size.

  When [47mprint-cl-cache[0m is called it prints a block about each non-empty
  cache line enumerated from 0, and listed in the order that the
  cache is searched when a [47mLAMBDA[0m object is [47mapply$[0md.  Each block
  contains:

    * [47m:lambda-object[0m - a fully translated [47mLAMBDA[0m object

    * [47m:status[0m - one of four keywords with the following meanings:

        * [47m:GOOD[0m - the [47mLAMBDA[0m object is well-formed and Common Lisp compliant
          (``guard verified'') in the current world

        * [47m:BAD[0m - the [47mLAMBDA[0m object is not well-formed or not Common Lisp
          compliant (``guard verified'') in the current world, but
          (with high probability) there is a world in which it is
          well-formed and compliant

        * [47m:UGLY[0m - the [47mLAMBDA[0m object is so ill-formed it can never be [47m:GOOD[0m in
          any world, e.g., [47m(LAMBDA (T) (CONS 3 . 4))[0m

        * [47m:UNKNOWN[0m - we do not know the status of this object in the current
          world and leave it to [47mapply$[0m to determine the proper status
          the next time this object is [47mapply$[0md.

    * [47m:abs-event-no[0m - the absolute event number (in the current world) at
      which the [47mLAMBDA[0m object was proved to have status [47m:GOOD[0m, or [47mNIL[0m
      if its status is not [47m:GOOD[0m

    * [47m:extracts[0m - some parts of the [47mLAMBDA[0m object sufficient to confirm
      well-formedness.  Well-formedness must be re-confirmed if the
      world is retracted to before the object became [47m:GOOD[0m

    * [47m:problem[0m - One of the following values.

        * [47mNIL[0m - no problem; status is [47m:GOOD[0m

        * [47mNOT-WELL-FORMED[0m - the [47mLAMBDA[0m is syntactically plausible but not
          well-formed but could, perhaps, become well-formed in a
          suitable extension of the current world, e.g., the body
          calls an undefined function (but perhaps it can be
          defined), the body contains a [47m:program[0m mode function (but
          perhaps that could be upgraded to [47m:logic[0m mode), the body
          contains an unbadged or unwarranted function symbol (but
          perhaps [47mdefbadge[0m or [47mdefwarrant[0m could resolve the issue),
          etc.

        * [47m(GUARD-USES-NON-COMPLIANT-FNS . fns)[0m - [47mfns[0m is a list of function
          symbols used in the guard of the [47mLAMBDA[0m object that have
          not yet had their guards verified.

        * [47m(BODY-USES-NON-COMPLIANT-FNS . fns)[0m - [47mfns[0m is a list of function
          symbols used in the body of the [47mLAMBDA[0m object that have not
          yet had their guards verified.

        * [47m(UNPROVED-GUARD-CLAUSES . cl-set)[0m - [47mcl-set[0m is the list of guard
          conjectures -- written as clauses -- that tau was unable to
          prove.

        * [47mRE-VALIDATION-INTERRUPTED[0m - an interrupt aborted the updating of this
          cache line

    * [47m:hits[0m - The number of times [47mapply$[0m has seen this [47mLAMBDA[0m object

    * [47m:guard-code[0m - [47mNIL[0m or the string ``<code>'' indicating that the guard
      has been compiled

    * [47m:lambda-code[0m - [47mNIL[0m or the string ``<code>'' indicating that the
      [47mLAMBDA[0m object has been compiled


Using This Information to Speed Up LAMBDA Application

  [3mRemember:[0m A lot of programmers spend enormous amounts of time and
  effort optimizing code that runs adequately fast!  Do not make the
  mistake of investing your time here unless you really have a
  critical ACL2 top-level read-eval-print form that you [3mknow[0m runs too
  slowly!

  If you see a [47mLAMBDA[0m object in the cache with [47m:status[0m [47m:BAD[0m then it is
  being interpreted.  If you believe it can be converted to [47m:GOOD[0m and
  thus compiled, [3mand you believe you will [47mapply$[0m[3m it often enough in
  the future to warrant trying to speed it up,[0m then here are some
  tips.

  To be converted from [47m:BAD[0m to [47m:GOOD[0m a [47mLAMBDA[0m has to be both
  well-formed and [guard] verified.  The cache doesn't try to verify
  objects that are not well-formed.  So first make sure your object
  is well-formed and then once it is make sure it is guard verified.

  If the [47m:problem[0m is [47mNOT-WELL-FORMED[0m the [47m:lambda-object[0m does not pass
  the [47m[well-formed-lambda-objectp][0m test.  That predicate gives no
  hint as to why, but if you call [47m:[0m[47m[translam][0m on the [47m:lambda-object[0m
  it might give you more information.  E.g.,

    ACL2 !>:translam (lambda (x) (bar x))

    ACL2 Error in TRANSLAM: The body of a LAMBDA object or lambda$ term
    should be fully badged but BAR is used in (BAR X) and has no badge.

  Other typical problems are that a function which was formerly in
  [47m:logic[0m mode is now in [47m:program[0m mode because of an undo, or the
  [47mLAMBDA[0m object is not [tame], as in

    ACL2 !>:translam (lambda (x) (apply$ (cons x 'nil) 'sq))

    ACL2 Error in TRANSLAM:  The body of a LAMBDA object or lambda$ term
    must be tame and (APPLY$ (CONS X 'NIL) 'SQ) is not.

  Here the [47mLAMBDA[0m is unfixable because the arguments to [47mapply$[0m are in
  the wrong order.  Typing the object correctly may fix the problem.

  In any case, you may need to extend the world to convert functions to
  [47m:logic[0m mode, obtain [warrant]s (or at least [badge]s for functions
  that return multiple results), or even use a different [47mLAMBDA[0m
  object.

  When you think you've got a well-formed [47mLAMBDA[0m object, you can get
  the cache to update itself by applying the (new?) object in the
  (new?)  world,

    ACL2 !>(apply$ '(lambda (x) (apply$ 'sq (cons x 'nil))) '(5))
    25
    ACL2 !>(print-cl-cache)

  and see if the status is [47m:GOOD[0m and, if not, what the [47m:problem[0m is.

  If the problem is one of [47mGUARD-USES-NON-COMPLIANT-FNS[0m,
  [47mBODY-USES-NON-COMPLIANT-FNS[0m, or [47mUNPROVED-GUARD-CLAUSES[0m, the [47mLAMBDA[0m
  object is well-formed but not guard verified.  Again, you may need
  to further extend the world by calling [47m[verify-guards][0m on the
  listed function symbols in first two problems or call [47mverify-guard[0m
  on the lambda object itself for an opportunity to supply [47m:hints[0m to
  prove the guard [clause]s listed in the third problem.

  For example, suppose we define [47msqu[0m with a guard of [47mnatp[0m,

    (defun$ squ (x)
       (declare (type (satisfies natp) x))
       (* x x))

  And suppose we define [47mnfixer[0m to always return a natural number but in
  such a way as its type-prescription is weak.

    (defun$ nfixer (x)
      (if (equal x (car (cons x x)))
          (nfix x)
          nil))

  Furthermore, let's disable [47mnfixer[0m so the prover has no way of
  discovering the proper type.

    (in-theory (disable nfixer))

  If we then

    ACL2 !>(apply$ '(lambda (x) (squ (nfixer x))) '(5))
    25

  and use [47mprint-cl-cache[0m, we see that the [47m:problem[0m is that [47mNFIXER[0m is
  not guard verified.  So we

    ACL2 !>(verify-guards nfixer)

  and try the [47mapply$[0m and the [47mprint-cl-cache[0m again.  This time the
  [47m:problem[0m is [47m(UNPROVED-GUARD-CLAUSES ((NATP (NFIXER X))))[0m.  So tau
  couldn't prove that [47mNFIXER[0m returns a [47mNATP[0m.  We can thus

    ACL2 !>(verify-guards (lambda (x) (squ (nfixer x)))
               :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable nfixer))))

  The [47mverify-guards[0m should succeed.  Successful calls of [47mverify-guards[0m
  on [47mLAMBDA[0m objects updates the cache, so we don't have to ``trick''
  the cache into updating itself by [47mapply$[0ming the [47mlambda[0m again.  We
  can now just do [47m(print-cl-cache)[0m and see the [47m:status[0m is [47m:GOOD[0m.

  Whether all this work is worth is depends on how often you're going
  to execute this [47mLAMBDA[0m object!


A Single Performance Comparison

  Suppose we have defined [47msqu[0m and [47mnfixer[0m, disabled [47mnfixer[0m, and verified
  the guards of [47mnfixer[0m as above.  Additionally, define the [scion]
  that maps a predicate over a list and checks that the predicate
  holds for every element.

    (defun$ always$ (pred lst)
           (if (endp lst)
               t
               (and (apply$ pred (list (car lst)))
                    (always$ pred (cdr lst)))))

  and define the function that builds a list of the first [47mn+1[0m naturals
  and use it to define the misleadingly named constant [47m*million*[0m
  which contains the first million and one naturals.

    (defun nats-ac (n ac)
      (if (zp n)
          (cons 0 ac)
          (nats-ac (- n 1) (cons n ac))))

    (defconst *million* (nats-ac 1000000 nil))

  Now observe that [47m(lambda (x) (natp (squ (nfixer x))))[0m suffers the
  same problem we witnessed above: tau cannot prove the guard clause
  because [47mnfixer[0m is disabled.  So we can do an experiment!  How long
  does it take to run this [47m:BAD[0m lambda object over the list
  [47m*million*[0m?  And then, how long does it take to do it again after
  verifying its guards and turning its status to [47m:GOOD[0m?

    ACL2 !>(time$ (always$ '(lambda (x) (natp (squ (nfixer x)))) *million*))
    ; (EV-REC *RETURN-LAST-ARG3* ...) took
    ; 4.35 seconds realtime, 4.35 seconds runtime
    ; (128,000,160 bytes allocated).
    T

    ACL2 !>(verify-guards (lambda (x) (natp (squ (nfixer x))))
             :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (enable nfixer))))

    ...[successful but output elided]...

    ACL2 !>(time$ (always$ '(lambda (x) (natp (squ (nfixer x)))) *million*))
    ; (EV-REC *RETURN-LAST-ARG3* ...) took
    ; 0.19 seconds realtime, 0.19 seconds runtime
    ; (32,000,064 bytes allocated).
    T

  So we dramatically sped up the computation.  But we almost certainly
  spent longer than the original 4.35 seconds debugging the problems
  and converting the object's status to [47m:GOOD[0m.  So unless we're going
  to be doing this repeatedly in the future, it probably wasn't worth
  it!")
 (PRINT-CONTROL
  (IO)
  "Advanced controls of ACL2 printing

  See [io] for a summary of printing in ACL2.  Here we document some
  advanced ways to control what is printed by ACL2's primary printing
  routines.

  See [47m[set-print-base-radix][0m, [47m[set-print-base][0m, [47m[set-print-radix][0m, and
  [47m[set-print-case][0m for discussions of the most common ways to control
  what is printed.  Indeed, these are the only ways to control the
  behavior of [47m[princ$][0m and [47mprin1$[0m.

  See [47m[set-fmt-hard-right-margin][0m for how to set the right margin for
  prover output and, more generally, output from formatted printing
  functions (see [47m[fmt][0m).  Note that [47mset-print-right-margin[0m, mentioned
  below, does not affect such printing.

  The rest of this topic is for advanced users of ACL2.  We refer to
  Common Lisp behavior, as described in any good Common Lisp
  documentation.

  [31;1mPrint-control variables[0m.  [47m[Set-print-base][0m, [47m[set-print-radix][0m, and
  [47m[set-print-case][0m assign to corresponding so-called ``[state] global
  variables'' [47m'print-base[0m, [47m'print-radix[0m, and [47m'print-case[0m, which can
  be accessed using the expressions [47m(@ print-base)[0m, [47m(@ print-radix)[0m,
  and [47m(@ print-case)[0m, respectively; see [assign].  Here is a table
  showing all print-control variables, their setters, and their
  defaults.  Also see [47m[set-print-base-radix][0m.

    print-base          set-print-base          10
    print-case          set-print-case          :upcase
    print-circle        set-print-circle        nil
      [but see remark on print-circle-files, below]
    print-escape        set-print-escape        t
    print-length        set-print-length        nil
    print-level         set-print-level         nil
    print-lines         set-print-lines         nil
    print-pretty        set-print-pretty        nil
    print-radix         set-print-radix         nil
    print-readably      set-print-readably      nil
    print-right-margin  set-print-right-margin  nil

  Each ACL2 print-control variable [47mprint-xxx[0m can correspond in function
  to Common Lisp variable [47m*PRINT-XXX*[0m.  Specifically, the evaluation
  of forms [47m(set-print-base t)[0m, [47m(set-print-radix t)[0m, and
  [47m(set-print-case t)[0m affects ACL2 printing functions in much the same
  way that setting to [47mt[0m Common Lisp variables [47m*PRINT-BASE*[0m,
  [47m*PRINT-RADIX*[0m, and [47m*PRINT-CASE*[0m, respectively, affects Common Lisp
  printing.  The same is true for [47mprint-escape[0m, except that this does
  not affect [47m[princ$][0m or [47mprin1$[0m, which correspond to Common Lisp
  functions [47mprinc[0m and [47mprin1[0m: [47mprinc[0m treats [47m*PRINT-ESCAPE*[0m as [47mnil[0m while
  [47mprin1[0m treats [47m*PRINT-ESCAPE*[0m as [47mt[0m.  Moreover, all print-control
  variables not mentioned in this paragraph are set to their defaults
  in [47m[princ$][0m and [47mprin1$[0m, as indicated by ACL2 constant
  [47m*print-control-defaults*[0m, except that [47mprint-readably[0m is set to [47mnil[0m
  in [47mprinc$[0m.

  [47m[Fmt][0m and its related functions are sensitive to state globals
  [47m'print-base[0m, [47m'print-radix[0m, [47m'print-case[0m, [47m'print-escape[0m, and
  [47m'print-readably[0m, in analogy with Common Lisp functions that don't
  fix [47m*PRINT-ESCAPE*[0m or [47m*PRINT-READABLY*[0m.  But the [47m[fmt][0m functions do
  not respect settings of other print-control variables; for example,
  they act as though [47m'print-circle[0m is [47mnil[0m.  Since ACL2 output is
  produced using the same underlying print routines as the [47m[fmt][0m
  functions, it also is insensitive to all print-control variables
  except for the five above.  To control the print-level and
  print-length used for producing ACL2 output, see [set-evisc-tuple].

  [Print-object$] and [print-object$-preserving-case] are sensitive to
  the values of all of the print-control variables except for
  [47m'print-escape[0m.

  Evaluate [47m(reset-print-control)[0m to restore all print-control variables
  to their original settings, as stored in constant
  [47m*print-control-defaults*[0m.

  (Remark on [47mprint-circle-files[0m: ACL2 typically binds [47m'print-circle[0m to
  [47mt[0m before writing [certificate] files, or auxiliary files that are
  compiled when [47m[make-event][0m forms are present in a book, or files in
  support of [47m:[0m[47m[comp][0m commands.  This binding allows for structure
  sharing that can keep these files from growing large.  End of
  Remark.)

  (Remark for those using ACL2 built on CLtL1 (non-ANSI) Gnu Common
  Lisp (GCL): Note that Common Lisp variables [47m*PRINT-LINES*[0m,
  [47m*PRINT-MISER-WIDTH*[0m, [47m*PRINT-READABLY*[0m, [47m*PRINT-PPRINT-DISPATCH*[0m, and
  [47m*PRINT-RIGHT-MARGIN*[0m do not have any effect for such GCL versions.)")
 (PRINT-GV
  (GUARD DEBUGGING)
  "Print a form whose evaluation caused a guard violation

  By default, ACL2 checks input constraints on functions, known as
  [guard]s.  When guards are violated, an informative message is
  printed; but sometimes it is useful to investigate why the guard
  check fails.  The utility [47mprint-gv[0m is provided for that purpose.
  (Alternatively, you may prefer to avoid guards entirely with
  [47m(set-guard-checking :none)[0m; see [set-guard-checking].)

    Example Forms:
    (print-gv)
    :print-gv ; same as above
    (print-gv ; same as above, showing all system defaults
     :conjunct nil
     :evisc-tuple (print-gv-evisc-tuple)
     :substitute nil)
    (print-gv :substitute 20)
    (print-gv :evisc-tuple (evisc-tuple 4 4 nil nil))
    (print-gv :evisc-tuple (evisc-tuple 4 4 nil nil))
    (print-gv :evisc-tuple (evisc-tuple 4 ; print-level
                                        5 ; print-length
                                        (world-evisceration-alist state nil)
                                        nil ; hiding-cars
                                        ))

    General Form:
    (print-gv :conjunct c
              :substitute s
              :evisc-tuple e)

  where the keyword arguments are optional.  These arguments have the
  following effects and system defaults, but note that the defaults
  can be changed by the user; see [set-print-gv-defaults].

    * The [47m:conjunct[0m argument is [47mnil[0m by default, indicating that a form is
      to be displayed whose evaluation represents the [guard]
      evaluation that produced [47mnil[0m.  A value of [47mt[0m indicates that ACL2
      should parse the guard into conjuncts, and display the conjunct
      that actually evaluated to [47mnil[0m.  It does this by evaluating
      each conjunct in turn until one produces a result of [47mnil[0m.

    * The [47m:evisc-tuple[0m argument should be an [evisc-tuple].  Its default is
      the value of the expression [47m(print-gv-evisc-tuple)[0m, which
      specifies hiding only the ACL2 logical [world], so that the
      symbol [47m<world>[0m is printed instead of the actual world.  See
      [evisc-tuple] for a discussion of evisc-tuples.

    * The [47m:substitute[0m argument is [47mnil[0m by default, indicating that the
      displayed form uses [47m[flet][0m, which avoids duplicate occurrences
      of actual parameters.  A value of [47mt[0m indicates that ACL2 should
      instead substitute those actuals into the guard.  Otherwise the
      value should be a natural number [47mn[0m, which behaves the same as
      [47mnil[0m except when large duplicated terms are to be avoided in the
      precise sense below, in which case the behavior is the same as
      [47mt[0m, that is, [47mflet[0m is used.  The latter case (using [47mflet[0m) applies
      when some variable in the guard or (if [47m:conjunct t[0m is
      specified) conjunct has at least two occurrences, and
      corresponds to an actual parameter with at least [47mn[0m conses.
      Note that the number of conses is counted in the ``translated''
      term (guard or conjunct); see [term].

  Again, the user can change these defaults; see
  [set-print-gv-defaults].  For example, one might wish to evaluate
  [47m(set-print-gv-defaults :substitute 20)[0m so that [47m[flet][0m is used only
  when that avoids certain duplicated large terms, as discussed just
  above.

  [31;1mRemarks[0m

    * (1) [47mPrint-gv[0m starts by temporarily replacing the current installed
      ACL2 [world] with the world that was installed at the time the
      guard violation took place.  The current world is re-installed
      when [47mprint-gv[0m returns.  We illustrate this point with an
      example at the end of this topic.

    * (2) The output from [47mprint-gv[0m always goes to the terminal.
      (Specifically, the output goes to the value of the constant
      [47m[*standard-co*][0m.)

    * (3) While [47mprint-gv[0m is a utility for debugging [guard] violations, see
      [guard-debug] for a different sort of utility, which assists in
      debugging failed proofs arising from guard verification.

  To see how one might use [47mprint-gv[0m, consider the following definition.

    (defun foo (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (integerp x)
                                  (posp (+ -2 x))
                                  (posp (+ 3 x))
                                  (posp (+ -4 x)))))
      x)

  Now suppose we evaluate a call of foo and obtain a guard violation.

    ACL2 !>(foo 1)


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The guard for the function call (FOO X),
    which is (AND (INTEGERP X) (POSP (+ -2 X)) (POSP (+ 3 X)) (POSP (+ -4 X))),
    is violated by the arguments in the call (FOO 1).
    See :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this
    check with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.

  Let us investigate this guard failure.  First we use [47mprint-gv[0m to
  obtain a form that represents our guard violation.  Just for fun,
  we'll check that it indeed evaluates to [47mnil[0m.

    ACL2 !>:print-gv

    (FLET ((FOO{GUARD} (X)
                       (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X))
                       (AND (INTEGERP X)
                            (POSP (+ -2 X))
                            (POSP (+ 3 X))
                            (POSP (+ -4 X)))))
          (FOO{GUARD} 1))

    ACL2 !>(FLET ((FOO{GUARD} (X)
                      (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X))
                      (AND (INTEGERP X)
                           (POSP (+ -2 X))
                           (POSP (+ 3 X))
                           (POSP (+ -4 X)))))
                 (FOO{GUARD} 1))
    NIL

  This form doesn't tell us which of the four conjuncts actually
  evaluated to [47mnil[0m.  We can fix that by using the [47m:conjunct[0m keyword
  argument.

    ACL2 !>(print-gv :conjunct t)

    Showing guard conjunct (#2 of 4) that evaluates to nil:

    (FLET ((FOO{GUARD} (X) (POSP (+ -2 X)))) (FOO{GUARD} 1)).

    ACL2 !>

  But here's another way to analyze the guard failure.  Let's change
  [47m[and][0m to [47m[list][0m in the result of [47m:print-gv[0m, to see which conjuncts
  are false.  Of course, the user can massage the guard form in any
  way that might be helpful.

    ACL2 !>(FLET ((FOO{GUARD} (X)
                      (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X))
                      (list (INTEGERP X)
                            (POSP (+ -2 X))
                            (POSP (+ 3 X))
                            (POSP (+ -4 X)))))
                 (FOO{GUARD} 1))
    (T NIL T NIL)
    ACL2 !>

  The [47mNIL[0m results show that the second and fourth conjuncts of the
  guard were false in our particular case.

  If you use local [stobj]s (see [with-local-stobj]) or stobj fields of
  stobjs, you may need to edit the output of [47mprint-gv[0m in order to
  evaluate it.  Consider the following example.

    (defstobj st fld)

    (defun g (x st)
      (declare (xargs :guard (consp x) :stobjs st)
               (ignore x))
      (fld st))

    (defun test ()
      (with-local-stobj
       st
       (mv-let (result st)
               (mv (g 3 st) st)
               result)))

    (test)

  Then [47m:print-gv[0m yields the result shown below.

    (FLET ((G{GUARD} (X ST)
                     (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X ST))
                     (AND (STP ST) (CONSP X))))
          (G{GUARD} 3 |<some-stobj>|))

  In this example you could replace ``[47m|<some-stobj>|[0m'' by ``[47mst[0m'' to
  obtain a result of [47mnil[0m.  But similar cases may require the use of a
  local stobj that is no longer available, in which case you may need
  to be creative in order to take advantage of [47m:print-gv[0m.  Here is
  such an example.

    (defstobj st2 fld2)

    (defun g2 (st2)
      (declare (xargs :guard (null (fld2 st2)) :stobjs st2))
      (mv 0 st2))

    (defun test2 ()
      (with-local-stobj
       st2
       (mv-let (result st2)
               (let ((st2 (update-fld2 17 st2)))
                 (g2 st2))
               result)))

    (test2)

  In this case, [47m:print-gv[0m yields the following.

    (FLET ((G2{GUARD} (ST2)
                      (DECLARE (IGNORABLE ST2))
                      (AND (ST2P ST2) (NULL (FLD2 ST2)))))
          (G2{GUARD} |<some-stobj>|))

  But if you replace ``[47m|<some-stobj>|[0m'' by ``[47mst[0m'', the guard holds; it
  is only the local stobj, which is no longer available, that
  produced a guard violation (because its field had been updated to a
  cons).

    ACL2 !>(FLET ((G2{GUARD} (ST2)
                             (DECLARE (IGNORABLE ST2))
                             (AND (ST2P ST2) (NULL (FLD2 ST2)))))
                 (G2{GUARD} st2))
    T
    ACL2 !>

  We conclude with an example that illustrates point (1) above,
  regarding the installation of the [world] that was in place at the
  time the guard violation took place.  In the following, the [47m[progn][0m
  call fails when the form [47m(foo 3)[0m causes a guard violation.

    (progn (defn g (x) (consp x))
           (defun foo (x) (declare (xargs :guard (g x))) (car x))
           (value-triple (foo 3)))

  We can then issue [47mprint-gv[0m:

    ACL2 !>:print-gv

    (FLET ((FOO{GUARD} (X) (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X)) (G X))) (FOO{GUARD} 3))

    ACL2 !>

  However, if you try to evaluate this form, you will get an error
  because the function [47mg[0m is not currently defined.  This problem can
  be solved by preceding [47m:print-gv[0m with [47m:redo-flat[0m, to re-run the
  events up to the one that failed; see [redo-flat].


Subtopics

  [Set-print-gv-defaults]
      Set default keyword values for [47m[print-gv][0m")
 (PRINT-OBJECT$
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Print an object to an open object output channel

    General Form:
    (print-object$ x channel state)

  where [47mx[0m is any ACL2 object and [47mchannel[0m is an open object output
  channel.  See [io].


Remarks

  A newline is printed just above [47mx[0m.  To eliminate that newline or
  print a given comment instead, see [print-object$+].

  [47mPrint-object$[0m pays attention to the values of [print-control]
  variables.  To provide them as keyword arguments rather than
  assigning them globally, see [print-object$+].

  By default, the output of [47mprint-object$[0m is human-readable.  To use
  [serialize] printing instead, first set the serialize character
  using [47m[set-serialize-character][0m.

  For a related utility, see [47m[write-list][0m.")
 (PRINT-OBJECT$+
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Print an object to an open output channel in a specified manner

    General Form:
    (print-object$+ x       ; an ACL2 object
                    channel ; an open object output channel
                    &key
                    header  ; nil or a comment string (see below)
                    serialize-character       ; as in with-serialize-character
                    print-base print-case ... ; print-control variables
                    )

  This macro is a more flexible variant of [47m[print-object$][0m.  Any of the
  print-control variables may be provided as a keyword; see
  [print-control].  All arguments are evaluated.

  The [47m:header[0m is printed so that it immediately precedes [47mx[0m.  By
  default, a single newline is what is printed for the header.  If
  [47m:header[0m is specified as [47mnil[0m then no such header is printed.
  Otherwise, the value [47m:header[0m should be a string for which the first
  non-whitespace character (if any) on each line is a semicolon ([47m;[0m).
  If the last character of the string is not a newline, then a
  newline will be printed to separate the string from [47mx[0m.

  The [47m:serialize-character[0m keyword argument has a default of [47mnil[0m.  If
  it is supplied a value other than [47mnil[0m or [47m'nil[0m (even if it is
  supplied an expression other than those two constants), then it is
  an error to supply other keyword arguments.  Otherwise printing is
  done without serialization (see [serialize]).")
 (PRINT-OBJECT$-PRESERVING-CASE (POINTERS)
                                "See [io].")
 (PRINT-SUMMARY-USER (POINTERS)
                     "See [finalize-event-user].")
 (PRINTING-TO-STRINGS
  (IO)
  "Printing to strings instead of files or standard output

  Each of ACL2's formatted printing functions, [47mFM*[0m, has an analogous
  macro [47mFM*-TO-STRING[0m indicated below.  These functions do not
  include a channel or [47m[state][0m as an argument, and [47mFM*-TO-STRING[0m
  returns the string that [47mFM*[0m would print to the state, in place of
  [47mstate[0m; see [fmt].  The legal keyword arguments are described below.

    General Forms:                                 result
    (fms-to-string string alist &key ...)          ; string
    (fmt-to-string string alist &key ...)          ; (mv col string)
    (fmt1-to-string string alist column &key ...)  ; (mv col string)
    (fms!-to-string string alist &key ...)         ; string
    (fmt!-to-string string alist &key ...)         ; (mv col string)
    (fmt1!-to-string string alist column &key ...) ; (mv col string)

  The legal keyword arguments are as follows.  They are all optional
  with a default of [47mnil[0m.

      [47m:Evisc-tuple[0m is evaluated, and corresponds exactly to the [47mevisc-tuple[0m
      argument of the corresponding [47mFM*[0m function; see [fmt].

      [47m:Fmt-control-alist[0m should typically evaluate to an alist that maps
      print-control variables to values; see [print-control].  Any
      alist mapping variables to values is legal, however.  By
      default the print controls are set according to the value of
      constant [47m*fmt-control-defaults*[0m; [47m:fmt-control-alist[0m overrides
      these defaults.  For example, [47m*fmt-control-defaults*[0m sets the
      right margin just as it is set in the initial ACL2 [state], by
      binding [47mfmt-soft-right-margin[0m and [47mfmt-hard-right-margin[0m to
      their respective defaults of [47m*fmt-soft-right-margin-default*[0m
      and [47m*fmt-hard-right-margin-default*[0m.  The following example
      shows how you can override those defaults, in this case
      arranging to print flat by setting the right margin to a large
      number.

        (fmt-to-string \"~x0\"
                       (list (cons #\\0 (make-list 30)))
                       :fmt-control-alist
                       `((fmt-soft-right-margin . 10000)
                         (fmt-hard-right-margin . 10000)))

  Also see [io] for a discussion of the utility
  [47mget-output-stream-string$[0m, which allows for accumulating the
  results of more than one printing call into a single string but
  requires the use of [47m[state][0m.

  [31;1mRemarks on deviation from the [47m[fmt][0m[31;1m functions.[0m

   1. The [47m[evisc-table][0m is ignored by these functions that print to
      strings.  Use the [47m:evisc-tuple[0m keyword instead.

   2. [Iprinting] is turned off during evaluation of calls of these
      functions, even if it is enabled globally.  The reason is that
      for each iprint index, [47mi[0m, that is bound during creation of the
      result string, that binding would disappear after the string is
      returned; so it would be misleading or an error to read [47m#@i#[0m
      after that return.

   3. The following remark is subtle; see [fmt!] for relevant background.
      By default, there is identical behavior for each pair of
      functions of the form [47mfm<..>-to-string[0m and [47mfm<..>!-to-string[0m:
      both functions act like the [47mfm<..>![0m function, which is to say,
      they both avoid printing a backslash ([47m\\[0m) when the right margin
      is exceeded, so that the results can be read by ACL2.  If you
      include the pair [47m(:WRITE-FOR-READ NIL)[0m in [47m:fmt-control-alist[0m,
      then the [47mfm<..>-to-string[0m functions will be free to insert such
      backslashes; but that pair will have no effect on the
      [47mfm<..>!-to-string[0m functions.")
