 (PROFILE
  (EVENTS)
  "Turn on profiling for one function

  NOTE: An alternative to this utility, which has significantly less
  functionality but doesn't use [hons-enabled] features of ACL2, may
  be found in community book [47mbooks/misc/profiling.lisp[0m.  See
  [hons-and-memoization] for a general discussion of [memoization],
  on which this [47mprofile[0m utility is built, and the related features of
  hash consing and applicative hash tables.

  [47mProfile[0m can be useful in Common Lisp debugging and performance
  analysis, including examining the behavior of ACL2 functions.

    Example:
    (profile 'fn)       ; keep count of the calls of fn
    (profile 'fn        ; as above, with some memoize options
             :trace t
             :forget t)
    (memsum) ; report statistics on calls of memoized functions (e.g., fn)
    (clear-memoize-statistics) ; clear memoization (and profiling) statistics

  Evaluation of [47m(profile 'fn)[0m redefines [47mfn[0m so that a count will be kept
  of the calls of FN.  The information recorded may be displayed, for
  example, by invoking [47m([0m[47m[memoize-summary][0m[47m)[0m or (equivalently)
  [47m(memsum)[0m.  If you wish to gather fresh statistics for the
  evaluation of a top-level form, see [clear-memoize-statistics].

  [47mProfile[0m is just a macro that calls [47m[memoize][0m to do its work.  [47mProfile[0m
  gives the two keyword parameters [47m:condition[0m and [47m:recursive[0m of
  [47m[memoize][0m the value [47mnil[0m.  Other keyword parameters for [47mmemoize[0m,
  which must not include [47m:condition[0m, [47m:condition-fn[0m, or [47m:recursive[0m,
  are passed through.  To eliminate profiling, use [47m[unmemoize][0m; for
  example, to eliminate profiling for function [47mfn[0m, evaluate
  [47m(unmemoize 'fn)[0m.

  Functions [47m[profile-all][0m and [47m[profile-ACL2][0m are available for
  profiling all functions or all ACL2 functions, respectively.")
 (PROG2$
  (PROGN$ ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Execute two forms and return the value of the second one

  See [hard-error], see [illegal], and see [cw] for examples of
  functions to call in the first argument of [47mprog2$[0m.  Also see
  [progn$] for an extension of [47mprog2$[0m that handles than two
  arguments.

  Semantically, [47m(Prog2$ x y)[0m equals [47my[0m; the value of [47mx[0m is ignored.
  However, [47mx[0m is first evaluated for side effect.  Since the ACL2
  [programming] language is applicative, there can be no logical
  impact of evaluating [47mx[0m.  However, [47mx[0m may involve a call of a
  function such as [47m[hard-error][0m or [47m[illegal][0m, which can cause
  so-called ``hard errors'', or a call of [47m[cw][0m to perform output.

  Here is a simple, contrived example using [47m[hard-error][0m.  The
  intention is to check at run-time that the input is appropriate
  before calling function [47mbar[0m.

    (defun foo-a (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (consp x)))
      (prog2$
       (or (good-car-p (car x))
           (hard-error 'foo-a
                       \"Bad value for x: ~x0\"
                       (list (cons #\\0 x))))
       (bar x)))

  The following similar function uses [47m[illegal][0m instead of [47mhard-error[0m.
  Since [47millegal[0m has a guard of [47mnil[0m, [guard] verification would
  guarantee that the call of [47millegal[0m below will never be made (at
  least when guard checking is on; see [set-guard-checking]).

    (defun foo-b (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (consp x) (good-car-p (car x)))))
      (prog2$
       (or (good-car-p (car x))
           (illegal 'foo-b
                    \"Bad value for x: ~x0\"
                    (list (cons #\\0 x))))
       (bar x)))

  We conclude with a simple example using [47m[cw][0m from the ACL2 sources.

    (defun print-terms (terms iff-flg wrld evisc-tuple)

    ; Print untranslations of the given terms with respect to iff-flg, following
    ; each with a newline.

    ; We use cw instead of the fmt functions because we want to be able to use this
    ; function in print-type-alist-segments (used in brkpt1), which does not return
    ; state.

      (if (endp terms)
          terms
        (prog2$
         (cw \"~Y01\"
             (untranslate (car terms) iff-flg wrld)
             evisc-tuple)
         (print-terms (cdr terms) iff-flg wrld evisc-tuple))))")
 (PROGN
  (EVENTS)
  "Evaluate some [events]

    Example Form:
    (progn (defun foo (x) x)
           (defmacro my-defun (&rest args)
             (cons 'defun args))
           (my-defun bar (x) (foo x)))

    General form:
    (progn event1 event2 ... eventk)

  where [47mk[0m >= 0 and each [47meventi[0m is a legal embedded event form (see
  [embedded-event-form]).  Each event is evaluated, in sequence.  If
  any event fails, the entire [47mprogn[0m call is deemed to have failed,
  and the logical [world] is rolled back to what it was immediately
  before the [47mprogn[0m call was evaluated.  A utility is provided to
  assist in debugging failures of such execution; see [redo-flat].

  See [set-inhibit-output-lst] for how to control the printing done for
  each event.  By default, each event is printed before it is
  evaluated and a suitable value is printed after successful
  completion.  (Technical note: successful completion produces a
  multiple-value return that is an [error-triple] [47m(mv nil val state)[0m;
  then the value [47mval[0m is printed.)  Printing of both the event and its
  value can both be inhibited by including the symbol, [47mEVENT[0m, in your
  call of [47mset-inhibit-output-lst[0m.

  NOTE: If the [47meventi[0m above are not all legal embedded event forms (see
  [embedded-event-form]), consider using [47m[er-progn][0m or (with great
  care!) [47m[progn!][0m instead.  If your goal is simply to execute a
  sequence of top-level forms that are not necessarily all legal
  embedded event forms, consider using [47mld[0m instead; see [ld].

  For a related event form that does allow introduction of
  [constraint]s and [47m[local][0m [events], see [encapsulate].

  ACL2 does not allow the use of [47mprogn[0m in definitions.  Instead, the
  macro [47m[er-progn][0m can be used for sequencing [state]-oriented
  operations; see [er-progn] and see [state].  If you are using
  single-threaded objects (see [stobj]) you may wish to define a
  version of [47m[er-progn][0m that cascades the object through successive
  changes.  ACL2's [47m[pprogn][0m is the [47mstate[0m analogue of such a macro.

  Remark on return value.  As with all [events], a call of [47mprogn[0m
  returns an [error-triple], [47m(mv erp val state)[0m, where [47merp[0m is nil
  when the event is successfully admitted.  In that case, this is the
  error-triple returned by the final event in the [47mprogn[0m call, or [47m(mv
  nil nil state)[0m if the form is just [47m(progn)[0m.")
 (PROGN!
  (EVENTS)
  "Evaluate some forms, not necessarily [events]

  [31;1mWARNING![0m This event is intended for advanced users who, in essence,
  want to build extensions of ACL2.  See see [defttag], in
  particular, the ``WARNING'' there, and see the warning about
  [stobj]s at the end of this documentation topic.

  [47mProgn![0m can be used like [47m[progn][0m, even in [books].  But unlike
  [47m[progn][0m, [47mprogn![0m does not require its constituent forms to be
  [events] (see [embedded-event-form]), except that the first form
  cannot be a symbol unless it is [47m:state-global-bindings[0m (advanced
  feature, described below).  However, see [make-event] for a
  ``Restriction to the Top Level'' that still applies under a call of
  [47mprogn![0m.

  Because [47mprogn![0m allows non-events, it differs from [47mprogn[0m in another
  important respect: [47mprogn![0m is illegal unless there is an active
  ttag; see [defttag].

  See community book [hacker] for two macros, [with-raw-mode] and
  [with-redef-allowed], each defined in terms of [47mprogn![0m, that allow
  arbitrary forms in contexts that would normally require legal
  embedded event forms.

  Given a form [47m(progn! form1 form2 ... formk)[0m, ACL2 will evaluate each
  [47mformi[0m in turn (for i from 1 to k).  If a form returns more than one
  value (see [mv]) where the first value returned is not [47mnil[0m, then no
  later form will be evaluated and the result returned by the [47mprogn![0m
  call will be [47m(mv erp val state)[0m for some non-[47mnil[0m value [47merp[0m,
  signifying an error (see [ld-error-triples]).  Otherwise the
  evaluation is considered to have succeeded, and will continue with
  later forms.  The value returned by a call of [47mprogn![0m with no such
  error is of the form [47m(mv nil v state)[0m, where [47mv[0m depends on the last
  form as follows.  If the last form evaluates to a single value,
  then [47mv[0m is that value, except if the value is a [stobj], say [47mST[0m,
  then [47mv[0m is the symbol [47mREPLACED-ST[0m.  Otherwise the last form
  evaluates to some [47m(mv nil x ...)[0m, and [47mv[0m is [47mx[0m unless after the final
  form's evaluation we are in raw-mode (see [set-raw-mode]), in which
  case the [47mprogn![0m call returns [47mnil[0m (so that ACL2 can at least print
  the result --- imagine Lisp returning a pathname object from a [47mload[0m
  call, for example).

  The normal undoing mechanism does not generally apply to forms within
  a [47mprogn![0m that are not legal ACL2 [events] (see
  [embedded-event-form]).  In particular, note that a non-[47m[local][0m
  call of [47mprogn![0m in an [47m[encapsulate][0m event will generally be
  evaluated twice: once on each pass.  This fact is worth keeping in
  mind if you are using [47mprogn![0m to change the state of the system; ask
  yourself if it is acceptable to apply that state-changing operation
  more than once.

  Please note that [47mprogn![0m may differ from [47m[progn][0m in the following
  sense: definitions within a call of [47mprogn![0m might not be compiled.
  For example, consider the following book.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defttag :test)
    (progn  (defun f1 (x) x))
    (progn! (defun f2 (x) x))

  If the underlying Lisp is GCL 2.6.7, then after including this
  certified book (where the default certification took place,
  creating a compiled file), then [47mf1[0m is a compiled function but [47mf2[0m is
  not.  For other Lisps supported by ACL2, both [47mf1[0m and [47mf2[0m are
  compiled, though we are not sure that every function under every
  call of [47mprogn![0m would similarly be compiled.

  We now describe, for system hackers only, a sophisticated extension
  of [47mprogn![0m not mentioned above: support for keyword argument
  [47m:state-global-bindings[0m.  If the first argument of [47mprogn![0m is this
  keyword, then the second argument is treated as a list of bindings
  as expected by ACL2 system function [47m[state-global-let*][0m.  Thus, in
  the ACL2 loop,

    (progn! :state-global-bindings bindings form1 form2 ... formk)

  is treated as follows:

    (progn! (state-global-let* bindings (progn! form1 form2 ... formk)))

  However, in raw Lisp the former is just:

    (progn form1 form2 ... formk)

  Thus, one should use the [47m:state-global-bindings[0m argument with care,
  since the behavior in the ACL2 loop can differ from that in Common
  Lisp.  The intention is that one bind only [state] global variables
  that are relevant to evaluation of the forms within the ACL2 loop
  and are harmlessly ignored for evaluation of those forms in raw
  Lisp.  Here is a typical sort of example, as [state] global
  [47mld-redefinition-action[0m is not relevant to the evaluation of [47m[defun][0m
  in raw Lisp.

    (progn! (remove-untouchable 'ld-redefinition-action nil)
            (progn! :state-global-bindings
                    ((ld-redefinition-action '(:doit . :overwrite)))
                    (defun foo (x)
                      (cons x x)))
            (push-untouchable 'ld-redefinition-action nil))

  Finally, we point out a pitfall of [47mprogn![0m related to [stobj]s.  The
  following book can cause a hard Lisp error, depending on the host
  Common Lisp and whether certifying or including it, when certified
  with a non-[47mnil[0m value for [47mcompile-flg[0m (see [certify-book]).

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defstobj st fld)
    (defttag :my-ttag)
    (progn! (update-fld 3 st))

  The problem is that the [stobj] variable [47mst[0m is not known to raw Lisp.
  The compilation problem disappears if the last form above is
  replaced with the following two forms.

    (include-book \"hacking/hacker\" :dir :system)
    (with-raw-mode
     (let ((live-st (cdr (assoc-eq 'st *user-stobj-alist*))))
       (update-fld 3 live-st)))")
