 (PROGRAMMING-KNOWLEDGE-TAKEN-FOR-GRANTED
  (INTRODUCTION-TO-THE-THEOREM-PROVER)
  "Background knowledge in ACL2 programming for theorem prover tutorial

  This brief review of the programming language is presented as a
  sequence of questions and answers meant to test your knowledge of
  the ACL2 programming language.  If you want a gentle introduction
  to the programming language, see
  [gentle-introduction-to-ACL2-programming].

  Before we get started with the programming drill, let us remind you
  that all we're interested in here is the language, not the
  ``program development environment.'' It's impossible to program in
  ACL2 or any other language without a decent environment, one that
  at the very least includes a way to prepare and edit files of
  programs.  The two most popular program development environments
  among ACL2 users are [Emacs] {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) and
  the Eclipse-based [ACL2-Sedan] {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]).
  The Sedan provides the most support for the new user, including
  real-time syntax checking and a facility for testing among many
  other features.  But in this drill we're not interested in the
  program development environment, we're interested in your
  understanding of the ACL2 language.

  [31;1mQ[0m: What do you think this command does?

    (defun rev (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (append (rev (cdr x)) (list (car x)))))

  [31;1mA[0m: It defines a function named [47mrev[0m that takes one argument, treats it
  like a list, and reverses the order of the elements in that list.
  To figure this out from the definition, you have to know that
  [47mappend[0m concatenates two lists.  Logically speaking, the [47mdefun[0m of
  [47mrev[0m adds the axiom:

    (rev x)
    =
    (if (endp x)
        nil
        (append (rev (cdr x)) (list (car x)))),

  implicitly quantified for all [47mx[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: Given the [47mdefun[0m of [47mrev[0m above, what are the [3mformal parameters[0m?
  What is the [3mbody[0m of the definition?  Write down a [3mcall[0m of [3mappend[0m
  that occurs in the body of [47mrev[0m.  What are the [3mactuals[0m of that call?
  [31;1mA[0m: The [3mformals[0m of [47mrev[0m is the list of variables after the first [47mrev[0m
  in the [47mdefun[0m, namely [47m(x)[0m.  We say [47mx[0m is the first (and only) [3mformal[0m
  here.  The [3mbody[0m of [47mrev[0m is the entire [47mif[0m-expression.  The only [3mcall[0m
  of [47mappend[0m in the body is

    (append (rev (cdr x)) (list (car x)))

  and the [3mactuals[0m of that call are, respectively, [47m(rev (cdr x))[0m and
  [47m(list (car x))[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: What do you get if you evaluate [47m(rev '(a b c d))[0m?  [31;1mA[0m: [47m(D C B A)[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: How did [47mrev[0m change the case of the elements, e.g., lowercase [47ma[0m was
  in the input list but uppercase [47mA[0m was in the output?  [31;1mA[0m: This is a
  trick question.  [47mRev[0m doesn't change the case of the elements.  ACL2
  typically converts to upper case when reading symbols (see
  [symbols] {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) for details).  The
  symbol [47ma[0m is read in as the symbol [47mA[0m.  Thus, when writing function
  names, for example, we can write [47mrev[0m, [47mRev[0m, [47mREV[0m, or even [47mReV[0m and
  always be referring to the function [47mREV[0m.  By default, ACL2 prints
  symbols in uppercase.

  [31;1mQ[0m: What does [47m(rev '((a b c) \"Abc\" \"a\" b #\\c))[0m return?  [31;1mA[0m: [47m(#\\c B \"a\"
  \"Abc\" (A B C))[0m.  If you thought the answer was either of these,
  then you need to think or read more carefully:

    (#\\C B \"A\" \"ABC\" (A B C))

    (#\\c B \"a\" \"Abc\" (C B A))

  The first wrong answer above is wrong because Lisp is ``case
  insensitive'' only for symbols, not for character objects like [47m#\\c[0m
  (the lowercase character c) or for strings.  Furthermore, [47m\"A\"[0m is a
  string, not a symbol; it is different from [47mA[0m.  The second wrong
  answer above is wrong because [47mrev[0m does not go into the individual
  elements of the list, it just reverses the order of the elements.
  So it doesn't change the element [47m(A B C)[0m to [47m(C B A)[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: In the question about what [47m(rev '(a b c d))[0m returns, we put a
  quote mark before the [47m(a b c d)[0m but not before the answer, [47m(D C B
  A)[0m.  Why?  [31;1mA[0m: The phrase ``[3mx[0m evaluates to [3my[0m'' treats [3mx[0m as a [3mterm[0m to
  be evaluated and [3my[0m as an [3mobject[0m.  [47m(Rev '(a b c d))[0m is a term to be
  evaluated and denotes a call of the function [47mrev[0m on the value of
  the argument term [47m'(a b c d)[0m.  The value of that argument term is
  the object [47m(a b c d)[0m.  The value of the call of [47mrev[0m is the object
  [47m(d c b a)[0m.  If you have an object, [3mobj[0m, and you wish to create a
  term whose value is [3mobj[0m, then you put a quote mark in front of it,
  [3m'obj[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: Can [47mrev[0m be applied to something other than a list?  [31;1mA[0m: Yes, every
  ACL2 function can be applied to any object.  ACL2 is an untyped
  programming language: every variable ranges over the entire
  universe of objects.  In normal usage, [47mrev[0m is applied to lists but
  there is nothing about the syntax of the language that prevents it
  being applied to non-lists.

  [31;1mQ[0m: So what does [47m(rev 23)[0m evaluate to?  [31;1mA[0m: [47mNil[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: Why?  [31;1mA[0m: Because [47m(endp 23)[0m is [47mt[0m, because [47mendp[0m is defined:

    (defun endp (x) (not (consp x)))

  Thus, if [47mrev[0m is applied to anything that is not a cons, it returns
  [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: So what does [47m(rev '(a b c . d))[0m evaluate to?  [31;1mA[0m: [47m(c b a)[0m.  To
  explain why requires demonstrating that you know what [47m(a b c . d)[0m
  means.  It is the object computed by evaluating:

    (cons 'a
          (cons 'b
                (cons 'c
                      'd))).

  That is, it is a list whose ``terminal marker'' is the atom [47mD[0m.  [47mRev[0m
  treats that list exactly as it treats the [47mnil[0m-terminated list of
  the same elements, [47m(a b c)[0m, because [47m(endp 'D)[0m = [47m(endp nil)[0m = [47mt[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: What does [47m(rev 1 2 3)[0m evaluate to?  [31;1mA[0m: That's a trick question.
  [47mRev[0m takes one argument, not three.  So [47m(rev 1 2 3)[0m is an ill-formed
  term.

  [31;1mQ[0m: What does [47m(rev '(a b c . d . nil))[0m evaluate to?  [31;1mA[0m: That is a
  trick question.  There is no such object.  In Lisp's ``dot
  notation'' every dot must be followed by a well-formed object and
  then a close parenthesis.  Usually that ``well-formed object'' is
  an atom.  If it is not an atom, i.e., if it is a cons, then the
  entire expression could have been written without that dot.  For
  example, [47m(a b c . (d e))[0m is an object, but it could be written [47m(a b
  c d e)[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: Do [47m(rev (rev x))[0m and [47mx[0m always evaluate to the same object?  [31;1mA[0m: No.
  [47m(Rev (rev 23))[0m evaluates to [47mnil[0m, not [47m23[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: Do [47m(rev (rev x))[0m and [47mx[0m always evaluate to the same object when [47mx[0m
  is a cons?  [31;1mA[0m: No.  [47m(rev (rev '(a b c . d)))[0m evaluates to [47m(a b c)[0m,
  not [47m(a b c . d)[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: When are [47m(rev (rev x))[0m and [47mx[0m equal?  [31;1mA[0m: When the terminal marker
  of [47mx[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: Can you define a Lisp function that recognizes [47mnil[0m-terminated
  lists?  [31;1mA[0m: Yes, but it is not necessary for the user to define that
  concept because Common Lisp provides such a function which is
  logically defined as follows:

    (defun true-listp (x)
      (if (consp x)
          (true-listp (cdr x))
          (equal x nil))).

  This can be paraphrased: [47m(true-listp x)[0m means that if [47mx[0m is a [47mcons[0m,
  its [47mcdr[0m is a [47mtrue-listp[0m and if [47mx[0m is not a [47mcons[0m, it must be [47mnil[0m.
  Thus, [47m(true-listp '(a b c))[0m is [47mt[0m and [47m(true-listp '(a b c . d))[0m is
  [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mQ[0m: Can you write a Lisp formula that says ``If [47mz[0m is a [47mnil[0m-terminated
  list then reversing the result of reversing [47mz[0m is [47mz[0m''?

  [31;1mA[0m: Yes:

    (implies (true-listp z)
             (equal (rev (rev z)) z)).

  [31;1mQ[0m: Is this all there is to ACL2 programming?  [31;1mA[0m: No!  ACL2 provides
  many other features.  For a full list of all the primitive
  functions in ACL2 see [programming] {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]).  Some highlights for the beginner are
  mentioned below, but all of the links below ought to be tagged with
  the {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) sign.

  * [47m[list][0m: build a [47mnil[0m-terminated list from the values of [3mn[0m terms,
  e.g., [47m(list x (+ 1 x) (+ 2 x))[0m returns [47m(3 4 5)[0m if [47mx[0m is [47m3[0m.

  * [list*]: build a non-[47mnil[0m terminated list of [3mn[0m objects from the
  values of [3mn+1[0m terms, e.g., [47m(list* x (+ 1 x) (+ 2 x) (* -1 x))[0m
  returns the list [47m(3 4 5 . -3)[0m if [47mx[0m is [47m3[0m.

  * [47m[and][0m, [47m[or][0m, [47m[not][0m, [47m[implies][0m, [47m[iff][0m: The propositional
  connectives.  [47mAnd[0m and [47mor[0m are allowed to take a varying number of
  arguments, e.g., [47m(and p q r)[0m is just an abbreviation for [47m(and p
  (and q r))[0m.  In Lisp, [47mand[0m returns [47mnil[0m if any of its arguments
  evaluates to [47mnil[0m; otherwise it returns the value of the last
  argument!  Thus, [47m(and t t 3)[0m returns [47m3[0m!  If you object to the idea
  that [47mand[0m is not Boolean, don't give it non-Boolean arguments!
  Similarly, [47mor[0m returns the value of the first argument that
  evaluates to non-[47mnil[0m, or [47mnil[0m if they all evaluate to [47mnil[0m.  Both [47mand[0m
  and [47mor[0m can be thought of as ``lazy'' in that they don't always have
  to evaluate all their arguments.  This is really accomplished by
  treating [47mand[0m and [47mor[0m as abbreviations for [47mif[0m nests.

  * [47m[+][0m, [47m[*][0m, [47m[-][0m, [47m[/][0m, [47m[floor][0m, [47m[mod][0m, [47m[<][0m, [47m[<=][0m, [47m[>=][0m, [47m[>][0m: the Lisp
  elementary arithmetic operators.  Both [47m+[0m and [47m*[0m allow varying
  numbers of arguments.  All the arithmetic operators default
  non-numeric arguments to [47m0[0m.  If you don't like the idea that [47m(+ 1 2
  t)[0m is [47m3[0m, don't ask [47m+[0m to add [47mt[0m to something!

  * [47m[natp][0m, [47m[integerp][0m, [47m[rationalp][0m, [47m[characterp][0m, [47m[stringp][0m,
  [47m[symbolp][0m, [47m[consp][0m: the recognizers for the primitive data types.
  The first three recognize subsets of the ACL2 numeric universe.
  The naturals are a subset of the integers, the integers are a
  subset of the rationals, and the rationals are a subset of the
  objects recognized by [47m[ACL2-numberp][0m, which also includes the
  [47m[complex-rationalp][0ms.  The other recognizers listed above recognize
  characters, strings, symbols, and conses.

  * [47m[cond][0m: a convenient way to write a cascading nest of [47mif[0ms, e.g.,

    (cond ((not (natp x)) 'non-natural)
          ((equal x 0) 'zero)
          ((evenp x) 'positive-even)
          (t 'positive-odd))

  abbreviates

    (if (not (natp x))
        'non-natural
        (if (equal x 0)
            'zero
            (if (evenp x)
                'positive-even
                'positive-odd))).

  * [47m[case][0m: a convenient way to case split on the identity of an
  object.

    (case key
      (non-natural -1)
      (zero 0)
      ((positive-even positive-odd) 'positive-natural)
      (otherwise 'unknown))

  abbreviates

    (cond ((eql key 'non-natural) -1)
          ((eql key 'zero) 0)
          ((member key '(positive-even positive-odd))
           'positive-natural)
          (t 'unknown)).

  * user defined macros: using [47m[defmacro][0m {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) you can introduce your own abbreviations.
  We recommend you not do this until you're good at list processing
  since macros are functions that build objects representing terms.

  * [47m[mutual-recursion][0m: allows you to define mutually-recursive
  functions.

  * [47m[mv][0m and [47m[mv-let][0m: allow functions to return ``multiple-values''.
  In Lisp, such functions return vectors of values, the vectors are
  represented as lists of values, but the implementations are
  generally more efficient.  For example, [47m(mv x y z)[0m returns a
  ``vector'' consisting of the values of [47mx[0m, [47my[0m, and [47mz[0m.

    (mv-let (a b c)
            (foo x)
            (bar a b c x))

  evaluates [47m(foo x)[0m, treats the result as a vector of three values,
  binds the variables [47ma[0m, [47mb[0m, and [47mc[0m to those three values, and
  evaluates and returns [47m(bar a b c x)[0m.

  ACL2 also provides many other features, such as single-threaded
  objects which may be ``destructively modified'' (see [stobj] {ICON}
  (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]), including a very special
  single-threaded object that records the [47m[state][0m {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) of the ACL2 system), file input and output
  (see [io] {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign])), applicative arrays
  (see [arrays] {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign])) and property
  lists (see [getprop] {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign])) and other
  facilities necessary for it to be a practical programming language.
  However, we [31;1mstrongly[0m recommend that as a new user you stay away
  from these features until you are good at proving theorems about
  elementary list processing!

  If this little drill made sense to you, you know enough of the
  programming language to get started. Use your browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m
  now to return to [introduction-to-the-theorem-prover].

  If you are uncomfortable with ACL2 programming, we recommend that you
  study [gentle-introduction-to-ACL2-programming] and
  {http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/publications/acl2-programming-exercises1.html
  |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/publications/acl2-programming-exercises1.html}.

  However, we strongly recommend that you first invest in learning
  either the [Emacs] or Eclipse-based [ACL2-Sedan] program
  development environments, since it is foolish to try to learn how
  to program in a stand-alone read-eval-print loop!

  While getting started, many users find the Hyper-Card a handy index
  into the documentation for the ACL2 language:

  {http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/publications/hyper-card.html |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/publications/hyper-card.html}

  Once you are comfortable with the ACL2 programming language, use your
  browser's [31;1mBack Button[0m to return to
  [introduction-to-the-theorem-prover].")
 (PROGRAMMING-WITH-STATE
  (STATE PROGRAMMING)
  "Programming using the von Neumannesque ACL2 [state] object

  This [documentation] section introduces some common techniques for
  programming using the ACL2 state object.  A prerequisite is thus a
  basic understanding of that object; see [state].  We hope this
  section is useful, and we invite suggestions for improvements and
  additions.

  A supplement to this section is the ACL2 source code, which uses most
  (and probably all) of the techniques discussed here.  That code is
  thus a source of many examples, which can serve as ``templates'' to
  guide one's own programming with state.

  Recall that ``ACL2'' stands for ``A Computational Logic for
  Applicative Common Lisp''.  In particular, the language is
  applicative: there are no global variables or side effects.  For
  many purposes this does not feel restrictive; for example, an ACL2
  user who is programming in raw Lisp may well be more comfortable
  coding a factorial function applicatively, using recursion, rather
  than using iteration with repeated assignment to the same variable.

  However, there are situations that call for reading or modifying the
  system state, such as performing input and output, signaling
  errors, saving information from one computation for use in a later
  one, or reading and updating system-level or environmental data.
  This section provides an introductory guide for writing functions
  that traffic in state.  We emphasize that this guide is intended as
  an introduction; more complete documentation may often be found by
  following links to documentation of individual utilities, and
  again, more examples may be found by searching the ACL2 source code
  for uses of the functions and macros mentioned below.  The rest of
  this section is organized as follows.

    ENABLING PROGRAMMING WITH STATE
    STATE GLOBALS AND THE ACL2 LOGICAL WORLD
    A REMARK ON GUARDS
    ERRORS AND ERROR TRIPLES
    SEQUENTIAL PROGRAMMING
    BINDING VARIABLES USING ERROR TRIPLES
    BINDING STATE GLOBAL VARIABLES
    INPUT AND OUTPUT
    TIMINGS
    ENVIRONMENT AND SYSTEM
    REMARKS ON EVENTS AND LD
    ADVANCED TOPICS

  ENABLING PROGRAMMING WITH STATE

  In order to submit a definition that takes [47m[state][0m as a formal
  parameter, you must either declare [47mstate[0m as a [47m:[0m[47m[stobj][0m (see
  [xargs]) or first evaluate the following form at the top level:
  [47m(set-state-ok t)[0m.

  Consider for example the following trivial definition.

    (defun foo (state)
      (mv 3 state))

  If you submit the above definition in a fresh ACL2 session, you will
  get this error message.

    ACL2 Error in ( DEFUN FOO ...):  The variable symbol STATE should not
    be used as a formal parameter of a defined function unless you are
    aware of its unusual status and the restrictions enforced on its use.
    See :DOC set-state-ok.

  If first you evaluate [47m(set-state-ok t)[0m, you can admit the above
  definition.  Alternatively, you can declare [47mstate[0m as a [47m:[0m[47m[stobj][0m, as
  follows.

    (defun foo (state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (mv 3 state))

  A difference in the two approaches is that for the latter, a [guard]
  proof obligation is generated by default.  See the section below
  entitled ``A remark on guards''.

  STATE GLOBALS AND THE ACL2 LOGICAL WORLD

  Recall (see [state]) that one of the fields of the ACL2 state object
  is the global-table, which logically is an alist associating
  symbols, known as ``state globals'' or ``state global variables'',
  with values.  But no such alist actually exists in the
  implementation.  Instead, ACL2 provides utilities for reading state
  globals --- see [@] and see [f-get-global] --- and utilities for
  writing them --- see [assign] and see [f-put-global].  The
  following log shows how they work; further explanation follows
  below.

    ACL2 !>(assign my-var (+ 3 4))
     7
    ACL2 !>(@ my-var)
    7
    ACL2 !>(f-put-global 'my-var (+ 1 5) state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(f-get-global 'my-var state)
    6
    ACL2 !>

  Note that the first result is indented by one space.  This is ACL2's
  way to indicate that the [47m[assign][0m expression returned an ``error
  triple'' and that no error was signaled.  We discuss error triples
  in more detail below; also see [error-triple].

  As illustrated above, the output signatures of the utilities for
  assigning to state globals differ from each other as follows:
  [47m[f-put-global][0m returns [47mstate[0m, but [47m[assign][0m returns an error triple
  [47m(mv nil val state)[0m where [47mval[0m is the value assigned to the state
  global.  The output signatures of the utilities for reading, [47m@[0m and
  [47mf-get-global[0m, are identical.  In fact, the form [47m(f-get-global
  'my-var state)[0m is the single-step macroexpansion of the form [47m(@
  my-var)[0m, as can be confirmed using [47m[trans1][0m.

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (@ my-var)
     (F-GET-GLOBAL 'MY-VAR STATE)
    ACL2 !>

  State globals are useful for conveying persistent state information.
  Consider for example the utility [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m.  The
  form [47m(set-inhibit-output-lst '(prove proof-tree))[0m is approximately
  equivalent to (assign inhibit-output-lst '(prove proof-tree)).  We
  say ``approximately'' because [47mset-inhibit-output-lst[0m additionally
  does some error checking to ensure that all the tokens in the new
  list are legal.  When deciding whether to print output, the ACL2
  system reads the value of state global variable [47minhibit-output-lst[0m.

  A particularly useful state global is [47mcurrent-acl2-world[0m, whose value
  is the ACL2 logical [world].  Because the ACL2 world is commonly
  accessed in applications that use the ACL2 state, ACL2 provides a
  function that returns the world: [47m(w state) = (f-get-global
  'current-acl2-world state)[0m.  While it is common to read the world,
  only functions [47mset-w[0m and [47mset-w![0m are available to write the world,
  but these are untouchable and should generally be avoided except by
  system implementors (see [remove-untouchable]).

  You may wish to modify state globals within a book, but this can be
  slightly problematic because only legal event forms (see
  [embedded-event-form]) may go into [books].  Fortunately there is a
  workaround using [47mmake-event[0m; see [make-event], in particular the
  section ``Examples Illustrating How to Access State''.

  A REMARK ON GUARDS

  For a function definition (see [defun]), if [47mstate[0m is specified as a
  [stobj] as with the form [47m(declare (xargs :stobjs state))[0m, then the
  [guard] for that function is considered to include the condition
  [47m(state-p state)[0m.  By default, [guard] verification will then be
  performed.  (Note for advanced system hackers: There is also a
  stronger predicate, [47m(state-p+ state)[0m, which for example implies the
  guards for [47mfmt[0m and related functions.

  We can illustrate this point by modifying the example above as
  follows, to read the value of state global [47mgag-mode[0m.

    (defun foo (state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (f-get-global 'gag-mode state))

  If you try this in a fresh ACL2 session, the proof will fail with the
  following key checkpoint, which says that the state global [47mgag-mode[0m
  is bound in the global-table of the state.

    (IMPLIES (STATE-P1 STATE)
             (ASSOC-EQUAL 'GAG-MODE (NTH 2 STATE)))

  How can we deal with this proof failure?  One way is simply to ignore
  the issue by defining the function in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode, as follows.

    (defun foo (state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state
                      :mode :program))
      (f-get-global 'gag-mode state))

  Perhaps a better way is to strengthen the guard to assert that the
  indicated state global is bound, as follows.

    (defun foo (state)
      (declare (xargs :guard (boundp-global 'gag-mode state)
                      :stobjs state))
      (f-get-global 'gag-mode state))

  Also see [guard-miscellany] for discussion of guard generation from
  [47m[xargs][0m fields of [declare] forms, specifically, for keywords
  [47m:guard[0m, [47m:stobjs[0m, and [47m:dfs[0m.

  ERRORS AND ERROR TRIPLES

  When evaluation returns three values, where the first two are
  ordinary objects and the third is the ACL2 state, the result may be
  called an ``error triple''.  (Whether it is treated as an error
  triple depends on the programmer.)  Error triples are often denoted
  [47m(mv erp val state)[0m, and common ACL2 programming idioms treat [47merp[0m as
  a flag indicating whether an error is being signaled and [47mval[0m as the
  ``value'' computed.  Also see [error-triple].

  Even ACL2 users who are not programmers encounter error triples,
  because these are the values returned by evaluation of ACL2
  [events].  Consider the following log, where the only user input is
  the [47mdefun[0m form following the [prompt].

    ACL2 !>(defun foo (x) x)

    Since FOO is non-recursive, its admission is trivial.  We observe that
    the type of FOO is described by the theorem (EQUAL (FOO X) X).

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN FOO ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     FOO
    ACL2 !>

  All output above results from explicit calls of output functions,
  except for the next-to-last line, which contains [47mFOO[0m.  Notice the
  single-space indentation preceding [47mFOO[0m.  That space indicates that
  in fact, the value returned by evaluation of the [47mdefun[0m form is the
  error triple whose error flag is [47mnil[0m and whose computed value is
  [47mFOO[0m.  By default, ACL2 prints any error triple [47m(mv nil val state)[0m
  by inserting a space before printing [47mval[0m.  You can change the
  default by setting state global [47m[ld-post-eval-print][0m to [47mt[0m; notice
  how the same result is printed below.

    ACL2 !>:u
              0:x(EXIT-BOOT-STRAP-MODE)
    ACL2 !>(set-ld-post-eval-print t state)
    (NIL T <state>)
    ACL2 !>(defun foo (x) x)

    Since FOO is non-recursive, its admission is trivial.  We observe that
    the type of FOO is described by the theorem (EQUAL (FOO X) X).

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN FOO ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
    (NIL FOO <state>)
    ACL2 !>

  The way error triples are printed by [47mld[0m is controlled not only by
  state global [47mld-post-eval-print[0m, but also by state global
  [47mld-error-triples[0m.  These are examples of ``ld specials''; see [ld],
  see [ld-post-eval-print], and see [ld-error-triples].

  It is so common to produce an error triple whose first (error flag)
  component is [47mnil[0m that ACL2 provides a handy macro, [47mvalue[0m, for this
  purpose.  Thus, [47m(value <expression>)[0m is equivalent to [47m(mv nil
  <expression> state)[0m.  Also see [value-triple] for a similar
  construct that is a legal event form.

  We turn now to the topic of errors.  The macro [47m[er][0m ``causes'' an
  error, but there are really two quite different kinds of errors:
  ``soft'' and ``hard'' errors.  We use the term ``soft error'' to
  refer to a form that returns an error triple [47m(mv erp val state)[0m for
  which [47merp[0m is non-[47mnil[0m.  Soft errors do not interrupt the normal flow
  of evaluation: the error triple is returned to the caller which
  interprets the [47merp[0m flag and [47mval[0m as directed by the programmer.
  Macros discussed below make it convenient to think about soft
  errors as short-circuiting the computation.  Hard errors, on the
  other hand, do actually rip control away from the current
  evaluation and return it to the top-level loop.  Logically
  speaking, expressions that cause hard errors return [47mnil[0m in the
  error case, but the [47mnil[0m is never seen in actual evaluation because
  control does not return to the caller.

  Note that the function [47m[abort!][0m, which you can invoke by typing
  [47m:[0m[47m[a!][0m, always returns to the top level.  Note that ACL2 can prove
  that [47m(abort!)[0m returns [47mnil[0m but that this cannot be confirmed by
  computation.

    ACL2 !>(thm (equal (abort!) nil))

    Q.E.D.

    Summary
    Form:  ( THM ...)
    Rules: ((:FAKE-RUNE-FOR-TYPE-SET NIL)
            (:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION ABORT!))
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    Proof succeeded.
    ACL2 !>(equal (abort!) nil)
    Abort to ACL2 top-level
    ...
    ACL2 !>

  (What actually happens with a hard error, including non-default
  cases, is a bit subtle; most readers will probably want to skip
  this paragraph.  The read-eval-print loop implemented by [47m[ld][0m is
  implemented by a call of the ACL2 evaluator function, [47mtrans-eval[0m,
  on each input form.  If a hard error occurs during evaluation of an
  input form, its [47mtrans-eval[0m call will return with a soft error.
  [47m[Ld][0m, in turn handles that soft error appropriately; see
  [ld-error-action].)

  The most common way to signal errors is the macro [47m[er][0m, which prints
  a formatted error message and returns a soft or hard error as
  specified by the call.  Note however that soft errors are signaled
  using [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions.

  Since the output signatures of soft and hard errors are different ---
  hard errors ``return'' a single value while soft errors return a
  triple --- mixing them in an expression requires embedding the hard
  error form in (an irrelevant) triple, as illustrated below.  All
  branches of the expression must produce an error triple if any
  branch does.

    ACL2 !>(defun chk-find-or-abort (e x state)
             (declare (xargs :mode :program))
             (if (endp x)
                 (value                          ; Note use of VALUE!
                   (er hard 'chk-find-or-abort
                       \"Did not find ~x0!\"
                        e))
                 (if (not (integerp (car x)))
                     (er soft 'chk-find-or-abort
                         \"Non-integer, ~x0, in list!\"
                         (car x))
                     (if (eql (car x) e)
                         (value x)
                         (chk-find-or-abort e (cdr x) state)))))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN CHK-FIND-OR-ABORT ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     CHK-FIND-OR-ABORT
    ACL2 !>(chk-find-or-abort 3 '(1 2 3 4 5) state)
     (3 4 5)
    ACL2 !>(chk-find-or-abort 3 '(1 A 3 4 5) state)

    ACL2 Error in CHK-FIND-OR-ABORT:  Non-integer, A, in list!

    ACL2 !>(chk-find-or-abort 3 '(1 2 4 5) state)

    HARD ACL2 ERROR in CHK-FIND-OR-ABORT:  Did not find 3!
    ...
    ACL2 !>

  See [er] for further discussion of errors.  For some other individual
  topics related to errors see [assert$], see [break-on-error], see
  [error1], see [hard-error], see [illegal], and see
  [ld-error-triples].

  In the next section we discuss soft errors further, in the context of
  programming.

  SEQUENTIAL PROGRAMMING

  This section describes handy ways to modify state in steps, using
  macros that implement a sequence of [47m[let][0m or [47m[mv-let][0m bindings.
  For example, suppose you want to assign the values 1 and 2 to two
  state globals [47mone-var[0m and [47mtwo-var[0m, respectively.  Because of ACL2's
  syntactic restrictions on [47m[state][0m, it is not legal simply to write
  [47m(f-put-global 'two-var 2 (f-put-global 'one-var 1 state))[0m.
  However, [47m[let][0m comes to the rescue as follows.

    (let ((state (f-put-global 'one-var 1 state)))
      (let ((state (f-put-global 'two-var 2 state)))
        state))

  It is so common to bind state successively in such a manner that ACL2
  provides a macro, [47m[pprogn][0m, for this purpose.  Thus, an equivalent
  solution to the problem above is

    (pprogn (f-put-global 'one-var 1 state)
            (f-put-global 'two-var 2 state)
            state)

  or, more simply, as follows.

    (pprogn (f-put-global 'one-var 1 state)
            (f-put-global 'two-var 2 state))

  See [pprogn].  Note that the last form is allowed to return multiple
  values; the only requirement on the last form is that its value
  include [47mstate[0m.

  It is also common to update the state using a sequence of forms such
  that each returns an error triple, where the intention is for
  evaluation to short-circuit immediately if a soft error is
  encountered.  Suppose [47m<expr1>[0m and [47m<expr2>[0m are expressions that
  return error triples, where the [47mstate[0m components of the error
  triples might be updated, and one wishes to evaluate [47m<expr1>[0m and
  then [47m<expr2>[0m, returning the (multiple) values returned by [47m<expr2>[0m
  unless the error triple returned by [47m<expr1>[0m is a soft error, in
  which case that error triple is returned.  One can of course do so
  as follows.

    (mv-let (erp val state)
            <expr1>
            (cond (erp (mv erp val state))
                  (t <expr2>)))

  But ACL2 provides a handy macro, [47m[er-progn][0m, for this purpose.  The
  following code is equivalent to the code just above.

    (er-progn <expr1> <expr2>)

  See [er-progn] for more details.  Note that unlike [47m[pprogn][0m, the
  return [signature] for the last expression must be the same as that
  of the others: an error triple.

  Let's consider how to use [47mpprogn[0m and [47mer-progn[0m together.  In the
  following example [47mf1[0m and [47mf2[0m both return [47mstate[0m, while each of [47mg1[0m and
  [47mg2[0m returns an error triple.  The following code modifies state by
  executing these in the order [47mf1[0m, [47mg1[0m, [47mf2[0m, and finally [47mg2[0m, returning
  [47m(mv nil val state)[0m where [47mval[0m is the value component of the error
  triple returned by [47mg2[0m --- except we return a soft error if [47mg1[0m or [47mg2[0m
  returns a soft error.

    (pprogn (f1 x state)
            (er-progn (g1 x state)
                      (pprogn (f2 x state)
                              (g2 x state))))

  Finally, consider the [events] [47m[progn][0m and [47m[progn!][0m.  These have
  similar behavior to that of [47m[er-progn][0m.  However, [47m[progn][0m and
  [47m[progn!][0m may only be used in event contexts, for example at the top
  level or immediately underneath a call of [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[progn][0m,
  while [47m[er-progn][0m has no such restriction.  So when writing code,
  use [47mer-progn[0m rather than [47m[progn][0m or [47m[progn!][0m.  In particular, the
  body of a [47m[defun][0m must not have any calls of [47mprogn[0m (or of [47mprogn![0m
  either), and the same restriction holds for any code to be
  executed, such as the body of a [47m[make-event][0m form.

  BINDING VARIABLES USING ERROR TRIPLES

  In this section we discuss the macro [47mer-let*[0m, which is a variant of
  the special form, [47m[let*][0m, that is useful when programming with
  state.  A related utility that avoids the use of state is
  [47mer-let*-cmp[0m; see [context-message-pair].

  The macro [47mer-let*[0m is useful when binding variables to the value
  components of error triples.  It is actually quite similar to
  [47mer-progn[0m, described above, except that [47mer-let*[0m binds variables.
  First consider the following example.

    (er-let* ((x1 (f1 state))
              (x2 (f2 x1 state)))
      (value (cons x1 x2)))

  The code just above is essentially equivalent to writing the
  following.

    (mv-let (erp x1 state)
            (f1 state)
            (cond (erp (mv erp x1 state))
                  (t (mv-let (erp x2 state)
                             (f2 x1 state)
                             (cond (erp (mv erp x2 state))
                                   (t (value (cons x1 x2))))))))

  As suggested by the example above, [47mer-let*[0m has the same syntax as
  [47mlet*[0m, except that declarations are not supported.  (But note that
  [47mignore[0m declarations are not needed; all variables that are bound
  are also used, at least in the error case.  Consider replacing
  [47m(cons x1 x2)[0m by [47mnil[0m in the example displayed immediately above, and
  note that [47mx1[0m and [47mx2[0m are still used.)  However, unlike [47mlet*[0m, [47mer-let*[0m
  requires that for each binding [47m(var expr)[0m, the expression [47mexpr[0m must
  evaluate to an error triple and, moreover, it requires that the
  second argument (the ``body'') of [47mer-let*[0m must evaluate to an error
  triple.  If one of the variable expressions (e.g., the [47mf1[0m and [47mf2[0m
  calls above) signals an error, its error triple is returned as the
  value of the [47mer-let*[0m.

  Of course, soft errors can be ``caught'' by using [47m[mv-let][0m instead of
  [47mer-let*[0m and simply ignoring the error flag or, more generally, by
  returning a non-erroneous error triple even if the error flag was
  on.

  BINDING STATE GLOBAL VARIABLES

  In this section we introduce a utility, [47m[state-global-let*][0m, that is
  an analogue of [47mlet*[0m for state global variables.  Consider the
  following example.

    (state-global-let*
     ((inhibit-output-lst (add-to-set-eq 'summary (@ inhibit-output-lst))))
     (thm (equal x x)))

  This form binds state global variable [47minhibit-output-lst[0m to the
  result of adding the symbol, [47msummary[0m, to the current value of that
  state global.  Thus (see [set-inhibit-output-lst]), the usual
  [summary] is not printed when evaluating this call of [47m[thm][0m.

  See [state-global-let*] for more complete [documentation].

