 (PROHIBITION-OF-LOOP$-AND-LAMBDA$
  (DEFCONST DEFMACRO DEFPKG)
  "Certain events do not allow [47m[loop$][0ms or [47m[lambda$][0ms

  Certain events, including [47m[defconst][0m, [47m[defmacro][0m, and [47m[defpkg][0m,
  prohibit the use of [47m[loop$][0m and [47m[lambda$][0m.  The prohibition of
  [47mloop$[0ms is due to their use of [47mlambda$[0ms, so we focus on why [47mlambda$[0ms
  are prohibited here.

  [47mLambda$[0m is prohibited in these events because of the way [books] are
  loaded.  To speed up the execution of [47minclude-book[0m the compiled
  file for a book is loaded before the events in the book are
  processed.  But the evaluation of [47mlambda$[0m expressions in raw Lisp
  depends on the [47mlambda$[0ms having been translated and recorded in the
  logical world and in the lambda cache (see [47m[print-cl-cache][0m).  So
  the execution of these events can't depend on [47mlambda$[0m.

  If you have submitted an event that provoked an error citing this
  topic you must restate the event to avoid those prohibited
  constructions.  [3mUnfortunately, you must also consider ancestral
  occurrences of the prohibited constructions![0m We first give two
  examples of the problem to drive home the message and then we
  discuss various ways the problem can be dealt with.


Examples of Prohibited Forms

    ACL2 !>(defconst *2^10* (apply$ (lambda$ (x) (expt 2 x)) '(10)))

    ACL2 Error in ( DEFCONST *2^10* ...):  We prohibit certain events,
    including DEFCONST, DEFPKG, and DEFMACRO, from being ancestrally dependent
    on loop$ and lambda$ expressions.  But at least one of these prohibited
    expressions occurs in this event.  See :DOC prohibition-of-loop$-and-
    lambda$.

  We will discuss how to avoid such errors in the next section.

  Here is an example of an ancestral use of [47mlambda$[0m in a macro.
  Suppose we wish to define the macro [47mrat[0m so that [47m(rat \"123.4567\")[0m
  expands to the rational [47m1234567/10000[0m.  Our idea is to use [47mloop$[0m in
  the definition of the function [47mstring-to-rat[0m and then to call
  [47mstring-to-rat[0m in our macro.  (We don't recommend this particular
  use of [47mloop$[0ms to parse a string into a rational but we use it here
  to make a point later.)

    (defun string-to-rat (str)
      (let* ((lst (coerce str 'list))
             (right-of-pt (length (cdr (loop$ for d in lst
                                              as tail on lst
                                              thereis
                                              (if (eql #. d) tail nil))))))
        (loop$ for tail on lst
               when (not (eql (car tail) #.))
               sum
               (* (- (char-code (car tail)) 48)
                  (expt 10
                        (- (loop$ for d in tail sum (if (eql d #.) 0 1))
                           (+ 1 right-of-pt)))))))

    (defmacro rat (str) (string-to-rat str))

  The attempt to define the macro will provoke the error shown above
  for [47m(defconst *2^10* ...)[0m.


Avoiding These Errors

  The only way to avoid these errors is to not use [47mlambda$[0m or [47mloop$[0m in
  these events!  We can fix the particular [47mdefconst[0m example above
  simply with

    (defconst *2^10* (apply$ 'expt '(2 10)))

  or, better yet, by

    (defconst *2^10* (expt 2 10))

  since there is reason [47mapply$[0m is involved at all.

  When it is hard to avoid the prohibited constructs you might be able
  to use [47mmake-event[0m to do the necessary computation during the
  creation of the event, though this technique seems mainly
  applicable to [47mdefconst[0m and much less useful to the [47mdefmacro[0m case.

    (make-event
      `(defconst *2^10*
        ',(apply$ (lambda$ (x) (expt 2 x)) '(10))))

  However, in general, removing all uses of [47mloop$[0m and [47mlambda$[0m can
  require a lot of re-coding.  For example, fixing the [47mrat[0m macro
  requires several old-fashioned recursive definitions to carry out
  the iterations done with [47mloop$[0m.

    (defun old-fashioned-string-to-rat1 (lst power)
      (cond
       ((endp lst) 0)
       ((eql (car lst) #.)
        (old-fashioned-string-to-rat1 (cdr lst) power))
       (t (+ (* (- (char-code (car lst)) 48)
                (expt 10 power))
             (old-fashioned-string-to-rat1 (cdr lst) (- power 1))))))

    (defun left-of-pt (lst)
      (cond ((endp lst) 0)
            ((eql (car lst) #.) 0)
            (t (+ 1 (left-of-pt (cdr lst))))))

    (defun old-fashioned-string-to-rat (str)
      (let ((lst (coerce str 'list)))
        (old-fashioned-string-to-rat1 lst (- (left-of-pt lst) 1))))

    (defmacro rat (str) (old-fashioned-string-to-rat str))

  Another way to eliminate [47mloop$[0m and [47mlambda$[0m is to replace the
  offending expressions with their translations.  There is no
  prohibition of fully-translated quoted [47m[lambda][0m objects, just of
  [47mlambda$[0m expressions.

    (defun string-to-rat (str)
      ((LAMBDA
        (LST)
        ((LAMBDA
          (RIGHT-OF-PT LST)
          (SUM$+
           '(LAMBDA
             (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
             (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS))
             ((LAMBDA
               (RIGHT-OF-PT TAIL)
               (BINARY-*
                (BINARY-+ '-48 (CHAR-CODE (CAR TAIL)))
                (EXPT
                 '10
                 (BINARY-+
                  (SUM$
                   '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                            (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LOOP$-IVAR))
                            ((LAMBDA (D) (IF (EQL D '#.) '0 '1))
                             LOOP$-IVAR))
                   TAIL)
                  (UNARY-- (BINARY-+ '1 RIGHT-OF-PT))))))
              (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)
              (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
           (CONS RIGHT-OF-PT 'NIL)
           (WHEN$+
            '(LAMBDA
              (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
              (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS))
              ((LAMBDA (TAIL)
                       (NOT (EQL (CAR TAIL) '#.)))
               (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
            'NIL
            (LOOP$-AS (CONS (TAILS LST) 'NIL)))))
         (LENGTH
          (CDR
           (THEREIS$+
            '(LAMBDA
              (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
              (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS))
              ((LAMBDA (D TAIL)
                       (IF (EQL '#. D) TAIL 'NIL))
               (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)
               (CAR (CDR LOOP$-IVARS))))
            'NIL
            (LOOP$-AS (CONS LST (CONS (TAILS LST) 'NIL))))))
         LST))
       (COERCE STR 'LIST)))

  If we define [47mstring-to-rat[0m as above then we can define the [47mrat[0m macro
  as originally desired and the [47mdefmacro[0m is accepted.

  But there are several arguments against this approach in general:

    * [47mDefconst[0m, [47mdefmacro[0m and [47mdefpkg[0m not only prohibit [47mloop$[0m and [47mlambda$[0m but
      also prohibit all uses of [47mapply$[0m and scions if user-defined
      functions are involved in the function objects.  This is
      because to compute logically with them one must have [warrant]
      hypotheses and there is no provision for supplying warrants
      with these events.  See [ignored-attachment].  It turns out
      that the hideous example above does not mention any
      user-defined functions in the function objects, so this
      restriction doesn't stop us here.

    * Eliminating [47mloop$[0m in favor of scions sacrifices execution speed if
      the [47mloop$[0ms are guard verified.  A guard-verified [47mloop$[0m executes
      in raw Lisp as a Common Lisp [47mloop[0m, whereas its translation into
      nested calls of [47mloop$[0m scions, even if guard verified, involves
      far more function calls.

    * It is much harder to ``maintain'' code derived this way!

  There may be cases where no user-defined functions are involved,
  efficiency doesn't matter, and the [47mloop$[0m scion translation of a
  [47mloop$[0m is as perspicuous as the [47mloop$[0m itself.  So don't dismiss this
  approach out of hand.")
 (PROJECT-DIR-ALIST
  (BOOKS EVENTS)
  "Support for moving project directories (also [47m:dir[0m arguments)

  This topic describes the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m, which supports the
  relocation of book directories so that their [books] are still
  treated as certified.  Each such directory is treated as a [3mproject[0m,
  which is represented by a [keyword].  In particular, the keyword,
  [47m:SYSTEM[0m, represents the [community-books] as such a project.  The
  [47mproject-dir-alist[0m also provides one way to interpret the [47m:dir[0m
  keyword argument of [47m[include-book][0m and [47m[ld][0m.

  It is theoretically possible to undermine soundness by using this
  capability inappropriately (see below for some discussion of
  appropriate usage).  For the utmost security, perform a fresh
  certification of your entire collection of books without using this
  capability.  See [certificate], specifically the discussion there
  about placing a ``burden'' on the user.

  To see the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m in your session, evaluate the form
  [47m(project-dir-alist (w state))[0m.

  We start below by introducing the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m and explaining
  how to set it up.  Next we describe its effects.  We conclude by
  discussing some details, limitations, and restrictions.


What is the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m and how is it established?

  The [47mproject-dir-alist[0m is an association list that maps keywords to
  directory names.  If we map keyword [47m:K[0m to directory [47m\"<dir>\"[0m, we say
  that the project name [47mK[0m is associated with project directory
  [47m\"<dir>\"[0m.

  The way to establish the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m is to set environment
  variable [47mACL2_PROJECTS[0m to the name of a file, which we will call
  the ``projects file''.  The projects file may have lines of the
  following form, where we write [47m:K[0m to denote an arbitrary keyword
  and [47m\"<dir>\"[0m to denote a directory name inside double-quotes.

    :K \"<dir>\"

  Such a line associates the project name [47mK[0m with the project directory
  [47m\"dir\"[0m.  Each remaining line in a projects file should either be
  blank (i.e., contain only whitespace) or else be a comment line,
  that is, a line for which the first non-whitespace character is a
  semicolon ([47m;[0m).

  The projects file is read when ACL2 starts up.  ACL2 creates the
  [47mproject-dir-alist[0m by using each line as above to associate the
  keyword [47m:K[0m with the directory represented by [47m\"<dir>\"[0m, which may be
  a relative or absolute pathname.  Relative pathnames are
  interpreted with respect to the directory of the projects file.

  If the keyword [47m:SYSTEM[0m is not specified in the projects file, then
  the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m will additionally include a pair that
  associates [47m:SYSTEM[0m with the [community-books] directory, which is
  generally the [47mbooks/[0m subdirectory of your ACL2 distribution.  That
  value for [47m:SYSTEM[0m can be overridden either by specifying it
  explicitly with [47m:SYSTEM[0m in the projects file or by setting
  environment variable [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m to that value --- where if
  both overrides are used, they must not conflict.

  The values of the environment variables mentioned above,
  [47mACL2_PROJECTS[0m and [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m, are pathnames that can be
  either relative or absolute.  A relative pathname is interpreted
  with respect to the directory in which ACL2 is invoked.  The final
  character need not be ``[47m/[0m''; if it's not, then that character will
  be added at the end before adding to the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m.

  The [47mproject-dir-alist[0m must have no duplicate keys and no duplicate
  directory names.


Effects of the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m

  There are the following two effects of the association of a keyword
  [47m:K[0m with a directory [47m\"<dir>\"[0m in the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m.

  [31;1m(1) [47m:K[0m[31;1m specifies a project directory that may be moved.[0m

      Consider first the default case, where there is a single association
      in the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m: [47m:SYSTEM[0m is associated with the
      [community-books] directory.  Suppose that the community books
      and their certificates are moved to a new directory, [47m\"<dir>\"[0m.
      Then if you run ACL2 in an environment where the
      [47mproject-dir-alist[0m associates [47m:SYSTEM[0m with [47m\"<dir>\"[0m, the
      relocated community books will still be treated as certified.

      The case of [47m:SYSTEM[0m described above generalizes naturally, as
      follows.  Let [47mS[0m be a set of books certified with a given
      [47mproject-dir-alist[0m, each of which includes only other books in
      [47mS[0m.  (For example, [47mS[0m contains just the community books directory
      in the special case above.)  Also assume that the absolute
      pathname of every book in [47mS[0m has a prefix among the directories
      in that [47mproject-dir-alist[0m.  Then all books in [47mS[0m, along with
      their [certificate] files, can be moved and those books will
      still be considered to be certified in any ACL2 session with a
      suitable [47mproject-dir-alist[0m, as follows.  For every keyword [47m:K[0m
      mapped to directory [47m\"<dir>\"[0m in the original [47mproject-dir-alist[0m
      (the one at certification time), the new [47mproject-dir-alist[0m
      should map [47m:K[0m to the directory [47m\"<dir2>\"[0m to which [47m\"<dir>\"[0m was
      moved.

  [31;1m(2) [47m:K[0m[31;1m can be used as a [47m:dir[0m[31;1m keyword, to reference [47m\"<dir>\"[0m[31;1m.[0m

      The optional [47m:dir[0m keyword argument of [47m[include-book][0m and [47m[ld][0m can
      have value [47m:K[0m, in which case the pathname argument is
      interpreted with respect to [47m\"<dir>\"[0m.  This behavior is the same
      as when [47m:K[0m is bound to [47m\"<dir>\"[0m using [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m or
      [47m[add-include-book-dir!][0m.


Additional details

      [31;1mPrefixes.[0m Suppose [47m:K1[0m and [47m:K2[0m are keywords bound in the
      [47mproject-dir-alist[0m to [47m\"<dir1>\"[0m and [47m\"<dir2>\"[0m, respectively, where
      [47m\"<dir1>\"[0m is a prefix of [47m\"<dir2>\"[0m (hence is strictly shorter,
      since duplicates are disallowed, as noted above).  Then it may
      be simplest to maintain that property --- that the value of [47m:K1[0m
      is a prefix of the value of [47m:K2[0m --- when creating a new
      [47mACL2_PROJECTS[0m directory to reflect relocation of these
      directories of books.  The actual requirement is that
      directories under the old value of [47m:K2[0m need to be moved to the
      same relative locations under the new value of [47m:K2[0m, and all
      other directories under the old value of [47m:K1[0m need to be moved
      to the same relative locations under the new value of [47m:K1[0m.

      [31;1m[47m[Save-exec][0m[31;1m.[0m By default, an executable created with [47m[save-exec][0m will
      have an unchanged [47mproject-dir-alist[0m.  However, a new
      [47mproject-dir-alist[0m will be created as described above if
      environment variable [47mACL2_PROJECTS[0m is set; also, if environment
      variable [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m is set, then the existing
      [47mproject-dir-alist[0m will be modified to map [47m:SYSTEM[0m to the value
      of [47mACL2_SYSTEM_BOOKS[0m.


Additional Limitations and Restrictions

      (Already noted above) [31;1mNo duplicates.[0m There must be no duplicate keys
      or duplicate directory names in the [47mproject-dir-alist[0m.

      [31;1mNo overlap.[0m No keyword may be bound with [47m[add-include-book-dir][0m or
      [47m[add-include-book-dir!][0m that is also bound in the
      [47mproject-dir-alist[0m.

      [31;1mAvoid using absolute pathnames in books.[0m Consider a form that
      contains an absolute pathname, such as [47m(defconst *c*
      \"/u/home/jones/bk\")[0m.  If that form is in a book [47mB[0m that is
      moved, then of course it remain in [47mB[0m after the move.  That is
      certainly fine, unless one expects this pathname to change as [47mB[0m
      is moved.  That said, an absolute pathname that extends a
      project directory is OK in an [47m[include-book][0m form among the
      [portcullis] commands for a book, in the following sense: it is
      replaced in that book's [certificate] by referencing the
      project's keyword name to avoid using an absolute pathname.


Subtopics

  [Sysfile]
      File representation using ACL2 project directories")
 (PROMPT
  (LD)
  "The prompt printed by [47m[ld][0m

  The prompt printed by ACL2 conveys information about various
  ``modes.'' See [default-print-prompt] and see [ld-prompt] for
  details.

  The prompt in raw Lisp, including [breaks] from the ACL2 loop, can be
  adjusted by ACL2 to include the string [47m\"[RAW LISP[0m\"], at least for
  most Common Lisp implementations.  That change can help to remind
  users not to submit ACL2 forms there.  That adjustment is made by
  executing the following form in raw Lisp (for example, after typing
  [47m:[0m[47m[q][0m).

    (install-new-raw-prompt)

  To return to printing the original Lisp prompt in raw lisp, evaluate
  the following in raw Lisp.

    (install-old-raw-prompt)")
 (PROOF-BUILDER
  (ACL2 DEBUGGING)
  "An interactive tool for controlling ACL2's proof processes.

  Call this up with [47m(verify ...)[0m.

  The [3minteractive proof-builder[0m provides a way to explore the
  development of ACL2 proofs below the level of the ACL2 prover (as
  typically used by [47m[thm][0m and [events] such as [47m[defthm][0m).  To use
  this tool, invoke the [47m[verify][0m command, for example as follows.

    ACL2 !>(verify (equal (append (append x y) z)
                          (append x (append y z))))
    ->:

  Then at the [47m\"->:\"[0m prompt, one submits commands that operate on a
  stack of goals, starting with the single goal that was supplied to
  [47mVERIFY[0m as shown above.  The final command (or ``instruction'') can
  be an [47mexit[0m command, which can print out a [47m[defthm][0m event if the
  goal stack is empty; see [proof-builder-commands], in particular
  the [47mexit[0m command.  That resulting [47mdefthm[0m event includes an
  [47m:[0m[47m[instructions][0m parameter, which directs replay of the interactive
  proof-builder commands (for example during certification of a book
  containing that event; see [books]).

  If you exit the interactive proof-builder's loop, you may re-enter
  that session at the same point using the command [47m(verify)[0m, i.e.,
  with no arguments.  The commands [47msave[0m and [47mretrieve[0m may be invoked
  to manage more than one session.

  The interactive proof-builder can be invoked on a specific subgoal,
  and the resulting [47m:instructions[0m can be given as a hint to the
  theorem prover for that subgoal.  See [instructions].

  A tutorial is available {here |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/kaufmann/tutorial/rev3.html}.  That
  tutorial illustrates more than just the interactive proof-builder.
  The portion relevant to the proof-builder may be accessed directly
  {here |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/kaufmann/tutorial/rev3.html#slide29}.

  See [set-evisc-tuple] for how to arrange that output is printed in
  abbreviated form.  In general, the interactive proof-builder uses
  the [47m:TERM[0m [evisc-tuple] described in that documentation.

  Individual proof-builder commands are documented in subsection
  [proof-builder-commands].  Note that the package name (see
  [symbol-package-name] is irrelevant for these commands (though not
  their arguments); for example [47m(dive 3)[0m, [47m(acl2::dive 3)[0m,
  [47m(acl2-pc::dive 3)[0m, and [47m(:dive 3)[0m are all equivalent.  For a list of
  perhaps the most commonly used commands, see
  [proof-builder-commands-short-list].

  The proof-builder supports user-defined macros, which are tactics
  that generate proof-builder instructions.  See [define-pc-macro].

  [3mRemark.[0m The ``pc-'' prefix, for example in ``define-pc-macro'' above,
  stems from an earlier name for the proof-builder, which was
  ``proof-checker''.  That also accounts for the string [47m\"PC\"[0m in the
  package name, [47m\"ACL2-PC\"[0m.


Subtopics

  [Define-pc-help]
      Define a macro command whose purpose is to print something

  [Define-pc-macro]
      Define a proof-builder macro command

  [Define-pc-meta]
      Define a proof-builder meta command

  [Dive-into-macros-table]
      Right-associated function information for the [proof-builder]

  [Instructions]
      Instructions to the interactive proof-builder

  [Macro-command]
      Compound command for the interactive proof-builder

  [Proof-builder-commands]
      List of commands for the interactive proof-builder

  [Proof-builder-commands-short-list]
      Perhaps the most commonly used [proof-builder] commands

  [Proof-checker]
      Old name for [proof-builder]

  [Retrieve]
      Re-enter a (specified) [proof-builder] state

  [Toggle-pc-macro]
      Change an ordinary macro command to an atomic macro, or vice-versa

  [Unsave]
      Remove a [proof-builder] state

  [Verify]
      Enter the interactive proof-builder")
 (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS
  (PROOF-BUILDER)
  "List of commands for the interactive proof-builder

  This documentation section contains documentation for individual
  commands that can be given inside the interactive [proof-builder]
  loop that is entered using [47m[verify][0m.  For a summary of (arguably)
  the most useful commands, see [proof-builder-commands-short-list].


Subtopics

