 (PROTECT-MEMOIZE-STATISTICS
  (MEMOIZE)
  "Ensure the integrity of memoization statistics even upon aborts

  Example Forms:

    (f-put-global 'protect-memoize-statistics t state)
    (assign protect-memoize-statistics t) ; same effect as above

  By default, if calls of [47m[memoize][0md functions are aborted, then
  statistics reported by [memsum] for those calls will often be
  incorrect.  Since aborts are relatively rare, this seems a
  reasonable default, since avoiding such inaccuracy incurs some
  additional computation time.  However, evaluation of either of the
  forms above will arrange that for functions memoized [3mafter[0m such
  evaluation, the accuracy of the statistics will not be adversely
  affected by aborts (again, at the expense of some additional
  computation time).

  Once again: evaluation of a form displayed above only affects
  behavior for future calls of [47m[memoize][0m, not for functions already
  memoized at the time of that form's evaluation (unless the function
  is subsequently [unmemoize]d and then once again [memoize]d).

  To revert to the default state in which performance is preferred to
  accuracy of memoization statistics after aborts, evaluate either of
  the following forms.

    (f-put-global 'protect-memoize-statistics nil state)
    (assign protect-memoize-statistics nil) ; same effect as above

  The following demo illustrates the effect of assigning to
  protect-memoize-statistics.

    (defun foo (n)
      (declare (xargs :guard (natp n)))
      (progn$
       (sleep n)
       n))

    ; OPTIONALLY:
    (assign protect-memoize-statistics t)

    (memoize 'foo)

    (clear-memoize-statistics) ; for good measure
    (memsum) ; should have nothing to report
    (foo 1)
    (memsum) ; reports 1 call, 1 second

    ; Now: submit this and then abort with an interrupt it after about 3 seconds.
    (foo 15)

    ; See explanation below.
    (memsum)

  If the ``OPTIONAL'' form above is omitted, we get the default
  behavior: the statistics from [47m[memsum][0m will show 2 calls of [47mfoo[0m
  taking only took 1 second total, for an average of only 0.5
  seconds, because no time was recorded for the aborted call.
  However, if the ``OPTIONAL'' form above is included, then you will
  see statistics that look accurate.")
 (PROVING_THEOREMS_ABOUT_MODELS
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "Proving Theorems about Models

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_Required_of_the_User{Q}])

  But ACL2 is a [31;1mlogic[0m.  We can [31;1mprove theorems about the model[0m.

  {IMAGE}

    [31;1mTheorem.  MC 'mult is a multiplier[0m
    (implies (and (natp x)
                  (natp y))
             (equal (lookup 'z (mc (s 'mult x y) (mclk x)))
                    (* x y))).

  This theorem says that a certain program running on the [31;1mmc[0m machine
  will correctly multiply [31;1many two natural numbers[0m.

  It is a statement about an [31;1minfinite[0m number of test cases!

  We know it is true about the model because we [31;1mproved[0m it.

  Of course, models of actual machines usually only accept a finite
  number of different inputs.  For example, engineers at Advanced
  Micro Devices (AMD), Centaur, and IBM have produced ACL2 models of
  floating point units that operate on double precision IEEE floating
  point numbers.  These are finite models.  But the size of their
  inputs is sufficiently large that they are verified by the same
  mathematical methods used to prove theorems about infinite state
  systems like our little [47mmc[0m.

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_Required_of_the_User{Q}])")
 (PROVISIONAL-CERTIFICATION
  (BOOKS-CERTIFICATION)
  "Certify a book in stages for improved book-level parallelism

  Provisional certification is a process that can increase parallelism
  when certifying books in parallel, typically using `make', when
  certifying a collection of [books].  We got this idea from Jared
  Davis, who developed rudimentary provisional certification schemes
  first at Rockwell Collins and later for his `Milawa' project.  Our
  design has also benefited from conversations with Sol Swords.

  Warning: as of November 2014, enabling provision certification via
  the [47mACL2_PCERT[0m flag can not be reliably enabled at the Makefile
  level (as is shown below).  See {Github Issue 255 |
  https://github.com/acl2/acl2/issues/255} for more information.

  To invoke provisional certification, see [books-certification].  For
  example, you could issue the following command.

    cert.pl --pcert-all -j 4 `find . -name '*.lisp'`

  Alternatively, see [books-certification-classic] for a discussion of
  classic ACL2 `make'-based certification (which may disappear in a
  future ACL2 release); here we extend those instructions to show how
  to use provisional certification.  (Also, you may wish to look at
  community books file [47mbooks/system/pcert/Makefile[0m for an example.)
  We begin by describing a few ways to do that.  A simple way is to
  add the line `[47mACL2_PCERT=t[0m' to a `make' command that you use for
  book certification, for example as follows.

    make -j 4 ACL2_PCERT=t

  The following works too, in a bash shell.

    (export ACL2_PCERT=t ; time make -j 4)

  Alternatively, add the line

    ACL2_PCERT ?= t

  to the [47mMakefile[0m residing in the directory, placed above the line that
  specifies the `[47minclude[0m' of file [47mMakefile-generic[0m.  A successful
  `make' will result in creating the desired [certificate] ([47m.cert[0m)
  files.

  Warning: If you put the line ``[47mACL2_PCERT ?= t[0m'' below the include of
  [47mMakefile-generic[0m, it might have no effect.  For example, try
  editing community books file [47mbooks/system/pcert/Makefile[0m by moving
  the line ``[47mACL2_PCERT ?= t[0m'' to the bottom of the file, and watch
  ``[47mmake top.cert[0m'' fail to invoke provisional certification.

  The description above may be sufficient for you to use provisional
  certification.  We provide additional documentation below for the
  reader who wants to understand more about this process, for example
  when not using `make'.  Below we assume prior familiarity with
  [books], in particular [47m[certify-book][0m and [47m[include-book][0m.  The
  remainder of this [documentation] topic is divided into sections:
  Summary, Correctness Claim and Issues, Combining Pcertify and
  Convert into Pcertify+, and Further Information.

  [31;1mSummary[0m

  Recall that certification of a book, [47mbk[0m, produces a [certificate]
  file [47mbk.cert[0m.  The provisional certification process produces this
  file as well, but as the last of the following three steps.  All of
  these steps are carried out by calls of [47m[certify-book][0m using its
  [47m:pcert[0m keyword argument.  We typically call these steps
  ``procedures'', to distinguish them from the steps of an individual
  call of [47m[certify-book][0m.

    * The ``Pcertify'' procedure (sometimes called the ``Create''
      procedure) is invoked by calling [47m[certify-book][0m with keyword
      argument [47m:pcert :create[0m.  It produces a file [47mbk.pcert0[0m,
      sometimes called the ``[47m.pcert0[0m'' file (pronounced ``dot pee
      cert zero'').  Proofs are skipped during this procedure, which
      can be viewed as an elaborate syntax check, augmented by
      compilation if specified (as it is by default).

    * The ``Convert'' procedure is invoked by calling [47m[certify-book][0m with
      keyword argument [47m:pcert :convert[0m.  It creates file [47mbk.pcert1[0m
      from [47mbk.pcert0[0m, by doing proofs for all events in [47mbk.lisp[0m.
      Note that the third argument (the `[47mcompile-flg[0m' argument) is
      ignored by such a call of [47mcertify-book[0m unless its value is [47m:all[0m
      (either explicitly or by way of environment variable
      [47mACL2_COMPILE_FLG[0m).  A subtlety is that if there is a compiled
      file at least as recent as the corresponding [47m.pcert0[0m file, then
      that compiled file's write date will be updated to the current
      time at the end of this procedure.  The usual
      [local-incompatibility] check at the end of [47m[certify-book][0m is
      omitted for the Convert procedure, since it was performed
      towards the end of the Create procedure.

    * The ``Complete'' procedure is invoked by calling [47m[certify-book][0m with
      keyword argument [47m:pcert :complete[0m.  It checks that every
      included book (including every one that is [47m[local][0mly included)
      has a [47m.cert[0m file that is at least as recent as the
      corresponding book.  The effect is to move [47mbk.pcert1[0m to
      [47mbk.cert[0m.  Note that all arguments of [47mcertify-book[0m other than
      the [47m:pcert[0m argument are ignored for this procedure, other than
      for some trivial argument checking.

  You can combine the Pcertify and Convert procedures into a single
  procedure, Pcertify+, which may be useful for books that contain
  expensive [47m[include-book][0m [events] but do few proofs.  We defer
  discussion of that feature to the section below, ``Combining
  Pcertify and Convert into Pcertify+''.

  The main idea of provisional certification is to break sequential
  dependencies caused by [47m[include-book][0m, that is, so that a book's
  proofs are carried out even when it includes books (sometimes
  called ``sub-books'') that have not themselves been fully
  certified.  For example, suppose that a proof development consists
  of books [47mA.lisp[0m, [47mB.lisp[0m, and [47mC.lisp[0m, where file [47mA.lisp[0m contains the
  form [47m(include-book \"B\")[0m and file [47mB.lisp[0m contains the form
  [47m(include-book \"C\")[0m.  Normally one would first certify [47mC[0m, then [47mB[0m,
  and finally, [47mA[0m.  However, the provisional certification process can
  speed up the process on a multi-core machine, as follows: the
  Pcertify (pronounced ``pee certify'') procedure respects this order
  but (one hopes) is fast since proofs are skipped; the Convert
  procedure essentially completes the certification in parallel by
  doing proofs and creating [47m.pcert1[0m files based on [47m.pcert0[0m files; and
  the Complete procedure respects book order when quickly renaming
  [47m.pcert1[0m files to [47m.cert[0m files.  In our example, the steps would be
  as follows, but note that we need not finish all Pcertify steps
  before starting some Convert steps, nor need we finish all Convert
  steps before starting some Complete steps, as explained further
  below.

    * Pcertify books \"C\", \"B\",and then \"A\", sequentially, skipping proofs
      but doing compilation.

    * Do proofs in parallel for the books using the Convert procedure,
      where each book relies on the existence of its own [47m.pcert0[0m file
      as well as a [47m.cert[0m, [47m.pcert0[0m, or [47m.pcert1[0m file for each of its
      included sub-books.  Write out a [47m.pcert1[0m file for the book when
      the proof succeeds.

    * Rename the [47m.pcert1[0m file to a corresponding [47m.cert[0m file when a [47m.cert[0m
      file exists and is up-to-date for each included book.

  The Convert step can begin for [47mbk.lisp[0m any time after [47mbk.pcert0[0m is
  built.  The Complete step can begin for this book any time after
  [47mbk.pcert1[0m and every [47msub-bk.cert[0m are built, for [47msub-bk[0m a sub-book of
  [47mbk[0m.

  The new procedures --- Pcertify, Convert, and Complete --- are
  invoked by supplying a value for the keyword argument [47m:pcert[0m of
  [47m[certify-book][0m, namely [47m:create[0m, [47m:convert[0m, or [47m:complete[0m,
  respectively.  Typically, and by default, the [47mcompile-flg[0m argument
  of [47m[certify-book][0m is [47mt[0m for the Pcertify procedure, so that
  [compilation] can take full advantage of parallelism.  This
  argument is treated as [47mnil[0m for the other procedures except when its
  value is [47m:all[0m in the Convert procedure, as mentioned above.

  For those who use [47m[make-event][0m, we note that expansion is done in the
  Pcertify procedure; the later steps use the expansion resulting
  from that procedure.  The reason is that although a call of
  [47m[make-event][0m is similar to a macro call, a difference is that the
  expansion of a [47mmake-event[0m form can depend on the [state].
  Therefore, we insist on doing such an expansion only once, so that
  all books involved agree on the expansion.  We say more about
  [47mmake-event[0m below.

  [31;1mCorrectness Claim and Issues[0m

  The Basic Claim for certification is the same whether or not the
  provisional certification process is employed: all books should be
  certified from scratch, with no files written to the directories of
  the books except by ACL2.  Moreover, no trust tags should be used
  (see [defttag]), or else it is the responsibility of the user to
  investigate every occurrence of ``[47mTTAG NOTE[0m'' that is printed to
  standard output.

  But common practice is to certify a set of books in stages: certify a
  few books, fix some books, re-certify changed books, certify some
  more books, and so on.  In practice, we expect this process to be
  sound even though it does not meet the preconditions for the Basic
  Claim above.  In particular, we expect that the use of [book-hash]
  values in [certificate]s will make it exceedingly unlikely that a
  book is still treated as certified after any events in the book or
  any sub-book, or any [portcullis] [command]s of the book or any
  sub-book, have been modified.

  Provisional certification makes it a bit easier for a determined user
  to subvert correctness.  For example, the Complete procedure only
  checks write dates to ensure that each sub-book's [47m.cert[0m file is no
  older than the corresponding [47m.lisp[0m file, but it does not look
  inside [47m.cert[0m files of sub-books; in particular it does not look at
  their [book-hash] information.  Of course, the automatic dependency
  analysis provided by classic ACL2 `make'-based certification avoids
  accidental problems of this sort.  And, book-hash information will
  indeed be applied at [47m[include-book][0m time, at least for sub-books
  included non-[local]ly.

  In short: while we believe that the provisional certification process
  can be trusted, we suggest that for maximum trust, it is best for
  all books in a project to be certified from scratch without the
  provisional certification process.

  [31;1mCombining Pcertify and Convert into Pcertify+[0m

  You can combine the Pcertify and Convert procedure into a single
  procedure, Pcertify+, which may be useful for books that contain
  expensive [47m[include-book][0m [events] but do few proofs.  If you are
  using `make' to do provisional certification as described above,
  just set `make' variable [47mACL2_BOOKS_PCERT_ARG_T[0m to the list of
  books for which you want the Pcertify+ procedure performed instead
  of separate Pcertify and Convert procedures.  Either of two common
  methods may be used to set this variable, as illustrated below for
  the case that books [47msub.lisp[0m and [47mmid.lisp[0m are the ones on which you
  want Pcertify+ performed.  One method is to add the following to
  your directory's Makefile, above the [47minclude[0m of [47mMakefile-generic[0m.

    ACL2_BOOKS_PCERT_ARG_T = sub mid

  Alternatively, you can specify the desired books on the command line,
  for example as follows.

    make -j 4 ACL2_BOOKS_PCERT_ARG_T='sub mid'

  Note that the books are given without their [47m.lisp[0m extensions.

  At the ACL2 level, the Pcertify+ procedure is performed when the
  value [47mt[0m is supplied to the [47m:pcert[0m keyword argument of
  [47m[certify-book][0m.  Thus, [47m:pcert t[0m can be thought of as a combination
  of [47m:pcert :create[0m and [47m:pcert :convert[0m.  However, what ACL2 actually
  does is to perform the Pcertify step without skipping proofs, and
  at the end of the [47mcertify-book[0m run, it writes out both the [47m.pcert0[0m
  and [47m.pcert1[0m file, with essentially the same contents.  (We say
  ``essentially'' because the implementation writes [47m:PCERT-INFO
  :PROVED[0m to the end of the [47m.pcert0[0m file, but not to the [47m.pcert1[0m
  file.)

  [31;1mFurther Information[0m

  Some errors during provisional certification cannot be readily
  solved.  For example, if there are circular directory dependencies
  (for example, some book in directory D1 includes some book in
  directory D2 and vice-versa), then classic ACL2 `make'-based
  certification will quite possibly fail.  For another example,
  perhaps your directory's Makefile is awkward to convert to one with
  suitable dependencies.  When no fix is at hand, it might be best
  simply to avoid provisional certification.  If you are using
  classic ACL2 `make'-based certification, you can simply add the
  following line to your directory's [47mMakefile[0m, or use ``[47mACL2_PCERT=
  [0m'' on the `make' command line, to avoid provisional certification.

    override ACL2_PCERT =

  We invite anyone who has troubleshooting tips to contact the ACL2
  developers with suggestions for adding such tips to this section.

  Our next remark is relevant only to users of [47m[make-event][0m, and
  concerns the interaction of that utility with state global
  [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m.  Normally, the global value of [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m
  is unchanged during [47mmake-event[0m expansion, except that it is bound
  to [47mnil[0m when the [47mmake-event[0m form has a non-[47mnil[0m [47m:check-expansion[0m
  argument.  But during the Pcertify procedure (not the Pcertify+
  procedure), [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m is always bound to [47mnil[0m at the start
  of [47mmake-event[0m expansion.  To see why, consider for example the
  community book [47mbooks/make-event/proof-by-arith.lisp[0m.  This book
  introduces a macro, [47mproof-by-arith[0m, that expands to a call of
  [47m[make-event][0m.  This [47mmake-event[0m form expands by trying to prove a
  given theorem using a succession of included arithmetic books,
  until the proof succeeds.  Now proofs are skipped during the
  Pcertify procedure, and if proofs were also skipped during
  [47mmake-event[0m expansion within that procedure, the first arithmetic
  book's [47m[include-book][0m form would always be saved because the
  theorem's proof ``succeeded'' (as it was skipped!).  Of course, the
  theorem's proof could then easily fail during the Convert step.  If
  you really want to inhibit proofs during [47mmake-event[0m expansion in
  the Pcertify step, consider using a form such as the following:
  [47m(state-global-let* ((ld-skip-proofsp nil)) ...)[0m.

  Finally, we describe what it means for there to be a valid
  [certificate] file for including a certified book.  Normally, this
  is a file with extension [47m.cert[0m.  However, if that [47m.cert[0m file does
  not exist, then ACL2 looks for a [47m.pcert0[0m file instead; and if that
  also does not exist, it looks for a [47m.pcert1[0m file.  (To see why does
  the [47m.pcert0[0m file take priority over the [47m.pcert1[0m file, note that the
  Convert procedure copies a [47m.pcert0[0m file to a [47m.pcert1[0m file, so both
  might exist --- but the [47m.pcert1[0m file might be incomplete if copying
  is in progress.)  Once the candidate certificate file is thus
  selected, it must be valid in order for the book to be considered
  certified (see [certificate]).  For the certificate file as chosen
  above, then in order for a compiled file to be loaded, it must be
  at least as recent as that certificate file.

  Again, as discussed above, a [47m.pcert0[0m or [47m.pcert1[0m file may serve as a
  valid certificate file when the [47m.cert[0m file is missing.  But when
  that happens, a warning may be printed that a ``provisionally
  certified'' book has been included.  No such warning occurs if
  environment variable [47mACL2_PCERT[0m has a non-empty value, or if that
  warning is explicitly inhibited (see [set-inhibit-warnings] and see
  [set-inhibit-output-lst]).")
 (PSEUDO-TERM-LISTP
  (TERM ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "A predicate for recognizing lists of term-like s-expressions

  See [pseudo-termp] for a predicate that recognizes a term-like
  s-expression.  The predicate [47mpseudo-term-listp[0m tests whether its
  argument is a list of those.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<pseudo-term-listp>

    (defun pseudo-term-listp (lst)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (cond ((atom lst) (equal lst nil))
            (t (and (pseudo-termp (car lst))
                    (pseudo-term-listp (cdr lst))))))")
 (PSEUDO-TERMP
  (TERM ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "A predicate for recognizing term-like s-expressions

    Example Forms:
    (pseudo-termp '(car (cons x 'nil)))      ; has value t
    (pseudo-termp '(car x y z))              ; also has value t!
    (pseudo-termp '(delta (h x)))            ; has value t
    (pseudo-termp '(delta (h x) . 7))        ; has value nil (not a true-listp)
    (pseudo-termp '((lambda (x) (car x)) b)) ; has value t
    (pseudo-termp '(if x y 123))             ; has value nil (123 is not quoted)
    (pseudo-termp '(if x y '123))            ; has value t

  If [47mx[0m is the quotation of a term, then [47m(pseudo-termp x)[0m is [47mt[0m.
  However, if [47mx[0m is not the quotation of a term it is not necessarily
  the case that [47m(pseudo-termp x)[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  See [term] for a discussion of the various meanings of the word
  ``term'' in ACL2.  In its most strict sense, a term is either a
  legal variable symbol, a quoted constant, or the application of an
  [47mn[0m-ary function symbol or closed [47mlambda[0m-expression to [47mn[0m terms.  By
  ``legal variable symbol'' we exclude constant symbols, such as [47mt[0m,
  [47mnil[0m, and [47m*ts-rational*[0m.  By ``quoted constants'' we include [47m't[0m (aka
  [47m(quote t)[0m), [47m'nil[0m, [47m'31[0m, etc., and exclude constant names such as [47mt[0m,
  [47mnil[0m and [47m*ts-rational*[0m, unquoted constants such as [47m31[0m or [47m1/2[0m, and
  ill-formed [47mquote[0m expressions such as [47m(quote 3 4)[0m.  By ``closed
  lambda expression'' we exclude expressions, such as [47m(lambda (x)
  (cons x y))[0m, containing free variables in their bodies.  Terms
  typed by the user are translated into strict terms for internal use
  in ACL2.

  The predicate [47mtermp[0m checks this strict sense of ``term'' with respect
  to a given ACL2 logical world; See [world].  Many ACL2 functions,
  such as the rewriter, require certain of their arguments to satisfy
  [47mtermp[0m.  However, if regarded simply from the perspective of an
  effective [guard] for a term-processing function, [47mtermp[0m checks many
  irrelevant things.  (Does it really matter that the variable
  symbols encountered never start and end with an asterisk?)  We have
  therefore introduced the notion of a ``pseudo-term'' and embodied
  it in the predicate [47mpseudo-termp[0m, which is easier to check, does
  not require the logical [world] as input, and is often perfectly
  suitable as a [guard] on term-processing functions.

  A [47mpseudo-termp[0m is either a symbol, a true list of length 2 beginning
  with the word [47mquote[0m, the application of an [47mn[0m-ary pseudo-[47mlambda[0m
  expression to a true list of [47mn[0m pseudo-terms, or the application of
  a symbol to a true list of [47mn[0m [47mpseudo-termp[0ms.  By an ``[47mn[0m-ary
  pseudo-[47mlambda[0m expression'' we mean an expression of the form
  [47m(lambda (v1 ... vn) pterm)[0m, where the [47mvi[0m are symbols (but not
  necessarily distinct legal variable symbols) and [47mpterm[0m is a
  [47mpseudo-termp[0m.

  Metafunctions may use [47mpseudo-termp[0m as a [guard].")
 (PSO
  (OUTPUT-CONTROLS)
  "Show the most recently saved output

  Evaluate [47m:pso[0m in order to print output that was generated in an
  environment where output was being saved, as in [gag-mode], which
  is active when ACL2 is invoked.  Also see [gag-mode].

    Example Forms:

    :pso                        ; Print saved output.

    (pso)                       ; equivalent to the above
    (pso :all)                  ; equivalent to the above
    (pso :all nil)              ; equivalent to the above

    (pso \"Subgoal *1/2''\")      ; Print saved output only for \"Subgoal *1/2''\".

    (pso '\"Subgoal *1/2'5'\"
          \"Subgoal *1/2'14'\")   ; Print saved output starting with the output
                                ; for '\"Subgoal *1/2'5'\" and ending just
                                ; before the output for \"Subgoal *1/2'14'\".

    (pso '(\"Subgoal *1/2'5'\"  \"Subgoal *1/2''\")
         '(\"Subgoal *1/2'14'\" \"Subgoal *1/2'12'\")
                                ; Print saved output
                                ; starting with the output for either
                                ; \"Subgoal *1/2'5'\" or \"Subgoal *1/2''\"
                                ; (whichever comes first)
                                ; and stopping just before the output for either
                                ; \"Subgoal *1/2'14'\" or \"Subgoal *1/2'12'\"
                                ; (whichever comes first).

    General Form:

    (pso start-goals-spec ; optional: default is :ALL
         stop-goals-spec  ; optional: default is NIL
         )

  where both arguments are optional and have the defaults shown above.
  Thus, the keyword command [47m:pso[0m is equivalent to each of [47m(pso)[0m, [47m(pso
  :all)[0m, and [47m(pso :all nil)[0m; see [keyword-commands].  The optional
  arguments restrict the output to specified goals, as follows; but
  the examples above probably provide an easier way to understand
  those arguments than the following explanation.

    * [47mStart-goals-spec[0m represents [3mthe set of start goal names[0m.  Its effects
      are described below.  The value [47m:all[0m represents the set of all
      goal names, a string represents the set consisting of just that
      one goal name, and a list represents the set of all goal names
      in that list.

    * [47mStop-goals-spec[0m represents [3mthe set of stop goal names[0m.  Its effects
      are described below, following the same conventions as above
      except that [47m:all[0m is not allowed.

    * Printing begins immediately if [47mstart-goals-spec[0m is [47m:all[0m, else begins
      with the first goal whose name is in the set of start goal
      names.

    * If the set of stop goal names is empty (as is the case when the
      second optional argument is omitted), then printing takes place
      only for the first goal whose name is in the set of start goal
      names.  Otherwise, printing continues starting with that goal,
      stopping only when a goal in the list of stop goal names is
      encountered, without any printing for that goal.

  Note that saved prover output is cleared at every top-level prover
  call, including such calls made by: [events] (e.g., [47m[defthm][0m and
  [47m[defun][0m), [47m[thm][0m, and [proof-builder] commands that invoke the
  prover.  A single event can make more than one top-level prover
  call, for example: in the case of [47m[defun][0m, one call made for
  termination and another for guard verification; and in the case of
  [47m[defthm][0m, one call made for the proposed theorem and one for each
  [corollary]. If you want to see more than one proof log for a
  single top-level form, then instead of using [47m:pso[0m, first evaluate
  [47m(set-gag-mode nil)[0m.

  The ``Time'' printed in the summary shows the original times for the
  proof attempt, not the times for processing the [47m:pso[0m command.

  Also see [pso!], [psog], [psof], [set-gag-mode],
  [set-inhibit-output-lst], and [set-print-clause-ids].")
 (PSO!
  (OUTPUT-CONTROLS)
  "Show the most recently saved output, including [proof-tree] output

  [47m:Pso![0m provides output just like [47m:pso[0m, except that [proof-tree] is
  included; see [47m[pso][0m.

  [47mPso![0m takes the same optional arguments as [47mpso[0m; see [pso], and also
  see [psog] and [psof].")
 (PSOF
  (OUTPUT-CONTROLS)
  "Show the most recently saved output

  For a similar utility, see [pso].  But unlike [47m:pso[0m, [47m:psof[0m takes a
  filename argument and writes output to that file, instead of
  printing to the terminal.  For large proofs, [47m:[0m[47m[psof][0m may complete
  much more quickly than [47m:[0m[47m[pso][0m.

  The first line of output from [47m:psof[0m directs the Emacs editor to use
  auto-revert mode.  You can change the frequency of auto-reverting
  the buffer connected to a file by evaluating a suitable command in
  Emacs.  For example, the command [47m(setq auto-revert-interval .1)[0m
  arranges for auto-revert mode to update as needed every 1/10 of a
  second.

  [47mPsof[0m takes the same optional arguments as [47mpso[0m; see [pso], and also
  see [pso!] and [psog].")
 (PSOG
  (OUTPUT-CONTROLS)
  "Show the most recently saved output with [gag-mode]

  [47m:Psog[0m is like [47m:pso[0m --- see [pso] --- but unlike [47m:pso[0m, [47m:psog[0m displays
  the output in [gag-mode]; more precisely, with gag-mode equal to [47mt[0m.

  [47mPsog[0m takes the same optional arguments as [47mpso[0m; see [pso], and also
  see [pso!] and [psof].")
 (PSTACK
  (DEBUGGING)
  "Seeing what the prover is up to

    General Forms:
    (pstack)      ; inspect break
    (pstack t)    ; inspect break, printing all calls in abbreviated form
    (pstack :all) ; as above, but only abbreviating the ACL2 world

  When the form [47m(pstack)[0m is executed during a break from a proof, or at
  the end of a proof that the user has aborted, a ``process stack''
  (or ``prover stack'') will be printed that gives some idea of what
  the theorem prover has been doing.  Moreover, by evaluating
  [47m(verbose-pstack t)[0m before starting a proof (see [verbose-pstack])
  one can get trace-like information about prover functions,
  including time summaries, printed to the screen during a proof.
  This feature is currently quite raw and may be refined considerably
  as time goes on, based on user suggestions.  For example, the usual
  control of printing given by [set-inhibit-output-lst] is irrelevant
  for printing the pstack.

  The use of [47m(pstack t)[0m or [47m(pstack :all)[0m should only be used by those
  who are comfortable looking at functions in the ACL2 source code.
  Otherwise, simply use [47m(pstack)[0m.

  Entries in the pstack include the following (listed here
  alphabetically, except for the first).

  [47mpreprocess-clause[0m, [47msimplify-clause[0m, etc. (in general,[47mxxx-clause[0m):
  top-level processes in the prover ``waterfall''

  [47mclausify[0m: splitting a goal into subgoals

  [47mev-fncall[0m: evaluating a function on explicit arguments

  [47mev-fncall+[0m: evaluating a function on explicit arguments while
  assuming that [warrant] hypotheses are true

  [47mev-fncall-meta[0m: evaluating a metafunction

  [47mforward-chain[0m: building a context for the current goal using
  [47m[forward-chaining][0m rules

  [47minduct[0m: finding an induction scheme

  [47mpop-clause[0m: getting the next goal to prove by induction

  [47mprocess-assumptions[0m: creating forcing rounds

  [47mremove-built-in-clauses[0m: removing built-in [clause]s (see
  [built-in-clause])

  [47mprocess-equational-polys[0m: deducing interesting equations

  [47mremove-trivial-equivalences[0m: removing trivial equalities (and
  equivalences) from the current goal

  [47mrewrite-atm[0m: rewriting a top-level term in the current goal

  [47msetup-simplify-clause-pot-lst[0m: building the linear arithmetic
  database for the current goal

  [47mstrip-branches[0m, [47msubsumption-replacement-loop[0m: subroutines of [47mclausify[0m

  [47mwaterfall[0m: top-level proof control


Subtopics

