      Seeing what the prover is up to (for system hackers)")
 (PUFF
  (HISTORY)
  "Replace a compound [command] by its immediate subevents

    Example Forms:
    ACL2 !>:puff :max
    ACL2 !>:puff :x
    ACL2 !>:puff 15
    ACL2 !>:puff \"book\"

    General Form:
    :puff cd

  where [47mcd[0m is a [command] descriptor (see [command-descriptor]) for a
  ``puffable'' [command] (see below).  [47mPuff[0m replaces the [command] at
  [47mcd[0m by the immediate subevents of the [command], executed as
  [command]s.  [47mPuff[0m then prints, using [47m[pcs][0m, the [47mpuff[0med region.

  We consider [47mpuff[0m to be a sort of hack; it is generally robust and
  sound, but that is not guaranteed.  If any existing ACL2 event
  resulted from [47mpuff[0m, ACL2 considers proofs to have been skipped, and
  thus [47m[certify-book][0m is disallowed until such events have been
  undone (see [ubt]).

  Other than the few exceptions noted later below, a ``puffable''
  [command] is an [47m[encapsulate][0m, [47m[include-book][0m, or [47m[certify-book][0m
  [command], or any command other than those consisting of a single
  primitive event.  For example, since [47m[defun][0m is a primitive event,
  a [47m[defun][0m command is not puffable.  But a macro form that expands
  into one or more [events] is puffable.  The only primitive events
  that are puffable are calls of [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[include-book][0m.  In
  this sense, [47m[make-event][0m is not considered primitive --- that is,
  it can be puffed --- and moreover, an immediate subevent that is a
  call of [47mmake-event[0m is generally replaced by its expansion (see
  [make-event]).  A puffable [command] contains (interesting)
  subevents, namely, the events in the body of the [47m[encapsulate][0m, in
  the file of the book included, or in the [command] block.

  The puff [command] ``lifts'' the immediate subevents of the indicated
  [command] so that they become [command]s themselves.  The [command]
  [47m[puff*][0m recursively puffs the newly introduced [command]s.  See
  [puff*], which also gives an example illustrating both [47mpuff[0m and
  [47m[puff*][0m.  [47mPuff[0m undoes the [command] at [47mcd[0m and replaces it by its
  immediate subevents.  Thus, in general the length of the [history]
  grows when a puff [command] is executed.  If [47mpuff[0m causes an error
  (see below), the logical [world] remains unchanged from its initial
  configuration.

  The intended use of [47mpuff[0m is to allow the user access to the [events]
  ``hidden'' inside compound [command]s.  For example, while trying
  to prove some theorem, [47mp[0m, about a constrained function, [47mfn[0m, one
  might find that the [47m[encapsulate][0m, [47mcd[0m, that introduced [47mfn[0m failed to
  include an important [constraint], [47mq[0m.  Without [47mpuff[0m, the only way
  to proceed is to undo back through [47mcd[0m, create a suitable
  [47m[encapsulate][0m that proves and exports [47mq[0m as well as the old
  [constraint]s, re-execute the new [47m[encapsulate][0m, re-execute the
  [events] since [47mcd[0m, and then try [47mp[0m again.  Unfortunately, it may be
  hard to prove [47mq[0m and additional [events] may have to be inserted
  into the [47m[encapsulate][0m to prove it.  It may also be hard to
  formulate the ``right'' [47mq[0m, i.e., one that is provable in the
  [47m[encapsulate][0m and provides the appropriate facts for use in the
  proof of [47mp[0m.

  Using [47mpuff[0m, the user can erase the [47m[encapsulate][0m at [47mcd[0m, replacing it
  by the [events] in its body.  Now the formerly constrained
  function, [47mfn[0m, is defined as its witness.  The user can experiment
  with formulations and proofs of [47mq[0m suitable for [47mp[0m.  Of course, to
  get into the ultimately desired [state] --- where [47mfn[0m is constrained
  rather than defined and [47mq[0m is exported by an [47m[encapsulate][0m at [47mcd[0m ---
  the user must ultimately undo back to [47mcd[0m and carry out the more
  tedious program described above.  But by using [47mpuff[0m it is easier to
  experiment.

  Similar applications of [47mpuff[0m allow the user of a book to expose the
  top-level [events] in the book as though they had all been typed as
  [command]s.  The user might then ``partially undo'' the book,
  keeping only some of the events in it.

  [47mPuff[0m operates as follows.  First, it determines the list of immediate
  subevents of the [command] indicated by [47mcd[0m.  It causes an error if
  there is only one subevent and that subevent is identical to the
  [command] --- i.e., if the [command] at [47mcd[0m is a primitive.  Next,
  [47mpuff[0m undoes back through the indicated [command].  This not only
  erases the [command] at [47mcd[0m but all the [command]s executed after
  it.  Finally, [47mpuff[0m re-executes the subevents of (the now erased) [47mcd[0m
  followed by all the [command]s that were executed afterwards.

  Observe that the [command]s executed after [47mcd[0m will generally have
  higher [command] numbers than they did before the puff.  For
  example, suppose 100 [command]s have been executed and that [47m:puff
  80[0m is then executed.  Suppose [command] 80 contains 5 immediate
  subevents (i.e., is an encapsulation of five [events]).  Then,
  after puffing, [command] 80 is the first event of the puffed
  [command], [command] 81 is the second, and so on; 104 [command]s
  appear to have been executed.

  When puffing an [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[include-book][0m, the [47m[local][0m
  [command]s are executed.  Note that this will replace constrained
  functions by their witnesses.

  Here are some details and a few exceptions.

    * An [47m[encapsulate][0m command that generates [unknown-constraints], such
      as one generated by the macro
      [47m[define-trusted-clause-processor][0m, is not puffable.

    * When an [47m[encapsulate][0m has a non-empty [signature], it is not puffable
      if the `puff' command issued is [47m:[0m[47m[puff*][0m, rather than [47m:[0m[47mpuff[0m.
      That is because puffing may fail in this case, as discussed
      immediately below, and it may be annoying to deal with the
      failure, for example if [47m:puff*[0m takes a long time to run.

    * An attempt to puff may fail for an [47m[encapsulate][0m with a non-empty
      [signature] that contains at least one use of [47m[make-event][0m and
      also comes from a certified book.  The reason is that local
      definitions may be elided.  Here is an example of such a book.

          (in-package \"ACL2\")

          (encapsulate
            ((f (x) t))
            (make-event '(local (defun f (x) (cons x x))))
            (defthm f-prop (consp (f x))))

      If we first certify this book, then we include it in a new ACL2
      session, and next we issue the command [47m:puff 1[0m, then we can see
      that the local definition in our [47mencapsulate[0m event has been
      elided:

          ACL2 !>:pcb! 1
                     1  (ENCAPSULATE
                             ((F (X) T))
                             (RECORD-EXPANSION
                                  (MAKE-EVENT '(LOCAL (DEFUN F (X) (CONS X X))))
                                  (LOCAL (VALUE-TRIPLE :ELIDED)))
                             (DEFTHM F-PROP (CONSP (F X))))
                         (DEFTHM F-PROP (CONSP (F X)))
          ACL2 !>

      Now an attempt to execute the command, [47m:puff 1[0m, causes an error
      because [47mf[0m lacks a definition.

          ACL2 !>:puff 1


          ACL2 Error in ( DEFTHM F-PROP ...):  The symbol F (in package \"ACL2\")
          has neither a function nor macro definition in ACL2.  Please define
          it.  See :DOC near-misses.  Note:  this error occurred in the context
          (F X).

          ACL2 !>

    * Similarly, a [47m[local][0m event that involves [47m[make-event][0m expansion may
      be elided when the event is from a book whose [47m[include-book][0m
      form has been puffed.  This will result in an error if the
      elided event is necessary to support later events in the [47mpuff[0m
      of the command.  Here is an example, where the attempt to puff
      an [47minclude-book[0m for this book will fail, giving an error for [47m(
      DEFUN G ...)[0m stating that the symbol [47mF[0m has not been defined.

          (in-package \"ACL2\")
          (make-event '(local (defun f (x) x)))
          (local (defun g (x) (f x)))

    * An attempt to [47mpuff[0m an [47m[include-book][0m command may fail for a book that
      has been modified, as described later in this documentation
      topic.

    * The [47mpuff[0m of an [47m[include-book][0m command for an uncertified book will
      simply expose the contents of the book.  However, if the book
      is certified then the [47mpuff[0m will replace each event by its
      [47m[make-event][0m expansion.  Also, ACL2 considers that (certified)
      book to have been included; future attempts to include that
      book will be considered to be [redundant].

  Finally, we note that it is an error to [47mpuff[0m in the presence of
  [47m[include-book][0m [events] for certified books that have been altered
  since they were included.  (Note that this restriction only applies
  to [47m[include-book][0m, not to [47m[certify-book][0m.)  To be specific, suppose
  [47m\"arith\"[0m is a certified book that has been included in a session.
  Suppose that after [47m\"arith\"[0m was included, the source file is
  modified.  (This might happen if the user of [47m\"arith\"[0m is not its
  author and the author happens to be working on a new version of
  [47m\"arith\"[0m during the same time period.)  Now suppose the user tries
  to [47mpuff[0m the [command] that included [47m\"arith\"[0m.  The attempt to obtain
  the subevents in [47m\"arith\"[0m will discover that the [book-hash] of
  [47m\"arith\"[0m has changed and an error will be caused.  No change is made
  in the logical [world].  A similar error is caused if, in this same
  situation, the user tries to puff any command that occurred before
  the inclusion of [47m\"arith\"[0m!  That is, [47mpuff[0m may cause an error and
  leave the [world] unchanged even if the [command] puffed is not one
  involving the modified book.  This happens because in order to
  reconstruct the [world] after the puffed [command], [47mpuff[0m must
  obtain the [events] in the book and if the book's source file has
  changed there is no assurance that the reconstructed [world] is the
  one the user intends.

  Warning: We do not detect changes to uncertified [books] that have
  been included and are then puffed or re-included!  The act of
  including an uncertified book leaves no trace of the [book-hash]
  value for the book.  Furthermore, the act prints a warning message
  disclaiming soundness.  In light of this, [47m:puff[0m quietly
  ``re-''executes the current contents of the book.")
 (PUFF*
  (HISTORY)
  "Replace a compound [command] by its subevents

    Example Forms:
    ACL2 !>:puff* :max
    ACL2 !>:puff* :x
    ACL2 !>:puff* 15
    ACL2 !>(puff* 15) ; same as just above
    ACL2 !>(puff* 15 t) ; same as just above, but keep partial results
    ACL2 !>:puff* \"book\"

    General Forms:
    :puff* cd
    (puff* 'cd) ; argument can be any expression evaluating to cd
    (puff* 'cd b) ; where b is t or nil

  where [47mcd[0m is a [command] descriptor (see [command-descriptor]).  See
  [puff] for the definition of ``puffable'' and for a description of
  the basic act of ``puffing'' a [command].  [47mPuff*[0m is just the
  recursive application of [puff]: it puffs not only the indicated
  command, but all of the commands thus generated, and recursively
  until none of the resulting commands can be puffed.  As noted in
  the documentation for [puff], [47m:puff[0m should be viewed as a sort of
  hack; hence so should [47mpuff*[0m.  [47mPuff*[0m prints the region [puff]ed,
  using [47m[pcs][0m.

  Thus, to [puff] a [command] is to replace it by its immediate
  subevents, each of which is executed as a [command].  To [47mpuff*[0m a
  [command] is to replace the [command] by each of its immediate
  subevents and then to [47mpuff*[0m each of the puffable [command]s among
  the newly introduced ones.  NOTE: because one call of [47m:puff*[0m may
  give rise to many calls of [47m:puff[0m, it can take considerable time for
  a call of [47m:puff*[0m to complete when many books are involved.

  For example, suppose [47m\"ab\"[0m is a book containing the following

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (include-book \"a\")
    (include-book \"b\")

  Suppose that book [47m\"a\"[0m only contained [47m[defun][0ms for the functions [47ma1[0m
  and [47ma2[0m and that [47m\"b\"[0m only contained [47m[defun][0ms for [47mb1[0m and [47mb2[0m.

  Now consider an ACL2 [state] in which only two [command]s have been
  executed, the first being [47m(include-book \"ab\")[0m and the second being
  [47m(include-book \"c\")[0m.  Thus, the relevant part of the display
  produced by [47m:[0m[47m[pbt][0m 1 would be:

    1 (INCLUDE-BOOK \"ab\")
    2 (INCLUDE-BOOK \"c\")

  Call this [state] the ``starting [state]'' in this example, because
  we will refer to it several times.

  Suppose [47m:puff 1[0m is executed in the starting [state].  Then the first
  [command] is replaced by its immediate subevents and [47m:pbt 1[0m would
  show:

    1 (INCLUDE-BOOK \"a\")
    2 (INCLUDE-BOOK \"b\")
    3 (INCLUDE-BOOK \"c\")

  Contrast this with the execution of [47m:puff* 1[0m in the starting [state].
  [47mPuff*[0m would first [puff] [47m(include-book \"ab\")[0m to get the [state]
  shown above.  But then it would recursively [47mpuff*[0m the puffable
  [command]s introduced by the first [puff].  This continues
  recursively as long as any [puff] introduced a puffable [command].
  The end result of [47m:puff* 1[0m in the starting [state] is

    1 (DEFUN A1 ...)
    2 (DEFUN A2 ...)
    3 (DEFUN B1 ...)
    4 (DEFUN B2 ...)
    5 (INCLUDE-BOOK \"c\")

  Observe that when [47mpuff*[0m is done, the originally indicated [command],
  [47m(include-book \"ab\")[0m, has been replaced by the corresponding
  sequence of primitive [events].  Observe also that puffable
  [command]s elsewhere in the [history], for example, [command] 2 in
  the starting [state], are not affected (except that their [command]
  numbers grow as a result of the splicing in of earlier [command]s).

  [47m:Puff*[0m may cause an error, for example because of a name conflict
  caused by two different local lemmas with the same name, or because
  a [47m[local][0m event in a book has been elided (see [puff]).  By
  default, the logical [world] is reverted to its value before that
  execution of [47mpuff*[0m.  However, if the optional Boolean second
  argument is [47mt[0m, then the world is preserved from the successful
  [47m:puff[0m commands executed before the failed one.  That behavior can
  help with debugging, since both the warning message and the return
  value tell you which command could not be puffed successfully.")
 (PUSH-UNTOUCHABLE
  (DEFTTAG)
  "Add name or list of names to the list of untouchable symbols

  Untouchables are functions that cannot be called, as well as [state]
  global variables (see [programming-with-state]) that cannot be
  modified or unbound.  Macros can also be untouchable in some sense;
  see [defmacro-untouchable].

    Examples:
    (push-untouchable my-var nil)
    (push-untouchable set-mem t)

    General Form:
    (push-untouchable name{s} fn-p)

  where [47mname{s}[0m is a non-[47mnil[0m symbol or a non-[47mnil[0m true list of symbols,
  and [47mfn-p[0m is any value (but generally [47mnil[0m or [47mt[0m).  If [47mname{s}[0m is a
  symbol it is treated as the singleton list containing that symbol.
  The effect of this event is to union the given symbols into the
  list of ``untouchable variables'' in the current world if [47mfn-p[0m is
  [47mnil[0m, else to union the symbols into the list of ``untouchable
  functions''.  This event is redundant if every symbol listed is
  already a member of the appropriate untouchables list (variables or
  functions).

  There is a further restriction on which function names may be made
  untouchable: If a function symbol, [47mg[0m, may be introduced into a
  proof by a metatheorem (via the metafunction or the hypothesis
  metafunction) or by a clause processor and the metatheorem or
  clause processor has a [47m:[0m[47m[well-formedness-guarantee][0m then [47mg[0m may not
  be made untouchable.

  As noted above, macros may not be made directly untouchable; the
  macro [47m[defmacro-untouchable][0m is provided for that purpose.  Thus,
  it is an error to evaluate [47m(push-untouchable F t)[0m if [47mF[0m is already a
  macro name, and it is also an error to define [47mF[0m as a macro when [47mF[0m
  has been made an untouchable function using [47m(push-untouchable F t)[0m.

  When a symbol is on the untouchables list it is syntactically illegal
  for any event to call a function of that name, if [47mfn-p[0m is non-[47mnil[0m,
  or to change the value of a state global variable of that name, if
  [47mfn-p[0m is [47mnil[0m.  Thus, the effect of pushing a function symbol, [47mname[0m,
  onto untouchables is to prevent any future event from using that
  symbol as a function, or as a state global variable (according to
  [47mfn-p[0m).  This is generally done to ``fence off'' some primitive
  function symbol from ``users'' after the developer has used the
  symbol freely in the development of some higher level mechanism.

  Also see [remove-untouchable].")
 (PUT-ASSOC
  (ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Modify an association list by associating a value with a key

    General Forms:
    (put-assoc name val alist)
    (put-assoc name val alist :test 'eql)   ; same as above (eql as equality test)
    (put-assoc name val alist :test 'eq)    ; same, but eq is equality test
    (put-assoc name val alist :test 'equal) ; same, but equal is equality test

  [47m(Put-assoc name val alist)[0m returns an alist that is the same as the
  list [47malist[0m, except that the first pair in [47malist[0m with a [47m[car][0m of
  [47mname[0m is replaced by [47m(cons name val)[0m, if there is one.  If there is
  no such pair, then [47m(cons name val)[0m is added at the end.  Note that
  the order of the keys occurring in [47malist[0m is unchanged (though a new
  key may be added).

  The [guard] for a call of [47mput-assoc[0m depends on the test.  In all
  cases, the last argument must satisfy [47m[alistp][0m.  If the test is
  [47m[eql][0m, then either the first argument must be suitable for [47m[eql][0m
  (see [eqlablep]) or the last argument must satisfy
  [47m[eqlable-alistp][0m.  If the test is [47m[eq][0m, then either the first
  argument must be a symbol or the last argument must satisfy
  [47m[symbol-alistp][0m.

  See [equality-variants] for a discussion of the relation between
  [47mput-assoc[0m and its variants:

      [47m(put-assoc-eq name val alist)[0m is equivalent to [47m(put-assoc name val
      alist :test 'eq)[0m;

      [47m(put-assoc-equal name val alist)[0m is equivalent to [47m(put-assoc name val
      alist :test 'equal)[0m.

  In particular, reasoning about any of these primitives reduces to
  reasoning about the function [47mput-assoc-equal[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<put-assoc-equal>

    (defun put-assoc-equal (name val alist)
      (declare (xargs :guard (alistp alist)))
      (cond ((endp alist) (list (cons name val)))
            ((equal name (caar alist))
             (cons (cons name val) (cdr alist)))
            (t (cons (car alist)
                     (put-assoc-equal name val (cdr alist))))))")
 (PUT-ASSOC-EQ (POINTERS)
               "See [put-assoc].")
 (PUT-ASSOC-EQL (POINTERS)
                "See [put-assoc].")
 (PUT-ASSOC-EQUAL (POINTERS)
                  "See [put-assoc].")
 (PUTPROP
  (WORLD ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Update fast property lists

    General form:
    (putprop symbol key value world-alist)

  See [world] for discussion of both utilities [47m[getprop][0m and [47mputprop[0m.
  Also see community book [47mbooks/misc/getprop.lisp[0m for an example that
  illustrates the use of ACL2 utilities [47mgetprop[0m and [47mputprop[0m to take
  advantage of under-the-hood Lisp (hashed) property lists.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<putprop>

    (defun putprop (symb key value world-alist)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (symbolp symb)
                                  (symbolp key)
                                  (plist-worldp world-alist))))
      (cons (cons symb (cons key value))
            world-alist))")
 (Q
  (LP)
  "Quit ACL2 (type [47m:q[0m) --- reenter with [47m(lp)[0m

    Example:
    ACL2 !>:Q

  The keyword command [47m:q[0m typed at the top-level of the ACL2 loop will
  terminate the loop and return control to the Common Lisp top-level
  (or, more precisely, to whatever program invoked [47m[lp][0m).  To reenter
  the ACL2 loop, execute [47m(acl2::lp)[0m in Common Lisp.  You will be in
  the same state as you were when you exited with [47m:q[0m, unless during
  your stay in Common Lisp you messed with the data structures
  representing the ACL2 [state] (including files, property lists, and
  single-threaded objects).

  You may also issue the command [47m(value :q)[0m to exit the ACL2 loop.
  More generally, if the result of evaluating a form in the ACL2 loop
  is the [error-triple] [47m(nil :q state)[0m, the ACL2 loop will be exited.

  WARNING: The issuance of commands to raw Lisp may render your ACL2
  session unsound.  Furthermore, evaluation of forms after exiting
  the ACL2 loop with [47m:q[0m (or [47m(value :q)[0m, etc.) is not guaranteed to
  agree with their evaluation in the ACL2 loop.  Specifically, Common
  Lisp may read an expression with the backquote character ([47m`[0m)
  differently from how ACL2 reads the expression.  (The technical
  reason, in Common Lisp parlance, is that ACL2 installs its own
  readtable in the ACL2 loop, which includes a custom backquote
  reader.)  Results of evaluation may have surprising differences
  depending on whether evaluation takes place in the ACL2 loop or in
  raw Lisp, as illustrated by the following log produced using an
  ACL2 executable built on SBCL.

    ACL2 !>(car (quote `(a b c)))
    QUOTE
    ACL2 !>:q

    Exiting the ACL2 read-eval-print loop.  To re-enter, execute (LP).
    * (car (quote `(a b c)))
    SB-INT:QUASIQUOTE
    *

  To minimize discrepancies (including that one) between ACL2 and raw
  Lisp, use [raw-mode] instead of exiting the ACL2 loop.

  Unlike all other keyword commands, typing [47m:q[0m is not equivalent to
  invoking the function [47mq[0m.  There is no function [47mq[0m.")
 (QUANTIFIER-TUTORIAL
  (DEFUN-SK)
  "A Beginner's Guide to Reasoning about Quantification in ACL2

  The initial version of this tutorial was written by Sandip Ray.
  Additions and revisions are welcome.  Sandip has said:

      ``This is a collection of notes that I wrote to remind myself of how
      to reason about quantifiers when I just started.  Most users
      after they have gotten the hang of quantifiers probably will
      not need this and will be able to use their intuitions to guide
      them in the process.  But since many ACL2 users are not used to
      quantification, I am hoping that this set of notes might help
      them to think clearly while reasoning about quantifiers in
      ACL2.''

  Many ACL2 papers start with the sentence ``ACL2 is a quantifier-free
  first-order logic of recursive functions.'' It is true that the
  [3msyntax[0m of ACL2 is quantifier-free; every formula is assumed to be
  universally quantified over all free variables in the formula.  But
  the [3mlogic[0m in fact does afford arbitrary first-order quantification.
  This is obtained in ACL2 using a construct called [47mdefun-sk[0m.  See
  [defun-sk].

  Many ACL2 users do not think in terms of [quantifiers].  The focus is
  almost always on defining recursive functions and reasoning about
  them using induction.  That is entirely justified, in fact, since
  proving theorems about recursive functions by induction plays to
  the strengths of the theorem prover.  Nevertheless there are
  situations where it is reasonable and often useful to think in
  terms of quantifiers.  However, reasoning about quantifiers
  requires that you get into the mindset of thinking about theorems
  in terms of quantification.  This note is about how to do this
  effectively given ACL2's implementation of quantification.  This
  does not discuss [47m[defun-sk][0m in detail, but merely shows some
  examples.  A detailed explanation of the implementation is in the
  ACL2 [documentation] (see [defun-sk]); also see
  [conservativity-of-defchoose].

  [Note: Quantifiers can be used for some pretty cool things in ACL2.
  Perhaps the most interesting example is the way of using
  quantifiers to introduce arbitrary tail-recursive equations; see
  the paper ``Partial Functions in ACL2'' by Panagiotis Manolios and
  J Strother Moore.  This note does not address applications of
  quantifiers, but merely how you would reason about them once you
  think you want to use them.]

  Assume that you have some function [47mP[0m.  I have just left [47mP[0m as a unary
  function stub below, since I do not care about what [47mP[0m is.

    (defstub P (*) => *)

  Now suppose you want to specify the concept that ``there exists some
  [47mx[0m such that [47m(P x)[0m holds''.  ACL2 allows you to write that directly
  using quantifiers.

    (defun-sk exists-P () (exists x (P x)))

  If you submit the above form in ACL2 you will see that the theorem
  prover specifies two functions [47mexists-p[0m and [47mexists-p-witness[0m, and
  exports the following constraints:

    1.  (defun exists-P () (P (exists-P-witness)))
    2.  (defthm exists-P-suff (implies (p x) (exists-p)))

  Here [47mexists-P-witness[0m is a new function symbol in the current ACL2
  theory.  What do the constraints above say?  Notice the constraint
  [47mexists-p-suff[0m.  It says that if you can provide any [47mx[0m such that [47m(P
  x)[0m holds, then you know that [47mexists-p[0m holds.  Think of the other
  constraint (definition of [47mexists-p[0m) as going the other way.  That
  is, it says that if [47mexists-p[0m holds, then there is some [47mx[0m, call it
  [47m(exists-p-witness)[0m, for which [47mP[0m holds.  Notice that nothing else is
  known about [47mexists-p-witness[0m than the two constraints above.

  [Note: [47mexists-p-witness[0m above is actually defined in ACL2 using a
  special form called [47mdefchoose[0m.  See [defchoose].  This note does
  not talk about [47mdefchoose[0m.  So far as this note is concerned, think
  of [47mexists-p-witness[0m as a new function symbol that has been
  generated somehow in ACL2, about which nothing other than the two
  facts above is known.]

  Similarly, you can talk about the concept that ``for all [47mx[0m [47m(P x)[0m
  holds.'' This can be specified in ACL2 by the form:

    (defun-sk forall-P () (forall x (P x)))

  This produces the following two constraints:

    1.  (defun forall-P () (P (forall-p-witness)))
    2.  (defthm forall-p-necc (implies (not (P x)) (not (forall-p))))

  To understand these, think of [47mforall-p-witness[0m as producing some [47mx[0m
  which does not satisfy [47mP[0m, if such a thing exists.  The constraint
  [47mforall-p-necc[0m merely says that if [47mforall-p[0m holds then [47mP[0m is
  satisfied for every [47mx[0m.  (To see this more clearly, just think of
  the contrapositive of the formula shown.) The other constraint
  (definition of [47mforall-p[0m) implies that if [47mforall-p[0m does not hold
  then there is some [47mx[0m, call it [47m(forall-p-witness)[0m, which does not
  satisfy [47mP[0m.  To see this, just consider the following formula which
  is immediately derivable from the definition.

    (implies (not (forall-p)) (not (P (forall-witness))))

  The description above suggests that to reason about quantifiers, the
  following Rules of Thumb, familiar to most any student of logic,
  are useful.

      RT1: To prove [47m(exists-p)[0m, construct some object [47mA[0m such that [47mP[0m holds
      for [47mA[0m and then use [47mexists-P-suff[0m.

      RT2: If you assume [47mexists-P[0m in your hypothesis, use the definition of
      [47mexists-p[0m to know that [47mP[0m holds for [47mexists-p-witness[0m.  To use
      this to prove a theorem, you must be able to derive the theorem
      based on the hypothesis that [47mP[0m holds for something, whatever
      the something is.

      RT3: To prove [47mforall-P[0m, prove the theorem [47m(P x)[0m (that is, that [47mP[0m
      holds for an arbitrary [47mx[0m), and then simply instantiate the
      definition of [47mforall-p[0m, that is, show that [47mP[0m holds for the
      witness.

      RT4: If you assume [47mforall-p[0m in the hypothesis of the theorem, see how
      you can prove your conclusion if indeed you were given [47m(P x)[0m as
      a theorem.  Possibly for the conclusion to hold, you needed
      that [47mP[0m holds for some specific set of [47mx[0m values.  Then use the
      theorem [47mforall-p-necc[0m by instantiating it for the specific [47mx[0m
      values you care about.

  Perhaps the above is too terse.  In the remainder of the note, we
  will consider several examples of how this is done to prove
  theorems in ACL2 that involve quantified notions.

  Let us consider two trivial theorems.  Assume that for some unary
  function [47mr[0m, you have proved [47m(r x)[0m as a theorem.  Let us see how you
  can prove that (1) there exists some x such that [47m(r x)[0m holds, and
  (2) for all [47mx[0m [47m(r x)[0m holds.

  We first model these things using [47m[defun-sk][0m.  Below, [47mr[0m is simply
  some function for which [47m(r x)[0m is a theorem.

    (encapsulate
     (((r *) => *))
     (local (defun r (x) (declare (ignore x)) t))
     (defthm r-holds (r x)))

    (defun-sk exists-r () (exists x (r x)))
    (defun-sk forall-r () (forall x (r x)))

  ACL2 does not have too much reasoning support for quantifiers.  So in
  most cases, one would need [47m:use[0m hints to reason about quantifiers.
  In order to apply [47m:use[0m [hints], it is preferable to keep the
  function definitions and theorems disabled.

    (in-theory (disable exists-r exists-r-suff forall-r forall-r-necc))

  Let us now prove that there is some [47mx[0m such that [47m(r x)[0m holds.  Since
  we want to prove [47mexists-r[0m, we must use [47mexists-r-suff[0m by RT1.  We do
  not need to construct any instance here since [47mr[0m holds for all [47mx[0m by
  the theorem above.

    (defthm exists-r-holds
      (exists-r)
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use ((:instance exists-r-suff)))))

  Let us now prove the theorem that for all [47mx[0m, [47m(r x)[0m holds.  By RT3, we
  must be able to prove it by definition of [47mforall-r[0m.

    (defthm forall-r-holds
      (forall-r)
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use ((:instance (:definition forall-r))))))

  [Note: Probably no ACL2 user in his or her right mind would prove the
  theorems [47mexists-r-holds[0m and [47mforall-r-holds[0m above.  The theorems
  shown are only for demonstration purposes.]

  For the remainder of this note we will assume that we have two
  stubbed out unary functions [47mM[0m and [47mN[0m, and we will look at proving
  some quantified properties of these functions.

    (defstub M (*) => *)
    (defstub N (*) => *)

  Let us now define the predicates [47mall-M[0m, [47mall-N[0m, [47msome-M[0m, and [47msome-N[0m
  specifying the various quantifications.

    (defun-sk all-M () (forall x (M x)))
    (defun-sk all-N () (forall x (N x)))
    (defun-sk some-M () (exists x (M x)))
    (defun-sk some-N () (exists x (N x)))

    (in-theory (disable all-M all-N all-M-necc all-N-necc))
    (in-theory (disable some-M some-N some-M-suff some-N-suff))

  Let us prove the classic distributive properties of quantification:
  the distributivity of universal quantification over conjunction,
  and the distributivity of existential quantification over
  disjunction.  We can state these properties informally in ``pseudo
  ACL2'' notation as follows:

    1.  (exists x: (M x)) or (exists x: (N x)) <=> (exists x: (M x) or (N x))
    2.  (forall x: (M x)) and (forall: x (N x)) <=> (forall x: (M x) and (N x))

  To make these notions formal we of course need to define the formulas
  at the right-hand sides of 1 and 2.  So we define [47msome-MN[0m and
  [47mall-MN[0m to capture these concepts.

    (defun-sk some-MN () (exists x (or (M x) (N x))))
    (defun-sk all-MN () (forall x (and (M x) (N x))))

    (in-theory (disable all-MN all-MN-necc some-MN some-MN-suff))

  First consider proving property 1.  The formal statement of this
  theorem would be: [47m(iff (some-MN) (or (some-M) (some-N)))[0m.

  How do we prove this theorem?  Looking at RT1-RT4 above, note that
  they suggest how one should reason about quantification when one
  has an ``implication''.  But here we have an ``equivalence''.  This
  suggests another rule of thumb.

      RT5: Whenever possible, prove an equivalence involving quantifiers by
      proving two implications.

  Let us apply RT5 to prove the theorems above.  So we will first
  prove: [47m(implies (some-MN) (or (some-M) (some-N)))[0m

  How can we prove this?  This involves assuming a quantified predicate
  [47m(some-MN)[0m, so we must use RT2 and apply the definition of [47msome-MN[0m.
  Since the conclusion involves a disjunction of two quantified
  predicates, by RT1 we must be able to construct two objects [47mA[0m and [47mB[0m
  such that either [47mM[0m holds for [47mA[0m or [47mN[0m holds for [47mB[0m, so that we can
  then invoke [47msome-M-suff[0m and [47msome-N-suff[0m to prove the conclusion.
  But now notice that if [47msome-MN[0m is true, then there is already an
  object, in fact [47msome-MN-witness[0m, such that either [47mM[0m holds for it,
  or [47mN[0m holds for it.  And we know this is the case from the
  definition of [47msome-MN[0m! So we will simply prove the theorem
  instantiating [47msome-M-suff[0m and [47msome-N-suff[0m with this witness.  The
  conclusion is that the following event will go through with ACL2.

    (defthm le1
      (implies (some-MN)
               (or (some-M) (some-N)))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition some-MN))
                      (:instance some-M-suff
                                 (x (some-MN-witness)))
                      (:instance some-N-suff
                                 (x (some-MN-witness)))))))

  This also suggests the following rule of thumb:

      RT6: If a conjecture involves assuming an existentially quantified
      predicate in the hypothesis from which you are trying to prove
      an existentially quantified predicate, use the witness of the
      existential quantification in the hypothesis to construct the
      witness for the existential quantification in the conclusion.

  Let us now try to prove the converse of le1, that is: [47m(implies (or
  (some-M) (some-N)) (some-MN))[0m

  Since the hypothesis is a disjunction, we will just prove each case
  individually instead of proving the theorem by a [47m:[0m[47mcases[0m hint.  So
  we prove the following two lemmas.

    (defthm le2
      (implies (some-M) (some-MN))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition some-M))
                      (:instance some-MN-suff
                                 (x (some-M-witness)))))))

    (defthm le3
      (implies (some-N) (some-MN))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition some-N))
                      (:instance some-MN-suff
                                 (x (some-N-witness)))))))

  Note that the hints above are simply applications of RT6 as in [47mle1[0m.
  With these lemmas, of course the main theorem is trivial.

    (defthmd |some disjunction|
      (iff (some-MN) (or (some-M) (some-N)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance le1)
                      (:instance le2)
                      (:instance le3)))))

  Let us now prove the distributivity of universal quantification over
  conjunction, that is, the formula: [47m(iff (all-MN) (and (all-M)
  (all-N)))[0m

  Applying RT5, we will again decompose this into two implications.  So
  consider first the one-way implication: [47m(implies (and (all-M)
  (all-N)) (all-MN))[0m.

  Here we get to assume [47mall-M[0m and [47mall-N[0m.  Thus by RT4 we can use
  [47mall-M-necc[0m and [47mall-N-necc[0m to think as if we are given the formulas
  [47m(M x)[0m and [47m(N x)[0m as theorems.  The conclusion here is also a
  universal quantification, namely we have to prove [47mall-MN[0m.  Then RT3
  tells us to proceed as follows.  Take any object [47my[0m.  Try to find an
  instantiation [47mz[0m of the hypothesis that implies [47m(and (M y) (N y))[0m.
  Then instantiate [47my[0m with [47mall-MN-witness[0m.  Note that the hypothesis
  lets us assume [47m(M x)[0m and [47m(N x)[0m to be theorems.  Thus to justify we
  need to instantiate [47mx[0m with [47my[0m, and in this case, therefore, with
  [47mall-MN-witness[0m.  To make the long story short, the following event
  goes through with ACL2:

    (defthm lf1
       (implies (and (all-M) (all-N))
                (all-MN))
        :rule-classes nil
        :hints ((\"Goal\"
                  :use ((:instance (:definition all-MN))
                        (:instance all-M-necc (x (all-MN-witness)))
                        (:instance all-N-necc (x (all-MN-witness)))))))

  This suggests the following rule of thumb which is a dual of RT6:

      RT7: If a conjecture assumes some universally quantified predicate in
      the hypothesis and its conclusion asserts a universally
      quantified predicate, then instantiate the ``necessary
      condition'' ([47mforall-mn-necc[0m) of the hypothesis with the witness
      of the conclusion to prove the conjecture.

  Applying RT7 now we can easily prove the other theorems that we need
  to show that universal quantification distributes over conjunction.
  Let us just go through this motion in ACL2.

    (defthm lf2
      (implies (all-MN)
               (all-M))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition all-M))
                      (:instance all-MN-necc
                                 (x (all-M-witness)))))))

    (defthm lf3
      (implies (all-MN)
               (all-N))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition all-N))
                      (:instance all-MN-necc
                                 (x (all-N-witness)))))))

    (defthmd |all conjunction|
      (iff (all-MN)
           (and (all-M) (all-N)))
     :hints ((\"Goal\" :use ((:instance lf1)
                           (:instance lf2)
                           (:instance lf3)))))

  The rules of thumb for universal and existential quantification
  should make you realize the duality of their use.  Every reasoning
  method about universal quantification can be cast as a way of
  reasoning about existential quantification, and vice versa.
  Whether you reason using universal and existential quantifiers
  depends on what is natural in a particular context.  But just for
  the sake of completeness let us prove the duality of universal and
  existential quantifiers.  So what we want to prove is the
  following:

    3.  (forall x (not (M x))) = (not (exists x (M x)))

  We first formalize the notion of [47m(forall x (not (M x)))[0m as a
  quantification.

    (defun-sk none-M () (forall x (not (M x))))
    (in-theory (disable none-M none-M-necc))

  So we now want to prove: [47m(equal (none-M) (not (some-M)))[0m.

  As before, we should prove this as a pair of implications.  So let us
  prove first: [47m(implies (none-M) (not (some-M)))[0m.

  This may seem to assert an existential quantification in the
  conclusion, but rather, it asserts the [3mnegation[0m of an existential
  quantification.  We are now trying to prove that something does not
  exist.  How do we do that?  We can show that nothing satisfies [47mM[0m by
  just showing that [47m(some-M-witness)[0m does not satisfy [47mM[0m.  This
  suggests the following rule of thumb:

      RT8: When you encounter the negation of an existential quantification
      think in terms of a universal quantification, and vice-versa.

  Ok, so now applying RT8 and RT3 you should be trying to apply the
  definition of [47msome-M[0m.  The hypothesis is just a pure (non-negated)
  universal quantification so you should apply RT4.  A blind
  application lets us prove the theorem as below.

    (defthm nl1
      (implies (none-M) (not (some-M)))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition some-M))
                      (:instance none-M-necc (x (some-M-witness)))))))

  How about the converse implication?  I have deliberately written it
  as [47m(implies (not (none-M)) (some-M))[0m instead of switching the
  left-hand and right-hand sides of [47mnl1[0m, which would have been
  equivalent.  Again, RH8 tells us how to reason about it, in this
  case using RH2, and we succeed.

    (defthm nl2
      (implies (not (none-M)) (some-M))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition none-M))
                      (:instance some-M-suff (x (none-M-witness)))))))

  So finally we just go through the motions of proving the equality.

    (defthmd |forall not = not exists|
      (equal (none-M) (not (some-M)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance nl1)
                      (:instance nl2)))))

  Let us now see if we can prove a slightly more advanced theorem which
  can be stated informally as: If there is a natural number [47mx[0m which
  satisfies [47mM[0m, then there is a least natural number [47my[0m that satisfies
  [47mM[0m.

  [Note: Any time I have had to reason about existential quantification
  I have had to do this particular style of reasoning and state that
  if there is an object satisfying a predicate, then there is also a
  ``minimal'' object satisfying the predicate.]

  Let us formalize this concept.  We first define the concept of
  existence of a natural number satisfying [47mx[0m.

    (defun-sk some-nat-M () (exists x (and (natp x) (M x))))
    (in-theory (disable some-nat-M some-nat-M-suff))

  We now talk about what it means to say that [47mx[0m is the least number
  satisfying [47mM[0m.

    (defun-sk none-below (y)
      (forall r (implies (and (natp r) (< r y)) (not (M r))))))
    (in-theory (disable none-below none-below-necc))

    (defun-sk min-M () (exists y (and (M y) (natp y) (none-below y))))
    (in-theory (disable min-M min-M-suff))

  The predicate [47mnone-below[0m says that no natural number less than [47my[0m
  satisfies [47mM[0m.  The predicate [47mmin-M[0m says that there is some natural
  number [47my[0m satisfying [47mM[0m such that [47mnone-below[0m holds for [47my[0m.

  So the formula we want to prove is: [47m(implies (some-nat-M) (min-M))[0m.

  Since the formula requires that we prove an existential
  quantification, RT1 tells us to construct some object satisfying
  the predicate over which we are quantifying.  We should then be
  able to instantiate [47mmin-M-suff[0m with this object.  That predicate
  says that the object must be the least natural number that
  satisfies [47mM[0m.  Since such an object is uniquely computable if we
  know that there exists some natural number satisfying [47mM[0m, let us
  just write a recursive function to compute it.  This function is
  [47mleast-M[0m below.

    (defun least-M-aux (i bound)
      (declare (xargs :measure (nfix (- (1+ bound) i))))
      (cond ((or (not (natp i))
                 (not (natp bound))
                 (> i bound))
             0)
           ((M i) i)
           (t (least-M-aux (+ i 1) bound))))

    (defun least-M (bound) (least-M-aux 0 bound))

  Let us now reason about this function as one does typically.  So we
  prove that this object is indeed the least natural number that
  satisfies [47mM[0m, assuming that [47mbound[0m is a natural number that satisfies
  [47mM[0m.

    (defthm least-aux-produces-an-M
      (implies (and (natp i)
                    (natp bound)
                    (<= i bound)
                    (M bound))
               (M (least-M-aux i bound))))

    (defthm least-<=bound
      (implies (<= 0 bound)
               (<= (least-M-aux i bound) bound)))

    (defthm least-aux-produces-least
      (implies (and (natp i)
                    (natp j)
                    (natp bound)
                    (<= i j)
                    (<= j bound)
                    (M j))
                (<= (least-M-aux i bound) j)))

    (defthm least-aux-produces-natp
      (natp (least-M-aux i bound)))

    (defthmd least-is-minimal-satisfying-m
      (implies (and (natp bound)
                    (natp i)
                     (< i (least-M bound)))
               (not (M i)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :in-theory (disable least-aux-produces-least least-<=bound)
                :use ((:instance least-<=bound
                                 (i 0))
                      (:instance least-aux-produces-least
                                 (i 0)
                                 (j i))))))

    (defthm least-has-m
      (implies (and (natp bound)
                    (m bound))
               (M (least-M bound))))

    (defthm least-is-natp
      (natp (least-M bound)))

  So we have done that, and hopefully this is all that we need about
  [47mleast-M[0m.  So we disable everything.

    (in-theory (disable least-M natp))

  Now of course we note that the statement of the conjecture we are
  interested in has two quantifiers, an inner [47mforall[0m (from
  [47mnone-below[0m) and an outer [47mexists[0m (from [47mmin-M[0m).  Since ACL2 is not
  very good with quantification, we hold its hands to reason with the
  quantifier part.  So we will first prove something about the [47mforall[0m
  and then use it to prove what we need about the [47mexists[0m.

      RT9: When you face nested quantifiers, reason about each nesting
      separately.

  So what do we want to prove about the inner quantifier?  Looking
  carefully at the definition of [47mnone-below[0m we see that it is saying
  that for all natural numbers [47mr[0m < [47my[0m, [47m(M r)[0m does not hold.  Well, how
  would we want to use this fact when we want to prove our final
  theorem?  We expect that we will instantiate [47mmin-M-suff[0m with the
  object [47m(least-M bound)[0m where we know (via the outermost existential
  quantifier) that [47mM[0m holds for [47mbound[0m, and we will then want to show
  that [47mnone-below[0m holds for [47m(least-M bound)[0m.  So let us prove that
  for any natural number (call it [47mbound[0m), [47mnone-below[0m holds for
  [47m(least-M bound)[0m.  For the final theorem we only need it for natural
  numbers satisfying [47mM[0m, but note that from the lemma
  [47mleast-is-minimal-satisfying-m[0m we really do not need that [47mbound[0m
  satisfies [47mM[0m.

  So we are now proving: [47m(implies (natp bound) (none-below (least-M
  bound)))[0m.

  Well since this is a standard case of proving a universally
  quantified predicate, we just apply RT3.  We have proved that for
  all naturals [47mi[0m < [47m(least-M bound)[0m, [47mi[0m does not satisfy [47mM[0m (lemma
  [47mleast-is-minimal-satisfying-M[0m), so we merely need the instantiation
  of that lemma with [47mnone-below-witness[0m of the thing we are trying to
  prove, that is, [47m(least-M bound)[0m.  The theorem below thus goes
  through.

    (defthm least-is-minimal
      (implies (natp bound)
               (none-below (least-M bound)))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance (:definition none-below)
                                 (y (least-M bound)))
                      (:instance least-is-minimal-satisfying-m
                                 (i (none-below-witness (least-M bound))))))))

  Finally we are in the outermost existential quantifier, and are in
  the process of applying [47mmin-M-suff[0m.  What object should we
  instantiate it with?  We must instantiate it with [47m(least-M bound)[0m
  where [47mbound[0m is an object which must satisfy [47mM[0m and is a natural.  We
  have such an object, namely [47m(some-nat-M-witness)[0m which we know have
  all these qualities given the hypothesis.  So the proof now is just
  RT1 and RT2.

    (defthm |minimal exists|
      (implies (some-nat-M) (min-M))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
                :use ((:instance min-M-suff
                                 (y (least-M (some-nat-M-witness))))
                      (:instance (:definition some-nat-M))))))

  If you are comfortable with the reasoning above, then you are
  comfortable with quantifiers and probably will not need these notes
  any more.  In my opinion, the best way of dealing with ACL2 is to
  ask yourself why you think something is a theorem, and the rules of
  thumb above are simply guides to the questions that you need to ask
  when you are dealing with quantification.

  Here are a couple of simple exercises for you to test if you
  understand the reasoning process.

  [31;1mExercise 1[0m.  Formalize and prove the following theorem.  Suppose
  there exists [47mx[0m such that [47m(R x)[0m and suppose that all [47mx[0m satisfy [47m(P
  x)[0m.  Then prove that there exists [47mx[0m such that [47m(P x) & (R x)[0m.  (See
  {http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/contrib/quantifier-exercise-1-solution.html
  |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/contrib/quantifier-exercise-1-solution.html}
  for a solution.)

  [31;1mExercise 2[0m.  Recall the example just before the preceding exercise,
  where we showed that if there exists a natural number [47mx[0m satisfying
