 (R-AND-I-AXIOMS
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: Axioms

  A formal mathematical theory is given by a formal syntax for
  [3mformulas[0m, a set of formulas designated as [3maxioms[0m, and some formula
  manipulation [3mrules of inference[0m that allow one to derive [3mnew[0m
  formulas from [3mold[0m ones.  A [3mproof[0m  of a formula [3mp[0m  is a derivation
  of [3mp[0m  from the given axioms using the given rules of inference.  If
  a formula can be proved, it is said to be a [3mtheorem[0m.  Formulas are
  given [3msemantics[0m similar to those described for terms.  Given an
  [3massignment[0m of values to variable symbols and an interpretation of
  the function symbols, every formula is given a truthvalue by the
  semantics.  Given an interpretation, a formula is [3mvalid[0m if it is
  given the value true under every possible assignment to the
  variable symbols.  A [3mmodel[0m of a theory is an interpretation that
  makes all the axioms valid.  Provided the rules of inference are
  validity preserving, every theorem is valid, i.e., [3malways true[0m.

  We assume you know all that, and won't go into it further.  The
  whole point of a practical formal theory is to use proof to
  determine truth: one way to determine if a formula is true is to
  prove it.

  If [3m\\alpha[0m  and [3m\\beta[0m  are terms, then [3m\\alpha[0m  = [3m\\beta[0m  is a [3mformula[0m.
  If [3mp[0m  and [3mq[0m  are formulas, then each of the following is a formula:

    * [3mp[0m --> [3mq[0m

    * [3mp[0m & [3mq[0m

    * [3mp[0m \\/ [3mq[0m

    * ~[3mp[0m

    * [3mp[0m <-> [3mq[0m.

  If [3m\\alpha[0m  and [3m\\beta[0m  are terms, then [3m\\alpha[0m != [3m\\beta[0m  is just an
  abbreviation for the formula ~([3m\\alpha[0m  = [3m\\beta[0m).

  We extend the notation [3mterm[0m/[3m\\sigma[0m  in the obvious way so that we
  can apply substitution [3m\\sigma[0m  to formulas, replacing all the
  variables bound by [3m\\sigma[0m  in all the terms of the formula.

  The axioms we will use for the initial part of our study are given
  below.  Note that Axioms 1 and 8 are actually axiom schemas, i.e.,
  they describe an infinite number of axioms.

      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m1.[0m	|  [47m'[0m[3m\\alpha[0m  != [47m'[0m[3m\\beta[0m,	|
      	|  	|  where [3m\\alpha[0m  and [3m\\beta[0m  are distinct integers, characters, strings,
      or symbols	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m2.[0m	|  [47mx[0m != [47mnil[0m --> [47m(if x y z)[0m = [47my[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m3.[0m	|  [47mx[0m = [47mnil[0m --> [47m(if x y z)[0m = [47mz[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m4.[0m	|  [47m(equal x y)[0m = [47mnil[0m \\/ [47m(equal x y)[0m = [47mt[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m5.[0m	|  [47mx[0m = [47my[0m <-> [47m(equal x y)[0m = [47mt[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m6.[0m	|  [47m(consp x)[0m = [47mnil[0m \\/ [47m(consp x)[0m = [47mt[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m7.[0m	|  [47m(consp (cons x y))[0m = [47mt[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m8.[0m	|  [47m(consp '[0m[3m\\alpha[0m[47m)[0m = [47mnil[0m,	|
      	|  	|  where [3m\\alpha[0m  is an integer, character, string, or symbol	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m9.[0m	|  [47m(car (cons x y))[0m = [47mx[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m10.[0m	|  [47m(cdr (cons x y))[0m = [47my[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m11.[0m	|  [47m(consp x)[0m = [47mt[0m --> [47m(cons (car x) (cdr x))[0m = [47mx[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m12.[0m	|  [47m(consp x)[0m = [47mnil[0m --> [47m(car x)[0m = [47mnil[0m	|
      [31;1mAxiom[0m	|  [31;1m13.[0m	|  [47m(consp x)[0m = [47mnil[0m --> [47m(cdr x)[0m = [47mnil[0m	|

  One axiom described by Axiom (schema) 1 is [47m't[0m != [47m'nil[0m.  Others are
  [47m'nil[0m != [47m'3[0m and [47m'\"Hello\"[0m != [47m'Hello[0m.  We refer to all of these as
  Axiom 1.

  One axiom described by Axiom (schema) 8 is [47m(consp 'nil)[0m = [47mnil[0m.
  Others are [47m(consp '3)[0m = [47mnil[0m and [47m(consp 'Hello)[0m = [47mnil[0m.  We refer to
  all of these as Axiom 8.

  Note that if [3m\\phi[0m  is an axiom or theorem and [3m\\sigma[0m  is a
  substitution, then [3m\\phi[0m/[3m\\sigma[0m  is a theorem, by the Rule of
  Instantiation.

  We assume you are familiar with the rules of inference for
  propositional calculus and for equality.  For example, we take for
  granted that you can recognize simple propositional tautologies,
  reason by cases, and substitute equals for equals.

  For example, we show a theorem below that you should be able to
  prove, using nothing but your knowledge of propositional calculus
  and equality (and Axiom 1).

  The proof shown below uses the Deduction Law of propositional
  calculus: we can prove [3mp[0m --> [3mq[0m  by assuming [3mp[0m  as a ``Given'' and
  deriving [3mq[0m.

      [31;1mTheorem.[0m
      (consp x) = t & x = (car z) --> (consp (car z)) != nil

      [31;1mProof.[0m

      1.	|  	|  [47m(consp x) = t[0m	|  	|  [3m{Given}[0m	|
      2.	|  	|  [47mx = (car z)[0m	|  	|  [3m{Given}[0m	|
      3.	|  	|  [47mt != nil[0m	|  	|  [3m{Axiom 1}[0m	|
      4.	|  	|  [47m(consp x) != nil[0m	|  	|  [3m{Equality Substitution, line 1 into line 3}[0m	|
      5.	|  	|  [47m(consp (car z)) != nil[0m	|  	|  [3m{Equality Substitution, line 2 into line 4}[0m	|
      	|  	|  Q.E.D.	|  	|  	|

  We will not write proofs in this style.  We will simply say that the
  formula is a theorem ``by propositional calculus, equality, and
  Axiom 1.''

  Recall that each function definition adds an axiom.  The definition

      (defun tree-copy (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (cons (tree-copy (car x))
                  (tree-copy (cdr x)))
            x))

  adds the axiom

      [31;1mAxiom[0m tree-copy
      (tree-copy x)
      =
      (if (consp x)
          (cons (tree-copy (car x))
                (tree-copy (cdr x)))
          x)

  Thus, by the Rule of Instantiation

      [31;1mTheorem.[0m
      (tree-copy (cons a b))
      =
      (if (consp (cons a b))
          (cons (tree-copy (car (cons a b)))
                (tree-copy (cdr (cons a b))))
          (cons a b))

  Next: Terms as Formulas (see [R-AND-I-TERMS-AS-FORMULAS]) (or Table
  of Contents (see [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
