 (R-AND-I-DEFINITIONS-REVISITED
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: Definitions Revisited

  [31;1mProblem 27.
  [0m Suppose we define

      (defun f (x) 1)

  and then prove some theorems and then ``redefine'' [47mf[0m with

      (defun f (x) 2)

  Now prove that [47m(equal 'June' 'July)[0m, using the theorems that [47m(equal
  (f x) 1)[0m, proved before redefining [47mf[0m, and [47m(equal (f x) 2)[0m, proved
  after.

  Flesh out your argument using axioms presented earlier (see
  [r-and-i-axioms] and see [r-and-i-definitions-revisited]).

  A consequence is that since anything provable is valid, then the
  formula [47m(equal 'June 'July) != nil[0m is true, hence [47m'June[0m = [47m'July[0m, a
  contradiction!  In the following Problems we explore further such
  consequences of permitting ``bad'' definitions.

  [31;1mProblem 28.
  [0m Suppose we define

      (defun f (x) (cons x y))

  Prove [47m(equal 1 2)[0m.  (Remark.  The definition [47mf[0m in this problem has
  nothing to do with the definition of [47mf[0m in the previous problem!  We
  tend to ``re-use'' function names like [47mf[0m, [47mg[0m and [47mh[0m from time to time
  simply to avoid inventing new names.)

  [31;1mProblem 29.
  [0m Suppose we define

      (defun f (x) (not (f x)))

  Prove [47m(equal t nil)[0m.

  These problems should disturb you!  We often think of
  ``definitions'' as being logically innocuous, allowing us to
  abbreviate complicated expressions.  And we think of proof as a way
  to determine truth.  So it is disturbing if after making a
  ``definition'' we are suddenly able to prove things we know aren't
  true, like that [47mJune[0m is [47mJuly[0m or that [47mt[0m is [47mnil[0m!  Something has gone
  terribly wrong.

  To prevent this kind of logical inconsistency, we impose some
  restrictions on our ability to introduce definitions.  The
  restrictions we'll impose will prevent us from defining many useful
  functions but guarantee that we don't ruin the logic with
  ``definitions'' like those shown above.  ACL2 is much more generous
  in its restrictions but they are spiritually similar: both here and
  in ACL2 the restrictions on definitions will guarantee that every
  defined function terminates.  We do not explain in this document
  why termination is enough to avoid inconsistencies.

  One way to make sure a function terminates is to insist that there
  is an argument that is being [47mcar[0m'd and/or [47mcdr[0m'd at least once every
  time the function recurs and that before it recurs the definition
  tests that the argument is a [47mcons[0m-pair.  For example, [47m(defun f (x)
  (not (f x)))[0m is disallowed by this restriction, and so is [47m(defun f
  (x) (not (f (cdr x))))[0m.  But

      (defun f (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (and (not (f (car x)))
                 (not (f (cdr (cdr x)))))
            t))

  is allowed because [47mx[0m is [47mcar[0m'd and/or [47mcdr[0m'd in every recursion and
  the function tests [47m(consp x)[0m before recurring.  We say [47m(consp x)[0m
  ``rules'' the two recursive calls above.

  So we have to define the notions of a ``[47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m'' nest and what
  terms ``rule'' the recursive calls.

  A [3mcar/cdr nest around[0m  [3mv[0m  is [47m(car[0m [3mv[0m[47m)[0m, [47m(cdr[0m [3mv[0m[47m)[0m, or a [47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m nest
  around [47m(car[0m [3mv[0m[47m)[0m or [47m(cdr[0m [3mv[0m[47m)[0m.  Thus, [47m(car (cdr (car x)))[0m is a [47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m
  nest around [47mx[0m.

  The idea in this next definition is to take a term [3m\\beta[0m  and a
  particular occurrence [3mr[0m  of some subterm in [3m\\beta[0m  and define the
  set of tests that rule [3mr[0m.  Then, if you have a function definition
  like [47m(defun[0m [3mf[0m  [47m([0m[3mv_1[0m ... [3mv_k[0m[47m)[0m [3m\\beta[0m[47m)[0m you can let [3mr[0m  be a particular
  recursive call of [3mf[0m  in [3m\\beta[0m  and then determine which tests rule
  that call.

  The [3mrulers[0m  of an occurrence of a term [3mr[0m  in another term [3m\\beta[0m  is
  the set defined as follows:

   1. if [3m\\beta[0m  is [47m(if[0m [3mp[0m [3mx[0m [3my[0m[47m)[0m and [3mr[0m  is in [3mx[0m, then the rulers of [3mr[0m  in
      [3m\\beta[0m  is the set obtained by adding [3mp[0m  to the set of rulers of
      [3mr[0m  in [3mx[0m;

   2. if [3m\\beta[0m  is [47m(if[0m [3mp[0m [3mx[0m [3my[0m[47m)[0m and [3mr[0m  is in [3my[0m, then the rulers of [3mr[0m  in
      [3m\\beta[0m  is the set obtained by adding [47m(NOT[0m [3mp[0m[47m)[0m to the set of
      rulers of [3mr[0m  in [3my[0m;

   3. otherwise, the rulers of [3mr[0m  in [3m\\beta[0m  is the empty set.

  Thus, in the term [47m(if a (if b (h c) (h d)) (g c))[0m, both [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m rule
  the first occurrence of [47mc[0m and the occurrence of [47m(h c)[0m.  In
  addition, [47ma[0m and [47m(not b)[0m rule the occurrences of [47md[0m and [47m(h d)[0m.
  Finally, [47m(not a)[0m rules the second occurrence of [47mc[0m and [47m(g c)[0m.

  Note that our definition of ``rulers'' does not include every test
  that has to be true in order to reach the occurrence in question.
  For example, [47mp[0m does not rule the occurrence of [47ma[0m in [47m(car (if p a
  b))[0m even though the only way evaluation can reach [47ma[0m is if [47mp[0m is
  true.  The rulers of the occurrence of [47ma[0m in that term is the empty
  set, because that term is not a call of [47mif[0m.  However, [47mp[0m does rule
  the occurrence of [47ma[0m in the equivalent term [47m(if p (car a) (car b))[0m.
  The reason we've defined rulers this way has to do with heuristics
  in the ACL2 theorem prover.

  [31;1mPrinciple of Structural Recursion:[0m A definition, [47m(defun[0m [3mf[0m  [47m([0m[3mv_1[0m ...
  [3mv_n[0m[47m)[0m [3m\\beta[0m[47m)[0m will be allowed (for now) only if it satisfies these
  four restrictions:

   1. The symbol being defined, [3mf[0m, must be ``new,'' i.e., not already in
      use as a function symbol in any axiom.

   2. The formal variables, [3mv_1[0m, ... , [3mv_n[0m, must be distinct variable
      symbols.

   3. The body, [3m\\beta[0m, must be a term, it must use no new function symbol
      other than (possibly) [3mf[0m, and the only variable symbols in it
      are among the formals.

   4. There is an [3mi[0m  such that [47m(consp[0m [3mv_i[0m[47m)[0m rules every recursive call of [3mf[0m
      in [3m\\beta[0m  and for every recursive call ([3mf[0m  [3ma_1[0m ... [3ma_n[0m) in
      [3m\\beta[0m, [3ma_i[0m  is a [47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m nest around [3mv_i[0m.  We call [3mv_i[0m a
      [3mmeasured formal[0m.

  An acceptable definition adds the axiom [47m([0m[3mf[0m  [3mv_1[0m   ...  [3mv_n[0m[47m) = [0m[3m\\beta[0m.

  (Maybe explore <<[47m[defun][0m>>?  Be advised, however, that ACL2's
  Definitional Principle does not insist on the last condition above,
  about there being a [47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m nest in a certain argument of every
  recursive call.  Instead it insists that ``some measure of the
  arguments decreases'' in every recursive call.  We'll make that
  clearer later.  But ACL2's Definitional Principle allows all the
  definitions described above.)

  [31;1mProblem 30.
  [0m Explain why these restrictions rule out the spurious definitions of
