  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: Function Definitions

  To define a function, we use the form [47m(defun[0m [3mf[0m  ([3mv_1[0m  ...  [3mv_n[0m)
  [3m\\beta[0m[47m)[0m where [3mf[0m  is the function symbol being defined, the [3mv_i[0m  are
  the formal variables or simply [3mformals[0m, and [3m\\beta[0m  is the body of
  the function.

  Operationally, a definition means that to compute [47m([0m[3mf[0m  [3ma_1[0m   ...
  [3ma_n[0m[47m)[0m one can evaluate the actuals, [3ma_i[0m, bind the formals, [3mv_i[0m , to
  those values, and compute [3m\\beta[0m  instead.  Logically speaking, a
  definition adds the axiom that [47m([0m[3mf[0m  [3mv_1[0m   ...  [3mv_n[0m[47m)[0m is equal to
  [3m\\beta[0m.

  Here are the Lisp definitions of the standard propositional logic
  connectives:

      (defun not (p) (if p nil t))

      (defun and (p q) (if p q nil))

      (defun or (p q) (if p p q))

      (defun implies (p q) (if p (if q t nil) t))

      (defun iff (p q) (and (implies p q) (implies q p)))

  Note that in Lisp, [47mand[0m and [47mor[0m are not Boolean valued.  E.g., [47m(and t
  3)[0m and [47m(or nil 3)[0m both return [47m3[0m.  This is unimportant if they are
  only used propositionally, e.g., [47m(and t 3)[0m <-> [47m(and 3 t)[0m <-> [47mt[0m, if
  ``<->'' means [47miff[0m.  In Lisp, any non-nil value is propositionally
  equivalent to [47mt[0m.

  Here is a recursive definition that copies a [47mcons[0m-structure.

      (defun tree-copy (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (cons (tree-copy (car x))
                  (tree-copy (cdr x)))
            x))

  For example, the term [47m(tree-copy '((1 . 2) . 3))[0m has the value [47m((1 .
  2) . 3)[0m.

  (Maybe explore <<[47m[defun][0m>>?  Be advised, however, that ACL2's
  Definitional Principle imposes restrictions that may not make sense
  just yet.)

  In the exercises below you may wish to define auxiliary (``helper'')
  functions as part of your solutions.

  [31;1mProblem 8.
  [0m Define [47mapp[0m to concatenate two lists.  For example [47m(app '(1 2 3) '(4
  5 6))[0m evaluates to [47m(1 2 3 4 5 6)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 9.
  [0m Define [47mrev[0m to reverse a list.  For example, [47m(rev '(1 2 3))[0m evaluates
  to [47m(3 2 1)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 10.
  [0m Define [47mmapnil[0m to ``copy'' a list, replacing each element by [47mnil[0m.
  Thus, [47m(mapnil '(1 2 3))[0m evaluates to [47m(nil nil nil)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 11.
  [0m The result of ``swapping'' the pair [47m([3mx[0m[47m  . [3my[0m[47m)[0m is the pair [47m([3my[0m[47m  . [3mx[0m[47m)[0m.
  Define [47mswap-tree[0m to swap every cons in a binary tree.  Thus,
  [47m(swap-tree '((1 . 2) . (3 . 4)))[0m evaluates to [47m((4 . 3) . (2 . 1))[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 12.
  [0m Define [47mmem[0m to take two arguments and determine if the first one
  occurs as an element of the second.  Thus, [47m(mem '2 '(1 2 3))[0m
  evaluates to [47mt[0m and [47m(mem '4 '(1 2 3))[0m evaluates to [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 13.
  [0m Define the list analogue of subset, i.e., [47m(sub x y)[0m returns [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m
  according to whether every element of [47mx[0m is an element of [47my[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 14.
  [0m Define [47mint[0m to take two lists and to return the list of elements that
  appear in both.  Thus [47m(int '(1 2 3 4) '(2 4 6))[0m evaluates to [47m(2 4)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 15.
  [0m Define [47m(tip e x)[0m to determine whether [47me[0m occurs as a tip of the
  binary tree [47mx[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 16.
  [0m Define [47m(flatten x)[0m to make a list containing the tips of the binary
  tree [47mx[0m.  Thus, [47m(flatten '((1 . 2) . (3 . 4)))[0m evaluates to [47m(1 2 3
  4)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 17.
  [0m Define [47mevenlen[0m to recognize lists of even length.  Thus, [47m(evenlen
