 (R-AND-I-MORE-PROBLEMS
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: More Problems

  [31;1mProblem 106.
  [0m Here is a way to flatten a binary tree without using an auxiliary
  function.  Admit this definition.

      (defun flatten! (x)
        (if (atom x)
            (cons x nil)
            (if (atom (car x))
                (cons (car x) (flatten! (cdr x)))
                (flatten! (cons (caar x) (cons (cdar x) (cdr x)))))))

  [31;1mProblem 107.
  [0m Prove [47m(equal (flatten! x) (flatten x))[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 108.
  [0m Here is a clever way to determine if two binary trees have the same
  fringe.  Admit this function (and its subroutine).

      (defun samefringe (x y)
       (if (or (atom x)
               (atom y))
           (equal x y)
           (and (equal (car (gopher x))
                       (car (gopher y)))
                (samefringe (cdr (gopher x))
                            (cdr (gopher y))))))

  where

      (defun gopher (x)
        (if (or (atom x)
                (atom (car x)))
            x
            (gopher (cons (caar x) (cons (cdar x) (cdr x))))))

  [31;1mProblem 109.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (samefringe x y)
             (equal (flatten x)
                    (flatten y)))

  [31;1mProblem 110.
  [0m The curious recursions in [47mgopher[0m and [47msamefringe[0m are due to John
  McCarthy, who viewed [47mgopher[0m as a model of a co-routine.  Explain
  what he was thinking.

  [31;1mProblem 111.
  [0m Below is a model of the functional behavior of QuickSort.  Note that
  [47mrel[0m is defined as a ``higher order'' function that can apply any of
  four relations.

      (defun rel (fn x y)
        (if (equal fn '<<=)
            (<<= x y)
            (if (equal fn '>>=)
                (<<= y x)
                (if (equal fn '<<)
                    (and (<<= x y) (not (equal x y)))
                    (and (<<= y x) (not (equal x y)))))))

      (defun filter (fn x e)
        (if (endp x)
            nil
            (if (rel fn (car x) e)
                (cons (car x) (filter fn (cdr x) e))
                (filter fn (cdr x) e))))

      (defun qsort (x)
        (if (endp x)
            nil
            (if (endp (cdr x))
                x
                (app (qsort (filter '<< (cdr x) (car x)))
                     (cons (car x)
                           (qsort (filter '>>= (cdr x) (car x))))))))

  Prove that [47mqsort[0m produces an ordered permutation of its input.

  [31;1mProblem 112.
  [0m Prove that the length of a list of distinct natural numbers is no
  greater than its maximum element plus one.  This is sometimes
  called the Pigeon Hole Principle.

  [31;1mProblem 113.
  [0m Imagine a simple list being treated as a ``memory.'' If [3ma[0m  is a
  natural number less than the length of the memory, then [3ma[0m  is an
  ``address'' and we can use [47mnth[0m to fetch the contents.  This is
  ``dereferencing'' [3ma[0m.  If [3ma[0m  is not an address, we will say it is
  ``data.'' Now consider the idea of taking an object and a memory
  and dereferencing until we get to data.  The following function
  counts the steps; it returns the symbol [47minfinite[0m if a loop is
  detected.

      (defun deref-cnt (ptr mem seen)
        (if (addressp ptr mem)
            (if (mem ptr seen)
                'infinite
                (inc (deref-cnt (nth ptr mem) mem (cons ptr seen))))
            0))

  where

      (defun len (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (+ 1 (len (cdr x)))
            0))

      (defun inc (x)
        (if (integerp x) (+ 1 x) 'infinite))

      (defun addressp (ptr m)
        (and (natp ptr)
             (< ptr (len m))))

  Admit [47mderef-cnt[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 114.
  [0m This is a fact every undergraduate knows.  The number of times you
  can dereference (without being in a loop) is bounded by the size of
  the memory (plus one for the initial probe).  Prove it.

      (<= (deref-cnt ptr mem nil)
          (+ 1 (len mem)))

  Since [47mderef-cnt[0m returns the non-number [47minfinite[0m if it is a loop, and
  since ACL2 arithmetic treats non-numbers as [47m0[0m, this theorem is
  trivial when the process loops.  You may therefore explicitly add
  the hypothesis [47m(integerp (deref-cnt ptr mem nil))[0m if you are
  uncomfortable dealing with the non-numeric defaults.

  [31;1mProblem 115.
  [0m Here is the familiar definition of the Fibonacci function and a more
  efficient one (``fast Fibonacci'').

      (defun fib (i)
        (if (zp i)
            0
            (if (equal i 1)
                1
                (+ (fib (- i 1))
                   (fib (- i 2))))))

      (defun ffib (i j k)
        (if (zp i)
            j
            (if (equal i 1)
                k
                (ffib (- i 1) k (+ j k)))))

  Prove they are equal in the following sense

      (equal (ffib n 0 1) (fib n))

  [31;1mProblem 116.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (rotn (len x) x) x)

  where

      (defun rot (x)
        (if (endp x)
            nil
            (app (cdr x) (cons (car x) nil))))

      (defun rotn (n x)
        (if (zp n)
            x
            (rotn (- n 1) (rot x))))

  [31;1mProblem 117.
  [0m In this problem we explore binary arithmetic.  Let a [3mbit vector[0m  be
  a list of Booleans; it is convenient to suppose that the least
  significant bit is the car.  A bit vector represents a natural
  number in the usual binary encoding.  Define [47m(vadd x y)[0m to return a
  bit vector representing the sum of the numbers represented by the
  bit vectors [47mx[0m and [47my[0m.  Formally specify and prove that [47mvadd[0m is
  correct.  Obviously, this problem can be expanded to include other
  binary arithmetic operations and implementations.

  Next: More Inadequacies of the Definitional Principle (see
  [R-AND-I-MORE-INADEQUACIES-OF-THE-DEFINITIONAL-PRINCIPLE]) (or
  Table of Contents (see [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-MUTUAL-RECURSION-INADEQUACIES
  (R-AND-I-MORE-INADEQUACIES-OF-THE-DEFINITIONAL-PRINCIPLE)
  "Recursion and Induction: Mutual Recursion Inadequacies

  [31;1mProblem 118.
  [0m Mutual recursion is (still) not allowed by our statement of the
  Definitional Principle.  However, suppose that somehow the axioms
  produced by the following pair of definitions were available.

      (defun fx (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (cons (fx (car x))
                  (gx (cdr x)))
            x))

      (defun gx (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (cons (gx (car x))
                  (fx (cdr x)))
            x))

  Prove

      (equal (fx x) x)

  [31;1mProblem 119.
  [0m Develop a methodology for dealing with mutual recursion.  That is,
  explain how you can use our Definitional Principle to introduce two
  functions [47mfx[0m and [47mgx[0m that can be shown to satisfy the ``defining
  equations'' for [47mfx[0m and [47mgx[0m above.  Your methodology should work for
  any clique of mutually recursive functions that can be shown to
  terminate under the [47mo<[0m relation.

  [31;1mProblem 120.
  [0m We say [47mx[0m is an [3mexpression[0m  if (a) [47mx[0m is a symbol or (b) [47mx[0m is a list
  of the form [47m([0m[3mf[0m  [3me_1[0m   ...  [3me_n[0m[47m)[0m, where [3mf[0m  is a symbol and the [3me_i[0m
  are expressions.  Define [47m(expr x)[0m to recognize expressions.

  [31;1mProblem 121.
  [0m An [3mexpression substitution[0m  is a list of 2-tuples of the form [47m([0m[3mv[0m
  [3mexpr[0m[47m)[0m, where [3mv[0m  is a symbol and [3mexpr[0m  is an expression.  Define
  [47msubstitution[0m to recognize expression substitutions.

  [31;1mProblem 122.
  [0m Define [47m(slash x s)[0m so that it substitutes the expression
  substitution [47ms[0m into the expression [47mx[0m, e.g., it returns [47mx[0m/[47ms[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 123.
  [0m Prove that if [47ms[0m is an expression substitution and [47mx[0m is an
  expression, then [47m(slash x s)[0m is an expression.

  (Maybe explore <<[mutual-recursion]>>?)

  Next: Problematic Nested Recursion (see
  [R-AND-I-PROBLEMATIC-NESTED-RECURSION]) (or Table of Contents (see
  [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-NUMBERS
  (R-AND-I-DATA-TYPES)
  "Recursion and Induction: Numbers

  The only numbers we will use are the integers, written in the usual
  way, e.g., [47m-3[0m, [47m0[0m, and [47m123[0m.  ACL2 allows integers to be written in
  other ways, e.g., [47m00123[0m, [47m+123[0m, [47m246/2[0m, [47m#b1111011[0m, [47m#o173[0m and [47m#x7B[0m are
  all ways to write [47m123[0m.  However, we will always write them in
  conventional decimal notation.

  ACL2 also supports rationals, e.g., [47m1/3[0m and [47m22/7[0m, and complex
  rationals, e.g., [47m#c(5 2)[0m, which is more commonly written 5 + 2[3mi[0m.
  Lisp, but not ACL2, supports floating point numbers, e.g., [47m3.1415[0m
  and [47m31415E-4[0m.

  (Maybe explore <<[numbers]>>?)

  Next: Characters (see [R-AND-I-CHARACTERS]) (or Table of Contents
  (see [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-ORDINALS
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: The Ordinals

  The ordinals are an extension of the naturals that captures an
  essential notion of ordering.  They were invented by Georg Cantor
  in the late nineteen century.  While controversial during Cantor's
  lifetime, ordinals are among the richest and deepest mines of
  mathematics.  We only scratch the surface here.

  Think of each natural number as denoted by a sequence of strokes,
  i.e.,

      0             0
      1             |
      2             | |
      3             | | |
      4             | | | |
       ...               ...
      \\omega             | | | | |  ...

  The limit of that progression is the ordinal [3m\\omega[0m, an infinite
  sequence of strokes.

  Ordinal addition is just concatenation.  Observe that adding one to
  the front of [3m\\omega[0m  produces [3m\\omega[0m  again, which gives rise to a
  standard definition of [3m\\omega[0m: the least ordinal such that adding
  another stroke at the beginning does not change the ordinal.

  We denote by [3m\\omega[0m  + [3m\\omega[0m  or [3m\\omega[0m\\times2 the ``doubly
  infinite'' sequence that we might write as follows.

      \\omega \\times 2         | | | | |  ...  | | | | |  ...

  One way to think of [3m\\omega[0m\\times2 is that it is obtained by
  replacing each stroke in 2 (| |) by [3m\\omega[0m.  Thus, one can imagine
  [3m\\omega[0m\\times3, [3m\\omega[0m\\times4, etc., which leads ultimately to the
  idea of [3m\\omega[0m\\times[3m\\omega[0m, the ordinal obtained by replacing each
  stroke in [3m\\omega[0m  by [3m\\omega[0m.  This is also written as [3m\\omega[0m^2.

      \\omega^2           | | | | |  ...  | | | | |  ...  | | | | |  ...  | | | | |  ...  | | | | |  ...   ...

  We can analogously construct [3m\\omega[0m^3 by replacing each stroke in
  [3m\\omega[0m  by [3m\\omega[0m^2 (which, it turns out, is the same as replacing
  each stroke in [3m\\omega[0m^2 by [3m\\omega[0m).  That is, we can construct
  [3m\\omega[0m^3 as [3m\\omega[0m  copies of [3m\\omega[0m^2, and so on.  This ultimately
  suggests [3m\\omega[0m^[3m\\omega[0m.  We can then stack [3m\\omega[0ms, i.e.,
  [3m\\omega[0m^([3m\\omega[0m^[3m\\omega[0m), etc.  Consider the limit of all of those
  stacks,

  [47m [3m\\omega[0m[47m^([3m\\omega[0m[47m^([3m\\omega[0m[47m^([3m\\omega[0m[47m^([3m\\omega[0m[47m^([3m\\omega[0m[47m^([3m\\omega[0m[47m^ ... )))))).
  [0m

  That limit is [3m\\epsilon_0[0m.  (As the subscript suggests, there are
  lots more ordinals!  But ACL2 stops with [3m\\epsilon_0[0m.)

  Despite the plethora of ordinals, we can represent all the ones
  below [3m\\epsilon_0[0m  in ACL2, using lists.  Below we begin listing
  some ordinals up to [3m\\epsilon_0[0m; the reader can fill in the gaps at
  his or her leisure.  We show in the left column the conventional
  notation and in the right column the ACL2 object representing the
  corresponding ordinal.

    ordinal	|  	|  ACL2 representation	|
    	|  	|  	|
    0	|  	|  0	|
    1	|  	|  1	|
    2	|  	|  2	|
    3	|  	|  3	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m	|  	|  ((1 . 1) . 0)	|
    [3m\\omega[0m  + 1	|  	|  ((1 . 1) . 1)	|
    [3m\\omega[0m  + 2	|  	|  ((1 . 1) . 2)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m\\times2	|  	|  ((1 . 2) . 0)	|
    ([3m\\omega[0m\\times2) + 1	|  	|  ((1 . 2) . 1)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m\\times3	|  	|  ((1 . 3) . 0)	|
    ([3m\\omega[0m\\times3) + 1	|  	|  ((1 . 3) . 1)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m^2	|  	|  ((2 . 1) . 0)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m^2 + [3m\\omega[0m\\times4 + 3	|  	|  ((2 . 1) (1 . 4) . 3)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m^3	|  	|  ((3 . 1) . 0)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m^[3m\\omega[0m	|  	|  ((((1 . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m^[3m\\omega[0m  + [3m\\omega[0m^99 + [3m\\omega[0m\\times4 + 3	|  	|  ((((1 . 1) . 0) . 1) (99 . 1) (1 . 4) . 3)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m^([3m\\omega[0m^2)	|  	|  ((((2 . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|
    [3m\\omega[0m^([3m\\omega[0m^[3m\\omega[0m)	|  	|  ((((((1 . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0) . 1) . 0)	|
    ...	|  	|  ...	|

  We say an ordinal is ``finite'' if it is not a cons and we define
  [47m(o-finp x)[0m to recognize finite ordinals.  Of course, if [47mx[0m is an
  ordinal and finite, it is a natural number.  But by defining [47mo-finp[0m
  this way we ensure that if an ordinal is not finite we can recur
  into it with [47mcdr[0m.

  To manipulate ordinals we define functions that access the first
  exponent, the first coefficient, and the rest of the ordinal:

      (defun o-first-expt (x)
        (if (o-finp x) 0 (car (car x))))

      (defun o-first-coeff (x)
        (if (o-finp x) x (cdr (car x))))

      (defun o-rst (x) (cdr x))

  For example, if [47mx[0m is the representation of [3m\\omega[0m^e\\times[3mc[0m  + [3mr[0m
  then [47m(o-first-expt x)[0m is [3me[0m, [47m(o-first-coeff x)[0m is [3mc[0m  and [47m(o-rst x)[0m
  is [3mr[0m.

  Here is the definition of [47mo-p[0m, the function that recognizes
  ordinals.

      (defun o-p (x)
        (if (o-finp x)
            (natp x)
            (and (consp (car x))
                 (o-p (o-first-expt x))
                 (not (equal 0 (o-first-expt x)))
                 (natp (o-first-coeff x))
                 (< 0 (o-first-coeff x))
                 (o-p (o-rst x))
                 (o< (o-first-expt (o-rst x))
                     (o-first-expt x)))))

  (The ACL2 definition is syntactically different but equivalent.)

  The function [47mo<[0m is the ``less than'' relation on ordinals.  We show
  its definition below.  But study the definition of [47mo-p[0m first.  It
  says that an ordinal is a list of pairs, terminated by a natural
  number.  Each pair [47m(e . c)[0m consists of an exponent [47me[0m and a
  coefficient [47mc[0m and represents ([3m\\omega[0m^e)\\times[3mc[0m.  The exponents are
  themselves ordinals and the coefficients are non-0 naturals.
  Importantly, the exponents are listed in strictly descending order.
  The list represents the ordinal sum of its elements plus the final
  natural number.  Thus, ordinals can be viewed as polynomials.

  By insisting on the ordering of exponents we can readily compare two
  ordinals, using [47mo<[0m below, in much the same way we can compare
  polynomials.

      (defun o< (x y)
        (if (o-finp x)
            (or (not (o-finp y))
                (< x y))
            (if (o-finp y)
                nil
                (if (equal (o-first-expt x)
                           (o-first-expt y))
                    (if (equal (o-first-coeff x)
                               (o-first-coeff y))
                        (o< (o-rst x)
                            (o-rst y))
                        (< (o-first-coeff x)
                           (o-first-coeff y)))
                    (o< (o-first-expt x)
                        (o-first-expt y))))))

  (The ACL2 definition is syntactically different but equivalent.)

  [31;1mProblem 90.
  [0m Which is smaller, ordinal a or ordinal b?

   1. a = [47m23[0m, b = [47m100[0m

   2. a = [47m1000000[0m, b = [47m((1 . 1) . 0)[0m

   3. a = [47m((2 . 1) . 0)[0m, b = [47m((1 . 2) . 0)[0m

   4. a = [47m((3 . 5) (1 . 25) . 7)[0m, b = [47m((3 . 5) (2 . 1) . 3)[0m

   5. a = [47m((((2 . 1) . 0) . 5) . 3)[0m, b = [47m((((1 . 1) . 0) . 5) (1 . 25) . 7)[0m

  [31;1mProblem 91.
  [0m The [47mo<[0m operation can be reduced to lexicographic comparison.  Define
  [47mm2[0m so that it constructs ``lexicographic ordinals'' from two
  arbitrary natural numbers.  Specifically, show that the following
  is a theorem:

      (implies (and (natp i1)
                    (natp j1)
                    (natp i2)
                    (natp j2))
               (and (o-p (m2 i1 j1))
                    (iff (o< (m2 i1 j1)
                             (m2 i2 j2))
                         (if (equal i1 i2)
                             (< j1 j2)
                             (< i1 i2)))))

  The crucial property of [47mo<[0m is that it is [3mwell-founded on the
  ordinals[0m.  That is, there is no infinite sequence of ordinals, [3mx_i[0m
  such that  ...  [3mx_3[0m  [47mo<[0m [3mx_2[0m  [47mo<[0m [3mx_1[0m  [47mo<[0m [3mx_0[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 92.
  [0m What is the longest decreasing chain of ordinals starting from the
  ordinal [47m10[0m?  What is the longest decreasing chain of ordinals
  starting from the ordinal [47m((1 . 1) . 0)[0m?

  [31;1mProblem 93.
  [0m Construct an infinitely descending [47mo<[0m chain of objects.  Note that
  by the well-foundedness of [47mo<[0m on the ordinals, your chain will not
  consist entirely of ordinals!

  [31;1mProblem 94.
  [0m Prove that [47mo<[0m is well-founded on our ordinals, i.e., those
  recognized by [47mo-p[0m.

  Caution: Using the logical machinery we have developed here, it is
  not possible to state that [47mo<[0m is well-founded on the ordinals: that
  requires an existential quantifier and infinite sequences.
  However, it can be done in a traditional set theoretic setting.
  That is, the theorem that [47mo<[0m is well-founded is a ``meta-theorem'',
  it can be proved about our system but it cannot be proved within
  our system.

  Our definitional and induction principles are built on the
  assumption that [47mo<[0m is well-founded on the ordinals recognized by
  [47mo-p[0m.  Thus, if some ordinal measure of the arguments of a recursive
  function decreases according to [47mo<[0m in every recursive call, the
  recursion cannot go on forever.

  The representation of ordinals described here is a version of
  Cantor's Normal Form.  See [reference 5] (see
  [R-AND-I-ANNOTATED-BIBLIOGRAPHY]) or the online documentation
  topics <<[ordinals]>> and <<[o-p]>>, from which some of the
  examples above have been chosen.

  Next: The Definitional Principle (see
  [R-AND-I-DEFINITIONAL-PRINCIPLE]) (or Table of Contents (see
  [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-PAIRS
  (R-AND-I-DATA-TYPES)
  "Recursion and Induction: Pairs

  Pairs are written in Lisp's [3mdot notation[0m.  Instead of conventional
  Cartesian notation, e.g., < [47m1[0m, [47m2[0m >, Lisp replaces the angle
  brackets with parentheses and the comma with a dot, [47m(1 . 2)[0m.  Thus,
  [47m((1 . 2) . (3 . 4))[0m is the pair containing the pair [47m(1 . 2)[0m in its
  left component and the pair [47m(3 . 4)[0m in its right.  In high school,
  you might have written this object as <<1, 2>,<3, 4>>.  Note that
  exactly one object must follow the dot; for example, the notation
  [47m((1 . 2) . 3 4)[0m is illegal.  However, one or more objects may
  precede the dot, as discussed below.

  In Lisp, pairs are called [3mconses[0m.  Non-conses are called [3matoms[0m.  The
  left component is called the [3mcar[0m  and the right component is called
  the [3mcdr[0m  (pronounced ``cudder'').  (Remark.  The names come from
  the original implementation of Lisp on the IBM 704.  That machine
  had a 36-bit word that was logically divided into two parts, the
  ``address'' and the ``decrement.'' Lisp used such words to
  represent a [47mcons[0m cell.  The address part pointed to the left
  component and the decrement part pointed to the right component.
  Thus, the operations were [47mcar[0m (``contents of address of register'')
  and [47mcdr[0m (``contents of decrement of register'').)  Lisp provides
  three conventions for writing parenthesized constants.

    * [47mNil[0m can be written [47m()[0m.

    * A pair of the form [47m([3mx[0m[47m  . nil)[0m may be written [47m([3mx[0m[47m)[0m.

    * A pair of the form [47m([3mx[0m[47m  . ([3my[0m[47m  ...))[0m may be written [47m([3mx[0m[47m [3my[0m[47m  ...)[0m.

  Thus, the cons [47m(1 . (2 . (3 . nil)))[0m may be written [47m(1 2 3)[0m.  This
  suggests the most common use of conses: to represent linked lists
  or sequences.  The special role of [47mnil[0m in these conventions is the
  only sense in which [47mnil[0m is ``the empty list.''

  Any object can be treated as a list!  If a cons is being treated as
  a list, then its car is the first element and its cdr is treated as
  a list of the remaining elements.  If an atom is treated as a list,
  it is treated as the empty list.  [47mNil[0m is just the most common atom
  used in this way.

  Thus, the elements of [47m(1 . (2 . (3 . nil)))[0m are [47m1[0m, [47m2[0m, and [47m3[0m,
  respectively.  The length of this list is three.  If [47m((1 . 2) . (3
  . 4))[0m is treated as a list, its elements are [47m(1 . 2)[0m and [47m3[0m; its
  length is two.  The [47m4[0m is just the terminal atom.  [47m((1 . 2) . (3 .
  nil))[0m and [47m((1 . 2) . (3 . 4))[0m are different objects, but when
  treated as lists they have the same two elements.

  Here is a list of the symbols naming the summer months: [47m(June July
  August)[0m.  It could also be written [47m(JUNE . (JULY . (AUGUST . NIL)))[0m
  and many other ways.

  (Maybe explore <<[conses]>>?)

  Next: identity (see [R-AND-I-IDENTITY]) (or Table of Contents (see
  [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-PEANO-ARITHMETIC
  (R-AND-I-ARITHMETIC)
  "Recursion and Induction: Peano Arithmetic

  Recall that the integers are being treated as atomic objects in this
  document.  But we can explore elementary arithmetic by thinking of
  a list of [3mn[0m  [47mnil[0ms as a representation for the natural number [3mn[0m.  We
  will call such a list a ``nat.'' Thus, [47m(nil nil nil)[0m is a nat, but
  [47m3[0m is a natural number.

  [31;1mProblem 65.
  [0m Define [47m(nat x)[0m to recognize nats.

  [31;1mProblem 66.
  [0m Define [47m(plus x y)[0m to take two arbitrary lists (even ones that are
  not nats) and to return the nat representing the sum of their
  lengths.  By defining [47mplus[0m this way we ensure that it always
  returns a nat and that it is commutative.

  [31;1mProblem 67.
  [0m Define [47m(times x y)[0m to take two arbitrary lists and to return the nat
  representing the product of their lengths.

  [31;1mProblem 68.
  [0m Define [47m(power x y)[0m to take two arbitrary lists and to return the nat
  representing the exponentiation of their lengths, i.e., if [47mx[0m and [47my[0m
  are of lengths [3mi[0m  and [3mj[0m, then [47m(power x y)[0m should return the nat
  representing [3mi^j[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 69.
  [0m Define [47m(lesseqp x y)[0m to return [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m according to whether the
  length of [47mx[0m is less than or equal to that of [47my[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 70.
  [0m Define [47m(evennat x)[0m to return [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m according to whether the
  length of [47mx[0m is even.

  [31;1mProblem 71.
  [0m Prove

      (implies (nat i)
               (equal (plus i nil) i))

  [31;1mProblem 72.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (plus (plus i j) k)
             (plus i (plus j k)))

  [31;1mProblem 73.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (plus i j) (plus j i))

  [31;1mProblem 74.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (times (times i j) k)
             (times i (times j k)))

  [31;1mProblem 75.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (times i j) (times j i))

  [31;1mProblem 76.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (power b (plus i j))
             (times (power b i) (power b j)))

  [31;1mProblem 77.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (power (power b i) j)
             (power b (times i j)))

  [31;1mProblem 78.
  [0m Prove

      (lesseqp i i)

  [31;1mProblem 79.
  [0m Prove

      (implies (and (lesseqp i j)
                    (lesseqp j k))
               (lesseqp i k))

  [31;1mProblem 80.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (lesseqp (plus i j) (plus i k))
             (lesseqp j k))

  [31;1mProblem 81.
  [0m Prove

      (implies (and (evennat i)
                    (evennat j))
               (evennat (plus i j)))

  Next: ACL2 Arithmetic (see [R-AND-I-ACL2-ARITHMETIC]) (or Table of
  Contents (see [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-PROBLEMATIC-NESTED-RECURSION
  (R-AND-I-MORE-INADEQUACIES-OF-THE-DEFINITIONAL-PRINCIPLE)
  "Recursion and Induction: Problematic Nested Recursion

  [31;1mProblem 124.
  [0m The following function cannot be admitted.  Explain why.

      (defun f5 (x)
        (if (zp x)
            0
            (+ 1 (f5 (f5 (- x 1))))))

  When the function being defined, [3mf[0m, is called recursively on the
  value of a nested recursive call, e.g., [47m([0m[3mf[0m   ...  ([3mf[0m   ... [47m))[0m, we
  say the definition exhibits [3mnested recursion[0m.  We have already seen
  some examples of nested recursion:

      (defun mcflatten (x a)
        (if (consp x)
            (mcflatten (car x)
                       (mcflatten (cdr x) a))
            (cons x a)))

      (defun ack (x y)
        (if (zp x)
            1
            (if (zp y)
                (if (equal x 1) 2 (+ x 2))
                (ack (ack (- x 1) y) (- y 1)))))

  [31;1mProblem 125.
  [0m What makes the recursion in [47mf5[0m, which cannot be admitted, different
  from that in [47mmcflatten[0m and [47mack[0m, which can be admitted?

  It is often possible to deal with problematic nested recursion, by
  admitting a different but related definition and then proving that
  it is equivalent to the one you wanted.  The next few problems
  illustrate this.

  [31;1mProblem 126.
  [0m Admit the following

      (defun f5 (x)
        (if (zp x)
            0
            (if (< (nfix (f5 (- x 1))) (nfix x))
                (+ 1 (f5 (f5 (- x 1))))
                'undef)))

  [31;1mProblem 127.
  [0m Prove

      (implies (natp x) (<= (f5 x) x))

  [31;1mProblem 128.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (f5 x)
             (if (zp x)
                 0
                 (+ 1 (f5 (f5 (- x 1))))))

  You may think that problematic nested recursion never arises in
  actual formal models.  Think again!  Consider a graph reachability
  algorithm, as might be implemented in the mark phase of a garbage
  collector.  Suppose a memory location can hold a pair of addresses,
  a single address, or data.  Given an initial address, mark all the
  reachable addresses, avoiding any possible cycles in the data.
  When the mark algorithm arrives at an address, [3mptr[0m, containing a
  pair, it marks that address, explores and marks one of the
  addresses, and then explores and marks the other.  The algorithm
  terminates because marking increases the number of marked addresses
  in memory and the number of addresses is bounded.  But note that
  when the algorithm explores and marks the second address it does so
  on the memory produced by exploring and marking the first address
  and the parent.  This is problematic nested recursion: if the inner
  recursion [3munmarked[0m  some of the marked addresses, the outer sweep
  might get caught in a cycle.  But we cannot prove that the
  algorithm does not unmark things until we have admitted the model!

  [31;1mProblem 129.
  [0m Let [47mm[0m be a list representing a RAM, with 0-based addressing modeled
  by [47mnth[0m.  An address is legal if it is a natural less than the
  length of the memory.  If an address contains a pair, we explore
  both the [47mcar[0m and the [47mcdr[0m.  If an address contains an address, we
  explore that address.  Cycles may be present.  The function below
  collects the list of all addresses reachable from a given one.
  This definition exhibits problematic nested recursion.

      (defun reachables (ptr m seen)
        (if (addressp ptr m)
            (if (mem ptr seen)
                seen
                (if (consp (nth ptr m))
                    (reachables (car (nth ptr m))
                                m
                                (reachables (cdr (nth ptr m))
                                            m
                                            (cons ptr seen)))
                    (reachables (nth ptr m) m (cons ptr seen))))
            seen))

  Use the method suggested by the [47mf5[0m problems to define a function
  satisfying the equation above.

  [31;1mProblem 130.
  [0m Consider the problem of reversing a list.  Our standard definition,
  [47mrev[0m, uses an auxiliary function, [47mapp[0m.  The tail-recursive version,
  [47mrev1[0m, uses an additional formal parameter.  Below is a definition
  of reverse that has only one parameter and no auxiliary functions.
  This definition was proposed by Rod M. Burstall in the early 1970s
  as an entertaining puzzle.  It exhibits problematic nested
  recursion.

      (defun rmb (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (if (consp (cdr x))
                (cons (car (rmb (cdr x)))
                      (rmb (cons (car x)
                                 (rmb (cdr (rmb (cdr x)))))))
                (cons (car x) nil))
            nil))

  Show how this definition can be derived from an admissible one.

  [31;1mProblem 131.
  [0m Suppose [47mrmb[0m is known to satisfy the ``defining equation'' that would
  be added by the [47mdefun[0m above had it been admissible.  That is,
  suppose that is the only equation known for [47mrmb[0m.  Prove

      (equal (rmb x) (rev x))

  Next: Still More Problems (see [R-AND-I-STILL-MORE-PROBLEMS]) (or
  Table of Contents (see [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-RELATIONS-BETWEEN-RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: Relations Between Recursion and Induction

  Informally speaking, a recursive definition is ``ok'' if there is an
  ordinal measure that decreases in every recursive call.  Thus, the
  recursion cannot go on forever.  In a simple recursion on naturals
  down to 0 by -1, the value of the function on 5 is determined
  recursively by its value on 4, which is determined recursively by
  its value on 3, which is determined recursively by its value on 2,
  which is determined recursively by its value on 1, which is
  determined recursively by its value on 0, which is specified
  explicitly in the definition.

  An inductive proof is ``ok'' if there is an ordinal measure that
  decreases in every induction hypothesis.  Thus, any concrete
  instance of the conjecture could be proved by ``pumping'' forward a
  finite number of times from the base cases.  Given a simple
  inductive proof over the naturals, the conjecture is true on 0
  because it was proved explicitly in the base case, so it is true on
  1 by the induction step, so it is true on 2 by the induction step,
  so it is true on 3 by the induction step, so it is true on 4 by the
  induction step, so it is true on 5 by the induction step.

  Clearly these two concepts are duals.  The formal statements of the
  two principles look more different than they are.  They both
  require us to prove that a measure returns an ordinal and that some
  substitutions make the measure decrease under some tests.  But
  there seems to be a lot less indexing going on in the Definitional
  Principle than in the Induction Principle.  That is due to language
  and the two different uses of the principles.  The Definitional
  Principle is designed to tell us whether a definitional equation is
  ok.  The Induction Principle is designed to tell us whether a set
  of formulas is an ok inductive argument.  So the Definitional
  Principle talks about the tests in [47mIF[0ms and the substitution built
  from each recursive call, whereas the Induction Principle talks
  about the tests in the [3mi[0mth formula and the substitution that
  created the [3mj[0mth induction hypothesis of the [3mi[0mth formula.

  But the key insight is: [3mEvery ok definition suggests an ok
  induction[0m!  We call this the induction [3msuggested by[0m  the
  definition.  It is easiest to see this by considering a particular,
  generic definition and thinking about what had to be proved to
  admit it, what induction it suggests, what has to be proved for
  that induction to be legal, and when the suggested induction might
  be useful.

  Suppose the following definition has been admitted, justified by
  measure term [47m(m x a)[0m.  Note that the body is an [47mIF[0m-tree, and there
  are three tips in the [47mIF[0m-tree; the first tip contains two recursive
  calls, the second tip contains one recursive call, and the third
  tip contains no recursive calls.

      (defun f (x a)
        (if (test1 x a)
            (if (test2 x a)
                (h                         ; tip 1
                 (f (d1 x a) (a1 x a))     ;   rec call 1,1
                 (f (d2 x a) (a2 x a)))    ;   rec call 1,2
                (g                         ; tip 2
                 (f (d3 x a) (a3 x a))))   ;   rec call 2,1
            (b x a)))                      ; tip 3

  To admit this definition we had to prove:

      [3mOrdinal Conjecture[0m
      (o-p (m x a))

      [3mMeasure Conjecture 1,1[0m
      (implies (and (test1 x a) (test2 x a))
               (o< (m (d1 x a) (a1 x a))
                   (m x a)))

      [3mMeasure Conjecture 1,2[0m
      (implies (and (test1 x a) (test2 x a))
               (o< (m (d2 x a) (a2 x a))
                   (m x a)))

      [3mMeasure Conjecture 2,1[0m
      (implies (and (test1 x a) (not (test2 x a)))
               (o< (m (d3 x a) (a3 x a))
                   (m x a)))

  Suppose we want to prove [47m(p x a)[0m, by induction according to the
  scheme ``suggested'' by [47m(f x a)[0m.  Here is the scheme:

      [3mBase Case[0m                               ; for tip 3
      (implies (not (test1 x a))
               (p x a))

      [3mInduction Step 1[0m                        ; for tip 1
      (implies (and (test1 x a)
                    (test2 x a)
                    (p (d1 x a) (a1 x a))    ;   for rec call 1,1
                    (p (d2 x a) (a2 x a)))   ;   for rec call 1,2
               (p x a))

      [3mInduction Step 2[0m                        ; for tip 2
      (implies (and (test1 x a)
                    (not (test2 x a))
                    (p (d3 x a) (a3 x a)))   ;   for rec call 2,1
               (p x a))

  This induction is produced by the following parameter choices in the
  Induction Principle:

      [3m\\psi[0m	|  [47m(p x a)[0m	|
      [3mm[0m	|  [47m(m x a)[0m	|
      [3mq_1[0m	|  [47m(and (test1 x a) (test2 x a))[0m	|
      [3mq_2[0m	|  [47m(and (test1 x a) (not (test2 x a)))[0m	|
      [3m\\sigma_{1,1}[0m	|  [47m{x <-- (d1 x a), a <-- (a1 x a)}[0m	|
      [3m\\sigma_{1,2}[0m	|  [47m{x <-- (d2 x a), a <-- (a2 x a)}[0m	|
      [3m\\sigma_{2,1}[0m	|  [47m{x <-- (d3 x a), a <-- (a3 x a)}[0m	|

  It should be obvious to you how these choices are determined from
  the definition of [47mf[0m with measure [47m(m x a)[0m.  For example, [3mq_1[0m  is the
  conjunction of the tests leading to the first tip containing
  recursive calls of [47mf[0m, and the substitutions [3m\\sigma_{1,j}[0m  are the
  substitutions derived from the recursive calls in that tip.

  The measure conjectures required by the Induction Principle for this
  choice of parameters are exactly the same as the measure
  conjectures verified when the Definitional Principle was used to
  admit [47mf[0m!  That is, to use an induction suggested by an
  already-admitted recursive function, no additional measure
  conjectures have to be proved.

  (Remark on ``exactly the same'' above.  We have propositionally
  simplified the defining condition for the Base Case.  The Induction
  Principle says it is [47m(and (not [3mq_1[0m[47m) (not [3mq_2[0m[47m))[0m and the literal
  instantiation of that here would be [47m(and (not (and (test1 x a)
  (test2 x a))) (not (and (test1 x a) (not (test2 x a)))))[0m, but that
  is propositionally equivalent to [47m(not (test1 x a))[0m.)

  But why might this induction be interesting or useful for proving [47m(p
  x a)[0m?  The answer depends on [47m(p x a)[0m, of course.  But the most
  common situation is that we choose the induction scheme suggested
  by some recursive function used in the conjecture to be proved.  So
  suppose [47m(f x a)[0m occurs in [47m(p x a)[0m.  Why is the suggested induction
  likely to be helpful?  Consider the Base Case and the two Induction
  Steps.

  In the Base Case, the [47m(f x a)[0m occurring in [47m(p x a)[0m can be replaced
  by tip 3 of the definition of [47mf[0m, [47m(b x a)[0m because the test in the
  Base Case of the induction is the test leading to the non-recursive
  exit from the definition.  So [47mf[0m has been eliminated from the proof
  of the Base Case.

  Now consider Induction Step 1.  The [47m(f x a)[0m occurring in [47m(p x a)[0m can
  be replaced by tip 1 of the definition of [47mf[0m, namely

      (h                         ; tip 1
       (f (d1 x a) (a1 x a))     ;   rec call 1,1
       (f (d2 x a) (a2 x a)))    ;   rec call 1,2

  because the tests in Induction Step 1 are the tests leading to tip 1
  of the definition.  But notice that the induction hypothesis
  labeled ``for rec call 1,1'' in Induction Step 1 gives us a
  hypothesis about recursive call 1,1 --- because the occurrence of
  [47m(f x a)[0m in [47m(p x a)[0m becomes an occurrence of [47m(f (d1 x a) (a1 x a))[0m
  when we apply the substitution [3m\\sigma_{1,1}[0m to it.  Similarly, the
  induction hypothesis labeled ``for rec call 1,2'' gives us a
  hypothesis about call 1,2.  Thus, the proof of Induction Step 1
  boils down to proving, ``if the two recursive calls in this tip
  have the property we're proving, then [47mh[0m of those two calls has the
  property.'' While not exactly eliminating [47mf[0m from the proof, it
  provides us with all the information that we have a right to
  suppose about [47mf[0m in this case.  Usually the proof of this step
  requires a lemma about [47mp[0m and [47mh[0m, e.g., ``if [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m have property [47mp[0m,
  then so does [47m(h a b)[0m.'' Such a lemma would eliminate [47mf[0m, and if we
  had that lemma the proof of Induction Step 1 would be done.  The
  proof of Induction Step 2 is analogous.

  Thus, we see that there may well be some heuristic value in using
  the induction suggested by [47m(f x a)[0m whenever you are trying to prove
  a property of [47m(f x a)[0m.  Occasionally it is necessary to use an
  induction suggested by a function not appearing in the conjecture,
  but when that occurs it is usually some easily recognized ``mash
  up'' of other functions appearing in the conjecture.

  [31;1mProblem 102.
  [0m Recall the previously admitted

      (defun f1 (i j)
        (if (and (natp i)
                 (natp j)
                 (< i j))
            (f1 (+ 1 i) j)
            1))

  Prove [47m(equal (f1 i j) 1)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 103.
  [0m Recall the previously admitted

      (defun f2 (x)
        (if (equal x nil)
            2
            (and (f2 (car x))
                 (f2 (cdr x)))))

  Prove [47m(equal (f2 x) 2)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 104.
  [0m Recall the previously admitted

      (defun f3 (x y)
        (if (and (endp x)
                 (endp y))
            3
            (f3 (cdr x) (cdr y))))

  Prove [47m(equal (f3 x y) 3)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 105.
  [0m Recall the previously admitted

      (defun f4 (x y q)
        (if (p x)
            (if q
                (f4 y (dn x) (not q))
                (f4 y (up x) (not q)))
            4))

  Prove [47m(equal (f4 x y q) 4)[0m.

  These simple inductive exercises drive home the point that once a
  function has been admitted (proved to terminate) then we can do
  inductions to ``unwind'' it.  Students so frequently see induction
  limited to ``[3mp(n)[0m  implies [3mp(n+1)[0m '' that it is easy to forget that
  every total recursive function gives rise to an induction that is
  appropriate for it.

  Next: More Problems (see [R-AND-I-MORE-PROBLEMS]) (or Table of
  Contents (see [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-STILL-MORE-PROBLEMS
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: Still More Problems

  The next several problems will lead you to define a tautology
  checker in ACL2 and to prove that it is sound and complete.  This
  is an excellent exercise if you are interested in theorem proving.

  [31;1mProblem 132.
  [0m An [3m[47mIF[0m[3m-expression[0m  is a cons whose car is [47mIF[0m.  A [3mquote[0m  is a cons
  whose car is [47mQUOTE[0m.  A [3mvariable[0m  is anything besides an
  [47mIF[0m-expression or a quote.  An [3mexpression[0m  is a variable, a quote,
  or an [47mIF[0m-expression.

  Note: By these definitions, any object is an expression.  But we
  typically think of [47mIF[0m-expressions as being of the form [47m(IF[0m [3ma_1[0m [3ma_2[0m
  [3ma_3[0m[47m)[0m and quotes being of the form [47m(QUOTE[0m [3ma[0m[47m)[0m.  But rather than check
  that they are of this form we will just use [47mcar[0m and [47mcdr[0m to chew
  into [47mIF[0ms and quotes to access the desired substructure, which we
  will call ``arguments'' one, two, and three.

  An [3massignment[0m  is a list of pairs, [47m([0m[3mvar[0m[47m . [0m[3mval[0m[47m)[0m, where [3mvar[0m  is a
  variable and [3mval[0m  is an arbitrary object (but is typically a
  Boolean).

  We define the [3mvalue[0m  of an expression under an assignment as
  follows.  The [3mvalue[0m  of a variable is the [3mval[0m  associated with that
  variable, or [47mnil[0m if the variable is not bound.  The [3mvalue[0m  of a
  quote is the first argument of the quote.  The value of an
  [47mIF[0m-expression depends on the value, [3mv[0m, of the first argument.  If [3mv[0m
  is [47mnil[0m, the value of the [47mIF[0m-expression is the value of its third
  argument; otherwise, it is the value of its second argument.

  Define these concepts.

  [31;1mProblem 133.
  [0m An expression is said to be in [3m[47mIF[0m[3m-normal form[0m  if no [47mIF[0m-expression
  in the expression has an [47mIF[0m-expression in its first argument.
  Thus, [47m(IF A (IF B C D) D)[0m is in [47mIF[0m-normal form, but [47m(IF (IF A B
  'NIL) C D)[0m is not.  It is possible to put an expression into
  [47mIF[0m-normal form while preserving its value, by repeatedly
  transforming it with the rule:

      (IF (IF a b c) x y) = (IF a (IF b x y) (IF c x y))

  Define the notion of [47mIF[0m-normal form and admit the function [47mnorm[0m that
  normalizes an expression while preserving its value.

  [31;1mProblem 134.
  [0m Given an expression in [47mIF[0m-normal form, define the function [47mtautp[0m
  that explores all feasible branches through the expression and
  determines that every output is non-[47mnil[0m.  We say a branch is
  [3minfeasible[0m if, in order to traverse it during evaluation under an
  assignment, the assignment would have to assign some variable both
  [47mnil[0m and non-[47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 135.
  [0m Define [47mtautology-checker[0m to recognize whether an expression has a
  non-[47mnil[0m value under all possible assignments.

  [31;1mProblem 136.
  [0m Prove that your [47mtautology-checker[0m is [3msound[0m: if it says an expression
  is a tautology, then the value of the expression is non-[47mnil[0m, under
  any assignment.

  [31;1mProblem 137.
  [0m Prove that your [47mtautology-checker[0m is [3mcomplete[0m: if it fails to
  recognize an expression, then some assignment falsifies the
  expression.

  This concludes [3mRecursion and Induction[0m.  Are you interested in
  learning how to use the ACL2 theorem prover?  (Maybe explore
  <<[introduction-to-the-theorem-prover]>>?)

