  [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-AND-I-STRUCTURAL-INDUCTION
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: Structural Induction

  [31;1mProblem 39.
  [0m Given the definition

      (defun f (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (f (cdr x))
            t))

  can you prove the theorem [47m(equal (f x) t)[0m using the logical
  machinery we have described so far?

  ACL2 supports inductive proofs.  Its Induction Principle is quite
  general and involves the notion of the ordinals and
  well-foundedness.  We use a much simpler principle for now.

  A substitution [3m\\sigma[0m  is a [3m[47mcar[0m[3m/[47mcdr[0m[3m substitution[0m  on [3mv[0m  if the
  binding (image) of [3mv[0m  under [3m\\sigma[0m  is a [47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m nest around [3mv[0m.
  The other bindings of [3m\\sigma[0m  are unrestricted.  For example,
  [3m\\sigma[0m  = {[47mx <-- (car x), y <-- (cons (cdr x) y)[0m} is a [47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m
  substitution on [47mx[0m.

  [31;1mPrinciple of Structural Induction:[0m Let [47m[3m\\psi[0m[47m[0m be the term representing
  a conjecture.  [47m[3m\\psi[0m[47m[0m may be proved by selecting an ``induction''
  variable [47mx[0m, selecting a set of [47mcar[0m/[47mcdr[0m substitutions on [47mx[0m [3m\\sigma_1[0m,
  ... , [3m\\sigma_n[0m, and by proving the following subgoals:

      [3mBase Case:[0m
      (implies (not (consp x))
               [3m\\psi[0m)

  and

      [3mInduction Step:[0m
      (implies (and (consp x)   ; [3mtest[0m
                    [3m\\psi[0m/[3m\\sigma_1[0m   ; [3minduction hypothesis 1[0m
                    .
                    .
                    .
                    [3m\\psi[0m/[3m\\sigma_n[0m)  ; [3minduction hypothesis n[0m
               [3m\\psi[0m)  ; [3minduction conclusion[0m

  Here is an example Induction Step.

      (implies (and (consp x)
                    [3m\\psi[0m/{x <-- (car x), y <-- (app x y)}
                    [3m\\psi[0m/{x <-- (cdr (cdr x)), y <-- (cons x y)}
                    [3m\\psi[0m/{x <-- (cdr (cdr x)), y <-- y})
               [3m\\psi[0m)

  Let us use structural induction to prove a theorem about [47mtree-copy[0m.
  Recall the definition.

      (defun tree-copy (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (cons (tree-copy (car x))
                  (tree-copy (cdr x)))
            x))

  [31;1mTheorem[0m [47m(equal (tree-copy x) x)[0m.

  [31;1mProof[0m.

  Name the formula above *1.

  We prove *1 by induction.  One induction scheme is suggested by this
  conjecture -- namely the one that unwinds the recursion in
  [47mtree-copy[0m.

  If we let [3m\\psi[0m  denote *1 above then the induction scheme we'll use
  is

      (and (implies (not (consp x))
                    [3m\\psi[0m)
           (implies (and (consp x)
                         [3m\\psi[0m/{x <-- (car x)}
                         [3m\\psi[0m/{x <-- (cdr x)})
                    [3m\\psi[0m)).

  When applied to the goal at hand the above induction scheme produces
  the following two nontautological subgoals.

      Subgoal *1/2
      (implies (not (consp x))
               (equal (tree-copy x) x)).

  But simplification reduces this to [47mt[0m, using the definition of
  [47mtree-copy[0m and the primitive axioms.

      Subgoal *1/1
      (implies (and (consp x)                             ; [3mhyp 1[0m
                    (equal (tree-copy (car x)) (car x))   ; [3mhyp 2[0m
                    (equal (tree-copy (cdr x)) (cdr x)))  ; [3mhyp 3[0m
               (equal (tree-copy x) x)).

  But simplification reduces this to [47mt[0m, using the definition of
  [47mtree-copy[0m and the primitive axioms.

  That completes the proof of *1.

  [31;1mQ.E.D.[0m

  Let us look more closely at the reduction of Subgoal *1/1.  Consider
  the left-hand side of the concluding equality.  Here is how it
  reduces to the right-hand side under the hypotheses.

      (tree-copy x)
      =                      [3m{def [0m[47mtree-copy[0m[3m}[0m
      (if (consp x)
          (cons (tree-copy (car x))
                (tree-copy (cdr x)))
          x)
      =                      [3m{hyp 1 and Axiom 6}[0m
      (if t
          (cons (tree-copy (car x))
                (tree-copy (cdr x)))
          x)
      =                      [3m{Axioms 2 and 1}[0m
      (cons (tree-copy (car x))
            (tree-copy (cdr x)))
      =                      [3m{hyp 2}[0m
      (cons (car x)
            (tree-copy (cdr x)))
      =                      [3m{hyp 3}[0m
      (cons (car x)
            (cdr x))
      =                      [3m{Axioms 11 and 6 and hyp 1}[0m
      x

  This proof is of a very routine nature: induct so as to unwind some
  particular function appearing in the conjecture and then use the
  axioms and definitions to simplify each case to [47mt[0m.

  The problems below refer to function symbols defined in previous
  exercises.  Try to prove them for the definitions you wrote.  But
  if you cannot, then use the definitions we use in our solutions.
  For each conjecture below that is not a theorem, show a
  counterexample and then try to write the theorem ``suggested'' by
  the conjecture.  For example, add a hypothesis that restricts some
  variable so that the conjecture holds; you may even need to
  introduce new concepts.

  [31;1mWarning:[0m Proving implications by induction is a little tricky.
  Suppose you're trying to prove [47m(implies (p x) (q x))[0m by induction
  and you choose the substitution that replaces [47mx[0m by [47m(cdr x)[0m.  So the
  Induction Step will be

    Induction Step.
    (implies (and (consp x)
                  (implies (p (cdr x)) (q (cdr x))))
             (implies (p x) (q x))).

  By propositional calculus, this is the same as

    (implies (and (consp x)
                  (implies (p (cdr x)) (q (cdr x)))
                  (p x))
             (q x)).

  Many students act like the induction hypothesis is that [47m(q (cdr x))[0m
  is true and use that to show [47m(q x)[0m is true.  But the induction
  hypothesis is actually [47m(implies (p (cdr x)) (q (cdr x)))[0m.  It tells
  us that [47m(q (cdr x))[0m is true [3mif[0m [47m(p (cdr x))[0m is true!  So it often
  happens that you will use the [47m(p x)[0m hypothesis to prove [47m(p (cdr x))[0m
  is true.  Only then can you use [47m(q (cdr x))[0m to work on [47m(q x)[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 40.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (app (app a b) c) (app a (app b c))).

  [31;1mProblem 41.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (app a nil) a)

  [31;1mProblem 42.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (mapnil (app a b)) (app (mapnil a) (mapnil b)))

  [31;1mProblem 43.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (rev (mapnil x)) (mapnil (rev x)))

  [31;1mProblem 44.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (rev (rev x)) x)

  [31;1mProblem 45.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (swap-tree (swap-tree x)) x)

  [31;1mProblem 46.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (mem e (app a b)) (or (mem e a) (mem e b)))

  [31;1mProblem 47.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (mem e (int a b)) (and (mem e a) (mem e b)))

  [31;1mProblem 48.
  [0m Prove

      (sub a a)

  [31;1mProblem 49.
  [0m Prove

      (implies (and (sub a b)
                    (sub b c))
               (sub a c))

  [31;1mProblem 50.
  [0m Prove

      (sub (app a a) a)

  [31;1mProblem 51.
  [0m Define

      (defun mapnil1 (x a)
         (if (consp x)
             (mapnil1 (cdr x) (cons nil a))
             a))

  Formalize and then prove the remark ``On lists of [47mnil[0ms, [47mmapnil1[0m is
  commutative.''

  [31;1mProblem 52.
  [0m Define [47m(perm x y)[0m so that it returns [47mt[0m if lists [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are
  permutations of each other; otherwise it returns [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 53.
  [0m Prove

      (perm x x)

  [31;1mProblem 54.
  [0m Prove

      (implies (perm x y) (perm y x)).

  [31;1mProblem 55.
  [0m Prove

      (implies (and (perm x y)
                    (perm y z))
               (perm x z))

  For several of the problems below it is necessary to have a total
  ordering relation.  Let [47m<<=[0m be a non-strict total order, i.e., a
  Boolean function that enjoys the following properties:

      (and (<<= x x)                 ; Reflexive
           (implies (and (<<= x y)   ; Anti-symmetric
                         (<<= y x))
                    (equal x y))
           (implies (and (<<= x y)   ; Transitive
                         (<<= y z))
                    (<<= x z))
           (or (<<= x y)             ; Total
               (<<= y x)))

  Actually, there is such a function in ACL2 and it is called
  [47mlexorder[0m.  But we use the more suggestive name ``[47m<<=[0m'' here.  On
  the integers, [47m<<=[0m is just [47m<=[0m, but it orders all ACL2 objects.

  [31;1mProblem 56.
  [0m Define [47m(ordered x)[0m so that it returns [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m according to whether
  each pair of adjacent elements of [47mx[0m are in the relation [47m<<=[0m.  For
  example, [47m(ordered '(1 3 3 7 12))[0m would evaluate to [47mt[0m and [47m(ordered
  '(1 3 7 3 12))[0m would evaluate to [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mProblem 57.
  [0m Define [47m(isort x)[0m to take an arbitrary list and return an [47mordered[0m
  permutation of it.

  [31;1mProblem 58.
  [0m Prove

      (ordered (isort x)).

  [31;1mProblem 59.
  [0m Prove

      (perm (isort x) x).

  [31;1mProblem 60.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (isort (rev (isort x)))
             (isort x)).

  We thank Pete Manolios for suggesting this problem.

  [31;1mProblem 61.
  [0m Define

      (defun rev1 (x a)
        (if (consp x)
            (rev1 (cdr x) (cons (car x) a))
            a))

  Prove

      (equal (rev1 x nil) (rev x))

  [31;1mProblem 62.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (mapnil1 x nil) (mapnil x))

  [31;1mProblem 63.
  [0m Prove

      (not (equal x (cons x y)))

  [31;1mProblem 64.
  [0m Define

      (defun mcflatten (x a)
        (if (consp x)
            (mcflatten (car x)
                       (mcflatten (cdr x) a))
            (cons x a)))

  Prove

      (equal (mcflatten x nil) (flatten x))

