 (R-AND-I-SYMBOLS
  (R-AND-I-DATA-TYPES)
  "Recursion and Induction: Symbols

  Unlike many languages, Lisp provides symbols as primitive data
  objects.  Some example symbols are [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m, [47mLOAD[0m, [47mSTORE[0m, [47mICONST_0[0m,
  [47mprime-factors[0m, [47m++[0m, and [47mfile77[0m.  For the purposes of this document,
  we will treat symbols as atomic objects, though it is actually
  possible to construct and deconstruct them in terms of strings.

  For the purposes of this document, a symbol is a sequence of
  alphabetic characters, digits, and/or certain signs (specifically,
  [47m+[0m, [47m-[0m, [47m*[0m, [47m/[0m, [47m=[0m, [47m<[0m, [47m>[0m, [47m?[0m, [47m![0m, [47m$[0m, [47m&[0m, and [47m_[0m (underscore)) that cannot be
  read as a number.  Case is unimportant.  Symbols are parsed to have
  the greatest length possible under the rules above.  Thus, [47mxy[0m is
  one symbol, not two symbols ([47mx[0m and [47my[0m) written without intervening
  whitespace!

  Note that [47mt[0m and [47mT[0m are different ways to write the same symbol, as
  are [47mnil[0m, [47mNil[0m, and [47mNIL[0m.  [47mT[0m and [47mnil[0m are called the [3mBoolean symbols[0m.
  [47mT[0m is frequently used to denote [3mtrue[0m and [47mnil[0m is used to denote
  [3mfalse[0m.  For reasons that will become apparent, [47mnil[0m is also used as
  the [3mempty list[0m.

  (Maybe explore <<[symbols]>>?)

  Next: Pairs (see [R-AND-I-PAIRS]) (or Table of Contents (see
  [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
