 (R-AND-I-TERMS-AS-FORMULAS
  (RECURSION-AND-INDUCTION)
  "Recursion and Induction: Terms as Formulas

  Logicians typically make a careful distinction between terms (whose
  values range over objects in the domain, like the integers, etc.)
  and formulas (whose values range over the truthvalues).  We have
  set up two systems of propositional calculus.  At the level of
  formulas we have the traditional equality relation, =, and the
  logical operators &, \\/, ~, -->, and <->.  At the level of terms,
  we have the primitive function [47mequal[0m and the defined propositional
  functions [47mand[0m, [47mor[0m, [47mnot[0m, [47mimplies[0m, and [47miff[0m.  In our term-level
  propositional calculus, [47mt[0m and [47mnil[0m play the role of truthvalues.
  Because terms can be written entirely with ASCII symbols (and
  easily entered on a keyboard!) we tend to write terms and use them
  as formulas.

  For example, we might say that

      (implies (and (consp x)
                    (not (consp y)))
               (not (equal x y)))

  is a theorem, or even say

      ((consp x) & ~(consp y))
    -->
      x != y

  is a theorem.

  But of course, the former cannot be a theorem because it is a term
  and only formulas are theorems, and the latter cannot be a theorem
  because it is not even a well-formed formula (it uses some terms as
  though they were formulas).

  If we use an ACL2 term [47mp[0m as though it were a formula then the term
  should be understood as an abbreviation for the formula [47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m.
  Thus, if we say term [47mp[0m is a theorem we mean it is a theorem that [47mp[0m
  is not [47mnil[0m.

  Since we assume the reader here is familiar with programming, we
  assume this isomorphism between one syntactic category and another
  is very familiar.  If this makes sense to you, we suggest you just
  skip the rest of this section, including the problems below, and go
  to Definitions Revisited (see [R-AND-I-DEFINITIONS-REVISITED]).
  But if not, read on.

  This abuse of terminology is justified by the following theorems.

  [31;1mTheorem.[0m [47mNOT[0m is Logical Negation:
  [47m(not p)[0m != [47mnil[0m <-> ~ ([47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m).

  [31;1mProof.[0m We handle the two directions of the <->.

  Case 1.
  [47m(not p)[0m != [47mnil[0m --> ~ ([47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m).

  This is equivalent to its contrapositive:

  [47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m --> [47m(not p)[0m = [47mnil[0m.

  By the definition of [47mnot[0m and Axiom 2 and the hypothesis [47mp[0m!= [47mnil[0m,
  [47m(not p)[0m = [47m(if p nil t)[0m = [47mnil[0m.

  Case 2.
  ~ ([47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m) --> [47m(not p)[0m != [47mnil[0m.

  The hypothesis is propositionally equivalent to [47mp[0m = [47mnil[0m.  By
  substitution of equals for equals, the conclusion is [47m(not nil)[0m !=
  [47mnil[0m.  By the definition of [47mnot[0m and Axioms 3 and 1, [47m(not nil)[0m = [47m(if
  nil nil t)[0m = [47mt[0m != [47mnil[0m.

  Q.E.D.

  [31;1mProblem 18.
  [0m Prove
  [47m(and p q)[0m != [47mnil[0m <-> ([47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m) & ([47mq[0m != [47mnil[0m).

  [31;1mProblem 19.
  [0m Prove
  [47m(or p q)[0m != [47mnil[0m <-> ([47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m) \\/ ([47mq[0m != [47mnil[0m).

  [31;1mProblem 20.
  [0m Prove
  [47m(implies p q)[0m != [47mnil[0m <-> ([47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m) --> ([47mq[0m != [47mnil[0m).

  [31;1mProblem 21.
  [0m Prove
  [47m(iff p q)[0m != [47mnil[0m <-> ([47mp[0m != [47mnil[0m) <-> ([47mq[0m != [47mnil[0m).

  [31;1mProblem 22.
  [0m Prove
  [47m(equal x y)[0m != [47mnil[0m <-> ([47mx[0m = [47my[0m)

  Note that these theorems allow us to change the propositional
  functions to their logical counterparts as we move the ``!= [47mnil[0m''
  into the term.  Furthermore, we can always drop a ``!= [47mnil[0m''
  anywhere it occurs in a formula since the term with which it
  appears would then be used as a formula and would mean the same
  thing.

  [31;1mProblem 23.
  [0m Using the theorems above, prove that

      (implies (and p (implies q r))
               s)

  is equivalent to

  [47m (p & (q --> r)) --> s [0m

  which is equivalent to

       ((p & ~ q) --> s)
      &
       ((p & q & r) --> s)

  When writing proofs on paper or the board, we tend to use formulas
  and the short symbols =, &, \\/, ~, -->, <-> instead of the longer
  term notation.

  [31;1mProblem 24.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (car (if a b c)) (if a (car b) (car c)))

  that is, prove [47m (car (if a b c)) = (if a (car b) (car c)) [0m

  [31;1mProblem 25.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (if (if a b c) x y)
             (if a (if b x y) (if c x y)))

  [31;1mProblem 26.
  [0m Prove

      (equal (tree-copy (cons a b))
             (cons (tree-copy a) (tree-copy b)))

  Next: Definitions Revisited (see [R-AND-I-DEFINITIONS-REVISITED]) (or
  Table of Contents (see [R-AND-I-TABLE-OF-CONTENTS]))")
 (R-EQLABLE-ALISTP
  (ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for a true list of pairs whose [47m[cdr][0ms are suitable for
  [47m[eql][0m

  The predicate [47mr-eqlable-alistp[0m tests whether its argument is a
  [47m[true-listp][0m of [47m[consp][0m objects whose [47m[cdr][0ms all satisfy
  [47m[eqlablep][0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<r-eqlable-alistp>

    (defun r-eqlable-alistp (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (cond ((atom x) (equal x nil))
            (t (and (consp (car x))
                    (eqlablep (cdr (car x)))
                    (r-eqlable-alistp (cdr x))))))")
 (R-SYMBOL-ALISTP
  (ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for association lists with symbols as values

  [47m(R-symbol-alistp x)[0m is true if and only if [47mx[0m is a list of pairs of
  the form [47m(cons key val)[0m where [47mval[0m is a [47m[symbolp][0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<r-symbol-alistp>

    (defun r-symbol-alistp (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (cond ((atom x) (equal x nil))
            (t (and (consp (car x))
                    (symbolp (cdr (car x)))
                    (r-symbol-alistp (cdr x))))))")
 (RANDOM$
  (STATE NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Obtain a random value

    Example:
    (random$ 10 state) ==> (mv 7 <state>)

  [47m(Random$ limit state)[0m, where [47mlimit[0m is a positive integer, returns a
  random non-negative integer together with a new [47m[state][0m.
  Logically, it simply returns the first element of a list that is a
  field of the ACL2 [47m[state][0m, called the [47macl2-oracle[0m (see
  [47m[read-ACL2-oracle][0m), together with the new state resulting from
  removing that element from that list.  (Except, if that element is
  not in range as specified above, then 0 is returned.)  However,
  [47mrandom$[0m actually invokes a Common Lisp function to choose the
  integer returned.  Quoting from the Common Lisp HyperSpec(TM),
  {http://www.lispworks.com/documentation/HyperSpec/Front |
  http://www.lispworks.com/documentation/HyperSpec/Front}: ``An
  approximately uniform choice distribution is used...  each of the
  possible results occurs with (approximate) probability 1/limit.''

  Consider enabling rules [47mnatp-random$[0m and [47mrandom$-linear[0m if you want
  to reason about [47mrandom$[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<random$>

    (defun random$ (limit state)
      (declare (type (integer 1 *) limit)
               (xargs :stobjs state))
      (mv-let (erp val state)
              (read-acl2-oracle state)
        (mv (cond ((and (null erp)
                        (natp val)
                        (< val limit))
                   val)
                  (t 0))
            state)))")
 (RANDOM-REMARKS-ON-REWRITING
  (REWRITE REWRITE-QUOTED-CONSTANT)
  "Some basic facts about the ACL2 rewriter

  The ACL2 rewriter is technically part of the simplifier, but in this
  documentation we often conflate the two and say things like ``the
  conclusion simplifies to [47mT[0m'' when in fact we should say ``the
  conclusion rewrites to [47mT[0m during simplification'' In addition, both
  the simplifier and the rewriter are enormously complicated and
  contain so many heuristics that users are not often helped by
  studying the details.  For example, the rewriter takes 18 arguments
  (some of which are bundles of more rarely used arguments) and is
  part of a mutually recursive clique containing 50 functions as of
  Version 8.3, including the linear (and non-linear) arithmetic
  semi-decision procedures.  The rewriter and its clique consume
  about 6500 lines of code.

  But to understand how [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[congruence][0m, [47m:[0m[47mforward-chaining[0m and other rules behave,
  the user has to have a fairly weak model of the simplifier and
  rewriter, so we sketch that here.

  In ACL2, the goals you see printed in proof output are represented
  internally as clauses.  See [clause].

  The job of the simplifier is to simplify a clause, returning a set of
  clauses whose conjunction is propositionally equivalent to the
  input clause.  The most desirable output set is the empty set,
  because the conjunction over the empty set is [47mT[0m, which means the
  goal clause was proved.  For related discussions, see
  [hints-and-the-waterfall], where we discuss how repeated
  simplifications are performed.

  Note that if you are trying to simplify the clause [47m(lit1 lit2 ...
  litk)[0m and you manage to rewrite, say, the [47mliti[0m to [47mT[0m, then the
  clause is true.  Furthermore, when rewriting a literal of a clause
  you may assume all the other literals false because if one were
  true the clause would be true.  Finally, when you rewrite a literal
  you are justified in replacing it with one that is propositional
  equivalent, since the literals are disjoined.

  The simplifier works by first generating several data structures,
  here called the ``context,'' which codify governing assumptions
  available for the rewriting of each literal.  These data structures
  include the [type-alist] and [linear-arithmetic] data base.  They
  are built using various rules derived from previously proved
  lemmas, including [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m, and
  [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rules.  It then calls the rewriter successively on each
  literal passing it the appropriate context and stipulating that the
  result of the rewriting must be propositionally equivalent to the
  input.  The rewriter returns a suitable term.  If the result is the
  unchanged, the simplifier just moves on to the next literal.  If
  the result contains no [47mIF[0m-expressions, the simplifier just replaces
  the old literal with the new one.  But if the new term contains
  [47mIF[0m-expressions, the simplifier splits them out into a set of clause
  segments, i.e., if the literal simplifies to [47m(IF a b c)[0m, then the
  simplifier gets two new clause segments, [47m((NOT a) b)[0m and [47m(a c)[0m, and
  then it splices each segment into the goal clause where the literal
  was, producing a set of new clauses, and then resumes rewriting
  literals in each new clause.  Heuristics try to prevent excessive
  case splitting, e.g., see [case-split-limitations].

  The job of the rewriter is to take a term, some context, and an
  equivalence relation, and return a ``simpler'' term that is
  equivalent to the input term under the assumptions in the context.

  The rewriter works by exploring the term, possibly changing the
  context and the equivalence relation as appropriate for the
  subterms it rewrites.

    * If the term is a variable, the rewriter just returns it (unless the
      context tells it something interesting about that variable,
      such as that the variable is equal to [47mnil[0m).  Sometimes you
      might wish that the value of a bound variable be rewritten,
      usually because the value found on the alist was rewritten
      under a different equivalence relation than the variable is now
      being used.  See [47mdouble-rewrite[0m.

    * If the term is a quoted constant, the rewriter tries to apply
      [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m rules to that constant.

    * If the term is [47m(IF a b c)[0m, it rewrites the test, [47ma[0m, under the
      equivalence relation [47mIFF[0m to get some (possibly) new term, [47ma'[0m.
      If [47ma'[0m is [47mnil[0m or is obviously different from [47mnil[0m (like [47mT[0m), the
      rewriter replaces the [47mIF[0m-term by [47mb[0m or [47mc[0m, appropriately, and
      rewrites that.  If on the other hand, the rewriter can't decide
      whether [47ma'[0m is [47mnil[0m or not, it rewrites [47mb[0m to [47mb'[0m in an extended
      context in which [47ma'[0m is assumed non-[47mnil[0m and rewrites [47mc[0m to [47mc'[0m in
      an extended context in which [47ma'[0m is [47mnil[0m.  In rewriting both
      branches it preserves the outer equivalence relation -- the one
      to be maintained while rewriting the [47mIF[0m-term.  Then the
      rewriter returns [47m(IF a' b' c[0m').

    * If the term is a call of an arithmetic inequality, like [47m(< a b)[0m, the
      rewriter rewrites [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m and then considers whether the
      context allows it deduce the truth or falsity of the inequality
      using a [linear-arithmetic] decision procedure.  The decision
      procedure, which is complete for linear inequalities over the
      rationals, uses heuristics to decide many integer cases, to
      select the order in which inequalities are combined to
      eliminate variables, and to handle some [non-linear-arithmetic]
      problems.

    * If the term is a call of a function symbol, [47mfn[0m, on some argument
      terms, [47ma1[0m, ..., [47man[0m, the rewriter rewrites each argument, [47mai[0m to
      [47mai'[0m.  For each argument the rewriter uses known [47m:[0m[47m[congruence][0m
      rules to determine which equivalence relations can be used to
      rewrite terms in that argument position so as to maintain the
      outer equivalence.  Then, having transformed [47m(fn a1 ... an)[0m to
      [47m(fn a1' ... an[0m') the rewriter tries to apply [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules
      to that transformed term, called the ``target.''.

    * Among the rewrite rules generally available for [47m(fn a1' ... an[0m') is
      the definition of [47mfn[0m itself.  The rewriter considers
      ``expanding'' the call, i.e., replacing it by the body of [47mfn[0m
      after substituting the [47mai'[0m for the formal variables, if the
      definition is [enable]d, and then rewriting that.  But
      heuristics are used to prevent the indefinite expansion of
      recursive definitions.

  For an elementary tutorial on the application of a [47m:[0m[47mrewrite[0m rule to a
  target term, see [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-1] and
  [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-2].  Those topics also lead to
  documentation on how to design effective rules.  But just to
  summarize very briefly, we offer the following.

  [47m:Rewrite[0m rules take the general form

    (implies (and hyp1 ... hypk) (equiv lhs rhs))

  where [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation.  Recall that the
  rewriter has a target term to rewrite in some context and is
  required to return a term that is equivalent modulo a given
  equivalence relation, here called the ``outer'' equivalence.
  Furthermore, if the target term is a function application, e.g.,
  [47m(fn a1' ... an[0m'), the argument subterms have already been
  rewritten.

  If the rule in question is enabled and its [47mequiv[0m refines the outer
  equivalence, the rewriter tries to match [47mlhs[0m with the target.  By
  ``match'' we mean it tries to find a substitution for the variables
  in [47mlhs[0m that make the instantiated [47mlhs[0m identical to the target.  We
  discuss matching further below.  Second, having found a suitable
  substitution, tries to ``relieve'' the hypotheses, as discussed
  below.  If successful, that means each [47mhypi[0m, when instantiated with
  the substitution, is non-[47mnil[0m.  Third, if all the hypotheses are
  relieved, the target is ``replaced'' by the result of rewriting [47mrhs[0m
  under the substitution.  We discuss that step more below too.

  [31;1mMatching[0m: Technically, ACL2 uses a restriction of ordinary
  first-order unification -- the restriction being that only
  variables in the pattern (here the [47mlhs[0m of the rule) may be
  instantiated.  Thus, the pattern [47m(G x (H x))[0m matches [47m(G (M A B) (H
  (M A B)))[0m by binding [47mx[0m to [47m(M A B)[0m.  It does not match [47m(G (M A B) (H
  (M A A)))[0m even though those two terms ``unify'' by binding [47mx[0m to [47m(M
  A A)[0m and [47mB[0m to [47mA[0m.  Our pattern matcher is the function [47mone-way-unify[0m
  in our source code.

  However, our pattern matcher knows some things about the structure of
  quoted constants.  For example, the pattern [47m(CONS x y)[0m matches the
  quoted constant [47m'(A B C)[0m, by binding [47mx[0m to [47m'A[0m and [47my[0m to [47m'(B C)[0m.  This
  can be seen by running [47mone-way-unify[0m on those two terms:

    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(cons x y) ''(A B C))
    (T ((Y QUOTE (B C)) (X QUOTE A)))

  Note the quote marks in our input.  Since [47mone-way-unify[0m expects both
  of its arguments to be fully translated formal terms, we have to
  quote every constant we write here.  And since the arguments to
  [47mone-way-unify[0m are evaluated before the function is called, we have
  to quote the two terms.  We see that [47mone-way-unify[0m returns two
  results.  The first is [47mT[0m, meaning that a suitable substitution was
  found.  The second is the substitution.  Lisp's abbreviation
  convention for printed ``dotted pairs,'' its ``quote convention,''
  its convention of reading symbols in uppercase, and our convention
  here of writing terms (and variables) in lower case but constants
  in upper case, all conspire to make the substitution a little hard
  to read for novices.  Another way to display the exact same
  substitution is:

    ((y . '(B C)) (x . 'A))

  i.e., [47mx[0m is bound to [47m'A[0m and [47my[0m is bound to [47m(B C)[0m.

  Below are some other examples, but we've re-displayed the
  substitutions.

    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(coerce x 'string) ''\"Hello!\")
    (T ((x . (#H #e #l #l #o #!))))
    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(intern-in-package-of-symbol x y) ''ACL2::TEMP)
    (T ((y . 'TEMP) (x . '\"TEMP\")))
    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(binary-+ '3 x) ''7)
    (T ((X . '4)))
    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(unary-- x) ''-7)
    (T ((X QUOTE 7)))
    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(unary-- x) ''7)
    (NIL NIL)
    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(binary-* '2 y) ''8)
    (T ((Y QUOTE 4)))
    ACL2 !>(one-way-unify '(binary-* x y) ''8)
    (NIL NIL)

  Note that the pattern matching algorithm is incomplete on quoted
  constants!  For example, it fails to match [47m(unary-- x)[0m with [47m'7[0m even
  though [47m(unary-- '-7)[0m is [47m'7[0m.  It similarly fails to match [47m(binary-*
  x y)[0m with [47m'8[0m even though [47m(binary-* '33 '8/33)[0m is [47m8[0m; indeed, there
  are in infinite number of suitable substitutions for this case, but
  [47mone-way-unify[0m is designed to return at most one so as to limit the
  applicability of rewrite rules to ``reasonable'' cases.

  [31;1mRelieving the Hypotheses[0m: The hypotheses of a rewrite rule are
  relieved one at a time starting with the left-most one.  The basic
  strategy is just to rewrite each hypothesis, assuming the current
  context and if [47mIFF[0m equivalence relation.  If the result comes back
  non-[47mNIL[0m the hypothesis is equivalent to [47mT[0m in the current context.
  But there are various special considerations.

    * Free variables: It is possible that a hypothesis contains variables
      that are not bound by the substitution generated by the match.
      We call these ``free variables'' and the system tries to bind
      them to make the instantiated hypothesis appear among the
      contextual assumptions.  New bindings are accumulated as
      hypotheses are relieved.  It is possible to find multiple
      successful substitutions, some possibly filtered out by
      subsequent hypotheses.  See [47mfree-variables[0m and the
      documentation topics it cites.

    * Pragmas: It is possible to mark a hypothesis so that it is
      temporarily assumed true so that the rewrite rule can be
      applied.  This essentially spawns another subgoal to prove the
      hypothesis and thus brings the full power of the prover (not
      just the rewriter) to bear on the hypothesis.  See [47m[force][0m and
      [47m[case-split][0m.  It is also possible to include hypotheses that
      are logically always true but which cause the attempt to apply
      the rule to fail if a certain user-specified computation so
      indicates.  This can be used to restrict the application of a
      rule to certain syntactic situations.  See [47msyntaxp[0m.

  [31;1mReplacement[0m: Before the target is replaced by the rewritten [47mrhs[0m, a
  heuristic check is made to prevent certain trivial forms of rewrite
  loops.  See [47m[loop-stopper][0m.  Otherwise, if the hypotheses are
  relieved (or assumed), the rewriter rewrites [47mrhs[0m under the
  substitution generated by the match and the hypotheses.  It then
  makes certain heuristic checks to decide if replacing the target by
  this new term is a ``good idea'' in the opinion of the ACL2
  implementors.  The two main checks are designed to avoid runaway
  expansion of recursive functions (see [definition]), and
  introducing ``too many [47m[if][0ms'' (see [too-many-ifs].  The latter
  check is used to prevent unmanageable case explosions.  (In
  general, the number of cases rises exponentially with the number of
  [47mIF[0ms.)")
