 (REDEFINING-PROGRAMS
  (PROGRAMMING)
  "An explanation of why we restrict redefinitions

  ACL2 does not in general allow the redefinition of functions because
  logical inconsistency can result: previously stored theorems can be
  rendered invalid if the axioms defining the functions involved are
  changed.  However, to permit prototyping of both [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m and
  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode systems, ACL2 permits redefinition if the user has
  accepted logical responsibility for the consequences by setting
  [47m[ld-redefinition-action][0m to an appropriate non-[47mnil[0m value.  The
  refusal of ACL2 to support the unrestricted redefinition of
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions may appear somewhat capricious.  After
  all, what are the logical consequences of changing a definition if
  no axioms are involved?

  Three important points should be made before we discuss redefinition
  further.

  The first is that ACL2 does support redefinition (of both [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m
  and [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m functions) when [47m[ld-redefinition-action][0m is non-[47mnil[0m.

  The second is that a ``redefinition'' that does not change the mode,
  formals, guards, type declarations, stobjs, or body of a function
  is considered redundant and is permitted even when
  [47m[ld-redefinition-action][0m is [47mnil[0m.  We recognize and permit redundant
  definitions because it is not uncommon for two distinct [books] to
  share identical function definitions.  When determining whether the
  body of a function is changed by a proposed redefinition, we
  actually compare the untranslated versions of the two bodies.  See
  [term].  For example, redundancy is not recognized if the old body
  is [47m(list a b)[0m and the new body is [47m(cons a (cons b nil))[0m.  We use
  the untranslated bodies because of the difficulty of translating
  the new body in the presence of the old syntactic information,
  given the possibility that the redefinition might attempt to change
  the [signature] of the function, i.e., the number of formals, the
  number of results, or the position of single-threaded objects in
  either.

  The third important point is that a ``redefinition'' that preserves
  the formals, guard, types, stobjs, and body but changes the mode
  from [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m to [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m is permitted even when
  [47m[ld-redefinition-action][0m is [47mnil[0m.  That is what [47m[verify-termination][0m
  does.

  This note addresses the temptation to allow redefinition of
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m functions in situations other than the three described
  above.  Therefore, suppose [47m[ld-redefinition-action][0m is [47mnil[0m and
  consider the cases.

  Case 1.  Suppose the new definition attempts to change the formals or
  more generally the [signature] of the function.  Accepting such a
  redefinition would render ill-formed other [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m functions
  which call the redefined function.  Subsequent attempts to evaluate
  those callers could arbitrarily damage the Common Lisp image.
  Thus, redefinition of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m functions under these
  circumstances requires the user's active approval, as would be
  sought with [47m[ld-redefinition-action][0m [47m'(:query . :overwrite)[0m.

  Case 2.  Suppose the new definition attempts to change the body (even
  though it preserves the [signature]).  At one time we believed this
  was acceptable and ACL2 supported the quiet redefinition of
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions in this circumstance.  However, because
  such functions can be used in macros and redundancy checking is
  based on untranslated bodies, this turns out to be unsound!
  (Aside: Perhaps this is not an issue if the function takes [47m[state][0m
  or a user-defined [stobj] argument; but we do not further consider
  this distinction.)  Such redefinition is therefore now prohibited.
  We illustrate such an unsoundness below.  Let [47mfoo-thm1.lisp[0m be a
  book with the following contents.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defun p1 (x) (declare (xargs :mode :program)) (list 'if x 't 'nil))
    (defmacro p (x) (p1 x))
    (defun foo (x) (p x))
    (defthm foo-thm1 (iff (foo x) x) :rule-classes nil)

  Note that the macro form [47m(p x)[0m translates to [47m(if x t nil)[0m.  The
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m function [47mp1[0m is used to generate this translation.  The
  function [47mfoo[0m is defined so that [47m(foo x)[0m is [47m(p x)[0m and a theorem
  about [47mfoo[0m is proved, namely, that [47m(foo x)[0m is true iff [47mx[0m is true.

  Now let [47mfoo-thm2.lisp[0m be a book with the following contents.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")
    (defun p1 (x) (declare (xargs :mode :program)) (list 'if x 'nil 't))
    (defmacro p (x) (p1 x))
    (defun foo (x) (p x))
    (defthm foo-thm2 (iff (foo x) (not x)) :rule-classes nil)

  In this book, the [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m function [47mp1[0m is defined so that [47m(p x)[0m
  means just the negation of what it meant in the first book, namely,
  [47m(if x nil t)[0m.  The function [47mfoo[0m is defined identically --- more
  precisely, the [3muntranslated[0m body of [47mfoo[0m is identical in the two
  [books], but because of the difference between the two versions of
  the [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m function [47mp1[0m the axioms defining the two [47mfoo[0ms are
  different.  In the second book we prove the theorem that [47m(foo x)[0m is
  true iff [47mx[0m is nil.

  Now consider what would happen if the [signature]-preserving
  redefinition of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m functions were permitted and these two
  [books] were included.  When the second book is included the
  redefinition of [47mp1[0m would be permitted since the [signature] is
  preserved and [47mp1[0m is just a [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m.  But then when the
  redefinition of [47mfoo[0m is processed it would be considered redundant
  and thus be permitted.  The result would be a logic in which it was
  possible to prove that [47m(foo x)[0m is equivalent to both [47mx[0m and [47m(not x)[0m.
  In particular, the following sequence leads to a proof of nil:

    (include-book \"foo-thm1\")
    (include-book \"foo-thm2\")
    (thm nil :hints ((\"Goal\" :use (foo-thm1 foo-thm2))))

  It might be possible to loosen the restrictions on the redefinition
  of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m functions by allowing [signature]-preserving
  redefinition of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m functions not involved in macro
  definitions.  Alternatively, we could implement definition
  redundancy checking based on the translated bodies of functions
  (though that is quite problematic).  Barring those two changes, we
  believe it is necessary simply to impose the same restrictions on
  the redefinition of [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions as we do on [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  mode functions.")
 (REDO-FLAT
  (DEBUGGING)
  "Redo on failure of a [47m[progn][0m, [47m[encapsulate][0m, or [47m[certify-book][0m

  When one submits an [47m[encapsulate][0m, [47m[progn][0m, or [47m[certify-book][0m command
  and there is a failure, ACL2 restores its logical [world] as though
  the command had not been run.  But sometimes one would like to
  debug the failure by re-executing all sub-events that succeeded up
  to the point of failure, and then re-executing the failed
  sub-event.  Said differently, imagine that the [events] under an
  [47mencapsulate[0m, [47mprogn[0m, or [47mcertify-book[0m form were flattened into a list
  of events that were then submitted to ACL2 up to the point of
  failure.  This would put us in the state in which the original
  failed event had failed, so we could now replay that failed event
  and try modifying it, or first proving additional events, in order
  to get it admitted.

  [47mRedo-flat[0m is provided for this purpose.  Consider the following
  (rather nonsensical) example, in which the [47m[defun][0m of [47mf3[0m fails (the
  body is [47my[0m but the formal parameter list is [47m(x)[0m).

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (defun f1 (x) x)
     (encapsulate ()
                  (local (defthm hack (equal (car (cons x y)) x))))
     (encapsulate ()
                  (local (defthm hack (equal (+ x y) (+ y x)))))
     (encapsulate ()
                  (make-event '(defun f2 (x) x))
                  (progn (defthm foo (equal x x) :rule-classes nil)
                         (defun f3 (x) y)))
     (defun f4 (x) x)
     (defun f5 (x) y))

  After this attempt fails, you can evaluate the following form.

    (redo-flat)

  This will first lay down a [47m[deflabel][0m event, [47m(deflabel r)[0m, so that
  you can eventually remove your debugging work with [47m(:ubt! r)[0m.  Then
  the successful sub-events that preceded the failure will be
  executed with proofs skipped (so that this execution is fast).
  Then, the failed event will be executed.  Finally, a [47m:[0m[47m[pbt][0m command
  is executed so that you can see a summary of the events that
  executed successfully.

  You can eliminate some of the steps above by supplying keyword
  values, as follows, where those arguments are not evaluated.

    (redo-flat
     :succ  succ ; Skip the successful sub-events if val is nil.
     :fail  fail ; Skip the failed sub-event if val is nil.
     :label lab  ; Skip deflabel if lab or succ is nil, else use (deflabel lab).
     :pbt   val  ; Skip the final :pbt if val, lab, or succ is nil.
     )

  Also, you can avoid skipping proofs for the successful sub-events by
  supplying keyword [47m:succ-ld-skip-proofsp[0m with a valid value for
  [47mld-skip-proofsp[0m; see [ld-skip-proofsp].  For example, you might
  want to execute [47m(redo-flat :succ-ld-skip-proofsp nil)[0m if you use
  the [47mmust-fail[0m utility from community book [47mmake-event/eval.lisp[0m,
  since for example [47m(must-fail (thm (equal x y)))[0m normally succeeds
  but would cause an error if proofs are skipped.  For another
  example: you may wish to do proofs when re-running certain
  [47m[make-event][0m forms, for example, when the expansion is [47m(:OR expr1
  expr2)[0m and the proof initially succeeds for [47mexpr2[0m rather than [47mexpr1[0m
  (see [make-event]).

  If you prefer only to see the successful and failed sub-events,
  without any events being re-executed, you may evaluate the
  following form instead.

    (redo-flat :show t)

  For the example above, this command produces the following output.

    List of events preceding the failure:

    ((DEFUN F1 (X) X)
     (ENCAPSULATE NIL
                  (LOCAL (DEFTHM HACK (EQUAL (CAR (CONS X Y)) X))))
     (ENCAPSULATE NIL
                  (LOCAL (DEFTHM HACK (EQUAL (+ X Y) (+ Y X)))))
     (MAKE-EVENT '(DEFUN F2 (X) X))
     (DEFTHM FOO (EQUAL X X)
             :RULE-CLASSES NIL))

    Failed event:

    (DEFUN F3 (X) Y)
    ACL2 !>

  [47mRedo-flat[0m uses a scheme that should not cause spurious name conflicts
  for [47m[local][0m events.  Above, it is mentioned that events are
  ``flattened''; now we clarify this notion.  Each sub-event that
  succeeds and is an [47m[encapsulate][0m or [47m[progn][0m is left intact.  Only
  such events that fail are replaced by their component events.
  Thus, in the example above, there is no conflict between the two
  [47m[local][0m sub-events named ``[47mhack[0m,'' because these are contained in
  successful [47mencapsulate[0m sub-events, which are therefore not
  flattened.  The [47m[progn][0m and two [47m[encapsulate][0m events surrounding
  the definition of [47mf3[0m are, however, flattened, because that
  definition failed to be admitted.

  Normally, [47mredo-flat[0m will have the desired effect even if you
  interrupted a proof (with control-c).  However, [47mredo-flat[0m will not
  produce the desired result after an interrupt if you have enabled
  the debugger using [47m(set-debugger-enable t)[0m or if you have disabled
  the default ``soft'' interrupt behavior (see [abort-soft]).")
