  [47mguard-checking-on[0m to [47m:none[0m, as [47mchk-with-guard-checking-arg[0m is just
  the identity function except for causing a hard error for an
  illegal input.

  The intended use of [47mreturn-last[0m is that the second argument is
  evaluated first in a normal manner, and then the third argument is
  evaluated in an environment that may depend on the value of the
  second argument.  (For example, the macro [47m[with-prover-time-limit][0m
  macroexpands to a call of [47mreturn-last[0m with a first argument of
  [47m'WITH-PROVER-TIME-LIMIT1-RAW[0m, a second argument that evaluates to a
  numeric time limit, and a third argument that is evaluated in an
  environment where the theorem prover is restricted to avoid running
  longer than that time limit.)  Although this intended usage model
  is not strictly enforced, it is useful to keep in mind in the
  following description of how calls of [47mreturn-last[0m are handled by
  the ACL2 evaluator.

  When a form is submitted in the top-level loop, it is handled by
  ACL2's custom evaluator.  That evaluator is specified to respect
  the semantics of the expression supplied to it: briefly put, if an
  expression [47mE[0m evaluates to a value [47mV[0m, then the equality [47m(equal E
  (quote V))[0m should be a theorem.  Notice that this specification
  does not discuss the side-effects that may occur when evaluating a
  call of [47mreturn-last[0m, so we discuss that now.  Suppose that the ACL2
  evaluator encounters the call [47m(return-last 'fn expr1 expr2)[0m.  First
  it evaluates [47mexpr1[0m.  If this evaluation succeeds without error,
  then it constructs an expression of the form [47m(fn *x* ev-form)[0m,
  where *x* is a Lisp variable bound to the result of evaluating
  [47mexpr1[0m and [47mev-form[0m is a call of the evaluator for [47mexpr2[0m.  (Those who
  want implementation details are invited to look at function
  [47mev-rec-return-last[0m in ACL2 source file [47mtranslate.lisp[0m.)  There are
  exceptions if [47mfn[0m is [47mprogn[0m, [47mec-call1-raw[0m, [47mwith-guard-checking1-raw[0m,
  or [47mmbe1-raw[0m, but the main idea is the same: do a reasonable job
  emulating the behavior of a raw-Lisp call of [47mreturn-last[0m.

  The following log shows how a [47m[time$][0m call can generate a call of the
  evaluator for the last argument of [47mreturn-last[0m (argument [47mexpr2[0m,
  above).  We use [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m to show single-step macroexpansions,
  which indicate how a call of [47m[time$][0m expands to a call of
  [47mreturn-last[0m.  The implementation actually binds the Lisp variable
  [47m*RETURN-LAST-ARG3*[0m to [47mexpr2[0m before calling the ACL2 evaluator,
  [47mev-rec[0m.

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (time$ (+ 3 4))
     (TIME$1 (LIST 0 NIL NIL NIL NIL)
             (+ 3 4))
    ACL2 !>:trans1 (TIME$1 (LIST 0 NIL NIL NIL NIL)
                           (+ 3 4))
     (RETURN-LAST 'TIME$1-RAW
                  (LIST 0 NIL NIL NIL NIL)
                  (+ 3 4))
    ACL2 !>(time$ (+ 3 4))
    ; (EV-REC *RETURN-LAST-ARG3* ...) took
    ; 0.00 seconds realtime, 0.00 seconds runtime
    ; (1,120 bytes allocated).
    7
    ACL2 !>

