 (REWRITE
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make some [47m:rewrite[0m rules (possibly conditional ones)

  See [rule-classes] for a general discussion of rule classes,
  including how they are used to build rules from formulas and a
  discussion of the various keywords in a rule class description.

  This doc topic discusses the rule-class [47m:rewrite[0m.  If you want a
  general discussion of how rewriting works in ACL2 and some guidance
  on how to construct effective rewrite rules, see
  [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-1] and then see
  [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-2].  If you want a flexible,
  convenient interface to the ACL2 rewriter that can be called
  programmatically, see [rewrite$].

    Examples:

    (defthm plus-commutes                 ; Replace (+ a b) by (+ b a) provided
      (equal (+ x y) (+ y x)))            ; certain heuristics approve the
                                          ; permutation.

    (defthm plus-commutes                 ; equivalent to the above
      (equal (+ x y) (+ y x))
      :rule-classes ((:rewrite :corollary (equal (+ x y) (+ y x))
                               :loop-stopper ((x y binary-+))
                               :match-free :all)))

    (defthm append-nil                    ; Replace (append a nil) by a, if
      (implies (true-listp x)             ; (true-listp a) rewrites to t.
               (equal (append x nil) x)))

    (defthm append-nil                    ; as above, but with defaults and
      (implies (true-listp x)             ; a backchain limit
               (equal (append x nil) x))
      :rule-classes ((:rewrite :corollary (implies (true-listp x)
                                                   (equal (append x nil) x))
                               :backchain-limit-lst (3) ; or equivalently, 3
                               :match-free :all)))

    (defthm member-append                 ; Replace (member e (append b c)) by
      (implies                            ; (or (member e b) (member e c) in
       (and                               ; contexts in which propositional
        (true-listp x)                    ; equivalence is sufficient, provided
        (true-listp y))                   ; b and c are true-lists.
       (iff (member e (append x y))
            (or (member e x) (member e y)))))

    General Form:
    (and ...
         (implies (and ...hi...)
                  (implies (and ...hk...)
                           (and ...
                                (equiv lhs rhs)
                                ...)))
         ...)

  Note: One [47m:rewrite[0m rule class object might create many rewrite rules
  from the [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m formula.  To create the rules, we first
  translate the formula, expanding all macros (see [trans]) and also
  removing [guard-holders].  Next, we then flatten the [47m[and][0m and
  [47m[implies][0m structure of the formula; for example, if the hypothesis
  or conclusion is of the form [47m(and (and term1 term2) term3)[0m, then we
  replace that by the ``flat'' term [47m(and term1 term2 term3)[0m.  (The
  latter is actually an abbreviation for the right-associated term
  [47m(and term1 (and term2 term3))[0m.)  During this flattening process, we
  eliminate [47m[lambda][0ms as necessary in order to continue flattening;
  one may think of this step as simply substituting to eliminate
  [47m[let][0m, [47m[let*][0m, and [47m[mv-let][0m in order to expose more calls of
  [47mimplies[0m and [47mand[0m.  The result is a conjunction of formulas, each of
  the form

    (implies (and h1 ... hn) concl)

  where no hypothesis is a conjunction and [47mconcl[0m is neither a
  conjunction nor an implication.  If necessary, the hypothesis of
  such a conjunct may be vacuous.  We then further coerce each [47mconcl[0m
  into the form [47m(equiv lhs rhs)[0m, where we continue to eliminate
  [47mlambdas[0m until we reach this form, and then we eliminate [47mlambdas[0m
  from the first argument of [47mequiv[0m but not the second argument.  Here
  [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation.  If we do not reach an
  equivalence relation, even after eliminating [47mlambdas[0m, then we
  replace the resulting term, [47mterm[0m by [47m(iff term t)[0m, except that we
  replace [47m(not term)[0m by [47m(iff term nil)[0m.  By these steps we reduce the
  given [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m to a sequence of conjuncts, each of which is of
  the form

    (implies (and h1 ... hn)
             (equiv lhs rhs))

  where [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation and [47mlhs[0m has no [47mlambda[0ms.
  See [equivalence] for a general discussion of the introduction of
  new [equivalence] relations.  At this point, we check whether [47mlhs[0m
  and [47mrhs[0m are the same term; if so, we cause an error, since this
  rule will loop.  (But this is just a basic check; the rule could
  loop in other cases, for example if [47mrhs[0m is an instance of [47mlhs[0m; see
  [loop-stopper].)

  You can experiment by creating some rewrite rules using [47m[defthm][0m and
  then using [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m to see how the rule was stored.

  We create a [47m:rewrite[0m rule for each such conjunct, if possible, and
  otherwise cause an error.  It is possible to create a rewrite rule
  from such a conjunct provided [47mlhs[0m is not a variable, a quoted
  constant, a [47m[let][0m-expression, or a [47mlambda[0m application.  Although it
  is legal to create a rewrite rule from an [47m[if][0m-expression [47m(if tst
  tbr fbr)[0m, note that the rewriter uses the truth or falsity of the
  test, [47mtst[0m, when rewriting the true and false branches [47mtbr[0m and [47mfbr[0m,
  respectively; so rewrite rules are most often unnecessary for
  [47mif[0m-expressions.

  A [47m:rewrite[0m rule is used when any instance of the [47mlhs[0m occurs in a
  context in which the [equivalence] relation is an admissible
  [congruence] relation.  First, we find a substitution that makes
  [47mlhs[0m equal to the target term.  Then we attempt to relieve the
  instantiated hypotheses of the rule.  Hypotheses that are fully
  instantiated are relieved by recursive rewriting.  Hypotheses that
  contain ``free variables'' (variables not assigned by the unifying
  substitution) are relieved by attempting to guess a suitable
  instance so as to make the hypothesis equal to some known
  assumption in the context of the target.  If the hypotheses are
  relieved, and certain restrictions that prevent some forms of
  infinite regress are met (see [loop-stopper]), the target is
  replaced by the instantiated [47mrhs[0m, which is then recursively
  rewritten.

  ACL2's rewriting process has some optimizations, including the
  following.

    * The suggestion, above, that the rewriter looks through the goal
      [clause] for ``any instance of the [47mlhs[0m'' is not quite true.
      [47m:Rewrite[0m rules are never applied to quoted constants or any
      term inside a call of [47m[hide][0m.  If you want to rewrite a quoted
      constant use a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m rule.

    * The notion of ``a substitution that makes [47mlhs[0m equal to the target
      term'' is a bit more generous than the most straightforward
      such notion.  Suppose for example that [47mlhs[0m is [47m(f (+ 3 x))[0m and
      the target term is [47m(f (+ 3 (g y))[0m.  (Aside: ACL2 deals in
      so-called translated terms, so since [47m+[0m is a macro, [47mlhs[0m and term
      would actually be [47m(f (binary-+ '3 x))[0m and [47m(f (binary-+ '3 (g
      y)))[0m; we will ignore this distinction, but if you want more
      information, see [term].)  Then of course, that substitution
      binds [47mx[0m to [47m(g y)[0m.  But now suppose that instead the target term
      is [47m(f 10)[0m.  You may be surprised to learn that the substitution
      binding [47mx[0m to [47m7[0m makes [47m(f (+ 3 x))[0m equal to [47m(f 10)[0m; for example,
      the rewrite rule [47m(equal (f (+ x 3)) (h x)))[0m would rewrite [47m(f
      10)[0m to [47m(h 7)[0m.  This would also be the case if [47mlhs[0m were instead
      [47m(f (+ x 3))[0m.  This sort of optimization occurs when the new
      constant has [47m[ACL2-count][0m no greater than the old constant.
      There are similar optimizations for [47m*[0m, [47m/[0m, [47m-[0m, strings, symbols,
      and conses (for details see ACL2 source function
      [47mone-way-unify1-quotep-subproblems[0m).

    * When a term [47mt1[0m is rewritten to a new term [47mt2[0m, the rewriter is then
      immediately applied to [47mt2[0m.  On rare occasions you may find that
      you do not want this behavior, in which case you may wish to
      use a trick involving [47m[hide][0m; see [meta], near the end of that
      documentation.

    * When the hypotheses and right-hand side are rewritten, ACL2 does not
      really first apply the substitution and then rewrite; instead,
      as it rewrites those terms it looks up the [3malready rewritten[0m
      values of the bound variables.  Sometimes you may want those
      bindings rewritten again, e.g., because the variables occur in
      slots that admit additional equivalence relations.  See
      [double-rewrite].

  See [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-1] and see
  [introduction-to-rewrite-rules-part-2] for an extended discussion
  of how to create effective rewrite rules.


