  This topic concerns an advanced control for the ACL2 prover.

  By default, the ACL2 rewriter may swap true and false branches of a
  call of [47mIF[0m, in particular when the test is a call of [47mNOT[0m.  Attach
  the function [47mconstant-t-function-arity-0[0m to defeat this behavior.
  Attach the function [47mconstant-nil-function-arity-0[0m to restore the
  default behavior.")
 (REWRITE-LAMBDA-MODEP
  (REWRITE)
  "switch controlling rewriting of lambda objects

  [47mRewrite-lambda-modep[0m is an identity function with no purpose other
  than to serve as a switch controlling whether and how the ACL2
  prover rewrites quoted [47mlambda[0m objects in argument positions of
  [ilk] [47m:FN[0m.  Behavior is determined by the enabled status of two
  runes associated with [47mrewrite-lambda-modep[0m, the
  executable-counterpart rune and the definition rune.  See
  [rewrite-lambda-object].")
 (REWRITE-LAMBDA-OBJECT
  (REWRITE)
  "rewriting lambda objects in :FN slots

  [47m[Lambda][0m objects are quoted constants passed to [scion]s and applied
  as functions by [47m[apply$][0m.  The ACL2 rewriter can be made to replace
  the bodies of quoted [47mlambda[0m objects with (supposedly) simpler
  bodies that are [47m[ev$][0m equivalent, when the lambda objects occur in
  slots of [ilk] [47m:FN[0m.  However, there are restrictions on which
  lambda objects can be so simplified, restrictions on the techniques
  available to the rewriter during the simplification of [47mlambda[0m
  bodies, and restrictions controlling whether the simplified body
  replaces the original or is ignored.  We explain below.


When an Occurrence of a [47mlambda[0m Objects Is Eligible

  An occurrence of a quoted [47mlambda[0m object is eligible to be simplified
  provided

    * (a) it occurs in a [47m:FN[0m position of a call of a [scion],

    * (b) the [47mlambda[0m object is well-formed (see
      [47m[well-formed-lambda-objectp][0m),

    * (c) every function symbol mentioned in the body has been warranted

  However, eligible quoted [47mlambda[0m objects are not modified at all if
  the [rune] [47m(:executable-counterpart rewrite-lambda-modep)[0m is
  [disable]d.  That rune is enabled by default.

  Condition (b) implies the body of the [47mlambda[0m is in fact a well-formed
  ACL2 term (so the rewriter can explore it), every function symbol
  in it is [badge]d (so that function objects mentioned are used
  properly), that every variable symbol occurring freely in the body
  is among the formals of the [47mlambda[0m object, and together with (c)
  implies that the term ``behaves'' as expected if the appropriate
  warrant hypotheses govern this occurrence of the object.  This last
  implication means that [47m[ev$][0m of the body is equal to unquoted body
  (under a suitable assignment), which means we can replace the
  unquoted body.

  If (a) holds but either (b) or (c) fails, then a
  [47m\"rewrite-lambda-object\"[0m warning message is printed during the
  proof.  However, this message is only printed once per [47mlambda[0m
  object per proof attempt because otherwise the presence of
  ill-formed [47mlambda[0m-like objects in a conjecture will litter the
  output with repeated warnings.  You may turn these warnings off
  with [47m([0m[47m[toggle-inhibit-warning][0m [47m\"Rewrite-lambda-object\")[0m.


Restrictions During Rewriting of a [47mLambda[0m Body

  The rewriter is restricted in three ways when rewriting the bodies of
  eligible occurrences of [47mlambda[0m objects.

  First, the user can specify one of two actions the rewriter can take
  on an eligible [47mlambda[0m object occurrence (in addition to taking no
  action as controlled by the [47mrewrite-lambda-modep[0m rune mentioned
  above).  The available actions are to recursively call the rewriter
  and normalize the result, or to do syntactic cleaning only.  The
  default is recursive rewriting, with the restrictions explained in
  below.  Syntactic cleaning just eliminates declarations, guards,
  and various tags irrelevant to the logical value of the body.  We
  describe how the user specifies the action to be used in
  [rewrite-lambda-object-actions].  We give more details about these
  two kinds of [47mlambda[0m body simplification in
  [rewriting-versus-cleaning-up-lambda-objects].

  Second, warrant hypotheses in the goal [clause] are the only
  contextual information ``imported'' from the goal clause and made
  available while rewriting a [47mlambda[0m body.  That means type
  information about variables and other terms is forgotten, as are
  any linear arithmetic relationships.  The reason is simple: the
  variables in the body are in a different scope than the variables
  outside the [47mlambda[0m object.  Put another way, we do not know, in
  general, to what the [47mlambda[0m object will eventually be applied and
  so, in ACL2's untyped logic, we know nothing about its formal
  variables.  (Actually, we not only ``import'' the warrants from the
  goal clause, we import every ground hypothesis governing the [47mlambda[0m
  object's occurrence.  But practically speaking that means we only
  import warrant hypotheses.  A more sophisticated handling of
  contextual information can be imagined.  For example, if the [47mlambda[0m
  object occurs as the first argument of a [47m[loop$][0m scion, like
  [47mcollect$[0m or [47msum$[0m, then the rewriter could perhaps extract type
  information from the target and import that information.  For
  example, perhaps every element of the target is a number.  In that
  case, since we know the [47mlambda[0m object in a [47mloop$[0m scion call is only
  applied to elements of that list, we would then be allowed to
  assume the corresponding formal of the [47mlambda[0m object is a number.
  But that more sophisticated handling of contextual information has
  not been implemented.)

  Third, recursive functions are never opened when rewriting [47mlambda[0m
  bodies.  For example, if [47m(len (cons e x))[0m occurs in a [47mlambda[0m body,
  you might expect it to be simplified to [47m(+ 1 (len x))[0m, because that
  is what generally happens to that term when occurrences outside
  [47mlambda[0m objects are rewritten.  ACL2 normally controls the expansion
  of recursive functions by reference to terms that already occur
  within the current goal.  But the [47mlambda[0m object effectively shares
  no variables with the surrounding goal and those heuristics are
  inapplicable.  Preliminary experiments with allowing expansions of
  recursive functions inside [47mlambda[0m objects have produced
  unsatisfactory results such as runaway expansions.  So at the
  moment we allow no recursive expansions.

  The ACL2 implementors hope to address both of the above problems in
  eventual future releases.

  Syntactic cleaning, in contrast to the restricted rewriting just
  described, eliminates declarations, guards, and other
  compiler-related tags introduced by translation of [47mlambda$[0m and
  [47mloop$[0m.  It does beta reduction, which eliminates local variable
  names (other than the formals of the [47mlambda[0m object).  And it
  replaces the last argument of calls of [47m[do$][0m by [47mnil[0m if that
  argument is a quoted constant other than [47mnil[0m.  (This argument is
  irrelevant to the value of the [47mdo$[0m term and only used in error
  reporting.)


What Happens After Rewriting a [47mLambda[0m Body

  After the specified action on the body, [3mb[0m, of [47m(lambda([0m[3mv1...vn[0m[47m)[0m[3mb[0m[47m)[0m to
  produce [3mb'[0m the decision must be made as to whether to return the
  [47mlambda[0m with the rewritten body, [47m(lambda([0m[3mv1...vn[0m[47m)[0m[3mb'[0m[47m)[0m, or to ignore
  the rewrite and return the original (unrewritten) object.  ACL2
  ignores the rewrite and returns the original [47mlambda[0m object if any
  of the following three cases obtains:

    * (a) [3mb'[0m contains variables other than the [47mlambda[0m formals [3mv1,...,vn[0m,

    * (b) [3mb'[0m is not tame, or

    * (c) some function symbol appearing in [3mb'[0m has no warrant hypothesis in
      the goal clause but forcing is disabled (see [47m[force][0m).

  In all cases, the rewriter prints a [47m\"rewrite-lambda-object\"[0m warning
  when the rewritten body is different from the original one but the
  rewrite is rejected.  The warning message displays the before and
  after [47mlambda[0m objects, lists the rewrite rules used, and explains
  which of the three conditions above was violated.  However, this
  message is only printed once per [47mlambda[0m object per proof attempt
  because otherwise the presence of a [47mlambda[0m object that rewrites
  inappropriately will litter the output with repeated warnings.

  Condition (a) can arise if a rewrite rule introduces a free variable;
  disabling that rewrite rule is recommended.  Condition (b) can
  arise if some rewrite rule introduces a function symbol that has
  not been warranted; disabling that rule can often solve that
  problem but perhaps a better response is to use [47m[defwarrant][0m to
  issue a warrant for the offending function symbol and then supply
  that warrant as a hypothesis to the goal; the latter response is
  perhaps better because it means all the ``usual'' rewriting is
  done, normalizing terms as expected.  Condition (c) can be
  addressed by using [47mdefwarrant[0m to issue a warrant for the offending
  function symbol and enabling forcing (see [47m[force][0m).

  The warning message noted above can become annoying.  You may turn
  these warnings off with [47m([0m[47m[toggle-inhibit-warning][0m
  [47m\"Rewrite-lambda-object\")[0m.

  Be advised that if the [47m\"rewrite-lambda-object\"[0m warning has been
  inhibited (by you or some book included in your session) and then,
  when looking at, say, the checkpoints in a failed proof, you see a
  [47mlambda[0m object that you expected to be simplified but was not, you
  may think rewriting was not attempted for it, for some reason, when
  in fact it was attempted but rejected.  You can
  [47m(toggle-inhibit-warning \"Rewrite-lambda-object\")[0m and replay the
  proof attempt to see (all) the rejected [47mlambda[0m object rewrites.


A Possible Confusion

  A metafunction that is included in the book [47mprojects/apply/top[0m can
  also cause quoted [47mlambda[0m objects to be rewritten.  This
  metafunction, called [47mrelink-fancy-scion[0m, is called on certain calls
  of the fancy [47mloop$[0m scions, e.g., calls of [47m[always$+][0m, [47m[collect$+][0m,
  [47m[sum$+][0m, etc.  The goal of that metafunction is to keep the list of
  ``global variables'' in some normal form.

  For example,consider the term

    (collect$+ (quote
                (lambda (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
                  (cons (car loop$-gvars)
                        (cons (car (cdr loop$-gvars))
                              (cons (car loop$-ivars) 'nil)))))
               (list a b)
               target)

  which is (essentially) the translation of [47m(loop$ for e in target
  collect (list a b e))[0m.  Suppose that in some case of a proof about
  this term the hypothesis [47m(equal a b)[0m governs this term.  The term
  would thus become

    (collect$+ (quote
                (lambda (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
                  (cons (car loop$-gvars)
                        (cons (car (cdr loop$-gvars))
                              (cons (car loop$-ivars) 'nil)))))
               (list a a)
               target)

  [47mRelink-fancy-scion[0m will rewrite that term to

    (collect$+ (quote
                (lambda (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
                  (cons (car loop$-gvars)
                        (cons (car loop$-gvars)
                              (cons (car loop$-ivars) 'nil)))))
               (list a)
               target)

  which eliminates the duplicate entry in the list of globals and, as a
  necessary side effect, also replaces the body of the lambda object
  to eliminate the term [47m(car (cdr loop$-gvars))[0m.

  The application of the metafunction [47mrelink-fancy-scion[0m can easily but
  mistakenly be attributed to the rewriting of [47mlambda[0m objects but it
  is not!  The metafunction is applied to the whole [47mcollect$+[0m term
  (and calls of every other fancy scion), not just the [47mlambda[0m object.

  If you want to avoid this normalization of the globals, disable the
  [rune] [47m(:meta relink-fancy-scion-correct)[0m.")
 (REWRITE-LAMBDA-OBJECT-ACTIONS
  (REWRITE)
  "actions available when rewriting lambda objects

  As explained in [rewrite-lambda-object] the rewriter can be applied
  to eligible occurrences of quoted [47mlambda[0m objects.  That
  documentation also describes eligibility, what happens during, and
  what happens after the specified action, with a focus on
  recursively rewriting the [47mlambda[0m body.  This topic discusses how to
  specify the desired action.

  The user can specify one of three actions by manipulating the enabled
  status of the [rune] [47m(:executable-counterpart rewrite-lambda-modep)[0m
  and the enabled status of [47m(:definition rewrite-lambda-modep)[0m.
  Recall that such runes can be abbreviated [47m(:e rewrite-lambda-modep)[0m
  and [47m(:d rewrite-lambda-modep)[0m.  But in this discussion, we'll
  abbreviate them still further by [3me[0m and [3md[0m, respectively.

  The actions available on the body of an eligible [47mlambda[0m object
  occurrence are

    * rewrite: [3me[0m and [3md[0m enabled

    * syntactically clean: [3me[0m enabled and [3md[0m disabled

    * hands-off (no action): [3me[0m disabled

  Since the action is determined by the enabled status of runes, it can
  be specified by the user by appropriate global [47m[in-theory][0m events
  or by goal-specific, local [47m:in-theory[0m [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m.  There are three
  0-ary macros that expand into appropriate theories [3mprovided[0m the
  only runes you wish to affect are our so-called [3me[0m and [3md[0m.

  The three macros are [47m[rewrite-lambda-objects-theory][0m,
  [47m[syntactically-clean-lambda-objects-theory][0m, and
  [47m[hands-off-lambda-objects-theory][0m, with the obvious meanings.  For
  example, [47m(syntactically-clean-lambda-objects-theory)[0m macroexpands
  to

    (e/d ((:executable-counterpart rewrite-lambda-modep))   ; enable ``e''
         ((:definition rewrite-lambda-modep)))              ; disable ``d''

  Since [3me[0m and [3md[0m are both initially enabled in ACL2, eligible [47mlambda[0m
  objects are rewritten by default unless you change the status of [3me[0m
  and/or [3md[0m.

  For example, to make the prover just syntactically clean eligible
  [47mlambda[0m objects in [47mSubgoal 3[0m of some proof attempt you could provide
  the [47m:hint[0m

      (\"Subgoal 3\"
        :in-theory (syntactically-clean-lambda-objects-theory))

  Note however that if you also need to adjust the status of some other
  rune in that same hint you must create a theory expression that
  includes the appropriate use of [3me[0m and [3md[0m.  For example, to also
  enable [47mLEMMA1[0m but disable [47mLEMMA2[0m while specifying use of syntactic
  cleaning you could write:

    (\"Subgoal 3\"
     :in-theory (e/d ((:executable-counterpart rewrite-lambda-modep)
                      LEMMA1)
                     ((:definition rewrite-lambda-modep)
                      LEMMA2)))

  For details on what syntactic cleaning is and why it is sometimes
  useful, see [rewriting-versus-cleaning-up-lambda-objects].")
 (REWRITE-LAMBDA-OBJECTS-THEORY
  (THEORIES THEORY-FUNCTIONS REWRITE)
  "how to specify rewriting of lambda objects

  The enabled status of two [47mrewrite-lambda-modep[0m runes are used as
  flags to determine the action taken when eligible [47mlambda[0m objects
  are encountered by the ACL2 rewriter.  See [rewrite-lambda-object]
  and [47m[rewrite-lambda-object-actions][0m.  To make the rewriter dive
  into the body of an eligible [47mlambda[0m object, both
  [47m(:executable-counterpart rewrite-lambda-modep)[0m and [47m(:definition
  rewrite-lambda-modep)[0m must be enabled in the then-current theory.
  The 0-ary function [47mrewrite-lambda-objects-theory[0m returns such a
  theory.

  In fact, diving into eligible quoted [47mlambda[0m object constants to
  rewrite the body is the default action when ACL2 starts up.  See
  [rewriting-versus-cleaning-up-lambda-objects] for why you might
  want to change the default action when eligible [47mlambda[0m objects are
  encountered by the rewriter.

  The expression [47m(rewrite-lambda-objects-theory)[0m macroexpands to the
  theory expression

    (e/d ((:executable-counterpart rewrite-lambda-modep)
          (:definition rewrite-lambda-modep))
         nil)

  which is a theory equal to the current theory except that the
  executable-counterpart rune and the definition rune of
  [47mrewrite-lambda-modep[0m are enabled.  This expansion is suitable for
  use in an [47m[in-theory][0m event or [47m:in-theory[0m hint (see [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m).

  Both these two runes are initially enabled, so eligible [47mlambda[0m object
  bodies are rewritten by default until and unless some event (e.g.,
  an [47m[in-theory][0m or [47m[include-book][0m) or a superior local subgoal hint
  changes the status of those runes.

  For example, if [47mlambda[0m object rewriting has been disabled globally
  and you wish to enable it for [47mSubgoal 3[0m of some proof, you could
  use the [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m

    (\"Subgoal 3\"
     :in-theory (rewrite-lambda-objects-theory))

  Note that if you also wish to enable or disable other runes in the
  same subgoal you must construct an appropriate theory.

  For example, if in [47mSubgoal 3[0m of some proof you wanted to enable
  [47mLEMMA1[0m and disable [47mLEMMA2[0m in a theory that will also allow
  rewriting of [47mlambda[0m objects, you might write

    (\"Subgoal 3\"
     :in-theory (set-difference-theories
                   (union-theories (rewrite-lambda-objects-theory)
                                   '(LEMMA1))
                   '(LEMMA2)))

  Some users might prefer

    (\"Subgoal 3\"
     :in-theory (e/d ((:executable-counterpart rewrite-lambda-modep)
                      (:definition rewrite-lambda-modep)
                      LEMMA1)
                     (LEMMA2)))

  See [theories] for general information about theories and how to
  create and use them.")
 (REWRITE-QUOTED-CONSTANT
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make a rule to rewrite a quoted constant

  See [rule-classes] for a general discussion of rule classes,
  including how they are used to build rules from formulas and a
  discussion of the various keywords in a rule class description.

  Note: It is helpful to know some basic facts about the ACL2 rewriter;
  see [random-remarks-on-rewriting].

    Example Forms:
    (defthm lambda-id
      (fn-equal '(lambda (x) x) 'identity)
      :rule-classes :rewrite-quoted-constant)

    (defthm set-normalizer
      (set-equalp (drop-duplicates-and-sort x) x)
      :rule-classes :rewrite-quoted-constant)

    (defthm lambda-id-generalized
      (implies (symbolp v)
               (fn-equal (list 'lambda (list v) v)
                         'identity))
      :rule-classes :rewrite-quoted-constant)

  To be accepted with rule-class [47m:rewrite-quoted-constant[0m a conjecture
  must have one of the following three

    General Forms:

    (IMPLIES hyps (equiv 'const1 'const2))        ; [1]

    (IMPLIES hyps (equiv (fn var) var))           ; [2]

    (IMPLIES hyps (equiv (constructor ...) rhs))  ; [3]

  where

    * [47mhyps[0m is a term (typically a conjunction) and may include [47m[force][0m and
      [47m[syntaxp][0m hypotheses with the usual semantics,

    * [47mequiv[0m is a known [equivalence] relation and, in the case of [47m[1][0m and
      [47m[2][0m, [47mequiv[0m is not [47mEQUAL[0m,

    * [47mvar[0m, in form [47m[2][0m, is a variable symbol, and

    * [47mconstructor[0m, in form [47m[3][0m, is an explicit value ``constructor''
      function symbol listed in [47m*one-way-unify1-implicit-fns*[0m.  The
      list just mentioned includes [47mcons[0m, which can be used to match
      non-empty list constants, [47mcoerce[0m, which can match string
      constants, [47mintern-in-package-of-symbol[0m, which can match symbol
      constants, and [47mbinary-+[0m and certain other arithmetic primitives
      which can match numeric constants.  See
      [random-remarks-on-rewriting] for some examples.

  If a rule meets the criteria for both a form [47m[2][0m rule and a form [47m[3][0m
  rule, then it is considered to be a form [47m[2][0m rule.

  The function, [47mfn[0m, in a form [47m[2][0m rule is called the ``normalizer.'' We
  explain this terminology as we discuss how such rules are used.

  The [47m:rewrite-quoted-constant[0m rule-class is permitted to specify a
  [47m:corollary[0m and/or a [47m:loop-stopper[0m, with the usual syntax and
  meaning.  See [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m.  Note that for forms [47m[1][0m and [47m[2][0m
  above a [47m:[0m[47mloop-stopper[0m is probably irrelevant as the forms do not
  usually contain multiple variable symbols.

  Once a [47m:rewrite-quoted-constant[0m rule is proved and stored, the
  rewriter behaves as follows.  When a quoted constant is encountered
  as the target term to be rewritten, the rewriter considers each
  enabled [47m:rewrite-quoted-constant[0m rule (most-recent first), looking
  for those whose [47mequiv[0m refines (see [47m:[0m[47m[refinement][0m) the equivalence
  relation being maintained by the rewriter for the given occurrence
  of the target.  The rewriter tries to apply each such rule in turn
  (as described below) and replaces the target by the rewritten
  result of the first applicable rule.

  A rule applies if the ``pattern'' (see below) in the conclusion
  ``matches'' the quoted constant and the [47mhyps[0m can be relieved in the
  usual sense, including the treatment of free variables, pragmatic
  directives like [47m[force][0m and [47m[syntaxp][0m, and heuristics such as those
  controlled by any [47m:[0m[47m[loop-stopper][0m options in the rule-class or
  [47m:[0m[47mrestrict[0m [hints].  If these conditions are met, the quoted
  constant is replaced by the ``result.'' But the exact meanings of
  ``pattern,'' ``match'' and ``result'', here, are a little different
  from their meanings for ordinary [47m:rewrite[0m rules and depend on which
  of the three forms is being applied.

    * A form [47m[1][0m rule, whose conclusion is [47m(equiv 'const1 'const2)[0m, has
      [47m'const1[0m as its pattern and that pattern matches the target
      constant [47m'const[0m only when [47mconst1[0m is exactly [47mconst[0m.  The result
      is [47m'const2[0m.  Note that it is impossible to prove a form [47m[1][0m
      rule whose equivalence is [47mequal[0m unless the two constants are
      identical, so we disallow that case.

    * A form [47m[2][0m rule, whose conclusion is [47m(equiv (fn var) var)[0m, is most
      easily understood by swapping the roles of left- and right-hand
      sides of its conclusion.  Its pattern is the right-hand side,
      [47mvar[0m, and its result is derived from the left-hand side, [47m(fn
      var)[0m.  The reason we use the form shown here is so that the
      same conjecture might also be used as an ordinary [47m:rewrite[0m rule
      without forcing the user to type a [47m:corollary[0m with the sides
      swapped.  Since form [47m[2][0m rules have a variable as the pattern
      they will match any quoted constant, [47m'const[0m, by binding [47mvar[0m to
      [47m'const[0m.  The result is the quoted constant, if any, obtained by
      rewriting [47m(fn var)[0m under that binding of [47mvar[0m.  If that rewrite
      does not result in a constant, no replacement is made, i.e., it
      is as though the rule did not match.  Note that it is pointless
      to prove a form [47m[2][0m rule whose equivalence is [47mequal[0m because it
      would replace a quoted constant by itself, so we disallow that
      equivalence.
      Intuitively, [47mfn[0m is a function that normalizes every member of the
      [47mequiv[0m equivalence class: given a constant that is a member of
      the class it returns its ``normal'' form.  That is why we call
      [47mfn[0m the ``normalizer'' for [47mequiv[0m.  However, you do not have to
      prove anything like normality or canonicality; you just have to
      prove that [47mfn[0m returns a member of the class.  Having several
      different normalizers is possible but it is best if only one is
      enabled at a time.
      When [47mvar[0m is bound to a constant, the rewriter will first try to
      reduce [47m(fn var)[0m to a constant by simple evaluation.  If that
      fails, it tries using lemmas.  If the normalizer or one of its
      subfunctions is disabled or is non-executable or has a disabled
      executable counterpart the attempt to simply evaluate [47m(fn var)[0m
      (under the assignment of [47m'const[0m to [47mvar[0m) may fail.  That is why
      full-blown rewriting of [47m(fn var)[0m is tried instead.  It might
      happen that evaluation fails but lemmas produce a constant.
      [31;1mThe fact that form [47m[2][0m[31;1m rules are used ``backwards,'' with the roles
      of the left- and right-hand sides swapped, has some
      ramifications worth noting.[0m First, you can also classify the
      same formula as a [47m:rewrite[0m rule (but of course as such it won't
      rewrite quoted constants); as a [47m:rewrite[0m rule it rewrites
      instances of [47m(fn var)[0m to the corresponding instance of [47mvar[0m.
      Second, when a [47m:rewrite-quoted-constant[0m rule is added, the new
      rule is compared to old rules for subsumption and warning
      messages are generated; however form [47m[2][0m rules are omitted from
      this subsumption check because their patterns match every
      constant (in the equivalence class).  Third, if you inspect a
      form [47m[2][0m rule with [47m[pr][0m, it reports the actual syntax typed,
      e.g., the ``[47mLhs[0m'' is [47m(fn var)[0m and the ``[47mRhs[0m'' is [47mvar[0m.  But, if
      you [47m:[0m[47m[monitor][0m a form [47m[2][0m rule and then interact with the
      resulting [break-rewrite] you will see that the [47m[brr-commands][0m
      [47m:lhs[0m and [47m:rhs[0m report the swapped results.  That is, in the
      break, [47m:lhs[0m will be [47mvar[0m and [47m:rhs[0m will be [47m(fn var)[0m.  This is
      different from the display by [47mpr[0m because [47mpr[0m reports the actual
      syntax but interactive breaks report how the rule is being
      used.  We think the interactive user, out of long habit, will
      type [47m:lhs[0m to see the pattern being matched and we did not think
      it wise to add a special command just to see the pattern in a
      form [47m[2][0m rule.  Fourth, if a form [47m[2][0m rule is mentioned in the
      report by [47m[pl][0m the ``New term'' is reported to be [47m(fn 'const)[0m,
      however replacement of [47m'const[0m occurs only if [47m(fn 'const)[0m
      reduces to a quoted constant by rewriting.

    * A form [47m[3][0m rule, whose conclusion is [47m(equiv (constructor ...)  rhs)[0m,
      matches the constant [47m'const[0m provided [47m'const[0m is an instance of
      [47m(constructor ...)[0m under some variable substitution [47ms[0m.  We
      illustrate cases where quoted constants are instances of
      ``constructor'' terms below and in
      [random-remarks-on-rewriting].  Note that form [47m[3][0m rules allow
      the equivalence relation to be [47mequal[0m.  Use of [47mequal[0m here could
      cause looping rewrites if the [47mrhs[0m reduces to a constant.  But
      we allow it because, as noted below, such a rule will also
      permit you to rewrite a quoted constant into an equivalent term
      that is not a quoted constant.

  It is important to realize that form [47m[1][0m and form [47m[3][0m rules only
  apply to the top-level of a quoted constant.  We discuss this at
  length because it can cause some confusion.  The examples below are
  drawn from the community book
  [47mbooks/demos/rewrite-quoted-constant-examples[0m.  That book introduces
  [47mset-equalp[0m as an equivalence relation and proves various
  [47m:rewrite-quoted-constant[0m rules to rearrange the quoted constants
  occurring in [47mset-equalp[0m contexts.  Assume all the quoted constants
  mentioned below occur in [47mset-equalp[0m contexts.

  The form [47m[1][0m rule [47m(set-equalp 77 nil)[0m will not cause [47m'(1 2 3 . 77)[0m to
  rewrite to [47m'(1 2 3)[0m.  Because ACL2 applications frequently involve
  huge quoted list constants, we believe that making the rewriter
  explore them down to the tips would be prohibitively expensive.

  However, form [47m[2][0m rules, of the general form [47m(implies hyps (equiv (fn
  var) var))[0m, allow the normalizer to be applied to every quoted
  constant occurring in a suitable [47mequiv[0m context.  The normalizer can
  then do a root-and-branch exploration of the constant to compute
  its replacement.

  For example, the [47mrewrite-quoted-constants-examples[0m book cited above
  defines the normalizer [47m(drop-dups-and-sort var)[0m to coerce its
  argument to a [47m[true-listp][0m, eliminate duplicate elements, and sort
  the remaining elements.  It then proves the form [47m[2][0m rule
  [47m(set-equalp (drop-dups-and-sort var) var)[0m.  Now consider how this
  rule is used when the rewriter encounters [47m'(3 1 1 2 . 77)[0m in a
  [47mset-equalp[0m context.  First, [47mvar[0m is bound to [47m'const[0m.  Then, the
  rewriter tries to relieve the instantiated hypotheses of the rule,
  if any.  The example rule here has no hypotheses.  But you can
  provide some and perhaps use them to prevent calling the normalizer
  on this particular constant.  Since [47mvar[0m is bound to the constant
  [47m'const[0m such hypotheses can usually just be computed.  Supposing the
  hypotheses are relieved, the rewriter then rewrites
  [47m(drop-dups-and-sort 'const)[0m.  Under the most common circumstance,
  this just causes your normalizer function, here [47mdrop-dups-and-sort[0m,
  to execute on the given constant.  (If you have verified the guard
  of the normalizer and [47m'const[0m satisfies the guard, then the
  execution is in the underlying raw Common Lisp.)  In the most
  common circumstance, the function will return an explicit constant.
  If that returned value is indeed a constant (and different from
  [47m'const[0m), the rewriter replaces this occurrence of [47m'const[0m by the
  returned value.  Otherwise the rule does not apply.

  As noted earlier, form [47m[3][0m rules are only applied at the top-level of
  a quoted constant.  For example, the rule [47m(set-equalp (cons x y)
  (my-cons x y))[0m will not cause [47m'(1 2 3)[0m to rewrite to [47m(my-cons 1
  (my-cons 2 (my-cons 3 nil)))[0m in [47mset-equalp[0m contexts.  Instead, it
  would produce [47m(my-cons 1 '(2 3))[0m.

  But since the rewriter will eventually be called on that term, and
  since that term contains another top-level quoted constant, namely
  [47m'(2 3)[0m, you might expect the rule to eventually be used to replace
  [47m'(2 3)[0m by [47m(my-cons 2 '(3))[0m.  Indeed, that is what happens -- if you
  have proved that [47mset-equalp[0m is a [congruence] for the second
  argument of [47mmy-cons[0m, maintaining [47mset-equalp[0m.  However, for
  technical reasons explained in comments in the book
  [47mrewrite-quoted-constant-examples[0m, each rewrite occurs in a separate
  simplification step.  So turning [47m'(1 2 3)[0m into [47m(my-cons 1 (my-cons
  2 (my-cons 3 nil)))[0m requires three simplification steps.  If the
  rule in question were [47m(set-equalp (cons x y) (cons x
  (double-rewrite y)))[0m it would happen in one simplification step.
  See [47mdouble-rewrite[0m and the examples in the book
  [47mrewrite-quoted-constant-examples[0m.


Subtopics

  [Random-remarks-on-rewriting]
      Some basic facts about the ACL2 rewriter")
 (REWRITE-STACK-LIMIT
  (REWRITE)
  "Limiting the stack depth of the ACL2 rewriter

  ACL2 users can create rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m (see [rule-classes])
  that have the potential of causing an infinite loop in the ACL2
  rewriter.  This could lead to Lisp stack overflows and even
  segmentation faults.  For this reason, the depth of certain calls
  of functions in the ACL2 rewriter is limited by default using the
  value of the form [47m(rewrite-stack-limit (w state))[0m.  To see the
  limit in action, execute the following forms.

    (defthm app-assoc-1
      (equal (append (append x y) z)
             (append x y z)))
    (defthm app-assoc-2
      (equal (append x y z)
             (append (append x y) z)))
    (thm (equal (append a b c) xxx)
         ; try without these hints to see a slightly different error message
         :hints ((\"Goal\" :do-not '(preprocess))))

  The ensuing error message shows a stack of one greater than the value
  of [47m(rewrite-stack-limit (w state))[0m, which by default is the value
  of the constant [47m*default-rewrite-stack-limit*[0m.  The error message
  also explains how to use [47m:[0m[47m[brr][0m[47m t[0m and [47m([0m[47m[cw-gstack][0m[47m)[0m to find looping
  rewrite rules.

  This limit can be changed; see [set-rewrite-stack-limit].

  For a related limit, see [backchain-limit].


Subtopics

  [Set-rewrite-stack-limit]
      Sets the rewrite stack depth used by the rewriter")
 (REWRITE_RULES_ARE_GENERATED_FROM_DEFTHM_EVENTS
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "Rewrite Rules are Generated from DEFTHM Events

  {IMAGE} (see [You_Must_Think_about_the_Use_of_a_Formula_as_a_Rule])

  By reading the documentation of [47m[defthm][0m {ICON} (see
  [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) (and especially of its [47m:[0m[rule-classes]
  {ICON} (see [A_Tiny_Warning_Sign]) argument) you would learn that
  when we submitted the command

    [31;1m(defthm associativity-of-app[0m
      [31;1m(equal (app (app a b) c)[0m
             [31;1m(app a (app b c))))[0m

  we not only command the system to prove that [47mapp[0m is an associative
  function but

    * [31;1mwe commanded it to use that fact as a rewrite rule[0m.

  That means that every time the system encounters a term of the form

    (app (app [31;1mx[0m [31;1my[0m) [31;1mz[0m)

  it will replace it with

    (app [31;1mx[0m (app [31;1my[0m [31;1mz[0m))!

