 (REWRITING-CALLS-OF-APPLY$-EV$-AND-LOOP$-SCIONS
  (APPLY$)
  "How the rewriter handles [47mapply$[0m, [47mev$[0m, and [47mloop$[0m terms

  This section focuses on how ACL2 rewrites calls of [47mapply$[0m, [47mev$[0m, and
  [47mloop$[0m scions.  Our purpose is to explain the various ways the user
  can control that rewriting.  We do so by taking you through a
  particularly simple example, starting with a [47mloop$[0m expression.

  We assume that the standard [47mapply$[0m book has been included,

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system).

  and we suppose the function [47msq[0m, of one argument, has been defined and
  warranted (see [47m[defwarrant][0m.

  Recall that each [47m[loop$][0m statement is translated into a call of some
  [47mloop$[0m [scion] involving [47mlambda[0m objects formed from the iterated
  expressions in the [47mloop$[0m statement.  Thus, for example,

    (loop$ for x in (cons a (cdr (cons x b))) collect (* (sq x) 3))

  is essentially translated into

    (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                       (BINARY-* (SQ LOOP$-IVAR) '3))
               (CONS A (CDR (CONS X B)))).

  Recall that you can view the cleaned up translation with [47m:[0m[47m[tc][0m, and
  the discussion at [47m[tc][0m explains what we mean by ``cleaned up''
  here.

  So [47mloop$[0m statements just introduce [47mlambda[0m objects and scions into any
  conjecture using [47mloop$[0m.  These are rewritten like any other term in
  ACL2: the arguments are rewritten recursively and then enabled
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules (including the definition of the scion) are tried.
  Recall that ACL2 can rewrite [47mlambda[0m constants. (See
  [rewrite-lambda-object] for when that can occur and how to control
  it.)

  So now consider what happens when the rewriter encounters the
  translation of the [47mloop$[0m above.

    (collect$ '(lambda (x) (binary-* (sq x) '3))  ; note where 3 occurs
              (cons a (cdr (cons x b)))

  it will rewrite the [47mlambda[0m object and the [47mcons[0m term, effectively
  turning the [47mcollect$[0m term into

    (collect$ '(lambda (x) (binary-* '3 (sq x)))  ; note where 3 occurs now
              (cons a b))

  Then, if other rewrite rules about [47mcollect$[0m don't rewrite that, the
  the definition of [47mcollect$[0m will be tentatively tried.  By that we
  mean the body of the definition of [47mcollect$[0m is rewritten (in an
  environment binding locals to actuals) and then the rewriter
  decides heuristically whether to use the original call (with its
  rewritten arguments) or the rewritten body.  We do not discuss
  those heuristics here because they're the same for [47mloop$[0m scions as
  for all other recursive functions in ACL2.

  The logical definition of [47mcollect$[0m is

    (defun collect$ (fn lst)
      (if (endp lst)
          nil
          (cons (apply$ fn (list (car lst)))
                (collect$ fn (cdr lst))))).

  And in this case, the body will be tentatively rewritten in the
  environment where the locals [47mfn[0m and [47mlst[0m are bound as follows.

    fn:  '(lambda (x) (binary-* '3 (sq x)))
    lst: (cons a b).

  As the rewriter descends through the body of [47mcollect$[0m it eventually
  encounters the [47mapply$[0m term.  After rewriting its arguments, the
  [47mapply$[0m term effectively becomes

    (apply$ '(lambda (x) (binary-* 3 '(sq x)))
            (cons a 'nil))

  and then rules about [47mapply$[0m are tried.

  Unless you disable it, the rule [47mbeta-reduction[0m, from the standard
  [47mapply$[0m book above, will fire, transforming the above [47mapply$[0m term to

    (ev$ '(binary-* '3 (sq x))
         (cons (cons 'x a) 'nil)).

  ([47mBeta-reduction[0m is enabled by default.  If you disable it, the [47mapply$[0m
  term will not change unless you enable [47m(:definition apply$)[0m, which
  is disabled by default.)

  But we recommend leaving [47mbeta-reduction[0m enabled and thus converting
  every [47mapply$[0m of a [47mlambda[0m object into an [47mev$[0m term.  There is a
  special feature of the rewriter for simplifying certain [47mev$[0m terms.
  It is based on the fact that that (roughly speaking) the [47mev$[0m of a
  quoted tame term under an alist is the unquoted term with the alist
  applied as a substitution.  That is, the [47mev$[0m term above is equal to
  [47m(binary-* '3 (sq a))[0m.

  More precisely, if a [3mx[0m is a [tame] term and every non-primitive
  function symbol in [3mx[0m has been warranted (see [47m[defwarrant][0m) and [3ma[0m is
  a [47mcons[0m nest term representing a substitution [3msigma[0m pairing the
  variable symbols occurring freely in [3mx[0m with terms, then [47m(ev$ '[0m[3mx  a[0m[47m)[0m
  is equal to [3mx[0m/[3msigma[0m, provided the warrants of all the non-primitive
  function symbols in [3mx[0m are assumed.

  This is implemented in the rewriter so that when an [47m(ev$ 'x a)[0m is
  encountered and the following conditions hold

    * the standard [47mapply$[0m book has been included,

    * the rune [47m(:rewrite ev$-opener)[0m is enabled

    * [47mx[0m is a tame term

    * all non-primitive function symbols in [47mx[0m have been warranted (whether
      the warrants are assumed or not), and

    * [47ma[0m is a [47mcons[0m term representing an alist on variables,

  then the following three actions are taken,

    * The [47mcons[0m term [47ma[0m is converted to a substitution, [47msigma[0m, and that
      substitution is extended to a substitution, [47msigma'[0m, by pairing
      with [47mnil[0m each variable of [47mx[0m that is unbound in [47msigma[0m.  It
      should be observed that an unbound variable is assigned the
      value [47mnil[0m by the definitions of [47mev$[0m (actually, of [47massoc[0m).

    * The warrant for each non-primitive function in [47mx[0m is [force]d if
      possible unless the warrant is already assumed in the current
      context.

    * The [47m(ev$ x a)[0m term is rewritten to the result of recursively
      rewriting [47mx[0m under the substitution [47msigma'[0m.

  Note that this eliminates [47mev$[0m.

  If you see an [47mev$[0m of a quoted term in a checkpoint produced by the
  prover, you will know that one of the conditions above is not
  satisfied or that a necessary warrant could not be forced because
  forcing was disallowed or the warrant was assumed false in the
  context.

  If you would rather [47mev$[0m did not just disappear like this, disable
  [47m(:rewrite ev$-opener)[0m.  This might be desirable if the expansion
  produces a lot of cases and you can prove the theorem without
  exposing them.

  Sometimes you may wish for [47mev$[0m to expand, but to do so step-by-step,
  gradually working its way down through [47mx[0m. Such behavior can be
  achieved by disabling [47mev$-opener[0m but enabling [47m(:definition ev$)[0m,
  which is disabled by default.  However, the tentative application
  of the mutually recursive definitions of [47mev$[0m and [47mapply$[0m can be
  quite slow when dealing with moderately large [47mlambda[0m objects.
  Step-by-step expansion of [47mev$[0m can be accomplished by the
  alternative but generally faster method of disabling [47m(:rewrite
  ev$-opener)[0m, leaving [47m(:definition ev$)[0m disabled, but proving your
  own version of [47mev$-opener[0m under a different name.

    (defthm my-version-of-ev$-opener
      (and (implies (symbolp x)
                    (equal (ev$ x a) (cdr (assoc x a))))
           (equal (ev$ (list 'quote obj) a) obj)
           (implies (suitably-tamep-listp 3 nil args)
                    (equal (ev$ (cons 'if args) a)
                           (if (ev$ (car args) a)
                               (ev$ (cadr args) a)
                               (ev$ (caddr args) a))))
           (implies (and (not (eq fn 'quote))
                         (not (eq fn 'if))
                         (tamep (cons fn args)))
                    (equal (ev$ (cons fn args) a)
                           (apply$ fn (ev$-list args a)))))
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use ev$-opener))).

  By having the [47mapply$[0m book's [47mev$-opener[0m disabled you shut off the
  special feature described above, and by having your own version of
  [47mev$-opener[0m enabled you push the [47mev$[0m through the quoted term with
  rewrite rules.  This can sometimes help narrow down which of the
  conditions above is unsatisfied, especially if you modify your
  version of the rule so that the [47mtamep[0m and [47msuitably-tamep-listp[0m
  hypotheses are [47m[force][0md.")
 (REWRITING-VERSUS-CLEANING-UP-LAMBDA-OBJECTS
  (REWRITE)
  "why change the default action on rewriting [47mlambda[0m objects

  See [rewrite-lambda-object] and [47m[rewrite-lambda-object-actions][0m for
  background information on how the ACL2 rewriter behaves on certain
  quoted [47mlambda[0m objects.  In this topic we discuss why you might want
  to change that behavior.

  There are three basic actions the rewriter might take when it
  encounters a suitable [47mlambda[0m object.

    * recursively rewrite the body to get a new body,
      by rewriting in the theory described in
      [47m[rewrite-lambda-objects-theory][0m

    * just clean it up syntactically,
      by rewriting in the theory described by
      [47m[syntactically-clean-lambda-objects-theory][0m, or

    * do nothing --- leave [47mlambda[0m object constants untouched.
      by rewriting in the theory described by
      [47m[hands-off-lambda-objects-theory][0m.

  The reason we have several options has to do with the representation
  of [47mlambda[0m objects as quoted constants in ACL2's first order logic.
  Distinct [47mlambda[0m objects are unequal and yet sometimes by rewriting
  their bodies to functionally equivalent terms under [47m[ev$][0m they can
  become identical.

  For example, [47m'(lambda (x) (+ 1 x))[0m and [47m'(lambda (x) (+ x 1))[0m are
  unequal list constants.  But they are functionally equal and by
  rewriting their bodies we can make them identical.

  The problem is greatly magnified by the way [47mlambda$[0m and [47mloop$[0m
  expressions macroexpand into formal terms.  Their expansions are
  complicated with guards and tags that play key roles in their
  Common Lisp compilation and execution efficiency within the ACL2
  top-level read-eval-print loop.  But those guards and tags are
  irrelevant to their logical meanings.  By eliminating the guards
  and tags from quoted [47mlambda[0m objects in slots of [47m[ilk][0m [47m:FN[0m we
  increase the chances that different objects become identical.

  Consider the translation of a [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m statement.  The [47mthereis[0m
  [47mloop$[0m operand looks for an element of the range that satisfies a
  given predicate and returns the first non-[47mnil[0m value of that
  predicate.  The predicate is formally a [47mlambda[0m object.  Below we
  translate a [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m and show the result.

    ACL2 !>:trans (loop$ for x on a
                         thereis
                         (if (equal (car x) b) x nil))

    (RETURN-LAST
     'PROGN
     '(LOOP$ FOR X ON A
             THEREIS
             (IF (EQUAL (CAR X) B) X NIL))
     (THEREIS$+
      '(LAMBDA
        (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
        (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (IF (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                   (IF (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) '1)
                                       (IF (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                           (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) '1)
                                           'NIL)
                                       'NIL)
                                   'NIL)
                        :SPLIT-TYPES T)
                 (IGNORABLE LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS))
        (RETURN-LAST
             'PROGN
             '(LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                       (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                                   (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 1)
                                                   (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                                   (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 1))))
                       (LET ((B (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                             (X (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))
                            (DECLARE (IGNORABLE E X))
                            (IF (EQUAL (CAR X) B) X NIL)))
             ((LAMBDA (B X)
                      (IF (EQUAL (CAR X) B) X 'NIL))
              (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)
              (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))))
      (CONS B 'NIL)
      (LOOP$-AS (CONS (TAILS A) 'NIL))))

    => *

  The predicate being mapped is the quoted [47mlambda[0m object in the first
  argument of the [47mthereis$+[0m.  When shorn of the logically irrelevant
  [47mDECLARE[0m and [47mRETURN-LAST[0m forms this particular quoted [47mlambda[0m object
  becomes

    '(LAMBDA
      (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
      ((LAMBDA (B X)
               (IF (EQUAL (CAR X) B) X 'NIL))
       (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)
       (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))

  But now consider the closely related [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m which is like the
  one translated above but uses different names for the variables.

    (loop$ for y on aaa
           thereis
           (if (equal (car y) bbb) y nil))

  The [47mlambda[0m object representing the predicate in this [47mloop$[0m, when
  shorn of logically irrelevant material, is

    '(LAMBDA
      (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
      ((LAMBDA (BBB Y)
               (IF (EQUAL (CAR Y) BBB) Y 'NIL))
       (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)
       (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)))

  But note that this [47mlambda[0m object contains the ``variables'' [47mBBB[0m and [47mY[0m
  where the earlier one contained [47mB[0m and [47mX[0m.  However, they are not
  really variables!  They are symbol constants because they occur in
  quoted list constants.  Thus, the [47mlambda[0m object generated for the
  first [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m is different from the [47mlambda[0m object generated
  from the ``closely related'' one.  They are just two different list
  constants.

  Thus, the translations of those two [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0ms are not instances
  of one another.  So if you proved a rewrite rule about the [47m(loop$
  for x on ...B...)[0m and then the rewriter encountered [47m(loop$ for y on
  ...BBB...)[0m the rule would not match.  (By the way, this problem has
  nothing special to do with [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0ms.  It happens on every
  kind of [47mloop$[0m because the problem has everything to do with
  representing [47mlambda[0m expressions as list constants.)

  Fortunately, it is sound to replace the body of a [47mlambda[0m object with
  one that is equivalent under [47m[ev$][0m.  That can be done either by
  rewriting the body or by syntactically cleaning the body.  It turns
  out that both rewriting and syntactic cleaning produce the same
  result in this case and reduce these two distinct [47mlambda[0m objects to
  this one.

    '(LAMBDA
      (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
      (IF (EQUAL (CAR (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
                 (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
          (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)
          'NIL))

  Actually, all that is needed in this case is the beta reduction of
  the two bodies.

  Syntactic cleaning eliminates declarations, guards, and other
  compiler-related tags introduced by translation of [47mlambda$[0m and
  [47mloop$[0m.  It does beta reduction, which eliminates local variable
  names (other than the formals of the [47mlambda[0m object).  And it
  replaces the last argument of calls of [47m[do$][0m by [47mnil[0m if that
  argument is a quoted constant other than [47mnil[0m.  (That argument is
  irrelevant to the value of the [47mdo$[0m term and only used in error
  reporting.)  The logical semantics of [47mloop$[0m is best understood not
  by looking at its translation as we did above but by looking at the
  result of syntactically cleaning its translation.  That is done by
  the command [47m:[0m[47m[tc][0m.  [47mTc[0m translates its argument, in this case the
  [47mloop$[0m statement we've been studying, obtaining the large form shown
  above, and then syntactically cleans it, returning the internal
  representation of the logical semantics.

    ACL2 !>:tc (loop$ for x on a
                      thereis
                      (if (equal (car x) b) x nil))
    (THEREIS$+ '(LAMBDA
                 (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                 (IF (EQUAL (CAR (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
                            (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                     (CAR LOOP$-IVARS)
                     'NIL))
               (CONS B 'NIL)
               (LOOP$-AS (CONS (TAILS A) 'NIL)))

  Note that the free variables of the term are [47mA[0m and [47mB[0m.  The iteration
  variable, [47mx[0m of the [47mloop$[0m does not appear.  Instances of this term
  are formed by choosing instantiations of [47mA[0m and [47mB[0m.

  (Note: The related command [47m:[0m[47m[tcp][0m translates, cleans, and then
  converts the internal form of the term into the ``pretty''
  user-level syntax.  E.g., it converts the quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m constant
  above to a [47mlambda$[0m expression, converts the [47mIF[0m expression to an
  [47mAND[0m, and converts the [47mCONS[0m terms into [47mLIST[0m terms.  But for this
  discussion it is really better to see the internal form.  That
  [47mLAMBDA[0m object above is really a list constant!)

  [31;1mBefore a newly proved [47m:[0m[31;1m[47m[rewrite][0m[31;1m or [47m:[0m[31;1m[47m[linear][0m[31;1m rule is stored, the
  conclusion is syntactically cleaned.[0m

  Like syntactic cleaning, rewriting eliminates declarations, guards,
  and other compiler-related tags and does beta reduction --- but
  these transformations are generally carried out by rules whose
  enabled status can be altered.  Furthermore, rewriting applies
  [47m:rewrite[0m and other rules which might commute terms, open function
  definitions, etc.  We don't rewrite the conclusions of newly proved
  rules simply because the enabled rules may change from one event
  (or subgoal) to another.

  As noted earlier, there are three basic actions the rewriter might
  take when it encounters a suitable [47mlambda[0m object.

    * recursively rewrite the body to get a new body,

    * just clean it up syntactically, or

    * do nothing --- leave [47mlambda[0m object constants untouched.

  Since rules are cleaned before storage, it is almost always the case
  that you will want the prover either to rewrite [47mlambda[0m objects or
  syntactically clean them.  In simple cases, like a rule about the
  first [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m above and a conjecture about the [47msecond[0m, either
  action by the prover reduces ``closely related'' [47mlambda[0m objects to
  identical objects, making it more likely that rules will fire.

  So which of the three actions do you want the rewriter to take when
  it encounters an eligible [47mlambda[0m object?

    * rewrite the body --- best if there are multiple [47mlambda[0m objects in the
      conjecture that need to be written to be identified, and in
      simple cases rewriting is equivalent to syntactic cleaning

    * syntactic cleaning --- best if you want the [47mlambda[0m objects in the
      conjecture to match [47m:rewrite[0m or [47m:linear[0m rules containing
      closely related [47mlambda[0m objects, but you find that otherwise
      necessary [47m:rewrite[0m rules cause the rewriter to ``overshoot''
      the syntactically cleaned term as illustrated further below

    * hands off --- fairly unuseful since rules are cleaned up before
      storage.  However, one use of this is if you prove a lemma
      containing a [47mlambda[0m object but store it with [47m:rule-classes nil[0m
      and then [47m:use[0m an instance of it in the [47m:hints[0m for some
      conjecture that involves the very same [47mlambda[0m objects.

  We now illustrate some typical problems that arise when proving
  theorems about [47mloop$[0ms.  These examples are documented in the book
  [47mbooks/projects/apply/rewriting-versus-cleaning-examples[0m.

  [31;1mWhy you might want to use syntactic cleaning:[0m Suppose you prove the
  [47m:rewrite[0m rule below.  It says that a certain [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m computes
  the same answer as the function [47m[last][0m.

    (defthm loop$-can-be-last
      (implies (listp a)
               (equal (loop$ for x on a
                             thereis
                             (if (atom (cdr x)) x nil))
                      (last a))))

  What term does this rule target?  We can answer that by using the
  [47m:[0m[47m[tc][0m command.

    ACL2 !>:tc (loop$ for x on a
                      thereis
                      (if (atom (cdr x)) x nil))
     (THEREIS$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                        (IF (ATOM (CDR LOOP$-IVAR))
                            LOOP$-IVAR 'NIL))
               (TAILS A))

  By the way, a more common way to see the rules created by an event is
  to use the [47m:[0m[47m[pr][0m command.  But that command displays the terms of
  the rule in user-level syntax and we want to see the internal form
  here.  The ``[47mlambda[0m expression'' is really a quoted constant.

  Now imagine you are proving a conjecture that involves that identical
  (!)  [47mloop$[0m.  Of course, translation will replace the [47mloop$[0m by a
  rather large tagged term containing compiler directives, etc.
  Shorn of that material the term would become the [47mthereis$[0m term
  above, but the translation is what is in the initial [47mGoal[0m.  Imagine
  further that you're doing this proof in a theory that allows the
  rewriter to recursively rewrite the bodies of quoted [47mlambda[0m
  objects, i.e., you are in a theory as described by
  [47m[rewrite-lambda-objects-theory][0m.  ACL2 starts up in such a theory
  and unless you've changed the enabled status of the
  [47mrewrite-lambda-modep[0m runes rewriting [47mlambda[0m objects is the default
  behavior.

  You might expect the [47mloop$-can-be-last[0m rule to fire and replace the
  [47mthereis$[0m term in the [47mGoal[0m by [47m(last a)[0m.  But that will not happen!
  The rule won't fire!

  The reason is that before the rewriter rewrites the [47mthereis$[0m term it
  rewrites its arguments.  The quoted [47mlambda[0m object in the Goal is
  rewritten first.  That is necessary because the translated [47mloop$[0m
  contains tags, etc.  But the rewriter does more than just clean up
  the body.  It ``overshoots'' and opens the nonrecursive function
  [47matom[0m and swaps the branches of the [47mif[0m to eliminate the [47mnot[0m thus
  introduced.  The quoted [47mlambda[0m object becomes

    '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
             (IF (CONSP (CDR LOOP$-IVAR))
                 'NIL
                 LOOP$-IVAR))

  So when the rewriter then tries to rewrite the [47mthereis$[0m terms the
  quoted [47mlambda[0m object in the rule does not match the one in the
  rewritten [47mGoal[0m.

  If the rewritten goal is printed, as it is likely to be a checkpoint,
  you will see the rewritten body with the [47mconsp[0m instead of [47matom[0m in
  it.  As usual, pay attention to the checkpoints.

  One way to respond to this problem would be to ``hobble'' the
  rewriter by shifting over to syntactic cleaning of quoted [47mlambda[0m
  objects.  This could be done by providing a local subgoal hint in
  which you specify

    :in-theory (syntactically-clean-lambda-objects-theory)

  which would prevent the rewriter from diving into the bodies of
  [47mlambda[0m objects and just clean them instead.

  [31;1mWhy you should probably use rewriting:[0m There is a deeper lesson here
  than just that you might want to hobble the rewriter to prevent it
  from diving into quoted [47mlambda[0m bodies.  In our view the actual
  problem is with the [47mloop$-can-be-last[0m rule itself.  ACL2 users are
  taught to express rules in maximally rewritten terms.  No
  experienced user would pose a rule with a non-recursive function
  like [47matom[0m in its lefthand side.  The best response to this
  situation, which may or may not be practical depending on how
  [47mloop$-can-be-last[0m came to be a rule in the session, is to change
  that rule to

    (defthm loop$-can-be-last
      (implies (listp a)
               (equal (loop$ for x on a
                             thereis
                             (if (consp (cdr x)) nil x))
                      (last a))))

  This version of the rule would not only rewrite the [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m
  above but rewrites

    (loop$ for x on a
           thereis
           (if (atom (cdr x)) x nil))

  because, as illustrated above, the rewriter by default dives into the
  translated [47mloop$[0m and ``normalizes'' the resulting [47mthereis$[0m.

  Furthermore, because [47mand[0m macroexpands to an [47mif[0m-term and beta
  reduction eliminates local variable names it would rewrite

    (loop$ for rest on (aaa aa)
           thereis
           (let ((z (cdr rest)))
             (and (atom z) rest))

  [31;1mBut not all such problems can be solved by switching between
  rewriting quoted [47mlambda[0m[31;1m objects and just cleaning them up![0m

  Consider this rewrite rule.  The [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m in the lefthand side
  of the rule below is exactly the [47mloop$[0m whose translation we showed
  at the beginning of this topic.  The cleaned up internal form of
  the lefthand side is the [47mthereis$+[0m term shown by the first [47m:tc[0m
  display in this topic.  The rule below says that the [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m
  in question computes [47mmember[0m.

    (defthm loop$-can-be-member
       (equal (loop$ for x on a
                     thereis
                     (if (equal (car x) b) x nil))
              (member b a)))

  You might expect that when the rewriter encounters (an instance of)
  such a [47mloop$[0m it will replace it by a [47mmember[0m term.  That is actually
  true!

  For example, if we then tried to prove a conjecture mentioning

    (loop$ for x on aaa
           thereis
           (if (equal (car x) bbb) x nil))

  the [47mloop$-can-be-member[0m rule would fire and replace that [47mloop$[0m by
  [47m(member bbb aaa)[0m.

  But it is easy to misjudge whether a given [47mloop$[0m is an instance of
  another.

  Suppose our goal conjecture contained

    (loop$ for x on (aaa aa)
           thereis (if (equal (car x) (bbb bb)) x nil))

  This [47mloop$[0m looks like the [47mloop$[0m in [47mloop$-can-be-member[0m except we've
  used [47m(aaa aa)[0m instead of [47ma[0m, and [47m(bbb bb)[0m instead of [47mb[0m.

  Will that [47mloop$[0m be rewritten to [47m(member (bbb v) (aaa u))[0m?  No!

  To understand why not, we first have to compare the internal forms of
  the two [47mloop$[0ms.  Recall that the lefthand side of rewrite rules are
  cleaned up before storage, so the internal form of the lefthand
  side of [47mloop$-can-be-member[0m is in fact the [47mthereis$+[0m term produced
  by [47m:tc[0m above.  If we are either rewriting [47mlambda[0m objects or just
  syntactically cleaning [47mlambda[0m objects in the proof we're looking
  at, the the internal forms of the [47mloop$[0m in the conjecture will be
  just the [47m:tc[0m of that [47mloop$[0m (because there's nothing interesting to
  rewrite here).  So here is the lefthand side of the rule
  side-by-side with the rewritten target in the conjecture.  We have
  highlighted the differences in uppercase and numbered the lines
  that contain differences.

    lhs of rule                   target
    (thereis$+                    (thereis$+
     '(lambda                      '(lambda
        (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)     (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
        (if (equal                    (if (equal
             (car (car loop$-ivars))       (car (car loop$-ivars))
             (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))            (BBB (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))) ; [1]
            (car loop$-ivars)             (car loop$-ivars)
            'nil))                        'nil))
     (cons B 'nil)                 (cons BB 'nil)                   ; [2]
     (loop$-as                     (loop$-as
      (cons (tails A) 'nil)))       (cons (tails (AAA AA)) 'nil)))  ; [3]

  Note that the target is not an instance of the lefthand side of the
  rule --- but only because of the difference on line [1].  Lines [2]
  and [3] of the lefthand side can be instantiated to become those
  lines in the target.  But because of [1] our [47mloop$-can-be-member[0m
  rule will not rewrite the target shown here.

  This kind of problem cannot be fixed by fiddling with how the prover
  treats [47mlambda[0m objects.  Instead, we need to transform the target
  [47mlambda[0m object into the functionally different [47mlambda[0m object in the
  rule while simultaneously changing how [47mthereis$+[0m applies it.  In
  particular we need to transform [47mtarget[0m to [47mtarget'[0m below by moving
  the [47mBBB[0m out of [1] and into [2] as shown below.

    target                         target'
    (thereis$+                     (thereis$+
     '(lambda                       '(lambda
        (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)      (loop$-gvars loop$-ivars)
        (if (equal                     (if (equal
             (car (car loop$-ivars))        (car (car loop$-ivars))
             (BBB (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)))       (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))      ; [1]
            (car loop$-ivars)              (car loop$-ivars)
            'nil))                         'nil))
     (cons BB 'nil)                 (cons (BBB BB) 'nil)            ; [2]
     (loop$-as                      (loop$-as
      (cons (tails (aaa aa)) 'nil))) (cons (tails (aaa aa)) 'nil)))

  Note that [47mtarget'[0m is in fact an instance of the lefthand side of the
  rule.  But we've changed the semantics of the [47mlambda[0m object and
  changed the way [47mthereis$+[0m uses it by moving the [47mBBB[0m from the inside
  to the outside of the [47mlambda[0m.  No [47mlambda[0m rewriting can do this.
  Instead, we need a rule that rewrites [47mthereis$+[0m.  (In fact, a great
  project would be to implement a metafunction that does this kind of
  optimization for all [47mloop$[0m scions.  We just haven't done that yet.
  Let us know if you do!)

  Here is a suitable rewrite rule for this particular instance of this
  phenomenon.  We write it as a [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m rule rather than a
  [47mthereis$+[0m, but they're the same.

    (defthm example-of-constant-subterm-abstraction
    (implies (warrant bbb)
             (equal (loop$ for xxx on a
                           thereis
                           (if (equal (car xxx) (bbb bb)) xxx nil))
                    (let ((zzz (bbb bb)))
                      (loop$ for xxx on a
                             thereis
                             (if (equal (car xxx) zzz) xxx nil))))))

  This illustrates another lesson.  Remember that we're imagining a
  proof of some conjecture that involves a [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m mentioning
  [47mBBB[0m and wondering whether our rewrite rule [47mloop$-can-be-member[0m will
  hit it.  Had the [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m in the conjecture been written in
  the style of the [47mlet[0m expression above, where a variable is bound to
  [47m(bbb bb)[0m and then that variable used in the [47mloop$[0m body, we wouldn't
  need to move the [47m(bbb bb)[0m out.  Instead, the [47mlambda[0m generated by
  translating the [47mloop$[0m would contain a variable instead of [47m(bbb bb)[0m.
  The name of the [47mlet[0m-bound variable outside the [47mlambda[0m is irrelevant
  since it won't appear in the cleaned up [47mlambda[0m object where it is
  replaced by a component of the global variables [47mLOOP$-GVARS[0m.  In
  the containing [47mthereis$+[0m the value of that global variable will be
  [47m(bbb bb)[0m, which means the free variable [47mb[0m in our
  [47mloop$-can-be-member[0m rule can be instantiated with [47m(bbb bb)[0m to allow
  the rule to match.

  Put another way, by writing the [47mloop$[0m in the inefficient way
  (requiring [47m(bbb bb)[0m to be recomputed on every iteration) we
  implicitly produce [47mlambda[0m object that is less general than one with
  the [47m(bbb bb)[0m on the outside.

  [31;1mKeep unchanging subterms of [47mloop$[0m[31;1m bodies as variables and compute
  their values outside of the [47mlambda[0m[31;1m.[0m Basically, try to write the
  most general [47mlambda[0m objects you can.

  Finally, these difficulties are exacerbated by the ease with which
  [47mloop$[0m statements can be written and the difference between their
  appearance and the formal terms they denote.  You might be more
  successful at learning to use [47mloop$[0ms in lemmas and theorems if you
  simply [31;1mdon't use [47mloop$[0m[31;1m![0m Instead, learn to write the corresponding
  terms, e.g., try writing a [47mthereis$[0m or a [47mthereis$+[0m term instead of
  a [47mthereis[0m [47mloop$[0m statement in your lemmas and theorems.  Since the
  prover's output contains such terms (rather than [47mloop$[0m statements),
  it will be easier to see where lemmas differ from the targets they
  were intended to hit.  After enough practice you can write [47mloop$[0m
  statements with a better appreciation of what they actually denote.")
 (RFIX
  (NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Coerce to a rational number

  [47mRfix[0m simply returns any rational number argument unchanged, returning
  [47m0[0m on a non-rational argument.  Also see [nfix], see [ifix], see
  [realfix], and see [fix] for analogous functions that coerce to a
  natural number, an integer, a real, and a number, respectively.

  [47mRfix[0m has a [guard] of [47mt[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<rfix>

    (defun rfix (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (if (rationalp x) x 0))")
