 (ROUND
  (NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Division returning an integer by rounding off

    Example Forms:
    ACL2 !>(round 14 3)
    5
    ACL2 !>(round -14 3)
    -5
    ACL2 !>(round 14 -3)
    -5
    ACL2 !>(round -14 -3)
    5
    ACL2 !>(round 13 3)
    4
    ACL2 !>(round -13 3)
    -4
    ACL2 !>(round 13 -3)
    -4
    ACL2 !>(round -13 -3)
    4
    ACL2 !>(round -15 -3)
    5
    ACL2 !>(round 15 -2)
    -8

  [47m(Round i j)[0m is the result of taking the quotient of [47mi[0m and [47mj[0m and
  rounding off to the nearest integer.  When the quotient is exactly
  halfway between consecutive integers, it rounds to the even one.

  The [guard] for [47m(round i j)[0m requires that [47mi[0m and [47mj[0m are rational
  ([real], in ACL2(r)) numbers and [47mj[0m is non-zero.

  [47mRound[0m is a Common Lisp function.  See any Common Lisp documentation
  for more information.  However, note that unlike Common Lisp, the
  ACL2 [47mround[0m function returns only a single value,

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<round>

    (defun round (i j)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (real/rationalp i)
                                  (real/rationalp j)
                                  (not (eql j 0)))))
      (let ((q (* i (/ j))))
        (cond ((integerp q) q)
              ((>= q 0)
               (let* ((fl (floor q 1)) (remainder (- q fl)))
                 (cond ((> remainder 1/2) (+ fl 1))
                       ((< remainder 1/2) fl)
                       (t (cond ((integerp (* fl (/ 2))) fl)
                                (t (+ fl 1)))))))
              (t (let* ((cl (ceiling q 1))
                        (remainder (- q cl)))
                   (cond ((< (- 1/2) remainder) cl)
                         ((> (- 1/2) remainder) (+ cl -1))
                         (t (cond ((integerp (* cl (/ 2))) cl)
                                  (t (+ cl -1))))))))))")
 (RULE-CLASSES
  (ACL2)
  "Adding rules to the database

  For an introduction to rule-classes, see [rule-classes-introduction].

    Example Form (from community book finite-set-theory/total-ordering.lisp):
    (defthm <<-trichotomy
      (implies (and (ordinaryp x)
                    (ordinaryp y))
               (or (<< x y)
                   (equal x y)
                   (<< y x)))
      :rule-classes
      ((:rewrite :corollary
                 (implies (and (ordinaryp x)
                               (ordinaryp y)
                               (not (<< x y))
                               (not (equal x y)))
                          (<< y x)))))

    General Form:
    a true list of rule class objects as defined below

    Special Cases:
    a symbol abbreviating a single rule class object

  When [47m[defthm][0m is used to prove a named theorem, rules may be derived
  from the proved formula and stored in the database.  The user
  specifies which kinds of rules are to be built, by providing a list
  of rule class [3mnames[0m or, more generally, rule class [3mobjects[0m, which
  name the kind of rule to build and optionally specify various
  attributes of the desired rule.  The rule class names are
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[built-in-clause][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[compound-recognizer][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[congruence][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[equivalence][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[generalize][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[refinement][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[tau-system][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[type-set-inverter][0m, and
  [47m:well-founded-relation[0m (see [well-founded-relation-rule]).  Some
  classes [3mrequire[0m the user-specification of certain class-specific
  attributes.  Each class of rule affects the theorem prover's
  behavior in a different way, as discussed in the corresponding
  documentation topic.  In this topic we discuss the various
  attributes that may be attached to rule classes.

  Note that not all [events] generate rules.  For example, a [47m[defthm][0m
  event that specifies [47m:rule-classes nil[0m does not generate a rule.
  Similarly, a [47m[defchoose][0m event generates an axiom that can be
  referenced by name in [47m:use[0m [hints], but it does not generate a
  rule.

  A rule class object is either one of the [47m:class[0m keywords or else is a
  list of the form shown below.  Those fields marked with ``(!)'' are
  required when the [47m:class[0m is as indicated.

    (:class
      :COROLLARY term
      :TRIGGER-FNS (fn1 ... fnk)   ; provided :class = :META (!)
      :WELL-FORMEDNESS-GUARANTEE x ; provided :class = :META
                                           or :class = :CLAUSE-PROCESSOR
      :TRIGGER-TERMS (t1 ... tk)   ; provided :class = :FORWARD-CHAINING
                                   ;       or :class = :LINEAR
      :TYPE-SET n                  ; provided :class = :TYPE-SET-INVERTER
      :TYPED-TERM term             ; provided :class = :TYPE-PRESCRIPTION
      :CLIQUE (fn1 ... fnk)        ; provided :class = :DEFINITION
      :CONTROLLER-ALIST alist      ; provided :class = :DEFINITION
      :INSTALL-BODY directive      ; provided :class = :DEFINITION
      :LOOP-STOPPER alist          ; provided :class = :REWRITE
                                           or :class = :REWRITE-QUOTED-CONSTANT
      :PATTERN term                ; provided :class = :INDUCTION (!)
      :CONDITION term              ; provided :class = :INDUCTION
      :SCHEME term                 ; provided :class = :INDUCTION (!)
      :MATCH-FREE all-or-once      ; provided :class = :REWRITE
                                   ;       or :class = :LINEAR
                                   ;       or :class = :FORWARD-CHAINING
      :BACKCHAIN-LIMIT-LST limit   ; provided :class = :REWRITE
                                   ;       or :class = :META
                                   ;       or :class = :LINEAR
                                   ;       or :class = :TYPE-PRESCRIPTION
      :HINTS hints                 ; provided instrs = nil
      :INSTRUCTIONS instrs         ; provided  hints = nil
      :OTF-FLG flg)

  When rule class objects are provided by the user, most of the fields
  are optional and their values are computed in a context sensitive
  way.  When a [47m:class[0m keyword is used as a rule class object, all
  relevant fields are determined contextually.  Each rule class
  object in [47m:rule-classes[0m causes one or more rules to be added to the
  database.  The [47m:class[0m keywords are documented individually under
  the following names.  Note that when one of these names is used as
  a [47m:class[0m, it is expected to be in the keyword package (i.e., the
  names below should be preceded by a colon but the ACL2
  [documentation] facilities do not permit us to use keywords below).

  See also [47m[force][0m, [case-split], [47m[syntaxp][0m, and [47m[bind-free][0m for
  ``pragmas'' one can wrap around individual hypotheses of certain
  classes of rules to affect how the hypothesis is relieved.

  Before we get into the discussion of rule classes, let us return to
  an important point.  In spite of the large variety of rule classes
  available, at present we recommend that new ACL2 users rely almost
  exclusively on (conditional) rewrite rules.  A reasonable but
  slightly bolder approach is to use [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m and
  [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m rules for ``type-theoretic'' rules, especially
  ones whose top-level function symbol is a common one like
  [47m[true-listp][0m or [47m[consp][0m; see [type-prescription] and see
  [forward-chaining].  However, the rest of the rule classes are
  really not intended for widespread use, but rather are mainly for
  experts.

  When in doubt, create a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule, which is the default.  See
  [rule-classes-introduction].

  [47m:Rule-classes[0m is an optional keyword argument of the [47m[defthm][0m (and
  [47m[defaxiom][0m) event.  In the following, let [47mname[0m be the name of the
  event and let [47mthm[0m be the formula to be proved or added as an axiom.

  If [47m:rule-classes[0m is not specified in a [47m[defthm][0m (or [47m[defaxiom][0m)
  event, it is as though what was specified was to make one or more
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules, i.e., as though [47m:rule-classes[0m [47m((:rewrite))[0m had
  been used.  Use [47m:rule-classes[0m [47mnil[0m to specify that no rules are to
  be generated.

  If [47m:rule-classes[0m class is specified, where class is a non-[47mnil[0m symbol,
  it is as though [47m:rule-classes[0m [47m((class))[0m had been used.  Thus,
  [47m:rule-classes[0m [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m is equivalent to [47m:rule-classes[0m
  [47m((:forward-chaining))[0m.

  We therefore now consider [47m:rule-classes[0m as a true list.  If any
  element of that list is a keyword, replace it by the singleton list
  containing that keyword.  Thus, [47m:rule-classes[0m [47m(:rewrite :elim)[0m is
  the same as [47m:rule-classes[0m [47m((:rewrite) (:elim))[0m.

  Each element of the expanded value of [47m:rule-classes[0m must be a true
  list whose [47m[car][0m is one of the rule class keyword tokens listed
  above, e.g., [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[elim][0m, etc., and whose [47m[cdr][0m is a
  ``keyword alist'' alternately listing keywords and values.  The
  keywords in this alist must be taken from those shown below.  They
  may be listed in any order and most may be omitted, as specified
  below.

      [47m:[0m[47m[Corollary][0m --- its value, [47mterm[0m, must be a term.  If omitted, this
      field defaults to [47mthm[0m.  The [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m of a rule class object
      is the formula actually used to justify the rule created and
      thus determines the form of the rule.  Nqthm provided no
      similar capability: each rule was determined by [47mthm[0m, the
      theorem or axiom added.  ACL2 permits [47mthm[0m to be stated
      ``elegantly'' and then allows the [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m of a rule class
      object to specify how that elegant statement is to be
      interpreted as a rule.  For the rule class object to be
      well-formed, its (defaulted) [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m, [47mterm[0m, must follow
      from [47mthm[0m.  Unless [47mterm[0m follows trivially from [47mthm[0m using little
      more than propositional logic, the formula [47m(implies thm term)[0m
      is submitted to the theorem prover and the proof attempt must
      be successful.  During that proof attempt the values of
      [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[instructions][0m, and [47m:[0m[47m[otf-flg][0m, as provided in the
      rule class object, are provided as arguments to the prover.
      Such auxiliary proofs give the sort of output that one expects
      from the prover.  However, as noted above, corollaries that
      follow trivially are not submitted to the prover; thus, such
      corollaries cause no prover output.  Note that no rule is
      stored for the theorem until all corollaries have been proved.

      Note that before [47mterm[0m is stored, all calls of macros in it are
      expanded away.  See [trans].

      [47m:[0m[47m[Hints][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[instructions][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[otf-flg][0m --- the values of these fields
      must satisfy the same restrictions placed on the fields of the
      same names in [47m[defthm][0m.  These values are passed to the
      recursive call of the prover used to establish that the
      [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m of the rule class object follows from the theorem
      or axiom [47mthm[0m.

      [47m:[0m[47m[Type-set][0m --- this field may be supplied only if the [47m:class[0m is
      [47m:[0m[47m[type-set-inverter][0m.  When provided, the value must be a
      type-set, an integer in a certain range.  If not provided, an
      attempt is made to compute it from the corollary.  See
      [type-set-inverter].

      [47m:Typed-term[0m --- this field may be supplied only if the [47m:class[0m is
      [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m.  When provided, the value is the term for
      which the [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m is a type-prescription lemma.  If no
      [47m:typed-term[0m is provided in a [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule class
      object, we try to compute heuristically an acceptable term.
      See [type-prescription].

      [47m:Trigger-terms[0m --- this field may be supplied only if the [47m:class[0m is
      [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m.  When provided, the value is
      a list of terms, each of which is to trigger the attempted
      application of the rule.  If no [47m:trigger-terms[0m is provided, we
      attempt to compute heuristically an appropriate set of
      triggers.  See [forward-chaining] or see [linear].

      [47m:Trigger-fns[0m --- this field must (and may only) be supplied if the
      [47m:class[0m is [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m.  Its value must be a list of function
      symbols (except that a macro alias can stand in for a function
      symbol; see [add-macro-alias]).  Terms with these symbols
      trigger the application of the rule.  See [meta].

      [47m:Well-formedness-guarantee[0m --- this field may be supplied only if the
      [47m:class[0m is [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m.  Its value must be of
      one of the following forms:

        [1]  thm-name1                ; :META or :CLAUSE-PROCESSOR rules
        [2]  (thm-name1)              ; :META rules
        [3]  (thm-name1 thm-name2)    ; :META rules

      where [47mthm-name1[0m and [47mthm-name2[0m are the names of previously proved
      theorems establishing that the results of applying the
      metafunction(s) or clause-processor will be syntactically
      well-formed.  See [47m:[0m[meta] and [47m:[0m[clause-processor] for details
      of the required forms of these well-formedness theorems.  Forms
      [1] and [2] may be used for [47m:meta[0m rules where no hypothesis
      metafunction is involved.  Form [3] must be used for [47m:meta[0m
      rules with hypothesis metafunctions; that is, if you provide a
      well-formedness guarantee for a metatheorem with a hypothesis
      metafunction you must guarantee the well-formedness of both the
      metafunction (with [47mthm-name1[0m) and the hypothesis metafunction
      (with [47mthm-name2[0m).  Form [1] must be used for [47m:clause-processor[0m
      rules.  In the absence of a proper [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m
      the well-formedness of the output of a both kinds of rules is
      checked every time the rule is fired.  These checks are skipped
      when a proper [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m is provided or when
      overridden as described in [47m[set-skip-meta-termp-checks][0m.

      [47m:Clique[0m and [47m:controller-alist[0m --- these two fields may only be
      supplied if the [47m:class[0m is [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m.  If they are omitted,
      then ACL2 will attempt to guess them.  Suppose the [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m
      of the rule is [47m(implies hyp (equiv (fn a1 ... an) body))[0m.  The
      value of the [47m:clique[0m field should be a true list of function
      symbols, and if non-[47mnil[0m must include [47mfn[0m.  These symbols are all
      the members of the mutually recursive clique containing this
      definition of [47mfn[0m.  That is, a call of any function in [47m:clique[0m
      is considered a ``recursive call'' for purposes of the
      expansion heuristics.  The value of the [47m:controller-alist[0m field
      should be an alist that maps each function symbol in the
      [47m:clique[0m to a list of [47mt[0m's and [47mnil[0m's of length equal to the arity
      of the function.  For example, if [47m:clique[0m consists of just two
      symbols, [47mfn1[0m and [47mfn2[0m, of arities [47m2[0m and [47m3[0m respectively, then
      [47m((fn1 t nil) (fn2 nil t t))[0m is a legal value of
      [47m:controller-alist[0m.  The value associated with a function symbol
      in this alist is a ``mask'' specifying which argument slots of
      the function ``control'' the recursion for heuristic purposes.
      Sloppy choice of [47m:clique[0m or [47m:controller-alist[0m can result in
      infinite expansion and stack overflow.

      [47m:Install-body[0m --- this field may only be supplied if the [47m:class[0m is
      [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m.  Its value must be [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m, or the default,
      [47m:normalize[0m.  A value of [47mt[0m or [47m:normalize[0m will cause ACL2 to
      install this rule as the new body of the function being
      ``defined'' ([47mfn[0m in the paragraph just above); hence this
      definition will be installed for future [47m:expand[0m [hints].
      Furthermore, if this field is omitted or the value is
      [47m:normalize[0m, then this definition will be simplified with the
      [normalization] procedure that is used by default when
      processing definitions made with [47m[defun][0m.  You must explicitly
      specify [47m:install-body nil[0m in the following cases: the arguments
      are not a list of distinct variables, [47mequiv[0m (as above) is not
      [47m[equal][0m, or there are free variables in the hypotheses or
      right-hand side (see [free-variables]).  However, supplying
      [47m:install-body nil[0m will not affect the rewriter's application of
      the [47m:definition[0m rule, other than to avoid using the rule to
      apply [47m:expand[0m hints.  If a definition rule equates [47m(f a1 ...
      ak)[0m with [47mbody[0m but there are hypotheses, [47mhyps[0m, then [47m:expand[0m
      [hints] will replace terms [47m(f term1 ... termk)[0m by corresponding
      terms [47m(if hyps body (hide (f term1 ... termk)))[0m.

      [47m:[0m[47m[Loop-stopper][0m --- this field may only be supplied if the class is
      [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m.  Its value must be a
      list of entries each consisting of two variables followed by a
      (possibly empty) list of function symbols, for example [47m((x y
      binary-+) (u v foo bar))[0m.  It will be used to restrict
      application of rewrite rules by requiring that the list of
      instances of the second variables must be ``smaller'' than the
      list of instances of the first variables in a sense related to
      the corresponding functions listed; see [loop-stopper].  The
      list as a whole is allowed to be [47mnil[0m, indicating that no such
      restriction shall be made.  Note that any such entry that
      contains a variable not being instantiated, i.e., not occurring
      on the left side of the rewrite rule, will be ignored.
      However, for simplicity we merely require that every variable
      mentioned should appear somewhere in the corresponding
      [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m formula.

      [47m:Pattern[0m, [47m:Condition[0m, [47m:Scheme[0m --- the first and last of these fields
      must (and may only) be supplied if the class is [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m.
      [47m:Condition[0m is optional but may only be supplied if the class is
      [47m:[0m[47m[induction][0m.  The values must all be terms and indicate,
      respectively, the pattern to which a new induction scheme is to
      be attached, the condition under which the suggestion is to be
      made, and a term which suggests the new scheme.  See
      [induction].

      [47m:Match-free[0m --- this field must be [47m:all[0m or [47m:once[0m and may be supplied
      only if the [47m:class[0m is either [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, or
      [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m.  (This field is not implemented for other
      rule classes, including the [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m rule class.)
      See [free-variables] for a description of this field.  Note:
      Although this field is intended to be used for controlling
      retries of matching free variables in hypotheses, it is legal
      to supply it even if there are no such free variables.  This
      can simplify the automated generation of rules, but note that
      when [47m:match-free[0m is supplied, the warning otherwise provided
      for the presence of free variables in hypotheses will be
      suppressed.

      [47m:Backchain-limit-lst[0m --- this field may be supplied only if the
      [47m:class[0m is either [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, or
      [47m:[0m[47m[type-prescription][0m.  It is further required that either only
      one rule is generated from the formula or, at least, every such
      rule has the same list of hypotheses.  The value for
      [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m must be [47mnil[0m; a non-negative integer; or,
      except in the case of [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules, a true list each element
      of which is either [47mnil[0m or a non-negative integer.  If it is a
      list, its length must be equal to the number of hypotheses of
      the rule and each item in the list is the ``backchain limit''
      associated with the corresponding hypothesis.  If
      [47mbackchain-limit-lst[0m is a non-negative integer, it is defaulted
      to a list of the appropriate number of repetitions of that
      integer.  The backchain limit of a hypothesis is used to limit
      the effort that ACL2 will expend when relieving the hypothesis.
      If it is [47mNIL[0m, no new limits are imposed; if it is an integer,
      the hypothesis will be limited to backchaining at most that
      many times.  Note that backchaining may be further limited by a
      global [47mbackchain-limit[0m; see [backchain-limit] for details.  For
      different ways to reign in the rewriter, see
      [rewrite-stack-limit] and see [set-prover-step-limit].  Jared
      Davis has pointed out that you can set the [47m:backchain-limit-lst[0m
      to 0 to avoid any attempt to relieve [47m[force][0md hypotheses, which
      can lead to a significant speed-up in some cases.

  Once [47mthm[0m has been proved (in the case of [47m[defthm][0m) and each rule
  class object has been checked for well-formedness (which might
  require additional proofs), we consider each rule class object in
  turn to generate and add rules.  Let [47m:class[0m be the class keyword
  token of the [47mi[0mth class object (counting from left to right).
  Generate the [rune] [47m(:class name . x)[0m, where [47mx[0m is [47mnil[0m if there is
  only one class and otherwise [47mx[0m is [47mi[0m.  Then, from the [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m
  of that object, generate one or more rules, each of which has the
  name [47m(:class name . x)[0m.  See the [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m entry for each rule class
  to see how formulas determine rules.  Note that it is in principle
  possible for several rules to share the same name; it happens
  whenever a [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m determines more than one rule.  This in
  fact only occurs for [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m, and [47m:[0m[47m[forward-chaining][0m
  class rules and only then if the [47m:[0m[47m[corollary][0m is essentially a
  conjunction.  (See the documentation for [rewrite], [linear], or
  [forward-chaining] for details.)


