 (RULERS
  (DEFUN)
  "Control for ACL2's termination and induction analyses

  [31;1mIntroduction[0m

  Consider the following recursive definition, which returns a list of
  threes of length one more than the length of [47mx[0m.

    (defun f (x)
      (cons 3
            (if (consp x)
                (f (cdr x))
              nil)))

  One might expect ACL2's termination analysis to admit this function,
  since we know that [47m(cdr x)[0m is ``smaller'' than [47mx[0m if [47m(consp x)[0m is
  true.  (ACL2's notion of ``smaller'' here is essentially ordinary
  natural-number [47m<[0m, and the argument [47mx[0m is measured by applying
  function [47m[ACL2-count][0m to [47mx[0m; see [defun].)  However, by default that
  termination analysis does not consider [47m[if][0m tests, like [47m(consp x)[0m
  above, when they occur under calls of functions other than [47mIF[0m, such
  as [47mCONS[0m in the case above; it considers only rulers, as we now
  discuss.

  In the example above, we say that the term [47m(consp x)[0m [3mgoverns[0m the
  recursive call [47m(f (cdr x))[0m shown above, but does not [3mrule[0m that
  call.  Roughly speaking, the set of [3mgovernors[0m of a subterm
  occurrence in a given term consists of all superior [47mIF[0m tests and
  their negations that must be true in order for evaluation to reach
  that subterm; however, the set of [3mrulers[0m of the occurrence only
  includes, at least by default, those tests and their negations from
  the top-level [47mIF[0m structure of the term --- that is, tests and their
  negations that are collected by walking through the true and false
  branches of [47mIF[0m calls, starting at the top.  Consider for example
  the following term, where [47mfoo[0m is assumed to be a function symbol.

    (if a
        (if (if b c d) e f)
      (if g
          (foo (if h i j))
        k))

  For the occurrence of [47mc[0m in that term, the only ruler is [47ma[0m; but both [47ma[0m
  and [47mb[0m are governors.  For the occurrence of [47mi[0m, [47m(not a)[0m and [47mg[0m are
  the only rulers; but its governors are [47m(not a)[0m, [47mg[0m, and [47mh[0m.

  We have seen that for a subterm occurrence in a term, every ruler is
  a governor but not necessarily vice-versa.  It is the rulers of a
  recursive call that affect its role in the termination and
  induction analysis for a function.

  One way to overcome the discrepancy between rulers and governors is
  to ``lift'' the [47mIF[0m test to the top level.  We can apply that
  technique to the [47mdefun[0m of [47mf[0m above, so that now [47m(consp x)[0m is a ruler
  of the recursive call, and not merely a governor (though it remains
  a governor as well, of course).

    (defun f (x)
      (if (consp x)
          (cons 3 (f (cdr x)))
        (cons 3 nil)))

  But another way to overcome the problem is to tell ACL2 to extend its
  termination (and induction) analysis through calls of [47mcons[0m, as
  follows, by designating that calls of [47mcons[0m should not block the
  accumulation of rulers.

    (defun f (x)
      (declare (xargs :ruler-extenders (cons)))
      (cons 3
            (if (consp x)
                (f (cdr x))
              nil)))

  Let's look at one more (contrived) example:

    (defun g (x)
      (if (atom x)
          x
        (len (if (symbolp (car x))
                 (g (cddr x))
               (g (cdr x))))))

  There are two recursive calls, and the set of rulers is [47m{(not (atom
  x))}[0m for each of those.  The set of governors for the first
  recursive call is [47m{(not (atom x)), (symbolp (car x))}[0m, and for the
  second, [47m{(not (atom x)), (not (symbolp (car x)))}[0m.  The rulers is
  extended to be the governors in each case if we specify: [47m(declare
  (xargs :ruler-extenders (len)))[0m.

  You may even wish to provide value [47m:all[0m instead of an explicit list
  of ruler-extenders, so that no function call blocks the termination
  analysis:

    (defun f (x)
      (declare (xargs :ruler-extenders :all))
      (cons 3
            (if (consp x)
                (f (cdr x))
              nil)))

  Alternatively, you can omit the [47mXARGS[0m [47m:RULER-EXTENDERS[0m form, instead
  modifying the global default set of ruler-extenders:

    (set-ruler-extenders :all)

    ; or, for example:
    (set-ruler-extenders '(cons return-last))

  You can call the function [47m[default-ruler-extenders][0m as follows to see
  the current global default set of ruler-extenders:

    (default-ruler-extenders (w state))

  We conclude this introduction by considering the handling of [47mLET[0m
  expressions by termination analysis.  Consider the following
  example.

    (defun fact (n)
      (the (integer 1 *)
           (if (posp n)
               (* n (fact (1- n)))
             1)))

  ACL2 treats the call of [47m[the][0m in the body of this definition as
  follows.

    (let ((var (if (posp n)
                   (* n (fact (1- n)))
                 1)))
      (if (and (integerp var) (<= 1 var))
          var
        <some_error>))

  A [47m[let][0m expression, in turn, is treated as a [47m[lambda][0m application:

    ((lambda (var)
       (if (if (integerp var)
               (not (< var 1))
             nil)
           var
         <some_error>))
     (if (posp n)
         (* n (fact (1- n)))
       1))

  Notice that the [47m[posp][0m test, which governs the recursive call of
  [47mfact[0m, is inside an argument of a function application, namely the
  application of the [47mLAMBDA[0m expression.  So by default, ACL2 will not
  consider this [47m[posp][0m test in its termination analysis.  The keyword
  [47m:LAMBDAS[0m in the list of ruler-extenders denotes all calls of lambda
  expressions, much as the inclusion of [47mCONS[0m in the ruler-extenders
  denotes all calls of [47mCONS[0m.  The following definition is thus
  accepted by ACL2.

    (defun fact (n)
      (declare (xargs :ruler-extenders (:lambdas)))
      (the (integer 1 *)
           (if (posp n)
               (* n (fact (1- n)))
             1)))

  As a convenience, ACL2 allows the symbol [47m:lambdas[0m in place of
  [47m(:lambdas)[0m, and in fact the former will also include the three
  basic ruler-extenders: [47m[return-last][0m, which comes from
  macroexpansion of calls of [47m[prog2$][0m, [47m[ec-call][0m, and others;
  [47m[mv-list][0m; and [47m[if][0m, which affects termination analysis through the
  first argument of calls of [47mIF[0m (it continues through the true and
  false branches of these calls even without [47mIF[0m being among the
  ruler-extenders).

  IMPORTANT REMARKS.

   1. Notice that the argument to [47mset-ruler-extenders[0m is evaluated, but the
      argument to [47m:RULER-EXTENDERS[0m in [47mXARGS[0m is not evaluated.

   2. Do not put macro names in your list of ruler-extenders.  For example,
      if you intend that [47m+[0m should not block the termination analysis,
      in analogy to [47mcons[0m in the example above, then the list of
      ruler-extenders should include [47mbinary-+[0m, not [47m+[0m.  Of course, if
      you use [47m:all[0m then this is not an issue, but see the next
      remark.

   3. Also please note that by taking advantage of the ruler-extenders, you
      may change the induction scheme computed for the function.
      This is especially worth remembering for functions containing
      [47m[let][0m or [47m[let*][0m expressions (which translate to [47m[lambda][0m
      applications; see [term]).  If the induction scheme suggested
      by such a function seems to provide more induction hypotheses
      than appear necessary, it might help to admit the function with
      [47m:lambdas[0m included among the ruler extenders even if that is not
      necessary for the termination proof.  This can cause the
      induction scheme to have a richer case analysis with fewer
      induction hypotheses on any given induction step.  While this
      can make it more difficult for the system to merge induction
      schemes to get an appropriate induction, it can also make the
      proof of each induction step easier.  Unfortunately, we have no
      more precise advice as to exactly when adding [47m:lambdas[0m will
      help.  See [induction-coarse-v-fine-grained].

  To see the ruler-extenders of an existing function symbol, [47mfn[0m, in a
  logical [world], [47mwrld[0m, evaluate [47m(get-ruler-extenders 'fn wrld)[0m
  after [47m(include-book \"std/system/get-ruler-extenders\" :dir :system)[0m.
  For example, evaluation of [47m(get-ruler-extenders 'fn (w state))[0m
  provides the ruler-extenders of [47mfn[0m in the current logical world.

  Below we describe the notion of ruler-extenders in detail, as well as
  how to set its default using [47mset-ruler-extenders[0m.

  [31;1mDetails[0m

  We begin by discussing how to set the ruler-extenders by using the
  macro [47mset-ruler-extenders[0m; below we will discuss the use of keyword
  [47m:ruler-extenders[0m in [47m[xargs][0m [47m[declare][0m forms.  NOTE: The legal
  values discussed below for [47mset-ruler-extenders[0m are the same as the
  legal values for the [47m:ruler-extenders[0m [47m[xargs][0m keyword.

    Examples:
    (set-ruler-extenders :basic) ; return to default
    (set-ruler-extenders *basic-ruler-extenders*) ; same as immediately above
    (set-ruler-extenders :all) ; every governing IF test rules a recursive call
    (set-ruler-extenders :lambdas) ; LET does not block termination analysis
    (set-ruler-extenders (cons :lambdas *basic-ruler-extenders*))
                                   ; same as immediately above
    (set-ruler-extenders '(f g)) ; termination analysis goes past calls of f, g

    General Form:
    (set-ruler-extenders val)

  where [47mval[0m evaluates to one of [47m:basic[0m, [47m:all[0m, [47m:lambdas[0m, or a true list
  of symbols containing no keyword other than, optionally, [47m:lambdas[0m.

  When a recursive definition is submitted to ACL2 (in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode),
  the recursion must be proved to terminate; see [defun].  More
  precisely, ACL2 explores the [47m[if][0m structure of the body of the
  definition to accumulate the tests that ``rule'' any given
  recursive call.  The following example reviews how this works.
  Suppose that [47mf[0m has already been defined.

    (defun g (x y)
      (declare (xargs :measure (+ (acl2-count x) (acl2-count y))))
      (if (consp x)
          (g (cdr x) y)
        (if (consp y)
            (f (g x (cdr y)))
          (f (list x y)))))

  ACL2 makes the following response to this proposed definition.
  Notice that the [47m:measure[0m proposed above must be proved to be an
  ACL2 ordinal --- that is, to satisfy [47mO-P[0m --- and that the arguments
  to each recursive call must be smaller (in the sense of that
  measure and [47mO<[0m, which here reduces to the ordinary [47m<[0m relation) than
  the formals under the assumption of the ruling [47mIF[0m tests.  The first
  [47mIMPLIES[0m term below thus corresponds to the recursive call [47m(g (cdr
  x) y)[0m, while the second corresponds to the recursive call [47m(g x (cdr
  y))[0m.

    For the admission of G we will use the relation O< (which is known
    to be well-founded on the domain recognized by O-P) and the measure
    (+ (ACL2-COUNT X) (ACL2-COUNT Y)).  The non-trivial part of the measure
    conjecture is

    Goal
    (AND (O-P (+ (ACL2-COUNT X) (ACL2-COUNT Y)))
         (IMPLIES (CONSP X)
                  (O< (+ (ACL2-COUNT (CDR X)) (ACL2-COUNT Y))
                      (+ (ACL2-COUNT X) (ACL2-COUNT Y))))
         (IMPLIES (AND (NOT (CONSP X)) (CONSP Y))
                  (O< (+ (ACL2-COUNT X) (ACL2-COUNT (CDR Y)))
                      (+ (ACL2-COUNT X) (ACL2-COUNT Y))))).

  Now consider the following alternate version of the above definition.

    (defun g (x y)
      (declare (xargs :measure (+ (acl2-count x) (acl2-count y))))
      (if (consp x)
          (g (cdr x) y)
        (f (if (consp y)
               (g x (cdr y))
             (list x y)))))

  The first test, [47m(consp x)[0m, still rules the first recursive call, [47m(g
  (cdr x) y)[0m.  And the negation of that test, namely [47m(not (consp x))[0m,
  still rules the second recursive call [47m(g x (cdr y))[0m.  But the call
  of [47mf[0m blocks the top-down exploration of the [47mIF[0m structure of the
  body of [47mg[0m, so [47m(consp y)[0m does not rule that second recursive call,
  which (again) is [47m(g x (cdr y))[0m.  As a result, ACL2 fails to admit
  the above definition.

  [47mSet-ruler-extenders[0m is provided to overcome the sort of blocking
  described above.  Suppose for example that the following event is
  submitted:

    (set-ruler-extenders '(f))

  Then the alternate definition of [47mg[0m above is admissible, because the
  call of [47mf[0m no longer blocks the top-down exploration of the [47mIF[0m
  structure of the body of [47mg[0m: that is, [47m(consp y)[0m becomes a ruler of
  the recursive call [47m(g x (cdr y))[0m.  In this case, we say that [47mf[0m is a
  ``ruler-extender''.  The same result obtains if we first submit

    (set-ruler-extenders :all)

  as this removes all function calls as blockers of the top-down
  analysis.  In other words, with [47m:all[0m it is the case that for every
  recursive call, every test argument of a superior call of [47mIF[0m
  contributes a ruler of that recursive call.

  ACL2 handles [47m[let][0m (and [47m[let*][0m) expressions by translating them to
  [47mLAMBDA[0m expressions (see [term]).  The next examples illustrates
  termination analysis involving such expressions.  First consider
  the following (admittedly inefficient) definition.

    (defun fact (n)
      (let ((k (if (natp n) n 0)))
        (if (equal k 0)
            1
          (* k (fact (+ -1 k))))))

  ACL2 translates the body of this definition to a [47mLAMBDA[0m application,
  essentially:

    ((lambda (k)
       (if (equal k 0)
           1
         (* k (fact (+ -1 k)))))
     (if (natp n) n 0))

  As with the application of any function other than [47mIF[0m, the top-down
  termination analysis does not dive into arguments: the [47mLAMBDA[0m
  blocks the continuation of the analysis into its argument.  But
  here, the argument of the [47mLAMBDA[0m is [47m(if (natp n) n 0)[0m, which has no
  recursive calls to consider anyhow.  What is more interesting: ACL2
  does continue its termination analysis into the body of the [47mLAMBDA[0m,
  in an environment binding the [47mLAMBDA[0m formals to its actuals.  In
  this case, the termination analysis thus continues into the term

    (if (equal k 0)
        1
      (* k (fact (+ -1 k))))

  in the environment that binds [47mk[0m to the term [47m(if (natp n) n 0)[0m.  Thus,
  the proof obligation is successfully discharged, as reported by
  ACL2:

    For the admission of FACT we will use the relation O< (which is known
    to be well-founded on the domain recognized by O-P) and the measure
    (ACL2-COUNT N).  The non-trivial part of the measure conjecture is

    Goal
    (IMPLIES (NOT (EQUAL (IF (NATP N) N 0) 0))
             (O< (ACL2-COUNT (+ -1 (IF (NATP N) N 0)))
                 (ACL2-COUNT N))).
    .....
    Q.E.D.

    That completes the proof of the measure theorem for FACT.

  But now consider the following definition, in which the recursion
  takes place inside the argument of the [47mLAMBDA[0m rather than inside
  the [47mLAMBDA[0m body.

    (defun app (x y)
      (let ((result (if (endp x)
                        y
                      (cons (car x)
                            (app (cdr x) y)))))
        (if (our-test result)
            result
          0)))

  Writing the body in [47mLAMBDA[0m notation:

    ((lambda (result)
       (if (our-test result)
           result
         0))
     (if (endp x)
         y
       (cons (car x)
             (app (cdr x) y))))

  By default, the [47mLAMBDA[0m call blocks the top-down termination analysis
  from proceeding into the term [47m(if (endp x) ...)[0m.  To solve this,
  one can submit the event:

    (set-ruler-extenders :lambdas)

  The above definition of [47mapp[0m is then admitted by ACL2, because the
  termination analysis is no longer blocked by the [47mLAMBDA[0m call.

  The example just above illustrates that the heuristically-chosen
  measure is suitably sensitive to the ruler-extenders.
  Specifically: that measure is the application of [47macl2-count[0m to the
  first formal parameter of the function that is tested along every
  branch of the relevant [47mIF[0m structure (as determined by the rulers)
  and occurs as a proper subterm at the same argument position in
  every recursive call.  The heuristics for choosing the
  controller-alist for a [47m[definition][0m rule are similarly sensitive to
  the ruler-extenders (see [definition]).

  The remarks above for [47m[defun][0m [events] are equally applicable when a
  definition sits inside a [47m[mutual-recursion][0m event, except of course
  that in this case, a ``recursive call'' is a call of any function
  being defined by that [47m[mutual-recursion][0m event.

  Rules of class [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m are sensitive to [47mset-ruler-extenders[0m in
  analogy to the case of [47mdefun[0m [events].

  This macro generates a call [47m(table acl2-defaults-table
  :ruler-extenders val)[0m and hence is [47m[local][0m to any [books] and
  [47m[encapsulate][0m [events] in which it occurs. See
  [ACL2-defaults-table].  The current list of ruler-extenders may be
  obtained as

    (cdr (assoc-eq :ruler-extenders
         (table-alist 'acl2-defaults-table (w state))))

  or more conveniently, as:

    (default-ruler-extenders (w state))

  Note that evaluation of [47m(set-ruler-extenders lst)[0m, where [47mlst[0m
  evaluates to a list, does not necessarily include the default
  ruler-extenders --- i.e., those included for the argument, [47m:basic[0m
  --- which are the elements of the list constant
  [47m*basic-ruler-extenders*[0m, namely [47m[return-last][0m [47m[mv-list][0m, and [47m[if][0m.
  You may, of course, include these explicitly in your list argument.

  We conclude our discussion by noting that the set of ruler-extenders
  can affect the induction scheme that is stored with a recursive
  definition.  The community book
  [47mbooks/misc/misc2/ruler-extenders-tests.lisp[0m explains how induction
  schemes are derived in this case.  Consider the following example.

    (defun tree-of-nils-p (x)
      (if (consp x)
          (and (tree-of-nils-p (car x))
               (tree-of-nils-p (cdr x)))
        (null x)))

  The above definition generates the following induction scheme.  Note
  that [47m(and u v)[0m expands to [47m(if u v nil)[0m, which explains why the term
  [47m(tree-of-nils-p (car x))[0m rules the recursive call [47m(tree-of-nils-p
  (cdr x))[0m, resulting in the hypothesis [47m(tree-of-nils-p (car x))[0m in
  the final conjunct below.

    (AND (IMPLIES (NOT (CONSP X)) (:P X))
         (IMPLIES (AND (CONSP X)
                       (NOT (TREE-OF-NILS-P (CAR X)))
                       (:P (CAR X)))
                  (:P X))
         (IMPLIES (AND (CONSP X)
                       (TREE-OF-NILS-P (CAR X))
                       (:P (CAR X))
                       (:P (CDR X)))
                  (:P X)))

  Now consider the following variant of the above definition, in which
  a call of the function [47midentity[0m blocks the termination analysis.

    (defun tree-of-nils-p (x)
      (if (consp x)
          (identity (and (tree-of-nils-p (car x))
                         (tree-of-nils-p (cdr x))))
        (null x)))

  This time the induction scheme is as follows, since only the
  top-level [47mIF[0m test contributes rulers to the termination analysis.

    (AND (IMPLIES (NOT (CONSP X)) (:P X))
         (IMPLIES (AND (CONSP X)
                       (:P (CAR X))
                       (:P (CDR X)))
                  (:P X)))

  But now suppose we first designate [47midentity[0m as a ruler-extender.

    (set-ruler-extenders '(identity))

  Then the induction scheme generated for the both of the above
  variants of [47mtree-of-nils-p[0m is the one shown for the first variant,
  which is reasonable because both definitions now produce
  essentially the same termination analysis.


