  (THEORIES)
  "A rule name

    Examples:
    (:rewrite assoc-of-app)
    (:linear delta-aref . 2)
    (:definition length)
    (:executable-counterpart length)

  Note: This topic discusses a basic notion of ``rule name'', or
  ``rune'' for short.  Users often use abbreviations for runes; for
  example, a [theory] expression [47m(DISABLE APPEND)[0m abbreviates the
  following set of runes: [47m{(:DEFINITION BINARY-APPEND), (:INDUCTION
  BINARY-APPEND)}[0m.  See [theories] for a discussion of so-called
  ``runic designators'', which include expressions like [47mAPPEND[0m (as
  above) as well as [47m(APPEND)[0m (for the [executable-counterpart] of
  [47mBINARY-APPEND[0m).  Runic designators can also be ``runic
  abbreviations'' such as [47m(:d APPEND)[0m, [47m(:e APPEND)[0m, [47m(:i APPEND)[0m, and
  [47m(:t APPEND)[0m, which designate the definition,
  executable-counterpart, induction, and type-prescription rules for
  [47mBINARY-APPEND[0m.  For a complete description of runic designators,
  see [theories]; we return now to the more basic notion of a rune.

  Background: The theorem prover is driven from a database of rules.
  The most common rules are [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rules, which cause the
  simplifier to replace one term with another.  [Events] introduce
  rules into the database.  For example, a [47m[defun][0m event may
  introduce runes for symbolically replacing a function call by its
  instantiated body, for evaluating the function on constants, for
  determining the type of a call of the function, and for the
  induction scheme introduced upon defining the function.  [47m[Defthm][0m
  may introduce several rules, one for each of the [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m
  specified (where one rule class is specified if [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m is
  omitted, namely, [47m:rewrite[0m).

  Every rule in the system has a name.  Each name is a structured
  object called a ``rune,'' which is short for ``rule name''.  Runes
  are always of the form [47m(:token symbol . x)[0m, where [47m:token[0m is some
  keyword symbol indicating what kind of rule is named, [47msymbol[0m is the
  event name that created the rule (and is called the ``base symbol''
  of the rune), and [47mx[0m is either [47mnil[0m or a natural number that makes
  the rule name distinct from that of rules generated by other
  [events] or by other [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m within the same event.

  For example, an event of the form

    (defthm name thm
      :rule-classes ((:REWRITE :COROLLARY term1)
                     (:REWRITE :COROLLARY term2)
                     (:ELIM    :COROLLARY term3)))

  typically creates three rules, each with a unique rune.  The runes
  are

    (:REWRITE name . 1), (:REWRITE name . 2), and (:ELIM name).

  However, a given formula may create more than one rule, and all rules
  generated by the same [47m:corollary[0m formula will share the same rune.
  Consider the following example.

    (defthm my-thm
      (and (equal (foo (bar x)) x)
           (equal (bar (foo x)) x)))

  This is treated identically to the following.

    (defthm my-thm
      (and (equal (foo (bar x)) x)
           (equal (bar (foo x)) x))
      :rule-classes ((:rewrite
                      :corollary
                      (and (equal (foo (bar x)) x)
                           (equal (bar (foo x)) x)))))

  In either case, two rules are created: one rewriting [47m(foo (bar x))[0m to
  [47mx[0m, and one rewriting [47m(bar (foo x))[0m to [47mx[0m.  However, only a single
  rune is created, [47m(:REWRITE MY-THM)[0m, because there is only one rule
  class.  But now consider the following example.

    (defthm my-thm2
      (and (equal (foo (bar x)) x)
           (equal (bar (foo x)) x))
      :rule-classes ((:rewrite
                      :corollary
                      (and (equal (foo (bar x)) x)
                           (equal (bar (foo x)) x)))
                     (:rewrite
                      :corollary
                      (and (equal (foo (bar (foo x))) (foo x))
                           (equal (bar (foo (bar x))) (bar x))))))

  This time there are four rules created.  The first two rules are as
  before, and are assigned the rune [47m(:REWRITE MY-THM . 1)[0m.  The other
  two rules are similarly generated for the second [47m:corollary[0m, and
  are assigned the rune [47m(:REWRITE MY-THM . 2)[0m.

  The function [47m[corollary][0m will return the [corollary] term associated
  with a given rune in a given [world].  Example:

    (corollary '(:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION DIGIT-TO-CHAR) (w state))

  However, the preferred way to see the corollary term associated with
  a rune or a name is to use [47m:pf[0m; see [pf].

  The [47m[defun][0m event creates as many as four rules.  [47m(:definition fn)[0m is
  the rune given to the equality axiom defining the function, [47mfn[0m.
  [47m(:executable-counterpart fn)[0m is the rune given to the rule for
  computing [47mfn[0m on known arguments.  A type prescription rule may be
  created under the name [47m(:type-prescription fn)[0m, and an [induction]
  rule may be created under the name [47m(:induction fn)[0m.

  Runes may be individually [enable]d and [disable]d, according to
  whether they are included in the current theory.  See [theories].
  Thus, it is permitted to [disable] [47m(:elim name)[0m, say, while
  enabling the other rules derived from name.  Similarly,
  [47m(:definition fn)[0m may be [disable]d while [47m(:executable-counterpart
  fn)[0m and the type prescriptions for [47mfn[0m are [enable]d.

  Associated with most runes is the formula justifying the rule named.
  This is called the ``[corollary] formula'' of the rune and may be
  obtained via the function [47m[corollary][0m, which takes as its argument
  a rune and a property list [world].  Also see [pf].  The
  [corollary] formula for [47m(:rewrite name . 1)[0m after the [47m[defthm][0m
  event above is [47mterm1[0m.  The corollary formulas for [47m(:definition fn)[0m
  and [47m(:executable-counterpart fn)[0m are always identical: the defining
  axiom.  Some runes, e.g., [47m(:definition car)[0m, do not have corollary
  formulas.  [47m[Corollary][0m returns [47mnil[0m on such runes.  In any case, the
  corollary formula of a rune, when it is non-[47mnil[0m, is a theorem and
  may be used in the [47m:use[0m and [47m:by[0m [hints].

  Note: The system has built-in rules that, for regularity, ought to
  have names but don't because they can never be [disable]d.  One
  such rule is that implemented by the [linear] arithmetic package.
  Because many of our subroutines are required by their calling
  conventions to return the justifying rune, we have invented the
  notion of ``fake runes.'' Fake runes always have the base symbol
  [47mnil[0m, use a keyword token that includes the phrase ``fake-rune'',
  and are always [enable]d.  Here is the list of fake runes.

      ((:fake-rune-for-linear nil)
       (:fake-rune-for-linear-equalities nil)
       (:fake-rune-for-type-set nil)
       (:fake-rune-for-cert-data nil))

  Occasionally the system will print a fake rune where a rune is
  expected.  For example, when [47m(:FAKE-RUNE-FOR-LINEAR NIL)[0m is
  reported among the rules used in a proof, it is an indication that
  the linear arithmetic package was used.  However, fake runes are
  not allowed in [theories], they cannot be [enable]d or [disable]d,
  and they do not have associated [corollary] formulas.  In short,
  despite the fact that the user may sometimes see fake runes
  printed, they should never be typed.


Subtopics

  [Find-rules-of-rune]
      Find the rules named rune")
