  warning if [47mflag[0m is [47mnil[0m, [3mexcept[0m in the course of carrying out an
  [47m[include-book][0m form or the second pass of an [47m[encapsulate][0m form.

  Note that because [47m[table][0m [events] that set the [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m
  are implicitly [47m[local][0m, [47mset-enforce-redundancy[0m events are ignored
  when including books.  However, the presence of the event
  [47m(set-enforce-redundancy t)[0m in a book guarantees that its subsequent
  definitions and theorems are redundant.  This can be a useful
  property to maintain in library development, as we now describe.

  An example of the use of this form can be found in the community
  [books] under directory [47mbooks/rtl/rel4/[0m.  The intention in that
  directory has been to put all the gory details in subdirectories
  [47msupport/[0m and [47marithmetic/[0m, so that the books in subdirectory [47mlib/[0m
  contain only the ``exported'' definitions and theorems.  This
  approach is useful for human readability.  Moreover, suppose we
  want to prove new theorems in [47mlib/[0m.  Typically we wish to prove the
  new theorems using the existing books in [47mlib/[0m; however, our
  methodology demands that the proofs go into books in [47msupport/[0m.  If
  every theorem in [47mlib/[0m is redundant, then we can [3mdevelop[0m the proofs
  in [47mlib/[0m but then when we are done, [3mmove[0m each book with such proofs
  into [47msupport/[0m as follows.  In any such book, we first replace
  [47m[include-book][0m forms referring to books in [47mlib/[0m by [47m[include-book][0m
  forms referring to corresponding books in [47msupport/[0m and/or
  [47marithmetic/[0m.  Then, we add suitable [47m[in-theory][0m events to get us
  back into the original [47mlib/[0m proof environment.

  The default behavior of the system is as though the
  [47m:enforce-redundancy[0m value is [47mnil[0m.  The current behavior can be
  ascertained by evaluating the following form.

    (cdr (assoc-eq :enforce-redundancy (table-alist 'acl2-defaults-table wrld)))")
 (SET-EVISC-TUPLE
  (IO)
  "Control suppression of details when printing

  ACL2 output is generally printed in full.  However, ACL2 can be
  directed to abbreviate, or ``eviscerate'', objects before printing
  them, through the use of a so-called ``evisc-tuple''.  See
  [evisc-tuple] for a discussion of evisc-tuples.  The utility
  [47mset-evisc-tuple[0m modifies certain global evisc-tuples, as explained
  below, to affect the extent to which ACL2 eviscerates objects
  during printing, for example during proof output or when printing
  top-level evaluation results.

    General Form:
    (set-evisc-tuple evisc-tuple  ; a legal evisc-tuple, or :DEFAULT
                    :iprint val   ; one of *iprint-actions*
                    :sites sites) ; either :ALL, or an element or a subset of
                                  ;   the list of legal sites (see below)

  where the value of [47m:iprint[0m is passed to [47m[set-iprint][0m, [47m:sites :all[0m
  abbreviates [47m:sites[0m '[47m(:term :ld :trace :abbrev :gag-mode :brr)[0m, and
  other documentation is provided below.  Note that all arguments are
  evaluated.

  See [without-evisc] for how to avoid evisceration for ACL2 output.

  The following example illustrates an invocation of [47mset-evisc-tuple[0m
  that limits the print-level to 3 --- only three descents into list
  structures are permitted before eviscerating a subterm --- and
  limits the print-length to 4 --- only the first four elements of
  any list structure will be printed.

    ACL2 !>(set-evisc-tuple (evisc-tuple 3   ; print-level
                                         4   ; print-length
                                         nil ; alist
                                         nil ; hiding-cars
                                         )
                            :iprint :same ; better yet, T
                            :sites :all)
     (:TERM :LD :TRACE :ABBREV ...)
    ACL2 !>'((a b ((c d)) e f g) u v w x y)
    ((A B (#) E ...) U V W ...)
    ACL2 !>

  We recommend however using [47m:iprint t[0m so that eviscerated terms may be
  read back in (see [set-iprint]), as shown here:

    ACL2 !>(set-evisc-tuple (evisc-tuple 3   ; print-level
                                         4   ; print-length
                                         nil ; alist
                                         nil ; hiding-cars
                                         )
                            :iprint t
                            :sites :all)

    ACL2 Observation in SET-EVISC-TUPLE:  Iprinting has been enabled.
     (:TERM :LD :TRACE :ABBREV . #@1#)
    ACL2 !>(without-evisc '(:TERM :LD :TRACE :ABBREV . #@1#))
    (:TERM :LD
           :TRACE :ABBREV
           :GAG-MODE :BRR)
    ACL2 !>

  Indeed, the [47m:iprint[0m argument is required as a reminder to the user to
  consider that issue, unless iprinting has been enabled at least
  once.  If [47m:sites[0m or a required [47m:iprint[0m argument is omitted,
  however, ACL2 will query the user for the missing arguments rather
  than causing an error.

  ACL2 eviscerates by default only in a few cases, primarily in
  informational messages for errors, warnings, and queries (i.e., in
  the [47m:EVISC[0m case below), and in response to [break-rewrite]
  commands.  Users can modify the default behavior by supplying a
  suitable argument to [47mset-evisc-tuple[0m.  The argument may be
  [47m:default[0m, which denotes the evisceration provided when ACL2 starts
  up.  Otherwise that argument is an evisc-tuple, which is either [47mnil[0m
  (no evisceration) or as described above.  Moreover, there are six
  evisc-tuple ``evisceration contexts'', each with its own
  evisceration control.  The value returned by [47mset-evisc-tuple[0m
  indicates the evisceration contexts whose evisc-tuple has been set.
  The evisceration contexts are as follows, all of which use a
  default value of [47mnil[0m for the hiding-cars.  Accessors are also shown
  for retrieving the corresponding evisc-tuple.

    * [47m:TERM[0m --- used for printing terms.  The accessor is [47m(term-evisc-tuple
      flg state)[0m, where [47mflg[0m is ignored if [47mset-evisc-tuple[0m has been
      called for [47m:term[0m with value other than [47m:default[0m, and otherwise
      (hence initially): a [47mflg[0m of [47mnil[0m indicates an evisc-tuple of
      [47mnil[0m, and otherwise the term-evisc-tuple has a print-level of 3
      and print-length of 4.

    * [47m:ABBREV[0m --- used for printing informational messages for errors,
      warnings, and queries.  Initially, the alist abbreviates the
      ACL2 [47mworld[0m, print-level is 5, and print-level is 7.  The
      accessor is [47m(abbrev-evisc-tuple state)[0m.

    * [47m:BRR[0m --- used for output from [brr-commands] issued in the
      [break-rewrite] loop.  When the value is [47m:DEFAULT[0m then the
      [3meffective value[0m of this evisc-tuple is the [47m:TERM[0m evisc-tuple
      with [47mflg = t[0m (see above).  Also see [brr-evisc-tuple].

    * [47m:GAG-MODE[0m --- used for printing induction schemes (and perhaps, in
      the future, for other printing) when [gag-mode] is on.  If
      gag-mode is off, the value used for this [evisc-tuple] is
      [47m(term-evisc-tuple nil state)[0m.  But if gag-mode is on (i.e.,
      [47m(gag-mode)[0m evaluates to a non-[47mnil[0m value), then with one
      exception, the value is an evisc-tuple or [47mnil[0m, to be used in
      gag-mode for printing of induction schemes and, during proofs,
      the ``The non-trivial part of the guard conjecture''.  The
      exceptional value is [47mt[0m, which indicates that in gag-mode, no
      printing of induction schemes should be and the guard
      conjecture should be printed using [47m(term-evisc-tuple t state)[0m.
      The accessor is [47m(gag-mode-evisc-tuple state)[0m.

    * [47m:LD[0m --- used by the ACL2 read-eval-print loop.  The accessor is
      [47m([0m[47m[ld-evisc-tuple][0m[47m state)[0m.

    * [47m:TRACE[0m --- used for printing [trace] output.  No accessor is
      available (though in raw Lisp, [47m(trace-evisc-tuple)[0m returns the
      trace-evisc-tuple).

  Each context [47mectx[0m also has an updater, [47m(set-ectx-evisc-tuple val
  state)[0m, where [47mval[0m is a legal value for [47mset-evisc-tuple[0m as described
  above: [47m:default[0m or an [evisc-tuple] (possibly [47mnil[0m).


