 (SET-PRINT-RADIX
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Control printing of the radix for numbers

  Also see [47m[set-print-base-radix][0m for a nice combined use of
  [47m[set-print-base][0m and [47mset-print-radix[0m.

  See [set-print-base] for background on how the print base affects the
  printing of numbers.  [47mset-print-radix[0m affects whether a radix
  indicated when a number is printed.  The radix is not indicated by
  default, or after evaluating [47m(set-print-radix nil state)[0m.  But if
  [47mset-print-radix[0m is called with a first argument that evaluates to a
  non[47mnil[0m value --- for example, [47m(set-print-radix t state)[0m --- then
  the radix is shown when printing.  (This behavior is consistent
  with the handling of Common Lisp global [47m*print-radix*[0m.)  The
  following log illustrates how this works.

    ACL2 !>(list 25 25/3)
    (25 25/3)
    ACL2 !>(set-print-base 16 state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(list 25 25/3)
    (19 19/3)
    ACL2 !>(set-print-radix t state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(list 25 25/3)
    (#x19 #x19/3)
    ACL2 !>(set-print-base 10 state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(list 25 25/3)
    (25. #10r25/3)
    ACL2 !>(set-print-radix nil state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(list 25 25/3)
    (25 25/3)
    ACL2 !>")
