 (SET-WRITE-ACL2X
  (BOOKS-REFERENCE)
  "Cause [47m[certify-book][0m to write out a [47m.acl2x[0m file

    Example Forms:
    (set-write-acl2x nil state)
    (set-write-acl2x t state)
    (set-write-acl2x '(nil) state) ; same as just above, but allow inclusion of
                                   ; uncertified books during certify-book
    (set-write-acl2x '(t) state)
    (set-write-acl2x '(include-book-with-locals) state)

    General Form:
    (set-write-acl2x val state)

  where [47mval[0m evaluates to [47mt[0m, [47mnil[0m, or a one-element list whose element is
  a legal value for the global [47m'ld-skip-proofsp[0m; see
  [ld-skip-proofsp].  The value returned is an error triple, which in
  the non-error case is [47m(mv nil v state)[0m, where [47mv[0m is the value of [47mval[0m
  and [47mstate[0m is the result of updating the input [state] by assigning
  state global [47m'write-acl2x[0m the value [47mv[0m.

  The command [47m(set-write-acl2x val state)[0m assigns the value of [47mval[0m to
  the [state] global variable [47m'write-acl2x[0m, affecting whether or not
  [47m[certify-book][0m writes out a file with extension [47macl2x[0m, called a
  ``[47m.acl2x[0m file'' and pronounced ``dot-acl2x file''.  Such a file is
  read or written by [47m[certify-book][0m when it is supplied with keyword
  argument [47m:acl2x t[0m.  By default, such a call of [47mcertify-book[0m reads a
  [47m.acl2x[0m file; but if the value of state global variable [47m'write-acl2x[0m
  is not [47mnil[0m, then [47mcertify-book[0m writes a [47m.acl2x[0m file (in which case
  it is illegal to specify a non-[47mnil[0m value for [47m[certify-book][0m keyword
  argument [47m:pcert[0m).  Consider for example [47m(certify-book \"foo\" 0 nil
  :acl2x t)[0m.  By default, this command reads file [47mfoo.acl2x[0m, which
  supplies replacements for some forms in [47mfoo.lisp[0m, as described
  later below.  But if the value of state global [47m'write-acl2x[0m is not
  [47mnil[0m, then instead, this [47mcertify-book[0m command writes such a file
  [47mfoo.acl2x[0m.

  Before we discuss the function of [47m.acl2x[0m files, we first explain more
  about how a non-[47mnil[0m value of [state] global [47m'write-acl2x[0m affects
  the behavior of a command [47m(certify-book ... :acl2x t ...)[0m.  A
  significant effect on the behavior is that after processing events
  in the given book, ACL2 writes out a [47m.acl2x[0m file and then returns,
  skipping the other subsequent actions typically performed by
  [47m[certify-book][0m: a [local-incompatibility] check, writing of a
  [certificate] file, and possibly [compilation].  Another effect is
  that proofs may be skipped when processing [events] assuming that
  the [47mcertify-book[0m command does not explicitly specify
  [47m:skip-proofs-okp nil[0m, as we now explain.  A non-[47mnil[0m value of
  [47m'write-acl2x[0m should either be [47mt[0m or a one-element list [47m(x)[0m, where [47mx[0m
  is a legal value for the [state] global [47m'ld-skip-proofsp[0m (see
  [ld-skip-proofsp]).  In both cases, [47mcertify-book[0m will process
  [events] to write out a [47m.acl2x[0m file as described above.  But in the
  latter (list) case, event processing will take place according to
  the value of [47mx[0m: in particular, proofs will be skipped when [47mx[0m is not
  [47mnil[0m, and if moreover [47mx[0m is the symbol [47minclude-book-with-locals[0m, then
  only one pass will be made through each [47m[encapsulate][0m form.  A
  third effect of a non-[47mnil[0m value of [47m'write-acl2x[0m, which is
  restricted to the list case, is that [include-book] events
  encountered during event processing are allowed to succeed on
  uncertified books, something that is prohibited during most calls
  of [47m[certify-book][0m.

  When [47m[certify-book][0m is used to write out a [47m.acl2x[0m file, there is
  typically a subsequent run of [47m[certify-book][0m that reads that file.
  Consider how this can work with a book [47mfoo.lisp[0m.  In the first call
  of [47mcertify-book[0m, a file [47mfoo.acl2x[0m is written that contains all
  [47m[make-event][0m expansions, but [47mfoo.cert[0m is not written.  In the
  second call of [47mcertify-book[0m, no [47m[make-event][0m expansion typically
  takes place, because [47mfoo.acl2x[0m supplies the expansions.  The
  command [47m(set-write-acl2x t state)[0m should be evaluated before the
  first certification (though another legal non-[47mnil[0m value may be used
  in place of [47mt[0m), setting the value of [47m[state][0m global [47m'write-acl2x[0m to
  [47mt[0m, to enable writing of [47mfoo.acl2x[0m; and the command [47m(set-write-acl2x
  nil state)[0m may be evaluated before the second run (though this is
  not necessary in a fresh ACL2 session) in order to complete the
  certification (writing out [47mfoo.cert[0m) using [47mfoo.acl2x[0m to supply the
  [47m[make-event][0m expansions.

  When [47m[Certify-book][0m is supplied with keyword argument [47m:acl2x t[0m it
  will read or write the book's [47m.acl2x[0m file; when supplied with
  [47m:acl2x nil[0m, it will not read or write that [47m.acl2x[0m file.  The value
  of [47m:acl2x[0m is [47mnil[0m by default.  The interaction of [47m[certify-book][0m
  with the corresponding [47m.acl2x[0m file is as follows.

    o If [47m:acl2x[0m is [47mt[0m, then:
      - If [47mset-write-acl2x[0m has been (most recently) called with a value of
        [47mt[0m for its first argument,then ACL2 writes the corresponding
        [47m.acl2x[0m file.
      - If [47mset-write-acl2x[0m has been (most recently) called with a value of
        [47mnil[0m for its first argument, or not called at all, then ACL2 insists
        on a corresponding [47m.acl2x[0m file that is at least as recent as the
        corresponding [47m.lisp[0m file, causing an error otherwise.
     o If [47m:acl2x[0m is [47mnil[0m,then:
      - If [47mset-write-acl2x[0m has been (most recently) called with a value
        [47mt[0m for its first argument, or if argument [47m:ttagsx[0m is supplied,
        then an error occurs.
      - If the [47m.acl2x[0m file exists, then regardless of whether or how
        [47mset-write-acl2x[0m has been called, ACL2 ignores the [47m.acl2x[0m file
        but issues a warning about it.

  Suppose you use the two-runs approach: first write a [47m.acl2x[0m file,
  then certify using (reading) that [47m.acl2x[0m file.  Then with scripts
  such as makefiles, then you may wish to provide a single
  [47m[certify-book][0m command to use for both runs.  For that purpose,
  [47m[certify-book][0m supports the keyword argument [47m:ttagsx[0m.  If this
  argument is supplied and [47mwrite-acl2x[0m is true, then this argument is
  treated as the [47m:ttags[0m argument, overriding a [47m:ttags[0m argument if
  present.  That is, for the two runs, [47m:ttagsx[0m may be used to specify
  the trust tags used in the first certification while [47m:ttags[0m
  specifies the trust tags, if any (else [47m:ttags[0m may be omitted), used
  in the second certification.  Note: If the argument [47m:ttagsx[0m is not
  supplied, then its value defaults to the (explicit or default)
  value of the [47m:ttags[0m argument.

  The built-in ACL2 Makefile support automatically generates suitable
  dependencies if you create a [47m.acl2[0m file with a [47m[certify-book][0m call
  matching the following regular expression, case-independent:

    (certify-book[^;]*:acl2x t

  For an example [47m.acl2[0m file with a [47mcertify-book[0m call matching the above
  pattern, see community books file
  [47mbooks/make-event/double-cert-test-1.acl2[0m.

  Note that [47m[include-book][0m is generally not affected by
  [47mset-write-acl2x[0m, other than through the indirect effect on
  [47m[certify-book][0m.  More precisely: All expansions are stored in the
  [certificate] file, so when [47m[include-book][0m is applied to a
  certified book, the [47m.acl2x[0m file is not consulted.

  An example of how to put this all together may be found in community
  book [47mbooks/make-event/double-cert-test-1.lisp[0m.  There, we see the
  following form.

    (make-event
     (progn (defttag :my-ttag)
            (progn! (let ((val (sys-call \"pwd\" nil)))
                      (value (list 'defun 'foo () val))))))

  Imagine that in place of the binding computed using [47m[sys-call][0m, which
  by the way requires a trust tag, is some computation of your choice
  (such as reading forms from a file) that is used to construct your
  own event, in place of the [47m[defun][0m event constructed above.  The
  [47mMakefile[0m in that directory contains the following added dependency,
  so that file [47mdouble-cert-test-1.acl2x[0m will be created:

    double-cert-test-1.cert: double-cert-test-1.acl2x

  There is also the file [47mdouble-cert-test-1.acl2[0m in that directory,
  which contains a single form as follows.

    (certify-book \"double-cert-test-1\" ? t :ttagsx :all :ttags nil)

  Thus, a call of `make' first creates file [47mdouble-cert-test-1.acl2x[0m,
  which uses the above [47m:ttagsx[0m argument in order to support the use
  of [47m[defttag][0m during [47m[make-event][0m expansion.  Then, `make' goes on
  to cause a second certification in which no trust tags are
  involved.  As a result, the parent book [47mdouble-cert-test.lisp[0m is
  ultimately certified without requiring any trust tags.

  The discussion above is probably sufficient for most users of the
  two-run approach it describes.  We conclude with further details
  for those who want more information.  Those who wish to see a yet
  lower-level explanation of how all this works are invited to read
  the comment in the ACL2 source code entitled ``Essay on .acl2x
  Files (Double Certification).

  Consider the [47m.acl2x[0m file produced by the first run as described
  above.  It contains a single expression, which is an association
  list whose keys are all positive integers, which occur in
  increasing order.  When the [47m.acl2x[0m file is present and at least as
  recent as the corresponding [47m.lisp[0m file, then for a subsequent
  [47m[certify-book][0m with argument [47m:acl2x t[0m and the (default) value of
  [47mnil[0m for [state] global [47m'write-acl2x[0m, that association list will be
  applied to the top-level events in the book, as follows.  Suppose
  the entry [47m(n . ev)[0m belongs to the association list in the [47m.acl2x[0m
  file.  Then [47mn[0m is a positive integer, and the [47mn[0mth top-level event in
  the book --- where the [47m0[0mth event is the initial [47m[in-package][0m form
  --- will be replaced by [47mev[0m.  In practice, [47mev[0m is the [47m[make-event][0m
  expansion created during certification for the [47mnth[0m top-level event
  in the book; and this will always be the case if the [47m.acl2x[0m file is
  created by [47m[certify-book][0m after execution of the form
  [47m(set-write-acl2x t state)[0m.  However, you are welcome to associate
  indices manually with any [events] you wish into the alist stored
  in the [47m.acl2x[0m file.

  Note: Also see the community book [47mmake-event/acl2x-help.lisp[0m for a
  useful utility that can be used to skip proofs during the writing
  of [47m.acl2x[0m files.")
 (SETENV$
  (PROGRAMMING-WITH-STATE ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Set an environment variable

  [47m(Setenv$ str val)[0m, where [47mstr[0m and [47mval[0m are strings, sets the
  environment variable [47mstr[0m to have value [47mval[0m, for subsequent read by
  [47mgetenv$[0m (see [getenv$]), and returns [47mnil[0m.  Or, if this operation is
  not implemented for the host Common Lisp, an error will occur.

    Example:
    (setenv$ \"FOO\" \"BAR\")

  It may be surprising that [47msetenv$[0m returns [47mnil[0m; indeed, it neither
  takes nor returns the ACL2 [state].  The reason is that [47m[getenv$][0m
  takes responsibility for trafficking in [state]; it is defined in
  the logic using the function [47m[read-ACL2-oracle][0m, which (again, in
  the logic) does modify state, by popping an entry from its
  acl2-oracle field.  See [getenv$].

  As suggested above, a call of [47m[getenv$][0m takes into account the most
  recent call of [47msetenv$[0m on the same environment variable.  It may
  also be the case that [47msetenv$[0m modifies the environment of the
  underlying process; for example, we think this is probably the case
  for most invocations of ACL2 built on a host Lisp that is CCL,
  SBCL, or CMUCL, as well as others perhaps.  If you want to rely on
  such behavior, however, we advise you to look at source code.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<setenv$>

    (defun setenv$ (str val)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (stringp str) (stringp val))))
      (declare (ignore str val))
      nil)")
