  See any Common Lisp documentation for details.")
 (SHARP-BANG-READER
  (READER PACKAGES)
  "Package prefix that is not restricted to symbols

    Examples:

    ACL2 !>(defpkg \"FOO\" nil)

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFPKG \"FOO\" ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     \"FOO\"
    ACL2 !>'#!foo(a b)
    (FOO::A FOO::B)
    ACL2 !>'#!foo(a #!acl2 b)
    (FOO::A B)
    ACL2 !>'#!foo(#!acl2 a b)
    (A FOO::B)
    ACL2 !>'#!foo(#!\"ACL2\" a b)
    (A FOO::B)
    ACL2 !>

  The ACL2 reader supports the syntax [47m#!pkg-name expr[0m where [47mpkg-name[0m is
  a string or symbol that names a package known to ACL2.  As
  illustrated above, this syntax nests as one might expect.  In the
  special case that [47mexpr[0m is a symbol, [47m#!pkg-name expr[0m is equivalent
  to [47mpkg-name::expr[0m.")
 (SHARP-D-READER
  (READER NUMBERS)
  "Read a rational number as a representable rational (see [df])

  When the ACL2 reader encounters the two-character sequence [47m#d[0m, it
  invokes the Lisp reader to obtain the next token, which should
  designate a Lisp double-precision floating-point number.
  (Technically, the Lisp value of variable
  [47m*read-default-float-format*[0m is the symbol [47mDOUBLE-FLOAT[0m when the
  floating-point token is read.)  The reader then returns the
  rational number represented by that floating-point token.

  The discussion above is complete, but it may make more sense to those
  familiar with so-called [3mdf[0m values in ACL2.  See [df].

  See also [sharp-f-reader], which reads input starting with [47m#f[0m.  As
  with [47m#d[0m, one reads floating-point notation and returns the
  corresponding rational number.  However, while [47m#d[0m reads the next
  token as a Lisp floating-point number, [47m#f[0m is specific to ACL2 and
  follows precise documented rules that, unlike [47m#d[0m, allow use of
  underscores ([47m_[0m) and hexadecimal notation.")
 (SHARP-DOT-READER
  (READER DEFCONST)
  "Read-time evaluation of constants

    Example:

    ACL2 !>(defconst *a* '(a b c))

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFCONST *A* ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     *A*
    ACL2 !>(quote (1 2 #.*a* 3 4))
    (1 2 (A B C) 3 4)
    ACL2 !>

  The ACL2 reader supports the syntax [47m#.*a*[0m where [47m*a*[0m was defined by
  [47m[defconst][0m.  In this case, the reader treats [47m#.*a*[0m as though it
  were reading the value of [47m*a*[0m.  This feature can be useful in
  conjunction with the use of [47m[evisc-table][0m to abbreviate large
  constants, so that the abbreviation can be read back in; see
  [evisc-table].

  Remarks.

  (1) The ACL2 reader only supports `[47m#.[0m' as described above, unlike
  Common Lisp.  Older versions (preceding 3.5) used `[47m#.[0m' to abort,
  but that functionality is now carried out by [47m(a!)[0m; see [a!].  For a
  related feature that only pops up one level, see [p!].

  (2) If you call [47m[certify-book][0m on a book that contains a form
  `[47m#.*foo*[0m', the [47m*foo*[0m must already be defined in the [world] in
  which you issue the [47mcertify-book[0m command.  The reason is that
  [47mcertify-book[0m reads the entire book before evaluating its forms.")
 (SHARP-F-READER
  (READER NUMBERS)
  "Read a rational number in floating-point syntax

  This topic is about reading expressions starting with [47m#f[0m.  See also
  [sharp-d-reader], which reads input starting with [47m#d[0m.  Both read a
  form of floating-point notation as a rational number.  However,
  while [47m#d[0m reads the next token as a Lisp floating-point number, [47m#f[0m
  is specific to ACL2 and follows rules documented explicitly below,
  including the use of underscores ([47m_[0m) and hexadecimal notation.

    Examples:

    ACL2 !>#f567
    567
    ACL2 !>#f_5_6__7_ ; underscores are allowed, and discarded
    567
    ACL2 !>
    ACL2 !>#f-567
    -567
    ACL2 !>#f+567
    567
    ACL2 !>#f5.67
    567/100
    ACL2 !>#f-5.67
    -567/100
    ACL2 !>#f5.67e3
    5670
    ACL2 !>#f-5.67e3
    -5670
    ACL2 !>#f-5.67e-3
    -567/100000
    ACL2 !>#f.67e2
    67
    ACL2 !>#f-.67e2
    -67
    ACL2 !>#f-.67e+4
    -6700
    ACL2 !>#f-0.67e+4
    -6700
    ACL2 !>#fx101
    257
    ACL2 !>#fx10.1
    257/16
    ACL2 !>#fx10.1p3
    257/2
    ACL2 !>#fx10.1p-4
    257/256
    ACL2 !>#fx-10.1p-4
    -257/256
    ACL2 !>#fx.1p4
    1
    ACL2 !>#fx.1p+8
    16
    ACL2 !>#fx.1p-2
    1/64
    ACL2 !>#fx-0_4p1_0
    -4096
    ACL2 !>

  As illustrated above, we support two forms of this reader: [47m#f[0m,
  indicating base 10 digits, and [47m#fx[0m, indicating base 16 digits.  Let
  {i} be notation representing a sequence of digits and (optionally)
  underscore ([47m_[0m) characters, optionally preceded by a sign ([47m-[0m or [47m+[0m),
  that the ACL2 reader reads as the integer i after discarding
  underscore characters; similarly for {j} and {n}, except that an
  initial sign is illegal for {j}, and for {n} the digits are always
  base 10 (even in the case of [47m#fx[0m).  Note that each such string is
  terminated at the first character from the input channel that is
  not a digit.  For the [47m#f[0m case we have the following semantics.

    #f{i}          => i
    #f{i}e{n}      => i * 10^n
    #f{i}.{j}      => i + (/ j 10^k) where k is the length of {j}
    #f{i}.{j}e{n}  => (i + (/ j 10^k)) * 10^n where k is the length of {j}

  We have the following similar semantics for the [47m#fx[0m case, except that
  {i} and {j} are now read in base 16.  Note that {n} is still read
  in base 10.

    #fx{i}         => i
    #fx{i}p{n}     => i * 2^n
    #fx{i}.{j}     => i + (/ j 16^k) where k is the length of {j}
    #fx{i}.{j}p{n} => (i + (/ j 16^k)) * 2^n where k is the length of {j}

  We thank Dmitry Nadezhin for pointing us to the following page, which
  explains an analogous ``floating point literal'' syntax for C++:

    http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/floating_literal

  Note, however, that in ACL2 we do not support the use of single quote
  ([47m'[0m) as a separator, using underscore ([47m_[0m) instead, which is
  consistent with another reader macro, [47m#u[0m (see [sharp-u-reader]).
  Moreover, unlike the syntax described on the page above, the ACL2
  [47m#f[0m reader allows an initial sign ([47m-[0m or [47m+[0m).  To see why, note that
  in Lisp, the string [47m\"-#\"[0m starts a symbol, for example:

    ? '-#f23
    |-#F23|
    ?

  Since the ACL2 reader is based on the Lisp reader, it would be
  awkward to provide support to read the string [47m\"-#f23\"[0m as a number,
  -23.  Instead, ACL2 accepts a sign immediately after the initial
  [47m\"#f\"[0m or [47m\"#fx\"[0m:

    ACL2 !>#f-23
    -23
    ACL2 !>")
 (SHARP-U-READER
  (READER NUMBERS)
  "Allow underscore characters in numbers

    Example:

    ACL2 !>#ub1000_1000_1000_
    2184
    ACL2 !>#b100010001000
    2184
    ACL2 !>#uo1_1
    9
    ACL2 !>#o11
    9
    ACL2 !>#u34_5
    345
    ACL2 !>#u345
    345
    ACL2 !>345
    345
    ACL2 !>#ux12_a
    298
    ACL2 !>#ux12a
    298
    ACL2 !>#u x12a
    298
    ACL2 !>#x12a
    298
    ACL2 !>#u123_456/7_919
    123456/7919
    ACL2 !>

  The ACL2 reader supports the use of [47m#ub[0m, [47m#uo[0m, and [47m#ux[0m where one would
  otherwise write [47m#b[0m, [47m#o[0m, and [47m#x[0m, respectively (for binary, octal,
  and hexadecimal numerals), but where underscore characters (`[47m_[0m')
  are allowed but ignored.  Also supported is the prefix [47m#u[0m in front
  of a an expression that is a decimal numeral except that underscore
  characters are allowed but ignored.

  The precise specification of [47m#u[0m is as follows.  The ACL2 reader reads
  one expression after the [47m#u[0m.  If the result is a number, then that
  number is returned by the reader.  Otherwise the result must be a
  symbol.  Let [47mN[0m be the [47m[symbol-name][0m of the symbol; thus [47mN[0m is a
  string.  If [47mN[0m begins with one of the characters `[47mB[0m', `[47mO[0m', or `[47mX[0m',
  then first obtain a string [47mS1[0m by prefixing [47mN[0m with the character,
  `[47m#[0m'; otherwise let [47mS1[0m be [47mN[0m.  Next, remove all underscore characters
  `[47m_[0m' from [47mS1[0m, obtaining [47mS2[0m.  The result is obtained by applying the
  ACL2 reader to [47mS2[0m; that result must be a number, or else an error
  occurs.")
