 (SIGNATURE
  (ENCAPSULATE)
  "How to specify the arity of a constrained function

  We start with a gentle introduction to signatures, where we pretend
  that there are no single-threaded objects (more on that below ---
  for now, if you don't know anything about single-threaded objects,
  that's fine!).  Here are some simple examples of signatures.

    ((hd *) => *)
    ((pair * *) => *)
    ((foo * *) => (mv * * *))

  The first of these says that [47mhd[0m is a function of one argument, while
  the other two say that [47mpair[0m and [47mfoo[0m are functions that each take
  two arguments.  The first two say that [47mhd[0m and [47mpair[0m return a single
  value.  The third says that [47mfoo[0m returns three values, much as the
  following definition returns three values:

    (defun bar (x y)
      (mv y x (cons x y)))

  Corresponding ``old style'' signatures are as follows.  In each case,
  a function symbol is followed by a list of formal parameters and
  then either [47mt[0m, to denote a single value return, or [47m(mv t t t)[0m, to
  denote a multiple value return (in this case, returning three
  values).

    (hd (x) t)
    (pair (x y) t)
    (foo (x y) (mv t t t))

  That concludes our gentle introduction.  The documentation below is
  more general, for example covering single-threaded objects (see
  [stobj]), dfs (see [df]), and keyword values such as [47m:guard[0m.  When
  reading what follows below, all you need to know about
  single-threaded objects (or ``stobjs'') that each has a unique
  symbolic name and that [47m[state][0m is the name of the only built-in
  single-threaded object.  All other stobjs are introduced by the
  user via [47m[defstobj][0m or [47m[defabsstobj][0m.  We also mention ``dfs'',
  which are ACL2 expressions that denote Lisp floating-point values;
  see [df].  An expression that is neither a stobj name nor a df is
  said to be ``ordinary''.

    Examples:
    ((hd *) => *)
    ((hd *) => * :formals (x) :guard (consp x))
    ((printer * state) => (mv * * state))
    ((mach * mach-state * state) => (mv * mach-state))
    ((df-mult3 :df :df :df) => :df)

    General Form:
    ((fn ...) => *)
    ((fn ...) => s) ; where s is a stobj name
    ((fn ...) => :df)
    or
    ((fn ...) => (mv x1 x2 ... xn)) ; where each xi is *, a stobj, or :df
    or for part1 and part2 as above,
    (part1 => part2 :kwd1 val1 ... :kwdn valn)

  where [47mfn[0m is the constrained function symbol and the optional [47m:kwdi[0m
  and [47mvali[0m are as described below.  ACL2 also supports an older style
  of signature, described below after we describe the preferred
  style.

  Signatures specify three syntactic aspects of a function symbol: (1)
  the ``arity'' or how many arguments the function takes, (2) the
  ``multiplicity'' or how many results it returns via [47mMV[0m, and (3) the
  arguments and results that are stobjs or dfs.

  A signature typically has the form [47m((fn x1 ... xn) => val)[0m.  Such a
  signature has two parts, separated by the symbol ``=>''.  The first
  part, [47m(fn x1 ... xn)[0m, is suggestive of a call of the constrained
  function.  The number of ``arguments,'' [47mn[0m, indicates the arity of
  [47mfn[0m.  Each [47mxi[0m must be a symbol.  If a given [47mxi[0m is the symbol ``*''
  then the corresponding argument must be ordinary.  If a given [47mxi[0m is
  any other symbol, that symbol must be the name of a single-threaded
  object and the corresponding argument must be that object, or else
  that symbol must be [47m:df[0m and the corresponding argument must be a df
  (see [df]).  No stobj name may occur twice among the [47mxi[0m.

  The second part, [47mval[0m, of a signature is suggestive of a term and
  indicates the ``shape'' of the output of [47mfn[0m.  If [47mval[0m is a symbol
  then it must be either the symbol ``*'', the keyword [47m:df[0m, or the
  name of a single-threaded object.  In either case, the multiplicity
  of [47mfn[0m is 1 and [47mval[0m indicates whether the result is ordinary, a df,
  or a stobj.  Otherwise, [47mval[0m is of the form [47m(mv y1 ... yk)[0m, where [47mk[0m
  > 1.  Each [47myi[0m must be either the symbol ``*'', the keyword [47m:df[0m, or
  the name of a stobj.  Such a [47mval[0m indicates that [47mfn[0m has multiplicity
  [47mk[0m and the [47myi[0m indicate which results are ordinary, which are dfs,
  and which are stobjs.  No stobj name may occur twice among the [47myi[0m,
  and a stobj name may appear in [47mval[0m only if appears among the [47mxi[0m.

  A signature may have the form [47m((fn x1 ... xn) => val . k)[0m, where [47mk[0m is
  a [47m[keyword-value-listp][0m, i.e., an alternating list of keywords and
  values starting with a keyword.  In this case [47m((fn x1 ... xn) =>
  val)[0m must be a legal signature as described above.  The legal
  keywords in [47mk[0m are generally [47m:GUARD[0m and [47m:FORMALS[0m, but see remarks at
  the end of this topic regarding [47m:GLOBAL-STOBJS[0m and [47m:TRANSPARENT[0m
  and, for ACL2(r), [47m:CLASSICALP[0m.  The value following [47m:FORMALS[0m is to
  be the list of formal parameters of [47mfn[0m, which must be consistent
  with the parameters specified in [47m(fn x1 ... xn)[0m: they must both
  specify the same arity (number of formal parameters) and the same
  [stobj] inputs.  The value following [47m:GUARD[0m is a term that is to be
  the [guard] of [47mfn[0m.  Note that this guard is never actually
  evaluated, and is not subject to the guard verification performed
  on functions introduced by [47m[defun][0m (see [verify-guards]).  Said
  differently: this guard need not itself have a guard of [47mt[0m.  Indeed,
  the guard is only used for attachments; see [defattach].  Note that
  if [47m:GUARD[0m is supplied, then [47m:FORMALS[0m must also be supplied as a
  list of distinct variables that includes all variables occurring
  free in the specified guard.  One final observation about guards:
  if the [47m:GUARD[0m keyword is omitted, then the guard defaults to [47mT[0m.

  Before ACL2 supported user-declared single-threaded objects there was
  only one single-threaded object: ACL2's built-in notion of [47m[state][0m.
  The notion of signature supported then gave a special role to the
  symbol [47mstate[0m and all other symbols were considered to denote
  ordinary objects.  ACL2 still supports the old form of signature,
  but it is limited to functions that operate on ordinary objects or
  ordinary objects and [47mstate[0m.

    Old Style General Form:
    (fn formals result . k)

  where [47mfn[0m is the constrained function symbol, [47mformals[0m is a suitable
  list of formal parameters for it, [47mk[0m is an optional
  [47m[keyword-value-listp][0m (see below), and [47mresult[0m is either a symbol
  denoting that the function returns one result or else [47mresult[0m is an
  [47m[mv][0m expression, [47m(mv s1 ... sn)[0m, where [47mn>1[0m, each [47msi[0m is a symbol,
  indicating that the function returns [47mn[0m results.  At most one of the
  formals may be the symbol [47mSTATE[0m, indicating that the corresponding
  argument must be ACL2's built-in [47m[state][0m.  If [47mstate[0m appears in
  [47mformals[0m then [47mstate[0m may appear once in [47mresult[0m.  All ``variable
  symbols'' other than [47mstate[0m in old style signatures denote ordinary
  objects, regardless of whether the symbol has been defined to be a
  single-threaded object name!

  The optional [47mk[0m is as described above for newer-style signatures,
  except that the user is also allowed to declare which symbols
  (besides [47mstate[0m) are to be considered stobjs names or dfs.  Thus
  [47m:STOBJS[0m and @9':DFS') are also legal keywords.  The form

    (fn formals result ... :stobjs stobj-names :dfs df-names ...)

  specifies that [47mstobj-names[0m is either the name of a stobj or else is a
  list of such names; also, both [47mstobj-names[0m and [47mdf-names[0m are lists
  that must be contained in the [47mformals[0m list.  Every name in
  [47mstobj-names[0m must have been previously defined as a stobj via
  [47m[defstobj][0m or [47m[defabsstobj][0m.

  As promised above, we conclude with a remark about additional
  keywords.  The keyword [47m:GLOBAL-STOBJS[0m specifies the use of the
  macro, [47mwith-global-stobj[0m, in attachments (see [defattach]); see
  [47m[with-global-stobj][0m for explanation of this keyword.  The keyword
  [47m:TRANSPARENT[0m specifies transparent functions; see
  [transparent-functions].  Finally, the keyword [47m:CLASSICALP[0m is legal
  for ACL2(r) only (see [real]).  The value of this keyword must be [47mt[0m
  (the default) or [47mnil[0m, indicating respectively whether [47mfn[0m is
  classical or not.")
 (SIGNED-BYTE-P
  (NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for signed integers that fit in a specified bit width

  [47m(Signed-byte-p bits x)[0m is [47mT[0m when [47mbits[0m is a positive integer and [47mx[0m is
  a signed integer whose 2's complement representation fits in a
  bit-width of [47mbits[0m, i.e., [47m-2^(bits-1) <= x < 2^(bits-1)[0m.

  Note that a [type-spec] of [47m(signed-byte i)[0m for a variable [47mx[0m in a
  function's [47m[declare][0m form translates to a [guard] condition of
  [47m(signed-byte-p i x)[0m.

  The [guard] for [47msigned-byte-p[0m is [47mT[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<signed-byte-p>

    (defun signed-byte-p (bits x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (and (integerp bits)
           (< 0 bits)
           (let ((y (expt 2 (1- bits))))
             (integer-range-p (- y) y x))))")
 (SIGNUM
  (NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Indicator for positive, negative, or zero

  [47m(Signum x)[0m is [47m0[0m if [47mx[0m is [47m0[0m, [47m-1[0m if [47mx[0m is negative, and is [47m1[0m otherwise.

  The [guard] for [47msignum[0m requires its argument to be rational ([real],
  in ACL2(r)) number.

  [47mSignum[0m is a Common Lisp function.  See any Common Lisp documentation
