 (SKIP-PROOFS
  (EVENTS)
  "Skip proofs for a given form --- a quick way to introduce unsoundness

    Example Form:
    (skip-proofs
      (defun foo (x)
        (if (atom x) nil (cons (car x) (foo (reverse (cdr x)))))))

    General Form:
    (skip-proofs form)

  where [47mform[0m is processed as usual except that the proof obligations
  usually generated are merely assumed.

  Normally [47mform[0m is an event; see [events].  If you want to put
  [47mskip-proofs[0m around more than one event, consider the following (see
  [progn]): [47m(skip-proofs (progn event1 event2 ... eventk))[0m.

  [31;1mWARNING[0m: The use of [47mskip-proofs[0m carries an implicit promise by the
  user that the ensuing proof obligations are indeed theorems of the
  current theory.  When that is not the case, [47mskip-proofs[0m can even
  allow inconsistent [events] to be admitted to the logic.  Use it at
  your own risk!  If your intention is truly to extend the current
  logical theory, consider using [47m[defaxiom][0m instead.

  Sometimes in the development of a formal model or proof it is
  convenient to skip the proofs required by a given event.  By
  embedding the event in a [47mskip-proofs[0m form, you can avoid the proof
  burdens generated by the event, at the risk of introducing
  unsoundness.  Below we list four illustrative situations in which
  you might find [47mskip-proofs[0m useful.

  1. The termination argument for a proposed function definition is
  complicated.  You presume you could admit it, but are not sure that
  your definition has the desired properties.  By embedding the
  [47m[defun][0m event in a [47mskip-proofs[0m you can ``admit'' the function and
  experiment with theorems about it before undoing (see [ubt]) and
  then paying the price of its admission.  Note however that you
  might still have to supply a measure.  The set of formals used in
  some valid measure, known as the ``measured subset'' of the set of
  formals, is used by ACL2's induction heuristics and therefore needs
  to be suitably specified.  You may wish to specify the special
  measure of [47m(:? v1 ... vk)[0m, where [47m(v1 ... vk)[0m enumerates the
  measured subset.

  2. You intend eventually to verify the [guard]s for a definition but
  do not want to take the time now to pursue that.  By embedding the
  [47m[verify-guards][0m event in a [47mskip-proofs[0m you can get the system to
  behave as though the [guard]s were verified.

  3. You are repeatedly recertifying a book while making many
  experimental changes.  A certain [47m[defthm][0m in the book takes a very
  long time to prove and you believe the proof is not affected by the
  changes you are making.  By embedding the [47m[defthm][0m event in a
  [47mskip-proofs[0m you allow the theorem to be assumed without proof
  during the experimental recertifications.

  4. You are constructing a proof top-down and wish to defer the proof
  of a [47m[defthm][0m until you are convinced of its utility.  You can
  embed the [47mdefthm[0m in a [47mskip-proofs[0m.  Of course, you may find later
  (when you attempt prove the theorem) that the proposed [47mdefthm[0m is
  not a theorem.

  Unsoundness or Lisp errors may result if the presumptions underlying
  a use of [47mskip-proofs[0m are incorrect.  Therefore, [47mskip-proofs[0m must be
  considered a dangerous (though useful) tool in system development.

  Roughly speaking, a [47m[defthm][0m embedded in a [47mskip-proofs[0m is essentially
  a [47m[defaxiom][0m, except that it is not noted as an axiom for the
  purposes of functional instantiation (see [lemma-instance]).  But a
  skipped [47m[defun][0m is much more subtle since not only is the
  definitional equation being assumed but so are formulas relating to
  termination and type.  The situation is also difficult to
  characterize if the [47mskip-proofs[0m [events] are within the scope of an
  [47m[encapsulate][0m in which constrained functions are being introduced.
  In such contexts no clear logical story is maintained; in
  particular, constraints aren't properly tracked for definitions.  A
  proof script involving [47mskip-proofs[0m should be regarded as
  work-in-progress, not as a completed proof with some unproved
  assumptions.  A [47mskip-proofs[0m event represents a promise by the
  author to admit the given event without further axioms.  In other
  words, [47mskip-proofs[0m should only be used when the belief is that the
  proof obligations are indeed theorems in the existing ACL2 logical
  [world].

  ACL2 allows the certification of [books] containing [47mskip-proofs[0m
  [events] by providing the keyword argument [47m:skip-proofs-okp t[0m to
  the [47m[certify-book][0m command.  This is contrary to the spirit of
  certified [books], since one is supposedly assured by a valid
  [certificate] that a book has been ``blessed.'' But certification,
  too, takes the view of [47mskip-proofs[0m as ``work-in-progress'' and so
  allows the author of the book to promise to finish.  When such
  [books] are certified, a warning to the author is printed,
  reminding him or her of the incurred obligation.  When [books]
  containing [47mskip-proofs[0m are included into a session, a warning to
  the user is printed, reminding the user that the book is in fact
  incomplete and possibly inconsistent.  This warning is in fact an
  error if [47m:skip-proofs-okp[0m is [47mnil[0m in the [47m[include-book][0m form; see
  [include-book].

  We conclude with a technical note.  [47mSkip-proofs[0m works by binding the
  [47m[ld][0m special [47m[ld-skip-proofsp][0m to [47mt[0m unless it is already bound to a
  non-[47mnil[0m value; see [ld-skip-proofsp].")
 (SLEEP
  (PROGRAMMING)
  "Sleep for some number of seconds

  The call [47m(sleep n)[0m returns [47mnil[0m.  However, it takes approximately [47mn[0m
  seconds of real time for this call to return.

  The [guard] for [47msleep[0m requires its argument to be a non-negative
