 (STANDARD-CHAR-LISTP
  (CHARACTERS LISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for a true list of standard characters

  [47m(standard-char-listp x)[0m is true if and only if [47mx[0m is a [47mnil[0m-terminated
  list all of whose members are standard [characters].  See
  [standard-char-p].

  [47mStandard-char-listp[0m has a [guard] of [47mt[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<standard-char-listp>

    (defun standard-char-listp (l)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (cond ((consp l)
             (and (characterp (car l))
                  (standard-char-p (car l))
                  (standard-char-listp (cdr l))))
            (t (equal l nil))))")
 (STANDARD-CHAR-P
  (CHARACTERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for standard characters

  [47m(Standard-char-p x)[0m is true if and only if [47mx[0m is a ``standard''
  character, i.e., a member of the list [47m*standard-chars*[0m.  This list
  includes [47m#\\Newline[0m and [47m#\\Space[0m [characters], as well as the usual
  punctuation and alphanumeric [characters].

  [47mStandard-char-p[0m has a [guard] requiring its argument to be a
  character.

  [47mStandard-char-p[0m is a Common Lisp function.  See any Common Lisp
  documentation for more information.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<standard-char-p>

    (defun standard-char-p (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard (characterp x)))
      (if (member x *standard-chars*) t nil))


Subtopics

  [Standard-char-p+]
      Recognizer for standard characters whose guard is [47mt[0m")
 (STANDARD-CHAR-P+
  (STANDARD-CHAR-P CHARACTERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Recognizer for standard characters whose guard is [47mt[0m

  Logically [47mstandard-char-p+[0m is the same as [47m[standard-char-p][0m.
  However, [47mstandard-char-p+[0m has a guard of [47mt[0m, while [47mstandard-char-p[0m
  is guarded by [47m[characterp][0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<standard-char-p+>

    (defun standard-char-p+ (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (and (characterp x)
           (standard-char-p x)))")
 (STANDARD-CO
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "The character output channel to which [47m[ld][0m prints

  [47mStandard-co[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(standard-co state)[0m and the updater is [47m(set-standard-co val state)[0m.
  [47mStandard-co[0m must be an open character output channel.  It is to
  this channel that [47m[ld][0m prints the [prompt], the form to be
  evaluated, and the results.  The event [command]s such as [47m[defun][0m,
  [47m[defthm][0m, etc., which print extensive commentary do not print to
  [47mstandard-co[0m but rather to a different channel, [47m[proofs-co][0m, so that
  you may redirect this commentary while still interacting via
  [47mstandard-co[0m.  See [proofs-co].

  ``Standard-co'' stands for ``standard character output.'' The initial
  value of [47mstandard-co[0m is the same as the value of [47m[*standard-co*][0m.")
 (STANDARD-OI
  (IO ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "The standard object input ``channel''

  [47mStandard-oi[0m is an [47m[ld][0m special (see [ld]).  The accessor is
  [47m(standard-oi state)[0m and the updater is [47m(set-standard-oi val state)[0m.
  [47mStandard-oi[0m must be an open object input channel, a true list of
  objects, or a list of objects whose last [47m[cdr][0m is an open object
  input channel.  It is from this source that [47m[ld][0m takes the input
  forms to process.  When [47m[ld][0m is called, if the value specified for
  [47mstandard-oi[0m is a string or a list of objects whose last [47m[cdr][0m is a
  string, then [47m[ld][0m treats the string as a file name and opens an
  object input channel from that file, where the connected book
  directory (see [cbd]) is used to resolve relative pathnames.  The
  channel opened by [47m[ld][0m is closed by [47m[ld][0m upon termination.

  ``Standard-oi'' stands for ``standard object input.'' The
  read-eval-print loop in [47m[ld][0m reads the objects in [47mstandard-oi[0m and
  treats them as forms to be evaluated.  The initial value of
  [47mstandard-oi[0m is the same as the value of [47m[*standard-oi*][0m.")
 (STANDARD-PART
  (REAL)
  "ACL2(r) function mapping limited numbers to standard numbers

  [47m(Standard-part x)[0m is, for a given [47m[i-limited][0m number [47mx[0m, the unique
  real number infinitesimally close (see [i-close]) to [47mx[0m.  This
  function is only defined in ACL2(r) (see [real]).")
 (STANDARDP
  (REAL)
  "ACL2(r) recognizer for standard objects

  [47m(Standardp x)[0m is true if and only if [47mx[0m is a ``standard'' object.
  This notion of ``standard'' comes from non-standard analysis and is
  discussed in Ruben Gamboa's dissertation.  In brief, all the
  familiar objects are standard: e.g., the familiar real numbers are
  standard, but non-zero infinitesimals are not standard, and the
  familiar integers are standard, but not those that exceed every
  integer that you can express in the usual way (1, 2, 3, and so on).
  Similarly, the familiar lists are standard, but not so a list that
  contains a large number of integers, where ``large'' means more
  than the standard integers.  The set of standard numbers is closed
  under the usual arithmetic operations, hence the sum of a standard
  number and a non-zero infinitesimal is not standard, though it is
  what is called ``limited'' (see [i-limited]).

  This predicate is only defined in ACL2(r) (see [real]).")
 (START-HERE
