  (LISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Test if every [47m[member][0m of one list is a [47m[member][0m of the other

    General Forms:
    (subsetp x y)
    (subsetp x y :test 'eql)   ; same as above (eql as equality test)
    (subsetp x y :test 'eq)    ; same, but eq is equality test
    (subsetp x y :test 'equal) ; same, but equal is equality test

  [47m(Subsetp x y)[0m is true if and only if every [47m[member][0m of the list [47mx[0m is
  a [47mmember[0m of the list [47my[0m.  The optional keyword, [47m:TEST[0m, has no effect
  logically, but provides the test (default [47m[eql][0m) used for comparing
  members of the two lists.

  The [guard] for a call of [47msubsetp[0m depends on the test.  In all cases,
  both arguments must satisfy [47m[true-listp][0m.  If the test is [47m[eql][0m,
  then one of the arguments must satisfy [47m[eqlable-listp][0m.  If the
  test is [47m[eq][0m, then one of the arguments must satisfy
  [47m[symbol-listp][0m.

  See [equality-variants] for a discussion of the relation between
  [47msubsetp[0m and its variants:

      [47m(subsetp-eq x lst)[0m is equivalent to [47m(subsetp x lst :test 'eq)[0m;

      [47m(subsetp-equal x lst)[0m is equivalent to [47m(subsetp x lst :test 'equal)[0m.

  In particular, reasoning about any of these primitives reduces to
  reasoning about the function [47msubsetp-equal[0m.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<subsetp-equal>

    (defun subsetp-equal (x y)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (true-listp y) (true-listp x))))
      (cond ((endp x) t)
            ((member-equal (car x) y)
             (subsetp-equal (cdr x) y))
            (t nil)))

  [47mSubsetp[0m is defined by Common Lisp.  See any Common Lisp documentation
  for more information.")
