 (SUBVERSIVE-RECURSIONS
  (MISCELLANEOUS)
  "Why we restrict [encapsulate]d recursive functions

  Subtleties arise when one of the ``constrained'' functions, [47mf[0m,
  introduced in the [signature] of an [47m[encapsulate][0m event, is
  involved in the termination argument for a non-local recursively
  defined function, [47mg[0m, in that [47mencapsulate[0m.  During the processing of
  the encapsulated events, [47mf[0m is locally defined to be some witness
  function, [47mf'[0m.  Properties of [47mf'[0m are explicitly proved and exported
  from the encapsulate as the constraints on the undefined function
  [47mf[0m.  But if [47mf[0m is used in a recursive [47mg[0m defined within the
  encapsulate, then the termination proof for [47mg[0m may use properties of
  [47mf'[0m --- the witness --- that are not explicitly set forth in the
  constraints stated for [47mf[0m.

  Such recursive [47mg[0m are said be ``subversive'' because if naively
  treated they give rise to unsound induction schemes or (via
  functional instantiation) recurrence equations that are impossible
  to satisfy.  We illustrate what could go wrong below.

  Subversive recursions are not banned outright.  Instead, they are
  treated as part of the constraint.  That is, in the case above, the
  definitional equation for [47mg[0m becomes one of the constraints on [47mf[0m.
  This is generally a severe restriction on future functional
  instantiations of [47mf[0m.  In addition, ACL2 removes from its knowledge
  of [47mg[0m any suggestions about legal inductions to ``unwind'' its
  recursion.

  What should you do?  Often, the simplest response is to move the
  offending recursive definition, e.g., [47mg[0m, out of the encapsulate.
  That is, introduce [47mf[0m by constraint and then define [47mg[0m as an
  ``independent'' event.  You may need to constrain ``additional''
  properties of [47mf[0m in order to admit [47mg[0m, e.g., constrain it to reduce
  some ordinal measure.  However, by separating the introduction of [47mf[0m
  from the admission of [47mg[0m you will clearly identify the necessary
  constraints on [47mf[0m, functional instantiations of [47mf[0m will be simpler,
  and [47mg[0m will be a useful function which suggests inductions to the
  theorem prover.

  Note that the functions introduced in the [signature] should not even
  occur ancestrally in the termination proofs for non-local recursive
  functions in the encapsulate.  That is, the constrained functions
  of an encapsulate should not be reachable in the dependency graph
  of the functions used in the termination arguments of recursive
  functions in encapsulate.  If they are reachable, their definitions
  become part of the constraints.

  The following event illustrates the problem posed by subversive
  recursions.

    (encapsulate
      (((f *) => *))
      (local (defun f (x) (cdr x)))
      (defun g (x)
        (if (consp x) (not (g (f x))) t)))

  Suppose, contrary to how ACL2 works, that the encapsulate above were
  to introduce no constraints on [47mf[0m on the bogus grounds that the only
  use of [47mf[0m in the encapsulate is in an admissible function.  We
  discuss the plausibility of this bogus argument in a moment.

  Then it would be possible to prove the theorem:

    (defthm f-not-identity
      (not (equal (f '(a . b)) '(a . b)))
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :use (:instance g (x '(a . b))))))

  simply by observing that if [47m(f '(a . b))[0m were [47m'(a . b)[0m, then [47m(g '(a .
  b))[0m would be [47m(not (g '(a . b)))[0m, which is impossible.

  But then we could functionally instantiate [47mf-not-identity[0m, replacing
  [47mf[0m by the identity function, to prove [47mnil[0m!  This is bad.

    (defthm bad
      nil
      :rule-classes nil
      :hints
      ((\"Goal\" :use (:functional-instance f-not-identity (f identity)))))

  This sequence of events was legal in versions of ACL2 prior to
  Version 1.5.  When we realized the problem we took steps to make it
  illegal.  However, our steps were insufficient and it was possible
  to sneak in a subversive function (via mutual recursion) as late as
  Version 2.3.

  We now turn to the plausibility of the bogus argument above.  Why
  might one even be tempted to think that the definition of [47mg[0m above
  poses no constraint on [47mf[0m?  Here is a very similar encapsulate.

    (encapsulate (((f *) => *))
      (local (defun f (x) (cdr x)))
      (defun map (x)
        (if (consp x)
            (cons (f x) (map (cdr x)))
          nil)))

  Here [47mmap[0m plays the role of [47mg[0m above.  Like [47mg[0m, [47mmap[0m calls the
  constrained function [47mf[0m.  But [47mmap[0m truly does not constrain [47mf[0m.  In
  particular, the definition of [47mmap[0m could be moved ``out'' of the
  encapsulate so that [47mmap[0m is introduced afterwards.  The difference
  between [47mmap[0m and [47mg[0m is that the constrained function plays no role in
  the termination argument for the one but does for the other.

  As a ``user-friendly'' gesture, ACL2 implicitly moves [47mmap[0m-like
  functions out of encapsulations; logically speaking, they are
  introduced after the encapsulation.  This simplifies the
  constraint.  When the constraint cannot be thus simplified the user
  is advised, via the ``infected'' warning, to phrase the
  encapsulation in the simplest way possible.  See
  [infected-constraints].

  The lingering bug between Versions 1.5 and 2.3 mentioned above was
  due to our failure to detect the [47mg[0m-like nature of some functions
  when they were defined in mutually recursively cliques with other
  functions.  The singly recursive case was recognized.  The bug
  arose because our detection ``algorithm'' was based on the
  ``suggested inductions'' left behind by successful definitions.  We
  failed to recall that mutually-recursive definitions do not, as of
  this writing, make any suggestions about inductions and so did not
  leave any traces of their subversive natures.

  We conclude by elaborating on the criterion ``involved in the
  termination argument'' mentioned at the outset.  Suppose that
  function [47mf[0m is recursively defined in an [47m[encapsulate][0m, where the
  body of [47mf[0m has no ``ancestor'' among the functions introduced in the
  signature of that [47m[encapsulate][0m, i.e.: the body contains no call of
  a signature function, no call of a function whose body calls a
  signature function, and so on.  Then ACL2 treats [47mf[0m as though it is
  defined in front of that [47mencapsulate[0m form; so [47mf[0m is not [47mconstrained[0m
  by the encapsulate, and its definition is hence certainly not
  subversive in the sense discussed above.  But suppose to the
  contrary that some function introduced in the signature is an
  ancestor of the body of [47mf[0m.  Then the definition of [47mf[0m is subversive
  if moreover, its termination proof obligation has an ancestor among
  the signature functions.  Now, that proof obligation involves terms
  from the first argument of selected calls of [47mIF[0m, as well as
  recursive calls; for a detailed discussion, see [rulers].  The
  important point here is that for the recursive calls, only the
  arguments in ``measured'' positions are relevant to the termination
  proof obligation.  Consider the following example.

    (encapsulate
     (((h *) => *))
     (local (defun h (n) n))
     (defun f (i n)
       (if (zp i)
           n
         (f (1- i) (h n)))))

  ACL2 heuristically picks the measure [47m(acl2-count i)[0m for the
  definition of [47mf[0m; thus, [47mi[0m is the only ``measured formal'' of [47mf[0m.
  Since [47mi[0m is the first formal of [47mf[0m, then for the recursive call of [47mf[0m,
  only the first argument contributes to the termination proof
  obligation: in this case, [47m(1- i)[0m but not [47m(h n)[0m.  Thus, even though
  [47mh[0m is a signature function, the definition of [47mf[0m is not considered to
  be subversive; an induction scheme is thus stored for [47mf[0m.  (This
  restriction to measured formal positions of recursive calls, for
  determining subversive definitions, is new in Version_3.5 of ACL2.)")
