 (SYNTAXP
  (REWRITE DEFINITION LINEAR META)
  "Attach a heuristic filter on a rule

  A call of [47msyntaxp[0m in the hypothesis of a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m,
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite-quoted-constant][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m, or [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rule is
  treated specially, as described below.  Similar treatment is given
  to the evaluation of a [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rule's hypothesis function call.

  For example, consider the [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule created from the following
  formula.

    Example:
    (IMPLIES (SYNTAXP (NOT (AND (CONSP X)
                                (EQ (CAR X) 'NORM))))
             (EQUAL (LXD X)
                    (LXD (NORM X)))).

  The [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis in this rule will allow the rule to be applied
  to [47m(lxd (trn a b))[0m but will not allow it to be applied to [47m(lxd
  (norm a))[0m.

    General Form:
    (SYNTAXP test)

  [47mSyntaxp[0m always returns [47mt[0m and so may be added as a vacuous hypothesis.
  However, when relieving the hypothesis, the test ``inside'' the
  [47msyntaxp[0m form is actually treated as a meta-level proposition about
  the proposed instantiation of the rule's variables and that
  proposition must evaluate to true (non-[47mnil[0m) to ``establish'' the
  [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis.

  Note that the test of a [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis does not, in general, deal
  with the meaning or semantics or values of the terms, but rather
  with their syntactic forms.  In the example above, the [47msyntaxp[0m
  hypothesis allows the rule to be applied to every target of the
  form [47m(lxd u)[0m, provided [47mu[0m is not of the form [47m(norm v)[0m.  Observe that
  without this syntactic restriction the rule above could loop,
  producing a sequence of increasingly complex targets [47m(lxd a)[0m, [47m(lxd
  (norm a))[0m, [47m(lxd (norm (norm a)))[0m, etc.  An intuitive reading of the
  rule might be ``[47mnorm[0m the argument of [47mlxd[0m unless it has already been
  [47mnorm[0med.''

  Note also that a [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis deals with the syntactic form
  used internally by ACL2, rather than that seen by the user.  In
  some cases these are the same, but there can be subtle differences
  with which the writer of a [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis must be aware.  You
  can use [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m to display this internal representation.

  There are two types of [47msyntaxp[0m hypotheses.  The simpler type may be a
  hypothesis of a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m, [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m, or [47m:[0m[47m[linear][0m rule
  provided [47mtest[0m contains at least one variable but no free variables
  (see [free-variables]).  In particular, [47mtest[0m may not use [stobj]s;
  any stobj name will be treated as an ordinary variable.  The case
  of [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m rules is similar to the above, except that it applies to
  the result of applying the hypothesis metafunction.  (Later below
  we will describe the second type, an [3mextended[0m [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis,
  which may use [47m[state][0m.)

  We illustrate the use of simple [47msyntaxp[0m hypotheses by slightly
  elaborating the example given above.  Consider a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule:

    (IMPLIES (AND (RATIONALP X)
                  (SYNTAXP (NOT (AND (CONSP X)
                                     (EQ (CAR X) 'NORM)))))
             (EQUAL (LXD X)
                    (LXD (NORM X))))

  How is this rule applied to [47m(lxd (trn a b))[0m?  First, we form a
  substitution that instantiates the left-hand side of the conclusion
  of the rule so that it is identical to the target term.  In the
  present case, the substitution replaces [47mx[0m with [47m(trn a b)[0m.

    (LXD X) ==> (LXD (trn a b)).

  Then we backchain to establish the hypotheses, in order.  Ordinarily
  this means that we instantiate each hypothesis with our
  substitution and then attempt to rewrite the resulting instance to
  true.  Thus, in order to relieve the first hypothesis above, we
  rewrite

    (RATIONALP (trn a b)).

  If this rewrites to true, we continue.

  Of course, many users are aware of some exceptions to this general
  description of the way we relieve hypotheses.  For example, if a
  hypothesis contains a ``free-variable'' --- one not bound by the
  current substitution --- we attempt to extend the substitution by
  searching for an instance of the hypothesis among known truths.
  See [free-variables].  [47m[Force][0md hypotheses are another exception to
  the general rule of how hypotheses are relieved.

  Hypotheses marked with [47msyntaxp[0m, as in [47m(syntaxp test)[0m, are also
  exceptions.  We instantiate such a hypothesis; but instead of
  rewriting the instantiated instance, we evaluate the instantiated
  [47mtest[0m.  More precisely, we evaluate [47mtest[0m in an environment in which
  its variable symbols are bound to the quotations of the terms to
  which those variables are bound in the instantiating substitution.
  So in the case in point, we (in essence) evaluate

    (NOT (AND (CONSP '(trn a b)) (EQ (CAR '(trn a b)) 'NORM))).

  This clearly evaluates to [47mt[0m.  When a [47msyntaxp[0m test evaluates to true,
  we consider the [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis to have been established; this
  is sound because logically [47m(syntaxp test)[0m is [47mt[0m regardless of [47mtest[0m.
  If the test evaluates to [47mnil[0m, we act as though we cannot establish
  the hypothesis and abandon the attempt to apply the rule; it is
  always sound to give up.

  The acute reader will have noticed something odd about the form

    (NOT (AND (CONSP '(trn a b)) (EQ (CAR '(trn a b)) 'NORM))).

  When relieving the first hypothesis, [47m(RATIONALP X)[0m, we substituted
  [47m(trn a b)[0m for [47mX[0m; but when relieving the second hypothesis, [47m(SYNTAXP
  (NOT (AND (CONSP X) (EQ (CAR X) 'NORM))))[0m, we substituted the
  quotation of [47m(trn a b)[0m for [47mX[0m.  Why the difference?  Remember that
  in the first hypothesis we are talking about the value of [47m(trn a b)[0m
  --- is it rational --- while in the second one we are talking about
  its syntactic form.  Remember also that Lisp, and hence ACL2,
  evaluates the arguments to a function before applying the function
  to the resulting values. Thus, we are asking ``Is the list [47m(trn a
  b)[0m a [47m[consp][0m and if so, is its [47m[car][0m the symbol [47mNORM[0m?'' The [47mquote[0ms
  on both [47m(trn a b)[0m and [47mNORM[0m are therefore necessary.  One can verify
  this by defining [47mtrn[0m to be, say [47m[cons][0m, and then evaluating forms
  such as

    (AND (CONSP '(trn a b)) (EQ (CAR '(trn a b)) 'NORM))
    (AND (CONSP (trn a b)) (EQ (CAR (trn a b)) NORM))
    (AND (CONSP (trn 'a 'b)) (EQ (CAR (trn 'a 'b)) NORM))
    (AND (CONSP '(trn a b)) (EQ '(CAR (trn a b)) ''NORM))

  at the top-level ACL2 prompt.

  See [syntaxp-examples] for more examples of the use of [47msyntaxp[0m.

  An extended [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis is similar to the simple type
  described above, but it uses two additional variables, [47mmfc[0m and
  [47mstate[0m, which must not be bound by the left hand side or an earlier
  hypothesis of the rule.  They must be the last two variables
  mentioned by [47mform[0m; first [47mmfc[0m, then [47mstate[0m.  These two variables give
  access to the functions [47mmfc-[0mxxx; see [extended-metafunctions].  As
  described there, [47mmfc[0m is bound to the so-called metafunction-context
  and [47mstate[0m to ACL2's [47m[state][0m.  See [syntaxp-examples] for an example
  of the use of these extended [47msyntaxp[0m hypotheses.

  We conclude with an example illustrating an error that may occur if
  you forget that a [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis will be evaluated in an
  environment where variables are bound to syntactic terms, not to
  values.  Consider the following [stobj] introduction (see
  [defstobj]).

    (defstobj st
      (fld1 :type (signed-byte 3) :initially 0)
      fld2)

  The following [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis is ill-formed for evaluation.
  Indeed, ACL2 causes an error because it anticipates that when
  trying to relieve the [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis of this rule, ACL2 would
  be evaluating [47m(fld1 st)[0m where [47mst[0m is bound to a term, not to an
  actual [47mstobj[0m as required by the function [47mfld1[0m.  The error message
  is intended to explain this problem.

    ACL2 !>(defthm bad
             (implies (syntaxp (quotep (fld1 st)))
                      (equal (stp st)
                             (and (true-listp st)
                                  (equal (len st) 2)
                                  (fld1p (car st))))))

    ACL2 Error in ( DEFTHM BAD ...):  The form (QUOTEP (FLD1 ST)), from
    a SYNTAXP hypothesis, is not suitable for evaluation in an environment
    where its variables are bound to terms.  See :DOC SYNTAXP.  Here is
    further explanation:
         The form ST is being used, as an argument to a call of FLD1, where
    the single-threaded object of that name is required.  But in the current
    context, the only declared stobj name is STATE.  Note:  this error
    occurred in the context (FLD1 ST).

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFTHM BAD ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    ACL2 Error in ( DEFTHM BAD ...):  See :DOC failure.

    ******** FAILED ********
    ACL2 !>

  Presumably the intention was to rewrite the term [47m(stp st)[0m when the
  [47mfld1[0m component of [47mst[0m is seen to be an explicit constant.  As
  explained elsewhere (see [free-variables]), we can obtain the
  result of rewriting [47m(fld1 st)[0m by binding a fresh variable to that
  term using [47mEQUAL[0m, as follows.

    (defthm good
      (implies (and (equal f (fld1 st))
                    (syntaxp (quotep f)))
               (equal (stp st)
                      (and (true-listp st)
                           (equal (len st) 2)
                           (fld1p (car st))))))

  The event above is admitted by ACL2.  We can see it in action by
  disabling the definition of [47mstp[0m so that only the rule above, [47mgood[0m,
  is available for reasoning about [47mstp[0m.

    (in-theory (disable stp))

  Then the proof fails for the following, because the [47msyntaxp[0m
  hypothesis of the rule, [47mgood[0m, fails: [47m(quotep f)[0m evaluates to [47mnil[0m
  when [47mf[0m is bound to the term [47m(fld1 st)[0m.

    (thm (stp st))

  However, the proof succeeds for the next form, as we explain below.

    (thm (stp (list 3 rest)))

  Consider what happens in that case when rule [47mgood[0m is applied to the
  term [47m(stp (list 3 rest))[0m.  (See [free-variables] for relevant
  background.)  The first hypothesis of [47mgood[0m binds the variable [47mf[0m to
  the result of rewriting [47m(fld1 st)[0m, where [47mst[0m is bound to the
  (internal form of) the term [47m(list 3 rest)[0m --- and that result is
  clearly the term, [47m'3[0m.  Then the [47msyntaxp[0m hypothesis is successfully
  relieved, because the evaluation of [47m(quotep f)[0m returns [47mt[0m in the
  environment that binds [47mf[0m to [47m'3[0m.


