 (SYS-CALL*
  (SYS-CALL ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Make a system call to the host OS, returning a status

  See [sys-call+] for a closely related utility.  There are only the
  following two differences between [47msys-call*[0m and [47msys-call+[0m.

    * Both return an [error-triple] [47m(mv erp val state)[0m, but for [47msys-call*[0m,
      [47mval[0m is always [47mnil[0m after printing as a side effect like
      [47m[sys-call][0m.  (For [47msys-call+[0m, [47mval[0m is the string produced as
      output by the command.)

    * Logically, [47msys-call*[0m pops the oracle once, not twice.")
 (SYS-CALL+
  (SYS-CALL ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Make a system call to the host OS, returning status and output

    Example Form:
    ; The following returns (mv nil s state), where s is the standard output
    ; from the command: ls -l ./
    (sys-call+ \"ls\" '(\"-l\" \"./\") state)

    General Form:
    (sys-call+ cmd args state)

  where [47mcmd[0m is a command to the host operating system and [47margs[0m is a
  list of strings.  Also see related utilities [sys-call] and
  [sys-call*].  There are three major differences between [47m[sys-call][0m
  and both [47msys-call+[0m and [47msys-call*[0m.  First, the latter two take an
  extra argument, [47mstate[0m.  Second, while [47msys-call[0m returns [47mnil[0m,
  [47msys-call+[0m and [47msys-call*[0m both return an [error-triple], [47m(mv erp val
  state)[0m.  Finally, [47msys-call+[0m and [47msys-call*[0m provide a command to the
  host operating system even during a proof, which could be useful in
  computed hints and clause processors.  While execution returns
  values as listed below, further below we explain the logical return
  values.  In the following, please keep in mind that the exact
  behavior depends on the platform; the description is only a guide.
  For example, on some platforms [47merp[0m might always be [47mnil[0m, even if in
  the error case, and [47mval[0m might or might not include error output.
  (For details, look at the ACL2 source code for function
  [47msystem-call+[0m, whose output is converted by replacing an [47merp[0m of [47mnil[0m
  by 0.)

      [47mErp[0m can be [47mnil[0m, indicating that after the command executes the
      process status returned is 0, indicating a normal exit.
      Otherwise [47merp[0m is a non-zero integer, which is the status
      returned by the process when it completes.

      [47mVal[0m is a string, typically the output generated by the call of [47mcmd[0m.

      [47mState[0m is an ACL2 [state].

  While the description above pertains to the values returned by
  executing [47msys-call+[0m, the logical values are as follows.  For a
  discussion of the [47macl2-oracle[0m field of an ACL2 state, see
  [47m[read-ACL2-oracle][0m and [state].

      [47mErp[0m is the first element of the [47macl2-oracle[0m of the input state if
      that element is a nonzero integer, and otherwise is [47mnil[0m.

      [47mVal[0m is the second element of the [47macl2-oracle[0m of the input state if it
      is a string, else the empty string, [47m\"\"[0m.

      [47mState[0m is the result of popping the [47macl2-oracle[0m field twice from the
      input state.

  Note that unlike [47m[sys-call][0m, a call of [47m[sys-call+][0m has no effect on
  subsequent calls of [47m[sys-call-status][0m.

  As is the case for [47msys-call[0m, a trust tag is required to call
  [47msys-call+[0m.  For discussion of this and more, see [sys-call].")
 (SYS-CALL-STATUS
  (SYS-CALL ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Exit status from the preceding system call

  This function returns two values, [47m(mv status state)[0m.  The first is
  the status resulting from the most recent call to the operating
  system by invoking function [47m[sys-call][0m.  The second is the ACL2
  [47m[state][0m object, which is also the input to this function.

  The function [47m[sys-call][0m provides a command to be executed by the host
  operating system (except when invoked during a proof; see
  [sys-call]) using a function supplied ``under the hood'' by the
  underlying Lisp system.  The status value is an integer, which is
  the status returned by the process when it completes.")
 (SYSFILE
  (BOOKS PROJECT-DIR-ALIST)
  "File representation using ACL2 project directories

  ACL2 supports relocation of book directories so that those books are
  still treated as certified.  See [project-dir-alist].

  A key data structure to support this feature is the [3msysfile[0m, which is
  a pair of the form [47m(:kwd . \"relpath\")[0m.  Here, [47m:kwd[0m is a key of the
  [47mproject-dir-alist[0m, bound to some absolute pathname [47m\"D/\"[0m of a
  directory, and [47m\"relpath\"[0m is a string that denotes a pathname
  relative to directory [47m\"D/\"[0m.  Thus, the sysfile [47m(:kwd . \"relpath\")[0m
  represents the same pathname as [47m\"D/relpath\"[0m.

  (Remark.  The name ``sysfile'' was originally coined to suggest
  ``system file'', suggesting the use of keyword [47m:SYSTEM[0m to indicate
  a file residing in the [community-books].  It now extends to [47m(:kwd
  . \"relpath\")[0m even for [47m:kwd[0m values other than [47m:SYSTEM[0m.  But
  ``sysfile'' is a convenient name, and it still seems reasonable
  since ``system file'' can suggest a file of the filesystem.)

  ACL2 rarely generates output that includes sysfiles; they are mostly
  used in the implementation, for example to denote included books in
  [certificate] files.  But they are occasionally relevant at the
  user level; for example see [add-include-book-dir].

  Finally we discuss the use of sysfiles in [certificate] files.  When
  the [community-books] directory is a prefix of a [full-book-name]
  string, ACL2 may choose to represent that full-book-name as
  [47m(:system . \"relpath\")[0m, where [47m\"relpath\"[0m is the result of removing
  the community-books directory from the front of the full-book-name.
  Here is an example.

    ; full-book-name:
    \"/Users/smith/acl2/acl2/books/std/portcullis.lisp\"

    ; sysfile representation
    ; (where \"/Users/smith/acl2/acl2/books/\" is the community-books directory):
    (:SYSTEM . \"std/portcullis.lisp\")

  This behavior applies to more than the community-books: it applies to
  the entire [47m[project-dir-alist][0m.  If that alist associates keyword
  [47m:K[0m with absolute directory name [47m\"<dir>\"[0m, then a full-book-name with
  prefix [47m\"<dir>\"[0m, say, [47m\"<dir>/relpath\"[0m, is written to a [certificate]
  file as [47m(:K . \"relpath\")[0m.  This capability supports relocating book
  directories; see [project-dir-alist] for a more complete
  discussion.")
 (SYSTEM-ATTACHMENTS
  (PROGRAMMING DEFATTACH)
  "System-level algorithms that users can modify with attachments

  This topic concerns advanced methods for modifying the behavior of
  ACL2.  See [defattach] for some relevant background.

  If you evaluate the form [47m(global-val 'attachments-at-ground-zero (w
  state))[0m, you will see a list of pairs of the form [47m(f . g)[0m, where [47mf[0m
  is a built-in constrained utility and [47mg[0m is its attachment.  At the
  end of this topic is a list that associates each such [47mf[0m with brief
  documentation (often, just a link).  Users are permitted to modify
  these attachments using [47m[defattach-system][0m, even without a trust
  tag (see [defttag]), because they do not affect soundness.

  Here are two functions that are useful for attaching to many
  attachable 0-ary system functions.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<constant-t-function-arity-0>

    (defun constant-t-function-arity-0 nil
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      t)

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<constant-nil-function-arity-0>

    (defun constant-nil-function-arity-0 nil
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
