 (TABLE (EVENTS)
  "User-managed tables

    Examples:
    (table tests 1 '(...))                ; set contents of tests[1] to '(...)
    (table tests 25)                      ; get contents of tests[25]
    (table tests)                         ; return table tests as an alist
    (table tests nil nil :clear)          ; clear table tests
    (table tests nil '((foo . 7)) :clear) ; set table tests to ((foo . 7))
    (table tests nil nil :guard)          ; fetch the table guard
    (table tests nil nil :guard term)     ; set the table guard

    General Form:
    (table table-name key-term value-term op term)

  where [47mtable-name[0m is a symbol that is the name of a (possibly new)
  table; [47mkey-term[0m and [47mvalue-term[0m, if present, are arbitrary terms
  involving (at most) the variables [47mWORLD[0m and [47mENS[0m with exceptions
  noted below; [47mop[0m, if present, is one of the table operations below;
  and [47mterm[0m, if present, is a term.  [47mTable[0m returns an ACL2
  [error-triple].  The effect of [47mtable[0m on [47m[state][0m depends on [47mop[0m and
  how many arguments are presented.  Some invocations actually have
  no effect on the ACL2 [world] and hence an invocation of [47mtable[0m is
  not always an ``event''.  We explain below, after giving some
  background information.

  [31;1mImportant Note:[0m The [47mtable[0m forms above are calls of a macro that
  expands to involve the special variable [47m[state][0m.  This will prevent
  you from accessing a table from within a hint or theory where you
  do not have the [47m[state][0m variable.  However, the form

    (table-alist 'tests world)

  returns the alist representation of the table named [47mtests[0m in the
  given [47m[world][0m.  Often you can provide a suitable expression for
  [47mworld[0m, for example, [47m(w state)[0m.

  The ACL2 system provides ``tables'' by which the user can associate
  one object with another.  Tables are in essence just conventional
  association lists --- lists of pairs --- but the ACL2 environment
  provides a means of storing these lists in the ``ACL2 world'' of
  the current [47m[state][0m.  The ACL2 user could accomplish the same ends
  by using ACL2 ``global variables''; however, limitations on global
  variable names are imposed to ensure ACL2's soundness.  Some
  features of the system use tables, and the user is invited to make
  free use of tables.  By convention, no user-defined table is
  important to ACL2's soundness.  Because tables are stored in the
  ACL2 [world] they are restored by [47m[include-book][0m and undone by
  [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m.  Many users of Nqthm requested a facility by which user
  data could be saved in Nqthm ``lib files'' and tables are ACL2's
  answer to that request.

  Abstractly, each table is an association list mapping ``keys'' to
  ``values.'' In addition, each table has a ``[47m:guard[0m,'' which is a
  term that must be true of any key and value used.  By setting the
  [47m:guard[0m on a table you may enforce an invariant on the objects in
  the table, e.g., that all keys are positive integers and all values
  are symbols.  Note that attachments are allowed when evaluating a
  table guard (see [defattach]).  Each table has a ``name,'' which
  must be a symbol.  Given a table name, the following operations can
  be performed on the table.

  [47m:put[0m --- associate a value with a key (possibly changing the value
  currently associated with that key).

  [47m:get[0m --- retrieve the value associated with a key (or nil if no value
  has been associated with that key).

  [47m:alist[0m --- return an alist showing all keys and non-nil values in the
  table.

  [47m:clear[0m --- clear the table (so that every value is nil), or if val is
  supplied then set table to that value (which must be an alist).

  [47m:guard[0m --- fetch or set the [47m:guard[0m of the table.  See
  [set-table-guard] for a convenient way to set a table's [47m:guard[0m to
  produce user-friendly error messages.

  When the operations above suggest that the table or its [47m:guard[0m are
  modified, what is actually meant is that the current [state] is
  redefined so that in it, the affected table name has the
  appropriate properties.  In such cases, the [47mtable[0m form is an event
  (see [events]).  In the [47m:put[0m case, if the key is already in the
  table and associated with the proposed value, then the [47mtable[0m event
  is redundant (see [redundant-events]).

  [47mTable[0m forms are commonly typed by the user while interacting with the
  system.  [47m:Put[0m and [47m:get[0m forms are especially common.  Therefore, we
  have adopted a positional syntax that is intended to be convenient
  for most applications.  Essentially, some operations admit a
  ``short form'' of invocation.

    (table name key-term value-term :put)   ; long form
    (table name key-term value-term)        ; short form

  evaluates the key- and value-terms, obtaining two objects that we
  call [47mkey[0m and [47mvalue[0m, checks that the [47mkey[0m and [47mvalue[0m satisfy the
  [47m:guard[0m on the named table and then ``modifies'' the named table so
  that the value associated with [47mkey[0m is [47mvalue[0m.  When used like this,
  [47mtable[0m is actually an event in the sense that it changes the ACL2
  [world].  In general, the forms that are evaluated to obtain the
  [47mkey[0m and [47mvalue[0m may involve two variables: [47mWORLD[0m, which is bound to
  the then-current [world]; and [47mENS[0m, which is bound to the enabled
  structure representing the current theory.  (The enabled structure
  is passed as a formal parameter to many built-in functions; for
  example, see [system-utilities] for a description of built-in
  utilities [47menabled-numep[0m and [47menabled-runep[0m.)  However, in the
  following two special cases, the [47mkey[0m and [47mvalue[0m terms may not
  contain any variables: when the table is named
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m; or when the table's [47m:guard[0m is a call of the
  unary function [47mstrict-table-guard[0m, which returns its argument
  unchanged but indicates this restriction on variables for
  specifying keys and values.  Essentially, the keys and values used
  in [events] setting such tables must be explicitly given constants.
  See [ACL2-defaults-table].

    (table name key-term nil :get)          ; long form
    (table name key-term)                   ; short form

  evaluates the key-term (see note below), obtaining an object, [47mkey[0m,
  and returns the value associated with [47mkey[0m in the named table (or,
  [47mnil[0m if there is no value associated with [47mkey[0m).  When used like
  this, [47mtable[0m is not an event; the value is simply returned.

    (table name nil nil :alist)             ; long form
    (table name)                            ; short form

  returns an alist representing the named table; for every key in the
  table with a non-[47mnil[0m associated value, the alist pairs the key and
  its value.  The order in which the keys are presented is
  unspecified.  When used like this, [47mtable[0m is not an event; the alist
  is simply returned.

    (table name nil val :clear)

  sets the named table to the alist [47mval[0m, making the checks that [47m:put[0m
  makes for each key and value of [47mval[0m.  When used like this, [47mtable[0m is
  an event because it changes the ACL2 [world].

    (table name nil nil :guard)

  returns the translated form of the guard of the named table.

    (table name nil nil :guard term)

  This event sets the [47m:guard[0m of the named table to [47mterm[0m, provided the
  following requirements are met.  The named table must be empty and
  it must not have been assigned a [47m:guard[0m yet.  [47mTerm[0m (which is not
  evaluated) should be a term that mentions at most the variables
  [47mKEY[0m, [47mVAL[0m, [47mWORLD[0m, [47mENS[0m, and [47mSTATE[0m.  In the common case [47mterm[0m will
  evaluate to a single value, but it can return two values as
  discussed later below; either way, it must not return [47mSTATE[0m.

  Whenever a subsequent [47m:put[0m occurs, [47mterm[0m will be evaluated with [47mKEY[0m
  bound to the key argument of the [47m:put[0m, [47mVAL[0m bound to the [47mval[0m
  argument of the [47m:put[0m, [47mWORLD[0m bound to the then current [world], [47mENS[0m
  bound to the enabled structure representing the current theory, and
  [47mSTATE[0m bound to the ACL2 [state].  The term is evaluated.  An error
  will be caused by the [47m:put[0m if the result of the evaluation is [47mnil[0m
  when a single value is returned; what if two values are returned?

  If the term returns multiple values [47m(mv okp msg)[0m, then an error will
  be caused if [47mokp[0m is [47mnil[0m.  In that case, if [47mmsg[0m is also [47mnil[0m then a
  generic error message is printed about the illegal key/value pair,
  just as in the single-value return case.  Otherwise [47mmsg[0m should be a
  [47m[msgp][0m --- a string or a cons suitable for printing with the [47m[fmt][0m
  directive, [47m~@[0m.  In that case, [47mmsg[0m is printed (using [47m[fmt][0m [47m~@[0m)
  instead of the generic error message.  Here is a simple example
  adapted from former code in the ACL2 sources.

    (defun my-table-guard (fn val wrld)
      (let ((msg0 ; nil if fn/val is OK as a key/value pair, else a msg
             (my-table-guard-msg fn val wrld)))
        (cond
         (msg0 (mv nil
                   (msg
                    \"Illegal my-table key and value (see :DOC ~
                     memoize-partial):~|key = ~y0value  = ~y1Reason:~%~@2~|~%\"
                    fn val msg0)))
         (t (mv t nil)))))

    (table my-table nil nil
           :guard
           (my-table-guard key val world))

  Note that it is not allowed to change the [47m:guard[0m on a table once it
  has been explicitly set.  Before the [47m:guard[0m is explicitly set, it
  is effectively just [47mt[0m.  After it is set it can be changed only by
  undoing the event that set it.  The purpose of this restriction is
  to prevent the user from changing the [47m:guards[0m on tables provided by
  other people or the system.

  The intuition behind the [47m:guard[0m mechanism on tables is to enforce
  invariants on the keys and values in a table, so that the values,
  say, can be used without run-time checking.  But if the [47m:guard[0m of a
  table is sensitive to the ACL2 [world], it may be possible to cause
  some value in the table to cease satisfying the [47m:guard[0m without
  doing any operations on the table.  Consider for example the [47m:guard[0m
  ``no value in this table is the name of an event.'' As described,
  that is enforced each time a value is stored.  Thus, [47m'bang[0m can be
  [47m:put[0m in the table provided there is no event named [47mbang[0m.  But once
  it is in the table, there is nothing to prevent the user from
  defining [47mbang[0m as a function, causing the table to contain a value
  that could not be [47m:put[0m there anymore.  Observe that not all
  state-sensitive [47m:guard[0ms suffer this problem.  The [47m:guard[0m ``every
  value is an event name'' remains invariant, courtesy of the fact
  that undoing back through an event name in the table would
  necessarily undo the [47m:put[0m of the name into the table.

  [47mTable[0m was designed primarily for convenient top-level use.  Tables
  are not especially efficient.  Each table is represented by an
  alist stored on the property list of the table name.  [47m:Get[0m is just
  a [47mgetprop[0m and [47m[assoc-equal][0m.  [47m:Put[0m does a [47mgetprop[0m to the get the
  table alist, a [47mput-assoc-equal[0m to record the new association, and a
  [47mputprop[0m to store the new table alist --- plus the overhead
  associated with [47m:guard[0ms and undoable [events], and checking (for
  redundancy) if the key is already bound to its proposed value.
  Note that there are never duplicate keys in the resulting [47malist[0m; in
  particular, when the operation [47m:clear[0m is used to install new [47malist[0m,
  duplicate keys are removed from that alist.

  A table name may be any symbol whatsoever.  Symbols already in use as
  function or theorem names, for example, may be used as table names.
  Symbols in use only as table names may be defined with [47m[defun][0m,
  etc.  Because there are no restrictions on the user's choice of
  table names, table names are not included among the logical names.
  Thus, [47m:pe name[0m will never display a table event (for a logical name
  other than [47m:here[0m).  Either [47m:pe name[0m will display a ``normal'' event
  such as [47m(defun name ...)[0m or [47m(defthm name ...)[0m or else [47m:pe name[0m will
  cause an error indicating that [47mname[0m is not a logical name.  This
  happens even if [47mname[0m is in use as a table name.


