 (TAIL-BITING
  (DEBUGGING)
  "Rewriting a true term to [47mNIL[0m

  On rare occasions, a true term can [rewrite] to [47mNIL[0m.  Such
  ``tail-biting'' behavior can make the prover fail to prove a
  theorem but will not make it ``prove'' a non-theorem.  This topic
  explains this behavior, first with one paragraph explaining it in
  high-level terms and then, for those interested in details, with a
  specific example.  That example includes discussion that exposes
  more of the ACL2 implementation than is usually exposed in
  documentation topics, so we expect that most readers will skip it.

  When backchaining to rewrite a hypothesis [47mH[0m of a [rewrite] or
  [linear] rule, ACL2 uses the following heuristic: assume that [47mH[0m is
  false when trying to prove it.  That is sound: proposition [47mP[0m is
  equivalent to proposition [47m(implies (not P) P)[0m.  We do not discuss
  why ACL2 does this or why it rarely results in so-called
  ``tail-biting'': rewriting the hypothesis to [47mNIL[0m by using the fact
  that it has been assumed false.  But that can happen on rare
  occasions.  So if you see a term rewrite to [47mNIL[0m when you know it to
  be true, consider whether this is because it was encountered
  earlier during the backchaining process, when it was assumed false.

  ===== Maybe stop here (lower-level explanation follows)! =====

  The example below shows how tail-biting can happen.  As noted above,
  beware: this explanation is closer to the implementation than is
  found in most of the ACL2 documentation.  We give the example in
  full first, and then we conclude with a more concise summary.  Key
  to this explanation is the notion of the [3mancestors-stack[0m, which is
  a data structure kept by the rewriter as it backchains through
  hypotheses, to record the negation of each hypothesis encountered
  during backchaining.

  We begin with a very simple definition and theorem.  Note that we
  treat [47m[member][0m below as [47m[member-equal][0m, to simplify the exposition.

    (defun copylist (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (cons (car x) (copylist (cdr x)))))

    (defthm key-rule
      (implies
       (not (member a lst)) ; hypothesis later denoted as ``H''
       (not (member a (copylist lst)))))

  The following ``weird'' rule backchains from [47m(member a (cdr lst))[0m to
  [47m(member a lst)[0m, sort of un-opening [47mmember[0m.

    (defthm weird
      (implies (and (consp lst)
                    (member a lst)
                    (not (equal a (car lst))))
               (member a (cdr lst))))

  Now here is our main theorem.  It ought to follow by simplification
  from [47mkey-rule[0m above, if we can just establish the hypothesis [47mH[0m of
  [47mkey-rule[0m from the hypotheses of [47mmain[0m.  The instance of [47mH[0m is [47m(not
  (member aaa (cdr xxx)))[0m.  The rewriter cannot establish this
  because it requires a proof by induction.  So the proof is bound to
  fail here.

    (defthm main
      (implies (and (consp (cdr xxx))
                    (nat-listp xxx)
                    (symbolp aaa))
               (not (member aaa (copylist (cdr xxx)))))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
               :do-not-induct t
               :do-not '(eliminate-destructors))))

  So we decide to monitor key-rule and see why it failed:

    (monitor! '(:rewrite key-rule) t)

    (defthm main ...) ; exactly as above

    :eval
    :a!

  And we see that [47m[brr][0m reports that ``[47m:HYP 1 rewrote to 'NIL[0m'', i.e.,
  that the relevant instance of [47mH[0m, [47m(not (member aaa (cdr xxx)))[0m,
  rewrote to [47mNIL[0m.  This seems to suggest [47maaa[0m is in [47m(cdr xxx)[0m.

  But we know it can't be!  The hypotheses of main say [47maaa[0m is a symbol
  and [47mxxx[0m is a list of natural numbers.  So [47maaa[0m can't be in [47m(cdr
  xxx)[0m. We can prove it:

    (defthm hyps-of-main-imply-hyp-1
      (implies (and (consp (cdr xxx))
                    (nat-listp xxx)
                    (symbolp aaa))
               (not (member aaa (cdr xxx)))))

  And using that rule we can now prove [47mmain[0m:

    (defthm main
      (implies (and (consp (cdr xxx))
                    (nat-listp xxx)
                    (symbolp aaa))
               (not (member aaa (copylist (cdr xxx)))))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
               :do-not-induct t
               :do-not '(eliminate-destructors))))

  So the question is: why did the hypothesis of [47mkey-rule[0m rewrite to
  [47mNIL[0m?

  Let's undo back through [47mhyps-of-main-imply-hyp-1[0m and monitor
  [47mkey-rule[0m, [47mweird[0m, and the definition of [47m[member-equal][0m, and also
  [trace] system function [47mancestors-check-builtin[0m, which queries the
  ancestor-stack, and then repeat the doomed proof attempt for [47mmain[0m.

    (ubt! 'hyps-of-main-imply-hyp-1)
    (monitor '(:rewrite key-rule) ''(:go))
    (monitor '(:rewrite weird) ''(:go))
    (monitor '(:definition member-equal) ''(:go))
    (trace$ ancestors-check-builtin)

    (defthm main
      (implies (and (consp (cdr xxx))
                    (nat-listp xxx)
                    (symbolp aaa))
               (not (member aaa (copylist (cdr xxx)))))
      :hints ((\"Goal\"
               :do-not-induct t
               :do-not '(eliminate-destructors))))

  Here is the series of breaks on (:REWRITE WEIRD), except that the
  second break is elided because you will see at the subsequent ``2x
  (:REWRITE WEIRD) failed...'' that everything that goes on there is
  irrelevant.  Also deleted are some irrelevant calls of
  [47mancestors-check-builtin[0m, but the important one remains.

    (1 Breaking (:REWRITE KEY-RULE) on (MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (COPYLIST (CDR XXX))):
    1 ACL2 >:GO

    (2 Breaking (:REWRITE WEIRD) on (MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (CDR XXX)):
    ...
    2x (:REWRITE WEIRD) failed because :HYP 2 rewrote to
    (MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (CDR XXX)).
    2)

    (2 Breaking (:DEFINITION MEMBER-EQUAL) on (MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (CDR XXX)):
    2 ACL2 >:GO

    (3 Breaking (:REWRITE WEIRD) on (MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (CDR (CDR XXX))):
    3 ACL2 >:GO
    1> (ANCESTORS-CHECK-BUILTIN (MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (CDR XXX))
                                (((MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (CDR XXX))
                                  (MEMBER-EQUAL AAA (CDR XXX))
                                  2 2 0 ((:REWRITE KEY-RULE))
                                  . 1))
                                ((:REWRITE WEIRD)))
    <1 (ANCESTORS-CHECK-BUILTIN T T)

    3 (:REWRITE WEIRD) produced 'T.
    3)

    2 (:DEFINITION MEMBER-EQUAL) produced 'T.
    2)

    1x (:REWRITE KEY-RULE) failed because :HYP 1 rewrote to 'NIL.  (See
    :DOC tail-biting if this surprises you.)
    1)

  So we've entered the break on [47mkey-rule[0m and backchained to prove its
  hypothesis, [47m(not (member aaa (cdr xxx)))[0m.  We thus assume the
  negation, [47m(member aaa (cdr xxx))[0m, on the ancestors stack and then
  open [47m(member aaa (cdr xxx))[0m with the definition.  That results in a
  call of [47m(member aaa (cdr (cdr xxx)))[0m and we backchain through [47mweird[0m
  to [47m(member aaa (cdr xxx))[0m and find it assumed true (on the
  ancestors-stack).  So [47mweird[0m rewrites [47m(member-aaa (cdr (cdr xxx)))[0m
  to [47mT[0m (propositionally) and so [47mmember-equal[0m returns [47mT[0m, so [47mH[0m rewrites
  to [47mNIL[0m.

  This is not unsound; it is just tail biting.  Here is a summary of
  what has happened.

  1. Attempt to rewrite [47m(not (member aaa (copylist (cdr xxx))))[0m to [47mT[0m.

  2. Attempt to rewrite [47m(member aaa (copylist (cdr xxx)))[0m to [47mNIL[0m.

  3. Backchain with [47mkey-rule[0m to [47m(not (member aaa (cdr xxx)))[0m.

  4. Assume [47m(member aaa (cdr xxx))[0m by putting it on the
  ancestors-stack.  This is sound because we are trying to prove [47m(not
  (member aaa (cdr xxx)))[0m, and it is sound to assume [47m(not P)[0m when
  proving [47mP[0m.

  5. Expand [47m(member aaa (cdr xxx))[0m, given [47m(consp (cdr xxx))[0m, to [47m(or
  (equal aaa (cadr xxx)) (member aaa (cddr xxx)))[0m

  6. Rewrite [47m(member aaa (cddr xxx))[0m) using [47mweird[0m.

  7. Backchain with weird on (member aaa (cddr xxx)) and relieve its
  hypotheses under the substitution [47ma := aaa[0m, [47mlst := (cdr xxx)[0m.

      * a. [47m(consp (cdr xxx))[0m is true (hypothesis of [47mmain[0m).

      * b. [47m(member aaa (cdr xxx))[0m is true (TAIL BITING!).

      * c. [47m(not (equal aaa (car (cdr xxx))))[0m is true, presumably from
        expansion of hypotheses of [47mmain[0m.

  8. So [47mweird[0m applies, hence the following are true:

      * From 6. [47m(member aaa (cddr xxx))[0m

      * From 5. [47m(member aaa (cdr xxx))[0m

