 (TAME
  (APPLY$)
  "Definitions of the various notions of tameness

  The adjective ``tame'' can be applied to four different kinds of
  objects, [47mLAMBDA[0m objects, function symbols, expressions, and lists
  of expressions.  Formally, these notions are defined mutually
  recursively as the macro [47mtamep-lambdap[0m and the [47m:logic[0m mode
  functions named [47mtamep-functionp[0m, [47mtamep[0m, and [47msuitably-tamep-listp[0m,
  respectively.  We exhibit the formal definitions at the end of this
  documentation.


Definitions

    * [31;1mtame [47mLAMBDA[0m[31;1m object[0m aka [47mtamep-lambdap[0m: An object is a tame [47mLAMBDA[0m
      object if is of the form [47m(LAMBDA vars body)[0m or [47m(LAMBDA vars dcl
      body)[0m where [47mvars[0m is a list of symbols and [47mbody[0m is a tame
      expression.  Formally, an object [47mx[0m is a tame [47mLAMBDA[0m object iff
      [47m(tamep-lambdap x)[0m.  [47mTamep-lambdap[0m is actually a macro.

    * [31;1mtame function[0m aka [47mtamep-functionp[0m: An object is a [3mtame function[0m iff
      it is either (a) a badged symbol and ilks is [47mT[0m, or (b) a tame
      [47mLAMBDA[0m object (see above).  Formally, an object [47mx[0m is a tame
      function iff [47m(tamep-functionp x)[0m.

    * [31;1mtame expression[0m aka [47mtamep[0m: An object is a [3mtame expression[0m iff it is a
      symbol, a quoted constant, the call of an badged function
      symbol on the correct number of suitably tame expressions with
      respect to the ilks of the function symbol, or the call of a
      tame [47mLAMBDA[0m expression on the correct number of tame
      expressions.  Formally, an object [47mx[0m is a tame expression iff
      [47m(tamep x)[0m.  Note that tameness implies every function symbol in
      the expression is badged but [3mnot necessarily warranted[0m.

    * [31;1msuitably tame with (respect to arity and ilks)[0m aka
      [47msuitably-tamep-listp[0m: A list of objects [47mx[0m is [3msuitably tame[0m with
      respect to an arity [47mn[0m and a list of [47milks[0m iff [47mx[0m is a true list
      of length [47mn[0m, and when an ilk is [47m:FN[0m the corresponding object is
      a [3mquoted[0m tame function, when an ilk is [47m:EXPR[0m the object is a
      [3mquoted[0m tame expression, and when an ilk is [47mNIL[0m the object is a
      tame expression.  Formally, an object [47mx[0m is suitably tame with
      respect to [47mn[0m and [47milks[0m iff [47m(suitably-tamep-listp n ilks x)[0m.
      Note in particular our use of the word ``quoted'' above.  We
      illustrate this in the example below.

  Note that the various notions of tameness make no mention of whether
  the function symbols involved are in [47m:program[0m or [47m:logic[0m mode.  The
  function symbols must have badges but need not have warrants.

  Intuitively, a tame expression can be built out of functions that are
  not themselves tame, e.g., scions, by making sure that every [47m:FN[0m
  slot is occupied by a quoted tame function.  Put another way, if we
  were to trace the calls of [47mapply$[0m while evaluating a tame
  expression every branch eventually bottoms out on a call of a
  primitive.  This is illustrated below.


Examples

  We assume the following events have been processed.

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

    (defun$ sq (x) (* x x))

    (defun$ foldr (lst fn init)
      (if (endp lst)
          init
          (apply$ fn
                  (list (car lst)
                        (foldr (cdr lst) fn init)))))

  [47mSq[0m and [47mfoldr[0m above are defined as [47m:logic[0m mode functions and are
  badged and warranted (note the use of [47mdefun$[0m).  But for our
  purposes they could have been introduced by [47mdefun[0m in [47m:program[0m mode
  and then badged with two calls of [47mdefbadge[0m.

  As a result, we see the following badges:

    symbol           badge

    CONS    (APPLY$-BADGE 2 1 . T)  ; . T means all args ordinary
    SQ      (APPLY$-BADGE 1 1 . T)  ; . T means all args ordinary
    FOLDR   (APPLY$-BADGE 1 1 NIL :FN NIL)

  Consider the following expression.

    (foldr lst (lambda$ (x y) (foldr y 'cons (list (sq x)))) nil)

  Forgetting about tameness for a moment, what does this term compute?
  Study the definition of [47mfoldr[0m if you're not familiar with it and
  work it out!  Answer: It collects, in reverse order, the squares of
  the elements of [47mlst[0m.  E.g., if [47mlst[0m is [47m(1 2 3 4)[0m the value of the
  [47mfoldr[0m expression above is [47m(16 9 4 1)[0m.

  But we are interested in whether it is tame.  Tameness is a concept
  applied to objects representing formal (fully translated) terms (or
  components of terms in the case of ``tame functions,'' ``tame
  lambdas,'' etc.).  Saying ``[3mx[0m is a tame expression'' is just a
  colloquial way of saying ``[47m(tamep [0m[3mx[0m[47m)[0m = [47mt[0m.  So we can evaluate this
  term in the top-level ACL2 loop,

    (tamep '(foldr lst (lambda$ (x y) (foldr y 'cons (list (sq x)))) nil))

  and the result is [47mnil[0m because [47mlambda$[0m is not a ``badged function
  symbol.'' In fact, [47mlambda$[0m is not a function symbol.  It is a
  macro.  We're really interested in the formal translation of the
  [47mfoldr[0m expression.  One way to understand the situation is that
  [47mtamep[0m does not expand macros.

  The translation of the above term is

    (FOLDR LST
           '(LAMBDA (X Y)
                    (DECLARE (IGNORABLE X Y))
                    (RETURN-LAST 'PROGN
                                 '(LAMBDA$ (X Y)
                                           (FOLDR Y 'CONS (LIST (SQ X))))
                                 (FOLDR Y 'CONS (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL))))
           'NIL)

  The [47mRETURN-LAST[0m subterm is a marker that records the original
  user-supplied [47mlambda$[0m expression, which is needed during
  compilation and execution in raw Lisp.  But the value of that
  [47mRETURN-LAST[0m is the expected [47mFOLDR[0m expression in its last argument.
  To simplify our discussion of tameness let's determine whether the
  following equivalent term is tame.

    (FOLDR LST
           '(LAMBDA (X Y)
              (FOLDR Y
                     'CONS
                     (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL)))
           'NIL)

  The answer is yes, it is a tame expression.  In particular

    (tamep '(FOLDR LST
                   '(LAMBDA (X Y)
                      (FOLDR Y
                             'CONS
                             (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL)))
                   'NIL))
    = T

  By the way, [47mtamep[0m answers the same way on the quotation of the actual
  translation of the [47mfoldr[0m term.

  We explain below how [47mtamep[0m computes this result.  But before we dive
  into details consider what this example illustrates.  [47mFOLDR[0m is [3mnot
  a tame function[0m according to the definitions above. But it can be
  used in the construction of tame expressions provided, mainly, that
  its second argument is a quoted tame function.  Indeed, the example
  illustrates that we can even call [47mFOLDR[0m within the [47mLAMBDA[0m
  expression passed to another [47mFOLDR[0m and still have a tame
  expression.

  If we were to [47m[trace$][0m the functions [47mtamep[0m, [47mtamep-functionp[0m, and
  [47msuitably-tamep-listp[0m, and then call [47mtamep[0m on the [47mFOLDR[0m term above
  we would see a tree of calls of the various tameness notions.  Here
  are selected calls from that tree.  All of the calls return [47mT[0m.  We
  discuss each of these calls below.

    (tamep '(CONS (SQ X) 'NIL))                                    ; [1]

    (suitably-tamep-listp 3                                        ; [2]
                          '(NIL :FN NIL)
                          '(Y 'CONS (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL)))

    (tamep '(FOLDR Y 'CONS (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL)))                    ; [3]

    (tamep-functionp '(LAMBDA (X Y)                                ; [4]
                              (FOLDR Y 'CONS (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL))))

    (tamep '(FOLDR LST                                             ; [5]
                   '(LAMBDA (X Y)
                            (FOLDR Y 'CONS (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL)))
                   'NIL))

  [47m[1][0m: The object here, [47m(CONS (SQ X) 'NIL)[0m, is tame because both [47mCONS[0m
  and [47mSQ[0m are tame functions and they are applied to the correct
  number of tame expressions.

  [47m[2][0m: The list of objects here, [47m(Y 'CONS (CONS (SQ X) 'NIL))[0m is a
  suitably tame list of length [47m3[0m with ilks [47mNIL[0m, [47m:FN[0m, and [47mNIL[0m because
  [47mY[0m and [47m'NIL[0m are both tame and [47m'CONS[0m is a [3mquoted[0m tame function, [47mCONS[0m.

  [47m[3][0m: The object here is tame because [47mFOLDR[0m is a badged function of
  arity 3 and its actuals are suitably tame with respect to its arity
  and ilks as shown by example [47m[2][0m.

  [47m[4][0m: The [47mLAMBDA[0m object here is a tame function because it is of the
  form [47m(LAMBDA vars body)[0m, where [47mvars[0m is the list of symbols [47m(X Y)[0m
  and body is the tame object shown in example [47m[4][0m.

  [47m[5][0m: The object here, a call of [47mFOLDR[0m, is tame because there are 3
  actuals, the first and third are tame expressions and the second is
  a [3mquoted[0m tame function as shown in Example [47m[4][0m.


Logical Definitions

  The various notions of tameness, [47mtamep-functionp[0m, [47mtamep[0m, and
  [47msuitably-tamep-listp[0m, are defined mutually recursively as [47m:logic[0m
  mode functions.  The definition employs the macro [47mtamep-lambdap[0m,
  which is used by [47mtamep-functionp[0m to handle the [47mLAMBDA[0m case.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<tamep-functionp>

    (defun tamep-functionp (fn)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (if (symbolp fn)
          (let ((bdg (badge fn)))
            (and bdg
                 (eq (access apply$-badge bdg :ilks) t)))
        (and (consp fn) (tamep-lambdap fn))))

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<tamep>

    (defun tamep (x)
     (declare (xargs :guard t))
     (cond
      ((atom x) (symbolp x))
      ((eq (car x) 'quote)
       (and (consp (cdr x)) (null (cddr x))))
      ((symbolp (car x))
       (let ((bdg (badge (car x))))
         (cond ((null bdg) nil)
               ((eq (access apply$-badge bdg :ilks) t)
                (suitably-tamep-listp (access apply$-badge bdg :arity)
                                      nil (cdr x)))
               (t (suitably-tamep-listp (access apply$-badge bdg :arity)
                                        (access apply$-badge bdg :ilks)
                                        (cdr x))))))
      ((consp (car x))
       (let ((fn (car x)))
         (and (tamep-lambdap fn)
              (suitably-tamep-listp (length (cadr fn))
                                    nil (cdr x)))))
      (t nil)))

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<suitably-tamep-listp>

    (defun suitably-tamep-listp (n flags args)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (natp n) (true-listp flags))))
      (cond ((zp n) (null args))
            ((atom args) nil)
            (t (and (let ((arg (car args)))
                      (case (car flags)
                        (:fn (and (consp arg)
                                  (eq (car arg) 'quote)
                                  (consp (cdr arg))
                                  (null (cddr arg))
                                  (tamep-functionp (cadr arg))))
                        (:expr (and (consp arg)
                                    (eq (car arg) 'quote)
                                    (consp (cdr arg))
                                    (null (cddr arg))
                                    (tamep (cadr arg))))
                        (otherwise (tamep arg))))
                    (suitably-tamep-listp (- n 1)
                                          (cdr flags)
                                          (cdr args))))))

  [31;1mMacro: [0m<tamep-lambdap>

    (defmacro tamep-lambdap (fn)
      (list 'let
            (list (list 'fn fn))
            '(and (lambda-object-shapep fn)
                  (symbol-listp (lambda-object-formals fn))
                  (tamep (lambda-object-body fn)))))

  At the top-level of the ACL2 loop you can determine whether an object
  satisfies one of these predicates by calling the appropriate formal
  notion on the object.  But because these functions are defined in
  terms of [47m[badge][0m these notions of tameness are evaluable only in
  the evaluation theory (where [47m[warrant][0ms are implicitly assumed).
  If you want to prove that an object is tame, you may need warrant
  hypotheses.  See [guarantees-of-the-top-level-loop].")
