  it constructs are well-formed only if the arguments to
  [47mmake-tau-interval[0m satisfy the rules above; [47mmake-tau-interval[0m does
  not coerce or adjust its arguments in any way.  Thus, it can be
  (mis-)used to create non-intervals.  Here are examples of
  [47mtau-intervalp[0m using [47mmake-tau-interval[0m.

    ; integers: 0 <= x <= 10:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP nil 0 nil 10))      = t

    ; integers: 0 <= x (i.e., the natural numbers):
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP nil 0 nil nil))     = t

    ; violations of domain rules:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP t 0 t 10))          = nil
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'INTEGERP nil 0 nil 10/11))   = nil

    ; violation of rule that bounds must be rational if non-nil:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'ACL2-NUMBERP t 0 t #c(3 5))) = nil

    ; violation of rule that lo <= hi:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'ACL2-NUMBERP t 0 t -10))     = nil

    ; rationals: 0 < x <= 22/7:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'RATIONALP t 0 nil 22/7))     = t

    ; numbers: -10 < x < 10:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval 'ACL2-NUMBERP t -10 t 10))    = t

    ; any: -10 < x < 10:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval nil t -10 t 10))              = t

    : any:
    (tau-intervalp (make-tau-interval nil nil nil nil nil))         = t

  Note that the second-to-last interval, with domain [47mnil[0m contains all
  non-numbers as well as numbers strictly between -10 and 10.  The
  reason is that the interval contains [47m0[0m and all non-numbers are
  coerced to [47m0[0m by the inequality functions.

  Note that the last interval contains all ACL2 objects.  It is called
  the ``universal interval.''")
 (TAU-STATUS
  (TAU-SYSTEM)
  "Query or set tau system status

    Examples:
    (tau-status)
    (tau-status :system t)
    (tau-status :auto-mode nil)
    (tau-status :system t :auto-mode nil)

    General Form:
    (tau-status :system a :auto-mode b)

  where [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m are Booleans.  Both keyword arguments are optional and
  they may be presented in either order.  Value [47ma[0m controls whether
  the [47m[tau-system][0m is used during subsequent proofs.  Value [47mb[0m
  controls whether tau system rules are added automatically
  (``greedily'') when rules of other [47m[rule-classes][0m are added.  If no
  arguments are supplied, this is not an event and just returns an
  [error-triple] indicating the current settings.  See
  [introduction-to-the-tau-system] for background details.

  The two flags are independent.  For example, the tau system may be
  disabled in proof attempts even though it is automatically (and
  silently) extending its database as rules of other classes are
  added.

  Flag (a) is actually toggled by enabling or disabling the
  [47m:[0m[47m[executable-counterpart][0m of [47m[tau-system][0m.  Flag (b) is toggled
  with the function [47m[set-tau-auto-mode][0m, which manipulates the
  [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m.

  This macro expands into zero, one, or two [events], as required by
  the supplied values of flags [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m.

  If no arguments are supplied the form is not an event and simply
  returns (as an [error-triple], [47m(mv nil ans state)[0m) the current
  settings of the two flags.  For example:

    ACL2 !>(tau-system)
     ((:SYSTEM NIL) (:AUTO-MODE T))

  intended to be self-explanatory.")
 (TAU-SYSTEM
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make a rule for the ACL2 ``type checker''

  This documentation topic describes the syntactic form of
  ``tau-system'' rules; these rules extend ACL2's ``type checker.''
  For an introduction to the tau system, see
  [introduction-to-the-tau-system].

  There happens to be a [3mfunction[0m named [47mtau-system[0m, defined as the
  identity function.  Its only role is to provide the rune
  [47m(:EXECUTABLE-COUNTERPART TAU-SYSTEM)[0m, which is used to enable and
  disable the tau system.  Otherwise the function [47mtau-system[0m has no
  purpose and we recommend that you avoid using it so you are free to
  enable and disable the tau system.

  When in the default (``greedy'') mode (see [47m[set-tau-auto-mode][0m),
  every [47m[defun][0m and every [47m:corollary[0m (see [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m) of every
  [47m[defthm][0m stored as a rule [3mof any[0m [47m:rule-class[0m is inspected to
  determine if it is of one of the forms below.  Rules of these forms
  are added to the tau database, even if they are not labeled as
  [47m:tau-system[0m rules, e.g., a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m rule might contribute to the
  tau database!  To add a rule to the tau database without adding any
  other kind of rule, tag it with [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m [47m:tau-system[0m.  If a
  theorem has [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m [47mnil[0m, it is not considered for the tau
  database.

    General Forms:
    [3mBoolean[0m:
    (booleanp (p v))

    [3mEval[0m:
    (p 'const) or
    (p *const*)

    [3mSimple[0m:
    (implies (p v) (q v))

    [3mConjunctive[0m:
    (implies (and (p1 v) ... (pk v)) (q v)), ; Here k must exceed 1.

    [3mSignature Form 1[0m:
    (implies (and (p1 x1) (p2 x2) ...)
             (q (fn x1 x2 ...)))

    [3mSignature Form 2[0m:
    (implies (and (p1 x1) (p2 x2) ...)
             (q (mv-nth 'n (fn x1 x2 ...))))

    [3mBounder Form 1 (or Form 2)[0m:
    (implies (and (tau-intervalp i1)
                  ...
                  (or (equal (tau-interval-dom i1) 'dom1-1)
                      ...)
                  ...
                  (in-tau-intervalp x1 i1)
                  ...)
             (and (tau-intervalp (bounder-fn i1 ...))
                  (in-tau-intervalp [3mtarget[0m
                                    (bounder-fn i1 ...))))

    where [3mtarget[0m is
    (fn x1 ... y1 ...)             in [3mForm 1[0m, and
    (mv-nth 'n (fn x1 ... y1 ...)) in [3mForm 2[0m

    [3mBig Switch[0m:
    (equal (fn . formals) body)

    [3mMV-NTH Synonym[0m:
    (equal (nth-alt x y) (mv-nth x y)) or
    (equal (mv-nth x y) (nth-alt x y))

  The symbols [47mp[0m, [47mq[0m, [47mp1[0m, etc., denote monadic (one-argument)
  Boolean-valued function symbols, or equalities in which one
  argument is constant, arithmetic comparisons in which one argument
  is a rational or integer constant, or the logical negations of such
  terms.  By ``equalities'' we allow [47m[equal][0m, [47m[eq][0m, [47m[eql][0m, and [47m[=][0m.
  By ``arithmetic comparison'' we mean [47m[<][0m, [47m[<=][0m, [47m[>=][0m, or [47m[>][0m.  Any
  of these tau predicates may appear negated.

  The notation [47m(p v)[0m above might stand for any one of:

    (INTEGERP X)
    (EQUAL V 'MONDAY)
    (<= I 16)
    (NOT (EQUAL X 'SUNDAY))

  The different rule forms above affect different aspects of the tau
  system.  We discuss each form in more detail below.

  The documentation below is written as though the tau system is in
  auto mode!  To ensure that the only rules added to the tau system
  are those explicitly assigned to [47m:rule-class[0m [47m:tau-system[0m, you
  should use [47m[set-tau-auto-mode][0m to select manual mode.

    General Form: [3mBoolean[0m:
    (booleanp (p v))

  Here [47mp[0m must be a function symbol and [47mv[0m must be a variable.  Such a
  [47m:tau-system[0m rule adds [47mp[0m to the list of tau predicates.  If [47mp[0m was
  recognized as Boolean when it was defined, there is no need to
  state this rule.  This form is needed if you define a monadic
  Boolean function in such a way that the system does not recognize
  that it is Boolean.

    General Form: [3mEval[0m:
    (p 'const) or
    (p *const*)

  Here [47mp[0m must be a function symbol.  In addition, recall that these
  general tau predicate forms may appear negated.  So the form above
  includes such theorems as [47m(NOT (GOOD-STATEP *INITIAL-STATE*))[0m.  A
  theorem of this form thus records whether a named predicate is true
  or false on the given constant.

  Generally, when the tau system must determine whether an enabled tau
  predicate is true or false on a constant, it simply evaluates the
  predicate on the constant.  This can be impossible or very
  inefficient if [47mp[0m is not defined but constrained, or if [47mp[0m is defined
  in a hard-to-compute way (e.g., [47m(defun p (x) (evenp (ack x x)))[0m
  where [47mack[0m is the Ackermann function), or perhaps if the constant is
  very large.  By proving a [47m:tau-system[0m rule of Eval form, you cause
  the tau system to note the value of the predicate on the constant
  and henceforth to look it up instead of evaluating the definition.

  A difficulty, however, is determining that a slow down is due to the
  evaluation of tau predicates and not some other reason.  The first
  step is determining that tau is slowing the proof down.  See
  [47m[time-tracker-tau][0m for an explanation of [47mTIME-TRACKER-NOTE[0ms output
  during some proofs involving tau reasoning.  These notes can alert
  you to the fact that significant amounts of time are being spent in
  the tau system.  [47m[Time-tracker-tau][0m gives some ways of determining
  whether tau predicate evaluation is involved.  (If worse comes to
  worst, consider the following hack: In the ACL2 source file
  [47mtau.lisp[0m, immediately after the definition of the system function
  [47mev-fncall-w-tau-recog[0m, there is a comment which contains some raw
  Lisp code that can be used to investigate whether tau's use of
  evaluation on constants is causing a problem.)  However, once a
  recognizer and the constants on which it is being evaluated are
  identified, the tau system can be sped up by proving Eval rules to
  pre-compute and store the values of the recognizer on those
  constants.  Alternatively, at the possible loss of some
  completeness in the tau system, the executable-counterpart of the
  recognizer can be disabled.

    General Form: [3mSimple[0m:
    (implies (p v) (q v))

  Here [47mv[0m must be a variable symbol.  This rule builds-in the
  information that anything satisfying [47mp[0m must also satisfy [47mq[0m, i.e.,
  the ``type'' [47mq[0m includes the ``type'' [47mp[0m.  Recall that the forms may
  be negated.  Most of the time, [47mp[0m and [47mq[0m will be predicate symbols
  but it is possible they will be equalities- or
  inequalities-with-constants.  Examples of Simple rules include the
  following, which are in fact built-in:

    (implies (natp x) (integerp x))
    (implies (integerp x) (rationalp x))
    (implies (integerp x) (not (true-listp x)))
    (implies (natp x) (not (< x 0)))
    (implies (symbol-alistp x) (alistp x))

  Because the tau system records the transitive closure of the Simple
  rules, any time a term is known to satisfy [47mnatp[0m it is also known to
  satisfy [47mintegerp[0m and [47mrationalp[0m, and known not to satisfy
  [47mtrue-listp[0m, and known to be non-negative.

    General Form: [3mConjunctive[0m:
    (implies (and (p1 v) ... (pk v)) (q v)), ; Here k must exceed 1.

  The [47mpi[0m and [47mq[0m may be any tau predicates or their negations, [47mv[0m must be
  a variable symbol, and [47mi[0m must exceed 1 or else this is a Simple
  rule.  An obvious operational interpretation of this rule is that
  if an object is known to satisfy all of the [47mpi[0m, then it is known to
  satisfy [47mq[0m.  However, the actual interpretation is more general.
  For example, if an object is known to satisfy all but one of the [47mpi[0m
  and is known not to satisfy [47mq[0m, then the object is known not to
  satisfy the ``missing'' [47mpi[0m.

  For example, the following Conjunctive rule allows tau to conclude
  that if weekday [47mD[0m is not [47mMON[0m, [47mTUE[0m, [47mTHU[0m or [47mFRI[0m, then it is [47mWED[0m:

    (implies (and (weekdayp d)
                  (not (eq d 'MON))
                  (not (eq d 'TUE))
                  (not (eq d 'WED))
                  (not (eq d 'THU)))
             (eq d 'FRI))

  The tau database is not closed under conjunctive rules; they are
  applied dynamically.

    General Form: [3mSignature Form 1[0m:
    (implies (and (p1 x1) (p2 x2) ... (pn xn) dep-hyp)
             (q (fn x1 x2 ... xn)))

  The [47mpi[0m and [47mq[0m may be any tau predicates or their negations, [47mfn[0m must be
  a function symbol of arity [47mn[0m, the [47mxi[0m must be distinct variable
  symbols and [47mdep-hyp[0m may be any term, provided it is not of the [47m(pi
  xi)[0m shape and the only variables in it are the [47mxi[0m.

  The Signature form actually allows multiple tau predicates to be
  applied to each variable, e.g., x1 might be required to be both an
  [47mINTEGERP[0m and [47mEVENP[0m.  The Signature form allows there to be multiple
  hypotheses classified as [47mdep-hyp[0ms, i.e., not fitting any of the
  previous shapes, and they are implicitly just conjoined.  The name
  ``dep-hyp'' is an abbreviation of ``dependent hypothesis'' and
  stems from the fact they often express relations between several of
  the function's inputs rather than type-like constraints on
  individual inputs.

  A Signature rule informs tau that the function [47mfn[0m returns an object
  satisfying [47mq[0m provided that the arguments satisfy the respective [47mpi[0m
  and provided that [47mdep-hyp[0m occurs in the current context.  Note: to
  be precise, dependent hypotheses are relieved only by applying
  ACL2's most primitive form of reasoning, type reasoning (see
  [TYPE-REASONING]) (using [type-set]).  In particular, tau reasoning
  is not used to establish dependent hypotheses.  The presence of a
  [47mdep-hyp[0m in a signature rule may severely restrict its
  applicability.  We discuss this after showing a few mundane
  examples.

  An example Signature rule is

    (implies (and (integer-listp x)
                  (integer-listp y))
             (integer-listp (append x y)))

  Of course, a function may have multiple signatures:

    (implies (and (symbol-listp x)
                  (symbol-listp y))
             (symbol-listp (append x y)))

  Here is a Signature rule for the function [47mpairlis$[0m:

    (implies (and (symbol-listp x)
                  (integer-listp y))
             (symbol-alistp (pairlis$ x y)))

  The tau system can consequently check this theorem by composing the
  last two rules shown and exploiting Simple rule stating that
  symbol-alists are also alists:

    (thm (implies (and (symbol-listp a)
                       (symbol-listp b)
                       (integer-listp y))
                  (alistp (pairlis$ (append a b) y))))

  Since [47ma[0m and [47mb[0m are known to be lists of symbols and a signature for
  [47mappend[0m is that it preserves that predicate, the first argument to
  the [47mpairlis$[0m expression is known to be a list of symbols.  This
  means the Signature rule for [47mpairlis$[0m tells us the result is a
  [47msymbol-alistp[0m, but the previously mentioned Simple rule, [47m(implies
  (symbol-alistp x) (alistp x))[0m, tells us the result is also an
  [47malistp[0m.

  When a Signature rule has an [47mdep-hyp[0m, that hypothesis is not an
  expression in the tau system.  Tau is not used to check that
  hypothesis.  Instead, tau uses the more primitive [type-set]
  mechanism of ACL2.  Here is an example of a Signature rule with a
  [47mdep-hyp[0m:

    (implies (and (natp n)
                  (integer-listp a)
                  (< n (len a)))
             (integerp (nth n a)))

  Note that the last hypothesis is a dependent hypothesis: it is not a
  tau predicate but a relationship between [47mn[0m and [47ma[0m.  It is relieved
  by [type-set].  If one is trying to compute the signature of an
  [47m(nth n a)[0m expression in a context in which [47m(< n (len a))[0m is
  explicitly assumed, then this mechanism would establish the
  dependent hypothesis.  But one can easily imagine an almost
  identical context where, say [47m(< n (len (rev a)))[0m is explicitly
  assumed.  In that context, the Signature rule would not be fired
  because [47m[type-set][0m cannot establish [47m(< n (len a))[0m from [47m(< n (len
  (rev a)))[0m, even though it would be easily proved by rewriting using
  the theorem [47m(equal (len (rev a)) (len a))[0m.

  Note also that if this signature could be phrased in a way that
  eliminates the dependency between [47mn[0m and [47ma[0m it would be more
  effective.  For example, here is a related Signature rule without a
  dependent hypothesis:

    (implies (and (natp n)
                  (register-filep a)
                  (< n 16))
             (integerp (nth n a)))

  In this theorem we require only that [47mn[0m be less than 16, which is a
  tau predicate and hence just an additional tau constraint on [47mn[0m.

    General Form: [3mSignature Form 2[0m:
    (implies (and (p1 x1) (p2 x2) ... (pn xn) dep-hyp)

             (q (mv-nth 'n (fn x1 x2 ... xn))))

  This form of signature rule is just like form 1 except that it is
  useful for functions that return [multiple-value]s and allows us to
  ``type-check'' their individual outputs.

    General Form: [3mBounder Forms 1 and 2[0m:
    (implies (and (tau-intervalp i1)
                  ...
                  (or (equal (tau-interval-dom i1) 'dom1-1)
                      ...)
                  ...
                  (in-tau-intervalp x1 i1)
                  ...)
             (and (tau-intervalp (bounder-fn i1 ...))
                  (in-tau-intervalp [3mtarget[0m
                                    (bounder-fn i1 ...))))

  where [3mtarget[0m is either [47m(fn x1 ... y1 ...)[0m in [3mForm 1[0m or [47m(mv-nth 'n (fn
  x1 ... y1 ...))[0m in [3mForm 2[0m.

  This form is for advanced users only and the schema given above is
  just a reminder of the general shape.  A ``bounder'' for a given
  function symbol, [47mfn[0m, is a function symbol [47mbounder-fn[0m that computes
  an interval containing [47m(fn x1 ... y1 ...)[0m (or its [47mn[0mth component in
  the case of Form 2 rules) from the intervals containing certain of
  the arguments of [47mfn[0m.  The correctness theorem for a bounder
  function informs the tau system that bounds for [47mfn[0m are computed by
  [47mbounder-fn[0m and sets up the correspondence between the relevant
  arguments, [47mxi[0m, of [47mfn[0m and the intervals containing those arguments,
  [47mii[0m to which [47mbounder-fn[0m is applied.  When the tau system computes
  the tau for a call of [47mfn[0m, it computes the tau of the relevant
  arguments and applies the bounder to the intervals of those tau.
  This provides a domain and upper and/or lower bounds for the value
  of the term.  The tau system then further augments that with
  signature rules.  See [bounders] for details on intervals,
  bounders, and bounder correctness theorems.

    General Form: [3mBig Switch[0m:
    (equal (fn . formals) body)

  In the Big Switch form, [47mfn[0m must be a function symbol, [47mformals[0m must be
  a list of distinct variable symbols, and [47mbody[0m must be a ``big
  switch'' term, i.e., one that case splits on tau predicates about a
  single variable and produces a term not involving that variable.
  An example of a Big Switch rule is

    (equal (conditional-type x y)
           (if (consp x)
               (consp y)
               (integerp y)))

  The idea is that the tau system can treat calls of [47mconditional-type[0m
  as a tau-predicate after determining the tau of an argument.

  Since equality-to-constants are tau predicates, a more common example
  of a Big Switch rule is

    (equal (dtypep x expr)
           (case x
                 (STMT (stmt-typep expr))
                 (EXPR (expr-typep expr))
                 (MODULE (module-typep expr))
                 (otherwise nil)))

  This is because [47m(case x (STMT ...) ...)[0m macroexpands in ACL2 to [47m(if
  (eql x 'STMT) ... ...)[0m and [47m(eql x 'STMT)[0m is a tau predicate about
  [47mx[0m.

  Big Switch rules are recognized when a function is defined (if tau is
  in automatic mode).  They generally do not have to be proved
  explicitly, though they might be when mutual recursion is involved.
  Only the first detected Big Switch rule about a function [47mfn[0m is
  recognized.

    General Form: [3mMV-NTH Synonym[0m:
    (equal (nth-alt x y) (mv-nth x y)) or
    (equal (mv-nth x y) (nth-alt x y))

  Rules of this form just tell the tau system that the user-defined
  function [47mnth-alt[0m is synonymous with the ACL2 primitive function
  [47m[mv-nth][0m.  Because ACL2's rewriter gives special handling to [47mmv-nth[0m
  by default, users sometimes define their own versions of that
  function so they can disable them and control rewriting better.  By
  revealing to the tau system that such a synonym has been introduced
  you allow Signature rules of Form 2 to be used.


