 (TC
  (MACROS)
  "translate form and clean it up

    Examples:
    :tc  (member e a)
    :tc  (list (cadr x) (loop$ for e in lst collect (+ 1 e)))
    :tcp (list (cadr x) (loop$ for e in lst collect (+ 1 e)))
    :tca (list (cadr x) (loop$ for e in lst collect (+ 1 e)))

    General Form:
    :tc form
    :tca form
    :tcp form
    (tc 'form)
    (tca 'form)
    (tcp 'form)

  The [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m command prints the translation of a form into a formal
  term.  However, sometimes the translation can be hard to understand
  because various tags and declarations are included, usually for
  execution efficiency.

  For example, consider [47m:trans[0m versus [47m:tcp[0m of [47m(member e a)[0m.

    ACL2 !>:trans (member e a)

    ((LAMBDA (X L)
       (RETURN-LAST 'MBE1-RAW
                    (MEMBER-EQL-EXEC X L)
                    (RETURN-LAST 'PROGN
                                 (MEMBER-EQL-EXEC$GUARD-CHECK X L)
                                 (MEMBER-EQUAL X L))))
     E A)

    => *

    ACL2 !>:tcp (member e a)
     (MEMBER-EQUAL E A)

  The [47m:tc[0m command and its variants, [47m:tca[0m and [47m:tcp[0m, translate the given
  form and then ``clean'' it up, returning the result in an
  [error-triple].  These commands are especially useful when [47m[loop$][0m,
  [scion]s or [47m[lambda$][0m forms are involved in the form to be
  translated.  But they are also useful when [47m[mbe][0m, [47m[return-last][0m,
  [47m[progn$][0m and other special forms are used or show up in the
  translation.  The [47mtc[0m commands are macros that expand into terms
  involving [47m[state][0m, since errors are signaled when ill-formed forms
  are submitted.

    * [47m:tc[0m [3mform[0m --- translate [3mform[0m and remove everything not relevant to the
      logical value.  This is the logical semantics [3mform[0m and is
      returned in the ``internal format'' of ACL2 terms, i.e., macros
      have been expanded and all constants are quoted.  The prover
      will reduce [3mform[0m to this term almost immediately.  Typically,
      this is the term that the rewriter will encounter during a
      proof involving this [3mform[0m.  Of course, rewrite rules can
      further change the form.  However, see the note below
      concerning warrants.

    * [47m:tcp[0m [3mform[0m --- translate [3mform[0m and remove everything not relevant to
      the logical value as above, and then [47m[untranslate][0m, restoring
      the use of such system macros as [47m[lambda$][0m, [47m[let][0m, [47m[and][0m,
      [47m[list][0m, [47m[cadr][0m, and [47m[+][0m and [47m[*][0m.  This is the semantics of [3mform[0m
      in a ``user friendly'' syntax.  The suffix ``p'' in [47m:tcp[0m stands
      for ``pretty.''

    * [47m:tca[0m [3mform[0m --- translate [3mform[0m and then [47muntranslate[0m as noted above.
      This effectively leaves ``annotations'' in place such as a
      [47mprog2$[0m joining each [47mloop$[0m in [3mform[0m to its semantics, [47mdeclare[0m
      forms in [47mlambda[0m objects, and [47m[let][0m forms associating user
      variable names to values.  This term is mainly meant as a
      pedagogical device to help you understand how [47mloop$[0ms are
      translated.  The suffix ``a'' in [47m:tca[0m stands for ``annotated.''

  The terms returned by the three flavors of [47mtc[0m are all provably
  equivalent to each other and to the original [3mform[0m provided the
  necessary warrants are assumed.

  For example, if [47msq[0m is a user-defined function of arity 1 and
  [47m(defwarrant sq)[0m has issued a warrant for [47msq[0m, then

    ACL2 !>:tc (loop$ for e in lst collect (sq e))    ; sq is warranted
    (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR) (SQ LOOP$-IVAR))
             LST)

  Nevertheless, the equivalence of the input and output of [47m:tc[0m above
  cannot be proved unless the warrants are assumed.  That is,

    (thm
      (equal (loop$ for e in lst collect (sq e))
             (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR) (SQ LOOP$-IVAR))
                       LST)))

  will fail with a checkpoint indicating that the warrant for [47msq[0m must
  be provided.  However,

    (thm
      (implies (warrant sq)
               (equal (loop$ for e in lst collect (sq e))
                      (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR) (SQ LOOP$-IVAR))
                                LST))))

  succeeds.

  If there is no warrant for [47msq[0m but there is a badge, then [47m:tc[0m cannot
  clean up the translation because without a warrant the [47mlambda[0m
  object calling [47msq[0m is not [tame].  Thus,

    ACL2 !>:tc (loop$ for e in lst collect (sq e))   ; sq is badged not warranted
    (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                       (RETURN-LAST 'PROGN
                                    '(LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                                              (LET ((E LOOP$-IVAR))
                                                   (DECLARE (IGNORABLE E))
                                                   (SQ E)))
                                    ((LAMBDA (E) (SQ E)) LOOP$-IVAR)))
              LST)

  By the way, if you see a quoted [47mlambda[0m objects like that above in
  output from the prover, it probably means the [47mlambda[0m object
  contains unwarranted user-defined symbols!

  The differences between [47m:trans[0m and the three commands ([47m:tc[0m, [47m:tcp[0m, and
  [47m:tca[0m) are perhaps best illustrated by considering their respective
  outputs on a single [47m[loop$][0m statement.

    ACL2 !>:trans (list (cadr x) (loop$ for e in lst collect (+ 1 e)))

    (CONS (CAR (CDR X))
          (CONS (RETURN-LAST
                 'PROGN
                 '(LOOP$ FOR E IN LST COLLECT (+ 1 E))
                 (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                                    (DECLARE (IGNORABLE LOOP$-IVAR))
                                    (RETURN-LAST 'PROGN
                                                 '(LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                                                    (LET ((E LOOP$-IVAR))
                                                      (DECLARE (IGNORABLE E))
                                                      (+ 1 E)))
                                                 ((LAMBDA (E) (BINARY-+ '1 E))
                                                  LOOP$-IVAR)))
                           LST))
                'NIL))

    => *

    ACL2 !>:tc  (list (cadr x) (loop$ for e in lst collect (+ 1 e)))
     (CONS (CAR (CDR X))
           (CONS (COLLECT$ '(LAMBDA (LOOP$-IVAR)
                                    (BINARY-+ '1 LOOP$-IVAR))
                           LST)
                 'NIL))

    ACL2 !>:tcp (list (cadr x) (loop$ for e in lst collect (+ 1 e)))
     (LIST (CADR X)
           (COLLECT$ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR) (+ 1 LOOP$-IVAR))
                     LST))

    ACL2 !>:tca (list (cadr x) (loop$ for e in lst collect (+ 1 e)))
     (LIST (CADR X)
           (PROG2$ '(LOOP$ FOR E IN LST COLLECT (+ 1 E))
                   (COLLECT$ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-IVAR)
                                      (LET ((E LOOP$-IVAR))
                                           (DECLARE (IGNORABLE E))
                                           (+ 1 E)))
                             LST)))

  First, notice that [47m:trans[0m also reports the output signature of the
  term but the [47m:tc[0m commands do not.  They all return the cleaned up
  translation in an error triple.  Second, [47m:tc[0m returns a term in the
  internal format: the [47mlambda[0m objects are quoted list constants and
  their bodies are in internal format, e.g., note the [47mbinary-+[0m and
  quoted constant [47m1[0m in the output of [47m:tc[0m above.  If you use the [47m:tc[0m
  command on the left-hand side of the conclusion of a [47m:rewrite[0m rule
  the term you see is the term under which the rule is stored, i.e.,
  the rule will be tried to rewrite instances of that term.

  The other two commands have ``prettied up'' the output into a more
  user-friendly format.  Third, the [47m:tca[0m command uses a [47mprog2$[0m to
  show the untranslated [47mloop$[0m as a list constant and then show its
  pretty semantics, with a [47mlet[0m form in the body of the [47mlambda$[0m
  reminding you that [47mloop$-ivar[0m is what was called [47me[0m.

  It is often helpful to look at the semantics of fancy [47mloop$[0ms.

    ACL2 !>:tca (loop$ for x in xlst
                       as  y in ylst
                       collect (+ (* a x) (* b y)))
     (PROG2$
         '(LOOP$ FOR X IN XLST AS
                 Y IN YLST COLLECT (+ (* A X) (* B Y)))
         (COLLECT$+
              (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                       (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                                   (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 2)
                                                   (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                                   (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 2))))
                       (LET ((A (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                             (B (CAR (CDR LOOP$-GVARS)))
                             (X (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
                             (Y (CAR (CDR LOOP$-IVARS))))
                            (DECLARE (IGNORABLE A B X Y))
                            (+ (* A X) (* B Y))))
              (LIST A B)
              (LOOP$-AS (LIST XLST YLST))))

  Notice what happens to the [47m:guard[0m of the [47mlambda$[0m if we insert type
  specifications with [47mof-type[0m.

    ACL2 !>:tca (loop$ for x of-type (satisfies natp) in xlst
                       as  y of-type integer in ylst
                       collect (+ (* a x) (* b y)))
     (PROG2$
      '(LOOP$ FOR X OF-TYPE (SATISFIES NATP)
              IN XLST AS Y OF-TYPE INTEGER
              IN YLST COLLECT (+ (* A X) (* B Y)))
      (COLLECT$+
       (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                (DECLARE (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                                            (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 2)
                                            (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                                            (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 2)
                                            (NATP (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
                                            (INTEGERP (CAR (CDR LOOP$-IVARS))))))
                (LET ((A (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                      (B (CAR (CDR LOOP$-GVARS)))
                      (X (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
                      (Y (CAR (CDR LOOP$-IVARS))))
                     (DECLARE (TYPE (SATISFIES NATP) X)
                              (TYPE INTEGER Y)
                              (IGNORABLE A B X Y))
                     (+ (* A X) (* B Y))))
       (LIST A B)
       (LOOP$-AS (LIST XLST YLST))))

  And notice how the [47m:guard[0m keyword after the [47mcollect[0m in the [47mloop$[0m
  statement is added to the [47m:guard[0m of the generated [47mlambda$[0m.

    ACL2 !>:tca (loop$ for x of-type (satisfies natp) in xlst
                       as  y of-type integer in ylst
                       collect
                       :guard (and (rationalp a)
                                   (complex-rationalp b))
                       (+ (* a x) (* b y)))
     (PROG2$
      '(LOOP$ FOR X OF-TYPE (SATISFIES NATP)
              IN XLST AS
              Y OF-TYPE INTEGER IN YLST COLLECT :GUARD
              (AND (RATIONALP A)
                   (COMPLEX-RATIONALP B))
              (+ (* A X) (* B Y)))
      (COLLECT$+
       (LAMBDA$
        (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
        (DECLARE
          (XARGS :GUARD (AND (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-GVARS)
                             (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-GVARS) 2)
                             (TRUE-LISTP LOOP$-IVARS)
                             (EQUAL (LEN LOOP$-IVARS) 2)
                             (NATP (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
                             (INTEGERP (CAR (CDR LOOP$-IVARS)))
                             (AND (RATIONALP (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
                                  (COMPLEX-RATIONALP (CAR (CDR LOOP$-GVARS)))))))
        (LET ((A (CAR LOOP$-GVARS))
              (B (CAR (CDR LOOP$-GVARS)))
              (X (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
              (Y (CAR (CDR LOOP$-IVARS))))
             (DECLARE (TYPE (SATISFIES NATP) X)
                      (TYPE INTEGER Y)
                      (IGNORABLE A B X Y))
             (+ (* A X) (* B Y))))
       (LIST A B)
       (LOOP$-AS (LIST XLST YLST))))

  Of course, while these declarations play a role in [47m:guard[0m
  verification and execution in raw Lisp, they are irrelevant to the
  formal semantics, as made clear by [47m:tcp[0m.

    ACL2 !>:tcp (loop$ for x of-type (satisfies natp) in xlst
                       as  y of-type integer in ylst
                       collect
                       :guard (and (rationalp a)
                                   (complex-rationalp b))
                       (+ (* a x) (* b y)))
     (COLLECT$+ (LAMBDA$ (LOOP$-GVARS LOOP$-IVARS)
                         (+ (* (CAR LOOP$-GVARS)
                               (CAR LOOP$-IVARS))
                            (* (CADR LOOP$-GVARS)
                               (CADR LOOP$-IVARS))))
                (LIST A B)
                (LOOP$-AS (LIST XLST YLST)))")
