 (TERM
  (MISCELLANEOUS)
  "The three senses of well-formed ACL2 expressions or formulas

  Also see [untranslate] for a relevant utility (and a more abbreviated
  explanation of terms).

    Examples of Terms:

    (cond ((caar x) (cons t x)) (t 0))   ; an untranslated term
    (if (car (car x)) (cons 't x) '0)    ; a translated term
    (car (cons x y) 'nil v)              ; a pseudo-term

  In traditional first-order predicate calculus a ``term'' is a
  syntactic entity denoting some object in the universe of
  individuals.  Often, for example, the syntactic characterization of
  a term is that it is either a variable symbol or the application of
  a function symbol to the appropriate number of argument terms.
  (Note that ACL2 is case-insensitive when dealing with symbols; see
  [symbols].)  Traditionally, ``atomic formulas'' are built from
  terms with predicate symbols such as ``equal'' and ``member;''
  ``formulas'' are then built from atomic formulas with propositional
  ``operators'' like ``not,'' ``and,'' and ``implies.'' Theorems are
  formulas.  Theorems are ``valid'' in the sense that the value of a
  theorem is true, in any model of the axioms and under all possible
  assignments of individuals to variables.

  However, in ACL2, terms are used in place of both atomic formulas and
  formulas.  ACL2 does not have predicate symbols or propositional
  operators as distinguished syntactic entities.  The ACL2 universe
  of individuals includes a ``true'' object (denoted by [47mt[0m) and a
  ``false'' object (denoted by [47mnil[0m), predicates and propositional
  operators are functions that return these objects.  Theorems in
  ACL2 are terms and the ``validity'' of a term means that, under no
  assignment to the variables does the term evaluate to [47mnil[0m.

  We use the word ``term'' in ACL2 in three distinct senses.  We will
  speak of ``translated'' terms, ``untranslated'' terms, and
  ``pseudo-'' terms.

  [3mTranslated Terms: The Strict Sense and Internal Form[0m

  In its most strict sense, a ``term'' is either a legal variable
  symbol, a quoted constant, or the application of an n-ary function
  symbol or closed [47mlambda[0m expression to a true list of n terms.

  The legal variable symbols are symbols other than [47mt[0m or [47mnil[0m which are
  not in the keyword package, do not start with ampersand, do not
  start and end with asterisks, and if in the main Lisp package, do
  not violate an appropriate restriction (see [name]).

  Quoted constants are expressions of the form [47m(quote x)[0m, where [47mx[0m is
  any ACL2 object.  Such expressions may also be written [47m'x[0m.

  Closed [47mlambda[0m expressions are expressions of the form [47m(lambda (v1 ...
  vn) body)[0m where the [47mvi[0m are distinct legal variable symbols, [47mbody[0m is
  a term, and the only free variables in [47mbody[0m are among the [47mvi[0m.

  The function [47mtermp[0m, which takes two arguments, an alleged term [47mx[0m and
  a logical world [47mw[0m (see [world]), recognizes terms of a given
  extension of the logic.  Its definition may be inspected with [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m
  [47mtermp[0m for a complete specification of what we mean by ``term'' in
  the most strict sense.  Most ACL2 term-processing functions deal
  with terms in this strict sense and use [47mtermp[0m as a [guard].  That
  is, the ``internal form'' of a term satisfies [47mtermp[0m, the strict
  sense of the word ``term.''

  [3mUntranslated Terms: What the User Types[0m

  While terms in the strict sense are easy to explore (because their
  structure is so regular and simple) they can be cumbersome to type.
  Thus, ACL2 supports a more sugary syntax that includes uses of
  macros and constant symbols.  Very roughly speaking, macros are
  functions that produce terms as their results.  Constants are
  symbols that are associated with quoted objects.  Terms in this
  sugary syntax are ``translated'' to terms in the strict sense; the
  sugary syntax is more often called ``untranslated.'' Translation
  includes the process of macroexpansion as well as the replacement
  of constant symbols by their quoted values, while checking all the
  rules governing the strict sense of ``term.''

  More precisely, macro symbols are as described in the documentation
  for [47m[defmacro][0m.  A macro, [47mmac[0m, can be thought of as a function,
  [47mmac-fn[0m, from ACL2 objects to an ACL2 object to be treated as an
  untranslated term.  For example, [47m[caar][0m is defined as a macro
  symbol; the associated macro function maps the object [47mx[0m into the
  object [47m(car (car x))[0m.  A macro form is a ``call'' of a macro
  symbol, i.e., a list whose [47m[car][0m is the macro symbol and whose
  [47m[cdr][0m is an arbitrary true list of objects, used as a term.
  Macroexpansion is the process of replacing in an untranslated term
  every occurrence of a macro form by the result of applying the
  macro function to the appropriate arguments.  The ``appropriate''
  arguments are determined by the exact form of the definition of the
  macro; macros support positional, keyword, optional and other kinds
  of arguments.  See [defmacro].

  In addition to macroexpansion and constant symbol dereferencing,
  translation implements the mapping of [47m[let][0m and [47m[let*][0m forms into
  applications of [47mlambda[0m expressions and closes [47mlambda[0m expressions
  containing free variables.  Thus, the translation of

    (let ((x (1+ i))) (cons x k))

  can be seen as a two-step process that first produces

    ((lambda (x) (cons x k)) (1+ i))

  and then

    ((lambda (x k) (cons x k)) (1+ i) k) .

  Observe that the body of the [47m[let][0m and of the first [47mlambda[0m expression
  contains a free [47mk[0m which is finally bound and passed into the second
  [47mlambda[0m expression.

  Translation also maps [47m[flet][0m forms into applications of [47mlambda[0m
  expressions.  See [flet].

  When we say, of an event-level function such as [47m[defun][0m or [47m[defthm][0m,
  that some argument ``must be a term'' we mean an untranslated term.
  The event functions translate their term-like arguments.

  To better understand the mapping between untranslated terms and
  translated terms it is convenient to use the keyword command
  [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m to see examples of translations.  See [trans] and also see
  [trans1].

  Note that translation produces a translated term that need not obey
  code restrictions: a translated term can be used in theorems but
  might not be allowed in definitions (except in [non-executable]
  contexts; see [defun-nx] and see [non-exec]).  Suppose for example
  that we make the following definition, so that [47mfoo[0m returns two
  values (see [mv]).

    (defun foo (x)
      (mv x x))

  Now consider the following translation of a use of [47m[mv-let][0m.

    ACL2 !>:trans (mv-let (a b) (foo x) (+ a b))

    ((LAMBDA (MV)
       ((LAMBDA (A B) (BINARY-+ A B))
        (MV-NTH '0 MV)
        (MV-NTH '1 MV)))
     (FOO X))

    => *

    ACL2 !>

  The first definition below is legal.  But the second definition ---
  which uses the translation of the body of the first definition ---
  is not legal.  That's because a [lambda] application, as with any
  function application, expects each of its arguments to represent a
  single value, but the argument [47m(foo x)[0m returns two values.

    (defun legal-def (x)
      (mv-let (a b) (foo x) (+ a b)))

    (defun illegal-def (x)
       ((LAMBDA (MV)
          ((LAMBDA (A B) (BINARY-+ A B))
           (MV-NTH '0 MV)
           (MV-NTH '1 MV)))
        (FOO X)))

  Finally, we note that the theorem prover prints terms in untranslated
  form.  But there can be more than one correct untranslated term
  corresponding to a given translated term.  For example, the
  translated term [47m(if x y 'nil)[0m can be untranslated as [47m(if x y nil)[0m
  and can also be untranslated as [47m(and x y)[0m.  The theorem prover
  attempts to print an untranslated term that is as helpful to the
  user as possible.  In particular, consider a term of the form [47m(nth
  k st)[0m where [47mst[0m is a single-threaded object (see [stobj]) and the
  [47mkth[0m accessor of [47mst[0m is, say, [47mkn[0m.  The theorem prover typically would
  expand [47m(kn st)[0m to [47m(nth k st)[0m.  If [47mk[0m is large then it could be
  difficult for the user to make sense out of a proof transcript that
  mentions the expanded term.  Fortunately, the untranslation of [47m(nth
  k st)[0m would be [47m(nth *kn* st)[0m; here [47m*kn*[0m would be a constant (see
  [defconst]) added by the [47m[defstobj][0m event introducing [47mst[0m, defined
  to have value [47mk[0m.  The user can extend this user-friendly style of
  printing applications of [47m[nth][0m to stobjs; see [add-nth-alias].
  These remarks about printing applications of function [47m[nth][0m extend
  naturally to function [47m[update-nth][0m.  Moreover, the prover will
  attempt to treat terms as [stobj]s for the above purpose when
  appropriate.  For example, if function [47mfoo[0m has [signature] [47m((foo *
  st) => (mv * * * st))[0m, where [47mst[0m is introduced with [47m(defstobj st f0
  f1)[0m, then the [term] [47m(nth '1 (mv-nth '3 (foo x st0)))[0m will be
  printed as [47m(nth *f1* (mv-nth 3 (foo x st0)))[0m.

  [3mPseudo-Terms: A Common Guard for Metafunctions[0m

  Because [47mtermp[0m is defined in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode, it cannot be used
  effectively in conjectures to be proved.  Furthermore, from the
  perspective of merely guarding a term processing function, [47mtermp[0m
  often checks more than is required.  Finally, because [47mtermp[0m
  requires the logical [world] as one of its arguments it is
  impossible to use [47mtermp[0m as a [guard] in places where the logical
  [world] is not itself one of the arguments.

  For these reasons we support the idea of ``pseudo-terms.'' A
  pseudo-term is either a symbol (but not necessarily one having the
  syntax of a legal variable symbol), a true list of length 2
  beginning with [47mquote[0m, or the ``application of'' a symbol or pseudo
  [47mlambda[0m expression to a true list of pseudo-terms.  A pseudo [47mlambda[0m
  expression is an expression of the form [47m(lambda (v1 ... vn) body)[0m
  where the [47mvi[0m are all symbols and [47mbody[0m is a pseudo-term.

  Pseudo-terms are recognized by the unary function [47m[pseudo-termp][0m.  If
  [47m(termp x w)[0m is true, then [47m(pseudo-termp x)[0m is true.  However, if [47mx[0m
  fails to be a (strict) term it may nevertheless still be a
  pseudo-term.  For example, [47m(car a b)[0m is not a term, because [47m[car][0m
  is applied to the wrong number of arguments, but it is a
  pseudo-term.

  The structures recognized by [47m[pseudo-termp][0m can be recursively
  explored with the same simplicity that terms can be.  In
  particular, if [47mx[0m is not a [47mvariablep[0m or an [47mfquotep[0m, then [47m(ffn-symb
  x)[0m is the function ([47msymbol[0m or [47mlambda[0m expression) and [47m(fargs x)[0m is
  the list of argument pseudo-terms.  A metafunction (see [meta]) or
  clause-processor (see [clause-processor]) may use [47m[pseudo-termp][0m as
  the [guard].


