 (THE_ASSOCIATIVITY_OF_APP
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "The Associativity of App

  {IMAGE} (see [The_Theorem_that_App_is_Associative])

  {IMAGE}

    ACL2!>[31;1m(let ((a '(1 2))[0m
                [31;1m(b '(3 4))[0m
                [31;1m(c '(5 6)))[0m
            [31;1m(equal (app (app a b) c)[0m
                   [31;1m(app a (app b c))))[0m
    T

  {IMAGE}

  Observe that, for the particular [47ma[0m, [47mb[0m, and [47mc[0m above, [47m(app (app a b) c)[0m
  returns the same thing as [47m(app a (app b c))[0m.  Perhaps [47mapp[0m is
  [31;1massociative[0m.  Of course, to be associative means that the above
  property must hold for all values of [47ma[0m, [47mb[0m, and [47mc[0m, not just the ones
  [31;1mtested[0m above.

  Wouldn't it be cool if you could type

    ACL2!>[31;1m(equal (app (app a b) c)[0m
                 [31;1m(app a (app b c)))[0m

  and have ACL2 compute the value [47mT[0m?  Well, [31;1myou can't![0m If you try it,
  you'll get an error message!  The message says we can't evaluate
  that form because it contains [31;1mfree[0m variables, i.e., variables not
  given values.  Click here (see [Free_Variables_in_Top-Level_Input])
  to see the message.

  We cannot evaluate a form on an infinite number of cases.  But we can
  prove that a form is a theorem and hence know that it will always
  evaluate to true.

  {IMAGE} (see [The_Theorem_that_App_is_Associative])")
