 (TRUNCATE
  (NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Division returning an integer by truncating toward 0

    Example Forms:
    ACL2 !>(truncate 14 3)
    4
    ACL2 !>(truncate -14 3)
    -4
    ACL2 !>(truncate 14 -3)
    -4
    ACL2 !>(truncate -14 -3)
    4
    ACL2 !>(truncate -15 -3)
    5
    ACL2 !>(truncate 10/4 3/4)
    3

  [47m(Truncate i j)[0m is the result of taking the quotient of [47mi[0m and [47mj[0m and
  dropping the fraction.  For example, the quotient of [47m-14[0m by [47m3[0m is [47m-4
  2/3[0m, so dropping the fraction [47m2/3[0m, we obtain a result for [47m(truncate
  -14 3)[0m of [47m-4[0m.

  The [guard] for [47m(truncate i j)[0m requires that [47mi[0m and [47mj[0m are rational
  ([real], in ACL2(r)) numbers and [47mj[0m is non-zero.

  [47mTruncate[0m is a Common Lisp function.  However, note that unlike Common
  Lisp, the ACL2 [47mtruncate[0m function returns only a single value, Also
  see [nonnegative-integer-quotient], which is appropriate for
  integers and may simplify reasoning, unless a suitable arithmetic
  library is loaded, but be less efficient for evaluation on concrete
  objects.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<truncate>

    (defun truncate (i j)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (real/rationalp i)
                                  (real/rationalp j)
                                  (not (eql j 0)))))
      (let* ((q (* i (/ j)))
             (n (numerator q))
             (d (denominator q)))
        (cond ((= d 1) n)
              ((>= n 0)
               (nonnegative-integer-quotient n d))
              (t (- (nonnegative-integer-quotient (- n)
                                                  d))))))")
