 (TYPE
  (POINTERS)
  "Disambiguation page for [3mtype[0m-related concepts.

  The word [31;1mtype[0m might mean many things in ACL2.

    Built-in Types
      ACL2 can reason about and compute with certain different kinds of
      objects, such as [numbers], [strings], [characters], and
      [conses].  See [About_Types] for basic background on the
      different kinds of ACL2 objects; also see [type-reasoning] for
      additional relevant background.
    User-Defined Types
      When modeling systems with ACL2, you may often want to introduce
      certain concepts as new data types.  For instance, if you are
      modeling a network, you might want [47mhost[0m and [47mconnection[0m objects.
      ACL2 does not directly support introducing new types.  Instead,
      such types are usually ``emulated'' by introducing a new
      [3mrecognizer[0m function, say [47mhostp[0m, and perhaps some related
      [3mconstructor[0m and [3maccessor[0m functions, say [47mmake-host[0m and
      [47mhost->name[0m.  Macro libraries can help to assist with
      introducing common types.  See for instance [std/util],
      [defdata], or [fty].
    Type Declarations
      For more efficient Common Lisp execution, ACL2 functions can be
      annotated with Common Lisp type specifiers (see [type-spec])
      that may boost performance by reducing run-time type checking.
      This mechanism is integrated with ACL2's [guard] mechanism, so
      you can prove your type declarations are correct.  See also
      [declare] and [the], and also [patbind-the].
    Type Prescriptions
      ACL2 includes a limited but efficient ``type reasoning (see
      [TYPE-REASONING])'' engine for determining whether objects are
      of certain [31;1mbuilt-in[0m types.  This engine can be extended with
      [type-prescription] rules.  Such rules are often inferred
      automatically when new functions are introduced with [defun].
      Type reasoning can assist other reasoning engines like
      [forward-chaining], [linear-arithmetic], and rewriting.
      Type-set information is stored in a [type-alist] data
      structure; see [type-reasoning] for relevant basic
      background...
    Tau
      ACL2 includes another reasoning engine, the [tau-system], for
      reasoning about type-like predicates.  Unlike [type-set], Tau
      can also be used for reasoning about [31;1muser-defined types[0m, and
      can also carry out certain interval arithmetic reasoning.  See
      the [introduction-to-the-tau-system] for more information about
      Tau.")
 (TYPE-ALIST
  (TYPE-SET)
  "An ACL2 representation of contextual knowledge

  See [type-reasoning] for basic background on type reasoning in ACL2.

  The ACL2 prover maintains many structures that need not be understood
  by the user.  One of these, the [3mtype-alist[0m structure, is usually in
  this category.  But some utilities refer to the type-alist, so we
  summarize it here.

  A type-alist is an association list, each element of which is of the
  form [47m(u ts . ttree)[0m, where [47mu[0m is a [term] (in internal,
  ``translated'' form), [47mts[0m is a [type-set], and [47mttree[0m is a tag-tree
  (see [ttree]).  Such an element means that the term [47mu[0m has the
  type-set [47mts[0m, which is a number.  While you are welcome to see the
  [documentation] of [type-set] for details, this is not necessary,
  since user-level utilities generally display a type-alist without
  displaying numeric type-sets.  Instead, they often use a symbol of
  the form [47m*TS-typ*[0m to denote the type described by [47mtyp[0m, and for
  complements they use [47m(TS-COMPLEMENT *TS-typ*)[0m.  For example, a
  context in which the terms [47m(p x y)[0m, [47m(no-duplicatesp-equal y)[0m, and
  [47m(natp x)[0m are assumed to be true (i.e., non-[47mnil[0m) may be displayed
  using the following lines.

    -----
    Terms with type *TS-T*:
    (NO-DUPLICATESP-EQUAL Y)
    -----
    Terms with type (TS-COMPLEMENT *TS-NIL*):
    (P X Y)
    -----
    Terms with type *TS-NON-NEGATIVE-INTEGER*:
    X
    -----

  These lines says that [47m(no-duplicatesp-equal y)[0m is the symbol [47mt[0m, [47m(p x
  y)[0m is not the symbol [47mnil[0m, and [47mx[0m is a non-negative integer (i.e., a
  [47m[natp][0m).

  ACL2 computes a type-alist based on contextual information including
  the hypotheses of the current goal (technically: the negations of
  the literals in the goal that are not currently being rewritten),
  [forward-chaining] from those literals, and the surrounding tests
  in calls of [47mif[0m.  The type-alist is of course not all that is known,
  since an entire logical [world] of facts is also known; and
  additional known information may be recorded in the current
  database of [linear] rules.")
 (TYPE-PRESCRIPTION
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Make a rule that specifies the type of a term

  See [rule-classes] for a general discussion of rule classes,
  including how they are used to build rules from formulas and a
  discussion of the various keywords in a rule class description.
  Also see [type-reasoning] for basic background on type reasoning in
  ACL2.

  In this topic we focus on user-defined type-prescription rules, but
  note that ACL2 also introduces type-prescription rules when
  introducing a function with [47m[defun][0m; see
  [type-prescription-debugging] for discussion of how to influence
  the generation of such rules.

    Examples:
    (defthm integerp-foo                       ; Assumes that foo has been
      (integerp (foo x y))                     ; defined; then, states that
      :rule-classes :type-prescription)        ; (foo x y) is of type integer.

    (defthm characterp-nth-type-prescription   ; (Nth n lst) is of type character
      (implies                                 ; provided the hypotheses can be
       (and (character-listp lst)              ; established by type reasoning.
            (<= 0 n)
            (< n (len lst)))
       (characterp (nth n lst)))
      :rule-classes :type-prescription)

    (defthm characterp-nth-type-prescription-alt ; equivalent to the above
      (implies
       (and (character-listp lst)
            (<= 0 n)
            (< n (len lst)))
       (characterp (nth n lst)))
      :rule-classes ((:type-prescription :typed-term (nth n lst))))

    (defthm demodulize-type-for-quote-value  ; (Demodulize a lst 'value ans) is
      (implies                               ; either a nonnegative integer or
       (and (atom a)                         ; of the same type as ans, provided
            (true-listp lst)                 ; the hyps can be established by type
            (member-equal a lst))            ; reasoning ;
       (or (and (integerp (demodulize a lst 'value ans))
                (>= (demodulize a lst 'value ans) 0))
           (equal (demodulize a lst 'value ans) ans)))
      :rule-classes :type-prescription)

  To specify the term whose type (see [type-set]) is described by the
  rule, provide that term as the value of the [47m:typed-term[0m field of
  the rule class object.

    General Form (after preprocessing; see below):
    (implies hyps
             (or type-restriction1-on-pat
                 ...
                 type-restrictionk-on-pat
                 (equal pat var1)
                 ...
                 (equal pat varj)))

  where [47mpat[0m is the application of some function symbol to some
  arguments, each [47mtype-restrictioni-on-pat[0m is a term involving [47mpat[0m
  and containing no variables outside of the occurrences of [47mpat[0m, and
  each [47mvari[0m is one of the variables of [47mpat[0m.  Generally speaking, the
  [47mtype-restriction[0m terms ought to be terms that inform us as to the
  type of [47mpat[0m.  Ideally, they should be ``primitive recognizing
  expressions'' about [47mpat[0m; see [compound-recognizer].  We describe
  preprocessing at the end of this topic.

  If the [47m:typed-term[0m is not provided in the rule class object, it is
  computed heuristically by looking for a term in the conclusion
  whose type is being restricted.  An error is caused if no such term
  is found.

  Roughly speaking, the effect of adding such a rule is to inform the
  ACL2 typing mechanism that [47mpat[0m has the type described by the
  conclusion, when the hypotheses are true.  In particular, the type
  of [47mpat[0m is within the union of the types described by the several
  disjuncts.  The ``type described by'' [47m(equal pat vari)[0m is the type
  of [47mvari[0m.

  More operationally, when asked to determine the type of a term that
  is an instance of [47mpat[0m, ACL2 will first attempt to establish the
  hypotheses.  [31;1mThis is done by type reasoning alone, not rewriting![0m
  However, if some hypothesis is a call of [47m[force][0m, then forcing may
  occur, which may ultimately invoke the rewriter; see [force] and
  see [case-split].  So-called free variables in hypotheses are
  treated specially; see [free-variables].  Provided the hypotheses
  are established by type reasoning, ACL2 then unions the types
  described by the [47mtype-restrictioni-on-pat[0m terms together with the
  types of those subexpressions of [47mpat[0m identified by the [47mvari[0m.  The
  final type computed for a term is the intersection of the types
  implied by each applicable rule.  Type prescription rules may be
  disabled.

  You can limit the recursive establishment of hypotheses of rules; see
  [set-backchain-limit].

  Because only type reasoning is used to establish the hypotheses of
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rules, some care must be taken with the
  hypotheses.  Suppose, for example, that the non-recursive function
  [47mmy-statep[0m is defined as

    (defun my-statep (x)
      (and (true-listp x)
           (equal (len x) 2)))

  and suppose [47m(my-statep s)[0m occurs as a hypothesis of a
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rule that is being considered for use in the
  proof attempt for a conjecture with the hypothesis [47m(my-statep s)[0m.
  Since the hypothesis in the conjecture is rewritten, it will become
  the conjunction of [47m(true-listp s)[0m and [47m(equal (len s) 2)[0m.  Those two
  terms will be assumed to have type [47mt[0m in the context in which the
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rule is tried.  But type reasoning will be
  unable to deduce that [47m(my-statep s)[0m has type [47mt[0m in this context.
  Thus, either [47mmy-statep[0m should be disabled (see [disable]) during
  the proof attempt or else the occurrence of [47m(my-statep s)[0m in the
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rule should be replaced by the conjunction into
  which it rewrites.

  While this example makes it clear how non-recursive predicates can
  cause problems, non-recursive functions in general can cause
  problems.  For example, if [47m(mitigate x)[0m is defined to be [47m(if
  (rationalp x) (1- x) x)[0m then the hypothesis [47m(pred (mitigate s))[0m in
  the conjecture will rewrite, opening [47mmitigate[0m and splitting the
  conjecture into two subgoals, one in which [47m(rationalp s)[0m and [47m(pred
  (1- x))[0m are assumed and the other in which [47m(not (rationalp s))[0m and
  [47m(pred x)[0m are assumed.  But [47m(pred (mitigate s))[0m will not be typed as
  [47mt[0m in either of these contexts.  The moral is: beware of
  non-recursive functions occurring in the hypotheses of
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rules.

  Because of the freedom one has in forming the conclusion of a
  type-prescription, we have to use heuristics to recover the
  pattern, [47mpat[0m, whose type is being specified.  In some cases our
  heuristics may not identify the intended term and the
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rule will be rejected as illegal because the
  conclusion is not of the correct form.  When this happens you may
  wish to specify the [47mpat[0m directly.  This may be done by using a
  suitable rule class token.  In particular, when the token
  [47m:type-prescription[0m is used it means ACL2 is to compute pat with its
  heuristics; otherwise the token should be of the form
  [47m(:type-prescription :typed-term pat)[0m, where [47mpat[0m is the term whose
  type is being specified.

  The defun event may generate a [47m:type-prescription[0m rule.  Suppose [47mfn[0m
  is the name of the function concerned.  Then [47m(:type-prescription
  fn)[0m is the rune given to the type-prescription, if any, generated
  for [47mfn[0m by [47m[defun][0m.  (The trivial rule, saying [47mfn[0m has unknown type,
  is not stored, but [47m[defun][0m still allocates the rune and the
  corollary of this rune is known to be [47mt[0m.)

  We close with a discussion of how, before a term is parsed into a
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rule, it is preprocessed.  We describe this
  preprocessing in some detail below, but first consider the
  following (contrived) example.

    (defthm append-tp-example
      (let ((result (append x y)))
        (implies (nat-listp x)
                 (implies (let ((second-hyp (integer-listp y)))
                            second-hyp)
                          (true-listp result))))
      :rule-classes :type-prescription)

  This theorem is parsed into a type-prescription rule with the
  following hypotheses and conclusion.

    (nat-listp x) ; first hypothesis
    ((lambda (second-hyp) second-hyp) (integer-listp y)) ; second hypothesis
    (true-listp (binary-append x y)) ; conclusion

  Notice that the top-level [47m[let][0m was expanded, i.e., [47m(append x y)[0m was
  substituted for [47mresult[0m --- more accurately, [47m(binary-append x y)[0m was
  substituted for [47mresult[0m, since [47m[append][0m is a macro that abbreviates
  [47m[binary-append][0m.  Also notice that the two hypotheses were
  ``flattened'' in the sense that they were gathered up into a list.
  Finally, notice that the [47m[let][0m in the second hypothesis was not
  expanded (it was merely translated to internal form, using [47mLAMBDA[0m).
  If you actually submit the theorem above, you will get warnings,
  which you may choose to ignore; the application of
  [47mtype-prescription[0m rules is somewhat subtle, so if you use them then
  you may wish to experiment to see which forms work best for you.

  Here is the detail promised above, for parsing a term into a
  [47m:type-prescription[0m rule.  There are two steps.  (1) ACL2 first
  translates the term, expanding all macros (see [trans]) and also
  removing [guard-holders].  (2) Then the translated term is
  traversed top-down, expanding away [47mlambda[0ms ([47m[let][0m, [47m[let*][0m, and
  [47m[mv-let][0m expressions) and flattening the [47m[implies][0m structure, until
  the conclusion is exposed; then the conclusion's [47mlambda[0ms are also
  expanded away.  The simplest way to understand (2) may be to look
  at the definition of ACL2 source function [47munprettyify-tp[0m, which
  implements Step (2), say by evaluating [47m:[0m[47m[pe][0m[47m unprettyify-tp[0m.


