 (TYPE-PRESCRIPTION-DEBUGGING
  (DEBUGGING TYPE-PRESCRIPTION)
  "Improve a built-in [type-prescription] rule

  A [type-prescription] rule is introduced automatically when a
  function is defined (see [defun]).  However, that rule might not be
  what you expect.  In this topic we explore how you might be able to
  improve that rule, by way of an example.

  First consider the following definition.

    ACL2 !>(defun f (x) (alistp x))

    Since F is non-recursive, its admission is trivial.  We observe that
    the type of F is described by the theorem
    (OR (EQUAL (F X) T) (EQUAL (F X) NIL)).  We used the :type-prescription
    rule ALISTP.

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN F ...)
    Rules: ((:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION ALISTP))
    Time:  0.01 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     F
    ACL2 !>

  Notice in particular the phrase, ``We used the :type-prescription
  rule ALISTP,'' and the corresponding [rune] [47m(:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION
  ALISTP)[0m in the list of rules printed in the [summary].  These are
  telling us that ACL2 used the indicated rule during the process of
  computing a [type-prescription] rule for [47mf[0m.

  Now suppose that in a different session we instead proceed as
  follows.

    ACL2 !>(in-theory (disable (:type-prescription alistp)))

    Summary
    Form:  ( IN-THEORY (DISABLE ...))
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.01 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.01)
     (:NUMBER-OF-ENABLED-RUNES 3298)
    ACL2 !>(defun f (x) (alistp x))

    Since F is non-recursive, its admission is trivial.  We could deduce
    no constraints on the type of F.

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN F ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)
     F
    ACL2 !>

  Notice that this time ``We could deduce no constraints on the type of
  F,'' because in this context, [type-set] reasoning does not see
  that the body of [47mf[0m is a Boolean, because [47m(:type-prescription
  alistp)[0m has been [disable]d.  Thus, the ``Rules'' field of the
  summary is empty this time.

  Consider the following story.  On Monday, you run the first session,
  and a subsequent proof uses [type-set] reasoning to conclude that
  [47m(f x)[0m is Boolean, even after you [disable] the [definition] of [47mf[0m.
  Then on Tuesday, you run the second session, and are surprised to
  find that ACL2 no longer reasons (during that same subsequent proof
  attempt) that [47m(f x)[0m is Boolean.  You want to debug this problem, so
  you look back in the log where you defined [47mf[0m, and you see the
  ``deduce no constraints'' message --- but then you look further
  back, at Monday's log, where you find the definition of [47mf[0m ``used
  the :type-prescription rule ALISTP'' to conclude that ``the type of
  F is described by the theorem (OR (EQUAL (F X) T) (EQUAL (F X)
  NIL))''.  The mention of ALISTP in that definition's output from
  Monday, together with the corresponding mention of
  [47m(:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION ALISTP)[0m in the ``Rules'' field of the summary,
  leads you to resolve the issue as follows.  On Monday, when
  [47m(:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION ALISTP)[0m was [enable]d, ACL2 used type-set
  reasoning to conclude that the body of [47mf[0m is Boolean, but on
  Tuesday, [47m(:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION ALISTP)[0m was disabled at that point and
  hence the body of [47mf[0m was not known to be Boolean by type-set
  reasoning.  Hence on Tuesday, no Boolean type-prescription rule was
  stored for [47mf[0m at definition time, which is why the subsequent proof
  attempt failed to use such a rule.  So you avoid disabling
  [47m(:TYPE-PRESCRIPTION ALISTP)[0m until after submitting the [47m[defun][0m for
  [47mf[0m, and now ACL2's type reasoning (see [TYPE-REASONING]) knows that
  [47mf[0m returns a Boolean.")
 (TYPE-REASONING
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "ACL2 reasoning with ``types''

  ACL2 has a ``type reasoning'' system, which may be viewed as a
  relatively basic rule-based theorem prover for questions about
  Common Lisp types, e.g., ``does this expression produce a
  [47m[consp][0m?'', ``does this expression produce some kind of ACL2
  number, e.g., an [47m[integerp][0m, a [47m[rationalp][0m, or a
  [47m[complex-rationalp][0m?'', ``does this expression produce a non-[47mnil[0m
  value (i.e., is it true)?'', etc.  This system uses built-in
  reasoning about types, for example that a rational number is not a
  cons; when the ACL2 prover reports that a goal is simplified using
  ``primitive type reasoning'', it is referring to such built-in type
  reasoning.  However, type reasoning is also driven by
  [type-prescription] rules, as we'll discuss shortly.

  ACL2 reasoning, including type reasoning, takes place with respect to
  a context that associates terms with values that represent their
  ``types''.  These contexts are called [type-alist]s, and those
  values are called [type-set]s; however, unless you write certain
  sophisticated tools, perhaps such as [clause-processor]s, you
  probably do not need to know more than that about these two
  notions.  (If you're curious about them, you can see [type-set] and
  see [type-alist].)  For example, when ACL2 attempts to prove a
  conjecture [47m(implies (and (p x) (integerp (f x))) (p2 x))[0m, it builds
  a type-alist recording that [47m(p x)[0m and [47m(integerp (f x))[0m are true
  when attempting to reason about [47m(p2 x)[0m.  That type-alist associates
  [47m(p x)[0m with a value representing the complement of the set, [47m{nil}[0m,
  meaning that [47m(p x)[0m is non-[47mnil[0m, i.e., true.  But for [47m(integerp (f
  x))[0m something a bit more clever takes place: since [47mintegerp[0m
  recognizes a Boolean combination of built-in types --- see
  [compound-recognizer] --- the context associates [47m(f x)[0m with a value
  representing the set of integers.  So for example, type reasoning
  can conclude from that type-alist that [47m(not (consp (f x)))[0m is true,
  since the built-in types for integer and cons are disjoint.

  As noted above, type reasoning can take advantage of
  [type-prescription] rules.  In short, such a rule tells ACL2 that
  any instance of the ``typed-term'' of the rule must have a
  specified type.  To relieve the hypotheses of type-prescription
  rules, the type-reasoning system recursively invokes itself.  This
  can be done for any hypothesis, whether it is ``type-like'' or not,
  since any proposition, [47mp[0m, can be phrased as the type-like question
  ``does [47mp[0m produce an object of type [47mnil[0m?'' However, as you might
  expect, the type system is not very good at establishing hypotheses
  that are not type-like, unless they are represented rather
  explicitly in the context in which the question is posed.  Consider
  the following example from the [type-prescription] documentation.

    (defthm characterp-nth-type-prescription   ; (Nth n lst) is of type character
      (implies                                 ; provided the hypotheses can be
       (and (character-listp lst)              ; established by type reasoning.
            (<= 0 n)
            (< n (len lst)))
       (characterp (nth n lst)))
      :rule-classes :type-prescription)

  It may seem impossible to relieve the hypothesis [47m(character-listp
  lst)[0m by type reasoning, since there is no suitable ``type'' for
  [47mlst[0m, i.e., [47mcharacter-listp[0m does not recognize a Boolean combination
  of ACL2 types (see [compound-recognizer]).  However, suppose we are
  attempting to prove a theorem of the form [47m(implies (and
  (character-listp x) p) q)[0m and ACL2 is in the process of simplifying
  either [47mp[0m or [47mq[0m.  In that case, the context --- that is, the
  type-alist --- will associate the term [47m(character-listp x)[0m with a
  value indicating that this term is true, i.e., non-[47mnil[0m.  Then if
  ACL2 encounters the term [47m(nth k x)[0m, it will try to apply the
  type-prescription rule above, matching [47mk[0m to [47mn[0m and matching [47mlst[0m to
  [47mx[0m.  The instantiated first hypothesis of that rule will then be
  [47m(character-listp x)[0m.  Since the type-alist designates
  [47m(character-listp x)[0m as true, that hypothesis is successfully
  relieved.  Again, there was no [47mcharacter-listp[0m ``type'' involved;
  what was typed was the entire term, [47m(character-listp x)[0m.

  The instantiated hypothesis above was relieved because it was in the
  context, so no rewriting was necessary to establish it.  See
  [force] for how to involve the ACL2 rewriter to establish
  hypotheses of type-prescription rules.


