  [Set-dwp!]
      Affect the effort made in [type-reasoning], non-[47m[local][0mly")
 (TYPE-SET
  (MISCELLANEOUS)
  "How type information is encoded in ACL2

  See [type-reasoning] for basic background on type reasoning in ACL2.

  To help you experiment with type-sets we briefly note the following
  utility functions.

  [47m(type-set-quote x)[0m will return the type-set of the object [47mx[0m.  For
  example, [47m(type-set-quote \"test\")[0m is [47m2048[0m and [47m(type-set-quote '(a b
  c))[0m is [47m512[0m.

  [47m(type-set 'term nil nil nil (ens state) (w state) nil nil nil)[0m will
  return the type-set of [47mterm[0m with respect to the current world and
  the top-level enabled structure.  For example,

    (type-set '(integerp x) nil nil nil (ens state) (w state) nil nil nil)

  will return (mv 192 nil).  192, otherwise known as [47m*ts-boolean*[0m, is
  the type-set containing [47mt[0m and [47mnil[0m.  The second result may be
  ignored in these experiments.  [47mTerm[0m must be in the [47mtranslated[0m,
  internal form shown by [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m.  See [trans] and see [term].

  [47m(type-set-implied-by-term 'x nil 'term (ens state)(w state) nil)[0m will
  return the type-set deduced for the variable symbol [47mx[0m assuming the
  [47mtranslated[0m term, [47mterm[0m, true.  The second result may be ignored in
  these experiments.  For example,

    (type-set-implied-by-term 'v nil '(integerp v)
                              (ens state) (w state) nil)

  returns [47m11[0m.

  [47m(convert-type-set-to-term 'x ts (ens state) (w state) nil)[0m will
  return a term whose truth is equivalent to the assertion that the
  term [47mx[0m has type-set [47mts[0m.  The second result may be ignored in these
  experiments.  For example

    (convert-type-set-to-term 'v 523 (ens state) (w state) nil)

  returns a term expressing the claim that [47mv[0m is either an integer or a
  non-[47mnil[0m true-list.  [47m523[0m is the [47mlogical-or[0m of [47m11[0m (which denotes the
  integers) with [47m512[0m (which denotes the non-[47mnil[0m true-lists).

  The ``actual primitive types'' of ACL2 are listed in
  [47m*actual-primitive-types*[0m, whose elements are shown below.  Each
  actual primitive type denotes a set --- sometimes finite and
  sometimes not --- of ACL2 objects and these sets are pairwise
  disjoint.  For example, [47m*ts-zero*[0m denotes the set containing 0
  while [47m*ts-negative-integer*[0m denotes the set containing all of the
  negative integers.

    *TS-ZERO*                  ;;; {0}
    *TS-ONE*                   ;;; {1}
    *TS-INTEGER>1*             ;;; integers greater than 1
    *TS-POSITIVE-RATIO*        ;;; positive non-integer rationals
    *TS-NEGATIVE-INTEGER*      ;;; negative integers
    *TS-NEGATIVE-RATIO*        ;;; negative non-integer rationals
    *TS-COMPLEX-RATIONAL*      ;;; complex rationals
    *TS-NIL*                   ;;; {nil}
    *TS-T*                     ;;; {t}
    *TS-NON-T-NON-NIL-SYMBOL*  ;;; symbols other than nil, t
    *TS-PROPER-CONS*           ;;; nil-terminated non-empty lists
    *TS-IMPROPER-CONS*         ;;; conses that are not proper
    *TS-STRING*                ;;; strings
    *TS-CHARACTER*             ;;; characters

  The actual primitive types were chosen by us to make theorem proving
  convenient.  Thus, for example, the actual primitive type [47m*ts-nil*[0m
  contains just [47mnil[0m so that we can encode the hypothesis ``[47mx[0m is [47mnil[0m''
  by saying ``[47mx[0m has type [47m*ts-nil*[0m'' and the hypothesis ``[47mx[0m is
  non-[47mnil[0m'' by saying ``[47mx[0m has type complement of [47m*ts-nil*[0m.'' We
  similarly devote a primitive type to [47mt[0m, [47m*ts-t*[0m, and to a third
  type, [47m*ts-non-t-non-nil-symbol*[0m, to contain all the other ACL2
  symbols.

  Let [47m*ts-other*[0m denote the set of all Common Lisp objects other than
  those in the actual primitive types.  Thus, [47m*ts-other*[0m includes
  such things as floating point numbers and CLTL array objects.  The
  actual primitive types together with [47m*ts-other*[0m constitute what we
  call [47m*universe*[0m.  Note that [47m*universe*[0m is a finite set containing
  one more object than there are actual primitive types; that is,
  here we are using [47m*universe*[0m to mean the finite set of primitive
  types, not the infinite set of all objects in all of those
  primitive types.  [47m*Universe*[0m is a partitioning of the set of all
  Common Lisp objects: every object belongs to exactly one of the
  sets in [47m*universe*[0m.

  Abstractly, a ``type-set'' is a subset of [47m*universe*[0m.  To say that a
  term, [47mx[0m, ``has type-set [47mts[0m'' means that under all possible
  assignments to the variables in [47mx[0m, the value of [47mx[0m is a member of
  some member of [47mts[0m.  Thus, [47m(cons x y)[0m has type-set [47m{*ts-proper-cons*
  *ts-improper-cons*}[0m.  A term can have more than one type-set.  For
  example, [47m(cons x y)[0m also has the type-set [47m{*ts-proper-cons*
  *ts-improper-cons* *ts-nil*}[0m.  Extraneous types can be added to a
  type-set without invalidating the claim that a term ``has'' that
  type-set.  Generally we are interested in the smallest type-set a
  term has, but because the entire theorem-proving problem for ACL2
  can be encoded as a type-set question, namely, ``Does [47mp[0m have
  type-set complement of [47m*ts-nil*[0m?,'' finding the smallest type-set
  for a term is an undecidable problem.  When we speak informally of
  ``the'' type-set we generally mean ``the type-set found by our
  heuristics'' or ``the type-set assumed in the current context.''

  Note that if a type-set, [47mts[0m, does not contain [47m*ts-other*[0m as an
  element then it is just a subset of the actual primitive types.  If
  it does contain [47m*ts-other*[0m it can be obtained by subtracting from
  [47m*universe*[0m the complement of [47mts[0m.  Thus, every type-set can be
  written as a (possibly complemented) subset of the actual primitive
  types.

  By assigning a unique bit position to each actual primitive type we
  can encode every subset, [47ms[0m, of the actual primitive types by the
  nonnegative integer whose ith bit is on precisely if [47ms[0m contains the
  ith actual primitive type.  The type-sets written as the complement
  of [47ms[0m are encoded as the two's-complement bitwise [47m`not'[0m of the
  encoding of [47ms[0m.  Those type-sets are thus negative integers.  The
  bit positions assigned to the actual primitive types are enumerated
  from [47m0[0m in the same order as the types are listed in
  [47m*actual-primitive-types*[0m.  At the concrete level, a type-set is an
  integer between [47m*min-type-set*[0m and [47m*max-type-set*[0m, inclusive.

  For example, [47m*ts-nil*[0m has bit position [47m7[0m.  The type-set containing
  just [47m*ts-nil*[0m is thus represented by [47m128[0m.  If a term has type-set
  [47m128[0m then the term is always equal to [47mnil[0m.  The type-set containing
  everything but [47m*ts-nil*[0m is the bitwise [47m`not'[0m of [47m128[0m, which is [47m-129[0m.
  If a term has type-set [47m-129[0m, it is never equal to [47mnil[0m.  By
  ``always'' and ``never'' we mean under all, or under no,
  assignments to the variables, respectively.

  Here is a more complicated example.  Let [47ms[0m be the type-set containing
  all of the symbols and the natural numbers.  The relevant actual
  primitive types, their bit positions and their encodings are:

    actual primitive type       bit    value

    *ts-zero*                    0       1
    *ts-one*                     1       2
    *ts-integer>1*               2       4
    *ts-nil*                     7     128
    *ts-t*                       8     256
    *ts-non-t-non-nil-symbol*    9     512

  Thus, the type-set [47ms[0m is represented by [47m(+ 1 2 4 128 256 512)[0m = [47m903[0m.
  The complement of [47ms[0m, i.e., the set of all objects other than the
  natural numbers and the symbols, is [47m-904[0m.  We can get ACL2 to
  provide such information as follows.

    ACL2 !>(decode-type-set 903)
    (TS-UNION *TS-SYMBOL* *TS-NON-NEGATIVE-INTEGER*)
    ACL2 !>(decode-type-set -904)
    (TS-COMPLEMENT (TS-UNION *TS-SYMBOL* *TS-NON-NEGATIVE-INTEGER*))
    ACL2 !>


