 (UBT
  (HISTORY UNDO)
  "Undo the [command]s back through a [command] descriptor

  See [undo] for an introduction to undoing commands.

    Examples:
    :ubt :max      ; undo back through the most recent command
                   ; (which just means undo the most recent command)
    :ubt :x        ; same as :ubt :max
    :u             ; same as :ubt :max with no questions asked
    :ubt fn        ; undo back through the introduction of fn
                   ; (including all the other events in fn's block)
    :ubt 5         ; undo back through the fifth command executed
    :ubt (:max -4) ; undo back through the most recent five commands
    :ubt (:x -4)   ; undo back through the most recent five commands

  See [command-descriptor].

  [47mUbt[0m takes one argument, a [command] descriptor, and undoes the
  [command]s from [47m:[0m[47m[max][0m (aka [47m:x[0m) through the one described.  See
  [command-descriptor].  [47m[Pbt][0m will print the [command]s that [47mubt[0m
  will undo.  [47m:[0m[47m[Oops][0m will undo the undo.  See [oops].

  [47mUbt[0m can cause errors but not queries.  To get queries as well, see
  [ubt?].  To get neither errors nor queries, see [ubt!]..

  It is important to remember that a [command] may create several
  [events].  That is, the [command] that introduces [47mfn1[0m may also
  introduce [47mfn2[0m.  Undoing the [command] that created either of these
  will undo them both.  The [events] created by a [command]
  constitute the [command]'s ``block'' and we can only undo entire
  blocks.  Use [47m[pcb][0m to print the [command] block of a [command] if
  you wish to see what will be lost by undoing the [command].

  [47mUbt[0m will not undo into ``prehistory''.  [47m:Ubt 1[0m will undo all of your
  [command]s.  But [47m:ubt -5[0m will cause an error, warning you that [47m:ubt[0m
  cannot undo system initialization.

  See [u] for how to undo just the latest command.  See [ubu], [ubu!],
  and [ubu?] for how to undo back up to, but not including, the
  current command.")
