  (SYSTEM-UTILITIES)
  "Show a user-level representation of a term

  ACL2 uses a strict notion of ``[term]'' for its internal
  computations; see [pseudo-termp].  Such expressions are sometimes
  called ``translated terms''.  However, ACL2 provides user-level
  output --- for example, in output from the theorem prover --- in
  the form of so-called ``untranslated terms''.  For example, the
  untranslated term [47m(<= 3 x)[0m corresponds to the translated term [47m(not
  (< x '3))[0m, as is illustrated by applying [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m to the
  untranslated term:

    ACL2 !>:trans (<= 3 x)

    (NOT (< X '3))

    => *

    ACL2 !>

  Notice for example that the macro [47m<=[0m has been eliminated in favors of
  the functions [47mnot[0m and [47m<[0m, and that the constant [47m3[0m has been quoted.

  The call [47m(untranslate term iff-flg wrld)[0m returns an untranslated term
  corresponding to the given translated term, [47mterm[0m, with respect to
  the given logical [world], [47mwrld[0m.  If [47miff-flg[0m is not [47mnil[0m, then that
  untranslated term is merely propositionally equivalent to the input
  term, as the following example illustrates.

    ACL2 !>:trans (and x t)

    (IF X 'T 'NIL)

    => *

    ACL2 !>(untranslate '(IF X 'T 'NIL) nil (w state))
    (AND X T)
    ACL2 !>(untranslate '(IF X 'T 'NIL) t (w state))
    X
    ACL2 !>

  The latter result, [47mx[0m, is what one might want to see if the term is
  being used as the first argument of a call of the function, [47mif[0m.
  Indeed, [47muntranslate[0m is defined so that the [47miff[0m flag is
  automatically set in the test position of an [47mif[0m call.

    ACL2 !>:trans (if (and x t) y z)

    (IF (IF X 'T 'NIL) Y Z)

    => *

    ACL2 !>(untranslate '(IF (IF X 'T 'NIL) Y Z) nil (w state))
    (IF X Y Z)
    ACL2 !>

  Also see [user-defined-functions-table] and [add-macro-fn].")
