      Arranging that source functions come up as guard-verified")
 (VERIFY-GUARDS+
  (EVENTS)
  "Verify the [guard]s of a function

  We assume familiarity with [guard] verification; see [verify-guards].
  Unlike [47mverify-guards[0m, the macro call [47m(verify-guards+ mac ...)[0m will
  verify guards for a function, [47mfn[0m, such that the macro [47mmac[0m is
  associated with the function symbol [47mfn[0m in [47m[macro-aliases-table][0m
  (also see [add-macro-alias]).  For example, if you define a macro
  [47mapp[0m and list append function [47mbinary-app[0m, and you associate macro
  [47mapp[0m with function symbol [47mbinary-app[0m in [47m[macro-aliases-table][0m, then
  evaluation of the form [47m(verify-guards+ app)[0m will have the effect of
  evaluating [47m(verify-guards fn)[0m.  Note that in this setting,
  evaluation of [47m(verify-guards app)[0m would cause an error, because [47mapp[0m
  is a macro and [47mverify-guards[0m, unlike [47mverify-guards+[0m, cannot handle
  macros.

  The rest of this [documentation] topic discusses why we do not simply
  arrange that [47mverify-guards[0m be permitted to take a macro alias.  The
  following example shows a soundness issue in doing so.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (defun f1 (x)
       (declare (xargs :guard (consp x)
                       :verify-guards nil))
       (car x))
     (defun f2 (x)
       (declare (xargs :guard t
                       :verify-guards nil))
       (cdr x))
     (defmacro mac (x)
       x)
     (add-macro-alias mac f2) ; silly macro alias ;
     (local (add-macro-alias mac f1)) ; alternate silly macro alias ;
     (verify-guards mac))

  If we were to allow macro aliases in [47m[verify-guards][0m, this event
  would be admitted, because on the first pass we are verifying
  guards of [47mf1[0m.  But after the [47m[encapsulate][0m form completes
  evaluation, it would appear that [47mf2[0m is guard-verified.  That could
  of course cause a raw Lisp error.

  The enhanced functionality provided by [47mverify-guards+[0m does not have
  the above problem, because it takes advantage of [47m[make-event][0m to
  avoid taking advantage of the contradictory results produced by the
  two calls of [47madd-macro-alias[0m.  See [make-event].  If the specific
  example above is modified by replacing [47mverify-guards[0m with
  [47mverify-guards+[0m, then the first pass through the [47m[encapsulate][0m form
  will expand the form [47m(verify-guards+ mac)[0m to [47m(verify-guards f1)[0m.
  That same expansion will be used for the [47mverify-guards+[0m call during
  the second pass through the [47mencapsulate[0m form, which is evaluated
  successfully and leaves us in a [world] where [47mf1[0m is guard-verified
  and [47mf2[0m is not.")
