 (VERIFY-GUARDS-FOR-SYSTEM-FUNCTIONS
  (VERIFY-TERMINATION VERIFY-GUARDS)
  "Arranging that source functions come up as guard-verified

  ACL2 is maintained solely by Matt Kaufmann and J Moore.  However, we
  anticipate that a few others will eventually contribute as well.
  Here we discuss how to put built-in functions into [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.
  Since ACL2 insists that its built-in [47m:logic[0m mode functions are
  [guard]-verified, we actually explain how to arrange that built-in
  [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode functions become built-in guard-verified [47m:logic[0m
  mode functions.

  To put a system function into guard-verified [47m:logic[0m mode, you might
  first need or want to modify your local copy of the ACL2 sources,
  for example replacing [47m(null lst)[0m by [47m(endp lst)[0m in a function's
  definition in support of the termination proof and then specifying
  [47m(true-listp lst)[0m in its guard.  You don't need to become a system
  developer to do this, but see [developers-guide] if you are an
  experienced ACL2 user and system development interests you.
  IMPORTANT: Eventually you will probably want to undo your changes;
  this will be explained further below.

  After making such changes, build an ACL2 executable image containing
  your modified code, to test that the modified code builds.  The
  next step is typically to create a new file, perhaps named after
  the main function(s) whose guards you want to verify, in directory
  [47mbooks/system[0m.  Community book [47mbooks/system/too-many-ifs.lisp[0m is a
  good example.  Ultimately, this file should be a certifiable book
  that verifies the desired termination and guards.  Include this
  book in [47mbooks/system/top.lisp[0m.  Of course, you can instead add to
  some existing file in the same directory that is already included
  in [47mbooks/system/top.lisp[0m, rather than creating a new book and
  including it there.

  In general, in this new file or new file addition, it is necessary to
  use both [47m[verify-termination][0m and [47m[verify-guards][0m on the system
  functions in question, in order to both put them in logic mode and
  verify their guards. However, as explained in the documentation of
  [47m[verify-termination][0m, sometimes [47m[verify-termination][0m also verifies
  the guards (see also [47m[set-verify-guards-eagerness][0m).  In this case,
  it is good practice to add a comment `[47m; and guards[0m' just after the
  [47m[verify-termination][0m form, on the same line, as can be seen in some
  of the files under [community-books] directory [47msystem/[0m.

  Now it is time to add entries to the value of constant
  [47m*system-verify-guards-alist*[0m in your local copy of the ACL2
  sources, which specifies functions whose guard-verification is
  completed by including [community-book]
  [47mbooks/system/devel-check.lisp[0m (which includes
  [47mbooks/system/top.lisp[0m.  Each entry is of the form [47m(function-symbol
  . measure)[0m.  For example, the entry [47m(ARITY-ALISTP ACL2-COUNT ALIST)[0m
  signifies that the function symbol [47marity-alistp[0m is to have measure
  [47m(acl2-count alist)[0m, while the entry [47m(ARGLISTP)[0m signifies that
  function symbol [47marglistp[0m has no measure (i.e., we use [47mnil[0m for the
  measure), presumably because its definition is not recursive.
  After you make those additions, then follow the steps below.

   1. Build a so-called ``devel'' copy in which the functions in
      [47m*system-verify-guards-alist*[0m remain in [47m:program[0m mode.  For
      example, the following command will create ``devel'' executable
      [47msaved_acl2d[0m.

          (time nice make ACL2_DEVEL=d) >& make-devel.log

   2. Check that the build seems to have worked, for example by finding the
      string [47m\"Successfully built\"[0m near the end of your log file.

   3. Run a ``devel'' regression, for example as follows if starting in the
      ACL2 sources directory.  Note that including
      ``[47mACL2_USELESS_RUNES= [0m'' as shown below may be necessary
      because of how proofs differ between normal and ``devel''
      versions of ACL2.

          make clean-books ACL2=`pwd`/saved_acl2d
          cd books
          (time nice make -j 8 \\
                     ACL2=`pwd`/../saved_acl2d \\
                     ACL2_USELESS_RUNES= \\
                     system/devel-check.cert) \\
            >& make-devel-regression.log&

      The last of these commands should run much more quickly than a
      normal regression.  You can of course check on it as follows.

          tail make-devel-regression.log

   4. Check that there are no errors, by checking that the following
      produces no output.

          fgrep -a '**' make-devel-regression.log

   5. Run the following check from your ACL2 sources directory.

          make devel-check ACL2=`pwd`/saved_acl2d

      You should see output like the following.

          SUCCESS for chk-new-verified-guards
          SUCCESS for check-system-events
          SUCCESS for devel-check

   6. Now clean the books (see [47mmake clean-books[0m above) and then do a normal
      build and regression.

   7. Finally, send your changes to Matt Kaufmann, with a request that they
      be incorporated into the ACL2 sources and books.

  We now return to the remark labeled ``IMPORTANT'' above: undoing your
  changes.  This isn't necessary if you will not be using that local
  copy of ACL2 in the future.  But otherwise you may run into
  problems when you try to use git to merge changes into that local
  ACL2+books copy.  One option is to throw your changes away by
  standing in your local ACL2 directory and using the shell command:
  [47mgit checkout -f[0m.  But be careful --- this will throw away all your
  work!  So you might want to wait until your changes make it into
  the main (master) git branch.")
 (VERIFY-GUARDS-FORMULA
  (GUARD-FORMULA-UTILITIES)
  "View the guard proof obligation, without proving it

  See [verify-guards] and see [guard] for a discussion of guards.  This
  utility, which does not evaluate its argument, provides output
  showing the guard proof obligation (also known as the guard
  theorem) as printed by [47mverify-guards[0m, but without then carrying out
  a proof attempt.  See also [guard-formula-utilities] for related
  utilities.

    Example Forms:
    (verify-guards-formula foo)
    (verify-guards-formula foo :guard-debug t)
    (verify-guards-formula foo :guard-debug t :guard-simplify :limited)
    (verify-guards-formula foo :rrp t :otf-flg dont-care :xyz whatever)
    (verify-guards-formula (+ (foo x) (bar y)) :guard-debug t)

  [47mVerify-guards-formula[0m allows all keywords, but only pays attention to
  [47m:guard-debug[0m and [47m:guard-simplify[0m, which have the same effect as in
  [47m[verify-guards][0m (also see [guard-debug] and
  [guard-simplification]), and to [47m:rrp[0m, described below.  Apply
  [47mverify-guards-formula[0m to a name just as you would use
  [47m[verify-guards][0m, but when you only want the output that shows the
  guard proof obligation, without attempting a proof or creating an
  event.  If the first argument is not a symbol, then it is treated
  as the body of a [47m[defthm][0m event for which you want the guard proof
  obligation.

  The [47m:rrp[0m argument (``return redundant p'') is [47mnil[0m by default.  If its
  value is not [47mnil[0m, then in the case that the first argument is a
  guard-verified function symbol, the keyword [47m:REDUNDANT[0m is returned
  without any output.")
 (VERIFY-TERMINATION
  (EVENTS)
  "Convert a function from :program mode to :logic mode

    Example:
    (verify-termination fact)

    General Forms:
    (verify-termination fn dcl ... dcl)
    (verify-termination (fn1 dcl ... dcl)
                        (fn2 dcl ... dcl)
                        ...)

  where [47mfn[0m and the [47mfni[0m are function symbols having [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode (see
  [defun-mode]) and all of the [47mdcl[0ms are either [47m[declare][0m forms or
  [documentation] strings.  The first form above is an abbreviation
  for

    (verify-termination (fn dcl ... dcl))

  so we limit our discussion to the second form.  Each of the [47mfni[0m must
  be in the same clique of mutually recursively defined functions,
  but not every function in the clique need be among the [47mfni[0m.

  [47mVerify-termination[0m attempts to establish the admissibility of the
  [47mfni[0m. [47mVerify-termination[0m retrieves their definitions, creates
  modified definitions using the [47mdcl[0ms supplied above, and resubmits
  these definitions.  You could avoid using [47mverify-termination[0m by
  typing the new definitions yourself.  So in that sense,
  [47mverify-termination[0m adds no new functionality.  But if you have
  prototyped your system in [47m:[0m[47m[program][0m mode and tested it, you can
  use [47mverify-termination[0m to resubmit your definitions and change
  their [defun-mode]s to [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m, adding [hints] without having to
  retype or recopy the code.

  The [47m[defun][0m [command] executed by [47mverify-termination[0m is obtained by
  retrieving the [47m[defun][0m (or [47m[mutual-recursion][0m) [command] that
  introduced the clique in question and then possibly modifying each
  definition as follows.  Consider a function, [47mfn[0m, in the clique.  If
  [47mfn[0m is not among the [47mfni[0m above, its definition is left unmodified
  other than to add [47m(declare (xargs :mode :logic))[0m.  Otherwise, [47mfn[0m is
  some [47mfni[0m and we modify its definition by inserting into it the
  corresponding [47mdcl[0ms listed with [47mfni[0m in the arguments to
  [47mverify-termination[0m, as well as [47m(declare (xargs :mode :logic))[0m.  In
  addition, we throw out from the old declarations in [47mfn[0m the [47m:mode[0m
  specification and anything that is specified in the new [47mdcl[0ms.

  For example, suppose that [47mfact[0m was introduced with:

    (defun fact (n)
      (declare (type integer n)
               (xargs :mode :program))
      (if (zp n) 1 (* n (fact (1- n))))).

  Suppose later we do [47m(verify-termination fact)[0m.  Then the following
  definition is submitted.

    (defun fact (n)
      (declare (type integer n))
      (if (zp n) 1 (* n (fact (1- n))))).

  Observe that this is the same definition as the original one, except
  the old specification of the [47m:mode[0m has been deleted so that the
  [defun-mode] now defaults to [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m.  Although the termination
  proof succeeds, ACL2 also tries to verify the [guard], because we
  have (implicitly) provided a [guard], namely [47m(integerp n)[0m, for this
  function.  (See [guard] for a general discussion of guards, and see
  [type-spec] for a discussion of how type declarations are used in
  guards.)  Unfortunately, the [guard] verification fails, because
  the subterm [47m(zp n)[0m requires that [47mn[0m be nonnegative, as can be seen
  by invoking [47m:args zp[0m.  (For a discussion of termination issues
  relating to recursion on the naturals, see [zero-test-idioms].)  So
  we might be tempted to submit the following:

    (verify-termination
     fact
     (declare (xargs :guard (and (integerp n) (<= 0 n))))).

  However, this is considered a changing of the guard (from [47m(integerp
  n)[0m), which is illegal.  If we instead change the guard in the
  earlier [47mdefun[0m after undoing that earlier definition with [47m:[0m[47m[ubt][0m[47m
  fact[0m, then [47m(verify-termination fact)[0m will succeed.

  It may be necessary to specify an appropriate [47m:ruler-extenders[0m in a
  [47mdcl[0m supplied to [47mverify-termination[0m.  This determines the [rulers]
  used in the termination proofs and also the case analysis in the
  induction scheme suggested by the admitted function.  See
  [induction-coarse-v-fine-grained].

  [31;1mRemark on system functions.[0m There may be times when you want to apply
  [47mverify-termination[0m (and also, perhaps, [47m[verify-guards][0m) to
  functions that are predefined in ACL2.  It may be necessary in such
  cases to modify the system code first.  See
  [verify-guards-for-system-functions] for a discussion of the
  process for contributing updates to the system code and [books]
  with such [47mverify-termination[0m or [47m[verify-guards][0m [events], perhaps
  resulting in more system functions being built-in as
  [guard]-verified.  To see which built-in functions have already
  received such treatment, see community books directory
  [47mbooks/system/[0m; or, evaluate the constant
  [47m*system-verify-guards-alist*[0m, which specifies a list of functions
  whose guard-verification is proved by including that book.  See the
  above URL for more details.

  Note that if [47mfn1[0m is already in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode, then the
  [47mverify-termination[0m call has no effect.  It is generally considered
  to be redundant, in the sense that it returns without error; but if
  the [47mfn1[0m is a constrained function (i.e., introduced in the
  signature of an [47m[encapsulate][0m, or by [47m[defchoose][0m), then an error
  occurs.  This error is intended to highlight unintended uses of
  [47mverify-termination[0m; but if you do not want to see an error in this
  case, you can write and use your own macro in place of
  [47mverify-termination[0m.  The following explanation of the
  implementation of [47mverify-termination[0m may help with such a task.

  We conclude with a discussion of the use of [47m[make-event][0m to implement
  [47mverify-termination[0m.  This discussion can be skipped; we include it
  only for those who want to create variants of [47mverify-termination[0m,
  or who are interested in seeing an application of [47m[make-event][0m.

  Consider the following proof of [47mnil[0m, which succeeded up through
  Version_3.4 of ACL2.

    (encapsulate
     ()
     (defun foo (x y)
       (declare (xargs :mode :program))
       (if (or (zp x) (zp y))
           (list x y)
         (foo (1+ x) (1- y))))
     (local (defun foo (x y)
              (declare (xargs :measure (acl2-count y)))
              (if (or (zp x) (zp y))
                  (list x y)
                (foo (1+ x) (1- y)))))
     (verify-termination foo))

    (defthm bad-lemma
      (zp x)
      :hints ((\"Goal\" :induct (foo x 1)))
      :rule-classes nil)

  How did this work?  In the first pass of the [47m[encapsulate][0m, the
  second [47m[defun][0m of [47mfoo[0m promoted [47mfoo[0m from [47m:program[0m to [47m:logic[0m mode,
  with [47my[0m as the unique measured variable.  The following call to
  [47mverify-termination[0m was then redundant.  However, on the second pass
  of the [47m[encapsulate][0m, the second ([47m[local][0m) definition of [47mfoo[0m was
  skipped, and the [47mverify-termination[0m event then used the first
  definition of [47mfoo[0m to guess the measure, based (as with all guesses
  of measures) on a purely syntactic criterion.  ACL2 incorrectly
  chose [47m(acl2-count x)[0m as the measure, installing [47mx[0m as the unique
  measured variable, which in turn led to an unsound induction scheme
  subsequently used to prove [47mnil[0m (lemma [47mbad-lemma[0m, above)

  Now, [47mverify-termination[0m is a macro whose calls expand to [47m[make-event][0m
  calls.  So in the first pass above, the [47mverify-termination[0m call
  generated a [47mdefun[0m event identical to the [47m[local][0m [47m[defun][0m of [47mfoo[0m,
  which was correctly identified as redundant.  That expansion was
  recorded, and on the second pass of the [47m[encapsulate][0m, the
  expansion was recalled and used in place of the [47mverify-termination[0m
  call (that is how [47m[make-event][0m works).  So instead of a measure
  being guessed for the [47mverify-termination[0m call on the second pass,
  the same measure was used as was used on the first pass, and a
  sound induction scheme was stored.  The attempt to prove [47mnil[0m (lemma
  [47mbad-lemma[0m) then failed.


Subtopics

  [Verify-guards-for-system-functions]
      Arranging that source functions come up as guard-verified

  [Verify-termination-on-raw-program-okp]
      Permit [47m[verify-termination][0m for functions with raw Lisp code.")
 (VERIFY-TERMINATION-ON-RAW-PROGRAM-OKP
  (VERIFY-TERMINATION)
  "Permit [47m[verify-termination][0m for functions with raw Lisp code.

  By default, it is not permitted to verify termination (see
  [VERIFY-TERMINATION]) for functions with raw Lisp code.  This
  restriction is important in general for preserving soundness; see
  [program-only].  However, in some cases it may be harmless to
  remove this restriction.  That can be done as follows.  Note that
  an active trust tag is required: in principle you can render ACL2
  unsound with this action!

    (defttag t)
    (remove-untouchable verify-termination-on-raw-program-okp nil)
    ; In the following, t can be replaced by an expression that evaluates to a
    ; list of symbols for which it is permitted to verify termination.
    (assign verify-termination-on-raw-program-okp t)")
 (VERSION
  (ABOUT-ACL2)
  "ACL2 Version Number

  To determine the version number of your copy of ACL2, evaluate the
  form [47m(@ acl2-version)[0m.  The value will be a string.  For example,

    ACL2 !>(@ acl2-version)
    \"ACL2 Version 3.4\"

  The part of the string after [47m\"ACL2 Version \"[0m is of the form [47mx.y[0m or
  [47mx.y.z[0m, optionally followed by a succession of values in
  parentheses, where [47mx[0m, [47my[0m, and [47mz[0m are natural numbers.  If [47mz[0m is
  omitted then it is implicitly 0.  We refer to [47mX[0m, [47my[0m, and [47mz[0m as the
  ``major'', ``minor'', and ``incrl'' fields, respectively.  The
  incrl field is used for incremental releases.  The discussion just
  below assumes that incremental releases are not employed at the
  user's site, i.e., the incrl fields are always 0.  We remove this
  assumption when we discuss incremental releases at the end of this
  documentation topic.

  [Books] are considered certified only in the same version of ACL2 in
  which the certification was done.  The [certificate] file records
  the version number of the certifying ACL2 and [include-book]
  considers the book uncertified if that does not match the current
  version number.  Thus, each time we release a new version of ACL2,
  previously certified books should be recertified.

  Note that there are over 150 constants in the system, most having to
  do with the fact that ACL2 is coded in ACL2.  Many of these, for
  example [47m*common-lisp-specials-and-constants*[0m and [47m*acl2-exports*[0m,
  may change from version to version, and this can cause unsoundness.
  For example, the symbol [47m'set-difference-eq[0m was added to
  [47m*acl2-exports*[0m in Version_2.9, so we can certify a book in
  Version_2.8 containing the following theorem, which is false in
  Version_2.9.

    (null (member 'set-difference-eq *acl2-exports*))

  Therefore, we need to disallow inclusion of such a book in a
  Version_2.9 session, which otherwise would allow us to prove [47mnil[0m.
  Furthermore, it is possible that from one version of the system to
  another we might change, say, the default values on some system
  function or otherwise make ``intentional'' changes to the axioms.
  It is even possible one version of the system is discovered to be
  unsound and we release a new version to correct our error.

  Therefore we adopted the draconian policy that books are certified by
  a given version of ACL2 and ``must'' be recertified to be used in
  other versions.  We put ``must'' in quotes because in fact, ACL2
  allows a book that was certified in one ACL2 version to be included
  in a later version, using [47m[include-book][0m.  But ACL2 does not allow
  [47m[certify-book][0m to succeed when such an [47m[include-book][0m is executed
  on its behalf.  Also, you may experience undesirable behavior if
  you avoid recertification when moving to a different version.
  Hence we recommend that you stick to the draconion policy of
  recertifying books when updating to a new ACL2 version.

  Furthermore, all bets are off if you certify a book using ACL2 built
  on one host Lisp implementation and include the book using ACL2
  built on a different host Lisp implementation.  For example, for
  most host Lisp implementations, the Lisp reader will interpret the
  token [47m2f[0m as a symbol; however, Common Lisp allows for the
  possibility that [47m2f[0m is read as a number.  In such a case, one could
  use the former ACL2 executable to certify a book containing the
  form

    (defthm my-thm
      (symbolp '2f)
      :rule-classes nil)

  but then use the latter ACL2 executable to include the book, even
  though for the latter ACL2 executable, [47m2f[0m is a number, not a
  symbol.  Of course, one would expect an error because of a
  [book-hash] mismatch) in such case.  But these do not provide
  guaranteed protection against including uncertified books.

  [3mIncremental releases.[0m

  From time to time, so-called ``incremental releases'' of ACL2 are
  made available.  These releases are thoroughly tested on at least
  two platforms; ``normal'' releases, on the other hand, are
  thoroughly tested on many more platforms (perhaps a dozen or so)
  and are accompanied by updates to the ACL2 home page.  We provide
  incremental releases in order to provide timely updates for ACL2
  users who want them, without imposing unnecessary burdens on either
  on the ACL2 implementors or on ACL2 users who prefer to update less
  frequently.  The implementors expect users to update their copies
  of ACL2 when normal releases are made available, but not
  necessarily when incremental releases are made available.

  Incremental releases are accompanied by a bump in the incrl field of
  the version field, while normal releases are accompanied by a bump
  in the minor or (much less frequently) major field and zeroing out
  of the incrl field.  Note that incremental releases are
  full-fledged releases.")
 (W
  (STATE)
  "The current ACL2 [world]

  The value of the form [47m(w state)[0m is the current ACL2 world.  See
  [world].")
 (WALKABOUT
  (DEBUGGING)
  "Explore an ACL2 cons tree

  By typing [47m(walkabout x state)[0m for an ACL2 term [47mx[0m whose value is a
  [47m[cons][0m tree, you can walk around that tree.  For example, ACL2
  developers often use this utility to explore the ACL2 logical
  [world].

  When you issue a [47mwalkabout[0m command, a summary of commands will be
  printed before you enter an interactive loop.

    Commands:
    1, 2, ..., up, nx, bk, pp, (pp n), (pp lev len), =, (= symb),
    (cmds c1 c2 ... cn), and q.

  In the interactive [47mwalkabout[0m loop, a positive integer n takes you to
  the nth position, while [47mup[0m takes you up a level.  The commands [47mnx[0m
  and [47mbk[0m take you to the next and previous position, respectively, at
  the same level.  The command [47mpp[0m prints the current object in full,
  while [47m(pp level length)[0m hides sub-objects below the indicated level
  and past the indicated length, if non-[47mnil[0m; see [evisc-tuple].  The
  command [47m(pp n)[0m abbreviates [47m(pp n n)[0m, so in particular [47m(pp nil)[0m is
  equivalent to [47mpp[0m.  The commands [47m=[0m and [47mcmds[0m are described below.

  Note that the commands above work in any package: [47mup[0m, [47mnx[0m, [47mbk[0m, [47mpp[0m, [47m=[0m,
  [47mcmds[0m, and [47mq[0m are converted to the [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package if the current
  package is not [47m\"ACL2\"[0m.  Also, as a convenience, the single
  character [47m0[0m is accepted as equivalent to [47mup[0m.

  The following example illustrates the commands described above.

    ACL2 !>(walkabout (append '(a (b1 b2 b3)) '(c d e f)) state)

    Commands:
    1, 2, ..., up, nx, bk, pp, (pp n), (pp lev len), =, (= symb),
    (cmds c1 c2 ... cn), and q.

    (A (B1 B2 B3) C ...)
    :2
    (B1 B2 B3)
    :3
    B3
    :up
    (B1 B2 B3)
    :nx
    C
    :nx
    D
    :up
    (A (B1 B2 B3) C ...)
    :pp
    (A (B1 B2 B3) C D E F)
    :(pp 4)
    (A (B1 B2 B3) C D ...)
    :(pp 1 4)
    (A # C D ...)
    :(pp nil 2)
    (A (B1 B2 ...) ...)
    :q
    ACL2 !>

  The command [47m(cmds c1 c2 ... cn)[0m just executes each of the [47mci[0m,
  sequentially.

  The command [47mq[0m simply causes an exit from the [47mwalkabout[0m loop.

  The command [47m=[0m also exits, but returns the current object as the value
  in an ACL2 [error-triple].

  The command [47m(= symb)[0m saves an association of [47msymb[0m with the current
  object, which can be retrieved outside the [47mwalkabout[0m loop using the
  macro [47mwalkabout=[0m, as illustrated below.

    ...
    :pp
    (B1 B2 B3)
    :(= my-list)
    (walkabout= MY-LIST) is
    (B1 B2 B3)
    :q
    ACL2 !>(walkabout= MY-LIST)
    (B1 B2 B3)
    ACL2 !>

  Finally, we remark that for trees that are not true-lists, [47mwalkabout[0m
  treats the dot as an object that can be ``walked about''.  The
  following example illustrates this point.

    ACL2 !>(walkabout '(c d e . f) state)

    Commands:
    1, 2, ..., up, nx, bk, pp, (pp n), (pp lev len), =, (= symb),
    (cmds c1 c2 ... cn), and q.

    (C D E . F)
    :3
    E
    :nx
    .
    :nx
    F
    :up
    (C D E . F)
    :4
    .
    :up
    (C D E . F)
    :5
    F
    :")
