 (WARRANT
  (APPLY$)
  "Giving [47m[apply$][0m permission to handle a user-defined function in
  proofs

  The word ``warrant'' is defined in the Merriam-Webster dictionary as
  ``a commission or document giving authority to do something....''

  The discussion below mentions the concept of [47m[badge][0ms, which are
  easily confused with warrants.  See the discussion of [31;1mBadges versus
  Warrants[0m at the top of [47m[defbadge][0m.  But roughly put, badges extend
  the ACL2 syntax and warrants extend the proof theory.  You'll need
  a badge for [47mfn[0m to allow the system to syntactically analyze [47m(apply$
  'fn ...)[0m.  You'll need a both a badge and a warrant for [47mfn[0m if you
  wish to reason about that term with ACL2.  Badges and warrants also
  have impact on evaluation of forms in the top-level loop.  See
  [guarantees-of-the-top-level-loop].

  In the ACL2 proof theory, the functions [47m[badge][0m and [47m[apply$][0m are
  undefined on user-defined function symbols.  To be precise, when
  presented with a user-defined function symbol, [47mbadge[0m calls the
  weakly constrained function [47mbadge-userfn[0m and [47mapply$[0m calls the
  weakly constrained function [47mapply$-userfn[0m.  Warrants are terms
  that, if available as hypotheses in a conjecture, further constrain
  those functions for specific function symbols.  If there is a
  warrant for [47mfn[0m among the hypotheses of a conjecture, [47m(badge 'fn)[0m
  and [47m(apply$ 'fn ...)[0m can be simplified appropriately.  In
  particular, [47m(badge 'fn)[0m is simplified to the actual badge of [47mfn[0m and
  [47m(apply$ 'fn ...)[0m is simplified to the appropriate application of [47mfn[0m
  provided the arguments are suitably [tame].  We think of the
  warrant for [47mfn[0m giving [47mbadge[0m and [47mapply$[0m authority to expand on [47m'fn[0m.
  For reasons of logical consistency not every [47mfn[0m can have a warrant.
  Warrants are issued, when possible, by [47m[defwarrant][0m.

  In the ACL2 evaluation theory --- a consistent extension of the proof
  theory --- all warrants issued by [47mdefwarrant[0m are implicitly
  assumed, meaning [47mbadge[0m and [47mapply$[0m can be executed on warranted
  user-defined function symbols at the top-level of the ACL2 loop
  without explicit mention of the warrants.  In fact, just as the
  evaluation theory can --- ``magically'' --- execute [47m:program[0m mode
  functions despite the absence of any axioms about them, the
  evaluation theory can execute a well-formed [47mapply$[0m on any [47m:program[0m
  mode function that has a [47m[badge][0m.  However, for a [47m:logic[0m mode
  function [47mfn[0m, the top-level loop cannot evaluate [47m(apply$ 'fn ...)[0m
  unless [47m(defwarrant fn)[0m has previously succeeded.  See
  [guarantees-of-the-top-level-loop].)  For a discussion of the
  restrictions on when a [47mfn[0m can be warranted, see [47m[defwarrant][0m.  This
  topic discusses warrants [3mper se[0m, their names, their logical
  meaning, when they must be explicitly added as hypotheses to
  conjectures, and their consistency.


Logical Definition of the Warrant of a Function

  If [3mfn[0m has a warrant, then the warrant is the term
  [47m(apply$-warrant-[0m[3mfn[0m[47m)[0m, i.e., a call of the 0-ary warrant function
  named, [47mapply$-warrant-[0m[3mfn[0m.  That warrant function name is admitted
  to the logic when [47mdefwarrant[0m succeeds on [47mfn[0m.

  The warrant function for [47mfn[0m, introduced by [47m(defwarrant fn)[0m, is
  defined with [47m[defun-sk][0m because the warrant must specify the values
  returned by [47m(apply$ 'fn args)[0m [3mfor all possible[0m [47margs[0m.  Recall that
  [47m[badge][0m and [47m[apply$][0m defer to two undefined functions, [47mbadge-userfn[0m
  and [47mapply$-userfn[0m, when they are applied to user-defined function
  symbols.  The warrant for [47mfn[0m is actually phrased in terms of those
  two undefined functions.  By stipulating their values on [47m'fn[0m the
  warrant determines the values of [47m(badge 'fn)[0m and [47m(apply$ 'fn ...)[0m.
  To create the warrant function for [47mfn[0m, [47mdefwarrant[0m must turn the
  ilks of [47mfn[0m into tameness requirements on the corresponding elements
  of the [47margs[0m to which [47mfn[0m will be applied by [47mapply$[0m.  Each [47m:FN[0m
  argument must be a [tame] function and each [47m:EXPR[0m argument must be
  a [tame] expression.

  It is easiest to understand the above paragraph by looking at the
  generated warrant function for [47mfoldr[0m, whose definition is shown at
  the top of the documentation for [47m[apply$][0m and whose [badge] is
  [47m(APPLY$-BADGE 3 1 NIL :FN NIL)[0m, indicating that [47mfoldr[0m takes 3
  arguments of ilks [47mNIL[0m, [47m:FN[0m, and [47mNIL[0m, and returns 1 result.  The
  warrant function for [47mfoldr[0m is defined as follows.

    (defun-sk apply$-warrant-foldr ()
      (forall (args)
        (implies (tamep-functionp (cadr args))
                 (and (equal (badge-userfn 'FOLDR)
                             '(APPLY$-BADGE 3 1 NIL :FN NIL))
                      (equal (apply$-userfn 'FOLDR args)
                             (foldr (car args)
                                    (cadr args)
                                    (caddr args))))))
      :constrain t)

  Notice also that the tameness hypothesis involves the universally
  quantified variable [47margs[0m, but that the first conjunct of the
  conclusion does not mention that variable.  So we can read
  [47m(apply$-warrant-foldr)[0m as equivalent to the conjunction of:

    (equal (badge-userfn 'FOLDR)
           '(APPLY$-BADGE 3 1 NIL :FN NIL))

  and

    (forall (args)
        (implies (tamep-functionp (cadr args))
                 (equal (apply$-userfn 'FOLDR args)
                        (foldr (car args)
                               (cadr args)
                               (caddr args)))))

  The first specifies the value of the undefined function used by [47mbadge[0m
  to find the badge of a user-defined function.  The second specifies
  the behavior of the undefined function used by [47mapply$[0m to [47mapply$[0m
  [47m'FOLDR[0m and requires that the second element of [47margs[0m be a [tame]
  function.

  Finally, notice that the warrant function for [47mfoldr[0m,
  [47mapply$-warrant-foldr[0m, ancestrally depends on [47mfoldr[0m: [47mfoldr[0m is called
  in the [47mdefun-sk[0m.  That is crucial to avoiding the [47mLOCAL[0m problem
  noted in [introduction-to-apply$].  If the warrant for [47mfoldr[0m is
  required for a theorem's proof (which it will be if the proof
  involves ``expanding'' [47m(apply$ 'FOLDR ...)[0m), then the theorem is
  ancestrally dependent on [47mfoldr[0m even though that function symbol may
  not be otherwise mentioned in the theorem.  That, in turn, means
  that [47mfoldr[0m may not be a locally defined symbol in the environment
  from which the theorem is exported.

  Now consider a function symbol that returns more than one result.
  Suppose the badge of [47madd-and-subtract[0m is [47m(APPLY$-BADGE 2 2 . T)[0m,
  meaning it takes two ordinary objects and returns two results.  The
  function is tame and so no tameness requirements are imposed on its
  application.  Its warrant is defined

    (defun-sk apply$-warrant-add-and-subtract ()
      (forall (args)
        (and (equal (badge-userfn 'add-and-subtract)
                    '(APPLY$-BADGE 2 2 . T))
             (equal (apply$ 'add-and-subtract args)
                    (mv-list 2
                             (add-and-subtract (car args) (cadr args)))))))


Rewrite Rules that Lift and Force the Warrant

  Once [47mdefwarrant[0m has introduced the warrant function for [3mfn[0m it proves
  two rewrite rules, conjoined under the name [47mapply$-[0m[3mfn[0m, that
  ``lifts'' the warrant from the level of the two undefined functions
  to the level of [47mbadge[0m and [47mapply$[0m.  In the case of [47mfoldr[0m the rules
  are:

    (defthm apply$-foldr
      (and (implies (force (apply$-warrant-foldr))
                    (equal (badge 'FOLDR)
                           '(APPLY$-BADGE 3 1 NIL :FN NIL)))
           (implies (and (force (apply$-warrant-foldr))
                         (tamep-functionp (car (cdr args))))
                    (equal (apply$ 'FOLDR args)
                           (foldr (car args)
                                  (car (cdr args))
                                  (car (cdr (cdr args))))))))

  Observe that these rules say that if [47m(apply$-warrant-foldr)[0m is
  available as a hypothesis, then [47m(badge 'FOLDR)[0m is [47m(APPLY$-BADGE 3 1
  NIL :FN NIL)[0m and [47m(apply$ 'FOLDR args)[0m has the naively expected
  behavior of calling [47mfoldr[0m, provided the second element of [47margs[0m is a
  [47mtamep-functionp[0m.  Also note that the warrant hypothesis is [47m[force][0md
  in both rules.

  The effect of these rules is if either [47m(badge 'FOLDR)[0m or any instance
  of [47m(apply$ 'FOLDR args)[0m arises during a proof, the warrant for
  [47mfoldr[0m is raised and either relieved or forced.  The [47mbadge[0m and
  [47mapply$[0m terms are simplified whether the warrant is present or not,
  but if the warrant is not among the hypotheses and the proof is
  otherwise successful, the warrant for [47mfoldr[0m will show up in a
  checkpoint.

  If no warrant has been issued for [47mfoldr[0m these terms will not
  simplify.


Determining the Necessary Warrants

  [3mThere is no easy way to determine the warrants you'll need to make a
  formula a theorem.[0m At first this seems to be a problem that could
  be solved with a few rules of thumb and indeed, there are a few
  useful rules.  But they don't guarantee success.

  [3mRule 1.[0m One way to determine sufficient warrants for a formula to be
  a theorem is to attempt to prove the formula without warrants and
  see the checkpoints.  This may have to be repeated to collect
  sufficient warrants and can be frustrating.  Furthermore, as
  illustrated further below, it can lead to unnecessary warrants.

  Most users attempt to anticipate what warrants are needed.

  [3mRule 2.[0m You will probably need a warrant hypothesis for every
  user-defined function symbol mentioned in the fully translated term
  occupying any slot of ilk [47m:FN[0m in the formula you're trying to
  prove.  The two common situations are quoted user-defined function
  symbols in such slots and [47m[lambda$][0m expressions.  In the latter
  case, you must consider every user-defined function symbol in the
  [3mfully translated[0m body of the [47m[lambda$][0m expression.  (If you've
  ignored our recommendations to use [47mlambda$[0m instead of hand-typed
  quoted [47mLAMBDA[0m objects, you'll have to look at those too.)

  But Rule 2 says ``probably need a warrant'' because whether you do or
  not depends on what you're proving.  In the examples below, suppose
  we have carried out these events.

    (include-book \"projects/apply/top\" :dir :system)

    (defun$ sq (x) (* x x))

  and recall that [47mcollect$[0m is [47m[loop$][0m scion logically defined as

    (defun$ collect$ (fn lst)
      (if (endp lst)
          nil
          (cons (apply$ fn (list (car lst)))
                (collect$ fn (cdr lst)))))

  No warrant is [3mreally[0m needed to prove

    (thm (equal (collect$ 'sq (append a b))
                (append (collect$ 'sq a) (collect$ 'sq b))))

  because it [3mcould[0m be proved by appealing to the more general

    (thm (equal (collect$ fn (append a b))
                (append (collect$ fn a)
                        (collect$ fn b))))

  which makes clear that the properties of [47msq[0m are totally irrelevant to
  the proof of this formula.

  But if you followed Rule 1 and just submitted

    (thm (equal (collect$ 'sq (append a b))
                (append (collect$ 'sq a)
                        (collect$ 'sq b))))

  the proof would fail with a checkpoint indicating that you need the
  warrant for [47msq[0m.  That happens because the :rewrite rules proved by
  [47mdefwarrant[0m, discussed in the previous section and named [47mapply$-sq[0m
  in the case of [47msq[0m, fire and force that warrant.  However, you could
  disable that rule and get the proof without a warrant.

  To further dim our hopes for a simple way to identify warrants,
  consider

    (defun$ square (i) (apply$ 'sq (list i)))

  and then the proof of

    (thm (equal (square i) (* i i)))

  This theorem requires the warrant for [47msq[0m even though [47m'sq[0m is not
  mentioned in the top-level statement of the theorem.  The problem,
  of course, is that the mention of [47m'sq[0m in a [47m:FN[0m slot is mentioned in
  the definition of [47msquare[0m.

  [3mRule 3.[0m You may need warrants for any symbols used in [47m:FN[0m slots in
  the definitions of any function appearing in the formula.

  And then of course there is the usual reason you need forgotten
  hypotheses: some lemma critical to your proof has that hypothesis.

  For example, one could imagine proving

    (defthm lemma
      (implies (apply$-warrant-sq)
               (equal (square x) (sq x))))

  and then try to prove

    (thm (equal (square x) (* x x))
         :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable square))))

  hoping the lemma would reduce [47m(square x)[0m to [47m(sq x)[0m and then [47m(sq x)[0m
  would expand to [47m(* x x)[0m.  But of course, the lemma isn't applicable
  because we can't establish its hypothesis.  The point here is that
  another source of required warrants can be the warrant hypotheses
  of any lemmas needed for the proof.  We could posit a ``Rule 4''
  but what's the point?

  Returning to the first thing we said in this section, there is no
  easy sure-fire way to determine the warrants you'll need.  It just
  depends on the functions you're manipulating, the cases explored in
  the proof, the hypotheses of crucial lemmas, etc.  However, our
  experience is that it's not nearly so hard as we're suggesting
  here!

  Basically you'll need a warrant for [47mfn[0m if the proof requires [47mapply$[0m
  to behave as naively expected on [47m'fn[0m or the warrant is required for
  some lemma.  Chances are you'll know when you're depending on the
  meaning of a quoted symbol and when you're not.  And Rule 1 will
  eventually get you there though it may generate unnecessary warrant
  hypotheses.

  There is another piece of good news perhaps best explained by
  example.

  Imagine that you've defined and warranted your own versions of the
  familiar list concatenation and list reverse functions under the
  names [47map[0m and [47mrv[0m.

    (defun$ ap (x y)
      (if (endp x)
          y
          (cons (car x)
                (ap (cdr x) y))))

    (defun$ rv (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (ap (rv (cdr x))
              (list (car x)))))

  What warrant(s) do you need to prove

    (implies (true-listp x)
             (equal (apply$ 'rv (list (apply$ 'rv (list x)))) x))?

  You clearly need the warrant for [47mrv[0m.  But do you need the warrant for
  its subfunction [47map[0m?  Some users fall into the trap of thinking they
  do.  They think they'll need warrants for all the subfunctions of
  any function requiring a warrant.  We fall into this trap when we
  think [3mall evaluation[0m is carried out by [47mev$[0m and [47mapply$[0m.  Put another
  way, if we defined

    (defun$ rv1 (x)
      (if (endp x)
          nil
          (apply$ 'ap
                  (list (rv1 (cdr x))
                        (list (car x))))))

  and then tried to prove

    (implies (true-listp x)
             (equal (apply$ 'rv1 (list (apply$ 'rv1 (list x)))) x))

  we would indeed need warrants for both [47mrv1[0m and [47map[0m, as per Rule 3,
  because [47mrv1[0m [47mapply$[0ms [47m'ap[0m.

  But the warrant for the original [47mrv[0m says that [47m(apply$ 'rv args)[0m is
  (unconditionally) equal to [47m(rv (car args))[0m.  There are no [47mapply$[0ms
  left in the problem once we get to a call of the ACL2 function [47mrv[0m.
  We don't need any warrants, even to evaluate a function we called
  via [47mapply$[0m unless the definition of the function itself involves
  further [47mapply$[0ms.


A Convenient Macro for Conjoining Warrants

  Because ``[47mAPPLY$-WARRANT-[0m[3mfn[0m'' is hard to remember, we provide a macro
  for referring to the conjunction of warrant terms for a list of
  functions.

    General Form:
    (warrant fn1 fn2 ... fnk)

  where each [47mfni[0m is the name of a defined function on which [47mdefwarrant[0m
  has been previously called and succeeded.  (Actually, we allow the
  [47mfni[0m to include certain primitive function symbols already built
  into [47mapply$[0m, but for the moment we ignore the possible presence of
  such symbols among the arguments to [47mwarrant[0m.)  [47m(Warrant fn1 fn2 ...
  fnk)[0m expands to:

    (AND (FORCE (APPLY$-WARRANT-fn1))
         (FORCE (APPLY$-WARRANT-fn2))
         ...
         (FORCE (APPLY$-WARRANT-fnk)))

  Because there are over 800 ACL2 primitives built into [47mapply$[0m, it can
  be hard to look at a conjecture involving, say, a [47m[lambda$][0m term,
  and list all and only the function names that need warrants.  For
  example, if the body of the [47mlambda$[0m term calls [47mlogeqv[0m it will
  expand into [47mbinary-logeqv[0m and the diligent user might anticipate,
  accurately, that the [47mev$[0m of that body will involve [47m(apply$
  'BINARY-LOGEQV ...)[0m and thus might suppose that [47mbinary-logeqv[0m be
  included among the [47mfni[0m listed in the [47mwarrant[0m hypothesis.  But no
  warrant for [47mbinary-logeqv[0m exists, that is,
  [47mapply$-warrant-binary-logeqv[0m is not defined, because [47mbinary-logeqv[0m
  is built into [47mapply$[0m.  For this reason, we do not insist that every
  [47mfni[0m in [47mwarrant[0m's argument be a function possessing a warrant.
  Instead, the [47mwarrant[0m macro ignores those [47mfni[0m built into [47mapply$[0m.  It
  does cause an error if one of the [47mfni[0m has no warrant and is not
  built in.

  The [47m(warrant fn1 fn2 ... fnk)[0m macro [47m[force][0ms the warrants because (a)
  we assume the only use of the macro is to add warrant hypotheses to
  conjectures and (b) by forcing warrants aggressively the prover
  ``almost completes'' more proofs and enters a forcing round that
  highlights the need to assume those warrants.  When a rewrite rule,
  for example, is conditioned on a warrant that is not forced (as
  would happen if you added the hyps [47m(apply$-warrant-fn1)[0m,
  [47m(apply$-warrant-fn2)[0m, etc.), then the rule will not fire if a
  subsequent conjecture omitted the warrants.  Of course, this raises
  the question ``But are all the warrants really true?''


Why Warrants Don't Render Theorems Vacuous

  Adding warrants to formulas certainly restricts (weakens) the
  resulting theorem since it is only applicable when the warrant is
  assumed.  But an important question to ponder is whether adding
  warrants can actually make a formula vacuously valid?  That is, can
  a set of warrants simply be unsatisfiable so that any formula
  having that set of warrants among its hypotheses is a theorem?  Put
  another way, is there a model for the set of all warrants
  introduced by [47m[defwarrant][0m?

  The answer is yes.  This is discussed in {``Limited Second-Order
  Functionality in a First-Order Setting'' |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/kaufmann/papers/apply/index.html} by
  Matt Kaufmann and J Strother Moore.  For any set of functions
  warranted by [47mdefwarrant[0m it is possible to [3mdefine[0m, under the
  standard ACL2 definitional principle, versions of those functions
  (together with [47mapply$[0m, [47mev$[0m, etc.) and then make attachments to the
  undefined [47mbadge-userfn[0m and [47mapply$-userfn[0m, and so that every warrant
  is provably equal to [47mT[0m.  In fact, the resultant theory is the basis
  of ACL2's evaluation theory where all warranted functions can be
  [47mapply$[0md (under the appropriate tameness requirements) without
  explicit mention of warrants.  The crux of the proof is admitting a
  big mutually recursive clique containing versions of [47mapply$[0m and all
  of its [scion]s, by inventing a measure that provably decreases as
  [47mapply$[0m and the scions call each other.  The keys to that measure's
  existence are the restrictions imposed by [47m[defwarrant][0m and
  [tame]ness.  See the paper for a sketch of the proof and see the
  comment titled [47mEssay on Admitting a Model for Apply$ and the
  Functions that Use It[0m in the ACL2 source file [47mapply-raw.lisp[0m.")
