  well-formedness, guard verification, and guard checking approve of
  the object and its application.")
 (WELL-FORMEDNESS-GUARANTEE
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Guarantee that a metafunction or clause-processor returns a
  well-formed answer

  A [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m is a keyword attribute available in the
  [47m:[0m[47m[meta][0m and [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m.  By default, when
  a metafunction or clause processor is applied, ACL2 checks that the
  output is well-formed (and does not contain certain ``forbidden''
  functions; see [47m[set-skip-meta-termp-checks][0m).  By providing a
  [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m when you store a [47m:meta[0m or
  [47m:clause-processor[0m rule, you can cause ACL2 to skip these runtime
  checks.

  This is considered an advanced feature that requires that you prove
  certain theorems; this is probably only worthwhile if your
  metafunctions or clause processors produce very large terms.
  Henceforth, we assume you are familiar with metafunctions and
  clause processors.

  This topic first exhibits a simple example of a well-formedness
  guarantee for a metafunction.  This example is really an outline; a
  self-contained, runnable example may be found in the
  [community-books] in file
  [47mbooks/demos/meta-wf-guarantee-example.lisp[0m.  Then we describe the
  acceptable values of the [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m keyword of both
  the [47m:meta[0m and [47m:clause-processor[0m rule-classes.  Next we show the
  forms of the theorems you must prove to provide such guarantees.
  Finally, we discuss the runtime effects of providing such
  guarantees.

  Example

  Suppose that [47mfn[0m is a vanilla metafunction that rewrites terms with
  top function symbol [47mTARGET[0m, of [47m[arity][0m 1.  The event storing [47mfn[0m as
  a metafunction would normally be something like this:

      (defthm fn-is-correct
        (implies (and (pseudo-termp x)
                      (alistp a))
                 (equal (evl x a)
                        (evl (fn x) a)))
        :rule-classes
        ((:meta :trigger-fns (TARGET))))

  where [47mevl[0m is an evaluator for all the function symbols known to [47mfn[0m.
  Suppose that to prove this theorem, [47mevl[0m must be able to interpret
  the symbols [47mTARGET[0m, [47mCONS[0m, and [47mIF[0m.  While the metatheorem for [47mfn[0m
  establishes that [47mfn[0m returns something with the same ``meaning''
  (under [47mevl[0m) as its input, it does not answer the question: does [47mfn[0m
  return a well-formed term, all of whose function symbols are in
  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode?  Given the above [47m:rule-class[0m, whenever the
  simplifier fires the [47m:meta[0m rule [47mfn-is-correct[0m by applying [47mfn[0m to a
  term, it checks that the value, [47mval[0m, is well-formed, by evaluating
  [47m(logic-termp val (w state))[0m.  The function [47m[logic-termp][0m checks
  that its argument is a [term] all of whose function symbols are in
  [47m:logic[0m mode.

  [31;1mFunction: [0m<logic-termp>

    (defun logic-termp (x wrld)
      (declare (xargs :guard (plist-worldp-with-formals wrld)))
      (and (termp x wrld)
           (logic-fnsp x wrld)))

  The [47mlogic-termp[0m check can be avoided if, before you store the rule
  [47mfn-is-correct[0m, you prove:

      (defthm fn-is-well-formed
        (implies (and (logic-termp x w)
                      (arities-okp '((TARGET . 1)
                                     (CONS . 2)
                                     (IF . 3))
                                   w))
                 (logic-termp (fn x) w))
        :rule-classes nil)

  and then you store [47mfn-is-correct[0m this way:

      (defthm fn-is-correct
        (implies (and (pseudo-termp x)
                      (alistp a))
                 (equal (evl x a)
                        (evl (fn x) a)))
        :rule-classes
        ((:meta :trigger-fns (TARGET)
                :well-formedness-guarantee fn-is-well-formed)))

  Acceptable Values

  Now we describe the values you may provide with the
  [47m:well-founded-guarantee[0m keyword of the [47m:meta[0m and the
  [47m:clause-processor[0m rule-classes.

      General Forms:
      :well-formedness-guarantee thm-name1
      :well-formedness-guarantee (thm-name1)
      :well-formedness-guarantee (thm-name1 thm-name2)

  where both [47mthm-name1[0m and [47mthm-name2[0m are the names of previously
  proved ``well-formedness theorems'' (see the next section);
  furthermore, [47mthm-name1[0m must be about the metafunction or clause
  processor being introduced, and [47mthm-name2[0m must be about the
  hypothesis metafunction (if any) associated with the metafunction.
  For [47m:meta[0m rules, all three forms are accepted, but the last form is
  required if the [47m:meta[0m rule involves a hypothesis metafunction.
  That is, to provide a [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m for a metatheorem
  with a hypothesis metafunction, you must supply both [47mthm-name1[0m and
  [47mthm-name2[0m.  For [47m:clause-processor[0m rules, you must use the first
  form.

  Each well-formedness theorem provides an ``arity alist'' that
  specifies the assumed arities of the function symbols known to the
  metafunction or clause processor.  The arities shown for the
  function symbols listed in that alist must be the same as the
  arities of those functions in the actual ACL2 logical [world]
  current at the time the [47m:meta[0m or [47m:clause-processor[0m is stored.
  Moreover, the function symbols in that alist must all be in
  [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m mode.

  Furthermore, none of the function symbols in the arity alists should
  be ``forbidden'' function symbols as explained in
  [47m[set-skip-meta-termp-checks][0m.

  When the [47m:meta[0m or [47m:clause-processor[0m rule is stored, notes are made
  that will prevent the function symbols on the arity alists from
  becoming untouchable (and thus forbidden).  See [47m[push-untouchable][0m.

  The only way a function's arity or forbidden status can change, or
  that a function can transition out of [47m:logic[0m mode, is if the user
  has engaged in redefinition or activated a trust tag to add or
  remove untouchables.  Thus, the restrictions above are unimportant
  to most users.

  Well-Formedness Theorems

  Theorems must be proved to establish that metafunctions and clause
  processors return well-formed results.  These are called
  ``well-formedness theorems.'' Note: To say a theorem is a
  ``well-formedness theorem'' is a remark about the shape of the
  formula, not its rule-class.  There is no [47m:well-formedness-theorem[0m
  rule-class and a well-formedness theorem may be stored with any
  [47m:[0m[47m[rule-classes][0m that accept the syntax of the formula or as
  [47m:rule-classes[0m [47mnil[0m.  Indeed, if one of your metafunctions uses
  another function to produce a subterm of the metafunction's answer,
  you might need to prove a well-formedness theorem for the
  sub-function and make it a [47m:rewrite[0m rule.

  Well-formedness theorems for metafunctions and clause processors are
  similar but slightly different.  We deal with metafunctions and
  their corresponding hypothesis metafunctions (if any) first.  The
  shapes of the well-formedness theorems for a metafunction and
  hypothesis metafunction are identical, but remember that you must
  prove a well-formedness theorem for both the metafunction and the
  associated hypothesis metafunction.  So suppose [47mfn[0m is a
  metafunction or a hypothesis metafunction and let [47m(fn x ...)[0m be a
  legal call on distinct variable symbols.  (Recall that extended
  metafunctions and their hypothesis functions can take three
  arguments.)  Then the general form a well-formedness theorem for [47mfn[0m
  states that [47mfn[0m returns a [47m[termp][0m when given one, provided the
  arities of certain function symbols are as expected:

      General Form of Well-Formedness Theorem for a Metafunction:
      (implies (and (logic-termp x w)
                    (arities-okp '<alist> w))
               (logic-termp (fn x ...) w))

  where [47m<alist>[0m is an alist pairing function symbols with their
  assumed arities as illustrated in the opening example.  Note that
  the first argument of [47marities-okp[0m in the theorem is a quoted
  constant.  You may omit or reorder the hypotheses above and you may
  use different variable symbols in place of [47mx[0m and [47mw[0m, but they must
  be distinct and different from the variables in the ``[47m...[0m.''

  An example of [47m<alist>[0m is [47m((TARGET . 1) (CONS . 2) (IF . 3))[0m.
  Generally, the [47m<alist>[0m you provide should assign arities to every
  symbol known to [47mfn[0m, i.e., every function symbol known to the
  evaluator used in your correctness theorem.  If you inspect the
  definition of [47m[termp][0m you will see that it uses [47mw[0m to obtain arities
  of the function symbols in [47mx[0m.  The [47m[arities-okp][0m hypothesis
  restricts [47mw[0m to worlds where the function symbols known to [47mfn[0m have
  the arities you expect and are in [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m-mode.  For example,
  given the [47m(arities-okp '<alist> w)[0m hypothesis for the [47m<alist>[0m
  above, the theorem prover will rewrite [47m(arity 'IF w)[0m to 3.  By
  default [47m[arity][0m and [47m[arities-okp][0m are disabled; and the following
  rewrite rules, which are part of ACL2's initial [world], will take
  care of calls like [47m(arity 'IF w)[0m and [47m(logicp fn w)[0m.

  [31;1mTheorem: [0m<arities-okp-implies-arity>

    (defthm arities-okp-implies-arity
      (implies (and (arities-okp user-table w)
                    (assoc fn user-table))
               (equal (arity fn w)
                      (cdr (assoc fn user-table)))))

  [31;1mTheorem: [0m<arities-okp-implies-logicp>

    (defthm arities-okp-implies-logicp
      (implies (and (arities-okp user-table w)
                    (assoc fn user-table))
               (logicp fn w)))

  Now we turn to the well-formedness theorem for a clause processor,
  [47mcl-proc[0m.  Let [47m(cl-proc x ...)[0m be a legal call on distinct variable
  symbols.  The theorem establishes that [47mcl-proc[0m returns a list of
  clauses when given a clause, provided certain functions have the
  expected arities.  But the recognizer for a clause is the function
  [47m[logic-term-listp][0m and the recognizer for a list of clauses is
  [47m[logic-term-list-listp][0m:

    General Forms of Well-Formedness Theorem for a Clause Processor
    (implies (and (logic-term-listp x w)
                  (arities-okp '<alist> w))
             (logic-term-list-listp (cl-proc x ...) w))

    (implies (and (logic-term-listp x w)
                  (arities-okp '<alist> w))
             (logic-term-list-listp (clauses-result (cl-proc x ...)) w))

  The first form above is for a [47mcl-proc[0m that returns a single value and
  the second form is for a [47mcl-proc[0m that returns multiple values.

  The discussion about metafunctions, above, applies here as well.
  [47m<Alist>[0m is an alist pairing function symbols with their assumed
  arities.  You may omit or reorder the hypotheses and you may use
  different variable symbols in place of [47mx[0m and [47mw[0m, but they must be
  distinct and different from the variables in the ``[47m...[0m.''

  Runtime Effects

  In the absence of a [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m, if a metafunction or
  clause processor is applied during a proof and produces [47mval[0m, then
  certain checks are made on [47mval[0m before it is used in the proof
  attempt.  In the case of a metafunction, [47m(logic-termp val (w
  state))[0m is checked and [47mval[0m is scanned to ensure that it contains no
  forbidden function symbols.  In the case of a clause processor,
  [47m(logic-term-list-listp val (w state))[0m is checked and [47mval[0m is scanned
  to ensure that it contains no forbidden function symbols.

  If [47mval[0m is large (e.g., because the input is large), these checks can
  take more time than the metafunction or clause processor did to
  produce [47mval[0m!  It is for this reason that we provide for
  [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0ms.

  When a [47m:well-formedness-guarantee[0m is provided no checks are made on
  [47mval[0m.  However, ACL2 will check that the arity alist(s) involved in
  the guarantee still correctly shows the arities of the function
  symbols listed.  Because those function symbols were not forbidden
  when the guarantee was made and are prohibited from being forbidden
  thereafter, no check is necessary to ensure that no forbidden
  symbols are introduced into the proof.")
 (WELL-FOUNDED-RELATION
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Show that a relation is well-founded on a set

  See [well-founded-relation-rule] for discussion of well-founded
  relations in ACL2.  A related utility, [47mget-well-founded-relation[0m,
  may be found in the [community-books], file
  [47mstd/system/get-well-founded-relation.lisp[0m.")
 (WELL-FOUNDED-RELATION-RULE
  (RULE-CLASSES)
  "Show that a relation is well-founded on a set

  See [rule-classes] for a general discussion of rule classes,
  including how they are used to build rules from formulas and a
  discussion of the various keywords in a rule class description.

    Example:
    (defthm lex2p-is-well-founded-relation
      (and (implies (pairp x) (o-p (ordinate x)))
           (implies (and (pairp x)
                         (pairp y)
                         (lex2p x y))
                    (o< (ordinate x) (ordinate y))))
      :rule-classes :well-founded-relation)

  The example above creates a [47m:well-founded-relation[0m rule, where of
  course the functions [47mpairp[0m, [47mlex2p[0m, and [47mordinate[0m would have to be
  defined first.  It establishes that [47mlex2p[0m is a well-founded
  relation on [47mpairp[0ms.  We explain and give details below.

  Exactly two general forms are recognized:

    General Forms
    (AND (IMPLIES (mp x) (O-P (fn x)))              ; Property 1
         (IMPLIES (AND (mp x)                       ; Property 2
                       (mp y)
                       (rel x y))
                  (O< (fn x) (fn y)))),

  or

    (AND (O-P (fn x))                               ; Property 1
         (IMPLIES (rel x y)                         ; Property 2
                  (O< (fn x) (fn y))))

  where [47mmp[0m, [47mfn[0m, and [47mrel[0m are function symbols, [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are distinct
  variable symbols, and no other [47m:well-founded-relation[0m theorem about
  [47mfn[0m has been proved.  When the second general form is used, we act
  as though the first form were used with [47mmp[0m being the function that
  ignores its argument and returns [47mt[0m.  The discussion below therefore
  considers only the first general form.

  Note: We are very rigid when checking that the submitted formula is
  of one of these forms.  For example, in the first form, we insist
  that all the conjuncts appear in the order shown above.  Thus,
  interchanging the two properties in the top-level conjunct or
  rearranging the hypotheses in the second conjunct both produce
  unrecognized forms.  The requirement that each [47mfn[0m be proved
  well-founded at most once ensures that for each well-founded
  relation, [47mfn[0m, there is a unique [47mmp[0m that recognizes the domain on
  which [47mrel[0m is well-founded.  We impose this requirement simply so
  that [47mrel[0m can be used as a short-hand when specifying the
  well-founded relations to be used in definitions; otherwise the
  specification would have to indicate which [47mmp[0m was to be used.

  We also insist that the new ordering be embedded into the ordinals as
  handled by [47m[o-p][0m and [47m[o<][0m and not some into previously admitted
  user-defined well-founded set and relation.  This restriction
  should pose no hardship.  If [47mmp[0m and [47mrel[0m were previously shown to be
  well-founded via the embedding [47mfn[0m, and you know how to embed some
  new set and relation into [47mmp[0m and [47mrel[0m, then by composing [47mfn[0m with
  your new embedding and using the previously proved well-founded
  relation lemma you can embed the new set and relation into the
  ordinals.

  [47mMp[0m is a predicate that recognizes the objects that are supposedly
  ordered in a well-founded way by [47mrel[0m.  We call such an object an
  ``[47mmp[0m-measure'' or simply a ``measure'' when [47mmp[0m is understood.
  Property 1 tells us that every measure can be mapped into an ACL2
  ordinal.  (See [o-p].)  This mapping is performed by [47mfn[0m.  Property
  2 tells us that if the measure [47mx[0m is smaller than the measure [47my[0m
  according to [47mrel[0m, then the image of [47mx[0m under [47mfn[0m is smaller than that
  of [47my[0m, according to the well-founded relation [47m[o<][0m.  (See [o<].)
  Thus, the general form of a [47m:well-founded-relation[0m formula
  establishes that there exists a [47mrel[0m-order preserving embedding
  (namely via [47mfn[0m) of the [47mmp[0m-measures into the ordinals.  We can thus
  conclude that [47mrel[0m is well-founded on [47mmp[0m-measures.

  Such well-founded relations are used in the admissibility test for
  recursive functions, in particular, to show that the recursion
  terminates.  To illustrate how such information may be used,
  consider a generic function definition

    (defun g (x) (if (test x) (g (step x)) (base x))).

  If [47mrel[0m has been shown to be well-founded on [47mmp[0m-measures, then [47mg[0m's
  termination can be ensured by finding a measure, [47m(m x)[0m, with the
  property that [47mm[0m produces a measure:

    (mp (m x)),                                     ; Defun-goal-1

  and that the argument to [47mg[0m gets smaller (when measured by [47mm[0m and
  compared by [47mrel[0m) in the recursion,

    (implies (test x) (rel (m (step x)) (m x))).    ; Defun-goal-2

  If [47mrel[0m is selected as the [47m:well-founded-relation[0m to be used in the
  definition of [47mg[0m, the definitional principal will generate and
  attempt to prove [47mdefun-goal-1[0m and [47mdefun-goal-2[0m to justify [47mg[0m.  We
  show later why these two goals are sufficient to establish the
  termination of [47mg[0m.  Observe that neither the ordinals nor the
  embedding, [47mfn[0m, of the [47mmp[0m-measures into the ordinals is involved in
  the goals generated by the definitional principal.

  Suppose now that a [47m:well-founded-relation[0m theorem has been proved for
  [47mmp[0m and [47mrel[0m.  How can [47mrel[0m be ``selected as the
  [47m:well-founded-relation[0m'' by [47m[defun][0m?  There are two ways.  First,
  an [47m[xargs][0m keyword to the [47m[defun][0m event allows the specification of
  a [47m:well-founded-relation[0m.  Thus, the definition of [47mg[0m above might be
  written

    (defun g (x)
     (declare (xargs :well-founded-relation rel))
     (if (test x) (g (step x)) (base x)))

  Alternatively, [47mrel[0m may be specified as the
  [47m:default-well-founded-relation[0m in [47m[ACL2-defaults-table][0m by
  executing the event

    (set-well-founded-relation rel).

  When a [47m[defun][0m event does not explicitly specify the relation in its
  [47m[xargs][0m the default relation is used.  When ACL2 is initialized,
  the default relation is [47m[o<][0m.

  Finally, though it is probably obvious, we now show that [47mdefun-goal-1[0m
  and [47mdefun-goal-2[0m are sufficient to ensure the termination of [47mg[0m
  provided [47mproperty-1[0m and [47mproperty-2[0m of [47mmp[0m and [47mrel[0m have been proved.
  To this end, assume we have proved [47mdefun-goal-1[0m and [47mdefun-goal-2[0m as
  well as [47mproperty-1[0m and [47mproperty-2[0m and we show how to admit [47mg[0m under
  the primitive ACL2 definitional principal (i.e., using only the
  ordinals).  In particular, consider the definition event

    (defun g (x)
     (declare (xargs :well-founded-relation o<
                     :measure (fn (m x))))
     (if (test x) (g (step x)) (base x)))

  Proof that [47mg[0m is admissible: To admit the definition of [47mg[0m above we
  must prove

    (o-p (fn (m x)))                        ; *1

  and

    (implies (test x)                               ; *2
             (o< (fn (m (step x))) (fn (m x)))).

  But *1 can be proved by instantiating [47mproperty-1[0m to get

    (implies (mp (m x)) (o-p (fn (m x)))),

  and then relieving the hypothesis with [47mdefun-goal-1[0m, [47m(mp (m x))[0m.

  Similarly, *2 can be proved by instantiating [47mproperty-2[0m to get

    (implies (and (mp (m (step x)))
                  (mp (m x))
                  (rel (m (step x)) (m x)))
             (o< (fn (m (step x))) (fn (m x))))

  and relieving the first two hypotheses by appealing to two instances
  of [47mdefun-goal-1[0m, thus obtaining

    (implies (rel (m (step x)) (m x))
             (o< (fn (m (step x))) (fn (m x)))).

  By chaining this together with [47mdefun-goal-2[0m,

    (implies (test x)
             (rel (m (step x)) (m x)))

  we obtain *2.  Q.E.D.")
 (WET
  (TRACE)
  "Evaluate a form and print subsequent error trace

  The acronym ``wet'' stands for ``with-error-trace''.  [47mWet[0m provides a
  convenient way to obtain a backtrace when evaluation causes a guard
  violation or other error.


Summary Documentation

    General Form:
    (wet form           ; an arbitrary form
         :book bk-form  ; optional, not evaluated; specify different wet book
         ;;; the rest are optional and evaluated:
         :compile c     ; :same, t, or nil; default :same (nil if :fns supplied)
         :fullp h       ; nil by default, else handle some raw Lisp errors
         :evisc-tuple e ; an evisc-tuple
         :fns fns       ; :all, or a list of functions to show in a backtrace

  [47mForm[0m is evaluated.  If there is an error, a backtrace stack is
  printed to the standard output ([47m[*standard-co*][0m), containing (by
  default) the user-defined function calls made before the error.
  Such printing is controlled by the [47m:evisc-tuple[0m if supplied;
  otherwise, hiding of large structures will occur.


Discussion

  The basic idea is that [47m(wet form)[0m evaluates [47mform[0m and, if there is an
  error, shows a backtrace of calls that led to that error.  Note
  however that by default only calls of user-defined (not built-in)
  functions ``supporting'' [47mform[0m in the following sense will show up
  in the backtrace: those that occur in the macroexpansion of [47mform[0m or
  (recursively) support any of those functions.  So for example,
  since [47m(make-event form)[0m macroexpands to [47m(make-event-fn (quote form)
  ...)[0m, calls of functions occurring in [47mform[0m will likely not show up
  in the backtrace by default.  The option [47m:fns all[0m overrides this
  default, with potential loss of speed; more on this below.

  The following example explains the use of [47mwet[0m.  First, submit the
  following three definitions:

    (defun foo (x) (declare (xargs :guard (consp x))) (car x))
    (defun bar (x) (foo (cdr x)))
    (defun g (x) (bar (cdr x)))

  Now imagine you have obtained the following guard violation:

    ACL2 !>(g '(3 4))

    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  The guard for the function call (FOO X),
    which is (CONSP X), is violated by the arguments in the call (FOO NIL).
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.  See
    :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this check
    with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.

    ACL2 !>

  With [47mwet[0m, you can get a backtrace of user-defined functions.  The
  package prefixes shown below, [47mACL2_*1*_[0m, indicate that the
  executable-counterparts of the corresponding submitted functions
  are being called; see [evaluation].  Don't forget to start with
  [47m(include-book \"misc/wet\" :dir :system)[0m.

    ACL2 !>(wet (g '(3 4)))
    ; Fast loading /projects/acl2/devel/books/misc/wet.fasl

    TTAG NOTE: Adding ttag :TRACE! from the top level loop.

    ACL2 Error in WET:  The guard for the function call (FOO X), which
    is (CONSP X), is violated by the arguments in the call (FOO NIL).
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.  See
    :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this check
    with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.

    Backtrace stack:
    ----------------
    1. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::FOO NIL)
    2. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::BAR (4))
    3. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G (3 4))

    ACL2 !>

  By default, large structures are hidden during the printing of the
  backtrace stack.  (Technical detail: by default the global
  abbrev-evisc-tuple is used, if bound; see [set-evisc-tuple].  But
  you can supply a value for keyword argument [47m:evisc-tuple[0m to modify
  the printing: [47mnil[0m to avoid hiding, else a suitable evisc-tuple, as
  shown below (see [evisc-tuple]).

    ACL2 !>(wet (g '(3 4)) :evisc-tuple (evisc-tuple 1 1 nil nil))
    ; Fast loading /projects/acl2/devel/books/misc/wet.fasl

    TTAG NOTE: Adding ttag :TRACE! from the top level loop.

    ACL2 Error in WET:  The guard for the function call (FOO X), which
    is (CONSP X), is violated by the arguments in the call (FOO NIL).
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.  See
    :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this check
    with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.

    Backtrace stack:
    ----------------
    1. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::FOO ...)
    2. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::BAR ...)
    3. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G ...)

    ACL2 !>

  For a backtrace as a data object, evaluate the form [47m(@ wet-stack)[0m.
  But note that this object may not be a legal ACL2 value, for
  example because of the ``[47m*1*[0m'' symbols shown above.


Keyword Arguments

  The [47m:fullp[0m option.  Consider the following example.

    (program)
    (defun foo (x) (declare (xargs :guard (consp x))) (car x))
    (defun bar (x) (foo (cdr x)))
    (defun g (x) (bar (cdr x)))
    ; Raw Lisp error:
    (g '(3 4 . 5))

  We may be initially disappointed using [47mwet[0m on this example, as
  follows --- it didn't work!

    ACL2 p!>(wet (g '(3 4 . 5)))

    TTAG NOTE: Adding ttag :TRACE! from the top level loop.

    ***********************************************
    ************ ABORTING from raw Lisp ***********
    ********** (see :DOC raw-lisp-error) **********
    Error:  Fault during read of memory address #x2D
    While executing: FOO
    ***********************************************

    The message above might explain the error.  If not, and
    if you didn't cause an explicit interrupt (Control-C),
    then it may help to see :DOC raw-lisp-error.

    To enable breaks into the debugger (also see :DOC acl2-customization):
    (SET-DEBUGGER-ENABLE T)
    ACL2 p!>

  The fix is to use [47m:fullp t[0m.

    ACL2 p!>(wet (g '(3 4 . 5)) :fullp t)

    TTAG NOTE: Adding ttag :TRACE! from the top level loop.


    ACL2 Error in WET:  The guard for the :program function call (FOO X),
    which is (CONSP X), is violated by the arguments in the call (FOO 5).
    See :DOC set-guard-checking for information about suppressing this
    check with (set-guard-checking :none), as recommended for new users.
    To debug see :DOC print-gv, see :DOC trace, and see :DOC wet.


    Backtrace stack:
    ----------------
    1. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::FOO 5)
    2. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::BAR (4 . 5))
    3. (ACL2_*1*_ACL2::G (3 4 . 5))

    ACL2 p!>

  Why doesn't [47mwet[0m always do things this way?  The answer pertains to
  performance.  When [47m:fullp[0m is non-nil, [47mwet[0m sets guard-checking to
  [47m:all[0m before evaluating the given form; see [set-guard-checking].
  This may slow down evaluation substantially, which is why it is not
  the default behavior.

  The [47m:fns[0m option.  As mentioned above, by default the [47mwet[0m backtrace
  shows user-defined functions that syntactically ``support'' the
  form being evaluated.  This default can be overridden by supplying
  an explicit list, [47mfns[0m, of functions, using option [47m:fns fns[0m; these
  will then be the functions whose calls are eligible for inclusion
  in the backtrace.  The special value [47m:fns :all[0m will allow all
  user-defined function calls in the backtrace.  This value can be
  useful when using [47m[magic-ev-fncall][0m, for example, since the
  function being applied isn't typically included as a syntactic
  supporter of the form being evaluated.

  The [47m:compile[0m option.  [47mWet[0m uses the [47m[trace$][0m utility to modify the
  definitions of affected functions so that they can record
  information for the backtrace.  As described above, these affected
  functions are those that syntactically ``support'' the form unless
  specified by the [47m:fns[0m option.  As is the case for [47mtrace$[0m --- see
  [trace$] --- the new definitions of these affected functions may or
  may not be compiled.  For [47mtrace$[0m and for [47mwet[0m, the default is to
  compile the new definition if and only if the original definition
  was compiled, except: For [47mwet[0m, if option [47m:fns :all[0m is provided,
  then the default is not to compile the affected definitions.  And
  for [47mtrace$[0m and [47mwet[0m, the [47m:compile[0m option overrides the default, to
  specify what will be compiled: value [47m:same[0m to compile each affected
  function if and only if its original definition was compiled, value
  [47mt[0m to compile all affected functions, and value [47mnil[0m to skip
  compilation.

  The [47m:book[0m option.  [47mWet[0m actually works by temporarily including a
  community book,

    (include-book \"misc/wet\" :dir :system)

  and then passing its arguments to macro [47mwet![0m, defined in that book.
  The keyword argument [47m:book[0m allows you to specify a different book
  that defines a macro [47mwet![0m to which to pass its arguments.  If the
  value of [47m:book[0m is a string, then the book named by that string is
  temporarily included using [47m[include-book][0m: [47m(include-book \"bk\")[0m.
  Otherwise [47m:book[0m should be a list of arguments, to be provided
  (unevaluated) to [47m[include-book][0m, for example [47m(\"my-wet\" :dir
  :my-utils)[0m.  Thus you can experiment by copying community book
  [47mbooks/misc/wet.lisp[0m to your own directory and making modifications
  to the copy.  If you make changes, we invite you to share them with
  the ACL2 community (see [books]).  Note that you can also supply
  [47m:book nil[0m, in which case the definition of [47mwet![0m in your current
  session will be used without including a book.


Concluding Remark

  Also see [trace$] for a general tracing utility.  As mentioned above,
  [47mwet[0m is implemented using [47mtrace$[0m.  [47mWet[0m actually first applies
  [47m[untrace$][0m; upon completion, [47mwet[0m then applies [47m[trace$][0m to re-trace
  any functions that it had untraced, using their original trace
  specs.")
 (WHAT_IS_ACL2{Q}
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "What Is ACL2?

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_a_Mathematical_Logic{Q}])

  ACL2 is a [31;1mmathematical logic[0m together with a [31;1mmechanical theorem
  prover[0m to help you reason in the logic.

  The logic is just a subset of applicative Common Lisp (see
  [Common_Lisp]).  (This link takes you off the main route of the
  tour.  You'll see some Common Lisp on the tour, so visit this
  later!)

  The theorem prover is an ``industrial strength'' version of the
  Boyer-Moore theorem prover, Nqthm.

  [31;1mModels[0m of all kinds of computing systems can be built in ACL2, just
  as in Nqthm, even though the formal logic is Lisp.

  Once you've built an ACL2 model of a system, you can run it.

  You can also use ACL2 to prove theorems about the model.

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_a_Mathematical_Logic{Q}])")
 (WHAT_IS_A_MATHEMATICAL_LOGIC{Q}
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "What is a Mathematical Logic?

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_a_Mechanical_Theorem_Prover{Q}])

  {IMAGE}

  A mathematical logic is a formal system of formulas ([31;1maxioms[0m) and
  [31;1mrules[0m for deriving other formulas, called [31;1mtheorems[0m.

  A [31;1mproof[0m is a derivation of a theorem.  To see a concrete proof tree,
  click here (see [A_Trivial_Proof]).

  Why should you care?  The neat thing about Theorems is that they are
  ``true.'' More precisely, if all the axioms are valid and the rules
  are validity preserving, then anything derived from the axioms via
  the rules is valid.

  So, if you want to determine if some formula is true, [31;1mprove it[0m.

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_a_Mechanical_Theorem_Prover{Q}])")
 (WHAT_IS_A_MECHANICAL_THEOREM_PROVER{Q}
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "What is a Mechanical Theorem Prover?

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_a_Mechanical_Theorem_Prover{Q}_{cont}])

  A [31;1mmechanical theorem prover[0m is a computer program that finds proofs
  of theorems.

  {IMAGE}

  The ideal mechanical theorem prover is [31;1mautomatic[0m: you give it a
  formula and it gives you a proof of that formula or tells you there
  is no proof.

  Unfortunately, automatic theorem provers can be built only for very
  simple logics (e.g., [31;1mpropositional calculus[0m) and even then
  practical considerations (e.g., how many centuries you are willing
  to wait) limit the problems they can solve.

  {IMAGE} (see [What_is_a_Mechanical_Theorem_Prover{Q}_{cont}])")
 (WHAT_IS_A_MECHANICAL_THEOREM_PROVER{Q}_{CONT}
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "What is a Mechanical Theorem Prover? (cont)

  {IMAGE} (see [About_Models]) To get around this, mechanical theorem
  provers often require help from the [31;1muser[0m.

  {IMAGE}

  Click here (see [ACL2_as_an_Interactive_Theorem_Prover]) to continue
  downward.

  {IMAGE} (see [About_Models])")
 (WHAT_IS_REQUIRED_OF_THE_USER{Q}
  (PAGES_WRITTEN_ESPECIALLY_FOR_THE_TOURS)
  "What is Required of the User?

  {IMAGE} (see [How_Long_Does_It_Take_to_Become_an_Effective_User{Q}])

  It is not easy to build ACL2 models of complex systems.  To do so,
  the user must [31;1munderstand[0m

    * the system being modeled, and

    * ACL2, at least as a programming language.

  It is not easy to get ACL2 to prove hard theorems.  To do so, the
  user must [31;1munderstand[0m

    * the model,

    * ACL2 as a mathematical logic, and

    * be able to construct a proof (in interaction with ACL2).

  ACL2 will help construct the proof but its primary role is to prevent
  logical mistakes.  The creative burden --- the mathematical insight
  into [31;1mwhy the model has the desired property[0m --- is the user's
  responsibility.

  {IMAGE} (see [How_Long_Does_It_Take_to_Become_an_Effective_User{Q}])")
