 (WHY-BRR
  (BREAK-REWRITE)
  "An explanation of why ACL2 has an explicit [47m[brr][0m mode

  Why isn't [47m[brr][0m mode automatically disabled when there are no
  monitored runes?  The reason is that the list of monitored runes is
  kept in a wormhole state.

  See [wormhole] for more information on wormholes in general.  But the
  fundamental property of the wormhole function is that it is a
  logical [47mno-op[0m, a constant function that does not take state as an
  argument.  When entering a wormhole, arbitrary information can be
  passed in (including the external state).  That information is used
  to construct a near copy of the external state and that ``wormhole
  state'' is the one with respect to which interactions occur during
  breaks.  But no information is carried by ACL2 out of a wormhole
  --- if that were allowed wormholes would not be logical no-ops.
  The only information carried out of a wormhole is in the user's
  head.

  [47m[Break-rewrite][0m interacts with the user in a wormhole state because
  the signature of the ACL2 rewrite function does not permit it to
  modify [47m[state][0m.  Hence, only wormhole interaction is possible.
  (This has the additional desirable property that the correctness of
  the rewriter does not depend on what the user does during
  interactive breaks within it; indeed, it is logically impossible
  for the user to affect the course of [47m[rewrite][0m.)

  Now consider the list of monitored runes.  Is that kept in the
  external state as a normal state global or is it kept in the
  wormhole state?  If it is in the external state then it can be
  inspected within the wormhole but not changed.  This is
  unacceptable; it is common to change the [monitor]ed rules as the
  proof attempt progresses, installing monitors when certain rules
  are about to be used in certain contexts.  Thus, the list of
  monitored runes must be kept as a wormhole variable.  Hence, its
  value cannot be determined outside the wormhole, where the proof
  attempt is ongoing.

  This raises another question: If the list of monitored runes is
  unknown to the rewriter operating on the external state, how does
  the rewriter know when to break?  The answer is simple: it breaks
  every time, for every rune, if [47m[brr][0m mode is enabled.  The wormhole
  is entered (silently), computations are done within the wormhole
  state to determine if the user wants to see the break, and if so,
  interactions begin.  For unmonitored runes and runes with false
  break conditions, the silent wormhole entry is followed by a silent
  wormhole exit and the user perceives no break.

  Thus, the penalty for running with [47m[brr][0m mode enabled when there are
  no monitored runes is high: a wormhole is entered on every
  application of every rune and the user is simply unaware of it.
  The user who has finally unmonitored all runes is therefore
  strongly advised to carry this information out of the wormhole and
  to do [47m:[0m[47m[brr][0m [47mnil[0m in the external state when the next opportunity
  arises.")
 (WINDOWS-INSTALLATION
  (INSTALLATION-SUPPORT)
  "Installing ACL2 on Windows

  Windows users will probably want to do one of the following to
  install and run ACL2 on their systems.  Thanks to David Rager for
  his help with this topic.

    * Use a Virtual Machine platform, such as VMware Player (free for
      non-commercial use) or Oracle Virtualbox (free even for
      commercial use) to install Linux, and then follow the normal
      [installation-instructions] to install ACL2.  As of 2014, at
      least a couple of our power users are very happy with this
      solution, as it provides first-class access to utilities
      relevant to maintaining the ACL2 system and books (like GNU
      Make and perl).

    * Set up {Windows Subsystem for Linux |
      https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows_Subsystem_for_Linux}
      (WSL) on a 64-bit version of Windows 10 (or later, once
      available).  Within that subsystem, follow the normal
      [installation-instructions] for ACL2. See the below section
      regarding the ACL2 Sedan Windows installation instructions for
      more info, as that involves installing the ACL2 Sedan in WSL on
      Windows.

    * Use the ACL2 Sedan (ACL2s) Windows installation instructions --- see
      [ACL2s::ACL2s-installation] for more details. This will install
      ACL2 and the ACL2s system (including a copy of the Eclipse IDE
      with ACL2s support) in a Windows Subsystem for Linux distro.
      This distro is configured to automatically open Eclipse when
      the distro starts up, but one can start the distro without
      Eclipse by running [47mwsl -d acl2s -e /bin/bash --noprofile[0m.

  You are welcome to {obtain a Windows installer for a previous ACL2
  release |
  http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/v3-6/distrib/windows/},
  which mimics some of Linux and provides Emacs.  Updated ACL2
  binaries have been successfully installed in such an environment.

  When installing ACL2 on Windows without a Unix-like environment,
  consider at least downloading a utility such as [47mdjtarnt.exe[0m to use
  with the [47m-x[0m option on gzipped tarfiles.

  Gnu tar is preferred, as there have been some problems with long file
  names when using at least one other tar program.  You may want to
  use the [47m-i[0m option, [47mtar xpvfi ...[0m, if you have problems with other
  than Gnu tar.  You can see if you have Gnu tar by running [47mtar -v[0m.

  WARNING: At least one user experienced CR/LF issues when using
  WinZIP, but we have received the suggestion that people untarring
  with that utility should probably turn off smart cr/lf conversion.

  Here are links to some older documentation topics, possibly out of
  date, that provide additional information for installing ACL2 on
  Windows.

    * See [windows-installation-gcl] for building an executable image on a
      Windows system using GCL

    * Click {here |
      https://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/contrib/windows7.html}
      for some older instructions for building ACL2 on Windows.

    * Click {here |
      https://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/moore/acl2/contrib/windows-gcl-jared.html}
      for yet older instructions for building ACL2 on Windows using
      mingw.


Subtopics

  [Windows-installation-gcl]
      Building an executable image on a Windows system using GCL")
 (WINDOWS-INSTALLATION-GCL
  (WINDOWS-INSTALLATION)
  "Building an executable image on a Windows system using GCL

  This topic is based on very old documentation that is probably
  superseded by the topic, [windows-installation].  But here are
  steps that [3mmay[0m be helpful when installing ACL2 on Windows with GCL
  as the host Common Lisp.

   1. [31;1mFIRST[0m get GCL running on your Windows system using [31;1mONE[0m of the
      following two options.  Note that GCL can be unhappy with
      spaces in filenames, so you should probably save the GCL
      distribution to a directory whose path is free of spaces.

        * {Obtain GCL for Windows systems | https://www.gnu.org/software/gcl/}
          if such a distribution is available.

        * [31;1mOR[0m, perhaps you can build GCL on your Windows system from the
          sources.  The mingw tools and the cygnus bash shell have
          been used to build distributed GCL executables.

   2. [31;1mSECOND[0m, create an appropriate GCL batch file.  When we tried running
      the script [47mgclm/bin/gclm.bat[0m, a separate window popped up, and
      with an error.  Many ACL2 users prefer running in an emacs
      shell buffer.  The following modification of [47mgclm.bat[0m seemed to
      solve the problem (your pathnames may vary).

          @
          % do not delete this line %
          @ECHO off
          set cwd=%cd%
          path C:\\gcl\\gclm\\mingw\\bin;%PATH%
          C:\\gcl\\gclm\\lib\\gcl-2.6.2\\unixport\\saved_gcl.exe -dir C:/gcl/gclm/lib/gcl-2.6.2/unixport/ -libdir  C:/gcl/gclm/lib/gcl-2.6.2/ -eval \"(setq si::*allow-gzipped-file* t)\" %1 %2 %3 %4 %5 %6 %7 %8 %9

   3. [31;1mTHIRD[0m, see [creating-executable], and follow the instructions in the
      section, ``Building an executable image on other than a
      Unix-like system''.  The resulting file may be called
      [47msaved_acl2.exe[0m rather than [47msaved_acl2[0m.

   4. [31;1mFINALLY[0m, create a suitable file [47macl2.bat[0m.

  If you experience problems, the following hints may help.


TROUBLESHOOTING

    * In an attempt to build ACL2 on Windows XP on top of GCL, the attempt
      broke at the end of the ``Initialization, first pass'' step
      (see [creating-executable]), while compiling [47mTMP1.lisp[0m.  That
      was easily remedied by starting up a fresh GCL session and
      invoking [47m(compile-file \"TMP1.lisp\")[0m before proceeding to the
      next step.

    * When you want to quit ACL2, invoke [47m(good-bye)[0m.  The point here is to
      avoid [47mcontrol-c control-d[0m, even though that often works fine in
      Emacs under Unix-like systems.

    * If the above batch file does not work for some reason, an alternate
      approach may be to set environment variables.  You may be able
      to add to the [47mPATH[0m variable [3mgcl-dir[0m[47m\\gcc\\bin[0m, where [3mgcl-dir[0m is
      the directory where GCL is installed.  To get to the place to
      set environment variables, you might be able to go to the
      control panel, under system, under advanced.  Alternately, you
      might be able to get there by opening [47mMy Computer[0m and
      right-clicking to get to [47mProperties[0m, then selecting the
      [47mAdvanced[0m tab.  At one time, when GCL/Windows was released as
      Maxima, Pete Manolios once suggested adding the system variable
      [47mLD_LIBRARY_PATH[0m with the value
      [47m\"maxima-dir\\gcc\\i386-mingw32msvc\\include\"[0m; this may or may not
      be necessary for your GCL installation (and the path would of
      course likely be different).")
 (WITH-BRR-DATA
  (DEBUGGING BREAK-REWRITE)
  "Finding the source of a term in prover output

  When a proof attempt fails, a good first step is typically to look at
  [3mcheckpoints[0m; see [the-method].  That step is often sufficient.  But
  occasionally a checkpoint contains an undesirable term whose source
  is a mystery, and it may be useful to find the origin of that term.
  [47mWith-brr-data[0m is a utility that collects such data that can be
  queried by various utilities, for example as follows.

    Example:

    ; Collect brr-data when attempting the indicated proof:
    (with-brr-data (thm (equal (append x y) (append y x))))

    ; Show a path leading to a rewrite result that contains
    ; (append y (cdr x)) as a subterm:
    (cw-gstack-for-subterm (append y (cdr x)))

    ; Same as above, except enter a loop to query for additional such results:
    (cw-gstack-for-subterm* (append y (cdr x)))

  The rest of this documentation topic is structured as follows.  It
  may suffice to read only the first two sections.

    * Introduction

    * Connections with [break-rewrite]

    * General form of [47mwith-brr-data[0m calls

    * General forms of queries

    * Keyword arguments of [47mwith-brr-data[0m (optional)

    * Low-level details (optional)

    * Using attachments to modify functionality of [47mwith-brr-data[0m


Introduction

  [47m(With-brr-data form)[0m evaluates [47mform[0m while collecting data on rule
  application --- often [rewrite] and [definition] rules, but also
  [linear] and [rewrite-quoted-constant] rules.  Query utilities
  allow one to search the collected data for a specified result of a
  rule application and print a stack of operations leading to that
  result.  The name ``brr'' is suggestive of the [break-rewrite]
  utility, which shares some aspects with the [47mwith-brr-data[0m utility:
  in particular, the [47m:path+[0m command of break-rewrite shows a stack of
  operations in the same way as the query utilities for
  [47mwith-brr-data[0m.  The next section, ``Connection with
  [break-rewrite]'', further explores the relationship between
  break-rewrite and [47mwith-brr-data[0m.

  The collection of break-rewrite data can slow down the theorem
  prover, but the slowdown will probably be modest, perhaps adding an
  extra 15% or so to the time.

  We turn now to examples that illustrate how [47mwith-brr-data[0m works with
  associated query utilities.  These examples are intended to make it
  clear how to use these utilities, but further information is
  provided in later sections for those who are interested.  Note that
  each of these examples attempts to prove a non-theorem; they are
  chosen simply to illustrate tool usage.

  Example Set 1

  Our first illustration of [47mwith-brr-data[0m and associated query
  utilities uses the following (non-theorem) example, mentioned
  earlier.

    (with-brr-data (thm (equal (append x y) (append y x))))

  ACL2 produces the following checkpoints, exactly as though the [47m[thm][0m
  call were evaluated without the surrounding call of [47mwith-brr-data[0m.
  (The optional section on ``Low-level details'' discusses the ``ACL2
  Observation:'' printed at the end by [47mwith-brr-data[0m.)

    *** Key checkpoint at the top level: ***

    Goal
    (EQUAL (APPEND X Y) (APPEND Y X))

    *** Key checkpoints under a top-level induction
        before generating a goal of NIL (see :DOC nil-goal): ***

    Subgoal *1/2''
    (IMPLIES (AND (CONSP X)
                  (EQUAL (APPEND (CDR X) Y)
                         (APPEND Y (CDR X))))
             (EQUAL (CONS (CAR X) (APPEND Y (CDR X)))
                    (APPEND Y X)))

    Subgoal *1/1''
    (IMPLIES (NOT (CONSP X))
             (EQUAL Y (APPEND Y X)))

    ACL2 Error [Failure] in ( THM ...):  See :DOC failure.

    ******** FAILED ********

    ACL2 Observation:  (LENGTH (@ BRR-DATA-LST)) = 16
    ACL2 !>

  Consider the term [47m(APPEND Y (CDR X))[0m occurring in one of these
  checkpoints.  It might not be clear, at least at first glance, how
  this term came about during the proof.  We can find at least one
  source of that term by issuing the [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m query
  shown below, which searches ``top-level'' rewrites --- those not
  invoked when relieving hypotheses of rules --- for a result that
  contains the given term, in this case [47m(append y (cdr x))[0m, as a
  subterm.  The output is similar to the output of the utility,
  [47m[cw-gstack][0m, followed by the result of the bottom-most rewriter
  call, which contains the given term as a subterm.  (See [term] for
  discussion of ``translated'' and ``untranslated'' terms.)

    ACL2 !>(cw-gstack-for-subterm (append y (cdr x)))
    1. Simplifying the clause
         ((NOT (CONSP X))
          (NOT (EQUAL (BINARY-APPEND (CDR X) Y)
                      (BINARY-APPEND Y (CDR X))))
          (EQUAL (BINARY-APPEND X Y)
                 (BINARY-APPEND Y X)))
    2. Rewriting (to simplify) the atom of the third literal,
         (EQUAL (BINARY-APPEND X Y)
                (BINARY-APPEND Y X)),
    3. Rewriting (to simplify) the first argument,
         (BINARY-APPEND X Y),
    4. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION BINARY-APPEND) to
         (BINARY-APPEND X Y)
    The resulting (translated) term is
      (CONS (CAR X)
            (BINARY-APPEND Y (CDR X))).
    ACL2 !>

  One might realize that [47m(BINARY-APPEND Y (CDR X))[0m comes about from
  expanding [47m(BINARY-APPEND X Y)[0m to expose the term [47m(BINARY-APPEND
  (CDR X) Y)[0m and then using the inductive hypothesis to swap the
  arguments.  In that case, the mystery of the term's appearance is
  solved!  But if that isn't clear, one at least has the clue from
  Frame 4 above that the term is produced by applying the definition
  of [47m[binary-append][0m.  With that information one can issue a
  [47m[monitor!][0m command as follows.

    :monitor! (:definition binary-append)
              (equal (brr@ :target) '(binary-append x y))

  We now issue the [47m[thm][0m call above, with or without the wrapper of
  [47mwith-brr-data[0m.  After breaking twice (see [break-rewrite]) and
  proceeding with [47m:go[0m to find two failed attempts to use the
  definition of [47mbinary-append[0m, we explore at the third break as
  follows.

    1 ACL2 >:type-alist

    Decoded type-alist:
    -----
    Terms with type *TS-T*:
    (EQUAL (APPEND (CDR X) Y)
           (APPEND Y (CDR X)))
    -----
    Terms with type *TS-CONS*:
    X

    ==========
    Use (GET-BRR-LOCAL 'TYPE-ALIST STATE) to see actual type-alist.
    1 ACL2 >:target
    (BINARY-APPEND X Y)
    1 ACL2 >:eval

    1! (:DEFINITION BINARY-APPEND) produced
    (CONS (CAR X) (BINARY-APPEND Y (CDR X))).

    1 ACL2 >

  The log above shows how the term [47m(BINARY-APPEND Y (CDR X))[0m was
  produced: the definition of [47mbinary-append[0m was expanded on the term
  [47m(BINARY-APPEND X Y)[0m, and the inductive hypothesis --- represented
  in the [47m:type-alist[0m command's output --- is applied to swap the
  resulting arguments.

  We can find more sources of the same term by using the ``[47m*[0m'' version
  of the [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m command as follows.  By repeatedly
  responding with ``[47my[0m'' to the query presented by the system, we find
  three additional paths leading to the desired subterm.

    (cw-gstack-for-subterm* (append y (cdr x)))

  While [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m and [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm*[0m may be the
  most useful queries in general, one may wish to search for paths
  leading to [3mexactly[0m a given term rather than paths to a term
  [3mcontaining[0m that term (as above).  The queries [47mcw-gstack-for-term[0m
  and [47mcw-gstack-for-term*[0m may be used for this purpose, for example
  as follows.

    (cw-gstack-for-term (append y (cdr x)))
    (cw-gstack-for-term* (append y (cdr x)))

  Finally, these queries support searching for [3minstances[0m of a term or
  subterm, by supplying a ``term'' of the form [47m(:free (v1 ... vn) u)[0m
  where [47mv1[0m through [47mvn[0m are distinct variables and [47mu[0m is a term.  Each
  of the following produces the path shown above.

    (cw-gstack-for-term (:free (u v) (cons u v)))
    (cw-gstack-for-term* (:free (u v) (cons u v)))

  Notice that the use of this [47m:free[0m construct allows one to search for
  the source of a call of any function symbol [47mf[0m, as with [47mcons[0m above.
  If [47mf[0m has k formals, then one can supply the argument [47m(:free (x1 ...
  xk) (f x1 ... xk))[0m to find a rewriter call that produces a call of
  [47mf[0m.

  Example Set 2

  Consider the following (admittedly contrived) example.

    (defstub f0 (x) t)

    (defun f1 (x)
      (cons x x))

    (defun f2 (x)
      (f1 (f0 x)))

    (defun f3 (x)
      (if (atom x)
          (f2 x)
        (f2 (car x))))

    (with-brr-data
     (thm (equal (f3 x)
                 yyy)))

  The call of [47m[thm][0m produces the following checkpoint.

    (IMPLIES (CONSP X)
             (EQUAL (CONS (F0 (CAR X)) (F0 (CAR X)))
                    YYY))

  In this simple example it's easy to figure out how the term [47m(F0 (CAR
  X))[0m was produced.  But suppose we were surprised to see that term.
  We might issue the following query but be disappointed in the
  result.

    ACL2 !>(cw-gstack-for-term (F0 (CAR X)))
    There are no results.
    ACL2 !>

  To see why there are no results, recall that [47mwith-brr-data[0m collects
  data from rule applications.  However, no rule produced the term
  [47m(F0 (CAR X))[0m.  This becomes clear if we issue a query that looks
  for that term as a subterm of a rule application's result.

    ACL2 !>(cw-gstack-for-subterm (F0 (CAR X)))
    1. Simplifying the clause
         ((EQUAL (F3 X) YYY))
    2. Rewriting (to simplify) the atom of the first literal,
         (EQUAL (F3 X) YYY),
    3. Rewriting (to simplify) the first argument,
         (F3 X),
    4. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION F3) to
         (F3 X)
    5. Rewriting (to simplify) the body,
         (IF (CONSP X) (F2 (CAR X)) (F2 X)),
       under the substitution
         X : X
    6. Rewriting (to simplify) the second argument,
         (F2 (CAR X)),
       under the substitution
         X : X
    7. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION F2) to
         (F2 (CAR X))
    8. Rewriting (to simplify) the rhs of the conclusion,
         (F1 (F0 X)),
       under the substitution
         X : (CAR X)
    9. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION F1) to
         (F1 (F0 (CAR X)))
    The resulting (translated) term is
      (CONS (F0 (CAR X)) (F0 (CAR X))).
    Note: The first lemma application above that provides a suitable result
    is at frame 4, and that result is
      (IF (CONSP X)
          (CONS (F0 (CAR X)) (F0 (CAR X)))
        (CONS (F0 X) (F0 X))).
    ACL2 !>

  We see that the term [47m(FO (CAR X))[0m is not itself the result of a rule
  application.  This example illustrates that one may often get
  better results using [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m rather than
  [47mcw-gstack-for-term[0m.

  It is also interesting to look at the ``Note'' printed at the end.
  The query utilities search for the first rule application that
  produced a suitable result, and then they search from that point
  for a maximally deeper rule application that produced a suitable
  result.  In this case, the first rule that produced a term
  containing [47m(FO (CAR X))[0m is shown in the frame at position 4, as per
  the Note.  The rule at frame 9 also produced such a term (though a
  different one than at frame 4), and there was no deeper such rule
  application --- that is, from the time the definition at frame 9
  was applied till the time its body was fully rewritten, no rule
  produced a term containing [47m(FO (CAR X))[0m.  (This notion of
  ``deeper'' is discussed at some length in the section below on
  ``General forms of queries''.)

  Next we'll explore a limitation of these tools and how to get around
  it.  We start as follows (following the definitions above).

    (with-brr-data
     (thm (equal (append (f3 x) y)
                 z)
          :hints ((\"Goal\" :in-theory (disable append)))))

  This proof attempt results in the following checkpoint.

    (IMPLIES (CONSP X)
             (EQUAL (APPEND (CONS (F0 (CAR X)) (F0 (CAR X)))
                            Y)
                    Z))

  The following log may be surprising, especially since the indicated
  term need only be a subterm of a rewriter result, and we see this
  exact term in the checkpoint above!

    ACL2 !>(cw-gstack-for-subterm (APPEND (CONS (F0 (CAR X)) (F0 (CAR X))) Y))
    There are no results.
    ACL2 !>

  A solution is to choose a subterm of that input term, as shown in the
  log below.  The resulting output shows why the query above yielded
  no results: frame 6 below shows that the first argument, [47m(F3 X)[0m, of
  [47mbinary-append[0m (see frame 3 below) generates a call of [47mIF[0m, so the
  rewrite at frame 3 will generate a term of the form [47m(BINARY-APPEND
  (IF ...)  ...)[0m, not of the form [47m(BINARY-APPEND (CONS ...) ...)[0m.

    ACL2 !>(cw-gstack-for-subterm (cons (f0 (car x)) (f0 (car x))))
    1. Simplifying the clause
         ((EQUAL (BINARY-APPEND (F3 X) Y) Z))
    2. Rewriting (to simplify) the atom of the first literal,
         (EQUAL (BINARY-APPEND (F3 X) Y) Z),
    3. Rewriting (to simplify) the first argument,
         (BINARY-APPEND (F3 X) Y),
    4. Rewriting (to simplify) the first argument,
         (F3 X),
    5. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION F3) to
         (F3 X)
    6. Rewriting (to simplify) the body,
         (IF (CONSP X) (F2 (CAR X)) (F2 X)),
       under the substitution
         X : X
    7. Rewriting (to simplify) the second argument,
         (F2 (CAR X)),
       under the substitution
         X : X
    8. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION F2) to
         (F2 (CAR X))
    9. Rewriting (to simplify) the rhs of the conclusion,
         (F1 (F0 X)),
       under the substitution
         X : (CAR X)
    10. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION F1) to
         (F1 (F0 (CAR X)))
    The resulting (translated) term is
      (CONS (F0 (CAR X)) (F0 (CAR X))).
    Note: The first lemma application above that provides a suitable result
    is at frame 5, and that result is
      (IF (CONSP X)
          (CONS (F0 (CAR X)) (F0 (CAR X)))
        (CONS (F0 X) (F0 X))).
    ACL2 !>

  The careful reader may notice another reason that the [47mappend[0m call
  does not yield a match: no such call is the result of a rewriter
  call.

  The moral of this story is that if a term fails to give you a match,
  then use a subterm of it.  You can also use the [47m:free[0m construct
  mentioned above, for example as follows --- this gives the same
  output as shown in the log just above.

    (cw-gstack-for-subterm (:free (v) (cons (f0 v) (f0 v))))


Connections with break-rewrite

  We noted above that after running [47mwith-brr-data[0m, the stacks (paths)
  displayed by query commands such as [47mcw-gstack-for-term[0m are the same
  as the stacks displayed by the [break-rewrite] command, [47m:path+[0m.

  But there are these additional connections between [47mwith-brr-data[0m and
  the break-rewrite utility.

    * The same rewriting processes are considered by [47mwith-brr-data[0m as by
      break-rewrite; in particular, abbreviation rules are not
      considered during preprocessing (see [monitor]).

    * When a query command ([47mcw-gstack-for-term[0m etc.) finds a match with a
      rewriting result, it discards the result if the input --- the
      [47m:target[0m, in the parlance of [break-rewrite] --- contains that
      match.

    * [Monitor]ed [rune]s are indeed monitored during evaluation of a call
      of [47mwith-brr-data[0m, even if break-rewrite has not been enabled
      globally (using [47m:[0m[47m[brr][0m or [47m[monitor!][0m).  If this is not desired,
      then [unmonitor] runes before calling [47mwith-brr-data[0m.

    * There is the following low-level way to collect brr-data for queries
      such as [47mcw-gstack-for-term[0m without calling [47mwith-brr-data[0m:
      [47m(assign gstack :brr-data)[0m.  But you may want to clear such data
      afterwards, which you can do by evaluating the form,
      [47m(clear-brr-data-lst)[0m.  Otherwise the brr-data from later proof
      attempts will be combined, probably in unexpected ways, with
      brr-data from earlier proof attempts.

    * [47mWith-brr-data[0m and its queries pay no attention to ``near-miss''
      breaks (see [break-rewrite] for discussion of these breaks).

  The first item above is worth emphasizing.  Consider the following
  example.

    (include-book \"std/lists/rev\" :dir :system)
    (with-brr-data
     (thm (implies (and (natp n)
                        (< n (len x)))
                   (equal (nth n (revappend x y))
                          (nth n (reverse x))))
          :hints ((\"Goal\" :do-not '(preprocess)))))
    (cw-gstack-for-subterm (APPEND (REV X) Y))

  The [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m query yields a result in this example, but
  not if we change it to remove the [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m.  If we use [47m:[0m[47m[pso][0m on
  the proof attempt without the [47m:hints[0m, we notice that the requested
  subterm was introduced by ``the simple :rewrite rule
  REVAPPEND-REMOVAL''; here ``simple'' indicates the use of the
  [3mpreprocess[0m process for simplification, which avoids the usual
  rewriter.  If we instead query the no-hints version with
  [47m(cw-gstack-for-subterm (REV X))[0m, the output below shows that a
  chain of rewrites generates [47m(APPEND (REV X) Y)[0m as an intermediate
  term but not as the result of a rewrite.

    ACL2 !>(cw-gstack-for-subterm (REV X))
    1. Simplifying the clause
         ((NOT (INTEGERP N))
          (< N '0)
          (NOT (< N (LEN X)))
          (EQUAL (NTH N (BINARY-APPEND (REV X) Y))
                 (NTH N (REVERSE X))))
    2. Rewriting (to simplify) the atom of the fourth literal,
         (EQUAL (NTH N (BINARY-APPEND (REV X) Y))
                (NTH N (REVERSE X))),
    3. Rewriting (to simplify) the second argument,
         (NTH N (REVERSE X)),
    4. Rewriting (to simplify) the second argument,
         (REVERSE X),
    5. Attempting to apply (:DEFINITION REVERSE) to
         (REVERSE X)
    6. Rewriting (to simplify) the body,
         (IF (STRINGP X)
             (COERCE (REVAPPEND (COERCE X 'LIST) 'NIL)
                     'STRING)
           (REVAPPEND X 'NIL)),
       under the substitution
         X : X
    7. Rewriting (to simplify) the third argument,
         (REVAPPEND X 'NIL),
       under the substitution
         X : X
    8. Attempting to apply (:REWRITE REVAPPEND-REMOVAL) to
         (REVAPPEND X 'NIL)
    9. Rewriting (to simplify) the rhs of the conclusion,
         (BINARY-APPEND (REV X) Y),
       under the substitution
         Y : 'NIL
         X : X
    10. Attempting to apply (:REWRITE APPEND-ATOM-UNDER-LIST-EQUIV) to
         (BINARY-APPEND (REV X) 'NIL)
    The resulting (translated) term is
      (REV X).
    Note: The first lemma application above that provides a suitable result
    is at frame 5, and that result is
      (IF (STRINGP X)
          (COERCE (REV (COERCE X 'LIST)) 'STRING)
        (REV X)).
    ACL2 !>

  The version of this example without [47m:hints[0m also illustrates the
  second item above, about discarding matches that occur in the
  [47m:target[0m of rewriting.  Without that restriction we would see a
  result for the query [47m(cw-gstack-for-subterm (APPEND (REV X) Y))[0m
  from an attempt to rewrite the term [47m(NTH N (APPEND (REV X) Y))[0m.
  But that would not help us to find the source of the term [47m(APPEND
  (REV X) Y)[0m.


General form of [47mwith-brr-data[0m calls

  The general form, including optional keyword arguments, is

    (with-brr-data form :global-var var :brr-data-returned bool)

  where [47mform[0m is typically an event but is only required to evaluate to
  an [error-triple].  By default (i.e., when the keyword arguments
  are omitted), that error triple is returned by the [47mwith-brr-data[0m
  call.

  There is probably little reason for most users to supply either
  keyword argument.  They are documented in the section after next.

  [31;1mNotes[0m.

    * The data collected by a call of [47mwith-brr-data[0m will persist until the
      next call of [47mwith-brr-data[0m.

    * Behavior is undefined for nested calls of [47mwith-brr-data[0m.  (If this
      presents a problem then you may ask the ACL2 implementors to
      consider specifying that behavior.)


General forms of queries

  The four queries (all illustrated above) are as follows, where the
  keyword argument is optional.

    (cw-gstack-for-subterm u :global-var var)
    (cw-gstack-for-subterm* u :global-var var)
    (cw-gstack-for-term u :global-var var)
    (cw-gstack-for-term* u :global-var var)

  In each case, [47mu[0m is either a term (which can be a user-level term,
  i.e., not necessarily translated; see [term]) or of the form [47m(:free
  (v1 ... vk) u1)[0m where [47mu1[0m is a term.  In the latter case, [47mv1[0m through
  [47mvk[0m must be distinct variables that all occur in (the translation
  of) [47mu1[0m.

  These queries are effective after data collection using
  [47mwith-brr-data[0m, which is typically wrapped around an event that
  calls the theorem prover.  To understand how the queries work, it
  is useful to view a proof attempt as generating ``initial'' calls
  of the rewriter, such as when rewriting a hypothesis or the
  conclusion of a goal (technically, a literal of the goal clause;
  see [clause]): that is, initial rewriter calls are those that are
  not under another rewriter call.  Then data collection under each
  initial call creates a tree of ``top-level'' calls --- those not
  made under an attempt to relieve a hypothesis --- for which that
  initial call is the root.  Thus each node of that tree corresponds
  to a rewriter call, and each child of that node is a top-level call
  of the rewriter that is immediately under that call, where the
  order of calls is respected by the ordering of child nodes from
  left to right.  For example, the top-level application a rewrite
  rule corresponds to a node, and the call to rewrite the right-hand
  side of the rule (with appropriate variable bindings) corresponds
  to a child of that node.  Another example is when an attempt to
  rewrite a function call leads to an attempt to rewrite an argument
  of that call: the latter corresponds to a child node of the former.
  Each of the four query utilities looks for the first root node, and
  the left-most branch under that node, that results in a rewriter
  result that ``matches'' the input in the following sense.

  This notion of ``matches'' depends on the call, as follows.  First
  assume that the input is a term.  For [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m and
  [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm*[0m, the result matches when it contains the
  given term as a subterm.  For [47mcw-gstack-for-term[0m and
  [47mcw-gstack-for-term*[0m, the result matches when it is exactly the
  given term.  Now suppose the input is [47m(:free (v1 ... vk) u)[0m.  Then
  the notion of ``matches'' is modified to allow any instance [47mu/s[0m for
  a substitution [47ms[0m that instantiates only the variables [47mvi[0m.

  When a matching node [47mN1[0m is found for the input term, or in the case
  of [47m(:free (v1 ... vk) u)[0m, some instance of [47mu[0m, then a further search
  below [47mN[0m is made to find a maximal left-most branch below it that
  terminates in a node [47mN2[0m that also matches, using the same
  substitution in the case of [47m:free[0m.  If no such branch is found,
  then [47mN2[0m is [47mN[0m.  Either way, the term or instance that matched at [47mN[0m
  (as a subterm in the case of [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m or
  [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm*[0m, else as an exact match) also matches at [47mN2[0m.
  The output then shows a path to [47mN2[0m, but if [47mN1[0m and [47mN2[0m differ then
  the output also notes that the first match was at [47mN1[0m, as we have
  seen in each ``Note'' at the end of logs displayed above.

  The discussion above characterizes the behavior of
  [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm[0m and [47mcw-gstack-for-term[0m; now let us consider
  [47mcw-gstack-for-subterm*[0m and [47mcw-gstack-for-term*[0m.  These ``[47m*[0m''
  versions query after each result to ask if another result is
  desired.  If so, the search starts strictly after [47mN[0m; it does not
  look for a second successor [47mN2[0m of [47mN[0m.  The search continues until
  either the user answers [47mn[0m (regardless of the package; the query
  cares only that the [47m[symbol-name][0m is [47m\"Y\"[0m or [47m\"N\"[0m) or there are no
  more results.


Keyword arguments of [47mwith-brr-data[0m (optional)

  As noted above, the general form of a call of [47mwith-brr-data[0m is as
  follows.

    (with-brr-data form :global-var var :brr-data-returned bool)

  The keywords have aspects that are quite technical, so unless you are
  somewhat familiar with ACL2 implementation issues you will probably
  want to skip this section.

  Neither keyword argument is evaluated.  The behavior of the keyword
  arguments depends on a list, [47mBDL[0m (``Brr-Data List''), that is
  typically made available by a call of [47mwith-brr-data[0m, as described
  below.  The members of [47mBDL[0m represent successful initial calls of
  the rewriter, in order.  Each of those members is a [47mbrr-data[0m
  record, as described in the next section.  After the [47mwith-brr-data[0m
  call returns, then [47m(mv nil BDL state)[0m is returned by evaluation of
  [47m(brr-data-lst state)[0m, until the next time that either a call of
  [47mwith-brr-data[0m or the form [47m(clear-brr-data-lst)[0m is evaluated.
  Implementation notes: information is stored in the [47mbrr-data[0m
  [wormhole]; [47m(brr-data-lst state)[0m extracts that information and
  reorders appropriately before producing [47mBDL[0m; and
  [47m(clear-brr-data-lst)[0m removes that information from the [47mbrr-data[0m
  wormhole.

  We now explain the keyword arguments in terms of the list [47mBDL[0m.

    * [47m:global-var var[0m
      The symbol [47mvar[0m defaults to the symbol, [47mbrr-data-lst[0m.  [47mVar[0m is either
      [47mnil[0m or a state global variable (see [programming-with-state]),
      which by default is [47mbrr-data-lst[0m.  If [47mvar[0m is not [47mnil[0m, then the
      [47mwith-brr-data[0m call modifies [state] by setting the value of the
      state global, [47mvar[0m, to [47mBDL[0m.

    * [47m:brr-data-returned bool[0m
      Suppose that the [47mform[0m argument of the [47mwith-brr-data[0m call evaluates
      to [47m(mv erp val state)[0m.  Then when [47mbool[0m is [47mnil[0m (which is the
      default), the [47mwith-brr-data[0m call returns that same
      [error-triple], [47m(mv erp val state)[0m.  If however [47mbool[0m is not
      [47mnil[0m, then instead the [47mwith-brr-data[0m call returns [47m(mv erp BDL
      state)[0m.


Low-level details (optional)

  This implementation-level section further describes the [47mbrr-data[0m
  structure mentioned above.  It is probably of interest only to
  those who consider writing their own tools to query the data
  produced by [47mwith-brr-data[0m.

  A [47mbrr-data[0m record is a structure that has a field [47m:brr-data-1[0m
  representing information from entry into the rewriter, a field
  [47m:brr-data-2[0m representing information from the corresponding exit
  from the rewriter, and a field [47m:completed[0m that is the list of
  [47mbrr-data[0m records produced between that entry and exit, in order.
  (Implementation detail: these are produced inside the [47mbrr-data[0m
  [wormhole] by the same functions, [47mbrkpt1[0m and [47mbrkpt2[0m, that implement
  the [break-rewrite] utility.)

    (defrec brr-data
      (pre post . completed)
      nil)

    (defrec brr-data-1
      (((lemma . target) . (unify-subst type-alist . geneqv))
       .
       ((pot-list . ancestors) . (rcnst initial-ttree . gstack)))
      nil)

    (defrec brr-data-2
      ((failure-reason unify-subst . brr-result)
       .
       (rcnst final-ttree . gstack))
      nil)

  The [47mbrr-data[0m definition introduces a recursive data type (as per ACL2
  source function [47mbrr-data-p[0m, since the [47m:completed[0m field is a list of
  [47mbrr-data[0m records.  These correspond to immediate sub-calls of the
  rewriter, yielding ``child nodes'' as described in the section,
  ``General forms of queries''.

  We have seen above that the use of [47mwith-brr-data[0m activates the
  collection of break-rewrite data.  Collection may also be activated
  by assigning the [state] global, [47mgstackp[0m, to the value [47m:brr-data[0m.
  (See [programming-with-state] for relevant programming background.)
  You can use [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m on a [47mwith-brr-data[0m call to see how
  [47mwith-brr-data[0m sets [47mgstackp[0m.  Note that setting (or binding) [47mgstackp[0m
  to [47m:brr-data[0m has the effect of [47m:[0m[47m[brr][0m[47m t[0m but enhanced with
  collection of [47mbrr-data[0m records.  When [47mgstackp[0m has value [47m:brr-data[0m,
  [47m:brr t[0m is treated as a no-op and [47m:brr nil[0m is an error, to avoid
  inadvertently turning off [47mbrr-data[0m record collection.  Note that
  although [47mwith-brr-data[0m is disallowed in ACL2(p) (see [parallelism])
  when [waterfall-parallelism] is enabled, this prohibition can be
  circumvented by assigning [47mgstackp[0m to [47m:brr-data[0m; however, in that
  case [47mwith-brr-data[0m might well not behave correctly.

  The programmer who manipulates [47mbrr-data[0m records may wish to look at
  the ACL2 source code and the [break-rewrite] documentation to
  understand the fields of the [47mbrr-data-1[0m and [47mbrr-data-2[0m records.
  The form [47m(brr-data-listp t lst)[0m recognizes a list of well-formed
  [47mbrr-data[0m records.  The [47m:failure-reason[0m field of the [47mbrr-data-2[0m
  record may be of particular interest; see ACL2 source function
  [47mtilde-@-failure-reason-phrase1[0m for the forms that this field may
  take.

  It is easy to see the list of [47mbrr-data[0m records created by a call of
  [47mwith-brr-data[0m.  A big hint is given in the output printed at the
  completion of a [47mwith-brr-data[0m call, as suggested in the first
  example in the introduction, with the following output.

    ACL2 Observation:  (LENGTH (@ BRR-DATA-LST)) = 16

  The form [47m(@ BRR-DATA-LST)[0m evaluates to the resulting list of [47mbrr-data[0m
  records, one for each so-called ``initial call'' of the rewriter,
  in the sense described in the section above on ``Keyword
  arguments''.  The utility [47mshow-brr-data-lst[0m can be applied to such
  a list to show the most interesting parts of each record, for
  example, [47m(show-brr-data-lst (@ brr-data-lst))[0m.  Also you can call
  the utility [47mshow-brr-data[0m on a single [47mbrr-data[0m record.


Using attachments to modify functionality of [47mwith-brr-data[0m

  The behavior of [47mwith-brr-data[0m is customizable by using attachments
  (see [defattach]).  The macro, [47mset-brr-data-attachments[0m, has been
  provided for this purpose.  This section provides only minimal
  documentation on this customizability; those who want to implement
  new such attachments can look at the following examples in the
  [community-books] to get a sense of how that might be done.  Note
  that the query utilities work the same regardless of the
  attachments, and the relevant data structures are also unchanged.

    books/kestrel/utilities/brr-data-all.lisp
    books/kestrel/utilities/brr-data-failures.lisp

  When the macro is called with no arguments, i.e.,
  [47m(set-brr-data-attachments)[0m, behavior is restored to the built-in
  behavior as described above.  But this macro can be given an
  argument that indicates a string: it is either a string or a
  symbol, where a symbol indicates its [47m[symbol-name][0m.  The indicated
  string, which we write below as [47m\"<S>\"[0m, is added as a suffix after a
  hyphen to create four system attachments.  These are [47m[defattach][0m
  calls rather than [47m[defattach-system][0m calls, so that their effect is
  not [local] to the enclosing book.

    (defattach (brkpt1-brr-data-entry brkpt1-brr-data-entry-<S> :system-ok t)
    (defattach (brkpt2-brr-data-entry brkpt2-brr-data-entry-<S> :system-ok t)
    (defattach (update-brr-data-1 update-brr-data-1-<S>) :system-ok t)
    (defattach (update-brr-data-2 update-brr-data-2-<S>) :system-ok t)

  The four [47m\"-<S>\"[0m functions must be defined to match the signatures of
  the functions to which they are attached.  Those functions have the
  following arguments and results, where [47m*[0m indicates an ordinary
  (non-[stobj]) value.

    (brkpt1-brr-data-entry ancestors gstack rcnst state) => *
    (brkpt2-brr-data-entry ancestors gstack rcnst state) => *
    (update-brr-data-1 lemma target unify-subst type-alist ancestors
                       initial-ttree gstack rcnst pot-lst whs-data) => *
    (update-brr-data-2 wonp failure-reason unify-subst gstack brr-result
                       final-ttree rcnst ancestors whs-data) => *

  Note that [47m(set-brr-data-attachments)[0m is actually equivalent to
  [47m(set-brr-data-attachments builtin)[0m.  That is, the initial
  (built-in) attachments to the functions above all have the suffix,
  [47m\"-BUILTIN\"[0m.")
 (WITH-CBD
  (BOOKS-REFERENCE)
  "To bind the connected book directory

  [47mWith-cbd[0m sets the connected book directory (see [cbd]) within its
  scope.

    Example Forms:

    ; Equivalent to (include-book \"dir1/dir2/foo\"):
    (with-cbd \"dir1\"
              (include-book \"dir2/foo\"))

    ; Equivalent to evaluation of the form,
    ; (include-book \"arithmetic/top\" :dir :system)'):
    (with-cbd (cdr (assoc-eq :system (project-dir-alist (w state))))
              (include-book \"arithmetic/top\"))

  See [cbd] for a description of the connected book directory.  The
  ``Technical Remark'' there, about Lisp using the [47mcbd[0m to elaborate
  relative pathnames, applied to [47mwith-cbd[0m as well.

    General Form:
    (with-cbd str form)

  where [47mstr[0m evaluates to a nonempty string that represents the desired
  directory (see [pathname]) and [47mform[0m evaluates to an [error-triple].

  The effect of [47m(with-cbd str form)[0m is to evaluate first [47m(set-cbd str)[0m
  and then to evaluate [47mform[0m, after which the connected book directory
  is restored to the value it had before that evaluation of [47mset-cbd[0m.
  However, the implementation is designed so that the connected book
  directory is restored even when the evaluation of [47mform[0m causes an
  error.

  The form [47m(with-cbd str ev)[0m is an [event] form if [47mev[0m is an event form;
  thus, it may occur in [books] as well as [47m[encapsulate][0m and [47m[progn][0m
  events.  See [embedded-event-form].  But in an [event] context, [47mstr[0m
  must be a string, not merely an expression that evaluates to a
  string.

  Finally, note that Lisp compilers may vary in how they handle
  functions defined within the scope of [47mwith-cbd[0m.  If you find an
  example of slower execution caused by using [47mwith-cbd[0m, please feel
  free to send it to the ACL2 implementors.")
 (WITH-CURRENT-PACKAGE
  (BOOKS-REFERENCE)
  "To bind the [current-package]

  Evaluation of a form [47m(with-current-package \"pkg\" form[0m) causes [47mform[0m to
  be evaluated with the [current-package] bound to [47m\"pkg\"[0m.

  Example and [31;1mWARNING[0m.  The current-package is not modified until after
  the form is read!  Consider the following log that was produced
  when the value of the current-package was the default, [47m\"ACL2\"[0m.

    ACL2 !>(with-current-package \"ACL2-USER\"
                                 (value (cw \"~x0~%\" 'abcd)))
    ACL2::ABCD
     NIL
    ACL2 !>

  We see that the call of [47m[cw][0m did its printing relative to the
  [47m\"ACL2-USER\"[0m package, producing output [47m\"ACL2::ABCD\"[0m.  This
  illustrates that the form was [3mread[0m with respect to package [47m\"ACL2\"[0m
  but that its [3mevaluation[0m took place with respect to package
  [47m\"ACL2-USER\"[0m.

    General Form:
    (with-current-package str form)

  where [47mstr[0m evaluates to a nonempty string that represents the desired
  package (see [current-package]) and [47mform[0m evaluates to an
  [error-triple].

  Evaluation of [47m(with-current-package str form)[0m first switches to the
  indicated package, as with [47m(in-package str)[0m, and evaluates [47mform[0m,
  after which the current-package is restored to the value it had
  before that evaluation of [47mwith-current-package[0m.  The implementation
  is designed so that the current-package is restored even when the
  evaluation of [47mform[0m causes an error.

  The following example drives home the behavior of
  [47mwith-current-package[0m; explanation follows.

    ACL2 !>'common-lisp::defun
    DEFUN
    ACL2 !>'acl2-user::defun
    DEFUN
    ACL2 !>(with-current-package
            \"ACL2-USER\"
            (er-progn (set-current-package \"ACL2-PC\" state)
                      (value (cw \"~x0~%\" 'defun))))
    COMMON-LISP::DEFUN
     NIL
    ACL2 !>

  The symbol [47mcommon-lisp::defun[0m is imported from the [47m\"COMMON-LISP\"[0m
  package into both the [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package and, as shown in the first two
  evaluation results above, the [47m\"ACL2-USER\"[0m package.  But it's not
  imported into the [47m\"ACL2-PC\"[0m package.  The call above of
  [47mwith-current-package[0m reads [47m'defun[0m into the current package, [47m\"ACL2\"[0m.
  Then for evaluation the package changes to [47m\"ACL2-USER\"[0m; then the
  package is changed again to [47m\"ACL2-PC\"[0m before evaluating the [47m[cw][0m
  call in that package, necessitating the [47m\"COMMON-LISP::\"[0m prefix
  since the [47mdefun[0m symbol in that [47mcw[0m call is not in the [47m\"ACL2-PC\"[0m
  package.  But notice that the prompt comes back as [47m\"ACL2 !>\"[0m,
  indicating that we are back in the [47m\"ACL2\"[0m package.  The original
  package is restored after the [47mwith-current-package[0m call completes
  evaluation, even when the current-package is changed during
  evaluation.

  The form [47m(with-current-package str ev)[0m is an [event] form if [47mev[0m is an
  event form; thus, it may occur in [books] as well as [47m[encapsulate][0m
  and [47m[progn][0m events.  See [embedded-event-form].  But in an [event]
  context, [47mstr[0m must be a string, not merely an expression that
  evaluates to a string.

  Finally, note that Lisp compilers may vary in how they handle
  functions defined within the scope of [47mwith-current-package[0m.  If you
  find an example of slower execution caused by using
  [47mwith-current-package[0m, please feel free to send it to the ACL2
  implementors.")
 (WITH-FAST-ALIST
  (FAST-ALISTS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "[47m(with-fast-alist name form)[0m causes [47mname[0m to be a fast alist for the
  execution of [47mform[0m.

  Logically, [47mwith-fast-alist[0m just returns [47mform[0m.

  Under the hood, we cause [47malist[0m to become a fast alist before
  executing [47mform[0m.  If doing so caused us to introduce a new hash
  table, the hash table is automatically freed after [47mform[0m completes.

  More accurately, under the hood [47m(with-fast-alist name form)[0m
  essentially expands to something like:

    (if (already-fast-alist-p name)
        form
      (let ((<temp> (make-fast-alist name)))
        (prog1 form
               (fast-alist-free <temp>))))

  Practically speaking, [47mwith-fast-alist[0m is frequently a better choice
  then just using [47m[make-fast-alist][0m, and is particularly useful for
  writing functions that can take either fast or slow alists as
  arguments.  That is, consider the difference between:

    (defun bad (alist ...)
      (let* ((fast-alist (make-fast-alist alist))
             (answer     (expensive-computation fast-alist ...)))
       (prog2$ (fast-alist-free fast-alist)
               answer)))

    (defun good (alist ...)
       (with-fast-alist alist
         (expensive-computation alist ...)))

  Either approach is fine if the caller provides a slow [47malist[0m.  But if
  the input [47malist[0m is already fast, [47mbad[0m will (perhaps unexpectedly)
  free it!  On the other hand, [47mgood[0m is able to take advantage of an
  already-fast argument and will not cause it to be inadvertently
  freed.

  See also the macro [47mwith-fast-alists[0m defined in the community book
  [47m\"books/centaur/misc/hons-extra.lisp\"[0m, which allows you to call
  [47m[with-fast-alist][0m on several alists simultaneously.

  The community book [47m\"books/centaur/misc/hons-extra.lisp\"[0m extends the
  [b*] macro with the [47mwith-fast[0m pattern binder.  That is, after
  executing [47m(include-book \"centaur/misc/hons-extra.lisp\" :dir
  :system)[0m you may write something like this:

    (b* (...
         ((with-fast a b c ...))
         ...)
      ...)

  which causes [47ma[0m, [47mb[0m, and [47mc[0m to become fast alists until the completion
  of the [47mb*[0m form.

  Note that [47mwith-fast-alist[0m will cause logically tail-recursive
  functions not to execute tail-recursively if its cleanup phase
  happens after the tail-recursive call returns.")
 (WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ
  (STOBJ ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Operate on a global single-threaded object

  See [stobj] for an introduction to single-threaded objects.  Also see
  [defstobj] for additional background.

  The [47mwith-global-stobj[0m macro is a relatively advanced utility that
  allows stobjs to be accessed directly from the ACL2 [state].
  Examples may be found in [community-book] file
  [47mbooks/system/tests/with-global-stobj-input.lsp[0m; we draw heavily
  from them below.

    Example Forms:

    ; Read-only form (length 3)
    (with-global-stobj st
      ;; body:
      (fld st))

    ; Updating form (length 4; (returns state))
    (with-global-stobj
      st ; bound stobj
      (st) ; output signature of body
      ;; body:
      (update-fld x st))

    ; Updating form (length 4; returns (mv * * state st2))
    (with-global-stobj
      st ; bound stobj
      (nil st nil state st2) ; output signature of body
      ;; body:
      (let* ((st (update-fld x st))
             (st2 (update-fld2 x st2)))
        (mv (fld st) st (fld st2) state st2)))

  In the forms above, we call [47mst[0m the stobj that is ``bound by'' the
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call, and the ``body'' of the form is the last
  argument.  The read-only form above, like all read-only forms, has
  a body that does not return the stobj bound by the form.  Each
  updating form above specifies an output signature as a list, which
  must contain the stobj bound by the form, whose elements are all
  [47mnil[0m (designating a non-stobj value) or a stobj name.  That output
  signature reflects the result of the body; the entire form does not
  return the bound stobj, but does return [47m[state][0m, as explained
  below.

  [47mWith-global-stobj[0m is a macro, and the example forms above expand as
  follows.

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (with-global-stobj st
                     (fld st))
     (LET ((ST (READ-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST STATE)))
          (FLD ST))
    ACL2 !>:trans1 (with-global-stobj st
                     (st)
                     (update-fld x st))
     (LET ((ST (READ-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST STATE)))
          (LET ((ST (UPDATE-FLD X ST)))
               (WRITE-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST ST STATE)))
    ACL2 !>:trans1 (with-global-stobj st
                     (nil st nil state st2)
                     (let* ((st (update-fld x st))
                            (st2 (update-fld2 x st2)))
                       (mv (fld st) st (fld st2) state st2)))
     (LET ((ST (READ-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST STATE)))
          (MV-LET ({WGS}0 ST {WGS}1 STATE ST2)
                  (LET* ((ST (UPDATE-FLD X ST))
                         (ST2 (UPDATE-FLD2 X ST2)))
                        (MV (FLD ST) ST (FLD ST2) STATE ST2))
                  (LET ((STATE (WRITE-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST ST STATE)))
                       (MV? {WGS}0 {WGS}1 STATE ST2))))
    ACL2 !>

  The first illustrates that in the read-only form, the bound stobj,
  which is [47mst[0m in that example, is bound to its value in the
  [47muser-stobj-alist[0m field of the ACL2 [state].  The second and third
  similarly bind the stobj, [47mst[0m, but then update that stobj according
  to the body of the [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call (its last argument) and
  then update the [47muser-stobj-alist[0m of the state with that stobj's
  resulting value.  You can of course use [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m in this way to
  see expansions of other [47mwith-global-stobj[0m calls.

  Note that ACL2 expects you to use [47mwith-global-stobj[0m, not its
  expansions in terms of the [non-executable] functions
  [47mread-user-stobj-alist[0m, which accesses the bound stobj from the
  user-stobj-alist of [47mstate[0m, and [47mwrite-user-stobj-alist[0m, which
  completes the write for updating [47mwith-global-stobj[0m forms.  These
  are defined as follows, in terms of the ACL2 state's
  user-stobj-alist field, which maps stobj names to their values.

    (defun-nx read-user-stobj-alist (st state)
      (declare (xargs :guard (symbolp st)
                      :stobjs state))
      (cdr (assoc-eq st (user-stobj-alist1 state))))

    (defun-nx write-user-stobj-alist (st val state)
      (declare (xargs :guard (symbolp st)
                      :stobjs state))
      (update-user-stobj-alist1
       (put-assoc-eq st val (user-stobj-alist1 state))
       state))

  [47mWith-global-stobj[0m can be useful when you want a function to read or
  write a stobj but you don't want to pass that stobj as a formal
  parameter.  As long as you pass [47mstate[0m as a formal parameter, you
  can access the stobj using [47mwith-global-stobj[0m.

  This topic is intended to be sufficient preparation for the use of
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m.  Those who want to read more about design and
  underlying theory are welcome to peruse the (long) ACL2 source code
  comments, ``Essay on the Design of With-global-stobj'' and ``Essay
  on Correctness of Evaluation with Stobjs''.


More Examples

  As noted above, examples may be found in [community-book] file
  [47mbooks/system/tests/with-global-stobj-input.lsp[0m.  Here we discuss
  some of those examples.

  Let us start by introducing a couple of stobjs.

    (defstobj st fld)
    (defstobj st2 fld2 :congruent-to st)

  Calls of [47mwith-global-stobj[0m are illegal at the top level (as opposed
  to occurrences in the bodies of a definition or a theorem).

    (with-global-stobj st (fld st))

  One solution may be to use [47m[top-level][0m.

    (top-level (with-global-stobj st (fld st)))

  Normally, however, [47mwith-global-stobj[0m is used inside definition
  bodies.  Here we read and write the stobj, [47mst[0m, directly from the
  ACL2 [state].

    (defun rd0 (state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (with-global-stobj st (fld st)))

    (defun wr0 (x state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (with-global-stobj st (st) (update-fld x st)))

  Let's see these in action, first writing and then reading.

    ACL2 !>(wr0 2 state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(rd0 state)
    2
    ACL2 !>(fld st)
    2
    ACL2 !>

  We can use various stobj operations, even the rather fancy
  [47m[swap-stobjs][0m, in the body of a [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call.  The
  following events are admissible.

    (update-fld 1 st)
    (update-fld 2 st2)

    (defun f3 (st2 state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st2 state)))
      (with-global-stobj
        st
        (st2 st)
        (swap-stobjs st2 st)))

    (f3 st2 state)

    (assert-event (and (equal (fld st) 2)
                       (equal (fld st2) 1)))

  The following function writes to both [47mst[0m and [47mst2[0m without passing in
  either one (just [47mstate[0m).  Notice that the inner [47mwith-global-stobj[0m
  call has a body that returns the indicated values [47mst[0m and [47mst2[0m, but
  since [47mst2[0m is bound by the call, it is dropped before returning from
  the call, and [47mstate[0m is added --- which explains the list [47m(st state)[0m
  supplied to the outer call.

    (defun write-global-st-st2 (fld fld2 state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (with-global-stobj st
        (st state)
        (let ((st (update-fld fld st)))
          (with-global-stobj st2
            (st st2)
            (let ((st2 (update-fld fld2 st2)))
              (mv st st2))))))

  Let's check that this works as expected.

    ACL2 !>(write-global-st-st2 'a 'b state)
    <state>
    ACL2 !>(fld st)
    A
    ACL2 !>(fld st2)
    B
    ACL2 !>

  We can also read both fields.

    (defun read-global-st-st2 (state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (with-global-stobj st
        (with-global-stobj st2
          (list (fld st) (fld st2)))))

  Then, continuing with the session above:

    ACL2 !>(read-global-st-st2 state)
    (A B)
    ACL2 !>

  We can reason about [47mwith-global-stobj[0m by reasoning about its
  expansions.  Consider the following theorem (continuing the session
  above).

    (defthm rd0-of-wr0
      (equal (rd0 (wr0 val state))
             val))

  This fails to prove, but each of the two checkpoints has a term of
  the form [47m(ASSOC-EQUAL 'ST (PUT-ASSOC-EQUAL 'ST _ _))[0m.  That
  suggests the following lemma.

    (defthm assoc-equal-put-assoc-equal
      (equal (assoc-equal key (put-assoc-equal key val alist))
             (cons key val)))

  This lemma proves automatically, after which [47mrd0-of-wr0[0m proves
  automatically.


Syntax and Semantics

  This section provides a reference for [47mwith-global-stobj[0m.  The next
  section discusses restrictions that avoid aliasing problems.

    General Forms:
    ; Read-only form (length 3):
    (with-global-stobj st form)
    ; Updating form (length 4):
    (with-global-stobj st lst form)

  where [47mst[0m is the name of a [stobj] that is user-defined (i.e., not
  [47mstate[0m), [47mform[0m is subject to syntactic restrictions discussed below,
  and [47mlst[0m is a list, sometimes called an ``output signature''.  That
  list indicates the list of [47mN[0m values returned by [47mform[0m, which must
  include the bound stobj, [47mst[0m: thus [47mlst[0m is [47m(st)[0m if [47mN[0m is 1, indicating
  that [47mform[0m returns an instance of that stobj; and otherwise [47mform[0m
  returns multiple values [47m(x0 x1 ... xk)[0m where [47mk[0m is [47mN-1[0m and for each
  [47mi[0m, [47mxi[0m is either [47mnil[0m if the [47mi[0mth value is an ordinary value or else
  is the name of a stobj returned in that position (and one such
  stobj name is the bound stobj).

  In each General Form above, [47mst[0m and [47mform[0m are called the ``bound
  stobj'' and ``body'' of the [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call (respectively).

  For the read-only form, the bound stobj (which is [47mst[0m above) must not
  be returned by the body of the form.

  For the updating form, the values actually returned are obtained by
  removing [47mst[0m from [47mlst[0m and then, if [47mstate[0m is not already in [47mlst[0m,
  adding [47mstate[0m at the end of [47mlst[0m.  Consider the following example.

    (defun f0 (st2 state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st2 state)))
      (with-global-stobj
        st
        (st st2 nil state)
        (mv st st2 nil state)))

  In this case, [47mlst[0m is [47m(st st2 nil state)[0m, and the following expansion
  shows that [47mst[0m has been dropped from the returned values.

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (with-global-stobj
                     st
                     (st st2 nil state)
                     (mv st st2 nil state))
     (LET ((ST (READ-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST STATE)))
          (MV-LET (ST ST2 {WGS}0 STATE)
                  (MV ST ST2 NIL STATE)
                  (LET ((STATE (WRITE-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST ST STATE)))
                       (MV? ST2 {WGS}0 STATE))))
    ACL2 !>

  Evaluation of an updating [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form always updates
  [47mstate[0m: specifically it updates its [47muser-stobj-alist[0m field (see
  [state]).  The following example is similar to the one above,
  except that this time the body of the [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call does
  not return state; nevertheless, the entire call does return state.
  It illustrates that when [47mstate[0m is not in the list given as the
  second argument of an updating [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call, then the
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form not only drops the bound stobj from its
  return values but also adds [47mstate[0m as the last returned value (or,
  if the bound stobj was the sole symbol in the list, then the call
  returns [47mstate[0m as the sole value).

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (with-global-stobj
                     st
                     (st st2 nil)
                     (mv st st2 nil))
     (LET ((ST (READ-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST STATE)))
          (MV-LET (ST ST2 {WGS}0)
                  (MV ST ST2 NIL)
                  (LET ((STATE (WRITE-USER-STOBJ-ALIST 'ST ST STATE)))
                       (MV? ST2 {WGS}0 STATE))))
    ACL2 !>

  Note that because [47mwith-global-stobj[0m updates [47mstate[0m, then [47mstate[0m must be
  a known stobj when [47mwith-global-stobj[0m is called.  In particular, in
  order to call [47mwith-global-stobj[0m in the body of a function, [47mstate[0m
  should be a formal parameter of that function.


Syntactic Restrictions to Avoid Aliasing

  For the examples in this section, we continue to assume that the
  following [47m[defstobj][0m [events] have been evaluated.

    (defstobj st fld)
    (defstobj st2 fld2 :congruent-to st)

  Consider the following definition.

    (defun foo (st state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st state)))
      (let ((state (with-global-stobj st
                     (st)
                     (update-fld 3 st))))
        (mv (fld st) state)))

  ACL2 admits that form, but causes an error with the following call of
  [47mfoo[0m.

    ACL2 !>(foo st state)


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  Illegal top-level form, (FOO ST STATE).
    The stobj ST occurs free, yet may be bound by an updating WITH-GLOBAL-
    STOBJ form, as the top-level form calls FOO, which makes an updating
    WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ call that binds ST.  See :DOC with-global-stobj.

    ACL2 !>

  Let us see why this call must be illegal; then we'll study the error
  message.  The [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form above will set the field, [47mfld[0m,
  of [47mst[0m to the value, 3.  Moreover, ACL2 uses destructive update on
  stobjs: the actual Lisp object representing [47mst[0m has value 3 in its
  field, and this is the same object for which we return [47m(fld st)[0m
  from [47mfoo[0m.  So if [47m(foo st state)[0m were allowed to execute, it would
  return the multiple values [47m(3 <state>)[0m.  However, ACL2 can prove
  that ACL2 returns [47m(fld st)[0m unchanged:

    (thm (implies (stp st)
                  (equal (mv-nth 0 (foo st state))
                         (fld st))))

  What we are seeing is a violation of single-threadedness.

  Now let's look at the error message above.  It explains that ``the
  stobj ST occurs free'' in [47m(foo st state)[0m: indeed, [47mst[0m is the first
  argument of that call.  Therefore, [47mst[0m can be accessed in that
  top-level form; indeed, we have seen that it is returned as the
  first value.  However, [47mst[0m can also be destructively modified
  because of the updating [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call in the body of [47mfoo[0m:
  ``the top-level form calls FOO, which makes an updating
  WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ call that binds ST.'' As we have discussed, that
  updating call destroys single-threadedness, and hence must be
  avoided.  We may call this an ``aliasing problem'', since the bound
  stobj shares structure with the formal parameter.

  By contrast, there is no such problem if we replace [47mst[0m by its
  congruent stobj, [47mst2[0m, in the top-level call.

    ACL2 !>(foo st2 state)
    (NIL <state>)
    ACL2 !>(fld st2)
    NIL
    ACL2 !>(fld st)
    3
    ACL2 !>

  In this case there is no aliasing problem.  The formal parameter [47mst[0m
  of [47mfoo[0m is bound to the (global) value of [47mst2[0m, which does not share
  structure with the (global) value of stobj [47mst[0m that is updated by
  the [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form.

  Note that the aliasing problem can be buried through a chain of
  function calls, as we now illustrate.  Function [47mfoo2[0m is like [47mfoo[0m
  above, with two changes: instead of updating the field with [47m3[0m we
  update it with the formal parameter, [47mval[0m; and that update is done
  inside the called function, [47mfoo2-sub[0m, rather than directly in the
  body of [47mfoo2[0m.

    (defun foo2-sub (val state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (with-global-stobj st
        (st)
        (update-fld val st)))

    (defun foo2 (val st state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st state)))
      (let ((state (foo2-sub val state)))
        (mv (fld st) state val)))

  The error message is essentially the same, except that the chain of
  calls is shown that leads to the problematic updating
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form.  The behavior on [47mst2[0m instead of [47mst[0m is fine,
  as before.

    ACL2 !>(foo2 3 st state)


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  Illegal top-level form, (FOO2 3 ST STATE).
    The stobj ST occurs free, yet may be bound by an updating WITH-GLOBAL-
    STOBJ form, as the top-level form calls FOO2, which calls FOO2-SUB,
    which makes an updating WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ call that binds ST.  See
    :DOC with-global-stobj.

    ACL2 !>(foo2 4 st2 state)
    (NIL <state> 4)
    ACL2 !>(fld st2)
    NIL
    ACL2 !>(fld st)
    4
    ACL2 !>

  So far we have seen just one aliasing problem, i.e., between a free
  stobj in a top-level form and a subsidiary updating
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form.  Another case is where the free stobj in a
  top-level form is actually returned (we might say, updated) by that
  form; in that case, any subsidiary [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form is
  problematic, even if it is read-only.  Here is an example.

    (defun g2 (val st state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st state)))
      (let ((st (update-fld val st)))
        (let ((f (with-global-stobj st (fld st))))
          (mv f (fld st) st state))))

    (g2 nil st state)

  As before, the definition is fine, but the ensuing top-level call is
  not.  And as before, if we replace the top-level stobj occurrence
  by one that is congruent to [47mst[0m, there is no error: [47m(g2 5 st2
  state)[0m.

  Note that there is no aliasing problem when there is no update of the
  stobj, either in the top-level form or in the subsidiary
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m form.  The following are perfectly legal, for
  example.

    (defun g1 (st state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs (st state)))
      (let ((f (with-global-stobj st (fld st))))
        (mv f state (fld st))))

    (g1 st state)

  So far we have seen two similar error cases due to aliasing: both are
  top-level calls involving a stobj occurrence that has an occurrence
  below bound by [47mwith-global-stobj[0m, where at least one of the two
  occurrences updates the stobj.  Consider this: a top-level call
  like [47m(foo st state)[0m could be viewed as grabbing [47mst[0m from the ACL2
  state, hence could be viewed as being [47m(with-global-stobj st (foo st
  state))[0m.  So we can think of the restrictions as being about nested
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m calls, and that leads us to the final two cases.
  Here is a summary of all the restrictions to prevent aliasing,
  starting with the two discussed above about top-level evaluation,
  and ending with the two new ones about nested [47mwith-global-stobj[0m
  calls.

    * In a form [47mu[0m that is legal at the top-level, where [47mu[0m has a free
      occurrence of stobj [47mst[0m, there is no updating [47mwith-global-stobj[0m
      call that binds [47mst[0m and is invoked during evaluation of [47mu[0m.

    * In a form [47mu[0m that is legal at the top-level, where [47mu[0m returns stobj [47mst[0m,
      there is no [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call that binds [47mst[0m and is invoked
      during evaluation of [47mu[0m.

    * In a form [47m(with-global-stobj st u)[0m, there is no updating
      [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call that binds [47mst[0m and is invoked during
      evaluation of [47mu[0m.

    * In an updating form [47m(with-global-stobj st lst u)[0m, there is no
      [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call that binds [47mst[0m and is invoked during
      evaluation of [47mu[0m.

  Our restrictions that prevent aliasing are syntactic ones, sufficient
  to prevent the invocations described above.  They are implemented
  by searching for calls of [47mread-user-stobj-alist[0m to identify
  expansions of [47mwith-global-stobj[0m calls, and by searing for calls of
  [47mwrite-user-stobj-alist[0m to identify expansions of updating
  [47mwith-global-stobj[0m calls.

  Finally, we note that the syntactic restrictions extend to [guard]s.
  Consider again the function [47mrd0[0m as defined above, and let's use it
  in the guard of a function.

    (defun rd0 (state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
      (with-global-stobj st (fld st)))

    (defun call-rd0-in-guard (state)
      (declare (xargs :stobjs state
                      :guard (rd0 state))
               (ignore state))
      17)

  Then as before, it is an error for a top-level form to update [47mst[0m and
  also call a function that may lead to a [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call that
  binds [47mst[0m.

    ACL2 !>(let ((st (update-fld 3 st)))
             (mv st (call-rd0-in-guard state)))


    ACL2 Error in TOP-LEVEL:  Illegal top-level form,
    (LET ((ST (UPDATE-FLD 3 ST))) (LIST ST (CALL-RD0-IN-GUARD STATE))).
    The stobj ST is returned by evaluation of that form, yet is bound by
    a WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ form, as the top-level form calls CALL-RD0-IN-GUARD,
    which calls RD0, which makes a WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ call that binds ST.
    See :DOC with-global-stobj.

    ACL2 !>


Constrained Functions and Defattach

  Consider the following constrained function introduction.

    (encapsulate
      (((crn0 state) => *))
      (local (defun crn0 (state)
               (declare (xargs :stobjs state))
               (state-p state))))

  If we try to attach [47mrd0[0m (defined above) to [47mcrn0[0m we get an error, as
  shown just below.  In short, this error says that since [47mrd0[0m may
  lead to a call of [47mwith-global-stobj[0m that binds [47mst[0m, then with this
  attachment, [47mcrn0[0m may lead to such a call; yet there is no record in
  the [world] that [47mcrn0[0m may lead to such a call.

    ACL2 !>(defattach crn0 rd0)


    ACL2 Error in ( DEFATTACH CRN0 RD0):  The attachment of RD0 to CRN0
    restricts stobjs bound by WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ under calls of RD0, according
    to the :GLOBAL-STOBJS keyword (default nil) in the signature introducing
    CRN0.  But this restriction is violated for stobj ST:  the attempt
    is to attach RD0, which makes a WITH-GLOBAL-STOBJ call that binds ST,
    yet that stobj is not specified by the :GLOBAL-STOBJS keyword of CRN0.
    See :DOC with-global-stobj.


    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFATTACH CRN0 RD0)
    Rules: NIL
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    ACL2 Error [Failure] in ( DEFATTACH CRN0 RD0):  See :DOC failure.

    ******** FAILED ********
    ACL2 !>

  The solution is to note, in the signature of the constrained
  function, that it may lead to a [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call.  This is
  accomplished by using the keyword, [47m:GLOBAL-STOBJS[0m, in the signature
  of the function.  The value of that keyword is [47mnil[0m by default,
  indicating that there is no such call.  Otherwise the value is a
  cons of the form [47m(r . w)[0m, where [47mr[0m and [47mw[0m are disjoint lists of
  stobjs.  Their interpretation is as follows: [47mw[0m includes all stobjs
  for which an attachment may have an updating [47mwith-global-stobj[0m
  call, and [47mr[0m includes all stobjs not in [47mw[0m for which an attachment
  may have a [47mwith-global-stobj[0m call.  Consider the following
  modification of the [47m[encapsulate][0m form above.

