  (GUARD ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Suppress or enable guard-checking for a form

    Example:

    ; Turn off all guard-checking for the indicated calls of append and car:
    (with-guard-checking :none
                         (append 3 4))

    General Form:
    (with-guard-checking val form)

  where [47mval[0m evaluates to a legal [guard]-checking value (see
  [set-guard-checking], or evaluate [47m*guard-checking-values*[0m to see
  the list of such values), and [47mform[0m is a form that does not mention
  [47m[state][0m (unless there is an active trust tag, in which case mention
  of [47mstate[0m can be unsound; see below).  See
  [with-guard-checking-error-triple] and [with-guard-checking-event]
  for analogous utilities to be used with forms that return
  [error-triple]s.  [47mForm[0m is to be evaluated in a scope such that, in
  essence, [47m(set-guard-checking val)[0m is first executed locally in that
  scope.  However, this guard-checking setting is ignored once
  evaluation passes into raw Lisp functions (see
  [guards-and-evaluation]).

  Note that the addition of [47mwith-guard-checking[0m does not change the
  proof obligations generated for guard verification.  However,
  [47m[ec-call][0m can be used in order to eliminate those proof
  obligations.  Consider the following example.

    (defun f1 (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (with-guard-checking nil (ec-call (nth t x))))

  Then guard verification succeeds, and evaluation of the form [47m(f1 '(1
  2 3))[0m will return [47m1[0m, without causing a guard violation.  However,
  if [47mec-call[0m is instead removed from this definition, then guard
  verification will fail, and for good reason, as illustrated by the
  following example.

    (defun f2 (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (with-guard-checking nil (nth t x)))

    (defun f3 (x)
      (declare (xargs :guard t))
      (f2 x))

  Suppose guard verification were to succeed for [47mf2[0m.  Then of course it
  would also succeed for [47mf3[0m.  But the call [47m(f3 nil)[0m would then cause
  a raw Lisp error (at least in many host Lisps), because it would
  lead to a raw-Lisp call of [47mnth[0m whose first argument is not a
  number.

  The remainder of this topic provides a remark for advanced users.
  [47mWith-guard-checking[0m is implemented using [47m[return-last][0m, which you
  can in principle call directly; use [47m:[0m[47m[trans][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[trans1][0m to see
  how a call of [47mwith-guard-checking[0m expands to a corresponding call
  of [47mreturn-last[0m.  However, ACL2 enforces a couple of checks that can
  only be circumvented if there is an active trust tag.  The
  following example from Jared Davis shows why those checks are
  required (in particular, it shows how circumventing them with a
  trust tag can be unsound).  We start by defining our own version of
  the utility, which omits the check that the form has no occurrences
