 (WITH-LOCAL-STATE
  (STOBJ ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Locally bind state

  This is an advanced topic, probably of interest only to system
  developers.

  Consider the following example form:

    (with-local-state
     (mv-let (result state)
             (compute-with-state x state)
             result))

  This is equivalent to the following form.

    (with-local-stobj
     state
     (mv-let (result state)
             (compute-with-state x state)
             result))

  By default, this form is illegal, because ACL2 does not have a way to
  unwind all changes to the ACL2 [state]; we say more on this issue
  below.  There may however be situations where you are willing to
  manage or overlook this issue.  In that case you may execute the
  following form to enable the use of [47mwith-local-state[0m, by enabling
  the use of [47m[with-local-stobj][0m on [47mstate[0m; but note that it requires
  an active trust tag (see [defttag]).

    (remove-untouchable create-state t)

  Please be aware that no local [state] is actually created, however!
  In particular, users of [47mwith-local-state[0m need either to ensure that
  channels are closed and state global variables are returned to
  their original values, or else be willing to live with changes made
  to state that are not justified by the code that has been
  evaluated.  You are welcome to look in the ACL2 source code at the
  definition of macro [47mchannel-to-string[0m, which employs
  [47mwith-local-state[0m to create a local [state] for the purpose of
  creating a string.

  Here is an example use of [47mwith-local-state[0m.  Notice the use of
  [47m[defttag][0m --- and indeed, please understand that we are just
  hacking here, and in general it takes significant effort to be sure
  that one is using [47mwith-local-state[0m correctly!

    (defttag t)

    (remove-untouchable create-state t)

    (set-state-ok t)

    (defun foo (state)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (mv-let
       (channel state)
       (open-input-channel \"my-file\" :object state)
       (mv-let (eofp obj state)
               (read-object channel state)
               (declare (ignore eofp))
               (let ((state (close-input-channel channel state)))
                 (mv obj state)))))

    (defun bar ()
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (with-local-state (mv-let (result state)
                                (foo state)
                                result)))

    ; Multiple-value return version:

    (defun foo2 (state)
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (mv-let
       (channel state)
       (open-input-channel \"my-file\" :object state)
       (mv-let (eofp obj state)
               (read-object channel state)
               (let ((state (close-input-channel channel state)))
                 (mv eofp obj state)))))

    (defun bar2 ()
      (declare (xargs :mode :program))
      (with-local-state (mv-let (eofp result state)
                                (foo2 state)
                                (mv eofp result))))

  Note for ACL2(p) users: When [parallel-execution] is enabled,
  invocations of [47mwith-local-state[0m are surrounded by a lock.")
 (WITH-LOCAL-STOBJ
  (STOBJ ACL2-BUILT-INS)
  "Locally bind a single-threaded object

  See [stobj] for an introduction to single-threaded objects.  Also see
  [defstobj] for additional background.

    Example Form:
    (with-local-stobj
     st
     (mv-let (result st)
             (compute-with-st x st)
             result))

  [47mWith-local-stobj[0m can be thought of as a macro, where the example form
  above expands as follows.

    (mv-let (result st)
            (let ((st (create-st)))
              (compute-with-st x st))
            (declare (ignore st))
            result)

  However, ACL2 expects you to use [47mwith-local-stobj[0m, not its expansion.
  More precisely, stobj creator functions are only allowed via
  [47mwith-local-stobj[0m or in logic-only situations (like theorems and
  hints).  Moreover, neither [47mwith-local-stobj[0m nor its expansions are
  legal when typed directly at the top-level loop.  See [top-level]
  for a way to use [47mwith-local-stobj[0m in the top-level loop.

    General Forms:
    (with-local-stobj stobj-name mv-let-form)
    (with-local-stobj stobj-name mv-let-form creator-name)

  where [47mstobj-name[0m is the name of a [stobj], [47mmv-let-form[0m is a call of
  [47m[mv-let][0m that binds [47mstobj-name[0m but does not return [47mstobj-name[0m ---
  in fact, if [47mmv-let-form[0m is [47m(mv-let (...)  ... body)[0m, then [47mbody[0m does
  not even reference [47mstobj-name[0m --- and if [47mcreator-name[0m is supplied
  then it should be the name of the creator function for [47mstobj-name[0m.
  For the example form above, its expansion would use [47mcreator-name[0m,
  if supplied, in place of [47mcreate-st[0m.  Note that [47mstobj-name[0m must not
  be [47m[state][0m (the ACL2 state), except in special situations probably
  of interest only to system developers; see [with-local-state].

  Note that if a stobj [47mST[0m is bound upon beginning evaluation of a form
  [47m(with-local-stobj ST ...)[0m, then the value of [47mST[0m is the same
  immediately before evaluating that form as it is immediately after
  that evaluation.  In other words, only a local version of [47mST[0m is
  modified inside that [47mwith-local-stobj[0m form.

  [47mWith-local-stobj[0m can be useful when a stobj is used to memoize
  intermediate results during a computation, yet it is desired not to
  make the [47mstobj[0m a formal parameter for the function and its callers.

  ACL2 can reason about these ``local stobjs,'' and in particular about
  stobj creator functions.  For technical reasons, ACL2 will not
  allow you to enable the [47m:EXECUTABLE-COUNTERPART[0m [rune] of a stobj
  creator function.

  Finally, here is a small example concocted in order to illustrate
  that [47mwith-local-stobj[0m calls can be nested.

    (defstobj st fld1)

    (defun foo ()
      (with-local-stobj
       st ; Let us call this the ``outer binding of st''.
       (mv-let (val10 val20 st)
         (let ((st (update-fld1 10 st)))
           ;; At this point the outer binding of st has fld1 = 10.
           (let ((result (with-local-stobj
                          st ; Let us call this the ``inner binding of st''.
                          (mv-let (val st)
                            (let ((st (update-fld1 20 st)))
                              ;; Now fld1 = 20 for the inner binding of st.
                              (mv (fld1 st) st))
                            val))))
             ;; So result has been bound to 20 above, but here we are once again
             ;; looking at the outer binding of st, where fld1 is still 10.
             (mv (fld1 st) result st)))
         (mv val10 val20))))

    (thm (equal (foo) (mv 10 20))) ; succeeds")
 (WITH-OUTPUT
  (OUTPUT-CONTROLS)
  "Suppressing or turning on specified output for an event

  The macro [47mwith-output[0m can be used to control ACL2 output.  It can be
  wrapped around an event to create a new event; see
  [embedded-event-form].  The macro [47mwith-output![0m is identical except
  that it does not create an event and, unlike [47mwith-output[0m, it can be
  called in code.


Examples

    ; Turn off all controllable output during evaluation of the indicated thm form.
    (with-output
     :off :all
     :gag-mode nil
     :inhibit-er-hard t
     (thm (equal (app (app x y) z) (app x (app y z)))))

    ; Equivalent to the example just above.
    (with-output
     :off :all!
     (thm (equal (app (app x y) z) (app x (app y z)))))

    ; Prove the indicated theorem with the event summary turned off and
    ; using the :goals setting for gag-mode.
    (with-output
       :off summary
       :gag-mode :goals
       (defthm app-assoc (equal (app (app x y) z) (app x (app y z)))))

    ; Same effect as just above:
    (with-output
       :on summary
       :summary-off :all
       :gag-mode :goals
       (defthm app-assoc (equal (app (app x y) z) (app x (app y z)))))

    ; Turn off all but the ``time'' and ``rules'' parts of the summary.
    (with-output
       :on summary
       :summary-off (:other-than time rules)
       :gag-mode :goals ; default: use gag-mode, with goal names printed
       (defthm app-assoc (equal (app (app x y) z) (app x (app y z)))))

    ; Same as specifying :off :all!, but with output types made explicit
    ; (that is, using the value of constant *valid-output-names*):
    (with-output
     :off (error warning warning! observation prove proof-builder event history
                 summary proof-tree)
     :gag-mode nil
     (thm (equal (app (app x y) z) (app x (app y z)))))

    ; Same as above, but :stack :push says to save the current
    ; inhibit-output-lst, which can be restored in a subsidiary with-output call
    ; that specifies :stack :pop.
    (with-output
     :stack :push
     :off :all
     :gag-mode nil
     (thm (equal (app (app x y) z) (app x (app y z)))))

    ; Abbreviate printing in gag-mode for guard goals and induction schemes, but
    ; defeat abbreviated printing (if any) for terms.
    (with-output
     :evisc (:gag-mode (evisc-tuple 3 4 nil nil)
             :term nil)
     (defun h (x)
       (declare (xargs :guard t))
       (append (cons (make-list 10) x) x)))

    ; The following use of :ctx causes the error message to start with
    ; ``ACL2 Error in MY-CUSTOM-CTX'' instead of
    ; ``ACL2 Error in ( DEFUN FOO ...)''.
    (with-output
     :ctx 'my-custom-ctx
     (defun foo (x) y))


General Form

    (with-output :key1 val1 ... :keyk valk form)

  where [47mform[0m should evaluate to an [error-triple].  That evaluation
  takes place with output that is according to the values [47mvali[0m of the
  keywords, [47m:keyi[0m, as described below.  When the scope of [47mwith-output[0m
  is exited, then all modifications are undone, including the states
  of output and summary inhibition, [47m[gag-mode][0m, and [evisc-tuple]s.
  Each keyword may occur at most once.

  Use of the argument [47m:off :all![0m is treated exactly the same as using
  arguments [47m:off :all :gag-mode nil[0m.  We assume below that any use of
  [47m:off :all![0m has been expanded away in that manner.


On-off specs

  Before discussing the keywords we introduce the notion of ``on-off
  specs'', which are the legal values of the keywords [47m:on[0m, [47m:off[0m,
  [47m:summary-on[0m, and [47m:summary-off[0m.  (As noted above, we ignore [47m:off
  :all![0m below, as it is just an abbreviation for [47m:off :all :gag-mode
  nil[0m.)  An on-off spec has one of the following forms, where each
  [47msymi[0m is a symbol, and subject to restrictions discussed below

    * [47m:all[0m

    * [47msym[0m ; a symbol that is not [47m:all[0m

    * [47m(sym1 ... symk)[0m

    * [47m(:other-than sym1 ... symk)[0m

  The set of ``associated valid symbols'' is defined as follows.  For
  [47m:off[0m or [47m:on[0m, these symbols are the [3moutput types[0m that can be
  inhibited --- that is, members of the list stored in the constant
  [47m*valid-output-names*[0m, which is the list [47m(proof-tree error warning!
  warning observation prove event summary proof-builder comment
  history)[0m --- and they are treated as in [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m.
  Similarly, for [47m:summary-on[0m or [47m:summary-off[0m, these are the [3msummary
  types[0m: the parts of the [summary] that can be inhibited as in
  [47m[set-inhibited-summary-types][0m, that is, members of the list stored
  in the constant [47m*summary-types*[0m, which is the list [47m(errors form
  header hint-events redundant rules splitter-rules steps
  system-attachments time value warnings)[0m.  An on-off spec consisting
  of associated valid symbols, [47m(sym1 ... symk)[0m, indicates the set of
  symbols, [47m{sym1,...,symk}[0m.  The other legal forms of on-off spec and
  their meanings are as follows: [47m:all[0m represents the set of all
  associated valid symbols, any other symbol [47msym[0m abbreviates [47m(sym)[0m,
  and [47m(:other-than sym1 ... symk)[0m represents the set of associated
  valid symbols that are not in the list [47m(sym1 ... symk)[0m.

  Note that these two notions of ``associated valid symbols'' --- the
  [3moutput types[0m controlled by keywords [47m:on[0m and [47m:off[0m, and the [3msummary
  types[0m controlled by keywords [47m:summary-on[0m and [47m:summary-off[0m ---
  operate independently in the following sense.  The keywords [47m:on[0m and
  [47m:off[0m control output types from the list [47m*valid-output-names*[0m
  displayed above, one of whose members is [47mSUMMARY[0m.  The keywords
  [47m:summary-on[0m and [47m:summary-off[0m control summary types from the list
  [47m*summary-types*[0m displayed above, indicating which types of summary
  are to be printed in the case that [47mSUMMARY[0m is among the output
  types that are on.  This summary control persists even as the
  [47mSUMMARY[0m type changes state between off and on.  Consider the
  following example.

    (with-output :off (summary)
     (with-output :summary-off (time)
      (with-output :on (summary)
       (thm (equal (car (append x y)) (if (consp x) (car x) (car y)))))))

  The resulting output does not include [47mTIME[0m output in the summary.
  The reason is that the second [47mwith-output[0m form specifies that [47mTIME[0m
  summary output is off; then when the third (innermost) output turns
  [47mSUMMARY[0m output on, still, the [47mTIME[0m summary output is off, so the
  [47mTHM[0m call does not print the [47mTIME[0m part of the summary output.  Note
  that the same reasoning applies if the third [47mwith-output[0m call above
  specifies [47m:on :all[0m instead of [47m:on (summary)[0m; that case also
  produces no time output in the summary.  That is, the use of [47m:on
  :all[0m specifies which output types are on, but does not affect which
  summary types are on; again, output types and summary types are
  controlled independently by the respective pairs [47m:on/:off[0m and
  [47m:summary-on/:summary-off[0m.


Keyword arguments

  We turn now to a discussion of the keyword arguments.

  [47m:on[0m, [47m:off[0m

  The values for these keywords, which are not evaluated, must be
  on-off specs for these keywords (as discussed above).  If [47m:on :all[0m
  is specified, then then every output type is turned on except for
  those in the set specified by the value of [47m:off[0m.  Otherwise, if
  [47m:off :all[0m is specified, then every output type is inhibited except
  as specified by the value of [47m:on[0m.  Otherwise [47m:all[0m is not specified
  for either [47m:on[0m or [47m:off[0m, and the currently-inhibited output types
  are reduced as specified by the value of [47m:on[0m and then extended as
  specified by the value of [47m:off[0m.

  [47m:summary-on[0m, [47m:summary-off[0m

  The values for these keywords, which are not evaluated, must be
  on-off specs for these keywords.  They are interpreted exactly as
  are the values for [47m:on[0m and [47m:off[0m as described above, except that
  instead of output types they are interpreted with respect to the
  summary types (i.e., in the terminology introduced above, with
  respect to the set of associated valid symbols for [47m:summary-on[0m and
  [47m:summary-off[0m).

  [47m:gag-mode[0m

  The value should evaluate to one of the legal values for
  [47m[set-gag-mode][0m.  The effect is as though [47mset-gag-mode[0m has been
  called with this argument, before modifying how output is
  inhibited, at the start of evaluating the given form.

  [47m:evisc[0m

  The value should evaluate to a [47m[keyword-value-listp][0m, where each key
  is a legal keyword for the [47m:sites[0m keyword argument of
  [47m[set-evisc-tuple][0m other than [47m:trace[0m and [47m:brr[0m (that is, a member of
  the list [47m(:term :ld :abbrev :gag-mode)[0m), and each value evaluates
  to a legal [evisc-tuple] for that keyword.  The effect is as though
  [47mset-evisc-tuple[0m has been called, before modifying how output is
  inhibited, with this argument at the start of evaluating the given
  form.

  [47m:ctx[0m

  The value should evaluate to a context --- see [ctx] --- that will
  generally be used throughout evaluation of the given form, in place
  of the usual event context (including the [47m\"Form\"[0m field of the
