  The value, which is not evaluated, must be [47m:push[0m or [47m:pop[0m.  The effect
  of [47m:push[0m is essentially to create a new environment for inferior
  calls of [47mwith-output[0m that can be reverted (popped) by inferior
  calls of [47mwith-output[0m, as discussed in the next section.  Note that
  the handling of the [47m:stack[0m argument pays no attention to the
  [47m:summary-on[0m or [47m:summary-off[0m arguments.

  [47m:inhibit-er-hard[0m

  By default, ACL2 prints messages for hard errors --- errors whose
  message starts with ``HARD ACL2 ERROR'' --- even when [47merror[0m output
  is inhibited (whether by using the keyword, [47m:off[0m, or by calling
  [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m).  This behavior holds when [state] global
  [47minhibit-er-hard[0m has its default value of [47mnil[0m; see
  [set-inhibit-output-lst].  When keyword [47m:inhibit-er-hard[0m is
  supplied an expression, the value [47mv[0m of that expression overrides
  the global value of [47minhibit-er-hard[0m: thus when error output is
  inhibited, hard error messages are printed if [47mv[0m is [47mnil[0m and they are
  not printed if [47mv[0m is not [47mnil[0m.  Note that this behavior automatically
  takes place when [47mwith-output[0m is supplied the arguments [47m:off :all![0m,
  but not [47m:off :all[0m.


More about the [47mstack[0m argument

  The [47m:stack[0m keyword's effect is illustrated by the following example,
  where ``[47m(encapsulate nil)[0m'' may replaced by ``[47m(progn[0m'' without any
  change to the output that is printed.

    (with-output
     :stack :push :off :all
     (encapsulate ()
       (defun f1 (x) x)
       (with-output :stack :pop (defun f2 (x) x))
       (defun f3 (x) x)
       (with-output :stack :pop :off warning (in-theory nil))
       (defun f4 (x) x)))

  The outer [47mwith-output[0m call saves the current output settings (as may
  have been modified by earlier calls of [47m[set-inhibit-output-lst][0m),
  by pushing them onto a stack, and then turns off all output.  Each
  inner [47mwith-output[0m call temporarily pops that stack, restoring the
  starting output settings, until it completes and undoes the effects
  of that pop.  Unless [47mevent[0m output was inhibited at the top level
  (see [set-inhibit-output-lst]), the following output is shown:

    Since F2 is non-recursive, its admission is trivial.  We observe that
    the type of F2 is described by the theorem (EQUAL (F2 X) X).

  And then, if [47msummary[0m output was not inhibited at the top level, we
  get the rest of this output:

    Summary
    Form:  ( DEFUN F2 ...)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

    Summary
    Form:  ( IN-THEORY NIL)
    Rules: NIL
    Warnings:  None
    Time:  0.00 seconds (prove: 0.00, print: 0.00, other: 0.00)

  Note that the use of [47m:off warning[0m suppresses a [47m\"Theory\"[0m warning for
  the [47m(in-theory nil)[0m event, and that in no case will output be
  printed for definitions of [47mf1[0m, [47mf3[0m, or [47mf4[0m, or for the [47m[encapsulate][0m
  event itself.

  The following more detailed explanation of [47m:stack[0m is intended only
  for advanced users.  After [47m:gag-mode[0m and [47m:evisc[0m are handled (if
  present) but before [47m:on[0m or [47m:off[0m is handled, the value of [47m:stack[0m is
  handled as follows.  If the value is [47m:push[0m, then [state] global
  [47minhibit-output-lst-stack[0m is modified by pushing the cons of the
  values of [state] globals [47minhibit-output-lst[0m and [47mgag-mode[0m onto the
  value of [state] global [47minhibit-output-lst-stack[0m, which is [47mnil[0m at
  the top level.  If the value is [47m:pop[0m, then [state] global
  [47minhibit-output-lst-stack[0m is modified only if non-[47mnil[0m, in which case
  its top element is popped and provides the values of [state]
  globals [47minhibit-output-lst[0m and [47mgag-mode[0m.


Concluding remarks

  [47mWith-output[0m has no effect in raw Lisp, in the sense that a call
  [47m(with-output ... form)[0m macroexpands to [47mform[0m in raw Lisp.  Normally
  this produces desired behavior, but occasionally you may be a bit
  surprised.  Consider for example the following book.

    (in-package \"ACL2\")

    (with-output
      :off :all
      (make-event (prog2$ (cw \"@@@ NOISE @@@\")
                          '(defun f (x) x))
                  :check-expansion t))

    (make-event (with-output!
                  :off :all
                  (value (prog2$ (cw \"@@@ QUIET @@@\")
                                 '(defun g (x) x))))
                :check-expansion t)

  When certifying this book, we do not see either `[47mNOISE[0m' or `[47mQUIET[0m'.
  But then when we include this book, we see `[47mNOISE[0m' (but not
  `[47mQUIET[0m').  To see why, we first note that both events are evaluated
  in raw Lisp when including the book (as discussed briefly in the
  documentation topic, [book-compiled-file]).  The first calls
  [47mwith-output[0m, which (as noted above) disappears during
  macroexpansion.  The second calls [47mwith-output![0m, which has the
  desired effect of suppressing output.")
