  Note that so-called comment-window output (see [cw] and see
  [observation-cw]) is not redirected by [47mwof[0m to a file, nor is
  printing from a [wormhole].")
 (WORLD
  (STATE)
  "ACL2 property lists and the ACL2 logical database

  The ACL2 logical world is a data structure that includes all logical
  content resulting from the [command]s evaluated, back through and
  including initialization, but not including commands that have been
  undone (see [ubt]).  Thus in particular, the world includes a
  representation of the current logical theory, as well as some
  extra-logical information such as the values of ACL2 [table]s.  The
  rest of this topic focuses on the structure of the ACL2 world and,
  more generally, the ``world'' data structure.

  A ``world'' is a list of triples, each of the form [47m(sym prop . val)[0m,
  implementing the ACL2 notion of property lists.  ACL2 permits the
  simultaneous existence of many property list worlds.  ``The world''
  is often used as a shorthand for ``the ACL2 logical world'' which
  is the particular property list world used within the ACL2 system
  to maintain a database that contains rules, [table]s, and so on.

  Common Lisp provides the notion of ``property lists'' by which one
  can attach ``properties'' and their corresponding ``values'' to
  symbols.  For example, one can arrange for the [47m'color[0m property of
  the symbol [47m'box-14[0m to be [47m'purple[0m and the [47m'color[0m property of the
  symbol [47m'triangle-7[0m to be [47m'yellow[0m.  Access to property lists is
  given via the Common Lisp function [47mget[0m.  Thus, [47m(get 'box-14 'color)[0m
  might return [47m'purple[0m.  Property lists can be changed via the
  special form [47msetf[0m.  Thus, [47m(setf (get 'box-14 'color) 'blue)[0m changes
  the Common Lisp property list configuration so that [47m(get 'box-14
  'color)[0m returns [47m'blue[0m.  It should be obvious that ACL2 cannot
  provide this facility, because Common Lisp's [47mget[0m ``function'' is
  not a function of its argument, but instead a function of some
  implicit state object representing the property list settings for
  all symbols.

  ACL2 provides the functions [47mgetprop[0m and [47mputprop[0m which allow one to
  mimic the Common Lisp property list facility.  However, ACL2's
  [47mgetprop[0m takes as one of its arguments a list that is a direct
  encoding of what was above called the ``state object representing
  the property list settings for all symbols.'' Because ACL2 already
  has a notion of ``[state]'' that is quite distinct from that used
  here, we call this property list object a ``world.'' A world is
  just a true list of triples.  Each triple is of the form [47m(sym prop
  . val)[0m.  This world can be thought of as a slightly elaborated form
  of association list and [47mgetprop[0m is a slightly elaborated form of
  [47m[assoc][0m that takes two keys.  When [47mgetprop[0m is called on a symbol,
  [47ms[0m, property [47mp[0m, and world, [47mw[0m, it scans [47mw[0m for the first triple whose
  [47msym[0m is [47ms[0m and [47mprop[0m is [47mp[0m and returns the corresponding [47mval[0m. [47mGetprop[0m
  has two additional arguments: one controls what it returns if no
  such [47msym[0m and [47mprop[0m exist in [47mw[0m, and the other allows an extremely
  efficient implementation.  To set some property's value for some
  symbol, ACL2 provides [47mputprop[0m.  [47m(putprop sym prop val w)[0m merely
  returns a new world, [47mw'[0m, in which [47m(sym prop . val)[0m has been
  [47m[cons][0med onto the front of [47mw[0m, thus ``overwriting'' the [47mprop[0m value
  of [47msym[0m in [47mw[0m to [47mval[0m and leaving all other properties in [47mw[0m unchanged.

  One aspect of ACL2's property list arrangement is that it is possible
  to have many different property list worlds.  For example, [47m'box-14[0m
  can have [47m'color[0m [47m'purple[0m in one world and can have [47m'color[0m [47m'yes[0m in
  another, and these two worlds can exist simultaneously because
  [47mgetprop[0m is explicitly provided the world from which the property
  value is to be extracted.

  The efficiency alluded to above stems from the fact that Common Lisp
  provides property lists.  Using Common Lisp's provisions behind the
  scenes, ACL2 can ``install'' the properties of a given world into
  the Common Lisp property list state so as to make retrieval via
  [47mgetprop[0m very fast in the special case that the world provided to
  [47mgetprop[0m has been installed.  To permit more than one installed
  world, each of which is permitted to be changed via [47mputprop[0m, ACL2
  requires that worlds be named and these names are used to
  distinguish installed versions of the various worlds.  See
  [extend-world] and [retract-world] for how to install a world.

  However, the ACL2 system uses a property list world, named
  [47m'current-acl2-world[0m, in which to store the succession of user
  [command]s and their effects on the logic.  This world is often
  referred to in our [documentation] as ``the world'' though it
  should be stressed that the user is permitted to have worlds and
  ACL2's is in no way distinguished except that the user is not
  permitted to modify it except via event [command]s.  The ACL2 world
  is part of the ACL2 [state] and may be obtained via [47m(w state)[0m.

  [31;1mWarning[0m: The ACL2 world is very large.  Its length as of this writing
  (Version 2.5) is over [47m40,000[0m and it grows with each release.
  Furthermore, some of the values stored in it are pointers to old
  versions of itself.  Printing [47m(w state)[0m is something you should
  avoid because you likely will not have the patience to await its
  completion.  For these practical reasons, the only thing you should
  do with [47m(w state)[0m is provide it to [47mgetprop[0m, as in the form

    (getprop sym prop default 'current-acl2-world (w state))

  or its convenient abbreviation

    (getpropc sym prop default)

  to inspect properties within it, or to pass it to ACL2 primitives,
  such as theory functions, where it is expected.

  Some ACL2 [command] forms, such as theory expressions (see
  [theories]) and the values to be stored in tables (see [table]),
  are permitted to use the variable symbol [47mworld[0m freely with the
  understanding that when these forms are evaluated that variable is
  bound to [47m(w state)[0m.  Theoretically, this gives those forms complete
  knowledge of the current logical configuration of ACL2.  However,
  at the moment, few world scanning functions have been documented
  for the ACL2 user.  Instead, supposedly convenient macro forms have
  been created and documented.  For example, [47m(current-theory :here)[0m,
  which is the theory expression which returns the currently
  [enable]d theory, actually macroexpands to [47m(current-theory-fn :here
  world)[0m.  When evaluated with [47mworld[0m bound to [47m(w state)[0m,
  [47mcurrent-theory-fn[0m scans the current ACL2 world and computes the set
  of [rune]s currently [enable]d in it.


