 (ZERO-TEST-IDIOMS
  (NUMBERS)
  "How to test for 0

  Below are six commonly used idioms for testing whether [47mx[0m is [47m0[0m.  [47m[Zip][0m
  and [47m[zp][0m are the preferred termination tests for recursions down
  the integers and naturals, respectively.

    idiom       logical              guard                 primary
                meaning                                 compiled code*

    (equal x 0) (equal x 0)          t                   (equal x 0)

    (eql x 0)   (equal x 0)          t                   (eql x 0)

    (zerop x)   (equal x 0)          x is a number       (= x 0)

    (= x 0)     (equal x 0)          x is a number       (= x 0)

    (zip x)     (equal (ifix x) 0)   x is an integer     (= x 0)

    (zp x)      (equal (nfix x) 0)   x is a natural      (int= x 0)

    (zpf x)     (equal (nfix x) 0)   x is a fixnum >= 0  (eql (the-fixnum x) 0)

  *See [guards-and-evaluation], especially the subsection titled
  ``Guards and evaluation V: efficiency issues''.  Primary code is
  relevant only if [guard]s are verified.  The ``compiled code''
  shown is only suggestive.

  The first four idioms all have the same logical meaning and differ
  only with respect to their executability and efficiency.  In the
  absence of compiler optimizing, [47m(= x 0)[0m is probably the most
  efficient, [47m(equal x 0)[0m is probably the least efficient, and [47m(eql x
  0)[0m is in between.  However, an optimizing compiler could always
  choose to compile [47m(equal x 0)[0m as [47m(eql x 0)[0m and, in situations where
  [47mx[0m is known at compile-time to be numeric, [47m(eql x 0)[0m as [47m(= x 0)[0m.  So
  efficiency considerations must, of course, be made in the context
  of the host compiler.

  Note also that [47m(zerop x)[0m and [47m(= x 0)[0m are indistinguishable.  They
  have the same meaning and the same [guard], and can reasonably be
  expected to generate equally efficient code.

  Note that [47m(zip x)[0m and [47m(zp x)[0m do not have the same logical meanings as
  the others or each other.  They are not simple tests for equality
  to [47m0[0m.  They each coerce [47mx[0m into a restricted domain, [47m[zip][0m to the
  integers and [47m[zp][0m to the natural numbers, choosing [47m0[0m for [47mx[0m when [47mx[0m
  is outside the domain.  Thus, [47m1/2[0m, [47m#c(1 3)[0m, and [47m'abc[0m, for example,
  are all ``recognized'' as zero by both [47m[zip][0m and [47m[zp][0m.  But [47m[zip][0m
  reports that [47m-1[0m is different from [47m0[0m while [47m[zp][0m reports that [47m-1[0m
  ``is'' [47m0[0m.  More precisely, [47m(zip -1)[0m is [47mnil[0m while [47m(zp -1)[0m is [47mt[0m.

  Note that the last five idioms all have [guard]s that restrict their
  Common Lisp executability.  If these last five are used in
  situations in which [guard]s are to be verified, then proof
  obligations are incurred as the price of using them.  If guard
  verification is not involved in your project, then the first five
  can be thought of as synonymous.

  [47m[Zip][0m and [47m[zp][0m are not provided by Common Lisp but are ACL2-specific
  functions.  Why does ACL2 provide these functions?  The answer has
  to do with the admission of recursively defined functions and
  efficiency.  [47m[Zp][0m is provided as the zero-test in situations where
  the controlling formal parameter is understood to be a natural
  number.  [47m[Zip][0m is analogously provided for the integer case.  We
  illustrate below.

  Here is an admissible definition of factorial

    (defun fact (n) (if (zp n) 1 (* n (fact (1- n)))))

  Observe the classic recursion scheme: a test against [47m0[0m and recursion
  by [47m[1-][0m.  Note however that the test against [47m0[0m is expressed with
  the [47m[zp][0m idiom.  Note also the absence of a [guard] making explicit
  our intention that [47mn[0m is a natural number.

  This definition of factorial is readily admitted because when [47m(zp n)[0m

  is false (i.e., [47mnil[0m) then [47mn[0m is a natural number other than [47m0[0m and so
  [47m(1- n)[0m is less than [47mn[0m.  The base case, where [47m(zp n)[0m is true,
  handles all the ``unexpected'' inputs, such as arise with [47m(fact -1)[0m
  and [47m(fact 'abc)[0m.  When calls of [47mfact[0m are evaluated, [47m(zp n)[0m checks
  [47m(integerp n)[0m and [47m(> n 0)[0m.  [Guard] verification is unsuccessful for
  this definition of [47mfact[0m because [47m[zp][0m requires its argument to be a
  natural number and there is no [guard] on [47mfact[0m, above.  Thus the
  primary raw lisp for [47mfact[0m is inaccessible and only the [47m:[0m[47m[logic][0m
  definition (which does runtime ``type'' checking) is used in
  computation.  In summary, this definition of factorial is easily
  admitted and easily manipulated by the prover but is not executed
  as efficiently as it could be.

  Runtime efficiency can be improved by adding a [guard] to the
  definition.

    (defun fact (n)
      (declare (xargs :guard (and (integerp n) (>= n 0))))
      (if (zp n) 1 (* n (fact (1- n)))))

  This [guard]ed definition has the same termination conditions as
  before --- termination is not sensitive to the [guard].  But the
  [guard]s can be verified.  This makes the primary raw lisp
  definition accessible during execution.  In that definition, the
  [47m(zp n)[0m above is compiled as [47m(= n 0)[0m, because [47mn[0m will always be a
  natural number when the primary code is executed.  Thus, by adding
  a [guard] and verifying it, the elegant and easily used definition
  of factorial is also efficiently executed on natural numbers.

  Now let us consider an alternative definition of factorial in which
  [47m(= n 0)[0m is used in place of [47m(zp n)[0m.

    (defun fact (n) (if (= n 0) 1 (* n (fact (1- n)))))

  This definition does not terminate.  For example [47m(fact -1)[0m gives rise
  to a call of [47m(fact -2)[0m, etc.  Hence, this alternative is
  inadmissible.  A plausible response is the addition of a [guard]
  restricting [47mn[0m to the naturals:

    (defun fact (n)
     (declare (xargs :guard (and (integerp n) (>= n 0))))
     (if (= n 0) 1 (* n (fact (1- n)))))

  But because the termination argument is not sensitive to the [guard],
  it is still impossible to admit this definition.  To influence the
  termination argument one must change the conditions tested.  Adding
  a runtime test that [47mn[0m is a natural number would suffice and allow
  both admission and [guard] verification.  But such a test would
  slow down the execution of the compiled function.

  The use of [47m(zp n)[0m as the test avoids this dilemma.  [47m[Zp][0m provides the
  logical equivalent of a runtime test that [47mn[0m is a natural number but
  the execution efficiency of a direct [47m[=][0m comparison with [47m0[0m, at the
  expense of a [guard] conjecture to prove.  In addition, if [guard]
  verification and most-efficient execution are not needed, then the
  use of [47m(zp n)[0m allows the admission of the function without a
  [guard] or other extraneous verbiage.

  While general rules are made to be broken, it is probably a good idea
  to get into the habit of using [47m(zp n)[0m as your terminating ``[47m0[0m
  test'' idiom when recursing down the natural numbers.  It provides
  the logical power of testing that [47mn[0m is a non-[47m0[0m natural number and
  allows efficient execution.

  We now turn to the analogous function, [47m[zip][0m.  [47m[Zip][0m is the preferred
  [47m0[0m-test idiom when recursing through the integers toward [47m0[0m.  [47m[Zip][0m
  considers any non-integer to be [47m0[0m and otherwise just recognizes [47m0[0m.
  A typical use of [47m[zip][0m is in the definition of [47m[integer-length][0m,
  shown below.  (ACL2 can actually accept this definition, but only
  after appropriate lemmas have been proved.)

    (defun integer-length (i)
      (declare (xargs :guard (integerp i)))
      (if (zip i)
          0
        (if (= i -1)
          0
          (+ 1 (integer-length (floor i 2))))))

  Observe that the function recurs by [47m(floor i 2)[0m.  Hence, calling the
  function on [47m25[0m causes calls on [47m12[0m, [47m6[0m, [47m3[0m, [47m1[0m, and [47m0[0m, while calling it
  on [47m-25[0m generates calls on [47m-13[0m, [47m-7[0m, [47m-4[0m, [47m-2[0m, and [47m-1[0m.  By making [47m(zip
  i)[0m the first test, we terminate the recursion immediately on
  non-integers.  The [guard], if present, can be verified and allows
  the primary raw lisp definition to check [47m(= i 0)[0m as the first
  terminating condition (because the primary code is executed only on
  integers).")
 (ZEROP
  (NUMBERS ACL2-BUILT-INS)
