 (ACL2-PC::=
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) attempt an equality (or equivalence) substitution

    Examples:
    =     -- replace the current subterm by a term equated to it in
             one of the hypotheses (if such a term exists)
    (= x) -- replace the current subterm by x, assuming that the prover
             can show that they are equal
    (= (+ x y) z)
          -- replace all occurrences of the term (+ x y) by the term z
             inside the current subterm, assuming that the prover can
             prove (equal (+ x y) z) from the current top-level
             hypotheses or that this term or (equal z (+ x y)) is among
             the current top-level hypotheses or the current governors
    (= & z)
          -- exactly the same as above, if (+ x y) is the current
             subterm
    (= (+ x y) z :hints :none)
          -- same as (= (+ x y) z), except that a new subgoal is
             created with the current goal's hypotheses and governors
             as its top-level hypotheses and (equal (+ x y) z) as its
             conclusion
    (= (+ x y) z 0)
          -- exactly the same as immediately above
    (= (p x)
       (p y)
       :equiv iff
       :otf-flg t
       :hints ((\"Subgoal 2\" :BY FOO) (\"Subgoal 1\" :use bar)))
          -- same as (= (+ x y) z), except that the prover uses
             the indicated values for otf-flg and hints, and only
             propositional (iff) equivalence is used (however, it
             must be that only propositional equivalence matters at
             the current subterm)

    General Forms:
    (= x)
    (= x y)
    (= x y :kwd1 val1 ... :kwdn valn)
    (= x y atom :kwd1 val1 ... :kwdn valn)

  where each [47m:kwdi[0m is one of [47m:hints[0m, [47m:otf-flg[0m, or [47m:equiv[0m, without
  repetition.  In the last form, [47matom[0m is a non-keyword atom and no
  [47mkwdi[0m may be [47m:hints[0m; that atom, if supplied, is equivalent to [47m:hints
  atom[0m, which indicates that instead of performing a proof that the
  two indicated terms (as described below) are suitably equivalent, a
  new such goal is created.

  If terms [47mx[0m and [47my[0m are supplied, then replace [47mx[0m by [47my[0m everywhere inside
  the current subterm if they are ``known'' to be equal, or more
  generally, equivalent in the sense described below.  Here ``known''
  means the following: except in the cases that no arguments are
  provided or else [47m:hints atom[0m is provided as described above, the
  prover is called as in the [47mprove[0m command (using keyword arguments
  [47m:otf[0m and [47m:hints[0m, if supplied, where the value of [47m:hints[0m is not an
  atom) to prove equivalence of [47mx[0m and [47my[0m under the current governors
  and top-level hypotheses.  By default, this equivalence is
  equality; however the keyword argument [47m:equiv[0m can specify a known
  equivalence relation.  In cases other than equality, substitution
  only takes place where justified by the equivalence maintained at
  the current subterm.

  For the keyword arguments, [47m:equiv[0m defaults to [47mequal[0m if not supplied
  or [47mnil[0m; if it is not [47mequal[0m (either explicitly or by default), then
  it should be the name of a known ACL2 [equivalence] relation, and
  substitution will only take place at subterm occurrences for which
  the [47m:equiv[0m is among the [equivalence] relations being maintained
  without the use of [patterned-congruence]s.


Remarks on defaults

    * If there are at least two arguments, then [47mx[0m may be the symbol [47m&[0m, in
      any package except the keyword package, which represents the
      current subterm.

    * The one-argument command [47m(= a)[0m is equivalent to [47m(= & a)[0m.

    * If there are no arguments, then we look for a top-level hypothesis or
      a governor of the form [47m(equal c u)[0m or [47m(equal u c)[0m, where [47mc[0m is
      the current subterm.  In that case we replace the current
      subterm by [47mu[0m.

    * As with the [47mprove[0m command, we allow goals to be given ``bye''s in the
      proof, which may be generated by a [47m:hints[0m keyword argument in
      [47mkeyword-args[0m.  These result in the creation of new subgoals.

    * It is allowed to use abbreviations (see [ACL2-pc::add-abbreviation])
      in the hints.")
 (ACL2-PC::ACL2-WRAP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) same as [47m(lisp x)[0m

    Example:
    (acl2-wrap (pe :here))

    General Form:
    (acl2-wrap form)

  Same as [47m(lisp form)[0m.  This is provided for interface tools that want
  to be able to execute the same form in raw Lisp, in the
  proof-builder, or in the ACL2 top-level loop [47m(lp)[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::ADD-ABBREVIATION
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) add an abbreviation

  Example: [47m(add-abbreviation v (* x y))[0m causes future occurrences of [47m(*
  x y)[0m to be printed as [47m(? v)[0m, until (unless) a corresponding
  invocation of [47mremove-abbreviations[0m occurs.  In this case we say
  that [47mv[0m ``abbreviates'' [47m(* x y)[0m.

    General Form:
    (add-abbreviation var &optional raw-term)

  Let [47mvar[0m be an abbreviation for [47mraw-term[0m, if [47mraw-term[0m is supplied,
  else for the current subterm.  Note that [47mvar[0m must be a variable
  that does not already abbreviate some term.

  A way to think of abbreviations is as follows.  Imagine that whenever
  an abbreviation is added, say [47mv[0m abbreviates [47mexpr[0m, an entry
  associating [47mv[0m to [47mexpr[0m is made in an association list, which we will
  call ``[47m*abbreviations-alist*[0m''.  Then simply imagine that [47m?[0m is a
  function defined by something like:

    (defun ? (v)
      (let ((pair (assoc v *abbreviations-alist*)))
        (if pair (cdr pair)
          (error ...))))

  Of course the implementation isn't exactly like that, since the
  ``constant'' [47m*abbreviations-alist*[0m actually changes each time an
  [47madd-abbreviation[0m instruction is successfully invoked.
  Nevertheless, if one imagines an appropriate redefinition of the
  ``constant'' [47m*abbreviations-alist*[0m each time an [47madd-abbreviation[0m is
  invoked, then one will have a clear model of the meaning of such an
  instruction.

  The effect of abbreviations on output is that before printing a term,
  each subterm that is abbreviated by a variable [47mv[0m is first replaced
  by [47m(?  v)[0m.

  The effect of abbreviations on input is that every built-in
  proof-builder command accepts abbreviations wherever a term is
  expected as an argument, i.e., accepts the syntax [47m(? v)[0m whenever [47mv[0m
  abbreviates a term.  For example, the second argument of
  [47madd-abbreviation[0m may itself use abbreviations that have been
  defined by previous [47madd-abbreviation[0m instructions.

  See also [ACL2-pc::remove-abbreviations] and see
  [ACL2-pc::show-abbreviations].")
 (ACL2-PC::AL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) same as apply-linear

    Example:
    (al 3)

  See [ACL2-pc::apply-linear], as [47mal[0m and [47mapply-linear[0m are identical.")
 (ACL2-PC::APPLY-LINEAR
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) apply a linear rule

    Examples:
    (apply-linear foo)
       -- apply the linear rule `foo'
    (apply-linear (:linear foo))
       -- same as above
    (apply-linear 2)
       -- apply the second linear rule, as displayed by show-linears
    rewrite
       -- apply the first rewrite rule, as displayed by show-rewrites
    (apply-linear foo ((y 7)))
       -- apply the linear rule foo with the substitution
          that associates 7 to the ``free variable'' y
    (apply-linear foo ((x 2) (y 3)) t)
       -- apply the linear rule foo by substituting 2 and 3 for free
          variables x and y, respectively, and also binding all other
          free variables possible by using the current context
          (hypotheses and governors)

    General Form:
    (apply-linear &optional rule-id substitution instantiate-free)

  Add a new top-level hypothesis by applying a [linear] rule to the
  current subterm.  The new hypothesis will be created according to
  the information provided by the [47mshow-linears[0m ([47msls[0m) command.

  A short name for this command is [47mal[0m.

  We assume familiarity with the interactive [proof-builder]'s [47mrewrite[0m
  ([47mr[0m) command (see [ACL2-pc::rewrite]).  In brief, the [47mapply-linear[0m
  command is an analogue of the [47mrewrite[0m command, but for [linear]
  rules in place of [rewrite] rules.  There is a significant
  difference: for the [47mapply-linear[0m command, instead of rewriting the
  current subterm as is done by the [47mrewrite[0m command, the conclusion
  of the applicable linear rule, suitably instantiated, is added as a
  new (and last) top-level hypothesis of the goal.  There is another
  significant difference: the automatic application of [linear] rules
  in the theorem prover is somewhat more complex than the automatic
  application of [rewrite] rules, so the [47mapply-linear[0m command may not
  correspond as closely to the prover's automatic use of a linear
  rule as the [47mrewrite[0m command corresponds to the prover's automatic
  use of a rewrite rule.

  Below, we refer freely to the [documentation] for the interactive
  proof-builder's [47mrewrite[0m command (see [ACL2-pc::rewrite]).

  The [47mrule-id[0m is treated just as it is by the [47mrewrite[0m command.  If
  [47mrule-id[0m is a positive integer [47mn[0m, then the [47mn[0mth rule as displayed by
  [47mshow-linears[0m is the one that is applied.  If [47mrule-id[0m is [47mnil[0m or is
  not supplied, then it is treated as the number 1.  Otherwise,
  [47mrule-id[0m should be either a symbol or else a [47m:linear[0m [rune].  If a
  symbol is supplied, then any [linear] rule of that name may be
  used.

  Consider the following example.  Suppose that the current subterm is
  [47m(< (g (h y)) y)[0m and that [47mfoo[0m is the name of the following linear
  rule.

    (implies (true-listp x)
             (< (g x) 15))

  Then the instruction [47m(apply-linear foo)[0m applies [47mfoo[0m by adding a new
  hypothesis [47m(< (g (h y)) 15)[0m.  In addition, a new goal with
  conclusion [47m(true-listp y)[0m is created unless the current context
  (top-level hypotheses and governors) implies [47m(true-listp y)[0m using
  only ``trivial reasoning'', just as for the [47mrewrite[0m command.

  If the [47mrule-id[0m argument is a number or is not supplied, then the
  system will store an instruction of the form [47m(apply-linear name
  ...)[0m, where [47mname[0m is the name of a [linear] rule; this is in order
  to make it easier to replay instructions when there have been
  changes to the history.  Except: instead of the name (whether the
  name is supplied or calculated), the system stores the [rune] if
  there is any chance of ambiguity.  (Formally, ``ambiguity'' here
  means that the rune being applied is of the form [47m(:rewrite name .
  index)[0m, where index is not [47mnil[0m.)

  Speaking in general, then, an [47mapply-linear[0m instruction works as
  follows.

  First, a [linear] rule is selected according to the arguments of the
  instruction.  The selection is made as explained under ``General
  Form'' above.

  Next, a trigger term of the rule (see [linear]) is matched with the
  current subterm, i.e., a substitution [47munify-subst[0m is found such
  that if one instantiates that trigger term of the rule with
  [47munify-subst[0m, then one obtains the current subterm.  If this match
  fails, then the instruction fails.

  Next, an attempt is made to relieve (discharge) the hypotheses,
  possibly handling free variables (see [free-variables]), exactly as
  is done with hypotheses when applying the interactive
  [proof-builder] command, [47mrewrite[0m ([47mr[0m).

  Finally, the instruction is applied exactly as the [47mrewrite[0m
  instruction is applied, except instead of replacing the current
  subterm, the rule's instantiated conclusion is added to the end of
  the list of top-level hypotheses of the goal.

  Note that as for the [47mrewrite[0m command, the substitution argument
  should be a list whose elements have the form [47m(variable term)[0m,
  where [47mterm[0m may contain abbreviations.")
 (ACL2-PC::BASH
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) call the ACL2 theorem prover's simplifier

    Examples:
    bash -- attempt to prove the current goal by simplification alone
    (bash (\"Subgoal 2\" :by foo) (\"Subgoal 1\" :use bar))
         -- attempt to prove the current goal by simplification alone,
            with the indicated hints

    General Form:
    (bash &rest hints)

  Call the theorem prover's simplifier, creating a subgoal for each
  resulting goal.

  Notice that unlike [47mprove[0m, the arguments to [47mbash[0m are spread out, and
  are all hints.

  [47mBash[0m is similar to [47mreduce[0m in that neither of these allows induction.
  But [47mbash[0m only allows simplification, while [47mreduce[0m allows processes
  [47meliminate-destructors[0m, [47mfertilize[0m, [47mgeneralize[0m, and
  [47meliminate-irrelevance[0m.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m All forcing rounds will be skipped (unless there are more
  than 15 subgoals generated in the first forcing round, an injustice
  that should be rectified, but might remain unless there is pressure
  to fix it).")
 (ACL2-PC::BDD
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) prove the current goal using bdds

    Examples:
    bdd
    (bdd :vars nil :bdd-constructors (cons) :prove t :literal :all)

  The general form is as shown in the latter example above, but with
  any keyword-value pairs omitted and with values as described for
  the [47m:[0m[47m[bdd][0m hint; see [hints].

  This command simply calls the theorem prover with the indicated bdd
  hint for the top-level goal.  Note that if [47m:prove[0m is [47mt[0m (the
  default), then the proof will succeed entirely using bdds or else
  it will fail immediately.  See [bdd].")
 (ACL2-PC::BK
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) move backward one argument in the enclosing term

    Example and General Form:
    bk

  For example, if the conclusion is [47m(= x (* (- y) z))[0m and the current
  subterm is [47m(* (- y) z)[0m, then after executing [47mbk[0m, the current
  subterm will be [47mx[0m.

  Move to the previous argument of the enclosing term.

  This is the same as [47mup[0m followed by [47m(dive n-1)[0m, where [47mn[0m is the
  position of the current subterm in its parent term in the
  conclusion.  Thus in particular, the [47mnx[0m command fails if one is
  already at the top of the conclusion.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::up], [ACL2-pc::dive], [ACL2-pc::top], and
  [ACL2-pc::nx].")
 (ACL2-PC::BOOKMARK
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) insert matching ``bookends'' comments

    Example:
    (bookmark final-goal)

    General Form:
    (bookmark name &rest instruction-list)

  Run the instructions in [47minstruction-list[0m (as though this were a call
  of [47mdo-all[0m; see [ACL2-pc::do-all]), but first insert a begin bookend
  with the given name and then, when the instructions have been
  completed, insert an end bookend with that same name.  See
  [ACL2-pc::comm] for an explanation of bookends and how they can
  affect the display of instructions.")
 (ACL2-PC::CASESPLIT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) split into two cases

    Example:
    (casesplit (< x y)) -- assuming that we are at the top of the
                           conclusion, add (< x y) as a new top-level
                           hypothesis in the current goal, and create a
                           subgoal identical to the current goal except
                           that it has (not (< x y)) as a new top-level
                           hypothesis

    General Form:
    (casesplit expr &optional use-hyps-flag do-not-flatten-flag)

  When the current subterm is the entire conclusion, this instruction
  adds [47mexpr[0m as a new top-level hypothesis, and create a subgoal
  identical to the existing current goal except that it has the
  negation of [47mexpr[0m as a new top-level hypothesis.  Also see
  [ACL2-pc::claim].  The optional arguments control the use of
  governors and the ``flattening'' of new hypotheses, as we now
  explain.

  The argument [47muse-hyps-flag[0m is only of interest when there are
  governors.  (To read about governors, see [ACL2-pc::hyps]).  In
  that case, if [47muse-hyps-flag[0m is not supplied or is [47mnil[0m, then the
  description above is correct; but otherwise, it is not [47mexpr[0m but
  rather it is [47m(implies govs expr)[0m that is added as a new top-level
  hypothesis (and whose negation is added as a top-level hypothesis
  for the new goal), where [47mgovs[0m is the conjunction of the governors.

  If [47mdo-not-flatten-flag[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then that is all
  there is to this command.  Otherwise (thus this is the default),
  when the claimed term (first argument) is a conjunction ([47mand[0m) of
  terms and the [47mclaim[0m instruction succeeds, then each (nested)
  conjunct of the claimed term is added as a separate new top-level
  hypothesis.  Consider the following example, assuming there are no
  governors.

    (casesplit (and (and (< x y) (integerp a)) (equal r s)) t)

  Three new top-level hypotheses are added to the current goal, namely
  [47m(< x y)[0m, [47m(integerp a)[0m, and [47m(equal r s)[0m.  In that case, only one
  hypothesis is added to create the new goal, namely the negation of
  [47m(and (< x y) (integerp a) (equal r s))[0m.  If the negation of this
  term had been [47mclaim[0med, then it would be the other way around: the
  current goal would get a single new hypothesis while the new goal
  would be created by adding three hypotheses.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m It is allowed to use abbreviations in the hints.")
 (ACL2-PC::CG
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) change to another goal.

    Examples:
    (cg (main . 1)) -- change to the goal (main . 1)
    cg              -- change to the next-to-top goal

    General Form:
    (CG &OPTIONAL goal-name)

  Same as [47m(change-goal goal-name t)[0m, i.e. change to the indicated and
  move the current goal to the end of the goal stack.")
 (ACL2-PC::CHANGE-GOAL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) change to another goal.

    Examples:
    (change-goal (main . 1)) -- change to the goal (main . 1)
    change-goal              -- change to the next-to-top goal

    General Form:
    (change-goal &optional goal-name end-flg)

  Change to the goal with the name [47mgoal-name[0m, i.e. make it the current
  goal.  However, if [47mgoal-name[0m is [47mnil[0m or is not supplied, then it
  defaults to the next-to-top goal, i.e., the second goal in the
  stack of goals.  If [47mend-flg[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then move the
  current goal to the end of the goal stack; else merely swap it with
  the next-to-top goal.  Also see [ACL2-pc::cg].")
 (ACL2-PC::CL-PROC
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) same as [47mclause-processor[0m

  This is simply an abbreviation for [ACL2-pc::clause-processor].")
 (ACL2-PC::CLAIM
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) add a new hypothesis

    Examples:
    (claim (< x y))   -- attempt to prove (< x y) from the current
                         top-level hypotheses and if successful, then
                         add (< x y) as a new top-level hypothesis in
                         the current goal
    (claim (< x y)
           :otf-flg t
           :hints ((\"Goal\" :induct t)))
                      -- as above, but call the prover using the
                         indicated values for the otf-flg and hints
    (claim (< x y) 0) -- as above, except instead of attempting to
                         prove (< x y), create a new subgoal with the
                         same top-level hypotheses as the current goal
                         that has (< x y) as its conclusion
    (claim (< x y) :hints :none)
                      -- same as immediately above

    General Form:
    (claim expr &rest rest-args)

  This command creates a new subgoal with the same top-level hypotheses
  as the current goal but with a conclusion of [47mexpr[0m.  If [47mrest-args[0m is
  a non-empty list headed by a non-keyword, then there will be no
  proof attempted for the new subgoal.  With that possible exception,
  [47mrest-args[0m should consist of keyword arguments.  The keyword
  argument [47m:do-not-flatten[0m controls the ``flattening'' of new
  hypotheses, just as with the [47mcasesplit[0m command (see
  [ACL2-pc::casesplit]).  The remaining [47mrest-args[0m are used with a
  call the [47mprove[0m command on the new subgoal, except that if [47m:hints[0m is
  a non-[47mnil[0m atom, then the prover is not called --- rather, this is
  the same as the situation described above, where [47mrest-args[0m is a
  non-empty list headed by a non-keyword.

  [31;1mRemarks:[0m (1) Unlike the [47mcasesplit[0m command, the [47mclaim[0m command is
  completely insensitive to governors. (2) It is allowed to use
  abbreviations in the hints.  (3) The keyword [47m:[0m[47mnone[0m has the special
  role as a value of [47m:[0m[47mhints[0m that is shown clearly in an example
  above.")
 (ACL2-PC::CLAUSE-PROCESSOR
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) use a clause-processor

    Example:
    (cl-proc :function
             note-fact-clause-processor
            :hint '(equal a a)) -- Invoke the indicated clause processor function
            with the indicated hint argument (see the beginning of community book
            [47mbooks/clause-processors/basic-examples.lisp[0m.

    General Form:
    (cl-proc &rest cl-proc-args)

  Invoke a clause-processor as indicated by cl-proc-args, which is a
  list of arguments that can serve as the value of a
  [47m:[0m[47m[clause-processor][0m hint; see [hints].

  This command calls the [47mprove[0m command, and hence should only be used
  at the top of the conclusion.")
 (ACL2-PC::COMM
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) display instructions from the current interactive session

    Examples:
    comm
    (comm 10)

    General Form:
    (comm &optional n)

  Prints out instructions in reverse order.  This is actually the same
  as [47m(commands n t)[0m --- or, [47m(commands nil t)[0m if [47mn[0m is not supplied.
  As for [47mcommands[0m (see [ACL2-pc::commands]), the final argument of [47mt[0m
  causes suppression of instructions occurring between so-called
  ``matching bookends,'' which we now explain.

  A ``begin bookend'' is an instruction of the form

    (COMMENT :BEGIN x . y).

  Similarly, an ``end bookend'' is an instruction of the form

    (COMMENT :END x' . y').

  The ``name'' of the first bookend is [47mx[0m and the ``name'' of the second
  bookend is [47mx'[0m.  When such a pair of instructions occurs in the
  current state-stack, we call them ``matching bookends'' provided
  that they have the same name (i.e. [47mx[0m equals [47mx'[0m) and if no other
  begin or end bookend with name [47mx[0m occurs between them.  The idea now
  is that [47mcomm[0m hides matching bookends together with the instructions
  they enclose.  Here is a more precise explanation of this
  ``hiding''; probably there is no value in reading on!

  A [47mcomm[0m instruction hides bookends in the following manner.  (So does
  a [47mcomment[0m instruction when its second optional argument is supplied
  and non-[47mnil[0m.)  First, if the first argument [47mn[0m is supplied and not
  [47mnil[0m, then we consider only the last [47mn[0m instructions from the
  state-stack; otherwise, we consider them all.  Now the resulting
  list of instructions is replaced by the result of applying the
  following process to each pair of matching bookends: the pair is
  removed, together with everything in between the begin and end
  bookend of the pair, and all this is replaced by the
  ``instruction''

    (\"***HIDING***\" :COMMENT :BEGIN name ...)

  where [47m(comment begin name ...)[0m is the begin bookend of the pair.
  Finally, after applying this process to each pair of matching
  bookends, each begin bookend of the form [47m(comment begin name ...)[0m
  that remains is replaced by

    (\"***UNFINISHED***\" :COMMENT :BEGIN name ...) .")
 (ACL2-PC::COMMANDS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) display instructions from the current interactive session

    Examples:
    commands
    (commands 10 t)

    General Forms:

    commands or (commands nil)
    Print out all the instructions (in the current state-stack) in
    reverse order, i.e. from the most recent instruction to the starting
    instruction.

    (commands n) [n a positive integer]
    Print out the most recent n instructions (in the current
    state-stack), in reverse order.

    (commands x abbreviate-flag)
    Same as above, but if abbreviate-flag is non-NIL, then do not
    display commands between ``matching bookends''.  See documentation
    for comm for an explanation of matching bookends.

  [31;1mRemark[0m: If there are more than [47mn[0m instructions in the state-stack,
  then [47m(commands n)[0m is the same as [47mcommands[0m (and also, [47m(commands n
  abb)[0m is the same as [47m(commands nil abb)[0m).")
 (ACL2-PC::COMMENT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) insert a comment

    Example:
    (comment now begin difficult final goal)

    General Form:
    (comment &rest x)

  This instruction makes no change in the state except to insert the
  [47mcomment[0m instruction.

  Some comments can be used to improve the display of commands; see
  [ACL2-pc::comm].")
 (ACL2-PC::CONTRADICT
      (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
      "(macro) same as [47mcontrapose[0m

  See [ACL2-pc::contrapose].")
 (ACL2-PC::CONTRAPOSE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(primitive) switch a hypothesis with the conclusion, negating both

    Example:
    (contrapose 3)

    General Form:
    (contrapose &optional n)

  The (optional) argument [47mn[0m should be a positive integer that does not
  exceed the number of hypotheses.  Negate the current conclusion and
  make it the [47mn[0mth hypothesis, while negating the current [47mn[0mth
  hypothesis and making it the current conclusion.  If no argument is
  supplied then the effect is the same as for [47m(contrapose 1)[0m.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m By ``negate'' we mean an operation that replaces [47mnil[0m by [47mt[0m, [47mx[0m
  by [47mnil[0m for any other explicit value [47mx[0m, [47m(not x)[0m by [47mx[0m, and any other
  [47mx[0m by [47m(not x)[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::DEMOTE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(primitive) move top-level hypotheses to the conclusion

    Examples:
    demote        -- demote all top-level hypotheses
    (demote 3 5)  -- demote hypotheses 3 and 5

  For example, if the top-level hypotheses are [47mx[0m and [47my[0m and the
  conclusion is [47mz[0m, then after execution of [47mdemote[0m, the conclusion
  will be [47m(implies (and x y) z)[0m and there will be no (top-level)
  hypotheses.

    General Form:
    (demote &rest hyps-indices)

  Eliminate the indicated (top-level) hypotheses, but replace the
  conclusion [47mconc[0m with [47m(implies hyps conc)[0m where [47mhyps[0m is the
  conjunction of the hypotheses that were eliminated.  If no
  arguments are supplied, then all hypotheses are demoted, i.e.
  [47mdemote[0m is the same as [47m(demote 1 2 ... n)[0m where [47mn[0m is the number of
  top-level hypotheses.

  [31;1mRemark[0m: You must be at the top of the conclusion in order to use this
  command.  Otherwise, first invoke [47mtop[0m.  Also, [47mdemote[0m fails if there
  are no top-level hypotheses or if indices are supplied that are out
  of range.")
 (ACL2-PC::DIVE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) move to the indicated subterm

    Examples:
    (DIVE 1)    -- assign the new current subterm to be the first
                   argument of the existing current subterm
    (DIVE 1 2)  -- assign the new current subterm to be the result of
                   first taking the 1st argument of the existing
                   current subterm, and then the 2nd argument of that

  For example, if the current subterm is

    (* (+ a b) c),

  then after [47m(dive 1)[0m it is

    (+ a b).

  If after that, then [47m(dive 2)[0m is invoked, the new current subterm will
  be

    b.

  Instead of [47m(dive 1)[0m followed by [47m(dive 2)[0m, the same current subterm
  could be obtained by instead submitting the single instruction
  [47m(dive 1 2)[0m.

    General Form:
    (dive &rest naturals-list)

  If [47mnaturals-list[0m is a non-empty list [47m(n_1 ... n_k)[0m of natural
  numbers, let the new current subterm be the result of selecting the
  [47mn_1[0m-st argument of the current subterm, and then the [47mn_2[0m-th subterm
  of that, ..., finally the [47mn_k[0m-th subterm.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m [47mDive[0m is related to the command [47mpp[0m, in that the diving is done
  according to raw (translated, internal form) syntax.  Use the
  command [47mdv[0m if you want to dive according to the syntax displayed by
  the command [47mp[0m.  Note that [47m(dv n)[0m can be abbreviated by simply [47mn[0m.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m Emacs users who load (into Emacs) the file [47memacs-acl2.el[0m
  (from a suitable directory; see [emacs]) will have defined a
  command, [47mControl-t Control-d[0m, that avoids the need to type [47mdive[0m
  commands.  After you print the current term using the [47mpp[0m command,
  you may position the cursor on a subterm and type [47mControl-t
  Control-d[0m.  Emacs will respond by pasting the appropriate [47mdive[0m
  command immediately after the interactive proof-builder's prompt.
  You can then simply type [47m<RETURN>[0m in order to dive to the desired
  subterm.")
 (ACL2-PC::DO-ALL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) run the given instructions

    Example:
    (do-all induct p prove)

    General Form:
    (do-all &rest instruction-list)

  Run the indicated instructions until there is a hard ``failure''.
  The instruction ``succeeds'' if and only if each instruction in
  [47minstruction-list[0m does.  (See [ACL2-pc::sequence] for an explanation
  of ``success'' and ``failure.'') As each instruction is executed,
  the system will print the usual prompt followed by that
  instruction, unless the value of [47m(access pc-info (@ pc-info)
  :print-prompt-and-instr-flg)[0m is [47mnil[0m.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m If [47mdo-all[0m ``fails'', then the failure is hard if and only if
  the last instruction it runs has a hard ``failure''.

  Obscure point: For the record, [47m(do-all ins_1 ins_2 ... ins_k)[0m is the
  same as [47m(sequence (ins_1 ins_2 ... ins_k))[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::DO-ALL-NO-PROMPT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) run the given instructions, halting once there is a
  ``failure''

    Example:
    (do-all-no-prompt induct p prove)

    General Form:
    (do-all-no-prompt &rest instruction-list)

  [47mDo-all-no-prompt[0m is the same as [47mdo-all[0m, except that the prompt and
  instruction are not printed each time, regardless of the value of
  [47mpc-print-prompt-and-instr-flg[0m.  Also, restoring is disabled.  See
  [ACL2-pc::do-all].")
 (ACL2-PC::DO-STRICT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) run the given instructions, halting once there is a
  ``failure''

    Example:
    (do-strict induct p prove)

    General Form:
    (do-strict &rest instruction-list)

  Run the indicated instructions until there is a (hard or soft)
  ``failure''.  In fact [47mdo-strict[0m is identical in effect to [47mdo-all[0m,
  except that [47mdo-all[0m only halts once there is a hard ``failure''.
  See [ACL2-pc::do-all].")
 (ACL2-PC::DOC
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) access documentation inside the interactive proof-builder

    Examples:

    (doc rewrite)          -- documentation on rewriting in ACL2
    (doc acl2-pc::rewrite) -- documentation on the proof-builder rewrite command
    (doc)                  -- same as (help doc) or (doc acl2-pc::doc)
    doc                    -- same as above

    General Forms:
    (doc) ; or, just: help
    (doc topic-name)

  For any [documentation] topic, [47mA[0m, [47m(doc A)[0m prints documentation on [47mA[0m
  to the terminal.  You can read that documentation online or in the
  Emacs [ACL2-Doc] browser; see [documentation].  This
  [proof-builder] command provides a direct interface to the ACL2
  [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m command at the terminal.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::help], which provides help for proof-builder
