 (ACL2-PC::DV
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) move to the indicated subterm

    Examples:
    (dv 1)    -- assign the new current subterm to be the first argument
                 of the existing current subterm
    (dv 1 2)  -- assign the new current subterm to be the result of
                 first taking the 1st argument of the existing
                 current subterm, and then the 2nd argument of that

  For example, if the current subterm is

    (* (+ a b) c),

  then after [47m(dv 1)[0m it is

    (+ a b).

  If after that, then [47m(dv 2)[0m is invoked, the new current subterm will
  be

    b.

  Instead of [47m(dv 1)[0m followed by [47m(dv 2)[0m, the same current subterm could
  be obtained by instead submitting the single instruction [47m(dv 1 2)[0m.

    General Form:
    (dv &rest naturals-list)

  If [47mnaturals-list[0m is a non-empty list [47m(n_1 ... n_k)[0m of natural
  numbers, let the new current subterm be the result of selecting the
  [47mn_1[0m-st argument of the current subterm, and then the [47mn_2[0m-th subterm
  of that, ..., finally the [47mn_k[0m-th subterm.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m [47m(dv n)[0m may be abbreviated by simply [47mn[0m, so we could have typed
  [47m1[0m instead of [47m(dv 1)[0m in the first example above.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m Emacs users who load (into Emacs) the file [47memacs-acl2.el[0m
  (from a suitable directory; see [emacs]) will have defined a
  command, [47mControl-t d[0m, that avoids the need to type [47mdv[0m commands.
  After you print the current term using the [47mp[0m or [47mth[0m command, you may
  position the cursor on a subterm and type [47mControl-t d[0m.  Emacs will
  respond by pasting the appropriate [47mdv[0m command immediately after the
  interactive proof-builder's prompt.  You can then simply type
  [47m<RETURN>[0m in order to dive to the desired subterm.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m A similar command is [47mdive[0m, which is related to the command
  [47mpp[0m, in that the diving is done according to raw (translated,
  internal form) syntax.  (See [ACL2-pc::dive].)  Use the command [47mdv[0m
  if you want to dive according to the syntax displayed by the
  command [47mp[0m.  Thus, the command ``[47mup[0m'' is the inverse of [47mdive[0m, not of
  [47mdv[0m.  The following example illustrates this point.

    ACL2 !>(verify (equal (* a b c) x))
    ->: p ; print user-level term
    (EQUAL (* A B C) X)
    ->: pp ; print internal-form (translated) term
    (EQUAL (BINARY-* A (BINARY-* B C)) X)
    ->: exit
    Exiting....
     NIL
    ACL2 !>(verify (equal (* a b c) x))
    ->: p
    (EQUAL (* A B C) X)
    ->: 1 ; same as (dv 1)
    ->: p ; print user-level term
    (* A B C)
    ->: pp ; print internal-form (translated) term
    (BINARY-* A (BINARY-* B C))
    ->: 3 ; dive to third argument of (* A B C)
    ->: p
    C
    ->: up ; go up one level in (BINARY-* A (BINARY-* B C))
    ->: p
    (* B C)
    ->: pp
    (BINARY-* B C)
    ->:


Subtopics

  [Dive-into-macros-table]
      Right-associated function information for the [proof-builder]")
 (ACL2-PC::ELIM
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) call the ACL2 theorem prover's elimination process

    Example and General Form:
    elim

  Upon running the [47melim[0m command, the system will create a subgoal will
  be created for each goal that would have been pushed for proof by
  induction in an ordinary proof, where [31;1monly[0m elimination is used; not
  even simplification is used!")
 (ACL2-PC::EQUIV
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) attempt an equality (or congruence-based) substitution

    Examples:
    (equiv (* x y) 3) -- replace (* x y) by 3 everywhere inside the
                         current subterm, if their equality is among the
                         top-level hypotheses or the governors
    (equiv x t iff)   -- replace x by t everywhere inside the current
                         subterm, where only propositional equivalence
                         needs to be maintained at each occurrence of x

    General form:
    (equiv old new &optional relation)

  Substitute [47mnew[0m for [47mold[0m everywhere inside the current subterm,
  provided that either [47m(relation old new)[0m or [47m(relation new old)[0m is
  among the top-level hypotheses or the governors (possibly by way of
  backchaining and/or refinement; see below).  If [47mrelation[0m is [47mnil[0m or
  is not supplied, then it defaults to [47mequal[0m.  If [47mrelation[0m is not
  [47mequal[0m (either explicitly or by default), then substitution of [47mnew[0m
  for [47mold[0m will only take place at occurrences for which [47mrelation[0m is
  among the [equivalence] relations being maintained without the use
  of [patterned-congruence]s.  Also see [47macl2-pc::=[0m for a much more
  flexible command.  Note that the [47mequiv[0m command fails if no
  substitution is actually made.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m No substitution takes place inside explicit values.  So for
  example, the instruction [47m(equiv 3 x)[0m will cause [47m3[0m to be replaced by
  [47mx[0m if the current subterm is, say, [47m(* 3 y)[0m, but not if the current
  subterm is [47m(* 4 y)[0m even though [47m4 = (1+ 3)[0m.

  The following remarks are quite technical and mostly describe a
  certain weak form of ``backchaining'' that has been implemented for
  [47mequiv[0m in order to support the [47m=[0m command.  In fact neither the term
  [47m(relation old new)[0m nor the term [47m(relation new old)[0m needs to be
  [31;1mexplicitly[0m among the current ``assumptions'', i.e., the top-level
  hypothesis or the governors.  Rather, there need only be such an
  assumption that ``tells us'' [47m(r old new)[0m or [47m(r new old)[0m, for [31;1msome[0m
  equivalence relation [47mr[0m that [31;1mrefines[0m [47mrelation[0m.  Here, ``tells us''
  means that either one of the indicated terms is among those
  assumptions, or else there is an assumption that is an implication
  whose conclusion is one of the indicated terms and whose hypotheses
  (gathered up by appropriately flattening the first argument of the
  [47mimplies[0m term) are all among the current assumptions.")
 (ACL2-PC::EX
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) exit after possibly saving the state

    Example and General Form:
    ex

  Same as [47mexit[0m, except that first the instruction [47msave[0m is executed.

  If [47msave[0m queries the user and is answered negatively, then the exit is
  aborted.")
 (ACL2-PC::EXIT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(meta) exit the interactive proof-builder

    Examples:
    exit                        -- exit the interactive proof-builder
    (exit t)                    -- exit after printing a bogus defthm event
    (exit append-associativity) -- exit and create a defthm
                                   event named append-associativity

    General Forms:

    exit --  Exit without storing an event.

    (exit t) -- Exit after printing a bogus defthm event, showing :INSTRUCTIONS.

    (exit event-name &optional rule-classes do-it-flg) --
    Exit, and perhaps store an event

  The command [47mexit[0m returns you to the ACL2 loop.  At a later time,
  [47m(acl2::verify)[0m may be executed to get back into the same
  interactive proof-builder state, as long as there hasn't been an
  intervening use of the proof-builder (otherwise see
  [ACL2-pc::save]).

  When given one or more arguments as shown above, [47mexit[0m still returns
  you to the ACL2 loop, but first, if the interactive proof is
  complete, then it attempts create a [47mdefthm[0m event with the specified
  [47mevent-name[0m and [47mrule-classes[0m (which defaults to [47m(:rewrite)[0m if not
  supplied).  The event will be printed to the terminal, and then
  normally the user will be queried whether an event should really be
  created.  However, if the final optional argument [47mdo-it-flg[0m is
  supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then an event will be made without a query.

  For example, the form

    (exit top-pop-elim (:elim :rewrite) t)

  causes a [47mdefthm[0m event named [47mtop-pop-elim[0m to be created with
  rule-classes [47m(:elim :rewrite)[0m, without a query to the user (because
  of the argument [47mt[0m).

  [31;1mRemark:[0m it is permitted for [47mevent-name[0m to be [47mnil[0m.  In that case, the
  name of the event will be the name supplied during the original
  call of [47mverify[0m.  (See [verify] and [ACL2-pc::commands].)  Also in
  that case, if [47mrule-classes[0m is not supplied then it defaults to the
  rule-classes supplied in the original call of [47mverify[0m.

  [47mComments[0m on ``success'' and ``failure''.  An [47mexit[0m instruction will
  always ``fail'', so for example, if it appears as an argument of a
  [47mdo-strict[0m instruction then none of the later (instruction)
  arguments will be executed.  Moreover, the ``failure'' will be
  ``hard'' if an event is successfully created or if the instruction
  is simply [47mexit[0m; otherwise it will be ``soft''.  See
  [ACL2-pc::sequence] for an explanation of hard and soft
  ``failures''.  An obscure but potentially important fact is that if
  the ``failure'' is hard, then the error signal is a special signal
  that the top-level interactive loop can interpret as a request to
  exit.  Thus for example, a sequencing command that turns an error
  triple [47m(mv erp val state)[0m into [47m(mv t val state)[0m would never cause
  an exit from the interactive loop.

  If the proof is not complete, then neither [47m(exit event-name ...)[0m nor
  [47m(exit t)[0m will cause an exit from the interactive loop.  However, in
  that case either one will print out the original user-supplied goal
  (the one that was supplied with the call to [47mverify[0m) and the current
  list of instructions.")
 (ACL2-PC::EXPAND
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(primitive) expand the current function call without simplification

    Examples:
    expand -- expand and do not simplify.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::x], which performs simplification, and see
  [ACL2-pc::x-dumb], which provides a simple interface to
  [47macl2-pc::expand[0m.

  For example, if the current subterm is [47m(append a b)[0m, then after
  [47mexpand[0m the current subterm will be the term:

    (if (consp a)
        (cons (car a) (append (cdr a) b))
      b)

  regardless of the top-level hypotheses and the governors.

    General Form:
    (expand &optional do-not-expand-lambda-flg)

  Expand the function call at the current subterm, and do not simplify.
  The options have the following meanings:

    do-not-expand-lambda-flg:   default is nil; otherwise, the result
                                should be a lambda expression")
 (ACL2-PC::FAIL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) cause a failure

    Examples:
    fail
    (fail t)

    General Form:
    (fail &optional hard)

  This is probably only of interest to writers of macro commands.  The
  only function of [47mfail[0m is to fail to ``succeed''.

  The full story is that [47mfail[0m and [47m(fail nil)[0m simply return [47m(mv nil nil
  state)[0m, while [47m(fail hard)[0m returns [47m(mv hard nil state)[0m if [47mhard[0m is
  not [47mnil[0m.  Also see [ACL2-pc::do-strict], [ACL2-pc::do-all], and
  [ACL2-pc::sequence].")
 (ACL2-PC::FINISH
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) require completion of instructions; save error if inside
  [47m:[0m[47m[hints][0m

    Example:
    (finish induct prove bash)

    General Form:
    (finish &rest instructions)

  Run the indicated instructions, stopping at the first failure.  If
  there is any failure, or if any new goals are created and remain at
  the end of the indicated instructions, then consider the call of
  [47mfinish[0m to be a failure.  See [proof-builder-commands] and see
  [ACL2-pc::sequence] for a discussion of the notion of ``failure''
  for interactive [proof-builder] commands.")
 (ACL2-PC::FORWARDCHAIN
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) forward chain from an implication in the hyps

    Example:
    (forwardchain 2) ; Second hypothesis should be of the form
                     ; (IMPLIES hyp concl), and the result is to replace
                     ; that hypothesis with concl.

    General Forms:
    (forwardchain hypothesis-number)
    (forwardchain hypothesis-number hints)
    (forwardchain hypothesis-number hints quiet-flg)

  This command replaces the hypothesis corresponding to the given
  index.  That hypothesis --- call it [47mH[0m --- must be of the form
  [47m(IMPLIES hyp concl)[0m.  The effect of this command is to replace [47mH[0m by
  [47mconcl[0m, which becomes the final hypothesis.

  The prover must be able to prove [47mconcl[0m from the hypotheses other than
  [47mH[0m (more precisely, to prove [47mconcl[0m after removing the hypothesis at
  the given index), or else the command will fail.  The [47m:hints[0m
  argument is used in this prover call, and should have the usual
  syntax of hints to the prover.

  Output is suppressed if [47mquiet-flg[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::FREE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) create a ``free variable''

    Example:
    (free x)

    General Form:
    (free var)

  Mark [47mvar[0m as a ``free variable''.  Free variables are only of interest
  for the [47mput[0m command; see [ACL2-pc::put].")
 (ACL2-PC::GENEQV
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) show the generated equivalence relation maintained at the
  current subterm

    General Forms:
    geneqv     ; show list of equivalence relations being maintained
    (geneqv t) ; as above, but pair each relation with a justifying rune

  This is an advanced command, whose effect is to print the so-called
  ``generated equivalence relation'' (or ``geneqv'') that is
  maintained at the current subterm of the conclusion.  That
  structure is a list of equivalence relations, representing the
  transitive closure [47mE[0m of the union of those relations, such that it
  suffices to maintain [47mE[0m at the current subterm: if that subterm, [47mu[0m,
  is replaced in the goal's conclusion, [47mG[0m, by another term equivalent
  to [47mu[0m with respect to [47mE[0m, then the resulting conclusion is Boolean
  equivalent to [47mG[0m.  Also see [defcong].

  The command `[47mgeneqv[0m' prints the above list of equivalence relations,
  or more precisely, the list of function symbols for those
  relations.  If however [47mgeneqv[0m is given a non-[47mnil[0m argument, then a
  list is printed whose elements are each of the form [47m(s r)[0m, where [47ms[0m
  is the symbol for an equivalence relation and [47mr[0m is a [47m:[0m[47m[congruence][0m
  [rune] justifying the inclusion of [47ms[0m in the list of equivalence
  relations being maintained at the current subterm.")
 (ACL2-PC::GENERALIZE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) perform a generalization

    Example:
    (generalize
     ((and (true-listp x) (true-listp y)) 0)
     ((append x y) w))

    General Form:
    (generalize &rest substitution)

  Generalize using the indicated substitution, which should be a
  non-empty list.  Each element of that list should be a two-element
  list of the form [47m(term variable)[0m, where [47mterm[0m may use abbreviations.
  The effect of the instruction is to replace each such term in the
  current goal by the corresponding variable.  This replacement is
  carried out by a parallel substitution, outside-in in each
  hypothesis and in the conclusion.  More generally, actually, the
  ``variable'' (second) component of each pair may be [47mnil[0m or a
  number, which causes the system to generate a new name of the form
  [47m_[0m or [47m_n[0m, with [47mn[0m a natural number; more on this below.  However,
  when a variable is supplied, it must not occur in any goal of the
  current state of the interactive proof-builder.

  When the ``variable'' above is [47mnil[0m, the system will treat it as the
  variable [47m|_|[0m if that variable does not occur in any goal of the
  current proof-builder state.  Otherwise it treats it as [47m|_0|[0m, or
  [47m|_1|[0m, or [47m|_2|[0m, and so on, until one of these is not among the
  variables of the current proof-builder state.  If the ``variable''
  is a non-negative integer [47mn[0m, then the system treats it as [47m|_n|[0m
  unless that variable already occurs among the current goals, in
  which case it increments n just as above until it obtains a new
  variable.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m The same variable may not occur as the variable component of
  two different arguments (though [47mnil[0m may occur arbitrarily many
  times, as may a positive integer).")
 (ACL2-PC::GOALS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) list the names of goals on the stack

    Example and General Form:
    goals

  [47mGoals[0m lists the names of all goals that remain to be proved.  They
  are listed in the order in which they appear on the stack of
  remaining goals, which is relevant for example to the effect of a
  [47mchange-goal[0m instruction.")
 (ACL2-PC::HELP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) proof-builder help facility

    Examples:

    (help rewrite)   -- documentation on the proof-builder rewrite command
    (help)           -- this help
    (help help)      -- same as (help) or simply, help
    (help all)       -- same as (doc proof-builder-commands)

    General Forms:
    (help) ; or, just: help
    (help command)
    (help all)

  For any interactive proof-builder command, [47mC[0m, [47m(help C)[0m prints
  documentation on [47mC[0m to the terminal.  You can read that
  documentation online or in the Emacs [ACL2-Doc] browser by viewing
  topic [47macl2-pc::C[0m; see [documentation].

  To see a list of all interactive proof-builder commands, you can
  submit [47m(help all)[0m.  This is actually equivalent to [47m(doc
  proof-builder-commands)[0m, which prints the documentation for topic
  [proof-builder-commands] (which, as discussed above, you can view
  online or with [ACL2-Doc]).

  Also see [ACL2-pc::doc], which provides a direct interface to the
  ACL2 [47m:[0m[47m[doc][0m command.  For example, submitting [47m(help rewrite)[0m to the
  interactive proof-builder is equivalent to submitting [47m(doc
  acl2-pc::rewrite)[0m to the proof-builder or submitting [47m:doc
  acl2-pc::rewrite[0m directly to the ACL2 loop.")
 (ACL2-PC::HYPS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the hypotheses

    Examples:
    hyps               -- print all (top-level) hypotheses
    (hyps (1 3) (2 4)) -- print hypotheses 1 and 3 and governors 2 and 4
    (hyps (1 3) t)     -- print hypotheses 1 and 3 and all governors

    General Form:
    (hyps &optional hyps-indices govs-indices)

  Print the indicated top-level hypotheses and governors.  (The notion
  of ``governors'' is defined below.)  Here, [47mhyps-indices[0m and
  [47mgovs-indices[0m should be lists of indices of hypotheses and governors
  (respectively), except that the atom [47mt[0m may be used to indicate that
  one wants all hypotheses or governors (respectively).

  The list of ``governors'' is defined as follows.  Actually, we define
  here the notion of the governors for a pair of the form [47m<term[0m,
  address>]; we're interested in the special case where the term is
  the conclusion and the address is the current address.  If the
  address is [47mnil[0m, then there are no governors, i.e., the list of
  governors is [47mnil[0m.  If the term is of the form [47m(if x y z)[0m and the
  address is of the form [47m(2 . rest)[0m or [47m(3 . rest)[0m, then the list of
  governors is the result of [47mcons[0ming [47mx[0m or its negation (respectively)
  onto the list of governors for the pair [47m<y, rest>[0m or the pair [47m<z,
  rest>[0m (respectively).  If the term is of the form [47m(implies x y)[0m and
  the address is of the form [47m(2 . rest)[0m, then the list of governors
  is the result of [47mcons[0ming [47mx[0m onto the list of governors for the pair
  [47m<y, rest>[0m.  Otherwise, the list of governors for the pair [47m<term, (n
  .  rest)>[0m is exactly the list of governors for the pair [47m<argn,
  rest>[0m where [47margn[0m is the [47mn[0mth argument of [47mterm[0m.

  If all goals have been proved, a message saying so will be printed.
  (as there will be no current hypotheses or governors!).

  The [47mhyps[0m command never causes an error.  It ``succeeds'' (in fact its
  value is [47mt[0m) if the arguments (when supplied) are appropriate, i.e.
  either [47mt[0m or lists of indices of hypotheses or governors,
  respectively.  Otherwise it ``fails'' (its value is [47mnil[0m).")
 (ACL2-PC::ILLEGAL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) illegal instruction

    Example:
    (illegal -3)

    General Form:
    (illegal instruction)

  Probably not of interest to most users; always ``fails'' since it
  expands to the [47mfail[0m command.

  The [47millegal[0m command is used mainly in the implementation.  For
  example, the instruction [47m0[0m is ``read'' as [47m(illegal 0)[0m, since [47mdive[0m
  expects positive integers.")
 (ACL2-PC::IN-THEORY
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(primitive) set the current proof-builder theory

    Example:
    (in-theory
       (union-theories (theory 'minimal-theory) '(true-listp binary-append)))

    General Form:
    (in-theory &optional atom-or-theory-expression)

  If the argument is not supplied, then this command sets the current
  interactive proof-builder theory (see below for explanation) to
  agree with the current ACL2 theory.  Otherwise, the argument should
  be a theory expression, and in that case the proof-builder theory
  is set to the value of that theory expression.

  The current interactive proof-builder theory is used in all calls to
  the ACL2 theorem prover and rewriter from inside the proof-builder.
  Thus, the most recent [47min-theory[0m instruction in the current
  [47mstate-stack[0m has an effect in the proof-builder totally analogous to
  the effect caused by an [47min-theory[0m hint or event in ACL2.  All
  [47min-theory[0m instructions before the last are ignored, because they
  refer to the current theory in the ACL2 [47m[state][0m, not to the
  existing proof-builder theory.  For example:

    ACL2 !>:trans1 (enable bar)
     (UNION-THEORIES (CURRENT-THEORY :HERE)
                     '(BAR))
    ACL2 !>:trans1 (CURRENT-THEORY :HERE)
     (CURRENT-THEORY-FN :HERE WORLD)
    ACL2 !>

  Thus [47m(in-theory (enable bar))[0m modifies the current theory of the
  current ACL2 world.  So for example, suppose that [47mfoo[0m is disabled
  outside the proof-builder and you execute the following
  instructions, in this order.

    (in-theory (enable foo))
    (in-theory (enable bar))

  Then after the second of these, [47mbar[0m will be enabled in the
  proof-builder, but [47mfoo[0m will be disabled.  The reason is that
  [47m(in-theory (enable bar))[0m instructs the proof-builder to modify the
  current theory (from outside the proof-builder, not from inside the
  proof-builder) by enabling [47mbar[0m.

  Note that [47min-theory[0m instructions in the proof-builder have no effect
  outside the proof-builder's interactive loop.

  If the most recent [47min-theory[0m instruction in the current state of the
  proof-builder has no arguments, or if there is no [47min-theory[0m
  instruction in the current state of the proof-builder, then the
  proof-builder will use the current ACL2 theory.  This is true even
  if the user has interrupted the interactive loop by exiting and
  changing the global ACL2 theory.  However, if the most recent
  [47min-theory[0m instruction in the current state of the proof-builder had
  an argument, then global changes to the current theory will have no
  effect on the proof-builder state.")
 (ACL2-PC::INDUCT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) generate subgoals using induction

    Examples:
    induct, (induct t)
       -- induct according to a heuristically-chosen scheme, creating
          a new subgoal for each base and induction step
    (induct (append (reverse x) y))
       -- as above, but choose an induction scheme based on the term
          (append (reverse x) y) rather than on the current goal

    General Form:
    (induct &optional term)

  Induct as in the corresponding [47m:induct[0m hint given to the theorem
  prover, creating new subgoals for the base and induction steps.  If
  term is [47mt[0m or is not supplied, then use the current goal to
  determine the induction scheme; otherwise, use that term.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m As usual, abbreviations are allowed in the term.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m [47mInduct[0m actually calls the [47mprove[0m command with all processes
  turned off.  Thus, you must be at top of the goal for an [47minduct[0m
  instruction.")
 (ACL2-PC::LEMMAS-USED
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the runes (definitions, lemmas, ...) used

  This is just an alias for [47mrunes[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::LISP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(meta) evaluate the given form in Lisp

    Example:
    (lisp (assign xxx 3))

    General Form:
    (lisp form)

  Evaluate [47mform[0m.  The [47mlisp[0m command is mainly of interest for side
  effects.  Also see [ACL2-pc::print], [ACL2-pc::skip], and
  [ACL2-pc::fail].

  The rest of the documentation for [47mlisp[0m is of interest only to those
  who use it in macro commands.  If the Lisp evaluation (by
  [47mtrans-eval[0m) of form returns an [error-triple] of the form [47m(mv erp
  ((NIL NIL STATE) . (erp-1 val-1 &)) state)[0m, then the [47mlisp[0m command
  returns the appropriate error triple

    (mv (or erp erp-1)
        val-1
        state) .

  Otherwise, the [47mtrans-eval[0m of form must return an error triple of the
  form [47m(mv erp (cons stobjs-out val) &)[0m, and the [47mlisp[0m command returns
  the appropriate error triple

    (mv erp
        val
        state).

  Note that the output signature of the form has been lost.  The user
  must know the signature in order to use the output of the [47mlisp[0m
  command.  Trans-eval, which is undocumented except by comments in
  the ACL2 source code, has replaced, in [47mval[0m, any occurrence of the
  current state or the current values of stobjs by simple symbols
  such as [47mREPLACED-STATE[0m.  The actual values of these objects may be
  recovered, in principle, from the [47mstate[0m returned and the
  [47muser-stobj-alist[0m within that state.  However, in practice, the
  stobjs cannot be recovered because the user is denied access to
  [47muser-stobj-alist[0m.  The moral is: do not try to write macro commands
  that manipulate stobjs.  Should the returned [47mval[0m contain
  [47mREPLACED-STATE[0m the value may simply be ignored and [47mstate[0m used,
  since that is what [47mREPLACED-STATE[0m denotes.")
 (ACL2-PC::NEGATE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) run the given instructions, and ``succeed'' if and only if
  they ``fail''

  Example: (negate prove)

    General form:
    (negate &rest instruction-list)

  Run the indicated instructions exactly in the sense of [47mdo-all[0m, and
  ``succeed'' if and only if they ``fail''.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m [47mNegate[0m instructions will never produce hard ``failures''.")
 (ACL2-PC::NIL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) used for interpreting [47mcontrol-d[0m

    Example and General form:
    nil

  (or, [47mcontrol-d[0m).

  The whole point of this command is that there have been Lisps where
  if you type [47mcontrol-d[0m then it seems, on occasion, to get
  interpreted as [47mnil[0m.  Without this command, one seems to get into an
  infinite loop.")
 (ACL2-PC::NOISE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(meta) run instructions with prover output

    Example:
    (noise induct prove)

    General Form:
    (noise &rest instruction-list)

  Run the [47minstruction-list[0m through the top-level loop with prover
  output.

  In fact, having prover output is the default.  [47mNoise[0m is useful inside
  a surrounding call of [47mquiet[0m, when one temporarily wants output.
  For example, if one wants to see output for a [47mprove[0m command
  immediately following an [47minduct[0m command but before an [47ms[0m command,
  one may want to submit an instruction like [47m(quiet induct (noise
  prove) s)[0m.  Also see [ACL2-pc::quiet] and [ACL2-pc::noise!].")
 (ACL2-PC::NOISE!
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(meta) run instructions with prover and proof-tree output

    Example:
    (noise! induct prove)

    General Form:
    (noise! &rest instruction-list)

  Run the [47minstruction-list[0m through the top-level loop with prover
  output and [proof-tree] output.  See [ACL2-pc::noise]; the [47mnoise![0m
  command is the same, except for adding proof-tree output.")
 (ACL2-PC::NX
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) move forward one argument in the enclosing term

    Example and General Form:
    nx

  For example, if the conclusion is [47m(= x (* (- y) z))[0m and the current
  subterm is [47mx[0m, then after executing [47mnx[0m, the current subterm will be
  [47m(* (- y) z)[0m.

  This is the same as [47mup[0m followed by [47m(dive n+1)[0m, where [47mn[0m is the
  position of the current subterm in its parent term in the
  conclusion.  Thus in particular, the [47mnx[0m command fails if one is
  already at the top of the conclusion.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::up], [ACL2-pc::dive], [ACL2-pc::top], and
  [ACL2-pc::bk].")
 (ACL2-PC::ORELSE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) run the first instruction; if (and only if) it ``fails'', run
  the second

    Example:
    (orelse top (print \"Couldn't move to the top\"))

    General form:
    (orelse instr1 instr2)

  Run the first instruction.  Then if it ``fails'', run the second
  instruction also; otherwise, stop after the first.

  This instruction ``succeeds'' if and only if either [47minstr1[0m
  ``succeeds'', or else [47minstr2[0m ``succeeds''.  If it ``fails'', then
  the failure is soft.")
 (ACL2-PC::P
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) prettyprint the current term in the usual user-level
  (untranslated) syntax

    Example and General Form:
    p

  Prettyprint the current term.  The usual user (untranslated) syntax
  is used, so that for example one would see [47m(and x y)[0m rather than
  [47m(if x y 'nil)[0m.  (See also [47mpp[0m.)  Also, abbreviations are inserted
  where appropriate; see [ACL2-pc::add-abbreviation].

  The ``current term'' is the entire conclusion unless [47mdive[0m commands
  have been given, in which case it may be a subterm of the
  conclusion.

  If all goals have been proved, a message saying so will be printed
  (as there will be no current [47mterm[0m!).")
 (ACL2-PC::P-TOP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) prettyprint the conclusion, highlighting the current term

    Example and General Form:
    p-top

  For example, if the conclusion is [47m(equal (and x (p y)) (foo z))[0m and
  the current subterm is [47m(p y)[0m, then [47mp-top[0m will print [47m(equal (and x
  (*** (p y) ***)) (foo z))[0m.

  Prettyprint the conclusion, highlighting the current term.  The usual
  user syntax is used, as with the command [47mp[0m (as opposed to [47mpp[0m).
  This is illustrated in the example above, where one would [3m*not*[0m see
  [47m(equal (if x (*** (p y) ***) 'nil) (foo z))[0m.

  [31;1mRemark[0m (obscure): In some situations, a term of the form [47m(if x t y)[0m
  occurring inside the current subterm will not print as [47m(or x y)[0m,
  when [47mx[0m isn't a call of a boolean primitive.  There's nothing
  incorrect about this, however.")
 (ACL2-PC::PL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the rules for a given name

    Examples:
    pl
    (pl foo)

    General Form:
    (pl &optional x)

  This command simply invokes the corresponding command of the
  top-level ACL2 loop; see [pl].  If no argument is given, or if the
  argument is [47mnil[0m, then the current subterm should be a call of a
  function symbol, and the argument is taken to be that symbol.

  If you want information about applying rewrite rules to the current
  subterm, consider the [47mshow-rewrites[0m (or equivalently, [47msr[0m) command.")
 (ACL2-PC::POT-LST
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) display the linear arithmetic database based on the current
  context

  This is a relatively advanced command.  For discussion of a related
  but more elementary command, including remarks about the utility of
  such a command, see [ACL2-pc::type-alist].  See [linear-arithmetic]
  for a description of the ACL2 linear arithmetic decision procedure

    Examples:
    (pot-lst nil t)   ; display linear pot-lst based on governors only (default)
    pot-lst           ; same as (pot-lst nil t) -- governors only (default)
    (pot-lst nil)     ; same as (pot-lst nil t) -- governors only (default)
    (pot-lst nil t nil nil) ; same as above
    (pot-lst nil t nil t)   ; same as above, except: raw format
    (pot-lst t t)     ; display pot-lst based on conclusion and governors
    (pot-lst t)       ; same as (pot-lst t nil) -- conclusion only
    (pot-lst nil nil) ; based on neither conclusion nor governors

    General Form:
    (pot-lst &optional concl-flg govs-flg rawp)

  where if [47mgovs-flg[0m is omitted then it defaults to [47m(not concl-flg)[0m, and
  each of the other optional arguments defaults to [47mnil[0m.

  This command displays the linear database, also known as the linear
  [3mpot-lst[0m, that is computed from a suitable set of assumptions.  That
  set of assumptions always includes all top-level hypotheses.  By
  default, and when [47mgovs-flg[0m is supplied a non-[47mnil[0m value, the set of
  assumptions includes all governors (which are based on surrounding
  if-expressions that must be true or false).  The negation of the
  current goal's top-level conclusion is also included in the
  assumptions when [47mconcl-flg[0m is supplied a non-[47mnil[0m value.

  The computed pot-lst is based on the result of forward chaining from
  the set of assumptions as described above.  By default, that
  pot-lst is displayed in a self-explanatory way.  Here is an
  (admittedly contrived) example.

    ACL2 !>(verify (implies (and (>= (- (nth 3 x) a) 7)
                                 (< (nth 3 x) b)
                                 (< c b))
                            (< (nth 3 x) d)))
    ->: promote
    ->: th
    *** Top-level hypotheses:
    1. (<= 7 (+ (NTH 3 X) (- A)))
    2. (< (NTH 3 X) B)
    3. (< C B)

    The current subterm is:
    (< (NTH 3 X) D)
    ->: pot-lst
    Current pot-lst:
    -----
    For maximal term B
    the list of polynomials is:
    ((A + 7 < B))
    -----
    For maximal term C
    the list of polynomials is:
    ((C < B))
    -----
    For maximal term (NTH '3 X)
    the list of polynomials is:
    (((NTH '3 X) < B) (A + 7 <= (NTH '3 X)))

    NIL
    ->: (pot-lst t t)
    Current pot-lst:
    -----
    For maximal term B
    the list of polynomials is:
    ((A + 7 < B))
    -----
    For maximal term C
    the list of polynomials is:
    ((C < B))
    -----
    For maximal term D
    the list of polynomials is:
    ((D < B))
    -----
    For maximal term (NTH '3 X)
    the list of polynomials is:
    (((NTH '3 X) < B) (A + 7 <= (NTH '3 X)) (D <= (NTH '3 X)))

    NIL
    ->:

  You can get the internal form of the pot-lst by supplying all
  optional arguments including a non-[47mnil[0m value for [47mrawp[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::PP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) prettyprint the current term in internal (translated) form

    Example and General Form:
    pp

  This is the same as [47mp[0m (see its documentation), except that raw syntax
  (internal form) is used.  So for example, one would see [47m(if x y
  'nil)[0m rather than [47m(and x y)[0m.  Abbreviations are however still
  inserted, as with [47mp[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::PR
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the rules for a given name

    Examples:
    pr
    (pr foo)

    General Form:
    (pr &optional x)

  This command simply invokes the corresponding command of the
  top-level ACL2 loop; see [pr].  If no argument is given, or if the
  argument is [47mnil[0m, then the current subterm should be a call of a
  function symbol, and the argument is taken to be that symbol.

  If you want information about applying rewrite rules to the current
  subterm, consider the [47mshow-rewrites[0m (or equivalently, [47msr[0m) command.")
 (ACL2-PC::PRINT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the result of evaluating the given form

    Example:
    (print (append '(a b) '(c d)))
    Print the list (a b c d) to the terminal

    General Forms:
    (print form)
    (print form t)

  Prettyprints the result of evaluating form.  The evaluation of [47mform[0m
  should return a single value that is not [47m[state][0m or a
  single-threaded object (see [stobj]).  The optional second argument
  causes printing to be done without elision (so-called
  ``evisceration''; see [evisc-tuple]).

  If the form you want to evaluate does not satisfy the criterion
  above, you should create an appropriate call of the [47mlisp[0m command
  instead.  Notice that this command always returns [47m(mv nil nil
  state)[0m where the second result will always be [47mREPLACED-STATE[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::PRINT-ALL-CONCS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print all the conclusions of (as yet unproved) goals

  Example and General Form: print-all-concs

  Prints all the conclusions of goals that remain to be proved, in a
  pleasant format, ordered as by the command, [47m[ACL2-pc::goals][0m.  Also
  see [ACL2-pc::print-all-goals].")
 (ACL2-PC::PRINT-ALL-GOALS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print all the (as yet unproved) goals

  Example and General Form: print-all-goals

  Prints all the goals that remain to be proved, in a pleasant format,
  ordered as by the command, [47m[ACL2-pc::goals][0m.  Also see
  [ACL2-pc::print-all-concs].")
 (ACL2-PC::PRINT-MAIN
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the original goal

    Example and General Form:
    print-main

  Print the goal as originally entered.")
 (ACL2-PC::PRO
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) repeatedly apply promote

    Example and General Form:
    pro

  Apply the [47mpromote[0m command until there is no change.  This command
  ``succeeds'' exactly when at least one call of [47mpromote[0m
  ``succeeds''.  In that case, only a single new proof-builder state
  will be created.

  Note: The [47msplit[0m command is probably more helpful in many cases than
  [47mpro[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::PROMOTE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) move antecedents of conclusion's [47mimplies[0m term to
  top-level hypotheses

    Examples:
    promote
    (promote t)

  For example, if the conclusion is [47m(implies (and x y) z)[0m, then after
  execution of [47mpromote[0m, the conclusion will be [47mz[0m and the terms [47mx[0m and
  [47my[0m will be new top-level hypotheses.

    General Form:
    (promote &optional do-not-flatten-flag)

  Replace conclusion of [47m(implies hyps exp)[0m or [47m(if hyps exp t)[0m with
  simply [47mexp[0m, adding [47mhyps[0m to the list of top-level hypotheses.
  Moreover, if [47mhyps[0m is viewed as a conjunction then each conjunct
  will be added as a separate top-level hypothesis.  An exception is
  that if [47mdo-not-flatten-flag[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then only one
  top-level hypothesis will be added, namely [47mhyps[0m.

  [31;1mRemark[0m: You must be at the top of the conclusion in order to use this
  command.  Otherwise, first invoke [47mtop[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::PROTECT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) run the given instructions, reverting to existing state upon
  failure

    Example:
    (protect induct p prove)

    General Form:
    (protect &rest instruction-list)

  [47mProtect[0m is the same as [47mdo-strict[0m, except that as soon as an
  instruction ``fails'', the state-stack reverts to what it was
  before the [47mprotect[0m instruction began, and [47mrestore[0m is given the same
  meaning that it had before the [47mprotect[0m instruction began.  See
  [ACL2-pc::do-strict].")
 (ACL2-PC::PROVE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(primitive) call the ACL2 theorem prover to prove the current goal

    Examples:
    prove -- attempt to prove the current goal
    (prove :otf-flg t
           :hints ((\"Subgoal 2\" :by foo) (\"Subgoal 1\" :use bar)))
          -- attempt to prove the current goal, with the indicated hints
             and with OTF-FLG set

    General Form:
    (prove &rest rest-args)

  Attempt to prove the current goal, where [47mrest-args[0m is as in the
  keyword arguments to [47mdefthm[0m except that only [47m:hints[0m and [47m:otf-flg[0m
  are allowed.  The command succeeds exactly when the corresponding
  [47mdefthm[0m would succeed, except that it is all right for some goals to
  be given ``bye''s.  Each goal given a ``bye'' will be turned into a
  new subgoal.  (See [hints] for an explanation of [47m:by[0m hints.)

  [31;1mRemark:[0m Use [47m(= t)[0m instead if you are not at the top of the
  conclusion.  Also note that if there are any hypotheses in the
  current goal, then what is actually attempted is a proof of
  [47m(implies hyps conc)[0m, where [47mhyps[0m is the conjunction of the top-level
  hypotheses and [47mconc[0m is the goal's conclusion.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m It is allowed to use abbreviations in the hints.")
 (ACL2-PC::PSO
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the most recent proof attempt from inside the
  proof-builder

    Example and General Form:
    pso

  Print the most recent proof attempt from inside the interactive
  proof-builder.  This includes prover calls, including for example
  [proof-builder] commands [47minduct[0m, [47msplit[0m, and [47mbash[0m, in addition to
  [47mprove[0m.  So for example, you can follow [47m(quiet prove)[0m with [47mpso[0m to
  see the proof, including [proof-tree] output, if it failed.

  Related [proof-builder] commands are [47mpsog[0m and [47mpso![0m; see
  [ACL2-pc::psog] and [ACL2-pc::pso!].")
 (ACL2-PC::PSO!
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the most recent proof attempt from inside the
  proof-builder

    Example and General Form:
    pso!

  Print the most recent proof attempt from inside the interactive
  proof-builder.  This includes prover calls, including for example
  [proof-builder] commands [47minduct[0m, [47msplit[0m, and [47mbash[0m, in addition to
  [47mprove[0m.  So for example, you can follow [47m(quiet prove)[0m with [47mpso![0m to
  see the proof, including [proof-tree] output, if it failed.

  Related [proof-builder] commands are [47mpso[0m and [47mpsog[0m; see [ACL2-pc::pso]
  and [ACL2-pc::psog].")
 (ACL2-PC::PSOG
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) print the most recent proof attempt from inside the
  proof-builder

    Example and General Form:
    psog

  Print the most recent proof attempt from inside the interactive
  proof-builder, including goal names.  This includes prover calls,
  including for example [proof-builder] commands [47minduct[0m, [47msplit[0m, and
  [47mbash[0m, in addition to [47mprove[0m.  So for example, you can follow [47m(quiet
  prove)[0m with [47mpsog[0m to see the proof, including [proof-tree] output,
  if it failed.

  Related [proof-builder] commands are [47mpso[0m and [47mpso![0m; see [ACL2-pc::pso]
  and [ACL2-pc::pso!].")
 (ACL2-PC::PUT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) substitute for a ``free variable''

    Example:
    (put x 17)

    General Form:
    (put var expr)

  Substitute [47mexpr[0m for the ``free variable'' [47mvar[0m, as explained below.

  A ``free variable'' is, for our purposes, a variable [47mvar[0m such that
  the instruction [47m(free var)[0m has been executed earlier in the
  state-stack.  What [47m(free var)[0m really does is to let [47mvar[0m be an
  abbreviation for the term [47m(hide var)[0m (see
  [ACL2-pc::add-abbreviation]).  What [47m(put var expr)[0m really does is
  to unwind the state-stack, replacing that [47mfree[0m instruction with the
  instruction [47m(add-abbreviation var expr)[0m, so that future references
  to [47m(? var)[0m become reference to [47mexpr[0m rather than to [47m(hide var)[0m, and
  then to replay all the other instructions that were unwound.
  Because [47mhide[0m was used, the expectation is that in most cases, the
  instructions will replay successfully and [47mput[0m will ``succeed''.
  However, if any replayed instruction ``fails'', then the entire
  replay will abort and ``fail'', and the state-stack will revert to
  its value before the [47mput[0m instruction was executed.

  If [47m(put var expr)[0m ``succeeds'', then [47m(remove-abbreviation var)[0m will
  be executed at the end.

  [31;1mRemark[0m: The [47mrestore[0m command will revert the state-stack to its value
  present before the [47mput[0m instruction was executed.")
 (ACL2-PC::QUIET
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(meta) run instructions without prover output

    Example:
    (quiet induct prove)

    General Form:
    (quiet &rest instruction-list)

  Run the [47minstruction-list[0m through the top-level loop with no output.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::noise] and [ACL2-pc::quiet!].")
 (ACL2-PC::QUIET!
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(meta) run instructions without prover output

    Example:
    (quiet! induct prove)

    General Form:
    (quiet! &rest instruction-list)

  Run the [47minstruction-list[0m through the top-level loop with no output.
  See [ACL2-pc::quiet]; the two commands are equivalent, except that
  the [47mquiet![0m command additionally suppresses the printing of goal
  identifiers (as with [47m[set-print-clause-ids][0m).")
 (ACL2-PC::R
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) same as rewrite

    Example:
    (r 3)

  See [ACL2-pc::rewrite].")
 (ACL2-PC::REDUCE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) call the ACL2 theorem prover's simplifier

    Examples:
    reduce -- attempt to prove the current goal without using induction
    (reduce (\"Subgoal 2\" :by foo) (\"Subgoal 1\" :use bar))
           -- attempt to prove the current goal without using
              induction, with the indicated hints

    General Form:
    (reduce &rest hints)

  Attempt to prove the current goal without using induction, using the
  indicated hints (if any).  A subgoal will be created for each goal
  that would have been pushed for proof by induction in an ordinary
  proof.

  Notice that unlike [47mprove[0m, the arguments to [47mreduce[0m are spread out, and
  are all hints.

  [47mReduce[0m is similar to [47mbash[0m in that neither of these allows induction.
  But [47mbash[0m only allows simplification, while [47mreduce[0m allows processes
  [47meliminate-destructors[0m, [47mfertilize[0m, [47mgeneralize[0m, and
  [47meliminate-irrelevance[0m.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m Induction will be used to the extent that it is ordered
  explicitly in the hints.")
 (ACL2-PC::REDUCE-BY-INDUCTION
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) call the ACL2 prover without induction, after going into
  induction

    Examples:
    reduce-by-induction
      -- attempt to prove the current goal after going into induction,
         with no further inductions

    (reduce-by-induction (\"Subgoal 2\" :by foo) (\"Subgoal 1\" :use bar))
      -- attempt to prove the current goal after going into induction,
         with no further inductions, using the indicated hints

    General Form:
    (reduce-by-induction &rest hints)

  A subgoal will be created for each goal that would have been pushed
  for proof by induction in an ordinary proof, except that the proof
  begins with a top-level induction.

  Notice that unlike [47mprove[0m, the arguments to [47mreduce-by-induction[0m are
  spread out, and are all hints.  Also see [ACL2-pc::prove],
  [ACL2-pc::reduce], and [ACL2-pc::bash].

  [31;1mRemark[0m: Induction and the various processes will be used to the
  extent that they are specified explicitly in the [47m:induct[0m and
  [47m:do-not[0m [hints].")
 (ACL2-PC::REMOVE-ABBREVIATIONS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) remove one or more abbreviations

    Examples:
    remove-abbreviations -- remove all abbreviations
    (remove-abbreviations v w)
                         -- assuming that V and W currently abbreviate
                            terms, then they are ``removed'' in the
                            sense that they are no longer considered to
                            abbreviate those terms

    General Forms:
    (remove-abbreviations &rest vars)

  If vars is not empty (i.e., not [47mnil[0m), remove the variables in [47mvars[0m
  from the current list of abbreviations, in the sense that each
  variable in [47mvars[0m will no longer abbreviate a term.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m The instruction fails if at least one of the arguments fails
  to be a variable that abbreviates a term.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::add-abbreviation] for a discussion of
  abbreviations in general, and see [ACL2-pc::show-abbreviations].")
 (ACL2-PC::REPEAT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) repeat the given instruction until it ``fails''

    Example:
    (repeat promote)

    General Form:
    (repeat instruction)

  The given [47minstruction[0m is run repeatedly until it ``fails''.  A call
  of [47m:repeat[0m always ``succeeds''.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m There is nothing here in general to prevent the instruction
  from being run after all goals have been proved, though it may then
  fail, thus causing [47m:repeat[0m to return.")
 (ACL2-PC::REPEAT-REC
      (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
      "(macro) auxiliary to [47mrepeat[0m

  See [ACL2-pc::repeat].")
 (ACL2-PC::REPLAY
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) replay one or more instructions

    Examples:
    REPLAY     -- replay all instructions in the current session
                  (i.e., state-stack)
    (REPLAY 5) -- replay the most recent 5 instructions
    (REPLAY 5
            (COMMENT deleted dive command here))
               -- replace the 5th most recent instruction with the
                  indicated comment instruction, and then replay it
                  followed by the remaining 4 instructions

    General Form:
    (REPLAY &OPTIONAL n replacement-instruction)

  Replay the last [47mn[0m instructions if [47mn[0m is a positive integer; else [47mn[0m
  should be [47mnil[0m or not supplied, and replay all instructions.
  However, if [47mreplacement-instruction[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then
  before the replay, replace the [47mnth[0m instruction (from the most
  recent, as shown by [47mcommands[0m) with [47mreplacement-instruction[0m.

  If this command ``fails'', then the [47mrestore[0m command will revert the
  state-stack to its value present before the [47mreplay[0m instruction was
  executed.")
 (ACL2-PC::RESTORE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(meta) remove the effect of an UNDO command

    Example and General Form:
    restore

  [47mRestore[0m removes the effect of an [47mundo[0m command.  This always works as
  expected if [47mrestore[0m is invoked immediately after [47mundo[0m, without
  intervening instructions.  However, other commands may also
  interact with [47mrestore[0m, notably ``sequencing'' commands such as
  [47mdo-all[0m, [47mdo-strict[0m, [47mprotect[0m, and more generally, [47msequence[0m.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m Another way to control the saving of proof-builder state is
  with the [47msave[0m command; see [ACL2-pc::save].

  The [47mrestore[0m command always ``succeeds''; it returns [47m(mv nil t state)[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::RETAIN
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) drop all [31;1mbut[0m the indicated top-level hypotheses

    Example:
    (RETAIN 2 3) -- keep the second and third hypotheses, and drop
                    the rest

    General Form:
    (retain &rest args)

  Drop all top-level hypotheses [31;1mexcept[0m those with the indicated
  indices.

  There must be at least one argument, and all must be in range (i.e.
  integers between one and the number of top-level hypotheses,
  inclusive).")
 (ACL2-PC::RETRIEVE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) re-enter the proof-builder

    Examples:
    (retrieve associativity-of-permutationp)
    retrieve

    General Form:
    (retrieve &optional name)

  Must be used from [47moutside[0m the interactive proof-builder loop.  If
  name (which must be a symbol) is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, this causes
  re-entry to the interactive proof-builder loop in the state at
  which [47msave[0m was last executed for the indicated name.  (See
  [ACL2-pc::save].)  If [47mname[0m is [47mnil[0m or is not supplied, then the user
  is queried regarding which proof-builder state to re-enter.  The
  query is omitted, however, if there only one proof-builder state is
  present that was saved with [47msave[0m, in which case that is the one
  that is used.  Also see [ACL2-pc::unsave].")
 (ACL2-PC::REWRITE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(primitive) apply a rewrite rule

    Examples:
    (rewrite reverse-reverse)
       -- apply the rewrite rule `reverse-reverse'
    (rewrite (:rewrite reverse-reverse))
       -- same as above
    (rewrite 2)
       -- apply the second rewrite rule, as displayed by show-rewrites
    rewrite
       -- apply the first rewrite rule, as displayed by show-rewrites
    (rewrite transitivity-of-< ((y 7)))
       -- apply the rewrite rule transitivity-of-< with the substitution
          that associates 7 to the ``free variable'' y
    (rewrite foo ((x 2) (y 3)) t)
       -- apply the rewrite rule foo by substituting 2 and 3 for free
          variables x and y, respectively, and also binding all other
          free variables possible by using the current context
          (hypotheses and governors)

    General Form:
    (rewrite &optional rule-id substitution instantiate-free)

  Replace the current subterm with a new term by applying a [rewrite]
  or [definition] rule.  The replacement will be done according to
  the information provided by the [47mshow-rewrites[0m ([47msr[0m) command.

  If [47mrule-id[0m is a positive integer [47mn[0m, then the [47mn[0mth rule as displayed by
  [47mshow-rewrites[0m is the one that is applied.  If [47mrule-id[0m is [47mnil[0m or is
  not supplied, then it is treated as the number 1.  Otherwise,
  [47mrule-id[0m should be either a symbol or else a [47m:rewrite[0m or [47m:definition[0m
  [rune].  If a symbol is supplied, then any ([47m:rewrite[0m or
  [47m:definition[0m) rule of that name may be used.  We say more about
  this, and describe the other optional arguments, below.

  Consider first the following example.  Suppose that the current
  subterm is [47m(reverse (reverse y))[0m and that there is a [rewrite] rule
  called [47mreverse-reverse[0m of the form

    (implies (true-listp x)
             (equal (reverse (reverse x)) x)) .

  Then the instruction [47m(rewrite reverse-reverse)[0m causes the current
  subterm to be replaced by [47my[0m and creates a new goal with conclusion
  [47m(true-listp y)[0m.  An exception is that if the top-level hypotheses
  imply [47m(true-listp y)[0m using only ``trivial reasoning'' (more on this
  below), then no new goal is created.

  If the [47mrule-id[0m argument is a number or is not supplied, then the
  system will store an instruction of the form [47m(rewrite name ...)[0m,
  where [47mname[0m is the name of a rewrite rule; this is in order to make
  it easier to replay instructions when there have been changes to
  the history.  Except: instead of the name (whether the name is
  supplied or calculated), the system stores the [rune] if there is
  any chance of ambiguity.  (Formally, ``ambiguity'' here means that
  the rune being applied is of the form [47m(:rewrite name . index)[0m,
  where index is not [47mnil[0m.)

  Speaking in general, then, a [47mrewrite[0m instruction works as follows:

  First, a [rewrite] or [definition] rule is selected according to the
  arguments of the [47mrewrite[0m instruction.  The selection is made as
  explained under ``General Form'' above.

  Next, the left-hand side of the rule is matched with the current
  subterm, i.e., a substitution [47munify-subst[0m is found such that if one
  instantiates the left-hand side of the rule with [47munify-subst[0m, then
  one obtains the current subterm.  If this match fails, then the
  instruction fails.

  Next, an attempt is made to relieve (discharge) the hypotheses, much
  as the theorem prover relieves hypotheses except that there is no
  call to the rewriter.  First, the substitution [47munify-subst[0m is
  extended with the [47msubstitution[0m argument, which may bind free
  variables (see [free-variables]).  Each hypothesis of the rule is
  then considered in turn, from first to last.  For each hypothesis,
  first the current substitution is applied, and then the system
  checks whether the hypothesis is ``clearly'' true in the current
  context.  If there are variables in the hypotheses of the rule that
  are not bound by the current substitution, then a weak attempt is
  made to extend that substitution so that the hypothesis is present
  in the current context (see [ACL2-pc::hyps]), much as would be done
  by the theorem prover's rewriter.

  If in the process above there are free variables (see
  [free-variables]), but the interactive proof-builder can see how to
  bind them to relieve all hypotheses, then it will do so in both the
  [47mshow-rewrites[0m ([47msr[0m) and [47mrewrite[0m commands.  But normally, if even one
  hypothesis remains unrelieved, then no automatic extension of the
  substitution is made.  Except, if [47minstantiate-free[0m is not [47mnil[0m, then
  that extension to the substitution is kept.  (Technical note: in
  the case of an unrelieved hypothesis and a non-[47mnil[0m value of
  [47minstantiate-free[0m, if a [47m[bind-free][0m hypothesis produces a list of
  binding alists, then the last of those alists is the one that is
  used to extend the substitution.)

  Finally, the instruction is applied as follows.  The current subterm
  is replaced by applying the final substitution described above to
  the right-hand side of the selected rule.  And, one new subgoal is
  created for each unrelieved hypothesis of the rule, whose top-level
  hypotheses are the governors and top-level hypotheses of the
  current goal and whose conclusion and current subterm are the
  instance, by that same final substitution, of that unrelieved
  hypothesis.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m The substitution argument should be a list whose elements
  have the form [47m(variable term)[0m, where [47mterm[0m may contain
  abbreviations.")
 (ACL2-PC::RUN-INSTR-ON-GOAL
      (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
      "(macro) auxiliary to THEN

  See [ACL2-pc::then].")
 (ACL2-PC::RUN-INSTR-ON-NEW-GOALS
      (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
      "(macro) auxiliary to [47mthen[0m

  See [ACL2-pc::then].")
 (ACL2-PC::RUNES
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) print the runes (definitions, lemmas, ...) used

    Examples and general forms:
    (runes t)   ; print all [rune]s used during this interactive proof
    (runes nil) ; print all [rune]s used by the most recent command
    (runes)     ; same as (runes nil)
    runes       ; same as (runes nil)

  This command does not change the state of the interactive
  [proof-builder].  Rather, it simply reports runes (see [rune]) that
  have participated in the interactive proof.

  Note that [47m(runes nil)[0m will show the [rune]s used by the most recent
  primitive or macro command (as displayed by [47m:comm[0m).")
 (ACL2-PC::S
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(primitive) simplify the current subterm

    Examples:
    S  -- simplify the current subterm
    (S :backchain-limit 2 :normalize t :expand (append x z))
       -- simplify the current subterm, but during the rewriting
          process, normalization, which will push IFs to the
          top-level, and also force the term (append x z) to be
          expanded during the rewriting process

    General Form:
    (s &key rewrite linear normalize backchain-limit repeat in-theory hands-off
            expand)

  Simplify the current subterm according to the keyword parameters
  supplied.  First [normalization] is applied (unless the [47m:normalize[0m
  argument is [47mnil[0m), so that for example, each subterm of the form [47m(f
  ... (if test x y) ...)[0m is replaced by the term [47m(if test (f ... x
  ...) (f ... y ...))[0m --- except, of course, when [47mf[0m is [47mif[0m and the
  indicated [47mif[0m subterm is in the second or third argument position.
  See [normalize] for details.  Then rewriting is applied (unless the
  rewrite argument is [47mnil[0m).  Finally this pair of actions is repeated
  --- until the rewriting step causes no change in the term.  A
  description of each parameter follows.

    :rewrite -- default t

  When non-[47mnil[0m, instructs the system to use ACL2's rewriter (or,
  something close to it) during simplification.

    :linear -- default t (except, default nil if :rewrite has value nil)

  When non-[47mnil[0m, instructs the system to use ACL2's linear arithmetic
  (see [LINEAR-ARITHMETIC]) to build a suitable context (a ``linear
  pot list'') from the assumptions (the top-level hypotheses and the
  governors (see [ACL2-PC::HYPS])).  Note that linear arithmetic is
  used by the rewriter regardless; by default, when [47m:s[0m invokes the
  rewriter then linear arithmetic can take advantage of the
  information in the top-level hypotheses and the governors.

    :normalize -- default t

  When non-[47mnil[0m, instructs the system to use [normalization] (as
  described above) during simplification.

    :backchain-limit -- default 0

  Sets the number of recursive calls to the rewriter that are allowed
  for backchaining.  Even with the default of 0, some reasoning is
  allowed (technically speaking, type reasoning (see
  [TYPE-REASONING]) is allowed) in the relieving of hypotheses.  The
  value should be [47mnil[0m or a non-negative integer, and limits
  backchaining only for rewriting, not for type reasoning.

    :repeat -- default 0

  Sets the number of times the current term is to be rewritten.  If
  this value is [47mt[0m, then the default is used (as specified by the
  constant [47m*default-s-repeat-limit*[0m).

    :in-theory, :hands-off, :expand

  These have their usual meaning; see [hints].

  [31;1mRemark:[0m if conditional rewrite rules are used that cause case splits
  because of the use of [47mforce[0m, then appropriate new subgoals will be
  created, i.e., with the same current subterm (and address) but with
  each new (forced) hypothesis being negated and then used to create
  a corresponding new subgoal.  In that case, the current goal will
  have all such new hypotheses added to the list of top-level
  hypotheses.")
 (ACL2-PC::S-PROP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) simplify propositionally

    Example:
    s-prop

    General Form:
    (s-prop &rest names)

  Simplify, using the default settings for [47ms[0m (which include
  [normalization] and rewriting without real backchaining), but with
  respect to a theory in which only basic functions and rules (the
  ones in [47m(theory 'minimal-theory)[0m), together with the names (or
  parenthesized names) in the [47m&rest[0m argument [47mnames[0m, are enabled.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::s].")
 (ACL2-PC::SAVE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) save the proof-builder state (state-stack)

    Example:
    (save lemma3-attempt)

    General Form:
    (save &optional name do-it-flg)

  Saves the current state of the interactive proof-builder by
  ``associating'' it with the given name, which must be a symbol.
  Submit [47m(retrieve name)[0m to Lisp to get back to this proof-builder
  state.  If [47mverify[0m was originally supplied with an event name, then
  the argument can be omitted in favor of that name as the default.

  [31;1mRemark[0m that if a [47msave[0m has already been done with the indicated name
  (or the default event name), then the user will be queried
  regarding whether to go ahead with the save --- except, if
  [47mdo-it-flg[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then there will be no query and
  the [47msave[0m will be effected.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::retrieve] and [ACL2-pc::unsave].")
 (ACL2-PC::SEQUENCE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(meta) run the given list of instructions according to a multitude of
  options

    Example:
    (sequence (induct p prove) t)

  This is a very general command that is used to define other
  sequencing commands; see [ACL2-pc::do-all], [ACL2-pc::do-strict],
  [ACL2-pc::protect], and [ACL2-pc::succeed].

    General Form:
    (sequence
     instruction-list
     &optional
     strict-flg protect-flg success-expr no-prompt-flg no-restore-flg)

  Each instruction in the list [47minstruction-list[0m is run, and the
  instruction ``succeeds'' if every instruction in [47minstruction-list[0m
  ``succeeds''.  However, it might ``succeed'' even if some
  instructions in the list ``fail''; more generally, the various
  arguments control a number of aspects of the running of the
  instructions.  All this is explained in the paragraphs below.
  First we embark on a general discussion of the instruction
  interpreter, including the notions of ``succeed'' and ``fail''.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m The arguments are [31;1mnot[0m evaluated, except (in a sense) for
  [47msuccess-expr[0m, as described below.

  Each primitive and meta instruction can be thought of as returning an
  [error-triple], say [47m(erp val state)[0m.  An instruction (primitive or
  meta) ``succeeds'' if [47merp[0m is [47mnil[0m and [47mval[0m is not [47mnil[0m; otherwise it
  ``fails''.  (When we use the words ``succeed'' or ``fail'' in this
  technical sense, we'll always include them in double quotes.)  If
  an instruction ``fails,'' we say that the failure is ``soft'' if
  [47merp[0m is [47mnil[0m; otherwise the failure is ``hard''.  The [47msequence[0m
  command gives the user control over how to treat ``success'' and
  ``failure'' when sequencing instructions, though we have created a
  number of handy macro commands for this purpose, notably [47mdo-all[0m,
  [47mdo-strict[0m and [47mprotect[0m.

  Here is precisely what happens when a [47msequence[0m instruction is run.
  The instruction interpreter is run on the instructions supplied in
  the argument [47minstruction-list[0m (in order).  The interpreter halts
  the first time there is a hard ``failure.'' except that if
  [47mstrict-flg[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then the interpreter halts the
  first time there is any ``failure.'' The error triple [47m(erp val
  state)[0m returned by the [47msequence[0m instruction is the triple returned
  by the last instruction executed (or, the triple [47m(nil t state)[0m if
  [47minstruction-list[0m is [47mnil[0m), except for the following provision.  If
  [47msuccess-expr[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then it is evaluated with the
  state global variables [47mpc-erp[0m and [47mpc-val[0m (in the \"ACL2\" package)
  bound to the corresponding components of the error triple returned
  (as described above).  At least two values should be returned, and
  the first two of these will be substituted for [47merp[0m and [47mval[0m in the
  triple finally returned by [47msequence[0m.  For example, if [47msuccess-expr[0m
  is [47m(mv erp val)[0m, then no change will be made to the error triple,
  and if instead it is [47m(mv nil t)[0m, then the [47msequence[0m instruction will
  ``succeed''.

  That concludes the description of the error triple returned by a
  [47msequence[0m instruction, but it remains to explain the effects of the
  arguments [47mprotect-flg[0m and [47mno-prompt-flg[0m.

  If [47mprotect-flg[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m and if also the instruction
  ``fails'' (i.e., the error component of the triple is not [47mnil[0m or
  the value component is [47mnil[0m), then the state is reverted so that the
  interactive proof-builder's state (including the behavior of
  [47mrestore[0m) is set back to what it was before the [47msequence[0m instruction
  was executed.  Otherwise, unless [47mno-restore-flg[0m is set, the state
  is changed so that the [47mrestore[0m command will now undo the effect of
  this [47msequence[0m instruction (even if there were nested calls to
  [47msequence[0m).

  Finally, as each instruction in [47minstruction-list[0m is executed, the
  prompt and that instruction will be printed, unless the global
  state variable [47mpc-print-prompt-and-instr-flg[0m is unbound or [47mnil[0m and
  the parameter [47mno-prompt-flg[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::SHOW-ABBREVIATIONS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) display the current abbreviations

    Examples:
    (show-abbreviations v w)
       -- assuming that v and w currently abbreviate terms,
          then this instruction displays them together with
          the terms they abbreviate
    show-abbreviations
       -- display all abbreviations

  Also see [ACL2-pc::add-abbreviation] for a general discussion of
  abbreviations and see [ACL2-pc::remove-abbreviations].

    General Form:
    (show-abbreviations &rest vars)

  Display each argument in [47mvars[0m together with the term it abbreviates
  (if any).  If there are no arguments, i.e. the instruction is
  simply [47mshow-abbreviations[0m, then display all abbreviations together
  with the terms they abbreviate.

  If the term abbreviated by a variable, say [47mv[0m, contains a proper
  subterm that is also abbreviate by (another) variable, then both
  the unabbreviated term and the abbreviated term (but not using [47m(?
  v)[0m to abbreviate the term) are displayed with together with [47mv[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::SHOW-LINEARS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) display the applicable [linear] rules

    Example:
    show-linears

    General Form:
    (show-linears &optional rule-id enabled-only-flg)

  This command displays [linear] rules with a trigger term that matches
  the current subterm, and shows how they can be applied.  This
  command is analogous to the [47mshow-rewrites[0m [proof-builder] command;
  see [ACL2-pc::show-rewrites].  Also see [ACL2-pc::apply-linear] for
  how to apply [linear] rules.")
 (ACL2-PC::SHOW-REWRITES
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) display the applicable [rewrite] rules

    Example:
    show-rewrites

    General Form:
    (show-rewrites &optional rule-id enabled-only-flg)

  This command displays [rewrite] rules whose left-hand side matches
  the current subterm, and shows how that command can be applied.
  For each rule displayed, hypotheses are shown that would need to be
  proved after the rule is applied.  Note that hypotheses are omitted
  from the display when the system can trivially verify that they
  hold; to see all hypotheses for each rule in a display that is
  independent of the arguments of the current subterm, use the [47mpl[0m or
  [47mpr[0m command.

  Here are details on the arguments and the output.  If [47mrule-id[0m is
  supplied and is a name (non-[47mnil[0m symbol) or a [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or
  [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m [rune], then only the corresponding rewrite rule(s)
  will be displayed, while if [47mrule-id[0m is a positive integer [47mn[0m, then
  only the [47mn[0mth rule that would be in the list is displayed.  In each
  case, the display will point out when a rule is currently disabled
  (in the interactive environment), except that if [47menabled-only-flg[0m
  is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then disabled rules will not be displayed
  at all.  Finally, among the free variables of any rule (see
  [free-variables]), those that would remain free if the rule were
  applied will be displayed.  Also see [rewrite].")
 (ACL2-PC::SHOW-TYPE-PRESCRIPTIONS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) display the applicable [type-prescription] rules

    Example:
    show-type-prescriptions

    General Form:
    (show-type-prescriptions &optional rule-id)

  Display [type-prescription] rules that apply to the current subterm.
  If [47mrule-id[0m is supplied and is a name (non-[47mnil[0m symbol) or a
  [47m:[0m[47m[rewrite][0m or [47m:[0m[47m[definition][0m [rune], then only the corresponding
  rewrite rule(s) will be displayed.  In each case, the display will
  point out when a rule is currently disabled (in the interactive
  environment).  Also see [type-prescription].")
 (ACL2-PC::SKIP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) ``succeed'' without doing anything

    Example and General Form:
    skip

  Make no change in the state-stack, but ``succeed''.  Same as
  [47m(sequence nil)[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::SL
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) simplify with lemmas

    Examples:
    sl
    (sl 3)

    General Form:
    (sl &optional backchain-limit)

  Simplify, but with all function definitions disabled (see
  [function-theory] in the top-level ACL2 loop), except for a few
  basic functions (the ones in [47m(theory 'minimal-theory)[0m).  The
  [47mbackchain-limit[0m has a default of 0, but if it is supplied and not
  [47mnil[0m, then it should be a nonnegative integer; see [ACL2-pc::s].

  WARNING: This command completely ignores [47min-theory[0m commands that are
  executed inside the interactive [proof-builder].")
 (ACL2-PC::SLS
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) same as SHOW-LINEARS

    Example:
    sls

    General Form:
    (sls &optional rule-id enabled-only-flg)

  See [ACL2-pc::show-linears].  NOTE: In analogy to the [47msr[0m abbreviation
  for [47mshow-rewrites[0m, one might expect this command to be [47msl[0m; but that
  name was taken (``simplify with lemmas'') before [47msls[0m was
  implemented.")
 (ACL2-PC::SPLIT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) split the current goal into cases

    Example:
    split

  For example, if the current goal has one hypothesis [47m(or x y)[0m and a
  conclusion of [47m(and a b)[0m, then [47msplit[0m will create four new goals:

    one with hypothesis X and conclusion A
    one with hypothesis X and conclusion B
    one with hypothesis Y and conclusion A
    one with hypothesis Y and conclusion B.

    General Form:
    SPLIT

  Replace the current goal by subgoals whose conjunction is equivalent
  (primarily by propositional reasoning) to the original goal, where
  each such goal cannot be similarly split.

  [31;1mRemark:[0m The new goals will all have their hypotheses promoted; in
  particular, no conclusion will have a top function symbol of
  [47mimplies[0m.  Also note that [47msplit[0m will fail if there is exactly one
  new goal created and it is the same as the existing current goal.

  The way [47msplit[0m really works is to call the ACL2 theorem prover with
  only simplification (and preprocessing) turned on, and with only a
  few built-in functions (especially, propositional ones) enabled,
  namely, the ones in the list [47m(theory 'minimal-theory)[0m.  However,
  because the prover is called, [type-reasoning] can be used to
  eliminate some cases.  For example, if [47m(true-listp x)[0m is in the
  hypotheses, then probably [47m(true-listp (cdr x))[0m will be reduced to
  [47mt[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::SR
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) same as SHOW-REWRITES

    Example:
    sr

    General Form:
    (sr &optional rule-id enabled-only-flg)

  See [ACL2-pc::show-rewrites].")
 (ACL2-PC::ST
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) same as SHOW-TYPE-PRESCRIPTIONS

    Example:
    sr

    General Form:
    (st &optional rule-id)

  See [ACL2-pc::show-type-prescriptions].")
 (ACL2-PC::SUCCEED
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) run the given instructions, and ``succeed''

    Example:
    (succeed induct p prove)

    General Form:
    (succeed &rest instruction-list)

  Run the indicated instructions until there is a hard ``failure'', and
  ``succeed''.  (See [ACL2-pc::sequence] for an explanation of
  ``success'' and ``failure''.)")
 (ACL2-PC::TH
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(macro) print the top-level hypotheses and the current subterm

    Examples:
    th               -- print all (top-level) hypotheses and the current
                        subterm
    (th (1 3) (2 4)) -- print hypotheses 1 and 3 and governors 2 and 4,
                        and the current subterm
    (th (1 3) t)     -- print hypotheses 1 and 3 and all governors, and
                        the current subterm

    General Form:
    (th &optional hyps-indices govs-indices)

  Print hypotheses and the current subterm.  The printing of hypotheses
  (and perhaps governors) are controlled as in the [47mhyps[0m command; see
  [ACL2-pc::hyps].

  Historical note: The name [47mth[0m is adapted from the Gypsy Verification
  Environment, where [47mth[0m abbreviates the command [47mtheorem[0m, which says
  to print information on the current goal.")
 (ACL2-PC::THEN
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) apply one instruction to current goal and another to new
  subgoals

    Example:
    (then induct prove)

    General Form:
    (then first-instruction &optional completion must-succeed-flg)

  Run [47mfirst-instruction[0m, and then run [47mcompletion[0m (another instruction)
  on each subgoal created by [47mfirst-instruction[0m.  If [47mmust-succeed-flg[0m
  is supplied and not [47mnil[0m, then halt at the first ``failure'' and
  remove the effects of the invocation of [47mcompletion[0m that ``failed''.

  The default for completion is [47mreduce[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::TOP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) move to the top of the goal

    Example and General Form:
    top

  For example, if the conclusion is [47m(= x (* (- y) z))[0m and the current
  subterm is [47my[0m, then after executing [47mtop[0m, the current subterm will be
  the same as the conclusion, i.e., [47m(= x (* (- y) z))[0m.

  [47mTop[0m is the same as [47m(up n)[0m, where [47mn[0m is the number of times one needs
  to execute [47mup[0m in order to get to the top of the conclusion.  The
  [47mtop[0m command fails if one is already at the top of the conclusion.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::up], [ACL2-pc::dive], [ACL2-pc::nx], and
  [ACL2-pc::bk].")
 (ACL2-PC::TYPE-ALIST
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) display the [type-alist] from the current context

    Examples:
    (type-alist nil t nil) ; display type-alist based on governors (default)
    type-alist             ; same as (type-alist nil t) -- governors only
    (type-alist t t)       ; display type-alist based on conclusion and governors
    (type-alist t t t)     ; as above, but also display forward-chaining report
    type-alist             ; same as (type-alist nil t) -- governors only
    (type-alist nil)       ; same as (type-alist nil t) -- governors only
    (type-alist t)         ; same as (type-alist t nil) -- conclusion only
    (type-alist nil nil)   ; based on neither conclusion nor governors
    (type-alist nil t nil nil)  ; same as type-alist (default) -- governors only
    (type-alist nil t nil :raw) ; governors only, raw alist format
    (type-alist nil t nil t)    ; governors only, simple alist format

    General Form:
    (type-alist &optional concl-flg govs-flg fc-report-flg alistp)

  where if [47mgovs-flg[0m is omitted then it defaults to [47m(not concl-flg)[0m, and
  each of the other optional arguments defaults to [47mnil[0m.

  Display the current assumptions as a [type-alist].  Note that this
  display includes the result of forward chaining.  When
  [47mfc-report-flg[0m is supplied a non-[47mnil[0m value, the display also
  includes a forward-chaining report; otherwise,the presence or
  absence of such a report is controlled by the usual global settings
  (see [forward-chaining-reports]).  By default, the display is
  organized by type, with terms shown of each type; but when [47malistp[0m
  is [47m:raw[0m then the underlying type-alist structure is shown, which is
  made more user-friendly when any other non-[47mnil[0m value of [47malistp[0m is
  provided.

  There are two basic reasons contemplated for using this command.

  1. The theorem prover has failed (either outside the interactive
  proof-builder or using a proof-builder command such as [47mbash[0m or
  [47mreduce[0m and you want to debug by getting an idea of what the prover
  knows about the context.

      a. You really are interested in the context for the current term.
      Include hypotheses and governors (i.e., accounting for tests of
      surrounding [47mif[0m-expressions that must be true or false) but not
      the current conclusion (which the theorem prover's heuristics
      would generally ignore for contextual information).  Command:
      [47m(type-alist nil t)[0m ; equivalently, [47mtype-alist[0m or [47m(type-alist nil)[0m

      b. You are not thinking in particular about the current term; you
      just want to get an idea of the context that the prover would
      build at the top-level, for forward-chaining.  Incorporate the
      conclusion but not the governors.  Command:
      [47m(type-alist t nil)[0m ; equivalently, [47m(type-alist t)[0m

  2. You intend to use one of the interactive [proof-builder-commands]
  that does simplification, such as [47ms[0m or [47mx[0m, and you want to see the
  context.  Then include the surrounding [47mif[0m-term governors but not
  the goal's conclusion.  Command:
  [47m(type-alist nil t)[0m ; equivalently, [47mtype-alist[0m or [47m(type-alist nil)[0m

  See [type-set] (also see [type-prescription]) for information about
  ACL2's type system, which can assist in understanding the output of
  the [47mtype-alist[0m command.")
 (ACL2-PC::UNDO
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(meta) undo some instructions

    Examples:
    (undo 7)
    undo

    General Forms:

    (undo n) -- Undo the last n instructions.  The argument n should be
                a positive integer.

    undo     -- Same as (undo 1).

  [31;1mRemark:[0m To remove the effect of an [47mundo[0m command, use [47mrestore[0m; see
  [ACL2-pc::restore].

  [31;1mRemark:[0m If the argument [47mn[0m is greater than the total number of
  interactive instructions in the current session, then [47m(undo n)[0m will
  simply take you back to the start of the session.

  The [47mundo[0m meta command always ``succeeds''; it returns [47m(mv nil t
  state)[0m unless its optional argument is supplied and of the wrong
  type (i.e. not a positive integer) or there are no instructions to
  undo.")
 (ACL2-PC::UNSAVE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(macro) remove a proof-builder state

    Example:
    (unsave assoc-of-append)

    General Form:
    (unsave &optional name)

  Eliminates the association of an interactive proof-builder state with
  [47mname[0m, if [47mname[0m is supplied and not [47mnil[0m.  The name may be [47mnil[0m or not
  supplied, in which case it defaults to the event name supplied with
  the original call to [47mverify[0m (if there is one --- otherwise, the
  instruction ``fails'' and there is no change).  The ACL2 function
  [47munsave[0m may also be executed outside the interactive loop, with the
  same syntax.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::save] and [ACL2-pc::retrieve].")
 (ACL2-PC::UP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(primitive) move to the parent (or some ancestor) of the current
  subterm

    Examples:  if the conclusion is (= x (* (- y) z)) and the
               current subterm is y, then we have:
    up or (up 1) -- the current subterm becomes (- y)
    (up 2)       -- the current subterm becomes (* (- y) z)
    (up 3)       -- the current subterm becomes the entire conclusion
    (up 4)       -- no change; can't go up that many levels

    General Form:
    (up &optional n)

  Move up [47mn[0m levels in the conclusion from the current subterm, where [47mn[0m
  is a positive integer.  If [47mn[0m is not supplied or is [47mnil[0m, then move
  up one level, i.e., treat the instruction as [47m(up 1)[0m.

  Also see [ACL2-pc::dive], [ACL2-pc::top], [ACL2-pc::nx], and
  [ACL2-pc::bk].")
 (ACL2-PC::USE
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS-SHORT-LIST)
  "(atomic macro) use a lemma instance

    Example:
    (USE true-listp-append
         (:instance assoc-of-append (x a) (y b) (z c)))
    -- Add two top-level hypotheses, one the lemma called
       true-listp-append, and the other an instance of the lemma called
       assoc-of-append by the substitution in which x is assigned a, y
       is assigned b, and z is assigned c.

    General Form:
    (use &rest args)

  Add the given lemma instances to the list of top-level hypotheses.
  See [hints] for the syntax of [47m:use[0m hints in [47mdefthm[0m, which is
  essentially the same as the syntax here (see the example above).

  This command calls the [47mprove[0m command, and hence should only be used
  at the top of the conclusion.")
 (ACL2-PC::WRAP
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) execute the indicated instructions and combine all the
  new goals

    Example:
    (wrap induct) ; induct, then replace first new goal by the conjunction of all
                  ; the new goals, and drop all new goals after the first

    General Form:
    (wrap &rest instrs)

  First the instructions in [47minstrs[0m are executed, as in [47mdo-all[0m.  If this
  ``fails'' then no additional action is taken.  Otherwise, the
  current goal after execution of [47minstrs[0m is conjoined with all
  ``new'' goals, in the sense that their names are not among the
  names of goals at the time [47minstrs[0m was begun.  This conjunction
  becomes the new current goal and those ``new'' goals are dropped.

  See the code for the interactive [proof-builder] command,
  [47mwrap-induct[0m, for an example of the use of [47mwrap[0m.")
 (ACL2-PC::WRAP-INDUCT
  (PROOF-BUILDER-COMMANDS)
  "(atomic macro) same as induct, but create a single goal

    Examples:
    wrap-induct
    (wrap-induct t)
    (wrap-induct (append (reverse x) y))

    General Form:
